property_value: dcterms:title "IUPAC Gold Book Compendium of Chemical Terminology" xsd:string
property_value: dcterms:license "CC-BY-SA-4.0" xsd:string
property_value: dcterms:description "The Compendium is popularly referred to as the \"Gold Book\"\, in recognition of the contribution of the late Victor Gold\, who initiated work on the first edition. It is one of the series of IUPAC \"Colour Books\" on chemical nomenclature\, terminology\, symbols and units \(see the list of source documents\)\, and collects together terminology definitions from IUPAC recommendations already published in Pure and Applied Chemistry and in the other Colour Books.\n\nTerminology definitions published by IUPAC are drafted by international committees of experts in the appropriate chemistry sub-disciplines\, and ratified by IUPAC's Interdivisional Committee on Terminology\, Nomenclature and Symbols \(ICTNS\). In this edition of the Compendium these IUPAC-approved definitions are supplemented with some definitions from ISO and from the International Vocabulary of Basic and General Terms in Metrology; both these sources are recognised by IUPAC as authoritative. The result is a collection of nearly 7000 terms\, with authoritative definitions\, spanning the whole range of chemistry.\n\nThe API can be accessed \[here\]\(https\://goldbook.iupac.org/pages/api\)" xsd:string
property_value: foaf:homepage "https\://goldbook.iupac.org" xsd:anyURI
property_value: doap:mailing-list "goldbook@iupac.org" xsd:string
property_value: doap:maintainer orcid:0000-0002-0703-7776 ! Stuart Chalk

[Term]
id: goldbook:08003
name: analytical chemistry
def: "Scientific discipline that develops and applies strategies\, instruments\, and procedures to obtain information on the composition and nature of matter in space and time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08004
name: chemical analysis
def: "Application of analytical chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08005
name: additive matrix effect
def: "Matrix effect that is independent of the measured quantity value of the measurand."
synonym: "background interference" []
synonym: "translational matrix effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08006
name: analytical run
def: "Set of measurements of the same quantity performed under repeatability conditions of measurement."
synonym: "run" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08007
name: background indication
def: "Indication obtained from a phenomenon\, body\, or substance similar to the one under investigation but for which a quantity of interest is supposed not to be present or is not contributing to the indication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08008
name: blank correction
def: "Step in a measurement procedure in which the effect of a blank indication is removed from an indication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08009
name: blank indication
def: "Indication obtained from a sample of blank material under the measurement conditions for the measurand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08010
name: blank material
def: "Material which contains no\, or as little as possible\, of the analyte of interest used in measurement to establish a blank indication."
synonym: "blank" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08011
name: calibration certificate
def: "Document issued by a technically competent organization providing information about a calibration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08012
name: calibration interval
def: "Time period between calibrations over which the performance of the measuring system may be expected to meet specified requirements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08013
name: certified property value
def: "Property value of a reference material\, accompanied by statements of associated uncertainty and traceability and identified as a certified property value in the reference material certificate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08014
name: chemical purity
def: "Mass \(amount of substance or number of entities\) of a specified component divided by the mass \(amount of substance or number of entities\, respectively\) of the system."
synonym: "purity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08015
name: consensus property value
def: "Property value derived from a collection of results."
synonym: "consensus value" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08016
name: conventional quantity value
def: "Quantity value attributed by agreement to a quantity for a given purpose."
synonym: "conventional value" []
synonym: "conventional value of a quantity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08017
name: critical value
def: "Measured quantity value for a quantity of a component in a material\, above which the component is declared to be present."
synonym: "critical level" []
synonym: "decision level" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08018
name: determination
def: "Set of operations that are carried out on an object in order to provide qualitative or quantitative information about this object."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08019
name: interferent
def: "Component of the matrix that embodies an influence quantity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08020
name: intermediate measurement precision
def: "Measurement precision under intermediate precision conditions of measurement."
synonym: "intermediate precision" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08021
name: laboratory bias
def: "Contribution to measurement bias that is attributed to systematic effects on measurement results made in a laboratory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08022
name: limit of quantification
def: "Smallest or largest measured quantity value\, obtained by a given measurement procedure\, which fulfils a requirement of fitness for purpose."
synonym: "quantification limit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08023
name: linearity of a measuring system
def: "Ability of a measuring system to provide measured quantity values that are directly proportional to the quantity value of the measurand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08024
name: linearity of calibration
def: "Closeness of agreement between indications obtained using calibrators in the first step of a calibration and indications predicted by the calibration function for the calibrators’ reference quantity values."
synonym: "calibration linearity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08025
name: material homogeneity
def: "Uniform structure or composition of a material with respect to one or more specified properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08026
name: material recovery
def: "Mass \(volume\, amount of substance\) of a specified component isolated from a system divided by the mass \(volume\, amount of substance\) of the system prior to isolation."
synonym: "recovery" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08027
name: material stability
def: "Constancy of a property of a material over time."
synonym: "stability" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08028
name: measurement procedure
def: "Detailed description of a measurement according to one or more measurement principles and to a given measurement method\, based on a measurement model and including any calculation to obtain a measurement result."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08029
name: measurement procedure bias
def: "Contribution to measurement bias that is attributed to systematic effects on measurement results made according to a measurement procedure."
synonym: "measurement method bias" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08030
name: measurement procedure with standard addition
def: "Measurement procedure in which indications are obtained for an analytical sample as well as for analytical samples with addition\(s\) of a measurement standard."
synonym: "standard addition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08031
name: measurement reproducibility
def: "Measurement precision under reproducibility conditions of measurement."
synonym: "reproducibility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08032
name: metrological compatibility of measurement results
def: "Property of a set of measurement results for a specified measurand\, such that the absolute value of the difference of any pair of measured quantity values from two different measurement results is smaller than some chosen multiple of the standard measurement uncertainty of that difference."
synonym: "metrological compatibility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08033
name: metrological equivalence of measurement results
def: "Property of two or more measurement results for a given measurand which have metrological compatibility of measurement results\, so that they are each acceptable for the same specified intended use."
synonym: "equivalence of measurement results" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08034
name: minimum sample size
def: "Lower limit of sample size stipulated in documentation taken for chemical analysis."
synonym: "minimum sample intake" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08035
name: multiplicative matrix effect
def: "Matrix effect that is proportional to the measured quantity value of the measurand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08036
name: non-certified property value
def: "Property value that is provided for information only but is not certified by a reference material producer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08037
name: object traceability
def: "Ability to trace the history\, application\, or location of an object."
synonym: "traceability (of an object)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08038
name: property value assignment
def: "Determination of property values obtained in the course of the production of a reference material."
synonym: "value assignment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08039
name: recovered quantity value ratio
def: "Measured quantity value relating a component to a system divided by a reference value."
synonym: "analytical recovery" []
synonym: "recovery" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08040
name: repeatability condition of measurement
def: "Condition of measurement\, out of a set of conditions that includes the same measurement procedure\, same operators\, same measuring system\, same operating conditions\, and same location\, and replicate measurements on the same or similar objects over a short period of time."
synonym: "repeatability condition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08041
name: run bias
def: "Contribution to measurement bias that is attributed to systematic effects on measurement results made in a single analytical run."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08042
name: sample pre-treatment
def: "Collective noun for all procedures used for conditioning a sample to a defined state which allows subsequent chemical analysis or long-term storage \(see material stability\)."
synonym: "sample preparation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08043
name: sampling
def: "Act of taking or constituting a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08044
name: sampling increment
def: "Individual portion of material collected by a single operation of a sampling device."
synonym: "increment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08045
name: sampling target
def: "Portion of the material\, at a particular time\, that the sample is intended to represent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08046
name: shelf life
def: "Time interval during which a reference material producer warrants the material stability of the reference material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08047
name: spike
def: "Material with known quantity values added to an analytical sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08048
name: systematic effect
def: "Recognized effect of an influence quantity on a measured quantity value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08049
name: testing
def: "Determination of one or more characteristics of an object of conformity assessment\, according to a specified procedure."
synonym: "test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08050
name: unitary quantity
def: "Quantity with a magnitude expressed as a reference quantity multiplied by a number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08051
name: working interval
def: "Set of quantity values over which a measuring instrument or measuring system provides measurement results with acceptable measurement uncertainty under defined conditions."
synonym: "working range" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08052
name: acceptance interval
def: "Interval of permissible measured quantity values."
synonym: "acceptance zone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08053
name: acceptance limit
def: "Specified upper or lower bound of permissible measured quantity values."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08054
name: accreditation of a laboratory
def: "Third-party attestation related to a laboratory conveying formal demonstration of its competence to carry out specific conformity assessment tasks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08055
name: analytical selectivity
def: "Selectivity of a measuring system in analytical chemistry."
synonym: "selectivity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08056
name: assessor
def: "Person\, assigned by an accreditation body\, with relevant professional expertise and experience who can determine the competence of a laboratory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08057
name: assigned value
def: "Value attributed to a particular property of an item and that serves as an agreed reference for comparison."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08058
name: audit
def: "Systematic\, independent\, and documented process for obtaining objective evidence and evaluating it objectively to determine the extent to which the audit criteria are fulfilled."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08059
name: candidate certified reference material
def: "Reference material subjected to a process of reference material certification."
synonym: "candidate CRM" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08060
name: candidate reference material
def: "Material subjected to the procedures necessary to show its fitness for intended use \(see fitness for purpose\)."
synonym: "candidate RM" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08061
name: capability
def: "Ability of an object to realize an output that will fulfil the requirements for that output."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08062
name: cause-and-effect diagram
def: "Diagram indicating the causes of a specific event or condition."
synonym: "Ishikawa diagram" []
synonym: "fishbone diagram" []
synonym: "herringbone diagram" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08063
name: certification
def: "Third-party attestation related to products\, processes\, systems\, or persons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08064
name: commutability of a reference material
def: "Property of a reference material\, demonstrated by the closeness of agreement between the relation among the measurement results for a stated quantity in this material\, obtained according to two given measurement procedures\, and the relation obtained among the measurement results for other specified materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08065
name: competence
def: "Ability to apply knowledge and skills to achieve intended results."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08066
name: conformance probability
def: "Probability that an item fulfils a specified requirement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08067
name: conformity assessment
def: "Activity to determine whether specified requirements relating to a product\, process\, system\, person\, or body are fulfilled."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08068
name: control chart
def: "Chart with control limits on which some statistical measure related to a series of samples is plotted in a particular order to steer the process with respect to that measure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08069
name: control limit
def: "Value defining an intended level of stability for a process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08070
name: coordinator of an interlaboratory comparison
def: "Person\(s\) with responsibility for organizing and managing all of the activities involved in the operation of an interlaboratory comparison."
synonym: "coordinator" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08071
name: cumulative sum control chart
def: "Control chart where the cumulative sum of deviations of successive measured quantity values from a reference quantity value is plotted to detect shifts in the level of the measurand."
synonym: "CUSUM chart" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08072
name: decision rule in conformity assessment
def: "Documented rule that describes how measurement uncertainty will be accounted for with regard to accepting or rejecting an item\, given a specified requirement and a measured quantity value."
synonym: "decision rule" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08073
name: fitness for purpose
def: "Ability of a product\, process\, or service to serve a defined purpose under specific conditions."
synonym: "fitness for intended use" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08074
name: good laboratory practice
def: "Quality system concerned with the organizational process\, and conditions under which non-clinical health and environmental safety studies are planned\, performed\, monitored\, recorded\, archived\, and reported."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08075
name: good manufacturing practice
def: "Combination of manufacturing and quality procedures aimed at ensuring that products are consistently manufactured to their specified requirements and to avoid contamination of the product by internal or external sources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08076
name: guard band
def: "Interval between a tolerance limit and a corresponding acceptance limit.\nFigure 1\: Guarded acceptance. Two-sided acceptance interval created by reducing the tolerance interval on either side by a guard band\, thus reducing the probability of falsely accepting a non-conforming item.Figure 2\: Guarded rejection. Two-sided acceptance interval created by increasing the tolerance interval on either side by a guard band\, thus reducing the probability of falsely rejecting a conforming item."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08077
name: Horwitz equation
def: "Empirical relationship providing a standard deviation under reproducibility conditions of measurement \(\\\(s_\{\\rm\{R\,H\}\}\\\)\) to be expected for typical measurements of the mass fraction \(\\\(w\\\)\) of a component in a material\: \\\(s_\{\\rm\{R\,H\}\} = 0.02 w^\{0.8495\}\\\)."
synonym: "Horwitz curve" []
synonym: "Horwitz horn curve" []
synonym: "Horwitz trumpet curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08078
name: Horwitz ratio
def: "Standard deviation of a mass fraction of a component under reproducibility conditions of measurement divided by the corresponding standard deviation calculated from the Horwitz equation.\nFigure 1\: Horwitz curve \(trumpet\, horn\). Expected relative standard deviation under reproducibility conditions of measurement\, \\\(\\pm s_\{\\rm\{R\,H\}\}/w\\\) plotted against logarithm of mass fraction\, \\\(w\\\)."
synonym: "Horrat" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08079
name: in-house reference material
def: "Reference material produced and used by an analytical laboratory."
synonym: "laboratory reference material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08080
name: inspection
def: "Examination of a product\, process\, service\, or installation or their design and determination of conformity with specific requirements or on the basis of professional judgment\, with general requirements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08081
name: interlaboratory comparison
def: "Organization\, performance\, and evaluation of measurements or tests on the same or similar items by two or more laboratories in accordance with predetermined conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08082
name: internal quality control
def: "Quality control within a specified laboratory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08083
name: intralaboratory comparison
def: "Organization\, performance\, and evaluation of measurements or tests on the same or similar items within the same laboratory in accordance with predetermined conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08084
name: laboratory information management system
def: "Information system which can provide services to one or more measuring systems."
synonym: "laboratory information system" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08085
name: material characterization study
def: "Intralaboratory comparison or interlaboratory comparison with the aim of assigning a reference quantity value to a quantity with stated measurement uncertainty or value to a reference nominal property value with stated examination uncertainty."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08086
name: matrix reference material
def: "Reference material that is characteristic of a real sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08087
name: measurement capability
def: "Ability to measure a quantity in a specified interval of quantity values\, embodied in a specified material\, as demonstrated by measurement uncertainty."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08088
name: measurement precision control material
def: "Quality control material with or without reference quantity value used in quality control to assess measurement precision."
synonym: "precision control material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08089
name: measurement trueness control material
def: "Quality control material with reference quantity value used in quality control to estimate a systematic measurement error."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08090
name: method performance study
def: "Intra- or interlaboratory study of one or more performance characteristics of a measurement procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08091
name: organization
def: "Person or group of people that has its own functions with responsibilities\, authorities\, and relationships to achieve its objectives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08092
name: out-of-control criteria
def: "Set of decision rules for identifying the presence of special causes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08093
name: participant in an interlaboratory comparison
def: "Laboratory\, organization\, or person that receives test items and submits results for review by the coordinator of an interlaboratory comparison."
synonym: "participant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08094
name: performance characteristic of a measurement procedure
def: "Aspect of a measurement procedure that may be quantitatively investigated and assessed as part of procedure validation."
synonym: "performance characteristic" []
synonym: "performance characteristic of a measurement method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08095
name: performance score
def: "Statistic\, derived from a result reported by a participant in a proficiency testing scheme\, used in interpretation of participant performance and to allow comparison with defined objectives."
synonym: "performance statistic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08096
name: precision limit
def: "Quantity value\, less than or equal to what the absolute difference between two measured quantity values obtained under specified conditions of measurement is expected to be with a given probability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08097
name: procedure validation
def: "Process of defining an analytical requirement and confirming that the procedure under consideration has capabilities consistent with that requirement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08098
name: procedure verification
def: "Confirmation\, through the provision of objective evidence\, that the procedure fulfils specified requirements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08099
name: process in a state of statistical control
def: "Process subject only to random causes."
synonym: "process in statistical control" []
synonym: "stable process" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08100
name: proficiency test item
def: "Sample\, product\, artefact\, reference material\, piece of equipment\, measurement standard\, data set\, or other information used for proficiency testing."
synonym: "PT item" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08101
name: proficiency testing
def: "Evaluation of the performance of a participant in an interlaboratory comparison against pre-established criteria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08102
name: proficiency testing provider
def: "Organization which takes responsibility for all tasks in the development and operation of a proficiency testing scheme."
synonym: "PT provider" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08103
name: proficiency testing round
def: "Single complete sequence from distribution of proficiency test items to reporting of the evaluation results to the participants."
synonym: "PT round" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08104
name: proficiency testing scheme
def: "Proficiency testing designed and operated in one or more proficiency testing rounds for a specified area of testing\, measurement\, calibration\, or inspection."
synonym: "PT scheme" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08105
name: protocol for interlaboratory comparison
def: "Detailed set of instructions describing the objectives\, design\, conduct\, and reporting of an interlaboratory comparison."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08106
name: quality
def: "Degree to which a set of inherent characteristics of an object fulfils requirements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08107
name: quality control material
def: "Material used for the purposes of quality control and subjected to the same or part of the same measurement procedure as that used for analytical samples."
synonym: "control material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08108
name: quality improvement
def: "Part of quality management focused on increasing the ability to fulfil requirements for quality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08109
name: quality management
def: "Coordinated activities to direct and control an organization with regard to quality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08110
name: quality management system
def: "Part of a set of interrelated or interacting elements of an organization to establish quality policies and quality objectives and processes to achieve those objectives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08111
name: quality manual
def: "Specification for the quality management system of an organization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08112
name: quality objective
def: "Result to be achieved related to quality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08113
name: quality plan
def: "Specification of the procedures and associated resources to be applied when and by whom to a specific object."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08114
name: quality planning
def: "Part of quality management focused on setting quality objectives and specifying necessary operational processes and related resources to achieve the quality objectives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08115
name: quality policy
def: "Intentions and direction of a laboratory as formally expressed by its top management related to quality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08116
name: reference laboratory
def: "Laboratory that applies a reference measurement procedure\, a reference examination procedure\, or performs a calibration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08117
name: reference material certificate
def: "Document\, issued by a reference material producer\, containing the essential information for the use of a certified reference material and confirming that the necessary procedures have been carried out to ensure the validity and traceability of the stated property values."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08118
name: reference material certification
def: "Process that formally establishes the certified property value\(s\) of a candidate certified reference material."
synonym: "certification of a reference material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08119
name: reference material certification report
def: "Document giving detailed information\, in addition to that contained in a reference material certificate."
synonym: "certification report" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08120
name: reference material producer
def: "Technically competent body that produces a reference material compliant with the appropriate guidance documents and issues the reference material documentation."
synonym: "RM producer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08121
name: rejection interval
def: "Interval of non-permissible measured quantity values."
synonym: "rejection zone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08122
name: requirement
def: "Need or expectation that is stated\, generally implied or obligatory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08123
name: ruggedness of a measurement procedure
def: "Ability of a measurement procedure to maintain acceptable performance under minor changes in operating conditions."
synonym: "robustness of a measurement procedure" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08124
name: sensitivity of a measuring system
def: "Quotient of the change in an indication of a measuring system and the corresponding change in a quantity being measured."
synonym: "analytical sensitivity" []
synonym: "sensitivity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08125
name: Shewhart control chart
def: "Control chart with Shewhart control limits intended primarily to distinguish between the variation in the plotted measure due to random causes and that due to special causes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08126
name: Shewhart control limit
def: "Control limit determined statistically from the variation of the process due to random causes alone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08127
name: Shewhart means chart
def: "Shewhart control chart in which the means of measured quantity values are plotted against time.\nFigure 1\: Shewhart means chart of the means of duplicate analyses of a quality control material\, twice per day \(d\) over 20 days. Each point is the mean of the day’s four results \(\\\(n = 4\\\)\). Upper and lower warning limits \(UWL and LWL\) are at \\\(\\bar\{x\} \\pm 2s/\\sqrt\{n\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\bar\{x\}\\\) is the process mean\, \\\(s\\\) is the standard deviation under repeatability conditions of measurement\, and upper and lower action limits \(UAL and LAL\) are at \\\(\\bar\{x\} \\pm 3s/\\sqrt\{n\}\\\). A simulated measurement bias of one standard deviation is applied after day 10."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08128
name: Shewhart range chart
def: "Shewhart control chart in which the ranges of measured quantity values are plotted against time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08129
name: specified requirement
def: "Need or expectation that is stated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08130
name: standard deviation for proficiency assessment
def: "Measure of dispersion used in the evaluation of results of proficiency testing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08131
name: standard operating procedure
def: "Authorized\, documented procedure or set of procedures with detailed instructions for specified activities."
synonym: "recommended operating procedure" []
synonym: "standard method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08132
name: statistical process control
def: "Activities focused on the use of statistical techniques to reduce variation\, increase knowledge about the process\, and steer the process in the desired way."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08133
name: target measurement uncertainty
def: "Measurement uncertainty specified as an upper limit and decided on the basis of the intended use of measurement results."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08134
name: tolerance
def: "Difference between upper and lower tolerance limits."
synonym: "specified tolerance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08135
name: tolerance interval
def: "Interval of permissible quantity values."
synonym: "specification zone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08136
name: tolerance limit
def: "Specified upper or lower bound of permissible quantity values of a property."
synonym: "specification limit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08137
name: total quality management
def: "Organization-wide efforts to install a permanent climate in which an organization continuously improves its ability to deliver high-quality products and services to customers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08138
name: validation
def: "Confirmation\, through the provision of objective evidence\, that the requirements for a specific intended use or application have been fulfilled."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08139
name: verification
def: "Confirmation\, through the provision of objective evidence\, that specified requirements have been fulfilled."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08140
name: Youden plot
def: "Graphical technique for evaluating an interlaboratory comparison when each participant has made two measurements on the same sample or one measurement on each of two different samples.Figure 1\: Youden plot of the \\\(z\\\) scores of samples #9 and #10 of Round 5 of U39 of the proficiency testing program held by the RCPA Australia for the analysis of creatinine in urine by 218 laboratories. \(Reproduced with permission\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08141
name: activated state
def: "In theories of unimolecular reactions an energized chemical species\, often characterized by the superscript \\\(^\{\\ddagger\}\\\)\, where the excitation is specific and the molecule is poised for reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08142
name: ambiphilic
def: "Both nucleophilic and electrophilic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08143
name: α
def: "1\) Designation applied to the carbon to which a functional group is attached.\n2\) In carbohydrate nomenclature\, a stereochemical designation of the configuration at the anomeric carbon.\n3\) Parameter in a Brønsted relation expressing the sensitivity of the rate of protonation to acidity.\n4\) Parameter in Leffler’s relation expressing the sensitivity of changes in Gibbs activation energy to changes in overall Gibbs energy for an elementary reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08144
name: bidentate
def: "Feature of a ligand with two potential binding sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08145
name: bifurcation
def: "Feature on a potential-energy surface whereby a minimum-energy reaction path \(MERP\) emanating from a saddle point \(corresponding to a transition structure\) splits in two and leads to alternative products without intervening minima or secondary barriers to overcome. A bifurcation arises when the curvature of the surface in a direction perpendicular to the MERP becomes zero and then negative; it implies the existence of a lower-energy transition structure with a transition vector orthogonal to the original MERP."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08146
name: bond-dissociation enthalpy
def: "Standard molar enthalpy required to break a given bond of some specific molecular entity by homolysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08147
name: bond enthalpy
def: "Average value of the gas-phase bond-dissociation enthalpies \(usually at a temperature of \\\(\\pu\{298 K\}\\\)\) for all bonds of the same type within the same chemical species."
synonym: "mean bond energy" []
synonym: "mean bond enthalpy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08148
name: bond-stretch isomers
def: "Two \(or more\) molecules with the same spin multiplicity but with different lengths for one or more bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08149
name: <i>β</i>\, <i>β</i><sub>nuc</sub>\, <i>β</i><sub>lg</sub>
def: "Parameter in a Brønsted relation expressing the sensitivity of the rate of deprotonation to basicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08150
name: β-elimination
def: "Transformation of the general type \\\[\\ce\{XABY -> A=B + XY \(or X + Y\, or X^\{+\} + Y^\{-\}\)\}\\\] where the central atoms \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) are commonly\, but not necessarily\, carbon."
synonym: "1,2-elimination" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08151
name: Catalán solvent parameters
def: "Quantitative measure of solvent polarity\, based on the solvent’s hydrogen-bond-donor ability\, hydrogen-bond-acceptor ability\, polarizability\, and dipolarity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08152
name: catalytic antibody
def: "Monoclonal antibody with enzymatic activity."
synonym: "abzyme" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08153
name: catalytic cycle
def: "Sequence of reaction steps in the form of a loop. One step is binding of a reactant to the active catalyst \(sometimes formed from a precatalyst\)\, and another step is the release of product and regeneration of the catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08154
name: cation-π interaction
def: "Noncovalent attractive force between a positive ion \(metal cation\, protonated Brønsted base\, etc.\) and a \\\(\\ce\{\\uppi\\ electron\}\\\) system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08155
name: chiral feature
def: "Structural characteristic rendering a molecule chiral."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08156
name: chiral recognition
def: "Attraction between molecules through noncovalent interactions that exhibit complementarity only between partners with specific chirality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08157
name: coarctate
def: "Feature of a concerted transformation in which the primary changes in bonding occur within a cyclic array of atoms but in which two nonbonding atomic orbitals on an atom interchange roles with two bonding orbitals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08158
name: condensed formula
def: "Linear representation of the structure of a molecular entity in which bonds are omitted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08160
name: curly arrows
def: "Symbols for depicting the flow of electrons in a chemical reaction or to generate additional resonance forms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08161
name: dendrimer
def: "Large molecule constructed from a central core with repetitive branching and multiple functional groups at the periphery."
synonym: "arborol" []
synonym: "cascade molecule" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08163
name: distortion interaction model
def: "Method for analyzing activation energy as the sum of the energies to distort the reactants into the geometries they have in transition states plus the energy of interaction between the two distorted reactants."
synonym: "activation strain model" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08164
name: donicity
def: "Quantitative experimental measure of the Lewis basicity of a molecule \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)\, expressed as the negative enthalpy of the formation of the 1\:1 complex \\\(\\ce\{B.SbCl5\}\\\)."
synonym: "donor number DN (misnomer)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08165
name: dynamic NMR
def: "NMR spectroscopy of samples undergoing chemical reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08166
name: electron pushing
def: "Method using curly arrows for showing the formal movement of an electron pair \(from a lone pair or a \\\(\\upsigma\\\) or \\\(\\ce\{\\uppi\\ bond\}\\\)\) or of an unpaired electron\, in order to generate additional resonance forms or to denote a chemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08167
name: electrophilic substitution
def: "Heterolytic reaction in which the entering group adds to a nucleophile and in which the leaving group\, or electrofuge\, relinquishes both electrons to its reaction partner\, whereupon it becomes another potential electrophile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08168
name: NMR exchange spectroscopy
def: "Two-dimensional NMR technique producing cross peaks corresponding to site-to site chemical exchange. The cross-peak amplitudes carry information about exchange rates."
synonym: "EXSY" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08169
name: formal charge
def: "Quantity \(omitted if zero\) attached to each atom in a Lewis structure according to \\\[Z_\{\\rm\{formal\}\} = N_\{\\rm\{valence\}\} - N_\{\\rm\{lonepairs\}\} - ½N_\{\\rm\{bonds\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:08170
name: frustrated Lewis acid-base pair
def: "Acid and base for which adduct formation is prevented by steric hindrance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08171
name: Gaussian orbital
def: "Function centered on an atom of the form \\\(\\phi\(r\) \\propto x^\{i\} y^\{j\} z^\{k\} \\exp \(-\\zeta r^\{2\}\)\\\)\, used to approximate atomic orbitals in the LCAO-MO method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08172
name: graphene
def: "Allotrope of carbon\, whose structure is a one-atom-thick planar sheet of \\\(\\ce\{sp^\{2\}\{-\}bonded\}\\\) carbon atoms in a honeycomb \(hexagonal\) crystal lattice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08173
name: halogen bond
def: "Association between a Lewis-acidic halogen atom in a molecular entity and a Lewis-basic region in another\, or the same\, molecular entity\, which acts as an electron-pair donor\, such that the balance of forces of attraction and repulsion results in net stabilization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08174
name: hapticity
def: "Topological description for the number of contiguous atoms of a ligand that are bonded to a central metal atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08175
name: HBA solvent
def: "Strongly or weakly basic solvent capable of acting as a hydrogen-bond acceptor \(HBA\) and forming strong intermolecular solute-solvent hydrogen bonds."
synonym: "hydrogen-bond acceptor solvent" []
synonym: "protophilic solvent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08176
name: HBD solvent
def: "Solvent with a sizable permanent dipole moment that bears suitably acidic hydrogen atoms to form strong intermolecular solvent-solute hydrogen bonds."
synonym: "dipolar HBD solvent" []
synonym: "hydrogen-bond donating solvent" []
synonym: "protic solvent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08177
name: high-throughput screening
def: "Automated method to quickly assay large libraries of chemical species for the affinity of small organic molecules toward a target of interest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08178
name: Huisgen reaction
def: "Cycloaddition of 1\,3 dipolar compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08179
name: hydronation
def: "Attachment of the ion \\\(\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}\\\) either in natural abundance or where it is not desired to distinguish between the isotopes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08180
name: ionic liquid
def: "Liquid that consists exclusively or almost exclusively of equivalent amounts of oppositely charged ions."
synonym: "ionic solvent" []
synonym: "molten salt" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08181
name: keto-enol tautomerization
def: "Interconversion of ketone and enol tautomers by hydron migration\, accelerated by acid or base catalysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08182
name: Laurence solvent parameters
def: "Quantitative measures of solvent polarity\, based on the solvent’s dispersion/induction interactions\, electrostatic interactions between permanent multipoles\, solute Lewis base/solvent Lewis acid interactions\, and solute HBD/solvent HBA interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08183
name: Leffler’s relation
def: "In a series of elementary reactions\, the changes in Gibbs activation energies are often found to be proportional to the changes in Gibbs energies for the overall reaction.\\\[\\updelta \\Delta^\{\\ddagger\} G = \\alpha \\Delta_\{\\rm\{r\}\} G^\{\\circ\}\\\]\nThis relation was interpreted in terms of the simple assumption that a small change in any transition-state property \\\(P_\{\\ddagger\}\\\) is a linear combination of changes in reactant- and product-state properties\, \\\(P_\{\\ce\{R\}\}\\\) and \\\(P_\{\\ce\{P\}\}\\\). \\\[\\updelta P_\{\\ddagger\} = \\alpha \\updelta P_\{\\ce\{P\}\} + \(1-\\alpha\)\\updelta P_\{\\ce\{R\}\}\\\]\nWithin the limits of this assumption\, the parameter \\\(\\alpha\\\) is an approximate measure of the fractional displacement of the transition state along the minimum-energy reaction path from reactants to products."
synonym: "Leffler’s assumption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08184
name: mass action\, law of
def: "Statement that the velocity of a reaction depends on the active mass\, i.e.\, the concentrations of the reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08185
name: Mayr–Patz equation
def: "Rate constants for the reactions of \\\(\\ce\{sp^\{2\}\{-\}hybridized\}\\\) electrophiles with nucleophiles can be expressed by the correlation \\\[\\lg \[k/\(\\pu\{dm3 mol-1 s-1\}\)\] = s_\{\\rm\{N\}\}\(E + N\)\\\] where \\\(E\\\) is the nucleophile-independent electrophilicity parameter\, \\\(N\\\) is the electrophile-independent nucleophilicity parameter\, and \\\(s_\{\\rm\{N\}\}\\\) is the electrophile-independent nucleophile-specific susceptibility parameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08186
name: minimum structural change\, principle of
def: "Principle that a chemical reaction is expected to occur with a minimum of bond changes or with a minimum redistribution of electrons \(although more complex reaction cascades are also possible\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08187
name: molecular recognition
def: "Attraction between specific molecules through noncovalent interactions that often exhibit electrostatic and stereochemical complementarity between the partners."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08188
name: nanomaterial
def: "Substance whose particles are in the size range of 1 to \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08190
name: non-Kekulé structure
def: "Compound with unpaired electrons for which no Lewis structures are possible with all bonding electrons paired in single or double bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08191
name: nucleophilic substitution
def: "Heterolytic reaction in which an entering group adds to the electrophilic part of the substrate and in which the leaving group\, or nucleofuge\, retains both electrons of the bond that is broken\, whereupon it becomes another potential nucleophile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08192
name: octanol-water partition ratio
def: "Equilibrium concentration of a substance in octan-1-ol divided by its equilibrium concentration in water."
synonym: "partition ratio" []
synonym: "water/octanol partition coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08193
name: organocatalysis
def: "Catalysis by small organic molecules\, as distinguished from catalysis by \(transition\) metals or enzymes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08194
name: parallel effect
def: "Change of the position of the transition state upon stabilization or destabilization of a structure \(or structures\) along the assumed minimum-energy reaction path."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08195
name: polarity
def: "Characteristic of a bond between atoms of different electronegativity\, such that the electrons in that bond are not shared equally."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08196
name: potential of mean force
def: "Free energy as a function of a set of coordinates\, the negative gradient of which gives the average force acting on that configuration averaged over all other coordinates and momenta within a statistical distribution. If the averaging is performed within a canonical ensemble \(constant volume\, temperature\, and number of particles\)\, the PMF is equivalent to the Helmholtz energy\, but if it is performed within an isobaric-isothermal ensemble \(constant pressure\, temperature\, and number of particles\)\, the PMF is equivalent to the Gibbs energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08197
name: protofugality
def: "Special case of nucleofugality\, describing the relative rates of transfer of a proton \(more generally\: hydron\) from a series of Brønsted acids \\\(\\ce\{H-X\}\\\) to a common Brønsted base."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08198
name: protonation
def: "Attachment of the ion \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H^\{+\}\}\\\) of relative isotopic mass approximately equal to 1."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08199
name: protophilicity
def: "Special case of nucleophilicity\, describing the relative rates of reactions of a series of Lewis bases with a common Brønsted acid. This term has the advantage over the commonly used 'kinetic basicity' that philicity and fugality are associated with kinetics\, while acidity and basicity are associated with thermodynamics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08200
name: push-pull conjugation
def: "Feature of an extended conjugated \\\(\\ce\{\\uppi\\ system\}\\\) bearing an electron donor group at one end and an electron acceptor group at the other end."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08201
name: π-electron donor
def: "Substituent capable of electron donation by resonance \(e.g.\, \\\(\\ce\{OCH3\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08202
name: quantitative structure–property relationship
def: "Regression model to correlate chemical properties such as boiling point or chromatographic retention time with predictor parameters based on measured or calculated features of molecular structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08205
name: resonance form
def: "One of at least two Lewis structures\, with fixed single\, double\, and triple bonds\, that is a contributing structure to the valence-bond wavefunction of a molecule that cannot be described by a single Lewis structure."
synonym: "canonical form" []
synonym: "contributing structure" []
synonym: "resonance structure" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08206
name: self-assembly
def: "Process whereby a system of single-molecule components spontaneously forms an organized structure\, owing to molecular recognition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08207
name: shift reagent
def: "Paramagnetic substance that induces an additional change of the NMR resonance frequency of a nucleus near any site in a molecule to which the substance binds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08208
name: skeletal formula
def: "Two-dimensional representation of a molecular entity in which bonds are indicated as lines between vertices representing octet carbon atoms with attached hydrogens omitted and in which other atoms are represented by their chemical symbols."
synonym: "bond-line formula" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08209
name: solvatomers
def: "Isomers that differ in their solvation environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08210
name: stereogenic center
def: "Atom within a molecule bearing groups such that interchanging any two of them leads to a stereoisomer of the original molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08212
name: superjacent orbital
def: "Second Lowest Unoccupied Molecular Orbital \(SLUMO\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08213
name: tautomers
def: "Constitutional isomers that can interconvert more or less rapidly\, often by migration of a hydron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08214
name: through-conjugation
def: "Phenomenon whereby electrons can be delocalized from any of three \(or more\) groups to any other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08215
name: zero-point energy
def: "Extent\, in consequence of Heisenberg’s Uncertainty Principle\, by which a particle or a set of particles has an energy greater than that of the minimum on the potential-energy surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08216
name: atomic spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of spectroscopy for the study of electromagnetic radiation absorbed and emitted by atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08217
name: molecular spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of spectroscopy for the study of rotational\, vibrational\, and electronic transitions of molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08218
name: nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of spectroscopy to measure the precession of magnetic moments placed in a magnetic induction based on absorption of electromagnetic radiation of a specific frequency by an atomic nucleus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08220
name: absorption index
def: "Imaginary part of the complex refractive index describing absorption of electromagnetic radiation."
synonym: "imaginary refractive index" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08221
name: accidental degeneracy
def: "Condition that occurs when two or more normal transitions have the same energy as a matter of coincidence\, as opposed to because of symmetry conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08222
name: angle of incidence
def: "Angle between a beam of electromagnetic radiation and a line normal to a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08223
name: anisotropic
def: "Having properties that depend on direction."
synonym: "nonisotropic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08224
name: apodization
def: "Technical term for changing the shape of a mathematical function\, electrical signal\, or optical transmission to remove or smooth a discontinuity at the edges\, literally meaning 'removing the foot'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08225
name: apodization function
def: "Mathematical function that effects apodization."
synonym: "window function" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08226
name: array detector
def: "Photoelectric detector in which a large number of pixels are distributed\, usually in regularly spaced lines and usually over a rectangular area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08227
name: attenuated total reflection
def: "Internal reflection from an absorbing material at angles of incidence at or above the critical angle."
synonym: "frustrated total internal reflection" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08228
name: beam splitter
def: "Device to split a beam of electromagnetic radiation into two parts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08229
name: Cauchy function
def: "Function \\\(y = a/\(b^\{2\} + x^\{2\}\)\\\) where \\\(a\\\) and \\\(b\\\) are constants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08230
name: charge coupled device
def: "Device that stores information in the form of charge packets in an array of closely spaced capacitors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08231
name: circular birefringence
def: "Difference between the refractive indices of an optically active medium for left\, \\\(n^\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\)\, and right\, \\\(n^\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\)\, circularly polarized radiation \(\\\(n^\{\\rm\{L\}\}-n^\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08232
name: dephasing
def: "Loss of coherence between the upper and lower states of a transition."
synonym: "phase relaxation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08233
name: detector gate width
def: "Time for signal integration after start of detection."
synonym: "detector integration time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08234
name: difference spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of spectroscopy in which spectral subtraction is used to help the study and identification of individual species in a mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08235
name: diffuse reflectance
def: "Diffuse reflected \(remitted\) radiant power \(\\\(P_\{\\rm\{rem\}\}\\\)\) divided by incident radiant power \(\\\(P_\{0\}\\\)\). \\\(R = P_\{\\rm\{rem\}\}/P_\{0\}\\\)."
synonym: "remission fraction" []
synonym: "remittance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08236
name: diffuse reflection
def: "Reflection in which electromagnetic radiation incident on a scattering surface at a certain angle is reflected \(remitted\) over all angles."
synonym: "remission" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08237
name: diffuse transmission
def: "Process in which radiation is transmitted by a scattering sample and leaves the sample in directions other than that required by Snell’s law of refraction. The process is complicated and involves transmission\, reflection\, and scattering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08238
name: dipole coupling
def: "Coupling of different motions through dipole-dipole forces."
synonym: "transition-dipole transition dipole coupling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08239
name: dispersive Fourier-transform spectroscopy
def: "Fourier-transform spectroscopy using a Michelson or other two-beam interferometer with the sample placed in one arm of the interferometer."
synonym: "asymmetric Fourier-transform spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08240
name: dispersive spectrometer
def: "Spectrometer in which electromagnetic radiation is separated spatially into its component wavenumbers by a dispersive element\, such as a prism or a diffraction grating."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08242
name: electromagnetic radiation
def: "Flow of energy through space propagated as synchronized sinusoidal waves of the electric field\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{E\}\\\)\, and the magnetic field \\\(\\boldsymbol\{H\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08243
name: electromagnetic transition
def: "Transition accompanied by the emission or absorption of a photon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08244
name: electron emission spectrometry
def: "Measurement principle of spectrometry for the study of electrons emitted by atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08245
name: electronic transition
def: "Transition in an atom\, ion\, or molecule from an electronic energy level \\\(E_\{1\}\\\) to another energy level \\\(E_\{2\}\\\)."
synonym: "transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08246
name: energy level
def: "Set of one or more stationary quantum states of a free atom\, ion\, or molecule having a particular internal energy."
synonym: "energy state" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08247
name: experimental absorbance
def: "Decadic absorbance as measured with no corrections for other processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08248
name: folding
def: "The appearance of peaks at incorrect frequencies in a mathematically-processed spectrum due to insufficient density of datapoints in the time-domain spectrum or from peaks outside of the sampled frequency range.This process can be simulated by folding a spectrum that shows the correct wavenumbers back onto itself at \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\) and its multiples."
synonym: "aliasing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08249
name: free induction decay
def: "Indication from a pulsed spectroscopy experiment that takes the form of damped sine waves arising from the excited system returning to equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08250
name: Fresnel reflection
def: "Reflection of radiation from a surface that is smooth and large with respect to the wavelength of the radiation. A reflected beam\, of intensity governed by the Fresnel equations\, is produced at an angle of reflection equal to the angle of incidence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08252
name: Gaussian band
def: "Spectral band with the shape \\\(H_\{\\rm\{G\}\} \\exp\[-4\\ln 2\{\(\\tilde\{\nu\} - \\tilde\{\nu\}_\{0\}\)\}^\{2\}/W^\{2\}\]\\\)\, where \\\(H_\{\\rm\{G\}\}\\\) is the band maximum \(peak height\)\, \\\(W\\\) is the full width at half maximum\, and \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{0\}\\\) is the peak wavenumber."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08253
name: Hadamard transform spectrometer
def: "Multiplexing spectrometer that uses the optical components of a dispersive spectrometer with a Hadamard encoding mask."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08255
name: high-pass filter
def: "Optical filter that excludes radiation of wavenumber lower \(wavelength higher\) than a certain value and passes radiation of wavenumber above \(wavelength lower\) than this value.\nElectrical filter that blocks signal with a modulation frequency lower than a certain cut-on value and passes signals of frequency above this value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08256
name: Hilbert transforms
def: "Functions that interrelate the real part\, \\\(f'\\\)\, and imaginary part\, \\\(f''\\\)\, of the refractive index\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\hat\{n\}\} = n + \{\\rm\{i\}\}k\\\)\, or relative permittivity\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\hat\{\\epsilon\}\} = \\epsilon' + \\rm\{i\}\\epsilon''\\\)\, through \\\[f'\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\) - f_\{\\infty\}' = \\frac\{1\}\{\\pi\} P \\int_\{-\\infty\}^\{\\infty\} \\frac\{f''\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\)\}\{\\tilde\{\nu\} - \\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}\\rm\{d\}\\tilde\{\nu\}\\\] \\\[f''\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\) - f_\{\\infty\}' = \\frac\{-1\}\{\\pi\} P \\int_\{-\\infty\}^\{\\infty\} \\frac\{f'\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\) - f_\{\\infty\}'\}\{\\tilde\{\nu\} - \\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}\\rm\{d\}\\tilde\{\nu\}\\\] where \\\(P\\\) means that the principal part of the integral is taken at the singularity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08257
name: instrument line shape
def: "Idealized form of a feature in a spectrometer corresponding to a transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08258
name: integrated intensity
def: "Area under a spectral band in an absorption spectrum where the absorption is linearly proportional to the amount of absorber in unit area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08259
name: integration range
def: "Frequency or wavenumber range over which the integrated intensity of an absorption band is measured by numerical integration of the area under the band."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08260
name: intensified charge coupled device detector
def: "Charge coupled device with a prior amplification system to improve sensitivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08261
name: internal absorbance
def: "Absorbance in the absence of reflection\, scattering or luminescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08263
name: Kramers–Kronig transforms
def: "Functions based on the physical principle of causality that interconvert the real and imaginary parts of complex optical quantities when they are known over a sufficiently wide \(strictly infinite\) wavenumber range. They are frequently used to interconvert the real part\, \\\(f'\\\)\, and imaginary part\, \\\(f''\\\)\, of the refractive index\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\hat\{n\}\} = n + \{\\rm\{i\}\}k\\\)\, the dielectric constant \(relative permittivity\)\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\hat\{\\epsilon\}_\{r\}\} = \\epsilon_\{r\}' + \\rm\{i\}\\epsilon_\{r\}''\\\)\, or the logarithm of the complex reflection coefficient \\\(re^\{i\\varphi\}\\\) through \\\[f'\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\) - f_\{\\infty\}' = \\frac\{2\}\{\\pi\} P \\int_\{0\}^\{\\infty\} \\frac\{\\tilde\{\nu\} f''\(\\tilde \nu\)\}\{\\tilde\{\nu\}^\{2\} - \\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}^\{2\}\} \{\\rm\{d\}\}\\tilde\{\nu\}\\\] \\\[f''\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\) = \\frac\{-2\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}\{\\pi\} P \\int_\{0\}^\{\\infty\} \\frac\{f'\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\) - f_\{\\infty\}'\}\{\\tilde\{\nu\}^2 - \\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}^2\} \\rm\{d\}\\tilde\{\nu\}\\\] where \\\(P\\\) means that the principal part of the integral is taken at the singularity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08264
name: light
def: "Electromagnetic radiation visible to the human eye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08265
name: linear Napierian absorption coefficient
def: "Napierian absorbance \(\\\(A_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\) divided by path-length \(\\\(l\\\)\). \\\(\\alpha = A_\{\\rm\{e\}\}/l\\\)."
synonym: "Napierian absorption coefficient" []
synonym: "absorption coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08266
name: liquid crystal tunable filter
def: "Optical device based on polarization interference caused by transmission through a series of birefringent liquid crystal layers of different thicknesses that allow a narrow wavelength region to be selected and tuned over a broad spectral range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08267
name: lock-in amplifier
def: "Amplifier that amplifies only those signals that have\, within a specified spectral bandwidth\, the same frequency as\, and a specified phase relation with\, the selected reference signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08268
name: low-pass filter
def: "Optical filter that excludes radiation of wavenumber greater \(wavelength lower\) than a certain value and passes radiation of wavenumber below \(wavelength greater than\) this value.\nElectrical filter that blocks signal with a modulation frequency above a certain cut-on value and passes signals of frequency below this value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08269
name: molar decadic absorption coefficient
def: "Decadic absorbance \(\\\(A_\{10\}\\\)\) divided by path-length \(\\\(l\\\)\) and amount concentration \(\\\(c\\\)\). \\\(\\varepsilon = A_\{10\}/\(cl\)\\\)."
synonym: "molar absorption coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08270
name: molar Napierian absorption coefficient
def: "Napierian absorbance \(\\\(A_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\) divided by path-length \(\\\(l\\\)\) and amount concentration \(\\\(c\\\)\). \\\(\\kappa = A_\{\\rm\{e\}\}/\(cl\)\\\)."
synonym: "absorption coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08271
name: multiple attenuated total reflection
def: "Process in which attenuated total reflection occurs sequentially several times with the same sample."
synonym: "frustrated multiple internal reflection (FMIR)" []
synonym: "multiple internal reflection (MIR)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08272
name: Napierian absorbance
def: "Absorbance calculated with logarithm base \\\(e\\\) \(natural or Napierian logarithm\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08273
name: net absorption cross-section
def: "Molar Napierian absorption coefficient divided by the Avogadro constant. \\\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{net\}\} = \\kappa/N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)."
synonym: "absorption cross-section ($\upsigma$)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08274
name: nominal spectral resolution
def: "Spectral resolution setting on an instrument that a user sets to record a spectrum."
synonym: "nominal resolution" []
synonym: "resolution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08275
name: Nyquist criterion
def: "Result of information theory that a sine wave can be completely reconstructed if its ordinate is known at two or more points in a cycle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08276
name: Nyquist frequency
def: "Highest frequency or wavenumber that can be characterized by sampling at a given rate in order to be able to fully reconstruct the signal without artefacts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08277
name: optical throughput
def: "Volume in phase space\, a function of the area of the emitting source \(\\\(S\\\)\) and the solid angle into which it propagates \\\(\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\).\{\\rm\{d\}\}^\{2\} G = \{\\rm\{d\}\}S \{\\rm\{d\}\}\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\\\)."
synonym: "étendue" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08278
name: path-length
def: "Length of the optical path through the sample."
synonym: "path length" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08279
name: radiofrequency
def: "Frequency or band of frequencies in the range \\\(\\pu\{E4\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{E12 Hz\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08280
name: received radiant flux density
def: "Radiant power incident on unit area."
synonym: "incident flux density" []
synonym: "irradiance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08281
name: reflection
def: "Process by which electromagnetic radiation is reflected by a sample. For non-scattering samples the term means Fresnel reflection; for scattering samples the term includes regular reflection and volume reflection or diffuse reflection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08282
name: signal-to-noise ratio
def: "Power of signal divided by power of noise."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08283
name: single beam spectrum
def: "Output of a single beam spectrometer as a function of wavenumber or wavelength."
synonym: "intensity spectrum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08284
name: slit width
def: "Distance between the blades of the entrance or exit slits in a monochromator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08285
name: spectral band
def: "Region of the absorption/transmission spectrum in which the absorbance/transmission passes through a maximum/minimum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08286
name: spectral bandwidth
def: "Upper frequency minus lower frequency in a continuous band of frequencies."
synonym: "bandwidth" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08287
name: spectral radiant energy density
def: "Radiant energy density in unit wavenumber \(wavelength\) interval at wavenumber \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\\\) \(wavelength \\\(\\lambda\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08288
name: spectral range
def: "Wavelength range for which a spectrometer can be used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08289
name: spectral resolution
def: "Smallest difference between wavenumbers \(or wavelengths or frequencies\) at which different spectral properties may be distinguished\, measured as the full width at half maximum."
synonym: "resolution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08290
name: spectral subtraction
def: "Ordinate of one spectrum minus ordinate of another spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08291
name: spectrum
def: "Graph of a quantity derived from the radiant power at each wavelength \(frequency or wavenumber\) as ordinate plotted against the wavelength \(frequency or wavenumber\) of electromagnetic radiation as abscissa."
synonym: "vibrational spectrum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08292
name: specular reflection
def: "Reflection of electromagnetic radiation from a surface such that each incident ray is reflected at the same angle to the surface normal as the incident ray\, but on the opposing side of the surface normal in the plane formed by incident and reflected rays."
synonym: "regular reflection" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08293
name: stray light
def: "Electromagnetic radiation that does not follow the usual path through a spectrometer and consequently appears in a spectrum at a frequency or wavenumber different from its initial value. The term is also used to refer to radiation that passes around the sample instead of through it\, and consequently is not modified by the sample and seriously affects absolute and relative intensities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08294
name: transmission spectrum
def: "General term for the spectrum of transmitted electromagnetic radiation."
synonym: "transmittance spectrum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08295
name: two-dimensional correlation spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of spectroscopy in which spectra are recorded at different magnitudes of an applied external perturbation of the sample and are processed to yield a 2-D correlation spectrum."
synonym: "2-D correlation spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08296
name: two-dimensional correlation spectrum
def: "Three-dimensional surface in which two of the dimensions show frequency axes \(\\\(\nu_\{1\}\\\)\, \\\(\nu_\{2\}\\\)\) and the third axis shows a correlation function of the spectral intensities observed at \\\(\nu_\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\nu_\{2\}\\\)."
synonym: "2-D correlation spectrum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08297
name: undersampling
def: "Sampling less frequently than is required by the Nyquist criterion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08298
name: Voigt function
def: "Convolution of Gaussian and Lorentzian \(Cauchy\) functions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08299
name: wavelength in medium
def: "Distance travelled by a wave of electromagnetic radiation in one cycle in a non-absorbing medium of real refractive index \\\(n\\\). \\\(\\lambda = \\lambda_\{0\}/n\\\)."
synonym: "wavelength" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08300
name: wavelength in vacuum
def: "Distance travelled by a wave of electromagnetic radiation in one cycle in vacuum."
synonym: "vacuum wavelength" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08301
name: wavenumber in medium
def: "Reciprocal of wavelength of electromagnetic radiation. \\\(\\sigma = 1/\\lambda\\\)."
synonym: "wavenumber" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08302
name: wavenumber in vacuum
def: "Reciprocal of the vacuum wavelength of electromagnetic radiation. \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\} = 1/\\lambda_\{0\}\\\)."
synonym: "vacuum wavenumber" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08303
name: working range
def: "Range of absorbance or intensity which a spectrometer can measure with accuracy and precision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08304
name: zero filling
def: "Addition of zeros to a free induction decay or interferogram to decrease the frequency or wavenumber spacing between points in the Fourier-transformed spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08305
name: zero path difference
def: "In a Michelson interferometer or other two-beam interferometer\, the position of the moving mirror at which the optical paths are equal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08306
name: <sup>13</sup>C-HMQC-NOESY
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one carbon and two hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08307
name: <sup>13</sup>C-NOESY-HSQC
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one carbon and two hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08308
name: <sup>15</sup>N-NOESY-HSQC
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one nitrogen and two hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08309
name: <sup>15</sup>N-TOCSY-HSQC
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one nitrogen and two hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08310
name: 180° pulse
def: "Pulsed radiofrequency signal in NMR that rotates the bulk magnetization vector \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{M\}_\{0\}\\\)\) of a nuclear spin to its opposing direction\, effectively inverting the signal\, \(e.g.\, from the \\\(+z\\\) to \\\(-z\\\) axis\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08311
name: 90° pulse
def: "Pulsed radiofrequency signal that rotates the bulk magnetization vector \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{M\}_\{0\}\\\)\) of a nuclear spin to its orthogonal plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08312
name: acquisition time
def: "Duration of the digitization of a free induction decay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08313
name: adiabatic pulse
def: "Radiofrequency pulse that inverts spins by using a frequency sweep during the pulse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08314
name: Bloch equations
def: "Set of coupled differential equations which can be used to describe the behaviour of a magnetization vector under any conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08315
name: Bloch–Siegert shift
def: "Frequency difference seen for a nuclear magnetic resonance signal when a radiofrequency field is applied during the acquisition time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08316
name: Boltzmann distribution of nuclear spins
def: "Distribution of nuclear spins among their possible energy levels at thermal equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08317
name: CBCA\(CO\)NH/HN\(CO\)CACB
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08318
name: CBCANH/HNCACB
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08319
name: CC\(CO\)NH
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08320
name: chemical shift anisotropy
def: "Chemical shift difference between isotropic and anisotropic states in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08321
name: combined rotation and multiple pulse spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for obtaining high-resolution solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectra on nuclei with high gyromagnetic ratio such as \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{^\{19\}F\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08322
name: constant time evolution
def: "Segment of a pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that allows evolution of chemical shift during a fixed time period rather than through the traditional symmetrical incremented delays."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08323
name: correlation spectroscopy
def: "Homonuclear two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy correlation experiment showing cross peaks representing spin-spin coupling between pairs of nuclei."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08324
name: correlation spectroscopy through long-range coupling
def: "Heteronuclear \(typically \\\(\\ce\{^\{13\}C\}\\\)\) detected long range 2D correlation experiment in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that has mostly been superseded by heteronuclear multiple bond correlation \(HMBC\) experiments and its variants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08325
name: correlation time
def: "Parameter related to the mean time during which a molecule maintains its spatial geometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08326
name: cross polarization with magic angle spinning NMR
def: "Combined use of cross polarization with magic-angle spinning for solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08327
name: cross polarization
def: "Technique in solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that enables polarization from abundant spins such as \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) or \\\(\\ce\{^\{19\}F\}\\\) to be transferred to dilute spins such as \\\(\\ce\{^\{13\}C\}\\\) or \\\(\\ce\{^\{15\}N\}\\\) to enhance signal-to-noise ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08328
name: decoupling in the presence of scalar interactions
def: "Pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy often used for isotropic mixing in total correlation spectroscopy \(TOCSY\) experiments due to its efficiency over large spectral bandwidths."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08329
name: diffusion ordered spectroscopy
def: "Pseudo-two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment that resolves NMR signals of a one-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum in a second dimension through the diffusion times of their corresponding molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08330
name: dipolar coupling
def: "Direct though-space interactions of nuclear magnetic moment vectors proportional to the inverse cube of internuclear distance."
synonym: "magnetic dipole–dipole interaction in NMR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08331
name: distortionless enhancement by polarization transfer
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy used for determining the presence of primary\, secondary and tertiary heteronuclei."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08332
name: double pulsed-field gradient spin-echo excitation
def: "Pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy in which pulsed-field gradients are applied to a doubled spin echo sequence to cleanly and precisely excite particular resonances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08333
name: double resonance
def: "Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment where excitation is applied independently to two distinct frequency ranges."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08334
name: double-labelled protein
def: "Shorthand for a protein which has been uniformly \\\(\\ce\{^\{15\}N\{-\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{^\{13\}C\{-\}labelled\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08335
name: double-quantum filtered correlation spectroscopy
def: "Variant of the COSY experiment with a double quantum filter to remove\, or at least reduce\, impact of large singlet peaks on dynamic range and artefacts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08336
name: dwell time
def: "Time interval between sampled data points."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08337
name: echo time
def: "In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy\, time interval between the middle of the first radiofrequency pulse and the peak of the spin echo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08338
name: electronic reference to access in vivo concentrations
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that generates an electronic signal in a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrometer which is detected simultaneously to the sample free induction decay during the acquisition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08339
name: Ernst angle
def: "When signal averaging in a single-pulse nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment\, flip angle giving the best signal-to-noise ratio for a given combination of spin-lattice relaxation time and repetition rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08340
name: exchange spectroscopy
def: "Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment\, which is the same as a NOESY\, but for the purposes of observing nuclei exchanging chemical environments during the course of the experiment through conformational or chemical exchange."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08341
name: excitation sculpting
def: "Robust method of generating selective excitation through combination of shaped pulses and pulsed-field gradients in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08342
name: fast field cycling NMR relaxometry
def: "Relaxometry to measure the nuclear spin-lattice relaxation rate constant as a function of the applied magnetic field strength in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08343
name: flip angle
def: "Rotation that the net magnetization experiences during the application of a radiofrequency pulse in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."
synonym: "tip angle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08344
name: gated decoupling
def: "Application of spin decoupling only during selected time periods in a pulse experiment in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08345
name: globally optimized alternating-phase rectangular pulses
def: "Spin decoupling pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy used to provide decoupling over a large frequency range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08346
name: gradient-selected experiment
def: "Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment where pulsed-field gradients are employed to filter out unwanted resonances from a spectrum rather than the classic phase-cycling approach."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08347
name: H\(CCO\)NH
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one nitrogen\, and two hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08348
name: HBHA\(CO\)NH
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one nitrogen and two hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08349
name: HCCH-COSY
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one carbon and two hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08350
name: HCCH-TOCSY
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with one carbon and two hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08351
name: heteronuclear decoupling
def: "Spin decoupling in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy between two different nuclei \(e.g.\, \\\(\\ce\{^\{13\}C\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\)\) by appropriate excitation of the decoupled nucleus."
synonym: "decoupling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08352
name: heteronuclear multiple bond correlation NMR
def: "Two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that gives correlations between carbons and protons that are separated by two\, three\, and\, sometimes in conjugated systems\, four bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08353
name: heteronuclear multiple-bond correlation over two bonds
def: "Variant of the heteronuclear single quantum correlation \(HSQC\) and heteronuclear multiple bond correlation \(HMBC\) experiment in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that almost exclusively correlates \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) nuclei with heteronuclei separated by two covalent bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08354
name: heteronuclear multiple-quantum correlation NMR
def: "Simplest implementation of an inverse detected \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) heteronuclear correlation nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08355
name: heteronuclear multiple-quantum correlation with additional TOCSY transfer
def: "Variant of heteronuclear multiple bond correlation \(HMBC\) spectroscopy that shows correlations from a heteronucleus \(e.g.\, \\\(\\ce\{^\{13\}C\}\\\)\) to not only its directly bound \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) but also to all other \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) nuclei within the spin system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08356
name: heteronuclear Overhauser effect spectroscopy
def: "Heteronuclear variant of nuclear Overhauser effect spectroscopy \(NOSEY\) showing through space interaction between \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) and a heteronucleus\, typically \\\(\\ce\{^\{13\}C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08357
name: heteronuclear shift correlation NMR
def: "Directly-observed one-bond heteronuclear correlation experiment in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08358
name: heteronuclear single quantum correlation
def: "\\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) \(inverse\) detected two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy heteronuclear experiment that correlates \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) nuclei with their directly coupled nucleus through a single bond."
synonym: "heteronuclear single quantum coherence" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08359
name: heteronuclear single quantum correlation with additional TOCSY transfer
def: "Variant of the heteronuclear single quantum correlation \(HSQC\) experiment that shows correlations from a heteronucleus \(e.g.\, \\\(\\ce\{^\{13\}C\}\\\)\) to not only its directly bound \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) but also to all other \\\(\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}\\\) nuclei within the spin system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08360
name: heteronuclear single quantum multiple-bond correlation
def: "Variant of the heteronuclear single quantum correlation \(HSQC\) experiment that provides pure absorption\, antiphase line shapes for precise\, direct measurement of \\\(^\{n\}J\(\\ce\{C\,H\}\)\\\) spin-spin coupling constants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08361
name: HN\(CA\)CO
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08362
name: HN\(CO\)CA
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08363
name: HNCA
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08364
name: HNCO
def: "Three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy for the assignment of protein structure with nitrogen\, carbon and hydrogen dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08365
name: homonuclear decoupling
def: "Spin decoupling in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy between two signals of the same nuclear isotope."
synonym: "decoupling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08366
name: imaginary NMR spectrum
def: "In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy\, one of two equally sized blocks of frequency data \\\(\\pu\{90\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\) out of phase to each other produced by Fourier transformation of the time domain signal."
synonym: "imaginary spectrum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08367
name: incredible natural-abundance double-quantum transfer experiment
def: "Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment designed to show homonuclear spin-spin coupling correlations between low natural abundance nuclei such as \\\(\\ce\{^\{13\}C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08368
name: insensitive nuclei enhanced by polarization transfer
def: "Pulse sequence in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that involves the transfer of nuclear spin polarization from spins with large Boltzmann population differences to nuclear spins of interest with low Boltzmann population differences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08369
name: inversion pulse
def: "Pulse in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that completely inverts the magnetization of a spin\, e.g.\, \\\(+z\\\) to \\\(-z\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08370
name: inversion recovery sequence
def: "Spin echo sequence preceded by a \\\(\\pu\{180\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\) inversion pulse used to determine spin-lattice relaxation times in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."
synonym: "inversion recovery" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08371
name: inversion time
def: "Time between the inversion pulse and the sampling pulse\(s\) in an inversion recovery sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08372
name: J-modulated spin-echo
def: "Nuclear magnetic resonance pulse sequence that refocuses spin vectors that have fanned out due to field inhomogeneity or chemical shift differences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08373
name: J-resolved spectroscopy
def: "Two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy resolving chemical shift information in one dimension and spin-spin coupling in the second."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08374
name: Knight shift
def: "Frequency shift in the nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum of a paramagnetic species that refers to the relative shift \\\(\\boldsymbol\{K\}\\\) in NMR frequency for atoms in a metal \(e.g.\, sodium\) compared with the same atoms in a nonmetallic environment \(e.g.\, as a salt\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08375
name: Larmor angular frequency
def: "Frequency at which nuclei with a nuclear spin precess \(Larmor precession\) around the direction of an external magnetic induction \\\(\\boldsymbol\{B\}_\{0\}\\\). \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}_\{\\rm\{L\}\} = -\\gamma \\boldsymbol\{B\}_\{0\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\gamma\\\) is the magnetogyric ratio of the nucleus."
synonym: "Larmor frequency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08376
name: Larmor precession
def: "Precession of the magnetic moment \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{M\}\\\)\) of an object at an angular frequency\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\omega\}_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\)\, about a static magnetic induction \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{B_\{0\}\}\\\)\)\, named after Joseph Larmor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08377
name: magic angle spinning
def: "Technique in solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy to remove or reduce the influence of anisotropic interactions by rapid sample rotation about the magic angle\, where \\\(\\boldsymbol\{B_\{0\}\}\\\) is external magnetic induction and \\\(\\uptheta\\\) is the axis of rotation of the sample at an angle of approx. \\\(\\pu\{54.74\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\) to the external magnetic induction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08378
name: multiple-quantum magic angle spinning NMR
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy used to obtain high-resolution NMR spectra of quadrupolar nuclei by removal of the anisotropy of the quadrupole interaction\, involving creating a triple-quantum \(or \\\(5\{\\rm\{Q\}\}\\\)\) coherence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08379
name: multiplet
def: "Feature in a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum that is split but is too complex to easily interpret."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08380
name: net dephasing time
def: "Measured time constant associated with spin-spin relaxation for loss of magnetization in the \\\(xy\\\) plane."
synonym: "T2-star" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08381
name: non-uniform sampling
def: "Technique employed within a high dimensionality nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment that acquires only a subset of the data points traditionally acquired linearly thus enabling a reduction in experiment time or an increased resolution in the resultant spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08382
name: nuclear electric quadrupole moment
def: "Parameter that describes the effective shape of the ellipsoid of nuclear charge distribution."
synonym: "quadrupole moment of a nucleus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08383
name: nuclear magnetic resonance relaxometry
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy to measure nuclear relaxation variables."
synonym: "relaxometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08384
name: nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum
def: "Representation of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy data with at least one dimension a frequency domain."
synonym: "NMR spectrum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08385
name: nuclear Overhauser effect
def: "Change of intensity of one resonance when the spin transitions of another are somehow perturbed from their equilibrium populations."
synonym: "nuclear Overhauser enhancement" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08386
name: nuclear Overhauser effect difference spectroscopy
def: "One dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that yields\, through subtraction of spectra generated with and without selective excitation of a specific resonance\, the nuclear Overhauser enhancement of nuclei spatially close to a selectively excited nucleus."
synonym: "nOe difference spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08387
name: nuclear Overhauser effect spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy based on the nuclear Overhauser effect to observe nuclei that are close to each other in space."
synonym: "nuclear Overhauser enhancement spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08388
name: nuclear quadrupole resonance spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy where splitting of the nuclear spin states is determined by the electrostatic interaction of the nuclear charge density with the external electric potential of the surrounding electron cloud."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08389
name: observation frequency
def: "Frequency corresponding to the centre of the spectrum window for a given nucleus under observation and the nominal frequency used to generate the hard\, full spectrum pulses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08390
name: Pake doublet
def: "Characteristic line shape seen in solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy arising from dipolar coupling between two nuclei."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08391
name: phase correction
def: "Linear combination of the real and imaginary parts of a 1-D nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum to produce peaks with pure absorption mode line shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08392
name: phase cycling
def: "Component of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment that repeats a pulse sequence changing only the phases of the pulse\(s\) and the phase-sensitive detector reference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08393
name: powder pattern
def: "Very broad and distinctive line shape seen in a static solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment with a typical magnitude of \\\(\\pu\{E2\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{E5 Hz\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08394
name: precession
def: "Classical description of the behaviour of nuclear magnetic moments\, in which the vectors rotate about the \\\(\\boldsymbol\{B_\{0\}\}\\\) axis at their Larmor angular frequencies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08395
name: presaturation
def: "Technique to suppress a particular nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy signal where a long low-power pulse is applied to that specific resonance prior to the main pulse sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08396
name: pulse sequence
def: "Timed series of radiofrequency pulses and magnetic field gradients that define a part or whole of a nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08397
name: pulsed-field gradient
def: "Short\, timed pulse in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that momentarily destroys the magnetic field homogeneity within the sample through a spatial-dependent field intensity."
synonym: "gradient pulse" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08398
name: pure shift yielded by chirp excitation
def: "Nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment generating homonuclear spin-spin decoupled spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08399
name: quadrature detection
def: "Collection of time domain nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy data on both the \\\(x\\\) and \\\(y\\ \(\\rm\{g\}\)\\\) axes of the rotating frame of reference to allow discrimination of positive and negative frequencies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08400
name: quadrupolar nuclide
def: "Nuclide with spin quantum number \\\(I \\gt &half;\\\) and a non-spherical distribution of charge in the nucleus giving rise to a nuclear electric quadrupole moment \(\\\(eQ\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08401
name: quadrupolar relaxation
def: "Relaxation mechanism in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy arising from intramolecular quadrupolar interactions with electric field gradients."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08402
name: radiation damping
def: "Line-broadening effect on intense signals in high field nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08403
name: radiofrequency coil
def: "In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy\, an inductor-capacitor resonant circuit used to set up \\\(B_\{1\}\\\) magnetic inductions in a sample and to detect the signal from the sample."
synonym: "RF coil" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08404
name: real spectrum
def: "In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy\, one of two equally sized blocks of frequency data \\\(\\pu\{90\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\) out of phase to each other produced by Fourier transformation of the time domain signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08405
name: relaxation reagent
def: "Paramagnetic species added to a sample in a nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy experiment to promote more rapid spin-lattice relaxation and allow a faster pulse repetition rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08406
name: repetition time
def: "Time duration between repetitions of a nuclear magnetic resonance pulse sequence including the pulse sequence\, acquisition time and relaxation delay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08407
name: residual dipolar coupling
def: "Dipolar coupling\, but of a reduced magnitude to that seen in solids\, observed in a liquid sample between spins in a molecule where there is incomplete averaging of spatially anisotropic dipolar couplings."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08408
name: rotating-frame NOE spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy yielding through-space correlations via spin-spin relaxation."
synonym: "cross-relaxation appropriate for minimolecules emulated by locked spins (CAMELPSIN)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08409
name: rotational echo double resonance
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy allowing recoupling of heteronuclear dipolar coupling under magic angle spinning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08410
name: saturation transfer difference spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy that allows detection of transient binding of small molecule ligands to macromolecular receptors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08411
name: selective decoupling
def: "Spin decoupling of a specific resonance by selective irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08412
name: selective excitation
def: "Application of a frequency selective radiofrequency pulse\(s\) to a single resonance or a discrete band of frequencies."
synonym: "selective irradiation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08413
name: selective pulse
def: "Radiofrequency pulse with a narrow frequency spectral bandwidth \(long\, low-power pulse\) to excite nuclei in a limited chemical shift range \(see selective excitation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08414
name: single crystal nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy on a single crystal in a similar manner to that used in X-ray diffraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08415
name: solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Application of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy to solids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08416
name: solvent suppression
def: "In nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy reduction or removal of large\, unwanted resonances from solvents to improve spectrum quality and dynamic range through selective excitation and exploiting differences in relaxation times between solvent and analyte signals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08417
name: spectral width
def: "Frequency range that describes the width of the spectrum window of a specific nucleus in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08418
name: spin
def: "Intrinsic angular momentum of a nucleus \(or other sub-atomic particle\)."
synonym: "nuclear spin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08419
name: spin decoupling
def: "Irradiation of a nucleus in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy to prevent spin-spin coupling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08420
name: spin echo
def: "Refocusing of spin magnetization usually by two consecutive radiofrequency pulses."
synonym: "Hahn echo" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08421
name: spin system
def: "Group of magnetic nuclei that interact amongst themselves through spin-spin coupling but do not interact with any nuclei outside the spin system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08422
name: spin-lattice relaxation
def: "Mechanism in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy by which the component of the magnetization vector along the direction of the static magnetic induction reaches thermodynamic equilibrium with its surroundings through loss of energy of the excited nuclear spins to the surrounding molecular lattice. The resultant exponential decay of signal is characterized by the spin-lattice relaxation time \\\(T_\{1\}\\\) \(reciprocal of the rate constant of this mechanism\)."
synonym: "$T1$ relaxation" []
synonym: "$T_1$ relaxation" []
synonym: "longitudinal relaxation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08423
name: spin-lattice relaxation time
def: "Reciprocal of the rate constant of longitudinal relaxation."
synonym: "spin-lattice time constant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08424
name: spinning sidebands
def: "Satellite peaks in a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum symetrically spaced either side of a main peak at a frequency offset related to the rate of spinning of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08425
name: spin-spin relaxation
def: "Loss of magnetization in the \\\(xy\\\) plane \(in the absence of \\\(\\boldsymbol\{B_\{0\}\}\\\) inhomogeneities\) through the interchange of energy between nuclear spins resulting in some precessing faster than the Larmor angular frequency and some slower."
synonym: "T2 relaxation" []
synonym: "transverse relaxation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08426
name: spin-spin relaxation time
def: "Reciprocal of the rate constant of spin-spin relaxation."
synonym: "transverse relaxation time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08427
name: static magnetic flux density
def: "Flux density of the magnetic field of a nuclear magnetic resonance spectrometer about whose direction the nuclear magnetic moment precesses."
synonym: "magnetic flux density" []
synonym: "static magnetic field" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08428
name: symmetrization
def: "Method of removing artefacts from a two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum that are symmetrical about the diagonal \(e.g.\, HH COSY\) or the \\\(\\rm\{f\}1\\\) axis \(e.g.\, tilted J-resolved spectra\). Values equidistant from midline are compared and replaced the lower \(or average\) value of the two."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08429
name: <em>t</em><sub>1</sub>
def: "Time domain arising from the regular increments of the delay period in two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."
synonym: "$t_1$ in 2-D NMR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08430
name: <em>t</em><sub>2</sub>
def: "Time domain in two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy arising from direct free induction decay detection."
synonym: "$t_2$ in 2-D NMR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08431
name: <em>t</em><sub>1</sub> noise
def: "Streaks of spurious artefact signals in a two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectrum parallel to the \\\(\\rm\{f\}1\\\) axis at the \\\(\\rm\{f\}2\\\) of a strong resonance."
synonym: "$t_1$ noise in 2-D NMR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08432
name: three-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy in which data are collected in three time domains."
synonym: "3-D NMR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08433
name: time domain
def: "Condition in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy where the independent variable of all functions is time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08434
name: time domain nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy run at low magnetic field strengths where data analysis is performed directly on the free induction decay\, often through the extraction of relaxation time constants"

[Term]
id: goldbook:08435
name: total correlation spectroscopy
def: "Two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance homonuclear correlation experiment\, which creates correlations between all protons within a given spin system\, not just between geminal or vicinal protons as in COSY."
synonym: "homonuclear Hartmann-Hahn spectroscopy (HOHAHA)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08436
name: total suppression of spinning sidebands
def: "Technique to suppress spinning sidebands in solid-state nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy with cross polarization in magic angle spinning experiments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08437
name: transverse relaxation optimized spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy\, usually in protein NMR\, that improves peak sharpness for molecules having a molecular mass of greater than \\\(\\pu\{100 kDa\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08438
name: triple-resonance nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy using three NMR-active nuclei."
synonym: "triple-resonance NMR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08439
name: two-dimensional nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy in which data are collected in two time domains\: acquisition of the free induction decay \(\\\(t_\{2\}\\\)\) and a successively incremented delay \(\\\(t_\{1\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "2-D NMR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08440
name: wideband\, alternating-phase\, low-power technique for residual splitting
def: "Cluster of pulses applied repeatedly for heteronuclear decoupling."
synonym: "WALTZ decoupling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08441
name: Zeeman levels
def: "Energy levels of a nucleus arising from the interaction of its magnetic dipole with an external magnetic field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08442
name: zero-field nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy in which nuclear magnetic resonance spectra are acquired in an environment carefully screened from magnetic fields\, including from the Earth’s field allowing the direct detection of J-spectra in the absence of Zeeman interactions \(see Zeeman levels\)."
synonym: "zero-field NMR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08443
name: ablation by sputtering
def: "Bombardment of a sample surface with ions resulting in the removal of atoms from their lattice sites\, and thus an ongoing discharge process that causes continuous ablation of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08444
name: ablation efficiency
def: "Mass of sample removed in laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy divided by the energy delivered by the laser beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08445
name: ablation rate
def: "Depth of sample layer removed in the glow discharge process per unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08446
name: ablation threshold fluence
def: "Minimum fluence required to achieve ablation of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08447
name: acid transient effects
def: "Increase in signal equilibration time or in wash out time caused by a modification in inorganic acid concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08448
name: additional gases
def: "Gases added to a combustion mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08449
name: aerosol transport phenomena
def: "Processes occurring inside the spray chamber or desolvation system responsible for the modification of the primary aerosol to finally yield the tertiary aerosol."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08450
name: analyte transport rate
def: "Mass of analyte reaching the plasma per unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08451
name: atomic absorption spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy for measuring the amount of a chemical element based on the measurement of the absorption of characteristic electromagnetic radiation by atoms in the vapour phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08452
name: atomic emission spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy for measuring the amount of a chemical element based on the measurement of the intensity of characteristic electromagnetic radiation emitted by atoms or molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08453
name: atomic excitation source
def: "Atomizer intended to convert free atoms to an excited state."
synonym: "excitation source" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08454
name: atomic fluorescence spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy for measuring the amount of a chemical element based on the measurement of the re-emission of characteristic electromagnetic radiation by atoms\, following the absorption of radiation in the vapour phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08455
name: atomic vapour
def: "Vapour containing free atoms of analyte."
synonym: "atomic vapor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08456
name: atomization curve
def: "Graph of absorbance against atomization temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08457
name: axial viewing mode
def: "Inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer configuration in which the entrance slit is aligned with the main plasma axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08458
name: background continuum emission
def: "Intense continuous background usually observed in laser-induced breakdown spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08459
name: background equivalent concentration
def: "Concentration of a given element measured at a given wavelength providing the same intensity as the background."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08460
name: breakdown threshold fluence
def: "Minimum fluence required to achieve measurable emission signals"

[Term]
id: goldbook:08461
name: capacitive microwave plasma
def: "Plasma generated by a capacitor in presence of a microwave field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08462
name: characteristic line
def: "Spectral line of an atom used for the measurement of analyte concentration by atomic spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08463
name: continuum source
def: "Source that emits electromagnetic radiation with relatively constant intensity over a broad spectral region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08464
name: depth-profiling
def: "Process by which the sample is sputtered 'atomic layer by layer' using suitable conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08465
name: direct current glow discharge optical emission spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method using optical emission spectroscopy in which atomization and excitation occur in a glow electric discharge sustained by a direct current electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08466
name: dispersion of a sample
def: "Conversion of the whole or part of a liquid or solid sample into a physical form sufficiently finely divided to allow it to be atomized \(see atomization\) upon introduction into the atomizer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08467
name: double-pulse laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy in which reactions are induced by the interaction of the evolved plasma and the remaining part of the laser pulse\, or with a following laser pulse provided from a second laser system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08468
name: dual viewing mode
def: "Inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer that allows axial viewing mode or radial viewing mode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08469
name: electric discharge
def: "Transmission of electrical current by a plasma in an applied electric field through a normally non-conducting medium."
synonym: "discharge" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08470
name: electron number density
def: "Number of free electrons in vapour phase per volume unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08471
name: electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of atomic absorption spectroscopy in which the sample is atomized by an electrothermal atomizer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08472
name: electrothermal vaporization
def: "Technique for sample introduction based on the use of a solid furnace heated at a controlled temperature to vaporize the sample prior to its introduction into an inductively-coupled plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08473
name: <em>E</em><sub>sum</sub>
def: "Energy that must be supplied to an atom to transfer it from the ground atomic state to a given ionic excited state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08474
name: flame atomic spectrometer
def: "Spectrometer to make measurements by flame atomic spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08475
name: flame atomic spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy that uses a flame to excite atoms."
synonym: "flame atomic spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08476
name: flame
def: "Continuously flowing mixture of hot gases with a stationary position that derives its heat content from a strongly exothermic\, irreversible chemical reaction between a fuel and an oxidant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08477
name: flow spoiler
def: "Component of a flame atomic spectrometer for creating turbulence in the stream of mist in the spray chamber and removing the largest droplets from this mist by deposition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08478
name: gate delay
def: "Time between the application of a laser pulse and the start of detection in laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy."
synonym: "time delay in laser induced breakdown spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08479
name: GD-OES spectrometers with simultaneous detection
def: "Spectrometers for glow discharge optical emission spectroscopy that allow simultaneous monitoring of selected spectral ranges or the whole emission spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08480
name: generator coupling efficiency
def: "Fraction of the energy generated being used for creating and maintaining the plasma in an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08481
name: glow discharge source
def: "Component of an atomic emission spectrometer employing a glow electric discharge used in atomic emission spectroscopy for qualitative and quantitative analysis of solid materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08482
name: glow electric discharge
def: "Electric discharge in gas at low pressure \(\\\(\\pu\{100\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{1000 Pa\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "glow discharge" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08483
name: glow-discharge optical emission spectroscopy
def: "Optical emission spectroscopy in which a glow electric discharge excites atoms at the surface of a sample\, which is made the cathode of the discharge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08484
name: Grimm-type cathode
def: "Cathode for a glow electric discharge consisting of a flat sample\, which is cooled during the discharge process so that thermal volatilization is suppressed and the sample material is removed by sputtering only."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08485
name: hollow-cathode discharge source
def: "Glow discharge source with a graphite cathode\, into which the sample is inserted in the form of drillings\, a pressed pellet or a dry solution residue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08486
name: hollow-cathode lamp
def: "Glow discharge source of spectral lines of an atom in which the cathode is made of the desired element."
synonym: "line source" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08487
name: incident power
def: "Power supplied by a generator to the plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08488
name: inductively-coupled plasma
def: "Plasma produced by induction by means of a high-frequency \(about \\\(\\pu\{2500 Hz\}\\\)\) electromagnetic field. The region of gas at high temperature and free from the field is taken as the region of observation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08489
name: inductively-coupled plasma spectrometry
def: "Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy that uses an inductively-coupled plasma to excite and ionize atoms."
synonym: "inductively-coupled plasma spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08490
name: inductively-coupled plasma mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method of atomic spectrometry that uses an inductively-coupled plasma to excite and ionize atoms with measurement of the number and kind of ions in the plasma using mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08491
name: inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy that uses an inductively-coupled plasma to excite and ionize atoms with measurement of the number and kind of ions in the plasma from their emission of electromagnetic radiation \(see optical emission spectroscopy\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08492
name: inductively-coupled plasma robustness
def: "Ability of an inductively-coupled plasma to withstand slight modification in the operating conditions and/or matrix composition with no significant changes in its fundamental characteristics \(i.e.\, temperatures\, electron number density\) or the analytical figures of merit \(i.e.\, sensitivity\, detection limits or signal-to-noise ratio\)"
synonym: "plasma robustness" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08493
name: inductively-coupled plasma thermal pinch
def: "Decrease in the inductively-coupled plasma volume normally caused when an organic volatile solution is delivered to it. Organic vapours diffuse towards the outermost area of the plasma and its volume decreases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08494
name: inductively-coupled plasma torch
def: "Quartz component of an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer where the plasma is generated and stabilized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08495
name: initial plasma radiation zone
def: "Inductively-coupled plasma volume in inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectroscopy where the analytically-relevant light emission begins to be observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08496
name: interference curve
def: "Graph of absorbance or intensity plotted as a function of the concentration of an interfering element in an atomic spectroscopy experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08497
name: inverse Bremsstrahlung
def: "Absorption of photon energy by free electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08498
name: ionic cloud
def: "Gas phase containing free ions of analyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08499
name: laser ablation
def: "Removal of material as an aerosol from a surface on irradiation by a laser beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08500
name: laser induced breakdown spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of atomic spectroscopy that uses a focused laser beam to ablate\, atomize and excite atoms from a sample surface with subsequent measurement of the generated electromagnetic radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08501
name: laser-induced breakdown spectrum
def: "Emission spectrum generated as a consequence of the decay of the excited states generated in a laser-induced plasma that consists of a continuous background and element-specific atomic or ionic lines and molecular bands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08502
name: laser-induced plasma
def: "Local plasma formed by the interaction between a laser beam and a solid sample and initiated by the production of primary electrons by multiphoton ionization and thermionic photoemission mechanisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08503
name: line-broadening
def: "Increase in the theoretical width of a spectral line owing to thermal motion of emitting atoms \(Doppler effect\)\, to electric field \(Stark effect\)\, to self-absorption and to pressure \(Lorentz effect\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08504
name: line profile
def: "Graph of the variation of emitted radiation intensity as a function of wavelength \(emission line\) or the variation of the absorption factor as a function of wavelength \(absorption line\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08505
name: low sample consumption system
def: "Liquid-sample introduction system to an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer adapted to work at sample flow rates below \\\(\\pu\{100 \\upmu L min-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08506
name: measurement efficiency
def: "Number of detector events per atom in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08507
name: microwave cavity
def: "Resonant cavity that permits focusing a microwave field inside a discharge tube thus giving rise to a standing wave."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08508
name: microwave-induced plasma
def: "Plasma generated in a microwave cavity."
synonym: "microwave plasma" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08509
name: nebulization
def: "Conversion of a liquid into an aerosol consisting of droplets suspended in a gas stream."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08510
name: nebulizer
def: "Device for producing nebulization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08511
name: non-specific emission or attenuation
def: "Electromagnetic radiation\, within the bandpass filter used\, emitted by\, or absorption caused by\, atoms\, molecules and radicals that are subject to the atomizer by all components\, apart from the analyte\, that are present there during measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08512
name: normal plasma analytical zone
def: "Inductively-coupled plasma volume mainly responsible for emission of analytically-useful light because processes leading to excited species\, such as droplet desolvation\, element vaporization\, atomization \(or ionization\) and excitation have been completed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08513
name: optical emission spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of atomic emission spectroscopy in which the emitted electromagnetic radiation is in the range of frequencies from the infrared\, through visible\, to the ultra-violet region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08514
name: overall efficiency of atomization
def: "In atomic spectroscopy\, mass of analyte converted into free atoms in the atomizer divided by mass of analyte entering the dispersion device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08515
name: Penning excitation
def: "Collisional excitation and ionization mechanism of inductively-coupled plasma spectrometry involving analyte atoms and metastable argon atoms."
synonym: "Penning ionization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08516
name: percentage transmission
def: "In atomic spectroscopy\, flux of mass of sample divided by the flux of mass of solvent blank\, measured under the same conditions\, expressed as a percentage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08517
name: permanent modifier
def: "Substance added together with the sample or standards in electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy that is refractory\, or forms a refractory species \(e.g.\, a carbide\)\, and thus does not need to be renewed for several atomization cycles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08518
name: plasma arc
def: "Plasma formed by an arc-discharge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08519
name: plasma excitation temperature
def: "Temperature governing the number density of atomic species in their ground state and excited states following a Boltzmann distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08520
name: plasma gases
def: "Gas streams used to maintain and stabilize the inductively-coupled plasma. The external stream is the main constituent of the plasma and confines the plasma avoiding melting of the torch walls; the intermediate gas stream keeps the plasma at a given height above the top of the torch and\, the central stream carries the aerosol and promotes its injection in the plasma central channel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08521
name: plasma induction zone
def: "Inductively-coupled plasma volume where there is a maximum interaction between the electromagnetic field and the plasma gas \(mostly argon\). This is the plasma’s hottest area and has a toroidal configuration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08522
name: plasma ionization temperature
def: "Temperature associated with the energy level of plasma ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08523
name: plasma local thermodynamic equilibrium
def: "Condition for which the temperatures estimated from different particles \(atoms\, molecules\, ions\, electrons\) constituting a plasma are locally coincident."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08524
name: plasma shielding
def: "Prevention of laser pulse energy from reaching a sample surface as a result of the presence of a laser-induced plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08525
name: plasma termination
def: "Extinction of the plasma after a laser pulse as a consequence of self-absorption \(quenching\) and recombination of electrons and ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08526
name: platform atomization
def: "Sample support made of graphite that is inserted into the atomizer tube in electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy. The sample\, a liquid or a solid\, is deposited onto this platform\, from which it undergoes all the transformations leading to atomization. The use of a platform delays atomization\, until a more stabilized temperature in the gas phase is reached."
synonym: "L’vov platform" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08527
name: pneumatic nebulizers
def: "Nebulizers for which the aerosol generation principle is based on the exposure of the liquid sample to a high velocity gas stream."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08528
name: preheating inductively-coupled plasma zone
def: "Inductively-coupled plasma volume where tertiary aerosols suffer from complete solvent evaporation\, element salt vaporization and dissociation into atoms."
synonym: "preheating plasma zone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08529
name: primary aerosol
def: "Aerosol generated by a nebulizer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08531
name: pulse energy
def: "Total optical energy content of a laser pulse. Integral of optical power over pulse duration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08532
name: pulsed laser
def: "Laser system that emits electromagnetic radiation in the form of pulses of a defined and constant duration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08533
name: pyrolysis curve
def: "Graph of absorbance against pyrolysis temperature in electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08534
name: quartz atomizer
def: "Atomizer that is used to atomize those elements that form volatile species \(e.g.\, hydrides\) at relatively low temperatures \(e.g.\, \\\(\\pu\{1000^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\) or below\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08535
name: radial viewing mode
def: "Inductively-coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer configuration in which the plasma and the entrance slit are perpendicular and from which the signal is taken perpendicularly to the main plasma axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08536
name: radiofrequency generator
def: "Component of an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer responsible for the generation of alternating current in the radiofrequency portion of the electromagnetic spectrum \(see table\)."
synonym: "RF generator" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08537
name: radiofrequency glow discharge optical emission spectroscopy
def: "Optical emission spectroscopy in which a radiofrequency-powered glow discharge is operated with frequencies in the low megahertz range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08538
name: reference flux density
def: "Intensity transmitted by the reference medium in a double-beam spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08539
name: reflected power
def: "Incident power that is not absorbed by the plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08540
name: repetition rate
def: "Number of laser pulses emitted by a pulsed laser system per unit time."
synonym: "pulse frequency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08541
name: resonant inelastic X-ray scattering
def: "Measurement method of inelastic X-ray spectroscopy that uses a hard or soft X-ray beam to excite transitions of core-level electrons into empty energy levels. The subsequent transition of an electron into the core state is accompanied by X-ray emission with characteristic momentum and energy."
synonym: "resonant X-ray Raman" []
synonym: "resonant X-ray emission" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08542
name: Saha’s ionization equation
def: "Equation that relates the ionization state of an element present in a gas in thermal equilibrium to the temperature and the pressure of the medium."
synonym: "Saha–Langmuir equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08543
name: sample flux density
def: "Intensity transmitted by the atomizer when the latter is supplied with the sample solution or a reference solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08544
name: saturator
def: "Buffer containing interfering element\(s\) in sufficient quantity to reach the limit of enhancement or dispersion \(i.e.\, saturation\) of the interference curve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08545
name: sequential detection
def: "Sequential spectrometers use a dispersive element \(grating\) in combination with entrance and exit slits for selection of specific wavelengths. Geometric arrangements include the Czerny-Turner and the Ebert-geometry\, both offering high resolution measurements. However\, they do not permit simultaneous recording of different spectral lines\, even for single emission line intensity\, and adjacent spectral background has also to be measured sequentially."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08546
name: solvent blank flux density
def: "Intensity transmitted by the atomizer when the latter is supplied with the solvent blank."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08547
name: solvent nucleation
def: "Solvent condensation on solid particles or droplets contained in an aerosol."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08548
name: solvent transport rate
def: "Mass of solvent\, both in liquid and vapour forms\, reaching an inductively-coupled plasma per unit of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08549
name: spectral background
def: "Electromagnetic radiation\, within the bandpass used\, emitted by\, and/or in the case of atomic absorption spectroscopy\, absorbed in\, the atomizer when nothing is supplied to the atomizer but the gases involved in its operation."
synonym: "flame background" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08550
name: spectral line
def: "In atomic spectroscopy\, a very narrow band of frequencies of electromagnetic radiation emitted or absorbed by atoms\, which undergo a single electronic transition."
synonym: "line" []
synonym: "spectral line" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08551
name: spray chamber
def: "Chamber of a nebulizer in which the sprayed liquid is converted to mist. Some of the droplets of this mist may evaporate\, coalesce\, or deposit in the chamber and subsequently drain away as waste."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08552
name: sputter rate
def: "Mass of material removed from a sample surface in unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08553
name: stop-flow conditions
def: "Stoppage to the otherwise constant flow of argon gas to the atomizer in electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy\, that can be programmed to occur during atomization\, for the purpose of increasing the residence time of the analyte atoms and thus the associated signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08554
name: synchrotron X-ray spectroscopy
def: "X-ray spectroscopy performed using a high intensity\, continuous polychromatic synchrotron X-ray source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08555
name: tertiary aerosol
def: "Aerosol that reaches an inductively-coupled plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08556
name: thermal dispersion
def: "Procedures whereby an aerosol is produced at a high temperature\, for example by sparks\, arcs\, furnaces\, lasers\, cathodic sputtering or electron-beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08557
name: thermal volatilization
def: "Process in glow-discharge atomic emission spectroscopy in which sample volatilization occurs by heating due to sample bombardment with argon ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08558
name: time-resolved laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of laser-induced breakdown spectroscopy in which the emitted radiation is monitored with time resolution sufficiently high to resolve different stages in the lifetime of the laser-induced plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08559
name: total sample consumption system
def: "Liquid-sample introduction system able to achieve \\\(\\pu\{100\\%\}\\\) transport efficiency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08560
name: transport efficiency
def: "Mass of analyte entering the atomizer of a flame atomic spectrometer or the plasma of an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer divided by the mass of analyte entering the dispersion device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08561
name: ultrasonic nebulizers
def: "Nebulizers whose aerosol generation principle is based on the transfer of the energy from a vibrating transducer to the liquid sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08562
name: wall atomization
def: "Electrothermal atomic absorption spectroscopy in which the sample is deposited directly onto the wall of the electrothermal atomizer tube\, from which it undergoes all the transformations leading to atomization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08563
name: wash out time
def: "Time required for the output signal of an inductively-coupled plasma spectrometer for a given analyte to fall back to baseline levels from the end of sampling time and/or after introducing a blank solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08564
name: X-ray emission spectrum
def: "Spectrum comprising the continuum Bremsstrahlung and the most energetic characteristic spectral lines defined by differences in binding energy between electron energy levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08565
name: amorphous material transmitting infrared radiation
def: "Amorphous glasses made from elements of groups 4\, 5 and 6 of the Periodic Table that transmit infrared radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08566
name: amplitude modulation
def: "Modulation of the amplitude of a beam of electromagnetic radiation; by the movement of a moving mirror in a rapid scan Fourier-transform spectrometer or by a mechanical chopper in other spectrometers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08567
name: anisotropic Raman scattering
def: "Raman scattering by the anisotropic part of the derived polarizability tensor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08568
name: anomalous dispersion
def: "Changes in real refractive index of a material in the vicinity of an absorption band in which real refractive index decreases markedly with decreasing wavenumber on the high wavenumber side of an absorption band\, then increases rapidly through the band centre and decreases markedly on the low wavenumber side of the band\, before resuming normal dispersion at wavenumbers far from the band."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08569
name: anomalously polarized Raman band
def: "Raman band with a depolarization ratio greater than \\\(\\pu\{0.75\}\\\)\, as is frequently observed in surface-enhanced resonance Raman spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08570
name: anti-Stokes Raman scattering
def: "Raman scattering of electromagnetic radiation in which the scattered radiation has greater energy \(greater wavenumber\) than the exciting radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08571
name: asymmetric top
def: "Rotor whose three principal moments of inertia are all different."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08572
name: atomic polar tensor
def: "3 × 3 tensor that gives the change in the components of the molecular dipole moment when the atom is displaced in three-dimensional space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08573
name: attenuated total reflection spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of vibrational spectroscopy based on internal reflection from an absorbing material at angles of incidence at or above the critical angle \(attenuated total reflection\)."
synonym: "frustrated total internal reflection spectroscopy" []
synonym: "internal reflection spectroscopy (IRS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08574
name: band shape
def: "Description of the profile of a spectral band."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08575
name: Bragg filter
def: "Optical filter used\, for example\, to attenuate Rayleigh scattering while transmitting light subject to Raman shift."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08576
name: centre burst
def: "Spectral region of an interferogram around zero-path-difference where intensity is greatest."
synonym: "center burst" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08577
name: Christiansen effect
def: "Collections of solid particles that are transparent to electromagnetic radiation of a particular wavelength and are slightly larger than the wavelength of the radiation transmit poorly due to reflection and scattering from the interfaces. The transmission increases markedly at wavenumbers at which the refractive index of the particles is close to that of the surrounding medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08578
name: Christiansen filter
def: "Optical filter based on the Christiansen effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08579
name: coherent anti-Stokes Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy that uses third-order susceptibility and is one of several four-wave mixing spectroscopies. The excitation is through lasers of wavenumber \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{2\}\\\)\, which coincide spatially and temporally in the sample and produce an output laser of wavenumber \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{3\} = 2\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{1\} - \\tilde\{\nu\}_\{2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08580
name: combination transition
def: "Transition in which more than one vibration changes its degree of excitation; i.e.\, in which more than one vibrational quantum number changes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08581
name: continuous scan interferometer
def: "Interferometer in which the optical path difference is continually changed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08582
name: Coriolis coupling
def: "Coupling between rotational and vibrational molecular motion caused by Coriolis forces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08583
name: Coriolis force
def: "In vibrational theory\, the motion of a polyatomic molecule is usually initially regarded as the independent superposition of a rotation and a vibration\, with the vibration considered in a coordinate system that rotates with the molecule. This initial treatment must be corrected for centrifugal forces and for Coriolis forces. The Coriolis force on an atom is given by \\\(F_\{\\rm\{Coriolis\}\} = 2m v_\{\\rm\{a\}\} \\omega \\sin \\varphi\\\)\, where \\\(m\\\) is the mass of the atom\, \\\(v_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) is its apparent velocity in the rotating coordinate system\, \\\(\\omega\\\) is the angular velocity of the coordinate system\, and \\\(\\varphi\\\) is the angle between the velocity vector and the axis of rotation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08584
name: critical angle
def: "Smallest angle of incidence at which total internal reflection occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08585
name: deformation vibration
def: "Vibration in which the dominant change from equilibrium is in one or more valence angles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08586
name: depolarization ratio
def: "Raman intensity with the electric vector of the scattered electromagnetic radiation perpendicular to that of the incident radiation divided by the Raman intensity with electric vectors parallel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08587
name: depolarized Raman band
def: "Raman band with depolarization ratio \\\(\\rho = 0.75\\\) for linear polarized incident radiation in normal Raman spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08588
name: difference transition
def: "Combination transition that does not start at the ground state and in which at least one vibration decreases and at least one vibration increases its degree of excitation; i.e.\, a transition in which more than one vibrational quantum number changes and at least one becomes smaller."
synonym: "difference band" []
synonym: "difference tone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08589
name: divided spinning cell
def: "Measuring instrument for Raman spectroscopy incorporating a dish-shaped cell that is divided by a vertical partition through its middle to allow different liquids in its two halves. It is rotated about a vertical axis through its centre and the Raman scattered radiation from each half is measured alternately. With a standard liquid in one half and a sample in the other it is used to determine Raman scattering intensities relative to the standard."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08590
name: dynamic spectrum
def: "Spectrum of a material under the influence of an applied external perturbation represented by a variable \\\(\\tau\\\)\, relative to a reference spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08591
name: electrical anharmonicity
def: "Influence of terms that are of \\\(2^\{\\rm\{nd\}\}\\\) and higher order in the normal coordinate \\\(Q\\\) in the expansion of the electric dipole moment."
synonym: "anharmonicity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08592
name: electrical harmonicity
def: "Influence of the linear term\, \\\(\\Sigma_\{k\} \(\\partial \\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}/\\partial Q_\{k\}\) Q_\{k\}\\\)\, in the expansion of the electric dipole moment. \\\(Q\\\) is the normal coordinate."
synonym: "harmonicity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08593
name: electron energy loss spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of vibrational spectroscopy in which a constant-energy beam of electrons passes through or is reflected from a sample and the energy distribution in the transmitted or reflected beam is analysed to give a spectrum of number of electrons against electron energy loss."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08594
name: electro-optic parameter
def: "Characteristic quantity used in the analysis of absolute infrared intensity data chosen on the assumption that the electric dipole moment of the molecule can be described as the vector sum of bond dipole moments."
synonym: "equilibrium charge, charge flux (ECCF)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08595
name: ellipticity
def: "Degree to which linearly polarized incident electromagnetic radiation becomes elliptically polarized in an absorbing optically active medium. \\\[\\tan\\;\\psi = \(E_\{\\rm\{R\}\} - E_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\)/\(E_\{\\rm\{R\}\} + E_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\) = \\tanh \(k^\{\\rm\{L\}\} - k^\{\\rm\{R\}\}\)\\pi l/\\lambda\\\] where \\\(E_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\) and \\\(E_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\) are the electric vectors of right and left circularly polarized radiation\, \\\(k^\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\) and \\\(k^\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\) are the absorption indices of the sample for left and right circularly polarized radiation\, \\\(\\lambda\\\) is the wavelength of the radiation and \\\(l\\\) is the path-length through the medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08596
name: evanescent wave
def: "Part of a standing-wave normal to a reflecting surface that extends beyond the reflecting surface when an electromagnetic wave undergoes total internal reflection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08597
name: Evans hole
def: "Unexpected minimum or hole in a broad absorption band due to a gap being created in a broad distribution of states by Fermi resonance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08598
name: excitation profile
def: "Graph of Raman intensity at the desired Raman shift against wavenumber of the excitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08599
name: external reflection spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of spectroscopy in which radiation is reflected from a sample of higher refractive index than that of the incident medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08600
name: extraordinary wave
def: "In a uniaxial crystal electromagnetic radiation with electric vector oblique to the optic axis and which does not obey the normal laws of refraction."
synonym: "extraordinary ray" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08601
name: Fermi resonance
def: "Cubic anharmonic resonance \(see anharmonicity\)\, i.e.\, any interaction caused by cubic terms in the vibrational potential energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08602
name: finesse
def: "Free spectral range divided by resolution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08603
name: fingerprint bands
def: "Infrared and Raman bands that are characteristic of a particular molecule rather than a functional group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08604
name: fingerprint region
def: "Region of a spectrum that contains fingerprint bands as well as bands with characteristic group frequencies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08605
name: Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy
def: "Infrared spectroscopy in which a Fourier-transform spectrometer is used to separate the transmitted electromagnetic radiation into its component wavenumbers."
synonym: "Fourier-transform infrared spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08606
name: Fourier-transform Raman spectroscopy
def: "Raman spectroscopy in which a Fourier-transform spectrometer is used to separate the scattered electromagnetic radiation into its component wavenumbers."
synonym: "Fourier-transform Raman spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08607
name: free spectral range
def: "For a Fabry-Perot or laser cavity\, wavenumber interval between successive longitudinal cavity modes of vibration; \\\(\\Delta \\tilde\{\nu\} = 1/\(2nl\)\\\)\, where \\\(l\\\) is the cavity spacing and \\\(n\\\) is the refractive index of the material in the cavity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08608
name: grazing incidence
def: "Angle of incidence of a beam of electromagnetic radiation greater than \\\(\\pu\{70\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08609
name: group frequency
def: "Frequency in vibrational spectroscopy that is characteristic of a particular chemical functional group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08610
name: harmonic constant
def: "Coefficient of \(\\\(\nu + &half;\\\)\) in the vibrational term value\, determined by the harmonic terms in the vibrational potential energy."
synonym: "harmonic wavenumber" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08611
name: homogeneous line-broadening
def: "Line-broadening by mechanisms that affect every molecule of the same species in the sample in the same way."
synonym: "homogeneous broadening" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08612
name: hot transition
def: "Electronic transition between an excited state and a state in which all vibrational quantum numbers are the same or greater."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08613
name: hyper-Raman spectroscopy
def: "Raman spectroscopy in which scattering occurs through hyperpolarizabilities. A two-photon technique in which an intense pulsed beam of electromagnetic radiation is focused onto the sample. When sufficient power is present in the pulse\, two photons may interact with the one molecule to create a virtual state at double the frequency of the laser excitation. Raman scattering from this virtual state to an excited vibrational state of the ground state then occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08614
name: hyperspectral imaging
def: "Measurement method to obtain information about the spatial composition of a sample by obtaining a spectrum at each spatially resolved point of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08615
name: improper rotation axis
def: "In spectroscopy\, a rotation-reflection axis\, which is a symmetry element such that rotation by \\\(2\\pi/n\\\) about the axis followed by reflection through a plane perpendicular to the axis transforms an object into itself."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08616
name: inelastic electron tunnelling spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of vibrational spectroscopy to obtain spectra of molecules on metal oxide surfaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08617
name: infrared radiation
def: "Electromagnetic radiation of wavelength between approximately \\\(\\pu\{780 nm\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{1000 \\upmu m\}\\\) or of wavenumber between approximately \\\(\\pu\{13000 cm-1\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{10 cm-1\}\\\)."
synonym: "infrared" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08618
name: infrared spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of vibrational spectroscopy using infrared radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08619
name: inhomogeneous broadening
def: "Line-broadening by mechanisms that arise from an ensemble of spectroscopically non-equivalent molecules of the same species. Such molecules exist\, for example\, when molecules occupy non-equivalent sites in a condensed phase\, and when gaseous molecules have different velocities and\, thus\, shift the observed wavenumber differently through the Doppler effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08620
name: interference fringes
def: "Sinusoidal intensity variation due to interference of electromagnetic radiation that undergoes multiple reflection between two flat and parallel surfaces."
synonym: "channel fringes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08621
name: interference record
def: "Record of the signal from the detector of a two-beam interferometer as the optical path difference between the two beams is varied."
synonym: "interference function" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08622
name: interferogram
def: "Pattern formed by wave interference produced by an interferometer\, especially one represented on a screen or chart recorder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08623
name: internal reflection
def: "Reflection of electromagnetic radiation from a material of lesser refractive index than that of the incident medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08624
name: internal reflection element
def: "Transparent or semi-transparent material of high refractive index that carries infrared radiation to a sample in internal reflection and attenuated total reflection measurements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08625
name: inverse Raman scattering
def: "Raman scattering showing absorption when Stokes Raman scattering exceeds anti-Stokes Raman scattering\, in an experiment in which a sample is simultaneously illuminated by coincident beams from a continuum source and a pulse laser at \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{0\}\\\). Energy is absorbed from the continuum at \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{0\} + \\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) where \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the wavenumber of a Raman-active vibration in the sample that is excited by this process\, and this energy is emitted at \\\(\\tilde\{\nu\}_\{0\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08626
name: inverse spatially offset Raman spectroscopy
def: "Variant of spatially offset Raman spectroscopy in which a sample is illuminated with a ring of laser light and the Raman scattering is collected from the centre of the ring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08627
name: IRTRAN™
def: "Kinds of crystal transparent to infrared radiation used as windows and filters in infrared spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08628
name: Jacquinot stop
def: "Aperture in the optics of a Fourier-transform spectrometer\, typically between the source and the interferometer\, designed to be the limiting aperture when no other optical element such as the detector serves this purpose."
synonym: "J-stop" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08629
name: kinetic coupling
def: "Coupling of vibrational displacements through terms in the vibrational kinetic energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08630
name: KRS-5
def: "Eutectic \(\\\(\\pu\{42 \\% TlBr\, 58 \\% TlI\}\\\)\) mixture of thallium bromide and thallium iodide transparent to infrared radiation used as window in infrared spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08631
name: Kubelka–Munk function
def: "\\\(f\(R_\{\\infty\}\) = \(1 – R_\{\\infty\}\)^\{2\}/2R_\{\\infty\}\\\)\, where \\\(R_\{\\infty\}\\\) is the diffuse reflectance from a sample of infinite depth."
synonym: "remission function" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08632
name: lifetime broadening
def: "Homogeneous line-broadening due to the limited lifetime of energy states involved in a transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08633
name: local mode
def: "Mode of vibration localized in one type of bond."
synonym: "local mode" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08634
name: local molar polarizability
def: "Defined under the assumption of the Lorentz local field by the Lorentz-Lorenz formula\: \\\[\\hat\{\\alpha\}_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\) = 3V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\epsilon_\{0\} \\frac\{\\hat\{\\epsilon\}\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\) - 1\}\{\\hat\{\\epsilon\}\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\) + 2\} = 3V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\{\\epsilon\}_\{0\}\\\,\\frac\{\\hat\{n\}^\{2\}\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\) - 1\}\{\\hat\{n\}^\{2\}\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\) + 2\}\\\] where \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) is the molar volume and \\\(\\hat\{\\epsilon\}\\\) and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\hat\{n\}\}\\\) are the complex dielectric constant and refractive index\, respectively. To allow description of absorption\, \\\(\\hat\{\\alpha\}_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\)\\\) is complex\: \\\(\\hat\{\\alpha\}_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\) = \\alpha_\{\\rm\{m\}\}'\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\) + \\rm\{i\}\\alpha_\{\\rm\{m\}\}''\(\\tilde\{\nu\}\)\\\)."
synonym: "molar polarizability" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08635
name: mechanical anharmonicity
def: "Influence of cubic and higher-order terms in vibrational potential energy."
synonym: "anharmonicity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08636
name: mechanical harmonicity
def: "Influence of quadratic terms in vibrational potential energy."
synonym: "harmonicity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08638
name: molar ellipticity
def: "Ellipticity divided by the amount concentration of the optically active absorbing material and by the path-length at temperature \(\\\(t\\\)\) and wavelength \(\\\(\\lambda\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08639
name: multiplex advantage
def: "Decrease in measurement uncertainty obtained by measuring intensity at many different wavelengths simultaneously\, as is done\, for example\, in Fourier-transform spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08641
name: natural linewidth
def: "Line width of a molecular spectral band that arises from the probability of spontaneous emission in the absence of radiation fields and interaction with other molecules or entities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08642
name: near-infrared radiation
def: "Infrared radiation of which vacuum wavenumbers range from approximately \\\(\\pu\{12800 cm-1\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{4800 cm-1\}\\\) and wavelengths range from approximately \\\(\\pu\{780 nm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{2500 nm\}\\\)."
synonym: "near-IR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08643
name: non-dispersive infrared spectroscopies
def: "Kinds of infrared spectroscopy in which the effect of the sample on the radiation from the source is measured as a whole without exploring the dependence on wavenumber."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08644
name: normal coordinate
def: "Formal mathematical description of a normal vibration. When the potential energy\, \\\(V\\\)\, is harmonic and the kinetic energy\, \\\(T\\\)\, is calculated in the limit of infinitesimally small displacements\, the normal coordinates are defined as independent entities such that '\(for non-linear molecules\)'\\\[V = &half;\\sum_\{k=1\}^\{3N-6\}\\lambda_\{k\}Q_\{k\}^\{2\}\\ \{\\rm\{and\}\}\\ T = &half;\\sum_\{k=1\}^\{3N-6\}\\dot Q_\{k\}^\{2\}\\\]where \\\(\\lambda_\{k\}\\\) is the \\\(k^\{\\rm\{th\}\}\\\) eigenvalue and \\\(\\dot \{Q\}\\\) is \\\(\\partial Q/\\partial t\\\). Normal coordinate \\\(Q_\{k\}\\\) is related to the internal displacement coordinates\, \\\(R_\{i\}\\\)\, through the elements\, \\\(L_\{ik\} = \\partial R_\{i\}/\\partial Q_\{k\}\\\) of the \\\(k^\{\\rm\{th\}\}\\\) column of the eigenvector matrix \\\(\\boldsymbol\{L\}\\\). Normal coordinates are not independent when the potential energy is anharmonic\, or the displacements are not infinitesimal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08645
name: normal coordinate analysis
def: "Calculation of the normal modes of vibrations of a molecule or crystal under the assumptions that the potential energy is harmonic and the displacements from equilibrium are infinitesimal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08646
name: normal dispersion of the refractive index
def: "Slow decrease of real refractive index of a material in a non-absorbing spectral region far from regions of strong absorption as the wavenumber decreases."
synonym: "normal dispersion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08647
name: normal incidence
def: "Incidence of a beam of electromagnetic radiation when the angle of incidence \\\(\\theta = \\pu\{0\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\)\, i.e.\, the incident radiation beam is normal to the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08648
name: normal mode
def: "One of the \\\(3N - 6\\\) \(\\\(3N - 5\\\) for a linear molecule\) modes of vibration that would add to give the total vibrational motion in a molecule if the interatomic potential were strictly quadratic in the displacements from equilibrium."
synonym: "normal mode" []
synonym: "normal vibration" []
synonym: "normal vibration mode" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08649
name: normalized tunnelling intensity
def: "Second differential of intensity with respect to bias voltage divided by first differential of intensity with respect to bias voltage."
synonym: "constant resolution tunnelling intensity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08650
name: optical absorption depth
def: "Depth in a sample at which the intensity of an incident beam is reduced to \\\(1/\\rm\{e\}\\\) of its value at the surface of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08651
name: optical constants
def: "Collective name for the real refractive index\, \\\(n\\\) and the absorption index \(imaginary refractive index\)\, \\\(k\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08652
name: optical null double beam spectrometer
def: "Spectrometer in which the ratio of the radiant power in the two beams at each wavenumber is measured opto-mechanically by inserting a linear optical attenuator into the reference beam until the two signals measured by the detector are equal\, then measuring the displacement of the optical attenuator."
synonym: "NTI double beam spectrometer" []
synonym: "null-balance double beam spectrometer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08653
name: optical path difference
def: "Path-length difference between the arms of a two-beam interferometer."
synonym: "optical retardation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08654
name: optical rotatory dispersion
def: "Wavelength dependence of the angle of rotation of the plane of linearly-polarized radiation when transmitted through optically active materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08655
name: ordinary wave
def: "In a uniaxial crystal electromagnetic radiation with electric vector perpendicular to the optic axis and which obeys the normal laws of refraction."
synonym: "ordinary ray" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08656
name: overtone transition
def: "Transition between the ground state and a state in which a single mode of vibration is multiply excited."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08657
name: pATR spectrum
def: "\\\(-\\log_\{10\}\\\) of the spectrum obtained in attenuated total reflection spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08658
name: photoacoustic saturation
def: "Condition in photoacoustic spectroscopy in which all bands with greater than a certain absorption intensity appear to have the same intensity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08659
name: photothermal spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of spectroscopy in which the absorption of electromagnetic radiation by a sample is detected as a result of the heat generated by the absorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08660
name: polarized Raman band
def: "Raman band with depolarization ratio \\\(\\rho \\le 0.75\\\) for linear polarized incident radiation in normal Raman spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08661
name: potential coupling
def: "Coupling of vibrational displacements through terms in the vibrational potential energy. The second-order \(quadratic\) terms cause harmonic potential coupling while the cubic and higher terms cause anharmonic potential coupling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08662
name: p-polarization
def: "Polarization when electromagnetic radiation is incident upon a surface with its electric vector in the plane of incidence and\, therefore\, inclined to the reflecting surface unless the incidence is normal."
synonym: "TM polarization" []
synonym: "transverse magnetic polarization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08663
name: pressure broadening
def: "Increase in the line width of a spectral line of a gas due to molecular collisions and other intermolecular interactions that reduce the lifetime of an excited state."
synonym: "collision broadening" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08664
name: quantum cascade laser
def: "Laser that emits mid-infrared radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08665
name: Raman band
def: "Spectral band in Raman spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08666
name: Raman optical activity
def: "Phenomenon that vibrations in chiral molecules and materials Raman scattering right circularly polarized incident radiation to a different extent than left circularly polarized incident radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08667
name: Raman scattering
def: "Inelastic scattering of electromagnetic radiation by molecules that are excited to higher energy levels."
synonym: "normal Raman scattering" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08668
name: Raman shift
def: "Difference in wavenumber between the incident and scattered electromagnetic radiation in Raman spectroscopy."
synonym: "Raman wavenumber shift" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08669
name: Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of vibrational spectroscopy based on Raman scattering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08670
name: Raman spectrum
def: "Graph of the intensity of scattered electromagnetic radiation in Raman spectroscopy against Raman shift."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08671
name: Raman wavenumber shift
def: "Wavenumber of exciting electromagnetic radiation minus wavenumber of the scattered radiation."
synonym: "Raman shift" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08672
name: rapid-scan Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy
def: "Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy in which the optical path difference is continually changed at a rate of \\\(\\pu\{0.05 cm s-1\}\\\) or greater."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08673
name: reflection-absorption at grazing incidence
def: "Reflection-absorption with very thin layers of an absorbing material on a metallic substrate and the angle of incidence between \\\(\\pu\{70\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{90\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08674
name: resonance Raman scattering
def: "Raman scattering that occurs through the polarizability of a molecule\, \(not the hyperpolarizabilities\) and is excited by electromagnetic radiation that is in resonance with electronic transitions or vibronic transitions in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08675
name: rotational branch
def: "Spectral lines in the rotation-vibration spectrum of a gas that derive from the same change in rotation quantum number\, \\\(J\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08676
name: rotation-vibration spectrum
def: "Spectrum that shows absorption or emission of radiation during transitions between rotational-vibrational states of a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08677
name: scattering geometry
def: "Relation between the propagation directions\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, and the polarization directions\, \\\(p_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)\, \\\(p_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, of the incident and scattered radiation\, usually expressed in terms of laboratory-fixed or crystal-fixed Cartesian axes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08678
name: site splitting
def: "Appearance of more than one band in the spectrum of a crystal when only one is seen in that of the gas\, when this splitting is attributed to either the molecule lying on a crystal site of lower symmetry than that of the gaseous molecule or to molecules occupying more than one type of crystal site. See crystal field splitting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08679
name: spatially offset Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy in which the sample is illuminated at one location\, and the Raman signal is collected from a different location on the sample surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08680
name: spherical top
def: "Molecule whose three principal moments of inertia are all the same. An isotropic molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08681
name: step-scan interferometer
def: "Interferometer \(typically used for Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy\) in which the optical path difference is changed by a certain amount\, held constant\, or modulated if phase modulation is desired\, while the signal at the current path difference is recorded\, then the process is repeated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08682
name: stimulated Raman scattering
def: "Two-photon Raman scattering occurs when a picosecond pump beam is used to create a virtual state and a probe beam of a frequency to match the frequency of Stokes Raman scattering from the virtual state to a specific vibrational level. The Stokes emission is stimulated thus depopulating the virtual state very quickly and increasing the scattering efficiency for the chosen vibration by orders of magnitude. SRS is a non-linear process\, so sufficient photons from both the pump and probe beams must be present on the molecule at the one time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08683
name: Stokes–Raman scattering
def: "Raman scattering of electromagnetic radiation in which the scattered radiation has lesser energy \(lesser wavenumber\) than the exciting radiation."
synonym: "Stokes scattering" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08684
name: sum band
def: "Band resulting from a sum transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08685
name: sum transition
def: "Combination transition between the ground state and a state in which more than one vibration is excited; i.e.\, a transition from the ground state in which more than one quantum number increases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08686
name: sum-frequency spectroscopy
def: "Nonlinear optical technique in which pulsed lasers at frequencies \\\(\\omega_\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\omega_\{2\}\\\) are overlapped at a surface and light emitted at the sum of the two frequencies\, \\\(\\omega_\{1\} + \\omega_\{2\}\\\)\, is detected. One laser is normally in the mid-infrared and the other in the visible or near-infrared."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08687
name: surface-enhanced hyper-Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy by which the Raman spectrum is significantly enhanced simultaneously by both the hyper-Raman \(see hyper-Raman spectroscopy\) and surface-enhanced Raman \(see surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy\) effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08688
name: surface-enhanced infrared absorption
def: "Measurement method of infrared spectroscopy by which the intensity of absorption bands of molecules within a few nanometers of the surface of metal particles is increased by interactions with surface plasmons \(the collective resonance of electrons near the surface of metal islands\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08689
name: surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy by which the intensity of vibrational bands in the Raman spectra of molecules within a few nanometers of the surface of microscopically rough metals\, metal colloids and metal nanoparticles is increased by several orders of magnitude."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08690
name: surface-enhanced resonance Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy \(SERS\) by which the Raman spectrum is significantly enhanced when the conditions occur that whilst undergoing SERS the molecules are excited by radiation that is in resonance with electronic transitions in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08691
name: surface-enhanced\, spatially offset Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy that combines spatially offset Raman spectroscopy and surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy \(SERS\) enabling the detection of a SERS signal deep within diffusely scattering samples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08692
name: symmetric
def: "Term used in at least two senses in vibrational spectroscopy. In general usage\, a symmetric molecule is a molecule with high symmetry. In more specific usage\, a property is symmetric with respect to a symmetry element if it remains unchanged by the action of the corresponding symmetry operation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08693
name: symmetric top
def: "Molecule that has two equal principal moments of inertia with the third one different."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08694
name: symmetry coordinate
def: "Each \\\(S_\{i\}\\\) is a linear combination of general coordinates\, usually internal coordinates\, constructed to take advantage of the molecular symmetry. The \\\(S_\{i\}\\\) are elements of the vector \\\(\\boldsymbol\{S\}\\\). They have the same units as the general coordinates from which they were constructed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08695
name: symmorphic space group
def: "Space group that does not contain screw-axes and glide planes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08696
name: thermal diffusion depth
def: "In photoacoustic spectroscopy\, \\\(L = \\sqrt\{\(D/\\pi f\)\}\\\)\, where \\\(D\\\) is thermal diffusivity and \\\(f\\\) is modulation frequency of the electromagnetic radiation."
synonym: "thermal wave decay length" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08697
name: thermal diffusivity
def: "Thermal conductivity divided by the product of the specific heat and the density."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08698
name: thermal wave decay coefficient
def: "Reciprocal of the thermal diffusion depth \(\\\(L\\\)\). \\\(a_\{\\rm\{s\}\} = 1/L\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08699
name: tip-enhanced Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy combining surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy and scanning using an atomic force microscope or scanning tunnelling microscope to give complementary maps of a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08700
name: total internal reflection
def: "Internal reflection from a non-absorbing material at angles of incidence at or above the critical angle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08701
name: transflectance
def: "Spectral intensity of a transflected beam divided by spectral intensity of the incident beam in a transflection experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08702
name: transflection
def: "Reflection absorption at near-normal incidence when the thickness of the absorbing medium is large enough to yield an interpretable spectrum. The substrate is usually a mirror for mid-infrared measurements and a ceramic disk for near-infrared measurements. The angle of incidence is typically between \\\(\\pu\{0\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{45\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08703
name: transmission Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy in which the sample is illuminated on one side and the Raman signal is collected from the opposite side."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08704
name: vibrational circular dichroism
def: "Circular dichroism\, \\\(k^\{\\rm\{L\}\} – k^\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\)\, for vibrational transitions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08705
name: vibrational eigenvector
def: "Part of the solution of the matrix equation of normal coordinate analysis\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{G\\!F\\!L\} = \\boldsymbol\{L \\lambda\}\\\). Each element \\\(L_\{ik\}\\\) of \\\(\\boldsymbol\{L\}\\\) is a vibrational eigenvector\, and gives the change in internal coordinate \\\(R_\{i\}\\\) during unit change in the normal coordinate \\\(Q_\{k\}\\\)\, as shown in matrix form by \\\(\\boldsymbol\{R\} = \\boldsymbol\{LQ\}\\\)\, i.e.\, \\\(L_\{ik\} = \\partial R_\{i\}/\\partial Q_\{k\}\\\).\nEigenvectors are sometimes expressed in terms of symmetry coordinates or Cartesian coordinates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08706
name: vibrational kinetic energy
def: "Kinetic energy of the molecule as a function of the displacements of the atoms from equilibrium positions. For Cartesian displacement coordinates \(\\\(x\, y\, z\\\)\)\, \\\[T = \\sum_\{\\alpha\}&frac12;m_\{\\alpha\}\(\\dot x_\{\\alpha\}^\{2\} + \\dot y_\{\\alpha\}^\{2\} + \\dot z_\{\\alpha\}^\{2\}\)\\\] where the sum is over all atoms and \\\(\\dot\{x\}_\{\\alpha\}\\\)\, etc.\, are the displacement velocities. For internal coordinates \\\[T = &frac12;\\boldsymbol\{P\}^\{t\}\\boldsymbol\{GP\} = &frac12;\\dot\{\\boldsymbol\{R\}\}^\{t\}\{\\boldsymbol\{G\}\}^\{-1\}\\dot \{\\boldsymbol\{R\}\} = &frac12;\\sum_\{ij\}G_\{ij\}^\{-1\}\\dot R_\{i\}\\dot R_\{j\}\\\] where \\\(\\dot \{\\boldsymbol\{R\}\}^\{t\}\\\) and \\\(\\dot \{\\boldsymbol\{R\}\}\\\) are the row and column vector\, respectively\, of the \\\(\\partial R_\{i\}/\\partial t\\\)\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{P\}^\{t\}\\\) and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{P\}\\\) are the row and column vector\, respectively\, of the momenta conjugate to the \\\(R_\{i\}\\\)\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{G\}^\{-1\}\\\) is the inverse of the \\\(\\boldsymbol\{G\}\\\) matrix and \\\(G_\{ij\}^\{-1\}\\\) is the \\\(ij^\{\\rm\{th\}\}\\\) element of \\\(\\boldsymbol\{G\}^\{-1\}\\\). The elements of the \\\(\\boldsymbol\{G\}\\\) matrix are defined by \\\[G_\{ij\} = \\sum_\{\\alpha\} \\frac\{1\}\{m_\{\\alpha\}\} B_\{i\\alpha\} B_\{j\\alpha\}\\\] where \\\(B_\{i\\alpha\}\\\) and \\\(B_\{j\\alpha\}\\\) relate the \\\(i^\{\\rm\{th\}\}\\\) and \\\(j^\{\\rm\{th\}\}\\\) internal coordinate to the \\\(\\alpha^\{\\rm\{th\}\}\\\) Cartesian coordinate through the equation \\\(\\boldsymbol\{R\} = \\boldsymbol\{BX\}\\\) in which \\\(\\boldsymbol\{X\}\\\) is the column vector of the Cartesian coordinates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08707
name: vibrational potential energy
def: "Potential energy of the molecule as a function of the displacements of the atoms from equilibrium positions. The harmonic terms are quadratic in the displacements and the anharmonic terms are of cubic\, quartic and higher orders in the displacements.\n\\\(Q\\\) are normal coordinates \\\[2V = \\sum\\limits_\{k\}\\lambda_\{k\}Q_\{k\}^\{2\} + \\sum\\limits_\{ijk\}k_\{ijk\}Q_\{i\}Q_\{j\}Q_\{k\} + higher\\\,order\\\,terms\\\]\nIn terms of internal coordinates\, \\\[2V = \\sum\\limits_\{i\} F_\{ii\} R_i^\{2\} + \\sum\\limits_\{i\,j\ne i\} F_\{ij\} R_\{i\} R_\{j\} + \\sum\\limits_\{i\,j\,k\} F_\{ijk\} R_\{i\} R_\{j\} R_\{k\} + higher\\\,order\\\,terms\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:08708
name: vibrational spectroscopy
def: "Measurement principle of spectroscopy to analyse molecular properties based on vibrations \(bond stretching or deformation modes\) in chemical species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08709
name: vibrational term value
def: "Vibrational energy\, \\\(E\\\)\, expressed in wavenumber units. \\\(G = E/\(hc\)\\\)\, where \\\(h\\\) is the Planck constant and \\\(c\\\) is the speed of light in vacuum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08710
name: volume reflection
def: "Re-emergence of electromagnetic radiation from the surface of incidence after penetrating into a powdered sample."
synonym: "Kubelka–Munk reflection" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08711
name: absolute counting efficiency
def: "Number of particles or photons counted by a radiation detector divided by the number emitted by a radiation source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08712
name: accelerator mass spectrometry
def: "Mass spectrometry technique in which atoms and molecules from a sample are ionized\, accelerated to mega-electron volt \(\\\(\\pu\{1 MeV\} = \\pu\{1.602176634E-13 J\}\\\)\) energies\, and separated according to their momentum\, charge\, and energy\, allowing high discrimination for the measurement of nuclide abundances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08713
name: activity growth curve
def: "Graph of the activity of a radioactive nuclide as a function of time and showing the increase of activity through the decay of the precursor or as a result of activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08714
name: backscattering analysis
def: "Measurement principle in which the backscattering of nuclear radiation impinging on a sample is applied to measure the structure and composition of materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08716
name: calibration\, k0 method
def: "Calibration method of neutron activation analysis in which the \\\(k_\{0\}\\\) proportionality factors\, together with a value of the neutron flux \(as a function of the neutron energy\) and with a value of the radiation detector's response for gamma radiation \(as a function of the gamma-ray energy\) are used for establishing a relationship between the number of counts measured and the corresponding amounts of elements in a material."
synonym: "<em>k</em><sub>0</sub> method of calibration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08717
name: calibrator
def: "Measured amount of an element with stated measurement uncertainty that is simultaneously irradiated with the sample during activation analysis."
synonym: "comparator (obsolete)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08718
name: channeling effect
def: "Range- \(traveling distance\) increasing effect caused by the decrease in interaction cross sections of the incident particles with atoms in a single crystal when a highly collimated particle beam impinges on a crystal along its principal axis or principal plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08719
name: channels ratio method
def: "Measurement method to obtain a quenching correction by counting the spectrum in two separate channels\, the ratio of which gives the degree of quenching."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08720
name: Compton scattering analysis
def: "Measurement principle in which wavelengths and angular distribution of Compton scattered electrons is applied in order to reconstruct X-ray and gamma-ray spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08721
name: cross reactivity
def: "Ability of substances other than the analyte to bind to the binding reagent and ability of substances other than the binding reagent to bind the analyte in competitive binding assays."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08722
name: cross section
def: "Characteristic area related to the probability of a specified interaction or reaction between an incident nuclear radiation and a target particle or system of particles."
synonym: "microscopic cross section" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08723
name: delayed-neutron activation analysis
def: "Neutron activation analysis where neutrons are counted after a delay to allow interfering species to decay."
synonym: "delayed-neutron analysis (DNA)" []
synonym: "delayed-neutron counting (DNC)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08724
name: directly-ionizing radiation
def: "Beam of particles capable of removing one or more orbital electrons in a single quantum event from a specified atom resulting in an ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08725
name: external standardization for quenching correction
def: "Measurement method to obtain a quenching correction through the use of a gamma-radiation source to generate a spectrum of Compton electrons within the sample vial."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08726
name: flux monitor
def: "Radiation detector to measure energy flux density."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08727
name: flux perturbation
def: "Change of energy flux density or energy distribution of particles or photons in an object as a result of effects such as flux depression and/or self-shielding."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08728
name: gamma-ray spectrometry
def: "Measurement principle of the quantitative study of the energy spectra of gamma-ray sources."
synonym: "gamma-ray spectroscopy (obsolete)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08729
name: half-value thickness
def: "Thickness of a specified material that\, when introduced into the path of a given beam of radiation\, reduces the intensity of a specified radiation by one half."
synonym: "half thickness" []
synonym: "half-value layer thickness" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08730
name: indirectly-ionizing radiation
def: "Beam of electrically neutral particles that cause ionization by interacting with atoms in a material\, producing electrically-charged particles that subsequently cause direct ionization in the material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08731
name: <em>in-vivo</em> neutron activation analysis
def: "Measurement method of neutron activation analysis in which a living organism is exposed to neutrons to measure the concentrations of elements in that living organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08732
name: ion beam analysis
def: "Measurement principle in which elementary particles resulting from nuclear reactions of charged particles with nuclei in a material are applied in order to measure the amount and depth distribution of elements in materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08733
name: isotopic label
def: "Radioactive or stable isotope of a specified element distinguishable by the observer but not by the system used to identify an isotopic tracer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08735
name: monoisotopic element
def: "Chemical element having only one stable nuclide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08736
name: Mössbauer spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which recoil-less resonance gamma ray scattering and absorption in solids is applied to measure the nuclear environment of atoms of elements in a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08737
name: muon induced X-ray emission analysis
def: "Measurement method of X-ray analysis in which characteristic X-radiation emitted upon irradiation of a material with a beam of muons of certain energy is used to measure the chemical composition and chemical state of an element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08738
name: near edge X-ray absorption fine structure
def: "Measurement method of X-ray absorption analysis similar to X-ray absorption near edge structure\, but usually reserved for soft X-rays with photon energy less than \\\(\\pu\{1 keV\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08739
name: neutron activation analysis
def: "Activation analysis using neutrons as the incident particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08740
name: neutron depth profiling
def: "Measurement method of activation analysis for near-surface-depth light elements in which a thermal neutron or cold neutron \(see\: neutron energy\) beam passes through a material with target nuclei that emit monoenergetic charged particles upon neutron absorption. The reduction of the energy of an emitted charged particle measures the depth of the target nuclei in a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08741
name: neutron diffraction analysis
def: "Measurement method in which the diffraction of neutrons is applied to measure parameters of the atomic and/or magnetic structure of a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08742
name: neutron energy
def: "Kinetic energy of a free neutron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08743
name: neutron scattering analysis
def: "Measurement method in which the elastic or inelastic scattering of neutrons by the target nuclei is applied to study the composition and structure of a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08744
name: non-radiative quenching
def: "Deactivation of an electronically-excited state by interaction with the external environment through a non-radiative process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08745
name: nuclear capture
def: "Process in which an atomic nucleus acquires an additional particle."
synonym: "capture" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08746
name: nuclide precursor
def: "Radionuclide which precedes a nuclide in a decay chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08747
name: particle flux density
def: "Number of particles \(or photons\) incident on a plane perpendicular to the incident radiation per unit time per unit area."
synonym: "fluence rate (obsolete)" []
synonym: "photon flux density" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08748
name: particle-induced gamma-ray-emission analysis
def: "Measurement principle in which the energies and intensities of characteristic prompt gamma-radiation emitted during nuclear reactions with charged particles other than electrons is used to measure the amounts of elements in a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08749
name: perturbed angular correlation spectrometry
def: "Measurement method of gamma-ray spectrometry in which coincidence counting is used for the measurement of parameters describing hyperfine interactions\, with internal or external electrical or magnetic field gradients\, of the spin of an intermediate level between two gamma-ray transitions in cascade emitted in the decay of a radionuclide."
synonym: "perturbed directional correlation spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08750
name: positron annihilation analysis
def: "Measurement principle in which the annihilation of positrons is applied to study the microscopic structure of materials."
synonym: "positron annihilation spectroscopy for chemical analysis (PASCA)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08751
name: positron emission tomography
def: "Imaging method based on the detection of pairs of gamma rays emitted indirectly by a positron-emitting isotopic tracer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08752
name: positron lifetime spectrometry
def: "Measurement method of positron annihilation analysis in which the time interval between the emission of positrons from a radioactive source and the detection of gamma rays due to the annihilation of these positrons with electrons from the surrounding matter is the lifetime of the positron or positronium."
synonym: "positron annihilation lifetime spectrometry (PALS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08753
name: prompt gamma radiation
def: "Gamma radiation emitted during the de-excitation of a compound nucleus formed in a nuclear capture reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08754
name: prompt gamma-ray analysis
def: "Measurement principle in which gamma radiation emitted during the de-excitation of the compound nucleus formed by neutron capture is applied to measure the amounts of elements in a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08755
name: pulse pile-up
def: "Processing by a radiation counter of pulses resulting from the simultaneous absorption of independent particles or photons in a radiation detector\, resulting in them being counted as one single particle or photon with energy between the individual energies and the sum of these energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08757
name: radiation filter
def: "Material interposed in the path of radiation to modify the energy distribution of the radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08758
name: radioactive steady state
def: "Among the radionuclides of a decay chain\, the state that prevails when the ratios between the activities of successive radionuclides remain constant."
synonym: "radioactive equilibrium" []
synonym: "secular equilibrium" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08759
name: recovery time
def: "Period of time after the dead time of a radiation counter during which the output pulses are smaller than the original."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08760
name: relative counting efficiency
def: "Absolute counting efficiency of a given radiation counter divided by the absolute counting efficiency of a reference radiation counter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08761
name: scanning proton microscopy
def: "Measurement method of X-ray fluorescence analysis in which protons are focused and collimated to form a micro beam to obtain an image of a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08762
name: selectively-labelled isotopic tracer
def: "An isotopically labelled compound is designated as selectively labelled when a mixture of isotopically substituted compounds is formally added to the analogous isotopically unmodified compound in such a way that the position\(s\)\, but not necessarily the number\, of each labelling nuclide is defined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08763
name: specific activity \(radionuclide\)
def: "Activity of a specified radionuclide \(\\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\)\) per unit mass of that nuclide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08764
name: synchrotron radiation induced X-ray fluorescence analysis
def: "Measurement method of X-ray fluorescence analysis in which synchrotron X-radiation is used to irradiate the substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08765
name: total reflection X-ray fluorescence analysis
def: "Measurement method of X-ray fluorescence analysis in which a collimated X-ray flux impinges on a smooth surface under a grazing angle; rendering total reflection is used to excite atoms in the top layers of the material for measurement of the amounts of elements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08766
name: X-ray absorption analysis
def: "Measurement principle of X-ray analysis in which the absorption spectrum is used to measure the number of atoms\, species\, and other parameters of chemical elements."
synonym: "X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08767
name: X-ray absorption edge
def: "Increase in X-ray absorption observed at the energy at which a strongly bound electron is released."
synonym: "absorption edge" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08768
name: X-ray absorption near edge structure
def: "Measurement method of X-ray absorption analysis in which the fine structure of the adsorption spectrum in the range \\\(\\pu\{30 eV\}\\\) below to \\\(\\pu\{50 eV\}\\\) above the absorption edge is used to measure parameters describing the chemical state\, coordination environment\, and local geometry distortion for the X-ray absorbing atom."
synonym: "X-ray absorption near edge fine structure" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08769
name: X-ray analysis
def: "Measurement principle in which characteristic X-radiation\, produced upon irradiation of a material with elementary particles or photons\, is applied to measure amounts of elements in a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08770
name: X-ray computed micro-tomography
def: "Measurement method based on the transmission of X-rays to obtain three-dimensional images of a sample."
synonym: "X-ray micro-tomography (XMT)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08771
name: X-ray diffraction analysis
def: "Measurement method using diffraction of X-radiation to obtain the spatial arrangement of atoms in a crystalline sample."
synonym: "X-ray diffraction (XRD)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08772
name: X-ray fluorescence microscopy
def: "Measurement method of X-ray fluorescence to obtain quantitative and spatial information about elements in a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08773
name: actuator
def: "Transducer that converts the energy supplied or taken from the surroundings into some mechanical motion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08774
name: antisoliton
def: "Soliton with the negative amplitude or opposite spin with respect to a given soliton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08775
name: charge carrier
def: "Particle or quasiparticle capable of transporting an electric charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08776
name: charge carrier concentration
def: "Number of charge carriers per unit volume."
synonym: "concentration of charge carriers" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08777
name: charge carrier diffusion
def: "Purely random\, thermally induced motion of charge carriers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08778
name: charge carrier generation
def: "Process whereby charge carriers are created."
synonym: "charge generation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08779
name: charge carrier injection
def: "Process whereby charge carriers in excess of the thermodynamic equilibrium level are introduced into a conductor\, a semiconductor or an insulator from another material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08780
name: charge carrier mobility
def: "Drift velocity \(\\\(v_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\)\) of charge carriers divided by the electric field strength \(\\\(E\\\)\). \\\[\\mu = v_\{\\rm\{d\}\}/E \\quad \(\\rm\{unit\}\{\:\}\\ \\pu\{m2 V-1 s-1\}\)\\\]"
synonym: "charge mobility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08781
name: charge carrier recombination
def: "Process whereby an electron in the conduction band \(in LUMO level\) loses its excess energy and re-occupies the energy state of a hole in the valence band \(in HOMO level\)."
synonym: "charge recombination" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08782
name: charge transfer
def: "Transfer of charge carriers between two sites of the same molecular entity \(intramolecular CT\) or between two molecular entities occurring in close proximity \(intermolecular CT\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08783
name: charge-transfer exciton
def: "Exciton formed by a charge-transfer transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08784
name: conductor
def: "Material having the ability to transmit energy or charge carriers or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08785
name: conjugated polymer
def: "A polymer composed of molecules whose backbone is a sequence of alternating single and multiple bonds\:  where \\\(\\ce\{R^\{1\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^\{2\}\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl or heteroaryl and Ar is arenediyl \(formerly\: arylene\) or heteroarenediyl \(formerly\: heteroarylene\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08786
name: π-conjugated system
def: "Molecule or a part of a molecule\, of which structure may be represented as a system of alternating single and multiple bonds\, in which\, eventually\, a multiple bond can be replaced by an atom with a pair of non-bonding electrons or an atom carrying a negative charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08787
name: coulomb radius
def: "Distance between two oppositely charged charge carriers at which the energy of their Coulombic attraction is equal to \\\(kT\\\)\, where \\\(k\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(T\\\) the absolute temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08788
name: dark electric conductivity
def: "Electric conductivity of a material in the dark where the free charge-carrier photogeneration is excluded."
synonym: "dark conductivity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08789
name: delocalization of electrons
def: "Redistribution of electron density in a molecular entity as compared with a model assuming individual atoms in the same valence states connected by localized bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08790
name: dielectric material
def: "Material which polarizes when exposed to an external electric field\, because it creates its own internal electric field oriented against the external field."
synonym: "dielectric" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08791
name: dispersive transport
def: "Irregular charge transport in which charge carriers move with an ill-defined mean velocity owing to the high variety of dwell times spent at different transport sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08792
name: double injection
def: "Simultaneous injection of both types of charge carriers \(electrons and holes\) into a material or a device."
synonym: "double charge carrier injection" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08793
name: drift current
def: "Electric current caused by electric drift."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08794
name: drift velocity
def: "Average velocity that a charge carrier attains due to an applied electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08795
name: electret
def: "Dielectric material with a permanent or quasi-permanent electric dipole."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08796
name: electric conductor
def: "Material that is able to transmit electric current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08797
name: electric drift
def: "Oriented flow of otherwise randomly moving charge carriers induced by an applied external electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08798
name: electrically conducting polymer composite
def: "Composite composed of a non-conducting polymer matrix in which irregular powdered or fibrous particles of an electrically conducting material are evenly dispersed in an amount exceeding the percolation threshold for electric conductivity and so the composite exhibits electric conductivity close to that of the conducting filler."
synonym: "conducting polymer composite" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08799
name: electrochromic polymer
def: "Polymer that exhibits electrochromism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08800
name: electrofluorescence
def: "Electroluminescence originated from singlet-state excitons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08801
name: electroinjection
def: "Process of charge carrier injection induced by an applied external electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08802
name: electromagnetic field
def: "Physical field composed of two interconnected\, mutually orthogonal fields\, an electric field and a magnetic field\, which mediates interactions of charged\, dipolar and multipolar objects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08803
name: electromagnetic field responsive polymer
def: "Polymer showing a noticeable response of its physical or chemical properties during or upon exposure to an electromagnetic field or one of its components\: electric field and magnetic field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08804
name: electron-deficient molecule
def: "Molecule that has fewer valence electrons than required for forming normal two-center two-electron chemical bonds and thus contains multicenter chemical bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08805
name: electron-rich molecule
def: "Molecule that has more valence electrons than is needed to link its atoms by single covalent bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08806
name: electron tunneling
def: "Quantum mechanical effect of passing an electron through a potential energy barrier separating two energy equivalent regions."
synonym: "electron quantum tunneling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08807
name: electro-optic effect
def: "Change in the optical properties of a material in response to a change in an external electric field strength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08808
name: electrophosphorescence
def: "Electroluminescence originated from triplet-state excitons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08809
name: electrorheological effect
def: "Reversible change in the apparent viscosity of a liquid system such as suspension of polar or good polarizable microparticles in an oil\, upon exposure of an electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08810
name: energy band
def: "Quasi-continuous range of discrete energy levels that an electron is either allowed \(an occupied band\) or forbidden \(forbidden band or band gap\) to occupy in a molecule or solid."
synonym: "electronic band" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08811
name: energy band theory
def: "Theory describing the electronic structure of a solid in terms of energy intervals\, called energy bands that are allowed or forbidden for occupation by electrons."
synonym: "electronic band theory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08812
name: energy gap
def: "Energy interval in a lattice where no electronic state exists."
synonym: "bandgap" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08813
name: exciton annihilation
def: "Interaction of two excitons resulting in their decay and radiative emission of the stored excitation energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08814
name: exciton diffusion
def: "Random movement of an exciton from one molecular entity to another or from one part of a molecular entity or a lattice to another part of the same kind."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08815
name: exciton dissociation
def: "Fission of an exciton into independent\, Coulombically unbound mobile charge carriers\: an electron and a hole\, or a negative and a positive polaron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08816
name: exciton mean free path
def: "Mean distance that an exciton travels from the site of its creation to the site where it radiatively or non-radiatively decays or dissociates into charge carriers."
synonym: "exciton mean pathway" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08817
name: exciton–phonon coupling
def: "Interaction in which a part of the exciton energy is converted into the lattice vibrational energy and vice versa."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08818
name: extent of electron delocalization
def: "Magnitude of the region in a molecule over which a molecular orbital occupied with delocalized electrons is extended."
synonym: "effective conjugation length" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08819
name: external quantum yield
def: "Quantum yield relating to the whole device or the overall process involved."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08820
name: extrinsic semiconductor
def: "Semiconductor whose electric properties are largely determined by the dopant 'impurities' present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08821
name: field-effect transistor
def: "Transistor in which the flow of charge carriers from source to drain is controlled by the potential applied to the gate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08822
name: fill factor
def: "Ratio of the real maximum output power\, \\\(P_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\)\, that can be obtained with an actual solar cell to the product of the open-circuit potential\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{oc\}\}\\\)\, and short-circuit photocurrent\, \\\(I_\{\\rm\{sc\}\}\\\)\: \\\[f_\{\\rm\{FF\}\} = P_\{\\rm\{max\}\}/V_\{\\rm\{oc\}\}I_\{\\rm\{sc\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:08823
name: free charge-carrier photogeneration
def: "Generation of free charge carriers by photons."
synonym: "charge-carrier photogeneration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08824
name: head-tail isomerism
def: "Constitutional isomerism of polymeric \(oligomeric\) chains composed of identical non symmetric repeating units consisting in different orientations of the units."
synonym: "HT-isomerism" []
synonym: "regioisomerism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08825
name: heterojunction
def: "Interface between dissimilar crystalline or amorphous solid phases that have unequal bandgap levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08826
name: hole
def: "Quasiparticle\, a conceptual charge carrier opposite to electron\, defined as the absence of electron at a position where it should be present if a lattice is in its ground state \(valence band normally filled\)."
synonym: "electron hole" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08827
name: hole-transporting material
def: "Material\, typically a semiconductor\, in which holes are predominant mobile charge carriers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08828
name: homojunction
def: "Interface occurring between two similar semiconductors with equal bandgaps."
synonym: "pn-junction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08829
name: hopping transport
def: "Incoherent transport of charge carriers by hopping between adjacent energetically nearly equivalent localized sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08830
name: hot electron
def: "Electron that is out of the thermal equilibrium with its environment and has enough kinetic energy to overcome potential barriers to travel between different regions of a molecule or lattice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08831
name: impact ionization
def: "Charge carrier generation by another charge carrier possessing a high kinetic energy\, which knocks an electron out of the HOMO into the LUMO or still higher state so creating a new electron-hole pair."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08832
name: internal quantum yield
def: "Quantum yield relating to a single component or a part of a device or a partial step of an overall process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08833
name: intrinsic semiconductor
def: "Semiconductor in which mobile charge carriers are generated solely by thermal excitation of electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08834
name: ion-conducting polymer
def: "Conducting polymer in which ionic species are mobile charge carriers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08835
name: lattice
def: "Periodic grid-like ideal structure consisting of regularly arranged elementary repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08836
name: light emitting diode
def: "Transducer-type device that converts an electric current into luminescence light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08837
name: lowest unoccupied molecular orbital
def: "Lowest-energy molecular orbital that is unoccupied in the ground state but occupied by one or more electrons if the molecular entity occurs in its first electronically excited state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08838
name: luminescent material
def: "Material \(polymer\) that exhibits a luminescence."
synonym: "luminescent polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08839
name: magnetoresistance
def: "Change in the electric resistance of a material in response to an external magnetic field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08840
name: memristor
def: "Electric component that can be reversibly switched between stable low- and high-resistive states."
synonym: "memristive system" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08841
name: molecular orbital theory
def: "Theory of quantum mechanics in which electrons in a molecule \(lattice\) are not assigned to particular bonds between atoms but move under the influence of all atoms."
synonym: "MO theory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08842
name: molecular switch
def: "Molecule that can be reversibly switched between two or more stable states in response to a change in\, e.g.\, its microenvironment \(pH\, the presence of a ligand\, temperature etc.\)\, illumination or electric current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08843
name: occupied band
def: "Energy band occupied by electrons\, typically the valence band."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08844
name: ohmic contact
def: "Electric junction which exhibits linear and symmetric current-potential dependence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08845
name: optically nonlinear polymer
def: "Polymer that exhibits a nonlinear optical effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08846
name: optoelectronics
def: "Branch of physics that deals with the optical-to-electric and electric-to-optical transducers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08847
name: percolation
def: "Formation of the long-range \(infinite\) functional connectivity in a disperse system composed of small active particles evenly dispersed in an inactive matrix\, which makes the system penetrable for a fluid or current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08848
name: percolation threshold
def: "Minimal volume fraction of functionally active small particles evenly dispersed in a non-active matrix\, at which the long-range \(infinite\) functional connectivity in the system is achieved."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08849
name: photochromic polymer
def: "Polymer exhibiting photochromism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08850
name: photoconducting polymer
def: "Polymer exhibiting electrical conductivity or a clear increase in electrical conductivity under irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08851
name: photodetrapping
def: "Removal of charge carriers from traps in the bandgap by irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08852
name: photoinduced discharge
def: "Discharging of an electrically charged material by irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08853
name: photoinjection
def: "Charge carrier injection by a process induced by irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08854
name: photorefractive polymer
def: "Optically nonlinear polymer that changes refractive index when exposed to light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08855
name: photovoltaic effect
def: "Phenomenon consisting in converting light to electricity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08856
name: polarization density
def: "Change in electric dipole moment per unit volume of dielectric material in response to its exposure to an electric field."
synonym: "dielectric polarization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08857
name: poling
def: "Orientation of dipoles or dipolar domains inside a material by using a sufficiently strong electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08858
name: Poole–Frenkel effect
def: "Increase in the electric conductivity of a semiconductor or insulator by exposing it to an electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08859
name: protonic conductivity
def: "Ionic conductivity mediated by protons \(more exactly by hydrogen cations properly called hydrons\)."
synonym: "hydronic conductivity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08860
name: proton doping
def: "Transformation of a semiconducting or insulating material to conducting state by protonation \(hydronation\) of its molecules."
synonym: "hydron doping" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08861
name: quantum confinement effect
def: "Effect occurring if at least one dimension of a structure is comparable to the de Broglie wavelength of the electron-hole pair and the motion of charge carriers is thus quantized by the structure boundary."
synonym: "quantum size effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08862
name: quantum tunneling polymer composite
def: "Electrically conducting polymer composite that\, however\, is conducting only when pressed due to thinning the insulating polymer matrix membranes so much that they allow electron tunneling through them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08863
name: quasiparticle
def: "Discrete disorder in a multi-particulate system that exhibits particle-specific behavior and properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08864
name: radiative charge carrier recombination
def: "Charge carrier recombination in which the energy released is emitted as a photon of corresponding energy."
synonym: "radiative recombination" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08865
name: redox doping
def: "Doping of a parent polymer consisting in oxidation or reduction of its molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08866
name: regioirregular polymer
def: "Regular polymer or an irregular polymer whose macromolecules contain non-uniformly oriented simple asymmetric repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08867
name: regiorandom polymer
def: "Regioirregular polymer with randomly oriented asymmetric constitutional repeating units in its chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08868
name: regioregular polymer
def: "Regular polymer whose chains are composed of uniformly oriented asymmetric constitutional repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08869
name: reticulate doped polymer
def: "Surface-conducting polymer composite obtained by crystallization of a low-molar-mass organic conductor on an insulating or semiconducting polymer support."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08870
name: Schottky barrier
def: "Potential energy barrier at a semiconductor-metal junction with the current rectifying characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08871
name: second harmonic generation
def: "Transformation of the input monochromatic radiation beam into a double frequency \(half wavelength\) beam."
synonym: "frequency doubling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08872
name: sensor
def: "Transducer-type device that responds to a specific property of its surroundings or to a coming stimulus by a visually readable change in its state or by generation of a signal that can be read by an observer or instrument."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08873
name: short circuit photocurrent
def: "Photocurrent flowing through a short circuited solar cell \(if the electric potential across the cell is zero\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08874
name: size effect
def: "Effect of size on properties of the material or device which incorporates it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08875
name: solar cell
def: "Device that directly transduces electromagnetic radiation into electricity based on the photovoltaic effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08876
name: stimulated radiative charge carrier recombination
def: "Radiative charge carrier recombination induced by incident photons of appropriate wavelength\, which synchronize emission of other photons as to their wavelength\, phase\, polarization and direction of travel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08877
name: thermal injection
def: "Charge carrier injection by a thermally activated processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08878
name: valence bond theory
def: "Quantum mechanics theory of chemical bonding that treats electrons as being assigned to individual bonds between particular atoms."
synonym: "VB theory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08879
name: work function
def: "Minimum thermodynamic work \(energy\) needed to remove an electron from a solid to a point in the vacuum nearby the solid surface.\\\[\\mathit\{\\Phi\} = -e\{\\mathit\{\\Psi\}\} - \\mu\\\]where \\\(e\\\) is the elementary charge \(\\\(-e\\\) is charge of electron\)\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Psi\}\\\) is the electric potential nearby the surface and \\\(\\mu\\\) is the energy of Fermi level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08880
name: fluoropolymers
def: "Polymers whose macromolecules are rich in fluorocarbon groups \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08881
name: metallo-supramolecular polymer
def: "Dynamic polymeric entity whose macromolecules are composed of alternating simple or oligomeric molecules with two or more chelate end-groups \(so-called unimers\) and metal ions \(so-called ion couplers\) that are linked to chains by reversible coordination bonds \(structure-based polymer class name\)\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:08882
name: polyacetylenes
def: "Polymers prepared from acetylene \(ethyne\)\, a substituted acetylene\, or both in admixture by a polymerization involving one or more triple bonds \(source-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08883
name: polyanilines
def: "Polymers prepared exclusively from aniline\, or substituted aniline\, or both in admixture \(source-based class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08884
name: polyarylenes
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively arenediyl \(formerly arylene\) or heteroarenediyl \(formerly heteroarylene\) units in the backbone\:  where Ar is a divalent arenediyl or heteroarenediyl group \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08885
name: polyarylenethynylenes
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating arenediyl or heterarenediyl \(formerly arylene or heteroarylene\, respectively\) and ethynediyl \(formerly ethynylene\) constitutional repeating units in the backbone\:  where Ar is a divalent arenediyl or heterarenediyl group \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08886
name: polyarylenevinylenes
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating arenediyl or heteroarenediyl \(formerly arylene or heteroarylene\, respectively\) and ethene-1\,2-diyl \(formerly vinylene\) or substituted vinylene constitutional repeating units in the backbone\:  where Ar is a divalent arenediyl or heteroarenediyl group and \\\(\\ce\{R^\{1\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^\{2\}\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, or an alkyl\, aryl or heteroaryl group \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08887
name: polyazomethines
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing azomethine \(azanylylidenemethanylylidene\) linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-N=CR-\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is hydrogen\, or an alkyl or aryl group \(structure-based polymer class name\)."
synonym: "poly (Schiff bases)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08888
name: polybenzimidazoles
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing benzimidazole groups in the backbone\, such as groups \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08889
name: polydiacetylenes
def: "Polymer prepared from buta-1\,3-diyne \(diacetylene\) or a substituted buta-1\,3-diyne or both in admixture \(source-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08890
name: poly\(ethene-1\,2-diyl\)
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules exclusively consisting of ethene-1\,2-diyl \(formerly called vinylene\) constitutional repeating units."
synonym: "poly(acetylene) (source-based name)" []
synonym: "poly(vinylene) (former structure-based name)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08891
name: poly\[3\,4-\(ethylenedioxy\)thiophene\]
def: "Polymer prepared from 3\,4-\(ethylenedioxy\)thiophene \(source-based polymer name\)."
synonym: "poly{3,4-[ethylene-1,2-bis(oxy)]thiophene-2,5-diyl}" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08892
name: polyfluorenes
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing fluorenediyl or substituted fluorenediyl \(mainly 2\,7-diyl\) constitutional repeating units in the backbone \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08893
name: polyimides
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing cyclic dicarboximide groupings in the backbone\:  where \\\(\\ce\{R^\{1\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{2\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{3\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^\{4\}\}\\\) are groups with connectivity one\, two\, three\, and four\, respectively \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08894
name: polyoxadiazoles
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively oxadiazole rings in the backbone \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08895
name: polyphenylenes
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively \\\(\\upsigma\\\)-bonded benzene or substituted benzene rings in the backbone\:  where \\\(\\ce\{R^\{1\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{2\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{3\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^\{4\}\}\\\) are each hydrogen or an univalent group \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08896
name: polyphenylenethynylenes
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating phenylene or substituted phenylene and ethynediyl \(formerly ethynylene\) constitutional repeating units in the backbone\:  where \\\(\\ce\{R^\{1\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{2\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{3\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^\{4\}\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, halogen\, trialkylsilyl etc. \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08897
name: polyphenylenevinylenes
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating phenylene or substituted phenylene and ethene-1\,2-diyl \(formerly vinylene\) or substituted ethene-1\,2-diyl constitutional repeating units in the backbone\:  where \\\(\\ce\{R^\{1\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{2\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{3\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{4\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^\{5\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^\{6\}\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, heteroaryl\, halogen\, trialkylsilyl\, etc. \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08898
name: polypyrroles
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules with backbones composed of pyrrole rings \(structure-based polymer class name definition\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08899
name: polysilanes
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively silicon atoms in the backbone\:  where \\\(\\ce\{R^\{1\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^\{2\}\}\\\) are each hydrogen or an alkyl\, aryl or heteroaryl group \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08900
name: polytetrazines
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing tetrazine or hydrogenated tetrazine rings in the backbone \(structure-based polymer class name\)\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:08901
name: polythiadiazoles
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing thiadiazole rings in the backbone \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08902
name: polythiazoles
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing thiazole rings in the backbone\:  where R is hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, heteroaryl\, substituted alkyl\, substituted aryl or substituted heteroaryl \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08903
name: polythiophenes
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively thiophene rings in the backbone \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08904
name: polyvinylcarbazoles
def: "Polymers prepared from a vinylcarbazole or a substituted vinylcarbazole or both in admixture \(source-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08905
name: polyvinylenes
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing exclusively ethene-1\,2-diyl \(formerly vinylene\) or substituted ethene-1\,2-diyl constitutional repeating units in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-CR^\{1\}=CR^\{2\}-\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R^\{1\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^\{2\}\}\\\) are each hydrogen or an alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl group \(structure-based polymer class name\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08906
name: polyzwitterions
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules consisting of zwitterionic repeating units \(structure-based polymer class name\)."
synonym: "poly(inner salts)" []
synonym: "polyamphions" []
synonym: "zwitterionic polymers" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08907
name: activated-monomer polymerization
def: "Chain polymerization in which propagation involves reaction between a growing-chain end and an activated monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08908
name: controlled polymerization
def: "Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of a polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08909
name: degenerative-transfer polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the deactivation of the active species involves degenerative chain transfer."
synonym: "degenerate-transfer polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08910
name: group-transfer polymerization
def: "Chain polymerization in which a specific atom or group is intramolecularly transferred to remain associated with the active chain-end during the course of polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08911
name: insertion polymerization
def: "Chain polymerization in which monomer units are sequentially inserted into a specific site within a polymer structure with retention of chain-end functionality."
synonym: "polyinsertion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08912
name: reversible-deactivation polymerization
def: "Chain polymerization\, propagated by chain carriers that are deactivated reversibly\, bringing them into active-dormant equilibria of which there might be more than one."
synonym: "controlled reversible-deactivation polymerization." []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08913
name: controlled radical polymerization
def: "Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of a radical polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed by radical polymerization\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08914
name: living radical polymerization
def: "Radical polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08915
name: radical ring-opening polymerization
def: "Radical polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08916
name: reversible-deactivation radical polymerization
def: "Chain polymerization\, propagated by radicals that are deactivated reversibly\, bringing them into active-dormant equilibria of which there might be more than one."
synonym: "controlled reversible-deactivation radical polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08917
name: aminoxyl-mediated radical polymerization
def: "Stable-radical-mediated polymerization in which the deactivation of the radicals involves reversible coupling with aminoxyl radicals."
synonym: "aminoxyl-mediated polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08918
name: cobalt-mediated radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by a cobalt complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08919
name: iniferter process
def: "Stable-radical-mediated polymerization where the initiator and dormant species are iniferters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08920
name: organometallic-mediated radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an organometallic reagent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08921
name: stable-radical-mediated polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization in which the deactivation involves reversible coupling with stable \(persistent\) radicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08922
name: reversible-addition-fragmentation radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization in which the dormant species is a stable \(persistent\) radical and the deactivation step involves reversible fragmentation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08923
name: carbonothioyl-mediated radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-addition-fragmentation radical polymerization mediated by a carbonothioyl compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08924
name: atom-transfer radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization in which the deactivation of the radicals involves reversible atom-transfer or reversible group transfer to usually\, though not exclusively\, transition-metal complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08925
name: inner-sphere electron transfer atom-transfer radical polymerization
def: "Atom-transfer radical polymerization where the activation step involves direct atom or group transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08926
name: non-metal-mediated atom-transfer radical polymerization
def: "Atom-transfer radical polymerization in which the deactivation of the radicals involves reversible atom transfer or reversible group transfer to a non-metallic reagent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08927
name: outer-sphere electron transfer ATRP
def: "Atom-transfer radical polymerization where the activation step involves consecutive single electron transfer and atom or group transfer."
synonym: "OSET-ATRP" []
synonym: "single-electron-transfer RDRP (SET-RDRP)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08928
name: transition-metal-mediated radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by transition-metal complexes or organometallic compounds."
synonym: "transition-metal-mediated reversible-deactivation radical polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08929
name: degenerative chain-transfer radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization in which the deactivation of the radicals involves degenerative transfer \(DT\) of a group \(or atom\)."
synonym: "degenerate chain-transfer radical polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08930
name: iodine-transfer polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an iodo-compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08931
name: organobismuthane-mediated radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an organobismuthane compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08932
name: organostibane-mediated radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an organostibane compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08933
name: organotellurium-mediated radical polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation radical polymerization mediated by an organotellurium compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08934
name: reverse iodine-transfer polymerization
def: "Iodine-transfer polymerization where the initial polymerization mixture comprises a conventional initiator \(e.g.\, an azo-compound or a peroxide\) and molecular iodine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08935
name: reversible-addition-fragmentation chain-transfer polymerization
def: "Degenerate-transfer radical polymerization in which chain activation and chain deactivation involve a degenerative chain-transfer process\, which occurs by a two-step addition-fragmentation mechanism."
synonym: "RAFT polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08936
name: activated monomer
def: "Reactive species generated reversibly from a monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08937
name: addition-fragmentation chain transfer
def: "Chain-transfer process that proceeds by a propagating species adding to a chain-transfer agent to form an intermediate that then fragments to provide an initiating species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08938
name: catalyst precursor
def: "Coordination compound that by reaction with an activator or cocatalyst provides an active catalyst during initialization of coordination polymerization."
synonym: "initiator (in coordination polymerization)" []
synonym: "precatalyst (in coordination polymerization)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08939
name: catalytic chain transfer
def: "Chain-transfer process involving transfer of an atom or group to a reagent \(the catalyst\) that in turn transfers that group to a monomer to reinitiate polymerization and regenerate the original reagent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08940
name: catalytic chain termination
def: "Chain termination process involving transfer of a group to a reagent \(the catalyst\) that in turn transfers that group to a propagating species to terminate a chain and regenerate the original reagent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08941
name: chain activation
def: "Unimolecular dissociation of a dormant species to form a mediating agent and a propagating species."
synonym: "activation (in SRMP)" []
synonym: "unimolecular activation" []
synonym: "unimolecular chain activation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08942
name: chain activation
def: "Reaction of an activator with a dormant species to form a deactivator and a propagating species."
synonym: "activation (in ATRP)" []
synonym: "bimolecular activation" []
synonym: "bimolecular chain activation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08943
name: chain deactivation
def: "Conversion of a chain carrier into an inactive species."
synonym: "deactivation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08944
name: chain propagation
def: "Chemical reaction between a chain carrier and a monomer that results in the growth of a polymer chain and the regeneration of at least one chain carrier."
synonym: "propagation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08945
name: chain reactivation
def: "Conversion of an inactive chain into a chain carrier."
synonym: "activation (in a chain polymerization)" []
synonym: "reactivation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08946
name: chain termination
def: "Chemical reaction in which a chain carrier is converted irreversibly into a non-propagating species without the formation of a new chain carrier."
synonym: "irreversible chain deactivation" []
synonym: "termination" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08947
name: chain-transfer agent
def: "Substance able to react with a chain carrier by a reaction in which the original chain carrier is deactivated and a new chain carrier is generated."
synonym: "transfer agent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08948
name: combination
def: "Chain termination\, in a chain polymerization\, between two propagating macromolecules that gives rise to a single macromolecule of molar mass equal to the sum of the molar masses of the two macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08949
name: deactivator
def: "Reagent that reacts reversibly with the initiating species or propagating species to form an initiator or a dormant chain\, respectively\, and an activator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08950
name: dead polymer chain
def: "Polymer chain which plays no active role in the polymerization."
synonym: "dead macromolecule (in RDP)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08951
name: degenerative chain transfer
def: "Chain-transfer reaction that generates a new chain carrier and a new chain-transfer agent with the same reactivity as the original chain carrier and chain-transfer agent."
synonym: "degenerate chain transfer" []
synonym: "degenerate transfer" []
synonym: "degenerative transfer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08952
name: dormant species
def: "Temporarily deactivated chain carrier."
synonym: "dormant macromolecule (in RDP)" []
synonym: "dormant polymer chain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08953
name: inactive chain
def: "Dead chains or dormant chains."
synonym: "inactive macromolecule (in RDP)" []
synonym: "non-propagating species" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08954
name: inifer
def: "Substance that combines the properties of an initiator and a reversible chain-transfer agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08955
name: iniferter
def: "Substance that combines the properties of an initiator\, a reversible chain-transfer agent and a chain terminator or reversible deactivator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08956
name: initialization
def: "Process for converting a starting coordination complex or precatalyst into an active catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08957
name: initializer
def: "Starting initiator or transfer agent in reversible-deactivation polymerization \(RDP\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08958
name: initialization efficiency
def: "Efficiency of conversion of a starting initiator or transfer agent into a macro-initiator or macro-chain-transfer agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08959
name: initiating species
def: "Species to which monomer adds or becomes inserted to start a chain polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08960
name: initiator efficiency
def: "Number of growing chains initiated divided by the number of active-centers generated from initiator molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08961
name: living polymer chain
def: "Polymer chains that possess the potential for chain growth under the polymerization conditions."
synonym: "living macromolecule (in RDP)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08962
name: macro-chain-transfer agent
def: "Macromolecule containing chain-transfer-agent functionality."
synonym: "macroCTA" []
synonym: "macroRAFT agent (in RAFT polymerization)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08963
name: macroinitiator
def: "Macromolecule containing initiator functionality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08964
name: mediating agent
def: "Species that mediates polymerization in reversible-deactivation polymerization."
synonym: "mediator" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08965
name: primary species
def: "Species that is formed from an initiator or monomer molecule and that is capable of initiating polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08966
name: propagating species
def: "Active species formed by addition to monomer that is capable of adding further monomer."
synonym: "active macromolecule (in RDP)" []
synonym: "active polymer chain" []
synonym: "growing chain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08967
name: retardation
def: "Diminished rate of polymerization brought about by the action of a retarder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08968
name: reversible-addition-fragmentation chain-transfer agent
def: "Chain-transfer agent that provides chain transfer by a reversible-addition-fragmentation mechanism."
synonym: "RAFT agent" []
synonym: "chain-transfer agent (CTA) (in RAFT polymerization)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08969
name: secondary species
def: "Species formed by the rearrangement or fragmentation of a primary species that is capable of initiating a polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08970
name: stable radical
def: "Mediating agent in stable-radical-mediated radical polymerization with sufficient kinetic stability such that it is essentially unreactive towards addition to monomer under the conditions of the experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08971
name: unimer
def: "Species comprising only one monomer unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08972
name: substitution chain transfer
def: "Chain-transfer process that proceeds by a one-step substitution reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08973
name: controlled ionic polymerization
def: "Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of an ionic polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed by ionic polymerization\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08974
name: ionic ring-opening polymerization
def: "Ionic polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08975
name: living ionic polymerization
def: "Ionic polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08976
name: reversible-deactivation ionic polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08977
name: anionic group-transfer polymerization
def: "Anionic polymerization in which a specific atom or group is intramolecularly transferred to remain associated with the active chain-end during the course of polymerization."
synonym: "group-transfer polymerization (GTP)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08978
name: anionic ring-opening polymerization
def: "Anionic polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08979
name: controlled anionic polymerization
def: "Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of an anionic polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed by anionic polymerization\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08980
name: living anionic polymerization
def: "Anionic polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08981
name: reversible-deactivation anionic polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are anions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08982
name: nucleophilic chain polymerization
def: "Chain polymerization in which the active centres are nucleophilic species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08983
name: ring-opening nucleophilic chain polymerization
def: "Nucleophilic chain polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08984
name: cationic ring-opening polymerization
def: "Cationic polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08985
name: controlled cationic polymerization
def: "Term indicating control of a certain kinetic feature of a cationic polymerization or structural aspect of the polymer molecules formed by cationic polymerization\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08986
name: electrophilic chain polymerization
def: "Chain polymerization in which the active centres are electrophilic species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08987
name: living cationic polymerization
def: "Cationic polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08988
name: reversible-deactivation cationic polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the chain carriers are cations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08989
name: anion-activated-monomer polymerization
def: "Activated-monomer polymerization where the propagating species is an electrophile and activating species is anionic."
synonym: "anionic activated-monomer polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08990
name: cation-activated-monomer polymerization
def: "Activated-monomer polymerization where the propagating species is a nucleophile and the activating species is cationic."
synonym: "cationic activated-monomer polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08991
name: living ring-opening metathesis polymerization
def: "Ring-opening metathesis polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08992
name: metathesis polymerization
def: "Polymerization in which unsaturated monomers are transformed into unsaturated polymers by a process of bond redistribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08993
name: ring-opening metathesis polymerization
def: "Metathesis polymerization in which an unsaturated cyclic monomer is converted into an unsaturated monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08994
name: electron-transfer ring-opening metathesis polymerization
def: "Ring-opening metathesis polymerization \(ROMP\) proceeding through one-electron redox processes in the absence of a metal carbene initiator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08995
name: coordination polymerization
def: "Chain polymerization that involves the preliminary coordination of a monomer molecule with a chain carrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08996
name: coordination-addition polymerization
def: "Coordination polymerization of \\\(\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\,\\!\\upbeta\\mbox\{-\}unsaturated\}\\\) amides or esters that proceeds by sequential monomer coordination and Michael addition events."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08997
name: coordination-insertion polymerization
def: "Coordination polymerization of olefins that proceeds by insertion of the olefin into a metal–carbon bond"
synonym: "insertion polymerization" []
synonym: "polyinsertion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:08998
name: living coordination polymerization
def: "Coordination polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:08999
name: reversible-deactivation coordination polymerization
def: "Reversible-deactivation polymerization in which the chain carriers are coordination complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09000
name: coordination ring-opening polymerization
def: "Coordination polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit that is either acyclic or contains fewer rings than the cyclic monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09001
name: chain-shuttling polymerization
def: "Coordinative chain-transfer polymerization that involves two different catalysts showing different specificity\, where chain propagation occurs sequentially on the two catalysts\, allowing for the formation of segmented or multiblock copolymers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09002
name: chain-walking polymerization
def: "Coordination polymerization in which the chain carrier migrates along the formed polymer chain through a series of \\\(\\ce\{\\upbeta\\mbox\{-\}hydrogen\}\\\) eliminations and reinsertions with inversion of stereochemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09003
name: coordinative chain-transfer polymerization
def: "Coordination polymerization in which a propagating chain is able to transfer reversibly between the catalyst and a chain-transfer agent\, thereby converting the active species into a dormant species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09004
name: heterogeneous catalysis coordination polymerization
def: "Coordination polymerization making use of a heterogeneous catalyst."
synonym: "heterogeneous coordination polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09005
name: homogeneous catalysis coordination polymerization
def: "Coordination polymerization making use of a homogeneous catalyst."
synonym: "homogeneous coordination polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09006
name: metallocene polymerization
def: "Coordination polymerization making use of a metallocene catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09007
name: pseudo-ionic polymerization
def: "Polymerization proceeding by insertion of a monomer into a polar bond without the generation of ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09008
name: rare-earth-metal-mediated coordination-addition polymerization
def: "Coordination-addition polymerization with propagation occurring within the coordination sphere of a rare-earth metal complex."
synonym: "lanthanide-mediated group-transfer polymerization" []
synonym: "rare-earth-metal-mediated coordination polymerization" []
synonym: "rare-earth-metal-mediated group-transfer polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09010
name: catalyst-transfer polymerization
def: "Catalysed condensative chain polymerization where the catalyst is transferred from reactant polymer to product polymer with high efficiency."
synonym: "catalyst-transfer chain polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09011
name: catalyst-transfer cross-coupling polymerization
def: "Catalyst-transfer polymerization where the propagation steps comprise cross-coupling reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09012
name: living condensative chain polymerization
def: "Condensative chain polymerization in which chain termination and irreversible chain transfer are absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09013
name: brush polymer
def: "Polymer composed of brush macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09014
name: locant
def: "Numeral or letter or a combination of both that identifies position\(s\) in a structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09015
name: senior constitutional repeating unit
def: "More \(most\) senior CRU of a structure of a macromolecule or oligomer molecule containing two or more different types of CRUs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09016
name: preferred constitutional repeating unit
def: "Constitutional repeating unit \(CRU\) written with the subunits in a linear series from the highest \(higher\) seniority to the lowest \(lower\) or as close as possible by the shortest path."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09017
name: subunit
def: "Largest main-chain \(backbone\) segment of the constitutional repeating unit \(CRU\) that can be named a single unit under organic nomenclature rules. This may be a ring or a ring system\, a heteroatom or a heteroatom chain\, or an acyclic carbon chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09018
name: dimensionless Henry’s law solubility constant
def: "Amount concentration \\\(c_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) of a species in the liquid phase divided by the amount concentration \\\(c_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) of that species in the gas phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{cc\}\: = \\underset\{c_\{\\rm\{l\}\} \\to 0\}\{\\rm\{lim\}\}\\frac\{c_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{c_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "dimensionless Henry solubility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09019
name: dimensionless Henry’s law volatility constant
def: "Amount concentration \\\(c_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) of a species in the gas phase divided by the amount concentration \\\(c_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) of that species in the liquid phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{cc\}\: = \\underset\{c_\{\\rm\{l\}\} \\to 0\}\{\\rm\{lim\}\}\\frac\{c_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\}\{c_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "dimensionless Henry volatility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09020
name: Henry’s law constant
def: "Proportionality factor describing the equilibrium distribution of a chemical species between the gas phase and a liquid solution at infinite dilution of the solute according to Henry’s law. It is an umbrella term for the reciprocal quantities Henry’s law solubility constant and Henry’s law volatility constant. Because of its ambiguity\, it is strongly recommended to specify whether a solubility or volatility constant is intended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09021
name: Henry’s law solubility constant
def: "Umbrella term for all variants of Henry’s law constants that are defined in such a way that the numerical value increases with increasing solubility of the solute. Variants include \\\(H_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{cp\}\\\)\, \\\(H_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{xp\}\\\)\, \\\(H_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{bp\}\\\)\, and \\\(H_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{cc\}\\\)\, as discussed elsewhere in this section."
synonym: "Henry solubility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09022
name: Henry’s law volatility constant
def: "Umbrella term for all variants of Henry’s law constants that are defined in such a way that the numerical value increases with increasing volatility of the solute. Variants include \\\(H_\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{pc\}\\\)\, \\\(H_\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{px\}\\\)\, \\\(H_\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{pw\}\\\)\, and \\\(H_\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{cc\}\\\)\, as discussed elsewhere in this section."
synonym: "Henry volatility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09023
name: Henry’s law volatility constant \(via p and c\)
def: "Partial pressure \\\(p\\\) of a species in the gas phase divided by the amount concentration \\\(c_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) of that species in the liquid phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{pc\}\: = \\underset\{c_\{\\rm\{l\}\} \\to 0\}\{\\rm\{lim\}\}\\frac\{p\}\{c_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09024
name: Henry’s law solubility constant \(via c and p\)
def: "Amount concentration \\\(c_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) of a species in the liquid phase divided by the partial pressure \\\(p\\\) of that species in the gas phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{cp\}\: = \\underset\{c_\{\\rm\{l\}\} \\to 0\}\{\\rm\{lim\}\}\\frac\{c_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{p\}\\\]"
synonym: "Henry solubility (defined via amount concentration and partial pressure)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09025
name: Henry’s law solubility constant \(via x and p\)
def: "Amount fraction \\\(x\\\) of a species in the liquid phase divided by the partial pressure \\\(p\\\) of that species in the gas phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{xp\}\: = \\underset\{x \\to 0\}\{\\rm\{lim\}\}\\frac\{x\}\{p\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09026
name: Henry’s law solubility constant \(via b and p\)
def: "Molality \\\(b\\\) of a species divided by the partial pressure \\\(p\\\) of that species in the gas phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{bp\}\: = \\underset\{b \\to 0\}\{\\rm\{lim\}\}\\frac\{b\}\{p\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09027
name: Henry’s law volatility constant \(via p and x\)
def: "Partial pressure \\\(p\\\) of a species in the gas phase divided by the amount fraction \\\(x\\\) of that species in the liquid phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{px\}\: = \\underset\{x \\to 0\}\{\\rm\{lim\}\}\\frac\{p\}\{x\}\\\]"
synonym: "Henry volatility (defined via partial pressure and liquid-phase amount fraction)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09028
name: Henry’s law volatility constant \(via p and w\)
def: "Partial pressure \\\(p\\\) of a species in the gas phase divided by the mass fraction \\\(w\\\) of that species in the liquid phase under equilibrium conditions at infinite dilution\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{pw\}\: = \\underset\{w \\to 0\}\{\\rm\{lim\}\}\\frac\{p\}\{w\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09029
name: link
def: "Constitutive part of a conjugation that binds an active species to a substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09030
name: prodrug conjugate
def: "Conjugate in which at least one conjugation includes a bioactive active species to be released by cleavage of the link where its action is desired."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09031
name: spacer arm
def: "Link used in a conjugation to distance the substrate from the active species that otherwise would have its activity affected by steric or other influences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09032
name: materials chemistry
def: "Materials chemistry is the scientific discipline that designs\, synthesizes\, and characterizes materials with particular interest on both their processing and the understanding of useful or potentially useful properties displayed by the materials designed and synthesized for specific applications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09033
name: pnictogen bond
def: "Weak attractive interaction between an electrophilic region on a pnictogen atom in a molecular entity \(wherein the pnictogen is involved in other stronger bonds\) and a nucleophilic region in another\, or the same\, molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09034
name: alkalinity
def: "Measure of the capacity of aqueous media to react with hydrogen ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09035
name: back titration
def: "Titration of remaining reagent after addition of excess reagent to the titrand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09036
name: blank titration
def: "Titration performed with titrand containing no analyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09037
name: cerimetric titration
def: "Redox titration using cerium\(IV\)\, in which an iron\(II\)-1\,10-phenanthroline complex \(ferroin\) color change indicates the end-point."
synonym: "cerimetry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09038
name: composite alkalinity
def: "Measurement by titration to the phenolphthalein end-point \(pH 8.3\) of that portion of alkalinity arbitrarily attributed to all the hydroxyl content and half the carbonate content of a titrand."
synonym: "phenolphthalein end-point alkalinity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09039
name: conventional mass
def: "Mass of a body that is balanced by a standard mass under conventionally chosen conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09040
name: direct titration
def: "Titration in which titrant is added directly to titrand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09041
name: end-point error
def: "Difference in the volume or mass of titrant\, or the corresponding difference in the volume or mass of titrand\, between the end-point value and equivalence-point value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09042
name: equivalence-point
def: "Stage of a titration at which the titrant has completely reacted with the titrand according to the stoichiometry of the reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09043
name: gravimetric analysis
def: "Measurement principle of quantitative chemical analysis based on measurement of mass."
synonym: "gravimetry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09044
name: gravimetric factor
def: "Mass fraction of analyte in a precipitate \(see precipitation\) obtained in a gravimetric analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09045
name: homogeneous precipitation
def: "Precipitation in gravimetric analysis in which the precipitating agent is produced from a homogeneously dissolved precursor to minimise local excesses and supersaturation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09046
name: indicator consumption error
def: "Systematic measurement error from the reaction of a visual indicator with a reactant in a titration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09047
name: potentiometric titration
def: "Redox titration in which the course of the reaction is followed by potentiometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09048
name: precipitation indicator
def: "Visual indicator precipitated\, with concomitant color change\, by reaction with excess titrant at the end-point of a titration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09049
name: redox indicator
def: "Visual indicator that reacts with excess titrant or titrand with a color change at the end-point of a redox titration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09050
name: stock solution
def: "Solution prepared by weighing an appropriate portion of a solid or by measuring out an appropriate volume of a liquid and dissolving it in the weighed mass of solvent or by adding solvent to a given volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09051
name: titrand
def: "Reactant solution being titrated in a titration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09052
name: titrimetric analysis
def: "Methods of quantitative analysis employing titration."
synonym: "titrimetry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09053
name: transition potential
def: "Potential at the end-point of a redox titration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09054
name: universal indicator
def: "Visual indicator composed of a solution of several pH indicators \(main components are thymol blue\, methyl red\, bromothymol blue and phenolphthalein\) that changes color through a range of pH values\, usually from 1 to 14."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09055
name: volumetric titration
def: "Titration in which the titrant is added by volume."
synonym: "volumetry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09056
name: von Weimarn ratio
def: "Difference between the concentration of the supersaturated solution \(see supersaturation\) required for nucleation \(\\\(Q\\\)\) and its solubility \(\\\(S\\\)\)\, divided by the solubility\, \\\(\(Q − S\)/S\\\)."
synonym: "relative supersaturation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09057
name: net faradaic current
def: "Algebraic sum of faradaic currents flowing through an electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09058
name: electrochemical cell
def: "System that consists of at least two electron conductors \(electrodes\) in contact with ionic conductors \(electrolytes\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09059
name: electrochemical constants
def: "In May 2019\, certain fundamental constants that had been measured in SI units have had their numerical values fixed\, with no uncertainty\, thus defining the units. Of importance to electroanalytical chemistry are the values of the Boltzmann constant\, the elementary charge\, and the Avogadro constant. These are given in the table below\, with calculated values of the Faraday constant \(\\\(F = e·N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\) and gas constant \(\\\(R = k\\cdot\\!N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09060
name: electrode
def: "Electron conductor in an electrochemical cell connected to the external circuit.\nHalf-cell consisting of at least one electron conductor and at least one ionic conductor \(electrolyte\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09061
name: electrolyte
def: "Conducting medium in which the flow of electric current is accompanied by the movement of ions.\nSubstance that provides ions on dissolution in a solvent or on melting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09062
name: electrolytic cell
def: "Electrochemical cell in which electrical energy is converted into chemical energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09063
name: electron number of an electrochemical reaction
def: "Number of electrons transferred in a charge transfer reaction between an electrode and a single entity \(ion\, radical-ion\, or molecule\) of an electroactive substance\, whose identity must be specified."
synonym: "charge number" []
synonym: "number of electrons transferred" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09064
name: equilibrium electrode potential
def: "Potential of an electrode when no electric current flows through the cell and all local charge transfer equilibria across phase boundaries that are represented in the cell diagram \(except at possible electrolyte-electrolyte junctions\) and local chemical equilibria are established."
synonym: "Nernst potential" []
synonym: "reversible potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09065
name: formal electrode potential
def: "Equilibrium electrode potential under conditions of unit concentration of species involved in the electrode reaction. \\\[E_\{\\rm\{eq\}\} = E^\{\\circ\\prime\} - \(RT/zF\) \\mathop \\sum \\limits_\{i\} \nu_\{i\} \\ln\(c_\{i\}/c^\{\\circ\)\\\] where \\\(E_\{\\rm\{eq\}\}\\\) is the equilibrium electrode potential\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(F\\\) the Faraday constant\, \\\(z\\\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction\, \\\(\nu_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) the stoichiometric coefficients \(numbers of species\) in the equation of the electrode reaction \(positive for products and negative for reactants\)\, and \\\(c_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) are the amount concentrations of the species involved \(most usually ions\)."
synonym: "formal potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09066
name: galvanic cell
def: "Electrochemical cell in which chemical reactions occur spontaneously and chemical energy is converted into electrical energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09067
name: line representation of electrochemical cells
def: "Notation giving electrodes\, electroactive substances\, and other relevant information for an electrochemical cell.\nThe notation starts and finishes with an electrode. Phase boundaries are denoted with a solid vertical bar \\\(|\\\)\, boundaries between miscible liquids by a vertical dashed bar \\\(¦\\\)\, and a liquid junction by double vertical dashed bars \\\(¦¦\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09068
name: Nernst equation
def: "Fundamental equation in electrochemistry that describes the dependence of the equilibrium electrode potential on the composition of the contacting phases\, written as a reduction\: \\\[E_\{\\rm\{eq\}\} = E^\{\\circ\}\\!-\\!\(RT/zF\)\\mathop \\sum \\limits_i \nu_i \\ln\(a_i\)\\\] where \\\(E_\{\\rm\{eq\}\}\\\) is the equilibrium electrode potential\, \\\(E^\{\\circ\}\\\) the standard electrode potential of the reaction\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(F\\\) the Faraday constant\, \\\(z\\\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction\, and \\\(\nu_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) are the stoichiometric coefficients \(numbers of species\) in the equation of the electrode reaction\, positive for products and negative for reactants\, while \\\(a_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) represents the activities of the species involved \(most usually ions\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09069
name: Cottrell equation
def: "Equation for diffusion current \\\(I_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) at time \\\(t\\\) at a large planar electrode in contact with a semi-infinite layer of unstirred solution containing excess supporting \(inert\) electrolyte and an electroactive substance of bulk concentration \\\(c\\\) when\, at the instant \\\(t = 0\\\)\, the potential of the electrode is suddenly changed to a value at which a mass-transport limited electrochemical reaction occurs and the concentration of the electroactive species at the electrode is zero\, \\\[I_\{\\rm\{d\}\} = zFA\{D^\{1/2\}\}c/\(\\pi^\{1/2\} t^\{1/2\}\)\\\] where \\\(z\\\) is the electron number of an electrochemical reaction in the charge transfer step of the species \(being positive for an oxidation and negative for a reduction\)\, \\\(F\\\) the Faraday constant\, \\\(A\\\) the electrode surface area\, and \\\(D\\\) the diffusion coefficient of the electroactive substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09070
name: current-potential curve
def: "Plot of current versus applied potential \(\\\(I\\!\{-\}\\!E\\\)\) for a given system."
synonym: "I-E curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09071
name: electrochemical biosensor
def: "Electrochemical sensor that has a biological recognition element."
synonym: "bioanalytical sensor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09072
name: electrochemical sensor
def: "Measuring system in which the response to an electrochemical reaction is converted\, via a suitable transducer\, into a measurable electrical signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09073
name: electrode coverage density
def: "Amount of a chemical modifier at an electrode surface per unit area."
synonym: "electrode coverage" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09074
name: electrode surface area
def: "Area of electrode–solution interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09075
name: Faraday’s laws of electrolysis
def: "Faraday’s First Law of electrolysis\: Mass \\\(m\\\) of electrochemically-transformed substance is proportional to the charge \\\(Q\\\) passed\, \\\(m\\! \\propto\\! Q\\\).\nFaraday’s Second Law of electrolysis\: When the same electric charge \(quantity of electricity\) \\\(Q\\\) is passed through several electrolytes\, the mass \\\(m_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) of the substances deposited are proportional to their respective chemical equivalent molar mass\, \\\(M_\{\\rm\{i\}\}/z_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\). \\\[m_\{1\}/m_\{2\} = \(M_\{1\}/z_\{1\}\)/\(M_\{2\}/z_\{2\}\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09076
name: galvanostat
def: "Instrument which controls the electric current between the working electrode and the auxiliary electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09077
name: open circuit potential
def: "Electrode potential of working electrode relative to the reference electrode when no potential or electric current is being applied to the electrochemical cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09078
name: polarizable electrode
def: "Electrode whose potential changes with an applied potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09079
name: potentiostat
def: "Measuring instrument for electric current that controls the potential difference between a working electrode and a reference electrode and measures the electric current between a working electrode and an auxiliary electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09080
name: salt bridge
def: "Means of making electrolytic connection between two half cells without introducing a significant liquid junction potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09081
name: three-electrode cell
def: "Electrochemical cell with a working electrode\, reference electrode\, and auxiliary electrode.Schematic of a three-electrode cell. 1 - working electrode\, 2 - reference electrode\, 3 - auxiliary electrode. The potential between 1 and 2 is set while passing and measuring current between 1 and 3."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09082
name: working potential range
def: "Range of electrode potentials of a given working electrode in a given electrolyte\, where the electric current from reactions of the electrode or electrolyte is negligible compared with the current from reactions of the system under investigation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09083
name: macroelectrode
def: "Electrode with smallest dimension of order millimetre or greater."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09084
name: microelectrode
def: "Electrode with smallest dimension of less than 1 millimetre and greater than 1 micrometre."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09085
name: ultramicroelectrode
def: "Electrode with smallest dimension of less than 25 micrometre."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09086
name: nanoelectrode
def: "Electrode with smallest dimension less than 1 micrometre"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09087
name: quasi-reference electrode
def: "Electrode that maintains a constant\, but not generally well-defined\, potential in an electrochemical cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09088
name: glassy carbon electrode
def: "Electrode made of glassy carbon material with an intertwined graphitic ribbon structure\, formed by pyrolysis of a resol precursor at temperatures up to \\\(\\pu\{3000^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)."
synonym: "vitreous carbon electrode" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09089
name: highly oriented pyrolytic graphite electrode
def: "Graphite electrode of material produced by high temperature deposition of carbon on a ceramic surface and subject to graphitization at \\\(\\pu\{3000^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09090
name: paraffin impregnated graphite electrode
def: "Graphite electrode based on a graphitic material impregnated with paraffin having a high melting point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09091
name: boron-doped diamond electrode
def: "Electrode composed of a boron-doped diamond film on an electron-conducting substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09092
name: biorecognition
def: "Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode \(CME\) in which the electrode is modified by a biological recognition element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09093
name: change of mass
def: "Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode \(CME\) in which the signal originates from accumulation and/or release of an analyte or reagent as well as other electrochemical processes which can result in changes in the mass of the CME."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09094
name: electrocatalysis
def: "Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode \(CME\) in which a charge transfer reaction between a CME and analyte proceeds at a lower overpotential than at the bare electrode. Usually\, the effect of electrocatalysis is also an increase of faradaic current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09095
name: redox mediation
def: "Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode \(CME\) in which an immobilized redox couple can shuttle electrons between the electrode and an analyte\, for instance\, one with difficult access to the electrode surface because of steric reasons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09096
name: carbon paste electrode
def: "Electrode of a composite of carbon powder and a pasting liquid \(including mineral oil\, Nujol\, bromoform\, bromonaphthalene\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09097
name: carbon ink electrode
def: "Development of a carbon paste electrode that is screen printed using a carbon/polymer mixture of suitable composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09098
name: solid amalgam electrode
def: "Electrode made of a solid amalgam of an appropriate metal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09099
name: gold electrode
def: "Foil\, wire or disc electrode made of gold which is easily fabricated into a variety of electrode geometries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09100
name: platinum electrode
def: "Foil\, wire\, disc\, or mesh electrode made of platinum\, which is the most commonly used metallic working electrode in electrochemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09101
name: silver electrode
def: "Electrode in the form of foil\, mesh\, wire\, rod\, tube\, powder\, pellets\, or single crystal of silver."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09102
name: mercury electrode
def: "Liquid metal electrode used in polarography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09103
name: dropping mercury electrode
def: "Mercury electrode formed by sequence of mercury drops falling from a small aperture."
synonym: "DME" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09104
name: bimetallic electrode
def: "Electrode containing two different metals \(e.g. platinum and ruthenium\) on its surface \(e.g. to modify its electrocatalytic properties\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09105
name: chemically-sensitive field effect transistor
def: "Non-faradaic electrode system in which electric field variations in the semiconductor gate region control the magnitude of the source-drain current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09106
name: chemically-modified electrode
def: "Electrode coated with a thin layer of a substance that exhibits particular chemical\, electrochemical\, and/or optical properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09107
name: enzyme-modified electrode
def: "Chemically-modified electrode in which the products of a reaction between enzymes in an immobilized enzyme layer and the enzyme reaction substrate are electrochemically active and are measured usually by amperometry or potentiometry.Schematic of the mediated oxidation of glucose to gluconolactone by glucose oxidase. The current passed to re-oxidise the mediator is proportional to the amount concentration of glucose."
synonym: "enzyme electrode" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09108
name: homogeneous crystalline membrane electrodes
def: "ISE in which the membrane is a crystalline material prepared from either a single compound or a homogeneous mixture of compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09109
name: heterogeneous crystalline membrane electrodes
def: "ISE prepared of an active substance\, or mixture of active substances\, mixed with an inert matrix \(such as silicone rubber or \\\(\\ce\{PVC\}\\\)\)\, or placed on hydrophobized graphite or conducting epoxy resin\, to form a heterogeneous sensing membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09110
name: non-crystalline membrane
def: "Kind of ion-selective membranes used in an ISE which is a support matrix\, containing an ion exchanger \(either cationic or anionic\)\, a plasticizer solvent\, and possibly an uncharged\, selectivity-enhancing species\, form the ion-selective membrane which is usually interposed between two aqueous solutions. The matrix can be either macroporous \(e.g.\, poly\(propylene carbonate\) filter\, glass frit\, etc.\) or microporous \(e.g.\, “thirsty” glass or polymeric material such as \\\(\\ce\{PVC\}\\\)\) yielding with the ion-exchanger and the solvent a “solidified” homogeneous mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09111
name: rigid membranes
def: "Kind of ion-selective membranes used in an ISE comprising synthetic cross-linked polymer with fixed sites or thin glass membrane."
synonym: "matrix membranes" []
synonym: "self-supporting membranes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09112
name: membranes with mobile charged sites
def: "Kind of ion-selective membranes used in an ISE comprising\:\n\nPositively-charged\, hydrophobic cations which\, when dissolved in a suitable organic solvent and held in an inert support \(e.g.\, poly\(propylenecarbonate\) filter or \\\(\\ce\{PVC\}\\\)\)\, provide membranes which are sensitive to changes in the activities of anions.\nNegatively-charged hydrophobic anions which\, when dissolved in a suitable organic solvent and held in an inert support \(e.g.\, poly\(propylene carbonate\) filter or \\\(\\ce\{PVC\}\\\)\)\, provide membranes which are sensitive to changes of cation activities.\nUncharged “carrier” electrodes based on solutions of molecular complexing agents of cations and anions\, which can be used for preparing ion exchanger membranes sensitive and selective to certain cations and anions.\nHydrophobic ion-pair electrodes of plasticized polymers \(e.g.\, \\\(\\ce\{PVC\}\\\)\) containing a dissolved hydrophobic ion-pair respond to the ion activities of those components\, which are relevant to the ion-pairs incorporated in the membrane. The responses can be Nernstian."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09113
name: combined electrode
def: "Ion-selective electrode cell in which the ion-selective electrode and reference electrode are combined in a single sensor assembly\, thereby avoiding the need for a separate external reference electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09114
name: electrode array
def: "Assembly of linked electrodes \(usually microelectrodes\, ultramicroelectrodes or nanoelectrodes\) acting as a single electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09115
name: glass pH-sensitive electrode
def: "pH-sensitive electrode\, usually consisting of a bulb\, or other suitable form\, of special glass attached to a stem of high-resistance glass\, complete with an internal reference electrode and internal filling solution system."
synonym: "glass electrode" []
synonym: "pH electrode" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09116
name: heterogeneous crystalline membrane ion-selective electrode
def: "Ion-selective electrode in which the ion-selective membrane is a crystalline material mixed with an inert matrix \(such as silicone rubber or polyvinyl chloride\)\, or placed on hydrophobized graphite or conducting epoxy resin\, to form a heterogeneous sensing membrane."
synonym: "heterogeneous crystalline membrane electrode" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09117
name: homogeneous crystalline membrane ion-selective electrode
def: "Ion-selective electrode in which the ion-selective membrane is a crystalline material prepared from either a single compound or homogeneous mixture of compounds."
synonym: "homogeneous crystalline membrane electrode" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09118
name: ion-selective field effect transistor
def: "Electrochemical sensor based on the principle of the field effect transistor \(FET\) in which the surface of the gate is modified in a way that makes the response ion-selective."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09119
name: ion-selective membrane
def: "Membrane with controlled permeability to a particular ion covering an electrode\, or separating two electrolyte solutions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09120
name: Nikolsky–Eisenman equation
def: "Equation describing the contribution of interfering ions\, \\\(i\\\)\, to the electrode potential of an ion-selective electrode that is mainly dominated by the primary ion \\\(\\ce\{X\}\\\). \\\[E = \{\\rm\{const\}\} + \\frac\{RT\}\{z_\{\\rm\{X\}\}F\} \\ln \\left\(a_\{\\ce\{X\}\} + \\sum\\limits_i K_\{\{\\ce\{X\}\}\,i\}^\{\\rm\{pot\}\}a_\{i\}^\{\(z_\{\\ce\{X\}\}/z_i\)\} \\right\)\\\] where \\\(E\\\) is the measured electrode potential of an ISE when the only variables are activities in the test solution. \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(z_\{\\rm\{X\}\}\\\) and \\\(z_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) are the numbers of electrons transferred by the analyte ion \\\(\\ce\{X\}\\\) and \\\(i\\\)\, respectively\, \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant\, \\\(a_\{\\rm\{X\}\}\\\) and \\\(a_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) the activity of \\\(\\ce\{X\}\\\) and \\\(i\\\)\, respectively\, and \\\(K_\{\\ce\{X\}\,i\}^\{\\rm\{pot\}\}\\\) is the potentiometric selectivity coefficient for interfering ion \\\(i\\\) with respect to the primary ion \\\(\\ce\{X\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09121
name: pH-sensitive electrode
def: "Potentiometric electrochemical sensor that measures pH as a linear function of electrode potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09122
name: potentiometric enzyme electrode
def: "Enzyme-modified electrode\, or an electrode modified by a substrate\, cofactor or inhibitor\, in which the activity of a product of the enzyme reaction is measured by an ion-selective electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09123
name: potentiometric gas sensor
def: "Ion-selective electrode cell that is responsive to an analyte gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09124
name: rotating disc electrode
def: "A disc electrode that is embedded in the centre of a cylinder which rotates in solution around the longitudinal cylinder axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09125
name: wall-jet electrode
def: "A disc electrode with a thin circular jet of fluid impinging on its centre."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09126
name: chronopotentiometry
def: "Potentiometry in which the potential is measured with time following a change in applied current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09127
name: potentiometry
def: "Electrochemical measurement principle where the potential difference between an indicator electrode and a reference electrode is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09128
name: amperometry
def: "Electrochemical measurement principle based on measurement of current at a controlled applied potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09129
name: chronoamperometry
def: "Amperometry in which the current is measured as a function of time after a change in the applied potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09130
name: AC voltammetry
def: "Voltammetry in which a sinusoidal alternating potential of small amplitude \(\\\(\\pu\{10 to 50 mV\}\\\)\) of constant frequency \(\\\(\\pu\{10 Hz to 100 kHz\}\\\)\) is superimposed on a slowly and linearly varying potential ramp. The resulting alternating current is plotted versus imposed DC potential. The obtained AC voltammogram is peak-shaped.Waveform for AC voltammetry. The applied potential as a function of time is a sinusoidal alternating potential superimposed on a linearly increasing ramp."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09131
name: chronocoulometric constant
def: "Empirically-determined quantity defined by the equation \\\[\{\\cal Q\} = \(1/Ac\)\\Delta Q/\\Delta \(t^\{1/2\}\)\\\] where \\\(A\\\) is the electrode surface area\, \\\(c\\\) is the bulk concentration of the electroactive substance\, \\\(Q\\\) is the charge passed\, and \\\(\\Delta Q/\\Delta\(t^\{1/2\}\)\\\) is the slope of a plot of \\\(Q\\\) against \\\(t^\{1/2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09132
name: chronopotentiometric constant
def: "Empirically-evaluated quantity defined by the equation \\\[\{\\cal T\} = I\\tau^\{1/2\}/\(A c_\{B\}\) = j\\tau^\{1/2\}/c_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\] where \\\(I\\\) is the electric current\, \\\(\\tau\\\) is the transition time\, \\\(A\\\) is the electrode surface area\, \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) is the bulk concentration of the electroactive substance \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)\, and \\\(j=I/A\\\) is the current density."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09133
name: current sampling
def: "Measurement of electric current in voltammetry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09134
name: cyclic voltammetry
def: "Voltammetry in which the electric current is recorded as the electrode potential is varied with time cyclically between two potential limits\, normally at a constant scan rate.Cyclic voltammetry. \(a\) Variation of applied potential with time. \(b\) Cyclic voltammogram of current against applied potential for a reversible redox couple."
synonym: "cyclic linear scan voltammetry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09135
name: DC polarography
def: "Linear scan voltammetry with slow scan rate in which a dropping mercury electrode is used as the working electrode. See figure.Electric current as a function of applied potential for DC polarography\, showing oscillations of current as the dropping mercury electrode grows and is detached."
synonym: "classical polarography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09136
name: differential pulse voltammetry
def: "Voltammetry in which small potential pulses \(constant height \\\(\\pu\{10 to 100 mV\}\\\)\, constant width \\\(\\pu\{10 to 100 ms\}\\\)\) are superimposed onto a linearly varying potential or onto a staircase potential ramp. The current is sampled just before the onset of the pulse \(e.g. \\\(\\pu\{10 to 20 ms\}\\\)\) and for the same sampling time just before the end of the pulse. The difference between the two sampled currents is plotted versus the potential applied before the pulse. Thus\, a differential pulse voltammogram is peak-shaped.\(a\) Potential waveform for differential pulse voltammetry \(not to scale\). Typical values for pulse parameters are $\\tau$=1 s\, $t_\{\\rm\{p\}\}$=50 ms\, $E_\{\\tau\}$=50 mV\, $E_\{\\rm\{p\}\}$=2 mV. Sampling interval indicated by horizontal curly brackets is typically 15 ms. \(b\) Differential pulse voltammogram. $I$ is the difference between currents sampled at ‘2’ and ‘1’."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09137
name: Heyrovský–Ilkovič equation
def: "The relationship that describes the electrode potential as a function of the current of a reversible redox system \(reversible electrode reaction\) in the steady state in voltammetry \\\[E = E^\{\\circ\\prime\} - \\frac\{RT\}\{zF\} \\ln \\left\(\\frac\{D_\{\\rm\{red\}\}\}\{D_\{\\rm\{ox\}\}\}\\right\)^\{s\} \\pm \\frac\{RT\}\{zF\} \\ln\\!\\left\(\\frac\{I_\{\\rm\{d\,lim\}\} - I\}\{I\}\\right\)\\\] where \\\(E^\{\\circ\\prime\}\\\) is the formal electrode potential\, \\\(z\\\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction\, \\\(D_\{\\rm\{red\}\}\\\) and \\\(D_\{\\rm\{ox\}\}\\\) the diffusion coefficients of the reduced and oxidized forms of the electroactive substance\, respectively\, \\\(I_\{\\rm\{d\,lim\}\}\\\) is the limiting diffusion current\, and \\\(I\\\) is the current at the potential being applied. The value of the exponent \\\(s\\\) is ½ for a stationary or dropping mercury electrode\, ⅔ for a hydrodynamic electrode \(see hydrodynamic voltammetry\)\, or 1 for a microelectrode. In the equation\, the last term is added for reduction and subtracted for oxidation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09138
name: hydrodynamic voltammetry
def: "Voltammetry with forced flow of the solution towards the electrode surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09139
name: Ilkovič equation
def: "Equation relating electric current at a dropping mercury electrode under purely diffusional control\, \\\(I_\{\{\\rm\{d\,\}\}t\}\\\)\, the amount concentration of an electroactive substance at the drop surface \\\(c\\\)\, and the concentration in bulk solution \\\(c_\{\\rm\{0\}\}\\\)\, \\\[I_\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\,t\} = -k\\\,z\\\,F\\\,D^\{1/2\}q_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{2/3\} t^\{1/6\}\(c - c_\{0\}\)\\\] where \\\(k\\\) is a numerical constant\, $z$ the electron number of an electrochemical reaction\, \\\(F\\\) the Faraday constant\, \\\(D\\\) the diffusion coefficient of the electroactive substance\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) the mass flux of mercury at the dropping electrode\, and \\\(t\\\) the time elapsed from the beginning of the drop."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09140
name: Levich equation
def: "Equation relating limiting current at a rotating disc electrode \(see hydrodynamic voltammetry\) to the amount concentration of an electroactive substance in bulk solution \\\(c_\{0\}\\\)\, \\\[I_\{\\rm\{lim\}\} = k\\\,z\\\,F\\\,A\\\,D^\{2/3\} \\omega^\{1/2\} \nu^\{−1/6\} c_\{0\}\\\] where \\\(k\\\) is a numerical factor\, \\\(z\\\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction\, \\\(F\\\) the Faraday constant\, \\\(A\\\) the electrode surface area\, \\\(D\\\) the diffusion coefficient of the electroactive substance\, \\\(\\omega\\\) the angular velocity \(rotation rate\)\, and \\\(\nu\\\) the kinematic viscosity of the solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09141
name: linear-scan voltammetry
def: "Voltammetry in which the electric current is recorded as the electrode potential is varied linearly with time. See figure.Linear scan voltammogram of current against applied potential for a reversible redox couple."
synonym: "linear-sweep voltammetry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09142
name: normal pulse voltammetry
def: "Voltammetry in which potential pulses of amplitude increasing by a constant increment and with a pulse width of \\\(\\pu\{2 to 200 ms\}\\\) are superimposed on a constant initial potential. The current is sampled at the end of the pulse and then plotted versus the potential of the pulse. Sigmoidal wave-shaped voltammograms are obtained. See figure.\(a\) Waveform for normal pulse voltammetry \(not to scale\). Typical values for pulse parameters are \\\(\\tau = \\pu\{1 s\}\\\)\, \\\(t_\{\\rm\{p\}\} = \\pu\{50 ms\}\\\)\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{p\}\} = \\pu\{2 mV\}\\\)\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{step\}\} = \\pu\{2 mV\}\\\). Sampling interval indicated by horizontal curly brackets is typically \\\(\\pu\{15 ms\}\\\). \(b\) Normal pulse voltammogram."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09143
name: Randles–Ševčík equations
def: "Equations describing the peak current \\\(I_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) in linear scan voltammetry and cyclic voltammetry as a function of the amount concentration\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{0\}\}\\\)\, of electroactive species in bulk solution. For a reversible charge transfer\: \\\[I_\{\\rm\{p\,rev\}\} = k\\\,z\\\,F\\\,A\\\,c_0 \(z\\\,F\\\,D\nu/RT\)^\{1/2\}\\\] for irreversible charge transfer\: \\\[I_\{\\rm\{p\,irrev\}\} = k^\{\\prime\}\(\\alpha z^\{\\prime\}\)^\{1/2\} zFA\{c_0\}\(FD\nu/RT\)^\{1/2\}\\\] where \\\(k\\\) and \\\(k^\{\\prime\}\\\) are numerical constants\, \\\(\\alpha\\\) is the charge transfer coefficient \(usually assumed to be close to 0.5\)\, \\\(z\\\) the electron number of an electrochemical reaction\, \\\(z^\{\\prime\}\\\) the number of electrons transferred before the rate determining step\, \\\(F\\\) the Faraday constant\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(A\\\) the electrode surface area\, \\\(D\\\) the diffusion coefficient\, and \\\(\nu\\\) the scan rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09144
name: rate of flow of liquid metal in polarography
def: "Mass of a drop of liquid metal in polarography at the instant when it is detached from the tip of the capillary divided by the drop time \\\(t_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\)."
synonym: "mass flux" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09145
name: sampled DC polarography
def: "DC polarography with current sampling at the end of each drop life mechanically enforced by a knocker at a preset drop time value. The current sampling and mechanical drop dislodge are synchronized."
synonym: "TAST polarography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09146
name: scan rate
def: "The rate of change of applied potential with time."
synonym: "potential scan rate" []
synonym: "potential sweep rate" []
synonym: "sweep rate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09147
name: square-wave voltammetry
def: "Voltammetry in which a square-wave potential waveform is superimposed on an underlying linearlyhyphen‐varying potential ramp or staircase ramp. The current is sampled just before the end of the forward-going pulse and of the backward-going pulse and the difference of the two sampled currents is plotted versus the applied potential of the potential or staircase ramp. The square-wave voltammogram is peak-shaped. See figure.\(a\) Waveform for square wave voltammetry \(not to scale\). Typical values for pulse parameters are \\\(\\tau = \\pu\{10 ms\}\\\)\, \\\(t_\{\\rm\{p\}\} = \\pu\{5 ms\}\\\)\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{\\tau\}\} = \\pu\{25 mV\}\\\)\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{step\}\} = \\pu\{4 mV\}\\\). Sampling interval indicated by horizontal curly brackets is typically \\\(\\pu\{1 ms\}\\\). \(b\) Square wave voltammogram of the difference between currents sampled at ‘2’ and ‘1’ against applied potential."
synonym: "Osteryoung square-wave voltammetry (OSWV)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09148
name: voltammetry
def: "Electrochemical measurement principle in which the electrode potential of a working electrode is controlled and the electric current measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09149
name: voltammogram
def: "Current-potential curve in a voltammetry experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09150
name: accumulation in stripping voltammetry
def: "Transfer of an analyte from a test solution to an electrode surface as the first step in stripping voltammetry."
synonym: "accumulation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09151
name: adsorptive stripping voltammetry
def: "Stripping voltammetry involving pre-concentration by adsorption of the analyte \(in contrast to electrochemical accumulation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09152
name: anodic stripping voltammetry
def: "Stripping voltammetry in which material accumulated at the working electrode is electrochemically oxidized in the stripping step."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09153
name: cathodic stripping voltammetry
def: "Stripping voltammetry in which material accumulated at the working electrode is electrochemically reduced in the stripping step. See figure.Applied potential against time during the accumulation and stripping steps in cathodic stripping voltammetry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09154
name: potentiometric stripping analysis
def: "Two-step electrochemical measurement in which 1\) material is accumulated at an electrode and 2\) the material is removed by chemical reaction or electrochemically at constant current with measurement of electrode potential. The time between changes in potential in step two is related to the concentration of analyte in the solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09155
name: stripping voltammetry
def: "Two-step electrochemical measurement in which 1\) material is accumulated at an electrode and 2\) the amount of an accumulated species is measured by voltammetry. The measured electric current in step 2 is related to the concentration of analyte in the solution by calibration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09156
name: chronocoulometry
def: "Direct coulometry at controlled potential in which the electric charge passed after the application of a potential step perturbation is measured as a function of time \(\\\(Q\{-\}t\\\) curve\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09157
name: coulometer
def: "Measuring instrument to obtain the electrical charge passed in an experiment\, or to produce a known amount of substance in a titration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09159
name: coulometry
def: "Electrochemical measurement principle in which the electric charge required to carry out a known electrochemical reaction is measured. By Faraday’s laws of electrolysis\, the amount of substance is proportional to the charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09160
name: direct coulometry at controlled current
def: "Coulometry at an imposed\, constant electric current in the electrochemical cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09161
name: direct coulometry at controlled potential
def: "Coulometry at a preselected constant potential of the working electrode.Electric current against time showing the representation of the electric charge in direct coulometry at controlled potential as the area under the graph."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09162
name: electrochemical piezoelectric microgravimetry
def: "Electrogravimetry using an electrochemical quartz crystal microbalance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09163
name: electrochemical quartz crystal microbalance
def: "Electrochemical cell in which the working electrode is located on one surface of a piezoelectric quartz crystal\, the oscillation frequency of which is measured."
synonym: "electrochemical quartz crystal nanobalance (EQCN)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09164
name: electrogravimetry
def: "Gravimetry in which the material to be weighed is obtained by electrochemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09165
name: conductometric titration
def: "Titration in which the electric conductivity of a solution is measured as a function of the amount of titrant added.Conductimetric titration curve of conductance \($G$\) against volume of titrant. The end-point of the titration is indicated by a change in slope of the graph."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09166
name: conductometry
def: "Measurement principle in which the electric conductivity of a solution is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09167
name: dielectrometry
def: "Electrochemical measurement principle based on the measurement of the dielectric constant of a sample resulting from the orientation of particles \(molecules or ions\) that have a dipole moment in an electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09168
name: electrochemical impedance spectroscopy
def: "Electrochemical measurement method of the complex impedance of an electrochemical system as a function of the frequency of a small amplitude \(normally \\\(\\pu\{5 to 10 mV\}\\\)\) sinusoidal voltage perturbation superimposed on a fixed value of applied potential or on the open circuit potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09169
name: impedimetry
def: "Measurement principle in which the complex electric impedance of a system is measured\, usually as a function of a small amplitude sinusoidal electrode potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09170
name: electrochemical detection in flowing systems
def: "Electrochemical techniques \(potentiometry\, amperometry\, voltammetry\, coulometry\, conductimetry\) used for analyte detection in flowing systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09171
name: spectroelectrochemistry
def: "Measurement methods combining spectrometric and electrochemical measurements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09172
name: Auger spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of electron emission spectroscopy that utilizes Auger electrons emitted from the target following excitation with an electron beam or X-ray beam."
synonym: "Auger electron spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09173
name: Auger photoelectron coincidence spectroscopy
def: "Detection of an Auger electron and a core-level photoelectron\, emitted from a solid irradiated by mono-energetic X-rays\, within a sufficiently short time that the Auger electron shall arise from the decay of the particular core-hole associated with the photoelectron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09174
name: elastic peak electron spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method in which an electron spectrometer is used to measure the energy\, intensity\, and/or energy broadening distribution of quasi-elastically scattered electrons from a solid or liquid surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09175
name: direct recoil spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method to elucidate composition and structure of the outermost atomic layers of a solid material\, in which principally monoenergetic neutral atoms or singly charged probe ions are used to sputter atoms or ions out of the solid surface\, their energy being recorded at one or more angle of scattering."
synonym: "elastic recoil detection" []
synonym: "elastic recoil detection analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09176
name: low-energy ion scattering spectrometry
def: "Measurement method to elucidate the composition and structure of the very outermost atomic layers of a solid material\, in which principally monoenergetic\, singly-charged probe ions scattered from the surface are detected and recorded as a function of their energy\, angle of scattering\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09177
name: medium-energy ion scattering spectrometry
def: "Measurement method to elucidate the composition and structure of the outermost atomic layers of a solid material\, in which principally monoenergetic\, singly-charged probe ions scattered from the surface are detected and recorded as a function of their energy\, angle of scattering\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09178
name: Rutherford backscattering spectrometry
def: "Measurement method to elucidate composition and structure of layers at the surface of a solid material\, in which principally monoenergetic\, singly charged probe ions scattered from the surface with a Rutherford cross section are detected and recorded as a function of their energy or angle of scattering\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09179
name: desorption atmospheric pressure chemical ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of a nearby corona discharge in a humid carrier gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09180
name: desorption atmospheric pressure photoionization mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of desorption by a jet of hot solvent vapour and ionization by ultraviolet light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09181
name: desorption electrospray ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of the bombardment by ionized solvent droplets generated by pneumatically assisted electrospray ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09182
name: direct analysis in real time
def: "Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of the interaction with a beam containing metastable\, excited atoms heated to about \\\(\\pu\{500^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09183
name: dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry
def: "Secondary-ion mass spectrometry analysis with primary ion current density sufficiently high for use in the analysis of sample surface layers in the depth direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09184
name: electrospray laser desorption ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge quotient and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air via ablation by a focused pulsed laser while simultaneously directing an electrospray jet through the desorbed plume to enhance sample ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09185
name: extractive electrospray ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of an inert gas being passed over the sample to pick up the molecules\, which are subsequently ionized by ionized solvent droplets generated by pneumatically assisted electrospray ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09186
name: G-SIMS
def: "Variant of static secondary-ion mass spectrometry in which the intensities for each mass in two spectra from the same area\, recorded with different beam energies or different bombarding ions\, are ratioed to each other and the result is used to scale one of the spectra to generate a new spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09187
name: laser ablation electrospray ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of laser ablation and electrospray ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09188
name: matrix-assisted laser desorption electrospray ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities from a sample that has been mixed with a matrix whose role is enhancing the ion yield produced by laser ablation and electrospray ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09189
name: matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a time-of-flight mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ions emitted\, as a result of a short pulse of laser illumination\, from a sample whose analyte is contained in a matrix that assists the formation of ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09190
name: plasma assisted desorption ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a mass spectrometer is used to measure the ratio of mass to charge and the abundance of ionized entities emitted from a sample in air as a result of the bombardment by a focused plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09191
name: reactive desorption electrospray ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Mode of desorption electrospray ionization mass spectrometry with reactive chemicals within the spray solution to enhance the analytical specificity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09192
name: secondary-ion mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement method in which a focused beam of primary ions produces secondary ions by sputtering from a solid surface. The secondary ions are analysed by mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09193
name: static secondary-ion mass spectrometry
def: "Method of secondary-ion mass spectrometry using low current densities for analysis of sample surface components\, in contrast with dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry\, which is used for analysis of components in the depth direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09194
name: surface-assisted laser desorption/ionization mass spectrometry
def: "Matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization mass spectrometry using a combined liquid and particulate matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09195
name: attenuated total reflection infrared spectroscopy
def: "Attenuated total reflection spectroscopy in which the infrared absorption spectrum of a sample in contact with a prism is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09196
name: diffuse reflection-absorption infrared spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method in which the infrared absorption spectrum of light reflected and transmitted from powdered samples is measured."
synonym: "diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09197
name: ellipsometry
def: "Measurement method for the thickness of transparent surface films and layers and for the refractive index of a reflecting material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09198
name: extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy
def: "X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy in the energy region from the core-level edge to an energy several hundred $\\pu\{eV\}$ greater."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09199
name: grazing-incidence small-angle X-ray scattering analysis
def: "Measurement method in which the elastically scattered intensity of X-rays from a reflecting surface is measured for small-angle deflections at grazing incidence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09200
name: off-specular X-ray reflectometry
def: "X-ray reflectometry in which significant scattered intensity arises from imperfections in the specimen\, the scattered intensity falling outside the specular condition \(see specular X-ray reflectometry\)."
synonym: "diffuse X-ray reflectometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09201
name: reflection-absorption infrared spectroscopy
def: "Spectroscopy based on reflection-absorption."
synonym: "infrared reflection-absorption spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09202
name: scanning near-field optical microscopy
def: "Measurement method in microscopy in which spectroscopic data may be collected with the detector closer to the sample than the optical wavelength employed."
synonym: "near-field optical microscopy" []
synonym: "near-field scanning optical microscopy (NSOM)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09203
name: small-angle X-ray scattering
def: "Measurement method in which the elastically scattered intensity of X-rays is measured for small-angle deflections."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09204
name: specular X-ray reflectrometry
def: "Mode of X-ray reflectrometry in which the angle between the detected beam and the sample surface is equal to that of the incident beam and the sample surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09205
name: sum frequency generation spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method from non-linear optics in which a structurally-ordered sample at an interface between two phases is irradiated by single frequency visible and variable frequency infrared pulses of light to generate a sum frequency vibrational spectrum of the interfacial species without any bulk phase contribution."
synonym: "vibrational sum frequency spectroscopy (VSFS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09206
name: surface extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy
def: "Extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy in which\, instead of measuring X-ray absorption\, electron emission arising from that absorption is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09207
name: surface-enhanced infrared spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method giving enhanced infrared absorption in the measured infrared spectrum of a sample adsorbed on a roughened thin metal substrate. The metal substrate is deposited onto a prism and data is collected by total internal reflection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09209
name: surface-enhanced resonant Raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy \(SERS\) by which the Raman spectrum is significantly enhanced under the conditions that\, whilst undergoing SERS\, the molecules are excited by radiation that is in resonance with electronic transitions in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09210
name: tip enhanced raman spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method of Raman spectroscopy combining surface-enhanced Raman spectroscopy and scanning using an atomic force microscope or scanning tunnelling microscope to produce maps of a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09211
name: total reflection X-ray fluorescence spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method in which an X-ray spectrometer is used to measure the energy distribution of fluorescence X-rays emitted from a surface irradiated by primary X-rays under the condition of total reflection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09212
name: X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy
def: "Measurement method to measure the absorption of X-rays at energies near and above \(typically several hundred \\\(\\pu\{eV\}\\\) greater\) an absorption edge\, over which fine structure \(modulation of the X-ray absorption coefficient\) can be detected."
synonym: "XAFS" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09213
name: X-ray absorption near-edge spectroscopy
def: "X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy in the energy region near \(within approximately \\\(\\pu\{100 eV\}\\\)\) an absorption edge."
synonym: "near-edge extended X-ray absorption fine structure spectroscopy (NEXAFS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09214
name: X-ray reflectometry
def: "Measurement method for surface and interface roughness and density\, layer thickness of thin layers\, and electron density profile achieved by reflecting X-rays from a flat sample and analysing the reflected intensity as a function of angle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09215
name: X-ray standing waves
def: "Measurement method based on the interference of X-rays that occurs near the Bragg condition at the surface of a crystalline solid\, which leads to intensity maxima in yields of photoelectrons as the Bragg condition is scanned by tilting the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09216
name: absolute elemental sensitivity factor
def: "Coefficient for an element by which the measured intensity for that element is divided to yield the atomic concentration or atomic fraction of the element present in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09217
name: altered layer
def: "Surface region of a material under particle bombardment where the chemical state or physical structure is modified by the effects of the bombardment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09218
name: analysis area
def: "Two-dimensional region of a sample surface measured in the plane of that surface\, from which the entire analytical signal or a specified fraction of that signal is detected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09219
name: analysis area
def: "Two-dimensional region of a sample surface at the analytical point\, but set in the plane at right angles to the spectrometer axis\, from which the entire analytical signal or a specified fraction of that signal is detected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09220
name: angle lapping
def: "Sample preparation in which a sample is mechanically polished at an angle to the original surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09221
name: angle of emission
def: "Angle between the trajectory of a particle or photon\, as it leaves a surface\, and the local or average surface normal."
synonym: "emission angle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09222
name: areic dose rate
def: "Areic dose introduced into a solid in time interval \\\(t\\\) divided by \\\(t\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09223
name: areic dose
def: "Number\, \\\(N\\\)\, of energetic particles of a specified type introduced into a solid through a surface area \\\(A\\\) divided by \\\(A\\\). \\\(D = N/A\\\)."
synonym: "dose" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09224
name: areic surface energy
def: "Energy required to increase a surface area at thermodynamic equilibrium divided by that increase in area."
synonym: "surface energy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09225
name: atomic mixing
def: "Migration of sample atoms due to energy transfer with incident particles in the surface region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09226
name: Auger neutralization
def: "Process in which an electron\, tunnelling from the conduction band of a solid\, neutralizes an incoming ion and an electron is ejected from a surface atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09227
name: average beam current
def: "Electric charge of specified polarity in a beam passing in time interval \\\(t\\\)\, divided by \\\(t\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09228
name: backscattering energy
def: "Energy of a particle from the primary beam after it has undergone a backscattering collision and escaped from the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09229
name: ball cratering
def: "Procedure in which the sample is abraded by a sphere in order to expose compositional changes in layers below the original surface\, with the intent that the depth of those layers can be related to the lateral position in the crater created by the abrasion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09230
name: binary elastic scattering
def: "Collision between a moving particle and a second particle in which total kinetic energy and total momentum are conserved."
synonym: "elastic scattering" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09231
name: Bragg’s rule
def: "Empirical rule formulated by W.H. Bragg and R. Kleeman that states that the stopping cross section of a compound sample is equal to the sum of the products of the elemental stopping cross sections for each constituent and its atomic fraction. \\\[S_\{\\ce\{AB\}\}\(\\varepsilon\) = xS_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\(\\varepsilon\) + yS_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\(\\varepsilon\)\\\] where \\\(S_\{\\ce\{AB\}\}\(\\varepsilon\)\\\) is the stopping cross section of the compound \\\(\\ce\{A\}_\{x\}\\ce\{B\}_\{y\}\\\)\, and \\\(S_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\(\\varepsilon\)\\\) and \\\(S_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\(\\varepsilon\)\\\) are the stopping cross sections of elements \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09232
name: cascade mixing
def: "Diffusion-like process in which atoms of material are moved randomly by energy deposited by incident particles slowing down in the sample surface region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09233
name: channeling
def: "Preferential motion of energetic particles along the crystal axes of a crystalline solid as the particles move through the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09234
name: charge neutralization
def: "Maintenance at a fixed potential\, usually near neutrality\, of the surface of a non-conducting or poorly conducting sample material under bombardment by primary particles or photons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09235
name: charging potential
def: "Electric potential of the surface region of an insulating sample\, caused by irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09236
name: chemical map
def: "Surface map using signals proportional to the amount of an element in a particular chemical state in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09237
name: chromatic aberration
def: "Non-ideal focus of an electron or ion optical system for electrons or ions of different energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09238
name: collision cascade
def: "Sequential energy transfer between atoms in a solid as a result of bombardment by an energetic species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09239
name: compositional depth profile
def: "Chemical composition measured as a function of distance normal to the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09240
name: crater depth
def: "Average depth of the region of a crater from which the measured signal is derived."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09241
name: cross-sectioning
def: "Sample preparation in which the sample is cleaved\, cut\, or polished in a plane perpendicular to the interface under study\, so that associated compositional differences or gradients can be observed in that plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09242
name: damage limit
def: "Particle fluence above which significant changes in the spectrum or in a stated peak\, arising from damage processes\, are observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09243
name: delta layer
def: "Layer of discrete composition\, one atom thick\, formed during the growth of material on a substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09244
name: depth profiling
def: "Monitoring of signal intensity as a function of a variable that can be related to distance normal to the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09245
name: depth resolution parameter
def: "Parameter that can be used as a coefficient in an analytic fit to a measured compositional depth profile or as a qualitative way of describing that profile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09246
name: elastic scattering cross section
def: "Cross section for binary elastic scattering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09247
name: electron flooding
def: "Irradiation of a sample with low-energy electrons in order to change or stabilize the charging potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09248
name: elemental map
def: "Surface map using signals proportional to the amount of an element present in a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09249
name: erosion rate
def: "Change in the position of the surface as a result of particle or photon irradiation divided by time of irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09250
name: field-induced migration
def: "Effect occurring in insulators or semi-conductors where internal electric fields cause the migration of sample atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09251
name: flux of a beam of particles
def: "\\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} = \{\\rm\{d\}\}N/\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\\\)\, where \\\(N\\\) is number of particles of a specified type and \\\(t\\\) is time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09252
name: focused ion beam system
def: "Ion beam system used for machining small regions with sub-micron precision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09253
name: fractional sputtering yield
def: "Number of atoms and ions of a particular species sputtered from a sample divided by the total number of atoms and ions sputtered from the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09254
name: image depth profile
def: "Three-dimensional representation of the spatial distribution of a particular elemental or molecular species\, as indicated by emitted secondary ions or electrons\, as a function of depth or material removed by sputtering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09255
name: impact energy
def: "Kinetic energy of particles on impact with a sample surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09256
name: implanted areic dose
def: "Number\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{imp\}\}\\\)\, of energetic particles of a specified type incident on a solid within a surface area \\\(A\\\) and stopped within the solid divided by \\\(A\\\). \\\(D_\{\\rm\{imp\}\} = N_\{\\rm\{imp\}\}/A\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09257
name: information depth
def: "Maximum depth\, normal to the surface\, from which useful information is obtained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09258
name: information radius
def: "Maximum radius of a circular region\, in the plane of the surface\, from which useful information is obtained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09259
name: instrumental depth resolution
def: "Depth resolution in a sample arising from parameters of the instrument."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09260
name: ion beam
def: "Directed flux of charged atoms or molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09261
name: ion implantation
def: "Injection of ions into a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09262
name: line scan
def: "Plot of output signal intensity from a spectrometer\, signal intensity from another detector\, or processed intensity information from the available software along a line corresponding to a line on the sample surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09263
name: matrix factor
def: "Factors\, arising from the composition of the matrix\, for multiplying the quotient of the measured intensity and the appropriate sensitivity factor in formulae to determine the composition using surface analytical techniques."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09264
name: mean escape depth
def: "Average depth normal to the surface from which specified particles or radiations escape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09265
name: partial sputtering yield
def: "Number of atoms and ions of a particular species sputtered from a sample divided by the total number of incident particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09266
name: peak energy
def: "Energy value corresponding to the intensity maximum in a direct spectrum or to the intensity minimum \(i.e. the negative excursion\) for a differential spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09267
name: photoionization cross section
def: "Total ionization cross section for an incident photon of a given energy interacting with a material to produce one or more photoelectrons from all sub-shells that are energetically accessible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09268
name: plasmon
def: "Excitation of valence-band electrons in a solid in which collective oscillations are generated."
synonym: "bulk plasmon" []
synonym: "volume plasmon" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09269
name: preferential sputtering
def: "Change in equilibrium surface composition of the sample\, which can occur when sputtering multicomponent samples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09270
name: primary beam
def: "Directed flux of beam particles or photons incident on a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09271
name: projected range
def: "Distance from the surface at which an energetic ion or atom comes to rest in the sample\, projected along the direction of the beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09272
name: radial sectioning
def: "Sample preparation in which a sample is polished by a cylinder in order to expose compositional changes below the original sample surface\, with the intent that the depth of these layers can be related to the position on the surface created by the cylinder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09273
name: range straggling
def: "Standard deviation of the projected ranges of energetic ions or atoms of a given energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09274
name: raster
def: "Two-dimensional pattern generated by the deflection of a primary beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09275
name: redeposition
def: "Deposition of sputtered sample material back onto the sample surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09276
name: relative instrument spectral response function
def: "In Auger electron spectroscopy \(AES\)\, secondary-ion mass spectrometry\, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, the spectrometer response function divided by the response function of a reference instrument\, or the average for several such instruments\, as a function of energy \(AES\, XPS\, etc.\) or mass \(SIMS\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09277
name: relative resolution
def: "Resolution of a spectrometer at a given energy\, mass\, or wavelength divided by that energy\, mass\, or wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09278
name: relative sputtering rate
def: "Sputtering rate of a sample divided by the sputtering rate of a reference sample sputtered under the same conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09279
name: sample bias
def: "Electric potential applied to the whole or part of the sample\, referenced to the electric potential of the sample holder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09280
name: sample charging
def: "Change in the electrical potential in the sample or on the sample surface caused by particle or photon bombardment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09281
name: sample voltage
def: "Electric potential difference of the sample referenced to ground."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09282
name: solid angle of analyser
def: "Solid angle allowing particles or photons to be transmitted from a point on the sample to the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09283
name: spectrometer response function
def: "Number of particles detected with a spectrometer divided by the number of such particles per solid angle and per interval of the dispersing parameter available for measurement as a function of the dispersing parameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09284
name: spectrometer transmission function
def: "Number of particles transmitted by the analyser divided by the number of such particles per solid angle and per interval of the dispersing parameter \(e.g. energy\, mass\, or wavelength\) available for measurement as a function of the dispersing parameter."
synonym: "analyser transmission function" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09285
name: sputter depth profile
def: "Compositional depth profile obtained when the surface composition is measured while material is removed by sputtering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09286
name: sputtering rate
def: "Amount\, mass\, or number of entities of sample material removed\, as a result of particle bombardment\, per unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09287
name: sputtering yield
def: "Number of atoms and ions sputtered from a sample divided by the total number of incident primary particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09288
name: standard vacuum level
def: "Electric potential \\\(\\pu\{4.500 eV\}\\\) above the Fermi level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09289
name: steady-state sputtering
def: "State of the sputtering process in which important operational and analytical parameters are unchanging over a meaningful timescale."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09290
name: steady-state surface composition sputtering
def: "Steady-state surface composition produced by sputtering a homogeneous sample under non-varying conditions."
synonym: "equilibrium surface composition sputtering" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09291
name: stoichiometric sputtering
def: "State of the sputtering process in which the relative amounts of the elemental components sputtered from a sample are equal to their stoichiometry within the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09292
name: surface excess amount
def: "For an interface\, the difference between the amount of component actually present in the system and that which would be present \(in a reference system\) if the bulk concentration in the adjoining phases were maintained up to a chosen geometrical dividing surface \(Gibbs dividing surface\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09293
name: surface map
def: "Two- or three-dimensional representation of the sample surface where the information at each point in the representation\, given by a brightness or colour or as a length in a third dimension\, is related to the output signal from a detector or processed intensity information from the available software."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09294
name: surface plasmon
def: "Collective motion of electrons on an interface of two phases which have different permittivity\, of which one must have electrical conductivity to sustain the collective motion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09295
name: surface segregation
def: "Partitioning of a species from the bulk of a material to the surface as a result of kinetic or thermodynamic effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09296
name: take-off angle
def: "Angle between the trajectory of a particle as it leaves a surface and the local or average surface plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09297
name: thermionic work function
def: "Parameter describing the apparent work function of a solid that controls the electronic current density\, \\\(J\\\)\, emitted from a conductor at temperature \\\(T\\\). \\\(W = -kT\\ln\(J/AT^\{2\}\)\\\)\, where \\\(k\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(A\\\) is the Richardson constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09298
name: topographic contrast
def: "Contrast in a surface map or image arising from the topography of the sample surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09299
name: total ion yield
def: "Number of ions of both signs sputtered from a sample divided by the number of incident particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09300
name: vacuum level
def: "Energy level of an electron positioned at rest just outside a solid\, i.e. with zero kinetic energy with respect to the sample surface."
synonym: "local vacuum level" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09301
name: vacuum level at infinity
def: "Electric potential of the vacuum at a point in space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09302
name: volume yield
def: "Volume sputtered from a sample divided by number of incident particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09303
name: X-ray linewidth
def: "Energy width of the principal characteristic X-ray line."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09304
name: X-ray monochromator
def: "Device used to eliminate photons of energies other than those in a narrow energy or wavelength band."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09305
name: adventitious carbon referencing
def: "In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, measurement of the charging potential of a particular sample from a comparison of the experimentally measured \\\(\\ce\{C 1s\}\\\) binding energy\, arising from adsorbed hydrocarbons on the sample\, with a standard binding energy value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09306
name: asymmetry parameter
def: "In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy factor which characterizes the intensity distribution\, \\\(L\(\\gamma\)\\\)\, of photoelectrons ejected by X-rays from isolated atoms at an angle \\\(\\gamma\\\) from the incident X-ray direction. In the dipole approximation\, \\\(L\(\\gamma\) = 1 + ½\\beta \[3\(\\sin^\{2\}\\gamma\)/2-1\]\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09307
name: Auger electron spectrum
def: "Plot of intensity of Auger electrons as a function of the electron kinetic energy\, usually as part of the energy distribution of detected electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09308
name: Auger parameter
def: "Kinetic energy of a narrow Auger electron peak in a spectrum minus the kinetic energy of the most intense X-ray photoelectron peak from the same element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09309
name: Auger vacancy satellite
def: "Emission of an Auger electron in which additional spectator holes are present in the initial state or the final state\) for the transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09310
name: average matrix relative sensitivity factor
def: "Coefficient\, proportional to the intensity\, calculated for an element in an average matrix\, by which the measured intensity for that element is divided in calculations to yield the atomic concentration or atomic fraction of the element present in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09311
name: backscattered electron
def: "Electron\, originating in the incident beam\, which is emitted after interaction with the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09312
name: backscattering correction factor
def: "In Auger electron spectroscopy\, factor equal to the total Auger-electron current arising from ionizations in the sample caused by both primary electrons and backscattered electrons divided by the Auger-electron current arising directly from the primary electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09313
name: backscattering fraction
def: "In Auger electron spectroscopy\, Auger-electron current arising from ionizations in the sample caused by backscattered electrons divided by Auger-electron current arising directly from the primary electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09314
name: backscattering yield
def: "Number of electrons emitted from the sample with energies greater than \\\(\\pu\{50 eV\}\\\) divided by the number of electrons incident at a given energy and angle of incidence."
synonym: "backscattering coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09315
name: characteristic electron energy losses
def: "Inelastic scattering of electrons in solids that produces a non-uniform energy loss spectrum determined by the characteristics of the material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09316
name: charge referencing
def: "Measurement of charging potential of a sample in order to correct the measured energies so that those energies correspond to a sample with no surface charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09317
name: chemical state
def: "State of an atom arising from its chemical interaction with neighbouring atoms in a molecule\, compound\, solid\, liquid\, or gas that leads to a characteristic energy or feature observable in electron spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09318
name: chemical state plot
def: "In X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, plot of the measured kinetic energy of a sharp Auger electron peak versus the binding energy of a photoelectron peak for the same element."
synonym: "Wagner plot" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09319
name: differential spectrum
def: "Differential of a direct spectrum with respect to energy by an analogue electrode modulation method or by the numerical differentiation of that spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09320
name: direct spectrum
def: "Spectrum of intensity of electrons transmitted and detected by a spectrometer with a dispersing energy analyser as a function of energy\, \\\(E\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09321
name: dynamic emittance matching
def: "Electron or ion optical method of steering a spectrometer axis to align with the impact area of the primary beam at all points of a raster scan on the sample surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09322
name: effective attenuation length
def: "Parameter that\, when introduced in place of the electron inelastic mean free path into an expression derived for Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy on the assumption that elastic scattering effects are negligible for a given quantitative application\, will correct that expression for elastic scattering effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09323
name: Einstein photoelectric equation
def: "Equation relating the kinetic energy of an electron\, emitted from a conductor illuminated by monochromatic photons\, and the photon energy and work function of the surface from which the photoelectron is emitted. \\\(½m_\{\\rm\{e\}\} \nu_\{\\rm\{max\}\}^\{2\} = h\nu - \\phi_\{0\}\\\)\, where \\\(m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the mass of an electron\, \\\(\nu_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\) the maximum velocity of the emitted electron\, \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant\, \\\(\nu\\\) the frequency of the illuminating photons\, and \\\(\\phi_\{0\}\\\) the photoelectric work function of the surface."
synonym: "Einstein equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09324
name: elastic peak
def: "Peak in the electron spectrum\, produced by quasi-elastically scattered electrons detected by an electron spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09325
name: electron energy analyser
def: "Measuring instrument for measuring the number of electrons\, or an intensity proportional to that number\, as a function of the electron kinetic energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09326
name: electron energy loss spectrum
def: "Energy spectrum of electrons from a nominally monoenergetic source emitted after inelastic interactions with the sample\, often exhibiting peaks due to specific inelastic loss processes \(see characteristic electron energy losses and plasmon\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09327
name: electron inelastic mean free path
def: "Average distance that an electron with a given energy travels between successive inelastic collisions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09328
name: electron retardation
def: "In Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, measurement method for kinetic energy distribution by retarding the emitted electrons before or within the electron energy analyser."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09329
name: electron spectrometer
def: "Measuring system\, the essential part of which is an electron energy analyser."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09330
name: elemental relative sensitivity factor
def: "In dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry\, coefficient for an element by which the measured intensity of a mass peak for that element\, divided by the measured intensity of a mass peak for the matrix\, is multiplied to yield the atomic concentration of the element present in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09331
name: extrinsic plasmon
def: "Plasmon excited as an electron travels through a condensed medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09332
name: Fermi level referencing
def: "Process for establishing the binding energy scale for a particular sample by assigning the kinetic energy corresponding to the Fermi level\, as determined by analysis of the spectrum obtained from X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy or ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy\, as the point of zero binding energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09333
name: final energy state
def: "Energy state of an atom after a particular Auger\, X-ray\, or photoemission process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09334
name: inelastic electron scattering background
def: "Intensity distribution in a spectrum for particles originally at one energy but which are emitted at lower energies due to one or more inelastic scattering processes."
synonym: "inelastic background" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09335
name: inelastic electron scattering background subtraction
def: "Subtraction of a chosen inelastic electron scattering background from a measured spectrum in Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09336
name: inelastic scattering cross section
def: "Cross section for inelastic scattering by an electron traversing a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09337
name: initial energy state
def: "Core-hole excited state of an atom prior to Auger transition or to X-ray emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09338
name: initial energy state
def: "Ground state of an atom prior to photoelectron emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09339
name: initial-state Auger parameter
def: "\\\(\\beta = 3E_\{\\rm\{B\}\} + E_\{\\rm\{K\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(E_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) and \\\(E_\{\\rm\{K\}\}\\\) are\, respectively\, the binding energy of a photoelectron peak and the Fermi level referenced \(see Fermi level referencing\) kinetic energy of an Auger electron peak\, each involving the same initial core level of the same element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09340
name: interatomic Auger process
def: "Auger transition in which at least one of the final electron vacancies is localized in valence levels or molecular orbitals of atoms adjacent to the atom in which the initial vacancy occurred."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09341
name: interface core-level shift
def: "Energy shift observed in core-level photoelectron spectroscopy arising from the changed bonding and/or from the displacements of atoms from their bulk positions near an interface between two materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09342
name: intrinsic plasmon
def: "Plasmon excited at the same time as the excitation of a core-level photoelectron or an Auger electron associated with the same locality as the excitation of the photoelectron or Auger electron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09343
name: modified Auger parameter
def: "Sum of the Fermi level referenced \(see Fermi level referencing\) kinetic energy of a narrow Auger electron peak in the spectrum and the binding energy of the most intense photoelectron peak from the same element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09344
name: pass energy
def: "Mean kinetic energy of detected particles in the energy-dispersive portion of an electron energy analyser."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09345
name: photoelectric work function
def: "Minimum photon energy required to liberate an electron from the surface of a conducting solid by absorption of that photon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09346
name: photoelectron X-ray satellite peaks
def: "Photoelectron peaks in a spectrum resulting from photoemission induced by characteristic minor X-ray lines associated with the X-ray spectrum of the anode material."
synonym: "satellite peaks" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09347
name: photoelectron X-ray satellite subtraction
def: "Removal of photoelectron X-ray satellite peaks from a spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09348
name: photoemission
def: "Emission of electrons from atoms or molecules caused by the photoelectric effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09349
name: pure-element relative sensitivity factor
def: "In electron spectroscopy\, coefficient\, proportional to the intensity measured for a pure sample of an element\, by which the measured intensity for that element is divided in calculations to yield the atomic concentration or atomic fraction of the element present in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09350
name: relaxation energy
def: "Energy associated with intra-atomic or extra-atomic electronic readjustment to the removal of an atomic electron\, so as to minimize the energy of the final state of the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09352
name: selected-area aperture
def: "Aperture in the electron or ion optical system restricting the detected signal to a small area of the sample surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09353
name: shakeoff
def: "Multi-electron process in which two or more electrons are emitted\, partitioning between them the excess kinetic energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09354
name: shakeup
def: "Multi-electron process in which an atom is left in an excited state following a photoionization or Auger electron process\, so that the outgoing electron has a characteristic kinetic energy slightly less than that of the parent photoelectron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09355
name: Shirley background
def: "In Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, background calculated to fit the measured spectrum at points at greater and lesser kinetic energy than the peak or peaks of interest\, such that the background contribution at a given kinetic energy is in a fixed proportion to the total peak area above that background for greater kinetic energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09356
name: spectator hole
def: "Electronic configuration of an atom containing an electronic vacancy that can be present during processes\, such as Auger electron and X-ray photoelectron emission\, but is not created or destroyed in the process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09357
name: sub-shell photoionization cross section
def: "Cross section for an incident photon interacting with a material to produce one or more photoelectrons from a given sub-shell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09358
name: surface core-level shift
def: "Energy shift observed in core-level photoelectron spectroscopy arising from the reduced coordination of the surface or near-surface atoms compared to bulk atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09359
name: total secondary-electron yield
def: "Total number of electrons emitted from a sample divided by the total number of electrons incident at a given energy and angle of incidence. \\\(\\sigma = \\delta + \\eta\\\)\, where \\\(\\delta\\\) is the secondary-electron yield and \\\(\\eta\\\) is the backscattering coefficient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09360
name: Tougaard background
def: "In Auger electron spectroscopy and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, intensity distribution of the background due to inelastic scattering obtained from the differential inelastic scattering cross section with respect to energy loss and the three-dimensional distribution of the emitting atoms in the surface region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09361
name: vacuum level referencing
def: "Method of establishing the kinetic energy scale in which the zero point corresponds to an electron at rest at the vacuum level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09362
name: valence-band spectrum
def: "X-ray photoelectron energy distribution arising from the excitation of electrons from the valence band of the sample material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09363
name: aligned incidence spectrum
def: "Backscattering spectrum recorded with the analysing beam aligned with crystallographic axes or planes of the sample that produce channelling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09364
name: backscattering spectrum
def: "Plot of backscattering yield versus backscattering energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09365
name: beam chopper
def: "Electrostatic or electromagnetic device used to generate pulses of ions from a continuous ion beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09366
name: binary elastic scattering peak
def: "In ion-scattering spectrometry\, increase in the spectrometer detection system response above the background signal\, which can be attributed to the binary elastic scattering of an incident ion by a surface atom of a particular mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09367
name: blocking geometry
def: "Experimental arrangement wherein the atom rows or planes of a single-crystal target are aligned parallel to a vector from the sample to the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09368
name: electronic stopping cross section
def: "Stopping cross section arising from energy transfer to the electrons of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09369
name: energy edge
def: "Values of the backscattering energy for an element\, or for an isotope\, that is located at the surface of a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09370
name: surface approximation energy
def: "Simplification of calculations involving the energy of an ion passing through a solid sample\, where the energy of the ion at the surface is used in place of a properly averaged energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09371
name: enhanced elastic cross section
def: "Cross section of an atom for elastic scattering that is greater than the Rutherford cross section due to partial penetration of a nucleus in the sample by the incident particle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09372
name: experimental scattered-ion intensity
def: "Measured response of the energy filtering and detection system as a consequence of bombarding the sample material with an ion beam\, usually presented as the ordinate of an ion-scattering spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09373
name: ion-scattering spectrum
def: "Plot of the intensities of ions\, scattered from a sample\, as a function of the scattered-ion energy divided by the incident-ion energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09374
name: kinematic factor
def: "Particle energy after an elastic collision divided by the energy before the collision\, in the laboratory frame of reference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09375
name: nuclear reaction cross section
def: "Cross section at a given beam energy and emission direction of the detected product for a particular nuclear reaction per atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09376
name: nuclear stopping cross section
def: "Stopping cross section arising from energy transfer to atomic nuclei of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09377
name: pileup
def: "Counts in a backscattering spectrum arising from two or more separate events that occur so closely in time that the signals are not resolved by the detection system and cause counts to be recorded in erroneous channels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09378
name: probe ion
def: "Ion intentionally produced by an ion source and directed onto the sample surface at a known angle of incidence and a known energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09379
name: random incidence spectrum
def: "Backscattering spectrum recorded with the analysing beam incident on the sample in a direction such as to produce no channelling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09380
name: resonance reaction
def: "Nuclear reaction that has a narrow peak in the nuclear reaction cross section as a function of energy\, the nuclear reaction cross section at the peak being so much larger than the nuclear reaction cross sections at adjacent energies on either side of the peak that essentially all the particles detected from the reaction are due to the peak."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09381
name: Rutherford cross section
def: "Elastic scattering cross section calculated using classical mechanics and a Coulomb potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09382
name: scattered-ion energy
def: "Kinetic energy of a primary ion after a collision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09383
name: scattered-ion energy ratio
def: "Scattered-ion energy divided by energy of the incident probe ion prior to a collision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09384
name: screening
def: "Response arising from electrons in an atom causing an apparent reduction in the coulomb potential of the nucleus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09385
name: screening function
def: "Factor by which the Rutherford cross section is reduced as a result of screening in a given experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09386
name: stopping cross section factor
def: "Energy loss of a particle scattered at a given depth in the sample\, and detected at a given angle\, by the product of the atomic density of the sample atoms and the depth of scattering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09387
name: stopping cross section
def: "Rate of energy loss of a particle with distance along its trajectory in a sample divided by the atomic density of sample atoms for a sample of infinitesimal thickness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09388
name: swift ion
def: "Ion moving with a velocity exceeding the Bohr speed of \\\(c/137\\\)\, where \\\(c\\\) is the velocity of light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09389
name: system resolution
def: "Energy resolution or depth resolution measured in a backscattering spectrum for a monoenergetic incident-ion beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09390
name: theoretical scattered-ion intensity
def: "Calculated intensity for probe ions scattered into a specified solid angle at a given direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09391
name: thin target
def: "In energetic-ion analysis and Rutherford backscattering spectrometry\, sample whose thickness is sufficiently small that the variation in energy of particles backscattered from atoms of each constitutive element is small with respect to the system resolution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09392
name: analyser blanking
def: "Action to prevent secondary ions from travelling through the mass spectrometer and being detected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09393
name: beam bunching
def: "Reduction in the spread of arrival times of an ion pulse by reduction in the speed of the leading ions or acceleration of those at the trailing edge of the pulse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09394
name: buncher
def: "Device to shorten the time length of a pulse of ions so that they arrive at a point over a reduced time interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09395
name: dilute limit
def: "Atomic fraction or concentration of impurity species in a homogeneous matrix below which the secondary-ion mass spectrometry can safely be assumed to be linear with composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09396
name: disappearance cross section
def: "Cross section for the loss of intensity of an ion signal observed as a result of the bombardment by primary ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09397
name: dual-beam profiling
def: "In secondary-ion mass spectrometry\, sputter depth profiling involving two ion guns."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09398
name: extraction field
def: "In secondary-ion mass spectrometry\, electric field above a sample\, operational during ion emission from the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09399
name: extractor voltage
def: "Electric potential\, referenced to the sample\, of the electrode defining the field above the sample and used to facilitate introduction of emitted ions into the mass spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09400
name: fast-atom bombardment secondary ion mass spectrometry
def: "Secondary ion mass spectrometry in which the primary-ion beam is replaced by a fast-atom beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09401
name: fractional ion yield
def: "Number of ions of a particular species sputtered from a sample divided by number of particles of that species sputtered from that sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09402
name: g index
def: "Power for the ratio of mass intensities in two mass spectra measured for two different primary ion conditions\, used to generate the G-SIMS spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09403
name: G-SIMS with fragmentation pathway mapping
def: "Development of G-SIMS in which the g index is varied in order to deduce the linkages between product ions and precursor ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09404
name: ion beam ratio
def: "Intensity of analyte ion divided by intensity of matrix ion\, both corrected for isotopic abundance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09405
name: liquid-metal ion gun
def: "Ion gun utilizing a liquid-metal Taylor cone as the source of the primary ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09406
name: mass accuracy
def: "Measured mass minus reference value for that mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09407
name: metastable background
def: "Intensity in a mass spectrum arising from ions that spontaneously fragment between emission and detection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09408
name: molecular fragment
def: "Ion or neutral particle that was part of a larger molecular structure and contains information about that structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09409
name: molecular image
def: "Image of a surface formed from ions characteristic of a particular molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09410
name: negative-ion yield
def: "Number of negatively-charged secondary ions sputtered from a sample divided by total number of incident primary particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09411
name: partial ion yield
def: "Number of ions of a particular species sputtered from a sample divided by the number of incident particles."
synonym: "partial ion sputtering yield" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09412
name: polyatomic fragment
def: "Ion or neutral particle composed of three or more atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09413
name: positive-ion yield
def: "Number of positively-charged secondary ions sputtered from a sample divided by the total number of incident primary particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09414
name: primary ion
def: "Ion extracted from a source and directed at a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09415
name: pulse rate
def: "Number of ion pulse cycles per second in secondary ion mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09416
name: pulse width
def: "Full width at half maximum of the time distribution of the pulse of ions generated by a beam chopper and\, optionally\, the use of beam bunching in secondary ion mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09417
name: pulsed extraction field
def: "Extraction field around the sample that is pulsed to the working value for extracting ions for the time necessary for operation of a time-of-flight mass spectrometer\, but is otherwise at a low value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09418
name: reflector voltage
def: "Electric potential set on the reflector electrode of a reflectron mass spectrometer with the zero referenced\, such that an ion emitted from the sample with an energy numerically equal to the reflector voltage would just be on the point of reflection or transmission by the reflector electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09419
name: relative isotopic sensitivity factor
def: "In dynamic secondary-ion mass spectrometry\, coefficient for an element by which the measured intensity for an isotope of that element\, divided by the measured intensity for a matrix ion\, is multiplied to yield the atomic concentration of that isotope of the element present in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09420
name: relative mass accuracy
def: "Mass accuracy divided by reference mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09422
name: secondary ion
def: "Ion ejected from a sample surface as a result of energy and momentum transfer from a primary ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09424
name: secondary-ion angular distribution
def: "Number of secondary ions as a function of angle of emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09425
name: secondary-ion energy distribution
def: "Number of secondary ions as a function of kinetic energy at a specified collection angle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09426
name: secondary-ion yield
def: "Number of ions sputtered from a sample divided by the number of ions incident with a given mass\, energy\, charge\, and angle of incidence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09427
name: shots per pixel
def: "Number of ion pulses incident at each pixel in an image for one raster frame in secondary ion mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09428
name: SIMS ion image
def: "Two-dimensional surface map of the spatial distribution of the amount of a particular secondary ion emitted from within a specific area of the sample in secondary ion mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09429
name: static limit
def: "Ion fluence above which any significant changes in the spectrum\, arising from beam damage\, are observed in secondary ion mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09430
name: stop event
def: "Registration of a particle by a time-to-digital converter in secondary ion mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09431
name: transformation probability
def: "Probability of a defined charged or neutral species being produced\, by consumption of a defined parent atomic or molecular configuration at a surface\, as a result of sputtering in secondary ion mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09432
name: ultra-shallow depth profile
def: "Depth profile in secondary-ion mass spectrometry where the depth over which significant changes occur is less than \\\(\\pu\{10 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09433
name: useful ion yield
def: "In secondary ion mass spectrometry\, number of ions of a particular isotope detected divided by the number of atoms of the same element sputtered from the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09434
name: useful spatial resolution
def: "Image resolution in secondary ion mass spectrometry obtained in practice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09435
name: absorption length
def: "Distance over which the transmitted X-ray intensity falls by \\\(1/e\\\)\, where \\\(e\\\) is the base of the natural logarithm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09436
name: beam footprint
def: "Area on the specimen irradiated by X-rays in X-ray reflectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09437
name: beam spill-off
def: "Fraction of the incident X-ray beam in X-ray reflectrometry missing the specimen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09438
name: delayed onset
def: "X-ray energy at which there is a significant increase of absorption and for which the increased absorption occurs at a greater energy than a core-level binding energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09439
name: dispersion plane
def: "Plane containing the source\, detector\, incident\, and specularly reflected X–ray beams in X-ray reflectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09440
name: total reflection of X-rays
def: "Condition where the glancing angle for the incident X-rays is at or less than the critical angle and the X-rays are either reflected or absorbed in a region very close to the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09441
name: wave vector
def: "Vector in reciprocal space describing the direction of propagation of X-rays and equal in magnitude to \\\(2\\uppi/\\lambda\\\)\, where \\\(\\lambda\\\) is the wavelength of the X-rays."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09442
name: X-ray jump ratio
def: "X-ray absorption coefficient at an energy just above an absorption edge divided by that at an energy just below the edge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09443
name: observed interface width
def: "In secondary-ion mass spectrometry\, Auger electron spectroscopy\, and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy\, distance over which a change in signal intensity from \\\(\\pu\{16\\%\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{84\\%\}\\\)\, or from \\\(\\pu\{84\\%\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{16\\%\}\\\)\, is measured at the junction of two dissimilar matrices\, the thicknesses of which are more than six times that distance"
synonym: "depth resolution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09444
name: surface plasmon resonance
def: "Resonant interaction between the natural frequency of a surface plasmon and incident light of the same frequency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09445
name: adhesion promoter
def: "Interfacial agent comprised of molecules possessing two or more functional groups\, each of which exhibits preferential interactions with the various types of phase domains in a composite."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09446
name: aerial image
def: "Image of a mask pattern that is projected onto the photoresist coated wafer by an optical system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09447
name: annealing
def: "Thermal treatment of a solid polymer material at a fixed or changing temperature that leads to desired changes in its physical structure and properties without involving complete melting or dissolution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09448
name: solvent vapor annealing
def: "Annealing via the exposure of a substrate to solvent vapor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09449
name: thermal annealing
def: "Annealing in which a heating step is used to activate chemical processes in a resist that depend on diffusion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09450
name: anti-reflective coating
def: "Material used in lithography or in other technologies that is applied to a surface in order to reduce reflection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09451
name: aspect ratio
def: "Proportional relationship between the width and height of an image produced by exposing a resist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09452
name: atomic force microscopy
def: "High-resolution scanning probe microscopy that maps a surface with a demonstrated resolution of the order of fractions of a nanometer."
synonym: "scanning force microscopy (SFM)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09453
name: base quencher
def: "Base added to a chemical amplification resist to neutralize low levels of acid that might diffuse into nominally unexposed regions and cause blur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09454
name: block copolymer lithography
def: "Process in which a thin film of block copolymer is formed into a regular nanoscale pattern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09455
name: bottom anti-reflective coating
def: "Layer placed between a photoresist and a substrate to prevent reflection from the latter resulting in a reduction in photoresist pattern quality\, as characterized by resolution\, line edge roughness\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09456
name: bottom-up lithography
def: "Pattern formation using self-assembly processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09457
name: basting solvent
def: "Solvent in which a resist or additional thin-film component \(typically a BARC\) is dissolved for the coating process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09458
name: clearance dose
def: "Dose of radiation required to completely remove a positive-tone resist from a large area."
synonym: "clearing dose" []
synonym: "dose to clear" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09459
name: contact hole
def: "Hole in a layer of a semiconductor device enabling contact with an underlying semiconductor layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09460
name: contrast
def: "Maximum of the absolute value of the gradient of a contrast curve taken as a measure of the ability of a resist to differentiate the regions that have been exposed to radiation from those that have not."
synonym: "contrast ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09461
name: contrast curve
def: "Plot of the fraction of the original thickness that remains after the development of a uniformly irradiated resist versus the logarithm of the incident exposure dose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09462
name: critical dimension
def: "Length of the smallest geometrical features that can be formed in a lithographic process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09463
name: dark loss
def: "Unwanted change in the solubility of a resist that takes place in the absence of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09464
name: defect
def: "Imperfection introduced during pattern formation."
synonym: "image defect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09465
name: defect-free pattern
def: "Imaged resist without imperfections in pattern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09466
name: deprotection
def: "Removal of a protecting group which leads to a change in the solubility of a polymeric resist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09467
name: depth of focus
def: "Tolerance of the placement of the image plane in relation to image quality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09468
name: development
def: "Process in which a developer is used to generate an image by removing the soluble portion of the latent pattern of an exposed resist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09469
name: dip-pen nanolithography
def: "Direct write\, tip-based patterning method that uses the tip of an AFM probe to act as a pen and deposit molecules on a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09470
name: directed self-assembly
def: "Process in which a thin film of block copolymer is formed into a regular nanoscale pattern on chemically or topographically nanopatterned surfaces \(or a combination thereof\)."
synonym: "DSA" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09471
name: dislocation
def: "Defect\, or irregularity\, within an ordered structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09472
name: dissolution inhibitor
def: "Compound that\, when included in a resist formulation\, slows the rate of dissolution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09473
name: dissolution promoter
def: "Compound that\, when included in a resist formulation\, increases the rate of dissolution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09474
name: dissolution rate
def: "Rate at which resist is removed during development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09475
name: doctor blading
def: "Coating of solution or slurry drawn to uniform film by use of a knife edge or doctor blade."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09476
name: doping \(p- and n-type\)
def: "Process that increases the thermal-equilibrium concentration of free charge carriers in a material to augment its electrical conductivity using chemical agents or additives \(i.e. dopants\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09478
name: double exposure
def: "Lithographic process requiring two exposures to radiation for a single resist layer to form a finished pattern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09479
name: double patterning
def: "Printing of two distinct lines at the edges of a single resist line pattern by means of a deposit-etch process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09480
name: dry deposition
def: "Assembly of a layer on a substrate by the use of a solvent-free process\, such as vapor deposition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09481
name: dry development
def: "Process in which a portion of a latent pattern in an exposed resist is removed to form the image without the use of a liquid developer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09482
name: dry-etch resistance
def: "Facility of an exposed resist to withstand etching conditions\, usually a plasma\, determined in comparison to a reference standard."
synonym: "etch resistance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09483
name: dual-tone resist
def: "Photoresist capable of being processed in either positive tone \(aqueous base development\) or negative tone \(organic solvent development\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09484
name: edge acuity
def: "Degree to which the edge of an image appears sharp and precise."
synonym: "edge sharpness" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09485
name: embossing
def: "Creating a pattern by pressing a master die into a malleable material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09486
name: epitaxial crystallization
def: "Growth of crystals on other crystals involving a precisely defined mutual orientation of their lattices."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09487
name: epitaxy
def: "Deposition of a crystalline over layer on a crystalline substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09488
name: etch
def: "Treatment to remove substrate to a requisite depth from those areas of a surface no longer protected following the exposure and development of an overlaid resist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09489
name: dry etching
def: "Process of removal of material\, typically of a masked pattern on a semiconductor\, by exposing it to a bombardment of ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09490
name: etch rate
def: "Rate of material removal using either a wet or a dry-etch process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09491
name: wet etching
def: "Process of removal of material\, typically of a masked pattern on a semiconductor\, by exposing it to a corrosive liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09492
name: exposure latitude
def: "Extent to which a light- or radiation-sensitive material can be overexposed or underexposed and still achieve acceptable imaging."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09493
name: focused ion beam
def: "Technique used for the site-specific analysis\, deposition\, ablation\, and micromachining of materials down to dimensions of \\\(\\pu\{10\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{15 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09494
name: hard bake
def: "High temperature annealing to solidify any resist material remaining after development in order to provide a durable protective layer for etching."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09495
name: image
def: "Pattern produced in or on a substrate during a lithographic process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09496
name: developed image
def: "Visible image produced by an effect of radiation on a substrate followed by subsequent lithographic development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09497
name: image blur
def: "Broadening during the development of a lithographic image."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09498
name: immersion fluid
def: "High refractive index liquid medium used in immersion lithography that replaces the air gap between the lens and the resist surface in conventional photolithography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09499
name: lift-off process
def: "Method whereby a film\, usually metallic\, deposited on a patterned substrate after etch processing\, is removed during resist strip\, which leaves only that portion of the film that is deposited directly onto the exposed regions of the substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09500
name: line edge roughness
def: "Variations along one edge of a linear resist feature over an extended length."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09501
name: line width roughness
def: "Variations in the width of a linear resist feature over an extended length."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09502
name: line-space patterning
def: "Developed lithographic pattern comprising alternating lines with spaces between."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09503
name: lithography
def: "Process whereby an image is created on an apparently planar surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09504
name: direct-write lithography
def: "Pattern created without recourse to irradiation through a mask of the image."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09505
name: electron beam lithography
def: "Lithography performed by exposing a resist to a beam of electrons."
synonym: "e-beam lithography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09506
name: electron-beam direct-write lithography
def: "Electron beam lithography whereby the pattern is written directly onto the photoresist without the use of a mask."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09507
name: extreme ultraviolet lithography
def: "Photolithography performed by exposing a resist to extreme ultraviolet \(\\\(\\pu\{13 nm\}\\\)\) radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09508
name: immersion lithography
def: "Photolithography that provides resolution enhancement by immersing a resist-coated substrate in a fluid during the exposure process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09509
name: imprint lithography
def: "Patterns created by mechanical deformation of an imprint resist and a subsequent curing process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09510
name: nanoimprint lithography
def: "Subset of imprint lithography that allows for the fabrication of nanometer scale patterns."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09511
name: interference lithography
def: "Technique for patterning regular arrays of fine features without the use of complex optical systems or photomasks."
synonym: "holographic lithography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09512
name: magnetolithography
def: "Lithography for direct patterning of surfaces based on the application of a magnetic field to the substrate using paramagnetic metal masks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09513
name: maskless lithography
def: "Lithography that does not need a mask of the image to make a pattern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09514
name: microlithography
def: "Lithography or pattern formation at a resolution of the order of microns."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09515
name: nanolithography
def: "Umbrella term that is used to describe all lithographic techniques that allow for the fabrication of nanometer scale patterns."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09516
name: nanosphere lithography
def: "Lithography in which material is deposited into the interstices of a 2D array of nanospheres that are subsequently removed to form a pattern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09517
name: photolithography
def: "Lithography performed by exposing a resist to an intense parallel light beam through an image mask."
synonym: "optical lithography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09518
name: scanning-probe lithography
def: "Lithographic method in which a microscopic or nanoscopic stylus is moved mechanically across a surface to form a pattern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09519
name: soft lithography
def: "Family of techniques for fabricating or replicating structures using elastomeric stamps\, molds\, and conformable photomasks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09520
name: X-ray lithography
def: "Lithography performed by exposing a resist to X-radiation through an image mask."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09521
name: 3D lithography
def: "Lithography performed in three dimensions\, typically carried out using nanoimprint patterning or 2-photon lithography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09522
name: 2-photon lithography
def: "Photolithography within a thick photoresist film\, within which the photochemically-induced solubility change results from the simultaneous absorption of two photons."
synonym: "two-photon lithography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09523
name: mask
def: "Transparent substrate pre-patterned with the desired image that is inserted into the incident beam in photo- and X-ray lithography."
synonym: "photomask" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09524
name: magnetic mask
def: "Mask with a pattern defined by a paramagnetic metal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09525
name: mask error enhancement factor
def: "Increase in error of pattern edge location during pattern formation when the wavelength of light used to expose a resist is greater than the feature size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09526
name: microcontact printing
def: "Form of soft lithography that uses micron-scale and smaller relief patterns on a master elastomer stamp to form images on the surface of a substrate through conformal contact."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09527
name: microfabrication
def: "Process whereby structures of micrometre scale and smaller are fabricated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09528
name: microfluidics
def: "Multi-disciplinary subject concerned with the behavior\, precise control\, and manipulation of fluids that are geometrically constrained within a small\, typically sub-millimeter\, scale channel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09529
name: micromolding
def: "Form of soft lithography that uses the relief patterns in a master template to make 3-dimensional structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09530
name: molecular glass
def: "Assembly of small molecules that vitrify\, rather than crystallize\, when cooled."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09531
name: molecular logic gate
def: "Molecule that acts as a device performing a logical operation based on one or more physical or chemical inputs and a single output."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09532
name: molecular template
def: "Molecular structure that transfers a pattern to or organizes another molecular scale structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09533
name: molecular wires
def: "Molecular-scale objects that conduct electrical current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09534
name: Moore’s law
def: "Empirical observation by Gordon E. Moore\, co-founder of Intel\, that\, over the history of computing hardware\, the number of transistors on integrated circuits doubles approximately every two years."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09535
name: nanodevice
def: "Device in which the manufactured size is between \\\(\\pu\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09536
name: nanoelectromechanical systems
def: "Devices that integrate electrical and mechanical functionality at the nanoscale. A miniaturization of microelectromechanical systems \(MEMS\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09537
name: nanoelectronics
def: "Application of nanotechnology to microelectronics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09538
name: nanoparticle
def: "Particle of any shape with an equivalent diameter of approximately \\\(\\pu\{1\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09539
name: nanopattern
def: "Patterns on a substrate with lesser dimensions of between \\\(\\pu\{1\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09540
name: nanophotonics
def: "Study of the interaction of light with particles or substances on a scale less than the wavelength of the light."
synonym: "nano-optics" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09541
name: nanostructure
def: "Structure or feature of size between \\\(\\pu\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09542
name: nanotechnology
def: "Manipulation of matter on an atomic and molecular scale."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09543
name: near-field scanning optical microscopy
def: "Microscopy applied to the investigation of nanostructures that breaks the farfield resolution limit by exploiting the properties of evanescent waves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09544
name: negative-tone development
def: "Process for revealing the latent image in a resist in which the exposed regions are less soluble in the developer than the unexposed regions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09545
name: normalized image log slope
def: "Measure of the information content of the aerial image and represents an energy \(intensity\) gradient at the position of the nominal line edge."
synonym: "normalized image slope" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09546
name: pattern collapse
def: "Destruction of pattern during and after development caused by the surface tension of the development solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09547
name: pattern transfer
def: "Transfer of the pattern of a developed lithographic image to an underlying substrate by processes such as etching."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09548
name: photoacid generator
def: "Compound that on exposure to radiation gives rise to a proton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09549
name: photoactive compound
def: "Compound that on exposure to radiation will release an active ionic or radical species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09550
name: photonic crystal
def: "Periodic structures consisting of a dielectric material that shows strong interaction with light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09551
name: photonics
def: "Science and technology of the production\, transmission\, absorption\, sensing\, and direction of light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09552
name: pitch division
def: "Method for making a lithographic pattern smaller than that deemed possible given the wavelength of the imaging radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09553
name: planarising layer
def: "Coating used to create a level surface during lithographic processing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09554
name: plasma enhanced chemical vapor deposition
def: "Process to deposit a solid film on a substrate resulting from plasma-induced reactions of precursor compounds\, either in the gaseous state or on the film surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09555
name: plasma etch
def: "Plasma processing used in the fabrication of integrated circuits that generates volatile products from the elements of the material etched and the reactive species generated by the plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09556
name: positive-tone development
def: "Process for revealing the latent image in a resist in which the exposed regions are more soluble in the developer than are the unexposed regions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09557
name: post-apply bake
def: "Thermal annealing step following the coating of a substrate with a resist material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09558
name: post-exposure bake
def: "Thermal annealing step following the exposure of a resist to imaging radiation."
synonym: "post bake" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09559
name: post-exposure delay
def: "Pause between successive patterning steps in lithographic processing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09560
name: protecting group
def: "Non-reactive group temporarily used to transform a reactive group into one that does not react under conditions where the non-protected group reacts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09561
name: proximity effect
def: "Widening of the exposure dose distribution\, and hence the developed pattern\, caused by the scattering of the primary beam electrons within the resist and substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09562
name: raster scan
def: "Rectangular pattern of line-by-line scanning for image capture used in electron beam and ion-beam lithography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09563
name: reactive ion etching
def: "Etching technology used in microfabrication that uses chemically reactive plasma to remove material from a substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09564
name: resist
def: "Material\, usually a polymer\, that when irradiated either undergoes a marked change in solubility in a given solvent or is ablated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09565
name: chemically amplified resist
def: "Resist that undergoes solubility change upon exposure of photoacid generator\, followed by thermally activated acid catalyzed deprotection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09566
name: electron beam resist
def: "Resist optimized for use under electron beam irradiation."
synonym: "e-beam resist" []
synonym: "electron-beam resist" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09567
name: ion beam resist
def: "Resist optimized for use under ion beam irradiation."
synonym: "ion-beam resist" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09568
name: multi-component resist
def: "Resist comprising more than one active component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09569
name: X-ray resist
def: "Resist optimized for use under X-ray irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09570
name: resist profile
def: "Topography of a resist pattern in cross section."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09571
name: resist strip
def: "Subsequent removal of the remaining resist that covers the protected regions of a substrate during development and etch processing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09572
name: shot noise
def: "Noise which originates from the discrete nature of elementary charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09573
name: silylation
def: "Introduction of a substituted silyl group \(\\\(\\ce\{R3Si\}\\\)\) to an alcohol\, carboxylic acid\, amine\, thiol\, or phosphate\, commonly as a protecting group."
synonym: "silation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09574
name: soft bake
def: "Short\, low temperature annealing step following the coating of a wafer with a photoresist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09575
name: stamp
def: "Three-dimensional template comprising the negative pattern used to leave an impression on the surface of a malleable material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09576
name: supramolecular assembly
def: "Molecular system comprising two or more self-assembled molecular\, or molecular and ionic\, entities held together by means of non-covalent intermolecular binding interactions."
synonym: "supramolecular species" []
synonym: "supramolecular structure" []
synonym: "supramolecule" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09577
name: surface roughness
def: "Measure of the texture of a surface that is quantified by vertical deviations from its ideal location."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09578
name: swing curve
def: "Sinusoidal variation in the lithographic clearance dose arising from changes in the phase difference between incoming and outgoing radiation that is induced by variations in resist thickness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09579
name: topcoat
def: "Protective layer spun directly on top of a resist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09580
name: top-down patterning
def: "Pattern formation using lithographic \(also embossing\) methods."
synonym: "top-down processing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09581
name: top-surface imaging
def: "Lithographic process in which the exposed region of resist is silylated and the image then formed through reactive oxygen etch."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09582
name: vapor deposition
def: "Growth of solid materials on a substrate by deposition from the gas phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09583
name: vector scan
def: "Pattern formation using a directed electron beam or ion beam that skips from region to region requiring exposure but is otherwise turned off."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09584
name: rotated Fischer projection
def: "Projection formula for macromolecules in which horizontal lines denote polymer backbone bonds. At each individual backbone carbon atom\, horizontal lines represent bonds directed below the projection plane and vertical lines represent bonds directed above that plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09585
name: m diad
def: "Pair of adjacent constitutionally equivalent stereogenic units that are of like configuration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09586
name: r diad
def: "Pair of adjacent constitutionally equivalent stereogenic units that are of unlike configuration. Figure 2\: Diagrammatic representations of the configurational repeating unit or stereorepeating unit \(upper\)\, and the diad sequence \(lower\) for isotactic poly\(oxypropylene\)\, both as a rotated Fischer projections \(left\) and as a zigzag projection \(right\). \[image2\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09587
name: heterotactic macromolecule
def: "Regular macromolecule having a configurational repeating unit comprising four configurational base units\, the first two stereogenic units of which are identical and enantiomorphic to the last two."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09588
name: asymmetric enantiomer-differentiating polymerization
def: "Asymmetric polymerization in which\, starting from a mixture of enantiomeric monomer molecules\, only one enantiomer is polymerized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09589
name: asymmetric polymerization
def: "Polymerization that proceeds in an unsymmetrical manner in terms of chirality under the influence of chiral features present in one or more components of the reaction system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09590
name: Bernoullian distribution
def: "The Bernoullian distribution is a discrete distribution having two possible outcomes labelled by \\\(n = 0\\\) and \\\(n = 1\\\)\, in which \\\(n = 1\\\) \('success'\) occurs with probability $p$ and \\\(n = 0\\\) \('failure'\) occurs with probability \\\(q = 1 - p\\\)\, where $0 \\lt p \\lt 1$. It is a special case of a binomial distribution corresponding to a random distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09591
name: bioassimilation
def: "Conversion of a substance into biomass by biochemical processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09592
name: biobased
def: "Composed of or derived from\, in whole or in part\, biological products derived from the biomass \(including plant\, animal\, and marine or forestry materials\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09593
name: biodegradable
def: "Qualifier for a substance or device that undergoes biodegradation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09594
name: bioerosion
def: "Surface degradation resulting from the action of cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09595
name: biorelated
def: "Qualifier for actions or substances that are connected to living systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09596
name: bioresorbable
def: "Qualifier used to indicate that a compound or a device is bioresorbed\, i.e.\, totally eliminated or bioassimilated by an animal or a human body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09597
name: composting
def: "Process of biological decomposition of organic matter performed by microorganisms\, mostly bacteria and fungi."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09598
name: degradable
def: "Qualifier to a substance that can undergo physical and/or chemical deleterious changes of some properties\, especially of integrity\, under stress conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09599
name: heterogeneous degradation
def: "Degradation or biodegradation occurring at different rates depending on the location within a matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09600
name: homogeneous degradation
def: "Degradation that occurs at the same rate regardless of the location within a polymeric item."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09601
name: stereocopolymer
def: "Copolymer formed of constitutional repeating units that are constitutionally identical but configurationally different."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09602
name: branch constitutional repeating unit
def: "Constitutional repeating unit of connectivity three or more connected at least to three other constitutional repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09603
name: core unit
def: "Constitutional unit in a dendrimer molecule\, from which the dendron\(s\) emanate\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09604
name: degree of branching
def: "Parameter characterizing the branched architecture of a hyperbranched polymer\, which is equal to unity for a polymer that contains no linear or semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit and is equal to 0 for a polymer that contains no dendritic or semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09605
name: dendrimer molecule
def: "Molecule consisting of one or more dendrons emanating from a single constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09606
name: dendritic block macromolecule
def: "Block macromolecule in which at least one block is a dendron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09607
name: dendritic constitutional repeating unit
def: "Constitutional repeating unit of connectivity $c \\ge 3$ connected to $c$ other constitutional repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09608
name: dendritic graft macromolecule
def: "Graft macromolecule in which at least one of the grafts contains a dendron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09609
name: dendritic macromolecule
def: "Macromolecule containing at least one dendron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09610
name: dendritic polymer
def: "Substance composed of dendritic macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09611
name: dendron
def: "Part of a molecule with only one free valence\, comprising exclusively dendritic and terminal constitutional repeating units and in which each path from the free valence to any end-group comprises the same number of constitutional repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09612
name: dendron direction
def: "For a constitutional repeating unit of a dendron\, the direction from the free valence to the end-groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09613
name: focal unit
def: "Core unit of a dendrimer molecule that comprises only one dendron"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09614
name: generation
def: "Set of constitutional repeating units separated from the free valence of a dendron by the same number of constitutional repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09615
name: hyperbranched macromolecule
def: "Highly branched macromolecule\, the constitutional repeating units of which are in substantial part branch constitutional repeating units and terminal constitutional repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09616
name: hyperbranched oligomer
def: "Substance composed of hyperbranched oligomer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09617
name: hyperbranched oligomer molecule
def: "Highly branched oligomer molecule\, the constitutional repeating units of which are in substantial part branch constitutional repeating units and terminal constitutional repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09618
name: hyperbranched polymer
def: "Substance composed of hyperbranched macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09619
name: irregular dendrimer
def: "Substance composed of irregular dendrimer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09620
name: irregular dendrimer molecule
def: "Dendrimer molecule that contains either different types of regular dendrons or at least one irregular dendron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09621
name: irregular dendron
def: "Dendron composed of either different types of constitutional units\, different types of end-groups or any combination of these."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09622
name: linear constitutional repeating unit
def: "Constitutional repeating unit connected to exactly two other constitutional repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09623
name: number of generations
def: "Number of constitutional repeating units on the path from the free valence to any end-group of a dendron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09624
name: number of generations
def: "Number of generation of the dendrons of a regular dendrimer molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09625
name: number of pseudo-generations
def: "Number of generations of the hypothetical dendrimer molecule that would present the same ratio of constitutional repeating units to core unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09626
name: regular dendrimer
def: "Substance composed of regular dendrimer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09627
name: regular dendrimer molecule
def: "Dendrimer molecule comprising only identical regular dendrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09628
name: regular dendron
def: "Dendron composed of only one type of constitutional repeating units and of only one type of end-groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09629
name: semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit
def: "Constitutional repeating unit of connectivity \\\(c \\ge 4\\\) connected to more than two but less than \\\(c\\\) other constitutional repeating units.Example of a hyperbranched macromolecule part with a semi-dendritic constitutional repeating unit of connectivity \\\(c = 4\\\) \(circled\)\, connected to three other constitutional repeating units and to one hydroxymethyl end-group. The preferred constitutional repeating unit is chosen such that the branch points are at its end in the dendron direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09630
name: terminal constitutional repeating unit
def: "Constitutional repeating unit connected to only one other constitutional repeating unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09631
name: chalcogen bond
def: "Net attractive interaction between an electrophilic region associated with a chalcogen atom in a molecular entity and a nucleophilic region in another\, or the same\, molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09632
name: bioanalytical chemistry
def: "Sub-field of analytical chemistry dealing with analysis of biomolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09633
name: biomolecule
def: "Molecule of biological origin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09634
name: biomacromolecule
def: "Macromolecule \(including proteins\, nucleic acids\, and polysaccharides\) formed by living organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09635
name: amniotic fluid
def: "Fluid that surrounds the foetus in the amniotic sac."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09636
name: blood
def: "Fluid that circulates through the heart\, arteries\, capillaries\, and veins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09637
name: blood cell
def: "Any cellular element of the blood\, including erythrocytes\, leucocytes\, and platelets."
synonym: "haematopoietic cell" []
synonym: "hematocyte" []
synonym: "hemocyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09638
name: blood cell count
def: "Number of red blood cells and white blood cells per unit volume in a specimen of venous blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09639
name: blood group
def: "Red cell phenotypes classified by their antigenic structural characteristics\, which are under the control of various allelic genes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09640
name: body fluids
def: "Liquids originating from the bodies of living or dead humans\, animals\, and other organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09641
name: cerebrospinal fluid
def: "Clear\, colourless fluid that fills spaces within and around the central nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09642
name: cord blood
def: "Blood contained in the vessels of the umbilical cord at the time of birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09643
name: lymph
def: "Yellowish\, slightly basic fluid circulating through the lymphatic system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09644
name: occult blood
def: "Blood present in such small amounts that its presence can be ascertained only by chemical analysis or by spectroscopic or microscopic examination; particularly the blood found in stools."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09645
name: peripheral blood
def: "Blood obtained from parts of the body that are located at some distance from the heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09646
name: protein-free filtrate
def: "Sample of blood\, serum\, or plasma from which all proteins have been removed by chemical or physical denaturation\, dialysis\, ultrafiltration\, or solvent extraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09647
name: saliva
def: "Clear\, viscous secretion from the parotid\, submaxillary\, sublingual\, and smaller mucous glands in the cavity of the mouth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09648
name: skin
def: "Soft outer covering of vertebrates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09649
name: sperm
def: "Seminal fluid containing male reproductive cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09650
name: sweat
def: "Fluid excreted by the sweat glands in the skin of mammals during perspiration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09651
name: tears
def: "Clear salty liquid that is secreted by the lachrymal gland of the eye to lubricate the surface between the eyeball and eyelid and to wash away irritants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09652
name: tissue
def: "Group of biological cells that perform a specific function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09653
name: urine
def: "Fluid containing metabolic products that is excreted by the kidneys\, stored in the bladder\, and normally discharged by way of the urethra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09654
name: bioinformatics
def: "Discipline encompassing the development and utilisation of computational tools to store\, analyse\, and interpret biological data."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09655
name: biological recognition element
def: "Component of biological origin that translates information from the biochemical domain into a physical or chemical domain with a defined selectivity."
synonym: "biochemical receptor" []
synonym: "biocomponent (at a biosensor)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09656
name: enzymatic decomposition
def: "Decomposition of organic materials \(e.g. starch\, sugars\, proteins\, lignin\, hemicelluloses\, etc.\) with specific enzymes or microbes in which the enzyme converts a high relative molecular mass compound into lower relative molecular mass species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09658
name: lysis
def: "Breaking down of a cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09659
name: microarray
def: "Two-dimensional arrangement of a device for analysis of sub-microlitre sample volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09660
name: sample quality control
def: "Test of the purity of the sample that will be subjected to detailed biochemical analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09661
name: affinity
def: "Strength of association between a single antibody and a single epitope of antigen."
synonym: "binding affinity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09662
name: agglutination inhibition
def: "Type of agglutination in which particulate and soluble antigen compete for soluble antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09663
name: antigenic determinant
def: "Part of the structure of an antigen molecule that is responsible for specific interaction with antibody molecules evoked by the same or a similar antigen."
synonym: "epitome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09664
name: avidity
def: "Net affinity of all binding sites of all antibodies in the antiserum under specified physicochemical reaction conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09665
name: binding capacity
def: "Amount of a ligand that binds to a receptor expressed in operational units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09666
name: blocking
def: "Coating of an immunospecific surface \(e.g. serum\, tissues\, cells\, biological macromolecules\, biosensor surface\) to form an active cover with selectivity or an inert layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09667
name: bound/free ratio
def: "Amount of analyte bound by a label divided by amount of unbound \(free\) analyte"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09668
name: complement
def: "Array of serum proteins \(some of which are enzymes\) that become sequentially activated after the first member of the series is activated by either antigen-antibody complexes or microbial products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09669
name: enzyme conjugate
def: "Conjugate that has an enzyme bound covalently."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09670
name: hemagglutination
def: "Agglutination reactions in which the particles used are erythrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09671
name: immunoblotting
def: "Technique that uses antibodies \(or other specific ligands in related techniques\) to identify target proteins among a number of unrelated protein species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09672
name: immunochemical specificity
def: "The extent to which the immunoassay responds only to \(all subsets of\) a specific analyte and not to other substances of similar structure that are present in the sample."
synonym: "specificity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09673
name: immunocomplex
def: "Product of an antigen-antibody reaction that may also contain components of the complement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09674
name: immunoglobulin class
def: "Classification of immunoglobulins based on antigenic and structural differences of the heavy peptide chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09675
name: immunoprecipitation
def: "Isolation and concentration of an antigen by forming a precipitate with an antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09676
name: immunoreactivity
def: "Kinetic property of the immune response caused by an antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09677
name: immunosensor
def: "Biosensor where one of the immunointeracting partners \(an antibody or an antigen as the analyte\) is immobilised on the surface of a physicochemical transducer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09678
name: indirect agglutination
def: "Agglutination in which antigen is first immobilised artificially onto the particulate surfaces\, either by physical adsorption or by chemical and/or immunochemical linkers."
synonym: "passive agglutination" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09679
name: lateral flow test
def: "Migration of the sample zone driven by capillary forces in a porous carrier strip."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09680
name: monoclonal
def: "Arising from a single clone of cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09681
name: monospecificity
def: "Immunoreactivity of an antiserum with its designated antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09682
name: polyclonal
def: "Arising from multiple\, different clones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09683
name: reverse passive agglutination
def: "Modification of the indirect agglutination assay\, where the particles are coated with antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09684
name: sandwich immunoassay
def: "Immunoassay using chemical or immunochemical binding of analyte to a solid phase carrying antibody and the immunochemical binding of a second \(labelled\) reagent to the analyte."
synonym: "two-site, noncompetitive immunoassay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09685
name: amplification of deoxyribonucleic acid
def: "Process of producing multiple copies of a deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) sequence by the repeated copying of a DNA segment."
synonym: "amplification of DNA" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09686
name: base pair
def: "Pair of two nucleobases\, consisting of one purine and one pyrimidine base\, held together by hydrogen bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09687
name: base sequence analysis
def: "Method for determining the nucleotide sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09688
name: basic local alignment search tool
def: "Computer algorithm that finds regions of local similarity between deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) or protein sequences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09689
name: cloning
def: "Specialised deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) technology for the production of multiple exact copies of a single gene or another segment of DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09690
name: cloning vector
def: "Deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) molecule into which another DNA fragment of appropriate size can be integrated without the loss of its capacity for self-replication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09691
name: complementary sequence
def: "Nucleotide sequence that can form a double-stranded structure with another deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) sequence by following base-pairing \(see base pair\) rules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09692
name: complete genome sequence
def: "High-quality\, low-error\, gap-free deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) sequence of an entire genome of an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09693
name: damage to deoxyribonucleic acid
def: "Alteration in the deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) chemical structure resulting from interactions with physical or chemical agents occurring in the environment\, generated in the organisms as by-products of metabolism\, or used as therapeutics."
synonym: "DNA damage" []
synonym: "damage to DNA" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09694
name: deletion
def: "Loss of a part \(one or more base pairs\) of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09695
name: deoxyribose
def: "Type of pentose sugar that is incorporated in the structure of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09696
name: deoxyribonucleic acid fragmentation
def: "Breaking and/or separation of large deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) molecules into smaller pieces \(fragments\)."
synonym: "DNA fragmentation" []
synonym: "fragmentation of DNA" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09697
name: deoxyribonucleic acid fragmentation index
def: "Fraction of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) having fragmented DNA."
synonym: "DNA fragmentation index" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09698
name: deoxyribonucleic acid integrity index
def: "Ratio of longer fragments to shorter deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\)."
synonym: "DNA integrity index" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09699
name: deoxyribonucleic acid mismatch
def: "Defect in the double stranded deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) structure in which the DNA double helix contains one \(or more\) non-standard base pairs."
synonym: "DNA mismatch" []
synonym: "mismatch in DNA" []
synonym: "multi-base mismatch" []
synonym: "single mismatch" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09700
name: deoxyribonucleic acid profiling
def: "Identification of individuals by matching characteristics of their genome deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) sequence."
synonym: "DNA profiling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09701
name: deoxyribonucleic acid replication
def: "Synthesis of new deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) strands using existing DNA as a template."
synonym: "DNA replication" []
synonym: "replication of DNA" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09702
name: deoxyribonucleic acid sequencing
def: "Determination of nucleotide sequence \(the deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) primary structure\)."
synonym: "DNA sequencing" []
synonym: "sequencing of DNA" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09703
name: double helix
def: "The twisted shape of two complementary strands of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09704
name: endonuclease
def: "Enzyme that cleaves nucleic acids by hydrolysing phosphodiester bonds between nucleotides within a nucleic acid molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09705
name: exogenous deoxyribonucleic acid
def: "Deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) molecule of foreign origin that has been introduced into an organism."
synonym: "exogenous DNA" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09706
name: exonuclease
def: "Enzyme that cleaves nucleotides from free ends of a linear nucleic acid molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09707
name: gene expression
def: "Process for converting a gene’s coded information into biomolecules present and operating in the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09708
name: Northern blot
def: "Technique involving hybridization of nucleic acids  used to study gene expression at the level of ribonucleic acid \(RNA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09709
name: nucleic acid aptamers
def: "Single stranded \(ss\) oligonucleotides \(mainly deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) or ribonucleic acid \(RNA\)\) originating from in vitro selection\, which\, starting from random sequence libraries\, optimise the nucleic acids for high-affinity binding to a given target."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09710
name: nucleobase
def: "Purine or pyrimidine nitrogenous base occurring in deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) or ribonucleic acid \(RNA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09711
name: nucleobase lesion
def: "Product of chemical modification of a nucleobase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09712
name: nucleotide sequence
def: "Order of nucleotides in a deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) or ribonucleic acid \(RNA\) molecule."
synonym: "base sequence" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09713
name: open reading frame
def: "Sequence of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) or ribonucleic acid \(RNA\) nucleotides\, located between the start-code sequence \(initiation codon\) and the stop-code sequence \(termination codon\)\, which is transcribed and translated to generate a protein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09714
name: peptide nucleic acid
def: "Synthetic deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) mimic\, which contains 2-aminoethylglycine linkages instead of the negatively charged phosphodiesteric backbone of oligonucleotides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09715
name: polymerase
def: "Enzyme that catalyses the synthesis of nucleic acids on pre-existing nucleic acid templates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09716
name: primer
def: "Short pre-existing polynucleotide chain attached to a complementary stretch in template deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) to which new deoxyribonucleotides can be added by DNA polymerase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09717
name: purine
def: "Nitrogen-containing\, double-ring\, basic compound that occurs in nucleic acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09718
name: pyrimidine
def: "Nitrogen-containing\, single-ring\, basic compound that occurs in nucleic acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09719
name: quantitative polymerase chain reaction
def: "Polymerase chain reaction to quantify target nucleotide sequences of interest."
synonym: "quantitative PCR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09720
name: real-time polymerase chain reaction
def: "Polymerase chain reaction where data are collected and monitored in real-time as the reaction proceeds."
synonym: "real-time PCR" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09721
name: recombinant deoxyribonucleic acid
def: "Deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) molecules joined together using recombinant deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) technology."
synonym: "recombinant DNA" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09722
name: recombination
def: "Process by which genetic material is broken and joined to other genetic material\, which can lead to a rearrangement of genetic sequences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09723
name: restriction fragment length polymorphism
def: "Variation between individuals in deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) fragment sizes cut by specific restriction enzymes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09724
name: reverse transcriptase
def: "Enzyme used to form a complementary deoxyribonucleic acid sequence \(cDNA\) from a ribonucleic acid \(RNA\) template."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09725
name: Sanger sequencing
def: "Method for determining a nucleotide sequence of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) based on incorporating chain-terminating dideoxynucleotides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09726
name: shotgun method
def: "Method used for determining the order of bases in long DNA using sequencing of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) broken up randomly into numerous small segments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09727
name: single nucleotide polymorphism
def: "Variant of nucleotide sequence of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) in which the purine or pyrimidine base of a single nucleotide is replaced by another such base."
synonym: "point mutation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09728
name: Southern blot
def: "Technique based on deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) hybridization used for the detection of a DNA fragment of specific nucleotide sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09729
name: strand break
def: "Interruption of the sugar-phosphate backbone of a polynucleotide chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09730
name: tandem repeat sequence
def: "Multiple consecutive copies of the same nucleotide sequence on a chromosome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09731
name: telomerase
def: "Enzyme that directs the replication of telomeres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09732
name: telomere
def: "Repetitive nucleotide sequences present at the ends of a chromosome that protect the chromosome from deterioration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09733
name: yeast artificial chromosome
def: "Vector used to clone large deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) fragments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09734
name: alternative splicing
def: "Process occurring during gene expression that allows the generation of multiple protein molecules from one gene. It generally involves the processing of messenger ribonucleic acid \(mRNA\) via elimination of introns."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09735
name: bottom-up proteomics
def: "Approach to identify proteins and characterise their amino acid sequences or post-translational modifications by sequential combination of proteolysis \(enzymatic proteolysis and chemical proteolysis\) prior to mass spectrometric analysis of the resulting proteolytic peptides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09736
name: C-terminal analysis
def: "Chemical method for C-terminal sequencing of proteins and peptides starting at their C-terminus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09737
name: C-terminus
def: "End of an amino acid chain \(protein or polypeptide\)\, terminated by a carboxylic group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09738
name: database searching
def: "Bioinformatics analysis of proteomic mass spectrometric data used in order to identify a protein or its post-translational modification. It results in a list of candidates presented mostly in order of the decreasing statistical significance of the hit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09739
name: difference gel electrophoresis
def: "Measurement method of two-dimensional gel electrophoresis used to study protein expression regulation by the differential staining of multiple gels representing different states of a proteome\, preferably with fluorescent dyes \(e.g. \\\(\\ce\{Cy\{3\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{Cy\{5\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{Cy\{2\}\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09740
name: disulfide bond cleavage
def: "Chemical reduction of protein disulfide bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09741
name: Edman degradation
def: "Method of N-terminal analysis in which the N-terminal amino acid residue is labelled and cleaved from the peptide without affecting other peptide bonds in the peptide chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09742
name: electron-transfer dissociation
def: "Ion fragmentation technique in mass spectrometry-driven proteomics based on the transfer of low energy electrons to the molecules selected for fragmentation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09743
name: electron-transfer/higher-energy collision dissociation
def: "Ion fragmentation technique in mass spectrometry-driven proteomics that is a combination of electron-transfer dissociation \(ETD\) and higher-energy collision dissociation \(HCD\) in Kingdon trap instruments \(Orbitrap™\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09744
name: electrospray ionisation
def: "Soft ionisation technique in mass spectrometry-driven proteomics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09745
name: expression proteomics
def: "Field of study in proteomics that deals with the determination of changes in protein expression and regulation in response to different stimuli\, internal or external. It also includes the analysis of post-translational modifications \(e.g. phosphorylation\, glycosylation\, etc.\)\, their identification\, and their localisation."
synonym: "differential proteomics" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09746
name: functional proteomics
def: "Field of study in proteomics studying protein interactions on different levels\: protein-protein interactions \(interactomics\)\, the formation of protein complexes\, and the interaction of proteins with other biomolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09747
name: gel electrophoresis
def: "Separation technique used to separate or fractionate proteins and larger peptides according to their relative molecular mass using an electric field in polyacrylamide gel \(PAGE\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09748
name: higher-energy collision dissociation
def: "Ion fragmentation technique in mass spectrometry-driven proteomics used mostly in Kingdon trap \(Orbitrap™\) instruments. It does not have low mass cut-off of collision-induced dissociation \(CID\) and therefore is used for the tandem mass tag of stable isotope labels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09749
name: isoelectric focusing
def: "Separation technique used to separate or fractionate proteins and peptides in a gel \(or similar type of media\) according to their isoelectric point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09750
name: mass fingerprinting
def: "Protein analysis where an unknown protein is chemically or enzymatically cleaved into peptide fragments whose masses are determined by mass spectrometry. The peptide masses are compared to peptide masses calculated for known proteins in a database and analysed statistically to determine the best match."
synonym: "mass mapping" []
synonym: "peptide mass fingerprinting" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09751
name: MS/MS data based identification
def: "Mass spectrometric method used for protein/peptide identification based on protein sequencing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09752
name: multidimensional protein identification technology
def: "Non-gel step-wise multidimensional technique for separating and identifying individual components of complex protein and peptide mixtures. It combines fractionation of proteolytic peptides in steps on a strong cation exchanger \(SAX\) using ion exchange chromatography \(IEC\); each step uses higher elution force. Then\, each fraction is separated on reversed phase liquid chromatography and analysed using mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09753
name: N-terminal analysis
def: "Chemical method for N-terminal sequencing of proteins and peptides starting at their N-terminus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09754
name: N-terminus
def: "The end of an amino acid chain \(protein or polypeptide\)\, terminating in a free primary amine group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09755
name: off-gel isoelectric focusing
def: "Separation technique used to fractionate proteins and peptides into discrete liquid fractions according to their isoelectric point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09756
name: off peptide product ions
def: "Protein/peptide sequencing approach using overlapping complementary information on a sequence acquired using proteolysis in bottom-up proteomic analysis by at least two different proteolytic approaches."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09757
name: peptide de novo sequencing
def: "Analytical process that derives an amino acid sequence of a peptide from its tandem mass spectrum without the assistance of a sequence database."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09758
name: peptide fragment ion
def: "Product ion which originates in a peptide precursor ion fragmentation process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09759
name: peptide fragmentation technique
def: "Mass spectrometric technique that serves to generate peptide fragment ions through the fragmentation of peptide precursor ions via different physicochemical processes in an ion source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09760
name: peptide precursor ion
def: "Peptide ion generated during the ionisation of peptides in an ion source or during tandem mass spectrometry of an intact protein in top-down proteomic analysis\, selected for further analysis by means of tandem mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09761
name: peptidomics
def: "Study of the set of peptides in an organism\, its peptidome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09762
name: protein complex
def: "Ensemble of two or more different associated proteins\, each with a specific function\, linked by non-covalent protein-protein interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09763
name: protein complex purification
def: "Set of methods serving to purify the intact protein complex for further analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09764
name: protein database
def: "Collection of protein sequences from several sources\, including translations from annotated genetic coding regions\, as well as original protein and peptide sequences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09765
name: protein folding
def: "Physicochemical process by which a polypeptide folds into its characteristic and functional three-dimensional structure from a random coil."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09766
name: protein fractionation
def: "Methodical approach to simplify a complex mixture of proteomic sample."
synonym: "protein separation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09767
name: protein fragmentation
def: "Process which happens during tandem mass spectrometry of intact proteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09768
name: protein identification
def: "Analytical approach leading to the identity of a protein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09769
name: protein machine
def: "Supramolecular interacting system\, such as a metabolic circuit\, intracellular signal transduction\, or cell-to-cell communication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09770
name: protein purification
def: "Series of processes conducted to isolate either the whole proteome or a subset from a biological sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09771
name: protein quantification
def: "Analytical methods and related techniques to measure the protein content."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09772
name: protein sequencer
def: "Equipment designed for the automatic determination of amino acid sequences of peptides using a method of N-terminal analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09773
name: protein staining
def: "Technique used to visualise proteins separated on electrophoretic slab gels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09774
name: proteoform
def: "All of the different molecular forms in which the protein product of a single gene can be found\, including changes due to genetic variations\, alternatively spliced ribonucleic acid \(RNA\) transcripts\, and post-translational modifications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09775
name: proteogenomics
def: "Field of biological research in-between proteomics and genomics using proteomic information \(mostly derived from mass spectrometry\) to improve gene annotations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09776
name: proteolysis
def: "Protein cleavage to generate proteolytic peptides for bottom-up proteomic analysis\, either enzymatic or chemical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09777
name: proteolytic digestion
def: "Exposure of a sample to proteolytic reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09778
name: proteolytic enzyme
def: "Group of enzymes that cleave the polypeptide into shorter oligopeptides \(peptides\) and amino acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09779
name: proteolytic peptide
def: "Peptide generated via proteolysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09780
name: proteomics
def: "Complex and comprehensive study of proteomes\, particularly proteins\, and the way they are expressed\, modified\, involved in metabolism\, as well as of their interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09781
name: relative quantification
def: "Type of protein/peptide quantification\, where the relative amounts of one protein in different samples is determined\, i.e. the ratio of the content of a protein/peptide in sample 1 to sample 2."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09782
name: stable isotope labelling
def: "Techniques which serve to quantify protein content by means of mass spectrometry. The principle lies in the introduction of a mass tag to an internal standard protein/peptide containing different stable isotopes than the protein/peptide in the sample. Mass resolved signals originating in the internal standard and the sample are read individually and evaluated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09783
name: structural proteomics
def: "Field in proteomics which focuses on higher orders of the protein structure\, mainly tertiary or quaternary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09784
name: targeted proteomics
def: "Approach in proteomics focused on the selected proteins of a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09785
name: tertiary structure of a protein
def: "Arrangement of all atoms of a protein molecule or a subunit of a protein molecule in space\, without regard to its relationship with neighbouring molecules or subunits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09786
name: top-down proteomics
def: "Approach to identify proteins and characterise their amino acid sequences or post-translational modifications either by direct mass spectrometric analysis of an ionised protein molecule or by sequential combination of protein/peptide fragmentation in the ion source or mass analyser with ion trapping mass spectrometers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09787
name: trypsin
def: "Serine protease with highly specific primary endoproteolytic activity that serves to generate proteolytic peptides in bottom-up proteomics analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09788
name: two-dimensional gel electrophoresis
def: "Technique used to separate proteins using isoelectric focusing in a gel. The proteins are then further separated in the second dimension using denaturing PAGE."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09789
name: Western blot
def: "Immunoanalytical technique for the identification of polypeptides separated using gel electrophoresis. The polypeptide is transferred by blotting to a membrane\, where it undergoes specific interaction with primary antibody that is specific to the protein of interest. The peptides are detected as complexes by a label and/or enzymatic reaction\, which may be conjugated to the primary antibody directly or via a separate \(secondary\) antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09790
name: cultivation
def: "Sample pretreatment to degrade complex metabolites in the presence of oxygen; a method for amplifying the concentration of native metabolites in solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09791
name: design of experiment
def: "Metabolomic workflow composed of sampling\, metabolism quenching\, metabolite extraction\, metabolite analyses\, data analysis\, and interpretation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09792
name: endometabolome
def: "Complete set of metabolites inside a cell \(intracellular metabolites\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09793
name: excretion
def: "Process removing compounds and their metabolites from the body\, usually through the kidneys \(via urine\) or in the feces. Unless excretion is complete\, the accumulation of foreign substances can adversely affect normal metabolism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09794
name: exometabolome
def: "Complete set of metabolites outside the cell \(extracellular environment\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09795
name: fingerprinting
def: "Qualitative analysis of the endometabolome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09796
name: fluxomics
def: "In vivo measurements of metabolic fluxes and integration of the results into stoichiometric network models."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09797
name: footprinting
def: "Qualitative analysis of the exometabolome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09798
name: glucuronide
def: "Glycoside of glucuronic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09799
name: metabolic pathway
def: "All metabolic products of the main compound \(e.g. phase 1 and phase 2 metabolites\) with the routes of metabolism and the excreted metabolites in body fluids\, e.g. in urine or blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09800
name: metabolism quenching
def: "Physical or chemical methodology used for rapidly breaking all biochemical processes in metabolomic samples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09801
name: metabolite fingerprinting and footprinting
def: "Unique pattern characterizing the metabolism in a given living phase that does not attempt to identify or quantify all the metabolites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09802
name: metabolite profiling
def: "Strategy to identify and quantify a particular set of metabolites belonging to a specific metabolic pathway or class of compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09803
name: metabolome
def: "Complete set \(from \\\(\\pu\{50\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{1000\}\\\) metabolites\) of organic or inorganic compounds of low relative molecular mass\, present in the cell or organism and which participate in the metabolic reactions required for growth\, maintenance\, and normal functioning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09804
name: metabolomics
def: "Complex and comprehensive study of metabolome; identification and quantification of the small molecule metabolic products of a biological system \(cell\, tissue\, organ\, biological fluid\, or organism\) at a specific point in time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09805
name: metabonomics
def: "Measurement of the dynamic multiparametric metabolic response of a living system to pathophysiological stimuli or genetic modification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09806
name: primary metabolite
def: "Metabolite directly involved in the normal life processes of each cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09807
name: activated nucleotide sugar
def: "Activated form of monosaccharide linked to a nucleotide to be available in a high-energy state for glycosyl transfer."
synonym: "nucleotide sugar" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09808
name: anomeric hydroxy group
def: "Hydroxy group that originates from the aldehydic or ketonic group of a monosaccharide after ring closure."
synonym: "glycosidic hydroxy group" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09809
name: carbohydrate
def: "Monosaccharide\, oligosaccharide and polysaccharide substances\, as well as substances derived from a monosaccharide by reduction of the carbonyl group \(alditol\)\, by oxidation\, including the oxidation of one or more terminal groups of a carboxylic acid\, or by replacement of one or more hydroxy groups by a hydrogen atom\, an amino group\, a thiol group\, or by similar heteroatomic groups. This term also includes derivatives of these of these compounds."
synonym: "glycan" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09810
name: comparative glycoproteomics
def: "Field of glycoproteomics that studies that the changes in the structures and expression levels of glycoproteins under different conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09811
name: comparative proteoglycomics
def: "Field of proteoglycomics that studies the changes in the structures and expression levels of proteoglycans under different conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09812
name: glycan microarray
def: "Glycans attached to a surface of a microarray."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09813
name: glycan profiling
def: "Determination of the diversity of glycan structures expressed on glycoconjugates or cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09814
name: glycan-binding protein
def: "Protein that recognises and binds to specific glycans and mediates their biological function."
synonym: "carbohydrate-binding protein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09815
name: glycoform
def: "Specific form of a glycoprotein containing a distinct glycan structure per glycosylation site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09816
name: glycogene
def: "Gene encoding an enzyme or other functional protein for glycan synthesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09817
name: glycolipid
def: "Compound containing a glycan covalently linked to a lipid aglycon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09818
name: glycome
def: "Complete set of glycans and glycoconjugates produced by a species\, tissue\, or cell in a specified time\, space\, and environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09819
name: glycomics
def: "Complex and comprehensive study of the glycome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09820
name: glycone
def: "Carbohydrate part of a glycoconjugate or a glycoside."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09821
name: glycoprotein
def: "Protein with one or more covalently bound glycans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09822
name: glycoproteomics
def: "Comprehensive analysis of glycoproteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09823
name: glycosidase
def: "Enzyme that catalyses the hydrolysis of glycosidic linkages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09824
name: glycoside
def: "Mixed acetal formally arising by the elimination of water between the hemiacetal or hemiketal hydroxy group of a sugar and a hydroxy group of a second compound. Glycan containing at least one glycosidic linkage to another glycan or an aglycon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09825
name: glycosidic linkage
def: "Linkage of a monosaccharide to another residue via the anomeric hydroxy group."
synonym: "glycosidic bond" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09826
name: glycosyl
def: "Monosaccharide ring without the anomeric oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09827
name: glycosylation
def: "Covalent attachment of a saccharide to a protein\, lipid\, polynucleotide\, saccharide\, or other organic compound\, generally catalysed by enzymes glycosyltransferases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09828
name: glycosyltransferase
def: "Enzyme that catalyses the transfer of a saccharide from a nucleotide di\(mono\)phosphate-activated sugar to an acceptor molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09829
name: hemiacetal
def: "Compound having the general formula \\\(\\ce\{RCH\(OH\)\(OR^\{\\prime\}\)\}\\\) \(\\\(\\ce\{R^\{\\prime\}\} \neq \\ce\{H\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09830
name: hemiketal
def: "Compound having the general formula \\\(\\ce\{R2C\(OH\)OR^\{\\prime\}\}\\\) \(\\\(\\ce\{R^\{\\prime\}\} \neq \\ce\{H\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09831
name: lectin
def: "Saccharide binding protein of non-immune origin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09832
name: lectin microarray
def: "Lectins with distinct saccharide-binding specificity arranged on a microarray platform."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09833
name: proteoglycomics
def: "Systematic study of the structure\, expression\, and function of proteoglycans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09834
name: sugar
def: "Term frequently applied to monosaccharides and lower oligosaccharides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09835
name: alkaline degradation
def: "Treatment with alkaline solution \(e.g. hydrazine\, \\\(\\ce\{KOH\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{NaOH\}\\\)\) useful for the cleavage of fatty acids."
synonym: "alkaline hydrolysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09836
name: core oligosaccharide
def: "Intermediate structural region between the O-polysaccharide and lipid A moiety of lipopolysaccharides \(LPS\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09837
name: fatty acyls
def: "Diverse group of lipid molecules synthesised by chain elongation of an acetyl-CoA primer with malonyl-CoA \(or methylmalonyl-CoA\) groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09838
name: glycerolipids
def: "Esters\, ethers\, and other O-derivatives of glycerol with alkyls or alkenyls\, including mono-\, di-\, and tri-substituted glycerols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09839
name: glycerophospholipids
def: "Lipids with glycerol backbone esterified with one or two fatty acids in position \\\(\\ce\{C-1\}\\\) and/or \\\(\\ce\{C-2\}\\\) and with phosphoric acid at position \\\(\\ce\{C-3\}\\\). The phosphate group is usually esterified with an alcohol \(choline\, ethanolamine\, serine\, inositol\, glycerol\) to form the 'lipid head group'."
synonym: "phospholipids" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09840
name: lipid A
def: "Glycolipid component of lipopolysaccharides \(LPSs\) that is integrated into the bacterial cell wall and is responsible for the beneficial \(immunostimulatory\) or toxic \(immunopathological\) effects of the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09841
name: lipid droplets
def: "Round-shaped intracellular inclusions consisting of hydrophobic cores \(mainly triacylglycerols\, sterol esters\, occasionally free fatty acids or squalene\) covered by a phospholipid monolayer containing a specific set of proteins."
synonym: "adiposomes" []
synonym: "lipid bodies" []
synonym: "lipid particles" []
synonym: "oil bodies" []
synonym: "oleosomes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09842
name: lipid extraction
def: "Isolation of lipids in their native state from samples of different origin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09843
name: lipid profiling
def: "Lipidomics technique conferring unbiased information about total lipids within a particular cell type\, tissue\, or body fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09844
name: lipid remodelling
def: "Changes in the lipid molecules composition after completion of their biosynthesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09845
name: lipidome
def: "Complete set of lipid species and their metabolites present within a particular cell type\, tissue\, or body fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09846
name: lipidomics
def: "Systematic and comprehensive study aimed at the identification\, profiling\, and quantification of lipidome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09847
name: lipooligosaccharide
def: "Lipopolysaccharide produced by specific wild-type bacteria that give a smooth-colony appearance when cultured on agar plates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09848
name: lipopolysaccharidomics
def: "Study of the structure-activity relationship of bacterial lipopolysaccharides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09849
name: membrane bilayer
def: "Planar structure spontaneously formed by amphipathic lipids in a polar \(aqueous\) environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09850
name: membrane rafts
def: "Dynamic\, nanometre-sized\, highly ordered\, membrane domains enriched in sterols and sphingolipids. They contain specific sets of membrane proteins that are sorted into rafts by their specific physical properties and specific lipid-protein interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09851
name: molecular shape of lipids
def: "Specific structure of lipid molecules \(glycerophospholipids\) in aqueous environments and membranes as a function of the ratio between the cross-sections of their polar heads and hydrophobic tails."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09852
name: O-polysaccharide
def: "Part of a lipopolysaccharide that consists of a polysaccharide chain extending outwards from the bacterial cell surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09853
name: plasma lipoproteins
def: "Assemblies of lipids and proteins in blood plasma transporting lipids between tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09854
name: polyunsaturated fatty acids
def: "Fatty acids with more than one double bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09855
name: prenol lipids
def: "Lipids containing a linear chain of isoprenoid groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09856
name: proteolipids
def: "Proteins with covalently linked lipids\, e.g. fatty acid or glycophosphatidylinositol \(GPI\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09857
name: saccharolipids
def: "Biomolecules where fatty acids are directly bound to a saccharide backbone\, thus forming structures similar to glycerolipids or glycerophospholipids\, with saccharide substituting for the glycerol."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09858
name: shotgun lipidomics
def: "Methodological approach based on the direct analysis of the total lipid extracts from cells or tissues without an online chromatographic separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09859
name: signalling lipids
def: "Lipid molecules involved in intracellular signalling and the regulation of cell death\, immune response\, or inflammation."
synonym: "lipid mediators" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09860
name: sphingolipids
def: "Complex family of lipids that contain a sphingoid base backbone and a fatty acyl chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09861
name: sterol lipids
def: "Structural derivatives of tetracyclic alcohols with a hydroxyl group in the position \\\(\\ce\{C-3\}\\\) and an aliphatic side chain in the position \\\(\\ce\{C-17\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09862
name: structural lipids
def: "Lipids \(mainly glycerophospholipids\, sterols and sphingolipids\) involved in the formation of biological membranes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09863
name: targeted lipidomics
def: "Analytical technique focusing on the quantification of specific lipids of interest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09864
name: unsaturation index
def: "Measure of the number of double bonds in fatty acids moieties of phospholipids\, calculated as the fraction of each unsaturated fatty acid multiplied by the number of double bonds within that fatty acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09865
name: capture probe
def: "Specifically-designed single-stranded nucleic acid with a defined \(known\) nucleotide sequence immobilised on a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09866
name: chromatin immunoprecipitation
def: "Method for studying deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\)–protein and/or protein–protein interactions in the context of chromatin\, suitable for the identification of binding sites of proteins in genome DNA\, etc. This method combines chemical cross-linking\, the isolation of specific protein conjugates by immunoprecipitation\, and polymerase chain reaction \(PCR\) amplification of co-precipitated DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09867
name: cross-linking
def: "Formation of a covalent bridge between two biomacromolecules or between two segments of the same biomacromolecule using reactive substances \(e.g. formaldehyde\, bifunctional aldehydes such as glutaraldehyde\, or cisplatin\) or photochemical processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09868
name: fluorescent <em>in situ</em> hybridization
def: "Cytogenetic technique used to detect and localise the presence or absence of specific deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) sequences on chromosomes. It uses fluorescent probes that only bind to those parts of the chromosome with which they show a high degree of sequence complementarity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09869
name: groove binding
def: "Process in which molecules are bound within a minor or major groove of the deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) double helix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09870
name: hybridization assay
def: "Assay with specifically designed single-stranded deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) probe with a defined \(known\) nucleotide sequence usually immobilised on a surface \(in such a case\, the nucleic acid probe is called the capture probe\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09871
name: interactome
def: "Virtual map depicting protein–protein interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09872
name: interactomics
def: "Complex and comprehensive study of the interactions between various proteins and between protein and other molecules within a cell\, such as nucleic acids and metabolites\, as well as the consequences of these interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09873
name: intermolecular interactions
def: "Interactions between \(parts of\) two biomolecule chains \(e.g. base pairing in deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) double helix\, or the interactions between two polypeptide chains\, or between a DNA and a protein molecule\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09874
name: intramolecular interactions
def: "Interactions between \(neighbouring or distant\) segments within the same biomolecule chain \(e.g. base pairing in hairpin or monomolecular tetraplex structures of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) or ribonucleic acid \(RNA\)\, the interactions stabilizing the secondary or tertiary structures of proteins\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09875
name: non-covalent interactions
def: "Interactions between biomolecules or within a biomolecule \(e.g. hydrogen bonds\, electrostatic interactions\, and van der Waals forces\) taking part in the stabilization of proper biomolecule structure and intermolecular interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09876
name: non-specific binding
def: "Binding of a protein to deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) independent of the nucleotide sequence or the conformation of the DNA\, often with a strong contribution of electrostatic interaction between the negatively charged sugar-phosphate backbone of the DNA and basic amino acid residues of the protein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09877
name: nucleic acid–protein interaction
def: "Formation of specific or non-specific complexes between deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) or ribonucleic acid \(RNA\) and proteins\, mediated and stabilised by a network of non-covalent interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09878
name: protein–protein interaction
def: "Association of one protein with one or more other proteins to form either homo- or heteromeric proteins or protein complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09879
name: pull-down assay
def: "Approach used for studying protein–protein interactions analogous to immunoprecipitation\, using \(instead of antibody\) one of the interacting proteins \(the 'bait'\) immobilised at a surface to capture the other protein \(the 'prey'\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09880
name: reporter probe
def: "Specifically designed single-stranded deoxyribonucleic acid \(ssDNA\) \(alternatively\, ribonucleic acid \(RNA\) or a synthetic nucleic acid analogue such as locked nucleic acid \(LNA\) or peptide nucleic acid \(PNA\)\) with a defined \(known\) nucleotide sequence used to detect complementary DNA or RNA sequence in a sample."
synonym: "signalling probe" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09881
name: specific binding
def: "Binding of proteins to specific sites in deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) based on the precise recognition of nucleotide sequence and/or conformation of the DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09882
name: stacking interactions
def: "Attractive interaction between planar aromatic rings\, in nucleic acids being one of the critical forces stabilizing double- and multistranded structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09883
name: Tunel test
def: "Method for the determination of strand breaks and DNA fragmentation in cells based on labelling and quantification of free 3′-OH ends of deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09884
name: two hybrid system
def: "Molecular biology method for studying protein–protein or protein–deoxyribonucleic acid \(DNA\) interactions in cellular \(yeast or bacterial\) milieu."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09885
name: chromatographic bed
def: "Form in which the stationary phase is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09886
name: immobilized stationary phase
def: "Stationary phase which has been immobilized on the support particles or on the inner wall of the column\, e.g. by a physical attraction \(coated stationary phase\)\, by chemical bonding \(bonded stationary phase\)\, or by in situ polymerisation \(cross-linked stationary phase\) after coating."
synonym: "immobilized stationary phase material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09887
name: countercurrent chromatography
def: "Partition chromatography which uses two immiscible liquid phases and no solid support. One liquid acts as the stationary phase and the other as the mobile phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09888
name: enantioselective chromatography
def: "Separation of enantiomeric species due to the enantioselectivity of their interaction with the chiral selector\(s\) of a chromatographic \(electrophoretic\) system."
synonym: "chiral chromatography" []
synonym: "chiral electrophoresis" []
synonym: "enantioselective electrophoresis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09889
name: hydrodynamic chromatography
def: "Liquid chromatography for the separation of macromolecules and particles based on the difference in the speed of the mobile phase flow between the column axis and column wall and the specific size-dependent distribution of particles across the column cross-section."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09890
name: hydrophilic-interaction liquid chromatography
def: "Liquid chromatography in which the molecules are partitioned between a water-enriched layer\, which is immobilised on a hydrophilic \(polar\) stationary phase\, and a less-polar mobile phase\, containing water and organic solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09891
name: hydrophobic-interaction chromatography
def: "Chromatography in which molecules are separated by interactions between their hydrophobic moieties and hydrophobic sites on the surface of the stationary phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09892
name: ion-exclusion chromatography
def: "Exclusion chromatography in which ionized compounds are excluded from the pores of the support and elute first\, while weakly ionized and non-ionic compounds elute later."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09893
name: ion-pair chromatography
def: "Liquid chromatography in which the mobile liquid phase contains an ionized additive \(an ion-pair reagent\) that can form an ion pair with an analyte molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09894
name: ligand exchange chromatography
def: "Chromatography based on the different ability of substances to form complexes with metal ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09895
name: micellar chromatography
def: "Reversed-phase liquid chromatography which uses a mobile phase containing a surfactant above its critical micellar concentration \(cmc\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09896
name: isocratic elution
def: "Chromatography in which the composition of the mobile phase remains constant during elution."
synonym: "isocratic analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09897
name: column diameter
def: "Inner diameter of the column tubing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09898
name: column length
def: "Length of that part of the column tube that contains the stationary phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09899
name: cross-sectional area of the column
def: "Cross-sectional area of the empty tube\: \\\(A_\{\\rm\{c\}\} = \\pi r_\{\\rm\{c\}\}^\{2\} = \\pi \(d_\{\\rm\{c\}\}/2\)^\{2\}\\\)\, where \\\(d_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) is the column diameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09900
name: liquid-phase film thickness
def: "Average thickness of the liquid stationary phase film coated on the inside wall of the column tubing of an open-tubular column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09901
name: monolithic column
def: "Column packed with a single piece of solid material \(monolith\) composed of interconnected skeletons \(forming macropores\) and interconnected flow paths \(mesopores\) as a stationary phase material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09902
name: asymmetry factor
def: "Width of a peak \(\\\(f + b\\\)\) divided by twice the width of the front of the peak \(\\\(f\\\)\)\, measured at \\\(\\pu\{10\\%\}\\\) of the peak height \(see figure\). \\\[A_\{\\rm\{s\}\} = \\frac\{\(f + b\)\}\{2f\}\\\] Peak asymmetry \(schematic\). \\\(R_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\) is the response of the detector.Peak asymmetry \(schematic\). \\\(R_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\) is the response of the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09903
name: peak width at base
def: "Peak width at peak base intercepted by tangents to the chromatogram drawn at the inflection points on either side of the peak. Represented by the line AB in the peak height figure."
synonym: "base width" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09904
name: peak width at half height
def: "Peak width at \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of the peak height that terminates at the intersections with the peak."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09905
name: tailing factor
def: "Width of the back of a peak \(\\\(b\\\)\) divided by the width of the front \(\\\(f\\\)\) of the peak measured at \\\(\\pu\{10\\%\}\\\) of the peak height. See figure in asymmetry factor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09906
name: effective plate height
def: "Column length \(\\\(L\\\)\) divided by effective plate number \(\\\(N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\[H_\{\\rm\{eff\}\} = \\frac\{L\}\{N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "height equivalent to one effective plate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09907
name: effective plate number
def: "Number indicative of column performance\, calculated from the ratios of measures of adjusted retention and peak width\: \\\[\\eqalign\{& N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\} = \\left\(\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{\\sigma\} \\right\)^\{2\} = \\left\(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{\\sigma\} \\right\)^\{2\} \\cr & N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\} = 16 \\left\(\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\} \\right\)^\{2\} = 16 \\left\(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\} \\right\)^\{2\} \\cr & N_\{\\rm\{eff\}\} \\approx 5.545 \\left\(\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\} \\right\)^\{2\} \\approx 5.545 \\left\(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\} \\right\)^\{2\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "number of effective plates" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09908
name: reduced plate height
def: "In liquid chromatography plate height divided by average particle diameter. \\\[h = H/d_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09909
name: detector
def: "Sensor that responds to components in the effluent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09911
name: capillary affinity electrophoresis
def: "Capillary electrophoresis in which the background electrolyte contains substances capable of specific\, often biospecific\, interactions with the analytes."
synonym: "ACE" []
synonym: "affinity capillary electrophoresis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09912
name: capillary electrochromatography
def: "Liquid chromatography\, where the movement of the mobile phase through a capillary\, filled\, packed\, or coated with a stationary phase\, is achieved by electroosmotic flow \(which may be assisted by pressure\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09913
name: capillary electrophoresis
def: "Separation method based solely on the differences in the electrophoretic mobilities of charged species in capillaries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09914
name: capillary gel electrophoresis
def: "Capillary sieving electrophoresis in which the capillary is filled with a gel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09915
name: capillary isoelectric focusing
def: "Electrophoretic technique for the separation of analytes according to their isoelectric points by an electric field along a pH gradient formed in a capillary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09916
name: capillary isotachophoresis
def: "Electrophoretic separation technique in a discontinuous buffer system in which analytes migrate according to their electrophoretic mobilities\, forming a chain of adjacent zones moving with equal speed \(velocity\) between two solutions\, leading and terminating electrolytes\, bracketing the mobility range of the analytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09917
name: capillary sieving electrophoresis
def: "Electrophoretic separation technique\, which takes place in a capillary containing a medium in the background electrolyte that retains molecules and particles based on their size and shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09918
name: effective length of the capillary
def: "Length of the capillary from the sample introduction point to the detection point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09919
name: electrokinetic chromatography
def: "Separation method based on a combination of electrophoresis and interactions of the analytes with additives \(e.g. surfactants\)\, which form a dispersed phase moving at a different speed \(velocity\). In order to achieve separation\, either the analytes or this secondary phase should be charged."
synonym: "electrokinetic capillary chromatography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09920
name: electrokinetic injection
def: "Injection of the analytical sample by applying a potential across the sample and capillary and using the electroosmotic flow to draw an aliquot into the end of the capillary tube. The sample is then replaced by the running electrolyte for the separation to take place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09921
name: electroosmosis
def: "Motion of a liquid through a capillary \(or membrane or plug\) because of the application of an electric field across the capillary \(or membrane or plug\)."
synonym: "electro-osmosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09922
name: electroosmotic velocity
def: "Effective length of the capillary \(\\\(L_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}\\\)\) divided by the electroosmotic hold-up time \(\\\(t_\{\\rm\{eo\}\}\\\)\). \\\[\\boldsymbol\{v\}_\{\\rm\{eo\}\} = L_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}/t_\{\\rm\{eo\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09923
name: electroosmotic volume flow rate
def: "Volume flow rate divided by electric field strength through the whole plug or capillary."
synonym: "electroosmotic volume flow" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09924
name: electrostacking
def: "Methods that use differences in migration behaviour in spatially defined regions of the capillary of different field strengths \(conductivities\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09925
name: hydrodynamic injection
def: "Introduction of the analytical sample into a capillary by a pressure difference between the ends of the capillary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09926
name: immunoelectrophoresis
def: "Biochemical methods for separation and characterization of proteins based on electrophoresis and reaction with antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09927
name: micellar electrokinetic chromatography
def: "Electrokinetic chromatography\, in which the secondary phase is a micellar dispersed phase in the capillary."
synonym: "micellar electrokinetic capillary chromatography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09928
name: microemulsion electrokinetic chromatography
def: "Electrokinetic chromatography\, where a microemulsion is employed as the dispersed phase."
synonym: "microemulsion electrokinetic capillary chromatography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09929
name: non-aqueous capillary electrophoresis
def: "Capillary electrophoresis\, which uses water-free organic solutions and modifiers to produce ions in solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09930
name: sample introduction
def: "In isotachophoresis a sample is introduced between a zone of fast leading electrolyte and a zone of slow terminating electrolyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09931
name: asymmetrical flow field-flow fractionation
def: "Flow field-flow fractionation in which only one channel wall is permeable\, meaning there is one channel inlet for the flow during the run and some of the channel flow is directed through the accumulation wall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09932
name: Brownian field-flow fractionation mode
def: "Mechanism of field-flow fractionation of sub-micron particles that relies on Brownian motion."
synonym: "BFFF mode" []
synonym: "NFFF mode" []
synonym: "normal FFF" []
synonym: "normal FFF mode (NFFF)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09933
name: coiled-tube field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in a long \(usually \\\(\\pu\{10 m\}\\\) or more\) rotating coiled column under the action of asymmetrical centrifugal field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09934
name: field-flow fractionation
def: "Separation based on the retention and elution of macromolecular\, colloidal\, and particulate matter achieved by the combined action of the non-uniform flow speed \(velocity\) profile of a carrier fluid \(mobile phase\) and a physical force field \(e.g. gravitational\, centrifugal\, electrical\) applied at right angles to a thin \(\\\(\\pu\{0.05 mm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{0.5 mm\}\\\)\) channel \(see figure\). Basic principle of field-flow fractionation.Basic principle of field-flow fractionation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09935
name: flow field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is a nonspecific hydrodynamic force field across the channel\, generated by a secondary mobile phase cross-flow in addition to the normal channel flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09936
name: gravitational field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is the Earth’s gravitational field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09937
name: hyperlayer focusing field-flow fractionation
def: "Mechanism of field-flow fractionation of particles greater than \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu m\}\\\) in diameter relying on hydrodynamic lift forces near the channel wall to move particles into faster flowing streams."
synonym: "hyperlayer field-flow fractionation mode" []
synonym: "lift-hyperlayer field-flow fractionation mode" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09938
name: sedimentation field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is a centrifugal force field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09939
name: split-flow thin-cell fractionation
def: "Separation in which laminar flow conditions are established between a liquid containing suspended sample and a carrier liquid\, with a physical force field applied across the streams."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09940
name: steric field-flow fractionation
def: "Mechanism of field-flow fractionation of particles greater than \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu m\}\\\) in diameter relying on proximity to the accumulation wall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09941
name: symmetrical flow field-flow fractionation
def: "Flow field-flow fractionation in which the speed of mobile phase is constant along the length of the channel due to constant channel width."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09942
name: thermal field-flow fractionation
def: "Field-flow fractionation in which the physical force field is created by a temperature difference across the channel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09943
name: elution mode
def: "Method of introduction of the mobile phase \(upper or lower\) into the rotating coil \(from the head or the tail\). Of four combinations\, only two elution modes should be used in high-speed counter-current chromatography\, i.e. either the lower phase from the head toward the tail or the upper phase from the tail toward the head."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09944
name: high-speed counter-current chromatography
def: "Counter-current chromatography system using a multilayer coil separation column\, which undergoes a type-J synchronous planetary motion \(see figure\).Type-J planetary motion of a multilayer coil separation column. The column holder rotates about its own axis and revolves around the centrifuge axis at the same angular velocity \(ω\) in the same direction. This planetary motion prevents twisting of the bundle of flow tubes\, allowing continuous elution through a rotating column without risk of leakage and contamination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09945
name: mobile-phase volume
def: "Volume of the liquid mobile phase contained in the column in counter-current chromatography \\\[V_\{\\rm\{M\}\} = V_\{\\rm\{C\}\} - V_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\] where \\\(V_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\) is the total volume of the column and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\) is the volume of the liquid stationary phase in the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09946
name: multilayer coil
def: "Separation column used for high-speed counter-current chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09947
name: pH-zone-refining counter-current chromatography
def: "Counter-current chromatography that produces a train of fused rectangular solute peaks called pH-zones\, each with its specific pH. Each zone contains highly concentrated solute\, often near saturation level\, and is separated from the neighbouring zone by a narrow strip called a mixing zone\, which contains a mixture of impurities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09948
name: retainer and eluter
def: "Most important reagents in pH-zone-refining counter-current chromatography. A retainer is added to the stationary organic phase to retain the analytes and the eluter added to the mobile aqueous phase to elute the analytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09949
name: retention volume of the stationary phase
def: "Volume of the stationary phase\, which stays permanently within the column in counter-current chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09950
name: type-J coil planet centrifuge
def: "Centrifuge that provides type-J synchronous planetary motion to the column holder\, the axis of which is parallel to the centrifuge axis \(see figure in high-speed counter-current chromatography\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09951
name: type-J synchronous planetary motion
def: "Unique type of planetary motion \(so named because the flow tubes form a letter J figure\, as shown in the figure in high-speed counter-current chromatography\) of the column holder\, which rotates around its own axis once during one revolution around the central axis of the centrifuge in the same direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09952
name: chiral additive
def: "Chiral selector that has been added as a component of a mobile phase or electrophoretic medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09953
name: comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography
def: "Type of two-dimensional chromatography in which all the effluent from the first separation step is transferred in fractions to a second separation step."
synonym: "comprehensive chromatography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09954
name: heart-cut separations
def: "Type of two-dimensional chromatography in which selected fractions from the first separation step are transferred to a second separation step."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09955
name: modulation frequency
def: "Number of modulations in a given time interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09956
name: modulation number
def: "Number of modulations for a given first-dimension peak."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09957
name: modulation period
def: "Duration of a complete cycle of modulation in a system for comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography."
synonym: "modulation time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09958
name: modulator
def: "Interface device between the two columns in a system for comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography that accumulates or samples narrow bands from the effluent of the first column for fast re-injection into the second column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09959
name: multi-dimensional chromatography
def: "Chromatography in which parts or all of the separated sample components are subjected to additional separation steps."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09960
name: wrap-around
def: "Occurrence of second dimension peaks in subsequent elution sequences\, caused by second-dimension retention times that exceed the modulation time in comprehensive two-dimensional chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09962
name: retention volume of an unretained compound
def: "Retention volume of a sample component the molecules of which are larger than the largest pores of the particles of the stationary phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09963
name: retention volume
def: "Retention volume of a sample component\, the molecules of which are smaller than the largest pores of the particles of the stationary phase but larger than the smallest pores."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09964
name: total mobile phase volume
def: "Retention volume of a sample component\, the molecules of which are smaller than the smallest pores of the particles of the stationary phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09965
name: relative molecular-mass exclusion limit
def: "Maximum value of the relative molecular masses of components in a specific polymer solvent system that can enter into the pores of the porous non-adsorbing material used in size-exclusion chromatography."
synonym: "molecular-weight exclusion limit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09966
name: specific resolution
def: "Peak resolution also considering the molar masses \(\\\(M_\{1\}\\\)\, \\\(M_\{2\}\\\)\) of the two test compounds\: \\\[R_\{\\rm\{sp\}\} = \\left\[\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R1\}\} - V_\{\\rm\{R2\}\}\}\{\(w_\{\\rm\{b1\}\} + w_\{\\rm\{b2\}\}\)/2\} \\right\] \\cdot \\left\[ \\frac\{1\}\{\\log \(M_\{2\}/M_\{1\}\)\} \\right\]\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:09967
name: flavans
def: "Flavans are compounds with a 2-phenyl-3\,4-dihydro-2\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran skeleton \(1 in flavonoids\)\, that may be substituted\, and include flavan-3-ols \(compounds derived from 2-phenyl-3\,4-dihydro-2\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran-3-ol skeleton 2\) and flavan-4-ones \(compounds derived from 2-phenyl-2\,3-dihydro-4\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran-4-one skeleton 3\)\, which are generically designated as flavanols and flavanones\, respectively. Compounds derived from 2-phenyl-3\,4-dihydro-2\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran-3\,4-diol skeleton 4 are flavan-3\,4-diols\, designated leukoanthocyanidins because these colourless compounds have their structure derived from that of an anthocyanidin\, specifically they are dehydronated 3\,4-dihydroxy-1\,2\,3\,4-tetrahydroanthocyanidins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09968
name: flavones
def: "Flavones and 3-hydroxyflavones \(flavonols\) are compounds with a 2-phenyl-4\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran-4-one \(2-phenyl-4\\\(H\\\)-chromen-4-one\) skeleton 5."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09970
name: isoflavans
def: "Isoflavans are compounds with a 3-phenyl-3\,4-dihydro-2\\\(H\\\)-1-benzopyran skeleton \(see isoflavonoids\). They may be substituted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09971
name: isoflavones
def: "Isoflavones are compounds with a 3-phenyl-4$H$-1-benzopyran-4-one \(3-phenyl-4$H$-chromen-4-one\) skeleton 9. They may be substituted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09973
name: neoflavans
def: "Neoflavans are compounds with a 4-phenyl-3\,4-dihydro-2$H$-1-benzopyran skeleton \(see neoflavonoids\). They may be substituted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09974
name: neoflavones
def: "Neoflavones are compounds with a 4-phenyl-2$H$-1-benzopyran-2-one \(4-phenyl-2$H$-chromen-2-one\) skeleton 11. They may be substituted."
synonym: "4-phenylcoumarins" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:09975
name: aurones
def: "Aurones are compounds derived from \(2$Z$\)-2-benzylidene-1-benzofuran-3\(2$H$\)-one \[alternative name\: \(2$Z$\)-2-\(phenylmethylidene\)-1-benzofuran-3\(2$H$\)-one\] \(aurone\, compound 14\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09976
name: pterocarpans
def: "Pterocarpans are compounds derived from \(6a$R$\,11a$R$\)-6a\,11a-dihydro-6$H$-\[1\]benzofuro\[3\,2-$c$\]\[1\]benzopyran \(15\)\, and their 6a\,11a-didehydro derivatives 16 are named coumestans. They are numbered systematically following the numbering rules for fused ring systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09977
name: flavonolignans
def: "Flavonolignans have structures based on flavonoids condensed with \\\(\\ce\{C6\{-\}C3\}\\\) lignan precursors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09978
name: biflavonoids
def: "Biflavonoid and other flavonoid oligomers\, which occur widely in nature\, are exemplified and named in this recommendation \(Flv-3.8\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09979
name: nominal property
def: "Property of a phenomenon\, body\, or substance\, where the property has no size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09980
name: kind-of-nominal-property
def: "Defining aspect\, common to mutually comparable nominal properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09981
name: category
def: "Kind-of-nominal-property indicating a class among nominal properties ordered according to nominal property values of a specified nominal property value set without relation to size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09982
name: taxon
def: "Category indicating a class of organisms within a recognized hierarchically structured nominal property value set."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09983
name: dedicated kind-of-nominal-property
def: "Kind-of-nominal-property with a sort of system and any pertinent sort\(s\) of component\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09984
name: examination
def: "Process of experimentally obtaining one or more nominal property values that can reasonably be attributed to a nominal property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09985
name: examinand
def: "Nominal property intended to be examined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09986
name: examining system
def: "Set of one or more devices\, including any reagent and supply\, assembled and adapted to give information used to generate examined values from a nominal property value set."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09987
name: influence nominal property
def: "Nominal property that\, in an examination\, does not affect the nominal property that is actually examined\, but affects the relation between the nominal indication on the examining system and the examination result."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09988
name: examination principle
def: "Phenomenon serving as a basis of an examination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09989
name: examination method
def: "Generic description of a logical organization of operations used in an examination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09990
name: examination procedure
def: "Detailed description of an examination according to one or more examination principles and to a given examination method and any decision algorithm necessary to obtain an examination result."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09991
name: nominal indication
def: "Nominal property value provided by an examining system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09992
name: blank nominal indication
def: "Nominal indication obtained from an object similar to the one under investigation\, except that the nominal property of interest is supposed not to be present\, or is not contributing to the nominal indication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09993
name: repeatability condition
def: "Condition of examination out of a set of conditions that includes the same examination procedure\, same operators\, same examining system\, same operating conditions\, same location\, and replicate examinations on the same or similar objects over a short period of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09994
name: intermediate precision condition
def: "Condition of examination out of a set of conditions that includes the same examination procedure\, same location\, and replicate examinations on the same or similar objects over an extended period of time\, but may include other conditions involving changes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09995
name: reproducibility condition
def: "Condition of examination out of a set of conditions that includes different locations\, operators\, examining systems\, and replicate examinations on the same or similar objects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09996
name: nominal property value
def: "Feature common to equivalent individual nominal properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09997
name: nominal property value set
def: "Set of possible nominal property values for nominal properties of a given kind-of-nominal-property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09998
name: reference nominal property value
def: "Nominal property value used as a basis for comparison with nominal property values of nominal properties of the same kind-of-nominal-property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:09999
name: examination result
def: "Set of nominal property values being attributed to an examinand together with any other available relevant information."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10000
name: examined value
def: "Nominal property value representing an examination result."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10001
name: true nominal property value
def: "Nominal property value consistent with the definition of a nominal property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10002
name: conventional nominal property value
def: "Nominal property value attributed by agreement to a nominal property for a given purpose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10003
name: examination trueness
def: "Fraction of examined values identical to one or more reference nominal property values among all the examined values provided."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10004
name: examination uncertainty
def: "Fraction of examined values that is different from a reference nominal property value among all the examined values provided."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10005
name: examination error
def: "Disagreement between an examined value and a reference nominal property value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10006
name: examination accuracy
def: "Closeness of agreement between an examined value and a true nominal property value of an examinand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10007
name: examination precision
def: "Closeness of agreement between nominal indications on an examining system or examined values obtained by replicate examinations on the same or similar objects under specified examination conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10008
name: examination repeatability
def: "Examination precision under repeatability condition of examination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10009
name: intermediate examination precision
def: "Examination precision under intermediate precision condition of examination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10010
name: examination reproducibility
def: "Examination precision under reproducibility condition of examination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10011
name: systematic examination error
def: "Fraction of examined values with examination error that in replicate examinations remains constant or varies in a predictable manner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10012
name: examination bias
def: "Estimate of a systematic examination error."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10013
name: target examination uncertainty
def: "Examination uncertainty specified as an acceptable fraction and decided on the basis of the intended use of examination results."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10014
name: nominal property value coverage set
def: "Subset of the nominal property value set containing the true nominal property value\(s\) of an examinand with a stated probability\, based on the information available."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10015
name: nominal property value coverage probability
def: "Probability that the set of true nominal property values of an examinand is contained within a specified nominal property value coverage set."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10016
name: examination traceability
def: "Property of an examination result whereby it can be related to a reference through a documented unbroken chain of examination calibrations\, each contributing to the examination uncertainty."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10017
name: examinational comparability
def: "Comparability of examination results\, for nominal properties of a given kind-of-nominal-property\, that are examined and traceable to the same reference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10018
name: examinational compatibility
def: "Property of a set of examination results for a specified examinand of a given kind-of-nominal-property that have overlapping nominal property value sets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10019
name: examination standard
def: "Realization of the definition of a given nominal property \(2.1\)\, with stated nominal property value \(3.1\) and associated examination uncertainty \(3.9\)\, used as a reference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10020
name: nominal reference material
def: "Material\, sufficiently homogeneous and stable with reference to a specified nominal property\, that has been established to be fit for its intended use in an examination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10021
name: examination calibration
def: "Process that confers to one or more persons or to a device the capacity to provide nominal property values from specified examinations after having examined one or more examination standards."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10022
name: reference examination procedure
def: "Examination procedure accepted as providing examination results fit for their intended use in assessing examination trueness of examined values\) obtained from other examination procedures for nominal properties of the same kind-of-nominal-property\, in examination calibration\, or in characterizing nominal reference materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10023
name: primary reference examination procedure
def: "Reference examination procedure used to obtain an examination result without relation to an examination standard for a nominal property of the same kind-of-nominal-property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10024
name: international examination standard
def: "Examination standard recognized by signatories to an international agreement and intended to serve worldwide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10025
name: national examination standard
def: "Examination standard recognized by national authority to serve in a state or economy as the basis for assigning nominal property values to other examination standards for the same kind-of-nominal-property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10026
name: primary examination standard
def: "Examination standard established using a primary reference examination procedure\, or created as an artefact\, chosen by convention."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10027
name: secondary examination standard
def: "Examination standard established through examination calibration with respect to a primary examination standard for a nominal property of the same kind-of-nominal-property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10028
name: reference examination standard
def: "Examination standard designated for the examination calibration of other examination standards for nominal properties of a given kind-of-nominal-property in a given organization or at a given location."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10029
name: working examination standard
def: "Examination standard that is used routinely to calibrate or verify examining systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10030
name: travelling examination standard
def: "Examination standard\, sometimes of special construction\, intended for transport between different locations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10031
name: transfer examination device
def: "Device used as an intermediary to compare examination standards."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10032
name: intrinsic examination standard
def: "Examination standard based on a reproducible nominal property of a phenomenon or substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10033
name: conservation
def: "Set of operations necessary to preserve the examinational properties of an examination standard within stated limits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10034
name: examination calibrator
def: "Examination standard used in examination calibration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10035
name: certified nominal reference material
def: "Nominal reference material accompanied by documentation issued by an authoritative body and providing one or more specified nominal property values with examination uncertainty and examination traceability\, using valid examination procedures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10036
name: mobile-phase speed
def: "Linear velocity of the mobile phase across the average cross-section of the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10037
name: adjusted retention time
def: "Total retention time \(\\\(t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\)\) minus hold-up time \(\\\(t_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\)\). \\\(t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\prime\} = t_\{\\rm\{R\}\} - t_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:10038
name: hold-up time
def: "Time required to elute a component which is not retained by the stationary phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10039
name: total retention time
def: "Time between sample injection and the emergence of the peak maximum of the sample component of interest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10040
name: retention time of an unretained compound
def: "Retention time of a sample component the molecules of which are larger than the largest pores of the particles of the stationary phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10042
name: response
def: "Measured or observed quantity in an experimental design."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10043
name: autocorrelation
def: "Correlation of a variable with itself over a successive time or space intervals \(lags\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10044
name: autoscaling
def: "Variance scaling of mean-centered data."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10045
name: categorical data
def: "Data\, values of which are one of a fixed number of nominal categories."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10046
name: contingency table
def: "Type of table in a matrix format that displays the multivariate frequency distribution of variables."
synonym: "cross tabulation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10047
name: data matrix
def: "Measurement results on a system arranged in a \\\(m \\times n\\\) matrix\, with \\\(m\\\) objects and \\\(n\\\) variables."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10048
name: data pre-processing
def: "Manipulation of raw data prior to a specified data analysis treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10049
name: dynamic time warping
def: "Process of synchronizing a data matrix so that it represents the same time shifts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10050
name: evaluation data
def: "Data used to validate a model."
synonym: "validation data" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10051
name: explanatory variable
def: "Variable that influences the value of a response variable\, and is used to build models of the response variable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10052
name: exploratory data analysis
def: "Summary of the main characteristics of data\, often using graphical methods."
synonym: "initial data analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10053
name: mean centering
def: "Data pre-processing in which the mean value of a variable is subtracted from data across all objects. \\\[a_\{i\,j\}^\{\\ast\} = a_\{i\,j\} - \\frac\{1\}\{n\} \\sum \\limits_\{i\\\,=\\\,1\}^\{n\} a_\{i\,j\}\\\]"
synonym: "centering" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10054
name: multiplicative scatter correction
def: "Data pre-processing in which a constant and a multiple of a reference data set is subtracted from data."
synonym: "multiplicative signal correction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10055
name: multivariate data
def: "Data having two or more variables per object."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10056
name: multi-way data
def: "Multivariate data having two or more kinds of explanatory variable per object."
synonym: "N-way data" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10057
name: random sampling
def: "Sampling in which the sample locations are selected randomly from the whole population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10058
name: raw data
def: "Data not yet subjected to analysis."
synonym: "primary data" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10059
name: sampling unit
def: "Defined quantity of material having a boundary which may be physical or temporal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10060
name: scaling
def: "Element-wise division of a data matrix by a scaling matrix."
synonym: "weighting" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10061
name: smoothing
def: "Transformation using an approximating function to capture important patterns in data\, while removing noise or other fine-scale structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10062
name: systematic sampling
def: "Sampling in which individual samples are taken at equal intervals in location."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10063
name: take-it-or-leave-it data
def: "Happenstance data that must be processed\, or not processed\, as is."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10064
name: variance scaling
def: "Scaling in which the scaling matrix is the standard deviation of each variable across the objects. \\\[a_\{i\,j\}^\{\\ast\} = \\frac\{a_\{i\,j\}\}\{s_\{j\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:10065
name: alias structure
def: "List of combinations of effects that are aliased \(confounded\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10066
name: aliased effects
def: "In a fractional-factorial design\, effects for which the information obtained are identical."
synonym: "confounded effects" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10067
name: coded experimental design
def: "Matrix of runs by factor levels in which each level is denoted by a code that represents the relative magnitude of the level."
synonym: "coded design" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10068
name: effect of a factor
def: "Coefficient of a term in a response model."
synonym: "effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10069
name: design matrix
def: "Matrix with rows representing individual experimental treatments \(possibly transformed according to the assumed model\) which can be extended by deduced levels of other functions of factor levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10070
name: dummy factor
def: "Factor that is known to have no effect on the response\, used in an experimental design\, to estimate repeatability standard deviation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10071
name: experimental design
def: "Efficient procedure for planning combinations of values of factors in experiments so that the data obtained can be analyzed to yield valid and objective conclusions."
synonym: "design of experiments" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10072
name: factor level
def: "Value of a factor in an experimental design."
synonym: "level" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10073
name: fractional-factorial design
def: "Experimental design obtained from a full-factorial design in which experiments are systematically removed to fulfill stated statistical requirements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10074
name: full-factorial design
def: "Experimental design with all possible combinations of factor levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10075
name: interaction effect
def: "Effect of a factor where the term is the product of two or more factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10076
name: main effect
def: "Effect of a factor where the term is a single factor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10077
name: model
def: "Equation describing the response as a function of values of the factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10078
name: <em>n</em><sup>th</sup>-order effect
def: "Effect of a factor where the term is a factor raised to the power \\\(n\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10079
name: optimization
def: "Minimization or maximization of a real function by systematically choosing the values of real or integer variables from within an allowed set."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10080
name: Plackett−Burman design
def: "Incomplete experimental design to estimate main effects for which each combination of factor levels for any pair of factors appears the same number of times."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10081
name: resolution of a design
def: "One more than the smallest nth-order interaction effect that some main effect is aliased with."
synonym: "resolution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10082
name: response surface methodology
def: "Experimental design in which the response is modelled in terms of one or more factor levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10083
name: alternating least squares regression
def: "Solution to the multivariate decomposition of a data matrix in which iteratively a solution of one output matrix is used to compute the second matrix\, after the application of constraints."
synonym: "alternating regression" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10084
name: autoregression
def: "Stochastic process in which future values are estimated based on a weighted sum of past values."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10085
name: biplot
def: "Combination plot of a scores plot as points and loadings plot as vectors for common factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10086
name: bootstrapping
def: "Estimation of parameters by multiple re-sampling from measured data to approximate its distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10087
name: canonical variables
def: "Linear combinations of data with the greatest correlation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10088
name: canonical variate analysis
def: "Multivariate technique which finds linear combinations of two sets of data that are most highly correlated."
synonym: "canonical analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10089
name: common factor analysis
def: "Factor analysis in which latent variables are calculated that maximise the correlation with observed variables."
synonym: "exploratory factor analysis" []
synonym: "factor analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10090
name: core consistency diagnostic
def: "Method to assess the appropriateness of a PARAFAC model."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10091
name: correspondence factor analysis
def: "Factor analysis applied to categorical data in which orthogonal factors are obtained from a contingency table."
synonym: "correspondence analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10092
name: evolving factor analysis
def: "Factor analysis that follows the change or evolution of the rank of the data matrix as a function of an ordered variable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10093
name: factor analysis
def: "Matrix decomposition of a data matrix \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{X\}\\\)\) into the product of a scores matrix \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{T\}\\\)\) and the transpose of the loadings matrix \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{P\}^\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10094
name: <em>G</em>-fold cross validation
def: "Cross validation of a data set of \\\(N\\\) objects in which \\\(N/G\\\) objects are removed at each iteration of the procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10095
name: latent variable
def: "Variable that is inferred through a mathematical model from other variables that are observed."
synonym: "hidden variable" []
synonym: "latent construct" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10096
name: loadings
def: "Projection of a factor onto the variables."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10097
name: loadings plot
def: "Plot of one loading against variable number\, or two or three loadings against each other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10098
name: maximum likelihood principal component analysis
def: "Principal-component analysis that incorporates information about measurement uncertainty to develop models that are optimal in a maximum likelihood sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10099
name: mean squared error of prediction
def: "In multivariate calibration the average of the squared deviation of estimated values from the values of evaluation data. For \\\(N\\\) evaluation data where \\\(c_\{i\}\\\) is an observed value and \\\(\\hat c_\{i\}\\\) is the predicted value. \\\[E_\{\\rm\{MSEP\}\} = \\frac\{\\sum\\limits_\{i\\\,=\\\,1\}^\{i\\\,=\\\,N\} \(\\hat c_\{i\}\\\,-\\\, c_\{i\}\)^2\}\{N\}\\\]"
synonym: "mean squared error of estimation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10100
name: multivariate curve resolution
def: "Factor analysis for the decomposition of multicomponent data into a linear sum of chemically-meaningful components when little or no prior information about the composition is available."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10101
name: non-linear iterative partial least squares
def: "Iterative decomposition of a data matrix to give principal components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10102
name: nonlinear mapping
def: "Projection of objects defined in a multivariate space onto two- or three-dimensional space so that the distances between objects are preserved as well as possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10103
name: parallel factors analysis
def: "Decomposition of a three-way data matrix into the sum of sets of two-way loadings matrices."
synonym: "canonical decomposition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10104
name: prediction error sum of squares
def: "In multivariate calibration for a prediction set of \\\(N\\\) data where \\\(c_\{i\}\\\) is an observed value and \\\(\\hat c_\{i\}\\\) is the predicted value. \\\[E_\{\\rm\{PRESS\}\} = \\sum\\limits_\{i\\\,=\\\,1\}^\{i\\\,=\\\,N\} \(\\hat c_\{i\} - c_\{i\}\)^2\\\]"
synonym: "residual sum of squares" []
synonym: "sum of squared errors of prediction" []
synonym: "sum of squared residuals" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10105
name: principal component-discriminant analysis
def: "Discriminant analysis on a multivariate data set that has been subject to principal-component analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10106
name: principal-component analysis
def: "Factor analysis in which factors are calculated that successively capture the greatest variance in the data set."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10107
name: principal-component factor
def: "Orthogonal factors obtained in a principal-component analysis."
synonym: "principal component" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10108
name: Procrustes analysis
def: "Comparison of shapes of multi-dimensional objects by a series of geometrical transformations to minimise the sum of squared distances between the transformed and target structures while maintaining the internal structure of the objects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10109
name: root mean square error of cross validation
def: "Root mean square error of prediction when the predicted data is obtained by cross validation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10110
name: root mean squared error of prediction
def: "In multivariate calibration or classification for \\\(N\\\) evaluation data where \\\(c_\{i\}\\\) is an observed value and \\\(\\hat c_\{i\}\\\) is the predicted value \\\[E_\{\\rm\{RMSEP\}\} = \\sqrt \{\\frac\{\\sum\\limits_\{i\\\,=\\\,1\}^\{i\\\,=\\\,N\} \(\\hat c_\{i\}\\\,-\\\,c_\{i\}\)^2\}\{N\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "root mean squared error of estimation" []
synonym: "standard error of estimation" []
synonym: "standard error of prediction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10111
name: simple-to-use interactive self-modelling mixture analysis
def: "Interactive method to obtain concentrations and pure spectra from spectra of mixtures using directly-measured variables."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10112
name: score
def: "Factor analysis projection of an object onto a factor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10113
name: scores plot
def: "Plot of one score against object number\, or two or three scores against each other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10114
name: simulated annealing
def: "Generic probabilistic meta-heuristic to locate a good approximation to the global optimum of a given function in a large search space\, in which there is a slow decrease in the probability of accepting worse solutions as the solution space is explored."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10115
name: singular value decomposition
def: "A factorization of an \\\(m \\times n\\\) matrix \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{M\}\\\)\) such that \\\(\\boldsymbol\{M\} = \\boldsymbol\{U\}\\boldsymbol\{\\Sigma\} \\boldsymbol\{V\}^\{T\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\boldsymbol\{U\}\\\) is an \\\(m \\times m\\\) matrix\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\Sigma\}\\\) is a \\\(m \\times n\\\) matrix and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{V\}^\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\) is a \\\(n \\times n\\\) matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10116
name: Tucker tri-linear analysis
def: "Decomposition of a three-way data matrix into a three-way core matrix\, and three\, two-way loadings matrices."
synonym: "Tucker3 model" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10117
name: backward chaining
def: "Inference method from hypothesis to data that supports the hypothesis"
synonym: "back chaining" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10118
name: backward propagation
def: "Supervised classification method for an artificial neural network in which weights of connections between nodes are calculated from the known output layer back to the input layer."
synonym: "back-propagation learning rule" []
synonym: "back-propagation of errors" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10119
name: backward stepwise linear discriminant analysis
def: "Linear discriminant analysis in which variables to build the discriminant functions are removed one at a time to minimise the loss of discrimination\, until there is a significant loss."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10120
name: city-block distance
def: "Distance \(\\\(d_\{i\,j\}\\\)\) between two objects \(\\\(i\\\) and \\\(j\\\)\) calculated as the sum of the absolute difference between \\\(k\\\) variables \(\\\(x\\\)\) that describe the objects. \\\[d_\{i\,j\} = \\sum\\limits_\{k\\\,=\\\,1\}^\{k\\\,=\\\,N\} \\vert x_\{i\,k\} - x_\{j\,k\}\\vert\\\]"
synonym: "Hamming distance" []
synonym: "Manhattan distance" []
synonym: "taxi distance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10121
name: complete linkage
def: "Linkage criterion in hierarchical clustering in which the distance between two clusters is the distance between those two objects \(one from each cluster\) that are farthest apart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10122
name: dendrogram
def: "Tree diagram used to illustrate the arrangement of clusters produced by hierarchical clustering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10123
name: disjoint principal component analysis
def: "Principal-component analysis \(PCA\) independently performed on each class as a step in classification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10124
name: Euclidean distance
def: "Distance \(\\\(d_\{i\,j\}\\\)\) between two objects \(\\\(i\\\) and \\\(j\\\)\) calculated as the square root of the sum of the squared differences between \\\(k\\\) variables \(\\\(x\\\)\) that describe the objects. \\\[d_\{i\,j\} = \\sqrt \{\\sum\\limits_\{k\\\,=\\\,1\}^\{k\\\,=\\\,N\} \(x_\{i\,k\} - x_\{j\,k\}\)^2\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:10125
name: forward chaining
def: "Inference method from data to hypothesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10126
name: forward stepwise linear discriminant analysis
def: "Linear discriminant analysis in which variables to build the discriminant functions are introduced one at a time to maximise the discrimination\, until there is no significant improvement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10127
name: fuzzy clustering
def: "Classification in which membership of an object in each possible class is given a weight between zero and one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10128
name: hidden layer
def: "Group of nodes in an artificial neural network between input layer and output layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10129
name: hierarchical clustering
def: "Pattern recognition in which objects are linked together by use of an appropriate measure of distance between pairs of objects\, and a linkage criterion\, which specifies the dissimilarity of sets as a function of the pairwise distances between objects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10130
name: <em>k</em>-means clustering
def: "Unsupervised classification method which partitions objects into \\\(k\\\) groups\, in which each object belongs to the group with the nearest mean."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10131
name: <em>k</em>-nearest neighbour
def: "Non-parametric supervised classification method for objects based on the closest training examples in the variable space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10132
name: Kohonen network
def: "Type of self-organising map with low dimensional grid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10133
name: linear discriminant analysis
def: "Discriminant analysis in which the criterion function is based on the pooled covariance matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10134
name: Mahalanobis distance
def: "Distance \(\\\(d_\{i\,j\}\\\)\) between an object characterised by vector of variables \\\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}_\{i\}\\\) and the centroid of a class \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{j\}\\\) with covariance matrix \\\(\\boldsymbol\{S\}\\\) calculated as \\\[d_\{i\,j\} = \\sqrt \{\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}_\{i\} - \\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{j\}\)^T \\boldsymbol\{S\}^\{-1\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}_\{i\} - \\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{j\}\)\}\\\] where \\\(\\boldsymbol\{S\}\\\) is the covariance matrix of the variables."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10135
name: misclassification rate
def: "Fraction of objects incorrectly assigned to a group in supervised classification."
synonym: "misclassification frequency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10136
name: one-class classification
def: "Classification identifying objects of a specific class amongst all objects\, by learning from training data containing only objects of the specific class."
synonym: "independent classification" []
synonym: "unary classification" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10137
name: node
def: "Locations in an artificial neural network that carry values that are calculated from values of connected nodes and weights by a transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10138
name: partial least squares discriminant analysis
def: "Linear classification in which the criterion function is obtained by partial least squares analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10139
name: quadratic discriminant analysis
def: "Discriminant analysis in which the criterion function is based on the individual within-group covariance matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10140
name: regularized discriminant analysis
def: "Discriminant analysis in which the criterion function is based on a combination of pooled covariance matrix and the individual within-group covariance matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10141
name: similarity index
def: "Quantity that describes the equivalence of two objects characterised by multivariate data."
synonym: "similarity distance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10142
name: single linkage
def: "Linkage criterion in hierarchical clustering in which the distance between two clusters is the distance between those two objects \(one from each cluster\) that are closest together."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10143
name: soft independent modelling of class analogy
def: "Supervised classification that performs a principal-component analysis on each class\, and then an unknown is assigned to the class with which it has the lowest residual variance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10144
name: supervised classification
def: "Classification in which\, in a first step\, a model is built using data from objects of known classes\, and in a second step\, the model is applied to new data to assign a class to unknown objects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10145
name: Tanimoto similarity index
def: "Fraction of variables that are considered to agree between two objects."
synonym: "Tanimoto index" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10146
name: unsupervised classification
def: "Classification in which no prior information about membership of groups is known."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10147
name: unweighted pair group method with arithmetic mean
def: "Linkage criterion in hierarchical clustering in which the distance between two clusters is the average of all distances between pairs of objects."
synonym: "average linkage" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10148
name: Ward’s minimum variance method
def: "Linkage criterion in hierarchical clustering in which the within-cluster variance is minimized."
synonym: "Ward’s method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10149
name: least squares regression
def: "Regression that minimizes the sum of squared differences between observed values of a variable and the values predicted by a model."
synonym: "LS regression" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10150
name: errors-in-variables regression
def: "Least squares regression in which both the response variable and predictor variable have measurement error."
synonym: "total least squares regression" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10151
name: mean squared error of calibration
def: "In calibration for \\\(N\\\) calibration data where \\\(c_\{i\}\\\) is an observed value and \\\(\\hat c_\{i\}\\\) is the value predicted by the calibration function. \\\[E_\{\\rm\{MSEC\}\} = \\frac\{\\sum\\limits_\{i\\\,=\\\,1\}^\{i\\\,=\\\,N\} \(\\hat c_\{i\} - c_\{i\}\)^2\}\{N\}\\\]"
synonym: "mean residual sum of squares" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10152
name: multilinear least squares regression
def: "Multivariate calibration in which the coefficients of the regression are calculated directly from the indications and values of standards."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10153
name: multivariate calibration
def: "Calibration in which the indications are multivariate data."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10154
name: ordinary least squares regression
def: "Least squares regression that minimizes the sum of squared differences between the known values of dependent variable and values predicted by a linear model."
synonym: "classical least squares regression" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10155
name: partial least squares regression
def: "Multivariate calibration which finds factors that maximise covariance between two blocks of data."
synonym: "partial least squares" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10156
name: principal components regression
def: "Multivariate calibration in which a dependent variable is regressed against the scores of a chosen number of factors obtained from principal-component analysis of the predictor variable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10157
name: root mean squared error of calibration
def: "In calibration for \\\(N\\\) calibration data where \\\(c_\{i\}\\\) is an observed value and \\\(\\hat c_\{i\}\\\) is the value predicted by the calibration function. \\\[E_\{\\rm\{RMSEC\}\} = \\sqrt \\frac\{\\sum\\limits_\{i\\\,=\\\,1\}^\{i\,=\\\,N\} \(\\hat c_\{i\} - c_\{i\}\)^2\}\{N\}\\\]"
synonym: "standard error of calibration (SEC)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10158
name: underfitting of a calibration model
def: "Condition in which a model fails to describe the data adequately."
synonym: "underfitting" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10159
name: weighted least squares regression
def: "Least squares regression in which a nonnegative constant is associated with each value of the dependent variable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10160
name: boundary layer thickness
def: "Distance from the interface beyond which mass transfer is determined by convection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10161
name: breakthrough volume
def: "Sample volume at which a defined amount of an analyte pumped continuously through an extraction device will begin to be eluted. Typical breakthrough curve indicating the breakthrough volume \\\(V_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) and the sample volume corresponding to the saturation capacity of the sorbent \\\(V_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\) \(\\\(V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\) corresponds to the chromatographic elution volume and \\\(\\upsigma_\{\\rm\{v\}\}\\\) the standard deviation of the derivative of the curve\).Typical breakthrough curve indicating the breakthrough volume \\\(V_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) and the sample volume corresponding to the saturation capacity of the sorbent \\\(V_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\) \(\\\(V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\) corresponds to the chromatographic elution volume and \\\(\\upsigma_\{\\rm\{v\}\}\\\) the standard deviation of the derivative of the curve\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10162
name: ion-pair extraction
def: "Transfer of ions from an aqueous phase to an immiscible liquid \(or solid\) phase by adding an ion of complementary charge to the aqueous phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10163
name: sorbent
def: "Condensed phase where extraction of analytes can occur at the surface and in the bulk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10164
name: acceptor phase
def: "Component of a multi-phase system into which the target analytes are extracted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10165
name: active sampling
def: "Continuous extraction with movement of the sample matrix with respect to the extraction phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10166
name: batch extraction
def: "Extraction in which the extraction phase and the sample are confined in an extraction vessel and processed according to a defined procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10167
name: calibrant in extraction phase
def: "Internal standard that is incorporated in the extraction phase for calibration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10168
name: charcoal tube
def: "Tube filled with charcoal typically used to adsorb analytes from the gas or liquid phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10169
name: continuous extraction
def: "Extraction in which the sample is continuously introduced into the extraction device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10170
name: countercurrent extraction
def: "Extraction in which both phases are continuously added \(or changed\) and flow \(or move\) in opposite directions as the extraction progresses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10171
name: donor phase
def: "In extraction\, the sample or sample solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10172
name: dynamic extraction
def: "Extraction in which clean \(or recycled\) extraction phase is continuously passed through the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10173
name: exhaustive extraction
def: "Type of extraction where the intent is a complete extraction of the analytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10174
name: external calibration
def: "Calibration employing a simplified sample matrix spiked with target analyte embodying a reference value of the measurand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10175
name: extraction phase
def: "Liquid\, solid\, or gas phase in contact with the sample for the purpose of removal of analytes from the sample matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10176
name: extraction rate
def: "Rate at which analytes are transferred from the sample to the extraction phase."
synonym: "kinetics of extraction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10177
name: feed
def: "Solution or gas containing analyte introduced into an extraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10178
name: fluorous extraction
def: "Extraction in a biphasic system in which the main component of one phase is a highly fluorinated solvent \(liquid-liquid extraction\) or highly fluorinated sorbent \(solid-phase extraction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10179
name: leaching
def: "Dissolution of sample components from a solid with a liquid in which the solid phase is not wholly soluble."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10180
name: matrix-matched calibration
def: "Calibration employing external calibration in which standard solutions of target are prepared in a solution of analyte-free matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10181
name: multiwell sample plate
def: "Plate with multiple wells for individual samples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10182
name: optimisation of extraction
def: "Adjustment of extraction parameters to achieve a defined goal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10183
name: passive sampling
def: "Extraction process driven by diffusion of analytes towards the extraction phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10184
name: salting in
def: "Addition of an electrolyte to an aqueous phase to decrease the distribution ratio of the analyte\(s\) or matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10186
name: sequential extraction
def: "Extraction wherein extractants of increasing strength or different chemical character are used in order to extract analytes of interest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10187
name: static extraction
def: "Batch extraction in which the extraction phase attains a constant composition before phase separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10188
name: steady state extraction
def: "State of a continuous process operating in such a way that the concentration of solutes in exit streams remains constant with respect to time for constant feed concentrations\, even though the two phases are not necessarily in thermodynamic equilibrium in any part of the process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10189
name: time-weighted average sampling
def: "A passive sampling or active sampling strategy over a relatively long time\, during which the amount of analyte\(s\) extracted is proportional to its average \(mean\) concentration over that time."
synonym: "TWA sampling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10190
name: chemical hydride generation
def: "Type of chemical vapour generation wherein volatile hydrides are generated by reaction with \(usually\) borane complexes in aqueous solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10191
name: closed-loop extraction
def: "Extraction by a fixed volume of gas continuously recycled through the sample and a sorbent trap by a pump in a closed-circuit arrangement.Typical apparatus for closed-loop stripping analysis."
synonym: "closed-loop stripping analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10192
name: cryogenic trap
def: "Device used to concentrate volatile analytes from the gas phase by condensation on a cold surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10193
name: denuder
def: "Apparatus for the extraction of volatile analytes from a flowing gas stream by diffusion to the wall of a collection vessel\, where they are retained by chemisorption or strong physisorption by the wall coating."
synonym: "denuder device" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10194
name: dynamic headspace analysis
def: "Continuous removal of headspace above a sample by a flow of inert gas over or through the sample until all or a significant portion of the analytes have been removed from the sample. Sample vapours are typically transferred to a sorbent trap or cryogenic trap for collection.Typical apparatus for analysis by dynamic headspace \(purge and trap\). The gas flow \(sparging gas\) enters at the bottom of the sample container transporting volatile compounds through the headspace and purge head to the desorption tube for collection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10195
name: extractive distillation
def: "Separation of close-boiling liquids by fractional distillation in the presence of a low-volatility solvent or salt that selectively interacts with one or more components of the mixture\, increasing the difference in vapour pressure of the sample components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10196
name: flash desorption
def: "Thermal desorption where the temperature of a packed tube or cryogenic trap is rapidly increased to transfer condensed or sorbed compounds to the gas phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10197
name: full-evaporation headspace analysis
def: "Small volume of sample in a headspace vessel maintained at a temperature sufficient to cause complete evaporation of analytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10198
name: headspace
def: "Volume of gas phase above a sample in a closed container."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10199
name: multiple headspace extraction
def: "Dynamic gas extraction carried out stepwise\, with sequential analysis of a number of headspace samples taken from the same vial."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10200
name: simultaneous steam distillation-solvent extraction
def: "Extraction in which a sample dispersed in water and a water immiscible organic solvent \(extraction phase\) in separate flasks are heated simultaneously and their vapours co-condensed and separated into two streams in which water is returned to the sample flask and the extraction phase to the organic solvent flask.Apparatus for simultaneous steam distillation-solvent extraction with a lighter-than-water extraction phase. A = aqueous sample solution or slurry; B = immiscible organic solvent and receiving flask for extracted compounds; C = bowl for phase separation; D\, E\, F\, and G are solvent or vapour transfer arms; and H = entry port for additional solvent or alternative sample introduction position for continuous liquid–liquid extraction. Switching the position of flasks A and B allows extraction with a heavier-than-water extraction solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10201
name: sorbent trap
def: "Tube filled with porous sorbent typically used to extract analytes from the gas phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10202
name: static headspace analysis
def: "Method of analysis\, in which a small aliquot of the gas phase in contact with the sample in a closed container is withdrawn for analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10203
name: thermal extraction
def: "Release of volatile compounds from a sample by heat."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10204
name: thin-layer headspace analysis
def: "Dynamic headspace analysis of a flowing sample solution in the form of a thin film on the surface of a heated tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10205
name: biphasic system
def: "Equilibrium system containing two or more solvents forming two phases with a single interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10206
name: chelation solvent extraction
def: "Extraction method wherein charged analytes are extracted from an aqueous phase into an organic phase\, assisted by a chelating agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10207
name: cloud point extraction
def: "Coacervative extraction at a temperature above the cloud point temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10208
name: coacervative extraction
def: "Extraction by a surfactant-rich\, water-immiscible phase formed by adding a dehydrating agent to a colloidal solution of the sample and a surfactant above its critical micelle concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10209
name: continuous liquid-liquid extraction
def: "Extraction in which a water-immiscible extraction solvent is continuously percolated through an aqueous solution and returned to the extraction solvent reservoir.Apparatus for continuous liquid–liquid extraction with a lighter-than-water extraction solvent \(left\) and a heavier-than-water extraction solvent \(right\). In each case the extraction solvent is contained in the round bottom flask and the aqueous sample in the vertical glass column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10210
name: Craig countercurrent distribution apparatus
def: "Apparatus for extraction based on a multistage countercurrent distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10211
name: crosscurrent extraction
def: "Extraction comprising a cascading series of states where the raffinate is brought into a subsequent stage and contacted with fresh solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10212
name: differential contactor
def: "Type of continuous multistage extraction equipment in which there is only one interface at which phase separation by settling occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10213
name: dispersing solvent
def: "Solvent used to disperse the extraction solvent as fine droplets throughout the sample solution in dispersive liquid–liquid microextraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10214
name: dispersive liquid-liquid microextraction
def: "Extraction of analyte\(s\) from an aqueous solution by fine droplets of extraction phase dispersed throughout the solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10215
name: liquid-ion exchange
def: "Liquid-liquid distribution process that involves the transfer of ionic species from the extractant to the aqueous phase in exchange for ions from the aqueous phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10216
name: liquid-phase microextraction
def: "Microextraction technique using a liquid as the extraction phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10217
name: multistage countercurrent distribution
def: "Multistage countercurrent separation based on a discontinuous\, differential migration process employing stepwise extraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10218
name: single-drop microextraction
def: "Extraction of analytes from the gas phase or solution by a single drop of extraction solvent.Typical experimental arrangement for single-drop microextraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10219
name: solidified floating organic drop
def: "Droplet of organic solvent recovered from the surface of the sample solution by solidification induced by cooling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10220
name: solidified floating organic drop microextraction
def: "Microextraction employing a solidified floating organic drop."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10221
name: stage
def: "Physically distinct part of an extraction process in which transfer of analyte\(s\) occurs\, followed by phase separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10222
name: stagewise contactor
def: "Type of continuous multi-stage liquid-liquid distribution equipment in which each stage has a physically distinct cycle of interphase contact and separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10223
name: two-phase aqueous partition
def: "Distribution in a biphasic system formed by two aqueous solutions containing structurally incompatible components \(two polymers or a polymer and a salt\) above a critical concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10224
name: matrix-solid phase dispersion
def: "Extraction in which samples are blended with an abrasive solid sorbent\, forming a free flowing powder from which target analytes are recovered by solvent elution.Typical work flow scheme for solid-phase matrix dispersion. \(i\) Sample is blended with an abrasive sorbent\, \(ii\) free flowing powder containing sample is packed in a short column\, and \(iii\) target analytes are recovered by elution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10225
name: microwave-assisted extraction
def: "Leaching using microwave energy to heat the solvent in contact with the sample or the sample directly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10226
name: pressurized hot-water extraction
def: "Extraction by water below its critical point \(\\\(\\pu\{374.15^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{22.064 MPa\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "subcritical water extraction" []
synonym: "superheated water extraction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10227
name: pressurized-liquid extraction
def: "Leaching performed at elevated temperature and pressure.Schematic diagram of a typical apparatus for pressurized-liquid extraction. Gas flow is used to displace the extraction solvent from the sample chamber."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10228
name: Soxhlet extraction
def: "Leaching with fresh solvent cycled multiple times through a fine solid sample contained in a porous thimble.Typical apparatus for classical Soxhlet extraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10229
name: supercritical fluid extraction
def: "Use of a supercritical fluid to extract analytes from a solid or supported liquid sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10230
name: QuEChERS \(quick\, easy\, cheap\, effective\, rugged and safe\) extraction
def: "Streamlined multiresidue method for extraction and clean-up of analytes in complex matrices."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10231
name: ultrasound-assisted extraction
def: "Extraction in which ultrasound energy is used to increase the extraction rate by enhancing the contact area between the sample and solvent and by promoting efficient agitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10232
name: acid digestion
def: "Process in which the sample \(analyte and matrix\) is dissolved by an acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10233
name: closed-vessel acid digestion
def: "Type of acid digestion performed in a closed vessel at elevated pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10234
name: digestion
def: "Sample dissolution with simultaneous matrix simplification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10235
name: microwave acid digestion
def: "Type of bomb acid digestion with microwave radiation used for sample heating."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10236
name: open-vessel acid digestion
def: "Type of acid digestion performed at ambient pressure wherein the digestion temperature is limited by the boiling point of the acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10237
name: UV photochemical digestion
def: "Formation of free metal ions in aqueous solution by oxidation under the influence of UV light and possibly an oxidizing agent \(e.g. hydrogen peroxide or peroxodisulfate\) in order to break down dissolved organic matter and metal complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10238
name: cartridge
def: "Short disposable column containing a bed of porous particles immobilized between two porous frits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10239
name: conditioning solvent
def: "Small volume of solvent used to prepare the sorbent for efficient sample processing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10240
name: elution solvent
def: "Small volume of strong solvent used to displace target analytes from the sorbent bed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10241
name: foamed polyurethane sorbent
def: "Sorbent consisting of spherical\, micron-sized\, agglomerated particles bound together in a rigid and highly porous structure used for the extraction of semi-volatile organic compounds from air in high-volume samplers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10242
name: immunosorbents
def: "Porous material with surface bound monoclonal or polyclonal antibodies capable of selective extraction of target analytes by formation of reversible antibody-antigen \(compound\) binding complexes."
synonym: "immunoaffinity sorbents" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10243
name: inorganic oxide adsorbent
def: "Adsorbent with site-specific polar and ion exchange functional groups that extract analytes from a gas or liquid phase by their complementary interactions with the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10244
name: ion-exchange sorbent
def: "Porous sorbent material  containing fixed ionic sites with a complementary charge to that of the target analyte\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10245
name: low-specificity sorbents
def: "Sorbent with a non-selective surface used almost exclusively to extract analytes from an aqueous solution. The sorbent has a largely passive role in the extraction\, with the driving force being the expulsion of the organic contaminants from waterto minimize disruption of the water structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10246
name: magnetic nanoparticle sorbent
def: "Sorbent consisting of nanometre-sized\, spherical particles with a magnetic or magnetisable core surrounded by a sorbent shell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10247
name: mixed-mode sorbent
def: "Porous sorbent material with surface co-bonded ion exchange and alkyl functional groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10248
name: molecularly imprinted polymer sorbent
def: "Porous polymer sorbent with synthetic recognition sites with a high specificity for the sorption of a targeted compound or group of structurally-related compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10249
name: multiwell extraction plate
def: "Regular array of wells in a single moulded body with a short sorbent bed or disc embedded at the base of each well.Multiwell extraction plate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10250
name: needle trap
def: "Gas-tight syringe with a sorbent-filled needle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10251
name: particle-embedded glass fibre disc
def: "Suspension of micron-sized sorbent particles woven into a glass fibre matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10252
name: particle-loaded membrane disc
def: "Suspension of micron-sized sorbent particles distributed throughout a web of poly\(tetrafluroethylene\) microfibrils formed into a thin disc of various diameters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10253
name: restricted-access sorbent
def: "Porous material with a biocompatible outer surface and a retentive interior surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10254
name: rinse solvent
def: "Weak solvent used to displace matrix components from a sorbent without displacing the target analyte\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10255
name: solid-phase extraction
def: "Extraction of analytes from a gas\, liquid\, or fluid by transfer to a solid sorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10256
name: solvent desorption
def: "Use of a solvent\, typically combined with agitation\, to remove target analytes from a sorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10257
name: sorbent-containing pipette tip
def: "Disposable pipette tip with a small packed bed\, disc\, or monolithic plug of sorbent at its narrow end.Sorbent containing pipette tips in an array for use with a multiwall sample plate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10258
name: sorptive-tape extraction
def: "Extraction from the surface of a solid by sorbent or polymer film in the form of a tape in direct contact with\, or in the headspace above\, the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10259
name: stir bar sorptive extraction
def: "Extraction of compounds from solution by a sorbent coated magnetic stir bar by rapid stirring \(direct immersion\) or by exposure to the headspace above a sample \(headspace extraction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10260
name: surface-bound macrocyclic sorbent
def: "Porous sorbent with surface-bound macrocyclic ligands designed for the extraction of metal ions and some anions from aqueous solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10261
name: surface-bound phenylboronic acid sorbent
def: "Porous sorbent with surface-bound phenylboronic acid groups for the selective extraction of bifunctional compounds by reversible formation of 5- or 6-membered covalent cyclic boronic acid complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10262
name: biocompatible solid-phase microextraction
def: "Extraction by a matrix-compatible immersion solid-phase microextraction device where the extraction phase and support are chosen to eliminate matrix interferences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10263
name: blade format
def: "Solid-phase microextraction device where the extraction phase \(sorbent\) is dispersed in the form of a thin coating on the surface of a blade."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10264
name: coated-vessel format
def: "Sample vessel with sorbent-coated walls."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10265
name: cold fibre solid-phase microextraction
def: "Solid-phase microextraction in the fibre format with a sorbent temperature maintained below that of the sample matrix during extraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10266
name: fibre format
def: "Extraction phase in the form of a thin layer supported on a fibre made of inert material\, such as fused silica\, stainless steel\, or nickel-titanium alloy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10267
name: headspace solid-phase microextraction
def: "Solid-phase microextraction where the extraction phase is placed in the headspace above the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10268
name: immersion solid-phase microextraction
def: "Solid-phase microextraction where the extraction phase is immersed directly into the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10269
name: in-needle capillary adsorption trap
def: "In-tube format device with a sorbent-coated capillary placed inside a needle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10270
name: in-tube solid-phase microextraction
def: "Extraction utilizing a coated open tubular or packed capillary tube as the extraction phase in a flow system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10271
name: matrix-compatible solid-phase microextraction
def: "Immersion solid-phase microextraction where the extraction phase is designed to minimize sorption of particles or macromolecules present in the sample matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10272
name: membrane-protected solid-phase microextraction
def: "Immersion solid-phase microextraction in which the extraction phase is surrounded by a permeable membrane that allows access of analytes to the extraction phase while shielding the extraction phase from matrix components with a potential to interfere in the extraction or subsequent analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10273
name: particle sorbent
def: "Extraction phase in the form of sorbent particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10274
name: rotating disk sorptive extraction
def: "Sorbent-coated disc consisting of a polytetrafluoroethylene \(PTFE\) base with an integrated magnet and a sorbent film attached to one side."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10275
name: solid-phase dynamic extraction
def: "Solid-phase microextraction in which the extraction phase is coated on the internal surface of a needle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10276
name: solid-phase microextraction
def: "Microextraction where the extraction phase is a solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10277
name: thin film format
def: "Solid-phase microextraction where the sorbent is in the form of a thin film on a support or self supported membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10278
name: electromembrane extraction
def: "Hollow-fibre liquid-phase microextraction in which the extraction is driven by an electrical potential sustained between the sample solution and the acceptor phase\, separated by a supported liquid membrane.Typical experimental arrangement for electromembrane extraction of cations \(anions extracted by reversing the polarity\). SLM\, supported liquid membrane in the form of a porous hollow fiber with solvent-filled pores."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10279
name: hollow fibre
def: "Narrow cylindrical tube prepared from a porous polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10280
name: hollow-fibre liquid-phase microextraction
def: "Liquid-phase microextraction of analytes from a liquid \(or gaseous\) sample by a supported liquid membrane in the form of a porous hollow fibre with solvent-filled pores. The analytes are transferred to an acceptor phase contained in the cavity of the hollow fibre.Typical experimental arrangement for hollow-fiber supported liquid membrane \(SLM\) for liquid-phase microextraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10281
name: membrane extraction
def: "Extraction of analytes from a sample by permeation through a membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10282
name: membrane extraction with sorbent interface
def: "Membrane extraction with a sorbent trap for concentration of the permeate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10283
name: microdialysis
def: "Dialysis by a short length of hollow-fibre membrane used for sampling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10284
name: non-porous membrane
def: "Membrane in which permeation occurs by processes other than flow through pores."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10285
name: parallel artificial liquid membrane extraction
def: "Extraction of analytes from aqueous samples in a multiwell plate with a porous membrane at the base of each well by migration through the solvent-filled membrane pores into an acceptor phase located in each well."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10286
name: perfusate
def: "Stream of extraction phase entering a dialysis module."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10287
name: permeation
def: "Process by which mass transfer occurs through a membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10288
name: porous membrane
def: "Membrane in which permeation occurs through its porous structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10289
name: retentate stream
def: "Sample left over in membrane separations after initial contact with the membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10290
name: semipermeable membrane device
def: "Membrane extraction in which the membrane fully encloses an acceptor phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10291
name: stream
def: "The sample in membrane separations prior to contact with the membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10292
name: stripping phase
def: "Extraction phase in a membrane separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10293
name: supported liquid membrane
def: "Porous membrane with solvent-filled pores."
synonym: "artificial liquid membrane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10294
name: at-line extraction
def: "Flow system with extraction device employing a local detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10295
name: automated extraction
def: "Extraction in a mechanical device regulated by feed-back of information without human intervention."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10296
name: automatic extraction
def: "Extraction according to manually preselected conditions without further human intervention."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10297
name: bead injection
def: "Fully renewable \(disposable\) packed sorbent bed for solid-phase extraction in a flow manifold or mesofluidic platform using programmable flow extraction.Bead-injection protocols for automated solid phase microextraction with sorbent renewal for each assay \[Reproduced from  https\://www.flowinjectiontutorial.com\].a\) Injection of a metered amount of particles or beads with reactive surfaces in the flow system followed by mechanical trapping or capture with the aid of external energy sources; b\) Injection of a metered volume of sample; c\) Interaction of analyte\(s\) with sorptive surfaces; d\) Preconcentration of analyte\(s\) followed by detection of native species or derivatization products; e\) Automatic removal of sorptive surfaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10298
name: bioaccessibility
def: "Fraction of analytes desorbed from a sample potentially available to organisms."
synonym: "environmental availabilty" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10299
name: dynamic bioaccessibility
def: "Leaching of analytes from a solid or semi-solid sample in a flow-through device to evaluate their bioaccessibility."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10300
name: flow-through extraction
def: "Extraction by flow of sample through or over the extraction phase\, or vice versa\, to enhance the extraction rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10301
name: flow-through solid-phase spectrometry
def: "Continuous optical interrogation of a packed-sorbent bed in a flow system at any stage of the extraction process prior to analyte elution and sorbent regeneration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10302
name: in-line extraction
def: "Coupling of a flow system with extraction device to a detector without flow interruption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10303
name: in-syringe microextraction
def: "Batch-type extraction in which the barrel of a syringe pump is used as the device for liquid-phase microextraction or dispersive solid-phase microextraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10304
name: lab-at-valve liquid-phase microextraction
def: "Automatic batch-type liquid-phase microextraction by sequential injection analysis using an external extraction container \(e.g. pipette tip\) connected to a multiposition rotary valve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10305
name: lab-on-a-chip extraction
def: "Microfluidic platform containing microconduits to facilitate extraction processes with pressure-driven or electroosmotic flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10306
name: lab-on-valve extraction
def: "Mesofluidic platform containing microconduits for extraction processes mounted on a multi-position rotary valve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10307
name: mechanized extraction
def: "Extraction using a device with at least one movable part that replaces or supplements human effort in any \(or all\) steps of the process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10308
name: mesofluidic extraction device
def: "Extraction device with channel widths from \\\(\\pu\{10 \\upmu m\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{2 mm\}\\\) integrated into a flow platform."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10309
name: microflow injection analysis extraction device
def: "Miniaturized flow injection analysis device for extraction with conduits of a diameter greater than \\\(\\pu\{0.1 mm\}\\\)."
synonym: "μFIA extraction device" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10310
name: microfluidic extraction device
def: "Extraction device with channel widths from \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{10 \\upmu m\}\\\) integrated into a flow platform."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10311
name: multicommutation flow extraction
def: "Programmable flow extraction device with microsolenoid valves for processing microliter sample volumes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10312
name: on-line extraction
def: "Coupling of a flow system with an extraction device to a detector via a discontinuous interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10313
name: programmable flow extraction
def: "Multi-bidirectional flow \(including stopped flow\) of sample and/or extractant through an extraction device controlled by a pump."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10314
name: segmented flow analysis
def: "Continuous-flow system attaining steady-state extraction/reaction conditions facilitated by injection of air bubbles. This might be extended to immiscible liquids injected as droplets in the flow system. Related term\: segmented flow extraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10315
name: segmented flow extraction
def: "Extraction by insertion of the sample solution as equal-size droplets \(segments\) in a flowing stream of immiscible extraction phase.Typical segmented flow extraction manifold. C\, carrier or liquid phase for sample; R\, reagent \(optional\); P\, propulsion unit; S\, sample; IV\, injection valve; MC\, mixing coil; DB\, displacement bottle \(functions as a constant volume pump\); ORG\, organic solvent; SG\, segmenter; EC\, extraction coil; PS\, phase separator; D\, detector \(optional\); RC\, restriction coil; and W\, waste."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10316
name: sequential injection analysis extraction device
def: "Automatic programmable flow extraction through a multi-position rotary valve housing a mesofluidic extraction device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10317
name: A/D ratio
def: "Ratio of the adult toxic dose to the developmentally toxic dose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10318
name: abdominal cavity
def: "Body cavity between the diaphragm and pelvis that contains the abdominal organs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10319
name: ablepharia
def: "Congenital absence of the eyelids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10320
name: abortion
def: "Premature termination of pregnancy with the death of the embryo or fetus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10321
name: induced abortion
def: "Intentional termination of a pregnancy with death of the embryo or fetus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10322
name: spontaneous abortion
def: "Non-intentional termination of pregnancy before the embryo or fetus has developed to the stage of independent viability\, or in humans before the 20th week of gestation."
synonym: "miscarriage" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10323
name: accessory rib
def: "Rib arising from a cervical vertebra \(cervical rib\)\, or supernumerary rib arising from a thoracic or lumbar vertebra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10324
name: accessory sex gland
def: "Any gland\, other than a gonad\, associated with the genital tract\, such as the bulbourethral gland and prostate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10325
name: accessory sex organ
def: "Organ or structure other than the gonads that matures at puberty and assists indirectly in sexual reproduction by nurturing and transporting gametes."
synonym: "secondary sex organ" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10326
name: β-<em>N</em>-acetylhexosaminidase
def: "Hydrolytic enzyme \(EC 3.2.1.52\) that acts on ganglioside \\\(\\rm\{G\}_\{\\rm\{M2\}\}\\\)\, producing N-acetyl-D-galactosamine and ganglioside \\\(\\rm\{G\}_\{\\rm\{M3\}\}\\\)."
synonym: "hexosaminidase A" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10327
name: achondroplasia
def: "Inherited disorder where ossification of cartilage is retarded\, especially affecting growth of long bones\, resulting in very short limbs and a comparatively large head. Type of dwarfism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10328
name: acinus
def: "Small sac-like cavity in a gland\, surrounded by secretory cells.\nTerminal region of the airways of the lung where gas exchange occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10329
name: acoustic meatus
def: "Either of two passages in the ear\, one leading to the tympanic membrane \(external acoustic meatus\)\, and one for passage of nerves and blood vessels \(internal acoustic meatus\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10330
name: acrania
def: "Rare congenital disorder that occurs in the human fetus\, in which the flat bones in the cranium are either completely or partially absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10331
name: acrocephaly
def: "Type of cephalic disorder where the top of the skull is pointed or conical due to premature closure of the coronal suture plus any other suture."
synonym: "acrocephalia" []
synonym: "oxycephaly" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10332
name: acromegaly
def: "Abnormal enlargement of the hands\, feet\, and face in adults\, caused by overproduction of growth hormone by the pituitary gland."
synonym: "acromegalia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10333
name: acrosome
def: "Organelle that develops over the anterior half of the head in the spermatozoon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10334
name: acrosome reaction
def: "Process that occurs in the acrosome of the sperm as it contacts the egg\, leading to structural changes that facilitate fusion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10335
name: activin
def: "Growth factor of the transforming growth factor \\\(\\upbeta\\\) \(TGF-\\\(\\upbeta\\\)\) superfamily\, originally identified as a gonadal factor that stimulated secretion of follicle-stimulating hormone\, also involved in many aspects of development including mesodermal induction \(see mesenchyme\)\, hematopoiesis\, and neuronal differentiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10336
name: acyclicity
def: "Irregular or absent estrous cycles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10337
name: adactyly
def: "Congenital absence of fingers or toes."
synonym: "adactylia" []
synonym: "adactylism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10338
name: adenocarcinoma
def: "Malignant tumor formed from glandular epithelial tissue or formed in a glandular pattern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10339
name: adermia
def: "Congenital absence of skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10340
name: adhesion factor
def: "Substance contributing to selective cell-cell and cell-matrix binding."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10341
name: aditus
def: "Entrance or opening to some interior space or cavity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10342
name: adolescence
def: "Stage of human development beginning with puberty and ending with adulthood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10343
name: adrenal 4 binding protein
def: "Transcription factor that regulates the expression of the enzymes of steroid synthesis and is expressed primarily in steroidogenic cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10344
name: adrenal gland
def: "Either of two small endocrine glands\, one located above each kidney\, consisting of a cortex\, which secretes several steroid hormones\, and a medulla\, which secretes adrenaline and noradrenaline."
synonym: "paranephric gland" []
synonym: "suprarenal gland" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10345
name: adrenaline
def: "Catecholamine hormone secreted by the adrenal glands that increases heart rate\, breathing rate\, blood pressure\, and carbohydrate metabolism."
synonym: "2-diol" []
synonym: "4-[(1R)-1-hydroxy-2-(methylamino)ethyl]benzene-1" []
synonym: "epinephrine" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10346
name: adrenocortocotropic hormone
def: "Hormone secreted by the pituitary gland and stimulating the adrenal cortex \(see adrenal gland\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10347
name: adult
def: "Person or animal that is fully grown\, developed and sexually mature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10348
name: adulthood
def: "State of being adult."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10349
name: agenesis
def: "Absence or partial development of an organ or body part observed at birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10350
name: age-specific birth rate
def: "Mean number of offspring born to a female in a specific age class in a given year\, expressed per \\\(\\pu\{1000\}\\\) females in that age class."
synonym: "age-specific fecundity" []
synonym: "age-specific fertility rate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10351
name: aggregation chimera
def: "Organism made by combining cells from two embryos of different genotypes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10352
name: agnathia
def: "Congenital absence or partial absence of the lower jaw."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10353
name: alar plate
def: "Part of the dorsal side of neural tube in the embryo\, involved in general somatic and visceral sensory communication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10354
name: albinism
def: "Congenital disorder characterized by the complete or partial absence of pigment in the skin\, hair and eyes\, due to an absence of or defect in tyrosinase\, a copper-containing enzyme involved in the production of melanin."
synonym: "achromasia" []
synonym: "achromatosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10355
name: alcohol-related neurodevelopmental disabilities
def: "Spectrum of functional neurologic \(behavioral\) defects resulting from exposure in utero to alcohol."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10356
name: allantois
def: "Extra-embryonic membrane formed early in development as an outpouching of the yolk sac into the area of the future umbilical cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10357
name: alopecia
def: "Hairlessness; absence or thinning of hair from areas of skin where it is usually present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10358
name: alpha-fetoprotein
def: "Protein coded by the AFP gene and produced by fetal tissues\, an abnormally high amount of which in the amniotic fluid or maternal serum may indicate a neural tube defect\, or some other loss of structural integrity in the fetus."
synonym: "α-1-fetoprotein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10359
name: alveolus
def: "Any of the many terminal air sacs of the airways in the lungs necessary for rapid gaseous exchange with the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10360
name: amastia
def: "Congenital absence of one or both mammary glands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10361
name: ambisexual
def: "Pertaining to or characterized by hermaphroditism.\nDenoting sexual characteristics common to both sexes\, e.g.\, pubic hair."
synonym: "bisexual" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10362
name: amelia
def: "Lacking one or more limbs or having a shrunken or deformed limb as a birth defect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10363
name: ameloblast
def: "Epithelial cell that deposits enamel during tooth development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10364
name: amelogenesis imperfecta
def: "Hereditary yellow\, brown\, or grey tooth enamel.\nDevelopmental disorder of the teeth in which they are covered with thin\, abnormal enamel resulting from defective structure or processing of enamel proteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10365
name: amenorrhea
def: "Absence or abnormal stoppage of menstruation."
synonym: "menostasis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10366
name: amniocentesis
def: "Transabdominal procedure in which amniotic fluid is sampled by means of a needle inserted into the amniotic sac."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10367
name: amniochorionic membrane
def: "Composite membrane\, formed by fusion of the amnion\, interiorly\, with the outer chorion that surrounds the developing fetus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10368
name: amnion
def: "Membrane that lines the amniotic cavity \(amniotic sac\) and encloses the embryo of a mammal\, bird\, or reptile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10369
name: amniote
def: "Any vertebrate animal\, such as a reptile\, bird\, or mammal\, that possesses an amnion\, chorion\, and allantois during embryonic development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10370
name: amniotic cavity
def: "Fluid-filled space that surrounds the developing embryo of a mammal\, bird\, or reptile\, bounded by the amniotic sac."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10371
name: amniotic sac
def: "Membranous structure in amniotes\, within which the embryo and fetus develop."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10372
name: amphibian metamorphosis assay
def: "Procedure in which a frog \(often _027793_\) is exposed to a substance starting at the tadpole stage\, and the growth and development of the animals is studied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10373
name: ampulla
def: "Flask-like dilatation of a canal or duct."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10374
name: anal canal
def: "Terminal part of the large intestine between the rectum and anus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10375
name: anal membrane
def: "In the embryo\, dorsal part of the cloacal membrane after its division of the urorectal septum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10376
name: anamniote
def: "Any vertebrate animal\, such as a fish or amphibian\, that lacks an amnion\, chorion\, and allantois during embryonic development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10377
name: anaphase
def: "Stage of mitosis and meiosis in which the chromosomes move from the equatorial plate toward opposite ends of the nuclear spindle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10378
name: anaphase lag
def: "Slowing or stopping of normal migration of chromosomes during anaphase\, resulting in chromosomes being excluded from one of the daughter cells causing aneuploidy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10379
name: anasarca
def: "Generalized edema in the subcutaneous connective tissue."
synonym: "dropsy" []
synonym: "hydrosarca" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10380
name: anastomosis
def: "Connection creating continuity between two tubular body structures\, e.g. blood vessels or loops of bowel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10381
name: fetal anatomic position
def: "Usual positioning of the developing fetus with the back curved forward\, head bent forward\, and limbs drawn in towards the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10382
name: anchoring villus
def: "Chorionic villus that is attached to the region of the endometrium \(see also decidua\) that interacts with the trophoblast during development of the placenta."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10383
name: androblastoma
def: "Rare benign tumor of the testis histologically resembling the fetal testis\: the epithelium contains Sertoli cells that may produce estrogen and cause feminization."
synonym: "Sertoli cell tumor" []
synonym: "arrhenoblastoma" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10384
name: androgen
def: "Substance\, such as a naturally occurring steroid hormone\, that binds to androgen receptors to activate the male accessory sex organs and induce male secondary sexual characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10385
name: androgenic
def: "Producing masculine characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10386
name: androgen receptor
def: "Nuclear receptor that is activated by binding of testosterone or dihydrotestosterone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10387
name: androstenedione
def: "Steroid hormone produced in the adrenal glands and gonads as a common precursor of male \(testosterone\) and female \(estrogen\) sex hormones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10388
name: anemia
def: "Decrease in the number of erythrocytes or total hemoglobin in the blood that results in a decrease in the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood\, sometimes inducing pallor and fatigue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10389
name: anencephalus
def: "Fetus lacking all or most of the neural tissues of brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10390
name: anencephaly
def: "Congenital absence of a major portion of the brain and its meninges\, skull\, and scalp that results from abnormal embryonic development."
synonym: "anencephalia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10391
name: anestrus
def: "Interval of sexual inactivity between two periods of estrus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10392
name: aneugen
def: "Agent inducing aneuploidy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10393
name: aneuploid
def: "Referring to a cell or organism with missing or extra chromosomes or parts of chromosomes\, and thus an abnormal number of chromosomes that is not an exact multiple of the haploid number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10394
name: aneuploidy
def: "State of being aneuploid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10395
name: aneurysm
def: "Abnormal bulging of the wall of an artery or a chamber of the heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10396
name: angioblast
def: "Cell taking part in blood vessel formation.\nPrimordial mesenchymal tissue from which embryonic blood cells and vascular endothelium are differentiated."
synonym: "vasoformative cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10397
name: angiogenesis
def: "Development of new blood vessels in the embryo or from pre-existing vessels; formation of capillary networks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10398
name: angiotensin converting enzyme
def: "Enzyme \(EC 3.4.15.1\) that converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II\, causing blood vessels to constrict thus increasing blood pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10399
name: ankyloglossia
def: "Congenital oral anomaly\, in which the lingual frenulum \(the membrane connecting the underside of the tongue to the floor of the mouth\) is unusually short and thick."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10400
name: anodontia
def: "Congenital absence of all primary or permanent teeth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10401
name: anogenital distance
def: "Distance along the perineum between the anus and the base of the vagina or penis\, or in the fetus between the anus and the base of the genital tubercle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10402
name: developmental anomaly
def: "Deviation in structure or function arising in the embryo or fetus\, due to genetic or other causes."
synonym: "congenital anomaly" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10403
name: anonychia
def: "Absence of nails."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10404
name: anophthalmia
def: "Congenital absence of one or both eyeballs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10405
name: anorchism
def: "Congenital absence of one or both testes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10406
name: anorectal
def: "Relating to the anus and rectum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10407
name: anotia
def: "Congenital absence of the \(outer ear\) pinna\, often with narrowing or absence of the ear canal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10408
name: anovulation
def: "Failure of the ovaries to produce\, to facilitate maturation of\, or to release ova."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10409
name: anoxia
def: "Total absence of dioxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10410
name: antemortem
def: "Before death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10411
name: antepartum
def: "Before birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10412
name: antimitotic
def: "Referring to inhibition of cell division by mitosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10413
name: anti-Müllerian hormone
def: "Protein that inhibits the development of the Müllerian ducts \(paramesonephric ducts\) in the male embryo."
synonym: "Müllerian inhibiting factor (MIF)" []
synonym: "Müllerian-inhibiting hormone (MIH)" []
synonym: "Müllerian-inhibiting substance (MIS)." []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10414
name: antrum
def: "Cavity or chamber\, often in bone\, with specific meanings in some hollow organs \(e.g.\, the pyloric end or gastric antrum of the stomach\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10415
name: anus
def: "External opening of the rectum to the body surface\, controlled by the anal sphincter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10416
name: aorta
def: "Great artery arising from the left ventricle\, being the main trunk from which the systemic arterial system proceeds"

[Term]
id: goldbook:10417
name: aorta-gonad-mesonephros region
def: "Region of the vertebrate embryonic mesoderm that gives rise to the genitourinary tract and its blood supply."
synonym: "AGM region" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10418
name: aortic arch
def: "Curved portion of the aorta between its ascending portion exiting the heart and its descending portion that proceeds to the arteries of the thoracic and abdominal cavities and the lower body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10419
name: aortopulmonary septum
def: "Spiral septum that\, during development\, separates the truncus arteriosus into a ventral pulmonary trunk and the ascending aorta dorsally."
synonym: "aorticopulmonary septum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10420
name: aphakia
def: "Absence of the lens of the eye\, occurring as a congenital defect or as a result of trauma or surgery."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10421
name: appendicular skeleton
def: "Bones and cartilage that support the appendages\, including the bones of the shoulders\, upper limbs\, pelvis\, and lower limbs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10422
name: appendix
def: "Tube-shaped sac \('vermiform appendix'\) attached to and opening into the upper end of the large intestine \(cecum\) in humans and some other mammals.\nAppendage\, blind sac\, or diverticulum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10423
name: arachnodactyly
def: "Extreme length and slenderness of the fingers or toes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10424
name: arachnoid membrane
def: "Web like membrane that lies between the outer \(and much thicker\) dura mater and the deeper pia mater\, and which covers the brain."
synonym: "arachnoid mater" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10425
name: arachnoid villus
def: "Small projection of the arachnoid membrane into some of the venous sinuses of the dura mater."
synonym: "arachnoid granulation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10426
name: areola
def: "A circular area of different color surrounding a central point\, such as that surrounding the nipple of the breast\, the part of the iris surrounding the pupil of the eye\, or an area surrounding a vesicle.\nAny minute space or interstice in a tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10427
name: Arnold–Chiari malformation
def: "Congenital herniation of the brainstem and lower cerebellum through the foramen magnum into the cervical vertebral canal."
synonym: "Chiari malformation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10428
name: aromatase
def: "Enzyme \(EC 1.14.14.1\) of the cytochrome P450 superfamily that converts testosterone to 17\\\(\\upbeta\\\)-estradiol and androstenedione to estrone."
synonym: "estrogen synthetase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10429
name: arteriogenesis
def: "Increase in the diameter of arterial vessels that leads to the formation of large conductance arteries from pre-existing arterioles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10430
name: arteriovenous shunt
def: "Connection between the arterial and venous sides of the circulation that bypasses the capillary beds."
synonym: "AV Shunt" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10431
name: artificial insemination
def: "Introduction of semen into the cervix\, uterus or Fallopian tubes by means other than the natural one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10432
name: aryl hydrocarbon receptor
def: "Ligand-activated transcription factor involved in the regulation of biological responses to planar aromatic hydrocarbons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10433
name: aryl hydrocarbon receptor nucleotide translocator protein
def: "Protein coded for by a gene on chromosome 1q21 that forms a complex with ligand-bound aryl hydrocarbon receptor\, resulting in translocation of the ligand-binding subunit to the nucleus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10434
name: atelectasis
def: "Total or partial collapse of the lung.\nCongenital condition characterized by the incomplete expansion of the lungs at birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10435
name: athelia
def: "Congenital absence of one or both nipples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10436
name: athymia
def: "Congenital absence of functioning thymus tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10437
name: atresia
def: "Congenital absence or abnormal narrowing of a normal opening or normally patent lumen."
synonym: "clausura" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10438
name: atrial-septal defect
def: "Defect in the septum between the atria of the heart\, due to failure of normal closure of the foramen ovale in the perinatal period."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10439
name: atrichia congenita
def: "Congenital baldness caused by an abnormality of the hairless gene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10440
name: atrioventricular
def: "Relating to the atrial and ventricular chambers of the heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10441
name: atrium
def: "Each of the two upper chambers of the heart from which blood is passed to the ventricles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10442
name: atrophy
def: "Wasting away of the body or of an organ or tissue\, involving a decrease in size and \(or\) numbers of cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10443
name: auditory tube
def: "Narrow channel connecting the middle ear and the nasopharynx."
synonym: "Eustachian tube" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10444
name: auricle
def: "An appendage to the atrium of the heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10445
name: autopsy
def: "Postmortem examination of the human organs and body tissue to determine cause of death or pathological condition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10446
name: autosite
def: "Independent twin of a pair of conjoined twins; the other twin is a parasitic twin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10447
name: autosomal dominant mutation
def: "Change in an autosomal gene capable of expression when carried by only one of a pair of homologous chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10448
name: autosomal recessive mutation
def: "Change in an autosomal gene that produces an effect in the organism only when it is homozygous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10449
name: autosome
def: "Any chromosome that is not a sex chromosome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10450
name: avascularity
def: "Absence of a blood supply."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10451
name: axial skeleton
def: "Bones of the body axis\, including the skull\, spinal column\, ribs and sternum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10452
name: azoospermia
def: "Absence of viable spermatozoa in the semen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10453
name: basal ganglia
def: "Complex structure at the base of the brain consisting of several groups of neurons\, the caudate nucleus\, the putamen\, the globus pallidus\, and the substantia nigra."
synonym: "basal nuclei" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10454
name: basement membrane
def: "Thin layer of connective tissue underlying an attached epithelial cell layer."
synonym: "basal lamina" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10455
name: bell stage
def: "Period in which the developing tooth takes on a bell shape in cross section\, immediately preceding the 'advanced bell stage'\, in which the hard tissues \(dentin and enamel\) form the crown of the tooth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10456
name: bicornate uterus
def: "Uterus that is divided into two lateral horns as a result of imperfect fusion of the paired embryonic tubes from which the uterus is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10457
name: bilaminar embryo
def: "Early blastula having only two of the three primary germ layers that it will ultimately have; the two layers present are the cells of the epiblast that will give rise to the ectoderm and those of the hypoblast that will become the endoderm during gastrulation - both attached to a basement membrane before the mesoderm has formed."
synonym: "bilaminar blastoderm" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10458
name: birth
def: "Start of life as a physically separate being\, e.g.\, the emergence of a baby from the body of its mother\, or the hatching of an egg."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10459
name: birth defect
def: "Physical or biochemical abnormality that is present at birth\, and that may either be inherited or be the result of environmental influence."
synonym: "congenital defect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10460
name: blastema
def: "Mass of cells from which an organ or a body part develops\, either in normal development or in the regeneration of a lost body part."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10461
name: blastocoel
def: "Fluid-filled cavity in the blastula of a developing embryo."
synonym: "cleavage cavity" []
synonym: "segmentation cavity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10462
name: blastocyst
def: "Modified blastula that is characteristic of placental mammals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10463
name: blastomere
def: "Any of the cells formed by cleavage of a fertilized egg."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10464
name: blastula
def: "Hollow mass of cells formed after a zygote has undergone approximately six cell divisions."
synonym: "blastocyst" []
synonym: "blastodermic vesicle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10465
name: blastulation
def: "Process by which the early embryo transforms from the morula into the blastula."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10466
name: blood–testis barrier
def: "Occluding barrier\, formed by the Sertoli cells of the seminiferous tubules\, that separates the more mature cells of spermatogenesis from blood-borne products."
synonym: "Sertoli cell barrier (SCB)" []
synonym: "blood-seminiferous tubule barrier" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10467
name: bone age
def: "Average age at which children reach a given stage of bone maturation\, denoting the stage of skeletal development of an individual fetus or child."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10468
name: bone morphogenetic protein
def: "Any of a family of growth factors involved in bone and cartilage formation and\, more generally\, in orchestrating tissue architecture through morphogenetic signals."
synonym: "bone morphogenic protein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10469
name: bone morphogenetic protein-4
def: "Bone morphogenetic protein member of the transforming growth factor \\\(\\upbeta\\\) \(TGF-\\\(\\upbeta\\\)\) superfamily."
synonym: "bone morphogenic protein-4 (BMP4)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10470
name: brachydactyly
def: "Congenital abnormal shortness of fingers and toes."
synonym: "brachydactylia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10471
name: branchial
def: "Of\, relating to\, or resembling the gills of a fish or the homologous embryonic structures and their derivatives in higher animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10472
name: branchial arch
def: "In embryology\, one of several arches\, resembling the gill arches of a fish\, found in the embryo of a higher vertebrate; these arches develop into structures of the ear and neck.\nIn biology\, one of several bony or cartilaginous arches located on either side of the pharynx and supporting the gills in fish and amphibians."
synonym: "gill arch" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10473
name: congenital branchial cyst
def: "Congenital cyst arising in the lateral aspect of the neck\, from epithelial remnants of a pharyngeal groove."
synonym: "branchiogenic cyst" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10474
name: brevicollis
def: "Shortness of the neck."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10475
name: bronchopulmonary segment
def: "Largest subdivision of a lobe of the lung with its air supply from a major branch of the bronchus and having its own arterial blood supply."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10476
name: buccopharyngeal membrane
def: "Membrane present in fetal life that separates the nasal cavities from the pharynx."
synonym: "oropharyngeal membrane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10477
name: bud
def: "Small protuberance resembling the bud of a plant and having the potential for growth and differentiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10478
name: olfactory bulb
def: "Region of the frontal lobe of the brain\, receiving input from neurons of the nasal mucosa and involved in the sense of smell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10479
name: bulbourethral gland
def: "One of two small glands located on each side of\, and inferior to\, the prostate\, draining to the urethra. Bulbourethral glands secrete a fluid component of the seminal fluid"
synonym: "Cowper’s gland" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10480
name: bulbus cordis
def: "Outflow tract of the embryonic heart between the primitive ventricle and the aorta."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10481
name: bundle of His
def: "Band of specialized cardiac muscle fibers connecting the atria with the ventricles of the heart."
synonym: "atrioventricular bundle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10482
name: bursa
def: "Padlike fluid-filled sac or sac-like cavity\, especially one reducing friction at a joint."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10483
name: Caesarian section
def: "Surgical operation for delivering a child by opening the mother’s abdominal wall and uterus."
synonym: "Caesarian delivery" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10484
name: canal
def: "In biology and medicine\, a relatively narrow tubular passage or channel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10485
name: cancer
def: "Disease resulting from the development of a malignant tumor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10486
name: capacitation
def: "Sum of biochemical changes undergone by mammalian spermatozoa in the female genital tract that enables them to penetrate and fertilize \(see fertilization\) an egg."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10487
name: caput epididymis
def: "Upper and larger extremity of the epididymis."
synonym: "globus major" []
synonym: "head of epididymis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10488
name: carcinogenesis
def: "Induction\, by chemical\, physical\, or biological agents\, of malignant neoplasms and thus of cancer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10489
name: cardiac
def: "Pertaining to the heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10490
name: cardiac jelly
def: "Gelatinous substance\, present between the endothelium and myocardium of the heart in early embryos\, that develops into the connective tissue of the endocardium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10491
name: cardiogenesis
def: "Development of the heart in the embryo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10492
name: cardiogenic
def: "Originating in the heart; describing anything caused by normal or abnormal function of the heart.\nPertaining to cardiogenesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10493
name: cardiovascular
def: "Pertaining to the heart and blood vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10494
name: cartilage
def: "Specialized\, fibrous connective tissue present in adults\, forming the temporary skeleton in the embryo\, providing a model in which the bones develop\, and constituting a part of the organism’s growth mechanism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10495
name: castration
def: "Removal of the testicles or ovaries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10496
name: cataract
def: "Partial or complete opacity \(clouding\) of the lens of the eye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10497
name: cauda epididymis
def: "Tail of the epididymis that opens into the ductus deferens; part of the reservoir of spermatozoa."
synonym: "globus minor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10498
name: caudal
def: "Situated more toward the cauda\, or tail\, than some specified reference point; toward the inferior \(in humans\) or posterior \(in animals\) end of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10499
name: cavitation
def: "Formation of a cavity\, as in formation of the amnion in mammalian development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10500
name: cecum
def: "Pouch connected to the junction of the small and large intestines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10501
name: central nervous system
def: "Part of an animal’s nervous system that exerts control over the rest of the nervous system; in vertebrates\, the brain and spinal cord protected within the dorsal body cavity \(cranial and spinal cavities\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10502
name: centromere
def: "Constricted region of a chromosome that joins the two chromatids to each other and attaches to spindle fibers in mitosis and meiosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10503
name: acrocentric centromere
def: "Having the centromere very close to one end."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10504
name: cerebral hemisphere
def: "Right or left half of the brain in sagittal section."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10505
name: cerebral palsy
def: "Condition marked by lack of muscle control\, resulting from brain damage before\, at\, or shortly after birth."
synonym: "spastic paralysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10506
name: cervical rib
def: "Supernumerary rib arising from a cervical vertebra."
synonym: "costa cervicalis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10507
name: cervix
def: "Narrow lower end of the uterus that opens into the vagina."
synonym: "cervix uteri" []
synonym: "uterine cervix" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10508
name: checkpoint pathway
def: "Type of pathway used in intracellular signaling\, activated in response to a cell’s own internal imbalance or to errors in its synthetic activities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10509
name: Chernoff–Kavlock assay
def: "Group of testing methods for assessing parturition\, postnatal growth\, and viability of prenatally exposed litters of test animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10510
name: chimera
def: "Animal consisting of genetically different cells derived from two \(or more\) different zygotes.\nSubstance\, such as an antibody\, created from the proteins or genes of two different species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10511
name: chondrification
def: "Formation of cartilage; transformation into cartilage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10512
name: chondroblast
def: "Cartilage-producing mesenchymal progenitor cell\, capable of proliferating and maturing into a chondrocyte or osteoblast."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10513
name: chondrocyte
def: "Any one of the cells embedded in the lacunae of the cartilage matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10514
name: chordin
def: "Secreted protein that dorsalizes early vertebrate embryonic tissues by binding to ventralizing transforming growth factor \\\(\\upbeta\\\) \(TGF-\\\(\\upbeta\\\)\)-like bone morphogenetic proteins\, sequestering them in latent complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10515
name: chorioallantoic placenta
def: "Placenta developed from the allantois and chorion\, establishing a nutritive and excretory connection between the blood of the fetus and that of the mother."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10516
name: chorion
def: "In human embryology\, the cellular\, outermost extra-embryonicmembrane\, composed of trophoblast lined with mesoderm.\nNote\: The chorion develops villi\, becomes vascularized by allantoic vessels\, and forms the fetal part of the placenta.\n\nIn mammalian embryology\, the cellular\, outer extra embryonic membrane\, not necessarily developing villi.\nIn biology\, the noncellular membrane covering eggs of various animals\, e.g.\, fish and insects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10517
name: chorionic gonadotropin
def: "Hormone secreted by the chorionic villi of the placenta in mammals\, especially human chorionic gonadotropin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10518
name: chorionic sac
def: "Outermost membranous sac that encloses the embryo in higher vertebrates \(reptiles\, birds\, and mammals\)\, lined by chorion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10519
name: chorionic villus
def: "Any of the tiny extensions from the chorion that contain fetal blood vessels and combine with the uterine tissue to form the placenta."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10520
name: chorioretinitis
def: "Inflammation of the choroid layer behind the retina of the eye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10521
name: choroid plexus
def: "Infoldings of blood vessels of the pia mater\, projecting into the cerebral ventricles\, covered with ependymal cells\, and secreting cerebrospinal fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10522
name: chromaffin cell
def: "Cell of the adrenal medulla that secretes epinephrine and norepinephrine\, and contains granules that are readily stained with chromate salts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10523
name: chromatid
def: "One of a pair of chromosomes arising by duplication during mitosis or by pairing during meiosis\, and joined together at the centromere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10524
name: chromosomal abnormality
def: "Abnormality in the number or structure of chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10525
name: chromosome deletion
def: "Loss of a chromosome or part of a chromosome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10526
name: chromosome ring
def: "Abnormal chromosome in which both ends have been lost and the two broken ends have reunited to form a ring-shaped structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10527
name: clastogen
def: "Agent causing chromosome breakage and \(or\) the consequent gain\, loss\, or rearrangement of pieces of chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10528
name: cleavage
def: "First few divisions of a fertilized egg."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10529
name: meroblastic cleavage
def: "Partial cleavage of the egg\, occurring in some animals\, such as reptiles and birds\, whose eggs have a large amount of yolk\, resulting in unequal blastomeres and only part of the egg progressing to further cell division; the yolk mass remains and has nutritive value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10530
name: holoblastic cleavage
def: "Cleavage producing separate\, equal blastomeres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10531
name: cleft
def: "Gap in soft tissue\, bone\, or both."
synonym: "fissure" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10532
name: cleft
def: "Split\, divided\, or partly divided into two."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10533
name: cleft lip
def: "Congenital malformation consisting of one or more clefts in the upper lip\, the result of failed closure in the embryo of the maxillary and median nasal processes."
synonym: "cheiloschisis" []
synonym: "hare lip" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10534
name: cleft palate
def: "Congenital fissure along the midline of the hard palate\, that may extend into the soft palate."
synonym: "palatoschisis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10535
name: cleft sternum
def: "Rare congenital malformation resulting from defective fusion in the embryo of paired mesodermal bands in the ventral midline."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10536
name: clitoris
def: "Erectile body in the female genitalia\, homologous with the penis in the male."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10537
name: cloaca
def: "Common cavity at the end of the digestive tract in vertebrates \(apart from most mammals\) into which are released both excretory and genital products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10538
name: cloacal membrane
def: "Membrane that covers the embryonic cloaca during the development of urinary and reproductive organs in those vertebrates where the cloaca occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10539
name: clover disease
def: "Condition caused by the continued ingestion of low but toxic levels of estrogens."
synonym: "estrogenism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10540
name: clubfoot
def: "Deformed foot that is twisted out of shape or position\, usually congenital \(congenital talipes equinovarus \(CTEV\)\)."
synonym: "talipes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10541
name: coarctation
def: "Narrowing or constriction of a short section of a blood vessel\, commonly of the aorta."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10542
name: cochlea
def: "Spiral cavity of the inner ear containing the organ of Corti that transduces sound into nerve impulses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10543
name: coelom
def: "Body cavity\, found in many animals\, lined with mesodermal epithelium and containing the digestive tract and other visceral organs"

[Term]
id: goldbook:10544
name: collagenous
def: "Rich in the fibrous connective tissue protein\, collagen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10545
name: collembolan reproduction test
def: "Procedure in which collembolans \(small soil-inhabiting insects\) are exposed to soil that has been treated with a test substance\, and adult mortality and reproductive output are studied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10546
name: coloboma
def: "Any defect resulting from incomplete closure of the retinal fissure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10547
name: combined repeated dose toxicity study
def: "Procedure to evaluate both general systemic toxicity\, with an emphasis on neurological endpoints\, and notably reproductive effects and developmental progression in rodents. Groups of males and females are administered a test substance in graded doses prior to mating\, during the mating period\, and subsequently \(up to two weeks post-mating in males and four days post-delivery in females\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10548
name: compaction
def: "Complication of labor in twin births in which there is attempted simultaneous expulsion of both twins\, so that the lower part of the mother’s pelvis is filled and further descent through the birth canal is prevented.\nIn embryology\, process during which blastomeres change their shape and align themselves tightly against each other to form the compact morula."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10549
name: conal growth hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that explains transposition of the great arteries of the heart by failure of the aortopulmonary septum to follow a spiral course during partitioning of the bulbus cordis and truncus arteriosus in the process of forming the aorta and pulmonary trunk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10550
name: conception
def: "Formation of a viable zygote by the union of a spermatozoon and an ovum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10551
name: conceptus
def: "Embryo and associated membranes in the uterus\, especially during the early stages of pregnancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10552
name: congenital
def: "Present from birth\, as of a disease or physical abnormality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10553
name: congenital adrenal hyperplasia
def: "Group of disorders caused by hyperplasia of the adrenal cortex or by malignant tumors\, resulting in excess secretion of adrenocortical androgenic hormones\, and characterized by masculinization of women\, feminization of men\, or precocious puberty in the male."
synonym: "adrenogenital syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10554
name: congenital malformation
def: "Malformation existing at birth\, or developing during the first month of life\, regardless of the cause."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10555
name: conjoined twin
def: "One of a pair of identical twins fused together with varying degrees of union and of residual duplication of organs."
synonym: "Siamese twin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10556
name: connective tissue
def: "Extracellular matrix of fibrous proteins and glycoproteins\, with associated cells such as fibroblasts\, that fills the spaces between and within organs and tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10557
name: contraceptive
def: "Anything that prevents\, or reduces the likelihood of pregnancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10558
name: convulsion
def: "Sudden\, violent\, irregular movement of the body caused by involuntary contraction of muscles and associated with brain disorders\, such as epilepsy\, fever in children\, or with drug or alcohol abuse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10559
name: corpus cavernosum
def: "Either of the two columns of erectile tissue forming the body of the clitoris \(corpus cavernosum clitoridis\) or penis \(corpus cavernosum penis\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10560
name: corpus luteum
def: "Glandular mass of yellowish tissue in the ovary\, formed by a Graafian ovarian follicle that has matured and released its oocyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10561
name: cortex
def: "Outer layer of an organ such as the kidney\, the cerebellum\, or the adrenal gland."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10562
name: cerebral cortex
def: "Outer layer of the cerebrum\, composed of folded grey matter\, playing an important role in consciousness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10563
name: cranial placodes
def: "Thickenings in the surface ectoderm of the embryo associated with future eye and ear regions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10564
name: cranial suture
def: "Line where the bony plates of the skull are joined together by fibrous bands of tissue\, easily felt in the newborn before closure by ossification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10565
name: craniofacial
def: "Pertaining to the cranium and the face."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10566
name: craniorachischisis
def: "Neural tube defect in which both the cranium and vertebral column remain open."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10567
name: cranioschisis
def: "Developmental failure of the cranial sutures to close completely\, especially at the occiput\, usually leading to grossly defective development of the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10568
name: craniosynostosis
def: "Premature closure of the cranial sutures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10569
name: cranium
def: "Bony structure surrounding the brain\, excluding the bones of the face."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10570
name: Cre/loxP
def: "Bacterial system in which the Cre protein mediates DNA recombination between specific DNA sequences known as lox-P sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10571
name: cretinism
def: "Developmental disorder caused by deficiency of thyroid hormone\, characterized by severe mental retardation and stunted physical growth\, sometimes resulting from maternal iodine deficiency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10572
name: cri du chat syndrome
def: "Hereditary congenital syndrome due to deletion of the short arm of chromosome 5."
synonym: "5p - syndrome" []
synonym: "Lejeune syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10573
name: crista
def: "One of the inward projections or folds of the inner membrane of a mitochondrion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10574
name: critical period
def: "Specific time in the development of a biological system when it is particularly vulnerable to injury or misdirection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10575
name: Crouzon syndrome
def: "Group of autosomal dominant genetic diseases characterized by midfacial hypoplasia\, craniosynostosis\, exophthalmos\, and a shortened head."
synonym: "branchial arch syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10576
name: crypt
def: "Small tubular gland\, pit\, or depression."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10577
name: cryptophthalmos
def: "Failure of the eyelids to develop with a fissure between them\, usually with defective formation of the eyeballs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10578
name: cumulus oophorus
def: "Mass of follicular cells surrounding the oocyte in the Graafian follicle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10579
name: cyanosis
def: "Bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes due to excessive concentration of deoxygenated haemoglobin\, owing to poor circulation or inadequate oxygenation of the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10580
name: reproductive cycle
def: "Cycle of physiological changes in the female reproductive organs\, from the time of fertilization of the oocyte through gestation and parturition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10581
name: cyclopia
def: "Rare form of holoprosencephaly\, a congenital disorder characterized by a single orbital fossa due to the failure of the embryonic prosencephalon to divide the orbits of the eye correctly into two cavities."
synonym: "cyclocephaly" []
synonym: "synophthalmia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10582
name: cyst
def: "In an animal or plant\, thin-walled hollow organ or cavity containing a liquid secretion.\nSac\, vesicle\, or bladder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10583
name: cytogenetics
def: "Branch of genetics in which the structure and function of chromosomes and of other cell constituents concerned with heritable properties and their expression are studied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10584
name: cytomegalovirus
def: "Type of Herpes virus that usually produces very mild symptoms in an infected person but may cause severe neurological damage in people with weakened immune systems and in the newborn."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10585
name: cytotrophoblast
def: "Inner cellular layer of the trophectoderm \(trophoblast\); part of the mammalian placenta."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10586
name: Daphnia magna reproduction test
def: "Procedure in which young female Daphnia are exposed to a substance added to the water and numbers of living offspring and surviving parent organisms are recorded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10587
name: decidua
def: "Modified endometrial layer that lines the uterus during pregnancy and is shed with the afterbirth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10588
name: decidual cell
def: "Enlarged\, ovoid\, connective tissue cell appearing in the endometrium during pregnancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10589
name: decidual cell response technique
def: "Biological test method in which pseudopregnant rats undergo a surgical treatment of the uterus to induce uterine differentiation and proliferation\, resulting in massive tissue growth that mimics the response of the uterus during normal blastocyst implantation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10590
name: decidualization
def: "Changes in response to progesterone that include the eosinophilic proliferation around arterioles after ovulation or progesterone action on endometrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10591
name: deciduum
def: "Swelling of a uterine crypt produced by its reaction to an implanted embryo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10592
name: decussate
def: "Pertaining to two or more things that cross or intersect each other to form an X."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10593
name: defeminized gonadotropin secretion
def: "Gonadotropin secretion without female sex hormones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10594
name: demasculinized
def: "Describing a male animal from which the testicles have been removed."
synonym: "castrated" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10595
name: dermis
def: "Layer of the skin deep under the epidermis\, consisting of a bed of vascularized connective tissue and containing the nerves and organs of sensation\, the hair roots\, and sebaceous and sweat glands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10596
name: desynapsis
def: "Failure of synapsis due to separation of homologous chromosomes after initial pairing in meiosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10597
name: developmental biology
def: "Study of biological development from fertilization to adulthood\, usually with an emphasis on the prenatal period and regulation of morphogenesis\, and neural development on through adolescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10598
name: developmental neurotoxicity study
def: "Procedure in which female rodents are exposed to a test substance from the time of implantation through all of lactation; offspring may be exposed during the preweaning period\, either directly or through milk\, and are studied with regard to neurologic and behavioral abnormalities during postnatal development and until adulthood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10599
name: developmental susceptibility gene
def: "Any gene that encodes a gene product that can be altered by environmental agents to cause disturbance of normal development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10600
name: developmental toxicology
def: "Study of adverse effects on the developing organism that result from exposure of either parent to an agent or substance prior to conception\, of the mother and fetus during prenatal development\, and of the child from birth until the time development is completed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10601
name: dextrocardia
def: "Congenital defect in which the apex of the heart is directed toward the right side of the thorax \(isolated dextrocardia\)\, or the heart is found in a mirror image position on the right side of the body \(dextrocardia situs inversus\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10602
name: dextroposition
def: "Displacement to the right."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10603
name: DiGeorge syndrome
def: "Condition caused by the deletion of a small piece of chromosome 22 near the middle of the chromosome at a location designated 22q11.2."
synonym: "DiGeorge anomaly" []
synonym: "Shprintzen syndrome" []
synonym: "Strong syndrome" []
synonym: "congenital thymic aplasia" []
synonym: "conotruncal anomaly face syndrome" []
synonym: "thymic hypoplasia" []
synonym: "velocardiofacial syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10604
name: diaphragm
def: "Dome-shaped muscular membranous partition separating the thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity in mammals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10605
name: diathesis
def: "Hereditary predisposition to a particular medical condition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10606
name: diencephalon
def: "Posterior part of the forebrain \(prosencephalon\)\, containing the hypothalamus and other thalamic components and enclosing the third ventricle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10607
name: diethylstilbestrol
def: "Synthetic nonsteroidal substance with estrogenic activity."
synonym: "4,4′-[(3<em>E</em>)-hex-3-ene-3,4-diyl]diphenol" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10608
name: diploid
def: "Cell or nucleus containing two complete sets of chromosomes\, one from each parent\, or an organism composed of such cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10609
name: embryonic disc
def: "Bilayer plate of cells in the blastocyst from which the mammalian embryo develops."
synonym: "blastodisc" []
synonym: "germinal disc" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10610
name: dispermy
def: "Entrance of two spermatozoa into one egg."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10611
name: disruption
def: "Disturbance that interrupts a process or disorganizes a structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10612
name: distal
def: "Situated away from the centre of the body or from the point of attachment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10613
name: diverticulum
def: "Outpouching of a hollow structure in the body\, that may be acquired \(as in colonic diverticulitis\) or be present at birth \(e.g.\, Meckel diverticulum or Zenker diverticulum\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10614
name: dominant
def: "Of a gene\, determining the phenotype even when present only in one copy or inherited from only one parent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10615
name: dorsal
def: "On or relating to the upper side or back of an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10616
name: drug metabolizing enzyme
def: "Any enzyme that metabolizes xenobiotics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10617
name: duct
def: "Vessel for transporting fluids such as lymph\, or glandular secretions such as tears or bile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10618
name: ductus arteriosis
def: "Fetal blood vessel connecting the pulmonary artery to the proximal descending aorta."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10619
name: dung fly test
def: "Procedure using dung flies of the order Diptera where a test substance is mixed with bovine feces to which eggs of the fly are added; number\, sex\, and morphology are determined when the last adult has emerged."
synonym: "Dipterian dung fly test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10620
name: duodenum
def: "First portion of the small intestine\, between the stomach and the jejunum\, where bile and pancreatic digestive juices enter the intestinal tract."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10621
name: dysgenesis
def: "Defective or abnormal development of an organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10622
name: dysgonesis
def: "Dysgenesis of the gonads."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10623
name: dysmenorrhea
def: "Difficult and painful menstruation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10624
name: dysmorphia
def: "Abnormality of shape of a body part or organ\, often referring to a birth defect."
synonym: "dysmorphism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10625
name: dysmorphogenesis
def: "Production of dysmorphia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10626
name: dysplasia
def: "Abnormal development of an organ or tissue identified by morphological examination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10627
name: dysostosis
def: "Defect in the normal ossification of fetal cartilage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10628
name: early life stage test
def: "Toxicity test using an organism in an early life stage such as the embryo or larva\, noting that this stage is often the most sensitive part of the species life cycle."
synonym: "ELS test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10629
name: earthworm reproduction test
def: "Procedure in which adult worms \(Eisenia sp.\) are exposed to a test substance mixed into the soil; number\, weight and behaviour of the adult worms are studied after four weeks and the number of juveniles hatched is counted after a further four weeks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10630
name: eclampsia
def: "Acute\, life-threatening complication of pregnancy characterized by tonic-clonic seizures \(convulsions\)\, usually in a woman who has developed pre-eclampsia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10631
name: ecogenetics
def: "Study of the influence of hereditary factors on the response of individuals or populations to environmental factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10632
name: ectoderm
def: "In an embryo\, outermost layer that develops into the epidermis of the skin and the nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10633
name: ectopia
def: "Abnormal location or malpositioning of an organ or body part\, usually congenital."
synonym: "ectopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10634
name: ectopic expression
def: "Expression of a gene in a tissue in which it is not normally expressed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10635
name: ectopic pregnancy
def: "Complication of pregnancy in which the embryo implants \(see implantation\) and develops outside the cavity of the uterus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10636
name: ectrodactyly
def: "Congenital absence or deficiency of one or more of the central digits of the hand or foot."
synonym: "cleft hand" []
synonym: "lobster-claw deformity" []
synonym: "split hand" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10637
name: ectrosyndactyly
def: "Congenital absence or deficiency of one or more of the central digits of the hand or foot with fusion of the existing ones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10638
name: edema
def: "Presence of abnormally large amounts of fluid in body cavities and intercellular spaces of tissues."
synonym: "oedema" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10639
name: efferent duct
def: "Seminal duct leading from the testis to the head of the epididymis."
synonym: "efferent ductule" []
synonym: "male efferent duct" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10640
name: egg
def: "Female gamete after completion of the second meiotic division."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10641
name: ejaculation
def: "Expulsion of semen from the genital ducts and urethra\, usually resulting from a reflex process during sexual stimulation"

[Term]
id: goldbook:10642
name: ejaculatory duct
def: "Passage formed by the junction of the duct of the seminal vesicles and the ductus deferens through which semen enters the urethra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10643
name: electroporation
def: "Means of introducing molecules into cells by transiently permeabilizing their membranes with brief electric field pulses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10644
name: embryogenesis
def: "Development and growth of an embryo in the period from formation of the bilaminar embryo to the beginning of the fetal period \(in humans\, the second through the eighth week after conception\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10645
name: embryoid body
def: "Structure resembling an embryo which is formed by embryonic stem cells or teratocarcinoma cells when they are removed from a growth-promoting medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10646
name: embryonic induction
def: "Process whereby the development of one group of cells\, called the competent region\, is altered by an inducing factor from another group\, called a signaling center or organizer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10647
name: embryo transfer
def: "Process of implanting a fertilized ovum into a uterus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10648
name: embryo transport rate analysis
def: "Method to evaluate the potential for early embryonic loss in rodents caused by accelerated or retarded arrival of embryos into the uterus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10649
name: embryology
def: "Science of the origin and development of the organism from fertilization of the oocyte to the beginning of fetal life \(in humans\, the end of the eighth week\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10650
name: embryonic period
def: "Period from fertilization to the end of major organogenesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10651
name: embryonic stem cell
def: "Undifferentiated\, pluripotent cell from an early stage of the pre-implantation embryo \(inner cell mass of the blastocyst\)."
synonym: "ES cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10652
name: encephalitis
def: "Inflammation of the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10653
name: encephalocele
def: "Congenital protrusion of brain tissue through a fissure or defect in the skull."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10654
name: endocardial cushion
def: "Subset of cells in the primordial tube-like heart that is essential to the development of the ventricular septum and the atrioventricular valves of the heart."
synonym: "atrioventricular canal cushion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10655
name: endocardium
def: "Endothelial lining of the heart chambers\, also containing small blood vessels and a few bundles of smooth muscle\, continuous with the endothelium of the great blood vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10656
name: endochondral ossification
def: "Formation and growth of bone tissue \(especially of the long bones\) during fetal development of the mammalian skeletal system\, and in repair of bone fractures\, that takes place in the presence of cartilage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10657
name: endocrine
def: "Pertaining to hormones or to glands that secrete hormones directly into the bloodstream."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10658
name: endocrine disrupter
def: "Exogenous substance that\, at some dose\, alters function\(s\) of the endocrine system and consequently causes adverse health effects in an intact organism\, its progeny\, or \(sub\)populations."
synonym: "endocrine modifier" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10659
name: endoderm
def: "Innermost of the three primary germ layers of an embryo\, developing into the gastrointestinal tract\, the lungs\, and associated structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10660
name: endometrium
def: "Inner lining of the uterus that is shed during menstruation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10661
name: endothelium
def: "Layer of flat cells that line the blood vessels\, lymphatic vessels\, and the chambers of the heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10662
name: environmental factor
def: "Physical\, chemical\, or biological agent or condition in the environment that has the potential to affect development and health of an organism\, either in a positive or a negative manner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10663
name: epaxial
def: "Located above or on the dorsal side of an axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10664
name: epiblast
def: "Primitive ectoderm of the early embryo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10665
name: epicardium
def: "Inner layer of the pericardium\, a sac of fibrous tissue that surrounds and lies upon the heart and the base of the great vessels."
synonym: "visceral pericardium" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10666
name: epidemiology
def: "Study of the distribution and determinants of health-related states or events in specified populations and the application of this study for the control of health problems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10667
name: epidermis
def: "In vertebrates\, protective outer stratified squamous layer of the skin consisting mainly of keratinocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10668
name: epididymis
def: "Tightly-coiled\, thin-walled tube that conducts sperm from the testis to the vas deferens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10669
name: epididymal appendix
def: "Cystic structure sometimes found on the head of the epididymis."
synonym: "appendix epididymidis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10670
name: epilepsy
def: "Chronic neurological disorder marked by sudden recurrent episodes of sensory disturbance\, loss of consciousness\, or convulsions\, associated with abnormal electrical activity in the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10671
name: epiphyseal plate
def: "The disk of hyaline cartilage between the metaphysis and the epiphysis of an immature long bone that permits the bone to grow longer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10672
name: epispadias
def: "Malformation in which the urethra does not develop to full length\, opening typically on the dorsum of the penis \(and rarely\, in females\, between the clitoris and labia\); frequently associated with exstrophy of the bladder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10673
name: epistasis
def: "Situation in which the phenotypic expression of one gene obscures the phenotypic effects of another gene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10674
name: epithelium
def: "Sheet of one or more layers of cells covering the internal and external surfaces of the body and hollow organs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10675
name: epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition
def: "Sequence of events\, where epithelial cells detach\, migrate\, and become mesenchymal stem cells that can differentiate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10676
name: epoxide hydrolase
def: "Detoxification enzyme \(EC 3.3.2.9\)\, located mainly in the endoplasmic reticulum\, that hydrolyzes epoxides\, converting them to metabolites that can be more rapidly excreted."
synonym: "epoxide hydratase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10677
name: erectile dysfunction
def: "Inability of a male to have or to maintain an erection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10678
name: estradiol
def: "Steroid sex hormone\, involved in many regulatory processes\, but most prominently in the development of female sex organs\, the menstrual cycle\, and pregnancy."
synonym: "17β-estradiol" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10679
name: estriol
def: "Hormonally active metabolite of estradiol\, eliminated in urine\, especially during pregnancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10680
name: estrogen
def: "Any substance\, natural or synthetic\, that exerts the hormonal effects of the natural estrogen\, estradiol\, usually by interaction with the estrogen receptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10681
name: <em>in vitro</em> estrogen activity assay
def: "Procedure in which a tumor cell line is exposed to a test substance and activation of the estrogen receptor is studied by a reporter molecule in order to assess the estrogen-sensitivity of the tumor.\nTest using estrogen-sensitive cells to evaluate estrogenic activity of a substance or environmental sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10682
name: estrogen-mimetic
def: "Behaving like an estrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10683
name: estrogenic
def: "Exhibiting hormonal activity similar to that of estrogens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10684
name: estrous cycle
def: "Sequence of recurring physiological uterine\, ovarian\, and other changes\, induced by reproductive hormones in higher female animals\, resulting in readiness for insemination. This cycle starts in adulthood \(at menarche\)\, transiently discontinues during pregnancy\, and disappears at the menopause."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10685
name: estrus
def: "Recurrent period of sexual receptivity and arousal in the female of most mammals except the higher primates\, during which conception is possible."
synonym: "heat" []
synonym: "oestrus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10686
name: estrus syndrome
def: "Persistent estrus caused by elevated estrogenic activity\, either due to endogenous estrogens \(e.g.\, in polycystic ovary syndrome\) or due to exogenous substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10687
name: euploid
def: "Referring to a cell or organism with chromosomes present in an integral multiple of the haploid number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10688
name: eutherian
def: "Subclass of mammals having a placenta through which the fetus is nourished."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10689
name: eventration
def: "Protrusion of omentum and/or intestine through a defect or weakness in the abdominal wall while the skin remains intact."
synonym: "herniation of intestines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10690
name: evocation
def: "Specific induction of a tissue within a developing embryo that results from the action of a single hormone or other chemical \(the evocator\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10691
name: excurrent
def: "Pertaining to a vessel \(or opening\) conveying fluid outwards."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10692
name: exencephaly
def: "Exposed brain resulting from failure of the neural tube to close and absence of the top of the skull."
synonym: "exencephalus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10693
name: exocrine
def: "Relating to or denoting glands that secrete through ducts opening on to superficial epithelium \(e.g.\, the intestinal tract\) rather than into the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10694
name: exomphalos
def: "Hernia in which bowel or omentum protrudes through the abdominal wall under the skin at the umbilicus."
synonym: "omphalocele" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10695
name: exophthalmia
def: "Marked protrusion of the eyeballs\, usually resulting from an increased volume of the orbital contents."
synonym: "exophthalmos" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10696
name: expressed sequence tag
def: "Partial or full complementary DNA sequence that can serve as a marker for a region of the genome that encodes an expressed product."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10697
name: exstrophy
def: "Congenital malformation in which a hollow organ has its interior exposed through eversion; most commonly observed in the urinary bladder \(ectopia vesicae\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10698
name: external genitalia
def: "Genitalia visible outside the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10699
name: extracellular matrix
def: "Mesh of molecules\, secreted by cells into the surrounding extracellular space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10700
name: extraembryonic membrane
def: "One of the membranes that surrounds the embryo\, which include the yolk sac\, allantois\, amnion\, and chorion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10701
name: extraembryonic mesoderm
def: "Cells outside the embryo that\, although derived from the zygote\, are not part of the embryo proper and contribute to the fetal membranes \(yolk sac\, allantois\, amnion\, and chorion\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10702
name: extrinsic pathway
def: "Apoptotic pathway of cell death initiated upon occupancy of so-called death receptors at the cell surface with their ligands\, such as Fas/CD95 and tumor necrosis factor-\\\(\\upalpha\\\)\, and involving activation of caspase 8."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10703
name: F1 generation
def: "Indicating ‘first filial’ generation\, denoting offspring from genotypically \(see genotype\) distinct parents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10704
name: facial cleft
def: "Congenital anomaly resulting from the failure of facial structures in the embryo to fuse properly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10705
name: facies
def: "In medicine\, facial expression of an individual that is typical of a particular syndrome\, disease\, or condition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10706
name: fallopian tube
def: "One of a pair of tubes\, on either side of the upper or outer extremity of the ovary\, that provides a path by which an ovum travels from the ovary to the fundus of the uterus."
synonym: "oviduct" []
synonym: "salpinx" []
synonym: "uterine tube" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10707
name: fecundation
def: "Process of fertilization."
synonym: "impregnation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10708
name: fecundity
def: "Ability to produce offspring within a given period of time.\nQuantity of reproductive output."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10709
name: feminization
def: "Development of female characteristics as part of normal female maturation\, or\, in a male\, pathologically as a result of endocrine imbalance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10710
name: female fertility
def: "Ability to conceive \(see conception\).\nProduction of live offspring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10711
name: male fertility
def: "Ability to induce conception."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10712
name: Fertility Assessment by Continuous Breeding
def: "Reproductive toxicity test in rodents in which female animals are repeatedly mated and frequency and size of litter and other parameters are measured; second or later generation fertility is also studied."
synonym: "Reproductive Assessment by Continuous Breeding (RACB)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10713
name: fertilization
def: "In reproductive biology\, union of male and female gametes to form a zygote from which an embryo can develop."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10714
name: fetal alcohol syndrome
def: "Condition developing in the fetus and resulting in congenital abnormalities\, caused by alcohol intake by the mother during pregnancy\, typically characterized by decreased cognitive development\, stunted growth and a characteristic facies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10715
name: fetal pole
def: "Thickening on the margin of the yolk sac of a fetus during pregnancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10716
name: FETAX
def: "Assay for teratogenicity using embryos of the frog\, Xenopus laevis."
synonym: "frog embryo teratogenesis assay <em>Xenopus</em>" []
synonym: "frog embryo teratogenesis assay Xenopus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10717
name: fetoscopy
def: "Procedure in which a fetus may be directly observed in utero\, using a fiber-optic endoscope \(fetoscope\) introduced through a small incision in the abdomen under local anesthesia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10718
name: fetus
def: "Young mammal within the uterus of the mother\, from the visible completion of characteristic organogenesis until birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10719
name: fibroblast
def: "Flat fibrous tissue cell\, with stellate or spindle shape\, associated with the formation of collagen fibers and intercellular extracellular matrix."
synonym: "desmocyte" []
synonym: "fibrocyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10720
name: fibroblast growth factor
def: "Any member of a family of more than twenty protein growth factors involved in angiogenesis\, wound healing\, and\, in early embryonic development\, induction of mesoderm\, patterning and morphogenesis\, and development of the limbs and brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10721
name: fish embryo test
def: "Procedure in which fish \(often zebrafish\, Danio rerio\) eggs and embryos are exposed to a test chemical and subsequent development or death of the animals is studied."
synonym: "fish embryo acute toxicity test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10722
name: fish reproduction assay
def: "Procedure in which sexually mature male and spawning female fish are exposed to a test substance and multiple endpoints\, including egg production\, endocrine activity\, vitellogenin\, and gonadal histopathology are measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10723
name: fish sexual development test
def: "Procedure in which fish are exposed from the time of fertilization of the egg until sexual differentiation is completed and markers of possible endocrine disruptor activity are studied\, including vitellogenin concentration\, gonadal histopathology\, sex ratios\, and the occurrence of intersex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10724
name: fistula
def: "Permanent abnormal passage from an internal organ to the body surface or between two internal organs or structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10725
name: fitness
def: "Ability to survive and reproduce."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10726
name: hepatic flexure
def: "Bend between the ascending colon and the transverse colon."
synonym: "right colic flexure" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10727
name: foetus
def: "Alternative spelling of fetus found in British English usage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10728
name: folic acid
def: "Water-soluble B vitamin involved in breakdown of carbohydrates\, releasing energy and promoting biosynthesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10729
name: follicle
def: "Small\, secretory sac\, such as the dental follicles that enclose the teeth before eruption or the hair follicles within the epidermis.\nFluid- or colloid-filled ball of cells in some glands such as the thyroid gland and the ovaries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10730
name: follicle-stimulating hormone
def: "Hormone secreted by the anterior pituitary that stimulates the Graafian follicles of the ovary and subsequent follicular maturation\, and in the male contributes to inducing spermatogenesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10731
name: follicle-stimulating hormone releasing hormone
def: "Hormone\, released by the hypothalamus\, inducing the secretion of follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone by the pituitary gland."
synonym: "gonadoliberin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10732
name: follicular atresia
def: "Degeneration and resorption of an ovarian follicle before it reaches maturity and ruptures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10733
name: follicular phase
def: "First half of the human menstrual cycle\, during which one or more follicles in the ovary may mature\, culminating in ovulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10734
name: fontanelle
def: "Any of several soft spots in specific locations on the skull of the newborn where the bones have not yet fused and the brain is covered by skin and a tough membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10735
name: foramen
def: "Opening\, hole."
synonym: "foramina" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10736
name: foramen magnum
def: "Large foramen in the base of the skull\, through which the spinal cord passes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10737
name: foramen ovale
def: "In the fetal heart\, opening that allows shunting of blood between the right and left atria and normally closes at birth.\nOne of the larger foramina in the base of the skull."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10738
name: forward genetics
def: "Approach to genetic investigation in which the aim is to identify the gene that governs a particular function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10739
name: fossa
def: "In anatomy\, a depression or hollow\, often in a bone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10740
name: fossa ovalis
def: "Oval depression in the heart\, on the lower part of the septum of the right atrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10741
name: frontonasal dysplasia
def: "Congenital malformation of the midface in which the nose has a flat\, wide appearance\, the eyes may be wide-set\, and there is a groove of varying size\, running down the middle of the face across the nose."
synonym: "Tessier cleft numbers 0/14" []
synonym: "frontonasal dysostosis" []
synonym: "frontonasal malformation" []
synonym: "median cleft face syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10742
name: functional genomics
def: "Development and implementation of technologies to characterize the mechanisms through which genes and their products function and interact with each other and with the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10743
name: fundus
def: "Part of a hollow organ \(e.g.\, the uterus\) that is furthest from its opening."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10744
name: gall bladder
def: "Pear-shaped muscular sac underneath the liver that stores bile secreted by the liver before its release into the duodenum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10745
name: gamete
def: "Reproductive cell \(e.g.\, sperm or egg\) containing a haploid set of chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10746
name: gamete intrafallopian transfer
def: "Technique of assisted reproduction by placing eggs and sperm into a woman’s Fallopian tubes in order that fertilization may occur there."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10747
name: gametogenesis
def: "Development of male or female gametes to maturity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10748
name: gastroschisis
def: "Congenital defect characterized by a fissure in the anterior abdominal wall through which the small and \(or\) large intestine protrude."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10749
name: gastrula
def: "Embryonic stage that develops from two to three germ layers during gastrulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10750
name: gastrulation
def: "Stage of embryo development in which the two-layered blastula transforms into the gastrula by developing ectoderm and endoderm\, as well as a third layer \(mesoderm\)\, through the movement of specific cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10751
name: gene product
def: "Both the messenger RNA resulting from transcription of a gene and the proteins and peptides translated from that mRNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10752
name: gene silencing
def: "Prevention of the expression of a specific gene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10753
name: gene targeting
def: "Use of homologous recombination to change a gene\, e.g.\, to delete a gene\, remove exons\, add a gene\, or introduce point mutations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10754
name: genital
def: "Of or relating to human or animal reproduction.\nOf or relating to the genital organs.\nOf or relating to the final stage of psychosexual maturation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10755
name: genitalia
def: "Plural of genital.\nExternally visible sex organs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10756
name: genital fold
def: "Embryonic structure that will develop into the penis and scrotum in males or the labia in females."
synonym: "genital ridge" []
synonym: "genitourinary ridge" []
synonym: "labioscrotal swelling" []
synonym: "urogenital ridge" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10757
name: genital organ
def: "Organ of reproduction or generation\, both external and internal to the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10758
name: genital tract
def: "Genital passages of the urogenital apparatus."
synonym: "genital duct" []
synonym: "reproductive tract" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10759
name: genital tubercle
def: "Primordium of the penis or clitoris."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10760
name: genotoxic
def: "Capable of causing a change to the structure of the genome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10761
name: germ cell gene mutation assay
def: "Procedure in which mutations induced by an agent are studied in germ line cells or somatic stem cells from a transgenic animal \(rodent\)\, containing reporter genes for detection of various mutations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10762
name: germ line cell
def: "Gamete or an embryonic cell that can develop into a gamete."
synonym: "germ cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10763
name: germ layer
def: "Any of three distinct layers of cells \(endoderm\, ectoderm\, and mesoderm\) that become recognizable as the embryo develops."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10764
name: gestation
def: "Period between conception and birth during which an embryo or fetus is carried in the uterus of a female mammal."
synonym: "pregnancy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10765
name: gestation period
def: "Time from conception to birth\, during which a fetus develops."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10766
name: gigantism
def: "Condition of abnormal size or overgrowth of the entire body or of any of its parts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10767
name: gland
def: "Organized aggregation of cells functioning as a secretory or excretory organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10768
name: glans
def: "Any small rounded mass or gland-like body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10769
name: glans clitoridis
def: "Rounded mass of sensitive erectile tissue that forms the head of the clitoris."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10770
name: glans penis
def: "Rounded mass of sensitive erectile tissue at the distal end \(head\) of the penis\, analogous to the glans clitoridis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10771
name: glaucoma
def: "Disease of the eye characterized by increased intraocular pressure\, resulting in atrophy of the optic nerve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10772
name: glomerulus
def: "Tuft or a cluster\, as of a plexus of capillary blood vessels or nerve fibers \(e.g.\, capillaries of the filtration apparatus of the kidney\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10773
name: glucocorticoid
def: "Any of a group of steroid hormones\, such as cortisone\, produced by the adrenal cortex\, inhibiting inflammation\, and mediating a response to stress that alters protein\, fat\, and carbohydrate metabolism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10774
name: glycolysis
def: "Biochemical breakdown of glucose into pyruvic acid with the production of ATP."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10775
name: gonad
def: "Organ in animals that produces gametes\, i.e.\, the testis or the ovary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10776
name: gonadotropin
def: "Glycopeptide hormone\, produced by the fetal placenta\, that maintains the function of the corpus luteum during the first few weeks of pregnancy."
synonym: "gonadotrophin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10777
name: gonadotropin-releasing hormone
def: "Any factor from the hypothalamus that stimulates the anterior pituitary to release both follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone."
synonym: "gonadoliberin" []
synonym: "luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone (LHRH)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10778
name: Graafian follicle
def: "Mature ovarian follicle within which an oocyte attains full size and which ruptures during ovulation to release the ovum."
synonym: "vesicular ovarian follicle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10779
name: granulocyte
def: "White blood cell\, distinct from a lymphocyte\, containing cytoplasmic granules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10780
name: granulosa
def: "Pertaining to cells of the cumulus oophorus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10781
name: grey matter
def: "Regions of the brain and spinal cord that are made up primarily of cell bodies and dendrites of nerve cells rather than of myelinated axons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10782
name: growth factor
def: "Naturally occurring or genetically engineered protein or steroid hormone that binds to a receptor to regulate cell growth\, proliferation\, or differentiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10783
name: growth hormone
def: "Peptide hormone secreted by the anterior pituitary that stimulates growth\, energy metabolism\, and cell proliferation."
synonym: "somatotropic hormone (STH)" []
synonym: "somatotropin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10784
name: growth hormone releasing factor
def: "Substance produced in the hypothalamus that regulates release of growth hormone by the anterior pituitary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10785
name: gubernaculum
def: "Fetal mesenchyme ligament that passes through the anterior abdominal wall and connects the lower pole of each developing gonad with the developing scrotum in men\, and with the developing labia majora in women."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10786
name: haploid
def: "State in which a cell contains only one set of chromosomes."
synonym: "monoploid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10787
name: hemal
def: "Pertaining to blood or blood vessels.\nSituated\, in common with the heart and major blood vessels\, on the ventral side of the body with respect to the vertebral column or its embryonic precursors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10788
name: hemangioma
def: "Benign tumor formed by a vascular malformation present at birth or developing during life\, in which proliferation of blood vessels leads to a vascular tangle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10789
name: hematopoiesis
def: "Development of each of the types of blood cell from common precursor cells\, located mainly in the bone marrow."
synonym: "hemopoiesis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10790
name: hemimelia
def: "Congenital partial absence of all or part of the distal half of a limb."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10791
name: hemivertebra
def: "Congenital defect of the vertebral column in which one side of a vertebra fails to develop completely due to failure of the chondrification center to form on that side."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10792
name: hemolytic disease of the newborn
def: "Severe form of anemia in a fetus or newborn infant caused by incompatibility with the mother’s blood type\, typically when the mother is rhesus factor negative \(Rh -ve\) and produces antibodies which attack \(Rh +ve\) fetal blood through the placenta."
synonym: "erythroblastosis" []
synonym: "erythroblastosis fetalis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10793
name: hepatosplenomegaly
def: "Enlargement of the liver and spleen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10794
name: hermaphrodite
def: "Having the reproductive organs and many of the secondary sex characteristics of both sexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10795
name: hermaphroditism
def: "State of being hermaphrodite."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10796
name: hernia
def: "Protrusion of an organ or structure through an abnormal opening in connective tissue or in the muscle wall of the cavity in which it is normally enclosed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10797
name: hiatal hernia
def: "Hernia of a part of the stomach through a rupture of the diaphragm at the esophageal opening."
synonym: "hiatus hernia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10798
name: herpes
def: "Family of viruses including herpes simplex types 1 and 2\, and herpes zoster \(also called varicella zoster\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10799
name: Hershberger bioassay
def: "Short-term in vivo procedure in which the androgenic or antiandrogenic effects of a test substance are studied in castrated\, peripubertal male rodents\, as indicated by changes in the weights of androgen-dependent tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10800
name: hippocampus
def: "Structural component of the limbic system of the vertebrate brain involved in short-term and long-term memory and spatial navigation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10801
name: Hirschsprung syndrome
def: "Absence of nerve ganglia throughout all or part of the gastrointestinal tract\, causing dysregulation of intestinal motility."
synonym: "aganglionic megacolon" []
synonym: "congenital aganglionic megacolon" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10802
name: hirsutism
def: "Presence of excessive bodily and facial hair."
synonym: "hypertrichosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10803
name: holoprosencephaly
def: "Birth defect in which the embryonic forebrain fails to divide completely to form the cerebral hemispheres."
synonym: "arhinencephaly" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10804
name: homeobox
def: "Any of a class of closely similar DNA sequences occurring in various genes and involved in regulating pattern formation\, segmentation\, and morphogenesis during embryonic development in many species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10805
name: homeobox gene
def: "Gene containing a homeobox sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10806
name: homeosis
def: "Replacement of part of one segment of an insect or other segmented animal by a structure characteristic of a different segment\, especially through mutation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10807
name: homologous
def: "Having the same relationship\, relative position\, or structure.\n\(Of organs\) Similar in position\, structure\, and evolutionary origin but not necessarily in function.\n\(Of chromosomes\) Pairing at meiosis and having the same structural features and pattern of genes.\n\(Of a series of chemical compounds\) Having the same functional group\(s\) but differing in structure by a fixed group of atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10808
name: homologous recombination
def: "Crossing over between two similar or identical strands of DNA\, resulting in exchange of corresponding stretches of DNA between two sister chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10809
name: honey bee larval toxicity test
def: "Procedure in which larvae of the honey bee \(Apis mellifera\) are exposed to a test chemical in the diet and subsequent mortality is recorded daily up to the \\\(\\pu\{72\\!\\!\{-\}\\!h\}\\\) \\\(\\pu\{LD_\{50\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10810
name: hormone
def: "Substance formed in one organ or part of the body and carried in the blood to another organ or part where it selectively alters functional activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10811
name: Human Genome Project
def: "International research project that started in 1990 with the goal of determining the base-pair sequence of the human genome. The project was declared complete in 2003."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10812
name: hydatidiform mole
def: "Vesicular or polycystic mass resulting from the proliferation of the trophoblast\, with hydropic degeneration and avascularity of the chorionic villi."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10813
name: hydranencephaly
def: "Rare condition in which the brain’s cerebral hemispheres are replaced by sacs filled with cerebrospinal fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10814
name: hydrocele
def: "Accumulation of serous fluid in a sacculated cavity; specifically\, such an accumulation in the space of the tunica vaginalis testis\, or in a separate pocket along the spermatic cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10815
name: hydrocephalus
def: "Accumulation of excess cerebrospinal fluid within the ventricles of the brain; head enlargement and brain damage may occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10816
name: hydromicrocephaly
def: "Microcephaly with excess cerebrospinal fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10817
name: hydronephrosis
def: "Swelling of the funnel-shaped part of the kidney\, where urine is collected to enter the ureter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10818
name: hydrops
def: "Gross edema of the entire body\, with severe anemia\, occurring in hemolytic disease of the newborn."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10819
name: hydroureter
def: "Dilation of a ureter with fluid."
synonym: "ureterectasia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10820
name: hygroma
def: "Accumulation of fluid in a sac\, cyst\, or bursa."
synonym: "hydroma" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10821
name: hymen
def: "Thin\, membranous fold of highly variable appearance that\, before its rupture\, partly occludes the opening of the vagina."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10822
name: hyoid arch
def: "Second pharyngeal or branchial arch of the developing embryo\, from which different tissues around the neck\, ear\, and face will develop."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10823
name: hypaxial
def: "Ventral to the long axis of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10824
name: hyperextension
def: "Extension of a limb or part of a limb beyond the normal limit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10825
name: hyperflexion
def: "Flexion of a limb or part of a limb beyond the normal limit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10826
name: hyperplasia
def: "Abnormal multiplication or increase in the number of normal cells in a tissue or organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10827
name: hypertelorism
def: "Abnormally wide space between two organs\, especially referring to the eyes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10828
name: hypertrophy
def: "Enlargement of an organ or tissue as a result of the increase in the size of its cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10829
name: hypoblast
def: "Innermost of the three primary germ layers\, adjacent to the blastocyst cavity\, that develops into the endoderm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10830
name: hypogonadism
def: "Inadequate functioning of the testes or ovaries as manifested by deficiencies in gametogenesis or in secretion of gonadal hormones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10831
name: hypoplasia
def: "Underdevelopment or atrophy of a tissue or organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10832
name: hypospadias
def: "Birth defect in which the urethra opens on the underside of the penis\, or into the vagina."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10833
name: hypothalamus
def: "Region at the base of the brain containing specialized nerve cells that help activate\, control\, and integrate peripheral autonomic mechanisms\, endocrine activities\, and some somatic functions such as body temperature\, sleep\, and appetite."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10834
name: hypoxia
def: "Abnormally low dioxygen content or tension.\nDeficiency of dioxygen in the inspired air\, in blood\, or in tissues\, short of anoxia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10835
name: ichthyosis
def: "Congenital disorder of keratinization\, characterized by dryness and scaling of the skin\, often associated with other defects and with abnormalities of lipid metabolism."
synonym: "fish-scale disease" []
synonym: "fish-skin disease" []
synonym: "xeroderma" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10836
name: identical twins
def: "Twins resulting from one zygote that\, at an early stage of embryonic development\, separated into two independently growing cell aggregations giving rise to two individuals of the same sex and identical genetic constitution."
synonym: "monozygotic twins" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10837
name: ileocecal
def: "Relating to the ileum and the cecum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10838
name: ileum
def: "Third portion of the small intestine\, between the jejunum and the cecum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10839
name: iliac artery
def: "Main artery of the pelvis."
synonym: "hypogastric artery" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10840
name: imperforate anus
def: "Congenital absence of an anal opening due to the persistence of epithelial plug \(persistence of the anal membrane\) or to complete absence of the anal canal."
synonym: "anal atresia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10841
name: implantation
def: "Embedding of the early embryo in the lining of the uterus."
synonym: "nidation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10842
name: imposex
def: "Pseudohermaphroditic condition in female gastropods \(snails\) manifested by the development \(imposition\) of male characteristics such as a penis or vas deferens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10843
name: imprinting
def: "Differential expression of a gene\, depending on whether it was transmitted through the sperm or the egg."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10844
name: inbred strain
def: "Strain of an animal that has been inbred by brother-sister matings for more than \\\(\\pu\{20\}\\\) generations\, and consequently all the individuals of the strain are more than \\\(\\pu\{98\\%\}\\\) genetically identical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10845
name: female fertility index
def: "In rodents\, number of pregnant females divided by the number of females mated\, multiplied by 100."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10846
name: gestation index
def: "In rodents\, number of females with live born offspring divided by number of females with evidence of pregnancy\, multiplied by \\\(\\pu\{100\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10847
name: male fertility index
def: "In rodents\, number of males impregnating females divided by number of males mated\, multiplied by \\\(\\pu\{100\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10848
name: tubular fertility index
def: "Percentage of seminiferous tubules containing identifiable spermatogonia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10849
name: induced pluripotent stem cell
def: "Pluripotent stem cell that is generated from an adult cell by genetic reprogramming."
synonym: "iPS cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10850
name: infertility
def: "Persistent inability of either a male or a female to achieve conception or to produce offspring."
synonym: "barrenness" []
synonym: "infecundity" []
synonym: "sterility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10851
name: infundibulum
def: "Anatomical funnel-shaped opening\, often referring to the hollow stalk that connects the hypothalamus and the posterior pituitary gland\, or to the portion of the Fallopian tube that receives the ovum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10852
name: inguinal canal
def: "Passage in the lower anterior abdominal wall\, which in men conveys the spermatic cord and in women the round ligament of the uterus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10853
name: inhibin
def: "Any of a group of peptide hormones secreted by the follicular granulosa cells of the ovary and the Sertoli cells of the testis\, inhibiting follicle-stimulating hormone secretion by the anterior pituitary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10854
name: iniencephaly
def: "Malformation producing a cranial defect at the occiput\, with the brain exposed\, often combined with a cervical rachischisis and retroflexion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10855
name: iniopagus
def: "Twins conjoined at the occiput."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10856
name: insemination
def: "Delivery of sperm into the female reproductive system for the purpose or with the result of causing pregnancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10857
name: integrin
def: "Any of a family of cell membrane glycoproteins that mediate cell-to-cell and cell-to-extracellular matrix interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10858
name: intermediate filament
def: "Any of a group of fibrous proteins \(including keratin fibres\, neurofilaments\, desmin\, and vimentin\) that make up part of the cytoskeleton of most eukaryotic cells; so named because\, at about \\\(\\pu\{10 nm\}\\\) diameter\, they are intermediate in thickness between actin filaments and microtubules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10859
name: intersexuality
def: "Condition of having both male and female characteristics\: thus\, the state of being intermediate between the sexes."
synonym: "intersex" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10860
name: intracytoplasmic sperm injection
def: "Infertility treatment in which the sperm is injected through the membrane of the egg into its cytoplasm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10861
name: intramembrous ossification
def: "Creation of bone tissue during fetal development of jawed vertebrates\, as well as during the healing of bone fractures; the tissue forms from mesenchymal stem cells residing in an extracellular matrix devoid of collagen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10862
name: intrauterine
def: "Within the uterus."
synonym: "in utero" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10863
name: intrauterine growth restriction
def: "Subnormal\, poor growth of the fetus."
synonym: "intrauterine growth retardation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10864
name: intrinsic pathway
def: "Apoptotic pathway of cell death initiated from within the cell by signals initiated by mitochondrial damage and \(or\) DNA damage\, a defective cell cycle\, detachment from the extracellular matrix\, hypoxia\, loss of cell survival factors\, or other types of severe cell stress; characterized by the activation of caspase 9."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10865
name: chromosomal inversion
def: "Rearrangement of a chromosome in which\, after breakage at two points\, a segment is reversed\, resulting in a change in sequence of nucleotides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10866
name: <em>in vitro</em> fertilization
def: "Fertilization outside the body\, used as a treatment for infertility."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10867
name: ischemia
def: "Local deficiency of blood supply\, and hence of dioxygen\, to an organ or tissue\, caused by constriction or obstruction of the blood vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10868
name: isotretinoin
def: "Vitamin A-like medication \(13-cis retinoic acid\) used\, among other things\, for acne treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10869
name: jejunum
def: "Second part of the small intestine between the duodenum and ileum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10870
name: karyolysis
def: "Dissolution of a cell nucleus\, especially during mitosis and meiosis but also following necrosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10871
name: karyorrhexis
def: "Irreversible fragmentation of the nucleus of a dying cell following karyolysis\, whereby its chromatin is distributed irregularly throughout the cytoplasm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10872
name: karyotype
def: "Characterization of the number\, form\, and size of the chromosomes of an individual or a species.\nPhotomicrograph of an individual’s chromosomes\, arranged according to a standard classification for the species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10873
name: keratin
def: "Fibrous protein forming the main structural constituent of hair\, feathers\, hoofs\, claws\, horns\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10874
name: keratinization
def: "Replacement of the outer epithelial layer of cells\, especially in skin\, with keratin-rich structures."
synonym: "cornification" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10875
name: ketoacidosis
def: "Acidosis\, as in diabetes\, pregnancy\, or starvation\, accompanied by the accumulation of ketone bodies in body tissues and fluids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10876
name: ketone body
def: "Ketone that is an intermediate product of the breakdown of fats in the body; any of three compounds\, acetoacetic acid\, acetone\, and \(or\) \\\(\\upbeta\\\)-hydroxybutanoic acid\, found in excess in blood and urine of persons with metabolic disorders."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10877
name: Klinefelter syndrome
def: "Syndrome in males resulting from a genetic defect in which an extra female X chromosome \(thus an XXY genotype\) is present in a male\, characterized by small testes\, long legs\, enlarged breasts\, reduced sperm production\, and mental retardation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10878
name: knockout mutation
def: "Mutation that leads to the loss of function of a particular gene."
synonym: "null mutation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10879
name: kyphosis
def: "Rearward curvature or convexity of the spine in excess of normal\, resulting in a protuberant upper back."
synonym: "hunchback" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10880
name: labor
def: "Physical efforts of expulsion of the fetus and the placenta from the uterus during birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10881
name: lactation
def: "Secretion of milk from the mammary gland\, usually to feed the infant during the neonatal period."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10882
name: lactation period
def: "Time following pregnancy during which the mammary glands secrete milk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10883
name: lamina propria
def: "Layer of connective tissue\, underlying the epithelium of a mucus membrane\, containing capillaries and lymphatic vessels\, as well as fibroblasts and cells of the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10884
name: larynx
def: "Part of the respiratory tract between the pharynx and the trachea\, having walls of cartilage and muscle and containing the vocal cords."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10885
name: leprosy
def: "Disease caused by infection with the bacterium Mycobacterium leprae\, often affecting the skin and nerves and causing body parts to become deformed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10886
name: leptocephaly
def: "Malformation characterized by an abnormally tall\, narrow cranium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10887
name: leptodactyly
def: "Abnormally slender digits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10888
name: leucism
def: "Lack of pigmentation in the skin\, hair\, or feathers as a result of a failure of pigment cells to develop\, or to migrate to those locations from their origin in the neural crest\, in the embryo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10889
name: leukopenia
def: "Abnormally low concentration of white cells \(leukocytes\) in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10890
name: levocardia
def: "Normal position of the heart in the left hemithorax with the apex pointed to the left\, and transposition of other viscera."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10891
name: Leydig cell
def: "Cell in the testis that produces androgens\, mainly testosterone\, in the presence of luteinizing hormone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10892
name: ligamentum arteriosum
def: "Fibrous remnant of the ductus arteriosis linking the aortic arch and the top of the left pulmonary artery."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10893
name: limb bud
def: "Embryonic outgrowth of mesoderm covered in ectoderm that will give rise to one of the forelimbs or hindlimbs."
synonym: "arm bud" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10894
name: linkage analysis
def: "Technique that studies patterns of heredity in high-risk families\, in order to locate a disease-causing gene mutation by identifying co-inherited traits\, usually by producing a LOD score."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10895
name: linkage disequilibrium
def: "Occurrence in members of a population of combinations of linked genes in non-random proportions\, implying that the genes are close enough together on a chromosome to make it unlikely that they will be separated by recombination \(see homologous recombination\) during meiosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10896
name: lipidosis
def: "Disorder of fat metabolism characterized by the accumulation of abnormal levels of certain lipids in the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10897
name: litter
def: "Multiple offspring produced by a mammal from one pregnancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10898
name: locus
def: "Specific location of a gene or DNA sequence on a chromosome."
synonym: "loci" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10899
name: LOD score
def: "Ratio of the likelihood of two or more loci remaining together when chromosomes recombine \(true linkage\) to the likelihood of this occurring by chance alone."
synonym: "logarithm (base 10) of odds" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10900
name: lordosis
def: "Forward \(ventral\) curvature of the spine in the lumbar and cervical regions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10901
name: lumbar puncture
def: "Piercing into the subarachnoid space of the lumbar region in order to obtain spinal fluid for diagnostic purposes or to inject a drug."
synonym: "spinal tap" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10902
name: luteal phase
def: "Phase of the human menstrual cycle\, usually lasting \\\(\\pu\{14 days\}\\\)\, beginning with formation of the corpus luteum and ending\, in the absence of fertilization\, with the onset of menstruation."
synonym: "menstrual cycle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10903
name: luteinizing hormone
def: "Hormone made by the pituitary gland that acts on the ovary to control egg maturation and to trigger ovulation. The same hormone acts in the testes to trigger production of testosterone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10904
name: lymphatic
def: "Relating to lymph or its secretion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10905
name: lymphoid
def: "Relating to or denoting tissue responsible for producing lymphocytes and antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10906
name: Lyon hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis \(named after Mary Lyon\) that random inactivation of all but one X chromosome occurs in each female mammalian cell in early development\, thus explaining why the effect of the X chromosome on phenotype is the same in males with one copy as in females with two copies."
synonym: "Lyon law" []
synonym: "X-inactivation" []
synonym: "lyonization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10907
name: lysosomal storage disease
def: "One of a group of rare inherited metabolic disorders resulting from a defect in lysosomal function\, commonly a deficiency of enzymes required for the metabolism of lipids\, glycoproteins\, or mucopolysaccharides\, resulting in their accumulation in the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10908
name: macrocephaly
def: "Congenital disorder characterized by an abnormally large head and \(or\) brain."
synonym: "megacephaly" []
synonym: "megalocephaly" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10909
name: macroglossia
def: "Congenital disorder characterized by an abnormally enlarged tongue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10910
name: macrognathia
def: "Congenital disorder characterized by enlargement or elongation of the jaw."
synonym: "megagnathia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10911
name: macromelia
def: "Congenital disorder characterized by an abnormally large limb or limbs."
synonym: "megalomelia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10912
name: macrosomia
def: "Congenital disorder characterized by an abnormally large body or body part."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10913
name: macrostomia
def: "Congenital disorder characterized by an abnormally large size of the mouth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10914
name: malformation
def: "Structural defect as a result of abnormal development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10915
name: malignant
def: "Occurring in a severe form\, tending to become progressively worse\, resistant to treatment\, and likely to result in death.\nIn cancer\, cells showing both uncontrolled growth and a tendency to invade and destroy other tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10916
name: mammary gland
def: "Milk-producing organ in female mammals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10917
name: Marfan syndrome
def: "Congenital connective tissue disorder associated with mutation of the fibrillin-1 gene FBN1."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10918
name: masculinization
def: "Development of male characteristics\, such as facial hair\, either as part of normal male maturation\, or\, pathologically\, by people of either sex as a result of hormonal imbalance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10919
name: maternal serum alpha-fetoprotein
def: "Protein made in the fetus that normally leaks\, in small amounts\, into the mother’s circulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10920
name: maxillary
def: "Relating to the maxilla \(upper jaw\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10921
name: Meckel diverticulum
def: "Congenital pouch in the ileum resulting from incomplete closure of the yolk sac."
synonym: "ileal diverticulum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10922
name: meconium
def: "First intestinal discharges of the newborn infant\, greenish in color and consisting of epithelial cells\, mucus\, and bile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10923
name: median teratogenic concentration
def: "Median concentration resulting in developmental malformations for \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of exposed test animals within a predetermined time\, e.g.\, \\\(\\pu\{96 h\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10924
name: mediastinum
def: "Region in the middle of the thorax between the two lungs\, containing the regional vessels\, trachea\, esophagus\, bronchi\, lymph nodes\, and heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10925
name: medulla
def: "\[medulla oblongata\] Lower part of the brain stem continuous with the spinal cord\, containing neural centers regulating the autonomic functions of breathing\, heart rate\, and blood pressure.\nMiddle \(central\) part of an organ\, as in adrenal medulla\, renal medulla\, or the medulla of a lymph node."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10926
name: megacolon
def: "Condition of extreme dilation of the colon that can be congenital \(as in Hirschsprung disease\) or acquired \(as when children refuse to defecate\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10927
name: megadactyly
def: "Disorder characterized by an abnormally large fingers and \(or\) toes."
synonym: "macrodactylia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10928
name: menarche
def: "First menstrual cycle of a woman."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10929
name: Mendelian gene
def: "Gene located in a chromosome that obeys the laws of Mendelian inheritance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10930
name: Mendelian inheritance
def: "Inheritance in which stable and indivisible characteristics are controlled entirely or overwhelmingly by a single genetic locus and transmitted over many generations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10931
name: Mendelian trait
def: "Phenotype that shows a pattern of Mendelian inheritance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10932
name: meninx
def: "Any of the three membranes \(the dura mater\, arachnoid\, and pia mater\) that line the skull and vertebral canal and enclose the brain and spinal cord."
synonym: "meninges" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10933
name: meningocele
def: "Herniation of the membranes of the brain or spinal cord \(see meninx\) through a defect in the cranium or spinal column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10934
name: meningomyelocele
def: "Birth defect following failure of the neural tube to close\, resulting in protrusion of a sac of nerve tissue and its covering membranes."
synonym: "meningoencephalocele" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10935
name: menopause
def: "Cessation of reproductive capability in a woman\, marked by declining ovarian function and an end to menses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10936
name: menstrual cycle
def: "The period\, normally lasting \\\(\\pu\{28 days\}\\\) in the human female\, during which an ovum matures\, is ovulated\, and enters the uterus through the Fallopian tubes. If fertilization occurs\, the cycle is interrupted by pregnancy; otherwise\, the cycle ends with menstruation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10937
name: menstruation
def: "Cyclic endometrial shedding and discharge of a bloody fluid from the uterus during the menstrual cycle."
synonym: "menses" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10938
name: meromelia
def: "Congenital absence of a part of a limb\, resulting in a shrunken and deformed extremity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10939
name: mesectoderm
def: "Embryonic migratory cells derived from the neural crest of the head that contribute to the formation of the meninges \(see meninx\) and become pigment cells."
synonym: "ectomesenchyme" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10940
name: mesencephalon
def: "Part of the vertebrate brain that develops from the middle section of the embryonic neural tube."
synonym: "midbrain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10941
name: mesenchymal-to-epithelial transition
def: "Process in which motile mesenchymal cells undergo a development to planar\, polarized fixed cells\, forming epithelia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10942
name: mesenchyme
def: "Meshwork of embryonic connective tissue in the mesoderm\, from which are formed the bone\, muscular\, and connective tissues of the body and also the urogenital system\, blood vessels\, and lymph vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10943
name: mesentery
def: "Membranous sheet attaching various organs to the body wall\, especially the peritoneal fold attaching the intestine to the dorsal body wall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10944
name: mesoderm
def: "Middle layer of cells in the embryo\, lying between the ectoderm and the endoderm. It includes the following tissues\:\nLateral mesoderm\: peripheral portion of intraembryonic mesoderm.\nIntermediate mesoderm\: origin of the nephrogenic cord.\nParietal \(somatic\) mesoderm\: cell source for the formation of the lateral and ventral body wall.\nVisceral \(branchial\, pharyngeal\, splanchnic\) mesoderm\: inner layer of lateral mesoderm that\, with the endoderm\, provides the cells from which the gut and lungs and their coverings arise."
synonym: "embryonic mesoderm" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10945
name: mesogastrium
def: "Part of the embryonic mesentery that is attached to the early stomach."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10946
name: mesonephric duct
def: "Embryonic duct of the mesonephros\, which in the male becomes the vas deferens and in the female becomes vestigial."
synonym: "Wolffian duct" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10947
name: mesonephros
def: "Middle part of the embryonic kidney in vertebrates\, becoming the adult kidney in fishes and amphibians and the epididymis in reptiles\, birds\, and mammals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10948
name: mesothelium
def: "Single layer of flattened cells forming an epithelium that lines serous cavities such as the peritoneum\, pleura\, and pericardium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10949
name: metabologen
def: "Morphogen\, including bone morphogenetic protein\, that affects metabolism and homeostasis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10950
name: metallothionein
def: "One of a family of low-molecular mass proteins that binds metals such as zinc\, copper\, cadmium and mercury."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10951
name: metanephros
def: "Primordium of the permanent kidney\, developing later than\, and caudal to\, the mesonephros."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10952
name: metaphase
def: "Stage of mitosis or meiosis in which the chromosomes become aligned on the equatorial plate of the cell\, separating the centromeres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10953
name: metaplasia
def: "Abnormal transformation of an adult\, fully differentiated tissue of one kind into a differentiated tissue of another kind."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10954
name: methylation
def: "Attachment of a methyl group to a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10955
name: microcephaly
def: "Congenital occurrence of an abnormally small head."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10956
name: microcheiria
def: "Congenital occurrence of abnormally small hands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10957
name: microglossia
def: "Congenital occurrence of an abnormally small tongue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10958
name: micrognathia
def: "Congenital occurrence of abnormally small jaws\, especially effecting the mandible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10959
name: micromass culture
def: "Laboratory technique in which dispersed cells from an embryonic organ or tissue\, such as the brain\, limb bud\, or cartilage\, are allowed to reaggregate in culture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10960
name: micronucleus test
def: "Test for mutagenicity in which animals are treated with a test agent\, after which time the frequency of micronucleated cells is determined.\nNote\: If a test group shows significantly increased levels of micronucleated cells compared to a control group\, the chemical is considered capable of inducing chromosomal damage.\nProcedure to detect clastogenic or aneugenic agents by microscopic examination of chromosomes.\nNote\: The test can be performed in vivo in rodents\, or in cell culture \(in vitro mammalian cell micronucleus test\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10961
name: microphallus
def: "Abnormally small penis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10962
name: microphthalmia
def: "Congenital occurrence of abnormally small eyeballs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10963
name: microsatellite
def: "Non-coding segment of DNA consisting of short nucleotide sequences \(\\\(\\pu\{2\\!\{-\}\\! 6\}\\\) base pairs\)\, typically occurring in \\\(\\pu\{10\\!\{-\}\\! 100\}\\\) consecutive \(tandem\) repeats."
synonym: "short tandem repeat (STR)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10964
name: microstomia
def: "Congenital occurrence of an abnormally small opening of the mouth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10965
name: midgut
def: "Middle section of the digestive tract in a vertebrate embryo\, from which the ileum\, jejunum\, and portions of the duodenum and colon develop."
synonym: "mesenteron" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10966
name: Minamata disease
def: "Neurological disease caused by methylmercury\, first seen in subjects ingesting contaminated fish from Minamata Bay in Japan."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10967
name: minisatellite
def: "Non-coding segment of DNA\, found throughout the genome\, usually near the ends of chromosomes\, that consists of tandem repeats of sequences of about \\\(\\pu\{10\\!\{-\}\\! 100\}\\\) base pairs."
synonym: "variable number tandem repeat (VNTR)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10968
name: miscarriage
def: "Lay term for spontaneous abortion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10969
name: molecular epidemiology
def: "Use in epidemiological studies of techniques of molecular biology\, such as DNA profiling and genetic analysis\, with the aim of detecting genetic patterns characteristic of susceptible populations or of groups at risk of disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10970
name: monophthalmos
def: "Congenital absence of one eye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10971
name: monosomy
def: "Disorder in which body cells have only one pair instead of the normal two pairs of a particular chromosome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10972
name: morphogen
def: "Any of various signalling factors in embryonic tissue that influences the movement and organization of cells during morphogenesis by forming a concentration gradient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10973
name: morphogenesis
def: "Shaping of an organism during embryological development by differentiation of cells\, tissues\, organs\, and organ systems\, according to the genetic program of the organism\, and influenced by morphogens and environmental conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10974
name: morphological
def: "Pertaining to structure or form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10975
name: morula
def: "Early multi-celled stage of the embryo from which the blastocyst is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10976
name: mosaicism
def: "Condition in which an individual or an organism that develops from a single zygote has two or more cell populations that differ in genetic constitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10977
name: sperm motility
def: "Energy-dependent forward movement of the sperm\, allowing it to reach and penetrate the egg."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10978
name: mucous membrane
def: "Layer of epithelial tissue with a thin underlying layer of connective tissue \(the lamina propria\) that lines many body cavities\, including the gut\, respiratory passages\, and tubular organs; it secretes\, and is covered in\, mucus."
synonym: "mucosa" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:10979
name: mucus
def: "Slippery viscous fluid\, consisting largely of water and glycoproteins\, secreted by mucous membranes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10980
name: multicotyledonary placentation
def: "Formation of a placenta with many lobes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10981
name: multifactorial inheritance
def: "Transmission from parents to offspring of a trait that is determined by multiple genetic and environmental factors\, each with a small effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10982
name: multigenerational study
def: "Animal test of reproductive toxicity in which two to three generations of the test organism are exposed to the substance being assessed.\nAnimal test of reproductive toxicity in which only one generation is exposed and effects on subsequent generations are assessed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10983
name: mycoplasma
def: "Very small infectious microorganism\, related to bacteria\, but without a cell wall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10984
name: myelencephalon
def: "Part of the brain in the embryo that gives rise to the medulla oblongata."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10985
name: myelination
def: "The acquisition\, development\, or formation of a myelin sheath around a nerve fiber."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10986
name: myeloblast
def: "Immature bone marrow cell that is a precursor of the granulocyte series."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10987
name: myelotoxic
def: "Harming bone marrow or any of its components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10988
name: myoblast
def: "Mononucleate cell that is committed to differentiate into muscle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10989
name: myocarditis
def: "Inflammation of the heart muscle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10990
name: myocardium
def: "Muscle of the heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10991
name: myometrium
def: "Muscular wall of the uterus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10992
name: myositis
def: "Inflammation of muscle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10993
name: myotome
def: "In the embryo\, that part of a somite that develops into skeletal muscle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10994
name: nasal
def: "Relating to the nose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10995
name: natality
def: "Rate of birth\, the number of newborn individuals per unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10996
name: necropsy
def: "Postmortem examination of the organs and body tissue of a non-human animal to determine cause of death or pathological condition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10997
name: neonate
def: "Newborn animal or human infant during the first four weeks of postnatal life."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10998
name: neoplasm
def: "New and abnormal formation of tissue as a consequence of growth by cell proliferation\, which may continue after the initial stimulus that initiated the proliferation has ceased\, and which may develop into a tumor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:10999
name: neural
def: "Pertaining to a nerve or to the nervous system.\nPertaining to the dorsal side of the vertebral bodies or to their precursors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11000
name: neural arch
def: "Dorsal bony covering of the spinal cord.\nCartilaginous structures surrounding the embryonicspinal cord."
synonym: "vertebral arch" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11001
name: neural crest
def: "Band of cells on either side of the neural tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11002
name: neural plate
def: "Area in the middle of the early embryo that rolls up to form the neural tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11003
name: neural tube
def: "Embryonic tubular structure that becomes the brain and spinal cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11004
name: neural tube defect
def: "Failure of the neural tube to close properly during gastrulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11005
name: neurobehavioral
def: "Pertaining to the function of the nervous system as it relates to behavior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11006
name: neuroblast
def: "Cell derived from neural stem cell that will differentiate into a neuron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11007
name: neuroblastoma
def: "Malignant neoplasm consisting of poorly differentiated embryonic nerve cells \(neuroblasts\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11008
name: neuroectoderm
def: "Central region of the early embryonic ectoderm that will give rise to the brain and spinal cord\, together with the neural crest cells that will form the peripheral nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11009
name: neuroendocrine system
def: "Combined cooperating network of the nervous system and hormonal systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11010
name: neuroendocrine
def: "Pertaining to the nervous and endocrine systems in anatomical or functional relationship."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11011
name: neurohypophysis
def: "Posterior lobe of the pituitary gland\, involved in the storage and secretion of oxytocin and vasopressin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11012
name: neuron
def: "Nerve cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11013
name: neuropore
def: "Opening at either the cranial \(caudal\) or anterior \(rostral\) end of the neural tube before it completes closure in the early embryonic stage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11014
name: neurotrophin
def: "One of several protein growth factors that regulates development\, maintenance\, and function of the vertebrate nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11015
name: neurulation
def: "Formation of the embryonic neural plate and its rolling up into the neural tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11016
name: nipple
def: "Projection on the apex of the breast through which the ducts of the milk-producing glands open."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11017
name: nodal
def: "Growth factor of the transforming growth factor \\\(\\upbeta\\\) \(TGF-\\\(\\upbeta\\\)\) superfamily\, coded by a gene designated NODAL\, that plays crucial roles in embryogenesis\, particularly in signalling from the primitive streak to the mesoderm to establish left-right asymmetry as well as aspects of stem cell differentiation."
synonym: "nodal protein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11018
name: noggin
def: "Signaling protein released by the notochord\, important in somite patterning and nervous system development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11019
name: nondisjunction
def: "Failure of chromosomes to separate during meiosis\, resulting in an uneven distribution of chromosomes in the two gametes \(\\\(\\pu\{24\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{22\}\\\) in humans\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11020
name: noradrenaline
def: "Catecholamine hormone acting as a postganglionic adrenergic mediator at $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}adrenergic\}$ receptors."
synonym: "2-diol" []
synonym: "4-[(1S)-2-amino-1-hydroxyethyl]benzene-1" []
synonym: "norepinephrine" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11021
name: notochord
def: "Rod-shaped structure of cells derived from mesoderm\, lying ventral to the neural tube and defining the primitive axis of the embryo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11022
name: nuclear spindle
def: "Structure\, formed from microtubules\, that draws the newly duplicated chromosomes apart during mitosis and meiosis."
synonym: "mitotic spindle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11023
name: nuclear type I receptor
def: "Steroid hormone receptor\, found in the cytosol\, that upon ligand binding translocates into the nucleus and interacts with DNA as a homodimer to drive specific gene transcription."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11024
name: nuclear type II receptor
def: "Steroid hormone receptor\, found in the nucleus\, that upon ligand binding interacts with DNA as a heterodimer \(usually with the retinoid X receptor\, RXR\) to drive specific gene transcription."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11025
name: nulligravida
def: "Never having been pregnant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11026
name: nulliparous
def: "Never having given birth to a live infant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11027
name: occiput
def: "Back part of the head or skull."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11028
name: odontoblast
def: "Dentin-forming cell of the pulp cavity of a tooth\, arising from the mesenchyme of the neural crest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11029
name: offspring
def: "Child or children of a person\, or the young of an animal\, in relation to the parent\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11030
name: oligodactyly
def: "Congenital occurrence of fewer than the usual number of digits\, resulting in humans having fewer than five fingers or toes on a hand or foot."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11031
name: oligodontia
def: "Congenital absence of some of the teeth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11032
name: oligomenomenorrhea
def: "Infrequent or scanty menstruation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11033
name: oligospermia
def: "Subnormal concentration of sperm in the male ejaculate \(see ejaculation\)."
synonym: "oligospermatism" []
synonym: "oligozoospermia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11034
name: omentum
def: "Fold of peritoneal membrane passing between the stomach and another abdominal organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11035
name: greater omentum
def: "Omentum passing from the greater curvature of the stomach in front of the small intestine\, folding back on itself to fuse into four layers of peritoneal membrane\, and ascending to the transverse colon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11036
name: lesser omentum
def: "Double-layer of peritoneal membrane \(omentum\) passing between the lesser curvature of the stomach and the duodenum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11037
name: omphalocele
def: "Congenital herniation of abdominal viscera into the base of the umbilical cord."
synonym: "exomphalos" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11038
name: omphalosite
def: "Lesser developed of two monozygotic twins that failed to separate completely during embryogenesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11039
name: one-generation reprotox study
def: "Procedure in which male rodents are dosed with a test substance for at least one spermatogenic cycle\, and females for two estrous cycles\, and then further exposed during mating\, pregnancy\, and nursing; adverse effects on reproduction\, parturition\, lactation and postnatal growth are studied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11040
name: oocyte
def: "Immature precursor of the ovum resident in the ovary."
synonym: "ovocyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11041
name: primary oocyte
def: "Oocyte during growth phase before completion of the first meiotic division."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11042
name: secondary oocyte
def: "Oocyte in which the first meiotic division to a haploid cell has been completed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11043
name: oogenesis
def: "Process of formation and development of an ovum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11044
name: oogonium
def: "Primitive germ cell that proliferates by mitosis and develops into a primary oocyte prior to birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11045
name: optic
def: "Relating to the eye or vision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11046
name: orbit
def: "Cavity in the skull of a vertebrate that contains the eye."
synonym: "eye socket" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11047
name: organ of Corti
def: "Specialized collection of epithelial hair cells in the inner ear\, involved in hearing."
synonym: "spiral organ" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11048
name: organogenesis
def: "Formation and development of organs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11049
name: orofacial cleft
def: "Failure of the lip or palate to fuse properly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11050
name: osteoclast
def: "Large multinucleate cell that resorbs bone\, allowing for the deposition of new bone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11051
name: osteogenesis
def: "Laying down of new bone tissue."
synonym: "ossification" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11052
name: ostium
def: "Opening into a vessel or cavity of the body."
synonym: "introitus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11053
name: otic
def: "Pertaining to\, or located near the ear."
synonym: "auricular" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11054
name: otocephaly
def: "Congenital anomaly characterized by the absence or extreme underdevelopment of the lower jaw\, producing closeness of the ears below the face."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11055
name: ovarian cycle
def: "Sequence of events occurring in the ovary involved in ovulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11056
name: ovarian follicle
def: "Cavity in the ovary containing a maturing ovum\, at any stage of development\, surrounded by its encasing cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11057
name: ovary
def: "One of the paired female reproductive glands containing the ova \(see ovum\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11058
name: ovotestis
def: "Abnormal gonad in which both ovarian and testicular tissues are present\, and thus a form of hermaphroditism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11059
name: ovulation
def: "Release of a mature ovum from the ovary into the Fallopian tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11060
name: ovum
def: "Mature female sex cell\, capable of undergoing fertilization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11061
name: oxytocin
def: "Peptide hormone secreted by the neurohypophysis that stimulates contraction of the myometrium during labor and secretion of milk during lactation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11062
name: palate
def: "Roof of the mouth\, separating the oral and nasal cavities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11063
name: palatine raphe
def: "Medial central ridge of the palate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11064
name: Papanicolaou smear
def: "Examination by light microscopy of a sample of cells scraped from the uterine cervix\, used to screen for cervical cancer."
synonym: "Pap smear" []
synonym: "Pap test" []
synonym: "Papanicolaou stain" []
synonym: "cervical smear" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11065
name: paracrine
def: "Type of signaling in which a cell secretes into the intercellular space a molecular messenger that diffuses and binds to receptors on nearby target cells\, producing a signal in those cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11066
name: parafollicular cell
def: "Neuroendocrine cell that migrates into the thyroid gland during embryonic development and secretes the hormone calcitonin."
synonym: "C cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11067
name: paramesonephric duct
def: "One of two paired embryonic ducts of mesodermal origin that will become the Fallopian tubes\, uterus\, cervix and upper part of the vagina in the female\, and will regress in the male."
synonym: "Müllerian duct" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11068
name: parametrium
def: "Connective tissue of the floor of the female pelvis that lies in front of the uterine cervix and separates it from the bladder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11069
name: parasitic twin
def: "Smaller of unequal conjoined twins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11070
name: paraxial mesoderm
def: "Area of mesoderm in the embryo that forms during neurulation\, lies along both sides of the neural tube\, and gives rise to the somites."
synonym: "somitic mesoderm" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11071
name: parenchymal cell
def: "Distinguishing or specific type of cell of an organ or gland\, contained in\, or supported by\, the connective tissue framework provided by the stromal cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11072
name: parietal
def: "Relating to or denoting the wall of the body or of a body cavity or hollow structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11073
name: gastric parietal cell
def: "Acid-producing cell in the stomach."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11074
name: parthenogenesis
def: "Type of nonsexual reproduction in which an unfertilized ovum develops into an embryo."
synonym: "apogamia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11075
name: parturition
def: "Process of giving birth by a maternal organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11076
name: patent ductus arteriosus
def: "Congenital disorder wherein the ductus arteriosis fails to close after birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11077
name: pelvic kidney
def: "Congenital abnormality in which a kidney develops and remains in the pelvic area."
synonym: "ectopic kidney" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11078
name: pericardium
def: "Serous membranous sac that surrounds the heart\, consisting of an inner visceral layer \(the epicardium\)\, and an outer parietal pericardium that is attached to the sternum and diaphragm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11079
name: perinatal
def: "Relating to the period from shortly before to shortly after birth\, in humans usually from the 20th to the 29th week of gestation to one to four weeks after birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11080
name: perineum
def: "Muscular structure of the lower pelvis\, between the legs and extending from the anus to the pubic symphysis\, or alternatively including the anus and vagina in the female and the region from the anus to the base of the scrotum in the male."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11081
name: peritoneal cavity
def: "Potential space between the two layers \(visceral and parietal\) of the peritoneum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11082
name: peritoneum
def: "Thin double layer of mesothelium and irregular connective tissue that lines the abdominal cavity and covers most of the abdominal organs."
synonym: "peritoneal membrane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11083
name: phage display
def: "Method that enables the presentation of large peptide libraries on the surface of phage particles\, from which proteins with desired functional properties can be selected rapidly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11084
name: pharyngeal groove
def: "One of several paired grooves in the embryonic endoderm\, lateral to the corresponding pharyngeal pouch in the ectoderm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11085
name: pharyngeal pouch
def: "One of several paired evaginations in the embryonic endoderm that develop into epithelial tissues and organs such as the thymus and thyroid gland."
synonym: "branchial pouch" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11086
name: pharynx
def: "Part of the digestive tube lying between the esophagus \(below\) and the mouth and nasal cavity \(above and anterior\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11087
name: phenylketonuria
def: "Autosomal recessive disorder resulting from mutations that impair the function of the enzyme phenylalanine 4-monooxygenase \(EC 1.14.16.1\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11088
name: philtrum
def: "Groove in the midline of the upper lip below the nose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11089
name: phocomelia
def: "Congenital abnormality in which one or more of the hands or feet is attached to an underdeveloped limb\, and therefore too close to the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11090
name: phytoestrogen
def: "Non-steroidal natural product from a plant that\, because of a structural similarity to 17\\\(\\upbeta\\\)-estradiol can exert mild estrogenic effects\, or antagonize them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11091
name: piebaldism
def: "Patchy absence of pigmentation of the hair or skin or\, depending on the species\, of feathers\, scales\, or other body surfaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11092
name: pineal gland
def: "Pea-sized conical mass of tissue behind the third ventricle of the brain\, secreting the hormone-like substance melatonin in some mammals."
synonym: "epiphysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11093
name: pinna
def: "Externally visible portion of the ear.See also\: auricle\nFeather or fin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11094
name: pituitary
def: "Small endocrine gland sitting in a bony cavity \(the sella turcica\) at the base of the brain and connected to the hypothalamus."
synonym: "hypophysis" []
synonym: "pituitary gland" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11095
name: placenta
def: "Organ of exchange of nutrients and waste products between mother and fetus\, having parts derived from both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11096
name: placental barrier
def: "Multilayered membrane of fetal tissue within the placenta that separates the maternal blood from the fetal blood and allows selective passage of substances between the two."
synonym: "placental membrane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11097
name: placental circulation
def: "Circulation of blood through the placenta during intrauterine life\, serving the needs of the fetus for oxygenation\, nutrition\, and elimination of waste."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11098
name: placental insufficiency
def: "Inadequate blood flow through the placenta to maintain the needs of the fetus for oxygenation and metabolic balance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11099
name: placental transfer
def: "Delivery of substances from the maternal circulation to the fetus\, across the placental barrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11100
name: pleiotropism
def: "Having more than one effect.\nRelating to a gene\, expression of which gives rise to multiple phenotypic traits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11101
name: pleura
def: "Each of a pair of serous membranes lining the thorax and enveloping the lungs in humans and other mammals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11102
name: pleural cavity
def: "Potential space between the pleural membranes lining the chest cavity \(parietal pleura\) and covering the lungs \(visceral pleura\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11103
name: pluripotent
def: "Able to differentiate into a variety of cell types."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11104
name: polycystic
def: "Composed of many cysts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11105
name: polydactyly
def: "Congenital occurrence of supernumerary fingers or toes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11106
name: polymorphism
def: "Occurrence of a gene in more than one distinct nucleotide sequence in a population\, resulting from mutation\(s\) and potentially producing gene products with different levels of function.\nNote\: It is sometimes considered that the least frequent of polymorphic gene sequences should be present in at least \\\(\\pu\{1\\%\}\\\) of the population; otherwise the variant is a mutation rather than a polymorphism.\nOccurrence of different phenotypes of a single trait."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11107
name: polyploidy
def: "Occurrence of more than two complete sets of chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11108
name: porencephaly
def: "Occurrence of a cyst in the substance of the brain that usually communicates with one of the lateral ventricles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11109
name: post-implantation
def: "Occurring after the early embryo embeds in the lining of the uterus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11110
name: postmortem
def: "After death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11111
name: postnatal
def: "Referring to events in the life of the mother or fetus following birth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11112
name: postpartum
def: "Period following the birth of an infant\, often considered to be about six weeks in the human."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11113
name: predatory mite test
def: "Procedure in which adult females of the predatory mite \(_027814_ considered representative of soil fauna\) are exposed to a test substance in artificial soil and the number of surviving females and of juveniles is determined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11114
name: pre-eclampsia
def: "Pathological condition in pregnancy with symptoms of high blood pressure\, kidney dysfunction and edema."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11115
name: pre-embryo
def: "Fertilized ovum up to \\\(\\pu\{14 days\}\\\) of age and before implantation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11116
name: pregnancy
def: "State of a female between conception and the termination of gestation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11117
name: pre-implantation
def: "Occurring before the early embryo embeds in the lining of the uterus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11118
name: premature birth
def: "Delivery before the expected length of gestation for the species; in humans taken as birth before a gestational age of \\\(\\pu\{37 weeks\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11119
name: prenatal
def: "Referring to events in the life of the mother or fetus preceding birth."
synonym: "antenatal" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11120
name: prenatal development study
def: "Procedure in which a substance is administered to pregnant animals \(usually rodents or rabbits\) from the time of implantation until closure of the hard palate. The animals are killed one to two days before a scheduled delivery\, whereupon uterine contents and the fetuses are evaluated for abnormalities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11121
name: preoptic area
def: "Region of the anterior hypothalamus primarily involved in regulating body temperature."
synonym: "preoptic region" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11122
name: prepubertal
def: "Stage of human development before the onset of puberty."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11123
name: prepubescence
def: "Period before pubescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11124
name: prepuce
def: "Skin protecting the glans penis in the male \(foreskin\) or the clitoris in the female \(clitoral hood\)."
synonym: "clitoral hood" []
synonym: "foreskin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11125
name: preputial separation
def: "Separation of the prepuce from the glans penis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11126
name: primary sex organ
def: "Gonads\, which form gametes; ovaries in the female and testes in the male."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11127
name: primitive axis
def: "Thickening of the embryonic tissue extending forward from the primitive streak."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11128
name: primitive groove
def: "Shallow depression in the primitive streak that extends anteriorly to communicate with the yolk sac."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11129
name: primitive node
def: "Group of cells at the anterior end of the primitive streak\, involved in secreting chemical signals that regulate differentiation of the germ layers during gastrulation."
synonym: "primitive knot" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11130
name: primitive pit
def: "Depression in the primitive node contiguous with the notochord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11131
name: primitive streak
def: "Structure forming in the blastula during early embryogenesis that establishes bilateral symmetry and determines the site of gastrulation and germ layer formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11132
name: primordial follicle
def: "Early stage of the oocyte in which it is surrounded by a single layer of follicular cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11133
name: primordial germ cell
def: "Germ cell in the earliest stages of development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11134
name: primordium
def: "Organ or tissue at its earliest recognizable stage of development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11135
name: proctodeum
def: "Ectodermal depression of the caudal end of the embryo\, where later the anus is formed."
synonym: "anal pit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11136
name: progesterone
def: "Steroidal hormone produced in the ovary by the corpus luteum."
synonym: "pregn-4-ene-3,20-dione" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11137
name: progestin
def: "Synthetic variant of progesterone\, often used in oral contraceptive pills or to suppress hyperplasia of the endometrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11138
name: progestogen
def: "Member of a family of hormones\, based on the 21-carbon pregnane skeleton\, involved in maintaining gestation and regulating the ovarian cycle."
synonym: "gestagen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11139
name: prolactin
def: "Hormone released from the anterior pituitary gland that stimulates milk production after childbirth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11140
name: prolapse
def: "Falling or slipping out of place of an organ or other body part."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11141
name: pronephros
def: "Collection of cells in the vertebrate embryo that represent the earliest stage of development of the kidney."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11142
name: pronucleus
def: "Haploid nucleus formed by either the head of the sperm or the nucleus of the oocyte after fertilization but before their nuclei fuse to form the diploid zygotic nucleus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11143
name: prophase
def: "First phase of meiosis or mitosis in which thickening and orientation of the chromosomes occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11144
name: prosencephalon
def: "Most forward part of the brain that includes most of the cerebral cortex\, the thalamus and hypothalamus\, and the basal ganglia."
synonym: "forebrain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11145
name: prostate
def: "Male exocrine gland whose secretion contributes alkaline fluid to the ejaculate \(see ejaculation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11146
name: protamine
def: "Small\, basic \(arginine-rich\) protein involved in sperm maturation and DNA packaging during spermatogenesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11147
name: protein kinase
def: "Any enzyme catalyzing protein phosphorylation\, often for the purpose of signal transduction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11148
name: proximal
def: "Nearer to an anatomical point of origin or attachment\, or the midline of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11149
name: pseudohermaphrodite
def: "Organism possessing either male \(testes\) or female \(ovaries\) gonads\, but with ambiguous external genitalia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11150
name: pseudopregnancy
def: "Development of signs of pregnancy in the absence of an embryo."
synonym: "false pregnancy" []
synonym: "pseudocyesis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11151
name: psychomotor retardation
def: "Delayed development of both cognitive and motor function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11152
name: puberty
def: "Process in which an individual undergoes sexual development\, including the onset of gametogenesis\, changes in hormonal secretions\, secondary sexual characteristics\, and reproductive competence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11153
name: pubescent
def: "Being in the early stage of puberty."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11154
name: pubic
def: "Relating to the region of the most ventral bone of the pelvis \(the pubic bone\)\, and thus the region around the genital area\, normally showing hair growth \(pubic hair\) in the adult."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11155
name: pubic symphysis
def: "Cartilaginous joint between the pubic bones forming the front of the pelvis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11156
name: pulmonary
def: "Pertaining to the lungs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11157
name: pulmonary artery
def: "Large vessel that transports blood from the heart to the lungs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11158
name: pulmonary valve stenosis
def: "Condition in which the flow of blood from the heart to the lungs is slowed by a deformity on or near the pulmonary valve that controls the blood flow from the right heart to the lungs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11159
name: pup
def: "\(n\) Young or newborn dog\, rat or seal.\n\(v\) Give birth to a pup."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11160
name: pyloric stenosis
def: "Narrowing of the sphincter \(the pylorus\) between the stomach and duodenum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11161
name: quickening
def: "Initial awareness of fetal movement by the pregnant mother\, usually occurring early in the second trimester."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11162
name: rachischisis
def: "Failure of the neural tube to close in utero\, resulting in a birth defect that leaves a fissure in the vertebral column with part of the spinal cord exposed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11163
name: nuclear receptor
def: "Receptor that encounters its ligand in the cytosol and translocates to the nucleus\, where it binds DNA and acts as a factor regulating gene transcription."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11164
name: recessive
def: "In genetics\, relating to or denoting heritable characteristics controlled by genes which are expressed in offspring only when inherited from both parents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11165
name: reciprocal mesenchymal-epithelial interaction
def: "Process in tissue or organ development in which epithelium induces changes in the mesenchyme and the mesenchyme also induces changes in the epithelium\, through paracrine signaling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11166
name: reciprocal translocation
def: "Chromosomal translocation in which crossing over between two non-homologous chromosomes leads to each carrying genetic material from the other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11167
name: genetic recombination
def: "Referring to new gene sequences\, and thus to new heritable information\, caused by gene crossovers\, occurring naturally in either meiosis or mitosis\, or constructed artificially for the purpose of introducing a new trait into an organism or cell line.\nIn immunology\, random scrambling of specific gene sequences to produce the vast array of possible immunoglobulin and T cell receptor sequences required to recognize\, potentially\, all possible antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11168
name: rectum
def: "Terminal part of the large intestine\, ending at the anus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11169
name: 5α-reductase
def: "Enzyme \(EC 1.3.99.5\) that converts testosterone to 5\\\(\\upalpha\\\)-dihydrotestosterone in peripheral tissues."
synonym: "alpha-reductase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11170
name: 5α-reductase deficiency
def: "Condition caused by a mutation of the $\\ce\{5\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}reductase\}$ type II gene\, with autosomal recessive inheritance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11171
name: renal
def: "Pertaining to the kidneys."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11172
name: reproductive senescence
def: "Progressive decline in reproductive capacity with age\, marked by menopause in females."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11173
name: reproductive toxicant
def: "Agent that interferes with reproductive function\, including sexual performance\, fertility\, and development of the fetus and embryo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11174
name: reproductive toxicity test
def: "Procedure based on specific guidelines in which adverse effects of a substance on reproduction and development are studied either in animals or by use of in vitro assays."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11175
name: resorption
def: "Removal of mineralized tissue by natural processes\, as in the breakdown of bone by osteoclasts or the disappearance of a tooth.\nDisintegration and assimilation of an embryo or fetus through a natural process involving lysis and removal of all the products of conception by cells of the maternal immune system.\nIn multigestational pregnancies\, death of one fetus followed by absorption of its tissue by another\, sometimes referred to as a 'vanishing twin'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11176
name: restriction site
def: "Short DNA nucleotide sequence that is recognized by a sequence-specific enzyme \(restriction enzyme\) that cleaves the double stranded DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11177
name: rete
def: "Fibrous mesh or network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11178
name: rete testis
def: "Rete in the testis that carries sperm from the seminiferous tubules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11179
name: retina
def: "Multicell-layer membrane in the back of the eye that is sensitive to light and transduces incoming optical information into signals that are necessary for vision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11180
name: retinoic acid
def: "Acidic metabolite of vitamin A."
synonym: "(2E,4E,6E,8E)-3,7-dimethyl-9-(2,6,6-trimethylcyclohex-1-en-1-yl)nona-2,4,6,8-tetraenoic acid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11181
name: retinopathy
def: "Noninflammatory degenerative disease of the retina."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11182
name: congenital retinoschisis
def: "Congenital splitting of the retina into two layers."
synonym: "X-linked retinoschisis" []
synonym: "juvenile retinoschisis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11183
name: retroperitoneal
def: "Outside of and posterior to the peritoneum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11184
name: reverse genetics
def: "Approach to elucidating the function of a gene by studying the effects on phenotype of expressed variations in a known DNA sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11185
name: rhesus factor
def: "Antigen that occurs on the red blood cells of about 85 per cent of humans and can cause hemolytic disease of the newborn \(erythroblastosis fetalis\) and hemolytic transfusion reactions."
synonym: "Rh factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11186
name: rhombencephalon
def: "Posterior portion of the brain that includes the cerebellum\, pons\, and medulla."
synonym: "hindbrain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11187
name: RNA interference
def: "Effect of small\, often double-stranded RNA targeting a specific messenger RNA \(mRNA\)\, blocking its translation\, and thereby silencing expression of the corresponding gene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11188
name: Robertsonian translocation
def: "Chromosomal translocation in which chromosomes with acrocentric centromeres break the centromere to fuse the long arms into a new large chromosome and the short arms into another small one that may be lost\, leaving a karyotype in the human of \\\(\\pu\{45\}\\\) chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11189
name: rostral
def: "Pertaining to\, resembling\, or having a rostrum or beak.\nSituated toward the beak \(thus\, oral and nasal region\)\, which may mean superior \(in relationships of areas of the spinal cord\) or anterior or ventral \(in relationships of brain areas\).\nCloser to the head."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11190
name: runt
def: "Smallest and weakest animal of a litter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11191
name: sacral agenesis
def: "Absence or significant underdevelopment of the lower part of the spine and the lower limbs."
synonym: "caudal regression syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11192
name: sagittal
def: "Vertical \(longitudinal\) plane dividing the body into right and left sections."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11193
name: salpingectomy
def: "Surgical removal of the Fallopian tubes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11194
name: saturation mutagenesis
def: "Mutagenesis screening in which a large number of mutations are introduced in a target area of the genome with the aim of identifying all the genes or their functions that are associated with that area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11195
name: scaphocephaly
def: "Elongated and narrow head with decrease of the parietal regions and conspicuous frontal and occipital protrusions; a type of craniosynostosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11196
name: scoliosis
def: "Abnormal lateral and rotational curvature of the vertebral spinal column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11197
name: scrotum
def: "Pouch of skin containing the testicles and their accessory tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11198
name: secondary sexual characteristic
def: "Feature of the adult animal\, appearing during puberty in humans and distinct between the sexes\, although not part of the reproductive system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11199
name: seizure
def: "Sudden change in neural activity\, leading to convulsions and \(or\) changes in consciousness of varying degrees of severity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11200
name: selector gene
def: "Member of a group of genes that codes factors driving differentiation and regional patterning during development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11201
name: sella turcica
def: "Depression on the upper surface of the sphenoid bone\, accommodating the pituitary gland."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11202
name: semen
def: "Fluid\, containing spermatozoa\, that is expelled from the penis during ejaculation."
synonym: "ejaculate (<em>n.</em>)" []
synonym: "seminal fluid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11203
name: seminal vesicle
def: "One of a pair of glands of the male reproductive system that together produce a significant portion of the semen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11204
name: seminiferous tubule
def: "One of numerous coiled tubes\, found in the testis\, in the walls of which spermatogenesis occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11205
name: sensitized strain
def: "Model organism containing a mutation in a pathway \(e.g.\, metabolic or signal transducing\) that does not itself cause a change in phenotype\, but makes the organism more sensitive to another change elsewhere in the pathway."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11206
name: septum
def: "In anatomy\, thin wall dividing two cavities or masses of softer tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11207
name: serous
def: "Containing\, resembling\, or secreting serum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11208
name: Sertoli cell
def: "Somatic cell in the seminiferous tubule that supports spermatogenesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11209
name: Sertoli–Leydig cell tumor
def: "Ovarian tumor composed of both Sertoli and Leydig cells."
synonym: "arrhenoblastoma" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11210
name: sex chromatin
def: "Condensed mass of one inactivated X chromosome seen inside the nuclear membrane of an interphase cell."
synonym: "Barr body" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11211
name: sex chromosome
def: "One of the pair of chromosomes determining sex; in humans designated an X chromosome and a Y chromosome\, with females having an XX genotype and males an XY genotype."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11212
name: sex-determining region Y protein
def: "Transcription factor coded by a gene on the Y chromosome that initiates development of the male sex organs \(i.e.\, testicular differentiation\) in the embryo."
synonym: "SRY protein" []
synonym: "testis-determining factor (TDF)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11213
name: sexual dimorphism
def: "Phenotypic difference between the sexes of a species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11214
name: sexually dimorphic nucleus
def: "Compact area of large cells in the anterior hypothalamus\, larger in men than women\, and believed to influence sexual behaviour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11215
name: sexual maturation
def: "Process of reaching the age or stage when an organism can reproduce sexually."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11216
name: sexual maturity
def: "Age or stage when an organism can reproduce sexually."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11217
name: sexual reproduction
def: "Creation of a new organism requiring combination of the genetic material from two different sexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11218
name: sibling
def: "One of two or more individuals with a common pair of parents."
synonym: "sib" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11219
name: signal transduction
def: "Process whereby a signal arising outside the cell is converted through a series of intermediate chemical reactions inside the cell to produce a functional change in the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11220
name: sinoatrial node
def: "Mass of specialized cardiac muscle cells \('pacemaker cells'\) that spontaneously depolarize to initiate rhythmic contraction of the heart."
synonym: "sinuatrial node" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11221
name: sinus
def: "Hollow or cavity.\nChannel for the passage of fluid\, lacking the usual lining of a blood or lymphatic vessel; especially a dilatation for the passage of venous blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11222
name: sinus venosus
def: "Cavity at the caudal end of the developing embryonic heart where intra- and extra-embryonic veins meet."
synonym: "venous sinus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11223
name: sister chromatid exchange
def: "Reciprocal exchange of chromatin between two replicated chromosomes that remain attached to each other until anaphase of mitosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11224
name: situs inversus
def: "Developmental anomaly in which major visceral organs are found in a mirrored position to their normal location."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11225
name: small interfering RNA molecule
def: "Short interfering RNA."
synonym: "siRNA molecule" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11226
name: somatic
def: "Pertaining to the body or describing cells that form the body other than germ line cells or undifferentiated stem cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11227
name: somatic hypermutation
def: "Programmed process of mutation affecting the variable \(V\) regions of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) genes. SHM affects only individual immune cells\, and the mutations are not transmitted to offspring."
synonym: "mutation (in immunology)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11228
name: human chorionic somatomammotropin
def: "Placental hormone that affects maternal metabolism and supports fetal growth by making more glucose and fatty acids available to the fetus."
synonym: "human placental lactogen (hPL)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11229
name: somatomedin
def: "Any of a group of peptide growth factors produced by the liver following stimulation by somatotropin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11230
name: somatotropin
def: "Hormone produced in the pituitary gland that stimulates the liver to produce somatomedin."
synonym: "growth hormone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11231
name: somite
def: "One of bilaterally paired\, segmented masses of mesodermal tissue lying along the notochord that gives rise to the vertebrae and associated muscle and connective tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11232
name: sonic hedgehog
def: "Protein morphogen produced in the notochord that plays a critical role in early development\, including vertebrate organogenesis\, brain development and limb formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11233
name: spawn
def: "\(v\) Release gametes. \(Of mature adult fish\, frogs\, molluscs\, crustacea\, etc.\).\n\(n\) Fertilized eggs of mature adult fish\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11234
name: spawning
def: "\(n\) Release of gametes \(eggs or sperm\) from mature adult fish\, frogs\, molluscs\, crustacea\, etc.\n\(adj\) Describing behavior related to the readiness of mature adult fish\, etc. to release gametes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11235
name: Spemann organizer
def: "Group of cells in the amphibian embryo that is important in orientation of surrounding cells and facilitates development of the central nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11236
name: sperm banking
def: "Storage of frozen donor sperm for use in artificial insemination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11237
name: spermatid
def: "Haploid cell in the late stage of developing into a spermatozoon\, derived from the secondary spermatocyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11238
name: spermatocide
def: "Agent that kills spermatozoa."
synonym: "spermicide" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11239
name: spermatocyte
def: "Parent cell of a spermatid\, derived by mitotic division from a spermatogonium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11240
name: spermatogenesis
def: "Entire process by which spermatogonial stem cells divide and differentiate into spermatozoa."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11241
name: spermatogonial chromosome aberration test
def: "Procedure in which rodents are exposed to a test substance and chromosome aberrations are then studied microscopically in germ cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11242
name: spermatogonium
def: "Primitive cell derived from mitosis of the germ cell that becomes the diploid primary spermatocyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11243
name: spermatozoon
def: "Mature male gamete."
synonym: "sperm" []
synonym: "sperm cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11244
name: spermiation
def: "Release of mature spermatozoa from the Sertoli cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11245
name: spermiogenesis
def: "Maturation of an immature spermatid into a spermatozoon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11246
name: sphenoid bone
def: "Irregular wing-shaped bone at the anterior base of the skull\, forming part of the orbit\, serving as an attachment site for muscles of mastication\, and containing foramina for nerves and vessels of the head and neck."
synonym: "sphenoid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11247
name: sphincter
def: "Ringlike muscle that maintains closure of a body passage or orifice by contracting\, and opens it by relaxing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11248
name: spina bifida
def: "Failure of the neural tube to close\, resulting in the absence of part of the vertebral arch at the midline of the spinal column and exposure of the spinal cord and its covering membranes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11249
name: spinal column
def: "Backbone."
synonym: "spine" []
synonym: "vertebral column" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11250
name: spinal cord
def: "Portion of the central nervous system outside the brain and inside the vertebral column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11251
name: spiral arteriole
def: "One of the corkscrew-like arterioles in premenstrual or progestational endometrium."
synonym: "coiled artery of the uterus" []
synonym: "corkscrew-like artery" []
synonym: "spiral artery" []
synonym: "spiral endometrial artery" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11252
name: splanchnic
def: "Relating to the visceral organs\, especially those of the abdomen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11253
name: spongioblast
def: "Embryonic epithelial cell that develops into a neuroglial cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11254
name: stalk
def: "In anatomy\, narrow connection with an organ or other structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11255
name: allantoic stalk
def: "Narrow connecting tube between the intra- and extra-embryonic parts of the allantois."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11256
name: stenosis
def: "Abnormal narrowing of a passage or opening in the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11257
name: sterility
def: "Infertility.\nAsepsis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11258
name: sterilization
def: "Process that makes an organism incapable of fertilization or reproduction\, e.g.\, castration\, vasectomy\, or salpingectomy.\nProcess that makes an object aseptic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11259
name: steroid
def: "Naturally occurring compounds and synthetic analogues\, based on the cyclopenta\[a\]phenanthrene carbon skeleton\, partially or completely hydrogenated; there are usually methyl groups at \\\(\\ce\{C-10\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{C-13\}\\\)\, and often an alkyl group at \\\(\\ce\{C-17\}\\\). By extension\, one or more bond scissions\, ring expansions\, and/or ring contractions of the skeleton may have occurred."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11260
name: steroid 21-monooxygenase
def: "Member \(EC 1.14.14.16\) of the cytochrome P450 family of enzymes required for the synthesis of some steroid hormones including aldosterone and cortisol."
synonym: "21-hydroxylase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11261
name: steroidogenesis assay
def: "Procedure in which the effect of a test substance on steroid synthesis is studied\, usually in a human adrenocortical carcinoma cell line."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11262
name: stillbirth
def: "Birth of a baby without any signs of life\, at a time variously taken as \\\(\\pu\{20\\!\{-\}\\! 28 weeks\}\\\) of pregnancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11263
name: stress-response pathway
def: "Signaling events induced when normal cell function or development is disrupted by environmental \(physical or chemical\) factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11264
name: stromal cell
def: "Cell producing the connective tissue framework of an organ or other anatomical structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11265
name: subthalamus
def: "Part of the diencephalon\, of which the major part is the subthalamic nucleus\, and which connects to the globus pallidus\, part of the telencephalon."
synonym: "prethalamus" []
synonym: "ventral thalamus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11266
name: sulcus
def: "Any long\, narrow groove\, furrow or shallow depression\, specifically referring to one of the fissures on the surface of the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11267
name: superfecundation
def: "Fertilization of two or more ova released in the same period of ovulation\, either by different males or by the same male in separate acts of intercourse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11268
name: superfetation
def: "Presence of two fetuses in the uterus at different stages of development\, resulting from fertilization of ova released in two successive periods of ovulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11269
name: supernumerary
def: "Exceeding the usual number; thus\, in anatomy\, anomalies such as a sixth finger on one hand or a third nipple \(in a human\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11270
name: pulmonary surfactant
def: "Surface-active layer of phospholipids and proteins coating the pulmonary alveoli that increases lung compliance\, facilitates lung expansion\, and stabilizes alveolar volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11271
name: synapsis
def: "Point-wise pairing of homologous chromosomes during the prophase of meiosis."
synonym: "syndesis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11272
name: synaptonemal complex
def: "Structure of filamentous proteins that forms between homologous chromosomes when they pair during meiosis; it may contribute to synapsis and genetic recombination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11273
name: syncephalus
def: "Conjoined twins with two bodies and a fused head."
synonym: "monocephalus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11274
name: syncytiotrophoblast
def: "Outer syncytial layer of trophoblast cells that invades the endometrium during implantation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11275
name: syncytium
def: "Referring to a large multinucleated mass of cellular contents that arises from the fusion of originally individual cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11276
name: syndactyly
def: "Congenital occurrence of fusion or webbing of the fingers or toes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11277
name: syndrome
def: "Group of signs and symptoms occurring together\, having a common cause and defining a disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11278
name: synophthalmia
def: "Congenital fusion of the orbits in the midline\, which then contains one eyeball."
synonym: "cyclopia" []
synonym: "monophthalmos" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11279
name: synotia
def: "Congenital malformation characterized by the union or approximation of the ears in front of the neck\, often accompanied by the absence or defective development of the lower jaw."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11280
name: tail bud
def: "In the vertebrate embryo\, mass of proliferating cells at the caudal end that arises from the primitive node."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11281
name: Tay–Sachs disease
def: "Autosomal recessive inheritance of hexosaminidase A deficiency leading to a lysosomal storage disease characterized by central and peripheral neuronal involvement and early death."
synonym: "infantile GM2 gangliosidosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11282
name: tegmentum
def: "Floor of the midbrain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11283
name: telencephalon
def: "Embryonic structure that develops into the cerebrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11284
name: telophase
def: "Final phase of both meiosis and mitosis in which distinct nuclei form in each daughter cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11285
name: teratocarcinoma
def: "Malignant teratoma\, occurring most often in the testis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11286
name: teratogen
def: "Chemical\, physical\, or biological agent that\, when administered to a parent either prior to conception or before birth of the child\, induces permanent structural malformations or birth defects in the offspring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11287
name: teratogenesis
def: "Process of development of malformations or birth defects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11288
name: teratogenetics
def: "Study of how genes and teratogens interact to cause birth defects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11289
name: teratogenic index
def: "Mortality of eggs expressed as a lethal concentration divided by the threshold concentration for production of abnormal embryos with nonheritable permanent structural malformations or defects following exposure to a teratogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11290
name: teratogenicity
def: "Inherent ability to act as a teratogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11291
name: teratology
def: "Study of the causes\, mechanisms\, and manifestations of developmental deviation of either structure or function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11292
name: teratoma
def: "Benign germ cell-derived tumor containing embryonic elements of the three primary germ layers\, such as skin\, hair\, and muscle\, occurring most frequently in the ovary."
synonym: "dermoid cyst" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11293
name: Tessier classification
def: "Classification of bony and soft tissue facial clefts based on their anatomic location."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11294
name: testicular feminization
def: "Type of male pseudohermaphroditism in which the individual has a male karyotype and testes present within the abdominal cavity\, but develops external female genitalia and female secondary sexual characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11295
name: testis
def: "Gonad of a male animal\, involved in the secretion of androgens and the site of sperm production."
synonym: "testicle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11296
name: testosterone
def: "Androgenic steroid hormone produced primarily in the testis\, responsible for the development of the male sexual organs and male secondary sexual characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11297
name: tetralogy of Fallot
def: "Set of four congenital cardiac defects involving an opening in the wall that should separate the right and left ventricles \(ventricular septal defect\)\, allowing the aorta to receive venous as well as arterial blood\: including stenosis of the pulmonary artery\, hypertrophy of the right ventricle\, and displacement of the aorta. Right ventricular hypertrophy is the fourth part of the tetralogy\, although it may also be a consequence of the other defects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11298
name: thalamus
def: "Either of two masses of grey matter\, the largest part of the diencephalon\, lying between the cerebral hemispheres on either side of the third ventricle\, relaying sensory information and acting as a centre for pain perception."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11299
name: thalidomide
def: "Teratogenic indole-based drug with sedative and anti-angiogenic properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11300
name: theca
def: "Bilayer covering of the ovarian follicle \(thus theca interna and externa\)\, of which the theca interna is involved in production of androstenedione."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11301
name: thoracic duct
def: "Largest of the lymphatic vessels; it conducts lymph from its origin in the abdomen to the venous circulation at the junction of the left subclavian and jugular veins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11302
name: thrombocytopenia
def: "Abnormally low concentration of platelets \(thrombocytes\) in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11303
name: thymus
def: "Pyramid-shaped organ in the thoracic or cervical region of mammals\, composed of lymphatic tissue in which minute concentric bodies \(thymic corpuscles\, the remnants of epithelial structures\) are found."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11304
name: thyroglossal duct
def: "Connection in the embryo between the site of initiation of development of the thyroid gland and its final location in the neck."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11305
name: thyroid gland
def: "Bilobar endocrine gland\, located below the prominence of cartilage surrounding the larynx \(the Adam’s apple\)\, that produces and secretes the hormones triiodothyronine \(T3\)\, thyroxin \(T4\)\, and calcitonin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11306
name: accessory thyroid tissue
def: "Ectopic thyroid tissue arising from remnants of the thyroglossal duct."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11307
name: thyroid stimulating hormone
def: "Pituitary hormone that stimulates the thyroid gland to produce thyroxine \(T4\)\, and then triiodothyronine \(T3\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11308
name: tonsil
def: "Small\, rounded mass of tissue\, especially of lymphoid tissue; generally used to designate one of the paired palatine tonsils."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11309
name: toxoplasmosis
def: "Infection with the parasite Toxoplasma gondii that\, when acquired by the fetus\, can lead to a variety of congenital abnormalities that may include hydrocephalus or microcephaly\, mental retardation\, and problems with vision and hearing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11310
name: trachea
def: "Thin-walled\, cartilaginous tube descending from the larynx to the bronchi and carrying air to the lungs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11311
name: transcription factor
def: "DNA-binding protein that is involved in regulating gene expression."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11312
name: transgenic
def: "Describing an organism that is genetically changed by the addition or deletion of genetic material or whose existing genes are altered by gene targeting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11313
name: chromosomal translocation
def: "Rearrangement involving transfer or exchange of genetic material between non-homologous chromosomes that may occur during gametogenesis or in somatic cells\, potentially leading to birth defects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11314
name: transplacental carcinogen
def: "Substance that crosses the placenta and subsequently causes cancer in the child or young adult."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11315
name: transposition of the great arteries
def: "Congenital cardiovascular malformation in which the position of the chief blood vessels of the heart \(aorta and pulmonary artery\) is reversed."
synonym: "transposition of the great vessels" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11316
name: Treacher Collins syndrome
def: "Congenital deformity characterized by structural abnormalities of the head and face."
synonym: "Treacher Collins–Franceschetti syndrome" []
synonym: "mandibulofacial dysostosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11317
name: trilaminar embryo
def: "Disc-like embryonic stage in which differentiation into three layers \(endoderm\, mesoderm\, and ectoderm\) has occurred."
synonym: "trilaminary blastoderm" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11318
name: trimester
def: "In pregnancy\, one of three equal timed divisions of the normal human gestation period\, each lasting approximately three months."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11319
name: triple screen
def: "Measurement of maternal serum levels of alpha-fetoprotein\, human chorionic gonadotropin\, and estriol\, usually in the second trimester of pregnancy\, that can serve as an indicator of risk for certain fetal chromosomal abnormalities and neural tube defects."
synonym: "triple test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11320
name: triploidy
def: "Presence of three haploid sets of chromosomes in the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11321
name: trisomy
def: "Condition of having three homologous chromosomes in each somatic cell instead of the normal pair."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11322
name: trisomy 8
def: "Presence of an extra chromosome 8."
synonym: "Warkany syndrome 2" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11323
name: trisomy 13
def: "Presence of part or all of an extra chromosome 13 from nondisjunction during meiosis."
synonym: "Patau syndrome" []
synonym: "trisomy D" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11324
name: trisomy 18
def: "Presence of part or all of an extra chromosome 18 from nondisjunction during meiosis."
synonym: "Edwards syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11325
name: trisomy 21
def: "Presence of an extra chromosome 21."
synonym: "Down syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11326
name: trophectoderm
def: "Outer layer of the blastocyst that contacts the endometrium\, establishes nutrition for the embryo\, and differentiates into the trophoblast."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11327
name: trophoblast
def: "Outer layer of the blastocyst that invades the endometrium and establishes nutrition for the embryo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11328
name: truncus arteriosus
def: "Arterial trunk\, opening from the fetal heart and developing into the aorta and pulmonary artery."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11329
name: tuberoinfundibular pathway
def: "Group of dopaminergic neurons in the hypothalamus involved in regulating prolactin secretion from the anterior pituitary gland."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11330
name: tumor
def: "Any abnormal swelling or growth of tissue\, whether benign or malignant."
synonym: "tumour" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11331
name: tunica albuginea
def: "Dense\, collagenous fibrous coat surrounding an anatomical structure\, and in particular surrounding the ovaries\, testicles\, and corpora cavernosa of the penis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11332
name: tunica vaginalis
def: "Membranous sheath\, derived from the peritoneum\, surrounding the testis and epididymis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11333
name: turner syndrome
def: "Phenotypic female lacking one X chromosome\, the genotype being designated XO."
synonym: "Ullrich–Turner syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11334
name: two-generation reprotox study
def: "Procedure in rodents wherein parents \(P0 generation\) and their offspring \(F1 generation\) are both exposed to a test substance and the second generation \(F2\) is examined for possible toxic effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11335
name: tympanic
def: "In anatomy\, relating to the resonant cavity and membrane of the inner ear."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11336
name: ultrasonography
def: "Imaging technique that creates a picture of internal body structures from differentially reflected ultrasound waves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11337
name: ultrasound
def: "Sound waves of frequency higher than the range audible to the human ear.\nImaging technique that uses such sound waves to create a picture of internal body structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11338
name: umbilical artery
def: "Paired artery that\, in the fetus\, returns deoxygenated blood from each half of the fetal body to the placenta\, via the umbilical cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11339
name: umbilical cord
def: "Cord-like structure connecting the fetus with the placenta and housing the umbilical artery and umbilical vein that carry nutrients from the mother and remove wastes from the fetus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11340
name: umbilical hernia
def: "Protrusion of bowel or omentum through the abdominal wall at the umbilicus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11341
name: umbilical vein
def: "Vessel within the umbilical cord\, entering the fetus at the umbilicus and carrying oxygenated blood from the placenta."
synonym: "left umbilical vein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11342
name: undescended testis
def: "Testis that has failed to descend completely from its developmental origin in the lower abdominal cavity into the scrotum."
synonym: "cryptorchid testis" []
synonym: "cryptorchidism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11343
name: urethra
def: "Duct by which urine is conveyed out of the body from the bladder\, and which in male vertebrates also conveys semen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11344
name: urogenital
def: "Concerning the urinary and genital organs."
synonym: "genitourinary" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11345
name: urogenital sinus
def: "Embryonic structure that forms in the ventral part of the cloaca when it separates from the anal canal to give rise to the genitourinary organs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11346
name: uterine crypt
def: "Chamber on the interior surface of the uterus that serves as a site of embryo homing and implantation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11347
name: uterine cycle
def: "Regular\, periodic changes that occur in the uterus and\, together with the ovarian cycle constitute the menstrual cycle necessary for female fertility."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11348
name: uterotrophic
def: "Having an effect on the uterus."
synonym: "uterotropic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11349
name: uterotrophic assay
def: "Procedure in which immature female rodents are treated for three days with a test substance. A resultant increase in uterine wet weight suggests an estrogenic activity of the substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11350
name: uterus
def: "Hollow muscular organ of the female reproductive system that receives the fertilized ovum \(see implantation\) and supports the subsequent development of the fetus."
synonym: "womb" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11351
name: uvula
def: "Muscular projection from the posterior edge of the soft palate."
synonym: "palatine uvula" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11352
name: vagina
def: "Fibromuscular canal passing between the uterine cervix and the opening to the vulva."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11353
name: vaginal cornification
def: "Conversion of the normal epithelium of the vagina to a keratinized squamous \(flattened\) epithelium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11354
name: vaginal patency
def: "Referring to the opening of the vagina that usually occurs at sexual maturity and is maintained by hormonal influences pre-menopausally."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11355
name: vaginal smear
def: "Examination by light microscopy of a sample of a vaginal discharge\, used in the diagnosis of a vaginal infection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11356
name: developmental variation
def: "Anatomical deviation that is not life-threatening."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11357
name: vasculogenesis
def: "De novo development of blood vessel endothelium in absence of preexisting vessels\, e.g.\, during fetal development or in connection with tissue repair\, initiated by migration of mesodermal precursor cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11358
name: vas deferens
def: "Secretory duct of the testis running between the epididymis and the ejaculatory duct."
synonym: "ductus deferens" []
synonym: "spermatic duct" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11359
name: vasectomy
def: "Surgical removal of all or part of each vas deferens\, typically as a means of sterilization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11360
name: vasopressin
def: "Pituitary hormone that acts to promote the retention of water by the kidneys and to increase blood pressure."
synonym: "antidiuretic hormone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11361
name: velopharyngeal insufficiency
def: "Inability to achieve closure of the velopharyngeal sphincter \(closure of the muscle of the soft palate\) during speech\, owing to muscular dysfunction\, cleft palate\, or other disorders."
synonym: "velopharyngeal incompetence" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11362
name: vena cava
def: "Large vein returning deoxygenated blood to the right heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11363
name: ventral
def: "Situated to the anterior side of the trunk; in humans\, to the front of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11364
name: ventricular septum
def: "Dividing wall between the two lower chambers \(ventricles\) of the heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11365
name: ventricular septal defect
def: "Congenital defect with an opening in the wall separating the right and left ventricles of the heart \(see ventricular septum\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11366
name: vertebra
def: "One of the bony segments that together make up the bony column surrounding the spinal cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11367
name: vertebrate
def: "Denoting an animal that has as part of its nervous system a spinal cord that is surrounded by a bony vertebral column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11368
name: vesicle
def: "Small fluid-filled sac\, bladder-like structure\, or blister."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11369
name: vestigial
def: "In anatomy\, pertaining to a remnant of an embryonic or fetal structure persisting in the adult."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11370
name: viability
def: "Ability to continue living."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11371
name: villus
def: "Projection from the surface\, typically of a mucous membrane.\nElongated projection of the dermis into an epidermal space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11372
name: vimentin
def: "Intermediate filament protein that is expressed in mesenchymal cells\, e.g.\, fibroblasts\, leukocytes\, and blood vessel endothelial cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11373
name: virilization
def: "Possession or acquisition of characteristics of a male body\, especially by a female or prepubescent male."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11374
name: viscera
def: "Soft and hollow organs of the body\, in vertebrates particularly those found in the thoracic and abdominal cavities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11375
name: vitellogenin
def: "Protein that forms part of the yolk of egg-laying vertebrates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11376
name: vulva
def: "External genitalia of the female."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11377
name: wean
def: "Accustomize a young mammal to independence from its mother’s milk as a source of nutrition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11378
name: web
def: "Tissue or membrane present between adjacent structures where it is not usually found\, e.g.\, between fingers or toes\, or between the neck and shoulder\, representing a congenital abnormality."
synonym: "webbing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11379
name: Wharton’s jelly
def: "Gelatinous substance that embeds the vessels of the umbilical cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11380
name: white matter
def: "Portion of the brain and spinal cord consisting mainly of myelinated axons \(see myelination\) and glial cells."
synonym: "substantia alba" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11381
name: whole embryo culture
def: "Technique\, used prior to embryo transfer during in vitro fertilization\, in which embryos undergoing organogenesis are maintained in formulated medium outside the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11382
name: wild type
def: "Of an allele or phenotype\, the naturally occurring\, unaltered\, or most frequent; if several alleles occur\, the most frequent one is considered to be the wild type."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11383
name: Williams syndrome
def: "Congenital disorder with characteristic facies \(described as elf-like\)\, short stature\, outgoing personality and mild mental retardation; associated with contiguous gene deletions on chromosome 7."
synonym: "elfin facies syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11384
name: Wnt
def: "Family of genes important in development\, the proteins they encode\, or the signal transduction pathways they determine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11385
name: X chromosome
def: "Chromosome determining female sex in the absence of a Y chromosome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11386
name: X-linked
def: "Carried by a gene located on the X chromosome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11387
name: Y chromosome
def: "Chromosome determining male sex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11388
name: yolk
def: "Nutritive material stored in the ovum for the nourishment of the embryo."
synonym: "vitellus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11389
name: yolk sac
def: "Fluid-filled\, membrane-bound pouch on the ventral side of the early embryo that provides nourishment until the circulatory system develops."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11390
name: yolk stalk
def: "Narrow tubular support that connects the yolk sac to the middle of the digestive tract of an embryo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11391
name: zinc finger
def: "Small tertiary protein structure in which parts of the protein\, typically with cysteine and histidine residues\, link to a divalent zinc ion\, forming a loop or 'finger' that attaches proteins to DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11392
name: zona pellucida
def: "Acellular glycoprotein-rich membrane surrounding the mature ovum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11393
name: zygosis
def: "Sexual union of two cells with fusion of their nuclei."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11394
name: zygote intrafallopian transfer
def: "Introduction of a zygote fertilized in vitro into one of the Fallopian tubes."
synonym: "tubal embryo transfer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11395
name: 1D property descriptor
def: "An observed or calculated property of the whole molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11396
name: 1D structure
def: "The structure of a molecule encoded into a string such as SMILES or InChI."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11397
name: 2D property
def: "A molecular property that is calculated from the structure diagram of the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11398
name: 2D structure
def: "The structure of a molecule presented as a drawing or a file that contains a description of the topology\, stereochemistry\, and atomic symbol of its atoms and the bonds connecting them\, but no explicit information about its three-dimensional structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11399
name: 2D quantitative structure-activity relationships
def: "A computational model of the quantitative relationship between the observed independent 1D property of a set of compounds and their dependent 1D properties or 2D properties."
synonym: "two-dimensional quantitative structure-activity relationships" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11400
name: 3D property
def: "A property that is observed or calculated from a 3D representation of a molecule\, which may depend on one or more conformations of the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11401
name: 3D searching
def: "A virtual screening method that processes a chemical database to discover those compounds that match a query that either contains a 3D pharmacophore\, a molecular shape or field\, the 3D structure\, or a combination of these."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11402
name: 3D structure
def: "The structure of the conformation of a molecule presented as a drawing or a file that describes atomic symbols of its atoms and the bonds connecting them as well as their coordinates in three-dimensional space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11403
name: 3D structure generation
def: "A method to generate one or more 3D structures\, conformations\, of a molecule from its topological molecular graph."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11404
name: 4D quantitative structure-activity relationships
def: "A computational model of the quantitative relationship between the target observed independent 1D properties for a set of compounds and the 3D properties of\n several of their conformations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11405
name: algorithm
def: "A step-by-step procedure for solving a problem or performing a function\, usually by a computer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11406
name: applicability domain
def: "The property or structure space for which the predictions of a computational model are considered to be reliable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11407
name: area under the curve
def: "The area under a graph curve\, such as the number of actives identified by an algorithm as a function of the number of compounds tested\, commonly used as a\n measure of the discriminating ability of an algorithm to correctly classify a test molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11408
name: autocorrelation vector
def: "A vector that describes a molecular structure in which each element corresponds to a distance \(number of bonds in a 2D structure or a binned interatomic\n distance in a 3D structure\) between atoms of a particular type and the count of the number of times that distance is found in the structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11409
name: basis set superposition error
def: "An artifactual increase in calculated stability of the supersystem \(the system formed by noncovalent interaction between two or more molecular entities\, e.g. hydrogen bond system\) resulting from the basis set of the supersystem being larger than for the component subsystems. The BSSE arises from a lowering of the quantum mechanical energy when the electron density of each subsystem spreads into the basis functions provided by the other subsystems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11410
name: Bayesian regularized neural network
def: "A feed-forward neural network that uses Bayesian statistics to optimize the complexity and predictive power of the model."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11411
name: Bayesian statistics
def: "A branch of statistics in which the evidence of the state of a system is expressed in terms of degrees of belief \(probabilities\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11412
name: Bayes’s theorem
def: "A method for calculating prior probability estimates of an event that can be revised in accordance with new observations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11413
name: belief theory
def: "A method to combine probability estimate states that given two or more probabilities \\\(P_\{i\}\\\) that a particular event is true\, the combined probability of the event is given by\: \\\[P = 1 - \\Pi \(1 - \{P_\{i\}\}\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:11414
name: bilinear equation
def: "A QSAR equation that describes the non-linear dependence of the relative biological potency \(\\\(\\log 1/C\\\)\) of a molecule on log P by the following form\: \\\[log\(1/\{C_i\}\) = a\\log P_\{i\} - b \\log \(\\beta P_\{i\} + 1\) + c\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:11415
name: bit string
def: "A description of a molecule in a fixed length vector\, each element of which is set from corresponding structural keys or calculated by hashing a molecular\n fingerprint."
synonym: "bitmap" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11416
name: Boltzmann enhanced discrimination of receiver operating characteristic
def: "A generalization of the area under the ROC curve to weight more heavily the early recognition of active compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11417
name: bootstrap resampling
def: "A procedure to evaluate the accuracy of the statistics of a model\, such as the overall R2 or the contribution of particular properties\, by systematically recomputing the statistics generated from many models developed using sample sets that contain the same number of observations as the original set\, but for which certain observations are randomly omitted and other observations are included more than once."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11418
name: calculated molar refractivity
def: "The calculated molar refractivity of a molecule or substituent used as a measure of size and polarizability in 2D-QSAR."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11419
name: canonical structure representation
def: "A unique representation of the chemical structure of a molecule that is independent of the sequence in which the atoms were ordered in the original input file\n but is dependent on the algorithm used for the canonicalization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11420
name: chance correlation
def: "An artificial correlation that can arise when too many properties are screened relative to the number of available observations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11421
name: cheminformatics
def: "The science of handling\, indexing\, archiving\, searching\, and evaluating information that is specific to chemical structures and is used in data mining\,\n information retrieval\, information extraction\, and machine learning."
synonym: "chemoinformatics" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11422
name: circular fingerprints
def: "Hashed molecular fingerprints that describe\, either by atom type or properties\, each atom in the molecule; the atoms connected to it \(for path length 2\); the\n atoms connected to them \(for path length 4\); and the atoms connected to them \(for path length 6\); etc.."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11423
name: clog P
def: "Calculated log of the octan-1-ol/water partition coefficient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11424
name: cluster centroid
def: "The geometric center of a cluster\, often exemplified as the object that is closest to the center."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11425
name: collinearity
def: "A linear relationship between two or more of the descriptors in a model\, which can lead to difficulties in interpreting the relative importance of these\n descriptors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11426
name: comparative molecular similarity analysis
def: "A 3D-QSAR method similar to CoMFA that instead uses Gaussian-type functions to calculate the 3D properties of the molecules by the similarity of each molecule\n to each probe at each lattice position surrounding the molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11427
name: concordance
def: "The ability of a two-group classification model to correctly classify all molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11428
name: confusion matrix
def: "A table layout that shows the results of a two-class/binary classifier in which columns correspond to the predicted classifications from the model and rows correspond to the observed classifications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11429
name: continuum
def: "The computational representation of a solvent as a continuous medium instead of individual explicit solvent molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11430
name: counterpoise correction
def: "A method to correct for basis set superposition error."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11431
name: data mining
def: "The extraction of useful information from large sets of observations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11432
name: Daylight fingerprints
def: "Path fingerprints that are generated from a structure where atoms are described by atomic number and aromaticity; bonds are described as single\, double\,\n triple\, or aromatic; and the results are hashed into a bit string of fixed length."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11433
name: de novo design
def: "The process whereby a computer algorithm designs new molecules to meet certain single or multiple criteria\, defined by an objective function\, for example predicted affinity for a binding site or predicted affinity plus appropriate lipophilicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11434
name: decision tree
def: "The result of a recursive partitioning classification method that shows at each branching point the value of the property responsible for the split and the resulting classifications after the split."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11435
name: density functional theory
def: "A quantum mechanical modeling method used to investigate the electronic structure of molecules using functionals that describe the spatially dependent electron density\, rather than the wavefunction as in\nab initio and semi-empirical quantum chemical methods."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11436
name: descriptor
def: "A qualitative or quantitative property of a molecule or a part of a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11437
name: docking
def: "Computational methods that optimize the placement of a ligand in a macromolecular binding site of known or proposed 3D structure and provide a score as to the quality of the fit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11438
name: drug-likeness
def: "An estimate of the probability that a chemical structure is similar to known drugs based on chemical or physical properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11439
name: energy of the highest occupied molecular orbital
def: "The energy of the highest energy level in the ground state of a molecule that contains electrons\, sometimes used as a measure of nucleophilicity in QSAR\n models."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11440
name: energy of the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital
def: "The energy of the lowest energy level of a molecule that contains no electrons in the ground state\, sometimes used as a measure of electrophilicity in QSAR\n models."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11441
name: expert system
def: "A system that predicts properties or activities of chemicals from rules devised by domain experts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11442
name: explicit solvent
def: "Individual solvent molecules that are included in a computation\, in contrast to those implied by a continuum approximation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11443
name: extended connectivity fingerprints
def: "Hashed circular fingerprints that encode each atom according to its atomic symbol and hybridization for path length 2\, ECFP2; path length 4\, ECFP4; and path\n length 6\, ECFP6."
synonym: "ECFP fingerprints" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11444
name: false negative
def: "A model prediction of a negative result\, such as no biological activity\, when the true result is positive\, i.e. activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11445
name: false positive
def: "A model prediction of a positive result\, such as biological activity\, when the true result is negative\, i.e. inactivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11446
name: force field
def: "A set of mathematical functions and their associated parameters used in a molecular mechanics or dynamics calculation of conformations\, flexibility\, and interactions of molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11447
name: fragment keys
def: "Description of the structure of a molecule as the presence or absence of each of a pre-determined set of molecular substructures\, usually associated with a\n particular location in a bit string."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11448
name: free energy perturbation
def: "A method that uses molecular dynamics or Metropolis Monte Carlo simulations to compute the relative free energy differences between two conditions\, typically the solution and bound states of a ligand-protein system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11449
name: functional class fingerprints
def: "Hashed circular fingerprints that encode each atom according to its property \(hydrophobic\, hydrogen bond donor\, hydrogen bond acceptor\, negatively charged\, or\n positively charged\) for path length 2\, FCFP2; path length 4\, FCFP4; and path length 6\, FCFP6."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11450
name: global model
def: "A computational model designed to cover all of chemistry space although it typically includes a measure of if the molecule for which the properties are to be\n predicted is within or outside the applicability domain of the model."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11451
name: graph-based method
def: "A computational method that converts the problem to be solved into a graph characterized by the character of the objects\, such the atoms of a molecule or molecules in a database\, and the distances between them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11452
name: Hansch equation
def: "A QSAR equation that describes the relationship between the logarithm of the relative biological potency of a molecule \(\\\(\\log \(1/C_\{i\}\)\\\)\) and its electronic and hydrophobic properties\: \\\[\\log \(1/C_\{i\}\) = \\rho \\sigma_\{i\} + b\\\,\\log P_\{i\} - c\\\,\(\\log P_\{i\}\)^\{2\}\\\] in which fitting to the square term provides a parabola with an associated optimum log P."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11453
name: Hansch–Fujita π-constant
def: "A constant that describes the contribution of a substituent to the octan-1-ol-water log P of a compound.\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:11454
name: hashing
def: "An algorithm that maps data of variable length\, such as the raw descriptors of path or circular fingerprints or InChIs\, to a smaller fixed length vector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11455
name: InChIKey
def: "A fixed length \(character\) condensed digital representation of the InChI that is not human-understandable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11456
name: International Chemical Identifier
def: "A non-proprietary canonical identifier of chemical substances that can be used in printed and electronic data sources to enable easier linking of diverse data\n compilations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11457
name: ISIS keys
def: "Predefined fragment keys that are used in 2D substructure and similarity searching with the ISIS software but are also generated by many other software\n programs and used for 2D-QSAR or cluster analysis of molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11458
name: jackknifing
def: "A procedure to evaluate the accuracy of the statistics of a model\, such as the overall R2 or the contribution of particular properties\, by systematically recomputing the statistics generated from models developed by leaving out one or more observations at a time from the sample set."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11459
name: kappa shape descriptor
def: "A type of topological index."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11460
name: kernel method
def: "Algorithms for pattern analysis that use functions such as Gaussians to enable them to operate in a high-dimensional descriptor space without ever computing\n the coordinates of the data in that space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11461
name: leave-some-out cross-validation
def: "A form of cross-validation in which more than one observation is left out at each run. Typically many runs with random samplings are performed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11462
name: leverage
def: "The influence of an observation on the coefficients of a fitted equation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11463
name: ligand efficiency
def: "Measure of the free energy of binding per heavy atom count \(i.e. non-hydrogen\) of a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11464
name: linear interaction energy
def: "A method that forecasts ligand binding free energies using force field estimations of the receptor-ligand interactions and thermal conformational sampling\n."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11465
name: lipophilic ligand efficiency
def: "A measure of the efficiency of ligand binding that is corrected for binding driven by lipophilicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11466
name: loading of a property
def: "The contribution of the property to a principal component or partial least squares latent variable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11467
name: local model
def: "A computational model that applies to only a subset of molecules\, typically a closely related series."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11468
name: log <em>D</em>
def: "The logarithm of the ratio of the total concentration \(neutral and charged species\) of a compound in a nonpolar phase\, traditionally water-saturated octan-1-ol\, to that in water at a given \\\(\\pu\{pH\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11469
name: log <em>P</em>
def: "The logarithm of the ratio of the concentration of the uncharged form of a compound in a nonpolar phase\, traditionally water-saturated octan-1-ol\, to that in\n water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11470
name: machine learning
def: "A computer algorithm that generates empirical models\, such as a model of biological potency as a function of the properties of the molecules\, that is derived\n from the analysis of a training set for which all the necessary data are available."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11471
name: macromolecular crystallographic information file
def: "A file that describes in detail the features of a macromolecular structure and the X‑ray diffraction experiment that was used to derive that structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11472
name: Markush structure
def: "A structure diagram that describes a set of related molecules with the structure of the common core and the structures of the substituents at each position."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11473
name: molecular diversity
def: "A measure of the spread of various properties or chemotypes within a set of compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11474
name: molecular fingerprints
def: "Descriptions of the structure of a molecule calculated from the properties of each of its atoms and bonds that are then usually condensed into a fixed-length string by a hashing algorithm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11475
name: molecular similarity
def: "The degree to which two molecules resemble one another as calculated from their respective 2D or 3D properties\, molecular fingerprints\, fragment keys\, or superimposed 3D structures that usually ranges from 1 \(identical\) to 0 \(dissimilar\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11476
name: multidimensional scaling
def: "A visualization method that converts a dataset of distances between molecules in property space into coordinates in lower dimension space with emphasis on\n preserving the relative distances between dissimilar molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11477
name: multiobjective optimization
def: "The search for those combinations of properties that produces the best compromise between the various objectives of the search\, for example properties of\n compounds that are the best trade-off between potency\, bioavailability\, and selectivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11478
name: orthogonal projections to latent structures
def: "A preprocessing method for partial least squares calculations that filters out variation that is not correlated to the property to be fit and so results in\n improved interpretability of a PLS model while maintaining its predictivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11479
name: overtraining
def: "Training a machine learning model for so long that the original input data is memorized with the result that model predictivity is degraded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11480
name: Pareto front
def: "The various combinations of predictor properties that lead to optimum predicted compromises in multiple target properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11481
name: Pareto optimization
def: "The search for solutions to functions that provide a compromise between several desirable outcomes\, such as potency\, lack of toxicity\, and good absorption\, distribution\, metabolism\, and excretion \(ADME\) properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11482
name: partial atomic charges
def: "Charges assigned to atoms\, arising from the uneven distribution of electrons in bonds\, that are used to calculate intermolecular interaction energy or to\n characterize the charge distribution of a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11483
name: path fingerprints
def: "Vectors of Boolean arrays generated from the properties of the atoms and bonds in linear paths\, typically two to seven bonds long\, hashed into an integer of\n fixed determined length."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11484
name: protein data bank file
def: "A text file that stores the atomic coordinates of a macromolecule\, usually a protein or nucleic acid with associated ligands\, solvent molecules and ions."
synonym: "PDB file" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11485
name: pharmacophore
def: "A proposal for the ensemble of steric and electronic features that define the optimal supermolecular intermolecular interaction of a ligand with a specific\n biological target structure with the result that it triggers or blocks its biological response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11486
name: pipelining programs
def: "Visual programming of the computational execution of sequential operations on a dataset\, with each node specifying an operation with options\, and the nodes processed sequentially."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11487
name: PLS latent variable
def: "One of the vectors of a linear combination of the input descriptors that a partial least squares calculation extracts from a dataset."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11488
name: PLS loading
def: "The contribution of a particular property to a PLS latent variable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11489
name: Poisson–Boltzmann equation
def: "A differential equation that uses a mean-field\, continuum model to describe the electrostatic interactions in ionic solutions where both ions and solvent are\n treated implicitly\, resulting in less computational effort to describe biomolecules in ionic aqueous solutions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11490
name: polar surface area
def: "Surface area over all polar atoms \(usually oxygen and nitrogen\)\, including any attached hydrogen atoms\, of a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11491
name: pose diagram
def: "A 2D diagram that shows the interactions between a ligand and a protein in the 3D structure of the complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11492
name: posterior probability
def: "The conditional probability that is assigned to a quantity after relevant evidence is taken into account. The prior and posterior probabilities are linked by\n the \(normalized\) likelihood function \(Bayes's theorem\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11493
name: prediction set
def: "Molecules that have been set aside from model development to test the reliability of the derived model."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11494
name: principal component loading
def: "The contribution\, scaled between \\\(\\pu\{-1.0\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{1.0\}\\\)\, of an original variable to a particular principal component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11495
name: principal component score
def: "A property of a molecule calculated from a principal components analysis of the original properties and the value of these properties for the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11496
name: prior probability
def: "The conditional probability that is assigned to a quantity before relevant evidence is taken into account\, for example\, the prior probability of an active\n compound would be the fraction of the compounds in the dataset that are active."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11497
name: protein data bank
def: "An archive of freely available macromolecular structural data of proteins\, nucleic acids\, and complex assemblies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11498
name: q2
def: "The square of the leave-one-out cross-validation regression coefficient calculated from the variance of the observed versus predicted values of the target\n property\, usually potency\, of each compound when eliminated during cross-validation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11499
name: r2
def: "The ratio of the sum of squares explained by a regression model \(SSR\) to the 'total' sum of squares around the mean \(SST\)\, or\, because SST equals SSR plus the sum of squares of the error or residuals from the fit \(SSE\)\: \\\[R^\{2\} = \\frac\{\{\\rm\{SSR\}\}\}\{\{\\rm\{SST\}\}\} = 1 - \\frac\{\{\\rm\{SSE\}\}\}\{\{\\rm\{SST\}\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "$R^{2}$" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11500
name: random forest method
def: "A classification method that produces many recursive partitioning models\, each with a random selection of predictor properties\, and then combines the models\n for predictions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11501
name: recall
def: "The fraction of the total number of actives\, in the top-scoring docking hits\, such as the fraction of true actives that are retrieved in the top \\\(\\pu\{1\\%\}\\\) scoring molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11502
name: receiver-operator characteristic curve
def: "A plot that shows the result of a test of the performance of a binary classifier by plotting the fraction of true positives identified versus the fraction of\n false positives as the discrimination threshold is varied."
synonym: "ROC curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11503
name: recursive partitioning
def: "A classification method that uses predictor properties to successively divide the data set into subsets such that each resulting subset is enriched in\n molecules that are in one class."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11504
name: regularization
def: "Introducing additional information in order to solve an ill-posed problem or to prevent overfitting\, usually taking the form of a penalty for complexity of the\n model."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11505
name: response scrambling
def: "Evaluation of the robustness of a QSAR or classification model by repeatedly randomizing the target property of compounds\, developing models based on this\n randomized property\, and comparing the statistics of fit of the scrambled models with those of the true model\, which should be superior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11506
name: R-group decomposition
def: "The process whereby the molecules in a dataset are partitioned into the core \('scaffold'\)\, usually user-specified\, and the specific substituents \('R-groups'\)\n found at each specific position."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11507
name: rule of five
def: "The rule of five states that molecules that violate two or more of the following rules are likely to have permeability problems; \(1\) CLOGP calculated octan-1-ol-water log P greater than 5.0; \(2\) molecular weight greater than 500; \(3\) more than five hydrogen bond donors; and \(4\) the sum of oxygen and nitrogen atoms is greater than 10."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11508
name: scoring
def: "A mathematical formula that estimates the binding affinity of a ligand to a macromolecular target based on the structure of the complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11509
name: structure-data format file
def: "A computer text file that follows a specified format in which each molecule is described by 2D or 3D coordinates\, atom type\, and connectivity to other atoms\n and also specific user-definable fields that contain other information such as name and biological or chemical properties."
synonym: "SDF file" []
synonym: "SDfile" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11510
name: sensitivity analysis
def: "Analysis\, often by finding approximate derivatives of the output with respect to each input\, that determines which of the descriptors in a model has the\n greatest influence on the output."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11511
name: similarity ensemble approach
def: "A method that predicts the probability that a molecule or set of molecules will be active against a particular target by considering its pairwise similarities\n to all known ligands for that target."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11512
name: similarity searching
def: "A virtual screening method that calculates the molecular similarity of an input 2D or 3D structure to each of the molecules in a database to identify a\n requested number of most similar molecules or those above some similarity threshold."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11513
name: SMARTS
def: "An expansion of the SMILES language that describes a particular specific or generalized substructure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11515
name: SMIRKS
def: "A derivative of the SMILES language that describes the transformation in a chemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11516
name: specificity
def: "The ability of a two-class model to detect known inactive or non-toxic molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11517
name: standard error of estimates
def: "The standard error of the errors or residuals\, observed minus calculated\, from a computational model\: \\\[\{\\rm\{SEE\}\} = \\sqrt \\frac\{\\sum \(Y_\{o\} - Y_\{c\}\)^\{2\}\}\{n\}\\\] in which \\\(Y_\{o\}\\\) is the observed value of \\\(Y\\\)\, \\\(Y_\{c\}\\\) is the calculated value of \\\(Y\\\)\, and \\\(n\\\) is the number of observations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11518
name: standard error of prediction
def: "The standard deviation of the errors or residuals\, observed minus calculated\, from a computational model\: \\\[\{\\rm\{SEP\}\} = \\sqrt \\frac\{\\sum \(Y_\{o\} - Y_\{p\}\)^\{2\}\}\{n - k\}\\\] in which \\\(Y_\{o\}\\\) is the observed value of \\\(Y\\\)\, \\\(Y_\{p\}\\\) is the predicted value of \\\(Y\\\)\, \\\(n\\\) is the number of observations\, and \\\(k\\\) is the number of terms in the model."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11519
name: structural keys
def: "A pre-established set of substructures the presence or absence of which are used to describe a molecule and are used during substructure searching as a filter\n to eliminate molecules that cannot match a query\, for clustering or similarity searching\, or for developing classification or regression models."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11520
name: structure diagram
def: "A 2D drawing that shows the atoms of a molecular structure and the bonds that connect them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11521
name: substructure searching
def: "A virtual screening method that uses a graph-based method to discover which molecules in a chemical structure database contain the substructure specified in\n the query."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11522
name: supervised learning
def: "A machine learning method that aims to discover a relationship between the predictor and the response values\, such as exists in a QSAR or recursive\n partitioning model."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11523
name: Tanimoto similarity
def: "A scale that ranges from \\\(\\pu\{0.0\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{1.0\}\\\) when calculated as the ratio of the fingerprint bits or structural keys that both molecules have set divided by the number of bits or keys set in both molecules plus those set either molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11524
name: test set
def: "The set of molecules not used in any way to devise a computational model but instead are used to test its predictivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11525
name: topological index
def: "A numerical value associated with chemical constitution for correlation of chemical structure with various physical properties\, chemical reactivity or biological activity. The numerical basis for topological indices is provided \(depending on how a molecular graph is converted into a numerical value\) by either the adjacency matrix or the topological distance matrix. In the latter the topological distance between two vertices is the number of edges in the shortest path between these."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11526
name: topological polar surface area
def: "An approximation to polar surface area that is calculated from the 2D structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11527
name: true negative
def: "A model prediction of a negative result\, such as no biological activity\, when the true result is also negative."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11528
name: true positive
def: "A model prediction of a positive result\, such as biological activity\, when the true result is also positive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11529
name: Tversky similarity
def: "An asymmetric measure in which a target structure is compared to a reference structure with user-selectable weighting of the relative importance of the fingerprint bits or structural keys that are present only in the reference versus the fingerprint bits or structural keys that are only in the target\, weightings for which the Tanimoto coefficient uses \\\(\\pu\{1.0\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11530
name: underdetermined system
def: "A system in which the number of descriptors is much larger than the number of observations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11531
name: validation set
def: "The molecules not used to devise a computational model but instead are used during model development to test for possible over-fitting as seen when additional\n properties are added to a model but the predicted potencies of the validation set become less accurate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11532
name: virtual reaction
def: "The computer encoding of a possible chemical reaction between two explicit or Markush structures or substructures\, often with a mapping between the atoms in\n the starting material and those in the product."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11533
name: virtual screening
def: "Computational methods that rank the molecules in a database by their forecast continuous or categorical biological or chemical properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11534
name: workflow
def: "The sequence of steps used to perform a certain task."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11535
name: apparent monomer
def: "Monomer which from the name of a polymer appears to have been used in the synthesis of the latter when it has actually been prepared from different precursor\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11536
name: block homopolymer
def: "Homopolymer that is composed of macromolecules comprising blocks that have at least one constitutional or configurational feature which is not present in the adjacent portions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11537
name: graft homopolymer
def: "Homopolymer composed of macromolecules with one or more species of block connected to the main chain as side chains\, these side chains having constitutional or configurational features that differ from those in the main chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11538
name: standardization
def: "Measurement of the concentration of a component of a solution with a reference measurement standard for the preparation of a secondary measurement standard"

[Term]
id: goldbook:11539
name: controlled release
def: "Detection principle for a chemically modified electrode \(CME\) in which an analyte or reagent accumulated at a CME undergoes quantitative release \(egress\, stripping\, expulsion\, or microdosing\) to the test solution\, under electrochemical or chemical control."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11540
name: all-solid-state ion-selective electrode
def: "Ion-selective electrode in which the ion-selective membrane contacts the electronic conductor without internal electrolyte solution."
synonym: "metal contact ion-selective electrode" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11541
name: self-assembled monolayer
def: "Film\, one molecule thick\, in an ordered assembly on a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11542
name: cationized molecule
def: "Ion formed by the association of a cation with a molecule \\\(\\ce\{M\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11543
name: nominal mass
def: "Mass of a molecular ion or molecule calculated using the isotope mass of the most abundant constituent element isotope of each element rounded to the nearest integer value and multiplied by the number of atoms of each element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11544
name: reflectron
def: "Component of a time-of-flight mass spectrometer that uses a static electric field to reverse the direction of travel of the ions and improves mass resolution by assuring that ions of the same m/z but different translational energy arrive at the detector at the same time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11545
name: adhesion
def: "Attachment of interfaces between phases or components that is maintained by intermolecular forces\, chain entanglements\, or both."
synonym: "tack" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11546
name: Michaelis–Menten equation
def: "The relationship between the initial rate \(velocity\) of an enzyme-catalysed reaction \\\(v_\{0\}\\\) and the initial substrate concentration \\\(\[\\ce\{S\}\]_\{0\}\\\) in the form \\\[v_\{0\} = V\[\\ce\{S\}\]_\{0\}/\(K_\{\\rm\{M\}\} + \[\\ce\{S\}\]_\{0\}\)\\\] where \\\(V\\\) \(the limiting rate\) and \\\(K_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\) \(the Michaelis constant\) are independent of the initial substrate concentration and constant at a given temperature and a given enzyme concentration. The reaction is then said to display Michaelis–Menten kinetics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11547
name: protein sequencing
def: "Determination of the primary structure of a protein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11548
name: zone pH
def: "pH of the solute zone in pH-zone-refining counter-current chromatography. \\\[\{\\rm\{pH\}\}_\{\\rm\{zone\}\} = \\pu\{p\}K_\{\\rm\{a\}\} + \\log\[\(K_\{\\rm\{DS\}\}/K_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\) - 1\]\\\]\, where \\\(K_\{\\rm\{DS\}\}\\\) is the partition ratio of solute $\\ce\{S\}$ \(an indicator for solute hydrophobicity\) and \\\(K_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\)\, the dissociation constant of the solute."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11549
name: intraparticle volume of the column
def: "Volume of the mobile phase within the pores of the particles in the stationary phase."
synonym: "intrastitial volume of the column" []
synonym: "stationary mobile-phase volume" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11550
name: distribution constant \(exclusion\)
def: "Fraction of the intraparticle volume \(the volume of the pores\) available to the molecules of a particular sample component for diffusion\: \\\[K_\{0\} = \\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\} - V_0\}\{V_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:11551
name: distribution constant calibration
def: "Calibration for extraction when close to equilibrium conditions\, using a known for the distribution constant for the analyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11552
name: microextraction \(methology\)
def: "Extraction method characterized by a low phase ratio\, so that at equilibrium the fraction extracted is low \(i.e. the extraction is not exhaustive\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11553
name: microextraction \(technique\)
def: "Extraction technique in which the volume \(mass\) of the extraction phase \(liquid or solid\) is substantially smaller than the sample volume \(mass\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11554
name: microextraction \(packed sorbent\)
def: "Solid-phase extraction using a syringe with a short sorbent bed located between the plunger and the needle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11556
name: acclimation
def: "Adjustment\, physiologically and \(or\) behaviorally\, of an organism to its environment or circumstances\, e.g.\, thermoregulation\, psychological adjustment."
synonym: "acclimatization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11557
name: acetylcholine
def: "Substance that functions as a neurotransmitter between nerve cells and between nerves and muscles."
synonym: "2-acetoxy-N,N,N-trimethylethanaminium" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11558
name: acetylcholinesterase
def: "Enzyme that hydrolyzes acetylcholine."
synonym: "acetylcholine hydrolase" []
synonym: "cholinesterase inhibitor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11559
name: acetylcholine receptor
def: "Integral membrane protein that allows the cell to respond to the binding of acetylcholine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11560
name: acoustic neuroma
def: "Non-malignant intracranial tumor of the eighth cranial nerve."
synonym: "acoustic neurinoma" []
synonym: "vestibular Schwannoma" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11561
name: acroparesthesia
def: "Abnormal feeling of numbness\, tingling\, or burning of the skin\, typically in the extremities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11562
name: action potential
def: "Brief\, spike-like depolarization associated with the passage of an impulse without decrement along the membrane of a muscle cell or nerve cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11563
name: active avoidance test
def: "Fear-motivated associative avoidance test based on presentation of an adverse stimulus \(e.g.\, an electric current\) as a source of discomfort\, to create an environment from which the animal or subject would prefer to escape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11564
name: addiction
def: "Compulsive\, uncontrolled reward-seeking behavior\, especially referring to substance use."
synonym: "physical dependence" []
synonym: "psychological dependence" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11565
name: adenoma
def: "Benign tumor arising from epithelial cells in glandular tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11566
name: adenosine receptor
def: "Any of four types of purinergic receptors that respond to the nucleoside\, adenosine\, two of which are involved in release of the neurotransmitters\, dopamine and glutamate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11567
name: adrenalin
def: "Catecholamine hormone secreted by the adrenal glands that increases heart rate\, breathing rate\, blood pressure\, and carbohydrate metabolism."
synonym: "(R)-4-(1-hydroxy-2-(methylamino)ethyl)benzene-1" []
synonym: "2-diol" []
synonym: "adrenaline" []
synonym: "epinephrine" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11568
name: adrenergic
def: "Relating to or denoting secretion of and \(or\) response to adrenalin\, noradrenalin or related substances; in particular referring to sympathetic nerve fibers.\nRelating to or denoting an agent that mimics the activity of adrenalin\, noradrenalin or related substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11569
name: α-adrenergic receptor
def: "Class of adrenergicreceptor mainly functioning in vasoconstriction and modulation of glandular secretions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11570
name: β-adrenergic receptor
def: "Member of a class of G protein-coupled receptors that are targets of the catecholamines\, especially adrenalin\, noradrenalin\, and related substances."
synonym: "adrenergic receptor" []
synonym: "adrenoceptor" []
synonym: "β-receptor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11571
name: afferent
def: "Inflowing. Of nerves\, those that conduct impulses toward the central nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11572
name: agnosia
def: "Impaired ability to recognize or comprehend the meaning of various sensations\, not attributable to faulty sensory input or general intellect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11573
name: alpha-synuclein
def: "Protein abundant in the human brain\, thought to regulate the release of dopamine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11574
name: Alzheimer disease
def: "Common and progressive neurodegenerative disease leading to loss of memory\, orientation\, and judgment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11575
name: γ-aminobutyric acid
def: "Major neurotransmitter at inhibitory synapses in the central nervous system of mammals."
synonym: "4-aminobutanoic acid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11576
name: γ-aminobutyric acid receptor
def: "Transmembrane receptor protein that binds and responds to the inhibitory neurotransmitter $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}aminobutyric acid\}$."
synonym: "GABA Receptor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11577
name: 2-amino-3-\(3-hydroxy-5-methyl-1\,2-oxazol-4-yl\)propanoic acid receptor
def: "Glutamate receptor in the central nervous system activated by the synthetic glutamate analog\, AMPA."
synonym: "AMPA Receptor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11578
name: amnesia
def: "Partial or total\, transient or permanent loss of memory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11579
name: amnesic shellfish poisoning
def: "Human illness caused by consumption of the marine toxin domoic acid that may lead to dizziness\, confusion\, motor weakness and seizures\, with permanent cognitive impairment and short term memory loss."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11580
name: amygdala
def: "Brain nucleus located in the median temporal lobe that is part of the limbic system and plays an important role in emotional responses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11581
name: β-amyloid
def: "Extracellular aggregate of misfolded fibrous proteins\, typically with cross-linked $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}sheet\}$ structure."
synonym: "amyloid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11582
name: amyloid plaque
def: "Structure representing extracellular deposition of $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}amyloid protein\}$ in the gray matter of the brain that occurs with aging and in increased amount in Alzheimer disease."
synonym: "neuritic plaque" []
synonym: "senile plaque" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11583
name: amyotrophic lateral sclerosis
def: "Debilitating degenerative disease of the motor neurons characterized by progressive muscle weakness\, muscle atrophy and fasciculations\, muscle spasticity\, difficulty speaking \(dysarthria\)\, difficulty swallowing \(dysphagia\)\, and difficulty breathing \(dyspnea\)."
synonym: "Lou Gehrig’s disease" []
synonym: "motor neuron disease" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11584
name: anaphrodisiac
def: "Anything tending to reduce libido."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11585
name: anesthetic
def: "Substance that reduces the experience of pain with or without loss of consciousness."
synonym: "anaesthetic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11586
name: angiography
def: "Radiography of blood or lymph vessels\, following the introduction of a radio-opaque substance."
synonym: "Arteriography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11587
name: annulus
def: "Tough outer ring of an intervertebral disc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11588
name: anorexia
def: "Eating disorder characterized by self-imposed food restriction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11589
name: anorexia nervosa
def: "Eating disorder characterized by obsessive desire to lose weight by reducing food intake or by repeated vomiting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11590
name: anosmia
def: "Loss of the sense of smell\, either total or selective."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11591
name: anticonvulsant
def: "Substance used to prevent or reduce the severity of convulsions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11592
name: antidepressant
def: "Substance used to alleviate depression."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11593
name: antiemetic
def: "Substance used to prevent nausea and vomiting \(emesis\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11594
name: antineuralgic
def: "Substance used to alleviate the pain of neuralgia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11595
name: antinociception
def: "Blockage of the perception of pain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11596
name: antipsychotic
def: "Psychoactive substance used to manage psychotic behavior or psychotic ideation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11597
name: antispasmodic
def: "Substance used to prevent muscle spasms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11598
name: anxiolytic
def: "Agent that reduces anxiety without causing excessive sedation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11599
name: aphasia
def: "Difficulty with\, or loss of the use of language in reading\, writing or speaking owing to specific lesions in the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11600
name: aphonia
def: "Loss of the voice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11601
name: aphrodisiac
def: "Anything that stimulates libido."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11602
name: apneusis
def: "Abnormal breathing pattern consisting of a pause at full inspiration."
synonym: "apneustic respiration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11603
name: apraxia
def: "Neurological condition characterized by loss of the ability to perform skilled or purposeful movement that a person is nevertheless physically able and willing to do."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11604
name: arachnoiditis
def: "Inflammation of the arachnoid membrane\, often characterized by perception of burning pain in the legs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11605
name: arborization
def: "Terminal branching of a neuron into a treelike structure of the dendrites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11606
name: arrhythmia
def: "Condition in which there is an irregular or abnormal heart beat."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11607
name: arteriolovenular anastomosis
def: "Communication in which blood is shunted from arterioles to venules without passing through capillaries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11608
name: arteriovenous
def: "Relating to both arteries and veins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11609
name: arteriovenous anastomosis
def: "Communication between an artery and a vein by collateral channels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11610
name: arteriovenous fistula
def: "Abnormal communication between an artery and a vein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11611
name: arteriovenous malformation
def: "Web of blood vessels with one or more abnormal communications between arteries and veins that bypass the capillary system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11612
name: associative avoidance test
def: "Avoidance test in which the test animal has learned to develop an association between the stimulus and the delivery of an aversive event."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11613
name: associative learning
def: "Process in which a new behavior arises as a consequence of association with a particular stimulus\, sometimes restricted to learning through classical conditioning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11614
name: astrocyte
def: "Glial cell of ectodermal origin in the central nervous system\, characterized by filamentous protoplasmic extensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11615
name: reactive astrocyte
def: "Astrocyte that\, after injury or excessive neural activity\, undergoes morphological \(including extension of pseudopodia\) and biochemical \(notably increased synthesis of glial fibrillary acidic protein\) changes and participates in astrocytic scar formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11616
name: astrocytic scar
def: "Clustering of reactive astrocytes separating a localized brain injury from surrounding uninjured tissue."
synonym: "glial scar" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11617
name: astrocytoma
def: "Tumor within the substance of the brain or spinal cord made up of astrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11618
name: astrogliosis
def: "Localized site of reactive astrocytes\, distinct from an astrocytic scar."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11619
name: ataxia
def: "Loss of muscular coordination \(see motor coordination\) resulting in abnormal clumsiness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11620
name: athetosis
def: "Condition in which inappropriate muscle contraction causes involuntary writhing movements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11621
name: atony
def: "Lacking muscular tone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11622
name: attention deficit hyperactivity disorder
def: "Neuropsychiatric disorder of childhood characterized by decreased attention span\, impulsiveness and hyperactivity."
synonym: "hyperkinetic disorder" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11623
name: aura
def: "Constellation of symptoms experienced before a migraine headache or seizure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11624
name: autism
def: "Neurodevelopmental disorder\, present from early childhood\, characterized by great difficulty in communicating and forming relationships with other people and in using abstract concepts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11625
name: autonomic nervous system
def: "Part of the peripheral nervous system controlling the bodily functions that are not consciously directed\, e.g.\, breathing\, heart rate\, and digestive processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11626
name: avoidance test
def: "Associative learning pairing an adverse event with a non-specific stimulus such that the animal learns to respond in a manner to avoid the adverse event."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11627
name: axon
def: "Long thread-like process of a neuron along which impulses are conducted from the cell body to the nerve ending."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11628
name: axonal degeneration
def: "Progressive loss of structure and function of an axon\, typically in a peripheral-to-central direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11629
name: axonal transport
def: "Movement of organelles and biomolecules to \(retrograde\) and from \(anterograde\) a neuron’s cell body\, within the cytoplasm of its axon \(the axoplasm\)."
synonym: "axoplasmic transport" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11630
name: axonopathy
def: "Damage \(peripheral neuropathy\) to multiple nerves in roughly the same areas on both sides of the body\, causing weakness\, numbness\, and paresthesia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11631
name: bar test
def: "Test for catalepsy in which a mouse is placed on a bar oriented parallel to and approximately \\\(\\pu\{2.5 cm\}\\\) off the ground. Typically\, if the mouse remains immobile on the bar for more than 20 seconds\, it is considered to be cataleptic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11632
name: Barnes maze
def: "Circular platform equipped with some 20 equidistant holes near the outer rim\, one of which is marked by visual cues and is an escape hole for a rodent that is placed near the middle of the platform and subjected to an adverse stimulus\, e.g.\, bright light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11633
name: Bayley scale
def: "Range of scores on play tasks performed by infants age 0–3 years\, intended to assess development of motor\, cognitive\, and language skills against norms for age-matched normal child development."
synonym: "Bayley scales of infant development (BSID)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11634
name: beading
def: "Focal bead-like swellings in dendrites and axons."
synonym: "neuritic beading" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11635
name: beam walking test
def: "Assessment of the ability of an animal to remain upright and to walk on an elevated and relatively narrow beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11636
name: Benton test
def: "Procedure in which patterns on cards are memorized by a test subject who then attempts to recognize them on other cards presented subsequently."
synonym: "Benton visual memory test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11637
name: Biel water maze
def: "A water labyrinth\, described by Biel \(1940\)\, where rodents are forced to swim until they find a small underwater platform that allows them to escape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11638
name: bipolar disorder
def: "Psychiatric illness characterized by mood shifts with periods of severe depressive illness and one or more episodes of exaggerated activity levels \(manic behavior\)."
synonym: "manic-depressive disorder" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11639
name: β-blocker
def: "Any of a class of drugs that targets the $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}adrenergic receptor\}$\, interferes with binding of adrenalin and other related substances to the receptor\, and weakens their effects."
synonym: "β-adrenergic antagonist" []
synonym: "β-adrenergic blocking agent" []
synonym: "β-adrenergic receptor antagonist" []
synonym: "β-adrenoreceptor antagonist" []
synonym: "β-antagonist" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11640
name: blood–brain barrier
def: "Selectively permeable structure that restricts the exit of blood cells and many substances from the brain capillaries into the extracellular space of the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11641
name: blood–cerebrospinal fluid barrier
def: "Functional restriction by cells of the choroid plexus of transit of substances from the cerebrospinal fluid into the extracellular space of the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11642
name: blood–retinal barrier
def: "Functional restriction of transit of substances from the blood to the retina by non-fenestrated endothelial cells with tight junctions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11643
name: Boston naming test
def: "Procedure in which 60 pictures are shown to a subject who has to name them quickly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11644
name: brachial plexus
def: "Bundle of nerve fibers that begins in the spine at the base of the neck\, travels through the axilla \(armpit region\)\, and into the arm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11645
name: bradycardia
def: "Abnormally slow heart rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11646
name: bradykinesia
def: "Abnormally slow body movement."
synonym: "hypokinesia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11647
name: brain
def: "That part of the central nervous system contained within the cranium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11648
name: brain-derived neurotrophic factor
def: "Protein secreted by the brain that acts on neurons in the central and peripheral nervous systems; a member of the neurotrophin family of growth factors\, related to the nerve growth factor \(NGF\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11649
name: brain herniation
def: "Movement of part of the brain across structures within the skull such as a shift across such structures as the falx cerebri\, the tentorium cerebelli\, and even sometimes through the foramen magnum in the base of the skull \(through which the spinal cord connects with the brain\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11650
name: brain slice
def: "Slice of brain tissue immersed in artificial cerebrospinal fluid\, used in electrophysiology experiments to allow study of a synapse or neural circuit in isolation from the rest of the brain\, and in metabolic studies\, under controlled physiological conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11651
name: brainstem
def: "Unpaired subdivision of the brain\, continuous with the spinal cord\, that includes the thalamus\, hypothalamus\, pons\, medulla oblongata\, and the mesencephalon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11652
name: Broca area
def: "Region in the frontal lobe of one of the hemispheres \(the speech-dominant hemisphere\, usually the left\) of the hominid brain that is involved in motor functions of speech production."
synonym: "Broca speech area" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11653
name: bruxism
def: "Involuntary habitual grinding of the teeth\, frequently during sleep."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11654
name: burr hole surgery
def: "Medical intervention in which a hole is drilled or scraped into the human skull\, exposing the dura mater\, to gain access during surgery\, to relieve intracranial pressure\, to evacuate a subdural hematoma\, etc."
synonym: "trepanning" []
synonym: "trephination" []
synonym: "trephining" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11655
name: butyrylcholinesterase
def: "Enzyme\, found mainly in the liver\, that hydrolyzes choline esters; it is non-specific and distinct from acetylcholinesterase."
synonym: "plasma cholinesterase" []
synonym: "pseudocholinesterase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11656
name: CA1-pyramidal cell
def: "Pyramidal neuron located in the cornu ammonis area 1\, in the hippocampus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11657
name: CA3-pyramidal cell
def: "Pyramidal neuron located in the cornu ammonis area 3\, in the hippocampus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11658
name: <i>Caenorhabditis elegans</i>
def: "Nematode with a well defined small neural network and homologues of human neurotransmitters\, used as a model organism for studying normal development as well as neurotoxicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11659
name: california verbal learning test
def: "Procedure in which some common words are read aloud and the test subject is asked to recall as many of these as possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11660
name: carcinoma
def: "Malignant tumor of an epithelial cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11661
name: carotid artery
def: "One of two paired large arteries on either side of the neck that supplies most of the cerebralhemisphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11662
name: carpel tunnel syndrome
def: "Median nerve entrapment neuropathy that causes paresthesia\, pain\, and numbness due to compression of the nerve at the wrist in the carpal tunnel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11663
name: cassavism
def: "Epidemic paralytic disease\, mainly affecting women and children\, occurring in remote rural areas of African and Latin American countries\, where cassava root is an important food."
synonym: "konzo" []
synonym: "mantakassa" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11664
name: cauda equina
def: "Bundle of long spinal nerve roots arising from the end of the spinal cord and filling the lower part of the spinal canal \(from approximately the thoraco-lumbar junction downwards\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11665
name: caudate nucleus
def: "Crescent-shaped mass of gray matter forming part of the corpus striatum and part of the basal ganglia."
synonym: "caudatum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11666
name: cephalic
def: "In or relating to the head."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11667
name: ceramide
def: "Family of lipid molecules\, each one composed of sphingosine and a fatty acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11668
name: cerebellar tonsillar herniation
def: "Abnormal protrusion of a portion of the cerebellum through the foramen magnum of the skull."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11669
name: cerebellum
def: "Lower part of the brain that is beneath the posterior portion of the cerebrum\, regulating the unconscious coordination of movement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11670
name: cerebroside
def: "Member of a group of glycosphingolipids called monoglycosylceramides which are important components in animal nerve and muscle cell membranes and the myelin sheath."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11671
name: cerebrum
def: "Principal part of the brain in vertebrates\, located frontwards in the skull\, consisting of the two hemispheres of the cerebral cortex separated by a fissure\, and certain subcortical structures such as the hippocampus\, basal ganglia\, and the olfactory bulb."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11672
name: cervical spine
def: "Part of the spine immediately inferior to the skull and connecting to the thoracic spine\, made up of seven vertebrae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11673
name: cervical vertebra
def: "Any of seven vertebrae that provide strength and structure to the cervical spine and support the head\, allowing for its rotation and flexion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11674
name: cholinergic
def: "Relating to or denoting a nerve that liberates acetylcholine at a synapse upon receiving an appropriate nerve impulse; in particular referring to parasympathetic nerve fibers.\nRelating to or denoting an agent that mimics or enhances the activity of acetylcholine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11675
name: cholinomimetic
def: "Having an action similar to acetylcholine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11676
name: chorea
def: "Neurological disorder characterized by rapid\, jerky involuntary movements affecting especially the limbs or facial muscles."
synonym: "choreia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11677
name: choreoathetosis
def: "Occurrence of involuntary movements in a combination of chorea and athetosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11678
name: chromatolysis
def: "Disintegration of nuclear chromatin.\nDisintegration of Nissl bodies following neuronal injury."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11679
name: chronic solvent-induced encephalopathy
def: "Constellation of neurological symptoms that may include polyneuropathies\, encephalopathy\, memory loss\, attention deficit and dementia following long-term exposure\, often occupational\, to organic solvents found in paints\, glues\, and other industrial products."
synonym: "organic solvent syndrome" []
synonym: "toxic solvent syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11680
name: ciguatera poisoning
def: "Food-borne illness caused by ingesting fish containing ciguatera toxins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11681
name: ciliary muscle
def: "Intrinsic smooth muscle of the eye that contracts to cause the lens to thicken\, allowing accommodation to near vision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11682
name: ciliary neurotrophic factor
def: "Polypeptide hormone and nerve growth factor that promotes neurotransmitter synthesis and neurite outgrowth in certain neural populations including astrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11683
name: clonus
def: "Alternate involuntary muscular contraction and relaxation in rapid succession.\nContinuous rhythmic reflex tremor initiated by the spinal cord below an area of spinal cord injury\, set in\nmotion by reflex testing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11684
name: cognition
def: "Sum of the mental processes by which sensory input is stored\, recovered\, and used by the consciousbrain\, including attention\, memory\, language usage\, learning\, reasoning\, problem solving\, and decision making."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11685
name: coma
def: "State of profound unconsciousness lasting more than several hours in which one cannot respond to stimuli and from which one cannot be roused."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11686
name: conditioned avoidance response test
def: "Experimental procedure in which an adverse effect \(e.g.\, foot shock\, drug withdrawal\) is delivered to rats or mice after presentation of a cue \(e.g.\, light or sound\); the rodents are trained to avoid rather than escape the shock after the start of its delivery."
synonym: "CAR Test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11687
name: classical conditioning
def: "Learning in which a behavior comes to be elicited as a reflex response by association with a neutral antecedent stimulus through repetition of the stimulus-response pairing."
synonym: "Pavlovian conditioning" []
synonym: "respondent conditioning" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11688
name: operant conditioning
def: "Learning in which appropriate behavior is rewarded\, or inappropriate behavior is punished; behavior is modified by learned association with antecedents and consequences."
synonym: "instrumental conditioning" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11689
name: confusion
def: "Mental state of bewilderment or disorientation in which reactions to environmental stimuli may be inappropriate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11690
name: conscious
def: "Aware of and responding to one’s surroundings."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11691
name: continuous performance test
def: "Any of several available neuropsychological procedures that measure a person’s sustained attention."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11692
name: contracture
def: "Persistent shortening and tightening of muscle\, tendon\, or other tissue."
synonym: "contraction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11693
name: contrast medium
def: "Any material \(usually opaque to X-rays\) employed to delineate or define a structure during a radiologic procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11694
name: cornu ammonis
def: "Part of the hippocampus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11695
name: coronal
def: "Vertical \(longitudinal\) plane dividing the body into ventral and dorsal \(front and back\) sections."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11696
name: coronal suture
def: "Transverse junction in the skull separating the frontal bone from the parietal bones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11697
name: corpus callosum
def: "Bundle of myelin-enriched neural fibers beneath the cortex connecting the two hemispheres of the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11698
name: corpus striatum
def: "Striatum together with the globus pallidus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11699
name: cramp
def: "Painful muscle spasm caused by prolonged tetanic contraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11700
name: cranial nerve
def: "Each of 12 pairs of nerves that arise directly from the brain\, not from the spinal cord\, and pass through separate apertures in the skull."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11701
name: craniopharyngioma
def: "Congenital tumor arising from the pituitary gland in the embryonic tissue between the brain and pharynx."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11702
name: cue
def: "Stimulus or associated feature of a stimulus that evokes a response or alerts the subject to a particular behavior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11703
name: delirium
def: "State of severe confusion\, characterized by restlessness\, hallucinations and hyperactivity\, during which the person affected may be isolated from normal communication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11704
name: dementia
def: "Serious loss of brain function in a previously unimpaired person\, affecting memory\, thinking\, and behavior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11705
name: presenile dementia
def: "Dementia\, often of the Alzheimer type \(see Alzheimer disease\) developing at or before age 65."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11706
name: senile dementia
def: "Dementia\, often of the Alzheimer type \(see Alzheimer disease\) developing after the age of 65."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11707
name: demyelination
def: "Destruction of the protective myelin sheath that surrounds nerve fibers\, resulting in diminished function and decreased action potential in those nerves."
synonym: "demyelinization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11708
name: dentate gyrus
def: "Narrow strip of cerebral cortex associated with the hippocampus that continues forward to the uncus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11709
name: chemical dependency
def: "Craving for or addiction to a substance."
synonym: "drug addiction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11710
name: depolarization
def: "Relative reduction in the resting membrane potential of an excitable cell\, generally making the inside less negative\, or even positive with respect to the outside."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11711
name: depression
def: "Reduction in the activity of the central nervous system.\nMental state or disorder characterized by feelings of sadness and despair."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11712
name: developmental neurotoxicity
def: "Adverse effects of toxic substances on the development of the nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11713
name: developmental neurotoxicity testing
def: "Any examination that allows detection of substance-induced changes in the structural or functional integrity of the nervous system of a developing organism\, fetus or child."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11714
name: diaphoretic
def: "n. Substance inducing perspiration.\nadj. Of a human\, sweating heavily."
synonym: "sudorific" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11715
name: diarrheal shellfish poisoning
def: "Serious illness characterized primarily by diarrhea with rapid onset and resolution after \\\(\\pu\{24 h\}\\\) that is a consequence of consumption of bivalve shellfish \(mollusks\) such as mussels\, oysters\, and clams that have ingested\, by filter feeding\, large quantities of dinoflagellates containing a group of high-molecular-weight structures with multiple ether linkages\, such as okadaic acid\, dinophysis toxins\, pectenotoxins\, and yessotoxin."
synonym: "diarrhetic shellfish poisoning (DSP)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11716
name: differentiation
def: "Process by which a cell is committed to a lineage and becomes more specialized in structure and function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11717
name: digit span test
def: "Test of memory in which numbers \(series of digits\) are presented orally or on a computer screen to a test subject who is asked to repeat each number from memory; the numbers increase in length until mistakes are made."
synonym: "digit span task" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11718
name: digit symbol substitution test
def: "Neuropsychological assessment in which the top two rows of a test sheet function as a key\, displaying numbers 1–9 \(upper row\) matched to 'non-sense' symbols \(lower row\). There follow pairs of rows where the numbers 1–9 appear in irregular sequence and the test subject must match the correct symbol from the key to each number\, as quickly as possible."
synonym: "digit symbol test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11719
name: diplopia
def: "Simultaneous perception of two images of a single object."
synonym: "double vision" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11720
name: intervertebral disc
def: "Flat circular structure\, consisting of a tough\, fibrous exterior and a gelatinous core\, interposed between adjacent vertebrae."
synonym: "spinal disc" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11721
name: disorientation
def: "Loss of sense of direction.\nIn clinical assessment\, lack of awareness of one’s identity\, present location\, or the current day and time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11722
name: domoic acid
def: "Neurotoxickainic acid analog produced by some algal blooms that causes amnesic shellfish poisoning."
synonym: "(2S,3S,4S)-4-[(2Z,4E,6R)-6-carboxy-6-methylhexa-2,4-dien-2-yl]-3-(carboxymethyl)pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11723
name: dopamine
def: "Naturally occurring substance functioning as a neurotransmitter in the sympathetic nervous system\, and a metabolic precursor of other substances including adrenalin."
synonym: "4-(2-aminoethyl)benzene-1,2-diol" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11724
name: dopaminergic
def: "Relating to a nerve that releases or responds to dopamine as a neurotransmitter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11725
name: dorsal root
def: "Outgrowth of a spinal nerve that carries sensory signals to an appropriate integration center in the brain."
synonym: "posterior root of spinal nerve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11726
name: dorsal root ganglion
def: "Nodule on a dorsal root of the spine that consists of afferent sensory neuronal cell bodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11727
name: dura mater
def: "Outermost and most fibrous of the three membranes of the meninges \(see meninx\) covering the brain and the spinal cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11728
name: dynein
def: "Protein associated with motile structures that exhibits adenosine triphosphatase activity; it forms 'arms' on the outer microtubules of cilia and flagella and functions as a molecular motor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11729
name: dynorphin
def: "Member of a class of opioid peptides that arise from the precursor protein prodynorphin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11730
name: dysaphia
def: "Impairment of the sense of touch."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11731
name: dysarthria
def: "Difficulty in speech owing to disturbances of muscular control resulting from central or peripheral nervous system damage or emotional stress."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11732
name: dysesthesia
def: "Condition in which a disagreeable sensation\, especially of touch\, is produced following damage to peripheral or central sensory pathways.\nAbnormal sensation experienced in the absence of stimulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11733
name: dysgraphia
def: "Difficulty in writing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11734
name: dyskinesia
def: "Difficulty or abnormality in voluntary movement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11735
name: tardive dyskinesia
def: "Involuntary\, repetitive body movement of slow onset."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11736
name: dyslexia
def: "Impairment in appreciation of spatial relations\, often leading to difficulty in reading."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11737
name: dysphagia
def: "Difficulty in swallowing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11738
name: dysphasia
def: "Difficulty in the use of language owing to a brain lesion\, without mental impairment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11739
name: autonomic dysreflexia
def: "Over-activity of the autonomic nervous system causing an abrupt onset of excessively high blood pressure\, tachycardia\, and constriction of peripheral blood vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11740
name: dystonia
def: "State of abnormal \(either hypo- or hypertonic\) muscle tone resulting in impairment of voluntary movement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11741
name: efferent
def: "Carrying outward; of nerves\, those that conduct impulses outward from the central nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11742
name: electroconvulsive therapy
def: "Therapeutic intervention in which seizures are electrically induced in patients with the aim of providing relief from a specific mental illness."
synonym: "electroshock therapy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11743
name: electroencephalogram
def: "Test or record of brain electrical activity detected by electroencephalography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11744
name: electroencephalography
def: "Recording of electrical activity from different parts of the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11745
name: electromyography
def: "Recording of the electrical activity of muscle\, or its representation as a visual display or audible signal\, using electrodes attached to the skin or inserted into a muscle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11746
name: electroneurography
def: "Non-invasive method used to examine the integrity and conductivity of a peripheral nerve\, in which a brief electrical stimulation is given to the nerve at one point\, while at the same time the induced electrical activity \(compound action potentials\) is recorded at another point of the nerve’s distribution and the interval is displayed on a video monitor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11747
name: electrophysiology
def: "Study of the electrical properties of cells and tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11748
name: electroretinogram
def: "Recording\, with electrodes placed on the cornea or nearby skin\, of the electrical activity of photoreceptors and other retinal cells in response to standardized\, patterned stimuli with light and colour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11749
name: elevated plus maze
def: "Stage with four orthogonal side arms elevated above the floor\, where two opposite arms have sidewalls and the other two arms have no walls; used as research tool for anxiety."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11750
name: emesis
def: "Vomiting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11751
name: emetic
def: "n. Substance that produces vomiting.\nadj. Producing vomiting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11752
name: encephalopathy
def: "Any disorder of the brain that affects its functioning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11753
name: endocrine disruptor
def: "Exogenous chemical that alters function\(s\) of the endocrine system and consequently causes adverse health effects in an intact organism\, its progeny\, or \(sub\)populations."
synonym: "endocrine modifier" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11754
name: endorphin
def: "One of several peptides \(including the enkephalins\) that activates the body’s opiate receptors\, causing an analgesic effect; literally\, endogenous morphine-like substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11755
name: enkephalin
def: "Either of two endogenous pentapeptides with opiate-like effects that are especially involved in modulating pain responses in the central nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11756
name: ependymal tissue
def: "Glial cells that line the cavities within the brain’sventricles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11757
name: ependymoma
def: "Growth in the brain or spinal cord arising from ependymal tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11758
name: epidural
def: "Immediately outside the dura mater."
synonym: "extradural" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11759
name: epithalamus
def: "Dorsal part of the diencephalon\, including the pineal gland and adjacent structures in the roof of the third ventricle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11760
name: ergotism
def: "Poisoning produced by eating food affected by ergot \(ergotamine alkaloids\)\, substances produced by the fungus Claviceps purpurea that contaminate rye and wheat."
synonym: "St Anthony’s fire" []
synonym: "ignis infernalis" []
synonym: "ignis sacer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11761
name: euphoria
def: "Characterized by or feeling intense excitement and happiness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11762
name: evoked potential
def: "Electrical potential that is triggered by presentation of a stimulus \(somatosensory\, auditory\, visual\) and measured with electrodes near the nerve or near the brain region in which the signal is processed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11763
name: evoked response
def: "Electrical potential recorded from the nervous system following exposure to a stimulus\, as distinct from spontaneous potentials detected by electroencephalography\, electromyography\, or other electrophysiological recording methods."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11764
name: excitable cell
def: "Cell that can be stimulated to create an electric current; muscle fibers and nerve cells are excitable \(see excitation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11765
name: excitatory post-synaptic potential
def: "Referring to a more positive membrane potential at the postsynaptic side of the synaptic cleft that renders the postsynaptic neuron more likely to generate an action potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11766
name: excitotoxicity
def: "Pathological process by which neurons are damaged and killed by the overactivation of receptors for the excitatory neurotransmitter glutamate\, such as the N-methyl-D-aspartic acid \(NMDA\) receptor and the 2-amino-3-\(3-hydroxy-5-methyl-1\,2-oxazol-4-yl\)propanoic acid \(AMPA\) receptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11767
name: facilitation
def: "Neural facilitation\: Increase in postsynaptic excitation as a result of very rapid firing of action potentials by a presynaptic neuron.\nPresynaptic facilitation\: Increase of presynaptic excitation when an excited neuron receives additional excitatory impulses from other neurons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11768
name: falx
def: "Continuation of dura mater between the right and left hemispheres of the brain."
synonym: "falx cerebri" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11769
name: fasciculation
def: "Involuntary contractions\, or twitchings\, of groups \(fasciculi\) of muscle fibers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11770
name: fibrillation
def: "Rapid contractions or twitching of muscle fibrils\, but not of the muscle as a whole."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11771
name: finger tapping test
def: "Procedure in which a test subject is asked to tap a finger at maximal rate on a surface\, a higher rate of tapping indicating better neuromuscular function."
synonym: "tapping test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11772
name: flaccid
def: "Relaxed\, flabby\, or without tone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11773
name: frontal lobe
def: "Most anterior of the four major lobes of each cerebral hemisphere\, containing the primary motor cortex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11774
name: frontotemporal dementia
def: "Neurodegenerative disease characterized by progressive neuronal defects in the frontal and temporal lobes of the brain\, leading to dementia."
synonym: "frontotemporal lobular degeneration (FTLD)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11775
name: functional domain
def: "In the brain\, referring to the concept that discrete regions are involved in specific aspects of cognitive function\, thus giving rise to modularity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11776
name: functional observational battery
def: "Structured set of tests to examine changes in neuromuscular\, arousal\, autonomic and sensory functions that assesses the presence\, absence or severity of specific neurological signs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11777
name: fused in sarcoma transcription factor
def: "Nuclear DNA- and RNA-binding protein involved in transcription\, splicing\, and RNA-transport."
synonym: "FUS transcription factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11778
name: GABAergic
def: "Pertaining to the action of $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}aminobutyric acid\}$ or to neural or metabolic pathways in which it functions as a neurotransmitter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11779
name: ganglion
def: "Cluster of nerve cell bodies located outside the brain and spinal cord\, i.e.\, in the peripheral nervous system.Note\: Ganglia interconnect to form a complex network known as a plexus and serve as relay stations where different neurons can modulate the signals of each other. Compare\: basal ganglion.\nFibrous cyst usually attached to a tendon sheath."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11780
name: ganglion cell
def: "Neuron located in a ganglion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11781
name: ganglioside
def: "Glycosphingolipid chemically similar to cerebrosides but containing one or more sialic \(N-acetylneuraminic or N-glycolylneuraminic\) acid residues\, found principally in nerve tissue\, spleen\, and thymus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11782
name: giant axon
def: "Very large axon \(typically \\\(\\pu\{0.5 mm\}\\\) in diameter\) that functions in the propulsion system of the squid."
synonym: "squid giant axon" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11783
name: giant axonal neuropathy
def: "Rare human genetic neurological disorder in which the neurofilaments become disordered\, resulting in abnormal size and shape of neurons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11784
name: glia
def: "Non-neuronal cellular elements of the central and peripheral nervous system having metabolic and support functions."
synonym: "neuroglia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11785
name: glial cell
def: "Cellular component of the glia\, including oligodendroglial cells\, astrocytes\, ependymal cells and microglial cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11786
name: glial-derived neurotrophic factor
def: "Protein that promotes and guides the growth of neurons and facilitates the survival of mature neurons by suppressing cell death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11787
name: glial fibrillary acidic protein
def: "Cytoskeletal intermediate filament protein found in mature fibrillary astrocytes and in neural progenitor cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11788
name: glioblastoma
def: "Glioma consisting chiefly of undifferentiated anaplastic cells of astrocytic origin."
synonym: "glioblastoma multiforme" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11789
name: glioma
def: "Neoplasm originating from any of the glia cells in the brain and spinal cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11790
name: reactive gliosis
def: "Damage-induced proliferation\, enlargement and overgrowth of glial cells\, often astrocytes in the central nervous system\, resulting in an astrocytic scar."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11791
name: globoside
def: "Glycosphingolipid\, specifically a ceramide tetrasaccharide \(tetraglycosylceramide\)\, isolated from kidney and erythrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11792
name: glutamate
def: "Salt of glutamic acid \(2-aminopentanedioic acid\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11793
name: glutamate-induced excitotoxicity
def: "Form of excitotoxicity where excess glutamate release in synapses \(glutamatergic storm\) leads to overstimulation\, followed by excess cellular calcium uptake\, cytotoxicity and apoptosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11794
name: Golgi staining
def: "Method of silver staining of histological sections of tissue from the nervous system\, in which a limited number of neurons stain in their entirety upon formation of microcrystals of silver chromate\, thus allowing visualization in the light microscope of some individual neurons in densely packed tissue."
synonym: "Golgi’s method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11795
name: G protein-coupled receptor
def: "Seven-transmembrane-domain receptor that interacts with guanosine triphosphate/diphosphate \(GTP/GDP\) binding proteins to initiate signal transduction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11796
name: granule cell
def: "One of several types of neurons with small cell bodies\, involved in neural networks in several regions of the brain\, including the cerebellum\, hippocampus\, and cerebral cortex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11797
name: Guillain–Barré syndrome
def: "Type of idiopathic polyneuritis in which autoimmunity to peripheral nerve\, myelin leads to a condition characterized by chronic demyelination of the spinal roots \(see spinal cord\)\, peripheral nerves\, and cranial nerves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11798
name: habituation
def: "Decrease or cessation of a response to a stimulus after its repeated presentation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11799
name: hallucination
def: "The apparent\, often strong\, subjective perception of an external object or event when no such stimulus or situation is present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11800
name: headache
def: "Pain in one or more parts of the cranium\, not confined to the area of distribution of any nerve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11801
name: rebound headache
def: "Headache caused by stopping analgesics following their overuse to relieve headaches."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11802
name: tension headache
def: "Headache associated with anxiety or other states of distress\, associated with chronic contraction of the scalp muscles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11803
name: hematoma
def: "Region of bleeding into a tissue or organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11804
name: hemiplegia
def: "Paralysis of one side of the body\, including the arm\, trunk and leg."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11805
name: dominant hemisphere
def: "Cerebralhemisphere containing the representation of speech and controlling arm and leg preference in skilled movements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11806
name: hemorrhage
def: "Copious escape of blood from a disrupted blood vessel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11807
name: hen test
def: "Procedure using birds to study acute and delayed neurotoxicity\, notably of organophosphates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11808
name: hippocampal slice culture
def: "Maintenance in vitro of fresh prepared slices of the hippocampus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11809
name: histamine
def: "Bioactive metabolite produced in living organisms by deamination of the amino acid histidine."
synonym: "2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanamine" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11810
name: histaminergic
def: "Pertaining to the action of histamine or to neural pathways in which it functions as a neurotransmitter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11811
name: Huntington disease
def: "Genetic autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disease resulting from a mutation in the Huntingtin gene and resulting notably in decline of cognitive function and a characteristic chorea."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11812
name: communicating hydrocephalus
def: "Hydrocephalus where flow of cerebrospinal fluid is not blocked but there is failure of its reabsorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11813
name: obstructive hydrocephalus
def: "Hydrocephalus where flow of the cerebrospinal fluid is blocked."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11814
name: hydromyelia
def: "Pathological dilation of the spinal cord due to expansion of the cerebrospinal fluid-filled central canal of the cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11815
name: 5-hydroxytryptamine
def: "Neurotransmitter found in the digestive tract\, platelets and central nervous system."
synonym: "3-(2-aminoethyl)-1H-indol-5-ol" []
synonym: "serotonin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11816
name: hypercapnia
def: "Abnormally increased carbon dioxide concentration in the blood resulting in a increased rate and depth of breathing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11817
name: hyperesthesia
def: "Excessive sensitivity to touch\, pain\, or other sensory stimuli."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11818
name: hyperpolarization
def: "Transient increase in membrane polarization of nerve cells or muscle cells; the opposite of depolarization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11819
name: hyperreflexia
def: "Increased or exaggerated reflex response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11820
name: hypertonia
def: "Extreme tension of the muscles or arteries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11821
name: hypnotic
def: "Agent that may reduce anxiety and induces sleep."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11822
name: hypocapnia
def: "Abnormally decreased carbon dioxide concentration in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11823
name: hypophysectomy
def: "Surgical removal of the pituitary gland \(hypophysis\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11824
name: hyporeflexia
def: "Diminished reflex response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11825
name: hypotension
def: "Subnormal arterial blood pressure.Note\: Hypotension can be a cause of dizziness\, weakness and fainting.\nLower than normal pressure or tension of any kind \(used less commonly\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11826
name: hypotonia
def: "Condition in which there is a diminution or loss of muscle tone.\nRelaxation of the arteries.\nLower than normal tension in any part\, as in intraocular pressure of the eyeball."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11827
name: ictus
def: "Sudden attack\, stroke or seizure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11828
name: inclusion body
def: "Particle in a cell\, detectable under the light microscope."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11829
name: inferior cerebellar peduncle
def: "Part of the cerebellum that is important for a number of motor functions\, including balance\, position sensing and coordination of movement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11830
name: inflammation
def: "Reaction of the body to injury or to infectious\, allergic\, or chemical irritation; characterized by redness\, swelling\, heat\, and pain resulting from dilation of the blood vessels accompanied by loss of plasma and leukocytes into the tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11831
name: infratentorial
def: "Below the tentorium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11832
name: inhibitory post-synaptic potential
def: "Referring to a more negative membrane potential at the postsynaptic side of the synaptic cleft that renders the postsynaptic neuron less likely to generate an action potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11833
name: insecticide
def: "Substance intended to kill insects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11834
name: insomnia
def: "Inability to sleep during the period when sleep should normally occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11835
name: intelligence quotient
def: "Numeric measure of a person’s cognitive function based on one of several standardized tests of a problem-solving nature."
synonym: "IQ test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11836
name: intracranial pressure
def: "Pressure within the cranial cavity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11837
name: intrathecal
def: "Within a sheath\, as within either the subarachnoid or the subdural space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11838
name: ion channel
def: "Pore-forming transmembrane protein that can gate the transmembrane flux of small ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11839
name: ion channelopathy
def: "Neuromuscular disease resulting from a genetic defect in an ion channel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11840
name: ionized calcium-binding adapter molecule 1
def: "Protein that is expressed in several tissues\, but in brain is a specific immunomarker for microglia."
synonym: "allograft inflammatory factor 1 (AIF-1)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11841
name: Irwin battery
def: "Set of neurobehavioral tests\, including observations of behavior in novel environments\, of reactions\, and of motor functions; used in various modifications and resembling the functional observational battery."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11842
name: kainic acid
def: "Natural amino acid produced by some seaweeds that activates a subclass of glutamate receptors \('kainate receptors'\) to produce excitotoxicity."
synonym: "(2S,3S,4S)-3-(carboxymethyl)-4-(prop-1-en-2-yl)pyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11843
name: kindling
def: "Increase of epileptogenic activities as a result of repeated\, longer-lasting subthreshold brain stimulation\, e.g.\, with electrical stimuli."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11844
name: lacrimation
def: "Secretion and discharge of tears."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11845
name: Lanthony D-15 color test
def: "Test of color vision using 15 distinct color chips with low color saturation that have to be arranged in a correct order."
synonym: "Lanthony D-15 desaturated hue panel" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11846
name: latent
def: "Not yet manifest; dormant but potentially discernible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11847
name: lathyrism
def: "Disease characterised by spastic paralysis of the legs and lower part of the body\, pain\, hyperesthesia\, and paresthesia."
synonym: "grass pea poisoning" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11848
name: lethargy
def: "Relatively mild impairment of consciousness resulting in reduced alertness and awareness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11849
name: levarterenol
def: "Levorotatory isomer of norepinephrine\, having greater pressor activity than the natural dextrorotatory isomer."
synonym: "2-diol" []
synonym: "4-[(1S)-2-amino-1-hydroxyethyl]benzene-1" []
synonym: "S-noradrenaline" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11850
name: libido
def: "Conscious or unconscious sexual desire; passionate interest in life force."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11851
name: ligand-gated ion channel
def: "Ion channel that opens upon binding an activating ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11852
name: limbic system
def: "Collection of interconnected neurons lying underneath the cerebrum on either side of the thalamus\, related more by anatomical location than as a single functional entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11853
name: lipoma
def: "Benign tumor of the adipose tissue\, often visible under the skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11854
name: lobe
def: "One of the four main regions of the cerebral cortex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11855
name: lower motor neuron
def: "Neuron that receives impulses from an upper motor neuron and whose axons innervate the skeletal muscles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11856
name: lumbar spine
def: "Portion of the vertebral column of the lower back between the thoracic spine and sacrum\, in humans consisting of five vertebrae designated L1 to L5."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11857
name: magnetic resonance spectroscopy
def: "Noninvasive method based on nuclear spin alignment that allows localization of levels and measurement of kinetics of an injected labeled substances in living tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11858
name: manganism
def: "Condition of manganese poisoning\, usually after chronic inhalation exposure to manganese fumes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11859
name: maze
def: "Labyrinthine system\, in which the ability of an animal \(usually a rodent\) to find a direct route from a starting point to a specific location is observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11860
name: medulloblastoma
def: "Highly malignant\, invasive brain tumor of embryonal origin\, arising in the posterior fossa\, usually from the cerebellum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11861
name: membrane polarization
def: "Occurrence of a potential difference across a biological membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11862
name: meninges
def: "Plural of meninx."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11863
name: meningioma
def: "Firm\, often vascular\, tumor arising from the meninges of the brain or spinal cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11864
name: meningitis
def: "Inflammation or infection of the meninges."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11865
name: meningoencephalitis
def: "Inflammation of the brain and meninges."
synonym: "cerebromeningitis" []
synonym: "encephalomeningitis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11866
name: meningoencephalocele
def: "Protrusion of both the meninges and brain tissue through a defect in the skull."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11867
name: mental retardation
def: "Formerly used term to describe intellectual disability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11868
name: mentation
def: "Mental activity\, conscious or unconscious\, but especially referring to thinking and reasoning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11869
name: <em>N</em>-methyl-D-aspartate
def: "Excitotoxic non-essential amino acid used experimentally to identify a specific subset of glutamatereceptors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11870
name: <em>N</em>-methyl-D-aspartate-type glutamate receptor
def: "One of the various types of receptors of the neurotransmitterglutamate."
synonym: "NMDA-type glutamate receptor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11871
name: 1-methyl-4-phenyl-1\,2\,3\,6-tetrahydropyridine
def: "Synthetic substance causing parkinsonism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11872
name: microglial cell
def: "Small glial cell; resident macrophage derived as a mononuclear phagocyte that populates the brain in early development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11873
name: microgliosis
def: "Morphological response of microglia to injury\, characterized by hypertrophy and amoeboid morphology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11874
name: migraine
def: "Recurrent syndrome usually characterized by unilateral head pain accompanied by symptoms including nausea and vomiting\, vertigo\, photophobia and other visual phenomena."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11875
name: migrainous infarction
def: "Stroke that occurs in connection with a migraine attack."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11876
name: Minnesota multiphasic personality inventory
def: "Questionnaire type of psychological test for ages 18 and older. The RF version has 338 true–false statements with seven validity and ten personality scales that may be used in either an individual or group format."
synonym: "MMPI-2-RF" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11877
name: miosis
def: "Abnormal contraction of the pupil of the eye to less than \\\(\\pu\{2 mm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11878
name: misfolded protein
def: "Protein that has the same amino acid sequence as the normal\, functional protein but exhibits a deviant tertiary structure and lacks normal function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11879
name: mitochondrial membrane potential
def: "Membrane potential across the inner membrane of the mitochondrion generated by the electron transport chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11880
name: mixed neuropathy
def: "Dysfunction or degeneration of both motor and sensory nerves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11881
name: Monro–Kellie hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that the intracranial volume is unchangeable and therefore a constant intracranial pressure requires that the sum of the volumes of the three compartments blood\, brain tissue and cerebrospinal fluid remains constant."
synonym: "Monro doctrine" []
synonym: "Monro–Kellie doctrine" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11882
name: morphine
def: "The major phenanthrene alkaloid of opium\, used as an analgesic\, sedative\, and anxiolytic."
synonym: "(5α,6α)-7,8-didehydro-4,5-epoxy-17-methylmorphinan-3,6-diol" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11883
name: motor activity
def: "Physical activity and performance of an animal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11884
name: motor coordination
def: "Cooperation of the motor neurons and the involved muscles to perform a body movement precisely according to spatiotemporal requirements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11885
name: motor cortex
def: "Region of the cerebral cortex\, located primarily in the frontal lobe\, involved in coordinating motor activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11886
name: motor endplate
def: "Complex structure by which the axon of a motor neuron establishes synaptic contact with a skeletal muscle fiber."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11887
name: motor neuron
def: "Nerve cell in the spinal cord having an axon that leaves the central nervous system to establish a functional connection with an effector \(muscle or glandular\) tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11888
name: motor neuron disease
def: "Generic term for a heterogeneous group of disorders\, all affecting motor neurons in the brain\, spinal cord\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11889
name: motor system
def: "Part of the central nervous system responsible for control of movement\, consisting of the pyramidal system controlling voluntary movement and the extrapyramidal system coordinating involuntary movement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11890
name: multiple chemical sensitivity
def: "Intolerance condition attributed to extreme sensitivity of individuals to various environmental chemicals\, found in air\, food\, water\, building materials\, or fabrics."
synonym: "environmental illness" []
synonym: "idiopathic environmental intolerance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11891
name: multiple sclerosis
def: "Common demyelinating \(see demyelination\) disorder of the central nervous system\, causing patches of sclerosis \(plaques\) in the brain and spinal cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11892
name: muscarinic
def: "Referring to agents that stimulate the postganglionicparasympatheticreceptor\, named after the prototypical mushroom alkaloid\, muscarine \[2\,5-anhydro-1\,4\,6-trideoxy-6-\(trimethylammonio\)-D-ribo-hexitol\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11893
name: muscarinic receptor
def: "One of the two main classes of acetylcholine receptors that responds to muscarinic agents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11894
name: muscle spindle
def: "Sensory organ in the body of a striated muscle containing afferent and efferentnerve fibers that detect passive muscle stretch and convey information to the central nervous system to cause reflex resistance to this stretch."
synonym: "neuromuscular spindle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11895
name: mydriasis
def: "Dilation of the pupil."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11896
name: myelin
def: "Electrically insulating lipoprotein layers that are produced by glial cells and surround the axons of the neurons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11897
name: myelin basic protein
def: "Major component of myelin\, that helps organizing the correct assembly of the lipids and proteins in myelin sheets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11898
name: myelin-associated glycoprotein
def: "Transmembrane glycoprotein that plays an important role in the interaction between axons and myelin and affects myelination during nerve regeneration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11899
name: myelin sheath
def: "Concentric layers of myelin surrounding the axons of some neurons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11900
name: myelogram
def: "X-ray of the spinal canal following injection of a contrast material into the surrounding cerebrospinal fluid spaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11901
name: myelomeningocele
def: "Protrusion of the spinal cord and its coverings through a defect in the vertebral column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11902
name: myelopathy
def: "Any functional or pathologic disturbance in the spinal cord.\nDisorder of the myelopoietic tissues of the bone marrow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11903
name: myopathy
def: "Any disease of muscle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11904
name: myotonia
def: "Prolonged contraction or delayed relaxation of a muscle after mechanical or brief electrical stimulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11905
name: NG2 cell
def: "Glial cell of the central nervous system\, distinct from an oligodendrocyte or astrocyte\, that may serve as a precursor of oligodendrocytes\, and that takes its name from expression of proteoglycan NG2."
synonym: "polydendrocyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11906
name: narcosis
def: "Nonspecific\, reversible depression of central nervous system function\, marked by stupor or unconsciousness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11907
name: narcotic
def: "Nonspecific usage – an agent that produces insensibility or stupor.\nSpecific usage – any natural or synthetic substance that has morphine-like actions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11908
name: neocortex
def: "Outer layer of the cerebral cortex that supports higher brain functions\, especially cognitive function and language in humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11909
name: nerve
def: "Ensheathed bundle of nerve fibers that conveys information between the central nervous system and other regions of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11910
name: nerve conduction study
def: "Measure of the functional integrity of a nerve with a stimulating electrode\, placed on the skin over the nerve\, and a more distal recording electrode\, to determine the velocity of the impulse that passes along the nerve between the electrodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11911
name: nerve conduction velocity
def: "Rate of progression of a nerve impulse in a peripheral nerve or in its largest component nerve fibers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11912
name: nerve fiber
def: "Axon with its surrounding myelin sheath."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11913
name: nerve gas
def: "Volatile chemical with neurotoxic features intended for use in warfare to disable or kill the organism rapidly after inhalation or skin contact\, and classified as a prohibited weapon of mass destruction by The Chemical Weapons Convention \(CWC\)."
synonym: "nerve agent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11914
name: nerve growth factor
def: "One of a family of proteins known as neurotropins that function as signaling molecules to induce axon growth\, branching\, elongation and survival of neurons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11915
name: nerve root
def: "Proximal end of a spinal nerve nearest its attachment to the spinal cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11916
name: nervous system
def: "The entire neuronal apparatus\, composed of both the central nervous system and the peripheral nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11917
name: neural activity
def: "Metabolic and electrical activity of neurons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11918
name: neuralgia
def: "Paroxysmal pain extending along the course of one or more nerves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11919
name: neural network
def: "A functionally interconnected set of neurons\, generally in the central nervous system."
synonym: "neuronal network" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11920
name: neural stem cell
def: "Multipotent cell\, with mitotic potential\, that may serve as a precursor for neuroprogenitor cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11921
name: neurectomy
def: "Excision of a nerve or part of a nerve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11922
name: neurite
def: "Outgrowth or projection of either an axon or a dendrite from the neuron cell body that is the structural basis of the neural network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11923
name: neurite outgrowth
def: "Phenomenon in which neurons tend to send out neurites to make contact with other nerve cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11924
name: neuritis
def: "Inflammation of a nerve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11925
name: neurobehavior core test battery
def: "A set of seven tests\, recommended by the World Health Organization \(WHO\) in 1983\, to detect neurotoxicity in human populations"

[Term]
id: goldbook:11926
name: neurodegenerative disease
def: "Disease of the brain and \(or\) peripheral nervous system with progressive loss of neuronal function\, accompanied by changes in morphology and subcellular organization of the nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11927
name: neurodevelopmental toxicity
def: "Adverse effect of a substance that disrupts developmental processes of the nervous system.\nToxicity in which the developing nervous system shows heightened sensitivity compared with the adult nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11928
name: neurofibril
def: "Part of a filamentous structure found in the cell body\, axons\, dendrites\, and sometimes synaptic endings of neurons\, consisting of bundles of neurofilaments and neurotubules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11929
name: neurofibrillary tangle
def: "Intracellular accumulation of aggregates of hyperphosphorylated tau protein or paired helical filaments consisting of the tau protein\, a microtubule-associated protein that normally stabilizes the cytoskeleton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11930
name: neurofibroma
def: "Tumor of the peripheral nervous system representing an abnormal collection of Schwann cells and connective tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11931
name: neurofilament
def: "Class of intermediate filament of the neuronal cytoskeleton\, providing support for the axon and dendrites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11932
name: neurogenesis
def: "Formation of nerve cells or of the nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11933
name: neurogenic
def: "Originating in\, starting from\, or caused by\, the nervous system or nerve impulses.\nRelating to neurogenesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11934
name: neurogenic shock
def: "Condition of decreased blood pressure\, slow heart rate and insufficient perfusion of the body with blood\, caused by loss of autonomic control following acute trauma to the brain or spinal cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11935
name: neuroimaging
def: "Process of producing images of the structure or activity of the brain or other parts of the nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11936
name: neuroinflammation
def: "Complex series of local immune responses within the nervous system to deal with a threat to the neural environment from infection\, injury\, or trauma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11937
name: neuroleptic
def: "Any of a class of psychotropic drugs used to treat psychosis\, particularly schizophrenia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11938
name: neurolysis
def: "Breakdown of nervous tissue from disease or injury.\nTherapeutic destruction of a nerve or nerves to treat intractable pain.\nSurgical freeing of a nerve from adhesions of the neural sheath."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11939
name: neuroma
def: "Tumor made up largely of nerve cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11940
name: neuronopathy
def: "Disorder involving destruction of the cell bodies of neurons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11941
name: neuron-specific enolase
def: "Enzyme of glucose metabolism\, normally found in nervous tissue\, that catalyzes the dehydration of 2-phosphoglycerate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11942
name: neuropathological examination
def: "Application of gross and histopathologic methods to study toxic changes in the morphology and histology of the nervous tissues and the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11943
name: neuropathy
def: "Pathological change in the peripheral nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11944
name: neuropathy target esterase
def: "Enzyme that is phosphorylated by organophosphate compounds and is believed to be involved in the delayed neuropathy of organophosphates."
synonym: "neurotoxic esterase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11945
name: neuroprogenitor cell
def: "Precursor cell that is capable of differentiating into specific cell types of the nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11946
name: neuroprotective
def: "Agent or property of an agent that protects neurons from damage and \(or\) from the process of aging or degeneration of the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11947
name: neuropsychological testing
def: "Assessment\, under standardized conditions\, of an individual’s brain function as it impacts upon cognitive function\, thought processes\, behavior and personality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11948
name: neurotoxic
def: "Poisonous to structures and \(or\) functions of the brain and nerves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11949
name: neurotoxicant
def: "Substance that can produce functional or structural damage to the nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11950
name: neurotoxicology
def: "Discipline applying principles of both neuroscience and toxicology to study the ability of certain substances to alter nervous system structure or function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11951
name: neurotoxic shellfish poisoning
def: "Toxic shellfish poisoning characterized by nausea\, vomiting\, and mild neurological symptoms such as dysarthria\, paresthesia\, ataxia\, and vertigo caused by ingestion of shellfish \(specifically\, bivalve molluscs\) contaminated with the polyether brevetoxin and its derivatives produced by the dinoflagellate Karenia brevis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11952
name: neurotoxin
def: "Any substance that has the potential to damage the nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11953
name: neurotransmission
def: "Process by which a presynaptic cell\, upon excitation\, releases a specific agent \(a neurotransmitter\) that crosses the synapse to stimulate or inhibit the postsynaptic cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11954
name: retrograde neurotransmission
def: "Neurotransmission wherein a neurotransmitter is sent from the postsynaptic to the presynaptic membrane in order to modify a signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11955
name: neurotransmitter
def: "Biochemical that is released upon excitation from a presynaptic membrane\, crosses the synaptic space and binds to a receptor on the postsynaptic membrane\, where it induces a signal that may be either stimulatory or inhibitory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11956
name: neurotropic
def: "Having an affinity for nerve cells or tissues of the nervous system."
synonym: "neurotrophic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11957
name: neurotubule
def: "Microtubule found in neurons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11958
name: neurovascular
def: "Relating to both the nervous system and the vascular system.\nRelating to the vasomotor nerves that supply the walls of the blood vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11959
name: nicotinic
def: "Referring to a substance or effect that mimics acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction\, at autonomic ganglia and at other locations in the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11960
name: nicotinic receptor
def: "One of the two main classes of acetylcholine receptors\, ligand-gated ion channels that open upon binding nicotine as well as acetylcholine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11961
name: nine-hole box test
def: "Behavioral test based on the five-choice serial reaction time task\, which allows the study of attention based on a test animal learning from which hole \(typically one of five open holes in an arc of nine\) a visual \(light\) cue was given\, with a food pellet reward for nose-poking the correct hole."
synonym: "5–9 hole attention box test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11962
name: Nissl body
def: "Granular structure consisting of rough endoplasmic reticulum\, found in neurons."
synonym: "Nissl granule" []
synonym: "Nissl substance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11963
name: nociception
def: "Physiological response to harmful stimuli by specialized nerve endings that transmit a signal perceived as pain by the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11964
name: node of Ranvier
def: "Short interval in the myelin sheath of a nerve fiber where the axon is invested only by scant cytoplasmic processes of the neighboring Schwann cells or\, in the central nervous system\, of oligodendroglia cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11966
name: nose-poke test
def: "Test allowing study of attention deficit based on noting the success of a test animal in gaining a food pellet reward for nosing the correct hole in a box."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11967
name: numbness
def: "Imprecise term for absent or reduced touch perception\, as well as paresthesia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11968
name: nystagmus
def: "Involuntary rhythmic oscillation of the eyeballs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11969
name: occipital lobe
def: "One of the four major lobes of each cerebral hemisphere\, situated within the occiput\, containing most of the neural apparatus of visual processing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11970
name: olfaction
def: "Detection of odors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11971
name: oligodendrocyte
def: "Type of glial cell that produces the myelin sheath in the central nervous system."
synonym: "oligodendroglial cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11972
name: oligodendroglioma
def: "Glioma arising from the oligodendrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11973
name: open field test
def: "Procedure to observe changes in motor activity and rearing\, in which a rodent is placed on a surface and distance and patterns of movement are recorded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11974
name: opiate receptor
def: "One of a group of G-protein-coupled receptors that bind morphine and endogenous substances that have morphine-like effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11975
name: opioid
def: "Synthetic narcotic having properties consistent with binding to opiate receptors\, but not derived from opium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11976
name: opium
def: "Extract of the opium poppy\, Papaver somniferum\, containing morphine as a major psychoactive component."
synonym: "Papaver somniferum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11977
name: organoleptic
def: "Relating to perception by a sensory organ.\nInvolving the use of sensory organs\, e.g.\, organoleptic tests."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11978
name: organometallic compound
def: "Compound having covalent bonds between one or more metal atoms and one or more carbon atoms of an organyl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11979
name: organophosphate
def: "Organic ester of phosphoric acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11980
name: organophosphate-induced delayed neuropathy
def: "Disorder\, typically with symptoms of pain and weakness in the extremities\, resulting from neuronal death associated with organophosphate poisoning \(either chronic or a late effect of acute poisoning\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11981
name: osmophobia
def: "Morbid fear of odors."
synonym: "olfactophobia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11982
name: oxidative stress
def: "Adverse effects occurring when the generation of reactive oxygen species \(ROS\) in a system exceeds the system’s ability to neutralize and eliminate them; excess ROS can damage a cell’s lipids\, protein\, and DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11983
name: pain
def: "Conscious awareness of an unpleasant sensation mediated by nerve conduction of a stimulus from a site of tissue injury to the brain.\nNote 1\: Chemical mediators of pain include histamine\, bradykinins\, and prostaglandins.\nNote 2\: The sensation may occur or persist when the injured tissue is no longer present\, as in phantom pain.\nSensation one feels when hurt in body or mind."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11984
name: neuropathic pain
def: "Pain initiated by nerve injury\, not mediated by nociception."
synonym: "neuropathic pain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11985
name: phantom pain
def: "Sensation that an amputated limb is still present\, often associated with pain."
synonym: "phantom limb pain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11986
name: paired helical filament
def: "Describing the aggregation of hyperphosphorylated tau protein into neurofibrillary tangles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11987
name: palpitation
def: "Unduly rapid or throbbing heartbeat that is noted by a patient; it may be regular or irregular.\nUndue awareness by a patient of a heartbeat that is otherwise normal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11988
name: papilledema
def: "Edema of the region of the head of the optic nerve in the retina \(the optic disc\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11989
name: paralysis
def: "Total or partial loss of motor function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11990
name: paralytic shellfish poisoning
def: "Illness that is a consequence of consumption of bivalve molluscs such as mussels\, oysters\, and clams that have ingested large quantities of microalgae containing saxitoxin or its derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11991
name: paraoxonase
def: "Any of several related enzymes that hydrolyze aryl esters and lactones\, notably organophosphate pesticides including the acetylcholinesterase inhibitor\, paraoxon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11992
name: paraplegia
def: "Paralysis of both lower limbs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11993
name: parasympathetic nervous system
def: "Division of the autonomic nervous system involved in stimulating digestive secretions\, slowing heart rate\, constricting the pupils of the eyes\, and dilating blood vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11994
name: parasympathomimetic
def: "n.\, Substance that produces effects resembling those of impulses transmitted by the postganglionic fibers of the parasympathetic nervous system.\nadj.\, Producing effects resembling those of impulses transmitted by the postganglionic fibers of the parasympathetic nervous system."
synonym: "cholinomimetic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11995
name: paresis
def: "Slight or incomplete paralysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11996
name: paresthesia
def: "Abnormal or unexplained tingling\, pricking\, or burning sensation on the skin."
synonym: "paraesthesia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11997
name: parietal lobe
def: "One of the four major lobes of each cerebral hemisphere situated between the frontal and occipital lobes\, above the temporal lobe."

[Term]
id: goldbook:11998
name: Parkinson disease
def: "Degenerative disorder of the central nervous system resulting from decreased production of the neurotransmitterdopamine in the basal ganglia \(notably the substantia nigra\)."
synonym: "idiopathic parkinsonism" []
synonym: "primary parkinsonism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:11999
name: secondary parkinsonism
def: "Neurological syndrome resembling Parkinson disease and often resulting from decreased dopamine production as a consequence of neurodegenerative changes in the substantia nigra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12000
name: passive avoidance test
def: "Fear-motivated test in which rodents learn to avoid an otherwise preferred environment in which they are exposed to an aversive stimulus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12001
name: paw-reaching test
def: "Test used to reveal motor deficits\, usually of central origin\, exhibited on the contralateral side by reduced ability of a test animal to extend the forelimb."
synonym: "staircase test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12002
name: PC-12 cell
def: "Cell of an established line originally derived from a pheochromocytoma of the rat adrenal medulla."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12003
name: peripheral nervous system
def: "One of the two major divisions of the nervous system\, made up of the nerves and ganglia lying outside the brain and spinal cord that transmit sensory and motor signals to and from the central nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12004
name: peripheral neuropathy
def: "Neuropathy affecting nerves of the peripheral nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12005
name: peristalsis
def: "Progression of a wave of smooth muscle contraction followed by relaxation along the length of a muscular tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12006
name: pheochromocytoma
def: "Tumor of the chromaffin cells of the adrenal medulla that results in excessive secretion of norepinephrine and other catecholamines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12007
name: phonophobia
def: "Abnormal fear of loud sound\, or occasionally of voices."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12008
name: photophobia
def: "Abnormal visual intolerance of light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12009
name: photoreceptor cell
def: "Specialized neuron found in the retina\, involved in detecting light and transducing the signals of vision"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12010
name: pia
def: "Innermost of three membranous layers\, together composing the meninges\, that surrounds and adheres to the brain and spinal cord."
synonym: "pia mater" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12011
name: plexus
def: "Network of interconnecting nerves\, blood vessels or lymphatic vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12012
name: pneumotaxic center
def: "Neural network in the brainstem involved in regulating respiration."
synonym: "pontine respiratory group" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12013
name: polyneuritis
def: "Inflammation of several peripheral nerves simultaneously."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12014
name: polyneuropathy
def: "Neuropathy \(distal axonopathy\) of the peripheral nervous system\, often affecting the extremities in a bilateral distribution\, and characterized by numbness\, tingling\, and paresthesia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12015
name: posterior fossa
def: "Pocket in the back of the skull that accommodates the cerebellum and the midbrain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12016
name: postsynaptic
def: "Event or structure at the distal side of a synaptic cleft."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12017
name: long-term potentiation
def: "Potentiation lasting up to several months."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12018
name: presynaptic
def: "Event or structure at the proximal \(transmitting\) side of a synaptic cleft."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12019
name: prodromal stage
def: "Phase before the onset of full-blown disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12020
name: prodrome
def: "Symptom\(s\) heralding impending onset of a disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12021
name: profile of mood state test
def: "Procedure in which a test subject is asked to indicate his/her mood during the past week\, by quickly marking specified emotional words on a scale between 'not at all' and 'extremely.'"
synonym: "POMS test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12022
name: proprioception
def: "Sense of the movement or position of the body independent of vision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12023
name: proprioceptor
def: "Sensory organ in a muscle\, tendon or joint capsule that senses position or state of contraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12024
name: pseudobulbar affect
def: "Involuntary emotional outburst \(typically crying or laughing\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12025
name: pseudomeningocele
def: "Part of the cerebrospinal fluid that\, in contrast to a meningocele\, is not surrounded by dura."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12026
name: pseudotumor cerebri
def: "Disorder with increased intracranial pressure\, cerebral edema\, and frequently papilledema\, symptoms that resemble the effects of a tumor but without the occurrence of one."
synonym: "idiopathic intracranial hypertension" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12027
name: psychoactive
def: "Having the ability to alter mood\, behavior\, cognitive function or mental state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12028
name: psychoneuroimmunology
def: "Study of links between the brain and the immune system\, recognizing an interaction between psychological states and the outcome of immune responses to disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12029
name: psychosis
def: "Disordered thought processes that may include changes in cognitive function\, perception\, belief\, behavior and personality; altered awareness of reality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12030
name: organic psychosis
def: "Psychosis with a known physical \(anatomic\, disease-related or toxic\) cause."
synonym: "organic brain syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12031
name: psychotropic
def: "Capable of affecting the mind\, emotions and behavior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12032
name: puffer fish
def: "Marine fish of the order Tetraodontiformes and family Tetraodontidae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12033
name: Purkinje cell
def: "Large neuron forming part of a layer of cells in the cerebellum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12034
name: pursuit aiming test
def: "Procedure in which a subject is instructed to place a dot in defined sites \(such as a number of circles\)\, as quickly as possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12035
name: putamen
def: "Part of the basal ganglia that plays a role in coordinating movement and learning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12036
name: pyknosis
def: "Clumping of nuclear chromatin and shrinkage of the cell nucleus during cell degeneration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12037
name: pyramidal cell
def: "Large neuron of the cerebral cortex having a triangular cell body in histological section with several dendrites at the base and one large apical axon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12038
name: pyrethroid
def: "Synthetic molecule with structural similarity to the natural pyrethrins found in plants of the genus Chrysanthemum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12039
name: quadriplegia
def: "Paralysis of both upper and both lower limbs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12040
name: radial arm maze
def: "Apparatus with a central platform and equidistant arms \(usually eight\)\, at the end of which is a reward \(e.g.\, a food pellet\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12041
name: radiculitis
def: "Inflammation of the spinal nerve roots leading to pain and hyperesthesia radiating along the nerve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12042
name: rearing
def: "Of a rodent or other animal raising itself on its hind legs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12043
name: rebound
def: "Reappearance of original symptom\(s\)\, often exacerbated\, following cessation of treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12044
name: reflex
def: "Involuntary reaction in response to a stimulus applied to the periphery following its transmission to the nervous centers in the brain or spinal cord."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12045
name: reflex sympathetic dystrophy
def: "Diffuse persistent pain usually in an extremity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12046
name: reinforcement
def: "Use of a stimulus in operant conditioning to increase the likelihood that a particular behavior will occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12047
name: relaxant
def: "Agent that reduces muscle tension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12048
name: reperfusion
def: "Restoration of the blood supply to an organ or tissue following a period of ischemia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12049
name: repolarization
def: "Returning of a cell’s membrane potential to its resting potential following depolarization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12050
name: resolution phase
def: "Return to a sexually unexcited state following orgasm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12051
name: resting potential
def: "Baseline membrane potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12052
name: retrograde axonal transport
def: "Transport of material from the nerve ending back to the cell body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12053
name: reuptake
def: "Transporter-dependent resequestration of a neurotransmitter by a presynapticneuron\, terminating synaptic transmission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12054
name: Rey–Osterrieth complex figure test
def: "Procedure in which the test subject first copies a complicated drawing and then is asked to draw it from memory."
synonym: "ROCF Test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12055
name: righting reflex
def: "Reflex that restores the correct orientation of the body when taken out of the upright position."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12056
name: righting test
def: "Experimental measurement of an animal’s \(usually a rodent’s\) ability to land in an upright position at the end of a fall."
synonym: "aerial righting test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12057
name: surface righting test
def: "Test animal is placed on its back and the time to retain an uprate position with four paws in contact with the surface is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12058
name: rotarod test
def: "Experimental observation of an animal’s \(usually a rodent’s\) ability to stay on a rotating rod without falling off."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12059
name: saccadic eye movement
def: "Rapid or jerky movement of the eyes from one line of sight to another\, as in reading or correction of a jerky nystagmus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12060
name: Saint Anthony fire
def: "Referring to inflammatory conditions of the skin such as erysipelas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12061
name: Santa Ana dexterity test
def: "Procedure in which a test subject stands in front of a plate with 48 square holes and an equal number of fitted pegs\, each having a cylindrical upper part and square base\, and is asked to fit the pegs into the holes as quickly as possible by turning them 180 degrees\, first with one hand and in a subsequent trial with the other hand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12062
name: schedule-controlled operant behaviour
def: "Experimental procedure in which operant behavior is assessed following a conditioning stimulus delivered at a pre-determined duration and frequency \('schedule of reinforcement'\)\, thus allowing comparison of the effects of magnitude\, frequency and duration of the stimulus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12063
name: schedule of reinforcement
def: "Frequency\, duration\, dose\, and \(or\) intensity of a conditioning stimulus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12064
name: Schwann cell
def: "Glial cell in the peripheral nervous system that produces myelin for the axons of the peripheral nerves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12065
name: sclerosis
def: "Hardening of an organ or tissue\, especially that due to excessive growth of fibrous tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12066
name: Scombroid poisoning
def: "Constellation of symptoms resembling an allergic reaction\, caused by eating the meat of decaying fish that has high levels of histamine \(in this context sometimes referred to as scombrotoxin\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12067
name: sedative
def: "Substance that exerts a soothing or tranquillizing effect\, or describing the effect itself."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12068
name: selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor
def: "Drug in a subclass of substances that interfere with the resequestration of serotonin by the presynaptic membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12069
name: senility
def: "Characteristics typically associated with old age\, especially relating to memory loss and other mental impairment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12070
name: sensory neuron
def: "Nerve cell specialized for detecting and transducing signals from external or somatic stimuli."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12071
name: serotoninergic
def: "Relating to a nerve that releases or responds to serotonin as a neurotransmitter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12072
name: shellfish poisoning
def: "Illness associated with consumption of seafood contaminated with toxins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12073
name: shunt
def: "n. Fistula or prosthetic device that diverts fluid from one system to another.\nvb. To bypass or divert."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12074
name: sick building syndrome
def: "Health effects or loss of a sense of well-being associated\, by those who experience it\, with time spent in a certain building or room."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12075
name: silver staining
def: "Method for visualization of macromolecules such as nucleic acids and proteins\, typically in electrophoretic gels or formalin-fixed histological sections\, based on the reduction of silver salts to metallic silver or formation of crystalline silver compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12076
name: simple reaction time test
def: "Procedure to assess psychomotor speed in which a test subject is presented at irregular intervals with a visual stimulus\, such as a coloured dot on a screen\, and is asked to press a button immediately after noticing the stimulus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12077
name: SLUDDE
def: "Acronym for the constellation of signs salivation\, lacrymation\, urination\, defecation\, dyspnea/gastrointestinal upset\, and emesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12078
name: social interaction test
def: "Behavioral test wherein a rodent stranger is placed in the cage of a domestic rodent and intensities and duration of interactions \(e.g.\, sniffing\, aggression\, avoidance\) between the socially unacquainted animals are observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12079
name: soporific
def: "Substance producing sleep."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12080
name: spasm
def: "Sudden involuntary contraction of one or more muscles; includes cramps and contractures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12081
name: spasmolytic
def: "Agent that relieves smooth muscle spasms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12082
name: spastic
def: "Relating to spasticity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12083
name: spasticity
def: "Increased muscle tone at rest\, characterized by resistance to passive stretch."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12084
name: sphingolipid
def: "Member of a class of lipids containing the aliphatic amino alcohol sphingosine\, or related structures\, thus including ceramide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12085
name: sphingomyelin
def: "One of a family of phospholipids found especially in the central nervous system\, containing 1-phosphocholine bound to a ceramide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12086
name: sphingosine
def: "Major lipid component of cell membranes."
synonym: "2-aminooctadec-4-ene-1,3-diol" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12087
name: startle reaction
def: "Rapid physiological and psychological response to a sudden\, unexpected stimulus that includes muscle contraction and avoidance posturing\, with an increase in heart rate\, respiration\, skin conductance\, and a negative affect."
synonym: "startle part" []
synonym: "startle response" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12088
name: startle reflex
def: "The earliest brainstem-mediated part of the reflex reaction to a stimulus perceived as threatening."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12089
name: acoustic startle response test
def: "Neurophysiological examination in which an animal is exposed to a brief \(e.g.\, \\\(\\pu\{40 ms\}\\\)\) acoustic stimulus\, followed by measurement of the animal’s motor reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12090
name: status epilepticus
def: "Repeated or prolonged seizure lasting more than \\\(\\pu\{30 min\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12091
name: stereology
def: "Three dimensional interpretation of two dimensional cross sections of tissue and other materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12092
name: unbiased stereology
def: "Stereological method that uses systematic analysis of defined spatial fractions of a sample to estimate numbers of neuronal nuclei independent of changes in size and shape that may occur during tissue processing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12093
name: stimulant
def: "Agent that rouses or increases mental or physical activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12094
name: stimulus
def: "Anything that produces a reaction in an organism.\nThat which causes a response in an excitable tissue such as a nerve\, muscle or gland."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12095
name: conditioned stimulus
def: "Stimulus that comes to evoke a response after repetitive pairing with another stimulus that automatically elicits that response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12096
name: unconditioned stimulus
def: "Stimulus that automatically elicits a paired response\, such as food eliciting salivation in a hungry animal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12097
name: striatum
def: "Subcortical structure of striations of gray matter and white matter in the forebrain that serves as the major input to the basal ganglia\, specifically the caudate nucleus\, the putamen and the striated structure linking them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12098
name: stroke
def: "Acute impairment of circulation to a part of the brain\, lasting more than \\\(\\pu\{24 h\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12099
name: stupor
def: "State of impaired consciousness showing diminution in response to environmental stimulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12100
name: subarachnoid
def: "Underneath the arachnoid membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12101
name: subarachnoid hemorrhage
def: "Loss of blood into the space between the arachnoid membrane and the pia mater."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12102
name: subdural
def: "Between the dura mater and the arachnoid membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12103
name: substantia nigra
def: "Component of the basal ganglia involved in production of the neurotransmitterdopamine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12104
name: supratentorial
def: "Intracranial contents located above the tentorium of the cerebellum\, notably the white matter of the cerebrum\, often describing functional symptoms of diseases of the white matter that lead to physical and mental disability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12105
name: supratentorial herniation
def: "Abnormal protrusion of brain structures into compartments above the tentorium\, or over the edge of the tentorium\, due to increased intracranial pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12106
name: swimming test
def: "Procedure in which a test animal \(rodent\) is forced to swim to avoid drowning."
synonym: "Porsolt forced swimming test" []
synonym: "behavioral despair test" []
synonym: "forced swimming test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12107
name: sympathetic nervous system
def: "Part of the autonomic nervous system originating in the thoracic and lumbar regions of the spinal cord that tends to inhibit or oppose the physiological effects of the parasympathetic nervous system\, as in tending to reduce digestive secretions\, increase the heart rate\, and contract blood vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12108
name: sympathomimetic
def: "n.\, Substance that produces effects resembling those of impulses transmitted by the postganglionic fibers of the sympathetic nervous system.\nadj.\, Having the property of producing such effects."
synonym: "adrenergic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12109
name: synapse
def: "Functional junction between two neurons\, where a nerve impulse is transmitted from one neuron to another\, by neurotransmitters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12110
name: synaptic cleft
def: "Narrow gap between the presynaptic and postsynaptic regions of two communicating neurons\, into which neurotransmitters are secreted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12111
name: synaptic inhibition
def: "Suppression of synaptic transmission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12112
name: synaptic plasticity
def: "Ability of synapses to form and reform continually\, including to allow learning.\nMalleability of signal strength arising at the level of synaptic transmission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12113
name: synaptic stripping
def: "Removal of dysfunctional synapses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12114
name: synaptic transmission
def: "Neurotransmitter release\, diffusion\, and receptor binding\, propagating an impulse across a synapse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12115
name: syringomyelia
def: "Cyst occurring within the spinal cord as a result of accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid."
synonym: "syrinx" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12116
name: T-maze
def: "Simple type of test apparatus with three arms shaped like the letter 'T.' After placing a rodent in the middle\, it is recorded how often the animal enters each arm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12117
name: tachycardia
def: "Abnormally fast heartbeat."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12118
name: tail flick test
def: "Procedure in which the tail of a rodent is heated and the time taken for the animal to flick its tail \(latency\) is taken as a measure of the relative degree of antinociception."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12119
name: tail immersion test
def: "Procedure in which the tail of a rodent is placed in a hot water bath \(typically between \\\(\\pu\{54^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{58^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)\) and the time it takes the animal to remove the tail from the water is interpreted as a measurement of the relative degree of antinociception."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12120
name: tail suspension test
def: "Procedure in which a mouse is suspended by its tail and the time spent attempting to right itself is observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12121
name: tauopathy
def: "One of a class of neurodegenerative diseases\, including Alzheimer disease\, associated with abnormal aggregation of tau proteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12122
name: tau protein
def: "One of a class of proteins that stabilizes microtubules\, particularly in neurons\, astrocytes\, and oligodendrocytes of the central nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12123
name: TDP-43 transcription factor
def: "Cleavage product \(\\\(\\pu\{43 kDa\}\\\)\) of the transactive response \(TAR\) DNA binding protein \(TARDBP\) that targets TAR DNA and transcriptionally represses HIV-1."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12124
name: temperature sensitivity
def: "Adverse responsiveness to heat or cold.\nNote\: It is associated with a number of medical conditions\, some physiologically well-defined \(as in cold intolerance in hypothyroidism\) and others less so \(as in reported chronic fatigue syndrome\).\nPhenotype arising from engineered or selected gene mutations in experimental organisms characterized by an altered temperature permissive for optimal growth or survival."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12125
name: temporal lobe
def: "One of the four major lobes of each cerebral hemisphere\, situated at the base of the brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12126
name: tentorium
def: "Fold of the dura mater separating the cerebrum from the cerebellum."
synonym: "tentorium cerebelli" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12127
name: tetanic
def: "Pertaining to tetanus\, characterized by tonic muscle spasm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12128
name: tetanus
def: "Sustained muscle contraction.\nDisease caused by tetanus toxin and characterized by painful\, sustained muscle contraction with spasticity and paralysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12129
name: tetanus toxin
def: "Neurotoxin produced by the bacterium Clostridium tetani growing in anaerobic conditions\, producing the disease tetanus."
synonym: "tetanospasmin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12130
name: tetanus toxin-binding ganglioside
def: "Ganglioside that binds tetanus toxin\, facilitating its internalization at the presynapticneuronal membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12131
name: tetany
def: "Neurological syndrome characterized by muscle twitches\, cramps\, and spasms; and in severe forms\, by spasm of the muscles of the larynx \(laryngospasm\) and seizures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12132
name: tetrad test
def: "Battery of tests typically for the effects of cannabinoids in rodents that includes hypomotility assessed by an open field test\, catalepsy assessed by the bar test\, hypothermia measured by rectal temperature\, and analgesia measured by avoidance of heat\, e.g.\, in the tail immersion test."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12133
name: tetrodotoxin
def: "Neurotoxin that inhibits action potentials by blocking sodium ion channels."
synonym: "4R,4aR,5R,6S,7S,8S,8aR,10S,12S)-2-azaniumylidene-4,6,8,12-tetrahydroxy-6-(hydroxymethyl)-2,3,4,4a,5,6,7,8-octahydro-1H-8a,10-methano-5,7-(epoxymethanooxy)quinazolin-10-olate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12134
name: tetrodotoxin-insensitive
def: "Denoting a class of sodium ion channels\, found mainly in the heart\, that is not blocked by tetrodotoxin."
synonym: "TTX-insensitive" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12135
name: thigmotaxis
def: "Movement of an organism toward or away from a mechanical stimulus\, physical contact\, or spatial discontinuity\, as in movement away from touch by a physical stimulus or exploring close to a wall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12136
name: thoracic spine
def: "Portion of the vertebral column of the upper back\, between the cervical spine and lumbar spine\, in humans consisting of 12 vertebrae designated T1 to T12."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12137
name: threshold potential
def: "Level of depolarization that must be reached to trigger an action potential in an excitable cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12138
name: thrombus
def: "Clot in the cardiovasculature formed from components of the blood\, which may be attached to the vessel wall and \(or\) occlusive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12139
name: tingling
def: "Pricking type of paresthesia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12140
name: tinnitus
def: "Continual noise in an ear\, such as ringing\, buzzing\, roaring\, or clicking."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12141
name: tomography
def: "Construction of an image of a three-dimensional structure from a series of planar sections obtained with penetrating waves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12142
name: torticollis
def: "Spasmodic contraction of neck muscles drawing the head to one side with the chin pointing to the other side."
synonym: "wry neck" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12143
name: toxic substance
def: "Substance causing injury to living organisms as a result of physicochemical interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12144
name: tranquilizer
def: "Drug that calms and pacifies with minimal sedating or depressant effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12145
name: transcutaneous electronic nerve stimulation
def: "Electrical stimulus applied across the skin to excite nerves for therapeutic purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12146
name: transsynaptic
def: "Referring to an event\, such as transmission of a nerve impulse\, across a synapse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12147
name: transtentorial herniation
def: "Bulging of brain tissue from the cerebrum through the notch of the tentorium\, caused by increased intracranial pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12148
name: treadmill test
def: "Procedure used to assess limb function and strength\, often to evaluate models of peripheral neuropathy\, in which a rodent is placed in an enclosed lane upon a surface that can be made to move at variable speed in a retrograde manner\, thus allowing observation of the animal’s gait dimension during sustained attempt at forward motion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12149
name: tremor
def: "Repetitive\, often regular and usually involuntary oscillatory movement caused by alternating contractions of opposing muscle groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12150
name: trigeminal nerve
def: "Fifth cranial nerve\, supplying sensation to the face and controlling motor activity of mastication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12151
name: trigeminal neuralgia
def: "Severe\, paroxysmal bursts of pain in one or more branches of the trigeminal nerve\, often induced by touching trigger points in or about the mouth."
synonym: "tic douloureux" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12152
name: triptan
def: "Member of a class of drugs based on tryptamine that act as serotonin receptor agonists."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12153
name: unconscious
def: "Not in a conscious state.\nIn psychoanalytic theory\, describing feelings and motivations of which one is unaware that nevertheless shape behavior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12154
name: uncus
def: "Anterior projection of the gray matter surrounding the hippocampus\, covered by a region of the temporal lobe\, involved in olfaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12155
name: upper motor neuron
def: "Neuron whose cell body lies in the cerebral cortex or brainstem and whose axon carries impulses to a lower motor neuron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12156
name: vagus nerve
def: "Tenth cranial nerve\, arising from the medulla oblongata."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12157
name: vasoconstriction
def: "Decrease in the diameter of the lumen of a blood vessel \(especially in the arteries and arterioles\) as a result of contraction of smooth muscles in the vessel walls."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12158
name: vasodilator
def: "Agent that induces vasorelaxation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12159
name: vasopressor
def: "Agent that induces vasoconstriction."
synonym: "pressor" []
synonym: "vasoconstrictor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12160
name: vasorelaxation
def: "Increase in the diameter of the lumen of the blood vessels as a result of relaxation of smooth muscles in the vessel walls."
synonym: "vasodilatation" []
synonym: "vasodilation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12161
name: vasospasm
def: "Contraction of the muscular wall of blood vessels with sustained vasoconstriction possibly leading to tissue ischemia."
synonym: "angiospasm" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12162
name: venom
def: "Animal toxin generally used for self-defense or predation and usually delivered by a bite or sting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12163
name: ventricle
def: "Normal cavity within an organ\, as in the brain or heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12164
name: ventriculo-atrial shunt
def: "Surgical shunt draining cerebrospinal fluid into a cardiac atrium\, to relieve pressure in hydrocephalus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12165
name: ventriculogram
def: "Radiographic image of the cerebralventricles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12166
name: ventriculomegaly
def: "Enlargement of the cerebralventricles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12167
name: ventriculo-peritoneal shunt
def: "Surgical shunt draining cerebrospinal fluid into the peritoneum of the abdominal cavity\, to relieve pressure in hydrocephalus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12168
name: vermis
def: "Narrow central part of the cerebellum between the two cerebellar hemispheres."
synonym: "vermis cerebelli" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12169
name: vertebral column
def: "Bony structure consisting of the vertebrae and enclosing the spinal cord."
synonym: "spine" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12170
name: vertigo
def: "Dizziness; an illusion of movement as if the external world were revolving around one’s self or as if one’s self were revolving in space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12171
name: vigilance
def: "Ability to maintain concentration or sustain attention over prolonged periods of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12172
name: vigilance decrement
def: "Decreased vigilance measured as a decline in the ability to maintain an accurate voluntary response to signals with time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12173
name: visual cortex
def: "Part of the cerebral cortex located in the occipital lobe\, responsible for processing the images of sight."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12174
name: voltage-gated ion channel
def: "Ion channel that opens and closes in response to changes in membrane potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12175
name: water maze test
def: "Procedure in which a rat or mouse is placed in a pool of water and observed to swim\, using visual environmental clues\, to an invisible submerged platform that allows escape from the water."
synonym: "Morris water maze test" []
synonym: "water navigation test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12176
name: Wernicke aphasia
def: "Type of aphasia associated with damage to Wernicke’s area or alternatively the medial temporal lobe of the brain\, characterized by preservation of speech but with loss of meaningful language and language comprehension."
synonym: "fluent aphasia" []
synonym: "receptive aphasia" []
synonym: "sensory aphasia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12177
name: Wernicke’s area
def: "Region of the brain in superior temporal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere\, associated with comprehension of written and spoken language."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12178
name: Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome
def: "Co-existence of Wernicke encephalopathy \(with gait\, ocular and mental disturbances\) and Korsakoff psychosis \(severe memory loss\, often with aphasia\, apraxia or agnosia\) caused by vitamin B1 deficiency in the context of chronic alcohol abuse."
synonym: "alcoholic encephalopathy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12179
name: Western Pacific amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and parkinsonism–dementia complex
def: "Neurodegenerative disease observed in the islands of Guam\, Indonesia\, Japan and Papua New Guinea\, showing combinations of characteristics of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis\, atypical parkinsonism with dementia\, or dementia alone that may present sequentially during life."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12180
name: Wisconsin card sorting test
def: "Procedure in which cards\, depicting different arrangements\, numbers\, and colors of symbols\, are presented to the test subject\, who is asked to make the most appropriate match of a test card without further instructions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12181
name: withdrawal
def: "Cessation of exposure to a substance\, being characterized by adverse symptoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12182
name: withdrawal effect
def: "Adverse event following withdrawal from a person or animal of a drug to which they have been chronically exposed or on which they have become dependent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12183
name: Wolfgram protein
def: "Acidic proteolipid protein component of central nervous systemmyelin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12184
name: wrist drop
def: "Inability to extend the wrist as a result of peripheral nerve damage\, causing drooping of the hand."
synonym: "Teleky’s sign" []
synonym: "radial nerve palsy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12185
name: zebrafish
def: "Tropical fish \(Danio rerio\) about \\\(\\pu\{4 cm\}\\\) long\, commonly used as a model organism in developmental biology."
synonym: "Danio rerio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12186
name: chain segment
def: "Group of contiguous skeletal atoms in a chain\, including their side groups\, if any\, such that the chain itself can be represented as a chain of segments."
synonym: "segment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12187
name: rotational isomeric state
def: "Rotamer defined by a relative rotation about a skeletal bond of a single-strand chain."
synonym: "bond-conformational state (in polymer science)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12188
name: chain conformation
def: "Conformation of a chain as defined by its sequence of bond-conformational states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12189
name: unperturbed conformational state
def: "Chain conformation corresponding to unperturbed dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12190
name: unperturbed radius of gyration
def: "Radius of gyration of a macromolecule in an unperturbed conformational state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12191
name: root-mean-square radius of gyration
def: "For a flexible macromolecule composed of \\\(n\\\) mass elements\, of masses \\\(m_\{i\}\\\)\, \\\(i = 1\, 2\, \\ldots\, n\\\)\, located at statistical-mechanical mean-square distances \\\(\\lt\\!s_\{i\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\\) from the centre of mass\, the root-mean-square radius of gyration is the square-root of the mass average of \\\(\\lt\\!s_\{i\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\\) over all mass elements\, i.e.\, \\\[\\lt\\!s^\{2\}\\!\\gt^\{1/2\}\\;= \\left\(\\sum\\limits_\{i=1\}^\{n\} m_\{i\} \\lt\\!s_\{i\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\!/\\sum\\limits_\{i=1\}^\{n\} m_\{i\} \\right\)^\{1/2\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12192
name: mean-square radius of gyration
def: "For a flexible macromolecule composed of \\\(n\\\) mass elements\, of masses \\\(m_\{i\}\\\) \, \\\(i = 1\, 2\, \\ldots\, n\\\)\, located at statistical-mechanical mean-square distances \\\(\\lt\\!s_\{i\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\\) from the centre of mass\, the mean-square radius of gyration is the mass average of \\\(\\lt\\!s_\{i\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\\) over all mass elements\, i.e.\, \\\[\\lt\\!s^\{2\}\\!\\gt = \\left\(\\sum\\limits_\{i=1\}^\{n\} m_\{i\} \\lt\\!s_\{i\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\!/\\sum\\limits_\{i=1\}^\{n\} m_\{i\} \\right\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12193
name: root-mean-square unperturbed radius of gyration
def: "Root-mean-square radius of gyration of a macromolecule having unperturbed dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12194
name: mean-square unperturbed radius of gyration
def: "Mean-square radius of gyration of a macromolecule having unperturbed dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12195
name: unperturbed end-to-end vector
def: "Vector connecting the two ends of a linear chain in a particular conformation\, with the chain having unperturbed dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12196
name: unperturbed end-to-end distance
def: "Magnitude of the unperturbed end-to-end vector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12197
name: mean-square end-to-end distance
def: "Statistical-mechanical mean-square end-to-end distance of a linear chain averaged over all chain conformations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12198
name: root-mean-square unperturbed end-to-end distance
def: "Root-mean-square end-to-end distance of a chain having unperturbed dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12199
name: mean-square unperturbed end-to-end distance
def: "Mean-square end-to-end distance of a chain having unperturbed dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12200
name: root-mean-square end-to-end distance of a freely rotating chain
def: "Root-mean-square end-to-end distance of a linear freely rotating chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12201
name: mean-square end-to-end distance of a freely rotating chain
def: "Mean-square end-to-end distance of a linear freely rotating chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12202
name: effective bond length
def: "Square root of the ratio of the unperturbed mean-square end-to-end distance of a linear chain to its number of skeletal bonds\, i.e.\, \\\[b = \(\\lt\\!r_\{\\rm\{o\}\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\!/n\)^\{1/2\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12203
name: equivalent freely jointed chain
def: "Hypothetical freely jointed chain with the same mean-square end-to-end distance and contour length as an actual linear chain having unperturbed dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12204
name: Kuhn segment
def: "Segment or link of an equivalent freely jointed chain."
synonym: "equivalent freely jointed link" []
synonym: "statistical segment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12205
name: Kuhn segment length
def: "Length of a Kuhn segment."
synonym: "freely jointed link length" []
synonym: "statistical segment length" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12206
name: self-avoiding random-walk chain
def: "Freely jointed chain subject to the condition that the segments cannot intersect one another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12207
name: macromolecular coil
def: "Depiction of a macromolecule in which its segments are distributed about some point in space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12209
name: chain stiffness
def: "Parameter describing the end-to-end distance of a linear\, single-strand macromolecular chain having unperturbed dimensions relative to that of a model macromolecular chain not subject to short-range interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12212
name: <i>g</i>-factor
def: "Ratio of the mean-square radius of gyration of a branched molecule\, \\\(\\lt\\!s_\{\\rm\{b\}\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\\)\, to that of an otherwise identical linear molecule\, \\\(\\lt\\!s_\{\\rm\{l\}\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\\)\, of the same relative molecular mass in the same solvent at the same temperature\, i.e.\, \\\(g = \\lt\\!s_\{\\rm\{b\}\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\!/\\!\\lt\\!s_\{\\rm\{l\}\}^\{2\}\\!\\gt\\\)."
synonym: "contraction factor" []
synonym: "geometric contraction factor" []
synonym: "radius of gyration contraction factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12213
name: <i>g</i>′-factor
def: "Ratio of the intrinsic viscosity of a branched molecule\, \\\(\[\\eta_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\]\\\)\, to that of an otherwise identical linear molecule\, \\\(\[\\eta_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\]\\\)\, of the same relative molecular mass in the same solvent at the same temperature\, i.e.\, \\\(g^\{\\prime\} = \[\\eta_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\]/\[\\eta_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\]\\\)."
synonym: "hydrodynamic contraction factor" []
synonym: "viscosity contraction factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12214
name: molar mass
def: "Mass divided by amount of substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12215
name: molar-mass average
def: "Any average of the molar mass\, molecular weight\, relative molar mass\, or relative molecular mass for a non-uniform polymer\, with the symbol \\\(\\rm\{k\}\\\) specifying the type of average."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12216
name: number-average molar mass
def: "The equation for number-average molar mass is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{n\}\} = \\frac\{1\}\{\\sum\\limits_\{M\} \(w_\{M\}/M\)\}\\\]\nThe equation for number-average molecular weight  is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{r\,n\}\} = \\frac\{1\}\{\\sum\\limits_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}\(w_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}/M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\)\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12217
name: mass-average molar mass
def: "The equation for mass-average molar mass is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{m\}\} \\equiv M_\{\\rm\{w\}\} = \\sum\\limits_\{M\} w_\{M\}M\\\]\nThe equation for mass-average molecular weight  is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{r\,m\}\} \\equiv \{M_\{\\rm\{r\,w\}\}\} = \\sum\\limits_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\} w_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\} M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12218
name: z-average molar mass
def: "The equation for z-average molar mass is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{z\}\} = \\frac\{\\sum\\limits_\{M\} w_\{M\}M^\{2\}\}\{\\sum\\limits_\{M\} w_\{M\}M\}\\\]\nThe equation for z-average molecular weight is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{r\,z\}\} = \\frac\{\\sum\\limits_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\} w_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}^\{2\}\}\{\\sum\\limits_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\} w_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12219
name: \(z + 1\)-average molar mass
def: "The equation for \(z + 1\)-average molar mass is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{z+1\}\} = \\frac\{\\sum\\limits_\{M\} w_\{M\}M^\{3\}\}\{\\sum\\limits_M w_\{M\}M^\{2\}\}\\\]\nThe equation for \(z + 1\)-average molecular weight is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{r\,z+1\}\} = \\frac\{\\sum\\limits_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\} w_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}M^\{3\}\}\{\\sum\\limits_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\} w_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}M^\{2\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12220
name: viscosity-average molar mass
def: "The equation for viscosity-average molar mass is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{v\}\} = \\left\[\\sum\\limits_\{M\} w_\{M\}M^\{a\} \\right\]^\{1/a\}\\\]\nThe equation for viscosity-average molecular weight is \\\[M_\{\\rm\{r\,v\}\} = \\left\[\\sum\\limits_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\} w_\{M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}^\{a\} \\right\]^\{1/a\}\\\] where \\\(a\\\) is the exponent in the Mark–Houwink equation\, \\\(\[\\eta\] = KM^\{a\}\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12221
name: apparent molar mass
def: "Molar mass\, molecular weight\, relative molar mass\, or relative molecular mass calculated from experimental data without the application of appropriate corrections."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12222
name: cumulative number-distribution function
def: "Cumulative distribution function in which the relative amount of a portion of a substance with values of the random variable or variables less than or equal to a given value or given values is expressed in terms of mole fraction."
synonym: "cumulative number distribution" []
synonym: "integral number distribution" []
synonym: "integral number-distribution function" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12223
name: cumulative mass-distribution function
def: "Cumulative distribution function in which the relative amount of a portion of a substance with values of the random variable or variables less than or equal to a given value or given values is expressed in terms of mass fraction."
synonym: "cumulative mass distribution" []
synonym: "integral mass distribution" []
synonym: "integral mass-distribution function" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12224
name: molar-mass dispersity
def: "Ratio of the mass-average molar mass\, \\\(M_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)\, molecular weight\, relative molar mass\, or relative molecular mass\, \\\(M_\{\\rm\{r\,m\}\}\\\)\, to the number-average molar mass\, \\\(M_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\)\, molecular weight\, relative molar mass\, or relative molecular mass\, \\\(M_\{\\rm\{r\,n\}\}\\\). \\\[Ð\\!_\{\\rm\{M\}\} = M_\{\\rm\{m\}\}/M_\{\\rm\{n\}\} = M_\{\\rm\{r\,m\}\}/M_\{\\rm\{r\,n\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "molecular-weight dispersity" []
synonym: "relative-molar-mass dispersity" []
synonym: "relative-molecular-mass dispersity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12225
name: degree-of-polymerisation dispersity
def: "Ratio of the mass-average degree of polymerisation\, \\\(X_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)\, to the number-average degree of polymerisation\, \\\(X_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\). \\\[Ð\\!_\{\\rm\{X\}\} = X_\{\\rm\{m\}\}/X_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12226
name: dispersity
def: "Ratio of \\\(M_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) to \\\(M_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\)\, or the ratio of \\\(X_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) to \\\(X_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\) for a homopolymer or an alternating copolymer of sufficiently large molar mass\, such that the effects of the distinct structures of the end-groups of the constituent macromolecules can be neglected\, giving \\\(X_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\) directly proportional to \\\(M_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\)\, \\\(X_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) directly proportional to \\\(M_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) and \\\(Ð\\!_\{\\rm\{M\}\} = Ð\\!_\{\\rm\{X\}\} = Ð\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12228
name: semi-dilute solution
def: "Polymer solution in the concentration range at which the domains occupied by the dissolved macromolecules or entities start to overlap."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12229
name: concentrated solution
def: "Polymer solution in the concentration range at which the domains occupied by the dissolved macromolecules or entities overlap to such an extent that it is not possible to distinguish the individual domains from one another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12230
name: entanglement
def: "Entanglement\, involving one or more macromolecular chains\, of duration at least equal to the period of observation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12231
name: mesh size
def: "Average distance between two adjacent entanglements on a given macromolecule in a semi-dilute solution\, a concentrated solution\, or bulk amorphous polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12232
name: blob
def: "Hypothetical spherical volume element occupied by a subchain of a macromolecule between two neighbouring entanglements on the same macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12233
name: scaling law
def: "Equation of the type \\\(y = k x^\{a\}\\\)\, relating two physical quantities\, \\\(x\\\) and \\\(y\\ \(\\rm\{g\}\)\\\)\, in which the value of the exponent \\\(a\\\) can be predicted by theory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12234
name: free volume
def: "Difference between specific volume of an amorphous polymer and that of its perfect crystalline form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12235
name: mean-field theory
def: "Theory reducing the difficult exact evaluation of interactions in a multi-body system to a simpler but approximate evaluation of an average effective interaction of one body with its surroundings."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12236
name: co-nonsolvency
def: "Decrease in solubility of a polymer in a solvent comprising more than one component\, each component of which by itself is a solvent for the polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12237
name: osmometer
def: "Apparatus for measuring osmotic pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12238
name: osmometry
def: "Measurement of the osmotic pressure of a solution using an osmometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12239
name: osmotic virial coefficient
def: "Coefficient in the expansion of the chemical potential of the solvent\, \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, in powers of the mass concentration\, \\\(c\\\)\, of the solute\, i.e.\, \\\[\\mu_\{\\rm\{s\}\} - \\mu_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{\\circ\} = -\\mathit\{\\Pi\} V_\{s\}^\{\\prime\} = -RT V_\{s\}^\{\\prime\} \(A_\{1\}c + A_\{2\}c^\{2\} + A_\{3\}c^\{3\} + \\ldots\)\\\] where \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{\\circ\}\\\) is the chemical potential of the solvent in the reference state at the temperature of the system and ambient pressure\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Pi\}\\\) is the osmotic pressure and \\\(V_\{s\}^\{\\prime\}\\\) is the partial molar volume of the solvent. In solvents comprising more than one component\, the definition applies to any solvent component."
synonym: "virial coefficient of the chemical potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12240
name: binodal
def: "Curve in the isobaric temperature-composition plane for a mixture defining the region of composition and temperature across which a transition occurs from miscibility of the components to conditions where single-phase mixtures are metastable or unstable."
synonym: "binodal curve" []
synonym: "coexistence curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12241
name: cloud point
def: "Point in the isobaric temperature-composition plane of a binary or multicomponent mixture at which a decrease in transparency is observed due to the turbidity caused by phase separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12242
name: cloud-point curve
def: "Curve of temperature vs. composition defined by the cloud points over range of compositions of two substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12243
name: cloud-point temperature
def: "Temperature at a cloud point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12244
name: lower critical solution temperature
def: "Critical temperature below which a mixture is miscible at any composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12245
name: translational diffusion
def: "Process by which the equilibrium statistical distribution of positions of molecules or entities in space is maintained or restored due to their translational Brownian motion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12246
name: translational frictional coefficient
def: "Frictional force\, \\\(F\\\)\, per unit velocity\, \\\(u\\\)\, experienced by an isolated spherical body moving unidirectionally in a viscous isotropic fluid\, i.e.\, \\\(F = fu\\\)."
synonym: "frictional coefficient (in polymer science)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12247
name: translational diffusion coefficient
def: "Proportionality constant relating the flux per unit area\, \\\(J_\{n\}\\\)\, in unidirectional flow of amount\, \\\(n\\\)\, of entities \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) to their concentration gradient in the direction \(\\\(x\\\)\) of flow\, \\\(\{\\rm\{d\}\}c_\{\\ce\{B\}\}/\{\\rm\{d\}\}x\\\)\, i.e.\, \\\(J_\{n\} = -D\{\\rm\{d\}\} c_\{\\ce\{B\}\}/\{\\rm\{d\}\}x\\\)."
synonym: "diffusion coefficient (in polymer science)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12248
name: rotational frictional coefficient
def: "Torque\, \\\(T\\\)\, per unit angular velocity\, \\\(\\omega\\\)\, required for rotation of a body about a given axis in a viscous isotropic fluid\, i.e.\, \\\(T = \\zeta\\omega\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12249
name: equilibrium sedimentation method
def: "Experimental method by which the distribution of the concentration of the solute or dispersed component in a dilute solution or dispersion along the centrifuge cell is measured at sedimentation equilibrium\, and the results are interpreted in terms of the molar masses of the solute species or their distribution functions\, or both."
synonym: "sedimentation-equilibrium method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12250
name: equilibrium sedimentation in a density gradient
def: "Equilibrium sedimentation method using a multicomponent solvent forming a density gradient in a centrifugal field."
synonym: "sedimentation equilibrium in a density gradient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12251
name: Kraemer equation
def: "Equation describing the dependence of the inherent viscosity\, \\\(\\eta_\{\\rm\{inh\}\}\\\)\, of a dilute solution on the mass concentration of the solute\, \\\(c\\\)\, with \\\[\\eta_\{\\rm\{inh\}\} = \(\\ln \\eta_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\)/c = \[\\eta\] + k_\{\\rm\{K\}\}\[\\eta\]^\{2\} c\\\] where \\\(k_\{\\rm\{K\}\}\\\) is the Kraemer coefficient and \\\(\[\\eta\]\\\) is the intrinsic viscosity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12252
name: Kraemer coefficient
def: "Parameter in the Kraemer equation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12253
name: hydrodynamic interaction
def: "In a solution or dispersion\, the perturbation of the flow of the solvent continuum or dispersing medium continuum around a solute molecule\, a segment of a dissolved macromolecule or dispersed entity caused by the presence of the other solute molecules\, segments\, or dispersed entities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12254
name: free-draining
def: "Adjective describing the undisturbed flow of solvent through the domain of a dissolved macromolecule."
synonym: "freely draining" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12255
name: impermeable
def: "Adjective describing the flow behaviour of a macromolecule in solution or a dispersed entity when\, due to hydrodynamic interaction\, the solvent or dispersion medium within the domain of the macromolecule or dispersed entity is essentially immobilised and moves with the dissolved macromolecule or dispersed entity."
synonym: "non-free-draining" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12256
name: partial free-draining
def: "Adjective describing the flow behaviour of a macromolecule in solution or a dispersed entity when\, due to hydrodynamic interaction\, the solvent within the domain of the macromolecule or a dispersed entity becomes progressively more immobilised with respect to the macromolecule or a dispersed entity in the direction from its outer fringes inward."
synonym: "partially draining" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12257
name: equivalent hydrodynamic volume
def: "Volume of the hydrodynamically equivalent sphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12258
name: equivalent hydrodynamic radius
def: "Radius of the hydrodynamically equivalent sphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12259
name: Stokes equation
def: "For an isolated dissolved molecule or dispersed entity\, an equation relating the translational frictional coefficient\, \\\(f\\\)\, to the equivalent hydrodynamic radius in translational diffusive flow\, \\\(r_\{D\}\\\)\, and to the viscosity of the solvent continuum or the dispersion medium continuum\, \\\(η_\{0\}\\\)\, with \\\[f = 6\\uppi \\eta_\{0\}r_\{D\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12260
name: Stokes–Einstein equation
def: "For an isolated dissolved molecule or dispersed entity\, an equation relating the translational diffusion coefficient\, \\\(D\\\)\, to the equivalent hydrodynamic radius in translational diffusive flow\, \\\(r_\{D\}\\\) \, and to the viscosity of the solvent continuum or the dispersion medium continuum\, \\\(\\eta_\{0\}\\\)\, with \\\[D = kT/\(6\\uppi \\eta_\{0\}r_\{D\}\)\\\] where \\\(k\\\) is the Boltzmann constant\, and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12261
name: Perrin equation
def: "For an isolated dissolved molecule or dispersed entity\, an equation relating the rotational diffusion coefficient\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Theta\}\\\)\, to the equivalent hydrodynamic radius in rotational diffusive flow\, \\\(r_\{\\mathit\{\\Theta\}\}\\\)\, and to the viscosity of the solvent continuum or the dispersion medium continuum\, \\\(\\eta_\{0\}\\\)\, with \\\[D = kT/\(6\\pi \\eta_\{\\rm\{0\}\} r_\{\\mathit\{\\Theta\}\}^3\)\\\] where \\\(k\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12262
name: pearl-necklace model
def: "Model describing the flow behaviour of a dissolved chain macromolecule in terms of a freely jointed sequence of beads\, each of which offers hydrodynamic resistance to the flow of the surrounding solvent continuum and is connected to the next bead by a rigid rod that offers no hydrodynamic resistance."
synonym: "bead-rod model" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12263
name: Kirkwood–Riseman theory
def: "Theory\, based on the pearl-necklace model\, that describes the translational diffusive and viscous flows of an isolated linear macromolecule in solution in the theta state and accounts for the gradual change from free-draining behaviour at lower molecular weights to impermeable behaviour at higher molecular weights."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12264
name: Flory–Fox assumption
def: "Assumption that the Kirkwood–Riseman theory can be applied to linear isolated macromolecules in solution\, independent of whether they are in the theta state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12265
name: Flory–Fox equation
def: "Equation relating intrinsic viscosity\, \\\(\\eta\\\)\, and molar mass\, \\\(M\\\)\, to the mean-square radius of gyration\, with \\\[\[\\eta\]M = \\mathit\{\\Phi\}^\{\\prime\}\\!\\lt\\!s^\{2\}\\!\\gt^\{3/2\}\\\] where \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\}^\{\\prime\}\\\) is a parameter the value of which depends on the molar-mass distribution\, macromolecular constitution\, and chain expansion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12266
name: Rouse theory
def: "Molecular theory for treating polymer chain dynamics based on the bead-spring model assuming the beads \(segments\) have zero excluded volume and there is no hydrodynamic interaction between the beads."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12267
name: Rouse chain
def: "Hypothetical polymer chain satisfying the assumptions made in the Rouse theory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12268
name: reptation
def: "Snake-like movement of a polymer chain in a semi-dilute or concentrated solution or bulk amorphous polymer through the hypothetical tube created by neighbouring chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12269
name: tube model
def: "Model to describe the motion of a polymer chain confined in the hypothetical tube created by neighbouring chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12270
name: tube renewal
def: "Dynamic renewal of tubes by the disentanglement and re-entanglement of polymer chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12271
name: primitive chain
def: "Polymer chain allowed to undergo reptation along the tube created by neighbouring chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12272
name: uniform dispersion
def: "Dispersion of particles of identical size\, shape\, and constitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12273
name: non-uniform dispersion
def: "Dispersion of particles not identical with respect to size\, shape\, or constitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12274
name: static light scattering
def: "Experimental method based on the measurement of the time-averaged intensity of light scattered from a polymer solutions or a dispersion of particles at various angles with respect to the incident beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12275
name: dynamic light scattering
def: "Experimental method based on the measurement of the time autocorrelation function of the intensity of light scattered from a polymer solution or a dispersion of particles."
synonym: "photon-correlation spectroscopy (PCS)" []
synonym: "quasi-elastic light scattering (QELS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12276
name: small molecule
def: "Molecule much smaller than the wavelength of the radiation in the solvent used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12277
name: large molecule
def: "Molecule of size similar to or larger than the wavelength of the radiation in the solvent used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12278
name: excess scattering
def: "Difference between the scattering intensity for a dilute solution or a dilute dispersion and that for the pure solvent or the dispersion medium at the same scattering angle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12279
name: static structure factor
def: "Function describing the dependence of relative scattering intensity on the magnitude of the scattering vector\, \\\(q\\\)\, for a polymer solution or a dispersion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12280
name: dynamic structure factor
def: "Function describing the temporal dependence of relative scattering intensity on the magnitude of the scattering vector\, \\\(q\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12281
name: elution time
def: "Time elapsed from the injection of the sample into a chromatography bed to the recording of a specified signal of the detector due to the sample having passed through the bed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12282
name: elution volume
def: "Volume of solvent that has passed through a chromatography bed from the injection of the sample to the recording of a specified signal of the detector due to the sample having passed through the bed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12283
name: transfer coefficient
def: "The anodic transfer coefficient \\\(\\alpha_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) and the cathodic transfer coefficient \\\(\\alpha_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) are defined by the following equations\: \\\[\\alpha_\{\\rm\{a\}\} = \(RT/F\)\(\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\ln\\! j_\{\\rm\{a\}\}/\{\\rm\{d\}\}E\); \\quad \\alpha_\{\\rm\{c\}\} = -\(RT/F\)\(\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\ln\\!\\vert \{j_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\} \\vert /\{\\rm\{d\}\}E\)\\\]\nIn these equations\, \\\(j_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) and \\\(j_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) are the anodic and cathodic current densities\, respectively\, corrected for any changes in the reactant concentration at the electrode surface with respect to its bulk value; incidentally\, the symbols \\\(\\ln\\! j_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\ln\\!\\vert j_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\vert\\\) imply that the argument of the logarithm is of dimension one\, obtained by division with the corresponding unit\, e.g.\, \\\(\\ln\\! j_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) meaning \\\(\\ln\(j_\{\\rm\{a\}\}/\\pu\{A m^\{-2\}\}\)\\\)\, and similarly for the other quantities \\\(\\vert j_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\vert\\\)\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\). \\\(E\\\) is the applied electric potential\, \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, and \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant. In practice\, \\\(\\alpha_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\alpha_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) are defined as the reciprocal of the corresponding Tafel slopes\, \\\(\{\\rm\{d\}\}E/\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\ln\\! j_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) and \\\(-\{\\rm\{d\}\}E/\{\\rm\{d\}\} \\ln\\! \\vert j_\{\\rm\{c\}\} \\vert\\\)\, made dimensionless by the multiplying factor \\\(RT/F\\\). Both the transfer coefficient and the Tafel slope are determined at constant temperature and pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12284
name: combined technique
def: "Application of two or more techniques to different samples at the same time. This can include thermal and nonthermal analytical techniques."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12285
name: controlled-rate thermal analysis
def: "Sample-controlled method where the feedback used to control the heating is the rate of transformation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12286
name: controlled temperature program
def: "Temperature history imposed on the sample during the course of the thermal analysis experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12287
name: cooling curve
def: "Experimental result of measuring the temperature of the sample as a function of time during cooling. Heating curves are obtained for temperature-time experiments during heating."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12288
name: derivative technique
def: "Pertaining to the \\\(1^\{\\rm\{st\}\}\\\) derivative \(mathematical\) of any curve with respect to temperature or time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12289
name: dielectric thermal analysis
def: "Technique where dielectric properties are measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12290
name: differential technique
def: "Pertaining to a difference in measured or measurable quantities usually between a sample and a reference or standard material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12291
name: heat-flow differential scanning calorimetry
def: "Technique where the difference between the heat-flow rates into a sample and a reference material is measured."
synonym: "heat-flow DSC" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12292
name: power compensation differential scanning calorimetry
def: "Technique where the difference between the electrical powers into a sample and a reference material is measured."
synonym: "power compensation DSC" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12293
name: dynamic technique
def: "Indicates\, especially in the mechanical analysis of materials\, that a parameter changes continuously during the experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12294
name: dynamic mechanical analysis
def: "Technique where storage and loss viscoelastic moduli are determined with the help of a periodical stress."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12295
name: gas flow
def: "Passage of gas from one part of the system to another. The gas may be either inert \(carrier gas\) or reactive\, either introduced into the system or evolving from the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12296
name: isothermal technique
def: "Applied to a technique to indicate that the temperature is maintained constant throughout the experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12297
name: material
def: "The substance which is studied and from which the sample is taken."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12298
name: micro- technique
def: "Prefix used to denote that the technique measures small quantities\, either with respect to the amount of sample studied\, or with respect to the change in the properties measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12299
name: modulated technique
def: "Indicates that a parameter changes in a periodic manner during the experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12300
name: modulated temperature technique
def: "Applied to the technique name to indicate that a temperature modulation has been applied to the temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12301
name: photo- technique
def: "Indicates that the experiment involves the illumination of the sample or measures the amount of light emitted from a sample. Where possible\, the wavelength range of the light should be specified."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12302
name: sample controlled technique
def: "Applied to the technique to indicate that a property of the sample is used either continuously or discontinuously to control the sample heating. Without this term\, it is assumed that the experiment is following a controlled-temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12303
name: scan
def: "A term used to describe the data produced from a thermal analysis experiment. More correct usage is a thermal analysis curve\, or\, for a specific technique thermogravimetric curve\, etc."
synonym: "thermal analysis curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12304
name: scanning technique
def: "Indicates that a specified experimental parameter\, if not temperature\, is changed in a controlled manner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12305
name: scanning calorimetry
def: "Techniques where heat is measured as a function of temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12306
name: simultaneous technique
def: "Indicates the measurement of two or more properties of a single sample at the same time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12307
name: static technique
def: "Indicates\, especially in the mechanical analysis of materials\, a constant parameter during the experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12308
name: stepwise technique
def: "Indicates discrete\, discontinuous changes in an experimental parameter\, e.g.\, force\, temperature\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12309
name: tan $\\delta$
def: "The dimensionless ratio of energy lost to energy returned during one cycle of a periodic process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12310
name: temperature-programmed desorption
def: "EGA using an inert atmosphere or vacuum\, in the absence of sample decomposition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12311
name: temperature-programmed oxidation
def: "Experiment using an oxidising atmosphere\, usually oxygen. Oxidation is monitored by any appropriate technique \(EGA\, TGA\, gas sorption\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12312
name: temperature-programmed reduction
def: "Experiment using a reducing atmosphere\, usually hydrogen. Reduction is monitored by any appropriate technique \(EGA\, TGA\, gas sorption\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12313
name: thermal curve
def: "Any graph of any combination of property\, time\, temperature derived from a thermal analysis technique."
synonym: "thermoanalytical curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12314
name: thermally stimulated current
def: "Electrical current observed during heating and caused by the thermally initiated relaxation of the frozen-in electrical polarization of a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12315
name: thermally stimulated depolarisation
def: "Relaxation of frozen-in electrical polarization caused by a temperature increase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12316
name: thermo- technique
def: "Indicating the use of changing temperature during the experiment\, when in the name of a thermal analysis technique."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12317
name: thermoanalytical
def: "Of\, or pertaining to\, thermal analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12318
name: thermodiffractometry
def: "A technique where the X-ray diffraction of the sample is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12319
name: thermogravimetric analysis
def: "A technique where the mass of the sample is measured\, also known as thermogravimetry."
synonym: "thermogravimetry (TG)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12320
name: thermomanometry
def: "A technique where the gas pressure is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12321
name: thermomechanical analysis
def: "A technique where the deformation of the sample is measured under constant load."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12322
name: thermomicroscopy
def: "A technique where the optical properties of the sample are observed and measured through a microscope."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12323
name: thermospectrometry
def: "A group of techniques where a spectrum of the sample is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12324
name: absolute quantitation of proteins
def: "Method for absolute quantitation of proteins via measurements on constituent proteolytic peptides using chemically synthesized isotope-labeled peptides as surrogate internal standards for mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12325
name: accelerating potential
def: "Electrical potential difference used to impart translational energy to ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12326
name: accurate mass
def: "Experimentally determined mass of an ion of known charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12327
name: accurate mass tag
def: "Value of the mass of a peptide from a protein that has been chemically or enzymatically cleaved into fragments. The mass is recorded with sufficiently high accuracy and precision that it can be used in identification of the protein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12328
name: a-ion
def: "Fragment ion containing the peptide \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)-terminus formed upon dissociation of a peptide ion at the peptide backbone \\\(\\ce\{C-C\}\\\) bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12329
name: alkyl cation
def: "Even-electron positively charged saturated hydrocarbon ion containing no aromatic groups\, a special case of carbenium ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12330
name: all-glass heated inlet system
def: "All-glass inlet system for ion sources operating under vacuum that can be heated to about \\\(\\pu\{500^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\) and allows low vapor pressure materials to be introduced into the mass spectrometer without breaking vacuum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12331
name: allyl cation
def: "Even-electron ion formulated as \\\(\\ce\{R^\{1\}R^\{2\}C=CR^\{3\}C+R^\{4\}R^\{5\}\}\\\) and its resonance forms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12332
name: ambient ionization
def: "Desorption of molecules and formation of ions outside the mass spectrometer directly from samples in their native environment with no or minimal sample preparation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12333
name: ambient mass spectrometry
def: "Mass spectrometry analysis performed using an ambient ionization technique."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12334
name: angle resolved mass spectrometry
def: "Technique used to investigate details of the collisional excitation process by studying trends relating scattering angle of the precursor ion\, internal energy transferred\, and the masses of the ion and its collision partner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12335
name: anionized molecule
def: "Ion formed by the association of an anion with a molecule\, \\\(\\ce\{M\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12336
name: atmospheric pressure chemical ionization
def: "Chemical ionization of a sample that is a gas or nebulized liquid\, using an atmospheric pressure corona discharge or beta emitter such as \\\(\\ce\{^\{63\}Ni\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12337
name: atmospheric pressure matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization
def: "Matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization in which the sample target is at atmospheric pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12338
name: atmospheric pressure photoionization
def: "Direct ionization of molecules at atmospheric pressure by electron detachment induced by photons forming \\\(\\ce\{M^\{+.\}\}\\\)\, or atmospheric pressure chemical ionization in which the reactant ions are generated by photoionization of suitable dopant species and subsequent ion/molecule reactions of their molecular ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12339
name: autodetachment
def: "Process whereby a negative ion in a discrete state with energy greater than the detachment threshold loses an electron spontaneously without further interaction with an energy source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12340
name: auxiliary gas
def: "Gas used in a spray ion source in addition to the nebulizing gas to aid in solvent removal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12341
name: average mass
def: "Mass of an ion or molecule weighted for its isotopic composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12342
name: axial ejection
def: "Ejection of an ion from an ion trap in a direction parallel to the longitudinal axis of the trap."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12343
name: axialization
def: "Technique used in Fourier-transform ion cyclotron resonance mass spectrometers to limit the translational motion of the trapped ions so that they occupy a small volume near the trap center\, thus optimizing performance criteria including resolving power."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12344
name: base peak chromatogram
def: "Chromatogram obtained by plotting the signal of the ions represented by the base peak detected in each of a series of mass spectra recorded as a function of retention time."
synonym: "base peak ion chromatogram" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12345
name: beam mass spectrometer
def: "Mass spectrometer in which an ion beam accelerated from the ion source is transmitted through a m/z analyzer\, or analyzers\, to the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12346
name: benzyl ion
def: "Even-electron ion \\\(\\ce\{C7H7^\{+\}\}\\\) with the same carbon skeleton as toluene that has lost a hydrogen atom from the methyl side-chain that carries the positive charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12347
name: b-ion
def: "Fragment ion containing the peptide \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)-terminus formed upon dissociation of a peptide ion at the peptide backbone \\\(\\ce\{C-N\}\\\) bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12348
name: blackbody infrared radiative dissociation
def: "Special case of infrared multiphoton dissociation wherein excitation of the reactant ion is caused by absorption of infrared photons radiating from heated blackbody surroundings\, which are usually the walls of a vacuum chamber."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12349
name: Brubaker lens
def: "Additional set of four short\, cylindrical electrodes\, mounted co-linearly with a transmission quadrupole mass spectrometer that is excited with some fraction of the AC potential but not the DC potential applied to the main quadrupole. The Brubaker lens thus acts as a high-pass filter for ion m/z\, but the resulting delay in application of the DC potential component also results in a reduction in defocusing by fringe fields and thus significantly improves overall ion transmission efficiency at a given mass resolution."
synonym: "Brubaker pre-filter" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12350
name: buffer gas
def: "Inert gas used for collisional reduction of the internal or translational energy of ions\, such as in ion traps."
synonym: "bath gas" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12351
name: centroid acquisition
def: "Procedure of recording mass spectra in which an automated computer-based system detects peaks\, calculates the centroid based on the average m/z value weighted by the intensity\, and assigns m/z values based on a calibration file. Only the centroid m/z value and the peak magnitude are stored."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12352
name: charged residue model
def: "Theoretical model for production of highly charged macromolecules by electrospray ionization in which the excess charges on electrosprayed droplets are transferred to and remain on molecules enclosed within the droplets after solvent evaporation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12353
name: charge inversion reaction
def: "Reaction of an ion with a neutral\, surface\, or ion of opposite polarity\, in which the charge on the product ion is reversed in sign with respect to the reactant ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12354
name: charge-mediated fragmentation
def: "Fragmentation of an ion in which the cleavage of one of its bonds is induced by the charge site. The cleaved bond is adjacent to the apparent charge site\, and its cleavage involves movement of the electron pair forming that bond to the original charge site and thus migration of the charge to the released fragment."
synonym: "heterolytic fragmentation; inductive cleavage" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12355
name: charge remote fragmentation
def: "Fragmentation of an ion in which the cleaved bond is isolated from the apparent charge site by a distance of several bond lengths."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12356
name: charge site derivatization
def: "Chemical derivatization of molecules to introduce a fixed charge site\, thus improving ionization efficiency by electrospray ionization or matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization and directing subsequent ion fragmentation in a manner that facilitates structure identification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12357
name: c-ion
def: "Fragment ion containing the peptide \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)-terminus formed upon dissociation of a peptide ion at the peptide backbone \\\(\\ce\{N-C\}\\\) bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12358
name: coaxial reflectron
def: "Reflectron that is arranged such that its main axis is coaxial with the ion source and first stage of the time-of-flight analyzer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12359
name: collector slit
def: "Slit installed in a magnetic sector mass spectrometer to allow only those m/z separated ions with a specific m/z into the detector system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12360
name: collisional focusing
def: "Effect where collisions with a buffer gas in a radio-frequency ion trap or transmission quadrupole causes ions to lose translational energy and concentrate at the center of the trap or axis of the linear quadrupole. The effect increases with gas pressure up to a point where ion scattering becomes the dominant process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12361
name: collision cell
def: "Chamber in the ion path between m/z separation elements\, or between ion source acceleration region and the first analyzer\, in tandem mass spectrometry in space configurations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12362
name: collision gas
def: "Inert gas used for collisional excitation or a reactive gas used for ion/molecule reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12363
name: collision quadrupole
def: "Transmission quadrupole to which an oscillating radio frequency potential is applied so as to focus a beam of ions through a collision gas or buffer gas with no m/z separation other than low m/z cut-off."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12364
name: collision reaction cell
def: "Collision cell for removal of interfering ions by ion/neutral reactions in inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12365
name: concentric nebulizer
def: "Pneumatic nebulizer in which the liquid flows through a central capillary and dispersion gas flows through a surrounding outer tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12366
name: consecutive reaction monitoring
def: "Multiple-stage mass spectrometry experiment with three or more stages of m/z separation in which products of sequential fragmentation or bimolecular reactions are selected for detection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12367
name: constant neutral mass gain spectrum
def: "Spectrum formed of all precursor ions that have undergone a selected m/z increase corresponding to the mass of a particular neutral species divided by the charge number of the precursor. The ions detected are the product ions produced by gain of a preselected neutral species mass following ion/molecule reactions of the precursor ions with a reactant gas in a collision cell."
synonym: "fixed neutral gain spectrum" []
synonym: "fixed neutral mass gain spectrum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12368
name: constant neutral mass loss spectrum
def: "Spectrum of all precursor ions that have undergone a selected m/z decrement corresponding to the mass of a particular neutral divided by the charge number of the precursor. The ions detected are the product ions produced by loss of a preselected neutral mass following collision-induced dissociation of the precursor ions in a collision cell."
synonym: "fixed neutral loss spectrum" []
synonym: "fixed neutral mass loss spectrum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12369
name: continuous dynode particle multiplier
def: "Ion-to-electron detector in which the ion strikes the inner surface of a continuous tube device and induces the production of secondary electrons that\, in turn\, impinge on the inner surfaces of the device to produce more secondary electrons. This avalanche effect produces an increase in the final measured current pulse."
synonym: "channel electron multiplier" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12370
name: continuous-flow fast atom bombardment
def: "Variant of fast atom bombardment in which the mixture of analyte and liquid matrix is continuously supplied to the sample probe tip."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12371
name: continuous-flow matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization
def: "Variant of matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization in which the analyte is dissolved in a liquid matrix that is continuously supplied to the sample probe tip."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12372
name: conversion dynode
def: "Surface that is held at high potential so that ions striking the surface produce secondary charged particles that are subsequently detected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12373
name: corona discharge
def: "Electric discharge generated in a gas\, usually at atmospheric pressure\, surrounding a conductor carrying a sufficiently high potential that the electric field around it ionizes the gas so as to form a plasma in the immediate vicinity of the conductor without causing complete electric breakdown or arcing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12374
name: corona discharge ionization
def: "Formation of ions in a corona discharge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12375
name: counter-current gas
def: "Stream of inert gas\, often heated\, that is used in an electrospray or other spray ionization source to aid in the evaporation of solvent from the sprayed particles. The flow of the gas is in the direction opposite to that of the spray."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12376
name: counter electrode
def: "Counter electrode in an electrospray ionization ion source is one of two electrodes between which the high electric potential is applied\, the other electrode being the electrospray needle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12377
name: cross-flow nebulizer
def: "Pneumatic nebulizer in which the dispersion gas flows at an angle\, usually \\\(\\pu\{90\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\)\, with respect to the column of liquid emerging from a capillary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12378
name: curved field reflectron
def: "Reflectron designed to provide a decelerating field strength that varies with depth of ion penetration and thus with ion translational energy\, permitting energy focusing of ions with a range of translational energies such as those formed as product ions from post-source decay of metastable ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12379
name: cyclotron motion
def: "Circular motion of a charged particle moving perpendicularly to the direction of a magnetic field that results from the Lorentz force."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12380
name: Daly detector
def: "Detector consisting of an off-axis conversion dynode\, scintillator\, and photomultiplier. The dynode at high potential emits secondary electrons when ions impinge on the surface. The secondary electrons are accelerated onto the scintillator that produces light that is then detected by the photomultiplier detector\, which can be outside the vacuum system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12381
name: data-dependent acquisition
def: "Mode of data collection in tandem mass spectrometry in which a fixed number of precursor ions whose m/z values were recorded in a survey scan are selected using predetermined rules and are subjected to a second stage of mass selection in an MS/MS analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12382
name: deconvoluted mass spectrum
def: "Mass spectrum processed with an algorithm designed to extract a desired signal or signals from raw experimental data in which the desired signals have been complicated \(convolved\) by some interferences or in some other way."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12383
name: delayed extraction
def: "Application of the accelerating voltage pulse after a time delay in desorption ionization from a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12384
name: deprotonated molecule
def: "Ion formed by the removal of a proton from a molecule \\\(\\ce\{M\}\\\) to produce an anion represented as \\\(\\ce\{\[M - H\]^\{-\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12385
name: desorption chemical ionization
def: "Formation of gas-phase ions by chemical ionization of gaseous molecules produced by the rapid heating and desorption of a solid or low vapour pressure liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12386
name: desorption ionization
def: "Formation of gas phase ions from a solid or liquid sample surface upon activation by heat\, strong electric field\, particles\, droplets\, or photon bombardment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12387
name: desorption ionization on silicon
def: "Soft ionization alternative to matrix-assisted desorption/ionization involving laser desorption ionization of a sample deposited on a porous silicon surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12388
name: diagnostic ion
def: "Product ion whose formation reveals structural or compositional information about its precursor ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12389
name: dielectric barrier discharge ionization
def: "Atmospheric pressure chemical ionization in which the low-temperature plasma is not generated by a corona discharge but by a device consisting of two electrodes with at least one dielectric layer that separates the electrode from the plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12390
name: d-ion
def: "High-energy fragment ion of a protonated peptide\, formally equivalent to a fragment ion formed by homolytic cleavage of a peptide \\\(\\ce\{C-N\}\\\) bond together with the part of the \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)-terminal side-chain starting at the \\\(\\ce\{\\upgamma\\mbox\{-\}carbon\}\\\) \(if any\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12391
name: direct exposure probe
def: "Device based on a modification of a direct insertion probe for exposing an involatile sample directly to the electron beam \(in an electron ionization source\) or to a chemical ionization plasma as in desorption chemical ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12392
name: direct infusion
def: "Method of liquid sample introduction in which the sample is continuously flowed into a mass spectrometer ion source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12393
name: direct insertion probe
def: "Device for introducing a single sample of a solid or liquid\, usually contained in a quartz or other nonreactive sample holder\, into a mass spectrometer ion source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12394
name: direct liquid introduction
def: "Direct delivery of a liquid sample at a low solvent flow rate into the chemical ionization ion source of a mass spectrometer. The solvent vapour can act as the reagent gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12395
name: discrete dynode particle multiplier
def: "Ion-to-electron detector in which the ion strikes the surface of an electrode and induces the production of secondary electrons. In turn\, these impinge in sequence on dynodes at a more positive electric potential to produce more secondary electrons. This avalanche effect produces an amplification of several orders of magnitude in the final measured current pulse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12396
name: dissociative electron capture
def: "Ionization mechanism in which capture of a low-energy electron by a molecule leads directly to a dissociative state of the nascent anion\, an example of dissociative ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12397
name: drift tube
def: "Cylindrical chamber containing a buffer gas. Ions introduced at one end are caused to move to the other end by either a flow of the buffer gas or by a uniform longitudinal electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12398
name: drying gas
def: "Inert gas that is used to promote the removal of solvent from aerosol particles in spray ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12399
name: dynamic exclusion
def: "Software method used to minimize repeat selections of identical precursor ions for collision-induced dissociation in replicate chromatography-tandem mass spectrometry analyses of complex mixtures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12400
name: einzel lens
def: "Three-element charged particle lens in which the first and third elements are maintained at the same electric potential. Such a lens produces focusing without changing the translational energy of the particle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12401
name: electrohydrodynamic ionization
def: "Ionization process that produces charged droplets and gas-phase ions from a liquid under vacuum with the application of an electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12402
name: electron capture dissociation
def: "Process in which multiply protonated molecules interact with low-energy electrons followed by fragmentation. The capture of an electron by the even-electron cation \\\(\\ce\{\[M + nH\]^\{n+\}\}\\\) liberates energy and reduces its charge state\, thus producing the corresponding \\\(\\ce\{\[M + nH\]^\{\(n - 1\)+.\}\}\\\) odd-electron ion\, which readily fragments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12403
name: electron capture ionization
def: "Ionization of a gas-phase atom or molecule by attachment of a free electron to form molecular ions of the \\\(\\ce\{M^\{-.\}\}\\\) type."
synonym: "electron capture negative ionization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12404
name: electrospray needle
def: "Narrow bore tube from which highly charged droplets are emitted in electrospray ionization."
synonym: "electrospray emitter" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12405
name: electrostatic energy analyser
def: "Device consisting of pairs of parallel conducting plates\, concentric cylinders\, or concentric spheres that separate charged particles according to their ratio of translational energy to charge by means of a potential difference applied between the pair."
synonym: "electric sector" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12406
name: even-electron rule
def: "Rule that states that even-electron ions tend to form even-electron fragment ions whereas odd-electron ions tend to dissociate to form either odd- or even-electron ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12407
name: exact mass
def: "Calculated mass of an ion or molecule with specified isotopic composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12408
name: extracted ion chromatogram
def: "Chromatogram created by plotting the intensity of the signal observed at a chosen m/z value or set of values in a series of mass spectra recorded as a function of retention time."
synonym: "mass chromatogram" []
synonym: "reconstructed ion chromatogram" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12409
name: extracted ion electropherogram
def: "Electropherogram created by plotting the intensity of the signal observed at a chosen m/z value or set of values in a series of mass spectra recorded as a function of electrophoresis time."
synonym: "extracted ion electrophorogram" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12410
name: extracted ion profile
def: "Any plot of signal intensity observed at a chosen m/z value or set of values in a series of mass spectra recorded as a function of time\, including extracted ion chromatogram\, extracted ion electropherogram\, or from flow injection analysis or any other time-dependent sampling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12411
name: fast ion bombardment
def: "Ionization of any species by the interaction of a focused beam of ions\, with a translational energy of several thousand \\\(\\pu\{eV\}\\\)\, with a solid or liquid sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12412
name: field-free region
def: "Section of a mass spectrometer in which there are no electric or magnetic fields."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12413
name: first stability region
def: "Stability region of a Mathieu stability diagram closest to the origin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12414
name: fixed precursor ion spectrum
def: "Spectrum obtained when data are acquired that determine the m/z values of product ions produced by the reaction of a selected precursor ion with a user-specified m/z value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12415
name: flow injection mass spectrometry
def: "Method of flow injection analysis in which an aliquot of sample is injected\, without chromatographic separation\, into a liquid carrier stream for on-line mass spectrometry."
synonym: "flow injection analysis mass spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12416
name: focal plane detector
def: "Detector for m/z spatially dispersed ion beams in which all ions simultaneously impinge on the detector plane to produce spatially defined signals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12417
name: forward geometry
def: "Double-focusing mass spectrometer configuration in which the ion beam traverses the electric sector before the magnetic sector."
synonym: "normal geometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12418
name: forward library search
def: "Procedure of comparing a mass spectrum of an unknown compound with a mass spectral library so that the unknown spectrum is compared in turn with the library spectra\, considering only the m/z peaks that have intensities greater than a specific threshold in the unknown."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12419
name: Fourier-transform mass spectrometry
def: "Mass spectrometry technique in which m/z values are represented by frequencies of ion motion and mass spectra are generated by Fourier transform mathematical operations from time domain transients produced by image current detection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12420
name: fringe field
def: "Electric or magnetic field that extends from the edge of an electric sector\, magnetic sector\, lens\, or other ion optics element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12421
name: gas chromatography-mass spectrometry
def: "Technique by which a mixture is separated into individual components by gas chromatography\, followed by detection with a mass spectrometer."
synonym: "gas chromatography/mass spectrometry (GC/MS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12422
name: glow discharge ionization
def: "Formation of ions from gaseous atoms or molecules or from the vapor of a solid sample at the cathode by applying a potential difference between two electrodes immersed in a low-pressure inert gas environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12423
name: gridless reflectron
def: "Reflectron design in which the decelerating/accelerating field is produced without the use of wire-mesh grids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12424
name: hard ionization
def: "Formation of gas-phase ions accompanied by extensive fragmentation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12425
name: high-field asymmetric waveform ion mobility spectrometry
def: "Separation of ions at atmospheric pressure between two electrodes due to simultaneous application of a high potential asymmetric waveform\, whereby ions migrate towards one of the two electrodes depending on the ratio of the high- to low-field mobility of the ion. A variable compensating DC potential is used to selectively transmit ions of interest."
synonym: "RF-DC ion mobility spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12426
name: hybrid mass spectrometer
def: "Mass spectrometer that combines m/z analyzers of different types to perform tandem mass spectrometry or higher-order \\\(\{\\rm\{MS\}\}^\{n\}\\\) experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12427
name: hydrogen/deuterium exchange
def: "Exchange of hydrogen atoms with deuterium atoms in a chemical species in solution prior to introduction into a mass spectrometer\, or by ion/molecule reaction with a neutral gas inside a mass spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12428
name: hydrogen shifts
def: "Sigmatropic reactions of ions in which the \\\(\\ce\{\\upsigma\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}\\\) broken and formed involve hydrogen atoms\, and are subject to the Woodward–Hofmann rules resulting from orbital symmetry considerations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12429
name: hyphenated mass spectrometry technique
def: "Analytical technique in which mass spectrometry is interfaced with a pretreatment step\, most often chromatographic separation but many other combinations are possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12430
name: image current detection
def: "Method of ion detection by measuring the current that results from the image charge induced by a coherent motion of ions passing near a conductor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12431
name: imaging mass spectrometry
def: "Procedure used to form chemically selective images of an object based on the mass spectrometric detection of ions desorbed from its surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12432
name: iminium ion
def: "Cation with the structure \\\(\\ce\{R2C=N^\{+\}R2\}\\\)."
synonym: "alkylideneaminylium ion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12433
name: infrared multiphoton dissociation
def: "Dissociation of a reactant ion as a result of the absorption of multiple infrared photons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12434
name: in-source collision-induced dissociation
def: "Dissociation of an ion as a result of collisional excitation during ion transfer from an atmospheric pressure ion source to the vacuum chamber of the mass spectrometer."
synonym: "in-source collisionally activated dissociation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12435
name: internal fragment
def: "Fragment ion of a peptide ion precursor formed by a double backbone cleavage that contains neither the original \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)- or \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)-terminal residues. Typically a combination of b- and y-type cleavages or occasionally an a- and y-type combination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12436
name: ion desolvation
def: "Removal of solvent molecules clustered around a gas-phase ion by means of heating and/or collisions with gas molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12437
name: ion enhancement
def: "Phenomenon in which the ionization efficiency of a compound is increased by the presence of another compound."
synonym: "ionization enhancement" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12438
name: ion evaporation model
def: "Mechanism for production of gas-phase ions from highly charged fine liquid droplets as in electrospray ionization that involves direct ejection of the ions from the droplet surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12439
name: ion funnel
def: "Series of stacked ring electrodes with progressively decreasing inner diameter to which a combined radio frequency and fixed potential is applied. The resulting field focuses ions travelling along the central axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12440
name: ion gate
def: "Set of plates or grid of wires in an ion mobility spectrometer\, time-of-flight mass spectrometer\, or other mass spectrometer that is used to apply a pulsed electric field with the purpose of selectively deflecting charged particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12441
name: ion/ion reaction
def: "Reaction between two ions\, generally of opposite polarity."
synonym: "ion-ion reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12442
name: ion mobility spectrometry
def: "Separation of ions according to their velocity through a buffer gas under the influence of an electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12443
name: ion/neutral complex
def: "Weakly bound species formed from an ion and neutral."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12444
name: ion optics
def: "Devices designed to control the formation\, focusing\, and deflection of charged particle beams in a vacuum under the influence of electric and magnetic fields."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12445
name: ion suppression
def: "Phenomenon in which the ionization efficiency of a species is lowered by the presence of a different species."
synonym: "ionization suppression" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12446
name: ion-to-photon detector
def: "Detector in which ions strike a conversion dynode to produce electrons that in turn strike a phosphor and the resulting photons are detected by a photomultiplier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12447
name: ion trap
def: "Device for spatially confining ions using electric and magnetic fields alone or in combination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12448
name: isobaric tag for relative and absolute quantitation
def: "Chemical labeling reagents used for relative or absolute quantitation in proteomics\, based on covalent labeling of the \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)-terminus and side-chain amines of peptides from protein digestions with tags of varying mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12449
name: isotope cluster
def: "Group of peaks representing ions of the same elemental composition\, but different isotopic compositions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12450
name: isotope coded affinity tag
def: "Chemical labeling reagents used for relative quantitation in proteomics\, consisting of a reactive group capable of labeling a defined amino acid side-chain\, an isotopically coded linker\, and a tag for the affinity isolation of labeled proteins and peptides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12451
name: isotope dilution mass spectrometry
def: "Quantitative mass spectrometry technique in which an isotopically enriched compound is used as a surrogate internal standard."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12452
name: isotope ratio
def: "Ratio of the number of atoms of one isotope to the number of atoms of another isotope of the same chemical element in the same system."
synonym: "isotope–number ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12453
name: isotope ratio mass spectrometry
def: "Measurement and study of the relative abundances of the different isotopes of an element in a material using a mass spectrometer."
synonym: "stable isotope mass spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12454
name: isotopolog ions
def: "Ions that differ only in the isotopic composition of one or more of the constituent atoms."
synonym: "isotopic homolog ions" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12455
name: isotopomeric ion
def: "Isomeric ions having the same numbers of each isotopic atom but differing in their positions within the ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12456
name: jet separator
def: "Gas chromatograph-to-mass spectrometer interface where the gas chromatography effluent expands into a lower-pressure region and then through a downstream orifice. The low mass carrier gas molecules diffuse from the jet axis\, leaving the sample stream enriched in the heavier analyte molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12457
name: kinetic energy release
def: "Translational energy of fragments resulting from dissociation of a metastable ion measured relative to the center-of-mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12458
name: kinetic energy release distribution
def: "Distribution of values of translational energies in the center-of-mass reference for an ensemble of metastable ions undergoing a specific dissociation reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12459
name: kinetic shift
def: "Excess energy above the thermochemical threshold required to observe dissociation of a precursor ion within the timeframe of a particular mass spectrometry experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12460
name: Kingdon trap
def: "Ion trapping device that consists of an outer barrel-like electrode and a coaxial inner spindle-like electrode that form an electrostatic field with a quadro-logarithmic potential distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12461
name: label-free quantitation
def: "Method for determining the concentration of proteins through a combination of protein digestion and liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry \(LC-MS\) without relying on stable isotope labeling. Protein quantification is achieved by measuring the signal intensity of peptide ions corresponding to a protein or by counting and comparing the number of fragment mass spectra identifying peptides of a given protein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12462
name: laser desorption
def: "Formation of gas-phase species from a solid or liquid sample surface upon activation using a short pulse of photons from a laser."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12463
name: laser desorption ionization
def: "Formation of gas-phase ions by the interaction of a laser with a solid or liquid material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12464
name: laser microprobe mass spectrometry
def: "Laser desorption ionization mass spectrometry\, particularly emphasizing spatially resolved composition information."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12465
name: linear ion trap
def: "Two-dimensional Paul ion trap in which ions are confined in the axial dimension by means of a static electric potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12466
name: liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry
def: "Technique by which a mixture of analytes is separated into individual components by liquid chromatography \(typically high-performance liquid chromatography\)\, followed by detection with a mass spectrometer."
synonym: "liquid chromatography/mass spectrometry (LC/MS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12467
name: liquid ion evaporation
def: "Spray ionization interface in which droplets are dispersed from a pneumatic nebulizer toward a high potential electrode that produces a static charge on the droplets leading to ion formation after solvent evaporation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12468
name: liquid junction interface
def: "Means of coupling capillary electrophoresis to a mass spectrometer in which a liquid reservoir surrounds the separation capillary and transfer capillary to the mass spectrometer and provides electric contact for the capillary electrophoresis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12469
name: liquid secondary ionization
def: "Ionization of any species by the interaction of a focused beam of ions or atoms with a sample that is dissolved in a solvent matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12470
name: liquid sheath
def: "Flow of make-up liquid in a sheath flow interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12471
name: lock mass
def: "Mass of an ion of known m/z value\, derived from a suitable standard compound introduced into an ion source together with the sample to be analyzed\, which permits real-time recalibration by correction of m/z shifts arising from instrumental drift."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12472
name: low-energy collision-induced dissociation
def: "Collision-induced dissociation process wherein the precursor ion has laboratory-frame translational energy lower than a given value\, often taken to be \\\(\\pu\{1 keV\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12473
name: magnetic sector
def: "Device that produces a magnetic field perpendicular to a charged particle beam that deflects the beam to an extent that is proportional to the ratio of particle momentum and charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12474
name: magnetron motion
def: "Slow rotation of the center of cyclotron motion around the central axis of a Penning ion trap that results from a drift of the cyclotron centre along the electric equipotential lines perpendicular to the magnetic field direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12475
name: make-up liquid
def: "Solvent that is mixed with the sample flow in a liquid introduction mass spectrometry interface in order to adjust the flow rate or solvent characteristics of the system\, for example\, to promote ionization efficiency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12476
name: mass-analyzed ion kinetic energy spectrometry
def: "Procedure by which translational energy spectra are obtained from a sector mass spectrometer that incorporates at least one magnetic sector plus one electric sector in reverse geometry \(a magnetic sector followed by an electric sector\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12477
name: mass calibration
def: "Means of determining m/z values of ions from experimentally detected signals using a theoretical or empirical relational equation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12478
name: mass defect
def: "Difference between the nominal mass and the monoisotopic mass of an atom\, molecule\, or ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12479
name: mass discrimination
def: "Differences in overall detection sensitivities for ions of different m/z values in a mass spectrum\, caused by variations in ionization efficiency\, transmission efficiency through the interface between the ion source and the analyzer vacuum system\, analyzer\, and detector response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12480
name: mass gate
def: "Ion gate used to transmit ions in a given m/z range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12481
name: mass limit
def: "Value of m/z above or below which ions cannot be detected in a mass spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12482
name: mass selective axial ejection
def: "Use of mass selective instability to eject ions of selected m/z values from an ion trap along the trap axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12483
name: mass selective instability
def: "Unstable trajectories in an ion trap for ions within a particular range of m/z values caused by an appropriate combination of oscillating electric fields."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12484
name: mass spectral library
def: "Collection of mass spectra of different compounds\, usually represented as arrays of signal intensity vs. the m/z values."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12485
name: mass spectrometry/mass spectrometry
def: "Acquisition and study of the spectra of the product ions or precursor ions of m/z selected ions\, or of precursor ions of a selected neutral mass loss."
synonym: "tandem mass spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12486
name: Mathieu stability diagram
def: "Graphical representation expressed in terms of dimensionless reduced coordinates that describes the stability or instability of charged particle motion in a transmission quadrupole mass spectrometer or Paul ion trap\, based on an appropriate form of the Mathieu differential equation."
synonym: "stability diagram" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12487
name: membrane introduction mass spectrometry
def: "Mass spectrometry measurement that uses a semi-permeable membrane separator that permits the passage of analytes directly from solutions or ambient air to the mass spectrometer ion source."
synonym: "membrane inlet mass spectrometry" []
synonym: "membrane interface mass spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12488
name: membrane separator
def: "GC-MS interface with a polymer membrane that selectively passes analyte molecules in preference to the carrier gas thereby enriching the analyte in the gas stream."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12489
name: microchannel plate
def: "Thin plate that contains a closely spaced array of channels each of which acts as a continuous dynode particle multiplier. A fast charged or neutral particle\, or a photon\, that strikes the plate causes a cascade of secondary electrons that ultimately exits the opposite side of the plate."
synonym: "channel electron multiplier array" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12490
name: microelectrospray
def: "Electrospray ionization at a low solvent flow rate\, typically several microliters per minute \(\\\(\\pu\{\\upmu L min-1\}\\\)\) or lower\, where a pump is used to provide the sample flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12491
name: molecular beam mass spectrometry
def: "Mass spectrometry measurement in which the sample is introduced into the ion source of the mass spectrometer as a collimated beam of molecules with a narrow velocity distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12492
name: molecular effusion separator
def: "GC-MS interface that subjects the GC effluent to effusive flow through a porous glass tube. The lighter carrier gas passes out of the tube\, leaving the gas within the tube enriched in analyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12493
name: momentum separator
def: "Mass spectrometer interface to inlets including chromatography and spray-based ion sources that enriches the sample by selecting the central part of the effluent or spray using an orifice or skimmer. Particles with a higher forward momentum diverge less from the spray axis for a given radial momentum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12494
name: monodisperse aerosol generating interface for chromatography
def: "Particle beam interface that incorporates a monodisperse aerosol generator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12495
name: monoisotopic mass
def: "Exact mass of an ion or molecule calculated using the mass of the most abundant isotope of each element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12496
name: moving belt interface
def: "Liquid sample mass spectrometer interface that uses two or more pulleys and a continuous loop of material. Samples are sprayed or deposited from solution onto the belt where they are transported into the vacuum system for vaporization or desorption and ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12497
name: MS/MS spectrum
def: "Mass spectrum obtained using tandem mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12498
name: MS<sup><i>n</i></sup>
def: "Symbol for multiple-stage mass spectrometry experiments designed to record product ion spectra where \\\(n\\\) is the number of product ion stages \(nth-generation product ions\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12499
name: multicollector mass spectrometer
def: "Mass spectrograph based on a double-focusing magnetic analyzer that spatially disperses ions of different m/z values on to an array of Faraday cup detectors\, generally used with inductively coupled plasma ion sources for isotopic composition measurements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12500
name: multiple reaction monitoring
def: "Application of selected reaction monitoring to multiple product ions from one or more precursor ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12501
name: multiple-stage mass spectrometry
def: "Multiple stages of precursor ion m/z selection followed by product ion detection for successive nth-generation product ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12502
name: nanoelectrospray
def: "Electrospray ionization at a very low solvent flow rate\, typically hundreds of nanoliters per minute of sample solution or lower\, often without the use of an external solvent delivery system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12503
name: negative ion chemical ionization
def: "Chemical ionization that results in the formation of negative ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12504
name: neutralization-reionization mass spectrometry
def: "Procedure by which neutral species are formed from m/z selected ions by charge transfer to a collision gas or by dissociation. The neutral species are separated from the remaining ions and ionized through collisions with a second gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12505
name: neutral loss
def: "Loss of an uncharged species from an ion during dissociation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12506
name: neutral loss scan
def: "Scan used to generate a constant neutral loss spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12507
name: nitrogen rule
def: "Rule stating that a neutral organic molecule containing one or more atoms of the elements \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{H\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{O\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{S\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{P\}\\\)\, or any halogen has an odd nominal mass if it contains an odd number of nitrogen atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12508
name: nozzle-skimmer dissociation
def: "Collisionally activated dissociation that is caused by collisions in the region between the nozzle and skimmer of an atmospheric pressure ionization source such as electrospray or atmospheric pressure chemical ionization."
synonym: "in-source collision-induced dissociation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12509
name: nozzle-skimmer voltage
def: "Electric potential difference applied between the electrospray needle and the skimmer\, which is typically held at the same potential as the counter electrode."
synonym: "needle voltage" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12510
name: <i>n</i><sup>th</sup>-generation product ion
def: "Serial product ions from dissociation or bimolecular reaction of selected precursor ions where \\\(n\\\) refers to the number of stages of dissociation or bimolecular reaction in a series of consecutive reactions that involve successive product ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12511
name: <i>n</i><sup>th</sup>-generation product ion spectrum
def: "Mass spectrum recorded from any mass spectrometer in which the appropriate scan function can be set to record the appropriate generation product ion or ions of m/z selected precursor ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12512
name: orthogonal electrospray
def: "Configuration for electrospray ionization in which the axis of the spray is at a right angle to the axis of the sampling cone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12513
name: orthogonal extraction
def: "Pulsed acceleration of ions perpendicular to their original direction of travel into a mass spectrometer\, typically time-of-flight."
synonym: "orthogonal acceleration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12514
name: partial isotopic scrambling
def: "Partial random mixing of isotopes among specified atom positions in an ion or neutral species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12515
name: particle beam interface
def: "Method for coupling liquid chromatography to mass spectrometry in which the effluent is passed through a heated capillary to form an expanding jet of vapour and aerosol particles. After passing through a skimmer that acts as a momentum separator\, the beam impinges on a heated surface to form ions through chemical ionization at the surface or ionization of the resulting vapour in a chemical ionization or electron ionization source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12516
name: Paul ion trap
def: "Ion trapping device that depends on the application of radio frequency potentials between a ring electrode and two end-cap electrodes to confine the ion motion to a cyclic path described by an appropriate form of the Mathieu equation. The choice of these potentials determines the m/z value below which ions are not trapped."
synonym: "quadrupole ion storage trap" []
synonym: "quadrupole ion trap" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12517
name: peak matching
def: "Procedure for measuring the accurate mass of an ion using scanning mass spectrometers\, in which the peak corresponding to the unknown ion and that for a reference ion of known m/z are displayed alternately and caused to overlap by adjusting appropriate electric fields."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12518
name: peak parking
def: "Method used to extend the amount of time available for mass spectrometric analysis of the components in a narrow chromatographic peak. The eluent flow rate is reduced to an ultra-low level at the leading edge of the peak and held at this low flow rate for the duration of compound elution\, thus enabling collection of sufficient data for reliable statistical analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12519
name: peak stripping
def: "Data analysis technique used in elemental mass spectrometry to correct the intensity of an unresolved multiplet peak monitored for a specific element for contributions from a multi-isotopic contaminant. The correction is calculated from the measured intensity of a different isotopic peak of the contaminant and the known natural isotopic abundance ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12520
name: Penning ionization
def: "Ionization via electron removal that occurs through the energy transfer from a neutral species \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\) internally excited to a metastable state \(such as a high Rydberg state\) to a neutral species \\\(\\ce\{M\}\\\) with lower ionization energy\, as represented by the following reaction\: \\\(\\ce\{N^\{\\ast\} + M -> N + M^\{+.\} + \\textit\{e\}^\{-\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12521
name: Penning ion trap
def: "Ion trapping device using a static magnetic field and a static electric potential well. The magnetic field confines the ion motion to circular orbits around the magnetic force line direction\, which aligns with the direction of the trap axis\, whereas the electric potential well confines the ion motion axially."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12522
name: peptide mass fingerprinting
def: "Method for protein analysis where an unknown protein is chemically or enzymatically cleaved into peptide fragments whose masses are determined by mass spectrometry. The peptide masses are compared to peptide masses calculated for known proteins in a database and analyzed statistically to determine the best match."
synonym: "mass mapping" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12523
name: peptide sequence tag
def: "Sequence of peptide ion fragment masses that can be used to aid in the identification of the amino acid sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12524
name: photodissociation
def: "Process wherein the reactant ion or molecule is dissociated as a result of absorption of one or more photons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12525
name: photographic plate recording
def: "Recording of ion currents by allowing them to strike a photographic plate\, which is subsequently developed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12526
name: $\\boldsymbol\{\\pi\}$/n rad magnetic sector
def: "Arrangement in which an ion beam is deflected magnetically through \\\(\\pi/n\\ \\rm\{rad\}\\\)\, where \\\(n \\gt 1\\\)."
synonym: "180º/<em>n</em> magnetic sector" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12527
name: pneumatically assisted electrospray ionization
def: "Electrospray ionization in which the nebulization of the liquid stream is assisted by a concentric stream of gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12528
name: point detector
def: "Detector in which the ion beam is focused onto a small area\, for example\, defined by a collector slit\, rather than dispersed by m/z spatially across a focal plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12529
name: post-acceleration detector
def: "Detector in which a high potential is applied after m/z separation to accelerate the ions and produce an improved signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12530
name: post-source decay
def: "Technique specific to reflectron time-of-flight mass spectrometers where product ions of metastable transitions or collision-induced dissociations generated in the flight tube prior to entering the reflectron are separated according to m/z to yield product ion spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12531
name: precursor ion spectrum
def: "Mass spectrum in which the appropriate m/z separation function records the precursor ions of selected product ions or specified neutral loss."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12532
name: pre-ionization state
def: "Electronic state capable of undergoing autoionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12533
name: probability-based matching
def: "Automated system for computer examination of the mass spectrum of an unknown mixture for the presence of a specific compound for which a standard spectrum is available in a library. The probability that the compound is present is given by a confidence index \\\(K\\\) such that \\\(2^\{K\}\\\) is the average number of randomly selected compounds whose mass spectra would have to be examined to find data which match the target spectrum to the same degree as does the unknown."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12534
name: product ion analysis
def: "Process whereby a precursor ion of a particular m/z is selected by m/z\, fragmentation is induced\, and the mass spectrum of the resulting product ions is recorded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12535
name: product ion spectrum
def: "Mass spectrum in which the appropriate m/z separation analysis function is set to record the product ions of a selected precursor ion selected by m/z."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12536
name: profile mode
def: "Method for acquiring a mass spectrum where each peak is displayed as a curve\, with the data points defining the curve corresponding to the signal intensities at each particular m/z value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12537
name: proton-bound dimer
def: "Dimeric ion consisting of two similar or dissimilar molecules bound together by hydrogen bonding with a shared proton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12538
name: pyrolysis mass spectrometry
def: "Mass spectrometry technique in which the sample is heated to the point of decomposition and the gas-phase decomposition products are characterized by mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12539
name: quadratic field reflectron
def: "Reflectron in which the electric field varies with the square of the distance from the entrance and compensates for kinetic energy spread to all orders."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12540
name: quadrupolar axialization
def: "Technique for axialization in Fourier transform-ion cyclotron resonance-mass spectrometers in which magnetron motion is converted into cyclotron motion by subjecting the trapped ions to a quadrupolar excitation field while they undergo collisions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12541
name: quadrupole time-of-flight
def: "Hybrid mass spectrometer consisting of a transmission quadrupole mass spectrometer coupled to an orthogonal acceleration time-of-flight mass spectrometer. A collision quadrupole is typically inserted between the two mass spectrometers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12542
name: quantitation by concatenated tryptic peptides
def: "Method for absolute quantitation of a protein or proteins in a multiplexed fashion using isotope-labeled peptides as surrogate internal standards. The labeled peptides are produced by synthesizing a gene coding for all desired peptides\, expressing this gene in E. coli culture containing appropriately labeled substrate\, and digesting with trypsin the expressed protein composed of the desired concatenated peptides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12543
name: quasi-equilibrium theory
def: "Statistical theory of unimolecular reaction rates \(isomerization or fragmentation\) of molecular species that assumes that reaction occurs from the ground electronic state of the reactant regardless of its mode of formation\, at a rate determined by the free statistical redistribution of internal energy into those vibrational and rotational modes that must be activated sufficiently to reach the appropriate transition state for the specified reaction to occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12544
name: radial ejection
def: "Ejection of an ion from an ion trap in a direction perpendicular to the longitudinal axis of the trap."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12545
name: Rayleigh limit
def: "Limiting droplet size at which self-fragmentation will occur for charged droplets generated in electrospray or other ionization processes. Self-fragmentation occurs when the electrostatic repulsion force generated by the excess charge exceeds the surface tension that maintains the integrity of the droplet."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12546
name: reagent gas
def: "Gas that reacts with ions to produce product ions through ion/molecule reactions; for example\, a gas such as methane\, ammonia\, or isobutane used in chemical ionization to create reagent ions or a gas used in ion traps or collision cells to perform gas-phase ion/molecule reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12547
name: reagent ion
def: "Ion that reacts with a neutral to produce an ionized form of such species through an ion/molecule reaction\, for example\, as in chemical ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12548
name: recombination energy
def: "Energy released when an electron is added to an ionized molecule or atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12549
name: rectilinear ion trap
def: "Quadrupole ion trap whose electrodes form a rectilinear polygon\, the simplest form of which consists of two pairs of rectangular electrodes supplied with radio frequency \(RF\) potentials and a pair of electrodes to which a DC potential is applied. Slits or apertures in the electrodes allow injection and ejection of charged particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12550
name: reference ion
def: "Stable ion whose structure or elemental formula is known with certainty. These ions are formed by direct ionization of a molecule of known structure or elemental formula\, and are used to verify by comparison the structure or thermochemistry of an unknown ion or to calibrate the m/z scale of the mass spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12551
name: relative isotope-ratio difference
def: "Measure of an isotope ratio in a sample of interest relative to a reference defined by the relation \\\[\\delta = \(R_\{\\rm\{sample\}\} - R_\{\\rm\{standard\}\}\)/R_\{\\rm\{standard\}\}\\\] where \\\(R\\\) is the isotope ratio of the element."
synonym: "delta notation" []
synonym: "isotope delta" []
synonym: "relative difference of isotope ratios" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12552
name: residual gas analyser
def: "Mass spectrometer used to measure the composition and pressure of gases in an evacuated chamber and to test for leaks in pressurized systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12553
name: resolution\: 10 per cent valley definition
def: "Value of \\\(\(m/z\)/\\Delta\(m/z\)\\\) measured for two peaks of equal height in a mass spectrum at m/z and \\\(m/z \\pm \\Delta\(m/z\)\\\) that are separated by a valley which at its lowest point is \\\(\\pu\{10\\%\}\\\) of the height of either peak. For peaks of similar height separated by a valley\, let the height of the valley at its lowest point be \\\(\\pu\{10\\%\}\\\) of the lower peak. Then the resolution \(\\\(\\pu\{10\\%\}\\\) valley definition\) is \\\(\(m/z\)/\\Delta\(m/z\)\\\) and should be given for a number of values of m/z."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12554
name: resolution\: peak width definition
def: "For a single peak corresponding to singly charged ions at mass \\\(m\\\) in a mass spectrum\, the resolution may be expressed as \\\(\(m/z\)/\\Delta\(m/z\)\\\)\, where \\\(\\Delta\(m/z\)\\\) is the width of the peak at a height which is a specified fraction of the maximum peak height. It is recommended that one of three values 50\, 5\, or \\\(\\pu\{0.5\\%\}\\\) should always be used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12555
name: resonance-enhanced multiphoton ionization
def: "Multiphoton ionization in which the ionization cross section is significantly enhanced because the energy of the incident photons is resonant with an intermediate excited state of the neutral."
synonym: "resonance ionization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12556
name: resonance ion ejection
def: "Mode of ion ejection in an ion trap that relies on an auxiliary radio frequency potential that is applied to the end-cap or trapping electrodes. The potential is tuned to the classical oscillation frequency of a particular ion to eject it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12557
name: retro Diels–Alder reaction
def: "Ion fragmentation mechanism in which the molecular ion \\\(\\ce\{M^\{+.\}\}\\\) of a cyclic alkene fragments to form a neutral diene and an alkene radical cation or a diene radical cation and an alkene. The reaction is the reverse of the Diels–Alder reaction of organic synthesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12558
name: reverse geometry
def: "Double-focusing mass spectrometer configuration in which the ion beam traverses the magnetic sector prior to the electric sector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12559
name: reverse library search
def: "Process of comparing a subset of the peaks in the mass spectrum of an unknown compound with peaks present in the library mass spectrum. Any peaks in the mass spectrum of the unknown compound that are not in the library spectrum are disregarded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12560
name: RF-only quadrupole
def: "Transmission quadrupole device in which a sinusoidal radio frequency \(RF\) potential is applied between the connected pairs of opposite rod electrodes in a linear quadrupole\, providing a transmission device for all ions with m/z values above a cut-off value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12561
name: ring and double bond equivalent
def: "Conventional measure of the degree of unsaturation of an organic molecule given by \\\(X - Y/2 + Z/2 + 1\\\) where \\\(X\\\) is the number of carbon atoms\, \\\(Y\\\) is the number of hydrogen or halogen atoms and \\\(Z\\\) is the number of nitrogen and phosphorus atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12562
name: saddle field gun
def: "Compact ion and fast atom sources for use when small size and high beam intensity are required\, as in fast atom bombardment mass spectrometry. The discharge is produced by electrons oscillating between two cold cathodes in an electric field that possesses a single saddle point. To produce and intensify the plasma the electrons must have long path lengths\, achieved by allowing the electrons to have an oscillatory motion in the saddle-shaped electric field between the cathodes separated by the anode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12563
name: sampling cone
def: "Component of a spray ionization interface that consists of a cone with a small orifice through which the spray passes into a differentially pumped chamber"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12564
name: scan cycle time
def: "Time required to obtain a mass spectrum \(or other mass spectrometric data quantity\) such as in a separation coupled to mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12565
name: secondary neutral mass spectrometry
def: "Mass spectrometry technique in which neutral species ejected from a sample surface as a result of bombardment by a primary beam of atoms or ions are ionized\, most often by photoionization\, prior to analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12566
name: sector mass spectrometer
def: "Mass spectrometer consisting of one or more magnetic sectors for recording a mass spectrum of a monoenergetic beam of ions. Such instruments may also have one or more electric sectors for energy dispersion that can be arranged for velocity-focusing by compensating for dispersion by the magnetic sector if the beam is not truly monoenergetic. Such a combination provides double focusing of ions of the same m/z\, i.e.\, focusing with respect to both direction and speed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12567
name: selected ion flow tube
def: "Device in which m/z selected ions are entrained in an inert carrier gas and subsequently undergo ion/molecule reactions with molecules introduced into the gas flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12568
name: selected reaction monitoring
def: "Data acquired from one or more specific product ions corresponding to m/z selected precursor ions recorded via two or more stages of mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12569
name: self-chemical ionization
def: "Chemical ionization of an analyte where the reagent ion is an ionized form of the analyte or one of its product ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12570
name: sheath flow interface
def: "Capillary electrophoresis to electrospray ionization interface that uses a coaxial flow of make-up liquid that is introduced through a tube that is concentric with the separation capillary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12571
name: sheath gas
def: "Gas introduced through a tube that is coaxial with the electrospray emitter to pneumatically assist the formation of the sprayed droplets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12572
name: sheath liquid
def: "Make-up liquid in a sheath flow interface"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12573
name: shotgun proteomics
def: "Protein identification using a combination of high-performance liquid chromatography and mass spectrometry in which the proteins in a mixture are digested\, usually by a specific enzyme\, and the resulting peptides are separated by liquid chromatography and identified by tandem mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12574
name: skimmer
def: "Cone with a central orifice that intercepts the center of a spray or jet expansion to sample the central portion of the expansion\, for example\, as in a momentum separator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12575
name: soft ionization
def: "Formation of gas-phase ions without extensive fragmentation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12576
name: sonic spray ionization
def: "Process in which both anions and cations are produced at atmospheric pressure from an ionic solution due to a statistically unbalanced charge distribution in the minute droplets formed by the action of a sonic atomizer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12577
name: space charge effect
def: "Result of mutual repulsion of particles of like charge \(ions\, electrons\, etc.\) that can limit the current in a charged-particle beam and can also cause some motion of ions in a beam or packet in addition to that caused by external fields."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12578
name: spectral skewing
def: "Deviation of the peak intensities in a mass spectrum from their expected values that occurs when the relative concentration of the various analytes change during acquisition of the mass spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12579
name: spray ionization
def: "General term used to describe a range of ionization techniques for analytes in solution that involve dispersing the liquid into fine droplets as a component step."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12580
name: stable isotope ratio analysis of amino acids in cell culture
def: "Proteomics technique that detects differences in protein abundance between cultured cell samples using stable isotopic labeling achieved with labeled amino acids in one cell culture and unlabeled ones in a separate culture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12581
name: stable isotope standards and capture by anti-peptide antibodies
def: "Method for absolute quantitation of a protein or proteins in a multiplexed fashion using isotope-labeled peptides as surrogate internal standards. The labeled peptides are produced by synthesizing a gene coding for each desired peptide\, cell-free transcription\, and translation of this gene in a medium containing \\\(\\ce\{^\{13\}C6\}\\\)-labeled lysine\, and digesting with trypsin the expressed protein composed of the desired concatenated peptides. For low-abundance proteins the sensitivity is increased by selective enrichment of the target peptides by antibody capture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12582
name: static field
def: "Electric or magnetic field that does not change in time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12583
name: Stevenson’s rule
def: "Rule stating that in competing fragmentations the product ion formed from its neutral species counterpart with the lower ionization energy will usually be the more abundant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12584
name: stored waveform inverse fourier transform
def: "Technique to create excitation waveforms for ions in Fourier transform-ion cyclotron resonance-mass spectrometers or Paul ion traps. An excitation waveform in the time-domain is generated by taking the inverse Fourier transform of an appropriate frequency-domain programmed excitation spectrum\, in which the resonance frequencies of ions to be excited are included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12585
name: supercritical fluid chromatography-mass spectrometry
def: "Combined technique in which a mixture of analytes is separated into solutions of individual components by chromatography that uses a supercritical fluid as mobile phase\, followed by the ionization of the separation effluent in the ion source of a mass spectrometer."
synonym: "supercritical fluid chromatography/mass spectrometry (SFC/MS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12586
name: surface-enhanced laser desorption/ionization
def: "Variant of matrix-assisted laser desorption/ionization \(MALDI\) in which the matrix is replaced by a surface coating of a chromatographic stationary phase designed to selectively retain different subsets of proteins or peptides from a mixture depending on their physico-chemical properties or biochemical affinity characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12587
name: surface-induced dissociation
def: "Fragmentation that results from the collision of an ion with a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12588
name: surface-induced reaction
def: "Process wherein a reactant ion interacts with a surface to produce either chemically different species or a change in the internal energy of the reactant ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12589
name: surrogate internal standard
def: "Compound added in accurately known amounts to a sample to be analyzed for a specified analyte. The surrogate internal standard is chemically similar to the analyte so that losses in the analytical procedure are the same for both and thus the analyte concentration can be determined relative to that of the standard."
synonym: "internal standard" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12590
name: sustained off-resonance irradiation
def: "Procedure associated with Fourier transform-ion cyclotron resonance-mass spectrometers to facilitate ion/neutral reactions such as low-energy collision-induced dissociation in which a radio-frequency electric field\, slightly off-resonance with respect to the cyclotron frequency of the reactant ion\, cyclically accelerates and decelerates the reactant ion that is confined in the Penning ion trap. As a consequence\, the orbit does not exceed the dimensions of the ion trap but maintains a high average translational energy for an extended time that activates an ion/neutral process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12591
name: tandem mass spectrometry in space
def: "Tandem mass spectrometry procedure in which product ion spectra are recorded for ion beams in m/z analyzers separated in space. Specific m/z separation functions are designed so that in one section of the instrument ions are selected\, dissociated in an intermediate region\, and the product ions are then transmitted to another analyzer for m/z separation and data acquisition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12592
name: tandem mass spectrometry in time
def: "Tandem mass spectrometry procedure in which product ion spectra are recorded in a single m/z analyzer \(such as a Paul ion trap\, linear ion trap\, or Fourier transform-ion cyclotron resonance-mass spectrometer\) in discrete steps over time. Ions in a specific m/z range are selected\, dissociated\, and the product ions analyzed sequentially in time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12593
name: Taylor cone
def: "Conical shape of the liquid emanating from a capillary under high potential\, as in electrospray or other electrohydrodynamic spray processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12594
name: thermospray ionization
def: "Spray ionization in which a liquid is flowed through a heated capillary to produce a spray of droplets and solvent vapor. Ions are formed due to the statistical imbalance of charges in the droplets or by a heated filament."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12595
name: thin layer chromatography-mass spectrometry
def: "Technique by which a mixture of analytes is separated into individual components by liquid chromatography\, followed by the ionization of the separated species by an ion source of a mass spectrometer."
synonym: "thin layer chromatography/mass spectrometry (TLC/MS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12596
name: time-lag focusing
def: "Energy focusing of ions formed in the gas phase in a time-of-flight mass spectrometer that is accomplished by introducing a time delay between the formation of the ions and the application of the accelerating potential pulse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12597
name: tolyl ion
def: "Even-electron \\\(\\ce\{C7H7^\{+\}\}\\\) ion with the same carbon skeleton as toluene that has lost a hydrogen atom from one of the ring carbons that carries the positive charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12598
name: toroidal ion trap
def: "Ion trapping device based on the three-dimensional structure formed by rotating the cross-section of a quadrupole ion trap about the edge perpendicular to the plane of the ring electrode. The resulting trapping region is toroidal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12599
name: total ion current electropherogram
def: "Plot of the total ion current in each of a series of mass spectra that are recorded as a function of electrophoresis time."
synonym: "total ion current electrophorogram" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12600
name: total ion current profile
def: "Any plot of total ion current in a series of mass spectra recorded as a function of time\, including total ion current chromatogram\, total ion current electropherogram\, flow injection mass spectrometry\, or any other time dependent sampling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12601
name: transmission efficiency
def: "Ratio of the number of ions leaving a region of a mass spectrometer to the number entering that region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12602
name: transmission quadrupole mass spectrometer
def: "Mass spectrometer that consists of an array of four parallel rod electrodes whose centers form the corners of a square and whose opposing electrode pairs are connected. The electric potential applied between the connected pairs of rods is a superposition of a static potential and a sinusoidal radio frequency potential. The motion of an ion in the \\\(x\\mbox\{-\}y\\\) plane perpendicular to the length direction of the rod electrodes is described by the Mathieu equation whose solutions show that ions in a particular m/z range can be transmitted along the \\\(z\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{axis\}\\\)."
synonym: "quadrupole mass analyzer" []
synonym: "quadrupole mass filter" []
synonym: "quadrupole mass spectrometer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12603
name: transport region
def: "Region in a mass spectrometer in which an ion beam is transported from the ion source to the ion optics or from one ion optical element to the next."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12604
name: triple quadrupole mass spectrometer
def: "Tandem mass spectrometer comprising two transmission quadrupole mass spectrometers in series\, with a \(non-selecting\) RF-only quadrupole \(or other multipole\) between them to act as a collision cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12605
name: unimolecular dissociation
def: "Fragmentation reaction in which the molecularity of the reaction system is unity. The dissociation may arise from the extra energy acquired by a metastable ion produced in the ion source or that provided by collisional excitation of a stable ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12606
name: unit mass resolution
def: "Mass resolution such that it is possible to clearly distinguish a peak corresponding to a singly charged ion from its neighbors \\\(\\pu\{1 u\}\\\) away\, usually with no more than \\\(\\pu\{5\\!-\\! 10\\%\}\\\) overlap."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12607
name: v-ion
def: "High-energy product ion of a protonated peptide formally equivalent to a y-ion with the ionizing proton on the \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)-terminus\, but with elimination of \\\(\\ce\{HR\}\\\) from the \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)-terminus where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is the side-chain on the \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)-atom of the \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)-terminal residue\, to give a \(\\\(\\ce\{H2N^\{+\}=CH-CO\\bond\{-\}\}\\\)\) structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12608
name: volumetric internal standard
def: "Compound added in a fixed reproducible amount to a solution to be analyzed by gas chromatography to permit corrections for uncontrolled variations in injected volumes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12609
name: Wahrhaftig diagram
def: "Diagram illustrating the relative contributions in unimolecular dissociation of an ion\, for example\, by homolytic cleavage of a single bond\, and fragmentation following rearrangement. The diagram is in two parts with a common horizontal axis representing the internal energy of the ion. The vertical axis in one part of the diagram shows the two types of rate constant on a logarithmic scale\, and that for the other portion of the diagram indicates the distribution function for internal energy of the ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12610
name: Wien filter
def: "Mass spectrometer in which m/z separation is achieved using crossed electric and magnetic fields."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12611
name: w-ion
def: "High-energy product ion of a protonated peptide formally equivalent to a z-ion that has lost the part of the \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)-terminal side-chain starting at the \\\(\\ce\{\\upgamma\\mbox\{-\}carbon\}\\\) \(if any\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12612
name: x-ion
def: "Fragment ion containing the peptide \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)-terminus formed upon dissociation of a peptide ion at the peptide backbone \\\(\\ce\{C-C\}\\\) bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12613
name: y-ion
def: "Fragment ion containing the peptide \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)-terminus formed upon dissociation of a peptide ion at the peptide backbone \\\(\\ce\{C-N\}\\\) bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12614
name: z-ion
def: "Fragment ion containing the peptide \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)-terminus formed upon dissociation of a peptide ion at the peptide backbone \\\(\\ce\{N-C\}\\\) bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12615
name: ADMET
def: "Acronym referring to the absorption\, distribution\, metabolism\, excretion\, and toxicity profile or processes for a xenobiotic upon its administration in vivo."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12616
name: allosteric antagonist
def: "Compound that binds to a receptor at a site separate from\, but actively coupled to\, that of the endogenous agonist to reduce actively receptor signals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12617
name: alpha-helix
def: "Secondary 3D structure of a protein or peptide containing a right-handed coiled or spiral conformation\, in which every backbone \\\(\\ce\{N-H\}\\\) group donates a hydrogen bond to the backbone \\\(\\ce\{C=O\}\\\) group of the amino acid four residues earlier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12618
name: analogue
def: "Chemical compound having structural similarity to a reference compound."
synonym: "analog" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12619
name: analogue-based drug discovery
def: "Strategy for drug discovery and/or optimization in which structural modification of an existing drug provides a new drug with improved chemical and/or biological properties."
synonym: "analog-based drug discovery" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12620
name: ATP binding cassette protein
def: "Large gene family of transporter proteins that bind ATP and use the energy to transport substrates \(e.g.\, sugars\, amino acids\, metal ions\, peptides\, proteins\, and a large number of hydrophobic compounds and metabolites\) across lipid membranes."
synonym: "ABC protein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12621
name: attrition rate
def: "Rate of loss of drug candidates during progression through the optimisation and developmental stages while on route to the marketplace."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12622
name: autoinduction
def: "Capacity of a drug to induce enzymes that mediate its own metabolism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12623
name: back-up compound
def: "Molecule selected as a replacement for the lead drug candidate should this subsequently fail during further preclinical evaluation or in clinical studies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12624
name: best-in-class
def: "Drug acting on a specific molecular target that provides the best balance between efficacy and adverse effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12625
name: beta-barrel
def: "Secondary 3D protein structure containing a large beta-sheet that twists and coils to form a closed structure in which the first-strand hydrogen bonds to the last."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12626
name: beta-sheet
def: "Secondary 3D structure of a protein that takes on a flat\, pleated appearance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12627
name: beta-sheet breaker
def: "Compound that on binding to a protein disrupts beta-sheet formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12628
name: biological agent
def: "Biopolymer-based pharmaceutical\, such as a protein\, applicable to the prevention\, treatment\, or cure of diseases or injuries to man."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12629
name: blockbuster drug
def: "Drug that generates annual sales of USD 1 billion \(\\\(\\$\\pu\{E9\}\\\)\) or more."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12630
name: carcinogen
def: "Agent \(chemical\, physical\, or biological\) that is capable of increasing the incidence of malignant neoplasms\, thus causing cancer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12631
name: chemical biology
def: "Application of chemistry to the study of molecular events in biological systems\, often using tool compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12632
name: chemical database
def: "Specific electronic repository for storage and retrieval of chemical information."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12633
name: chemical library
def: "Collection of samples \(e.g.\, chemical compounds\, natural products\, over-expression library of a microbe\) available for biological screening.\nSet of compounds produced through combinatorial chemistry or other means that expands around a single core structure or scaffold."
synonym: "compound collection" []
synonym: "compound library" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12634
name: chemical space
def: "Set of all possible stable molecules based on a specific chemical entity that interacts at one or more specific molecular targets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12635
name: chemogenomics
def: "Systematic screening of chemical libraries of congeneric compounds against members of a target family of proteins."
synonym: "chemical genomics" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12636
name: chemokine
def: "Member of a superfamily of proteins with the primary function to control leukocyte activity and trafficking through tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12637
name: co-drug
def: "Two chemically linked synergistic drugs designed to improve the drug delivery properties of one or both drugs."
synonym: "mutual prodrug [1]" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12638
name: constitutive activity
def: "Receptor or enzymatic function displayed in the absence of an agonist or activator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12639
name: contract research organisation
def: "Commercial organisation that can be engaged to undertake specifically defined chemical\, biological\, safety\, or clinical studies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12640
name: covalent drug
def: "Ligand that binds irreversibly to its molecular target through the formation of a new chemical bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12641
name: designed multiple ligands
def: "Compounds conceived and synthesised to act on two or more molecular targets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12642
name: diversity
def: "Unrelatedness of a set of molecules \(e.g.\, building blocks or members of a compound library\)\, as measured by properties such as atom connectivity\, physical properties\, or computationally generated descriptors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12643
name: diversity-oriented synthesis
def: "Efficient production of a range of structures and templates with skeletal and stereochemical diversity as opposed to the synthesis of a specific target molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12644
name: drug cocktail
def: "­\(in drug therapy\) Administration of two or more distinct pharmacological agents to achieve a combination of their individual effects.\nNote 1\: The combined effect may be additive\, synergistic\, or designed to reduce side-effects.\nNote 2\: This term is often used synonymously with that of 'drug combination' but is preferred in order to avoid confusion with medications in which different drugs are included in a single formulation.\n\(in drug testing\) Administration of two or more distinct compounds to test simultaneously their individual behaviours \(e.g.\, pharmacological effects in high-throughput screens or drug metabolism\)."
synonym: "drug combination" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12645
name: drug delivery
def: "Process by which a drug is administered to its intended recipient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12646
name: drug distribution
def: "Measured amounts of an administered compound in various parts of the organism to which it is given."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12647
name: druggability
def: "Capacity of a molecular target to be modulated in a favourable manner by a small-molecule drug."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12648
name: drug repurposing
def: "Strategy that seeks to discover new applications for an existing drug that were not previously referenced and not currently prescribed or investigated."
synonym: "drug repositioning" []
synonym: "drug reprofiling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12649
name: drug safety
def: "Assessment of the nontolerable biological effects of a drug."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12650
name: dual binding site
def: "Presence of two distinct ligand binding sites on the same molecular target."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12651
name: effective concentration
def: "Concentration of a substance that produces a defined magnitude of response in a given system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12652
name: effective dose
def: "Dose of a substance that causes a defined magnitude of response in a given system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12653
name: efflux pump
def: "Transporter protein located in the membrane of cells that utilizes active transport to move a compound from the internal to the external environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12654
name: equilibrium solubility
def: "Analytical composition of a mixture or solution that is saturated with one of the components in the designated mixture or solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12655
name: fast follower
def: "Compound selected as a rapid successor to a lead drug candidate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12656
name: fingerprint
def: "Representation of a compound or chemical library by attributes \(descriptors\) such as atom connectivities\, 3D structure\, or physical properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12657
name: first-in-class
def: "First drug acting on a hitherto unaddressed molecular target to reach the market."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12658
name: follow-on drug
def: "Drug having a similar mechanism of action to an existing drug."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12659
name: fragment
def: "Low-molar-mass ligand \(typically smaller than \\\(\\pu\{200 Da\}\\\)\) that binds to a target with low affinity but high ligand efficiency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12660
name: fragment-based lead discovery
def: "Screening libraries of low-molar-mass compounds \(typically \\\(\\pu\{120\\!\{-\}\\! 250 Da\}\\\)\) using sensitive biophysical techniques capable of detecting weakly binding lead compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12661
name: frequent hitter
def: "Structural feature that regularly results in a positive response in a variety of high-throughput or primary screens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12662
name: G protein
def: "Member of a family of membrane-associated proteins which on activation by cellular receptors lead to signal transduction."
synonym: "guanine binding protein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12663
name: green chemistry
def: "Invention\, design\, and application of chemical products and processes to reduce or eliminate the use and generation of hazardous substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12664
name: hit
def: "Molecule that produces reproducible activity above a defined threshold in a biological assay and whose structural identity has been established."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12665
name: hit expansion
def: "Generation of additional compound sets that contain chemical motifs and scaffolds that have activity in the primary screen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12666
name: hit-to-lead chemistry
def: "Process by which a proven molecule or series derived from high-throughput screening or primary screens is chemically optimised to a viable lead or series."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12667
name: homology model
def: "Computational representation of a protein built from the 3D structure of a similar protein or proteins using alignment techniques and homology arguments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12668
name: hydrophobic fragmental constant
def: "Representation of the lipophilicity contribution of a constituent part of a structure to the total lipophilicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12669
name: inhibitory concentration at 50%
def: "The concentration of an enzyme inhibitor or receptor antagonist that reduces the enzyme activity or agonist response by \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12670
name: inhibition constant
def: "Equilibrium dissociation constant of an enzyme-inhibitor complex\: \\\(K_\{\\rm\{i\}\} = \[\\ce\{E\}\]\[\\ce\{I\}\]/\[\\ce\{EI\}\]\\\).\nThe equilibrium dissociation constant of a receptor-ligand complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12672
name: intrinsic solubility
def: "Equilibrium solubility of the uncharged form of an ionisable compound at a pH where it is fully unionised. \\\[\\ce\{HA\(s\)  HA\(aq\)\} \\quad \(1\)\\\] \\\[\\ce\{HA\(aq\)  H^\{+\}\(aq\) + A^\{-\}\(aq\)\} \\quad \(2\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12673
name: investigational new drug
def: "Drug not yet approved for general use by the national authority\, such as the Food and Drug Administration of the United States of America\, but undergoing clinical investigation to assess its safety and efficacy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12674
name: ionotropic receptor
def: "Transmembrane ion channel that opens or closes in response to the binding of a ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12675
name: kinase
def: "Enzyme that transfers a phosphate group from high-energy donor molecules\, such as ATP\, to specific target molecules."
synonym: "phosphotransferase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12676
name: kinetic solubility
def: "Composition of a solution with respect to a compound when its induced precipitate first appears."
synonym: "turbidimetric solubility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12677
name: lead
def: "Compound \(or compound series\) that satisfies predefined minimum criteria for further structure and activity optimization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12678
name: lead validation
def: "Process by which a lead compound is authenticated by the confirmation of its expected pharmacological properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12679
name: ligand-based drug design
def: "Method of drug discovery and/or optimization in which the pursuit of new structures and/or structural modifications is based upon one or more ligands known to interact with the molecular target of interest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12680
name: metabotropic receptor
def: "Receptor that modulates electric potential-gated channels via G-proteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12681
name: me-too drug
def: "Term applied to drug analogues that offer no significant advantage over the prototype compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12682
name: microRNA
def: "Small single-stranded RNA molecules that play a significant role in the post-transcriptional regulation of gene expression."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12683
name: molecular descriptor
def: "Parameter that characterizes a specific structural or physicochemical aspect of a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12684
name: molecular target
def: "Protein \(e.g.\, receptor\, enzyme\, or ion channel\)\, RNA\, or DNA that is implicated in a clinical disorder or the propagation of any untoward event."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12685
name: multidrug resistance
def: "Characteristic of cells that confers resistance to the effects of several different classes of drugs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12686
name: multiparameter optimisation
def: "Drug-likeness penetrability algorithm derived from CLOGP\, clogD\, molar mass\, topological polar surface area\, number of hydrogen-bond donors\, and \\\(\\pu\{p\}K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12687
name: multitarget-directed ligand
def: "Ligand acting on more than one distinct molecular target. Targets may be of the same or different mechanistic classes."
synonym: "multitarget drug" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12688
name: multitarget drug discovery
def: "Deliberate design of compounds that act on more than one molecular target."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12689
name: Murcko assembly
def: "Core scaffold of a molecule that remains after all chain substituents that do not terminate in a ring are removed. Single atoms connected by a double bond are typically also retained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12690
name: neglected disease
def: "Term used to emphasise an imbalance between therapeutic needs and resource allocation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12691
name: neutral antagonist
def: "Ligand that blocks the responses of a receptor to both agonists and inverse agonists with the same intensity. It binds to the receptor without evoking any change of conformation or change to the ratio of activated to inactivated conformations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12692
name: new chemical entity
def: "Drug that contains no active moiety previously approved for use by the national authority\, such as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12693
name: new molecular entity
def: "Active ingredient that has never before been approved in any form by the national authority\, such as the U.S. Food and Drug Administration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12694
name: noncompetitive antagonist
def: "Functional antagonist that either binds irreversibly to a receptor or to a site distinct from that of the natural agonist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12695
name: nuclear hormone receptor
def: "Ligand-activated transcription factor that regulates gene expression by interacting with specific DNA sequences upstream of its target gene\(s\)."
synonym: "nuclear receptor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12696
name: obviousness
def: "Term associated with intellectual property wherein the latter’s patentability is assessed relative to the combination of more than one item of 'prior art'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12697
name: off-target effect
def: "Pharmacological action induced by any molecule at molecular/biological sites distinct from that for which it was designed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12698
name: orphan disease
def: "Disease for which drug research\, development\, and marketing is economically unfavourable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12699
name: orphan drug
def: "Pharmaceutical agent that has been approved specifically to treat a rare and commercially unfavourable medical condition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12700
name: orphan receptor
def: "Receptor for which an endogenous ligand has yet to be identified."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12701
name: parallel synthesis
def: "Simultaneous preparation of sets of discrete compounds in arrays of physically separate reaction vessels or microcompartments without interchange of intermediates during the assembly process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12702
name: patentability
def: "Set of criteria that must be satisfied in order to achieve commercial exclusivity for an invention."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12703
name: peptoid
def: "Peptide-like oligomer consisting of repeating \\\(N\\\)-substituted glycine units  where \\\(\\ce\{R1\}\\\) to \\\(\\ce\{R4\}\\\) are typically alkyl or aryl and may be the same or different."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12704
name: P-glycoprotein
def: "ATP-binding cassette transporter responsible for the efflux of small molecules from cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12705
name: pharmacogenetics
def: "Study of inherited differences \(variation\) in drug metabolism and response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12706
name: pharmacogenomics
def: "General study of all of the many different genes that determine drug behaviour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12707
name: phase 0 clinical studies
def: "Exploratory first-in-human trials that involve microdosing of drug to allow the assessment of pharmacokinetic parameters with limited drug exposure."
synonym: "exploratory investigational new drug" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12708
name: phase I clinical studies
def: "Initial introduction of an investigational new drug into humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12709
name: phase II clinical studies
def: "Controlled clinical studies conducted in a limited number of individuals to obtain some preliminary data on the effectiveness of an investigational new drug for a particular indication or indications in patients with the disease or condition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12710
name: phase III clinical studies
def: "Expanded controlled and uncontrolled trials in humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12711
name: phase IV clinical studies
def: "Extended post-marketing studies in humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12712
name: phenotypic screening
def: "Evaluation of compounds \(small molecules\, peptides\, siRNA\, etc.\) in cells\, tissues\, or organisms for their ability to modify the system in a measurable manner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12713
name: pipeline
def: "Discovery and development compound portfolio of a pharmaceutical company or research organization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12714
name: pivotal study
def: "Experiment that provides strong support for or against the drug or molecular target under investigation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12715
name: potential genotoxic impurity
def: "Confirmed or suspected presence of one or more known genotoxic materials in an active pharmaceutical ingredient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12716
name: preclinical candidate
def: "Optimised lead compound successfully passing key screening\, selectivity\, and physicochemical criteria sufficient to warrant further detailed pharmacological and pharmacokinetic evaluation in animal models."
synonym: "safety assessment candidate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12717
name: privileged structure
def: "Substructural feature that confers desirable \(often drug-like\) properties on compounds containing that feature. They often consist of a semi-rigid scaffold that presents multiple hydrophobic residues without undergoing hydrophobic collapse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12718
name: proof of concept
def: "Procedure by which a specific therapeutic mechanism or treatment/diagnostic paradigm is shown to be beneficial."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12719
name: proteinase
def: "Enzyme that catalyses the hydrolysis of proteins."
synonym: "protease" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12720
name: prototype drug
def: "Early compound that has biological properties suitable for target validation but may not necessarily be adequate for clinical studies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12721
name: QTc interval
def: "Time between the start of the Q wave and the end of the T wave in the heart’s electrical cycle. When corrected for individual heart rate it becomes known as the corrected QT\, or QTc interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12722
name: retrosynthesis
def: "Process of conceptually deconstructing complex molecules into simpler fragments capable of chemical manipulation to reform the parent compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12723
name: rule of three
def: "Set of molecular descriptors used to assess the quality of hit or lead molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12724
name: scaffold
def: "Core portion of a molecule common to all members of a chemical library or compound series."
synonym: "template" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12725
name: scaffold hopping
def: "Exchange of one scaffold for another while maintaining molecular features that are important for biological properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12726
name: site-directed mutagenesis
def: "Molecular biology technique in which mutations are created at one or more defined sites in a DNA molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12727
name: small inhibitory double-stranded RNA
def: "Small RNA fragments that can combine with specific genes to silence their expression."
synonym: "small interfering double-stranded RNA" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12728
name: spare receptor
def: "Residual binding site still available to an endogenous ligand after sufficient sites have already been filled to elicit the maximal response possible for that particular biological system."
synonym: "receptor reserve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12729
name: structural alert
def: "Chemical features present in a hit or lead molecule indicative of potential toxicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12730
name: supersaturated solution
def: "Solution that has a greater composition of a solute than one that is in equilibrium with undissolved solute at specified values of temperature and pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12731
name: systems biology
def: "Integration of high-throughput biology measurements with computational models that study the projection of the mechanistic characteristics of metabolic and signalling pathways onto physiological and pathological phenotypes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12732
name: targeted drug delivery
def: "Approach to target a drug to a specific tissue or molecular target using a prodrug or antibody recognition systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12733
name: target validation
def: "Process by which a protein\, RNA\, or DNA is implicated in a biological pathway thought to be of relevance to a disease or adverse pathology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12734
name: tautomer
def: "Structural isomer that can readily convert to another form that differs only by the attachment position of a hydrogen atom and the location of double bond\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12735
name: therapeutic index
def: "Ratio of the exposure/concentration of a therapeutic agent that causes beneficial effects to that which causes the first observed adverse effect."
synonym: "therapeutic ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12736
name: unmet medical need
def: "Term used for diseases or other disorders for which no optimal therapeutic options exist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12737
name: virtual chemical library
def: "Collection of chemical structures constructed solely in electronic form or on paper."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12738
name: volume of distribution
def: "Apparent \(hypothetical\) volume of fluid required to contain the total amount of a substance in the body at the same concentration as that present in the plasma assuming equilibrium has been attained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12739
name: wild-type receptor
def: "Receptor that occurs naturally in human and other species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12740
name: alpha relaxation
def: "Highest-temperature or lowest-frequency relaxation in the loss curve of a polymer."
synonym: "α-relaxation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12741
name: alpha relaxation peak
def: "Highest-temperature peak or lowest-frequency peak in the loss curve of a polymer."
synonym: "alpha loss peak" []
synonym: "α-loss peak" []
synonym: "α-relaxation peak" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12742
name: amorphous polymer
def: "Polymer in the amorphous state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12743
name: amorphous state
def: "State of matter characterised by the absence of long-range molecular order."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12744
name: beta relaxation
def: "Second-highest-temperature or second-lowest-frequency relaxation in the loss curve of a polymer."
synonym: "β-relaxation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12745
name: beta relaxation peak
def: "Second-highest-temperature or second-lowest-frequency peak in the loss curve of a polymer."
synonym: "beta loss peak" []
synonym: "β-loss peak" []
synonym: "β-relaxation peak" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12746
name: characteristic temperatures
def: "Temperatures obtained from a differential scanning calorimeter \(DSC\) experiment or a differential thermal analysis \(DTA\) experiment that characterise the physical processes occurring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12747
name: clearing temperature
def: "Temperature at which the transition between the mesophase with the highest temperature range and the isotropic phase occurs."
synonym: "clearing point" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12748
name: cold crystallisation
def: "Polymer crystallisation brought about from a glass\, from a liquid-crystalline state\, or a state displaying a low degree of crystallinity at temperatures a little above the glass-transition temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12749
name: conformational enthalpy
def: "Enthalpy change associated with conformational changes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12750
name: conformational entropy
def: "Entropy change associated with conformational changes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12751
name: conformational melting
def: "Step-like increase in the conformational entropy of macromolecules\, or parts of macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12752
name: conformational transition
def: "Transition between two conformational states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12753
name: constant oscillation-amplitude method
def: "Forced-resonance method to determine the viscoelastic properties of a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12754
name: crystalline state
def: "State of matter ideally characterised by three-dimensional\, long-range order on an atomic scale."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12755
name: crystallisable polymer
def: "Polymer that is able to partially crystallise."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12756
name: damping curve
def: "Decrease in the deformation amplitude of a material specimen plotted vs. time when the specimen is subjected to a free oscillation."
synonym: "loss curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12757
name: devitrification
def: "Formation of a polymer melt from a polymer glass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12758
name: differential scanning calorimeter
def: "Apparatus for conducting a differential scanning calorimetry \(DSC\) experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12759
name: differential thermal analyser
def: "Apparatus for conducting a differential thermal analysis experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12760
name: dilatometer
def: "Apparatus for conducting a dilatometry experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12761
name: dilatometry
def: "The measurement of changes with time in the volume\, length\, width\, or thickness of a material sample caused by temperature changes under negligible load or by chemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12762
name: dynamic-load thermomechanical analysis
def: "Thermomechanical analysis carried out using a sinusoidal stress."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12763
name: dynamic mechanical analyser
def: "Apparatus for conducting a dynamic mechanical analysis experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12764
name: dynamic viscometer
def: "Apparatus that uses a forced\, non-resonance method to determine the viscoelastic properties of a material sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12765
name: enthalpy relaxation
def: "Enthalpy change observed near the glass transition when a sample is heated at a rate different from that at which it was cooled down\, or observed after annealing in the glassy state below the glass-transition temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12766
name: equilibrium melting temperature
def: "Temperature at which perfect polymer crystals of infinite thickness melt under equilibrium conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12767
name: forced sinusoidal oscillation
def: "Deformation of a material by the application of a small sinusoidal strain \(\\\(\\gamma\\\)\) such that \\\[\\gamma = \\gamma_\{0\} \\cos \\omega t\\\] where \\\(\\gamma_\{0\}\\\) and \\\(\\omega\\\) are positive constants."
synonym: "forced oscillation" []
synonym: "sinusoidal oscillation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12768
name: fragile glass
def: "Glass characterised by a large value of the fragility index \(m\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12769
name: fragility index
def: "Quantity characterising the fragility or the strength of a glass at a given temperature \\\[m = \\left \(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\} \\log_\{10\} \\tau\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\} \(T_\{\\rm\{g\}\}/T\)\} \\right\)_\{T = T_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\}\\\] \\\(\\tau\\\) = relaxation time \[\\\(\\pu\{s\}\\\)\]; \\\(T_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) = glass-transition temperature \[\\\(\\pu\{K\}\\\)\]; \\\(T\\\) = temperature \[\\\(\\pu\{K\}\\\)\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12770
name: free oscillation
def: "Oscillatory deformation of a material specimen with the motion generated without the continuous application of an external force."
synonym: "free sinusoidal oscillation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12771
name: gamma relaxation
def: "Third-highest-temperature or third-lowest-frequency relaxation in the loss curve of a polymer."
synonym: "γ-relaxation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12772
name: gamma relaxation peak
def: "Third-highest-temperature or third-lowest-frequency peak in the loss curve of a polymer."
synonym: "gamma loss peak" []
synonym: "γ-loss peak" []
synonym: "γ-relaxation peak" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12773
name: glass
def: "Amorphous matter in the glassy state\, i.e.\, below its glass-transition temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12774
name: glassy state
def: "State of amorphous matter below its glass-transition temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12775
name: Hoffman–Weeks plot
def: "Plot of melting temperature\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)\, vs. crystallisation temperature\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{cr\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12776
name: isotropisation temperature
def: "Temperature at which a transition from any anisotropic state to an isotropic liquid occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12777
name: Kauzmann temperature
def: "Temperature at which the extrapolation of the entropy of a super-cooled liquid is equal to the entropy of its crystalline state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12778
name: Kohlrausch–Williams–Watts equation
def: "Equation describing the enthalpy relaxation\, \\\(\\Delta H\\\) which is observed during the annealing of polymers in the glassy state at a temperature\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\, lower than \\\(T_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) \\\[\\Delta H\(t_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\) = \\Delta c_\{p\}\(T_\{\\rm\{g\}\} - T_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\) \\exp\\!\\left\[- \\left \(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}\{\\tau\} \\right\)^\{\\beta\} \\right\]\\\] \\\(\\Delta H\\\) = difference of the enthalpy in the initial\, glassy state\, and the enthalpy of the glassy state after annealing; \\\(t_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) = time of annealing; \\\(\\Delta c_\{p\}\\\) = difference in heat capacity at the glass-transition temperature\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\)\, and the starting temperature of the annealing process\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\); \\\(\\tau\\\) = relaxation time at \\\(T_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\); \\\(\\beta\\\) = exponential factor."
synonym: "KWW Equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12779
name: linear thermodilatometry
def: "Technique in which one dimension of a substance under negligible load is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12780
name: logarithmic decrement
def: "Natural logarithm of the ratio of the displacement of a damping curve separated by one period of the displacement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12781
name: loss curve
def: "Plot of loss tangent \(\\\(\\tan \\delta\\\)\) vs. the temperature or the frequency of the forced sinusoidal oscillation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12782
name: loss modulus
def: "Ratio of the amplitude of the stress \\\(\\pu\{90\\!^\{\\circ\}\}\\\) out of phase with the strain \(\\\(\\sigma_\{0\} \\sin \\delta\\\)\) to the amplitude of the strain \(\\\(\\gamma_\{0\}\\\)\) in the forced sinusoidal oscillation of a material. \\\[M^\{\\prime\\prime\} = \\frac\{\\sigma_\{0\} \\sin \\delta\}\{\\gamma_\{0\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12783
name: loss tangent
def: "Tangent of the phase angle \(\\\(\\delta\\\)\) between stress and strain during a forced sinusoidal oscillation."
synonym: "loss factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12784
name: master curve
def: "Viscoelastic spectrum of a polymer or polymer material constructed using the time–temperature superposition \(TTS\) principle\, so that the spectrum covers a wide range of lengths of time or frequencies of measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12785
name: mechanical melting
def: "Mechanically induced transition of matter in the crystalline or glassy state to its melt."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12786
name: melt
def: "Matter in the amorphous state above its glass-transition temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12787
name: melt flow index
def: "Mass of a polymer in grams that flows through a calibrated orifice at constant temperature under a specified load over a \\\(\\pu\{10\\!\{-\}min\}\\\) period."
synonym: "MFR" []
synonym: "melt flow rate" []
synonym: "melt mass flow rate" []
synonym: "melt volume flow" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12788
name: melting temperature
def: "Temperature at which the last trace of crystallinity disappears upon heating."
synonym: "melting point" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12789
name: modulated heat flow
def: "Heat flow in a modulated-temperature DSC experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12790
name: modulated-temperature DSC
def: "DSC experiment in which a small sinusoidal modulation overlays the change in temperature with time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12791
name: multiplexing
def: "Technique used in dynamic mechanical analysis in which a specimen is subjected to the superposition of a number of forced sinusoidal oscillations of different frequencies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12792
name: non-reversing heat flow
def: "In modulated-temperature DSC\, the component of the total heat flow that is out-of-phase with the temperature modulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12793
name: polymer glass
def: "Amorphous polymer at temperatures below its glass-transition temperature."
synonym: "glassy polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12794
name: polymer melt
def: "Polymer in the amorphous state above its glass-transition temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12795
name: relaxation map
def: "Plot of the logarithm of the relaxation time vs. the reciprocal temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12796
name: relaxation spectrum
def: "Range of relaxation times describing stress relaxation in a polymer."
synonym: "spectrum of relaxation times" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12797
name: reversing heat flow
def: "In modulated-temperature DSC the heat-capacity component of the total heat flow that is in-phase with the temperature modulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12798
name: secondary relaxation
def: "Second-\, third-\, fourth-\, … highest-temperature or second-\, third-\, fourth-\, … lowest-frequency relaxation in the loss curve of a polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12799
name: secondary relaxation peak
def: "Any peak in a loss curve either at a lower temperature than the temperature of the alpha relaxation peak or at a higher frequency than the frequency of the alpha relaxation peak."
synonym: "secondary loss peak" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12800
name: secondary relaxation temperature
def: "Temperature characterising a secondary relaxation peak identified as the temperature at which the loss modulus \(or loss tangent\)\, measured by DMA or DETA\, exhibits a maximum at the measurement frequency\, due to the occurrence of localised motions involving only short segments of the polymer chains\, or side groups of the polymer chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12801
name: shift factor
def: "Multiplier used to change the interval of time or the range frequencies of measurement of a viscoelastic property at a given temperature\, \\\(T_\{1\}\\\)\, so that the values of the property superimpose on the values measured at a reference temperature\, \\\(T_\{0\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12802
name: storage modulus
def: "Ratio of the amplitude of the stress in phase with the strain \(\\\(\\sigma_\{0\} \\cos \\delta\\\)\) to the amplitude of the strain \(\\\(\\gamma_\{0\}\\\)\) in the forced sinusoidal oscillation of a material. \\\[M^\{\\prime\} = \\frac\{\\sigma_\{0\} \\cos \\delta\}\{\\gamma_\{0\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:12803
name: stress relaxation
def: "Change in stress with time after the instantaneous application of a constant strain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12804
name: strong glass
def: "Glass characterised by a small value of the fragility index \(\\\(m\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12805
name: supercooled polymer melt
def: "Metastable melt of a crystallisable polymer at temperatures below its equilibrium melting temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12806
name: Tammann’s 2/3rd rule
def: "Empirical rule that states that in a partially crystalline polymer the glass-transition temperature can be reasonably approximated by a value equal to two-thirds of the melting temperature\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)/\\\(\\pu\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12807
name: thermal history
def: "Thermal treatment a polymer material has experienced before its characterisation or measurement of its properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12808
name: thermal lag
def: "Delay of the response of a system to a change in temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12809
name: thermogravimetric analyser
def: "Apparatus for conducting thermogravimetric analysis."
synonym: "thermobalance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12810
name: thermoluminescence analysis
def: "Technique that monitors the light that is emitted from a sample against time or temperature while the temperature of the sample\, in a specified atmosphere\, is heated in a controlled manner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12811
name: thermomechanical analyser
def: "Apparatus for conducting thermomechanical analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12812
name: thermomechanical properties
def: "The temperature dependence of deformability or mechanical properties of a polymer sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12813
name: time–temperature superposition principle
def: "Principle stating that the value of a viscoelastic property measured at a given temperature\, after a given length of time or at a given frequency\, is equal to the value of the property at a different temperature after a length of time or at a frequency equal to the given length of time or given frequency multiplied by a shift factor\, \\\(a_\{T\}\\\) \, with the value of the shift factor being independent of length of the time or frequency."
synonym: "TTS Principle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12814
name: torsion pendulum
def: "Device consisting of a disc or other body of large moment of inertia mounted on one end of a torsionally flexible elastic rod or wire\, the other end of which is fixed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12815
name: torsional pendulum analysis
def: "Dynamic mechanical analysis performed using a torsion pendulum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12816
name: Vicat softening temperature
def: "Temperature at which a thermoplastic polymer reaches a specific degree of softness as measured by a standard test."
synonym: "Vicat hardness" []
synonym: "Vicat softening point" []
synonym: "softening point" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12817
name: viscoelastic spectrum
def: "Double logarithmic plot of a viscoelastic property vs. frequency of measurement or interval of time of measurement at constant temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12818
name: vitrification
def: "Formation of a polymer glass from a polymer melt."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12819
name: Vogel–Fulcher–Tammann equation
def: "Equation describing the time-dependence of a relaxation time that deviates from an Arrhenius-type temperature dependence. \\\[\\tau = \\tau_\{0\} \\exp\\!\\left\(\{\\frac\{B\}\{T - T_\{0\}\}\}\\right\)\\\] \\\(\\tau\\\)/\\\(\\pu\{s\}\\\) = relaxation time; \\\(\\tau_\{0\}\\\)/\\\(\\pu\{s\}\\\) = relaxation time at the Kauzmann temperature\, \\\(B\\\)/\\\(\\pu\{K\}\\\) = constant; \\\(T\\\)/\\\(\\pu\{K\}\\\) = temperature; \\\(T_\{0\}\\\)/\\\(\\pu\{K\}\\\) = Kauzmann temperature."
synonym: "VFT Equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12820
name: volume thermodilatometry
def: "Technique in which the volume of a substance under negligible load is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is heated or cooled in a controlled programme."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12821
name: Williams–Landel–Ferry equation
def: "Equation describing the temperature dependence of the shift factor\, \\\(a_\{T\}\\\)\, for time–temperature superposition \\\[\\log_\{10\}a_\{\\rm\{T\}\} = \\frac\{-C_\{1\}\(T_\{1\} - T_\{0\}\)\}\{C_\{2\} + \(T_\{1\} - T_\{0\}\)\}\\\] \\\(C_\{1\}\\\) and \\\(C_\{2\}\\\) are empirical constants\, \\\(T\\\) is the temperature of measurement\, and \\\(T_\{0\}\\\) is the reference temperature."
synonym: "WLF Equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12822
name: coordination polymer
def: "A coordination compound with repeating coordination entities extending in 1\, 2\, or 3 dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12823
name: coordination network
def: "A coordination compound extending\, through repeating coordination entities\, in one dimension\, but with cross-links between two or more individual chains\, loops\, or spiro-links\, or a coordination compound extending through repeating coordination entities in two or three dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12824
name: metal-organic framework
def: "A metal-organic framework\, abbreviated to MOF\, is a coordination network with organic ligands containing potential voids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12825
name: α\:β T-cell receptor
def: "T-cell receptor \(TCR\) consisting of a heterodimer of two different glycoprotein chains\, designated \\\(\\upalpha\\\) and \\\(\\upbeta\\\)\, assembled into an \\\(\\upalpha\{\:\}\\upbeta\\\) heterodimer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12826
name: α\:β T cell
def: "Lymphocyte whose T-cell receptor \(TCR\) is a heterodimer of an \\\(\\upalpha\\\) chain and a \\\(\\upbeta\\\) chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12827
name: ABL oncogene
def: "Gene resulting from a chromosomal translocation \(9;22\) that fuses sequences from the BCR \(breakpoint cluster region\) gene with the ABL gene."
synonym: "BCR-ABL" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12828
name: ABO blood group system
def: "System of antigens expressed on erythrocytes and used for typing human blood for transfusion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12829
name: abzyme
def: "Antibody or antibody construct with catalytic activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12830
name: accessory cell
def: "Cell that assists in the adaptive immune response but does not directly mediate specific antigen recognition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12831
name: accessory molecule
def: "Molecule other than immunoglobulin \(Ig\)\, T-cell receptor \(TCR\)\, or major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecule that participates in T lymphocyte recognition and response to antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12832
name: acquired immunity
def: "State of protection against pathogen-induced injury\, with rapid immune elimination of pathogenic invaders owing to previous immunization or vaccination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12833
name: acquired immunodeficiency syndrome
def: "Disease caused by infection with the human immunodeficiency virus \(HIV\)\, assuming clinical relevance when an infected patient has lost most of his/her CD4+ T cells\, so that infections with opportunistic pathogens occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12834
name: activation-induced cell death
def: "Process by which immune responses end in the death of most of the responding lymphocytes\, leaving only a small number of resting memory cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12835
name: active immunization
def: "Immunization with antigen\, as distinct from the transfer of antibody to an unimmunized individual\, which is called passive immunization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12836
name: active systemic anaphylaxis test
def: "Test for determining whether a drug can cause anaphylactic reactions in an animal following immunization with the drug."
synonym: "ASA test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12837
name: acute lymphoblastic leukemia
def: "Highly aggressive\, undifferentiated form of lymphoid malignancy derived from a progenitor cell that is thought to be able to give rise to both T- and B-cell lineages."
synonym: "Acute Lymphocytic Leukemia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12838
name: acute myelogenous leukemia
def: "Cancer characterized by rapid growth of abnormal granulocytes that accumulate in the bone marrow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12839
name: acute-phase protein
def: "Serum protein\, mostly produced in the liver\, which rapidly changes in concentration \(some acute-phase proteins increase\, some decrease\) during the initiation of an inflammatory response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12840
name: acute-phase response
def: "Physiological response stimulated by cytokines including interleukin-1\, interleukin-6\, interferons\, and tumor necrosis factor \(TNF\)\, characterized by increased vascular permeability\, fever\, and increased levels of proteins \(thus called acute-phase proteins\) such as C-reactive protein \(CRP\)\, occurring within a few hours of initiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12841
name: acute rejection
def: "Rejection of a tissue or organ graft from a genetically unrelated donor\, typically occurring within 10 to 13 days of transplantation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12842
name: acute respiratory distress syndrome
def: "Acute lung failure characterized by alveolar and interstitial edema\, perivascular pulmonary edema\, and hyaline membrane formation\, resulting from a variety of underlying diseases that result in increased pulmonary vascular permeability."
synonym: "adult respiratory distress syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12843
name: adaptive immune response
def: "Immune response based on the principle of clonal recognition\, such that upon first exposure to an antigen\, primed lymphocytes either differentiate into immune effector cells or form an expanded pool of memory cells that respond to secondary exposure to the same antigen by mounting an amplified and more rapid response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12844
name: adaptive immune system
def: "Part of the immune system responsible for the adaptive immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12845
name: Addison disease
def: "Adrenocortical hypofunction characterized by hypotension\, weight loss\, anorexia\, and weakness. The most common form is idiopathic Addison disease\, mediated by autoimmune mechanisms. Autoantibodies specific to the adrenal cortex are specific diagnostic markers of this form."
synonym: "Addison autoimmune disease" []
synonym: "Addison's autoimmune disease" []
synonym: "Addison's disease" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12846
name: addressin
def: "Extracellular protein of the venular endothelium serving as a ligand to a homing receptor for lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12847
name: adenosine deaminase deficiency
def: "Lack of the enzyme adenosine deaminase \(ADA\)\, which catalyzes the deamination of adenosine and deoxyadenosine to produce inosine and deoxyinosine\, respectively. Affected individuals have a form of severe combined immunodeficiency \(SCID\)."
synonym: "ADA Deficiency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12848
name: adhesion molecule
def: "Molecule belonging mainly to the immunoglobulins\, integrin superfamily \(e.g.\, LFA-1\, ICAM-1\) or selectins\, expressed on the cell membrane of various cells including those of the immune system. Interaction of adhesion molecules with each other as receptor and corresponding ligand facilitates cooperation \(cross-talk\) of cells\, signal transduction\, and information transfer between cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12849
name: adjuvant arthritis
def: "Experimental model of immunopathology with features of rheumatoid arthritis \(RA\)\, induced in rats by injection with bacterial products\, which may be used to study anti-inflammatory therapies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12850
name: adoptive transfer
def: "Transfer\, by transplantation of immunocompetent cells\, of the capacity to mount an immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12851
name: adverse drug reaction
def: "Appreciably harmful or unpleasant reaction resulting from an intervention related to the administration of a pharmaceutical product."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12852
name: adverse immunostimulation
def: "Antigen-non-specific\, inappropriate\, or unintended activation of a component of the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12853
name: aeroallergen
def: "Any airborne particle\, such as a pollen grain or spore\, that triggers an allergic reaction in sensitive individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12854
name: affinity maturation
def: "Increase in antibody affinity for an antigen observed as the humoral immune response progresses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12855
name: agranulocytosis
def: "Failure of the bone marrow to make sufficient granulocytes\, especially neutrophils."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12856
name: allelic exclusion
def: "Phenomenon whereby\, following successful rearrangement of one allele of an antigen receptor gene\, rearrangement of the other parental allele is suppressed\, thereby ensuring each lymphocyte expresses only a single specificity of antigen receptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12857
name: allergen
def: "Immunostimulant antigenic substance that may or may not cause a clinically significant effect but which is capable of producing an immune reaction\, often immediate-type hypersensitivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12858
name: allergic
def: "Immunologically hypersensitive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12859
name: allergy
def: "Symptoms or signs occurring in sensitized individuals following exposure to a previously encountered substance \(allergen\) that would otherwise not cause such symptoms or signs in non-sensitized individuals. The most common forms of allergy are rhinitis\, urticaria\, asthma\, and contact dermatitis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12860
name: alloantibody
def: "Antibody produced against an antigen from another member of the same species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12861
name: alloantigen
def: "Antigen\, present in some but not all individuals of a particular species\, that arises from polymorphisms at the major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) loci and stimulates intense reactions to allograft tissues from other individuals of the same species that do not produce it."
synonym: "isoantigen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12862
name: allogeneic
def: "Genetically different\, referring to individuals of the same species."
synonym: "allogenic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12863
name: allograft
def: "Tissue or organ graft between allogeneic individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12864
name: allotype
def: "Allelic variant of an antigen that\, because it is not present in all individuals\, may be immunogenic in members of the same species that have a different version of the allele."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12865
name: alloreactivity
def: "Reactivity of an antibody with an alloantigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12866
name: alternative pathway
def: "Activation pathway involving complement components C3\, Factor B\, Factor D\, and properdin that\, in the presence of a stabilizing activator surface such as microbial polysaccharide\, generates the alternative pathway C3 convertase C3bBb."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12867
name: alveolar macrophage
def: "Macrophage found in the lung alveoli; it may remove and sometimes retain inhaled particulate matter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12868
name: alveolitis
def: "1\) Inflammation of alveoli.\n2\) Inflammation of a tooth socket."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12869
name: amyloid
def: "Fibrous protein secreted into the extracellular space where it forms amorphous deposits in multiple organs in some pathological states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12870
name: anamnestic
def: "Describing immunological memory that gives rise to a rapid increase in immunological response after reexposure to antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12871
name: anaphylactic shock
def: "Immediate overreaction of the immune system to a drug or other agent in an individual who has previously encountered the agent and has produced antibodies to that agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12872
name: anaphylactoid
def: "Of or resembling anaphylaxis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12873
name: anaphylatoxin
def: "Complement-derived protein fragment \(e.g.\, C3a\, C4a\, or C5a\) capable of directly triggering mast cell degranulation\, chemotaxis\, smooth muscle contraction\, and inflammation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12874
name: anaphylaxis
def: "Life-threatening type I hypersensitivity allergic reaction occurring in a person or animal exposed to an antigen or hapten to which they have previously been sensitized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12875
name: anergy
def: "Lack of an immune response\, usually taken as lack of response to common recall antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12876
name: angioedema
def: "Swelling that occurs in the tissue just below the surface of the skin\, most often around the lips and eyes\, the mucous membranes and occasionally the viscera."
synonym: "angioneurotic edema" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12877
name: ankylosing spondylitis
def: "Chronic inflammatory disease affecting the spine\, sacroiliac joints\, and large peripheral joints\, having a major genetic predisposition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12878
name: therapeutic antibody
def: "Antibody administered with the aim of treating a disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12879
name: antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity
def: "Cytotoxic reaction in which an antibody-coated target cell is directly killed by an Fc receptor-bearing leukocyte\, e.g.\, NK cell\, macrophage\, neutrophil\, or eosinophil."
synonym: "antibody-dependent cell-mediated cytotoxicity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12880
name: antibody-forming cell assay
def: "Assay that measures the humoral immune response mediated by the concerted actions of antigen-presenting cells\, T lymphocytes\, and B lymphocytes\, generally by determination of murine primary IgM or IgG antibodies directed against the T-cell-dependent sheep red blood cell \(SRBC\) surface antigens after in vivo sensitization."
synonym: "AFC assay" []
synonym: "Jerne plaque assay" []
synonym: "hemolytic plaque assay" []
synonym: "plaque-forming cell (PFC) assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12881
name: antibody therapy
def: "Use of an antibody to target specific cells \(often tumor cells\) or signal transduction pathways \(often via chemokines/cytokines\) in the treatment of disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12882
name: lupus anticoagulant
def: "Autoantibody that binds to phospholipids and/or proteins of the cell membrane in systemic lupus erythematosus \(SLE\)\, and can interfere with blood clotting and tests of clotting function."
synonym: "antibody" []
synonym: "lupus" []
synonym: "lupus antibody" []
synonym: "lupus anticoagulant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12883
name: anti-DNA antibody
def: "Antibody directed against single-stranded or double-stranded DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12884
name: anti-erythrocyte antibody
def: "Antibody against molecules of the red blood cell\, usually against membrane proteins\, causing lysis of the red blood cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12885
name: antigenic
def: "Capable of stimulating lymphocytes to produce antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12886
name: antigenicity
def: "Ability of an antigen to bind to a specific antibody or T-cell receptor \(TCR\); often a measure of its ability to produce immunity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12887
name: antigen presentation
def: "Display of antigen as peptide fragments bound to major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules on the surface of a cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12888
name: antigen-presenting cell
def: "Cell\, such as a dendritic cell or macrophage\, that is responsible for making antigens accessible to lymphocytes and other immune effector and regulatory cells\, making possible specific recognition by receptors on the cell surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12889
name: antigen-presenting groove
def: "1\) Pocket in a major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecule that binds antigen for antigen presentation.\n2\) Hydrophobic binding region in the CD1 protein that anchors lipid-containing antigens causing exposure of the peptide or carbohydrate moiety in a position enabling T-cell receptor \(TCR\) contact."
synonym: "antigen-binding groove" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12890
name: antigen processing
def: "Cleavage of protein antigens in antigen-presenting cells \(APCs\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12891
name: antigen receptor
def: "Specific antigen-binding receptor on T or B lymphocytes that is transcribed and translated following rearrangements and translocation of V\, D\, and J genes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12892
name: antigen recognition
def: "Ability of highly specialized proteins of the immune system to recognize antigens and specifically bind to them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12893
name: anti-idiotypic antibody
def: "Antibody raised against antigenic determinants unique to the variable region of a single antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12894
name: anti-isotypic antibody
def: "Antibody against universal features of a given constant \(C\) region isotype \(such as $\\upgamma\\!$ or $\\upmu\\!$\) of one species that is made by immunizing a member of another species with that isotype."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12895
name: antimitochondrial antibody
def: "Autoantibody producing a mitochondrial staining pattern on sections of various tissues and on tumor cell monolayers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12896
name: antineutrophil cytoplasmic autoantibody
def: "Antibody against antigens in the cytoplasm of neutrophils."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12897
name: antineutrophil cytoplasmic autoantibody associated vasculitis
def: "One of a group of autoimmune systemic vasculitides associated with antineutrophil cytoplasmic autoantibodies \(ANCAs\)\, e.g.\, Wegener granulomatosis \(WG\)\, microscopic polyangiitis\, and Churg–Strauss syndrome."
synonym: "ANCA-associated vasculitis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12898
name: antinuclear antibody
def: "Antibody\, detectable in the blood\, that has the capability of binding to a substance\, e.g.\, DNA\, within the nucleus of the cells."
synonym: "antinuclear factor (ANF)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12899
name: antiphospholipid syndrome
def: "One of the most common autoimmune diseases\, characterized by thrombosis\, recurrent spontaneous abortions\, and the presence of antiphospholipid antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12900
name: anti-sheep red blood cell IgM response assay
def: "Test to determine T-cell-dependent antibody response\, usually by enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay \(ELISA\). Sheep red blood cells \(SRBCs\) are used as an antigen. Often referred to as the plaque assay."
synonym: "SRBC Assay" []
synonym: "plaque assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12901
name: antitoxin
def: "Antibody specific for exotoxins produced by certain microorganisms such as the causative agents of diphtheria and tetanus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12902
name: aplastic anemia
def: "Greatly decreased formation of erythrocytes and hemoglobin\, usually associated with pronounced granulocytopenia and thrombocytopenia\, as a result of hypoplastic or aplastic bone marrow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12903
name: arthritis
def: "Inflammation of a joint or joints."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12904
name: Arthus reaction
def: "Local antibody-mediated hypersensitivity reaction in which antigen-antibody complexes which fix complement are deposited in the walls of small vessels\, often in the skin\, causing acute inflammation with an infiltration of neutrophils."
synonym: "Gell and Coombs Type III reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12905
name: asthma
def: "Chronic respiratory disease characterized by bronchoconstriction\, excessive mucus secretion\, and edema of the pulmonary alveoli\, resulting in difficulty in exhaling\, wheezing\, and cough."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12906
name: ataxia telangiectasia
def: "Disease characterized by staggering gait\, multiple disorganized blood vessels\, and an immunodeficiency\, associated with a protein called ataxia telangiectasia-mutated \(ATM\)\, a protein kinase thought to be important in detection of double-stranded DNA breaks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12907
name: atopic allergy
def: "Immunoglobulin E \(IgE\)-mediated hypersensitivity\, including asthma\, eczema\, hay fever\, and food allergy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12908
name: atopic dermatitis
def: "Inflammation of the skin in atopic individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12909
name: atopic eczema
def: "Chronic skin disease\, often localized on flexural surfaces\, in individuals with propensity to develop immunoglobulin E \(IgE\)-mediated allergy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12910
name: atopy
def: "Of\, relating to\, or caused by a hereditary predisposition toward developing certain hypersensitivity reactions\, such as hay fever\, asthma\, or chronic urticaria\, upon exposure to specific antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12911
name: attenuated vaccine
def: "Vaccine made from a live organism that targets cells of the immune system but has been engineered or weakened so as not to cause disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12912
name: atypical interstitial pneumonia
def: "Acute or chronic respiratory distress in cattle\, in the absence of toxemia or other systemic signs that are characteristic of other pneumonias."
synonym: "exogen allergic alveolitis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12913
name: autoantibody
def: "Immunoglobulin \(Ig\) antibody that is directed against the organism’s own antigen\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12914
name: autoantigen
def: "Antigenic component of an individual’s tissues which may be a target of autoimmune responses by autoreactive B cells \(autoantibodies\) or T cells\, including proteins \(e.g.\, enzymes\, structural proteins\)\, glycoproteins \(e.g.\, 2-glycoprotein I\)\, nucleic acids\, \(e.g.\, double-stranded DNA\)\, phospholipids \(e.g.\, cardiolipin\)\, and glycosphingolipids \(e.g.\, gangliosides\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12915
name: autochthonous
def: "Pertaining to self."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12916
name: autocrine
def: "Type of signaling in which a cell secretes a chemical messenger that binds to receptors on the same cell\, leading to changes in the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12917
name: autograft
def: "Tissue transplant from one site to another in an individual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12918
name: autoimmune
def: "Of or relating to an immune response by the body against one of its own cells or tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12919
name: autoimmune disease
def: "Pathological condition resulting when an organism produces antibodies or specific cells that bind to constituents of its own tissues \(autoantigens\) and cause tissue injury."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12920
name: autoimmune hemolytic anemia
def: "Autoimmune disease in which antibodies initiate complement lysis of erythrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12921
name: autoimmune hepatitis
def: "Chronic autoimmune-mediated hepatic inflammation usually characterized by antinuclear \(ANA\)\, smooth muscle \(SMA\)/anti-F-actin\, liver-kidney microsomal \(LKM\)\, and/or soluble liver antigen \(SLA\) antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12922
name: autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome
def: "Disease characterized by lymphadenopathy\, hepatosplenomegaly\, autoimmune cytopenias\, and hypergammaglobulinemia."
synonym: "Canale–Smith syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12923
name: autoimmune polyendocrine syndrome type 1 or 2
def: "Heterogeneous group of rare diseases characterized by autoimmune activity against more than one endocrine organ\, although non-endocrine organs can also be affected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12924
name: autoimmune regulator
def: "DNA-binding protein involved in immunoregulation \(probably in the establishment and maintenance of tolerance\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12925
name: autoimmunity
def: "Immune response to 'self' tissues or components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12926
name: autoinflammatory disease
def: "Unregulated inflammation without significant levels of autoantibodies or autoactivated T cells\, or evidence of infection\, but rather caused by genetic disturbance of the mechanisms that initiate and control inflammation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12927
name: autologous
def: "From the same individual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12928
name: autologous antibody
def: "Antibody derived from a specific individual\, acting within that individual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12929
name: autophagy
def: "Digestion and breakdown in lysosomes of a cell’s own proteins and/or organelles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12930
name: autoreactivity
def: "Immune response directed at self-antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12931
name: β2-microglobulin
def: "Small \(\\\(\\pu\{12 kDa\}\\\)\) protein forming part of the structure of major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I-encoded molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12932
name: B-1/B-2 cells
def: "Two major subpopulations of B lymphocytes."
synonym: "B-1/B-2 lymphocytes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12933
name: B7 molecule
def: "Co-stimulatory molecule on the surface of T-cell-activating antigen-presenting cells \(APCs\)\, allowing full activation of T cells that are bound to major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\)-peptide complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12934
name: B-cell linker protein
def: "Adaptor protein operating in B cells that\, upon phosphorylation\, recruits signaling molecules to membrane lipid rafts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12935
name: B-cell stimulatory factor
def: "Generic name given to B-cell-specific growth and differentiation factors involved in the T-cell-dependent activation of B cells. Many are now identified as specific interleukins \(ILs\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12936
name: B-cell receptor
def: "Receptor on the surface of a B cell consisting of transmembrane immunoglobulin \(Ig\) that recognizes a specific antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12937
name: B lymphocyte
def: "Bone marrow-derived lymphocyte\, expressing an antigen-receptor complex composed of membrane-bound immunoglobulin \(Ig\) and associated molecular chains."
synonym: "B cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12938
name: B-lymphocyte chemokine
def: "Chemokine that attracts B cells and activated T cells into the follicles of peripheral lymphoid tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12939
name: B-lymphocyte-induced maturation protein 1
def: "Transcriptional repressor in B cells\, that switches off genes required for B cell proliferation in the germinal center\, and for class switching and affinity maturation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12940
name: bacille Calmette–Guérin
def: "Attenuated Mycobacterium tuberculosis used both as a specific vaccine for tuberculosis and as an adjuvant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12941
name: BALB/c mouse
def: "Inbred albino mouse strain\, substrains of which produce plasmacytomas on injection with mineral oil\, useful for the production of monoclonal antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12942
name: bare lymphocyte syndrome
def: "Rare\, recessive genetic condition in which the products of one or more genes required to switch on major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I or MHC class II genes are defective or absent. As a consequence\, MHC class I \(BLS1\) or MHC class II \(BLS2\) genes are not expressed\, leading to severe immunodeficiency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12943
name: basophil
def: "Type of granulocyte found in the blood and resembling the tissue mast cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12944
name: basophilic degranulation
def: "Loss of granules in basophilic cells\, associated with the release of active substances from the cells\, characteristic of type I immediate hypersensitivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12945
name: Bcl-2
def: "Member of the Bcl protein family that protects cells from apoptosis by binding to the mitochondrial membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12946
name: Behcet disease
def: "Chronic vasculitis of unknown origin\, characterized by ulcerations and skin rash and treated by immunosuppression."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12947
name: Bence–Jones protein
def: "Excess circulating kappa \($\\kappa$\)-light chain found in the urine of patients with multiple myeloma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12948
name: benign monoclonal gammopathy
def: "Nonmalignant overproduction of $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}globulin\}$ by a single clone of lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12949
name: biolistics
def: "Use of small particles\, e.g.\, colloidal gold\, as a vehicle for carrying agents \(drugs\, nucleic acid\, etc.\) into a cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12950
name: biopanning
def: "Technique for selection of peptides with high affinity binding to a chosen target."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12951
name: bispecific antibody
def: "Artificially produced hybrid antibody in which each of the two antigen-binding arms is specific for a different antigenic epitope. Such antibodies\, which can be produced either by chemical cross-linkage or by recombinant DNA techniques\, can be used to link together two different antigens or cells\, e.g.\, a cytotoxic T lymphocyte and a tumor cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12952
name: blast
def: "Immature stage in cell development\, before the appearance of the definitive characteristics of the cell; used also as a word termination\, as in erythroblast\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12953
name: Blau syndrome
def: "Rare familial inflammatory disease characterized by arthritis\, dermatitis\, and uveitis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12954
name: blocking antibody
def: "Antibody that prevents other antibodies from combining with a specific antigen but does not itself produce an immunological response when combined with that antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12955
name: blood dyscrasia
def: "Presence of abnormal material in the blood\, usually applied to diseases affecting blood cells or platelets."
synonym: "dyscrasia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12956
name: blood group antigen
def: "Surface antigen on erythrocytes\, detectable with a specific antibody from other individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12957
name: blood–placenta barrier
def: "Physiological interface between maternal and fetal blood circulations that filters out some substances that could harm the fetus while favoring the passage of others such as nutrients."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12958
name: Bloom syndrome
def: "Disease caused by mutations in a DNA helicase and characterized by low T cell numbers\, reduced antibody levels\, and an increased susceptibility to respiratory infections\, cancer\, and radiation damage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12959
name: bone marrow
def: "Soft fatty vascular substance in the cavity of bone where blood cells are formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12960
name: bone marrow transplantation
def: "Transfer of bone marrow from a donor to a recipient whose bone marrow has been ablated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12961
name: booster
def: "Portion of an immunizing agent given at a later time to stimulate the effects of a previous dose of the same agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12962
name: bradykinin
def: "Vasoactive peptide and inflammatory mediator produced at sites of tissue damage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12963
name: bronchial provocation test
def: "Test of alteration of lung function induced by inhalation of an allergen or airway-constricting agent\, providing information on bronchial responsiveness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12964
name: Buehler assay
def: "Skin sensitization test for contact allergic dermatitis in which a test substance is applied to the shaved flank of a guinea pig in an occlusive patch for 6 h at 0\, 1\, and 2 weeks\, followed by challenge of the untreated flank at 4 weeks."
synonym: "Buehler test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12965
name: autoimmune bullous skin disease
def: "Any of several autoimmune diseases characterized by intraepidermal or subepidermal blisters \(e.g.\, pemphigus vulgaris\, bullous pemphigoid\) and highly specific autoantibodies against components of the desmosome or hemidesmosome \(e.g.\, desmoglein 3\, BP180\)."
synonym: "autoimmune bullous skin disease" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12966
name: Burkitt lymphoma
def: "Lymphoma caused by Epstein–Barr virus \(EBV\)\, occurring mainly in sub-Saharan Africa."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12967
name: bursa of Fabricius
def: "Primary lymphoid organ in avian species\, located at the cloacal-hind gut junction; it is the site of B-cell maturation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12968
name: bystander effect
def: "Positive or negative effect on the immune system seen after exposure to certain drugs\, radiation\, and other agents\, induced by non-immune mechanisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12969
name: bystander suppression
def: "Suppression of an immune response to an antigen due to tolerance to an unrelated \(hence bystander\) antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12970
name: C1 inhibitor
def: "Plasma glycoprotein secreted primarily by the liver and acting as a serine proteinase inhibitor \(serpin\)\, inhibiting C1 components of the complement system."
synonym: "C1 esterase inhibitor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12971
name: CD1
def: "Cell surface protein of antigen-presenting cells \(APCs\) involved in presentation of lipopeptide or glycolipid antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12972
name: CD3
def: "Molecule composed of five polypeptide chains associated with the heterodimer T-cell receptor \(TCR\)\, forming the T-cell receptor complex \(TCR/CD3\); CD3 transduces the activating signals when antigen binds to the TCR."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12973
name: CD4
def: "Cell surface antigen belonging to the immunoglobulin \(Ig\) superfamily of molecules and a marker of T helper cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12974
name: CD4+/CD25+ T cell
def: "Subtype of CD4+ regulatory T cell \(Treg\) with potential role in the regulation of immune homeostasis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12975
name: CD5+ B lymphocyte
def: "Lymphocyte of type B-1a\, which is predominant in fetal lymphoid organs and in neonatal cord blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12976
name: CD8
def: "Cell surface molecule belonging to the immunoglobulin \(Ig\) superfamily of molecules found\, among others\, on cytotoxic T cells\, which binds to major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12977
name: CD8+ T suppressor cell
def: "Outdated term for CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocyte \(CTL\, Tc\). The term 'suppressor T cell' is nowadays reserved for regulatory T cell \(Treg\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12978
name: CD16
def: "Low-affinity $\\ce\{Fc\\mbox\{-\}\\upgamma\\! receptor\}$ \($\\ce\{Fc\\mbox\{-\}\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}RIII\}$\) expressed mainly on natural killer \(NK\) cells\, granulocytes\, and macrophages\, mediating antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity \(ADCC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12979
name: CD23
def: "Low-affinity $\{\\rm\{Fc\\mbox\{-\}\}\}\\upvarepsilon\\!$ receptor induced by interleukin-4 \(IL-4\) and expressed on see activated B cells and macrophages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12980
name: CD25
def: "\\\(\\ce\{\\upalpha\\mbox\{-\}chain\}\\\) of the interleukin-2 \(IL-2\) receptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12981
name: CD40 ligand
def: "Essential molecule for normal switching signaling through binding to CD40 on B cells. The interaction of CD40L and CD40 is also critical for optimal T cell function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12982
name: C-reactive protein
def: "Serum protein produced by liver cells as part of the acute-phase response\, which acts as a stimulus of the classical pathway of complement activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12983
name: C-type lectin
def: "Any of a large family of \\\(\\ce\{Ca^\{2+\}\}\\\)-dependent lectins; members of this family share primary structural homology in their carbohydrate-recognition domains and include many endocytic receptors\, many proteoglycans\, and all known collectins and selectins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12984
name: cadherin
def: "Calcium-dependent\, transmembrane glycoprotein occurring in cell-cell contacts \(desmosomes\) and functioning as an adhesion molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12985
name: calcineurin inhibitor
def: "Any member of a group of immunomodulating drugs that bind to the cytosolic protein cyclophilin of T lymphocytes\, thus inhibiting the phosphatase calcineurin\, an inducer of interleukin-2 \(IL-2\) formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12986
name: candidiasis
def: "Infection with a fungus of the genus Candida\, especially C. albicans\, that usually occurs in the skin and mucous membranes of the mouth\, respiratory tract\, or vagina but may invade the bloodstream\, especially in immunocompromised individuals."
synonym: "candidosis" []
synonym: "moniliasis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12987
name: capping
def: "Active process whereby cross-linking of cell surface molecules \(e.g.\, by antibody\) leads to aggregation and subsequent migration of the molecules to one pole of the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12988
name: carcinoembryonic antigen
def: "Membrane glycoprotein epitope normally present in the fetal gastrointestinal tract and elevated in many patients with various carcinomas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12989
name: cardiolipin
def: "1\,3-Bis\(sn-3-phosphatidyl\)-sn-glycerol\, the main target of antiphospholipid antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12990
name: caspase
def: "Any member of a family of intracellular cysteine proteinases that cleave proteins at specific aspartic acid residues to initiate or promote apoptosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12991
name: caveola
def: "Invagination on the cell surface \(plasma membrane\)\, enriched in caveolin proteins\, cholesterol\, and glycosphingolipids\, important in various processes including pinocytosis and signal transduction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12992
name: celiac disease
def: "Autoimmune disorder occurring in genetically predisposed individuals\, characterized by immune intolerance to the \\\(\\ce\{\\upalpha\\mbox\{-\}gliadin\}\\\) component of gluten\, a protein found in wheat\, barley\, and rye. Resultant inflammation of the mucosa of the upper small intestine is associated with malabsorption of nutrients and a wasting illness follows from this."
synonym: "gluten enteropathy" []
synonym: "gluten intolerance" []
synonym: "non-tropical sprue" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12993
name: cell-mediated cytotoxicity
def: "Lysis of a target cell initiated by a T lymphocyte binding to surface antibodies or antigen-bound major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12994
name: cell-mediated immune response
def: "Specific immune response in which T lymphocytes mediate the effects\, either through the release of cytokines or through cytotoxicity."
synonym: "cell-mediated response" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12995
name: cell-mediated immunity
def: "Immune response mediated by antigen-specific T lymphocytes\, either through the release of cytokines or through cytotoxicity\, in contrast with humoral immunity\, which is antibody-mediated."
synonym: "cellular immunity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:12996
name: central tolerance
def: "Specific immunological tolerance due to the induction of lymphocyte apoptosis or anergy within the primary lymphoid organs \(bone marrow in the case of B cell tolerance and the thymus for T cells\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12997
name: challenge
def: "Induction or evaluation of an immune response in an organism by administration of a specific antigen to which it has been sensitized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12998
name: chaperone-mediated autophagy
def: "Type of autophagy targeting only those proteins that are recognized by the binding of an hsc70-containing chaperone/co-chaperone complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:12999
name: chemotactic factor
def: "Biologically active substance\, such as a chemokine or anaphylatoxin\, that induces a concentration gradient-dependent movement of cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13000
name: chemotaxis
def: "1\) General. Movement of an organism or cell along a concentration gradient of a chemical.\n2\) In immunology. Movement of cells up a concentration gradient of a chemical attractant \(chemotactic factor\)\, such as a chemokine or other cytokine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13001
name: chemotype
def: "Character of a plant or microorganism based on a metabolite distinct from that found in other members of the same species."
synonym: "chemovar" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13002
name: chloracne
def: "Acne-like eruption caused by exposure to certain chlorinated organic substances such as polychlorinated biphenyls or 2\,3\,7\,8-tetrachlorodibenzo\[1\,4\]dioxin \[2\,3\,7\,8-tetrachlorooxanthrene\] and other polychlorinated dibenzodioxins and furans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13003
name: chromate uptake assay
def: "Method used in immunotoxicology to quantify the toxic effect of a substance on leukocytes by measuring uptake of \\\(\\ce\{^\{51\}Cr\{-\}labeled\}\\\) chromate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13004
name: chromium release assay
def: "Immunotoxicity assay that can be used to assess either natural killer \(NK\) cell or cytotoxic T cell activity or macrophage activity. When incubated with \\\(\\ce\{^\{51\}Cr\{-\}labeled\}\\\) chicken red blood cells \(cRBCs\)\, NK and cytotoxic T cells lyse the cRBC and thus induce the release of \\\(\\ce\{^\{51\}Cr\}\\\) into the medium. In contrast\, macrophages phagocytose cRBC\, and are then lysed to assess the amount of \\\(\\ce\{^\{51\}Cr\}\\\) they have taken up."
synonym: "<sup>51</sup>Cr release assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13005
name: chronic allergic inflammation
def: "Disease of airways\, skin\, eyes and other organs\, resulting from repeated or continuous exposure to an allergen and involving immunoglobulin E \(IgE\) formation\, IgE-induced histamine-release from mast cells\, liberation of mediators such as cytokines and prostaglandins\, and local inflammation characterized by infiltrating leucocytes; it results in augmented susceptibility to the allergen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13006
name: chronic granulomatous disease
def: "Immunodeficiency disease in which multiple granulomas form as a result of defective elimination of bacteria by phagocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13007
name: chronic lymphocytic leukemia
def: "B cell tumor found in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13008
name: chronic myelogenous leukemia
def: "Cancer characterized by overgrowth of the bone marrow with malignant white blood cells\, usually exhibiting a chromosomal abnormality \(Philadelphia chromosome\)\, which causes uncontrolled proliferation of cells that are released into peripheral blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13009
name: chronic rejection
def: "Immunologically triggered reaction occurring in a transplanted organ or tissue\, leading to progressive destruction and finally failure of the transplanted organ occurring two months to many years after transplantation."
synonym: "chronic graft rejection" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13010
name: class switching
def: "Process by which a B cell changes the class but not specificity of a given antibody it produces\, e.g.\, switching from an immunoglobulin M \(IgM\) to an immunoglobulin G \(IgG\) antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13011
name: classical pathway
def: "Activation pathway involving complement components C1\, C2\, and C4 which\, following fixation of C1q\, e.g.\, by antigen-antibody complexes\, produces the classical pathway C3 convertase C4b2a."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13012
name: clonal anergy
def: "Form of self-tolerance developing as a consequence of negative selection during thymic selection processes. Clones of thymocytes whose antigen receptors \[T-cell receptors \(TCRs\)\] bind with high affinity to self-antigens in association with major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules are inactivated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13013
name: clonal deletion
def: "Process by which contact with antigen \(e.g.\, self-antigen\) at an early stage of lymphocyte differentiation leads to cell death by apoptosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13014
name: clonal expansion
def: "Proliferation of B lymphocytes and T lymphocytes activated by clonal selection in order to produce a clone of identical cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13015
name: clonal indifference
def: "Failure of B or T cells expressing anti-self-receptors to interact with antigen \(e.g.\, by low valency\, low concentration\, or sequestration of antigens; low receptor avidity; or lack of co-stimulatory molecules\)."
synonym: "clonal ignorance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13016
name: clonal selection
def: "Selection and activation by antigen of a lymphocyte\, bearing a complementary receptor\, which then proliferates to form an expanded clone."
synonym: "selection theory (in immunology)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13017
name: cluster determinant
def: "Cluster of antigens representing a cell surface marker with which antibodies react. Not to be confused with cluster of differentiation \(CD\) antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13018
name: cluster of differentiation antigen
def: "Any of a family of molecular markers on cell surfaces that may be used operationally to define phenotype\, origin\, and activation state of the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13019
name: coagulation system
def: "Proteolytic cascade of plasma enzymes that triggers blood clotting when blood vessels are damaged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13020
name: cognate T cell
def: "Helper T lymphocyte \(Th\) primed by the same antigen as the B cell to which it gives help."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13021
name: cold autoantibody type
def: "Autoantibody that reacts optimally at low temperatures \(\\\(\\pu\{\{0\}\{-\}\{5\} ^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)\) with surface antigens of erythrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13022
name: collagen arthritis
def: "Autoimmune model of rheumatoid arthritis \(RA\)\, arising from combined cellular immunity and humoral immunity against collagen type II\, and characterized by rapid and severe erosions of cartilage and bone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13023
name: collectin
def: "Any member of a structurally related family of calcium-dependent carbohydrate-binding proteins or lectins containing collagen-like sequences\, e.g.\, mannose-binding lectin \(MBL\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13024
name: colony-stimulating factor
def: "Factor that permits the proliferation and differentiation of hematopoietic cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13025
name: combinatorial diversity
def: "Component of antibody and T-cell receptor \(TCR\) diversity that is generated by the recombination of variable \(V\)\, diversity \(\\\(D\\\)\, for immunoglobulin heavy chains\, and for TCR \\\(\\upbeta\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{\\updelta\\ chains\}\\\)\)\, and joining \(J\) gene segments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13026
name: combinatorial joining
def: "Merging of DNA segments generating new genetic information."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13027
name: common variable immunodeficiency
def: "Relatively common deficiency in antibody production\, of unknown pathogenesis but strongly associated with genes mapping within the major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13028
name: competitive inhibition assay
def: "Method of measuring an antigen in a sample using a labeled species of the antigen as a competitive inhibitor of its binding in a specific antigen–antibody reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13029
name: complementarity-determining region
def: "Hypervariable amino acid sequence within antibody and T-cell receptor \(TCR\) variable \(V\) regions\, which interacts with complementary amino acids on the antigen or major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\)–peptide complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13030
name: complement deficiency
def: "Congenital deficiency in any of the various components of the complement system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13031
name: complement reaction
def: "Physiological reaction to the presence of a foreign microorganism in which a cascade of enzymatic reactions\, triggered by molecular features of the microorganism\, result in lysis or phagocytosis of the foreign material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13032
name: complement receptor
def: "Cell-surface protein that recognizes and binds complement proteins that have bound an antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13033
name: complement system
def: "Group of serum proteins with the capacity to interact with each other when activated in a chain reaction which results in formation of a lytic complex and the release of several biologically active peptides of low relative molecular mass \(anaphylatoxins\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13034
name: concanavalin A
def: "Hemagglutinin\, isolated from jack bean \(Canavalia ensiformis\) meal\, which reacts with polyglucosans in the blood of mammals\, causing agglutination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13035
name: conformational epitope
def: "Epitope on a protein antigen formed from several separate regions in the primary sequence of a protein brought together by protein folding."
synonym: "discontinuous epitope" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13036
name: congenic
def: "Differing at a single genetic locus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13037
name: allergic conjunctivitis
def: "Irritation of the ocular conjunctiva\, resulting from the activation of conjunctival mast cells by airborne allergens such as pollens or house dust."
synonym: "allergic conjunctivitis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13038
name: constant gene
def: "Gene that encodes the constant \(C\) region of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) chains or T-cell receptor \(TCR\) chains."
synonym: "C gene" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13039
name: constant region
def: "Part of an immunoglobulin \(Ig\) or T-cell receptor \(TCR\) that is relatively constant in amino acid sequence among different Ig or TCR molecules."
synonym: "C region" []
synonym: "constant (C) region" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13040
name: contact allergen
def: "Substance that causes a skin hypersensitivity reaction by direct contact."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13041
name: contact dermatitis
def: "Skin inflammation that occurs when the skin’s surface comes in contact with a substance originating outside the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13042
name: contact hypersensitivity
def: "Immune response following skin contact with an antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13043
name: contact sensitivity
def: "State of immunological sensitization in which an eczematous epidermal reaction may occur when a hapten is applied to the skin of a sensitized individual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13044
name: contact urticaria
def: "Urticaria provoked by contact with inducing agents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13045
name: conventional dendritic cell
def: "Predominant type of dendritic cell active in antigen presentation to and activation of naïve T cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13046
name: convergent evolution
def: "Independent evolution of similarity between molecules or between species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13047
name: convertase
def: "Enzyme activity that converts complement protein into its reactive form by cleaving it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13048
name: Coombs test
def: "Diagnostic test using anti-immunoglobulin \(Ig\) to agglutinate antibody-coated erythrocytes."
synonym: "direct Coombs test" []
synonym: "direct antiglobulin test (DAT)" []
synonym: "indirect Coombs test" []
synonym: "indirect antiglobulin test (IAT)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13049
name: coreceptor
def: "Cell surface protein receptor that recognizes a substance bound to a primary receptor\, and binds to a signaling molecule that thereby enhances the activity of another receptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13050
name: corticosteroid
def: "Any of the family of steroid molecules that are produced in the adrenal cortex\, or their synthetic analogs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13051
name: co-stimulation
def: "Delivery of a second signal\, in addition to that of antigen binding or antigen presentation\, that is required for lymphocyte proliferation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13052
name: counter-regulation hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that all types of infection early in childhood may protect against the development of atopy by driving the production of cytokines such as interleukin-10 \(IL-10\) and transforming growth factor $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$ \($\\ce\{TGF\\mbox\{-\}\\upbeta\\!\}$\)\, which downregulate both Th1 and Th2 responses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13053
name: Crohn disease
def: "Chronic inflammatory bowel disease \(IBD\) thought to result from an abnormal overresponsiveness to commensal gut flora."
synonym: "Crohn's disease" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13054
name: cross-matching
def: "1\) Test for determining the compatibility between the blood of a donor and that of a recipient before transfusion; the clumping of erythrocytes indicates incompatibility.\n2\) Test for determining tissue compatibility between a transplant donor and the recipient before transplantation\, in which the recipient’s serum is tested for antibodies that may react with the lymphocytes or other cells of the donor.\n3\) Process of performing one of these tests."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13055
name: cross-presentation
def: "Activation of CD8+ lymphocytes by the presentation of exogenous antigens in association with major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I molecules."
synonym: "cross-priming" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13056
name: cross-reacting antibody
def: "Antibody that is able to react with an antigen that did not specifically stimulate its production."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13057
name: cross-reacting antigen
def: "Antigen that is able to react with an antibody induced by a different antigen.\nNote\: The two antigens may share the same antigenic determinants or carry determinants that are sufficiently alike stereochemically to enable the antibody to react with both.\nAntigen that has an identical structure in two strains of microorganism\, so that antibody raised against one strain will react with the other strain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13058
name: cross sensitivity
def: "State of immunological hypersensitivity to one substance that predisposes an individual to sensitivity to other substances that are related in chemical structure \(cross-reacting antigens\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13059
name: cross-tolerance
def: "State of immunological tolerance to one substance produced by priming an animal with another substance that bears a cross-reacting antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13060
name: cryoglobulin
def: "Immunoglobulin \(Ig\) that forms insoluble aggregates at temperatures below body temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13061
name: cryoglobulinemic vasculitis
def: "Cutaneous or systemic vasculitis caused by cold-labile proteins \(cryoglobulins\, cryofibrinogen\) that leads to increased viscosity\, protein precipitation or gelatinification\, complement activation\, and endothelial cell damage\, especially in the cold."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13062
name: cryptic epitope
def: "Epitope that cannot be recognized by a lymphocyte receptor until the antigen has been broken down and processed or modified by hapten binding."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13063
name: cutaneous lymphocyte antigen
def: "Cell-surface molecule that is involved in lymphocyte homing to the skin in humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13064
name: cutaneous T-cell lymphoma
def: "Malignant growth of T cells that home to the skin."
synonym: "mycosis fungoides" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13065
name: cytokine
def: "Any of a group of soluble small proteins released from a variety of cells\, typically of the immune system\, that affect cell behavior in an autocrine or paracrine fashion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13066
name: cytokine capture assay
def: "Method to detect cytokine release by injecting an animal with a neutralizing biotinylated antibody to the cytokine\, which inhibits its utilization and allows its accumulation in the blood. Recovered cytokineantibody complexes are then measured in an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay \(ELISA\) with an antibody to a second epitope on the cytokine."
synonym: "<i>in vivo</i> cytokine capture assay (IVCCA)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13067
name: cytokine profile
def: "Characteristic pattern of cytokine production associated with a defined immunological state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13068
name: cytokine release assay
def: "Method of quantifying cytokines released by viable cells\, using cytokine-specific antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13069
name: immune cytolysis
def: "1\) Cell lysis caused by a lesion produced by the action of complement proteins on the antibody-coated plasma membrane of a cell.\n2\) Cell lysis following plasma membrane reaction with perforins released by natural killer \(NK\) cells."
synonym: "immune cytolysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13070
name: cytopenia
def: "Decrease in the number of one or more types of blood cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13071
name: cytotoxic
def: "Causing damage to cell structure or function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13072
name: cytotoxic T lymphocyte
def: "Any of a subset of T lymphocytes bearing the CD8 surface marker and able to kill target cells \[infected somatic or tumor cells\, recognized by the antigen receptor complex \(TCR/CD3\)\] after induction of a specific immune response to the antigens bound to major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I molecules."
synonym: "CD8+ T cell" []
synonym: "cytolytic T cell" []
synonym: "cytotoxic T cell" []
synonym: "killer T cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13073
name: cytotoxic T lymphocyte antigen 4
def: "High affinity receptor for B7 molecules expressed on T cells\, binding of which inhibits T cell activation."
synonym: "CD152" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13074
name: cytotoxic T lymphocyte assay
def: "Assay based on quantitation of cell death caused by sensitized lymphocytes or splenocytes cultured with a fixed number of tumor or other target cells that have been prelabeled with \\\(\\ce\{^\{51\}Cr\}\\\). The \\\(\\ce\{^\{51\}Cr\}\\\) is taken up and reversibly binds to cytosolic proteins. When these target cells are incubated with sensitized lymphocytes\, the target cells are killed and the \\\(\\ce\{^\{51\}Cr\}\\\) is released."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13075
name: cytotoxin
def: "Substance with a specific toxic effect on certain cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13076
name: DEC-205
def: "Surface antigen characteristic of dendritic cells."
synonym: "CD205" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13077
name: DNA vaccination
def: "Injection of DNA into muscle\, followed by its transcription and translation into a protein or proteins that elicit antibody and T-cell responses."
synonym: "DNA immunization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13078
name: DP\, DQ\, and DR molecules
def: "Major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class II molecules occurring on human B lymphocytes and antigen-presenting cells \(APCs\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13079
name: danger hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that suggests it is not foreignness of a molecule per se but rather its ability to cause cell damage or stress that induces an immune response. Often implicated in causation of idiosyncratic drug reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13080
name: death receptor
def: "Cell-surface receptor whose binding to extracellular ligands stimulates apoptosis in the receptor-bearing cell through the extrinsic pathway."
synonym: "death ligand receptor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13081
name: death domain
def: "Protein-interaction domain originally discovered in proteins involved in programmed cell death or apoptosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13082
name: decay-accelerating factor
def: "Cell-surface molecule that protects cells from lysis by complement."
synonym: "CD55" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13083
name: defensin
def: "Any member of a family of oligopeptides made within the body\, notably by neutrophils and macrophages\, and having potent antimicrobial properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13084
name: degranulation
def: "Cell reaction that releases antimicrobial cytotoxic molecules and other substances \(e.g.\, histamine\) from secretory vesicles called granules found inside some cells."
synonym: "mast cell degranulation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13085
name: delayed-type hypersensitivity
def: "Form of T cell-mediated immunity in which the ultimate effector cell is the activated mononuclear phagocyte \(macrophage\); the response of DTH appears fully over \\\(\\pu\{24\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{48 h\}\\\). Previous exposure is required."
synonym: "Gell and Coombs Type IV reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13086
name: delayed-type hypersensitivity assay
def: "In vivo assay of cell-mediated immune response\, elicited by antigen in the skin and mediated by CD4+ Th1 cells."
synonym: "DTH assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13087
name: dendritic cell
def: "Ameboid cell that is major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class II-positive\, Fc receptor-negative\, and presents processed antigens to T cells in the T-cell areas of secondary lymphoid tissues."
synonym: "interdigitating dendritic cell" []
synonym: "interdigitating reticular cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13088
name: immune complex deposition
def: "Precipitation of immune complex in organs and tissues\, often causing local inflammation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13089
name: dermatitis
def: "Inflammation of the skin showing redness\, swelling\, infiltration\, scaling\, and sometimes vesicles and blisters\: contact dermatitis is due to local exposure and may be caused by irritation\, allergy\, or photocontact dermatitis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13090
name: dermatomyositis
def: "Disease characterized by the presence of inflammation of the skin and muscles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13091
name: desensitization
def: "Generally transient state of specific non-reactivity in a previously sensitized individual\, resulting from repeated antigen exposures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13092
name: diabetes mellitus type 1
def: "Disease in which the \\\(\\ce\{\\upbeta\\ cells\}\\\) of the pancreatic islets of Langerhans are destroyed with the result that no insulin is produced."
synonym: "diabetes mellitus" []
synonym: "insulin-dependent" []
synonym: "insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus (IDDM)" []
synonym: "type 1 diabetes mellitus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13093
name: diapedesis
def: "Movement of blood cells\, particularly leukocytes\, from the blood across blood vessel walls into tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13094
name: differential
def: "Reported blood count that distinguishes the percentages of the different blood cells present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13095
name: differential splicing
def: "Utilization and splicing of different exons from a primary RNA transcript in order to generate different mRNA sequences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13096
name: differentiation antigen
def: "Cell surface molecule expressed at a particular stage of development or on cells of a given lineage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13097
name: disintegrin and metalloproteinase domain-containing protein 33
def: "Member of the ADAM \(A Disintegrin And Metalloproteinase domain\) family of transmembrane proteins\, possibly related to asthma susceptibility."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13098
name: diversity gene
def: "Small segment of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) heavy-chain or T-cell receptor DNA between the variable \(V\) gene and joining \(J\) gene segments\, coding for the third hypervariable region of the receptors."
synonym: "D gene" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13099
name: domain
def: "Structural element of a polypeptide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13100
name: dot plot
def: "Two-dimensional graphical representation of individual data points."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13101
name: double-negative cell
def: "Immature T cell in the thymus that lacks expression of the two coreceptors\, CD4 and CD8."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13102
name: double-positive cell
def: "Immature T cell in the thymus that is characterized by expression of both CD4 and CD8 coreceptor proteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13103
name: draining lymph node
def: "Lymph node that is downstream of a site of infection and thus receives antigens and microbes from the site via the lymphatic system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13104
name: Draize test
def: "Context\: ocular; Test for potential to cause irritation to the eye in which the test substance is applied directly to the eye of restrained rabbits.\nNote\: Widely criticized as unethical. Now often replaced with an acute eye irritation test using cadaveric chicken eyes.\nContext\: skin; Test for the potential of a material to cause irritation or corrosion following local dermal application. Generally performed with rabbits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13105
name: drug-induced autoimmunity
def: "Immune-mediated idiosyncratic drug reaction against self-antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13106
name: drug-induced lupus
def: "Idiosyncratic drug reaction similar in character to systemic lupus erythematosus \(SLE\) and having antinuclear antibodies \(ANA\)\, but generally milder in nature and resolving when drug exposure is discontinued."
synonym: "lupus-like syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13107
name: eczema
def: "Acute or chronic skin inflammation with erythema\, papules\, vesicles\, pustules\, scales\, crusts\, or scabs\, alone or in combination\, of varied etiology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13108
name: effector cell
def: "Cell that carries out an immune function\, e.g.\, cytokine release\, cytotoxicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13109
name: elicitation
def: "Production of a cell-mediated or antibody-mediated allergic response by exposure of a sensitized individual to an allergen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13110
name: endogenous
def: "Produced within or caused by factors within an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13111
name: endosome
def: "Intracellular smooth surfaced vesicle in which endocytosed material passes on its way to the lysosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13112
name: endotoxin
def: "Pathogenic cell wall-associated lipopolysaccharides of Gram-negative bacteria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13113
name: enhancing antibody
def: "Antibody that binds to an antibody-antigen complex or idiotypic-anti-idiotypic antibody complex and strengthens the interaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13114
name: enterotoxin
def: "Toxin affecting intestinal cells and thus causing food poisoning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13115
name: enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay
def: "Procedure for detection or quantitation of an antibody or antigen using a ligand \(e.g.\, an anti-immunoglobulin\) conjugated to an enzyme that changes the color of a substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13116
name: enzyme-linked immunospot assay
def: "Adaptation of enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay \(ELISA\) in which cells are placed over antibodies or antigens attached to a plastic surface. The antigen or antibody traps the cells’ secreted products\, which can then be detected using an enzyme-coupled antibody that cleaves a colorless substrate to make a localized colored spot. This allows quantitation of the number of cells producing a given product such as a cytokine."
synonym: "ELISPOT assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13117
name: eosinophil
def: "Circulating granular leukocyte \(granulocyte\) having prominent granules that stain specifically by eosin and containing numerous lysosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13118
name: eosinophil chemotactic factor of anaphylaxis
def: "Cytokine that facilitates anaphylaxis and attracts eosinophils during substrate release by mast cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13119
name: eotaxin
def: "CC chemokine that acts predominantly on eosinophils."
synonym: "CCL11" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13120
name: epitope retrieval
def: "Treatment of denatured antigen with heat or other agents in order to regenerate \(unmask\) lost antibody-binding capacity."
synonym: "antigen retrieval" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13121
name: epitope spreading
def: "Increase in the number of epitopes targeted by autoantibodies and/or T cells. The epitopes may be on the same autoantigen \(intramolecular epitope spreading\) and/or on other autoantigens \(intermolecular epitope spreading\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13122
name: Epstein–Barr virus
def: "Virus responsible for infectious mononucleosis and Burkitt’s lymphoma. This virus is used to immortalize human B lymphocytes in vitro."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13123
name: equivalence
def: "Ratio of antibody to antigen at which immunoprecipitation of the reactants is virtually complete."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13124
name: equivalence zone
def: "Range of concentrations of antigen and antibody in which neither is in significant excess in a precipitin reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13125
name: erythema
def: "Redness of the skin produced by congestion of the capillaries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13126
name: erythroblastosis fetalis
def: "Two potentially disabling or fatal blood disorders in infants\: Rhesus \(Rh\) factor incompatibility disease and ABO blood group incompatibility disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13127
name: erythrocyte
def: "Red blood cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13128
name: erythropoiesis
def: "Erythrocyte production."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13129
name: erythropoietin
def: "Glycoprotein hormone that stimulates the production of erythrocytes by stem cells in bone marrow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13130
name: euglobulin
def: "Member of a class of proteins insoluble in water but soluble in saline solutions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13131
name: exocytosis
def: "Release of the content of an intracellular vesicle to the exterior of the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13132
name: exotoxin
def: "Pathogenic protein secreted by bacteria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13133
name: extravasation
def: "Movement of cells or fluid from within blood vessels to the surrounding tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13134
name: exudate
def: "Extravascular fluid containing proteins and cellular debris that accumulates during inflammation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13135
name: Fab fragment
def: "Monovalent antigen-binding \(‘ab’\) fragment obtained following papain digestion of immunoglobulin \(Ig\). It consists of an intact light chain and the N-terminal VH and C H1 domains of the heavy chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13136
name: F\(ab'\)2 fragment
def: "Bivalent antigen-binding fragment obtained following pepsin digestion of immunoglobulin. Consists of both light chains and the N-terminal part of both heavy chains linked by disulfide bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13137
name: Fc fragment
def: "Crystallizable\, non-antigen-binding fragment of an immunoglobulin \(Ig\) molecule obtained following papain digestion. Consists of the C-terminal portion of both heavy chains\, responsible for binding to Fc receptors and complement factor C1q."
synonym: "Fc region" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13138
name: Fc receptor
def: "Receptors expressed on a wide range of cells\, interacting with the Fc portion of immunoglobulins \(Ig\) belonging to various isotypes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13139
name: Fd fragment
def: "Heavy chain portion of an immunoglobulin \(Ig\) N-terminal to the papain hydrolysis site\, after reduction and separation of the light chain portion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13140
name: fMLP peptide
def: "Formyl-methionyl-leucyl-phenylalanine\, a chemoattractant \(see chemotactic factor\, chemotaxis\) derived from degradation of bacterial or mitochondrial proteins. It causes neutrophil activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13141
name: Foxp3
def: "Transcription repressor that is specifically expressed in CD4+CD25+ T cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13142
name: Fv fragment
def: "Variable \(V\) region fragment of an antibody heavy chain or light chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13143
name: familial cold autoinflammatory syndrome
def: "Episodic autoinflammatory disease\, induced by exposure to cold\, and caused by mutations in the gene CSA1\, encoding cryopyrin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13144
name: familial hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis
def: "Progressive and potentially lethal inflammatory disease caused by an inherited deficiency of perforin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13145
name: farmer’s lung
def: "Hypersensitivity disease caused by the interaction of immunoglobulin G \(IgG\) antibodies with large amounts of an inhaled allergen in the alveolar wall of the lung\, causing alveolar wall inflammation and compromising gas exchange."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13146
name: Fas
def: "Member of the tumor necrosis factor \(TNF\) receptor gene family. Engagement of Fas on the surface of the cell by the Fas ligand \(CD178\) present on cytotoxic cells can trigger apoptosis in the Fas-bearing target cell."
synonym: "CD95" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13147
name: fetal antigens
def: "Human cancer cell antigens\, which cross-react serologically with antigens normally expressed by embryonic tissue\, e.g.\, carcinoembryonic antigen \(CEA\)\, or $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}fetoprotein\}$ \(AFP\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13148
name: fetal tolerance
def: "Lack of rejection of a fetal allograft carrying paternal major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) and minor histocompatibility antigens that differ from those of the mother."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13149
name: fibrinolysis
def: "Enzymatic lysis of a fibrin clot."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13150
name: fibrinolytic
def: "Pertaining to\, characterized by\, or causing the breakdown of fibrin \(fibrinolysis\)\, usually by the action of plasmin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13151
name: first line of defence
def: "Surface tissues\, notably the skin\, respiratory tract\, and gastrointestinal tract."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13152
name: flow cytometry
def: "Method of sorting and measuring types of cells by fluorescent labeling of markers on the surface of the cells. It is sometimes referred to as fluorescence-assisted \(or activated\) cell sorting \(FACS\) analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13153
name: fluorescein isothiocyanate
def: "Green fluorescent dye used to 'tag' antibodies for use in immunofluorescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13154
name: fluorescence-activated cell sorting
def: "Fluorescence-assisted cell sorting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13155
name: fluorescent antibody
def: "Antibody conjugated to a fluorescent dye such as fluorescein isothiocyanate \(FITC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13156
name: follicular dendritic cell
def: "Major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class II-negative Fc receptor-positive dendritic cell which bears immune complexes on its surface and is probably involved in the generation of antibody-secreting cells and maintenance of B-cell memory \(see memory cell\) in germinal centers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13157
name: footpad test
def: "Test for allergic contact dermatitis in which the test substance is injected into the front footpad of a guinea pig and challenge is performed seven days later."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13158
name: Forssman antigen
def: "Glycolipid heterophile antigen \(globopentosylceramide\) expressed on cell surfaces during embryonic and adult life in rodents and other mammals\, and found in many pathogens\, but probably not in humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13159
name: framework region
def: "One of several relatively conserved amino acid sequences that flank the hypervariable regions in immunoglobulin \(Ig\) and T-cell receptor \(TCR\) variable \(V\) regions and maintain a common overall structure for all V region domains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13160
name: Freund’s adjuvant
def: "Complete Freund’s adjuvant is an emulsion of aqueous antigen in mineral oil that contains heat-killed Mycobacteria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13161
name: γ\:δ T cell
def: "Lymphocyte whose T-cell receptor \(TCR\) is a heterodimer of a \\\(\\ce\{\\upgamma\\mbox\{-\}chain\}\\\) and a \\\(\\ce\{\\updelta\\mbox\{-\}chain\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13162
name: γ\:δ T-cell receptor
def: "T-cell receptor \(TCR\) composed of two different glycoprotein chains\, designated \\\(\\upgamma\\\) and \\\(\\updelta\\\)\, assembled in a \\\(\\ce\{\\upgamma\\mbox\{\:\}\\updelta\\ heterodimer\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13163
name: γ-interferon
def: "Member of a group of cytokines\, the interferons\, which has as its primary action the activation of macrophages and can induce cells to resist viral replication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13164
name: gp120
def: "Viral glycoprotein\, non-covalently associated with gp41 in the viral envelope of human immunodeficiency virus \(HIV\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13165
name: gammaglobulin
def: "One of a group of serum proteins\, mostly immunoglobulins \(Ig\)\, which have the greatest mobility towards the cathode during electrophoresis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13166
name: autoimmune gastritis
def: "Autoimmune-mediated destruction of the gastric mucosa that may result in the development of pernicious anemia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13167
name: Gell and Coombs classification
def: "Classification of immune mechanisms of tissue injury into four types\: type I\, immediate-type hypersensitivity reactions\, mediated by interaction of immunoglobulin E \(IgE\) antibody and antigen causing the release of histamine and other mediators; type II\, antibody-mediated hypersensitivity reactions\, due to antibody–antigen interactions on cell surfaces; type III\, immune complex\, local or general inflammatory responses due to formation of circulating immune complexes and their deposition in tissues; and type IV cell-mediated hypersensitivity reactions\, initiated by sensitized T lymphocytes either by release of lymphokines or by T-cell-mediated cytotoxicity \[see cytotoxic T lymphocyte \(CTL\)\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13168
name: gene knockout
def: "Method of disabling a specific gene by homologous recombination with an introduced DNA construct designed for that purpose."
synonym: "gene targeting" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13169
name: gene rearrangement
def: "Structural alteration in a chromosome that changes the order of its genetic loci\, occurring by DNA recombination\, e.g.\, during development or in some cancers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13170
name: germ-free
def: "Descriptor applied to animals \(usually mice\) raised in the complete absence of intestinal and other microorganisms."
synonym: "gnotobiotic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13171
name: germ line
def: "Referring to genes in their unrearranged state rather than those rearranged for production of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) or T-cell receptor \(TCR\) molecules."
synonym: "in immunology" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13172
name: germinal center
def: "Discrete area within lymph nodes and spleen where B-cell maturation and memory development occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13173
name: giant cell
def: "Large multinucleate cell derived from fused macrophages and often present in granulomas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13174
name: glomerulonephritis
def: "Any of a group of kidney diseases involving inflammation of the renal glomeruli\, often as a result of antibody–antigen complexes that localize in the kidney."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13175
name: glomerulonephropathy
def: "Disease of the renal glomeruli\, which may show either thickening of the glomerular basement membrane due to the accretion of proteins or a minimal change disease\, in which there is functional damage but little structural change by light microscopy."
synonym: "glomerulopathy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13176
name: glutamic acid decarboxylase
def: "Main autoantigen in diabetes mellitus type 1 and stiff-person syndrome \(a neurological autoimmune disease\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13177
name: gluten
def: "1\) Any of the prolamins found in cereal grains\, especially the prolamins in wheat\, rye\, barley\, and possibly oats\, which cause digestive disorders\, notably celiac disease.\n2\) In wheat\, mixture of water-insoluble proteins\, including gliadins\, that give wheat dough its elastic texture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13178
name: Goodpasture syndrome
def: "Autoimmune disease of humans in which glomerulonephritis and pulmonary hemorrhage are produced by complement-mediated tissue damage caused by antibodies directed against the renal glomerular and alveolar basement membranes."
synonym: "Goodpasture disease" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13179
name: graft rejection
def: "Destruction of grafted tissue by host lymphocytes following an adaptive immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13180
name: graft-versus-host disease
def: "Disease that occurs following bone marrow transplantation between genetically non-identical people and in which mature T cells in the transplanted bone marrow attack and destroy the recipients tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13181
name: graft-versus-host reaction
def: "Reaction occurring when T lymphocytes present in a graft recognize and attack host cells."
synonym: "GVH reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13182
name: granulocyte colony-stimulating factor
def: "Hematopoietic \(see hematopoiesis\) factor augmenting the production of neutrophils in the bone marrow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13183
name: granulocyte–macrophage colony-stimulating factor
def: "Cytokine that stimulates production of granulocytes and monocytes from stem cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13184
name: granulocytopenia
def: "Decreased circulating granulocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13185
name: granuloma
def: "Mass or nodule of granulation tissue\, with actively growing fibroblasts and capillary buds\, characterized by the presence of aggregates of modified macrophages resembling epithelial cells \(epithelioid histiocytes\)\, and lymphocytes\, surrounded by a rim of giant multinucleate cells\, either of the Langerhans or foreign body type."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13186
name: granzyme
def: "Member of a family of serine esterases present in the granules of cytotoxic T lymphocytes and natural killer \(NK\) cells. They induce apoptosis in the target cell that they enter through perforin channels inserted into the target cell membrane by the cytotoxic lymphocyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13187
name: Graves disease
def: "Hyperthyroidism associated with diffuse hyperplastic goitre resulting from production of a thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor \(TSHR\)-binding autoantibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13188
name: guinea pig maximization test
def: "Skin sensitization test for allergic contact dermatitis in which shaved guinea pig skin is exposed to intradermal injections of a test agent together with complete Freund’s adjuvant\, followed by an occlusive patch for boosting for \\\(\\pu\{48 h\}\\\) beginning on day 7\, and challenged on day 21."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13189
name: gut-associated lymphoid tissue
def: "Lymphoid cells and tissues lining the mucosa that seem to serve as the first point of antigen contact via this route\, including Peyer’s patches\, the appendix\, tonsils\, adenoids\, and mesenteric lymph nodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13190
name: H-2
def: "Region of mouse chromosome 17 coding the major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13191
name: H-2 complex
def: "Mouse major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) coded by H-2."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13192
name: hairy cell leukemia
def: "B-cell neoplasm\, usually occurring during middle age\, constituting about \\\(\\pu\{2\\%\}\\\) of all leukemias and responding well to chemotherapy. The neoplastic cells have fine\, hairlike surfaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13193
name: haplotype
def: "Set of allelic variants present at a given genetic region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13194
name: haptenic
def: "Able to act as a hapten."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13195
name: Hashimoto thyroiditis
def: "Autoimmune disease where the body’s own antibodies attack the cells of the thyroid."
synonym: "chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13196
name: hay fever
def: "Allergic condition affecting the mucous membranes of the upper respiratory tract and the eyes\, characterized by nasal discharge\, sneezing\, and itchy\, watery eyes; and usually caused by an abnormal sensitivity to airborne pollen."
synonym: "pollenosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13197
name: heat shock proteins
def: "Evolutionarily conserved proteins\, found in many organisms\, that are induced upon exposure of cells to various types of stress\, including heat\, oxidative or hypoxic stress\, and deprivation of nutrients or essential ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13198
name: heavy chain
def: "Larger of the two types of polypeptide chain found in immunoglobulin \(Ig\) molecules."
synonym: "H-chain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13199
name: helper T lymphocyte
def: "Member of a subclass of T lymphocytes that provides help \(in the form of cytokines and/or cognate interactions\) necessary for the expression of effector function by other cells in the immune system."
synonym: "T helper cell" []
synonym: "helper T cell" []
synonym: "helper cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13200
name: hemagglutinin
def: "Any molecule that agglutinates erythrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13201
name: hematopoietic stem cell
def: "Self-renewing stem cell that is capable of giving rise to all of the formed elements of the blood \(i.e.\, leukocytes\, erythrocytes\, and platelets\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13202
name: hemolytic anemia
def: "Anemia resulting from the lysis of erythrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13203
name: heparin-induced thrombocytopenia
def: "Most frequent antibody-mediated drug-induced thrombocytopenia\, which occurs in \\\(\\pu\{1-\}\\pu\{2\\%\}\\\) of patients treated with heparin intravenously for longer than four days."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13204
name: hereditary angioneurotic edema
def: "Clinical name for a genetic deficiency of the C1 inhibitor of the complement system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13205
name: heteroantigen
def: "Identical or similar cross-reacting antigen shared by several species \(e.g.\, Forssman antigen\).Antibodies produced against one of these antigens will also react with the other antigens of the set even though these are derived from a different species; such antibodies are also called heterophile antibodies."
synonym: "heterogenetic antigen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13206
name: heterologous antibody
def: "Antibody derived from another individual of the same species or another species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13207
name: heterologous antigen
def: "Antigen that reacts with an antibody other than the one whose formation it induced."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13208
name: heterophagy
def: "Digestion of exogenous material taken up by a cell by phagocytosis or pinocytosis\, following fusion of the phagocytic or pinocytic vacuole with a lysosome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13209
name: heterophile antibody
def: "Antibody raised against an antigen from one species\, which also reacts against antigens from other species."
synonym: "heterophilic antibody" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13210
name: heterophile antigen
def: "Antigen that stimulates production in a vertebrate of antibodies capable of reacting with tissue components having related antigens in a wide range of other species."
synonym: "heterophilic antigen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13211
name: heterozygous
def: "Possessing different alleles at a given locus on the two homologous chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13212
name: high endothelial venule
def: "Specialized venule in lymphoid tissues that has walls permeable to migrating lymphocytes\, allowing them to move from blood into the lymphoid tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13213
name: highly active antiretroviral therapy
def: "Approach to the treatment of acquired immune deficiency syndrome \(AIDS\) patients involving a combination of nucleoside analogs\, which prevent reverse transcription\, with drugs that inhibit responses to the virus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13214
name: high-mobility group box protein
def: "Any of a group of universal sensors of nucleic acids\, required for the induction of transmembrane and cytoplasmic receptor-mediated innate immune responses."
synonym: "HMGB Protein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13215
name: hinge region
def: "Amino acids between the Fab and Fc regions of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) that permit flexibility of the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13216
name: histaminosis
def: "Histamine intolerance\, which may contribute to food intolerance as part of a pseudoallergic reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13217
name: histocompatibility
def: "Relationship between a tissue or organ donor and a recipient who have sufficient similarity in major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) antigens to avoid rejection of a graft."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13218
name: histoincompatibility
def: "Relationship between a tissue or organ donor and a recipient who have sufficient differences in major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) antigens to cause rejection of a graft."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13219
name: hives
def: "Vascular reaction pattern of the skin marked by the transient appearance of smooth\, slightly elevated patches \(wheals\) that are either more red or more pale than the surrounding skin and are accompanied by severe itching."
synonym: "nettle rash" []
synonym: "urticaria" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13220
name: Hodgkin disease
def: "Immune system tumor characterized by large cells called Reed–Sternberg cells that derive from mutated B-lineage cells."
synonym: "Hodgkin lymphoma" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13221
name: homing
def: "Movement of leukocytes to specific locations in the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13222
name: homing receptor
def: "Cell surface molecule that directs leukocytes to specific locations in the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13223
name: homocytotropic antibody
def: "Antibody that binds preferentially to cells from the same species rather than to cells from other species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13224
name: homozygous
def: "Possessing the same allele at a given locus on the two homologous chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13225
name: horror autotoxicus
def: "Term \(Latin\: dread of self-poisoning\) to describe the body’s innate aversion to immunological self destruction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13226
name: host defense
def: "Ability of an individual to resist invasion by opportunistic agents and production of disease associated with exposure to microorganisms\, foreign tissue\, and particulates\, as well as certain types of neoplasia. Host defense may be either nonspecific \(innate or natural immunity\) or specific \(adaptive or acquired immunity\) in nature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13227
name: host-resistance assay
def: "Evaluation of host resistance in rodents to in vivo challenge with an infectious organism under normal conditions and after pharmacological and nutritional alterations of the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13228
name: human immunodeficiency virus
def: "Causative agent of acquired immune deficiency syndrome \(AIDS\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13229
name: humanized antibody
def: "Genetically engineered monoclonal antibody of non-human origin in which all but the antigen-binding complementarity-determining region \(CDR\) sequences have been replaced with sequences derived from human antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13230
name: human leukocyte antigen
def: "Antigen coded by genes of the human major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13231
name: human leukocyte antigen polymorphism
def: "Discontinuous genetic variation in the major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) that results in the occurrence of several different forms of MHC molecules among people."
synonym: "HLA Polymorphism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13232
name: human leukocyte antigen type I
def: "Molecules that enable the body to recognize infected cells and tumor cells and destroy them with cytotoxic T lymphocytes \(CTLs\)."
synonym: "HLA Type I" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13233
name: human leukocyte antigen type II
def: "Molecules that enable CD4+ lymphocytes to recognize epitopes of exogenous antigens and discriminate self from non-self."
synonym: "HLA Type II" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13234
name: humoral
def: "Pertaining to extracellular fluid such as plasma and lymph."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13235
name: humoral immune response
def: "Immune response in which specific antibodies induce the effector functions \(such as phagocytosis and activation of the complement system\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13236
name: humoral immunity
def: "Specific immune response that is mediated primarily by humoral factors circulating in solution in the blood plasma and lymph \(i.e.\, antibodies and complement\)."
synonym: "humorally mediated immunity (HMI)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13237
name: hypergammaglobulinemia
def: "Increase of gammaglobulins in the blood by paraproteinemia or increased production of immunoglobulins \(Ig\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13238
name: hyperimmunoglobulin E syndrome
def: "Rare primary immunodeficiency syndrome characterized by recurrent severe staphylococcal skin abscesses \(hence Job syndrome\)\, lung infections\, and markedly elevated serum immunoglobulin E \(IgE\) levels."
synonym: "Job syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13239
name: hyperimmunoglobulin M syndrome
def: "Primary T cell defect due to mutations in the CD40 ligand \(CD40L\)\, characterized by recurrent \(opportunistic\) infections and very low levels of immunoglobulin G \(IgG\) and immunoglobulin A \(IgA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13240
name: hyperreactivity
def: "Abnormally increased response to a stimulus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13241
name: hypersensitivity
def: "State in which an individual reacts with allergic effects following exposure to a certain substance \(allergen\) after having been exposed previously to the same substance. It is sometimes used loosely for any increased response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13242
name: hypersensitivity pneumonitis
def: "Immune-mediated inflammatory disease of the lung parenchyma caused by exposure to an inhaled chemical allergen or organic dust."
synonym: "extrinsic allergic alveolitis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13243
name: hypersensitization
def: "Development of excessive immune reactivity to an antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13244
name: hypersusceptibility
def: "Adverse immunological effects in an individual occurring under conditions of exposure to an allergen that result in no effects in the great majority of the population\, or an individual exhibiting exaggerated effects in comparison with the great majority of those showing some adverse effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13245
name: hypervariable region
def: "Amino acid sequence within the immunoglobulin \(Ig\) and T-cell receptor \(TCR\) variable \(V\) regions that shows the greatest variability and contributes most to the antigen or peptide-major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecule binding site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13246
name: hypogammaglobulinemia
def: "Immunodeficiency state marked by abnormally low levels of all classes of immunoglobulins \(Ig\)\, associated with increased susceptibility to infectious diseases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13247
name: hyposensitization therapy
def: "Dermal application of a selected allergen to an allergic person in stepwise increasing dosage\, with the aim of reducing the immune system’s tendency for the corresponding allergic reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13248
name: Ia antigen
def: "Isoantigen encoded by the Ia region of the mouse major histocompatibility complex \(H-2 complex\)."
synonym: "I region-associated antigen" []
synonym: "immune response-associated antigen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13249
name: idiopathic
def: "Term that describes a 'primary' symptom or disease in which no underlying cause or associated disorder can be found."
synonym: "immune thrombocytopenic purpura" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13250
name: idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura
def: "Thrombocytopenia of unknown cause\, probably related to the production of anti-platelet antibodies\, and leading to bruising \(purpura\)."
synonym: "immune thrombocytopenic purpura" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13251
name: idiosyncratic drug reaction
def: "Rare and unpredictable adverse drug reaction occurring only in susceptible individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13252
name: idiotype
def: "The combined antigenic determinants \(idiotopes\) expressed in the variable \(V\) region of immunoglobulins \(Ig\) of an individual that determine antibody specificity to a particular antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13253
name: idiotype network
def: "Regulatory network based on interactions of anti-idiotypic antibodies and idiotypes present on antibodies and T-cell receptors \(TCRs\)\, resulting in feedback inhibition of ongoing B cell or T cell responses."
synonym: "idiotypic network" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13254
name: immature
def: "Describing cells that are not fully developed and unable to participate immediately in an immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13255
name: immediate-type hypersensitivity
def: "Reaction provoked by re-exposure to an antigen \(allergen\) that causes plasma cells to secrete immunoglobulin E \(IgE\) which binds to Fc receptors on the surface of tissue mast cells and blood basophils\, sensitizing them\, so that later exposure to the same allergen cross-links the bound IgE\, leading to degranulation and the secretion of pharmacologically active mediators such as histamine\, leukotriene\, and prostaglandin that act immediately on the surrounding tissues."
synonym: "immediate-type allergy" []
synonym: "type I hypersensitivity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13256
name: immune adherence
def: "Attachment of particulate antigen coated with C3b \(see complement\) to cells expressing C3b receptors\, which results in enhanced phagocytosis of bacteria by macrophages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13257
name: immune complex
def: "Product of an antigen–antibody reaction\, which may also contain components of the complement system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13258
name: immune complex disease
def: "Illness resulting from deposition of antigen–antibody complexes in tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13259
name: immune deviation
def: "Regulatory mechanism of the preferential activation of one aspect \(cellular or humoral\) of the adaptive immune system at the expense of the other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13260
name: immune elimination
def: "Removal of pathogens or tumor cells by the immune system; the first step of immunoediting of tumors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13261
name: immune enhancement
def: "Therapeutic stimulation of the immune response\, especially in tumor therapy."
synonym: "enhancement (in immunology)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13262
name: immune equilibrium
def: "State in carcinogenesis where there is an equilibrium between elimination of tumor cells by the immune system and growth of non-immunogenic tumor cells; the second step in immunoediting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13263
name: immune escape
def: "Biological feature of an infectious agent or of a tumor cell that prevents its elimination by the immune system. Such a feature can be acquired by a selection process. Immune escape is the third step in immunoediting of tumors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13264
name: immune evasion
def: "Property of a pathogen that results in avoidance of attack by the immune system\, e.g.\, because of reduced phagocytosis\, lack of recognition by the innate immune system\, or suppression of the immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13265
name: immune modulation
def: "Alteration of the immune response to alleviate harmful effects of the immune system or to promote its activity by changes in regulatory factors; often part of deliberate therapeutic intervention."
synonym: "immunomodulation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13266
name: immune regulation
def: "Capacity of the immune system to regulate itself so that an immune response does not become excessive and cause tissue damage\, autoimmune reactions\, or allergic reactions."
synonym: "immunoregulation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13267
name: immune reserve hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that the immune response involves multiple redundancies that are capable of compensating for acute reductions in certain immune functions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13268
name: immune response-associated protein
def: "Protein found in antigen-presenting cells \(APCs\) and B lymphocytes in mice."
synonym: "Ia protein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13269
name: immune response genes
def: "Genes\, including those within the major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\)\, that together determine the overall level of immune response to a given antigen."
synonym: "ir genes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13270
name: immune surveillance
def: "Recognition\, and in some cases elimination\, of tumor cells by the immune system before they become clinically detectable."
synonym: "immunosurveillance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13271
name: immune system
def: "Integrated network of organs\, glands\, and tissues that has evolved to protect the body from foreign substances\, including bacteria\, viruses\, and other infection-causing parasites and pathogens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13272
name: immunity
def: "Inherited\, acquired\, or induced resistance to infection by a specific pathogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13273
name: immunoactivation
def: "Activation of the immune system as a whole to react more rapidly to antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13274
name: immunoactivator
def: "Substance that activates the immune system as a whole to react more rapidly to antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13275
name: immunoadsorption
def: "Method for removal of antibody or antigen by allowing it to bind to a corresponding antigen or antibody immobilized in the solid phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13276
name: immunoblot
def: "Supporting substrate \[often a nitrocellulose\, nylon\, or poly\(vinylidene fluoride\) membrane\] onto which proteins that have been separated by gel electrophoresis are transferred and then identified by the binding of specific antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13277
name: immunocompetence
def: "Having the ability to exhibit an immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13278
name: immunocompromised
def: "Unable to mount a full or effective immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13279
name: immunoconjugate
def: "Antibody or antibody fragment to which a functional molecule\, such as a drug\, has been chemically linked."
synonym: "antibody conjugate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13280
name: immunocytokine
def: "Antibody or antibody fragment to which a cytokine has been chemically linked."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13281
name: immunodeficiency
def: "Inability to produce a normal repertoire of antibodies or immunologically sensitized T lymphocytes\, especially in response to specific antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13282
name: immunodominant epitope
def: "Epitope in an antigen that is preferentially recognized by T cells\, which then come to dominate the immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13283
name: immunodysregulation–polyendocrinopathy–enteropathy\, X-linked
def: "X-linked syndrome characterized by immunodysregulation\, polyendocrinopathy \(diabetes mellitus type 1\, thyroiditis\)\, hemolytic anemia\, thrombocytopenia\, dermatitis\, and enteropathy\, caused by mutations in the gene encoding Foxp3."
synonym: "IPEX syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13284
name: immunoediting
def: "Process of continuing immunosuppression of immunogenic tumor cells\, which may finally result in the selection of non-immunogenic tumor cells. It consists of the steps immune elimination\, immune equilibrium\, and immune escape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13285
name: immunofluorescence
def: "Technique for detection of cell- or tissue-associated antigens by the use of a fluorescently tagged ligand\, e.g.\, an anti-immunoglobulin \(Ig\) conjugated to fluorescein isothiocyanate \(FITC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13286
name: immunoglobulin A
def: "Class of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) characterized by $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ heavy chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13287
name: immunoglobulin D
def: "Class of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) characterized by $\\updelta\\!$ heavy chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13288
name: immunoglobulin E
def: "Class of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) characterized by $\\upvarepsilon\\!$ heavy chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13289
name: immunoglobulin Y
def: "Immunoglobulin \(Ig\) found in chickens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13290
name: immunoglobulin E-binding Fc receptors
def: "High-affinity IgE-binding $\{\\rm\{Fc\\mbox\{-\}\}\}\\upvarepsilon\\!\{\\rm\{\\mbox\{-\}R\}\}$ type I receptors are expressed on mast cells and basophils and interact with IgE antibodies with high affinity."
synonym: "IgE-binding FC receptors" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13291
name: immunoglobulin E-mediated hypersensitivity
def: "State in which an individual reacts with allergic effects caused fundamentally by the reaction of antigen-specific immunoglobulin E \(IgE\) following exposure to a certain substance \(allergen\) after having been exposed previously to the same substance."
synonym: "IgE-Mediated Hypersensitivity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13292
name: immunoglobulin G
def: "Class of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) characterized by \\\(\\upgamma\\\) heavy chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13293
name: immunoglobulin M
def: "Class of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) characterized by \\\(\\upmu\\\) heavy chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13294
name: immunoglobulin gene superfamily
def: "Genes encoding proteins containing one or more immunoglobulin \(Ig\) domains \(homology units\) that are homologous to either Ig variable \(V\) region or constant \(C\) region domains. Cell surface and soluble molecules mediating recognition\, adhesion\, or binding functions in and outside the immune system\, derived from the same precursor\, belong to this family of molecules \(e.g.\, immunoglobulin\, T-cell receptor \(TCR\)\, major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I and class II molecules\, CD4\, CD8\, and $\\ce\{Fc\\upgamma\\!R\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13295
name: immunoglobulin superfamily
def: "Large family of proteins characterized by possession of 'immunoglobulin-type' domains of approximately 110 amino acids folded into two \\\(\\ce\{\\upbeta\\mbox\{-\}pleated sheets\}\\\). Members include immunoglobulins \\\(A\\\)\, \\\(E\\\)\, \\\(G\\\)\, \\\(M\\\) \(IgA\, IgE\, IgG\, and IgM\)\, T-cell receptors \(TCRs\)\, and human leukocyte antigen \(HLA\) major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13296
name: immunohistochemistry
def: "Detection of cell-associated molecules in the light microscope with antibodies labeled with enzymes that change a substrate into a colored precipitate."
synonym: "immunocytochemistry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13297
name: immunological ignorance
def: "Absence of a pathogenic autoimmune response in spite of the concomitant presence in the host of the autoantigen and T cells bearing the specific autoreactive T-cell receptor \(TCR\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13298
name: immunological incompetence
def: "Inability of the immune system to function in a normal fashion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13299
name: immunologically privileged site
def: "Any of various sites in the body where foreign tissue grafts do not induce an immune reaction."
synonym: "privileged site" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13300
name: immunological memory
def: "Ability of the immune system to respond faster and more effectively to subsequent exposures to an antigen following a primary immune response to the same antigen."
synonym: "immunological anamnesis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13301
name: immunological synapse
def: "Contact point between the T cell and antigen-presenting cell \(APC\) that is generated by reorganization and clustering of cell surface molecules in lipid rafts. The synapse facilitates interactions between T-cell receptor \(TCR\) and major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules\, and between adhesion molecules\, thereby potentiating a TCR-mediated activation signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13302
name: immunological tolerance
def: "Persistent specific immunological unresponsiveness toward a substance that would normally be expected to elicit an immune response\, resulting from previous non-sensitizing exposure to an antigen."
synonym: "tolerance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13303
name: immunology
def: "Science that deals with the immune system including cell-mediated immunity \(CMI\) and humoral aspects of immunity and immune responses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13304
name: immunomagnetic separation
def: "Method of separating specific cell types or macromolecules with the aid of cell-specific antibodies coupled to paramagnetic beads."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13305
name: immunomodulation
def: "Modification of the functioning of the immune system by the action of a substance that increases or reduces the ability to produce an immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13306
name: immunopathology
def: "1\) Study of diseases of the immune system.\n2\) The occurrence of such diseases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13307
name: immunopathy
def: "Disease of the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13308
name: immunopharmacology
def: "1\) Study of the immune system using drugs as diagnostic tools.\n2\) Use of pharmaceutical molecules to support and stimulate the immune system in patients with immunodeficiency\, or to suppress or specifically influence the reactivity of the immune system in order to control a pathological immune reaction or disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13309
name: immunophenotyping
def: "Use of a panel of antibodies to determine a subset of proteins expressed on the surface of a cell or heterogeneous group of cells\, often for diagnostic purposes\, e.g.\, identifying the presence of leukemia cells in a population of lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13310
name: immunophilin
def: "Any of a group of cytoplasmic proteins in T cells that are targets of the immunosuppressant drugs cyclosporin A\, tacrolimus \(fujimycin\)\, rapamycin\, and related compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13311
name: immunopotentiation
def: "Enhancement of the capacity of the immune system to produce an effective response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13312
name: immunoprecipitate
def: "Precipitate formed in an antigen–antibody reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13313
name: immunoreceptor tyrosine-based activation motif
def: "Consensus sequence for src-family tyrosine kinases\, found in the cytoplasmic domains of several signaling molecules including the signal transduction units of lymphocyte antigen receptors and of Fc receptors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13314
name: immunoreceptor tyrosine-based inhibitory motif
def: "Consensus sequence present in the cytoplasmic domains of certain cell surface molecules \[e.g.\, Fc \\\(\\upgamma\\\) RIIB\, inhibitory natural killer \(NK\) cell receptors\] and which mediates inhibitory signals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13315
name: immunosensitivity
def: "Reactivity to antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13316
name: immunosensitizer
def: "Substance that makes the immune system more reactive to antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13317
name: immunostimulating complex
def: "Immunological adjuvant composed of saponin\, cholesterol\, phospholipid\, and an immunogen\, usually protein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13318
name: immunostimulation
def: "Increase in immune function\, e.g.\, by use of bacille Calmette–Guérin \(BCG\) vaccine or drugs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13319
name: immunosuppressant
def: "Substance that depresses the function of the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13320
name: immunosuppressive
def: "Causing depression of the normal function of the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13321
name: immunosurveillance
def: "Mechanisms by which the immune system is able to recognize and destroy malignant cells before the formation of an overt tumor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13322
name: immunotherapy
def: "Treatment or prevention of a disease using agents that can modify the immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13323
name: immunotoxic
def: "Harmful to the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13324
name: immunotoxicant
def: "Substance that is harmful to the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13325
name: immunotoxicology
def: "Discipline applying cardinal principals of both immunology and toxicology to study the ability of certain substances to alter the immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13326
name: immunotoxin
def: "Biochemical conjugate\, or recombinant fusion protein\, consisting of an immune targeting molecule such as an antibody or antibody fragment together with a cytotoxic molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13327
name: inactivated vaccine
def: "Vaccine formulated from the whole microorganism that has been rendered unable to reproduce or cause disease\, often by mutation or heat denaturation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13328
name: indirect immunofluorescence
def: "Method of visualizing an antigen in which an unlabeled antibody against the antigen is reacted with it first\, and then is detected by staining with a second fluorescently tagged immunoglobulin G \(IgG\) directed against the first antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13329
name: indolamine-2\,3-dioxygenase
def: "Enzyme catalyzing the initial rate-limiting step of tryptophan degradation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13330
name: inducible co-stimulatory protein
def: "Highly specific receptor for the protein B7H/B7RP-1 that is expressed on the surface of B cells and macrophages\, and also appears on the surface of T cells during the process of T-cell activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13331
name: infectious tolerance
def: "Continuing state of tolerance that can be transferred by T lymphocytes from a tolerant animal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13332
name: autoimmune infertility
def: "Infertility caused by sperm antibodies\, autoimmune ovarian inflammation \(oophoritis\)\, or autoimmune orchitis."
synonym: "autoimmune infertility" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13333
name: inflammasome
def: "High-molecular-weight complex that activates inflammatory caspases and the cytokines interleukin-1$\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$ \($\\ce\{IL\\mbox\{-\}1\\upbeta\\!\}$\) and IL-18."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13334
name: inflammatory bowel disease
def: "Group of chronic inflammatory conditions resulting in the inappropriate and persistent activation of the mucosal immune system of the bowel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13335
name: innate immunity
def: "Immunity that is not intrinsically affected by prior contact with antigen\, i.e.\, all aspects of immunity not directly mediated by lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13336
name: intercellular adhesion molecule
def: "Member of the immunoglobulin superfamily that interacts with integrins and is found on the surface of several cell types\, including antigen-presenting cells \(APCs\) and T cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13337
name: interclonal competition
def: "Process which favors survival of foreign-specific lymphocytes at the expense of self-specific lymphocytes and is a secondary mechanism involved in the induction and maintenance of self-tolerance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13338
name: interleukin
def: "Member of a group of immunoregulatory glycoproteins\, also called lymphokines\, monokines\, or cytokines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13339
name: internal image
def: "Epitope on an anti-idiotype that binds in a way that structurally and functionally mimics the antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13340
name: intolerance
def: "Extreme sensitivity or allergy to a drug\, food\, or other substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13341
name: intradermal test
def: "Diagnostic test for a possible cause of hypersensitivity in an individual in which a small drop of antigen is placed on a scarified skin surface or injected intradermally."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13342
name: intra-epithelial lymphocyte
def: "Lymphocyte found in the epithelial layer of mammalian mucosal linings\, such as those of the gastrointestinal and reproductive tracts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13343
name: invariant chain
def: "Protein \(\\\(\\pu\{31 kDa\}\\\)\) of the endoplasmic reticulum lumen that binds to newly formed major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) heterodimeric proteins\, preventing binding of endogenous peptides to their groove."
synonym: "CD74" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13344
name: islet cell antibodies
def: "Autoantibodies reacting with endocrine \(pancreatic islet\) cells and detectable by indirect immunofluorescence on pancreatic cryostat sections."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13345
name: isograft
def: "Tissue transplanted \(see graft\) between two genetically identical individuals."
synonym: "syngraft" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13346
name: isohemagglutinin
def: "Naturally occurring immunoglobulin M \(IgM\) antibody specific for the erythrocyte antigens of the ABO blood groups\, thought to result from immunization by bacteria in the gastrointestinal and respiratory tracts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13347
name: isotype
def: "Class of antibody that differs from others in the constant region of the heavy chain \(Fc portion\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13348
name: isotype control
def: "Antibody exhibiting the non-specific characteristics of the antibody-isotype but lacking any specificity for the antigen in question; used as a negative control in antibody-antigen detection assays\, notably in flow cytometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13349
name: isotype switch
def: "Process by which a B cell switches from making antibody of one isotype to another without altering the specificity of the antibody\, producing an isotype with the same variable \(V\) regions but a different heavy chain constant \(C\) region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13350
name: JAK/STAT signaling pathway
def: "Mechanism that transmits information from chemical signals outside the cell to gene promoters on the DNA in the cell nucleus. The mechanism is activated by the binding of Janus-family tyrosine kinase \(JAK\) to the nonpolypeptide portion of occupied receptors for some interleukins \(IL\). JAK phosphorylates signal transducer and activator of transcription \(STAT\) proteins apart from STAT 5a and 5b."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13351
name: JAM test
def: "Method which uses labeled nucleotide precursors to estimate DNA fragmentation during cell-mediated cytotoxicity or apoptosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13352
name: Janus-family tyrosine kinase
def: "Tyrosine kinase activated by cytokines binding to cell receptors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13353
name: joining chain
def: "Polypeptide that forms part of the polymeric structure of pentameric immunoglobulin M \(IgM\) and dimeric immunoglobulin A \(IgA\)."
synonym: "J chain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13354
name: joining gene
def: "Gene coding for a joining segment that connects variable \(V\) regions and constant \(C\) regions of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) or T-cell receptor \(TCR\) chains that\, upon gene rearrangement\, encodes part of the third hypervariable region of the antigen receptors."
synonym: "J gene" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13355
name: junctional diversity
def: "Diversity of the splice junctions in the recombined variable \(V\) gene\, diversity \(D\) gene \[for immunoglobulin \(Ig\) heavy chains\, and for TCR b and d chains\]\, and joining \(J\) gene segments of antibody and T-cell receptor \(TCR\) genes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13356
name: kallikrein
def: "Any of a group of enzymes found in the blood and body fluids that acts on certain plasma globulins to produce bradykinins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13357
name: Kaposi sarcoma
def: "Form of skin cancer that can involve internal organs. It is most often found in patients with acquired immunodeficiency syndrome \(AIDS\) and can be fatal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13358
name: kappa-light chain
def: "Smaller of the two types of light chain found in human immunoglobulins \(Ig\)\, the other type being a lambda \($\\uplambda\\!$\)-light chain."
synonym: "κ-light chain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13359
name: keratinocyte
def: "Epidermal cell that produces keratin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13360
name: killer activatory receptor
def: "Receptor found on natural killer \(NK\) cells\, and some $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\{\:\}\\updelta\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\{\:\}\\upbeta\\! T cells\}$."
synonym: "killer cell immunoglobulin-like receptor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13361
name: killer cell
def: "Cell that displays cell-mediated cytotoxicity\, including cytotoxic T lymphocytes \(CTL\, Tc\)\, natural killer \(NK\) cells\, and natural killer T \(NKT\) cells. Some also consider activated macrophages\, monocytes\, and interferon \(IFN\)-activated neutrophils to be killer cells."
synonym: "K cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13362
name: killer cell immunoglobulin-like receptor
def: "Receptor expressed on natural killer \(NK\) cells that binds to major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I molecules on target cells; binding MHC class I inhibits the signaling that would otherwise lead to target cell killing."
synonym: "killer inhibitory receptor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13363
name: killer lectin-like receptor
def: "Killer cell receptor that binds to major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I molecules and\, like the C-type lectin receptors also found on these cells\, can either inhibit or activate the killer cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13364
name: kinin
def: "Any of a family of polypeptides that is released during inflammatory responses and that increases vascular permeability and smooth muscle contraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13365
name: knockout
def: "Use of homologous genetic recombination in embryonic stem cells to replace a functional gene with a defective copy of the gene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13366
name: knockout mouse
def: "Mouse produced from embryonic cells containing DNA that has been genetically engineered so that it does not express a particular gene or group of genes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13367
name: Kupffer cell
def: "Fixed tissue macrophage found lining the blood sinuses in the liver."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13368
name: Kveim reaction
def: "Test for sarcoidosis\, eliciting a granuloma at the site of intrradermal injection of a lymph node extract from a person with active disease. Generally considered non-specific."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13369
name: L-selectin
def: "Any of a group of leukocyte surface adhesion molecules; they are classed as CD62 in the cluster of differentiation \(CD\) antigen marker system. All have lectin family carbohydrate binding domains and epidermal growth factor \(EGF\) repeats."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13370
name: Ly-6
def: "Family of multi-gene-encoded\, small\, generally glycosylphosphatidyl inositol-anchored membrane proteins thought to be involved in cell adhesion and signaling\, and in antigen-independent activation of lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13371
name: Lambert–Eaton myasthenic syndrome
def: "Paraneoplastic neurological disorder associated with small-cell lung cancer and caused by autoantibodies against voltage-gated calcium channels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13372
name: lambda \(λ\)-light chain
def: "One of the two types of light chain of human immunoglobulins \(Ig\)\, the other type being the kappa \($\\kappa$\)-light chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13373
name: Langerhans cell
def: "Immature\, phagocytic dendritic cell of the mammalian skin\, characterized by the presence of Fc receptors\, major histocompatibility \(MHC\) class II molecules\, and epidermal dendritic cell marker CD1. It contains a cytoplasmic organelle called the Birbeck granule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13374
name: large granular lymphocyte
def: "Lymphocyte of greater than normal size that contains cytoplasmic granules and functions as a natural killer \(NK\) cell or killer \(K\) cell. Activated CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes \(CTL\, Tc\) also assume an LGL morphology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13375
name: late phase reaction
def: "Immunoglobulin E \(IgE\)-mediated immune response occurring \\\(\\pu\{5\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{8 h\}\\\) after exposure to antigen\, after the 'wheal and flare' reaction of immediate hypersensitivity has diminished\, with inflammation peaking around \\\(\\pu\{24 h\}\\\)\, and then subsiding."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13376
name: latex agglutination test
def: "Use of latex beads coated with antibodies to detect antigen in biological samples."
synonym: "latex fixation test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13377
name: latex allergen
def: "Allergen found in natural rubber latex \(NRL\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13378
name: leukemia
def: "Progressive\, malignant disease of the blood-forming organs\, characterized by excessive proliferation and development of leucocytes and their precursors in the bone marrow and blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13379
name: leukocyte
def: "White blood cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13380
name: leukocyte common antigen
def: "Antigen common to both T lymphocytes and B lymphocytes."
synonym: "CD45" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13381
name: leukocyte functional antigen
def: "Any of a group of cell-surface antigens involved in intercellular adhesion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13382
name: leukocytopenia
def: "Amount of leukocytes below normal values that is a characteristic feature of systemic autoimmune diseases \[e.g.\, Felty syndrome\, systemic lupus erythematosus \(SLE\)\, Sjögren syndrome\, mixed connective tissue disease \(MCTD\)\]."
synonym: "leukopenia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13383
name: leukocytosis
def: "Abnormal increase in the number of leukocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13384
name: leukotriene
def: "Metabolic product of arachidonic acid that promotes inflammatory processes \(e.g.\, chemotaxis\, increased vascular permeability\) and is produced by a variety of cell types including mast cells\, basophils\, and macrophages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13385
name: ligand of inducible co-stimulatory protein
def: "Protein involved\, after binding to inducible co-stimulatory protein \(ICOS\)\, in several harmful immune responses\, such as autoimmunity\, allergy\, or graft-versus-host \(GVH\) reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13386
name: light chain
def: "Small polypeptide subunit of an antibody or immunoglobulin \(Ig\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13387
name: Limulus test
def: "Method of detecting bacterial endotoxins based on gelation of a blood cell lysate from the horseshoe crab\, Limulus polyphemus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13388
name: linear epitope
def: "Antigenic determinant that is contiguous in the amino acid sequence of a protein and therefore does not require the protein to be folded into its native conformation for antibody to bind."
synonym: "continuous epitope" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13389
name: linked recognition
def: "Requirement for a helper T lymphocyte \(Th\) and B lymphocyte to interact with separate but physically linked epitopes in the same antigen\, in order for an immune response to a thymus-dependent \(TD\) antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13390
name: lipid raft
def: "Cholesterol- and glycosphingolipid-rich membrane subdomain in which molecules involved in cellular activation become concentrated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13391
name: live attenuated vaccine
def: "Vaccine prepared from living attenuated organisms or from viruses that have been attenuated but can still replicate in the cells of the host organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13392
name: liver-kidney microsomal antibodies
def: "Autoantibodies directed against cytochrome P450 and uridine diphosphate \(UDP\)-glucuronosyltransferase \(UGT\) antigens typically found in patients with immune-mediated hepatitis."
synonym: "LKM antibodies" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13393
name: lymphadenopathy
def: "Enlarged lymph nodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13394
name: lymphangion
def: "Morphological or functional unit of lymphatic vessels consisting of the region between adjacent lymphatic valves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13395
name: lymphatic system
def: "Describing vessels whose special function is the collection of lymph and its ultimate conveyance to the blood circulation and thoracic duct."
synonym: "lymphatic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13396
name: lymphatic tissue
def: "Tissue involved in the collection\, production\, and handling of lymph\, including the lymphatic vessels and lymph nodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13397
name: lymph node
def: "Any of the small rounded gland-like structures of the lymphatic system\, which occur along the lymphatic vessels and which are responsible for removing bacteria\, viruses\, and foreign molecules from the lymph stream and for producing lymphocytes and antibodies."
synonym: "lymph gland" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13398
name: lymphoblast
def: "Abnormal cell with a large nucleus and scanty cytoplasm\, thought to be the precursor of the lymphocyte\, found in blood and blood-forming organs in patients with lymphoblastic leukemia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13399
name: lymphocyte
def: "Small leucocyte found in the blood\, lymph\, and lymphoid tissues that has a single round nucleus and little or no granulation in the cytoplasm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13400
name: lymphocyte-activating factor
def: "Interleukin-1 \(IL-1\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:13401
name: lymphocyte activation
def: "Sum of the biochemical processes necessary to stimulate a resting lymphocyte to become an immune effector cell\, requiring antigen and co-stimulatory molecules."
synonym: "activation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13402
name: lymphocyte function-associated antigen-1
def: "Adhesion molecule found on lymphocytes\, macrophages\, and neutrophils\, involved in adhesion of lymphocytes to antigen-presenting cells \(APCs\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13403
name: lymphocyte homing
def: "Process that directs lymphocyte subsets to specialized microenvironments that control their differentiation\, regulate their survival\, and target immune effector cells to sites of antigenic or microbial invasion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13404
name: lymphocyte homing receptor
def: "Cell surface glycoprotein on lymphocytes and other leukocytes that mediates adhesion to specialized blood vessels\, the high endothelial venules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13405
name: lymphocyte proliferation test
def: "Test of immune sensitization in which white blood cells are exposed in vitro to an antigen and multiply if already sensitized to that antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13406
name: immunoglobulin or T-cell receptor lymphocyte repertoire
def: "Profile of immunoglobulins \(Ig\) or T-cell receptors \(TCRs\) available within a specified group of cells."
synonym: "T-Cell Receptor" []
synonym: "immunoglobulin lymphocyte repertoire" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13407
name: lymphocyte subpopulation
def: "Population forming part of the whole population of lymphocytes\, usually characterized by sensitivity to a given antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13408
name: lymphocyte transformation test
def: "Test for increased DNA synthesis followed by cell division and differentiation of lymphocytes in response to antigens or mitogens; an in vitro test of lymphocyte function."
synonym: "blastogenesis assay" []
synonym: "lymphocyte blastogenesis assay (LBA)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13409
name: lymphocytopenia
def: "Deficiency of lymphocytes in the blood compared with normal values."
synonym: "lymphopenia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13410
name: lymphocytosis
def: "Condition characterized by an abnormal increase in the number of lymphocytes in blood\, usually a result of infection or inflammation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13411
name: lymphocytotoxicity
def: "Capability of lysing lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13412
name: lymphoid follicle
def: "Region of clustered B cells\, allowing the selection of antigen-binding B cells by dendritic cells during adaptive immune responses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13413
name: lymphoid stem cell
def: "Stem cell giving rise to the lineage of lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13414
name: lymphoid tissue
def: "Vertebrate tissue that is made up predominantly of lymphocytes\, e.g.\, lymph\, lymph nodes\, spleen\, thymus\, Peyer’s patches\, adenoids\, pharyngeal tonsils\, and\, in birds\, bursa of Fabricius and cecal tonsils."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13415
name: lymphokine
def: "Cytokine produced by lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13416
name: lymphokine-activated killer cell
def: "Killer \(K\) cell or natural killer \(NK\) cell activated in vitro by interleukin \(IL\)-2 to give enhanced killing of target cells."
synonym: "LAK cell" []
synonym: "lymphocyte-activated killer cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13417
name: lymphoma
def: "Neoplasm\, usually malignant\, of the lymphatic tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13418
name: lymphopoiesis
def: "Production of lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13419
name: lymphoproliferation
def: "Proliferation of lymphocytes in response to stimulation with cellular activators\, including antigens or mitogens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13420
name: lymphosum
def: "Sum of B cells + T cells + natural killer \(NK\) cells determined separately \(by flow cytometry\)\, compared to the total number of lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13421
name: lymphotoxin
def: "T-cell-derived cytokine that is cytotoxic for certain tumor cells and also has immunoregulatory functions."
synonym: "Tumor necrosis factor β (TNF-β)." []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13422
name: lysosome
def: "Cytoplasmic granule containing hydrolytic enzymes involved in the digestion of phagocytosed material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13423
name: macroautophagy
def: "Sequestration and breakdown of organelles and long-lived proteins in a double-membrane vesicle\, called an autophagosome or autophagic vacuole\, inside the cell."
synonym: "non-specific autophagy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13424
name: macrophage
def: "Large \(\\\(\\pu\{10\\!\{-\}\\! 20 \\upmu m\}\\\) diameter\) ameboid and phagocytic cell found in many tissues\, especially in areas of inflammation\, derived from blood monocytes and playing an important role in host defense mechanisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13425
name: residential macrophage
def: "Macrophage stationed at a point where microbial invasion or accumulation of dust is likely to occur."
synonym: "fixed macrophage" []
synonym: "resident macrophage" []
synonym: "residential macrophage" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13426
name: macrophage function test
def: "Any assay suitable to characterize the effect of substances on macrophage function in in vivo or in vitro."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13427
name: macrophage mannose receptor
def: "Receptor involved in pathogen recognition\, in clearance of endogenous serum glycoproteins\, and in antigen presentation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13428
name: macropinocytosis
def: "'High-volume' pinocytosis by which dendritic cells engulf relatively large volumes of fluid from their surroundings\, enabling them to take in many antigens nonspecifically."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13429
name: major basic protein
def: "Small basic arginine-rich peptide \(\\\(\\pu\{pI 10.9\}\\\)\, molecular mass of \\\(\\pu\{13.8 kDa\}\\\)\) in the granules of eosinophils that kills helminths and protozoa."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13430
name: major histocompatibility complex
def: "Cluster of genes encoding cell surface molecules involved in antigen presentation to T cells. In humans\, they are found on chromosome 6 and in mice on chromosome 17. They are polymorphic and code for antigens that lead to rapid graft rejection between members of a single species that differ at these loci."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13431
name: major histocompatibility complex class I molecule
def: "Surface protein encoded by the major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) expressed on all nucleated cells\, presenting antigen-derived peptides."
synonym: "MHC class I molecule" []
synonym: "class I MHC gene product" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13432
name: major histocompatibility complex class II molecule
def: "Surface protein encoded by the major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) expressed on antigen-presenting cells \(APCs\)\, presenting antigen-derived peptides."
synonym: "MHC class II molecule" []
synonym: "class II MHC gene product" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13433
name: major histocompatibility complex class III molecule
def: "Antigen encoded by the major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) including several complement factors\, tumor necrosis factor \(TNF\) $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$\, and lymphotoxin."
synonym: "MHC class III molecule" []
synonym: "class III MHC gene product" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13434
name: major histocompatibility complex molecule
def: "Any of the cell-surface glycoprotein alloantigens encoded by the major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) that are involved in the regulation of immune responses and can cause the rejection of grafted tissues\, cells\, and tumors bearing them."
synonym: "MHC molecule" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13435
name: major histocompatibility complex restriction
def: "Necessity that T cells recognize processed antigen only when presented by major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules of the original haplotype associated with T-cell priming."
synonym: "MHC restriction" []
synonym: "restriction (in immunology)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13436
name: mammalian target of rapamycin
def: "Intracellular signaling molecule \(phosphoinositide-3-kinase\) involved in control of cell growth\, whose activity is negatively influenced by the immunosuppressive drug rapamycin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13437
name: mannan-binding lectin-associated serine peptidase-1 and -2
def: "Complement-dependent bactericidal factors that bind to the Ra and R2 polysaccharides expressed by certain enterobacterium strains\, such as Ra chemotype strains of Salmonella."
synonym: "mannose-binding protein-associated serine protease (MASP)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13438
name: mannose-binding protein
def: "Member of the collectin family of calcium-dependent lectins\, and an acute-phase protein."
synonym: "mannose-binding lectin (MBL)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13439
name: mantle zone
def: "Outer ring of small lymphocytes surrounding the center of a lymphatic nodule \(lymphoid follicle\). It contains transient lymphocytes and is the location of the lymphoma in mantle cell lymphoma."
synonym: "corona" []
synonym: "mantle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13440
name: marginal zone
def: "Outer area of the splenic periarteriolar lymphoid sheath \(PALS\) that is rich in B cells\, particularly those responding to thymus-independent antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13441
name: margination
def: "Leukocyte adhesion to the endothelium of blood vessels in the early phase of an acute inflammatory response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13442
name: mast cell
def: "Large connective tissue cell that contains inflammatory substances such as histamine\, proteases \(including tryptases\, chymases\, and carboxypeptidase A\)\, heparin and chondroitin sulfate proteoglycans\, and eicosanoid lipid mediators \(the precursors of leukotrienes and prostaglandins\). These substances are released in allergic reactions or in response to injury or inflammation."
synonym: "mastocyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13443
name: mast cell activation disorder
def: "Disorder or syndrome in which there is evidence of systemic\, inappropriate release of mast cell mediators."
synonym: "mast cell activation syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13444
name: mast cell stabilizer
def: "Non-steroidal medication that reduces the release of substances from mast cells\, e.g.\, sodium cromoglycate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13445
name: mastocytoma
def: "Nodule of mast cells that can involve the skin\, subcutaneous tissue\, and sometimes muscle."
synonym: "mast cell tumor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13446
name: mastocytosis
def: "Rare disease characterized by the presence of too many mast cells in various organs and tissues. The condition can be a chronic\, long-term illness or it can develop suddenly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13447
name: maturation
def: "Process by which B cells produce antibodies with increased affinity for antigen during the course of an immune response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13448
name: mature B cell
def: "B cell with immunoglobulin M \(IgM\) and immunoglobulin D \(IgD\) on its surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13449
name: megakaryocyte
def: "Bone marrow precursor of platelets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13450
name: membrane attack complex
def: "Complex of complement components C5b–C9 that inserts as a pore into the membrane of target cells leading to cell lysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13451
name: memory
def: "Characteristic of the acquired immune response \(see acquired immunity\) of lymphocytes whereby a second encounter with a given antigen produces a secondary immune response that is faster\, greater\, and longer lasting than the primary immune response."
synonym: "immunological memory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13452
name: memory cell
def: "Clonally expanded T cell or B cell produced during a primary immune response and which is primed to mediate a secondary immune response to the original antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13453
name: memory lymphocyte immunostimulation assay
def: "Patented modification of the lymphocyte transformation test \(LTT\) based on the enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay \(ELISA\) principle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13454
name: mesenteric lymph node
def: "Lymph node lying between layers of the mesentery."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13455
name: metabolic activation
def: "Formation of one or more chemically reactive intermediate\(s\) as a result of modification of a molecule by the xenobiotic-metabolizing enzymes of liver and other organs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13456
name: microautophagy
def: "Process in which lysosomes directly engulf cytoplasm by invagination\, protrusion\, and/or septation of the lysosomal limiting membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13457
name: microfold cell
def: "Cell found in the follicle-associated epithelium of the Peyer’s patch that has the unique ability to sample antigen from the lumen of the small intestine and deliver it via transcytosis to antigen-presenting cells \(APCs\) and lymphocytes located in a unique pocket-like structure on their basolateral side."
synonym: "M cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13458
name: microglobulin
def: "Any small globular protein found on the surface of many cells\, including lymphocytes\, and in the blood plasma distinct from albumin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13459
name: microscopic polyangiitis
def: "ANCA-associated necrotizing\, pauci-immune vasculitis of the small vessels \(capillaries\, venules\, arterioles\) frequently associated with rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis and/or hemorrhagic alveolitis as well as autoantibodies against myeloperoxidase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13460
name: minor histocompatibility antigen
def: "Non-major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\)-encoded cell surface processed peptide which\, in association with MHC-encoded molecules\, contributes to graft rejection\, albeit not usually as severe as that due to MHC mismatch."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13461
name: mitogen
def: "Substance that induces lymphocyte transformation or\, more generally\, mitosis and cell proliferation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13462
name: mitogen-activated protein kinase
def: "Member of a kinase network in which 'upstream' kinases activate 'downstream' kinases that\, in response to phosphorylation\, translocate to the nucleus and activate transcription factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13463
name: mitophagy
def: "Autophagy-related pathway specific for mitochondria; it can be subdivided into macromitophagy and micromitophagy depending upon the degree of mitophagy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13464
name: mixed lymphocyte response
def: "T-cell-proliferative response induced by cells expressing allogeneic major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules."
synonym: "mixed lymphocyte response" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13465
name: mixed connective tissue disease
def: "Human autoimmune disease in which the immune system attacks the body\, producing symptoms that combine features of polymyositis\, systemic lupus erythematosus \(SLE\)\, and systemic scleroderma\, and thus being considered as an overlap syndrome."
synonym: "Sharp’s syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13466
name: molecular mimicry
def: "Concept that identity or similarity of epitopes expressed by a pathogen and by a self molecule may lead to production of antibodies reacting to the self-antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13467
name: monocyte
def: "Mononuclear phagocyte found in blood and which is the precursor of the tissue macrophage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13468
name: monokine
def: "Alternative name for a cytokine produced by monocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13469
name: mononuclear phagocyte system
def: "Network of phagocytic cells throughout the body. System comprising blood monocytes and tissue macrophages."
synonym: "reticuloendothelial system (RES)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13470
name: mouse IgE test
def: "Procedure in which serum immunoglobulin E \(IgE\) levels are measured in mice following topical exposure to a test substance. Substances that cause a significant increase in IgE are considered to be potential respiratory allergens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13471
name: mouse ear-swelling test
def: "Test for sensitization in which the test substance is applied topically to the ear of a mouse for three consecutive days. The change in ear thickness is then measured following challenge on day 8."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13472
name: mucocutaneous
def: "Pertaining to or affecting the mucous membranes and/or the skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13473
name: mucosa
def: "Moist tissue that lines some organs and body cavities\, including the nose\, mouth\, lungs\, and digestive tract."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13474
name: mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue
def: "Secondary lymphoid tissue present in the surface mucosa of the respiratory tract and bronchus \(BALT\)\, gastrointestinal tract \(GALT\)\, nasal passages \(NALT\)\, larynx \(LALT\)\, conjunctiva \(CALT\)\, and genitourinary tract\, and in the skin sub-epidermis \(SALT\)."
synonym: "bronchus-associated lymphoid tissue (BALT)" []
synonym: "nasal-associated lymphoid tissue (NALT)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13475
name: mucosal addressin cell adhesion molecule-1
def: "Protein involved in trafficking of lymphocytes to mucosal endothelium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13476
name: mucosal tolerance
def: "State of lymphocyte hyporesponsiveness to protein antigens applied across mucosal surfaces by oral or nasal instillation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13477
name: multiple myeloma
def: "Plasma cell malignancy resulting in high levels of monoclonal immunoglobulin \(Ig\) in serum and of free light chains \(Bence–Jones protein\) in urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13478
name: multiplex immunoassay
def: "Type of particle immunoassay employing sets of microspheres with various physicochemical features\, each labeled with a different antibody. The method allows detection in a single measurement of an array of 100 or more proteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13479
name: multipotent progenitor cell
def: "Undifferentiated stem cell\, obtained from adult bone marrow or other non-embryonic tissue sources\, that possesses the ability to differentiate into a variety of cell types\, especially into cells of a closely related family of cells that are expanded in vitro and deposited in master cell banks for 'off-the-shelf' use\, with potential hematopoiesis-inducing and immunomodulating activities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13480
name: murine local lymph node assay
def: "Predictive test of sensitization in which a test substance is applied topically to the skin of mice \(usually three times at daily intervals\) and the weight of the local draining lymph nodes is measured at six days. Usually lymphoproliferation in the nodes is measured following injection of \[\\\(\\ce\{^\{3\}H\}\\\)\]thymidine or bromodeoxyuridine."
synonym: "local lymph node assay (LLNA)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13481
name: acquired myasthenia gravis
def: "Autoimmune disease characterized by muscle weakness usually affecting ocular and oropharyngeal muscles due to an autoimmune attack against components of the neuromuscular junction \(e.g.\, the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor\)."
synonym: "acquired myasthenia gravis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13482
name: myelodysplastic syndrome
def: "Diverse collection of hematological conditions united by ineffective production \(or dysplasia\) of myeloid blood cells and risk of transformation to acute myelogenous leukemia \(AML\). Anemia requiring chronic blood transfusion is frequently present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13483
name: myeloma
def: "Tumor composed of cells derived from hematopoietic cells \(see hematopoiesis\) of the bone marrow\, or from mast cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13484
name: myeloid
def: "Referring to the nonlymphocytic groups of white blood cells\, including granulocytes\, monocytes\, and platelets."
synonym: "myelogenous" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13485
name: myeloid stem cell
def: "One of two groups of stem cells produced in the bone marrow. Myeloid stem cells may mature into several types of blood cells\, including acidic basophils\, eosinophils\, erythrocytes\, macrophages\, megakaryocytes\, and neutrophils."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13486
name: myeloid tissue
def: "Tissue within red bone marrow that produces the blood cells. It is found around the blood vessels and contains various cell types that are precursors of the blood cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13487
name: myeloma protein
def: "Monoclonal antibody secreted by myeloma cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13488
name: myeloperoxidase
def: "Enzyme found in azurophilic granules of neutrophils\, the major target of antineutrophil cytoplasmic autoantibody \(ANCA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13489
name: myelopoiesis
def: "Process of formation and development of myeloid blood cells in the bone marrow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13490
name: myelosuppression
def: "Decrease in myeloid cells\, including pancytopenia\, anemia\, leukopenia\, lymphopenia\, thrombocytopenia\, and other blood dyscrasias."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13491
name: autoimmune myositis
def: "Rare systemic inflammatory myopathy\, including primary polymyositis\, primary dermatomyositis\, myositis associated with malignancy\, childhood dermatomyositis\, and myositis with multisystem autoimmune disease \[e.g.\, mixed connective tissue disease \(MCTD\)\, systemic sclerosis \(SSc\)\]."
synonym: "autoimmune myositis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13492
name: NACHT domain-\, leucine-rich repeat \(LRR\)-\, and pyrin \(N-terminal homology\) domain \(PYD\)-containing protein 3
def: "NOD-like receptor \(NLR\)\, found in certain inflammasomes\, that binds to aggregated proteins and the peptides that compose them. Its activation leads to the generation of inflammatory cytokines\, such as interleukin \(IL\)-1\, and neurotoxic factors."
synonym: "cryopyrin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13493
name: N-nucleotides
def: "Non-templated nucleotides added to the junctions between antibody and T-cell receptor \(TCR\) variable \(V\) gene\, diversity \(D\) gene\, and joining \(J\) gene segments during gene rearrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13494
name: NOD-like receptor
def: "Any of a large family of cytoplasmic proteins containing a nucleotide-binding domain and leucine-rich repeats \(LRRs\)\, involved in regulation of inflammation and apoptosis; many family members are thought to function as pattern recognition receptors \(PRRs\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13495
name: N-region
def: "Highly variable region on the H-chain of immunoglobulins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13496
name: naïve lymphocyte
def: "Mature T cell or B cell that has not yet been activated \(see lymphocyte activation\) by encounter with antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13497
name: natural autoantibodies
def: "Part of the naturally occurring repertoire of polyreactive antibodies that bind to autoantigens with low affinity. They are mainly of immunoglobulin M \(IgM\) isotype and produced by CD5+ B lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13498
name: natural immunity
def: "Host defense mechanisms that do not require prior exposure to antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13499
name: natural killer cell
def: "Large granular lymphocyte which does not rearrange nor express either immunoglobulin or T-cell receptor \(TCR\) genes but is able to recognize and destroy certain tumor and virally infected cells in a major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecule- and antibody-independent manner."
synonym: "NK cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13500
name: natural killer cell activity assay
def: "Immunotoxicity test in which lymphoid tissue or blood that has been treated with a test compound is co-incubated with target cells. The cytotoxic effect on target cells is measured\, e.g.\, with the chromium release assay. It is used under circumstances where differences from untreated cells are attributed to natural killer \(NK\) cell activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13501
name: natural killer T cell
def: "Lymphoid cells with a morphology and granule content intermediate between T cells and natural killer \(NK\) cells."
synonym: "NKT cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13502
name: natural resistance-associated macrophage protein
def: "Iron transporter that plays a critical role in macrophage activation and differentiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13503
name: negative selection
def: "Deletion by apoptosis in the thymus of T cells that recognize self-peptides presented by self-major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules\, thus preventing the development of autoimmune T cells. Negative selection of developing B cells is also thought to occur if they encounter high levels of self-antigen in the bone marrow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13504
name: neoantigen
def: "Antigen newly expressed on a tumor or virally infected cell\, or arising after macromolecule-hapten binding."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13505
name: neonatal tolerance
def: "Tolerance to specific foreign antigens induced by the exposure of a bird or mammal to the foreign antigens during embryonic or neonatal life\, depending upon species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13506
name: autoimmune nephritis
def: "Inflammation of the kidney due to immunological reaction to renal antigens."
synonym: "autoimmune nephritis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13507
name: autoimmune neuropathy
def: "Autoimmune disease of the nervous system."
synonym: "autoimmune neuropathy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13508
name: neutralization
def: "Blocking by an antibody of the effects of a virus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13509
name: neutropenia
def: "Abnormally low concentration of neutrophils in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13510
name: neutrophil
def: "Granular leukocyte\, the major circulating phagocytic polymorphonuclear granulocyte\, having a nucleus with three to five lobes and fine cytoplasmic granules stainable by neutral dyes."
synonym: "polymorphonuclear leucocyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13511
name: neutrophil activation
def: "Change in morphology and behavior of a neutrophil resulting from exposure to a cytokine\, chemokine\, cellular ligand\, or soluble factor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13512
name: nitric oxide
def: "Generic term for nitrogen monoxide species\, including nitrosonium ion\, nitric oxide radical\, etc.\, that can function in a cell-signaling capacity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13513
name: nondepleting antibody
def: "Cell surface antibody that does not provoke attack by killer cells and thus does not provoke depletion of a group of cells in the body\, usually referring to B cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13514
name: non-Hodgkin lymphoma
def: "Any of various malignant lymphomas characterized by the absence of Reed–Sternberg cells and producing symptoms similar to those of Hodgkin disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13515
name: non-obese diabetic mouse
def: "Genetically modified mouse with a susceptibility to spontaneous development of automimmune diabetes mellitus type I."
synonym: "NOD mouse" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13516
name: non-self
def: "Of\, relating to\, or designating a cell or tissue that has not been produced by the individual organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13517
name: nuclear factor of activated T cells
def: "Transcription factor involved in rapid cell response to stress\, such as injury or invading pathogens\, and in cell proliferation and survival."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13518
name: nuclear factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells
def: "Protein complex that controls the transcription of genes involved in cytokine production\, cellular adhesion\, inflammation\, and apoptosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13519
name: nucleotide-binding oligomerization domain-containing protein 1\, 2
def: "Members of a family of human intracellular proteins involved in the detection of invasive bacteria and activation of the $\\ce\{NF\\kappa B\}$ transcription factor pathway."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13520
name: nucleotide-binding oligomerization domain-containing protein-like receptor
def: "Cytosolic protein that contains a central nucleotide-binding oligomerization domain\, an N-terminal effector-binding domain and C-terminal leucine-rich repeats \(LRRs\)."
synonym: "NOD-like receptor protein (NLRP)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13521
name: nude mouse
def: "Mouse that is T-cell-deficient due to a homozygous gene defect \(\\\(nu/nu\\\)\) resulting in the absence of a thymus \(and also lack of body hair\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13522
name: null cell
def: "Lymphocyte with neither T- nor B-cell differentiation antigens on its surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13523
name: OKT3
def: "Brand name for muromonab-CD3\, an immunosuppressant drug given intravenously to reverse acute rejection of transplanted organs\, including the heart\, kidneys\, and liver."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13524
name: oligoclonal
def: "Having a few different clones\, or the product of a few different clones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13525
name: oncofetal antigen
def: "Antigen whose expression is normally restricted to the fetus but which may be expressed during malignancy in adults."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13526
name: opsonization
def: "Coating of antigen with an opsonin to enhance phagocytosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13527
name: oral tolerance
def: "Orally induced and immune-mediated non-responsiveness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13528
name: oral vaccination
def: "Immunization against a pathogen with a vaccine administered by mouth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13529
name: Ouchterlony technique
def: "Double-radial immunodiffusion procedure for the detection of precipitating antibodies."
synonym: "Ouchterlony double diffusion assay" []
synonym: "double diffusion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13530
name: P-nucleotides
def: "Palindromic nucleotide sequences generated at the junctions between antibody and T-cell receptor \(TCR\) variable \(V\)\, diversity \(D\)\, and joining \(J\) gene segments during gene rearrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13531
name: P-selectin
def: "Cell adhesion molecule expressed on the surface of activated endothelial cells and platelets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13532
name: pancytopenia
def: "Decrease in the number of red blood cells\, white blood cells\, and platelets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13533
name: paracortical area
def: "Region of a lymph node enriched in T lymphocytes."
synonym: "paracortex" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13534
name: paramone
def: "Paracrine agonist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13535
name: paraneoplastic autoimmune syndrome
def: "Any of several autoimmune diseases that are caused by tumor-induced perturbations of the immune system with damaging effects on various organ systems \(e.g. cancer-associated retinopathy\, paraneoplastic neurological syndromes\, paraneoplastic cutaneous syndromes\). In most cases\, autoantibodies generated by antitumor immunity are responsible for the tissue injury."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13536
name: paratope
def: "Site in the variable region of an antibody or T-cell receptor \(TCR\) that binds to an epitope of an antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13537
name: particle immunoassay
def: "Immunoassay in which antibody is bound to the surface of microspheres. After incubation with the antigen-containing biological fluid\, and subsequent manipulation\, detection is usually performed by flow cytometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13538
name: passive cutaneous anaphylaxis test
def: "Assay for allergenicity in which an antibody is injected intradermally into a test animal followed at a later time \(anywhere from \\\(\\pu\{3\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{48 h\}\\\)\) with an intravenous injection of a mixture of a test antigen and a dye \(typically Evans blue\). Increased vascular permeability at the site of antigen-antibody reaction is visualized by extravasation of the dye."
synonym: "PCA test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13539
name: passive hemagglutination
def: "Method of measuring antibody titer\, in which antigen-coated erythrocytes are agglutinated by adding antibody specific for the antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13540
name: passive immunization
def: "Immunization of an individual by the transfer of antibody synthesized in another individual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13541
name: patch test
def: "Test for allergy that is performed by placing the suspected allergen in direct contact with the skin or mucosa."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13542
name: pathogen-associated molecular pattern
def: "Describing repetitive motifs of molecules such as lipopolysaccharide \(LPS\)\, peptidoglycan\, lipoteichoic acids\, and mannans\, which are widely expressed by microbial pathogens but are not present on host tissues. They are therefore utilized by the pattern recognition receptors \(PRRs\) of the immune system to distinguish pathogens from self-antigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13543
name: pattern recognition receptor
def: "Receptor found on many different cell types in the immune system that enables them to recognize pathogen-associated molecular patterns \(PAMPs\). Amongst the large number of different PRRs are the mannose receptor \(CD206\)\, macrophage scavenger receptor \(CD204\)\, and the toll-like receptors \(TLRs\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13544
name: pauci-immune
def: "Referring to vasculitis characterized by relatively little deposition of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) and complement factors\, usually describing a form of glomerulonephritis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13545
name: pemphigus
def: "Rare\, serious autoimmune disease marked by successive outbreaks of blisters\, which appear suddenly and disappear\, leaving pigmented spots."
synonym: "pemphigus vulgaris" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13546
name: peptide tetramer
def: "Structure consisting of four identical major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\)-encoded peptides\, presenting four binding sites for biotin attached to the tail of the MHC molecule\, and held together by fluorescent streptavidin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13547
name: peptide vaccine
def: "Preparation of an immunologically active peptide found in a disease-causing organism or substance\, or any of their products\, which is specially treated for use in vaccination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13548
name: peptide-binding groove
def: "Structural feature found in major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I and class II heterodimeric proteins that can bind endogenous peptides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13549
name: perforin
def: "Molecule produced by cytotoxic T cells and natural killer \(NK\) cells that\, like complement component C9\, polymerizes to form a pore in the membrane of the target cell leading to cell death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13550
name: periarteriolar lymphoid sheath
def: "Lymphoid tissue that forms the white pulp of the spleen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13551
name: periodontitis
def: "Inflammatory reaction of the tissues surrounding a tooth \(periodontium\)\, usually resulting from the extension of gingival inflammation \(gingivitis\) into the periodontium\, and involving inflammation and infection of the ligaments and bones that support the teeth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13552
name: peripheral blood leukocyte
def: "Leukocyte derived from the peripheral circulatory system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13553
name: peripheral blood mononuclear cell
def: "Monocyte produced by the bone marrow from hematopoietic stem cell precursors\, circulating in the bloodstream for about one to three days before moving into tissues throughout the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13554
name: peripheral lymphoid organ
def: "Any of the lymphoid organs \(see lymphoid tissue\) other than the thymus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13555
name: autoimmune peripheral neuropathy
def: "Acute or chronic inflammatory neuropathy leading to demyelination and axonal damage of nerves and nerve roots associated with high-titeredautoantibodies against gangliosides \[e.g.\, Guillain–Barré syndrome \(GBS\)\, Miller–Fisher syndrome\, acute sensory ataxic neuropathy\]."
synonym: "autoimmune peripheral neuropathy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13556
name: peripheral tolerance
def: "Specific immunological tolerance occurring outside of the primary lymphoid organs \(see lymphoid tissue\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13557
name: pernicious anemia
def: "Disease in which the erythrocytes are abnormally formed\, due to an inability to absorb vitamin \\\(\{\\rm\{B\}\}_\{12\}\\\). True pernicious anemia refers specifically to a disorder of atrophied parietal cells leading to absent intrinsic factor\, resulting in an inability to absorb \\\(\{\\rm\{B\}\}_\{12\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13558
name: pexophagy
def: "Autophagy selective for degradation of peroxisomes\, which can be separated into macropexophagy and micropexophagy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13559
name: Peyer’s patch
def: "Part of the gut-associated lymphoid tissue \(GALT\) and found as a distinct lymphoid nodule\, mainly in the small intestine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13560
name: phagocyte
def: "Cell\, especially of monocyte/macrophage or neutrophil lineage\, that is specialized for the engulfment of cellular and particulate matter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13561
name: phagocytic activity assay
def: "Method of quantifying phagocytosis in which yeast cells\, stained with May–Grünwald stain\, are incubated with macrophages\, and subsequently\, after a fixed time\, yeast cells engulfed in the macrophages are counted."
synonym: "phagocytosis assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13562
name: phagocytosis
def: "Process by which particulate material is endocytosed by a cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13563
name: phagolysosome
def: "Intracellular vacuole where killing and digestion of phagocytosed material occurs following the fusion of a phagosome with a lysosome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13564
name: phagosome
def: "Intracellular vacuole produced following invagination of the cell membrane around phagocytosed material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13565
name: Philadelphia chromosome
def: "Chromosome resulting from a reciprocal translocation between human chromosomes 9 and 22\, most commonly associated with chronic myelogenous leukemia \(CML\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13566
name: phorbol 12-myristate 13-acetate
def: "Mitogenic phorbol ester that directly stimulates protein kinase C \(PKC\) and acts as a tumor promoter."
synonym: "tetradecanoyl phorbol acetate (TPA)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13567
name: phospholipase Cγ
def: "Enzyme that cleaves phosphatidylinositol bisphosphate into diacylglycerol and inositol trisphosphate\, leading to the activation of two major cell signaling pathways."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13568
name: photoallergy
def: "Type IV hypersensitivity reaction in which photoactivation of a substance produces a hapten that then acts as a sensitizer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13569
name: photocontact dermatitis
def: "Type of contact dermatitis arising when substances are transformed into either irritants or allergens upon exposure to light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13570
name: photosensitivity
def: "Skin reddening due to an abnormal reaction to sunlight\, characteristic of systemic autoimmune diseases \[e.g.\, systemic lupus erythematosus \(SLE\)\, mixed connective tissue disease \(MCTD\)\]\, and cutaneous and subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13571
name: phototoxicity test
def: "Procedure used to identify the phototoxic potential of a test substance. The substance is administered either systemically or topically on the skin\, and light is subsequently shone on the skin to photoexcite the substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13572
name: phytohemagglutinin
def: "Plant lectin that acts as a T-cell mitogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13573
name: plantibody
def: "Animal antibody \(or fragment thereof\)\, expressed in a genetically modified plant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13574
name: plaque-forming cell
def: "Antibody-secreting plasma cell detected in vitro by its ability to produce a 'plaque' of lysed antigen-sensitized erythrocytes in the presence of complement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13575
name: plasmablast
def: "Highly proliferative cell that is a developmental intermediate between small B lymphocytes and immunoglobulin \(Ig\)-secreting mature plasma cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13576
name: plasma cell
def: "Terminally differentiated B lymphocyte\, with little or no capacity for mitotic division\, that actively secretes large amounts of antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13577
name: plasmacytoid dendritic cell
def: "Dendritic cell of distinct lineage\, found in blood and peripheral lymphoid tissue\, which secretes large amounts of interferon \(IFN\) upon activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13578
name: plasmacytoma
def: "Mass of neoplastic monoclonal plasma cells growing in bone or soft tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13579
name: plasmapheresis
def: "Technique of extracorporeal separation of blood cells from plasma\, with return of the cells to the patient."
synonym: "plasma exchange" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13580
name: platelet
def: "Small irregular or disc-shaped cell found in large numbers in mammalian blood\, essential for blood clotting."
synonym: "thrombocyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13581
name: platelet-activating factor
def: "Alkyl phospholipid released by a variety of cell types including mast cells and basophils\, which has immunoregulatory effects on lymphocytes and monocytes/macrophages\, as well as causing platelet aggregation and degranulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13582
name: platelet-derived growth factor
def: "Protein synthesized by platelets that is released into the serum during blood clotting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13583
name: pluripotent stem cell
def: "Self-replicating cell capable of developing into cells and tissues of the three primary embryonic germ layers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13584
name: pokeweed mitogen
def: "Plant lectin which is a T-cell-dependent B-cell mitogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13585
name: polyarteritis nodosa
def: "Systemic disease characterized by widespread inflammation of small and medium-sized arteries in which many of the foci are nodular."
synonym: "disseminated necrotizing periarteritis" []
synonym: "panarteritis" []
synonym: "periarteritis" []
synonym: "periarteritis nodosa" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13586
name: polyclonal activator
def: "Substance that induces activation of multiple clones of B lymphocytes or T lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13587
name: autoimmune polyendocrinopathy
def: "Autoimmune disease affecting multiple endocrine organs."
synonym: "APECED syndrome" []
synonym: "autoimmune polyendocrinopathy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13588
name: poly I\:C
def: "Polyinosinic\:polycytidylic acid\, a synthetic immunostimulant mimicking viral double-stranded RNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13589
name: poly ICLC
def: "Polyinosinic\:polycytidylic acid\, poly-ʟ-lysine\, and carboxymethylcellulose \(letter roots of the acronym underlined\)\, a synthetic immunostimulant mimicking viral double-stranded RNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13590
name: poly-Ig receptor
def: "Receptor molecule that specifically binds J chain-containing polymeric immunoglobulin \(Ig\)\, i.e.\, dimeric secretory immunoglobulin A \(IgA\) and pentameric immunoglobulin M \(IgM\)\, and transports it across mucosal epithelium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13591
name: polymorphonuclear leukocyte
def: "Mature leukocyte with granular cytoplasm and a segmented and irregularly shaped nucleus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13592
name: polymyositis
def: "Disorder characterized by inflammation and degeneration of skeletal muscle\, causing pain\, weakness\, and wasting in affected \(usually proximal\) muscles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13593
name: popliteal lymph node assay
def: "Test for immunosensitization measuring popliteal lymph node hyperplasia after subcutaneous injection of a test substance into the footpad of the hindpaw of a rodent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13594
name: positive selection
def: "Selection of those developing T cells in the thymus that are able to recognize self-major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecules. This occurs by preventing apoptosis in these cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13595
name: pre-B cell
def: "Cell in the B-lymphocyte lineage that has rearranged heavy chain but not light chain genes; it expresses surrogate light chains and \\\(\\upmu\\\) heavy chain at its surface in conjunction with immunoglobulins Ig _012643_ and Ig _012644_. All these molecules comprise the pre-B-cell receptor \(pre-BCR\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13596
name: precipitating antibody
def: "Antibody that is capable of reacting with a soluble antigen with the formation of an insoluble antigenantibody complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13597
name: precipitin
def: "Precipitate of antibody and multivalent antigen due to the formation of high-molecular-weight complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13598
name: pre-T cell
def: "Cell differentiating into a T lymphocyte in the thymus gland."
synonym: "single-positive cell" []
synonym: "thymocyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13599
name: prick test
def: "Test carried out by placing a drop of a suspected allergenic extract on the skin of the forearm and gently pricking it into the upper layer of skin. Sensitivity to a substance is indicated if the spot swells slightly and becomes red within about \\\(\\pu\{15 min\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13600
name: primary biliary cirrhosis
def: "Autoimmune liver disease that results in the destruction of bile ducts\, leading to fibrosis and cirrhosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13601
name: primary immune response
def: "Immune response to the first encounter with an antigen\, characterized by slow production of antibody molecules and the priming of lymphoid tissue in readiness for the production of a secondary immune response on subsequent challenge with the same antigen."
synonym: "primary response" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13602
name: primary immunization
def: "First introduction of a vaccine into the body for the purposes of inducing immunity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13603
name: primary lymphoid follicle
def: "Region of a secondary lymphoid organ containing predominantly unstimulated B lymphocytes that develops into a germinal center following antigen stimulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13604
name: primary lymphoid organ
def: "Site at which immunocompetent lymphocytes develop\, i.e.\, bone marrow and thymus in mammals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13605
name: primary myxedema
def: "Atrophic form of diffuse autoimmune thyroiditis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13606
name: primary sclerosing cholangitis
def: "Chronic\, non-bacterial\, inflammatory narrowing of the bile ducts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13607
name: prime
def: "Process of giving an initial sensitization to antigen."
synonym: "priming" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13608
name: professional antigen-presenting cell
def: "Highly efficient antigen-presenting cell \(APC\)\, generally referring to dendritic cells\, B cells\, or macrophages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13609
name: progressive systemic sclerosis
def: "Systemic autoimmune disease marked by formation of hyalinized and thickened collagenous fibrous tissue\, with thickening and adhesion of skin to underlying tissues\, especially of the hands and face and vascular alterations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13610
name: proinflammatory cytokine
def: "Cytokine that initiates or enhances inflammation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13611
name: proliferation assay
def: "Any test that determines the effect on increase in cell number of a test agent such as a cytokine or mitogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13612
name: properdin
def: "Relatively heat-labile\, normal serum protein \(a euglobulin\) that\, in the presence of complement component C3 and magnesium ions\, is involved in the alternative pathway of complement activation\, and acts nonspecifically against Gram-negative bacteria and viruses and may contribute to the lysis of erythrocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13613
name: proteasome
def: "Cytoplasmic and nuclear multiprotein proteolytic complex involved in degradation of excess\, damaged\, or misfolded proteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13614
name: protectin
def: "Member of the Ly-6 family of cell surface molecules that prevents insertion of the membrane attack complex \(MAC\) into the membrane\, thereby protecting cells from complement-induced lysis."
synonym: "CD59" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13615
name: protective immunity
def: "Protection against infectious agents conferred by vaccination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13616
name: protein A
def: "Staphylococcus aureus cell wall protein that binds to the Fc region of immunoglobulin G \(IgG\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13617
name: proteinase 3
def: "Multifunctional enzyme of azurophilic granules of neutrophils and monocytes and the major target of antineutrophil cytoplasmic autoantibodies \(ANCA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13618
name: protein G
def: "Streptococcal cell wall protein that binds to the Fc region of immunoglobulin G \(IgG\)\, with a wider species specificity than protein A."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13619
name: protein kinase C
def: "Member of a kinase family with broad substrate specificity\, activated by calcium\, diacylglycerol\, and \(or\) phorbol 12-myristate 13-acetate \(PMA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13620
name: prozone effect
def: "Loss of immunoprecipitation or agglutination that occurs when antibody concentration is increased to an extent that the antibody is in such excess that it is no longer able to effectively cross-link the antigen. A similar phenomenon may occur in antigen excess."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13621
name: pseudoallergy
def: "Inflammatory or anaphylactic reaction with symptoms similar to an allergy but not involving an antigen-specific immune response\, e.g.\, salicylate intolerance\, reaction to contrast reagents."
synonym: "pseudoallergic reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13622
name: psoriasis
def: "Skin disorder that has a hereditary component and is characterized by erythematous patches covered with silvery scales\, especially on the elbows\, knees\, and scalp."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13623
name: psoriatic
def: "Of\, relating to\, or associated with psoriasis; affected with psoriasis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13624
name: purified protein derivative
def: "Partially purified derivative of tuberculin used in the Mantoux test for tuberculosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13625
name: purpura
def: "Purple discoloration occurring in patches on the skin\, mucus membranes\, or organs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13626
name: pyrogen
def: "Any substance that produces fever."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13627
name: pyrogen test
def: "Any of various tests used to determine whether an agent\, usually an infusion or injection fluid\, is free of pyrogens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13628
name: Qa antigen
def: "'Non-classical' major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) class I molecule in mice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13629
name: radial immunodiffusion
def: "Method for quantifying an antigen by measuring the diameters of circular precipitates around an antigen-spiked cavity in an antibody-containing agar gel."
synonym: "Mancini immunodiffusion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13630
name: radioallergosorbent test
def: "Solid-phase radioimmunoassay \(RIA\) for detecting immunoglobulin E \(IgE\) antibody specific for a particular antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13631
name: radioimmunoconjugate
def: "Biochemical conjugate consisting of an immune-targeting molecule such as an antibody or antibody fragment together with a cytotoxic radionuclide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13632
name: Raynaud phenomenon
def: "Intermittent bilateral attacks of vasospasm and ischemia of the fingers or toes and sometimes of the ears and nose\, marked by a severe pallor and often accompanied by paresthesia and pain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13633
name: reactive airways dysfunction syndrome
def: "Syndrome characterized by reversible airflow limitation and complicating bronchial hyperresponsiveness induced by acute exposure to high concentrations of non-sensitizer irritant gases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13634
name: reactive oxygen species
def: "Intermediates in the reduction of molecular dioxygen\, \\\(\\ce\{O2\}\\\)\, to water."
synonym: "reactive oxygen intermediate (ROI)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13635
name: reagin
def: "Historic term for antibodies of the immunoglobulin E \(IgE\) class."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13636
name: recall antigen
def: "Substance recognized by memory cells that stimulates rapid \(secondary\) immune responses\, often associated with hypersensitivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13637
name: recombinant antibody
def: "Antibody fragment manufactured by use of genetically modified microorganisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13638
name: recombination-activating gene
def: "Gene whose products are involved in V\(D\)J recombination in B cells and T cells. Two such genes that have been identified are RAG-1 and RAG-2."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13639
name: recombination signal sequence
def: "Any conserved heptamer \(7-nucleotide\)-nonamer \(9-nucleotide\) sequence\, separated by a 12 or 23 base spacer\, which occurs 3′ of variable gene segments\, 5′ and 3′ of diversity gene segments\, and 5′ of joining gene segments\, in both immunoglobulin \(Ig\) and T-cell receptor \(TCR\) genes. It functions as a recognition sequence for the recombinase enzymes that mediate the gene rearrangement process involved in the generation of lymphocyte antigen receptor diversity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13640
name: red pulp
def: "Parenchymal tissue of the spleen consisting of cords of cells and sinuses infiltrated with erythrocytes and responsible for removal of aged or damaged erythrocytes."
synonym: "splenic pulp" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13641
name: Reed–Sternberg cell
def: "Large transformed lymphocyte\, often binucleate\, considered pathognomonic of Hodgkin lymphoma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13642
name: regulated upon activation normal T cell expressed and secreted
def: "Chemokine secreted by T cells and macrophages upon stimulation by mitogens\, which acts as a chemoattractant and stimulates eosinophils and basophils."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13643
name: regulatory idiotope
def: "Antibody or T-cell receptor \(TCR\) idiotope capable of regulating immune responses via interaction with lymphocytes bearing complementary idiotopes \(anti-idiotopes\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13644
name: regulatory T cell
def: "T cell that controls the maintenance of normal immune homeostasis. Treg cells are involved in controlling \(anergizing or counter-regulating\) autoreactive cells that escaped from thymic negative selection."
synonym: "natural regulatory T cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13645
name: rejection
def: "Immune response leading to destruction of a transplanted organ or tissue."
synonym: "transplant rejection" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13646
name: respiratory burst
def: "Generation of cytotoxic superoxide from dioxygen due to increased NADPH oxidase activity\, typically occurring in activated neutrophils."
synonym: "NADPH oxidase" []
synonym: "oxidative burst" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13647
name: respiratory hypersensitivity assay
def: "Test of the ability of a substance to induce hyperreactivity in the airways\, typically measured as an increase in the rate of respiration of guinea pigs during inhalation of the substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13648
name: reverse immunology
def: "High-throughput procedure where information on potential immunogenic tumor proteins is gained from the amino acid sequences of gene products specifically expressed by the tumor\, followed by predicted fitting of putative antigenic peptides to a peptide-binding groove\, and finally experimental verification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13650
name: rheumatic fever
def: "Inflammatory disease that may develop following a streptococcal infection such as strep throat\, and may affect the heart\, joints\, skin\, and brain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13651
name: rheumatoid arthritis
def: "Episodic inflammatory systemic disease with autoimmune pathogenetic mechanisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13652
name: rheumatoid factor
def: "Immunoglobulins IgM\, IgG\, and IgA autoantibodies to the Fc region of IgG."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13653
name: rhinitis
def: "Inflammation of the mucous membrane of the nose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13654
name: allergic rhinitis
def: "Nasal discharge resulting from an allergic response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13655
name: rocket electrophoresis
def: "Technique in which a test antigen is electrophoretically driven through an antibody-containing gel. The rocket-shaped tails of precipitation give information on the antigen concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13656
name: rosette
def: "Particles or cells bound to the surface of a lymphocyte \(e.g.\, sheep erythrocytes around a human T cell\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13657
name: scFv
def: "Single-chain molecule composed of the variable \(V\) regions of an antibody heavy and light chain joined together by a flexible linker."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13658
name: sarcoidosis
def: "Chronic\, progressive\, generalized granulomatous reticulosis of unknown etiology\, involving almost any organ or tissue\, including the skin\, lungs\, lymph nodes\, liver\, spleen\, eyes\, and small bones of the hands and feet. It is characterized histologically by the presence in all affected organs or tissues of noncaseating epithelioid cell tubercles."
synonym: "sarcoid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13659
name: scavenger receptor
def: "Cell surface receptor\, for example on phagocytes\, which recognizes cells or molecules that require clearance from the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13660
name: scleroderma
def: "Chronic progressive autoimmune disease characterized by systemic fibrosis and vascular changes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13661
name: secondary challenge
def: "Second exposure of primed lymphocytes to a given antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13662
name: secondary immune response
def: "Qualitatively and quantitatively improved immune response that occurs upon the second encounter of primed lymphocytes with a given antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13663
name: secondary lymphoid tissue
def: "Tissue \(organ\) in which antigen-driven proliferation and differentiation of mature B and T lymphocytes take place following antigen recognition."
synonym: "secondary lymphoid organ" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13664
name: second set rejection
def: "Accelerated rejection of an allograft in a primed recipient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13665
name: secretory component
def: "Proteolytic cleavage product of the poly-Ig receptor which remains associated with dimeric immunoglobulin A \(IgA\) in sero-mucus secretions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13666
name: secretory immunoglobulin A
def: "Dimeric immunoglobulin A \(IgA\) found in sero-mucus secretions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13667
name: selectin
def: "Any member of a family of cell-surface adhesion molecules found on leukocytes and endothelial cells and that bind to sugars on glycoproteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13668
name: selective IgA deficiency
def: "Most common form of primary immunodeficiency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13669
name: self-tolerance
def: "Specific immunological unresponsiveness to a defined autoantigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13670
name: sepsis
def: "Spread of bacteria or bacterial products throughout the blood\, eliciting a life-threatening\, systemic inflammatory reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13671
name: seroconversion
def: "The appearance in the blood serum of detectable antibodies against a specific infectious agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13672
name: serology
def: "Study of serum\, especially blood serum\, frequently used to detect antibodies to microorganisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13673
name: serum sickness
def: "Hypersensitive reaction to the administration of a foreign serum\, characterized by fever\, swelling\, skin rash\, and enlargement of the lymph nodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13674
name: severe combined immunodeficiency
def: "Immunodeficiency affecting both T and B lymphocytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13675
name: sheep red blood cell antigen
def: "T-cell-dependent target antigen often used in hemolytic plaque assays of immune responsiveness."
synonym: "SRBC antigen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13676
name: signaling lectin
def: "Any member of a large family of lectins that bind sialylated glycans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13677
name: signal peptide
def: "Any sequence of amino acid residues that\, when linked to a newly synthesized protein\, identifies it to transport mechanisms that guide the protein to a specific location among the organelles of a eukaryotic cell\, or from the cytoplasm to the periplasmic space of prokaryotic cells."
synonym: "signal sequence" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13678
name: signal transducer and activator of transcription
def: "Any member of a family of cytoplasmic proteins that act as second messengers to activate gene transcription in response to cytokines and growth factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13679
name: single-chain antibody
def: "Small antibody construct in which the variable fragment \(Fv\) \[see variable \(V\) region\] of a heavy chain is linked via a synthetic peptide to the variable fragment of a light chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13680
name: Sjögren syndrome
def: "Chronic inflammation of the lachrymal and salivary glands\, often accompanied by rheumatoid arthritis \(RA\) and the presence of autoantibodies in the blood\, occurring chiefly among women."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13681
name: skin immune system
def: "Skin-associated cells that participate in an immune response\, including Langerhans cells\, dendritic cells\, and keratinocytes \(mainly responsible for production of cytokines\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13682
name: skin sensitization test
def: "Any test used to study skin hypersensitivity such as the guinea pig maximization test or the Buehler test."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13683
name: skin test
def: "Procedure for evaluating immunity status\, involving the introduction of a reagent into or under the skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13684
name: slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis
def: "Group of leukotrienes released by mast cells during anaphylaxis\, which induces a prolonged contraction of smooth muscle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13685
name: small G protein
def: "Any member of a family of monomeric G proteins\, with molecular mass typically \\\(\\pu\{20\\!\{-\}\\! 40 kDa\}\\\)\, that also bind guanine nucleotides and are involved in signal transduction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13686
name: small outer capsid protein
def: "Protein from the shell of the bacteriophage T4\, commonly used in construction of phage antibody libraries."
synonym: "SOC protein" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13687
name: somatic diversification theory
def: "Theory that very few immunoglobulins \(Ig\) are inherited\, but that extensive antibody diversity arises from mutations in non-reproductive cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13688
name: somatic gene conversion
def: "Nonreciprocal exchange of nucleic acid sequences between genes in which part of the donor gene or genes is 'copied' into an acceptor gene\, but only the acceptor gene is altered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13689
name: somatic recombination
def: "Process giving rise to increased antibody diversity by cutting and splicing immunoglobulin \(Ig\) genes during lymphocyte differentiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13690
name: spleen
def: "Largest of the secondary lymphoid organs\, composed of white pulp\, rich in lymphoid cells\, and red pulp\, which contains many erythrocytes and macrophages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13691
name: splenic
def: "Of\, pertaining to\, connected with\, or situated in the spleen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13692
name: splenocyte
def: "Any splenic cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13693
name: split adjuvant technique
def: "Test for allergic contact dermatitis in which guinea pig skin is exposed to repeated applications of a test substance and intradermal injections of complete Freund’s adjuvant are administered separately."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13694
name: spondylitis
def: "Inflammation of the vertebral column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13695
name: spontaneous autoimmune thyroiditis
def: "Autoimmune thyroiditis \(see thyroiditis\, autoimmune\) that develops spontaneously \(without any apparent cause or manipulation\) in certain strains of mice and rats \(e.g.\, NOD mice\, BB and BUF rats\) as well as in other animals \(e.g.\, OS chickens\, marmoset monkeys\, beagles\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13696
name: Stevens–Johnson syndrome
def: "Allergic reaction\, often to a medication \(see adverse drug reaction\) or infection\, characterized by blistering of the skin and ulceration of mucosal membranes."
synonym: "bullous erythema multiforme" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13697
name: stroma
def: "Supporting tissue of an organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13698
name: subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus
def: "Chronic remitting form of dermatitis characterized by severe photosensitivity and Ro/SS-A and La/SS-B autoantibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13699
name: superantigen
def: "Antigen which reacts with all the T cells belonging to a particular T-cell receptor \(TCR\) variable \(V\) region family\, and which therefore stimulates \(or deletes\) a much larger number of cells than does conventional antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13700
name: superfamily
def: "Large group of proteins related by structural homology and function. The term also refers to the genes that encode them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13701
name: suppression
def: "Dominant immunological tolerance\, a phenomenon that plays an active role in regulating T-cell and B-cell responses to both foreign antigens and autoantigens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13702
name: suppressor cell
def: "T cell that suppresses the immune response of B cells and other T cells to an antigen. In more current usage classified as a regulatory T cell."
synonym: "suppressor T cell" []
synonym: "suppressor T lymphocyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13703
name: supramolecular adhesion complex
def: "Collection of molecules forming at the contact point of a T cell and an antigen-presenting cell \(APC\)\, enriched in T-cell receptor \(TCR\)\, adhesion molecules\, and signaling molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13704
name: surrogate light chain
def: "Light chain-like structure formed when the proteins encoded by the \\\(\{\\rm\{V\}\}_\{\\rm\{preB\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\uplambda_\{5\}\\\) genes associate with each other. It can form immunoglobulin \(Ig\)-like complexes which are expressed on the surface of pre-B cells at different stages of development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13705
name: switch sequence
def: "Highly conserved repetitive sequence that mediates class switching in the immunoglobulin \(Ig\) heavy chain gene locus."
synonym: "switch region" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13706
name: sympathetic ophthalmia
def: "Autoimmune injury to one eye that occurs after penetrating injury or surgery to the other eye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13707
name: syngeneic
def: "Genetically identical\, e.g.\, a fully inbred strain of mice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13708
name: systemic
def: "1\) Relating to the body as a whole.\n2\) Occurring at a site in the body remote from the point of contact with a substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13709
name: systemic autoimmune disease
def: "Autoimmune disease affecting a number of organs or tissues\, or the whole body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13710
name: systemic lupus erythematosus
def: "Chronic autoimmune disease that is potentially debilitating and sometimes fatal as the immune system attacks the body’s cells and tissues\, with inflammation and tissue damage."
synonym: "lupus" []
synonym: "lupus erythematosus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13711
name: systemic sclerosis
def: "Fibrosing disease of unclear etiology that affects multiple organ systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13712
name: drug-specific T cell
def: "T-memory cell that is specific for a drug allergen."
synonym: "drug-specific T cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13713
name: T-cell-dependent antibody response
def: "Immunotoxicity test that evaluates the ability of animals to produce antibodies to a T-dependent antigen \[e.g.\, sheep red blood cells \(SRBCs\) or keyhole limpet hemocyanin \(KLH\)\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13714
name: T-cell receptor
def: "Antigen-specific receptor on T cells composed of one set of heterodimeric chains. Two types of TCR heterodimers are known \(\\\(\\upalpha/\\upbeta\\\) and \\\(\\upgamma/\\updelta\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13715
name: T-dependent antigen
def: "Antigen that requires helper T lymphocytes \(Th\) in order to elicit an antibody response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13716
name: TdT-dependent dUTP-biotin nick end labeling assay
def: "Method for detecting apoptotic cells in situ based on characteristic DNA fragmentation. It is based on the ability of terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase \(TdT\) to transfer a labeled deoxyuridine triphosphate to the terminal ends arising from DNA cleavage."
synonym: "TUNEL assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13717
name: Th0 cell
def: "Helper T lymphocyte \(Th\) with a less restricted cytokine profile than Th1 and Th2 cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13718
name: Th1 cell
def: "Helper T lymphocyte \(Th\) producing mainly interleukin-2 \(IL-2\)\, interferon \(IFN-$\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\}$\)\, and tumor necrosis factor $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$ \($\\ce\{TNF\\mbox\{-\}\\upbeta\\!\}$\)\, and thereby responsible for phagocyte-dependent host responses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13719
name: Th2 cell
def: "Helper T lymphocyte \(Th\) in mice producing interleukins IL-4\, IL-5\, IL-6\, IL-9\, IL-10\, and IL-13."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13720
name: Th3 cell
def: "Helper T lymphocyte \(Th\) producing and responding to transforming growth factor $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$ \($\\ce\{TGF\\mbox\{-\}\\upbeta\\!\}$\) and helping immunoglobulin A \(IgA\) antibody responses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13721
name: Th9 cell
def: "Helper T lymphocyte \(Th\) producing interleukin IL-9\, which stimulates mast cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13722
name: Th17 cell
def: "Helper T lymphocyte \(Th\)\, distinct from Th1 and Th2 cells\, that secretes the interleukin IL-17."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13723
name: T-independent antigen
def: "Antigen that is able to elicit an antibody response in the absence of T cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13724
name: T lymphocyte
def: "Lymphocyte that matures in the thymus and has the ability to recognize specific peptide antigens through the receptors on its cell surface."
synonym: "T cell" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13725
name: Tr1 cell
def: "T lymphocyte that regulates Th1 cell responses. It resembles the regulatory T cell \(Treg\) and is possibly related to the Th3 cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13726
name: tandem conjugate
def: "Molecular construct with two fluorochromes where the first excites the second by its emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13727
name: target cell
def: "Cell killed by one of the body’s killer cells\, such as a cytotoxic T lymphocyte or natural killer \(NK\) cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13728
name: terminal deoxynucleotidyl transferase
def: "Enzyme that inserts non-coded nucleotides at the junctions of V\, D\, and J gene segments of immunoglobulin \(Ig\) and T-cell receptor \(TCR\) locus DNA\, thus increasing the diversity of antigen-specific recognition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13729
name: tetanus toxoid
def: "Detoxified tetanus toxin used to produce active immunity \(see active immune response\) against tetanus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13730
name: tetramer staining
def: "Technique used for selective staining of antigen-specific T lymphocytes in vitro or in situ. The antigen is presented to T cells by peptide tetramer constructs of major histocompatibility \(MHC\) class I molecules. It is also used to isolate antigen-specific T-cell populations for clonal expansion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13731
name: tertiary lymphoid tissue
def: "Ectopic lymphoid aggregates that accumulate during the process of chronic immune stimulation\, and exhibit characteristics usually associated with the secondary lymphoid organs."
synonym: "Tertiary Lymphoid Organ" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13732
name: thromboxane
def: "Any of several substances \(predominantly thromboxanes A2 and B2\) synthesized by platelets from arachidonic acid precursor\, that cause vasoconstriction of vascular and bronchial smooth muscle and facilitates platelet aggregation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13733
name: thrush
def: "Oral infection with Candida albicans\, often in patients with immunosuppression."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13734
name: thymic
def: "Pertaining to the thymus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13735
name: thymic atrophy
def: "Involution of the thymus gland leading to a diminished capacity to generate new T cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13736
name: thymic education
def: "Process by which T cells\, developing in the thymus\, are screened for potentially harmful self-reactive T cells \(which are removed\)\, while potentially beneficial T cells are promoted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13737
name: thymoma
def: "Rare\, usually benign\, tumor arising from tissue of the thymus gland."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13738
name: thymus-dependent antigen
def: "Antigen that requires the participation of T lymphocytes to elicit an immune response in B lymphocytes."
synonym: "TD antigen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13739
name: thymus-independent antigen
def: "Antigen that does not require the participation of T lymphocytes to elicit an immune response in B cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13740
name: thyroglobulin
def: "Glycoprotein secreted by thyroid follicular cells that is a major autoantigen in autoimmune diseases of the thyroid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13741
name: autoimmune thyroiditis
def: "Inflammatory destruction of the thyroid gland \(ranging from a mild focal thyroiditis to extensive lymphocytic infiltration and scarring\) often associated with goiter and hypothyroidism."
synonym: "autoimmune thyroiditis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13742
name: thyroid-stimulating hormone receptor
def: "Main autoantigenic target in patients with Graves disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13743
name: thyroid peroxidase
def: "Thyroid enzyme that is a major autoantigen in autoimmune diseases of the thyroid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13744
name: tissue transglutaminase
def: "Main target of autoantibodies in celiac disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13745
name: titer
def: "Reciprocal of the highest dilution of a titration of an antigen with an antibody that gives a measurable effect \(e.g.\, agglutination\)\, and thus an empirical measure of the avidity of an antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13746
name: tolerogen
def: "Antigen used to induce tolerance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13747
name: toll-like receptor
def: "Member of a family of pattern recognition receptors involved in the detection of structures associated with pathogens or damaged host tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13748
name: toxic epidermal necrolysis
def: "A severe form of Stevens–Johnson syndrome with extensive detachment of the skin\, often as a result of an allergic adverse drug reaction."
synonym: "Lyell syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13749
name: toxic shock syndrome
def: "Systemic reaction produced by the toxin derived from the bacterium Staphylococcus aureus; the toxin acts as a superantigen\, which activates a high proportion of CD4+ T lymphocytes to produce cytokines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13750
name: toxoid
def: "Chemically or physically modified toxin that is no longer harmful but retains immunogenicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13751
name: transcytosis
def: "Vesicular transport of macromolecules from one side of a cell to the other\, through the cell’s interior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13752
name: transforming growth factor β
def: "Secreted member of the protein superfamily of the same name that functions in controlling the cell cycle and apoptosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13753
name: blood transfusion
def: "Transference of blood or blood-based products from one individual into the circulation of another."
synonym: "blood transfusion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13754
name: transplantation
def: "Grafting solid tissue \(e.g.\, cornea\) or organ \(e.g.\, kidney or heart\)\, or cells \(particularly bone marrow\)\, from one individual to another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13755
name: transporters associated with antigen processing
def: "Molecules that carry antigenic peptides from the cytoplasm into the lumen of the endoplasmic reticulum for incorporation into major histocompatibility \(MHC\) class I molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13756
name: tryptophan
def: "Essential amino acid for humans\, and required for T-cell proliferation."
synonym: "(2S)-2-amino-3-(1H-indol-3-yl)propanoic acid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13757
name: tuberculin
def: "Antigen found in extracts of Mycobacteria\, used in a skin test for tuberculosis \(Mantoux test\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13758
name: tuberculin test
def: "Diagnostic test in which antigens derived from the organism causing tuberculosis \(Mycobacterium tuberculosis\) are injected subcutaneously; individuals who have been exposed to the organism develop a delayed-type hypersensitivity \(DTH\) response at the injection site \\\(\\pu\{24\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{48 h\}\\\) later."
synonym: "Mantoux test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13759
name: tumor antigen
def: "Antigen whose expression is associated with tumor cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13760
name: tumor immunology
def: "Application of immunology to understanding tumor biology\, immune system avoidance by tumors\, and immunotherapy directed against tumors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13761
name: tumor-infiltrating lymphocyte
def: "Mononuclear lymphocyte derived from the inflammatory infiltrate of a solid tumor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13762
name: tumor necrosis factor
def: "Protein produced and secreted by several of the body’s cell types\, including leukocytes."
synonym: "TNF-α" []
synonym: "cachectin" []
synonym: "cachexin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13763
name: tumor necrosis factor receptor-associated factor
def: "Family of proteins involved in regulating inflammation and apoptosis through interaction with the tumor necrosis factor receptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13764
name: tumor rejection antigen
def: "Antigen present specifically on a tumor cell that may target it for destruction by the immune system or for antibody therapy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13765
name: tumor-specific transplantation antigen
def: "Antigen\, present only on a tumor cell\, and targetable by the immune system or by antibody therapy in order to destroy the tumor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13766
name: tyrosine kinase
def: "Any member of a family of enzymes that phosphorylates target proteins on tyrosine residues\, thus playing a crucial role in signal transduction."
synonym: "protein tyrosine kinase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13767
name: ubiquitin
def: "Highly conserved 76 amino acid peptide abundant in eukaryotic cells. Its covalent attachment to a protein by a ubiquitin ligase complex targets that protein for destruction by the proteasome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13768
name: unresponsiveness
def: "Inability to respond to an antigenic stimulus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13769
name: Vα-Jα rearrangement
def: "Preferential partnering of \\\(\\ce\{J\\upalpha\\ gene\}\\\) segments with \\\(\\ce\{V\\upalpha\\ gene\}\\\) segments during rearrangement in the T-cell receptor \(TCR\) gene\, possibly following deletion of the \\\(\\ce\{V\\updelta\\ gene\}\\\) region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13770
name: V\(D\)J recombination
def: "Mechanism for generating antigen-specific receptors of T cells and B cells; it involves the joining of V\, D\, and J gene segments\, mediated by the enzyme complex V\(D\)J recombinase\, and products of the recombination-activating genes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13771
name: vaccination
def: "Immunization with a vaccine against a pathogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13772
name: variable gene
def: "Gene\, segment\(s\) of which rearrange together with diversity \(D\) gene and joining \(J\) gene segments in order to encode the variable \(V\) region amino acid sequences of immunoglobulins \(Ig\) and T-cell receptors \(TCRs\)."
synonym: "V Gene" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13773
name: variable region
def: "N-terminal portion of an immunoglobulin \(Ig\) or T-cell receptor \(TCR\) that contains the antigen-binding region of the molecule. V regions are formed by the recombination of V\(D\) and J gene segments."
synonym: "V domain" []
synonym: "V region" []
synonym: "variable domain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13774
name: vascular addressin
def: "Cell adhesion molecule present on the luminal surface of blood and lymph vessel endothelium\, recognized by homing molecules that direct leukocytes to tissues with the appropriate 'address'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13775
name: vascular cell adhesion molecule
def: "Molecule\, expressed on the surface of endothelial cells that functions in the adhesion of lymphocytes\, monocytes\, eosinophils\, and basophils to vascular endothelium to the vascular surface."
synonym: "CD106" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13776
name: vasculitis
def: "Group of disorders that share a common underlying problem of inflammation of a blood vessel or vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13777
name: vasoactive amine
def: "Substance containing amino group\(s\) that increases vascular permeability and smooth muscle contraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13778
name: very early activating antigen
def: "Specific antigen expressed on lymphocytes very soon after activation by phorbol myristate acetate \(PMA\)."
synonym: "CD69" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13779
name: very late activation antigen
def: "T-cell surface antigen\, named for its delayed appearance after T-cell activation; a member of the integrin superfamily involved in cell adhesion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13780
name: vetoing
def: "Elimination by apoptosis of a self-peptide-major histocompatibility complex \(MHC\) molecule recognizing lymphocyte by a self-peptide-presenting \(veto\) cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13781
name: warm autoantibody type
def: "Autoantibodies that react optimally at higher temperatures \(\\\(\\pu\{37^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)\) with surface antigens of erythrocytes. They mediate autoimmune hemolytic anemia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13782
name: Wegener granulomatosis
def: "Granulomatous inflammation involving the respiratory tract\, and necrotizing vasculitis affecting small to medium-sized vessels \(e.g.\, capillaries\, venules\, arterioles\, and arteries\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13783
name: wheal
def: "Circumscribed papule or plaque of edema of the skin\, occurring as an urticarial lesion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13784
name: wheal and flare
def: "Dermatological reaction at a skin site where an antigen is injected into an allergic individual. The reaction is characterized by a 'flare' of erythema and a wheal produced by serum exuding into tissue\, causing local edema."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13785
name: white pulp
def: "Collections of lymphocytes in the spleen\, responsible for its immune function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13786
name: Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome
def: "Condition characterized by chronic eczema\, chronic suppurative otitis media\, anemia\, and thrombocytopenic purpura \(accompanied by bloody diarrhea\); it is an immunodeficiency syndrome transmitted as an X-linked recessive trait\, in which there is poor antibody response to polysaccharide antigens and dysfunction of cell-mediated immunity."
synonym: "eczema-thrombocytopenia-immunodeficiency syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13787
name: X-linked agammaglobulinemia
def: "Sex-linked impairment of the ability to produce mature B cells\, thus characterized by recurrent infections."
synonym: "Bruton agammaglobulinemia" []
synonym: "Bruton syndrome" []
synonym: "agammaglobulinemia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13788
name: X-linked severe combined immunodeficiency
def: "Sex-linked trait carried on the X-chromosome of severe combined immunodeficiency syndrome resulting from nonfunctional B lymphocytes and lack of T lymphocytes and natural killer \(NK\) lymphocytes\, leading to recurrent\, persistent\, and severe infections."
synonym: "combined immunodeficiency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13789
name: xenogeneic
def: "Exhibiting genetic differences between species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13790
name: xenograft
def: "Tissue or organ graft between individuals of different species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13791
name: xenophagy
def: "Autophagy selective for degradation of intracellular bacteria and viruses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13792
name: Zeta chain-associated protein kinase-70 kDa
def: "T-lymphocyte-specific tyrosine kinase involved in T-lymphocyte activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13793
name: zymosan
def: "Crude preparation of yeast cell walls\, consisting chiefly of polysaccharide\, activating the alternative pathway of the complement system in the presence of properdin and used in the immunoassay of this compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13795
name: amphiphile
def: "Substance made up of molecules containing groups that are solvophilic and other groups that are solvophobic with respect to a given solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13796
name: boloamphiphile
def: "Amphiphile\, the constituent molecules of which have solvophilic groups at each end of a solvophobic chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13797
name: barotropic mesophase
def: "Mesophase formed by varying the pressure at constant temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13798
name: calamitic
def: "Having a rod- or lath-like shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13799
name: carceplex
def: "Host-guest complex formed by a carcerand and a completely entrapped guest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13800
name: carcerand
def: "Host molecule that completely entraps a guest molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13801
name: co-assembly
def: "Self-assembly of two or more self-assembling species that might or might not incorporate a non-assembling component into an ordered structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13802
name: coil-to-globule transition
def: "Change in conformation of a macromolecular chain in solution from that of a coil to a relatively compact structure in which the chain has collapsed upon itself in response to an external stimulus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13803
name: colloidal crystal
def: "Two- or three-dimensional ordered array of molecules or nanoparticles having a dimension roughly between \\\(\\pu\{1 nm\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu m\}\\\) in at least one direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13804
name: columnar phase
def: "Cylindrical assembly of molecules within a distinct series of layers in which the axes of the molecules lie in the planes of the layers and are identically aligned and oriented in adjacent layers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13805
name: core-crosslinked micelle
def: "Core-shell structure formed by self-assembly of amphiphiles and subsequently covalently crosslinked through functionalities in the core."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13806
name: core-shell structure
def: "Molecular entity of spherical\, elliptical\, or cylindrical symmetry in which the constitution of the interior\, the core\, differs from that of the external surface\, the shell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13807
name: crystal lattice defect
def: "Interruption to the regular pattern of atomic arrangement in crystal lattices."
synonym: "crystallographic defect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13808
name: cubic
def: "Pertaining to assemblies of atoms\, molecules\, or ions with an overall three-dimensional order of cubic symmetry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13809
name: cubic mesophase
def: "Mesophase with an overall three-dimensional order of cubic symmetry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13810
name: director
def: "Local symmetry axis for the singlet\, orientational distribution of the molecules of a mesophase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13811
name: discotic
def: "Having a disc-like shape."
synonym: "discoid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13812
name: discotic mesogen
def: "Mesogen composed of relatively flat\, disc- or sheet-shaped molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13813
name: dynamer
def: "Assembly or supramolecular assembly\, the components of which are linked through reversible bondings that enable structural reassembly by reshuffling in response to changes in the chemical or physical environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13814
name: electroclinic polymer
def: "Polymer with constitutional units that are susceptible to a change in orientation under the influence of an electric field that causes the system to have induced polarity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13815
name: micro-encapsulation
def: "Confinement of microscopic particles with a host."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13816
name: molecular encapsulation
def: "Confinement of an individual molecule within a larger molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13817
name: external field
def: "Field surrounding a molecular or ionic species emanating from a source external to that species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13818
name: fluorophilic
def: "Qualifier indicating attraction to fluorous compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13819
name: fluorophilic interaction
def: "Attractive interaction of fluorous structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13820
name: fluorophobic
def: "Qualifier indicating repulsion by fluorous compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13821
name: fluorophobic interaction
def: "Repulsive interaction of fluorous and non-fluorous structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13822
name: fluorous
def: "Pertaining to the presence of a sufficient number of carbon-fluorine bonds as to cause a substance to be insoluble in water or common organic solvents but soluble in fluorocarbons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13823
name: foldamer
def: "Polymer or oligomer molecule that adopts a secondary structure stabilized by noncovalent interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13824
name: globule
def: "Conformation of a macromolecule in which the constitutional units collectively arrange into a compact ovoid or spherical shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13825
name: globule-to-coil transition
def: "Reverse of a coil-to-globule transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13826
name: hemicarceplex
def: "Complex formed by a hemicarcerand and an entrapped guest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13827
name: hemicarcerand
def: "Host molecule that allows guest molecules to enter and exit its cavities in accordance with a change in the equilibrium conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13828
name: hexagonal mesophase
def: "Columnar mesophase characterized by a hexagonal packing of the molecular column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13829
name: honeycomb polymer film
def: "Hexagonal array of holes in a polymer film."
synonym: "honeycomb polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13830
name: hydrophilic interaction
def: "Attractive interaction between hydrophilic groups\, molecules\, or substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13831
name: hydrophobic
def: "Qualifier indicating capacity of a molecular entity or of a substituent to interact repulsively with polar molecules\, in particular with water or with polar groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13832
name: inverse opal
def: "Colloidal crystal of spherical voids with a periodic structure within a solid polymer continuum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13833
name: lamella
def: "Organized structure extended in two dimensions and of uniform thickness resembling a thin layer\, a plate\, or a membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13834
name: lamellar block copolymer
def: "Assembly of block copolymer molecules in which like blocks are segregated in lamella."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13835
name: layer-by-layer assembly
def: "Process whereby multilayered structures of molecules or particles of a substance are assembled on a solid substrate by sequential adsorption of monolayers held together by noncovalent interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13837
name: mechanically interlocked molecular architecture
def: "Supramolecular structure in which molecules are linked only as a consequence of their topology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13838
name: metallomesogen
def: "Mesogen composed of molecules incorporating one or more metal atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13839
name: micellar rod
def: "Rod-like mesogens with the capacity to form micellar aggregates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13840
name: microstructure
def: "Structure of material in which at least one of the features repeats over distances of the order of micrometers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13841
name: molecular imprinting
def: "Technique to create templated cavities in polymer matrices with memory of the template molecules to be used in molecular recognition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13842
name: nanodomain
def: "Region of a self-assembled entity with an equivalent diameter of approximately \\\(\\pu\{\(1\\!-\\! 100\) nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13843
name: nanoscopic polymer film
def: "Polymer film that has lateral dimensions in the range of \\\(\\pu\{\(1\\!-\\! 100\) nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13844
name: organophilic
def: "Qualifier indicating tendency of a molecular entity or of a pendent group thereof to interact favourably with nonpolar organic solvents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13845
name: organophilic interaction
def: "Attractive interaction between organophilic molecules or materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13846
name: orientation parameter
def: "Order parameter applied to macroscopic assemblies of ordered materials\, such as crystal assemblies in fibers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13847
name: pendent chain
def: "Oligomeric or polymeric offshoot from a macromolecular chain."
synonym: "branch" []
synonym: "side-chain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13848
name: polymersome
def: "Vesicle with radius ranging from \\\(\\pu\{50 nm\}\\\) to greater than \\\(\\pu\{5 \\upmu m\}\\\) constructed from an amphiphilic synthetic block copolymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13849
name: polymolecular
def: "Consisting of many molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13850
name: polyrotaxane
def: "Polymer comprising mechanically interlocked molecular architectures typically of a macromolecule threaded through one or more macrocycles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13851
name: ditopic receptor
def: "Receptor with two binding sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13852
name: rod-coil copolymer
def: "Copolymer the molecules of which consist of rigid and flexible blocks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13853
name: Scatchard equation
def: "Equation used to calculate the association constant\, \\\(K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\, of a ligand with a protein molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13854
name: Scatchard plot
def: "Linear plot of \\\(r/c\\\) against \\\(r\\\) in accordance with the Scatchard equation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13855
name: main-chain self-assembly
def: "Inter- or intra-molecular self-assembly of constitutional units within the backbones of polymer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13856
name: side-chain self-assembly
def: "Inter- or intra-molecular self-assembly of side-chains or constitutional units of polymer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13857
name: shell-crosslinked micelle
def: "Core-shell structure formed by self-assembly of amphiphiles and subsequently covalently crosslinked through functionalities in the shell."
synonym: "knedel" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13858
name: smectic
def: "Pertaining to the arrangement and orientation of molecules in a smectic mesophase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13859
name: smectic mesophase
def: "Mesophase that has the molecules arranged in layers with a well-defined layer spacing or periodicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13860
name: spherand
def: "Macrocyclic compound capable of completely enveloping a cation\, having donor atoms \(\\\(\\ce\{O\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{N\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{S\}\\\)\) arranged such that they provide a solvation sphere to the encapsulated cation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13861
name: π-π stacking
def: "Inter- or intra-molecular alignment of aromatic groups within molecules induced by the interactional overlap of their \\\(\\ce\{\\uppi\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}\\\)."
synonym: "pi-pi stacking" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13862
name: T-stacking
def: "Orthogonal alignment of constitutional units within molecules that lead to favourable interactions."
synonym: "edge-face interaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13863
name: static self-assembly
def: "Self-assembly at equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13864
name: stimuli-responsive polymer
def: "Polymer that responds reversibly to external stimulus or stimuli."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13865
name: solvophilic
def: "Qualifier indicating an affinity for solvents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13866
name: solvophobic
def: "Qualifier indicating a lack of affinity for solvents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13867
name: supramolecular association
def: "Self-assembly involving reversible\, noncovalent\, directional interactions of molecules that result in a high-molecular-mass entity."
synonym: "Lehn association" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13868
name: template polymerization
def: "Polymerization of monomer molecules adsorbed and oriented \(a\) on a surface\, \(b\) in a polymer lattice\, \(c\) on polymer molecules in solution\, or \(d\) round a metal ion whereby the structure of the polymer chains formed is controlled by the orientation of the monomer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13869
name: lipid vesicle
def: "Vesicle constructed from a lipid bilayer."
synonym: "liposome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13870
name: artificial
def: "Qualifier for something that is made by human activity\, rather than occurring naturally."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13871
name: artificial polymer
def: "Man-made polymer that is not a biopolymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13872
name: bioactive
def: "Qualifier for a substance that provokes any response from a living system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13873
name: bioactivity
def: "Capability of a substance\, such as a drug or a vaccine\, to provoke a response from a living matter."
synonym: "biological activity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13874
name: bioadhesion
def: "Adhesion of cells or tissues to the surface of a material."
synonym: "bioattachment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13875
name: bioalteration
def: "Cell-mediated chemical modification without main chain scission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13877
name: biodegradability
def: "Capability of being degraded by biological activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13878
name: biodisintegration
def: "Disintegration resulting from the action of cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13879
name: biofilm
def: "Aggregate of microorganisms in which cells that are frequently embedded within a self-produced matrix of extracellular polymeric substance \(EPS\) adhere to each other and/or to a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13880
name: biofragmentation
def: "Fragmentation resulting from the action of cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13881
name: biomaterial
def: "Material exploited in contact with living tissues\, organisms\, or microorganisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13882
name: biomineralization
def: "Mineralization caused by cell-mediated phenomena."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13883
name: biostability
def: "Resistance to the deleterious action of living systems that allows preservation of the initial characteristics of a substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13884
name: biotic
def: "Pertaining to\, or produced by\, living cells or organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13885
name: bulk degradation
def: "Homogeneous degradation affecting the volume of a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13886
name: controlled delivery
def: "Supply of a substance according to desired rate and amount over time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13887
name: degradability
def: "Capability of undergoing degradation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13888
name: degradable macromolecule
def: "Macromolecule that is able to undergo chain scissions under specific conditions\, resulting in a decrease of molar mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13889
name: degradable polymer
def: "Polymer in which macromolecules are able to undergo chain scissions\, resulting in a decrease of molar mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13890
name: degradation
def: "Progressive loss of the performance or of the characteristics of a substance or a device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13891
name: degradation
def: "Degradation that results in desired changes in the values of in-use properties of the material because of macromolecule cleavage and molar mass decrease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13892
name: degree of bioassimilation
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is bioassimilated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13893
name: degree of biodegradation
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is biodegraded under specified conditions as measured through specified phenomena or techniques sensitive to mineral and biomass formations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13894
name: degree of biodisintegration
def: "Mass fraction of a biodisintegrated substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13895
name: degree of biofragmentation
def: "Mass fraction of the original material that is biofragmented."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13896
name: degree of biomineralization
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is biomineralized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13897
name: degree of degradation
def: "Mass fraction of a polymer that is degraded under specified conditions as measured through a specified phenomenon sensitive to molecular dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13898
name: degree of disintegration
def: "Mass fraction of the particles of defined size issued from a fragmented substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13899
name: degree of fragmentation
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is fragmented."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13900
name: degree of mineralization
def: "Mass fraction of a substance that is mineralized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13901
name: depolymerase
def: "Enzyme that is able to catalyse the depolymerization of a biomacromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13902
name: deterioration
def: "Deleterious alteration of a material in quality\, serviceability\, or vigor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13903
name: disintegration
def: "Fragmentation to particles of a defined size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13904
name: durability
def: "Ability of a material to retain the values of its properties under specified conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13905
name: enzymatic degradation
def: "Degradation caused by the catalytic action of enzymes."
synonym: "enzymatic decomposition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13906
name: erosion
def: "Degradation that occurs at the surface and progresses from it into the bulk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13907
name: genetic engineering
def: "Process of inserting new genetic information into existing cells in order to modify a specific organism for the purpose of changing its characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13908
name: maximum degree of biodegradation
def: "Greater value of the degree of biodegradation that can be reached under selected experimental conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13909
name: microcapsule
def: "Hollow microparticle composed of a solid shell surrounding a core-forming space available to permanently or temporarily entrapped substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13910
name: microparticle
def: "Particle with dimensions between \\\(\\pu\{1E-7\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{1E-4 m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13911
name: microsphere
def: "Microparticle of spherical shape without membrane or any distinct outer layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13912
name: mineralization
def: "Process through which an organic substance becomes impregnated by or turned into inorganic substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13913
name: nanocapsule
def: "Hollow nanoparticle composed of a solid shell that surrounds a core-forming space available to entrap substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13914
name: nanosphere
def: "Nanoparticle of spherical shape without membrane or any distinct outer layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13915
name: plastic
def: "Generic term used in the case of polymeric material that may contain other substances to improve performance and/or reduce costs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13917
name: solid dispersion
def: "Solid multiphasic mixture with at least one polymer component dominating."
synonym: "solid dispersion polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13919
name: stimulus-responsive polymer
def: "Polymer that responds or that is designed to respond to a stimulus like pH\, light\, heat\, etc. change\, and provides a predetermined action."
synonym: "smart polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13920
name: sustained delivery
def: "Supply of a substance from a container where it is temporarily entrapped for the sake of achieving a prolonged action."
synonym: "prolonged delivery" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13921
name: synthetic biopolymer
def: "Copy of a biopolymer man-made using abiotic chemical routes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13922
name: theoretical degree of biodegradation
def: "Degree of biodegradation that corresponds to conversion of all the organic matter present in an original polymer-based item to minerals and biomass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13923
name: ultimate biodegradation
def: "Complete breakdown of a compound to either fully oxidized or reduced simple molecules \(such as carbon dioxide/methane\, nitrate/ammonium\, and water\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13924
name: artificial organ
def: "Medical device that replaces\, in part or in whole\, the function of one of the organs of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13925
name: biomedical
def: "Qualifier for a domain grouping scientific and practical activities related to therapy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13926
name: bioprosthesis
def: "Implantable prosthesis that consists totally or substantially of nonviable\, treated donor tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13927
name: bioresorbability
def: "Ability to be eliminated from an animal or human body through natural pathways."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13928
name: bioresorption
def: "Disappearance of a substance from an organism by processes of metabolism\, secretion\, or excretion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13929
name: bone cement
def: "Synthetic\, self-curing organic or inorganic material used to fill up a cavity or to create a mechanical fixation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13930
name: carcinogenicity
def: "Ability or tendency to produce cancer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13931
name: drug carrier
def: "Macromolecule or polymer used to transport a pharmacologically active compound to be released later on due to an abiotic or biotic process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13932
name: foreign body reaction
def: "Cellular response of the inflammatory and wound healing processes following introduction of a foreign object in a human or animal body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13933
name: host response
def: "Reaction of a living system to the presence of a substance or a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13934
name: hybrid artificial organ
def: "Artificial organ that is a combination of viable cells and one or more biomaterials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13935
name: immunogenicity
def: "Ability of a material or substance to elicit a cellular immune response and/or antibody production."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13936
name: implant
def: "Medical device made from one or more biomaterials that is intentionally placed within the body\, either totally or partially buried beneath an epithelial surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13937
name: macromolecular prodrug
def: "Prodrug in which the temporary chemical entity is a macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13938
name: medical device
def: "Instrument\, apparatus\, implement\, machine\, contrivance\, in vitro reagent\, or other similar or related article\, including any component\, part of accessory\, which is intended for use in the diagnosis of disease or other conditions\, or in the cure\, mitigation\, treatment\, or prevention of disease in man."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13939
name: pharmaceutical
def: "Qualifier for substances or systems\, including polymers\, exploited by the pharmaceutical industry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13940
name: pharmacological
def: "Qualifiers for substances\, including macromolecules or polymers\, and actions involved in pharmacology."
synonym: "pharmacologic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13941
name: pharmacologically active
def: "Qualifier for a substance that exhibits bioactivity of pharmacological interest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13942
name: pharmacology
def: "Science of drugs including their origin\, composition\, pharmacokinetics\, pharmacodynamics\, therapeutic use\, and toxicology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13943
name: prodrug
def: "Compound that undergoes biotransformation before exhibiting pharmacological effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13944
name: prosthesis
def: "Device that replaces a limb\, organ\, or tissue of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13945
name: stealth
def: "Qualifier for a macromolecule\, a surface\, or a device that is not detected by defense proteins of the complement and the Mononuclear Phagocyte System\, especially macrophages\, after introduction in parental compartments."
synonym: "stealth biomedical polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13946
name: targeting
def: "Exploitation of specific or nonspecific interactions to target a particular part of a living system\, or a particular type of cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13947
name: therapeutic polymer
def: "Polymer aimed at helping therapists in treating diseases or trauma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13948
name: thrombogenicity
def: "Property of a material \(or substance\) that induces and/or promotes the formation of a thrombus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13949
name: tissue engineering
def: "Use of a combination of cells\, engineering and materials methods\, and suitable biochemical and physico-chemical factors to improve or replace biological functions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13950
name: transplant
def: "Complete structure\, such as an organ that is transferred from a site in a donor to a site in a recipient for the purpose of the reconstruction of the recipient site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13951
name: aerobic biodegradation
def: "Biodegradation in the presence of molecular oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13952
name: anaerobic biodegradation
def: "Biodegradation in the absence of oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13953
name: bioplastic
def: "Biobased polymer derived from the biomass or issued from monomers derived from the biomass and which\, at some stage in its processing into finished products\, can be shaped by flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13954
name: compost
def: "Solid product resulting from the decomposition of organic wastes by fermentation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13955
name: conditioning film
def: "Film that is rapidly formed on the surface of a solid in contact with a biological system \(in the widest sense\) that conditions the surface for subsequent interaction with constituents of the biological system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13956
name: ecotoxicity
def: "Consequence of adverse effects caused by a substance on the environment and on organisms living in it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13957
name: environmentally degradable polymer
def: "Polymer that can be degraded by the action of the environment\, through\, for example\, air\, light\, heat\, or microorganisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13958
name: environmentally friendly
def: "Qualifiers for a substance\, device\, or process that has minimal deleterious impact on the environment\, which is air\, water\, minerals\, living systems\, etc."
synonym: "ecocompatible" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13959
name: green polymer
def: "Polymer that conforms to the concept of green chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13960
name: life cycle assessment
def: "Investigation and valuation of the environmental impacts of a given product or service caused or necessitated by its existence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13961
name: mulching film
def: "Polymer film aimed at covering seeded area in order to protect the growing plants from weeds and cold and preserve humidity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13962
name: sustainability
def: "Developments that meet the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their needs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13963
name: waste
def: "Residue left when a compound or a product reaches the end of its initial usefulness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13964
name: waste management
def: "Control of the collection\, treatment and disposal of wastes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13965
name: macrocyclic component
def: "Molecule that has at least one ring \(cycle\) large enough to allow it to be threaded onto a linear subchain of another molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13966
name: macromolecular component
def: "Macromolecule that is part of a rotaxane assembly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13967
name: macromolecular pseudorotaxane
def: "Pseudorotaxane\, at least one component of which is a macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13968
name: macromolecular rotaxane
def: "Rotaxane\, at least one component of which is a macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13969
name: main-chain macromolecular rotaxane
def: "Macromolecular rotaxane in which rotaxane constitutional units are exclusively located within the main macromolecular chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13970
name: main-chain rotaxane polymer
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecular assemblies that are main-chain macromolecular rotaxanes."
synonym: "main-chain polyrotaxane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13971
name: molecular shuttle
def: "Rotaxane or pseudorotaxane comprising a threading component upon which is threaded a macrocyclic component that\, depending on the conditions\, can reside at either a specific recognition site on the threading component or away from any recognition site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13972
name: pseudorotaxane
def: "Rotaxane-like molecular assembly in which the threading component\(s\) has\(have\) ends small enough to permit threading or dethreading of the macrocyclic component\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13973
name: pseudorotaxane polymer
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules that are macromolecular pseudorotaxanes."
synonym: "polypseudorotaxane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13974
name: regular macromolecular rotaxane
def: "Macromolecular rotaxane\, the structure of which essentially comprises the repetition of a single constitutional unit of the rotaxane type\, referred to as the rotaxane constitutional repeating unit\, rot-CRU\, with all units connected identically with respect to directional sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13975
name: rotaxane constitutional repeating unit
def: "Constitutional repeating unit \(CRU\) that has a rotaxane structure."
synonym: "rotaxane CRU" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13976
name: rotaxane constitutional unit
def: "Constitutional unit \(CU\) that has a rotaxane structure."
synonym: "rotaxane CU" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13977
name: rotaxane end-unit
def: "Constitutional unit of a macromolecular rotaxane consisting of an end-group of its macromolecular component and a complementary threading or macrocyclic component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13978
name: rotaxane monomer
def: "Monomer that is a rotaxane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13979
name: rotaxane monomeric unit
def: "Monomeric unit \(monomer unit\, mer\) that has a rotaxane structure."
synonym: "rotaxane MU" []
synonym: "rotaxane monomer unit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13980
name: rotaxane polymer
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules that are macromolecular rotaxanes."
synonym: "polymeric rotaxane" []
synonym: "polyrotaxane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13981
name: side-chain macromolecular rotaxane
def: "Macromolecular rotaxane in which rotaxane constitutional units are located exclusively in side chains of the macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13982
name: side-chain rotaxane polymer
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecular assemblies that are side-chain macromolecular rotaxanes."
synonym: "side-chain polyrotaxane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13983
name: stopper
def: "Group bulky enough to prevent dethreading of a given macrocyclic component from a threading component or its translocation to another linear section of the threading component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13984
name: threadable ring
def: "Key part of a macrocyclic component\: molecular ring \(cycle\) large enough to be threaded onto a linear subchain of a threading component."
synonym: "threadable cycle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13985
name: threading component
def: "Molecule with at least one linear section onto which at least one macrocyclic component is threaded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13986
name: absorbed local spectral photon flux density
def: "Local value of the spectral photon radiance\, or spectral photon flux density\, defined for a three-dimensional space\, often in terms of napierian properties."
synonym: "local spectral photon density absorption rate" []
synonym: "local volumetric rate of photon absorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13987
name: absorbed local spectral radiance
def: "Local value of the spectral radiance\, or energy density absorption rate\, defined for a three-dimensional space\, often in terms of napierian properties."
synonym: "local volumetric rate of energy absorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13988
name: absorbed radiant density
def: "Monochromatic radiant energy\, per time interval \(spectral radiance\) coming from all directions and absorbed by a system integrated over the whole volume and averaged per volume \\\(V\\\)\, \\\(\\langle L_\{\\lambda\}^\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\left\(t \\right\) \\rangle_\{V\}\\\)."
synonym: "absorbed spectral radiant density" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13989
name: accumulation layer
def: "Subsurface space where the majority carriers accumulate near the surface of a semiconductor because of band bending caused by the surface potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13990
name: active state
def: "Electronically excited surface centre\, or a surface centre \(surface site\) with a trapped photogenerated charge carrier that interacts with species at the solid/liquid\, liquid/liquid\, solid/gas\, or liquid/gas interfaces with formation of\, e.g.\, chemisorbed species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13991
name: active state
def: "Electronically excited surface centre\, or a surface centre \(surface site\) with a trapped photogenerated charge carrier or reactive intermediate that interacts with molecular entities at an interface with the formation of reaction products or intermediates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13992
name: area-related parameters
def: "Quantities expressed in terms of 'per area' are denoted as area-related parameters."
synonym: "areal parameters" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13993
name: artificial photosynthesis
def: "Photocatalytic production of substances from simple compounds \(e.g.\, \\\(\\ce\{H2\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{O2\}\\\) from water\, \\\(\\ce\{H2\}\\\) from hydrogen sulfide\, etc.\) using ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation absorbed by chromophoric systems\, often included in microheterogeneous media\, mimicking the action of antennae and reaction centres in natural photosynthetic organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13994
name: band-to-band recombination
def: "Recombination of charge carriers occurring via a one-step electron transition from the conduction band to the valence band in a semiconductor or insulator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13995
name: black body
def: "Idealised object absorbing all incident radiant power\, regardless of wavelength and transforming it all into heat. No electromagnetic radiation passes through it and none is reflected. A black body is a perfect absorber of radiant energy. Therefore\, it is also a perfect emitter of radiation. The wavelength distribution of the radiation of a black body depends only on its temperature and not on its internal nature or structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13996
name: black-body-like photocatalytic reactor
def: "Reactor used to determine quantum yields of photoadsorption and photocatalysed reactions when the catalyst is in the form of a powdered layer \(or powder dispersion\). The incident photons are \(nearly\) all absorbed by the catalyst\, provided that the dispersion layer is sufficiently thick \(or the dispersion is sufficiently concentrated\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13997
name: Brunauer–Emmett–Teller surface area
def: "Specific surface area \(\\\(\\pu\{m2 kg-1\}\\\)\) determined from the adsorption isotherm of a gas with the help of the Brunauer\, Emmett\, and Teller model using the conventional value for the molecular cross-section."

[Term]
id: goldbook:13998
name: catalyst photoactivation
def: "Generation or alteration of the catalytic activity \(selectivity\) of a substance after its interaction with light."
synonym: "catalyst photomodification" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:13999
name: catalytic site
def: "Site at which catalysed chemical transformations take place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14000
name: catalysed photochemical reaction
def: "Enhancement \(alteration\) of efficiency of photochemical reactions upon direct excitation of photoactive reactants via an intermediate interaction of these reactants with certain compounds that act as catalysts \(promoters\) of the appropriate chemical transformations of the reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14001
name: Coekelbergs effect
def: "Adsorption at the surface of a pre-irradiated adsorbent \(the photocatalyst\) occurring in the dark\, after the irradiation in the presence of the photoadsorbate entities is terminated\, i.e.\, after preliminary photoadsorption."
synonym: "Coekelbergs–Solonitsyn effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14002
name: colour centres
def: "Lattice defects \(typically vacancies\) that trap one or more electrons or holes in ionic crystals. They constitute a long-lived deep trap with a low efficiency of trapping of a carrier of the opposite sign."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14003
name: deep energy trap
def: "Trap defined by an energy level \(of trapped carriers\) within the bandgap energy of a semiconductor or insulator with an energy difference between it and the conduction band \(for electrons\) or valence band \(for holes\) much greater than the energy of thermal excitation of the solid at a given temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14004
name: depletion layer
def: "Subsurface space where the majority carriers are forced away owing to band bending caused by the surface potential. \(i\) The subsurface layer of an n-type semiconductor becomes depleted of electrons\, which are the majority carriers\, as a consequence of upward band bending. \(ii\) The subsurface layer of a p-type semiconductor becomes enriched by electrons \(depleted of holes\) as a consequence of downward band bending."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14005
name: direct bandgap semiconductor
def: "Semiconductor or dielectric materials whose first band-to-band transition occurs from the highest level in the valence band to the lowest level in the conduction band possessing the same electron momentum\, i.e.\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{max\}\} = \\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{min\}\}\\\)\, with \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{min\}\}\\\) the momentum vectors of electrons of the highest level in the valence band and the lowest level in the conduction band\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14006
name: dynamic photoadsorption process
def: "Simultaneous \(or synchronous\) formation of adsorbed species \(adsorbate\) via photoadsorption and detachment by photodesorption to yield the same species\, both processes occurring under photoexcitation of the solid photocatalyst \(adsorbent\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14007
name: effective radiation-catalytic activity
def: "Number of product entities \(atoms\, molecules\, or radicals\) generated by each \\\(\\pu\{100 eV\}\\\) of radiation incident on the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14008
name: electrical double layer
def: "Array of charged species and oriented dipoles existing at a solid-solution interface. The solid may be a metal or a semiconductor. Double refers to the fact that the surface charge and its counter charge at the solution side are included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14009
name: exciton absorption
def: "Absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation yielding optical information on the excitons in solids. Exciton absorption bands in a spectrum of a semiconductor or insulator are located on the long wavelength side of the fundamental absorption edge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14010
name: extrinsic absorption
def: "Absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation in semiconductors and insulators corresponding to optical transitions of electrons from defect energy levels \(within the bandgap energy\) to conduction band levels\, or holes to valence band levels \(photoionisation of defects\)\, or from the ground state level to excited levels of a given intrinsic defect of any sort."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14011
name: extrinsic defect
def: "Imperfection in a solid semiconductor \(or insulator\) connected with surface defects of any sort involving extrinsic chemical units in relation to the chemical formula of the solid\, particularly adsorbed species of any sort."
synonym: "extrinsic surface defect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14012
name: F centres
def: "Anion vacancies occupied by electrons in semiconductors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14013
name: Fermi level splitting
def: "Splitting of the Fermi level in irradiated semiconductors and insulators into two Fermi quasi-levels for electrons \\\(E_\{\\rm\{F\}\}^\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) and for holes \\\(E_\{\\rm\{F\}\}^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\\\)\, displaced toward the bottom of the conduction band and toward the top of the valence band\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14014
name: free charge carriers
def: "Applies to free electrons or free holes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14015
name: free electrons
def: "Photoexcited electrons from the valence band into the conduction band in semiconductors and insulators."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14016
name: fundamental absorption
def: "Absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation in semiconductors and insulators inducing optical transitions of electrons originating exclusively from the valence band to the conduction band to yield free electron-hole pairs and/or exciton absorption bands."
synonym: "intrinsic absorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14017
name: fundamental absorption edge
def: "High wavelength limit of the fundamental absorption band in the spectrum of semiconductors and of insulators."
synonym: "fundamental absorption threshold" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14018
name: heterogeneous photocatalysis
def: "Photocatalysis taking place at the interfacial boundary between two phases \(solid/liquid\, solid/gas\, liquid/gas\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14019
name: heterogeneous radiation catalysis
def: "Radiation catalysis taking place in heterogeneous systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14020
name: homogeneous photocatalysis
def: "Photocatalysis taking place in homogeneous systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14021
name: homogeneous radiation catalysis
def: "Radiation catalysis taking place in homogeneous systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14022
name: indirect bandgap semiconductor
def: "Semiconductor or dielectric materials with a first band-to-band transition from the highest level in the valence band to the lowest level in the conduction band requiring the change of electron momentum\, that is \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{max\}\} \neq \\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{min\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{p\}_\{\\rm\{min\}\}\\\) are the momentum vectors of electrons of the highest level in the valence band and the lowest level in the conduction band\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14023
name: in-scattering
def: "Physical phenomenon considering scattered radiative energy from rays in any direction \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}'\\\) into the direction \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\\\) of the rays considered in the radiative transfer equation \(RTE\)."
synonym: "scattering in (into the beam under consideration)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14024
name: interparticle electron transfer
def: "Electron transfer taking place from a photoexcited semiconductor onto another semiconductor \(or insulator\) in its ground state leading to vectorial \(irreversible\) charge transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14025
name: intrinsic emission
def: "Band-to-band recombination of electrons and holes \(or exciton decay\) in a semiconductor or insulator leading to luminescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14026
name: intrinsic defects
def: "Imperfections connected with surface point defects \(zero-dimensional\)\, linear defects \(one-dimensional\)\, or square defects \(two-dimensional\) not including extrinsic chemical units in relation to the chemical formula of the solid."
synonym: "intrinsic surface defects" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14027
name: inversion layer
def: "Layer formed when band bending places the Fermi level on the surface close to the band of the bulk minority charge carriers. This leads to a higher concentration of the minority charge carriers on the surface relative to the concentration of the majority charge carriers\, an opposite situation to that in the bulk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14028
name: local differential absorption
def: "Absorption term in the radiative transfer equation \(RTE\). Mathematical expression is \\\[\\alpha_\{\\lambda\} \(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\) L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\} \(\\boldsymbol\{x\}/t\)\\\] with \\\(\\alpha_\{\\lambda\} \(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) the space- and time-dependent linear napierian absorption coefficient and \\\(L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\} \(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) the spectral radiance arriving at position \\\(x\\\) and time \\\(t\\\) from every possible angular directions \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14029
name: natural photosynthesis
def: "Production of complex organic substances by higher plants\, algae and photosynthesising bacteria from simple compounds \(e.g.\, carbon dioxide and water\, hydrogen sulfide\, etc.\) using ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation energy absorbed by\, e.g.\, chlorophyll\, chlorophyll-protein complexes\, and natural chromoproteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14030
name: out-scattering
def: "Physical process by which the incoming electromagnetic radiation is forced to deviate from a straight trajectory by non-uniformities in the medium."
synonym: "scattering out (out of the beam under consideration)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14031
name: phase function
def: "Distribution function representing the fraction of photons scattered from any direction \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}'\\\) into the direction \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\\\) under consideration in the radiative transfer equation \(RTE\). Dimension one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14032
name: photoadsorbate
def: "Entity \(molecule\, atom\, or ion\) bound to the surface of a solid \(semiconductor or insulator\) particle \(typically to a photoadsorption centre\) as the result of photoexcitation of either the solid or the entity \(molecule\, atom\, or ion\)."
synonym: "photoadsorbed species" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14033
name: photoadsorption area number
def: "Maximum amount of substance photoadsorbed per area on the uniformly irradiated surface of an adsorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14034
name: photoadsorption capacity
def: "Maximal number of entities \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\) per regular surface site that can be photoadsorbed at a uniformly irradiated surface of an adsorbent\, \\\(\\theta_\{\\rm\{max\}\} = N_\{\\rm\{max\}\}/N^\{\\rm\{ss\}\}\\\) where \\\(N_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\) is the maximal number of entities photoadsorbed and \\\(N^\{\\rm\{ss\}\}\\\) is the number of surface sites \(mainly regular surface sites\, i.e.\, regular lattice ions or atoms; typically\, \\\(N^\{\\rm\{ss\}\}\\\) ca. \\\(\\pu\{E15 cm-2\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14035
name: photoadsorption centre
def: "Surface site or defect in its active state after photoexcitation capable of photoadsorbing species by a chemical interaction with the adsorbate entities \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14036
name: photoadsorption efficiency
def: "Ratio of the maximal \(typically the initial\) rate of photoadsorption of entities \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\) to the integrated incident \(superscript 0\, zero\) photon flux\, \\\(\\int_\{\\lambda_\{1\}\}^\{\\lambda_\{2\}\} q_\{\\rm\{p\}\,\\lambda\}^\{0\}\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\lambda = q_\{\\rm\{p\}\}^\{0\} = \{\\rm\{d\}\}N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}^\{0\}/\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\\\)\, in the wavelength range \\\(\\lambda_\{1\}\{-\}\\lambda_\{2\}\\\). Both rates should be measured under otherwise identical conditions \(preferably as initial rates\) in order to avoid concentration and/or surface area effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14037
name: photoadsorption fraction
def: "Number of entities \(atoms\, molecules or ions\) photoadsorbed per regular surface site at a uniformly irradiated surface of an adsorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14038
name: photoadsorption photonic yield
def: "Ratio of the maximal \(typically the initial\) rate of photoadsorption of entities \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\) on a surface to the incident \(superscript 0\, zero\) photon flux\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{p\}\,\\lambda\}^\{0\} = \{\\rm\{d\}\}N_\{\\rm\{p\}\,\\lambda\}^\{0\}/\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\\\) at a given wavelength. Both rates should be measured under otherwise identical conditions \(preferably as initial rates\) in order to avoid concentration and/or surface area effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14039
name: photoadsorption quantum yield
def: "Ratio of the number of photoadsorbed entities \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) \(molecules\, atoms\, or ions\) on a surface to the number of photons of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation \(at a given wavelength \\\(\\lambda\\\)\) absorbed by the system \(typically by the adsorbent\) during the initial stage of irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14040
name: photocarrier
def: "Nonequilibrium free electron or free hole generated in a solid by ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared absorption and occupying energy levels in the conduction band or the valence band\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14041
name: photocatalytic active centre
def: "Surface centre\, i.e.\, either a surface defect or a regular surface site\, at which a chemical transformation takes place after converting the centre into an active state by a photophysical process\, e.g.\, by photoexcitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14042
name: photocatalytic activity
def: "Number of entities \(product\) formed in a photocatalytic process \(alternatively the number of reactant entities disappearing\) divided by the number of photons absorbed by the photocatalyst at one single wavelength or within a defined wavelength range. For one wavelength it has the same meaning as quantum yield\, whereas within a defined wavelength range it has the same meaning as quantum efficiency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14043
name: photocatalytic centre
def: "Surface centre\, i.e.\, a surface defect or a regular surface site\, in an active or electronically excited state at which a chemical transformation takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14044
name: photocatalytic efficiency
def: "Number of entities \(product\) formed \(alternatively the number of reactant entities disappearing\) divided by the number of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared photons incident \(prior to absorption\) on the system and in a wavelength range \\\(\\lambda_\{1\}\{-\}\\lambda_\{2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14045
name: photocatalytic oxidation
def: "Commonly used to describe the oxidation of pollutants by the action of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation and a catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14046
name: photocorrosion
def: "Decomposition of the photocatalyst or of the electrodes as a result of a photoinduced reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14047
name: photocurrent
def: "Photogeneration and movement of charge between two electrodes in a photovoltaic cell or a photoelectrochemical cell as a result of photoprocesses induced by light absorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14048
name: photodesorption
def: "Desorption induced by the absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation either by the adsorbate or by the adsorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14049
name: photodesorption quantum yield
def: "Number of entities evolving into the medium from a surface divided by the number of photons absorbed at a given wavelength by a solid or by an adsorbed entity treated as a surface extrinsic defect \(centre\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14050
name: photoelectrocatalysis
def: "Electrochemically assisted photocatalysis. The role of the photocatalyst is played by a photoelectrode\, often a semiconductor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14051
name: photoelectrode
def: "Electrode capable of initiating electrochemical transformations after absorbing ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14052
name: photoelectrosynthesis
def: "Process by which ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation is absorbed by a semiconductor\, electron transfer occurs at the interface between the semiconductor and a liquid and the output is chemical energy\, i.e.\, radiant energy is stored as chemical energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14053
name: photogenerated catalysis
def: "Initiation of chemical transformations through the photochemical formation of molecular species\, which \(once formed\) are catalytically active even without the action of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14054
name: photogenerated charge carrier
def: "Charge carrying entity\, especially an electron or a hole\, in a semiconductor\, produced by the absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14055
name: photoinitiator
def: "Agent initiating certain chemical transformations under the action of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation and consumed therewith."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14056
name: photomineralisation
def: "Complete photodegradation of an organic compound into carbon dioxide and water\, and other simple inorganic compounds \(depending on the hetero-atom in the compound\) upon absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation by the photocatalyst or by the adsorbate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14057
name: photonic efficiency
def: "Ratio of the rate of the photoreaction measured for a specified time interval \(usually the initial conditions\) to the rate of incident photons within a defined wavelength interval inside the irradiation window of the reactor."
synonym: "formal quantum efficiency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14058
name: photonic yield
def: "Ratio of the rate of the photoreaction measured for a specified time interval \(usually the initial conditions\) to the rate of incident photons of monochromatic light inside the irradiation window of the reactor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14059
name: photonic yield spectrum
def: "Plot of the photonic yield of a given photoprocess\, measured at the same photon flux of incident \(prior to absorption\) light\, as a function of a measure of the energy of the photons\, i.e.\, for monochromatic irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14060
name: photoradiation catalysis
def: "Radiation catalysis under conditions not allowing a distinction between the effect of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared and of ionising radiation as well as the case in which the ionising radiation alone generates emission of chemically active light \(e.g.\, the 'Cherenkov' light emission\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14061
name: photosensitised reaction
def: "Reaction initiated by photosensitisation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14062
name: photothermocatalysis
def: "Catalysis occurring by the combined action of electronic excitation due to absorption of ultraviolet or visible radiation and thermal energy \(infrared radiation or exothermic reactions\) including catalytic phenomena."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14063
name: physical pathway
def: "Set of physical processes leading to the relaxation of the electronically excited system without chemical transformations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14064
name: radiant dose
def: "Radiant energy \(\\\(Q\\\)\, SI unit is \\\(\\pu\{J\}\\\)\) or amount of photons \(in \\\(\\pu\{mol\}\\\) or the non-SI unit einstein\) absorbed per area or volume by an object irradiated during a given exposure time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14065
name: radiation-activation
def: "Increasing of catalytic activity or alteration of selectivity of a substance after its exposure to high-energy radiation."
synonym: "radioactivation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14066
name: radiation adsorption
def: "The word combination may be misleading. Usually radiation is absorbed."
synonym: "radiation-induced adsorption" []
synonym: "radioadsorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14067
name: radiation catalysis
def: "Change in the rate of a chemical reaction under the action of ionising radiation in the presence of a radiation catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14068
name: radiation catalyst
def: "Substance absorbing ionising radiation and able to produce chemical transformations of the reaction partners\, repeatedly producing intermediate transformations and regenerating its chemical composition after each cycle of such interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14069
name: radiation-chemical yield
def: "Number of entities of a product formed \(alternatively\, the number of entities of a reactant disappearing\) generated per \\\(\\pu\{100 eV\}\\\) of energy absorbed from ionising radiation."
synonym: "G value" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14070
name: radiation desorption
def: "Desorption from solid or liquid interfaces induced by absorption of radiation by the solid or liquid adsorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14071
name: radiation-generated catalysis
def: "Initiation of chemical transformations through the formation\, by absorption of high-energy radiation\, of substances that are catalytically active even in the absence of the radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14072
name: radiation-induced adsorption
def: "Adsorption of a molecular entity at solid or liquid interfaces induced by absorption of high-energy radiation by the solid or the liquid adsorbent."
synonym: "radiation adsorption" []
synonym: "radioadsorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14073
name: radiation sensitisation
def: "Chemical change or physical process occurring in one entity \(e.g.\, biological molecules\, cells\, and living organisms\) as a result of initial absorption of high-energy radiation by the medium in the presence of another entity called the radiation sensitiser."
synonym: "radiosensitisation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14074
name: radiation sensitiser
def: "Chemical substance that increases the radiation sensitivity of\, in particular\, biological molecules\, cells and biological organisms."
synonym: "radiosensitiser" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14075
name: radiation-stimulated diffusion
def: "Increase of diffusion of an adsorbate under the action of ionising radiation absorbed by either the adsorbent and/or the adsorbate."
synonym: "radiostimulated diffusion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14076
name: radiation thermal catalysis
def: "Radiation catalysis under the action of large radiation fluxes that simultaneously heat the system as a consequence of absorption of energy from radiationless deactivation."
synonym: "radiothermal catalysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14077
name: radiative transfer equation
def: "When a photon beam of radiance \\\(L_\{\\lambda\}\\\) and angular distribution \\\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\\\) passes along a single direction of propagation through a medium\, which is totally absorbing and/or scattering\, i.e.\, the processes occurring are described by the following equation\: \\\[\\begin\{array\} \\\\ \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}L_\{\\Omega\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}s\} + \\underbrace\{\\alpha_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\) L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}_\{\\rm\{adsorption\}\} + \\underbrace\{\\sigma_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\) L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}_\{\\rm\{out\\mbox\{-\}scattering\}\} = \\\\ \\underbrace\{j_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}_\{\\rm\{emission\}\} + \\underbrace\{\\frac\{\\sigma_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}\{4\\pi\}B_\{\\lambda\}\(\\mathit\{\\Omega\}' \\to \\mathit\{\\Omega\}\) L_\{\\mathit\{\\Omega\}\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\mathit\{\\Omega\}'\}_\{\\rm\{in\\mbox\{-\}scattering\}\} \\end\{array\}\\\] where \\\(\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}L_\{\\Omega\,\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}s\}\\\) is the directional derivative along the direction \\\(s\\\) at a point \\\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\\\) in the reaction space of the incident radiance\, \\\(\\alpha_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) is the space- and time-dependent spectral linear napierian absorption coefficient\, \\\(\\sigma_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) is the space- and time-dependent spectral linear napierian scattering coefficient\, \\\(j_\{\\lambda\}\(\\boldsymbol\{x\}\,t\)\\\) represents the spontaneous and/or induced emission\, and \\\(B_\{\\lambda\}\\\) is the phase function. All quantities are a function of time. The RTE is written for the direction \\\(s\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14078
name: rate of generation
def: "Number of carriers \(or electron-hole pairs\, or excitons\) produced per time interval and per volume of a solid photocatalyst particle due to the absorption of light."
synonym: "rate of photogeneration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14079
name: rate of surface recombination
def: "Decay of carriers at the surface of a solid or interface between different phases\, e.g.\, at the crystal surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14080
name: recombination of photocarriers
def: "Two-step transition of an electron from the conduction band to a defect centre and then further to the valence band\, or from the defect centre to the valence band \(first step\) and a subsequent transition of the conduction band electron to the defect centre \(second step\)."
synonym: "recombination of photocarriers via defects" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14081
name: recombination centre
def: "Defect in semiconductors and insulators capable of sequential capture of free charge carriers of both signs with high efficiency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14082
name: regular surface
def: "Perfect surface of a solid without heterogeneity or defect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14083
name: regular surface site
def: "Zero-dimension site \(atom\, ion\, or interstitial position\) at a regular surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14084
name: relative photonic efficiency
def: "Ratio of the photonic efficiency \(\\\(\\xi\\\)\) of a chemical process to the corresponding photonic efficiency of a standard process under otherwise identical conditions in the same reactor configuration and operation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14085
name: scattering albedo
def: "Efficiency of scattering\, i.e.\, ratio of scattering coefficient\, \\\(\\sigma\(\\lambda_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\)\\\)\, to the total attenuance coefficient\, \\\(\\beta\(\\lambda_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\)\\\)\, at a particular wavelength \\\(\\lambda_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14086
name: shallow trap
def: "Energy level \(of trapped carrier\) within the bandgap energy\, near the bottom of the conduction band \(electron shallow trap\) and near the top of the valence band \(hole shallow band\) at an energy such that \\\(E_\{\\rm\{trap\}\} \\approx kT\\\)\, with \\\(k\\\) the Boltzmann constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature."
synonym: "shallow energy trap" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14087
name: Solonitsyn effect
def: "Adsorption \(or reaction\) after preliminary irradiation of a photocatalyst in vacuum \(or in the absence of reagent\)."
synonym: "post-adsorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14088
name: sonophotocatalytic reactor
def: "Reactor in which a hybrid technique combining 'sonochemical' and photocatalytic reactions is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14089
name: space charge region
def: "Region formed within a semiconductor in contact with another phase in which there is a band bending that can be upward or downward."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14090
name: specific photoadsorption number
def: "Maximum number of entities \(molecules\, atoms or radicals\) adsorbed as a result of irradiation divided by the surface area and by the mass of adsorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14091
name: specific absorption coefficient
def: "Linear napierian\, \\\(\\alpha_\{\\lambda\}^\{\\ast\}\\\)\, or linear decadic\, \\\(a_\{\\lambda\}^\{\\ast\}\\\)\, absorption coefficient divided by the mass concentration\, \\\(\\rho\\\)."
synonym: "specific linear Napierian spectral absorption coefficient" []
synonym: "specific linear decadic spectral absorption coefficient" []
synonym: "specific spectral absorption coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14092
name: specific napierian attenuance coefficient
def: "Attenuance \(napierian\)\, \\\(D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\, divided by the absorption pathlength\, \\\(l\\\)\, and the mass concentration\, \\\(\\rho\\\)."
synonym: "specific Napierian extinction coefficient" []
synonym: "specific Napierian spectral extinction coefficient" []
synonym: "specific spectral Napierian attenuance coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14093
name: specific napierian scattering coefficient
def: "Fraction of attenuance \(napierian\) not due to absorbance\, divided by the pathlength\, \\\(l\\\)\, within the scattering medium and the mass concentration\, \\\(\\rho\\\)."
synonym: "specific spectral napierian scattering coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14094
name: spectral photon irradiance
def: "Derivative of photon irradiance\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, with respect to wavelength\, \\\(\\lambda\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14095
name: spectral photonic yield
def: "Dependence of the photonic yield on the photon energy \(wavelength\, frequency\, or wavenumber\) of the radiation for a given photoreaction obtained at a certain incident \(prior to absorption\) photon irradiance in a given spectral range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14096
name: spectral quantum yield
def: "Dependence of the quantum yield on the photon energy \(wavelength\, frequency or wavenumber\) of the radiation absorbed by the reactant for a given photoreaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14097
name: surface-active site
def: "Surface regular or defect site that can initiate the chemical transformation of reagents."
synonym: "surface-active centre" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14098
name: surface recombination
def: "Recombination of charge carriers at a solid surface or at an interface between different phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14099
name: thermal bleaching
def: "Loss of absorption by the colour centres because of heating of the solid semiconductor or insulator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14100
name: thermophotocatalysis
def: "A photocatalytic phenomenon occurring when the system is also heated by the ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation."
synonym: "light-initiated thermocatalytic phenomena" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14101
name: turnover number
def: "Number of times\, \\\(n\\\)\, that the overall reaction \(the photochemical transformation\) goes through a photocatalytic cycle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14102
name: V centres
def: "Trapped holes in semiconductors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14103
name: volatile organic chemical/compounds
def: "Term commonly used to designate organic gas-phase pollutants frequently degraded by photocatalytic procedures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14104
name: absorbance assay
def: "Assay in which response is determined by the detection of light absorption by an assay component."
synonym: "optical density assay (obsolete)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14105
name: active
def: "Sample that produces a response or signal above a defined threshold at a tested concentration in a single assay or screen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14106
name: activity distribution
def: "Graphical representation of the number of samples present in each activity range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14107
name: allosteric site
def: "Binding site on a protein\, distinct from the site recognized by an endogenous agonist \(i.e.\, the orthosteric site\) in the case of a receptor\, or substrate in the case of an enzyme\, that when occupied by a ligand effects a conformational change in the protein\, thus altering its signaling\, catalytic properties\, or its capability to interact with other macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14108
name: artifact
def: "Observation\, effect\, or result that is inaccurate because it is produced by the methodology used in scientific investigation or by experimental error."
synonym: "artefact" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14109
name: assay validation
def: "Experiments conducted to verify that the output measurements of the assay are consistently reflective of the activity against the target."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14110
name: automated device
def: "Device that performs one or more functions without human intervention by means of direct control or by programmed operations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14111
name: binding assay
def: "Assay in which the specific physical association or interaction between two molecules \(e.g.\, ligand–receptor\, antibody–antigen\, protein–protein\, ligand–transport protein\) is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14112
name: binding isotherm
def: "Graphical representation of the relationship between the amount of ligand that is specifically bound to a receptor per mass of binding material and the amount of free ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14113
name: cell-based assay
def: "Assay in which the response of live cells to a test sample is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14114
name: cell membrane preparation
def: "Preparation from tissue or cells\, in which the membranous elements\, generally the cell membrane\, are enriched in relation to other cellular constituents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14115
name: cell mortality
def: "Ratio of counted dead cells to the total number of observed cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14116
name: cell viability
def: "Ratio of counted live cells to the total number of observed cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14117
name: channel blocker
def: "Compound that reduces or eliminates the conductance of an ion channel by impeding the movement of ions through that channel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14118
name: channel opener
def: "Compound that increases the conductance of an ion channel by a mechanism other than alteration of membrane potential or activation of the cognate receptor."
synonym: "channel activator" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14119
name: chemotaxis assay
def: "Cell-based assay in which the rate and/or direction of cell migration is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14120
name: combinatorial library
def: "Set of compounds prepared by combinatorial chemistry. It may consist of a collection of pools\, or sublibraries. Its composition may be described by the chemset notation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14121
name: compound collection
def: "Set of chemicals that has been assembled and annotated for easy storage and retrieval and that is available for screening."
synonym: "compound deck" []
synonym: "compound library" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14122
name: concentration–response relationship
def: "Association between compound concentration and the resultant magnitude of the continuously graded change produced\, in an assay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14123
name: confirmed hit
def: "Sample that produces activity above the hit threshold in an assay and whose structural identity has been confirmed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14124
name: counter-screen
def: "Screen in which test samples are assessed against a target for unwanted activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14125
name: cytotoxicity assay
def: "Cell-based assay in which damage to cell structure or function caused by a test sample is determined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14126
name: efficacy
def: "Extent to which a compound activates a receptor to produce a response in an assay under saturating conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14127
name: electrochemiluminescence assay
def: "Assay in which light emitted by electrogenerated luminescence is detected and used to quantify a label\, e.g.\, ruthenium\(II\) tris-\(bipyridine\)\, on one of the two assay binding components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14128
name: end-point assay
def: "Kinetic assay run for a set constant incubation time. Typically\, at the end of the set incubation time\, a reagent that stops the reaction is added to allow postponed measurement of the signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14129
name: epitope mapping
def: "Identification and localization of the specific regions of protein molecules that are recognized by the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14130
name: equilibrium assay
def: "Assay in which there is sufficient incubation time for the plateau phase of the signal to be reached and equilibrium has been established between the reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14131
name: equilibrium association constant
def: "Ratio\, at equilibrium\, of \\\(\[\\ce\{AB\}\]/\[\\ce\{A\}\]\[\\ce\{B\}\]\\\) for the reversible binding interaction of \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) to yield the complex \\\(\\ce\{AB\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14132
name: equilibrium dissociation constant
def: "Ratio\, at equilibrium\, of \\\(\[\\ce\{A\}\]\[\\ce\{B\}\]/\[\\ce\{AB\}\]\\\)\, for the reversible binding interaction of \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) to yield the complex \\\(\\ce\{AB\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14133
name: flow cytometry assay
def: "Cell-based assay in which the response is dependent on the detection of the phenotype of cells as determined through the light-scattering properties of each cell\, and\, typically\, the fluorescence intensity of a relevant probe."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14134
name: fluorescence correlation assay
def: "Assay in which fluctuations in fluorescence intensity are detected and used to characterize a species that is labeled with a fluorescent probe."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14135
name: fluorescence polarisation
def: "Dimensionless value determined from the fluorescence intensities \(\\\(I\\\)\) measured parallel \(\\\(I_\{\\Vert\}\\\)\) and perpendicular \(\\\(I_\{\\perp\}\\\)\) to the excitation plane\: \\\[p = \\frac\{I_\{\\Vert\} - I_\{\\perp\}\}\{I_\{\\Vert\} + I_\{\\perp\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14136
name: fluorescence polarisation assay
def: "Assay in which the association between an intrinsically fluorescent ligand\, or a ligand linked to a fluorescent probe\, and a larger macromolecule is detected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14137
name: fluorescence resonance energy transfer assay
def: "Assay based on the nonradiative energy transfer that occurs when the emission spectrum of a fluorescent donor molecule overlaps with the absorption spectrum of an energy acceptor molecule."
synonym: "FRET assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14138
name: functional assay
def: "Assay in which the biological or physiological activity of the target is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14139
name: heterogeneous assay
def: "Assay in which the response is detected only after the physical separation by methods such as filtration or centrifugation of one or more assay components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14140
name: heterogeneous binding assay
def: "Assay in which one binding component is immobilized to a surface \(e.g.\, cellular membrane\, glass\, gold\, bead\, or biopolymer layer\) while its binding partner is freely diffusible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14141
name: high-content screening assay
def: "A cell-based assay that produces multiple biological readouts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14142
name: high-performance liquid chromatography assay
def: "Assay in which response is determined after the physical separation of assay components using high-performance liquid chromatography \(HPLC\) with concurrent detection\, usually by monitoring absorbance at an appropriate wavelength."
synonym: "HPLC Assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14143
name: high throughput
def: "Relative term\, applied to the generation of a large number of results in a short timeframe \(e.g.\, \\\(\\pu\{E5\}\\\) in a week or a month\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14144
name: hit rate
def: "Proportion of hits from a screen that displays confirmed activity beyond a minimum defined level\, the hit threshold. It is expressed as a percentage of the number of samples screened."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14145
name: hit threshold
def: "Minimum activity that defines actives in a primary screen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14146
name: homogeneous assay
def: "Assay in which all reagents and reactants are of a uniform phase \(typically\, liquid phase\) and assay response is detected without the need for physical separation of assay components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14147
name: homogeneous binding assay
def: "Single-phase system used to qualitatively discriminate between specific and nonspecific interactions or quantitatively determine the concentration of a component part of a mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14148
name: image-based assay
def: "Assay in which image analysis algorithms that determine the compartmentalization of fluorescent probes give topological as well as quantitative information."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14149
name: immobilised metal ion affinity-based fluorescence polarisation assay
def: "Assay in which the interaction between nanoparticles coated with triply charged metal ions and phosphate groups on a substrate that carries a fluorescent tag is detected."
synonym: "IMAP™ assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14150
name: inactive
def: "Sample that\, at the tested concentration\, does not produce a response above the hit threshold in an assay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14151
name: inverse agonist
def: "Ligand that decreases signaling through a receptor below the level of constitutive activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14152
name: kinetic assay
def: "Assay in which the time dependence of the signal intensity is measured. Signal values are typically acquired at several time intervals in order to calculate kinetic parameters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14153
name: label-free assay
def: "Assay in which there is no requirement to modify one of the interacting components in order to facilitate signal detection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14154
name: label-free detection
def: "Direct detection of compound activity on a target without the requirement for a labeled reagent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14155
name: lead identification
def: "Process that is targeted toward the generation of at least one compound series that meets the requirements for progression to lead optimization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14156
name: lead optimisation
def: "Process in which the drug-like properties of an initial lead or lead series are improved."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14157
name: library
def: "Collection of samples \(e.g.\, chemical compounds\, natural products\, over-expression library of a microbe\) available for screening.\nSet of compounds produced through combinatorial chemistry or other means that expands around a single core structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14158
name: ligand-activated channel
def: "Any ion channel that is gated \(i.e.\, opened or closed\) by a ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14159
name: liquid handler
def: "Automated device that accurately and precisely delivers programmed\, predefined quantities of liquid to a microplate."
synonym: "liquid handling machine" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14160
name: luminescence assay
def: "Assay in which response is measured by the detection of light of a nonradiative origin\, such as bioluminescence or chemiluminescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14161
name: microplate
def: "Any of a number of plates containing a series of wells which may be used for storage or to perform individual assays."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14162
name: microplate standards
def: "Defined footprint\, height\, flanges\, and well positions for 96-\, 384-\, and 1536-well microplates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14163
name: miniaturisation
def: "Experimental design aimed at decreasing the reaction volume of an assay and consequently the amount and cost of reagents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14164
name: module
def: "Individual automated device within a fully automated assay system that usually performs a complete single assay step or procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14165
name: molecular imprinted polymers
def: "Synthetic polymers in which well-defined volume cavities \(imprints\) created by template molecules mimic a biomolecular specific ligand–receptor interaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14166
name: multiplex assay
def: "Assay in which the activity of a single sample against more than one target is measured in a single test."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14167
name: natural product
def: "Complex mixture derived from natural \(biological\) sources used in screening as a resource for identification of lead compounds.\nPure compound of natural \(biological\) origin \(whether obtained by purification of natural mixtures or by laboratory synthesis\)\, as opposed to compounds originating from synthetic chemistry.Note\: Typical origins include extracts from microbes and higher organisms from terrestrial or marine environments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14168
name: negative assay control
def: "Experimental conditions designed to produce the signal reflective of the absence of the process under investigation."
synonym: "low control" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14169
name: noncompetitive binding assay
def: "Assay in which the interaction of a ligand with a receptor is measured without a requirement for added competing agents. It can be homogeneous or heterogeneous."
synonym: "indirect binding assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14170
name: nuclease assay
def: "Method used to determine the presence or quantify the amount of specific target nucleotide sequences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14171
name: off-rate
def: "Rate of dissociation of the enzyme-substrate \(\\\(\\ce\{ES\}\\\)\) complex to enzyme \(\\\(\\ce\{E\}\\\)\) and substrate \(\\\(\\ce\{S\}\\\)\) or of ligand–receptor \(\\\(\\ce\{LR\}\\\)\) to receptor \(\\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\)\) and ligand \(\\\(\\ce\{L\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14172
name: on-rate
def: "Rate of formation of the enzyme-substrate \(\\\(\\ce\{ES\}\\\)\) or ligand–receptor \(\\\(\\ce\{LR\}\\\)\) complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14173
name: orthosteric site
def: "Binding site recognized by the endogenous agonist of a receptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14174
name: partial agonist
def: "Agonist that is unable to induce a maximal receptor response in a given biological assay\, regardless of the amount applied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14175
name: pipettor
def: "Device that aspirates and dispenses liquid \(\\\(\\pu\{nL\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{mL\}\\\) range\) for the purposes of transferring from a source to destination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14176
name: plate format
def: "Number and configuration of wells on a microplate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14177
name: plate gripper
def: "Handling device that positions microplates on a workstation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14178
name: plate map
def: "Layout of samples and controls configured on a plate during an assay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14179
name: plate reader
def: "Automated device that uses optical and/or computer vision techniques to detect biological\, chemical\, or physical events in samples stored in microplates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14180
name: plate stacker
def: "Automated device that loads\, unloads\, and restacks microplates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14181
name: plate washer
def: "Automated device used to wash the wells of microplates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14182
name: positive assay control
def: "Experimental conditions designed to produce the signal reflective of maximum biological effect in an assay."
synonym: "high control" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14183
name: potency
def: "Dose of drug required to produce a specific effect of given intensity as compared to a standard reference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14184
name: primary screen
def: "Initial screen applied to assess the activity of a collection of compounds and identify hits or actives against a biological target of interest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14185
name: radioligand
def: "Ligand into which a radioisotope has been incorporated as a label."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14186
name: robot
def: "Automated device that performs tasks \(i.e.\, screen functions\) that would normally be performed by a human."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14187
name: scheduling software
def: "Computer program used to organize the chronological sequence of experimental methods to be followed to execute an automated high-throughput screening campaign."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14188
name: scintillation proximity assay
def: "Homogeneous assay in which beads incorporated with scintillant emit light in the presence of radiolabeled molecules within the proximity of the bead’s scintillant detection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14189
name: screen
def: "Execution\, analysis\, and interpretation of a large number of assays to evaluate the activity of a collection of samples against a target."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14190
name: screen validation
def: "Assay conditions\, as determined by assay validation\, are performed in the chosen plate format with an acceptable signal to background ratio as described by the Z′ factor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14191
name: secondary screen
def: "Screen applied to independently confirm actives from the primary screen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14192
name: selectivity assay
def: "Assay used to determine the relative potency of active or lead compounds toward an alternative target."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14193
name: stably transfected cells
def: "Eukaryotic cells into which recombinant DNA has been introduced and incorporated into the genome\, so that the cells replicate the new DNA in a stable fashion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14194
name: standard error of the mean
def: "Sample standard deviation divided by the square root of the sample size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14195
name: sublibrary
def: "Portion of a library that is grouped or selected based on similarity of structure or biological effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14196
name: surface plasmon resonance assay
def: "Assay in which the binding of a ligand to a target immobilized on the gold surface of a chip is detected by changes in the angle of the light reflected from the surface."
synonym: "SPR Assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14197
name: targeted library
def: "Library designed\, on the basis of preexisting information\, to generate enhanced activity or hit rate against a particular biological target or target class."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14198
name: total internal reflection fluorescence
def: "Spectroscopic technique that discriminates between labeled molecules in the homogeneous phase and those interacting with binding partners immobilized at a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14199
name: transiently transfected cells
def: "Eukaryotic cells into which recombinant DNA has been introduced and expressed by cellular transcription\, but without incorporation into the stable genome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14200
name: ultra-high throughput
def: "Relative term\, currently applied to the screening of \\\(\\pu\{E5\}\\\) test samples in a \\\(\\pu\{24\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) period."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14201
name: voltage-activated channel
def: "Ion channel that is specifically activated\, or gated\, by the surrounding potential difference across the cell membrane near the channel \(or near the cell\, neuron\, or synapse\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14202
name: workstation
def: "Programmable device used to automate a single operation \(such as liquid handling\) on a microplate supplied to the device by a robot \(automated stacker system or a robotic manipulator\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14203
name: Z factor
def: "Dimensionless statistical parameter that provides a practical assessment of assay performance in the presence of test compounds. \\\[Z = 1 - 3\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{s\}\} + \\sigma_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\)/\\vert\\mu_\{\\rm\{s\}\} - \\mu_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\vert\\\] where \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) denotes the mean of the library sample signal\, \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) the mean of the control signal and \\\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) the corresponding standard deviations. \\\(1 \\gt Z \\ge 0.5\\\) generally categorizes an excellent assay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14204
name: Z′ factor
def: "Dimensionless statistical parameter used extensively in biomolecular screening\, and a characteristic of an assay without the intervention of test compounds. It provides a practical index of the quality and reliability of the assay\, and can be used as a guide to assay development and optimization. \\\[Z' = 1 - 3\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{c^\{+\}\}\} + \\sigma_\{\\rm\{c^\{-\}\}\}\)/\(\\vert\\mu_\{\\rm\{c^\{+\}\}\} - \\mu_\{\\rm\{c^\{-\}\}\}\\vert\)\\\] where \\\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{c^\{+\}\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\sigma_\{\\rm\{c^\{-\}\}\}\\\) are the standard deviations of the high and low controls and \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{c^\{+\}\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\mu_\{\\rm\{c^\{-\}\}\}\\\) are the means of the high and low controls\, respectively.\nA graphical illustration of the parameters used in the calculation of \\\(Z'\\\) is provided below."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14205
name: two-phase model
def: "Model based on the assumption that the polymer sample consists only of domains of a crystalline phase and of an amorphous phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14206
name: axial advancement per constitutional repeating unit
def: "Projected length of a constitutional repeating unit \(CRU\) on the chain axis."
synonym: "unit height" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14207
name: directional chain
def: "Polymer chain that may be oriented in two distinguishable\, opposite directions along the chain axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14208
name: directional stem
def: "Stem of a directional chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14209
name: helix pitch
def: "Distance between successive helical windings projected on the chain axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14210
name: ideal crystalline chain conformation
def: "Minimum energy conformation of an isolated polymer chain constructed according to the equivalence postulate and approximating the conformation found in a crystalline phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14211
name: left-handed helix
def: "Helix in which the continuous helical line\, approximating the sequence of bonds along the polymer main chain\, winds clockwise moving towards the observer\, i.e.\, seen from the positive axis direction \(see the figures in chain identity period\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14212
name: multiple helix
def: "Helical arrangement comprising two or more intertwined helical chains sharing the same axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14213
name: right-handed helix
def: "Helix in which the continuous helical line\, approximating the sequence of bonds along the polymer main chain\, winds anticlockwise moving towards the observer\, i.e.\, seen from the positive axis direction \(see the figures in chain identity period\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14214
name: rod group
def: "Symmetry group of a three-dimensional array of atoms\, regularly repeating along one direction."
synonym: "line repetition group" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14215
name: rotation per helix residue
def: "Angle of rotation about the chain axis per helix residue."
synonym: "unit twist" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14216
name: translational chain repeating unit
def: "Smallest structural unit of a polymer chain that is repeated by translation in the crystal lattice along the chain axis."
synonym: "fiber repeat unit" []
synonym: "physical repeating unit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14217
name: anticlined stems
def: "Stems in a crystal having opposite side-group orientations with respect to the chain axis \(see \(a\) in figure\).\(a\) Pairs of anticlined stems and \(b\) pairs of isoclined stems of regioregular poly\(3-butylthiophene\)\, i.e.\, poly\(3-n-butylthiophene-2\,5-diyl\). Since regioregular poly\(3-n-butylthiophene-2\,5-diyl\) is also a directional chain\, \(a\) is an example of antidirectional packing of two chains\, while \(b\) is an example of isodirectional packing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14218
name: antidirectional packing
def: "In a parallel-chain crystal\, the crystalline packing of directional stems with opposite chain axis orientations."
synonym: "antiparallel packing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14219
name: crystal polymorph
def: "One of the different crystalline phases in which a given substance is able to crystallize."
synonym: "crystal modification" []
synonym: "crystalline form" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14220
name: frustration
def: "Feature of crystalline packing characterized by different crystalline arrangements of chain stems at two \(or more\) sites that would be expected to be equivalent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14221
name: isochiral stem packing
def: "Crystalline packing of stems having the same helicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14222
name: isoclined stems
def: "Stems in a crystal having the same side-group orientation with respect to the chain axis \(see figure \(b\) in anticlined stems\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14223
name: isodirectional packing
def: "In a parallel-chain crystal\, the crystalline packing of directional stems with the same chain axis orientation."
synonym: "parallel packing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14224
name: packing of chain stems
def: "Spatial arrangement of polymer chain stems in a polymer crystal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14225
name: polymorphic polymer crystal
def: "Polymer crystal formed by domains of different crystal polymorphs of the same polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14226
name: racemic packing
def: "Packing in a polymer crystal in which stems of opposite helicity coexist in equal numbers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14227
name: symmetry breaking
def: "Vanishing\, in a specific crystal polymorph\, of selected symmetry elements present in other crystal polymorphs\, or in selected minimum energy conformations of isolated molecules of a given substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14228
name: orientational disorder
def: "Structural disorder at given crystallographic positions\, resulting from the statistical coexistence within crystals of directional stems with opposite orientations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14229
name: paracrystal
def: "Crystal model characterized by lattice distortions and hence by limited order along given lattice directions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14230
name: paracrystalline disorder of the first kind
def: "Structural disorder in a paracrystal\, resulting from uncorrelated displacements of the atoms around their average positions."
synonym: "uncorrelated paracrystalline disorder" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14231
name: paracrystalline disorder of the second kind
def: "Structural disorder in a paracrystal\, resulting from permanent atomic displacements from the ordered crystallographic positions."
synonym: "correlated paracrystalline disorder" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14232
name: paracrystalline distortion factor
def: "Ratio of the statistical fluctuation \\\(\\Delta a_\{ik\}\\\) of a lattice vector \\\(a_\{i\}\\\) along the direction \\\(a_\{k\}\\\) \\\[g_\{ik\} = \\frac\{\\Delta a_\{ik\}\}\{a_\{k\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14233
name: crystalline polymer-inclusion complex
def: "Crystal comprising a polymer guest within a low-molar-mass host."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14234
name: crystalline-polymer molecular complex
def: "Crystalline molecular complex between different polymers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14235
name: isomorphous polymer co-crystal
def: "Crystal consisting of two or more structurally different polymers\, resulting in macromolecular isomorphism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14236
name: copolymer isomorphism
def: "Macromolecular isomorphism involving copolymers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14237
name: homopolymer isomorphism
def: "Macromolecular isomorphism involving two homopolymers with different constitutional repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14238
name: nanoporous polymer crystal
def: "Polymer crystal presenting empty volume elements that can host low-molar-mass guest species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14239
name: polymer clathrate
def: "Polymer co-crystal in which low-molar-mass guest species are hosted in molecular-sized cages or cavities containing more than one guest molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14240
name: polymer co-crystal
def: "Crystal structure in which at least two substances coexist\, one of which is a polymer."
synonym: "polymer mixed crystal" []
synonym: "polymer-based crystalline molecular complex" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14241
name: irregular adjacent re-entry model
def: "Adjacent re-entry model in which the fold period can vary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14242
name: regular adjacent re-entry model
def: "Idealized adjacent re-entry model in which the fold periods are assumed to have all the same length."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14243
name: chain-extended crystal
def: "Folded-chain crystal in which each macromolecule makes one\, or\, at most\, a few folds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14244
name: fold period
def: "Thickness of a lamellar parallel-chain crystal in the direction of its stems."
synonym: "fold length" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14245
name: non-parallel-chain crystal
def: "Polymer crystal in which the stems are aligned along two or more different crystallographic directions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14246
name: solidification model
def: "Model of crystalline polymer formed by lamellar crystallization from the melt\, in which some stems of a macromolecule belong to one crystal\, whilst other stems of the same macromolecule are in different crystals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14247
name: amorphous domain
def: "Phase domain consisting of matter in the amorphous state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14248
name: bundle crystal
def: "Crystal in which the chains predominantly extend from the crystal surface\, resulting in a fringed-micelle model morphology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14249
name: hedrite
def: "Polyhedral aggregate of lamellar crystals resulting in a morphology that resembles that of a polymer single crystal\, at least from one viewing direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14250
name: lamellar crystal sector
def: "Portion of a lamellar crystal\, often bounded by a well-defined crystal face."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14251
name: long period
def: "Average periodicity \(repeating distance\) of the layers of lamellar crystals in a stacked assembly."
synonym: "long spacing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14252
name: polycrystalline polymer
def: "Crystalline polymer in which a large number of polymer crystallites coexist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14253
name: polymer fibre
def: "Uniaxially oriented polymer filament with macroscopic cylindrical symmetry\, in which the average direction of the polymer chain axis coincides with the cylindrical symmetry axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14254
name: polymer fibril
def: "Small diameter polymer fiber consisting of a small number of polymer chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14255
name: polymer single crystal
def: "Individually distinct polymer crystal whose lattice\, at crystallization\, is characterized by a single orientation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14256
name: row structure
def: "Polycrystalline polymer morphology resulting from nucleation of polymer lamellar crystals by multiple fibrillar structures or by macroscopic linear objects such as fibers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14257
name: dominant lamellae
def: "Lamellar crystals radiating from the center of the spherulite which\, by splaying\, constitute the initial skeleton of the spherulite."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14258
name: subsidiary lamellae
def: "Lamellar crystals nucleated either epitaxially or incoherently on dominant lamellae."
synonym: "infilling lamellae" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14259
name: transcrystalline layer
def: "Polycrystalline layer nucleated and shaped by a pre-existing planar surface within or in contact with a polymer melt."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14260
name: twinned polymer crystal
def: "Polymer crystal in which two or more single crystalline domains of the same crystal polymorph\, with different lattice orientations\, coexist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14261
name: cellulation
def: "Division of the growth front of individual or groups of dominant lamellae into divergent 'cells'\, with the impurities and the less crystallizable molecules segregated between the cells and at the spherulite boundaries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14262
name: confined-spaces crystallisation
def: "Polymer crystallization occurring inside elongated cavities\, spheres\, galleries\, or layers formed by a host material different from the polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14263
name: degree of supercooling
def: "Temperature difference between the equilibrium melting temperature and the temperature of a supercooled melt."
synonym: "degree of undercooling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14264
name: heteroepitaxy
def: "Epitaxy between crystals of different substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14265
name: homoepitaxy
def: "Epitaxy between crystals of the same substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14266
name: isothermal crystallisation
def: "Crystallization taking place at constant temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14267
name: non-isothermal crystallisation
def: "Crystallization taking place under varying temperature conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14268
name: primary nucleus
def: "The smallest crystalline entity\, the further growth of which is thermodynamically favored."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14269
name: secondary nucleation
def: "Nucleation of a new molecular layer on a growing crystal face.Note\: Secondary nucleation refers to the further growth of an already-existing crystal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14270
name: self-nucleation
def: "Heterogeneous nucleation by crystals of the same polymer."
synonym: "self-seeding" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14271
name: polymer co-crystallisation
def: "Crystallization\, within the same crystal\, of a polymer and at least one other component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14272
name: supercooled melt
def: "Metastable melt of a crystallizable polymer at temperatures below its equilibrium melting temperature \\\(T^\{\\circ\}_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14273
name: supercooling
def: "The process of cooling a melt to a temperature below its equilibrium melting point\, without crystallization occurring."
synonym: "undercooling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14274
name: polymer particle
def: "Particle of polymer of any shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14275
name: polymer bead
def: "Sphere of polymer\, usually with a diameter in the range from one-tenth to a few millimeters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14276
name: polymer microparticle
def: "Particle of polymer of any shape with an equivalent diameter from approximately \\\(\\pu\{0.1 \\upmu m\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 \\upmu m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14277
name: polymer microsphere
def: "Polymer microparticle of spherical shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14278
name: polymer nanoparticle
def: "Particle of polymer of any shape and an equivalent diameter from approximately \\\(\\pu\{1 nm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14279
name: polymer nanosphere
def: "Polymer nanoparticle of spherical shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14280
name: polymer gel particle
def: "Particle of gel in which the network component is a polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14281
name: gel microparticle
def: "Particle of gel of any shape with an equivalent diameter of approximately \\\(\\pu\{0.1 \\upmu m\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 \\upmu m\}\\\)."
synonym: "microgel" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14282
name: gel nanoparticle
def: "Particle of gel particle of any shape and an equivalent diameter of approximately \\\(\\pu\{1 nm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)."
synonym: "nanogel" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14283
name: polymer network particle
def: "Particle having any shape and composed of a polymer network and possibly species of finite molar mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14284
name: artificial latex
def: "Latex obtained by emulsification of a polymer or oligomer solution in a liquid or by emulsification of a liquid polymer or liquid oligomer in a liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14285
name: hybrid latex
def: "Latex comprising a polymer of relatively high molar mass and an oligomer or an alkyd resin\, in which there is usually chemical bonding between the two components\, formed either during latex synthesis or subsequently after formation of a film from the latex.\nLatex comprising multicomponent particles that contain both organic and inorganic material phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14286
name: inverse latex
def: "Nonaqueous latex in which the dispersed phase comprises hydrophilic polymer usually swollen with water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14287
name: latex particle
def: "Polymer particle that is present in a latex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14288
name: natural latex
def: "Latex\, the dispersed phase of which is obtained from various plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14289
name: synthetic latex
def: "Latex obtained as a product of an emulsion\, mini-emulsion\, micro-emulsion\, or dispersion polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14290
name: equivalent particle diameter
def: "Diameter of a hypothetical spherical particle of the same composition that\, using a given particle-size determination method\, would give the same diameter as a substance composed of spherical or nonspherical particles at the same concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14291
name: number-average particle diameter
def: "Calculated using the following equation\: \\\[\\left\\langle d_\{\\rm\{N\}\} \\right\\rangle = \\bar d_\{\\rm\{N\}\} = \\frac\{\\Sigma N_\{i\} d_\{i\}\}\{\\Sigma N_\{i\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14292
name: surface-average particle diameter
def: "Calculated using the following equation\: \\\[\\left\\langle \{d_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\} \\right\\rangle = \\bar d_\{\\rm\{s\}\} = \\frac\{\\Sigma \{N_\{i\}\} \{d_\{i\}^\{3\}\}\}\{\\Sigma \{N_\{i\}\} \{d_\{i\}^\{2\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14293
name: mass-average particle diameter
def: "Calculated using the following equation\: \\\[\\left\\langle d_\{\\rm\{m\}\} \\right\\rangle = \\bar d_\{\\rm\{m\}\} = \\frac\{\\Sigma \{N_\{i\}\} \{d_\{i\}^\{4\}\}\}\{\\Sigma \{N_\{i\}\} \{d_\{i\}^\{3\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14294
name: z-average particle diameter
def: "Calculated using the following equation\: \\\[\\left\\langle d_\{\\rm\{z\}\} \\right\\rangle = \\bar d_\{\\rm\{z\}\} = \\frac\{\\Sigma N_\{i\} d_\{i\}^\{5\}\}\{\\Sigma N_\{i\}d_\{i\}^\{4\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14295
name: volume-average particle diameter
def: "Calculated using the following equation\: \\\[\\left\\langle d_\{\\rm\{v\}\} \\right\\rangle = \\bar d_\{\\rm\{v\}\} = \\left\(\\frac\{\\Sigma N_\{i\} d_\{i\}^\{3\}\}\{\\Sigma N_\{i\}\}\\right\)^\{1/3\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14296
name: particle-diameter dispersity
def: "Calculated using the following equation\: \\\[Ɖ_\{\\rm\{d\}\} = \\left\\langle d_\{\\rm\{m\}\} \\right\\rangle /\\!\\left\\langle d_\{\\rm\{N\}\} \\right\\rangle = \\bar d_\{\\rm\{m\}\}/\\bar d_\{\\rm\{N\}\} = \\frac\{\\Sigma N_\{i\}\\Sigma N_\{i\} d_\{i\}^\{4\}\}\{\\Sigma N_\{i\} d_\{i\} \\Sigma N_\{i\} d_\{i\}^\{3\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14297
name: homogeneous particle
def: "Particle that is spatially uniform with respect to chemical composition of the constituent polymer\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14298
name: multicomponent particle
def: "Inhomogeneous particle consisting of two or more immiscible components.Examples of two-phase particle morphology."
synonym: "composite particle" []
synonym: "structured particle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14299
name: core-shell particle
def: "Polymer particle comprising at least two phase domains\, one of which \(the core\) lies within the other\(s\) that form the polymeric outer layer\(s\) \(the shell\(s\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14300
name: inverted core-shell particle
def: "Core-shell particle in which polymer synthesized in the second stage is located in the core of the particle and polymer constituting the initial seed is located in the shell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14301
name: multilayered particle
def: "Multicomponent particle made of at least two different polymers\, with an inner core of one polymer and with at least two layers of different polymers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14302
name: occluded particle
def: "Multicomponent particle in which one polymer forms more than one phase domain within a matrix of another polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14303
name: partially engulfed particle
def: "Multicomponent particle in which one or more polymer\(s\) cover\(s\) most\, but not all\, of the particle surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14304
name: macroporous particle
def: "Particle containing pores of diameters exceeding about \\\(\\pu\{50 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14305
name: mesoporous particle
def: "Particle containing pores of diameters between approximately \\\(\\pu\{2 \\upmu m\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{50 \\upmu m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14306
name: microporous particle
def: "Particle containing pores of diameters not exceeding \\\(\\pu\{2 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14307
name: dispersed phase
def: "Phase constituted of particles of any size and of any nature dispersed in a continuous phase of a different composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14308
name: continuous phase
def: "Phase not interrupted in space"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14309
name: dispersion medium
def: "Matrix for the dispersed phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14310
name: nonaqueous dispersion
def: "Dispersion in which the continuous phase is nonaqueous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14311
name: polymer dispersion
def: "Dispersion in which the dispersed phase consists of a polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14312
name: colloid stabiliser
def: "Compound increasing stability of a colloid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14313
name: polymer colloid
def: "Colloidal dispersion in which at least one of the phases is a polymer\, either organic\, or inorganic or some combination of the two."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14314
name: polymer emulsion
def: "Emulsion in which the dispersed phase is a liquid polymer or a polymer solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14315
name: macro-emulsion
def: "Emulsion in which the particles of the dispersed phase have diameters from approximately \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu m\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 \\upmu m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14316
name: mini-emulsion
def: "Emulsion in which the particles of the dispersed phase have diameters in the range from approximately \\\(\\pu\{50 nm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14317
name: micro-emulsion
def: "Dispersion made of water\, oil\, and surfactant\(s\) that is an isotropic and thermodynamically stable system with dispersed domain diameter varying approximately from \\\(\\pu\{1 nm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{100 nm\}\\\)\, usually \\\(\\pu\{10 nm\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{50 nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14318
name: hemi-micelle
def: "Type of micelle that exists in relatively small numbers below the critical micelle concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14319
name: ad-micelle
def: "Surfactant bilayer formed on a charged adsorbing surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14320
name: micellar aggregation number
def: "Number of molecules constituting a micelle."
synonym: "micellar degree of association" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14321
name: micellar charge
def: "Combined charge of the surfactant ions and counterions tightly bound to a micelle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14322
name: micellar relative molar mass
def: "Mass of a mole of micelles divided by the molar mass constant. The relative molar mass of micelles \(mic\) is thus \\\(M_\{\\rm\{r\,mic\}\} = M_\{\\rm\{mic\}\}/M_\{\\rm\{u\}\}\\\)."
synonym: "micellar molecular weight" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14323
name: particle number concentration
def: "Number of particles per volume of suspending medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14324
name: solids content
def: "Mass fraction of nonvolatile material in a polymer dispersion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14325
name: polymer content
def: "Mass fraction of polymer in a polymer dispersion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14326
name: dispersed-phase concentration
def: "Amount concentration of a species within the dispersed phase."
synonym: "dispersed-phase amount concentration" []
synonym: "particle-phase amount concentration" []
synonym: "particle-phase concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14327
name: continuous-phase concentration
def: "Concentration of species within the continuous phase of a dispersion."
synonym: "continuous-phase amount concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14328
name: polymer mass fraction
def: "Mass fraction of polymer within the dispersed phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14329
name: emulsion polymerisation
def: "Polymerization whereby monomer\(s\)\, initiator\, dispersion medium\, and possibly colloid stabilizer constitute initially an inhomogeneous system resulting in particles of colloidal dimensions containing the formed polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14330
name: ab initio emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization in which no seed particles are added."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14331
name: batch emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization in which all the ingredients are placed in a reactor prior to reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14332
name: continuous emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization in which all the ingredients are added continuously and the product latex is removed continuously."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14333
name: emulsifier-free emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization carried out without the addition of a colloid stabilizer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14334
name: encapsulating emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization leading to the encapsulation of a solid within polymer particles or liquid polymer droplets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14335
name: inverse emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization in a nonaqueous medium in which the dispersed phase is usually an aqueous solution\, initially of monomer\(s\)\, and finally of polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14336
name: monomer-flooded emulsion polymerisation
def: "Semi-continuous emulsion polymerization in which the monomer\(s\) is\(are\) fed to the reactor at a rate that exceeds the rate of polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14337
name: monomer-starved emulsion polymerisation
def: "Semi-continuous emulsion polymerization in which the polymerization rate is controlled by the feed rate\(s\) of monomer\(s\)\, in such a way that\, for most of the process\, the polymerization rate equals the monomer\(s\) feed rate\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14338
name: power-feed emulsion polymerisation
def: "Semi-continuous emulsion copolymerization in which the instantaneous composition of the formed copolymer is the same as that of the added monomer mixture\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14339
name: seeded emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization with seed particles are formed in situ or added initially to the polymerizing mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14340
name: semi-continuous emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization in which some of the ingredients are initially placed in a reactor and the remaining ingredients are added during the polymerization."
synonym: "semi-batch emulsion polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14341
name: vesicle polymerisation
def: "Polymerization inside the bilayer of a vesicle leading to formation of polymer inside the bilayer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14342
name: micro-emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization in which the starting system is a micro-emulsion and the final latex comprises colloidal particles of polymer dispersed in an aqueous medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14343
name: inverse micro-emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization in which the starting system is a micro-emulsion and the final system is composed of an organic continuous phase with an aqueous polymer solution as the dispersed phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14344
name: micellar polymerisation
def: "Polymerization of a polymerizable surfactant in solution above its critical micelle concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14345
name: mini-emulsion polymerisation
def: "Polymerization of a mini-emulsion of monomer in which all of the polymerization occurs within preexisting monomer particles without the formation of new particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14346
name: inverse mini-emulsion polymerisation
def: "Emulsion polymerization in which the starting system is a mini-emulsion and the final system is composed of an organic continuous phase with an aqueous polymer solution as the dispersed phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14347
name: precipitation polymerisation
def: "Polymerization in which monomer\(s\)\, initiator\(s\) and colloid stabilizer\(s\) are dissolved in a solvent and this continuous phase that is a nonsolvent for the formed polymer beyond a critical molecular weight."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14348
name: dispersion polymerisation
def: "Precipitation polymerization in which monomer\(s\)\, initiator\(s\)\, and colloid stabilizer\(s\) are dissolved in a solvent forming initially a homogeneous system that produces polymer and results in the formation of polymer particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14349
name: seeded dispersion polymerisation
def: "Dispersion polymerization in which seed particles are formed in situ or added prior to initiation of the polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14350
name: precipitation polycondensation
def: "Precipitation polymerization proceeding by polycondensation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14351
name: dispersion polycondensation
def: "Dispersion polymerization proceeding by polycondensation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14352
name: precipitation polyaddition
def: "Precipitation polymerization proceeding by polyaddition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14353
name: dispersion polyaddition
def: "Dispersion polymerization proceeding by polyaddition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14354
name: suspension polymerisation
def: "Polymerization in which polymer is formed in monomer\, or monomer-solvent droplets in a continuous phase that is a nonsolvent for both the monomer and the formed polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14355
name: micro-suspension polymerisation
def: "Suspension polymerization in which the diameter of the monomer droplets is of the order of a few \\\(\\pu\{\\upmu m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14356
name: average number of radicals per particle
def: "Ratio of the total number of radicals in particles to the number of particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14357
name: critical oligomer degree of polymerisation for irreversible entry
def: "Lowest degree of polymerization of aqueous-phase oligomer-radicals needed for irreversible capture by colloidal particles\, micelles\, or both during a polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14358
name: critical oligomer degree of polymerisation for precipitation
def: "Lowest degree of polymerization of oligomer-radicals that precipitate from the continuous phase during a polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14359
name: intervals in emulsion polymerisations
def: "Periods in an emulsion polymerization defined by the formation of polymer particles\, and the presence or absence of monomer droplets in the polymerizing mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14360
name: interval 1 in emulsion polymerisation
def: "Period in a batch ab initio emulsion polymerization during which the formation of particles takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14361
name: interval 2 in emulsion polymerisation
def: "Period in an emulsion polymerization during which no new particles are formed and monomer droplets are present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14362
name: interval 3 in emulsion polymerisation
def: "Period in an emulsion polymerization during which no new particles are formed and no monomer droplets are present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14363
name: zero-one behaviour
def: "Limiting behaviour in an emulsion\, mini-emulsion\, or micro-emulsion polymerization during which the entry of a radical into a particle that contains a growing radical results in termination before significant propagation has occurred."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14364
name: compartmentalisation behaviour
def: "Zero-one behaviour wherein radicals are isolated\, each being located within a different latex particle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14365
name: pseudo-bulk behaviour
def: "Behaviour in an emulsion\, mini-emulsion\, micro-emulsion\, suspension\, or dispersion polymerization wherein the kinetics are such that the rate equations are the same as those for polymerization in bulk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14366
name: homogeneous micellisation nucleation
def: "Formation of primary particles as a result of micelle formation from surface-active oligomer radicals formed in a polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14367
name: micellar nucleation
def: "Formation of primary particles as a result of polymerization within monomer-swollen micelles initiated by the capture of primary radicals or oligomer-radicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14368
name: coagulative nucleation
def: "A process combining nucleation by any mechanism with subsequent coagulation being a significant event in formation of colloidally stable particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14369
name: phase-transfer event in a dispersed system
def: "Transport of any species \(radical\, monomer\, chain-transfer agent\, etc.\) from the continuous to the discrete phase and vice-versa."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14370
name: radical entry
def: "Irreversible transport of a radical from the continuous to the dispersed phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14371
name: radical entry frequency
def: "Average number of entry events per particle per unit interval of time."
synonym: "entry frequency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14372
name: radical exit
def: "Reversible or irreversible transport of a radical from the dispersed to the continuous phase."
synonym: "radical desorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14373
name: radical exit frequency
def: "Average number of exit events per particle per unit interval of time per radical."
synonym: "exit frequency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14375
name: primary radical
def: "Radicals formed from an initiator molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14376
name: seed particle
def: "Particle in a polymer colloid that is the locus of subsequent polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14377
name: surfmer
def: "Monomer with the properties of a surfactant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14378
name: inisurf
def: "Initiator with the properties of a surfactant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14379
name: transurf
def: "Chain-transfer agent with the properties of a surfactant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14380
name: breaking of an emulsion
def: "Formation of a system with separate macrophases from an emulsion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14381
name: critical coagulation concentration
def: "Minimum concentration of electrolyte at and above which rapid coagulation occurs."
synonym: "critical coagulation amount concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14382
name: heterocoagulation
def: "Coagulation of particles of different kinds or sizes\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14383
name: homocoagulation
def: "Coagulation of colloidal particles of the same size and kind."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14384
name: fast coagulation rate
def: "Rate of coagulation in the absence of any repulsive barrier between particles."
synonym: "rapid coagulation rate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14385
name: fast coagulation rate coefficient
def: "Rate coefficient for fast coagulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14387
name: flocculation rate coefficient
def: "Rate coefficient for flocculation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14388
name: micellisation
def: "Process in which surface-active molecules or ions aggregate into micelles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14389
name: orthokinetic coagulation
def: "Coagulation due to collisions of particles induced by hydrodynamic motion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14390
name: particle monolayer
def: "Monolayer of particles deposited at an interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14391
name: perikinetic coagulation
def: "Coagulation due to collisions of particles caused by their Brownian motion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14392
name: slow coagulation rate
def: "Rate of coagulation in presence of repulsive barriers between particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14393
name: slow coagulation rate coefficient
def: "Rate coefficient for slow coagulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14394
name: stability ratio
def: "Ratio \\\(W = k_\{\\rm\{fast\}\}/k_\{\\rm\{slow\}\}\\\) or \\\(W = k_\{\\rm\{fast\}\}/k_\{\\rm\{floc\}\}\\\)\, for coagulation or flocculation\, respectively\, with \\\(k_\{\\rm\{fast\}\}\\\)\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{slow\}\}\\\)\, and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{floc\}\}\\\) measured under the same mixing \(or hydrodynamic\) conditions."
synonym: "Fuchs stability ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14395
name: colloidally stable system
def: "System in which the particles essentially do not aggregate or sediment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14396
name: electrostatic stabilisation
def: "Stabilization of a colloid resulting from the mutual repulsion of the electrical double layers surrounding its particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14397
name: electrosteric stabilisation
def: "Stabilization of a colloid that has both steric and electrostatic stabilization characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14398
name: steric stabilisation
def: "Stabilization of a colloid resulting from covering particles with a layer of molecules solvated by the continuous medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14399
name: ecotoxicology
def: "Study of the toxic effects of chemical and physical agents on all living organisms\, especially on populations and communities within defined ecosystems; it includes transfer pathways of these agents and their interactions with the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14400
name: mutagenicity
def: "Ability of a physical\, chemical\, or biological agent to induce \(or generate\) heritable changes \(mutations\) in the genotype in a cell as a consequence of alterations or loss of genes or chromosomes \(or parts thereof\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14401
name: ultrafine particle
def: "Particle in air of aerodynamic diameter \\\(\\pu\{\\lt 0.1 \\upmu m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14402
name: toxicogenetics
def: "Study of the influence of hereditary factors on the effects of potentially toxic substances on individual organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14403
name: reproductive toxicity
def: "Ability of a physical\, chemical\, or biological agent to induce adverse effects in the reproductive system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14404
name: reproductive toxicology
def: "Study of the non-heritable adverse effects of substances on male and female reproductive function or capacity and on resultant progeny."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14405
name: risk assessment management process
def: "Global term for the whole process from hazard identification to risk management."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14406
name: risk management
def: "Decision-making process involving considerations of political\, social\, economic\, and engineering factors with relevant risk assessments relating to a potential hazard so as to develop\, analyze\, and compare regulatory options and to select the optimal regulatory response for safety from that hazard."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14407
name: safety
def: "Reciprocal of risk\: practical certainty that injury will not result from a hazard under defined conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14408
name: safety data sheet
def: "Single page giving toxicological and other safety advice\, usually associated with a particular preparation\, substance\, process\, use pattern\, or exposure scenario."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14409
name: immunotoxicity
def: "Ability of a physical\, chemical\, or biological agent to induce adverse effects in the immune system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14410
name: dormant polymer chain
def: "Temporarily deactivated chain carrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14411
name: reversible chain deactivation
def: "Deactivation of a chain carrier in a chain polymerisation\, reversibly converting the active center into an inactive one and then\, within the lifetime of a growing macromolecule\, regenerating an active center on the same original chain carrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14412
name: degenerate-transfer radical polymerisation
def: "Controlled reversible-deactivation radical polymerization in which the deactivation of the radicals involves degenerative transfer \(DT\) of a group \(or atom\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14413
name: absorbate
def: "Substance that enters and is retained inside a solid or semisolid matrix \(absorbent\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14414
name: absorbent
def: "Solid or semisolid matrix that is able to accommodate and retain an absorbate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14415
name: abundance
def: "Total number of individual organisms in a population\, seen over a defined period of time in a certain place.Note\: For fish\, an estimate of total weight may replace number.\nTotal number of organisms per unit of habitat space seen over a defined period.\nAmount of an element that exists in nature\, usually expressed in relative terms as a percentage of the total amount of all elements in a given medium \(e.g.\, the Earth’s crust\).\nAmount of an isotope of an element that exists in nature\, usually expressed in relative terms as a percentage of the total amount of all isotopes of the element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14416
name: abundant element
def: "Element which is of common occurrence in the Earth’s crust."
synonym: "abundant metal" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14417
name: accessible
def: "Capable of being entered or reached; easy of access; such as one can go to\, come into the presence of\, reach\, or lay hold of; get-at-able."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14418
name: acclimatisation
def: "Processes\, including selection and adaptation\, by which a population of microorganisms develops the ability to degrade a substance\, or develops a tolerance to it.\nIn experimental systems\, allowing an organism to adjust to its environment prior to undertaking a study.\nModification of biological processes or structures in the maintenance of homeostasis in response to change in some environmental quality such as temperature\, salinity\, light\, radiation\, or toxicant concentration. Note\: It is an expression of phenotypic plasticity of individuals in response to a sublethal change in some environmental factor."
synonym: "acclimatation" []
synonym: "acclimation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14419
name: acid precipitation
def: "Rain\, fog\, snow\, sleet\, or other particulate matter from the atmosphere with a _000078_ below \\\(\\pu\{5.6\}\\\) deposited from the air."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14420
name: acid-volatile sulfide
def: "Soil- and sediment-associated solid-phase sulfides extractable with cold hydrochloric acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14421
name: acute
def: "Of short duration\, in relation to exposure or effect; the effect usually shows a rapid onset.\nNote 1\: In regulatory toxicology\, 'acute' refers to studies where dosing is either single or limited to one day although the total study duration may extend to two weeks to permit appearance of toxicity in susceptible organ systems.\nNote 2\: In aquatic ecotoxicology\, exposure of the test organisms is typically continuous and of four days or less.\nIn clinical medicine\, sudden and severe\, having a rapid onset."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14422
name: acute-to-chronic toxicity ratio
def: "Numerical dimensionless quantity that is the ratio of an acute toxicity test result \(e.g.\, \\\(\\pu\{LC_\{50\}\}\\\)\) to a chronic toxicity test result \(e.g.\, maximum acceptable toxicant concentration\, MATC\) where both are expressed in the same units \(e.g.\, \\\(\\pu\{mg L-1\}\\\)\). Ideally\, the data are for the same chemical species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14423
name: additive effect
def: "Consequence that follows exposure to two or more physicochemical agents that act jointly but do not interact\: the total effect is the simple sum of the effects of separate exposures to the agents under the same conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14424
name: additive index
def: "Quantification of the joint action of toxicants in mixture by adding measures of their toxicity calculated in relation to the toxicity of a reference toxicant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14425
name: adenylate energy charge
def: "Index reflecting the balance of energy transfer between catabolic and anabolic processes\, calculated from the equation \\\[\\rm\{AEC\} = \(\[\\ce\{ATP\}\] + ½\[\\ce\{ADP\}\)/\(\[\\ce\{ATP\}\] + \[\\ce\{ADP\}\] + \[\\ce\{AMP\}\]\)\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{ATP\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{ADP\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\ce\{AMP\}\\\) are the amount concentrations of adenosine tri-\, di-\, and monophosphate\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14426
name: adsorption factor
def: "Ratio of the amount of substance adsorbed at the interface of a condensed layer and a liquid or gaseous phase to the total amount of the substance available for adsorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14427
name: age class
def: "Group of organisms of the same age within a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14428
name: age composition
def: "Distribution of organisms among the various age classes present in the population. The sum of the number of individuals in all age classes equals the population size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14429
name: age distribution
def: "Composition of a population in terms of how its abundance is distributed across age classes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14430
name: age-specific death rate
def: "Mean number of deaths as tabulated for a life table interval or for a specific age class in a given year\, expressed per \\\(\\pu\{1000\}\\\) in that interval or age class."
synonym: "age-specific mortality" []
synonym: "age-specific number of individuals dying" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14431
name: aggregation error
def: "Error in Bayesian probability analysis of model systems resulting from the use of a single set of parameters to represent a collection of distinct entities\, such as individuals\, in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14432
name: air pollution tolerance index
def: "Empirical index used to assess the tolerance of individual plants to contaminated air. The numerical value of \\\(T_\{\\rm\{I\}\}\\\) is obtained by combining various quantities according to the recipe \\\(\[A \(T + P\) + R\]/10\\\)\, where \\\(A\\\) is the mass of ascorbic acid divided by the dry mass of a leaf\, expressed as \\\(\\pu\{mg/g\}\\\)\, \\\(T\\\) is the total mass of chlorophyll divided by the fresh mass\, expressed as \\\(\\pu\{mg/g\}\\\)\, \\\(P\\\) is the pH of a leaf extract\, and \\\(R\\\) is the mass of water in the leaf divided by the wet mass\, expressed as percent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14433
name: alga
def: "Any of various chiefly aquatic\, eukaryotic\, photosynthetic organisms\, ranging in size from single-celled forms to the giant kelp."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14434
name: algal bloom
def: "Rapid increase in the abundance of phytoplankton or benthic algae in a given area\, often as a result of an increased availability of nutrients or light\, or increase in temperature \(e.g.\, the spring bloom\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14435
name: algicide
def: "Substance intended to kill algae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14436
name: algistatic
def: "Inhibiting algal population growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14437
name: allogenic succession
def: "Sequential appearance of species driven by external influences that alter local conditions; e.g.\, silt deposits changing a marshland to woodland."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14438
name: ambient
def: "Surrounding \(applied to environmental media such as air\, water\, sediment\, or soil\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14439
name: ambient monitoring
def: "Continuous or repeated measurement of agents in the environment to evaluate ambient exposure and health risk by comparison with appropriate reference values based on knowledge of the probable relationship between exposure and resultant adverse health effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14440
name: anadromous
def: "Showing anadromy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14441
name: anadromy
def: "Life-history pattern that is characterized by egg incubation and early juvenile development in freshwater\, migration to seawater for adult development\, and a return to freshwater for spawning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14442
name: analysis plan
def: "Scheme that defines the exact format and design of the assessment\, explicitly states the data needed\, and describes the methods and design for analyzing these data."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14443
name: anthropogenic
def: "Caused by or influenced by human activities.\nDescribing a conversion factor used to calculate a dose or concentration affecting a human that has been derived from data obtained with another species\, e.g.\, the rat."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14444
name: antisymmetry
def: "Quality of a population of bilaterally symmetrical individuals in which the difference in measurement of a trait made from the right and left sides of individuals from that population produces a bimodal distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14445
name: arcsine square root transformation
def: "\\\(\\arcsin \\sqrt\{P\}\\\)\, where \\\(P\\\) is the value of a measured effect\, e.g.\, the proportion of exposed organisms in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14446
name: artificial soil test
def: "Test with earthworms in which the toxicity of a substance by skin and gut uptake is determined by adding the earthworms to an artificial soil made of sand\, clay mineral\, and peat\, containing the substance of concern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14447
name: artisol test
def: "Test with earthworms in which the toxicity of a substance by skin and gut uptake is determined by placing the worms in an artificial substrate consisting of silica\, water\, and glass balls\, containing the substance of concern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14448
name: aryl hydrocarbon hydroxylase
def: "Enzyme activity attributed to cytochrome P-450 monooxygenase isoforms and often measured in units of benzo\[a\]pyrene hydroxylation."
synonym: "unspecific mono-oxygenase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14449
name: as low as reasonably achievable
def: "Describing the situation in which everything practicable is done to reduce risks to the minimum with the approval of the regulatory authorities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14450
name: assemblage
def: "Set of coexisting populations defined by phylogeny\, location\, or life style. Assemblages are intermediate between populations and communities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14451
name: assessment endpoint
def: "Ecological property that is to be protected and the precise parameter to be measured for this property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14452
name: Association Française de Normalisation test
def: "Commercially available test kit\, certified by AFNOR as valid and equivalent to a standardized method."
synonym: "AFNOR test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14453
name: asymptotic threshold concentration
def: "Concentration of a chemical at which some percentage of a population of test organisms is in a state of approximate homeostasis for a prolonged period of time \(\\\(\\pu\{48 h\}\\\) or more\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14454
name: atmospheric deposition
def: "Process that transfers a chemical from the atmosphere to the Earth’s surface \(land\, water\, or vegetation\) by either dry impingement or by transport in rain or snow."
synonym: "deposition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14456
name: aufwuchs
def: "Floral and \(or\) or faunal communities attached to submerged surfaces in aquatic ecosystems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14457
name: autogenic succession
def: "Sequential appearance of species driven by processes operating within the community environment \(compare allogenic succession\)\, e.g.\, primary and secondary successions that occur on newly exposed land."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14458
name: autotroph
def: "Organism that is independent of outside sources for organic food materials and manufactures its own organic material from inorganic sources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14459
name: autotrophic succession
def: "Sequential appearance of species in a location principally involving plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14460
name: axenic culture
def: "Growth of organisms of a single species in the absence of cells or living organisms of any other species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14461
name: baseline toxicity
def: "General\, nonspecific\, reversible mode of toxic action that can be produced in most living organisms by the presence of sufficient amounts of many organic chemicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14462
name: Bayesian
def: "Attributed to Thomas Bayes \(1702–1761\)\, English mathematician. The term is used to designate concepts relating to inverse probability\, especially Bayes’ theorem\, a theorem expressing the probability of one of a number of mutually exclusive events \\\(H_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)\, given some other event \\\(E\\\)\, in terms of the probabilities of all the \\\(H_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) independently of \\\(E\\\) and the probabilities of \\\(E\\\) given each \\\(H_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14463
name: Bayesian probability
def: "Subjective or objective concept of probability as a 'measure of the state of knowledge'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14464
name: behavioural teratology
def: "Study of behavioral abnormalities in otherwise apparently normal individuals after exposure in the embryonic state to a substance or physical agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14465
name: behavioural toxicology
def: "Study of abnormal behavior produced by exposure to a substance or physical agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14466
name: beneficial metal
def: "Non-essential metal which in some form \(chemical species\) at an appropriate dose can improve health of defined organisms\, commonly human beings."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14467
name: benefit
def: "Advantage to\, or improvement in condition of\, an individual\, a population\, a species\, an assemblage\, a community\, or an ecosystem."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14468
name: benthic
def: "Living on the bottom of an aquatic system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14469
name: bioaccessible
def: "Able to come in contact with a living organism and perhaps interact with it with the possibility of absorption into the organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14470
name: bioaccumulation
def: "Progressive increase in the amount of a substance in an organism or part of an organism that occurs because the rate of intake from all contributing sources and by all possible routes exceeds the organism’s ability to eliminate the substance from its body."
synonym: "accumulation in biology" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14471
name: bioaccumulation factor
def: "Ratio of tissue chemical residue to chemical concentration in an external environmental phase \(e.g.\, sediment\, water\, soil\, air\, or food\). BAF is measured at a steady state in situations where organisms are exposed to multiple sources \(e.g.\, water\, sediment\, food\)\, unless noted otherwise."
synonym: "accumulation factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14472
name: bioactivation
def: "Metabolic conversion of a xenobiotic to a more toxic derivative or to one that has more of an effect on living organisms."
synonym: "activation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14473
name: bioavailable
def: "Able to be absorbed by living organisms."
synonym: "available" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14474
name: biocenosis
def: "All the interacting organisms living together in a specified habitat."
synonym: "biocoenosis" []
synonym: "biological community" []
synonym: "biotic community" []
synonym: "ecological community" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14475
name: biocide
def: "Substance intended to kill living organisms.\nNonagricultural pesticide used to control the severity and incidence of pests or diseases\, e.g.\, algicide\, slimicide\, or disinfectant for the control of algal\, fungal\, or bacterial growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14476
name: bioconcentration
def: "Process leading to a higher concentration of a substance in an organism than in environmental media to which it is exposed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14477
name: bioconcentration factor
def: "Measure of the tendency for a substance in water to accumulate in aquatic organisms defined as the ratio of the concentration of the substance of concern in the organism to the concentration in water at equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14478
name: lipid-based bioconcentration factor
def: "Ratio of the concentration of the substance of concern in the lipid fraction of the test organism to the concentration in the ambient water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14479
name: biodiversity
def: "Occurrence of a number of species within a given ecosystem or biome\, or the presence of a number of community types in a given area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14480
name: biodiversity action plan
def: "Internationally recognized program designed to protect and restore biological systems containing threatened species and habitats."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14481
name: biogeochemical cycle
def: "Movement of elements or molecules among organisms and nonliving compartments of the atmosphere\, lithosphere\, and hydrosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14482
name: biological indicator
def: "Any biological species or group of species whose performance\, abundance\, or population status is used to determine the health of an environment or ecosystem by systematic monitoring of chemical\, physiological\, or behavioral changes."
synonym: "bioindicator" []
synonym: "indicator species" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14483
name: biological determinant
def: "Property of living organisms that affects human or environmental health."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14484
name: biomagnification
def: "Sequence of processes by which higher concentrations of a substance are attained in organisms at higher trophic levels.\nResult of these processes of bioconcentration and bioaccumulation by which tissue concentrations of bioaccumulated chemicals increase as the chemical passes up through two or more trophic levels.\nNote\: Biomagnification occurs in a food chain as a consequence of efficient transfer of a substance from food to consumer accompanied by the lack of\, or very slow\, excretion or degradation of the substance."
synonym: "bioamplification" []
synonym: "ecological magnification" []
synonym: "trophic enrichment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14485
name: biomagnification factor
def: "Ratio of concentrations of a compound at two consecutive trophic levels at steady state. It can also be expressed in terms of a rate constant-based bioaccumulation model \\\[B = C_\{n\}/C_\{n-1\} = \\alpha f/k_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\alpha\\\) is assimilation efficiency\, \\\(f\\\) is feeding rate\, and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the first-order elimination constant. \\\(B\\\) can be calculated from field data on assumed trophic relations or from laboratory feeding experiments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14486
name: biomarker of effect
def: "Biomarker that\, depending upon the magnitude\, can be recognized as associated with an established or possible health impairment or disease."
synonym: "effect biomarker" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14487
name: biomarker of exposure
def: "Biomarker that relates exposure to a xenobiotic to the levels of the substance or its metabolite\, or of the product of an interaction between the substance and some target molecule or cell\, that can be measured in a compartment within an organism."
synonym: "exposure biomarker" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14488
name: biomarker of susceptibility
def: "Biomarker of an inherent or acquired ability of an organism to respond to exposure to a specific substance."
synonym: "susceptibility biomarker" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14489
name: biome
def: "Set of plants and animals that occupy a certain geographic area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14490
name: biomodification
def: "Alteration of the chemical or physical properties of a substance by the action of living organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14491
name: biomonitor
def: "n. Organism that provides quantitative information on the quality of the environment around it.Note\: The ideal substance biomonitor would indicate the presence of a specific pollutant and provide additional information about the amount present.\nv. To use organisms to monitor pollutants and to deduce possible effects on biota or routes of toxicant exposure to humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14492
name: biomonitoring type 1
def: "Determination of community changes along a gradient or among sites differing in levels of pollution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14493
name: biomonitoring type 2
def: "Determination of bioaccumulation in organisms among sites notionally varying in the level of contamination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14494
name: biomonitoring type 3
def: "Determination of effects on organisms using tools such as biochemical markers in sentinel species or some measure of diminished fitness or condition of individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14495
name: biomonitoring type 4
def: "Determination of genetically based resistance in populations of contaminated areas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14496
name: biopesticide
def: "Biological agent with pesticidal activity\, e.g.\, the bacterium Bacillus thuringiensis when used to kill insects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14497
name: bioremediation
def: "Use of biological organisms such as plants or microbes to aid in removing hazardous substances from an area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14498
name: biota-sediment accumulation factor
def: "Ratio of lipid-normalized tissue chemical residue to carbon-normalized sediment substance concentration \(units of mass lipid/mass organic carbon\)."
synonym: "accumulation factor (AF)" []
synonym: "bioavailability index (BI)" []
synonym: "biota-sediment factor (BSF)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14499
name: biotic index
def: "One of several ranking systems calculated from the presence or abundance of sensitive species relative to tolerant species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14500
name: biotic ligand
def: "Component of a living organism to which an ion or other substance in aqueous solution can bind\, usually with subsequent beneficial or harmful effect on the physiology of the organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14501
name: biotic ligand model
def: "Model that integrates the interactions of metals with ligands in water to calculate the speciation \(based on hardness\, salinity\, and presence of other metals\) and the concentration of ionic species producing toxicity. The BLM chemically and mathematically models the organism’s target site as ligand\(s\) competing with nonbiotic ligands\, based on the concept that toxicity occurs when a metal-\(biotic ligand\) complex reaches a critical concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14502
name: biotic score
def: "Weighted measurement of organisms present—the weighted part is determined by each group’s pollution tolerance or intolerance. As pollution increases in a stream\, the biotic score value tends to increase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14503
name: biotope
def: "Habitat shared by many species\, most often an area that is uniform in environmental conditions and in its distribution of animal and plant life."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14504
name: bioturbation
def: "Group of processes whereby organisms affect the structure of sediment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14505
name: biphasic dose–effect model
def: "Model of adverse effect vs. dose relationship that\, owing to hormesis\, dips down from the control level before increasing with the dose; individuals exposed to low\, but nontoxic concentrations are healthier than individuals not exposed to the chemical.Note\: Biphasic dose–effect curves occur for any nutrient. Hormesis is a phenomenon associated with compounds that have no nutrient properties.\nModel of adverse effect vs. dose relationship that changes slope owing to a change in the mechanism of toxic action.Note\: This may\, e.g.\, be due to saturation of a transport or metabolic system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14506
name: birth rate
def: "\(in demography\) Number of live births in a defined administrative jurisdiction in a calendar year divided by a midyear population of the jurisdiction\, with the customary multiplier of \\\(\\pu\{1000\}\\\) to produce a whole number rather than a decimal or a fraction. Note\: An alternative statistic is total fertility rate\, the average number of children born to each woman over the course of her life. In general\, the total fertility rate is a better indicator of fertility rates because\, unlike the crude birth rate\, it is not affected by the age distribution of the population.\n\(in ecology\) Number of births\, \\\(B\\\)\, in a population of a defined size\, \\\(N\\\)\, during a specified time interval \\\(\\Delta t\\\). The rate is calculated from the formula \\\[B = \\Delta N/\\Delta t\\\] where \\\(\\Delta N\\\) is the change in the number of births in a limited time period \\\(\\Delta t\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14507
name: boomerang effect
def: "Delayed damage from earlier environmental contamination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14508
name: borderline metal ions
def: "Metal ions intermediate in properties between class a and class b metal ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14509
name: bottom-up ecotoxicological study
def: "Approach to investigating ecotoxicological effects that starts with a determination of the presence and nature of any adverse effects via responses at the suborganismal \(cellular and biochemical\) levels of organization rather than via the community and \(or\) ecosystem levels of organization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14510
name: Brillouin index
def: "Quantitative value for community species diversity calculated as \\\[H_\{\\rm\{B\}\} = \(\\ln N! - \\sum \\ln n_\{i\}!\)/N\\\] where \\\(N\\\) is the total number of individuals and \\\(n_\{i\}\\\) is the number of individuals in the \\\(i^\{\\rm\{th\}\}\\\) species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14511
name: broodstock
def: "Adult fish producing either eggs or sperm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14512
name: calcinosis
def: "Any pathological condition characterized by the deposition of calcium salts in tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14513
name: calcium sink
def: "Tissue such as arthropod cuticle\, bivalve shell\, or vertebrate bone that renders calcium or elements with similar biological behavior \(such as lead or cadmium\) less bioavailable during trophic interactions\, thus providing a mechanism for trophic dilution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14514
name: carrying capacity
def: "Maximum population size expressed as total number of individuals\, biomass\, or population density that a given unit of habitat is capable of sustaining."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14515
name: catadromous
def: "Describing a species exhibiting catadromy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14516
name: catadromy
def: "Life-history pattern that is characterized by egg incubation and early juvenile development in seawater\, migration to freshwater for adult development\, and return to seawater for spawning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14517
name: catagenesis
def: "Long-term geochemical alteration to organic matter\, involving high temperatures and pressures deep below the surface of the Earth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14518
name: cetaceans
def: "Whales\, dolphins\, and porpoises\, in the order Cetacea."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14519
name: Chapman mechanism
def: "Series of reactions by which ozone is formed in the stratosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14520
name: chemical time bomb
def: "Substance or substances in a component of the environment which are not currently bioavailable but which may become bioavailable at an unknown time in the future with harmful consequences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14521
name: chloride cell
def: "Specialized cell with a role in ion regulation\, characterized by large numbers of mitochondria\, an extensive intracellular tubular network and a high concentration of \\\(\\ce\{Na+\}\\\)/\\\(\\ce\{K+\}\\\) ATPase. This cell is found in salt-secreting glands like the rectal gland of elasmobranchs\, the nasal gland of seabirds\, and the gills of both seawater and freshwater fish\, mainly on the primary lamellae but also on the secondary lamellae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14522
name: chlorinity
def: "Proportion by mass of dissolved chloride ions in water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14523
name: chlorofluorocarbon
def: "Carbon-based chemical containing chlorine and fluorine\, responsible for ozone depletion in the stratosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14524
name: chronic effect
def: "Consequence that develops slowly and \(or\) has a long-lasting course\: may be applied to an effect which develops rapidly and is long lasting."
synonym: "long-term effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14525
name: chlorosis
def: "Loss of green color in plants owing to the lack of production or the destruction of chlorophyll."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14526
name: cladistics
def: "Systematic classification of groups of organisms on the basis of shared characteristics thought to derive from a common ancestor. Also\, the study of the branching of evolutionary lines of descent and the relationship between branches."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14527
name: cladogram
def: "Dendrogram illustrating the supposed evolutionary relationships between clades; a diagram showing cladistic relationships."
synonym: "evolutionary tree" []
synonym: "phylogenetic tree" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14528
name: climax community
def: "Community of plants and animals in a steady state due to ecological succession resulting in a composition of the community best adapted to average conditions in the area. The term is sometimes applied to soil development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14529
name: cold condensation theory
def: "Theory that pollutants with high vapor pressure\, e.g.\, mercury or persistent organic pollutants \(POPs\)\, in the air will condense onto soil\, water\, and biota at cool temperatures. Consequently\, the ratios for POP concentrations in the air and on condensed phases decrease as one moves from warmer to cooler climates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14530
name: cometabolism
def: "Microbial transformation of a compound \(that is normally unable to support cell replication\) in the requisite presence of a transformable cosubstrate that supports cell replication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14531
name: community
def: "Assembly of populations of different species of living organisms\, usually interdependent on and interacting with each other\, within a specified location in space and time."
synonym: "biocommunity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14532
name: community conditioning hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that ecological communities retain information about events in their history and will not return to their original state after perturbation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14533
name: community stability
def: "Tendency of a community to return to its original state after a disturbance \(competition\, temporarily changing environment\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14534
name: community resilience
def: "Ability of a community to return to its former state after perturbation."
synonym: "ecological resilience" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14535
name: community resistance
def: "Ability of a community to avoid displacement from its present state by a disturbance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14536
name: community structure
def: "Species present in a community and their relative abundances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14537
name: compartment
def: "Conceptualized part of the body \(organs\, tissues\, cells\, or fluids\) considered as an independent system for purposes of modeling and assessment of distribution and clearance of a substance.\nPart of an ecosystem considered as an independent system for purposes of assessment of uptake\, distribution\, and dissipation of a substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14538
name: competitive exclusion
def: "Outcompetition from a niche of one species by another that uses the available resources of the niche more efficiently\, eventually resulting in the exclusion of the outcompeted species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14539
name: complimentary niches
def: "Niches where coexisting species occupy a similar position along one niche dimension\, e.g.\, altitude\, but differ along another\, e.g.\, diet."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14540
name: concentration–response curve
def: "Graph of the relation between exposure concentration and the proportion of individuals in a population responding with a defined effect."
synonym: "exposure–response curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14541
name: conceptual model
def: "Linkage and interrelationship of assessment endpoint\(s\) and stressors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14542
name: conceptual model diagram
def: "Figure showing pathways of exposure of organisms and illustrating areas of uncertainty or concern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14543
name: conceptual site model
def: "Integration of information on contaminants of potential concern \(COPCs\)\, ecological resources\, evaluated exposure routes\, fate and transport mechanisms\, evaluated COPC toxicity\, identified assessment endpoints\, and risk questions and hypotheses\, into a description of all that is known and \(or\) expected about a contaminated site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14544
name: condition
def: "State of health determining potential for reproduction and growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14545
name: condition factor
def: "Parameter used to describe growth in fish. It reflects real growth and nutritional status better than simple measurement of body mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14546
name: condition index
def: "Any quantitative measure of the condition in relation to a defined baseline."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14547
name: conservation biology
def: "Science applied to the conservation of genes\, populations\, species\, and ecosystems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14548
name: conservation ecology
def: "Science of analyzing and protecting the Earth’s biological diversity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14549
name: contaminant
def: "Minor impurity present in a substance.\nExtraneous material inadvertently added to a sample prior to or during chemical or biological analysis.\nIn some contexts\, as in relation to gas cleaning equipment\, used as a synonym for 'pollutant'\, especially on a small scale.\nUnintended component in food that may pose a hazard to the consumer.\nAny undesirable solid\, liquid\, or gaseous matter occurring\, as a result of human activities\, in a solid\, liquid\, or gaseous environmental medium\, even without adverse effects being observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14550
name: continuous effect
def: "Change that can be measured on a continuum from zero \(or even a negative value\) to positive values such as growth and reproduction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14551
name: control
def: "Treatment in a toxicity test that duplicates all the conditions of the exposure treatments but contains no test material\, in order to determine the absence of toxicity under basic test conditions \(e.g.\, health of test organisms\, quality of dilution water\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14552
name: cooxidation
def: "Cometabolism whereby growing microorganisms oxidize a compound without using either carbon or energy derived from the oxidation of that compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14553
name: copepod
def: "Minute marine or freshwater crustacean\, usually having six pairs of limbs on the thorax; some are abundant in plankton and others are parasitic on fish\, marine mammals\, and macro-invertebrates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14554
name: corrosive
def: "n. Substance having the capability or tendency to cause slow destruction.\nadj. Gradually destructive; steadily harmful."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14555
name: cost–benefit analysis
def: "Procedure for determining the relationship of the expected benefits of a proposed action to the expected costs in order to decide whether the action provides the best option for the investment of limited resources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14556
name: cost-effectiveness analysis
def: "Procedure for determining whether the expected beneficial effects of a defined course of action justify the cost when selecting among competing options for the use of limited resources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14557
name: cough
def: "Abrupt\, periodic reversal of water flow over the gills that dislodges and eliminates excess mucus from the gills’ surfaces."
synonym: "gill purge" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14558
name: critical body residue
def: "Whole-body concentration of a chemical that is associated with a given adverse biological response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14559
name: critical life-stage testing
def: "Toxicity testing focused on the species life stage thought to be the most sensitive to the toxicant\, such as newly hatched individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14560
name: cross-resistance
def: "Condition in which enhanced tolerance to one toxicant also enhances tolerance to another."
synonym: "cotolerance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14561
name: culture
def: "n. Stock of healthy cells\, tissue\, microorganisms\, or plants raised under well-defined and controlled conditions.\nv. To maintain a stock of healthy organisms under well-defined and controlled conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14562
name: cumulative dose
def: "Total dose resulting from repeated exposures or continuous exposure over a defined time to a substance or to radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14563
name: cumulative exposure
def: "Total exposure resulting from repeated and continuous exposure over a defined time to a substance or to radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14564
name: Daphnia
def: "Small\, mostly planktonic\, crustaceans\, between \\\(\\pu\{0.2 mm\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{5 mm\}\\\) in length."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14565
name: dead-end metabolite
def: "Substance formed by a microbe’s metabolism of a substrate \(most often during cometabolism\) that cannot be further metabolized by that organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14566
name: death
def: "In assessing population ecology\, death of an individual is loss of the ability to reproduce."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14567
name: death
def: "Irreversible cessation of all vital functions of an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14568
name: death rate
def: "Number of deaths \\\(D\\\) in a closed population of size \\\(N\\\) during a specified time interval\, \\\(\\Delta t\\\). The rate is calculated from the formula \\\[D = -\\Delta N/\\Delta t\\\] where \\\(\\Delta N\\\) is the change in number of deaths over a specified time period."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14569
name: dechlorinated water
def: "Chlorinated water \(usually municipal drinking water\) that has been treated to remove chlorine and chlorinated compounds from solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14570
name: decomposer
def: "Organism that breaks down dead matter or wastes of other organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14571
name: defoliant
def: "Substance used for removal of leaves by its toxic action on living plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14572
name: demographic stochasticity
def: "Variability in population growth rates arising from random differences among individuals in survival and reproduction within a season."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14573
name: demography
def: "Study of populations\, especially their age structure and growth rates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14574
name: density dependence
def: "Variation in characteristics of individuals or of a population produced by changes in the density of the population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14575
name: deposit feeder
def: "Animal that feeds on particles of matter in the soil or sediment\, usually the top soil or sediment where it is filled with organic matter. Feeding takes place either by ingesting soil or sediment or by trapping particles as they fall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14576
name: depuration
def: "Loss of a substance from an organism owing to elimination and degradation."
synonym: "depuration time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14577
name: derived characteristic
def: "Predicted property of a substance that is dependent upon\, or is an approximation of\, a fundamental property and the prevailing environmental conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14578
name: deterministic
def: "Term applied to effects\, of which the extent varies with the dose and for which a threshold is believed to exist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14579
name: deterministic analysis
def: "Detailed study in which all population and environmental parameters are assumed to be constant and accurately specified."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14580
name: deterministic model
def: "Mathematical model that is fully specified and does not include a stochastic component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14581
name: detritus
def: "Organic debris from decomposing plants and animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14582
name: detrivorous
def: "Describing an organism that survives by eating detritus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14583
name: developmental reference dose
def: "Reference dose determined for developmental consequences of a single\, maternal exposure during development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14584
name: developmental stability
def: "Potential of an organism to develop into a consistent phenotype in an environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14585
name: diatom
def: "Member of a major group of eukaryotic algae\, among the most common types of phytoplankton. Unicellular\, some form chains or simple colonies. Diatom cells are encased in a frustule\, of widely diverse form\, made of silica \(hydrated silicon dioxide\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14586
name: diffuse source
def: "Release into the environment from multiple sources covering a wide area as opposed to point source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14587
name: dilution paradigm
def: "Belief that pollution is alleviated by dilution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14588
name: dilution water
def: "Water used to dilute the test material in an aquatic toxicity test in order to prepare either different concentrations of a test chemical or different percentages of an effluent for the various test treatments."
synonym: "diluent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14589
name: direct ecological effect
def: "Effect where a stressor acts immediately on an ecological component of interest and not through the consequences of effects on other components of the ecosystem."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14590
name: directional asymmetry
def: "Deviation within a population from a mean of zero for the difference between a trait measured from the right and left sides of bilaterally symmetrical individuals from that population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14591
name: direct toxicity
def: "Toxicity that results from\, and is readily attributable to\, substances acting at the sites of toxic action in and \(or\) on the exposed organisms that are exhibiting the adverse biological response in question."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14592
name: discharge
def: "Release of any waste into the environment from a point source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14593
name: discrimination ratio
def: "Ratio measuring the degree of isotopic discrimination\, with a ratio of 1 indicating no discrimination."
synonym: "discrimination factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14594
name: dispersant
def: "Substance used to disperse liquid spills\, e.g.\, oil spills in water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14595
name: dissolved organic carbon
def: "Amount concentration of carbon found dissolved in water samples from aquatic systems\, measured as total elemental carbon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14596
name: dissolved oxygen content
def: "Amount concentration of oxygen dissolved in water at a particular temperature and pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14597
name: dissolved organic matter
def: "Analogous to dissolved organic carbon\, but refers to the entire organic pool dissolved in water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14598
name: disturbance
def: "Event that introduces\, removes\, or redistributes organisms\, changing the colonization potential of a given environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14599
name: diversity index
def: "Mathematical descriptor of species variation within a community."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14600
name: dose–effect
def: "Relation between dose and the magnitude of a measured biological change."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14601
name: dose–effect curve
def: "Graph of the relation between dose and the magnitude of the biological change produced measured in appropriate units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14602
name: dose–effect relationship
def: "Association between dose and the resulting magnitude of a continuously graded change\, either in an individual or in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14603
name: dose–response curve
def: "Graph of the relation between dose and the proportion of individuals in a population responding with a defined biological effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14604
name: dose–response relationship
def: "Association between dose and the incidence of a defined biological effect in an exposed population usually expressed as percentage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14605
name: drainage basin
def: "Area of land where water from rain or snow melt drains downhill into a body of water\, such as a river\, lake\, dam\, estuary\, wetland\, sea\, or ocean."
synonym: "catchment" []
synonym: "catchment area" []
synonym: "catchment basin" []
synonym: "drainage area" []
synonym: "river basin" []
synonym: "water basin" []
synonym: "watershed" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14606
name: dynamic energy budget theory
def: "Theory that simple quantitative rules may be applied to define the organization of metabolism of individual organisms."
synonym: "DEB theory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14607
name: dynamically fragile community
def: "Community that is stable for only a very limited range of environmental characteristics\, e.g.\, a stable and predictable environment with respect to predator–prey ratios or disturbances caused by humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14608
name: dynamically robust community
def: "Community that is stable within a wide range of conditions\, e.g.\, in very variable and unpredictable environments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14609
name: dynamic stability hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that long food chains are less stable than short food chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14610
name: ecology
def: "Branch of biology that studies the interactions between living organisms and all factors \(including other organisms\) in their environment. Such interactions encompass environmental factors that determine the distributions of living organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14611
name: ecological character
def: "Sum of biological\, physical\, and chemical components of an ecosystem and their interactions that maintain the ecosystem and its products\, functions\, and attributes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14612
name: ecological energetics
def: "Study of the flow of energy within an ecological system from the time the energy enters the living system until it is irretrievably lost from the system as heat."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14613
name: ecological epidemiology
def: "Application of epidemiological methods to determine the cause\, incidence\, prevalence\, and distribution of adverse effects in nonhuman species inhabiting contaminated sites; it is frequently associated with retrospective ecological risk assessment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14614
name: ecological imbalance
def: "Change in any biological\, physical\, or chemical components of an ecosystem\, or in their interactions\, which result in change in ecological character and its functions and attributes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14615
name: ecological mortality
def: "Toxicant-related diminution of fitness of an individual functioning within an ecosystem that is of a magnitude sufficient to be equivalent to somatic death."
synonym: "ecological death" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14616
name: ecological parameter
def: "Measurable variable whose value is a determinant of the characteristics of an ecosystem."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14617
name: ecophysiology
def: "Study of physiology and tolerance limits of species that enhances understanding of their distribution in relation to abiotic conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14618
name: ecoregion
def: "Any of a number of regions into which a continent\, country\, etc.\, can be divided according to their distinct environmental conditions and habitat types.\nLarge area of land or water with a characteristic\, geographically distinct assemblage of natural communities and species comprising a recurring pattern of ecosystems associated with characteristic combinations of soil and landform.Note\: The biodiversity of ecosystems in an ecoregion is often distinct from that of other ecoregions."
synonym: "bioregion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14619
name: ecosystem
def: "Grouping of organisms \(microorganisms\, plants\, animals\) interacting together\, with and through their physical and chemical environments\, to form a functional entity within a defined environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14620
name: ecotone
def: "Area of gradual transition between two or more ecosystems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14621
name: ecotoxicologically relevant concentration
def: "Concentration of a pesticide \(active ingredient\, formulations\, and relevant metabolites\) that is likely to affect a determinable ecological characteristic of an exposed system."
synonym: "environmentally relevant concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14622
name: edaphic
def: "Pertaining to the soil."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14623
name: eddy motion
def: "Movement in a current of water or air\, that is contrary to the direction of the main current\, especially in a circular motion\, leading to irregularity in the motion of molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14624
name: edge effect
def: "Influence of the closeness of contrasting environments on an ecosystem\, seen at boundaries between natural habitats\, especially forests\, and disturbed or developed land."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14625
name: effect time
def: "Time taken for a substance to produce a precisely defined effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14626
name: effects assessment
def: "Component of risk assessment concerned with quantifying the manner in which the frequency and intensity of effects increase with increasing exposure to a contaminant or other source of stress \(also known as dose–response assessment or toxicity assessment\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14627
name: elasticity
def: "Ability of a community to return to its prestressed condition."
synonym: "community elasticity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14628
name: elutriate
def: "Aqueous solution obtained after adding water to a solid substance \(e.g.\, sediment\, tailings\, drilling mud\, or dredging spoil\)\, shaking the mixture\, then centrifuging or filtering it or decanting the supernatant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14629
name: elutriate test
def: "Test in which a nonbenthic species\, such as Daphnia magna\, is exposed to an elutriate produced by mixing test sediment with water and then centrifuging the mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14630
name: emergent properties
def: "New properties emerging with upward steps in hierarchical systems\, such as ecological communities or ecosystems\, that cannot be predicted solely from our limited understanding of the system’s parts or components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14631
name: emission standard
def: "Quantitative limit on the emission or discharge of a substance from a source\, usually expressed in terms of a time-weighted average concentration or a ceiling value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14632
name: endemic
def: "Pertaining to a species found in a discrete geographical unit\, such as an island\, habitat type\, or other defined area or zone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14633
name: endemism
def: "Ecological state of being unique to a place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14634
name: endotherm
def: "'Warm-blooded organism' that regulates its body temperature to be \(almost\) constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14635
name: endpoint
def: "Objective of measurement of a toxicity test."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14636
name: endpoint assessment
def: "Quantitative or quantifiable expression of the environmental value considered to be at risk in a risk analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14637
name: environmental assessment
def: "Short\, preliminary assessment of potential environmental harm used to determine if a full environmental impact statement \(EIS\) is required."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14638
name: environmental availability
def: "Portion of the total amount of a substance present in the environment that is involved in a particular process and is subject to physicochemical and biological modifying influences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14639
name: environmental bioavailability
def: "Ratio of uptake clearance to the rate at which an organism encounters a given contaminant in an environmental medium \(e.g.\, soil\, sediment\, water\, food\) being processed by the organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14640
name: environmental epidemiology
def: "Subdiscipline of human epidemiology concerned with diseases caused by chemical or physical agents in the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14641
name: environmental fate
def: "Destiny of a chemical or biological pollutant after release into the natural environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14642
name: environmental impact assessment
def: "Appraisal of the possible environmental consequences of a past\, ongoing\, or planned action\, resulting in the production of an environmental impact statement or 'finding of no significant impact' \(FONSI\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14643
name: environmental impact statement
def: "Report resulting from an environmental impact assessment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14644
name: environmental quality objective
def: "Overall state to be aimed for in a particular aspect of the natural environment\, for example\, 'water in an estuary such that shellfish populations survive in good health'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14645
name: environmental quality standard
def: "Amount concentration or mass concentration of a substance that should not be exceeded in an environmental system\, often expressed as a time-weighted average measurement over a defined period."
synonym: "ambient standard" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14646
name: environmental risk analysis
def: "Determination of the probability of adverse effects on humans and other biota resulting from an environmental hazard \(a chemical\, physical\, or biological agent occurring in or mediated by the environment\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14647
name: environmental risk assessment
def: "Estimate of the probability that harm will result from a defined exposure to a substance in an environmental medium. The estimate is valid only for a given species and set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14648
name: environmental security
def: "United Nations concept\, defined as the relative stability of Earth’s natural ecosystems against human activity\, most notably\n\nglobal climate change caused by human release of greenhouse gas;\ndeforestation caused by so-called 'clearing' of lands; or\nsoil depletion and desertification caused by intensive monoculture techniques."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14649
name: environmental transformation
def: "Chemical transformation of a substance resulting from interactions in the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14650
name: environmental transport
def: "Movement of contaminants from their point of release through the various compartments to locations where exposure is assumed to occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14651
name: enzootic
def: "Present in a community or among a group of animals; term applied to a disease prevailing continually in a region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14652
name: epibenthic
def: "Living on the bed of an aquatic system\, normally on sediment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14653
name: epifauna
def: "Animals that live on the surface of soil or of the bed of an aquatic system\, normally on sediment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14654
name: episodic
def: "Discontinuous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14655
name: epizootic
def: "Outbreak of disease in a population or in a large number of individuals of a species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14656
name: equilibrium
def: "State of a system in which the macroscopic properties of the system become uniform and independent of time. If the temperature is uniform throughout the system\, a state of thermal equilibrium has been reached; if the pressure is uniform\, a state of hydrostatic equilibrium has been reached; and if the chemical potential of each component is uniform\, a state of chemical equilibrium has been reached. If all these quantities become uniform\, the system is said to be in a state of complete thermodynamic equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14657
name: equilibrium partitioning
def: "Tendency for a substance to achieve the same fugacity \(chemical activity\) in different compartments of a complex system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14658
name: equilibrium partitioning approach
def: "Approach to estimating the fate of chemicals in the aquatic environment that is based on equilibrium partitioning."
synonym: "EqP approach" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14659
name: essential nutrient
def: "Substance that is absolutely required for the normal growth and development of a defined organism throughout a complete life cycle."
synonym: "essential element" []
synonym: "essential metal" []
synonym: "nutrient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14660
name: estuarine
def: "Belonging to an estuary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14661
name: estuary
def: "\(general\) Tidal opening\, an inlet\, or creek through which the tide enters; an arm of the sea indenting the land. Rare in modern use.\n\(specific\) Tidal mouth of a great river\, where the tide meets the current of freshwater.\nNote 1\: An estuary is a semi-enclosed coastal body of water with a free connection to the open sea made up of brackish water\, typically the tidal mouth of rivers\, often associated with sedimentation of material from terrestrial runoff.\nNote 2\: An estuary is often associated with a high rate of biological productivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14662
name: etiologic agent
def: "Factor contributing to the cause of a disease."
synonym: "etiological agent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14663
name: Euler–Lotka equation
def: "Equation used to estimate the intrinsic rate of increase from life-table data."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14664
name: eutrophic
def: "Describing an environment having a high concentration of nutrients. Note\: The term is usually used to describe nutrient-rich bodies of water or soil having a high or excessive rate of biological production.\nIn medicine\, tending to promote nutrition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14665
name: eutrophication
def: "Process producing a high concentration of nutrient salts and a high or excessive rate of biological production in water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14666
name: eutrophy
def: "State of being eutrophic.\n\(in medicine\) Good nutrition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14667
name: evolution
def: "Transformation of animals\, plants\, and other living organisms into different forms by the accumulation of changes over successive generations.\nTransmutation of species.\nOrigination or transformation of an organism\, organ\, physiological process\, biological molecule\, etc.\, by a series of changes.\nNote\: The theory of evolution \(in general\) is the proposition that all living organisms have undergone a process of alteration and diversification from simple primordial forms during the Earth’s history; \(in particular\) it is a scientific theory proposing a mechanism for this process\, now especially that based on Darwin’s theory of the natural selection of genetically inherited and adaptive variation \(see neo-Darwinism\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14668
name: exchange diffusion
def: "Diffusion across a membrane by means of a carrier molecule that requires no energy and involves the exchange of two ions across the membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14669
name: expected life span
def: "Average length of time an organism can be expected to survive\, or a substance can be expected to persist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14670
name: experimental ecosystem
def: "Manmade construction intended to simulate a natural environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14671
name: exploitation competition
def: "Interspecies competition in which species compete for some limiting resource such as food."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14672
name: exponential growth
def: "Growth of cells\, organisms\, or populations in which the number or mass increases exponentially and growth at any time is proportional to the number or mass present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14673
name: exposure assessment
def: "Process of measuring or estimating concentration \(or intensity\)\, duration\, and frequency of exposures to an agent present in the environment or\, if estimating hypothetical exposures\, that might arise from the release of a substance\, or radionuclide\, into the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14674
name: exposure characterisation
def: "Description of the presence and characteristics of contact between the contaminant and the ecological entity of concern\, and a summary of this information in an exposure profile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14675
name: exposure pathway
def: "Route by which an individual is exposed to a contaminant\, including the source and point of contact."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14676
name: exposure profile
def: "Outline of the magnitude and spatio-temporal pattern of exposure developed during the process of ecological risk assessment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14677
name: exposure scenario
def: "Model of the likely exposure of selected populations following release of a substance into a natural environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14678
name: extinction probability
def: "Probability that a population will eventually become extinct.\nProbability that a population will become extinct within a specified interval of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14679
name: extrapolation
def: "Calculation\, based on quantitative observations in exposed test species or in vitro test systems\, of predicted dose–effect and dose–response relationships for a substance in the same species at other doses or in humans and other species at similar doses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14680
name: extrapolation factor
def: "Quantity used in effect and exposure assessments to adjust estimated exposures or concentrations/doses for uncertainties\, to make corrections in the data\, or to improve safety."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14681
name: facilitated diffusion
def: "Passive diffusion down a gradient\, not requiring energy\, but occurring at a rate faster than expected by simple diffusion alone."
synonym: "facilitated transport" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14682
name: fate
def: "Disposition of a material in various environmental compartments \(e.g.\, soil or sediment\, water\, air\, biota\) as a result of transport\, partitioning\, transformation\, and degradation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14683
name: feasibility study
def: "Part of a remedial investigation that compares the various options available for remediation and identifies those that are practicable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14684
name: fecundity selection
def: "Component of the life cycle of an individual in which natural selection can occur\, involving the production of more offspring by matings of certain genotype pairs than are produced by other genotype pairs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14685
name: fertilisation effect
def: "Enhanced growth of plants as a result of exposure to low levels of pollutants such as nitrogen and sulfur oxides in acid precipitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14686
name: fertiliser
def: "Substance applied to soil or hydroponic systems for improving the root nutrition of plants with the aim of increasing crop yields and \(or\) controlling production."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14687
name: final acute value
def: "Estimate of the concentration of a substance corresponding to a cumulative probability of \\\(\\pu\{0.05\}\\\) in the acute toxicity values for all species for which acceptable acute tests have been conducted on the material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14688
name: final chronic value
def: "Estimate of the concentration of a substance corresponding to a cumulative probability of \\\(\\pu\{0.05\}\\\) in the chronic toxicity values for all genera for which acceptable chronic tests have been conducted on the material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14689
name: finding of no significant impact
def: "Statement of no significant impact of a major federal action concluded after an environmental assessment \(EA\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14690
name: finite rate of increase
def: "Rate of increase of population size measured over set intervals\, such as between age classes of a life table or generations of a population with nonoverlapping generations\, e.g.\, an annual plant."
synonym: "multiplicative growth factor per generation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14691
name: fish acute toxicity syndromes
def: "Behavioral\, physiological\, and biochemical responses of fish used to classify chemicals by mode of action."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14692
name: fitness advantage
def: "Increased relative fitness of a resistant genotype in a polluted environment compared to a nonresistant genotype."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14693
name: fitness cost
def: "Reduced relative fitness of a resistant genotype in an unpolluted environment compared to a nonresistant genotype."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14694
name: flow-through system
def: "Exposure system for aquatic toxicity tests in which the test material solutions and control water flow into and out of test chambers on a once-through basis either intermittently or continuously."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14695
name: flow-through test
def: "Aquatic toxicity test performed in a flow-through system."
synonym: "continuous flow test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14696
name: fluctuating asymmetry
def: "Deviation from perfect bilateral symmetry for a population of a bilaterally symmetrical species that is thought to reflect developmental instability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14697
name: fluid feeder
def: "Organism that feeds on the fluids of other animals or of plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14698
name: food chain
def: "Hierarchical sequence of transfer of substances from prey organism to predator organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14699
name: food-mass feeder
def: "Animal that feeds on the body parts of other animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14700
name: fossil
def: "Mineralized or otherwise preserved remnant or trace \(such as a footprint\) of an animal\, plant\, and other organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14701
name: free ion activity model
def: "Model for acute metal toxicity to aquatic organisms based on the idea that toxicity occurs when the concentration of the free metal cation in solution reaches a certain level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14702
name: Freundlich adsorption isotherm
def: "Empirical equation that describes the adsorption of a contaminant to soil. The equation is \\\[x/m = \(K_\{\\rm\{f\}\} C_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\) \\exp \(1/n\)\\\] where \\\(x/m\\\) is the mass ratio of concentrations of adsorbed substance at equilibrium divided by the concentration of the contaminant in soil\, \\\(C_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the contaminant concentration in the aqueous phase at equilibrium\, \\\(K_\{\\rm\{f\}\}\\\) is the equilibrium constant \(the Freundlich adsorption constant\)\, and \\\(1/n\\\) is a contaminant specific exponent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14703
name: fulvic acid
def: "Humic substance that is soluble at all \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\)s."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14704
name: functional redundancy
def: "Apparently unaltered maintenance of community function despite changes in structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14705
name: functional response
def: "Change in some predator function\, such as prey consumption rate\, in response to changes in prey density."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14706
name: fundamental niche
def: "Environmental conditions under which a species can thrive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14707
name: Gaia hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis proposed by James Lovelock that the Earth’s temperature\, albedo\, and surface chemistry are homeostatically regulated by the sum of all the biota of the Earth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14708
name: gametic selection
def: "Natural selection resulting from differential success of gametes produced by heterozygotes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14709
name: gastrointestinal excretion
def: "Excretion through the intestinal mucosa by active or passive processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14710
name: general adaptation syndrome
def: "Specific syndrome associated with Selyean stress composed of three phases\: the alarm reaction\, adaptation or resistance\, and exhaustion; adaptation in all phases of the GAS results in restoration of homeostasis or reduced deviation from homeostasis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14711
name: genetic adaptation
def: "Result of random genetic variation due to mutation and \(or\) to changes in allele frequencies\, causing variation in the survival and reproductive success of individuals and hence of groups of organisms\, with the consequence that those best adjusted to their environment flourish."
synonym: "adaptation" []
synonym: "evolutionary adaptation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14712
name: genetic drift
def: "Evolutionary process of change in the allele frequencies in a population due to random changes in the frequency by which different alleles are transferred to the next generation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14713
name: genetic hitchhiking
def: "Situation in which a scored locus is acting only as a marker for a closely linked gene that is actually responsible for the difference in tolerance among genotypes. More generally\, it is the condition 'in which a given allele changes in frequency as a result of linkage or gametic phase disequilibrium with another selected locus'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14714
name: genetic risk
def: "Risk to the progeny of an exposed individual of an adverse effect associated with heritable genetic damage\, e.g.\, damage to germ cells leading to a nonviable fetus or an offspring with a birth defect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14716
name: genus
def: "Low-level taxonomic rank used in the classification of living and fossil organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14717
name: geographic information system
def: "Computerized system to handle spatial data at a reasonable cost; most allow one to archive\, organize\, integrate\, statistically analyze\, and display many kinds of spatial information using a common coordinate system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14718
name: global distillation
def: "Process by which persistent and relatively volatile organochlorine compounds are distilled from warmer regions of use to cooler regions of the globe."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14719
name: global fractionation
def: "Process by which some persistent organic pollutants \(POPs\) move more rapidly than others toward the polar regions. Because POPs differ in their individual rates of degradation\, vapor pressure\, and lipophilicity\, a fractionation occurs. The net result is a redistribution of the different POPs from the equator or site of origin toward the cold polar regions of the Earth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14720
name: global warming
def: "Heating up of the Earth’s climate thought to result from the increased atmospheric carbon dioxide \(\\\(\\ce\{CO2\}\\\)\) concentrations from fossil fuel burning\, release of other greenhouse gases\, and the worldwide destruction of forests."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14721
name: good laboratory practice principles
def: "Fundamental rules incorporated in Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development \(OECD\) guidelines and national regulations concerned with the process of effective organization and the conditions under which laboratory studies are properly planned\, performed\, monitored\, recorded\, and reported."
synonym: "GLP principles" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14722
name: graded effect
def: "Consequence that can be measured on a graded scale of intensity or severity and its magnitude related directly to the dose or concentration of the substance producing it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14723
name: grasshopper effect
def: "Global distillation of persistent organic pollutants \(POPs\) involving seasonal cycling of temperatures such that movement toward the higher latitudes occurs in annual pulses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14724
name: greenhouse gases
def: "Atmospheric gases that are relatively transparent to sunlight entering the atmosphere but absorb infrared radiation generated at the Earth’s surface. They include water vapor\, carbon dioxide \(\\\(\\ce\{CO2\}\\\)\)\, methane\, dinitrogen oxide \(nitrous oxide\)\, chlorofluorocarbons \(CFCs\)\, chloroethane \(methylchloroform\)\, carbon tetrachloride\, and the fire retardant\, halon. Ozone in the troposphere can also act as a greenhouse gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14725
name: growth
def: "Increase in size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14726
name: growth dilution
def: "Decrease in contaminant concentration in a growing organism because the amount of tissue in which the contaminant is distributed is increasing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14727
name: guild
def: "Group of functionally similar species whose members interact strongly with one another but weakly with the remainder of the community."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14728
name: habitat
def: "Spatial area \(geographical area\) where a particular species population lives\, large enough to comprise a breeding population.\nNote\: A microhabitat or microenvironment is the immediate surroundings and other physical factors of an individual plant or animal within its habitat.\nPhysical conditions that surround a species\, or species population\, or assemblage of species\, or community."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14729
name: hardness
def: "Concentration of all metallic cations\, except those of the alkali metals\, present in water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14730
name: hard water
def: "Water that contains mineral salts of divalent cations\, commonly calcium and magnesium and sometimes iron\(II\) \(ferrous\) ions\, principally as hydrogencarbonates\, chlorides\, and sulfates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14731
name: Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium
def: "State of a population if the frequencies of different alleles of all genes in the genome remain constant between generations."
synonym: "genetic equilibrium" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14732
name: harm
def: "Adverse effect to an ecosystem\, community\, population\, species\, individual organism\, organ\, tissue\, or cell."
synonym: "damage" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14733
name: hazard assessment
def: "Determination of factors controlling the likely effects of a hazard such as the dose–effect and dose–response relationships\, variations in target susceptibility\, bioaccumulation potential\, persistence\, and mechanism of toxicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14734
name: hazard evaluation
def: "Identification and assessment of the potential adverse effects that could result from manufacture\, use\, and disposal of a material in a specified quantity and manner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14735
name: hazard identification
def: "Determination of substances of concern\, their adverse effects\, target populations\, and conditions of exposure\, taking into account toxicity data and knowledge of effects on human health\, other organisms\, and their environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14736
name: hazard index
def: "Sum of the hazard quotients for substances that affect the same target organ or organ system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14737
name: hazard quotient
def: "Ratio of toxicant exposure \(estimated or measured\) to a reference value regarded as corresponding to a threshold of toxicity; often this is determined as the predicted environmental concentration \(PEC\) divided by the predicted no-effect concentration \(PNEC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14738
name: hazardous concentration
def: "Concentration of a substance producing a defined effect on \\\(\\pu\{\\textit\{p\} \\%\}\\\) \(usually \\\(\\pu\{5\\%\}\\\)\) of species of concern\, derived by means of a statistical extrapolation procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14739
name: heavy metal
def: "Term used commonly but erroneously when referring to metal toxicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14740
name: heterotherm
def: "Animal that partially regulates body temperature\, sometimes maintaining its body temperature at a certain level\, e.g.\, when active\, but at other times allowing it to fluctuate with the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14741
name: heterotroph
def: "Organism obtaining organic food by eating other organisms or their excreta."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14742
name: heterotrophic succession
def: "Temporal sequential appearance of different heterotrophic species at a location\, most often decomposer organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14743
name: heterozygote advantage
def: "Greater Darwinian fitness \(reproductive output\) from heterozygous than from homozygous individuals of a species. This tends to preserve variation in the population gene pool."
synonym: "heterosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14744
name: homeostasis
def: "Totality of processes occurring in an open system or a closed system\, especially a living organism\, enabling it to regulate its internal environment to maintain stable\, constant conditions or the outcome of these processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14745
name: hormesis
def: "Unexpected and unexplained but favorable biological response to low-level exposure to a substance or physical agent generally considered to be harmful."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14746
name: humic substances
def: "Nonvolatile organic anionic polyelectrolytes\, of biological origin\, in the molecular mass range \\\(\\pu\{500\\!\{-\}\\! 5000\}\\\)\, which have complex structures and are variable in composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14747
name: Hutchinsonian niche
def: "Intersection of ranges of tolerances for sets of resources utilized by organisms\, represented mathematically by a multidimensional hypervolume whose dimensions correspond to environmental variables. Any species is considered unimodally distributed\, i.e.\, confined to a habitat in the niche."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14748
name: hybrid
def: "Result of interbreeding between two animals or plants of different taxa.Note\: Hybrids between different species within the same genus are sometimes known as interspecific hybrids or crosses. Hybrids between different subspecies within a species are known as intra-specific hybrids. Hybrids between different genera are sometimes known as intergeneric hybrids.\nCross-breed between populations\, breeds\, or cultivars within a single species.Note\: Hybridization is often used in plant and animal breeding to obtain desirable characteristics not found or inconsistently present in the parent individuals or populations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14749
name: hypoxemia
def: "Deficient oxygenation of the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14750
name: hypoxic
def: "Dioxygen-deficient"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14751
name: incipient LC<sub>50</sub>
def: "Concentration of a chemical which is lethal to \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of the test organisms as a result of exposure for periods long enough for acute lethal action to cease; in other words\, the concentration below which \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of individuals will have a normal lifespan despite the previous exposure to a toxicant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14752
name: incremental unit risk estimate
def: "For an air pollutant\, the additional lifetime cancer risk occurring in a hypothetical population in which all individuals are exposed continuously from birth throughout their lifetimes to a concentration of \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu g m-3\}\\\) of the pollutant in the air they breathe."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14753
name: independent joint action
def: "Production of an effect by each toxicant in a given exposure that is independent of the others and occurs by a different mode of action."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14754
name: index of biological integrity
def: "Composite index combining 12 qualities of fish communities of warm-water\, low-gradient streams to determine the level of stream degradation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14755
name: indicator species
def: "Species whose presence shows the occurrence of defined environmental conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14756
name: indigenous
def: "Native to a given region or ecosystem."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14757
name: indirect ecological effect
def: "Effect resulting from imposed biotic or abiotic factors which alter ecosystem properties that in turn favor or disfavor an ecological component of importance \(e.g.\, a species\) and thus indirectly lead to improved or reduced fitness of that species.\nResult of imposed biotic or abiotic factors allowing indigenous or new species to dominate\, thereby affecting ecosystem composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14758
name: indirect photolysis
def: "Degradation of a contaminant through interaction with other molecules in solution that have absorbed light energy. This can occur through energy transfer or by chemical reaction with short-lived reactive species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14759
name: indirect toxicity
def: "Adverse effects that result from agent\(s\) acting on and producing changes in the chemical\, physical\, and \(or\) biological environment external to the organisms under study \(e.g.\, decrease in food for predatory species due to direct toxicity from a chemical to prey may produce adverse effects in the predator species due to starvation rather than inducing any direct chemical toxicity in predator organisms\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14760
name: individual
def: "One whole organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14761
name: individual effective dose
def: "Concept forming the basis for most dose–response models\, which holds that there exists a smallest dose needed to kill any particular individual. The IED is a characteristic of an individual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14762
name: industrial ecology
def: "Study of the flows of materials and energy in the industrial environment and the effects of these flows on natural systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14763
name: industrial melanism
def: "Gradual increase to predominance of melanic forms in populations from industrialized regions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14764
name: inertia
def: "Community’s ability to resist change."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14765
name: infauna
def: "Animals living in the sediment of aquatic systems but not on the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14766
name: initiating event
def: "Specific action that results in a risk being incurred."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14767
name: integrated risk information system
def: "USEPA database containing reference doses\, slope factors\, and drinking-water health advisories \(one-day\, ten-day\, longer-term\, and lifetime advisories\)\, and associated information."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14768
name: interference competition
def: "Interspecies competition in which one species interferes with another\, as might occur with territoriality or aggressive behavior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14769
name: interspecies competition
def: "Interference with or inhibition of one species by another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14770
name: interspecific competition
def: "Competition by different species for the same \(limited\) resources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14771
name: interspecific interaction
def: "Relations between different species in a community."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14772
name: interstitial water
def: "Water in sediment or soil that surrounds the solid particles. The amount of interstitial water is calculated and expressed as the percentage ratio of the mass of water in the sediment to the mass of the wet sediment."
synonym: "pore water" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14773
name: intraspecific competition
def: "Competition by individuals of the same species \(population\) for the same \(limited\) resources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14774
name: intrinsic rate of growth
def: "\\\(r = \(\{\\rm\{natality\}\} + \{\\rm\{immigration\}\}\) - \(\{\\rm\{mortality\}\} + \{\\rm\{emigration\}\}\)\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14775
name: intrinsic rate of increase
def: "Rate of increase in the size of a population growing under no constraints."
synonym: "Malthusian rate of increase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14776
name: isobole approach
def: "Approach used to visualize or quantify joint action of chemical mixtures by plotting a graph \(isobologram\) of equally effective dose pairs \(isoboles\) for a single effect level. A particular effect level is selected\, such as \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of the maximum\, and doses of drugs \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) \(each alone\) that give this effect are plotted as axial points in a Cartesian plot. The straight line connecting \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) is the locus of points \(dose pairs\) that will produce this effect in a simply additive combination. This line of additivity allows a comparison with the actual dose pair that produces this effect level experimentally."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14777
name: isotopic discrimination
def: "The differential behavior of isotopes occurring if the rate or extent of participation in some biological or chemical process depends significantly on the mass of the isotope. Also called the isotope effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14778
name: iteroparous species
def: "Species that reproduces more than once."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14779
name: Kamofsky’s law
def: "Any agent will be teratogenic if it is present at concentrations or intensities producing cell toxicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14780
name: keystone habitat
def: "High-quality habitat patch essential to maintaining the vitality of the metapopulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14781
name: keystone species
def: "Species\, usually a top predator\, that influences the ecological community by its activity or role\, not its numerical dominance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14782
name: k-strategy
def: "Equilibrium strategy for species involving effective interactions with each other in the community\, allowing coexistence of many species. Equilibrium species are more effective competitors than opportunistic species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14783
name: larva
def: "Recently hatched insect\, fish\, or other organism that has different physical characteristics than those seen in the adult\, requiring metamorphosis to reach the adult body structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14784
name: law of frequence
def: "According to this 'law'\, there are comparatively many rare \(low abundance\) species\, and possibly comparatively many common \(high abundance\) species\, but relatively few in the middle \(of medium abundance\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14785
name: leach
def: "With reference to a soluble chemical or mineral\, drain away from soil\, ash\, or similar material by the action of percolating liquid\, especially rainwater."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14786
name: leachate
def: "Water or waste-water that has percolated through a column of soil or solid waste in the environment\, carrying with it substances dissolved from the soil or waste."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14787
name: lentic
def: "Related to standing water riparian-wetland areas such as lakes\, ponds\, seeps\, bogs\, and meadows."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14788
name: lentic water
def: "Nonflowing or still inland water; e.g.\, lakes\, ponds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14789
name: Leslie matrix
def: "Square matrix used in population biology to predict population growth. The top row of the matrix is the fecundity for each age class of mothers\, and a subdiagonal is constructed from the number of individuals surviving from age class \\\(x\\\) to \\\(x + 1\\\). Multiplied by the population vector of the number of female offspring in each age class \\\(x\\\) at time \\\(t\\\)\, it predicts the age distribution of the population at the next time step \\\(t + 1\\\)\, corresponding to the age class interval \\\(x + 1\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14790
name: lethal
def: "Deadly; fatal; causing death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14791
name: lethal body burden
def: "Total body uptake of a substance that is associated with mortality in short-term exposures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14792
name: lethal time
def: "Time taken for a defined percentage\, usually \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\)\, of a test population to die."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14793
name: lethality
def: "Ability to cause death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14794
name: Liebig's law of the minimum
def: "Observation that a population’s size \(number of individuals or biomass\) is limited by some essential factor in the environment that is scarce relative to the amount of other essential factors\, e.g.\, phosphorus-limited algal growth in a lake."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14795
name: life cycle
def: "Series of stages\, from a given point in one generation to the same point in next generation\, e.g.\, egglarva-adult-egg \(hyphenated when used as an adjective\, e.g.\, life-cycle strategy\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14796
name: life-cycle study
def: "Comprehensive study to determine the impact of a substance or mixture on the survival\, growth\, reproduction\, development\, or other important qualities at all stages of a species life cycle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14797
name: life history
def: "Description of life-cycle events through which an organism passes\, with particular reference to those events that influence survival and reproduction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14798
name: life table
def: "Statistical table that follows a hypothetical cohort of \\\(\\pu\{100000\}\\\) persons born at the same time as they progress through successive ages\, with the cohort reduced from one age to the next according to a set of death rates by age until all persons eventually die."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14799
name: life-table response experiment
def: "Retrospective comparison of two or more populations in which the response variable is a life table or a complete set of stage-specific vital rates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14800
name: limit value
def: "Limit concentration at or below which Member States of the European Community must set their environmental quality standard and emission standard for a particular substance according to Community Directives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14801
name: limited life-span paradigm
def: "Model based on the assumption that the maximum life span of an individual organism is an inherent\, genetically defined property of that organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14802
name: limnocorrals
def: "Artificial enclosures placed in the pelagic region of ponds\, lakes\, or marine environments. These systems vary in size from as little as \\\(\\pu\{2 L\}\\\) to over \\\(\\pu\{2.5E6 L\}\\\); however\, most of these systems have a volume between \\\(\\pu\{1000 L\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{E4 L\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14803
name: limnology
def: "Study incorporating the study of all aspects of inland freshwater habitats including lakes\, ponds\, rivers\, streams\, swamps\, wetlands\, groundwaters\, and reservoirs that make up inland water systems"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14804
name: linear free energy relationship
def: "An empirical relationship in which numerical parameters are associated with small perturbations in a parent molecule and are subsequently correlated with the change in the Gibbs energy of a certain reaction of the parent molecule vs. the perturbed molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14805
name: Litchfield method
def: "Simple\, semigraphical method for analyzing survival time data and estimating \\\(\\pu\{LT_\{50\}\}\\\) values."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14806
name: Litchfield–Wilcoxon method
def: "Semigraphical method for estimating a median lethal concentration \(\\\(\\pu\{LC_\{50\}\}\\\)\)\, median lethal dose \(\\\(\\pu\{LD_\{50\}\}\\\)\)\, or median effective concentration \(\\\(\\pu\{EC_\{50\}\}\\\)\). Although very easy to perform\, it is the most subjective method for such estimations because it involves fitting a line to data by eye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14807
name: littoral
def: "Belonging to the shore of a lake\, river\, pond\, or especially the sea.\nIntertidal zone\, sometimes referring specifically to the shallow well-lit region along the shore."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14808
name: littoral enclosures
def: "Isolated shore regions of freshwater ponds separated from the main body of water by plastic dividers used for ecotoxicological testing. These test systems generally have a volume of \\\(\\pu\{1000 L\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{5E4 L\}\\\) and a maximum depth of \\\(\\pu\{2 m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14809
name: loading
def: "Ratio of animal biomass to the volume of test solution in an exposure chamber."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14810
name: logistic curve
def: "Function\, often applied to growth curves\, fitting the general equation \\\[y = k/\(1 + e^\{a+bt\}\)\\\] where \\\(t\\\) represents time\, \\\(y\\\) the body weight or population size\, \\\(k\\\) the rate of growth\, and \\\(a\\\) and \\\(b \\gt 0\\\) are constants. In the logistic equation the percentage rate of increase decreases linearly as size increases. The resulting curve continually rises\, slowly at first\, more rapidly in the middle phase\, and slowly again near the end of growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14811
name: logistic growth
def: "Growth of a population under environmental constraints that set a maximum population size\, giving an S-shaped curve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14812
name: logit transformation
def: "In competitive binding assays\, the logit-log dose relationship\, in which the response is defined by \\\[R = \{\\rm\{logit\}\}\\\,\(y\) = \\ln \[y/\(1 - y\)\]\\\] where \\\(y = b/b_\{0\}\\\)\, \\\(b\\\) is fraction of tracer bound and \\\(b_\{0\}\\\) is the value of \\\(b\\\) with no unlabelled ligand in the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14813
name: log-normal transformation
def: "Transformation of data with a logarithmic function that results in a normal distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14814
name: Lorax incongruity
def: "Delusion of selfless motivation in environmental stewardship or advocacy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14815
name: loss of life expectancy
def: "Calculated estimate of loss in lifetime associated with a risk factor; it is estimated as the simple difference between life expectancy without the risk factor and life expectancy with the risk factor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14816
name: lotic
def: "Related to flowing continental waters\, such as rivers and streams."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14817
name: lotic mesocosms
def: "Streams of various sizes used to evaluate the effects of substances. Unlike lentic systems\, no standardized design has been developed for flowing-water test systems."
synonym: "lotic microcosms" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14818
name: lotic water
def: "Flowing continental waters\, such as rivers and streams."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14819
name: lowest effective dose
def: "Lowest dose of a chemical inducing a specified effect in a specified fraction of exposed individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14820
name: lowest-observed-effect concentration
def: "Lowest concentration of a material used in an aquatic toxicity test that has a statistically significant adverse effect on the exposed population of test organisms compared with controls."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14821
name: l<i>x</i> life table or schedule
def: "Life table that summarizes mortality data for populations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14822
name: l<i>x</i>m<i>x</i> life table
def: "Life table that summarizes both mortality and natality data for populations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14823
name: MacArthur–Wilson model
def: "Model of island colonization giving the mathematical relationship \\\[S\(t\) = S_\{\\rm\{EQ\}\}\(1 - e^\{-Gt\}\)\\\] where \\\(S\(t\)\\\) is the number of species present at time \\\(t\\\)\, \\\(S_\{\\rm\{EQ\}\}\\\) is the equilibrium number of species for the island\, and \\\(G\\\) is the rate constant for colonization of the island."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14824
name: macrocosm
def: "Large multi-species test system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14825
name: macronutrient
def: "Imprecise term referring to a nutrient required for survival\, or present in biological fluids or compartments at a level easily measured by existing analytical techniques."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14826
name: macrophyte
def: "Aquatic plant large enough to be seen easily with the naked eye \(as distinct from phytoplankton and small algae\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14827
name: male-mediated toxicity
def: "Disease or birth defects produced by a father’s exposure to a physical or chemical agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14828
name: Malthusian theory
def: "Model\, developed by Thomas R. Malthus \(1766–1834\)\, implying that\, unchecked by environmental or social constraints\, human populations would double every 25 years\, regardless of the initial population size. Thus\, if \\\(X_\{i\}\\\) denotes the population size during time period \\\(i\\\) and \\\(r\\\) the population growth rate\, the Malthusian population model gives the relationship \\\[X\(I + 1\) = \(1 + r\)X_\{i\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14829
name: mass balance equation
def: "Equation that expresses the total mass of a chemical in terms of all the various forms and concentrations in different environmental compartments \(including biota\) in which it occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14830
name: maturity index
def: "Index for pollution based on the proportions of species in a soil nematode community that fall into various categories ranging from colonizers \(\\\(r\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{strategists\}\\\)\) to persisters \(\\\(k\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{strategists\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14831
name: maulstick incongruity
def: "Assignment of ecological or biological significance of a contaminant’s effect based primarily on statements of statistical significance but contrary to biological probability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14832
name: maximum acceptable toxicant concentration
def: "Geometric mean of the lowest exposure concentration that causes a statistically significant adverse effect \(lowest-observed-effect concentration\, LOEC\) and the highest exposure concentration where no effect is observed \(no-observed-effect concentration\, NOEC\) in a life-cycle \(full chronic\) or partial lifecycle \(partial chronic\) test."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14833
name: maximum likelihood estimation
def: "Parametric method used to fit dose- or concentration-effect data to the log-normal\, log-logistic\, or other models."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14834
name: mean absorption time
def: "Mean time required for absorption of a drug or contaminant calculated as the difference in mean residence time \(MRT\) of the material introduced by the \(noninstantaneous\) route of interest and the MRT for the same material injected intravenously."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14835
name: mean generation time
def: "Average time interval required for a bacterial cell to divide or for the population to double under a defined set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14836
name: measurement endpoint
def: "Measurable response to a stressor \(e.g.\, fledglings produced per nest each year\) that is related to the valued qualities of the assessment endpoint \(e.g.\, reproductive success of bald eagles\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14837
name: median effective time
def: "For sublethal or ambiguously lethal effects\, the median time until \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of the exposed individuals respond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14838
name: median inhibitory time
def: "Time required for a toxicant to inhibit a specified process in \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of a population under test conditions."
synonym: "inhibitory time (IT)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14839
name: median time to death
def: "Median time resulting in death for \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of the exposed organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14840
name: median tolerance limit
def: "Concentration of a substance in air\, water\, sediment\, or soil at which \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of the test organisms survive after a specified time of exposure. The \\\(\\pu\{TL_\{50\}\}\\\) \(equivalent to the \\\(\\pu\{TL_\{m\}\}\\\)\) is usually expressed as a time-dependent value \(e.g.\, \\\(\\pu\{24\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{96\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) \\\(\\pu\{TL_\{50\}\}\\\); the estimated concentration at which \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of test organisms survive after \\\(\\pu\{24 h\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{96 h\}\\\) of exposure\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14841
name: meiofauna
def: "Small benthic invertebrates living in interstices of soil or sediment or in aquatic systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14842
name: meiotic drive
def: "Any process that causes some alleles to be over-represented in the gametes which are formed during meiosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14843
name: melanic forms
def: "Individuals or subspecies with increased dark pigmentation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14844
name: melanism
def: "Increased black or nearly black pigmentation of skin\, feathers\, or hair of an organism\, resulting from \(increased\) synthesis of melanin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14845
name: Mendelism
def: "Fundamental principles of inheritance \(especially the laws of segregation and independent assortment and the existence of dominant and recessive characters\)\, propounded originally by Gregor Mendel \(1822–1884\) and forming the basis for the science of classical genetics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14846
name: mesocosm
def: "Enclosed and essentially self-sufficient \(but not necessarily isolated\) experimental environment or ecosystem that is on a larger scale than a laboratory microcosm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14847
name: meta-analysis
def: "Process of using statistical methods to combine the results of different studies. In the biomedical sciences\, the systematic evaluation of a problem using information \(commonly in the form of statistical tables and other data\) from a number of independent studies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14848
name: metallothionein-like proteins
def: "Poorly characterized\, cysteine-rich metal-binding proteins or proteins not conforming precisely to the classic properties of metallothioneins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14849
name: metameter
def: "Measurement or a transformation of a measurement used in the analysis of biological tests\, e.g.\, the probit metameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14850
name: metapopulation
def: "Set of local populations which interact via dispersing individuals among local populations; though not all local populations in a metapopulation need interact directly with every other local population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14851
name: method of multiple working hypotheses
def: "Method proposed to reduce precipitate explanation by considering all plausible hypotheses simultaneously in testing so that equal amounts of effort and attention are provided to each."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14852
name: microcosm
def: "Artificial multi-species test system that simulates major characteristics of the natural environment for the purposes of ecotoxicological effects and risk assessment; such systems are normally terrestrial or aquatic and may contain plants\, animals \(vertebrates and invertebrates\)\, and microorganisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14853
name: microevolution
def: "Evolutionary change below the species level; a small change in the genetic make-up of a population from generation to generation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14854
name: microphyte
def: "Plant of microscopic size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14855
name: Microtox® test
def: "Test involving luminescent marine bacteria of the Vibrio sp. \[e.g.\, V. \(Photobacterium\) phosphoreum\, V. fischeri\, V. harveyi\]. A decrease in bioluminescence is thought to reflect toxic action."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14856
name: mineral
def: "Naturally occurring substance\, usually crystalline\, which has a particular chemical composition and specific physical properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14857
name: minimum significant difference
def: "Difference between groups \(in tests with\, e.g.\, salmonid fish\, the difference in average weights or average mortality\) that would have to occur before it could be concluded that there was a significant difference between the groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14858
name: minimal time to effect or response
def: "Minimum time required to get an effect or response. Regardless of the toxicant concentration\, the effect\, or response\, cannot occur any faster than this minimum time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14859
name: minimum viable population
def: "Smallest population size of a species allowing survival in the wild."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14860
name: mixing zone
def: "Area where an effluent discharge undergoes initial dilution and is extended to cover the secondary mixing in the ambient water body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14861
name: model error
def: "Elements of uncertainty associated with the discrepancy between the model and the real world."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14862
name: monitoring test
def: "Test designed to be applied on a routine basis\, with some degree of control\, to ensure that the quality of an environmental compartment\, biological endpoint\, or effluent has not exceeded some prescribed criteria range. In a biomonitoring test\, organisms are used as 'sensors' to detect changes in the quality of water or effluent. A monitoring test implies generation of information\, on a continuous or other regular basis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14863
name: Montreal protocol
def: "Officially the Protocol on Substances That Deplete the Ozone Layer\, a treaty signed on 16 September 1987\, in Montreal by 25 nations; there are now 168 subscribing nations. The protocol sets limits on the production of chlorofluorocarbons \(CFCs\)\, halons\, and related substances that release chlorine or bromine to the ozone layer of the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14864
name: morphodynamics
def: "Study of the interaction and adjustment of the seafloor topography and fluid hydrodynamic processes\, seafloor morphologies\, and sequences of change dynamics involving the motion of sediment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14865
name: mortality
def: "Death rate\, the number of dead individuals per unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14866
name: most-sensitive-species approach
def: "Ecotoxicological approach in which results for the most sensitive of all tested species are used as an indicator of the toxicant concentration below which the entire community is protected from adverse effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14867
name: moving average method
def: "Method of estimating \\\(\\pu\{LC_\{50\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\pu\{EC_\{50\}\}\\\)\, or \\\(\\pu\{LD_\{50\}\}\\\). It can be implemented with straightforward equations if the toxicant concentrations are set in a geometric series and there are equal numbers of individuals exposed in each treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14868
name: multiple heterosis
def: "Higher fitness of an individual as a composite or summed effect of heterozygote superiority \(heterosis\) at each of a series of loci."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14869
name: mutualism
def: "Interaction between two or more species\, giving fitness benefit to all the involved species\, e.g.\, increased carrying capacity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14870
name: National Academy of Sciences paradigm of risk assessment
def: "Model for risk assessment with four components—hazard identification\, exposure assessment\, dose–response assessment\, and risk characterization."
synonym: "NAS paradigm of risk assessment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14871
name: natural selection
def: "Evolutionary theory\, originally proposed by Darwin\, of the preferential survival and reproduction of organisms better adapted to their environment as a result of genetic adaptation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14872
name: natural radiation background
def: "Cosmic radiation emitted from stars and long-lived terrestrial radionuclides that are ubiquitously present in the Earth’s soils."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14873
name: nekton
def: "Aggregate of actively swimming animals in a body of water ranging from microscopic organisms to whales."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14874
name: neo-Darwinism
def: "Theory of biological evolution \(widely accepted since the 1920s\) based on Darwin’s theory of natural selection but incorporating the theories of later biologists regarding genes\, inheritance\, and mutation\, particularly those of Weismann and Mendel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14875
name: neophyte
def: "Plant found newly in an area where it had not been recorded previously."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14876
name: net reproductive rate
def: "Expected number of females to be produced during the lifetime of a newborn female as estimated with a life table."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14877
name: niche
def: "Group of conditions and resources\, facilitating but limiting survival\, growth\, and reproduction of a defined group of organisms or species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14878
name: niche preemption
def: "Rapid use and preemption of resources by a species that exploits them to the exclusion or severe disadvantage of another species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14879
name: niche width
def: "Term referring to the area which a species could physically inhabit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14880
name: not in my backyard principle
def: "Public acceptance of necessary provisions \(e.g.\, waste incinerators\) provided they do not affect the individual’s quality of life."
synonym: "NIMBY principle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14881
name: nine aspects of disease association
def: "Nine aspects of evidence\, defined by Bradford-Hill \(1965\)\, fostering the accuracy of linkage between a risk factor and disease\: strength of association\, consistency of association\, specificity of association\, temporal association\, biological gradient \(dose-response\) in the association\, biological plausibility\, coherence of the association\, experimental support of association\, and analogy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14882
name: \"no action\" alternative
def: "Scenario in which one assesses if the contaminants at the waste site pose\, or will pose in the future\, a risk if left alone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14883
name: nonstochastic health effects
def: "Effects that are dependent on the magnitude of the dose in excess of a threshold."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14884
name: nontarget organisms
def: "Organisms that are not the intended targets of a particular use of a pesticide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14885
name: no-observed-effect concentration
def: "Special case of the no-observed-effect level \(NOEL\)\, commonly used in aquatic toxicology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14886
name: no-response level
def: "Maximum dose of a substance at which no specified response is observed in a defined population and under defined conditions of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14887
name: normit
def: "Metameter equal to the normal equivalent deviation \(NED\). The resulting analysis of dose– or concentration-effect data with the normit metameter is often called normit analysis and is essentially equivalent to probit analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14888
name: numerical response
def: "Change in predator or grazer number through increased reproductive output\, decreased mortality\, or increased immigration in response to changes in prey or food densities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14889
name: octanol-air partition coefficient
def: "Partition coefficient for a compound between octanol and air. Like \\\(K_\{\\rm\{ow\}\}\\\)\, it is a measure of lipophilicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14890
name: octanol-water partition coefficient
def: "Ratio of the solubility of a chemical in octanol to its solubility in water at equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14891
name: octaves
def: "Log2 classes \(e.g.\, 1–2\, 2–4\, 4–8\, 8–16\, 16–32\,... individuals\) used in species-abundance curves and representing doubling of the numbers of individuals in a species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14892
name: Oklo natural reactors
def: "Naturally occurring nuclear reactors arising through biogeochemical processes approximately 1.8 billion years ago in Oklo \(Gabon\, Africa\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14893
name: oligotrophic
def: "Describing an environment having a low concentration of nutrients."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14894
name: oligotrophy
def: "State of being oligotrophic.\nObligate or facultative capacity to live in low-nutrient habitats."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14895
name: optimal-foraging theory
def: "Theory that the ideal forager will obtain a maximum net rate of energy gain by optimally allocating its time and energy to the various components of foraging."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14896
name: optimal stress response
def: "Optimal stress response involves a shift in the balance in energy allocation between somatic growth rate and longevity \(survival\) to optimize Darwinian fitness under stressful conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14897
name: ozone layer
def: "Part of the Earth’s atmosphere\, mainly located in the lower portion of the stratosphere\, approximately \\\(\\pu\{15\\!-\\! 35 km\}\\\) above the Earth's surface\, containing relatively high concentrations \(a few micromoles per litre\) of ozone \(\\\(\\ce\{O3\}\\\)\) which is higher than concentrations in the lower atmosphere. The thickness varies seasonally and geographically."
synonym: "ozonosphere" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14898
name: palaeontology
def: "Branch of science that deals with extinct and fossil humans\, animals\, and plants\, or more generally with evidence of organic life during the geological past."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14899
name: paradigm
def: "Model or template.\nBody of concepts that\, in a particular branch of science\, has withstood rigorous testing\, and is generally accepted by scientists working in that field as offering true explanations of fact and observation.Note\: This usage is derived from the work of the philosopher T. S. Kuhn \(1922–1996\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14900
name: partial kill
def: "Treatment in a toxicity test in which some\, but not all\, exposed individuals are killed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14901
name: pathway
def: "Sequence of enzymatic or other reactions by which one biological material is converted to another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14902
name: pedosphere
def: "Part of the Earth made up of soils and where important soil processes are occurring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14903
name: pelagic
def: "Of or relating to the open sea\, as distinguished from the shallow water near the coast; dwelling on or near the surface of the open sea or ocean; oceanic. Now chiefly\: \(Ecology\) designating\, relating to\, or inhabiting that region of the sea which consists of open water of any depth\, away from or independent of both the shore and the sea floor \(and so contrasted with the littoral and benthic regions\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14904
name: per capita birth rate
def: "Average number of offspring per population member per time unit. Calculated from birth rate \\\(B\\\) and population size \\\(N\\\) as \\\[b = B/N\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14905
name: per capita death rate
def: "Average number of deaths per population member per time unit. Calculated from death rate \\\(D\\\) and population size \\\(N\\\) as \\\[m = D/N\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14906
name: per capita rate of increase
def: "Relative increase\, \\\(r\\\)\, in the population per unit of time expressed per capita. \\\[r = b - m\\\] where \\\(b\\\) is per capita birth rate and \\\(m\\\) is per capita death rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14907
name: periphyton
def: "Matrix of algae\, microbes\, and detritus attached to submerged surfaces in aquatic ecosystems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14908
name: persistent inorganic pollutant
def: "Inorganic substance that is stable in the environment\, is liable to long-range transport\, may bioaccumulate in human and animal tissue\, and may have significant impacts on human health and the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14909
name: persistent organic pollutant
def: "Organic chemical that is stable in the environment\, is liable to long-range transport\, may bioaccumulate in human and animal tissue\, and may have significant impacts on human health and the environment."
synonym: "bioaccumulative chemicals of concern (BCC)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14910
name: phenology
def: "Life history."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14911
name: photoperiod
def: "The duration of illumination and darkness over a \\\(\\pu\{24\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) day."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14912
name: pH-partition hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that bioavailability is governed by the diffusion of the unionized form of an ionizable substance through the gastrointestinal lumen\, as determined by \\\(\\pu\{p\}K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14913
name: phylogenetics
def: "Branch of biology that deals with phylogeny\, especially with the deduction of the historical relationships between groups of organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14914
name: phylogeny
def: "Pattern of historical relationships between species or other groups resulting from divergence during evolution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14915
name: phylum
def: "Taxonomic rank at the level below Kingdom and above Class"

[Term]
id: goldbook:14916
name: physiological adaptation
def: "Change in an organism\, in response to changing conditions of the environment\, which takes place without any irreversible disruptions of the given biological system and without exceeding normal \(homeostatic\) capacities of its response.\nProcess by which an organism stabilizes its physiological condition after an environmental change.Note\: If this process exceeds the homeostatic range\, it becomes pathological and results in symptoms of disease \(adverse effects\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14917
name: phytochelatin
def: "Class of cysteine-rich peptides in plants that are induced by and bind to metals. They can function in the regulation and detoxification of metals by plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14918
name: phytoplankton
def: "Photosynthesizing organisms found in the plankton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14919
name: phytotoxic
def: "Toxic to plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14920
name: Pielou’s <i>J</i>
def: "Measure of species evenness for a sample from a community. It is calculated from the following equation\: \\\[\{\\rm\{Pielouߴs\}\}\\ J = \\frac\{H'\}\{\\log S\}\\\] where \\\(H'\\\) is the Shannon–Wiener diversity measure and \\\(S\\\) is the average species richness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14921
name: pinnipeds
def: "Seals\, sea lions\, and walruses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14922
name: piscivorous
def: "Feeding on fish."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14923
name: planktivorous
def: "Feeding on plankton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14924
name: plankton
def: "Organisms living suspended in the water column and incapable of moving against water currents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14925
name: planktotrophic larva
def: "Planktonic-dispersing larva that derives its nourishment by feeding in the plankton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14926
name: poikilotherm
def: "'Cold-blooded organism' \(such as an amphibian\, reptile\, or fish\) with a body temperature varying with\, but usually slightly higher than\, the temperature of its environment."
synonym: "ectotherm" []
synonym: "exotherm" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14927
name: point source
def: "Single emission source in a defined location."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14928
name: pollutant
def: "Any undesirable solid\, liquid\, or gaseous matter occurring\, as a result of human activities\, in a solid\, liquid\, or gaseous environmental medium and causing adverse effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14929
name: pollution-induced community tolerance
def: "Increase in tolerance to pollution resulting from species composition shifts in the community\, acclimatization of individuals\, and genetic changes in populations in the community."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14930
name: pollution tolerance index
def: "Means of measuring environmental quality\, usually water quality\, by determining the presence of indicator species\, classified into three groups—sensitive\, facultative\, and tolerant. Each group is assigned an index value 1\, 2\, and 3\, with the sensitive group having the highest index value. The number of species present from the list included in each group is identified in a representative environmental sample and the group index multiplied by the number of species in the group. The scores for each group are finally added to give the PTI for the environmental medium under consideration. The environmental quality is directly proportional to the value of the index."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14931
name: polygenic control
def: "Control of a phenotypic trait by several genes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14932
name: population
def: "In ecology\, any group of interacting and interbreeding organisms of the same species occupying a given area at the same time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14933
name: population biomass
def: "Total mass or weight of organisms in a population\, given by the sum of the masses \(or weights\) of all the individual members of the population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14934
name: population cycle
def: "Changes in the numbers of individuals in a population that repeatedly oscillate between periods of high and low density."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14935
name: population density
def: "Number of individuals divided by area \(\\\(\\pu\{m-2\}\\\)\) or volume \(\\\(\\pu\{m-3\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14936
name: population dynamics
def: "Variations in time and space in the sizes and densities of populations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14937
name: population ecology
def: "Study of the variations in time and space in the sizes and densities of populations\, and of the factors causing these variations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14938
name: population fluctuation
def: "Variations over time in the size of a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14939
name: population growth rate
def: "Change in population size \\\(\\Delta N\\\) during a specified time period \\\(\\Delta t\\\) \\\[\\Delta N/\\Delta t = \(b - d\)N\\\] where \\\(N\\\) is population size\, \\\(b\\\) is per capita birth rate\, and \\\(d\\\) is per capita death rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14940
name: population pyramid
def: "Diagrammatic illustration of the age structure of a population by depicting the youngest age class at the base and stacking successive age classes above it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14941
name: population size
def: "Total number of organisms in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14942
name: porous pot test
def: "Biodegradation test that simulates the continuous activated sludge \(sewage treatment\) system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14943
name: precautionary principle
def: "Approach to risk management that can be applied in circumstances of scientific uncertainty\, reflecting a perceived need to take action in the face of a potentially serious risk without waiting for definitive results of scientific research."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14944
name: predicted environmental concentration
def: "Concentration of a substance likely to be found in an environmental compartment calculated from estimates of quantities released\, discharge patterns\, and inherent disposition of the substance \(fate and distribution\) as well as the nature of the specific receiving ecosystems."
synonym: "estimated environmental concentration (EEC)" []
synonym: "expected environmental concentration (EEC)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14945
name: predicted no-effect concentration
def: "Concentration that is expected to cause no adverse effect to any naturally occurring population in an environment at risk from exposure to a given substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14946
name: predictive risk assessment
def: "Risk assessment performed for a proposed future action\, such as the use of a new chemical or the release of a new effluent."
synonym: "prospective risk assessment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14947
name: prevalence
def: "Number of instances of existing cases of a given disease or other condition in a given population at a designated time; sometimes used to mean prevalence rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14948
name: prevalence rate
def: "Total number of individuals who have an attribute or disease at a particular time \(or during a particular period\) divided by the population at risk of having the attribute or disease at this point in time or midway through the period."
synonym: "prevalence ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14949
name: primary lamellae
def: "Gill structures extending outward at right angles from the branchial arches."
synonym: "filaments" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14950
name: primary producer
def: "Organism capable of using the energy derived from light or a chemical substance in order to manufacture energy-rich organic compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14951
name: primary succession
def: "Sequential colonization by species which begin to colonize the bare ground and modify the environmental conditions after a region is completely denuded; e.g.\, behind a retreating glacier\, early colonizing organisms provide the soils needed by succeeding organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14952
name: principle of allocation
def: "Concept that there exists a cost or trade-off to every allocation of energy resources. Energy spent by an individual organism on one function\, process\, or structure cannot be spent on another. Optimal allocation of resources enhances Darwinian fitness."
synonym: "concept of strategy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14953
name: probit
def: "Probability unit obtained by adding 5 to the normal deviates of a standardized normal distribution of results from a dose-response study."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14954
name: probit log transform
def: "Probability unit obtained from the standardized normal distribution plotted against the logarithm of the concentration or dose of a substance when a quantal or graded response has been measured. A linear plot provides evidence that the distribution is log-normal. Estimates of the \\\(\\pu\{L\(E\)C_\{50\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{L\(E\)D_\{50\}\}\\\)\, as well as the standard deviation for the distribution\, can then be made."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14955
name: problem formulation
def: "Planning and scoping phase that establishes the framework around which the risk assessment is done."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14956
name: product-limit method
def: "Nonparametric method for analyzing time-to-death or survival-time data that does not require a specific model for the survival curve."
synonym: "Kaplan–Meier method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14957
name: proliferation
def: "Multiplication\, i.e.\, an increase by frequent and repeated reproduction or growth by cell division."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14958
name: propagule
def: "Portion of a plant\, fungus\, etc.\, that is capable\, when detached\, of giving rise to a new individual by asexual reproduction \(e.g.\, a cutting\, leaf bud\, bulbil\, seed\, or spore\).\nLess commonly\, any of the products of asexual reproduction in certain invertebrates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14959
name: propagule rain
def: "Relative to metapopulation dynamics\, the presence of a seed bank or dormant stage for a species that continually introduces individuals to the patch regardless of the density of occupancy in the surrounding patches. This propagule rain increases the likelihood of population reappearance and decreases the likelihood of patch extinction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14960
name: Ptolemaic incongruity
def: "False assertion that any particular level of biological organization holds a more central or important role than any other in the science of ecotoxicology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14961
name: pyrogenic
def: "Describing anything that causes fire.\nDescribing products of fire; e.g.\, organic compounds produced by the high-temperature combustion of complex organic substances are pyrogenic compounds.Examples\: Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons \(PAHs\) are examples of pyrogenic compounds.\nDescribing a substance that produces fever."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14962
name: quality criteria
def: "Quality guidelines based on the evaluation of scientific data."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14963
name: quality guidelines
def: "Numerical limits or text statements established to support and maintain designated uses of the environment or to protect human health."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14964
name: quality standards
def: "Fixed upper limits for exposure to certain chemicals recognized under law by one or more levels of government. Well-known examples include the air\, water\, and soil quality standards\, as well as threshold limit values for air pollutants in the workplace."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14965
name: quantal effect
def: "Condition that can be expressed only as 'occurring' or 'not occurring'\, such as death or occurrence of a tumor."
synonym: "all-or-none effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14966
name: quantitative structure–metabolism relationship
def: "Quantitative association between the physicochemical and \(or\) the structural properties of a substance and its metabolic behavior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14967
name: quotient method
def: "Calculation of the quotient of the measured or predicted environmental concentration \(PEC\) of a contaminant and the predicted no-effect level \(PNEC\)\, used as an expression of hazard or risk. Higher quotients constitute greater evidence of a hazard or a greater risk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14968
name: rain-out
def: "Removal of pollutants from air by incorporation into developing rain droplets of rain clouds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14969
name: rarefaction estimate of richness
def: "Estimate of species richness \(\\\(S\\\)\) expressed relative to that of a sample having a standard number of individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14970
name: rate constant-based model
def: "Compartment model that employs rate constants to quantify the rate of change in concentration or amount of toxicant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14971
name: rate-of-living theory of aging
def: "Theory that the total metabolic expenditure of a genotype is generally fixed\, and longevity depends on the rate of energy expenditure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14972
name: rate ratio
def: "Value obtained by dividing the rate in an exposed population by the rate in an unexposed population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14973
name: realised niche
def: "Portion of a species’ fundamental niche that it actually occupies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14974
name: reasonable worst case
def: "Semiquantitative term referring to the lower portion of the high end of the exposure\, dose\, or risk distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14975
name: reasonable maximum exposure
def: "Highest exposure that is reasonably expected to occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14976
name: receiving water
def: "Surface water \(e.g.\, in a stream\, river\, or lake\) that has received\, or is about to receive\, a discharged waste \(i.e.\, the surface water immediately around the discharge point\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14977
name: recommended limit
def: "Maximum concentration of a potentially toxic substance that is expected to be safe."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14978
name: reconstituted water
def: "De-ionized or glass-distilled water to which reagent-grade chemicals have been added. The resultant synthetic freshwater is expected to be free from contaminants and have the desired \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\) and hardness characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14979
name: redundancy hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that many species are redundant\, and their loss will not influence the community function as long as crucial \(e.g.\, keystone and dominant\) species populations are maintained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14980
name: reference environment
def: "Generalized description of the environment into which contaminants will be released and in which organisms will be exposed. Reference environments are used when there is no specific site at risk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14981
name: reference sediment
def: "Whole sediment near an area of concern used to assess sediment conditions in the absence of substances of concern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14982
name: reference site
def: "Relatively unpolluted site used for comparison with polluted sites in environmental monitoring studies\, often incorrectly referred to as a control site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14983
name: reference toxicant
def: "Chemical used in an aquatic toxicity test as a positive control in contrast to the negative control provided by exposure water without the test chemical. Information collected is used to determine the general health and viability of the test organisms and assess consistency in testing protocol implementation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14984
name: reference toxicity test
def: "Test conducted in conjunction with sediment tests to determine possible changes in condition of the test species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14985
name: region
def: "Area of the Earth’s surface differentiated by its specific characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14986
name: relative risk
def: "Ratio of the risk of disease or death among the exposed to the risk among the unexposed.\nRatio of the cumulative incidence rate in the exposed to the cumulative incidence rate in the unexposed."
synonym: "risk ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14987
name: remedial investigation
def: "Study that has three parts\: characterization or the type and degree of the contamination\, human risk assessment\, and ecological risk assessment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14988
name: remedial investigation and feasibility study
def: "For an EPA Superfund site\, a study that has as its goal the implementation of 'remedies that reduce\, control\, or eliminate risks to human health and the environment' or\, more specifically\, the accumulation of 'information sufficient to support an informed risk management decision regarding which remedy appears to be most appropriate for a given site'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14989
name: remediation
def: "Giving a remedy.\nRemoval of pollution or contaminants from environmental media such as soil\, groundwater\, sediment\, or surface water for the general protection of human health and the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14990
name: remote sensing
def: "Technologies that allow the acquisition and analysis of data without requiring physical contact with the land or water surface being studied. Most determine qualities or characteristics of areas of interest based on measurements of visible light\, infrared radiation\, or radio energy coming from them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14991
name: reproductive value
def: "Expected contribution of offspring during the life of an individual of an age class \\\(x\\\) in a life table."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14992
name: rescue effect
def: "Increased probability of a vacated-patch reoccupation in a metapopulation as the number of nearby\, occupied patches increases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14993
name: resilience
def: "Ability of a community to maintain its structure and function in the face of disturbance\, and to reorganize following disturbance-driven change."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14994
name: retention effect
def: "Observed effect of the properties of persistent organic pollutants \(POPs\) with high lipophilicity which causes them to be bound more firmly than less lipophilic POPs in solid phases such as soil and vegetation; consequently\, they spend less time in the atmosphere and are less available for transport in that medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14995
name: retrospective risk assessment
def: "Risk assessment dealing with an existing condition."
synonym: "retroactive risk assessment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14996
name: ring test
def: "Part of an external quality assurance program for assessment of an analytical method or test. A reference institute sends identical samples\, which have to be analyzed or tested for specified parameters\, to a number of different laboratories. The laboratory has a deadline before which to provide results. Statistical evaluation and interpretation of the results permits assessment of the reliability of the methods used and a comparison of the laboratories’ proficiency."
synonym: "round-robin test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:14997
name: riparian
def: "Interface between land and a flowing body of surface water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14998
name: risk analysis
def: "Process for controlling situations where an organism\, system\, or subpopulation could be exposed to a hazard."

[Term]
id: goldbook:14999
name: risk characterisation
def: "Outcome of hazard identification and risk estimation applied to a specific use of a substance or occurrence of an environmental health hazard."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15000
name: risk hypotheses
def: "Clear statements of postulated or predicted adverse effects of a toxicant on an assessment endpoint."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15001
name: risk source
def: "Agent\, medium\, process\, procedure\, or site with the potential to cause an adverse effect or effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15002
name: risk quotient
def: "Ratio of predicted exposure concentration \(PEC\) to predicted no-effect concentration \(PNEC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15003
name: risk reduction
def: "Taking measures to protect humans or the environment against identified risks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15004
name: rivet popper hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that each species in a community contributes to its proper functioning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15005
name: r-strategy
def: "Opportunistic strategy favoring species that establish themselves quickly\, grow quickly to exploit as many resources as possible\, and produce many offspring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15006
name: rules of practical causal inference
def: "Fox’s rules of practical causal inference that are used in ecotoxicology \(environmental epidemiology\) to infer causality for toxicant exposure/effect scenarios."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15007
name: run-off
def: "Portion of the wet precipitation on the land that ultimately reaches streams and\, eventually\, the sea."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15008
name: safe concentration
def: "Concentration of a substance to which prolonged exposure will cause no adverse effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15009
name: salinity
def: "Mass of dissolved salts in seawater\, brackish water\, brine\, or other saline solution divided by the mass of the solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15010
name: salt tolerance index
def: "Quotient calculated as total plant \(shoot + root\) dry mass at different salt concentrations compared to the total plant dry mass obtained for the controls\, as indicated below\: \\\[S_\{\\rm\{T\}\} = m_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\(\{\\rm\{x\}\}\)/m_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\(\{\\rm\{i\}\}\)\\\] where \\\(m_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) is the total dry mass\, and \\\(x\\\)\, \\\(i\\\) refer to a control treatment and a defined salt treatment\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15011
name: saprobic
def: "Living in or being an environment rich in organic matter but lacking oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15012
name: saprobic index
def: "Means of classifying the saprobic state of running waters\, covering the full range from unpolluted to extremely polluted waters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15013
name: saprobic water classification
def: "Biological classification of water quality according to five categories\:\noligosaprobic\: clear\, with no or only slight pollution and high dissolved oxygen \(DO\) content;\np-mesosaprobic\: moderately polluted with still high DO content;\nx-mesosaprobic\: polluted with not very high DO content;\npolysaprobic\: strongly polluted\, with negligible DO content; and\nantisaprobic\: so polluted that no living organism is capable of living in the water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15014
name: saprobien spectrum
def: "Characteristic change in community composition at different distances below the discharge of putrescible organic waste to a river or stream."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15015
name: satellite groups
def: "Organisms or groups of organisms treated in a similar fashion to those in standard toxicity tests for the purpose of special additional studies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15016
name: scope for growth
def: "Index \(\\\(P\\\) = production\) calculated as the amount of energy taken into the organism in its food \(\\\(A\\\)\) minus the energy used for respiration \(\\\(R\\\)\) and excretion \(\\\(U\\\)\)\: \\\[P = A - R - U\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15017
name: scope of activity
def: "Difference between the rates of oxygen consumption of an organism under maximal and minimal activity levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15018
name: screening level
def: "Decision limit or cut-off point at which a screening test is regarded as positive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15019
name: screening test
def: "Test conducted to estimate the concentrations to be used for a definitive test.\nAcute test used early in a testing program to evaluate the potential of a substance to produce a given adverse effect \(e.g.\, mortality\)."
synonym: "preliminary test" []
synonym: "range-finding test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15020
name: secondary lamellae
def: "Parallel rows of projections on the dorsal and ventral sides of each primary lamella of the fish gill. They are the primary sites of gas exchange of the gills."
synonym: "respiratory lamellae" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15021
name: secondary poisoning
def: "Poisoning of a predator as a result of eating prey that has accumulated a toxicant as a result of biomagnification through its food chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15022
name: secondary substrate metabolism
def: "Microbial growth on a nutrient substrate while transforming another substrate without gaining energetic benefit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15023
name: secondary succession
def: "Sequential appearance of species following major changes to an established ecosystem."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15024
name: selection components
def: "Components of the life cycle of an individual upon which natural selection can act. They are viability selection\, sexual selection\, meiotic drive\, gametic selection\, and fecundity selection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15025
name: Selyean stress
def: "Syndrome named for Hans Selye \(1907–1982\) that consists of all the nonspecifically induced changes within a biological system following and during environmental stress."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15026
name: semelparous species
def: "Species that reproduces once."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15027
name: semi-continuous activated sludge test
def: "Test for inherent biodegradability of organic substances in activated sludge by measurement of the decrease in dissolved oxygen content \(DOC\) in the test system."
synonym: "SCAS test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15028
name: sentinel species
def: "Feral\, caged\, or endemic species used in measuring and indicating the level of contamination or effect during a biomonitoring exercise."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15029
name: seston
def: "Minute living organisms and particles of nonliving matter that float in water and contribute to turbidity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15030
name: sexual selection
def: "Natural selection involving differential mating success of individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15031
name: Shannon–Wiener diversity index
def: "Index based on the assumption that all species are represented in the sample studied and that the sample was obtained randomly\, giving the following mathematical relationship\: \\\[H = -\\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^S p_\{i\} \\log p_\{i\}\\\] where \\\(p_\{i\}\\\) is the fraction of individuals belonging to the \\\(i\\\)-th species and \\\(S\\\) is the number of species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15032
name: Shelford’s law of tolerance
def: "Species’ tolerance\(s\) along an environmental gradient \(or series of environmental gradients\) will determine its population distribution and size in the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15033
name: silt
def: "Sediment particles with a grain size between \\\(\\pu\{0.004 mm\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{0.062 mm\}\\\)\, i.e.\, coarser than clay particles but finer than sand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15034
name: Simpson’s diversity index
def: "Dominance index weighted toward the abundance of the most common species\, giving the probability of any two individuals drawn at random from an infinitely large community belonging to different species. The bias corrected mathematical form of Simpson’s index is \\\[D = \\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{S\} p_\{i\}^\{2\}\\\] where \\\(p_\{i\}\\\) is the fraction of all organisms which belong to the \\\(i\\\)-th species and \\\(S\\\) is the number of species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15035
name: simulated field studies
def: "Experimental ecosystem that should be\: physically confined; self-maintaining; multitrophic; have a duration time exceeding the generation time of the penultimate trophic level present; and of size sufficient to enable pertinent sampling and measurements to be made without seriously influencing the structure and dynamics of the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15036
name: soil
def: "Naturally occurring\, unconsolidated mineral and \(or\) organic material at the surface of the Earth that is capable of supporting plant growth. It extends from the surface to \\\(\\pu\{15 cm\}\\\) below the depth at which properties produced by soil-forming processes can be detected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15037
name: solvent drag
def: "Movement of a solute \(e.g.\, a contaminant\) along with the bulk movement of the solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15038
name: somatic death
def: "Death of an individual organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15039
name: somatic risk
def: "Risk of an adverse effect to the exposed individual associated with genetic damage to somatic cells\, e.g.\, damage leading to cancer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15040
name: sorbate
def: "Noncommittal term used instead of adsorbate or absorbate when the sorption process is undefined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15041
name: sorption constant
def: "Quantity describing the distribution of a substance between a solvent and a sorbent\, typically water and sediment\, at equilibrium\, e.g.\, \\\[K_\{\\rm\{d\}\} = \[\\pu\{C\(sediment\)\}\]/\[\\pu\{C\(water\)\}\]\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15042
name: source term
def: "Estimate of the total amount released\, or the temporal pattern of the rate of release\, of a pollutant from a source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15043
name: Spearman–Karber method
def: "Nonparametric method to estimate the \\\(\\pu\{LC_\{50\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\pu\{EC_\{50\}\}\\\)\, or \\\(\\pu\{LD_\{50\}\}\\\) when it is difficult or unnecessary to assume a specific model for the dose- or concentration effect data."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15044
name: species–area relationship
def: "Common pattern in which the number of species on islands decreases as island area decreases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15045
name: species assemblage
def: "Operationally defined subset of the entire community."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15046
name: species–deletion stability
def: "Tendency in a model community for the remaining species to remain at locally stable equilibria after a species is made extinct."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15047
name: species differences in sensitivity
def: "Quantitative or qualitative differences of response to the action\(s\) of a potentially toxic substance on various species of living organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15048
name: species diversity
def: "Heterogeneity of an ecological community\, considering both species richness \(\\\(S\\\)\) and species evenness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15049
name: species evenness
def: "Degree to which the individuals in the community are evenly or uniformly distributed among species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15050
name: species imbalance
def: "Change in the species numbers or diversity in an ecosystem\, or in their interactions\, which results in change in ecological character and its functions and attributes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15051
name: species richness
def: "Total number of species in an ecosystem."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15052
name: species sensitivity distribution
def: "Statistical relationship between exposure concentration and a defined effect derived from a combination of single-species test data to predict concentrations affecting only a certain percentage of the total number of species in a defined community."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15053
name: species-specific sensitivity
def: "Quantitative and qualitative features of response to the action\(s\) of a potentially toxic substance that are characteristic for a particular species of living organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15054
name: specific action concept
def: "Assumption made in radiotracer usage concerning specificity of action of a radionuclide used to trace or quantify the movement of a stable nuclide \(e.g.\, \\\(\\ce\{^\{14\}C\}\\\) for stable \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)\)\, implying that the radionuclide behaves identically in chemical and biological processes to its nonradioactive analog \(e.g.\, stable \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "specific activity concept" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15055
name: spiked bioassay approach
def: "Sediment toxicity test method to generate a concentration response model for effects to individuals placed in sediments spiked with different amounts of toxicant."
synonym: "SB approach" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15056
name: spillover hypothesis
def: "Hypothesis that toxic effects of metals will begin to occur after the metal concentration exceeds the capacity of the amount of metallothionein present to bind the metal. The unbound metals then 'spill over' to interact at sites of adverse action. This is based on the assumption that binding by metallothionein sequesters toxic metals away from sites of action."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15057
name: stable age distribution
def: "Abundance of relative age classes that a population approaches if it is allowed to grow exponentially."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15058
name: stable population
def: "Population with a constant distribution of individuals among the various age classes and a zero growth rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15059
name: standard
def: "That which is established as a measure or model to which others of a similar nature should conform."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15060
name: standard
def: "Technical specification\, usually in the form of a document available to the public\, drawn up with the consensus or general approval of all interests affected by it\, based on the consolidated results of science\, technology\, and experience\, aimed at the promotion of optimum community benefits and approved by a body recognized on the national\, regional\, or international level."
synonym: "technical directive" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15061
name: static-renewal test
def: "Modified static aquatic toxicity test in which solutions are completely or partially replaced with new solutions at set periods during exposures or in which organisms are periodically transferred to new solutions."
synonym: "batch-replacement test" []
synonym: "renewal test" []
synonym: "renewed static test" []
synonym: "semi-static test" []
synonym: "static-replacement test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15062
name: static-toxicity test
def: "Aquatic toxicity test in which the exposure water is not changed during the test."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15063
name: stratification
def: "Process by which materials form or are deposited in layers\, as in sedimentary rocks and some igneous rocks. The atmosphere and the ocean also exhibit stratification\, with the warmer air or water occupying the upper layers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15064
name: stratified sampling
def: "Sampling of individual subgroups \(strata\) of a population after its division into homogeneous strata."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15065
name: stress
def: "Any condition that results in reduced growth of an organism or that prevents an organism from realizing its 'genetic potential'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15066
name: stressor
def: "Any physical\, chemical\, or biological factor causing an adverse response on any component of an ecosystem."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15067
name: stress-protein fingerprinting
def: "Proposed use of the patterns of stress-protein induction seen in the field to suggest the particular toxicant inducing the response\, after patterns from organisms sampled in the field are compared with those obtained with single-candidate toxicants in the laboratory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15068
name: stress proteins
def: "Several classes of proteins coded by genes transcriptionally activated by acute stresses\, generally serving a protective or adaptive function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15069
name: stress theory of aging
def: "Theory that stress shortens longevity by accelerating energy expenditure. Selection takes place for resistance to stress\, and as an epiphenomenon\, individuals resistant to stress will predominate in extreme age classes of a population. The diminution of homeostasis under stress with age should be slowest in individuals with highest longevity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15070
name: structural diversity
def: "Range of types of physical structure in a community that may provide habitats for species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15071
name: structure–metabolism relationship
def: "Association between the physicochemical and \(or\) the structural properties of a substance and its metabolic behavior."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15072
name: Sturm test
def: "Biodegradation test based on the measurement of \\\(\\ce\{CO2\}\\\) production."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15073
name: stygobiont
def: "Organism that lives only in groundwater."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15074
name: stygophile
def: "Organism that lives in groundwater and in surface water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15075
name: subcooled
def: "Term applied to a substance that is a liquid at a temperature lower than the saturation temperature for the existing pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15076
name: subcooled liquid vapour pressure
def: "Vapor pressure of a subcooled liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15077
name: succession
def: "Orderly sequential progression of changes in community composition that occurs during development of new populations in any area\, from initial colonization to the attainment of the climax typical of a particular geographic area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15078
name: supercooled
def: "Term describing a substance that persists as a liquid at a temperature below its freezing point without solidification or crystallization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15079
name: surrogate organism
def: "Test organism\, or population that is cultured under laboratory conditions to serve as a substitute in toxicity testing for indigenous organisms\, communities\, or populations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15080
name: surrogate toxicant
def: "Relatively well-studied substance whose properties are assumed to apply to an entire chemically and toxicologically related class."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15081
name: surveillance
def: "Systematic ongoing collection\, collation\, and analysis of data and the timely dissemination of information to those who need to know in order that action can be taken to initiate investigative or control measures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15082
name: survival time
def: "Time interval between initial exposure of an organism to a harmful substance and death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15083
name: survivorship
def: "Proportion of animals surviving between two specified ages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15084
name: survivorship curve
def: "Graph showing how survivorship from birth varies with age."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15085
name: susceptibility
def: "Condition of an organism or ecological system that makes it more vulnerable to a given exposure than the majority of the population or group of ecological systems to which it belongs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15086
name: suspension feeder
def: "Animal that feeds by straining suspended matter and food particles from water\, typically by passing the water over a specialized structure\, such as the baleen of baleen whales."
synonym: "filter feeder" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15087
name: syngenetic
def: "Describing mineral deposits formed at the same time as the enclosing rocks; characterized by or pertaining to a formation contemporaneous with the enclosing or surrounding rock."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15088
name: systematics
def: "Study of the diversity of past and present life forms and of relationships among them through time."
synonym: "taxonomy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15089
name: taxocene
def: "Taxonomically defined subset of an entire community."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15090
name: taxonomy
def: "Science applied to the allocation of biological names and the rules of naming."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15091
name: teratogenic
def: "Capable of causing nonheritable permanent structural malformations or defects in the offspring of an exposed parent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15092
name: terrestrial
def: "Relating to land\, as distinct from water or air."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15093
name: threshold-effect concentration
def: "Concentration calculated as the geometric mean of no-observed-effect concentration \(NOEC\) and lowest-observed-effect concentration \(LOEC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15094
name: threshold theory
def: "Theory that\, for a given substance\, no toxic effect can occur below a defined low dose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15095
name: tiered testing
def: "Structured approach to assessment of the fate and effects of substances\, where a tier of relatively simple tests is used initially to select substances of concern and to define their toxicity. If the information from these tests is inadequate for regulatory decisions\, further more complex tests \(higher tier tests\) may be required. For example\, under a tiered structure\, testing might progress from acute studies to chronic laboratory studies to field studies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15096
name: time-independent test
def: "Acute toxicity test with no predetermined temporal endpoint."
synonym: "TI test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15097
name: tolerance index
def: "Quotient of an adequate parameter acquired under treated and control environments\, multiplied by \\\(\\pu\{100\}\\\)\, thus\: \\\[\\rm\{TI\} = \(\\rm\{parameter\\ treated\}/\\rm\{parameter\\ control\}\) \\times 100\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15098
name: top-down ecotoxicological study
def: "Approach to investigating ecotoxicological effects that starts with a determination of the presence and nature of any adverse effects via responses at community and ecosystem levels of organization rather than the suborganismal levels of organization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15099
name: total organic carbon
def: "Organic matter content of soil\, sediment\, or water determined by measurement of organic carbon as the ratio of mass of organic carbon/mass of solid or of water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15100
name: toxic
def: "Able to cause injury to living organisms as a result of physicochemical interaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15101
name: toxic unit
def: "Dose or concentration of a toxicant expressed in units of lethality such as units of \\\(\\pu\{LD_\{50\}\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{LC_\{50\}\}\\\)."
synonym: "toxicity unit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15102
name: toxicity curve
def: "Curve obtained by plotting the median survival times of a group of test organisms against the concentration of a substance on a logarithmic scale."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15103
name: toxicity equivalency factor
def: "Ratio of the toxicity of a chemical to that of another structurally related chemical \(or index compound\) chosen as a reference.\nIn risk assessment\, ratio of the toxicity of a chemical to that of another structurally related chemical \(or index compound\) chosen as a reference. Factor used to estimate the toxicity of a complex mixture\, commonly a mixture of chlorinated dibenzo-p-dioxins \[oxanthrenes\]\, furans\, and biphenyls\: in this case\, TEF is based on relative toxicity to 2\,3\,7\,8-tetrachlorodibenzo-p-dioxin \[2\,3\,7\,8-tetrachlorooxanthrene\] for which the \\\(f = 1\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15104
name: toxicity equivalent
def: "Contribution of a specified component \(or components\) to the toxicity of a mixture of related substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15105
name: toxicity identification and evaluation
def: "Systematic pretreatment \(e.g.\, \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\) change\, filtration\, or aeration\) of a sample to obtain defined fractions that are subsequently tested for their toxicity. This fractionation is designed to separate out defined substances and thus to identify the agent\(s\) primarily responsible for lethal or sublethal toxicity of a complex mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15106
name: toxicity value
def: "Factor used to estimate risk. It may be a reference dose or it may be calculated from the following equation\: \\\[T_\{\\rm\{x\}\} = m\\ C\\\] where \\\(m\\\) is the slope of a published dose-effect relationship and \\\(C\\\) is the toxicant concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15107
name: trace nutrient
def: "Substance required in very small quantities by a defined organism to maintain health."
synonym: "micronutrient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15108
name: trade-off
def: "Exchange of one advantageous character for another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15109
name: trigger values
def: "Criteria applied to results from tests \(for fate or effects\) which prompt further studies\, e.g.\, moving to the next tier of tests\, which are generally more complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15110
name: trophic
def: "Relating to nutrition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15111
name: trophic cascade
def: "Situation arising when predators in a food chain suppress the abundance of their prey\, thereby releasing the next lower trophic level from predation \(or herbivory if the intermediate trophic level is an herbivore\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15112
name: trophic dilution
def: "Decrease in contaminant concentration as trophic level increases; this results from a net balance of ingestion rate\, uptake from food\, internal transformation\, and elimination processes favoring loss of contaminant that enters the organism via food."
synonym: "biominification" []
synonym: "bioreduction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15113
name: trophic level
def: "Position in a food chain\, assessed by the number of energy-transfer steps to reach that level."
synonym: "trophic position" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15114
name: trophic structure
def: "Organization of an ecological community described in terms of energy flow through its various trophic levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15115
name: trophic transfer
def: "Transfer of a substance from one trophic level to another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15116
name: trophic transfer factor
def: "Ratio between the concentration of a compound in a predator and in its prey."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15117
name: turbidity of water
def: "Extent to which the clarity of water is reduced by the presence of suspended or other matter that causes light to be scattered and absorbed rather than transmitted \(in straight lines\) through the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15118
name: twin-tracer technique
def: "Experimental method for evaluating assimilation that introduces simultaneously a radiotracer of the substance being assimilated and an inert tracer that will not be assimilated\, thus providing a basis for evaluating the assimilation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15119
name: type A organism
def: "Animal or plant living in contact with sediments but unable to ingest particulates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15120
name: type B organism
def: "Animal or plant living in contact with sediments and capable of ingesting particulates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15121
name: uncertainty factor
def: "In assay methodology\, confidence interval or fiducial limit used to assess the probable precision of an estimate.\nIn toxicology\, value used in extrapolation from experimental animals to humans \(assuming that humans may be more sensitive\) or from selected individuals to the general population.Example\: A value applied to the no-observed-effect-level \(NOEL\) or no-observed-adverse-effect-level \(NOAEL\) to derive an acceptable daily intake \(ADI\) or tolerable daily intake \(TDI\).Note\: The NOEL or NOAEL is divided by the value to calculate the ADI or TDI."
synonym: "application factor (AF)" []
synonym: "assessment factor" []
synonym: "modifying factor (MF)" []
synonym: "safety factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15122
name: upstream water
def: "Water in a rivulet\, river\, or lake that is situated above a defined point\, in a direction opposite to that of the current flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15123
name: uptake rate constant
def: "First-order one-compartment constant to describe the uptake of a substance by an organism from water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15124
name: viability selection
def: "Component of the life cycle of an individual in which natural selection can occur through the differential survival of individuals. It begins at the formation of the zygote and continues throughout the life of the individual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15125
name: vital rates
def: "Measures of how fast vital statistics change in a population \(usually expressed per \\\(\\pu\{1000\}\\\) individuals\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15126
name: vulnerability
def: "Susceptibility to harm by toxicants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15127
name: Wahlund effect
def: "Net deficit of heterozygotes when two populations\, each in Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium but with different allele frequencies\, are mixed and the genotype frequencies quantified in a combined population sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15128
name: waldsterben
def: "Widespread and substantial decline in growth and the change in behavior of many softwood and hardwood forest ecosystems in central Europe."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15129
name: waste-water
def: "General term describing effluents\, leachate\, and elutriates which enter the natural environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15130
name: weakest-link incongruity
def: "Questionable extension of the critical life-stage concept \(see critical life-stage testing\) that protection of the most sensitive life stage will ensure protection of all life stages; it assumes that exposure of field populations to concentrations identified in laboratory testing as causing significant mortality at a critical stage of life will result in significant impact on the field population"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15131
name: Weibull model
def: "Dose–response model of the form \\\[P\(d\) = \\gamma + \(1 - \\gamma\)\(1 - e^\{-\\beta d^\{\\alpha\}\}\)\\\] where \\\(P\(d\)\\\) is the probability of a tumor \(or other response\) from lifetime\, continuous exposure at dose \\\(d\\\) until age \\\(t\\\) \(when tumor is fatal\)\, \\\(\\alpha\\\) is a fitted dose parameter \(sometimes called the Weibull parameter\)\, \\\(\\beta\\\) is a fitted dose parameter\, and \\\(\\gamma\\\) is the background response rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15132
name: weight composition
def: "Distribution of organisms among the various weight classes present in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15133
name: weight of evidence
def: "\(in general\) Quantitative\, semiquantitative\, or qualitative estimate of the degree to which the evidence supports or undermines a given conclusion.\n\(in toxicology\) Estimate of the extent to which the available biomedical data support the hypothesis that a substance causes a defined toxic effect such as cancer in humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15134
name: Weismannism
def: "Theory of evolution and heredity propounded by the German biologist\, August Weismann\, especially in regard to the continuity of the germ-plasm and the nontransmission of acquired characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15135
name: wet deposition
def: "Transfer of chemicals from the atmosphere to the Earth’s surface in atmospheric water precipitation \(atmospheric deposition\)\, e.g.\, rain\, snow\, or hail\, of pollutants that occur in the precipitation\, e.g.\, as a result of Brownian capture\, nucleation\, dissolution\, or impaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15136
name: wetland
def: "Area of land consisting of soil that is saturated with moisture\, such as a swamp\, marsh\, or bog."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15137
name: whole-effluent toxicity
def: "Total toxic effect of an effluent measured directly with aquatic organisms in a toxicity test."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15138
name: whole sediment
def: "Sediment and associated interstitial water that have had minimal manipulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15139
name: zooplankton
def: "Small floating or weakly swimming animals that drift with water currents and which\, with phytoplankton\, make up the planktonic food supply upon which almost all oceanic organisms ultimately depend."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15140
name: normal equivalent deviation
def: "Proportion dying in a toxicity test expressed in terms of standard deviations from the mean of a normal curve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15141
name: absolute bioavailability
def: "Fraction of the administered dose of a drug from a dosage form absorbed intact into the systemic circulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15142
name: active transport of drugs
def: "Carriage of a solute across a biological membrane\, which requires a suitable carrier and the expenditure of energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15143
name: administration
def: "Introduction of a substance to an organism by a defined route."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15144
name: administration of drugs ocular route
def: "Administration of drugs through the eye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15145
name: administration of drugs oral route
def: "Administration of drugs through the mouth to swallow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15146
name: administration of drugs parenteral route
def: "Method of introducing substances into an organism\, avoiding the gastrointestinal tract."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15147
name: administration of drugs rectal route
def: "Administration of drugs into the rectal cavity or through the rectum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15148
name: administration of drugs respiratory route
def: "Delivery to the lower respiratory tract in order to obtain a local or systemic effect."
synonym: "introduction of drugs by inhalation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15149
name: administration of drugs topical route
def: "Administration of drugs on surfaces of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15150
name: administration of drugs transdermal route
def: "Introduction of products through unbroken skin by means of a specific drug delivery system \(such as a patch containing a semi-solid formulation of the drug\) for systemic and/or prolonged drug effect."
synonym: "diadermic administration" []
synonym: "percutaneous administration" []
synonym: "transcutaneous administration" []
synonym: "transdermic administration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15151
name: administration of drugs vaginal route
def: "Introduction of products into the vagina normally for local effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15152
name: angle of repose
def: "Characteristic angle of slope formed with the horizontal by the side of a static conical mound of powder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15153
name: appertisation
def: "Process by which food is rendered free from pathogenic\, toxigenic\, and spoilage organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15154
name: bioequivalence
def: "Relationship between two preparations of the same drug in the same dosage form that have a similar bioavailability.\nDosage forms containing the same drug are said to be bioequivalent if they do not differ significantly in the bioavailability \(e.g.\, AUC\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\)\, \\\(t_\{\\rm\{max\}\}\\\)\) of the active constituent/ingredient\, when administered in the same dose under similar experimental conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15155
name: biological product
def: "Virus\, therapeutic serum\, toxin\, antitoxin\, or analogous product applicable to the prevention\, treatment\, or cure of diseases or injuries in humans and/or animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15156
name: biopharmaceutics
def: "Branch of pharmaceutical science that deals with the fate of drugs in the living system; particularly the release of the drug from its dosage form into a biological medium\, its passage across membranes into the systemic circulation\, metabolism\, and elimination\, and the application of this knowledge to obtain the desired therapeutic effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15157
name: buccal tablet
def: "Usually a small\, flat\, and soft tablet\, which is designed to be placed inside the cheek to be directly absorbed through the mucosa for systemic effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15158
name: bulk density
def: "Characteristic of a powder rather than of individual particles given by the mass of powder occupying a known volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15159
name: capsule
def: "Small edible package made usually from gelatin or other materials that can be filled with drugs \(solids or liquids\) to produce a unit dose\, mainly for oral use. Hard capsules consist of two pieces that fit one inside the other which are produced empty and filled in a separate operation\, and soft\, liquid-filled capsules\, which are manufactured and filled in one operation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15160
name: carrier-mediated drug transport
def: "Transfer of a drug across a membrane by a transporter \(often a protein\) constituent of the cytosol membrane. Also known as active transport as opposed to passive diffusion/absorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15161
name: coating
def: "Technological process consisting of the application of a substance\, which forms a layer \(e.g.\, to protect the drug and/or the tablet\, to mask taste\, to control the rate of drug release \(e.g.\, film coating\, sugar coating\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15162
name: compressed tablet
def: "A solid dosage form prepared to a desired shape\, usually in large-scale production\, by means of high pressure in a punch and die."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15163
name: controlled-release dosage form
def: "Medication\, which due to its special technological construction provides for drug release having predefined kinetics \(zero order\, \\\(t^\{1/2\}\\\)\, first order\, etc.\) at a sufficient rate to maintain the desired therapeutic level over an extended period of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15164
name: critical moisture content
def: "A stage in the drying of solids\, above which the drying rate \(derived from the plot of the loss of moisture content against time\) is linear\, at which the drying rate ceases to be linear\, until it reaches the equilibrium moisture content."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15165
name: crossover study
def: "Type of comparative bioavailability study designed in such a way as to take into account differences in bioavailability arising from differences between patients suffering from disease\, participating in the study."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15166
name: crystalline
def: "Term that describes a solid of regular shape and the presence of three-dimensional order on the level of atomic dimensions\, for a given molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15167
name: delayed-release dosage form
def: "Pharmaceutical preparation that releases the drug\(s\) at a time other than promptly after administration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15168
name: deliquescent
def: "Substance that absorbs sufficient moisture from the atmosphere to dissolve itself."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15169
name: depot
def: "Deposit of a drug in a body created by injection or by a similar mode of introduction to form a source of slow release."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15170
name: detergency
def: "Property\, which serves as basis for the process whereby surfactants are used for the removal of foreign matter from surfaces \(including dirt from clothes or body surfaces\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15171
name: diffusion barrier
def: "Obstacle such as coating or embedding\, which acts as a factor controlling the rate of drug release."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15172
name: disperse system
def: "Dosage form in which the active ingredient is insoluble in the carrier; includes aerosols \(solids or liquids in gas\)\, suspensions \(solids in liquids\)\, emulsions \(liquids in liquids\)\, and foams \(gas in liquid\)\, or ointments/creams \(solid in solid or in semi-solid\, or liquid in solid\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15173
name: divided granule
def: "Formulation in which individual doses of a granulated dosage form are separated \(e.g.\, gelatin capsules\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15174
name: divided powder
def: "Powder formulation in which individual doses of a powdered dosage form are separately wrapped \(e.g.\, sachets\, envelopes\, or gelatin capsules\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15175
name: dosage form
def: "Formulated preparation of molecules/drugs that are rarely if ever suitable for administration to patients without additives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15176
name: dosage regimen
def: "Dose and dosing interval of a drug."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15177
name: drug delivery system
def: "Sophisticated dosage form\, which\, by its construction\, is able to modify/control the availability of the drug substance to the body by temporal or spatial considerations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15178
name: drug-eluting stent
def: "Refers to a stent with an active drug that is intended to produce a therapeutic effect \(e.g.\, reduction of restenosis\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15179
name: dusting powder
def: "Usually intended for external use."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15180
name: effervescent tablet
def: "Solid preparation that on contact with water breaks apart by the effect of gas \(usually \\\(\\ce\{CO2\}\\\)\) evolution\, resulting commonly from the reaction of hydrogen carbonate with citric or tartaric acid\, in order to facilitate dissolution or dispersion of the active ingredient before ingestion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15181
name: efflorescent
def: "Substance that loses water to form a lower hydrate or becomes anhydrous spontaneously."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15182
name: elixir
def: "Sweet \(often colored\) dilute alcohol-based\, 'hydroalcoholic'\, liquid used in the compounding of drugs to be taken by mouth in order to improve palatability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15183
name: embedding
def: "Technological process\, which consists of mixing or inclusion of the therapeutic substance with an excipient or their mixtures\, typically as a matrix dosage form\, in order to change the rate of release."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15184
name: enemas
def: "Solutions \(aqueous or oily\)\, emulsions\, or suspensions for rectal administration of medicaments for cleansing\, diagnostic\, or therapeutic purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15185
name: enteric coating
def: "Used on tablets\, granules\, pellets\, and capsules to make them resistant to gastric fluids but designed to disintegrate\, disrupt\, or dissolve when the preparation enters the duodenum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15186
name: equilibrium moisture content
def: "Final stage reached after drying of a solid\, beyond the critical moisture content."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15187
name: fast-dissolving tablet
def: "A tablet formulation intended for a rapid release of its active agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15188
name: formulation
def: "Summary of operations carried out to convert a pharmacologically active compound into a dosage form suitable for administration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15189
name: gargle
def: "Aqueous solution used for the prevention and treatment of mouth and throat infections."
synonym: "mouthwash" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15190
name: gastric emptying rate
def: "Pace at which a drug along with the stomach content leaves and enters the duodenum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15191
name: gene therapy
def: "Use of products containing genetic material \(e.g.\, pDNA\, antisense DNA\, siRNA\) to treat a disease or condition\, or to modify or manipulate the expression of genetic material or to alter the biological properties of living cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15192
name: generic
def: "Drug\(s\) or formulation\(s\) of drug\(s\) or dosage forms\, which no longer have patent protection."
synonym: "generics" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15193
name: granules
def: "Powder particles\, which have been aggregated to form larger irregular particles\, usually of \\\(\\pu\{0.5\\!\{-\}\\! 2 mm\}\\\) diameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15194
name: granulation
def: "Process in which powder particles are made to aggregate to larger particles called granules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15195
name: hydrate
def: "Crystalline form of a compound in which water molecules are part of the crystal structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15196
name: hydrophile-lipophile balance system
def: "Empirical scale \(of \\\(0\\!-\\!20\\\)\) used to classify surfactants and emulsifying agents."
synonym: "HLB System" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15197
name: hygroscopicity
def: "Tendency of a substance to absorb water from the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15198
name: immediate-release tablet
def: "Dosage form that releases the drug immediately."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15199
name: inactivation factor
def: "Number that expresses the reduction in the numbers of a microorganism\, brought about by a sterilization process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15200
name: inhalation therapy
def: "Administration of drugs directly to the respiratory tract\, mostly by aerosols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15201
name: injection
def: "Delivery of a generally sterile liquid medication into the body\, or a vessel\, tissue\, or organ via syringe and needle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15202
name: liniment
def: "Liquid intended for massaging into the skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15203
name: loading dose
def: "Initial\, typically larger than the maintenance dose of a drug given to a patient at the start of pharmacotherapy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15204
name: lotion
def: "Solution\, emulsion\, or suspension to be applied to the skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15205
name: lozenge
def: "Tablet\, which does not contain a disintegrant and which is sucked to dissolve in the mouth to produce either a local \(e.g.\, antiseptic\) or systemic \(e.g.\, vitamins\) effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15206
name: lubricant
def: "Used as processing aid in tablet and capsule manufacturing\, to facilitate the movement of the formulation into the dye and punch and to reduce the energy of compression."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15207
name: matrix formulation
def: "Specific case of drug embedding in insoluble excipients \(typically in a polymer\) in order to achieve extended release."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15208
name: maximum additive concentration
def: "Maximum concentration of a drug\, which will form a clear solution with a given concentration of surfactant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15209
name: maximum safe concentration
def: "Concentration of a drug in the plasma\, above which side effects are likely to occur in a patient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15210
name: minimum effective plasma concentration
def: "Concentration of a drug that must be achieved in the plasma before any desired therapeutic or pharmacological effect can be obtained."
synonym: "minimum therapeutic plasma concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15211
name: minimum inhibitory concentration
def: "Lowest concentration of an antibacterial drug necessary to inhibit the growth of a microorganism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15212
name: modified release
def: "Release of a drug from a dosage form that it is not immediate \(e.g.\, sustained release\, retarded release\, delayed-action preparations\, controlled release\, extended release\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15213
name: moistening agents
def: "Usually water or low-molecular-weight alcohols or compounds\, used in topical applications and in wet granulation\, for the production of tablets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15214
name: multicompartment formulation
def: "Dosage form \(capsule\, tablet\) comprising several elements \(e.g.\, microspheres or coated pellets\) differing in the rate of drug release."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15215
name: multilayer tablet
def: "Consists of several different layers that are compressed on top of each other\, to form a single tablet composed of two or more layers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15216
name: nanoencapsulation
def: "Formation of nanoparticles encapsulating a drug."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15217
name: ointment
def: "Greasy\, semi-solid preparation for external application\, often anhydrous\, containing dissolved or dispersed medicaments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15218
name: one-compartment model
def: "Kinetic model\, where the whole body is thought of as a single compartment in which a substance is distributed rapidly\, achieving an equilibrium between blood and tissue immediately."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15219
name: onset of drug action
def: "Time required to achieve the minimum effective plasma concentration following administration of the dosage form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15220
name: paste
def: "Ointment containing \\\(\\gt \\pu\{0\\%\}\\\) of powder\, dispersed in a fatty base."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15221
name: pellet
def: "Very small tablet or pilule.\nImplantable polymeric matrix.\nSpherical granule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15222
name: pelletisation
def: "Process of agglomeration that converts fine powders or granules of bulk drugs and excipients into small\, free-flowing\, spherical\, or semi-spherical units\, referred to as pellets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15223
name: pharmaceutical equivalence
def: "To be pharmaceutically equivalent\, the generic and proprietary formulations must \(1\) contain the same amount of active ingredient; \(2\) contain the same active ingredient in the same dosage form; \(3\) be intended for the same route of administration; and \(4\) generally be labeled for the same conditions of use."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15224
name: pharmaceutics
def: "Science of preparation of drugs\, dosage forms\, and drug delivery systems taking into account the pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics of the drug as well as its physical and chemical properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15225
name: polymorph
def: "Solid material that exists at least in two different molecular arrangements\, i.e.\, distinctly different crystal species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15226
name: powder grades
def: "Defined for powders used pharmaceutically\, according to particle sizes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15227
name: powder
def: "Dry solid material consisting of many\, usually free flowing\, fine particles. Conventionally\, the title 'powder' should be restricted to powder mixes for internal use and alternative terms are used for other powdered formulations presented in this way\, e.g.\, dusting powders\, which are for external use."
synonym: "powders" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15228
name: preformulation
def: "Exploratory activity that begins early in pharmaceutics\, involving studies designed to determine the compatibility of excipients with the active substance for a biopharmaceutical; physicochemical and bioanalytical investigation in support of promising experimental formulations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15229
name: pseudopolymorph
def: "Different crystalline form\(s\) of a solvated compound that differ in the identity and/or the stoichiometry of the solvating molecule."
synonym: "pseudopolymorphs" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15230
name: repeat action dosage form
def: "Tablet or capsule distinguished from a sustained-release dosage form\, by the fact that it releases the medicinal agent\, or part of it\, at any time other than promptly after administration as opposed to a slow\, controlled manner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15231
name: sieving
def: "Process that differentiates or separates solid particles according to their size using a meshed or perforated device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15232
name: slugging
def: "Method by which powder particles are compressed into a large tablet\, called a slug\, which is subsequently dry-screened and compressed into a tablet."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15233
name: solubilising agents
def: "Additives making a substance soluble or more soluble\, especially in water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15234
name: solvate
def: "Crystalline form of a compound in which one or more solvent molecules are part of the crystal structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15235
name: spheronisation
def: "Process of making dense\, spherical pellets by means of special spheronizing or pelletizing equipment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15236
name: stent
def: "Scaffold placed into narrowed\, diseased vessels \(mainly coronary arteries\) or a device implanted in a vessel used to help keep it open."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15237
name: sublingual tablets
def: "Usually small\, flat\, and soft tablets\, which are designed to be placed under the tongue to allow direct absorption of the active ingredient through the mucosa for systemic effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15238
name: suppositories
def: "Dosage form\, semi-solid\, used for the administration of drugs via the rectal route\, for systemic or local effect. When application is via other routes \(e.g.\, the vaginal route\)\, suppositories are termed differently\, inserts\, pessaries\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15239
name: sustained release
def: "Dosage form designed to release the drug contained therein at a continuous and controlled rate for a longer period of time that can normally be achieved with its conventional\, nonsustained counterpart."
synonym: "extended release" []
synonym: "gradual release" []
synonym: "long acting" []
synonym: "prolonged action" []
synonym: "slow release" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15240
name: syrup
def: "Liquid preparation of high sugar concentration with or without medicinal and additional flavoring substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15241
name: tablet
def: "Solid dosage form compressed into a specific shape containing medicinal substances with or without suitable diluents."
synonym: "pastille" []
synonym: "pellet" []
synonym: "pill" []
synonym: "troche" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15242
name: tablet coating
def: "Solid layers based typically on cellulose derivatives and may include plasticizers and pigments. They are usually employed for one or more of the following reasons\:\n\nProtection of the ingredients \(from light or moisture\).\nMasking the bad taste of the drug.\nMasking possible batchwise differences in the appearance of raw materials and hence allaying possible patient concern over tablets of differing appearance.\nCoating confers mechanical strength and facilitates handling.\nColored coating aids in the rapid identification of a product.\nFunctional film coating is used to impart enteric or controlled-release properties to the tablet."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15243
name: troche
def: "Applied to compressed lozenges. In lay language\, lozenge and troche are used interchangeably."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15244
name: acrylic polymer
def: "Polymer prepared from acrylic acid or a derivative of acrylic acid."
synonym: "acrylate polymer" []
synonym: "acrylic" []
synonym: "polyacrylic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15245
name: amino polymer
def: "Polymer prepared by polycondensation of an amino component such as amine or amide with an aldehyde\, or by curing reactive oligomers prepared from an amino component and an aldehyde."
synonym: "amide-aldehyde polymer" []
synonym: "amine-aldehyde polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15246
name: coumarone-indene polymer
def: "Polymer prepared from a mixture containing 1-benzofuran \(formerly coumarone\) and indene."
synonym: "1-benzofuran-indene polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15247
name: epoxy polymer
def: "Polymer prepared by reaction of compounds carrying two or more epoxy groups \(reactive epoxides\) with curing agents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15248
name: furan polymer
def: "Polymer prepared from furan or a furan derivative."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15249
name: phenolic polymer
def: "Polymer prepared by the polycondensation of phenols and aldehydes or by curing reactive oligomers prepared from phenols and aldehydes."
synonym: "phenol-aldehyde polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15250
name: polybismaleimide
def: "Polymer prepared from a bismaleimide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15251
name: polycarborane
def: "Polymer prepared from a carborane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15252
name: polydiene
def: "Polymer prepared from a diene\, or a substituted diene\, or both in admixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15253
name: polyisocyanate
def: "Polymer prepared from an isocyanate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15254
name: polyisocyanide
def: "Polymer prepared from an isocyanide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15255
name: polyolefin
def: "Polymer prepared from an olefin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15256
name: polyvinyl acetal
def: "Polymer prepared by acetalization of poly\(vinyl alcohol\) with an aldehyde."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15257
name: polyvinyl ester
def: "Polymer prepared from a vinyl ester."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15258
name: polyvinyl ether
def: "Polymer prepared from a vinyl ether."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15259
name: polyvinyl halide
def: "Polymer prepared from a vinyl halide \(monohalogenoethene\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15260
name: polyvinyl ketone
def: "Polymer prepared from a vinyl ketone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15261
name: styrene polymer
def: "Polymer prepared from styrene\, or a substituted styrene\, or both in admixture."
synonym: "polystyrene" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15262
name: vinyl polymer
def: "Polymer prepared from a monomer containing the vinyl group \\\(\\ce\{-CH=CH2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15263
name: vinylidene polymer
def: "Polymer prepared from a monomer containing the ethene-1\,1-diyl group \\\(\\gt\\!\\ce\{C=CH2\}\\\)\, formerly called vinylidene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15264
name: polyalkenylene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alkene-α\,ω-diyl \(formerly alkenylene\) constitutional repeating units\: \\\[\\ce\{-\[-CH=CH-\[CH2\]_\{m\}\\bond\{-\}\]_\{n\}\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15265
name: polyalkylene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alkane-α\,ω-diyl \(formerly alkylene\) constitutional repeating units\: \\\[\\ce\{-\[-CR^\{1\}R^\{2\}\\bond\{-\}\]_\{n\}\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, alkyl\, or cycloalkyl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15266
name: polyalkynylene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alkyne-α\,ω-diyl \(formerly alkynylene\) constitutional repeating units\: \\\[\\ce\{-\[-C#C-\[CH2\]_m\\bond\{-\}\]_n\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15267
name: polyamide
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing carboxamide groups as linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-NR-CO\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15268
name: polyamide acid
def: "Polyamide containing pendent carboxy groups\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is a constitutional unit with a connectivity three or higher."
synonym: "polyamic acid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15269
name: polyamine
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing imino groups as linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-NR\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15270
name: polyanhydride
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing carboxylic anhydride groupings as linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-CO-O-CO\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15271
name: polybenzothiazole
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing benzothiazole groups in the backbone\, such as the groups\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15272
name: polybenzoxazole
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing benzoxazole linkages in the backbone\, such as the linkages\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15273
name: polycarbamate
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing carbamate \(formerly urethane\) or N-substituted carbamate linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-NR-CO-O\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, heteroaryl\, substituted alkyl\, substituted aryl\, or substituted heteroaryl."
synonym: "polyurethane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15274
name: polycarbodiimide
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing carbodiimide linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-N=C=N\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15275
name: polycarbonate
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing carbonate linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-O-CO-O\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15276
name: polycarbosilane
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing carbon-silicon linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-SiR^\{1\}R^\{2\}\\bond\{-\}CR^\{3\}R^\{4\}\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^3\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\ce\{R^4\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15277
name: polycyanurate
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing 1\,3\,5-triazine-2\,4\,6-triyltrioxy constitutional units \(formerly cyanurate groups\) in the backbone\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15278
name: polyester
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing carboxylic ester linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-O-CO\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15279
name: polyether
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing ether linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-O\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15280
name: polygermane
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively germanium atoms in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-\[-GeR2\\bond\{-\}\]_\{n\}\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is alkyl\, aryl or heteroaryl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15281
name: polyglutarimide
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing glutarimide rings in the backbone\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is hydrogen or alkyl \(usually methyl\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\) is hydrogen\, or an alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15282
name: polyhydantoin
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing hydantoin \(imidazolidine-2\,4-dione\) rings in the backbone\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is hydrogen\, or an alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15283
name: polyhydrazide
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing hydrazide linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-CO-NR^1\\bond\{-\}NR^2\\bond\{-\}CO\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15284
name: polyimidazole
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing imidazole rings in the backbone\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, or an alkyl or aryl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15285
name: polyisocyanurate
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing 2\,4\,6-trioxo-1\,3\,5-triazinane-1\,3\,5-triyl \(formerly isocyanurate\) rings in the backbone\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15286
name: polyketone
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing keto groups as linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-CO\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15287
name: polymetaloxane
def: "Organometallic polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating metal and oxygen atoms in the main chain\: \\\[\\ce\{-ML_\{x\}-O\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{M\}\\\) is a metal atom such as \\\(\\ce\{Al\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{Ge\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{Sn\}\\\)\, or \\\(\\ce\{Ti\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\ce\{L\}\\\) is a metal-bonded substituent \(ligand\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15288
name: polyoxyalkylene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively oxyalkane-α\,ω-diyl \(formerly oxyalkylene\) constitutional repeating units in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-\[-O-Y\\bond\{-\}\]_\{n\}\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{Y\}\\\) is an alkane-α\,ω-diyl \(formerly alkylene\) or substituted alkane-α\,ω-diyl group contributing at least two carbon atoms to the main chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15289
name: polyoxyarylene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively oxyarenediyl \(formerly oxyarylene\) constitutional repeating units in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-\[-O-Ar\\bond\{-\}\]_\{n\}\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{Ar\}\\\) is an arenediyl \(formerly arylene\)\, heteroarenediyl \(formerly heteroarylene\)\, substituted arenediyl or substituted heteroarenediyl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15290
name: polyoxymethylene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively oxymethylene or substituted oxymethylene constitutional repeating units in the backbone\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl."
synonym: "polyacetal" []
synonym: "polyketal" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15291
name: polyoxyphenylene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively oxyphenylene or ring-substituted oxyphenylene constitutional repeating units in the backbone\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^3\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\ce\{R^4\}\\\) are each hydrogen or an alkyl\, aryl\, or aralkyl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15292
name: polyparabanic acid
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing imidazolidine-2\,4\,5-trione \(parabanic acid\) rings in the backbone\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15293
name: polyphenylenemethylene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating phenylene or substituted phenylene and methylene or substituted methylene constitutional units in the backbone\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^3\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^4\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^5\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\ce\{R^6\}\\\) are each hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, halogen\, trialkylsilyl\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15294
name: polyphosphate
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing phosphate constitutional repeating units\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15295
name: polyphosphazene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively nitrilophosphanylylidene \(formerly phosphazene\) constitutional repeating units in the backbone\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\) are each a monovalent group\, such as a halogen or an alkoxy group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15296
name: polypyrrone
def: "Polymer composed of single strand or ladder macromolecules comprising isoindolo\[2\,1-a\]benzimidazole \(formerly pyrrone\) constitutional repeating units such as\:  where \\\(\\ce\{Z\}\\\) is a divalent linking group or atom."
synonym: "polybenzimidazolimides" []
synonym: "polybenzimidazolones" []
synonym: "polybenzimidazopyrrolones" []
synonym: "polyimidazopyrrolones" []
synonym: "polyisoindolobenzimidazole" []
synonym: "polyisoindolobenzimidazoles" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15297
name: polyquinoline
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing quinoline moieties in the backbone\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15298
name: polyquinoxaline
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing quinoxaline groups in the backbone\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15299
name: polysilazane
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively alternating nitrogen and silicon atoms in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-\[-NR^\{1\}\\bond\{-\}SiR^\{2\}\\!\\\,R^\{3\}\\bond\{-\}\]_\{n\}\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\)\, \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\ce\{R^3\}\\\) are each hydrogen or an alkyl or aryl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15300
name: polysiloxane
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing alternating oxygen and silicon atoms in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-\[-O\\bond\{-\}SiR^\{1\}\\!\\\,R^\{2\}\\bond\{-\}\]_\{n\}\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\) are each hydrogen or an alkyl or aryl group."
synonym: "polyoxysilylenes" []
synonym: "silicones" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15301
name: polysilsesquioxane
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing silicon and oxygen atoms in the ratio \\\(1\{\:\}1.5\\\) \(ideal ratio for infinite macromolecule\)\, which alternate in a hyperbranched or ladder structure such as\:   where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15302
name: polystannane
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing exclusively tin atoms in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-\[-SnR^\{1\}\\!\\\,R^\{2\}\\bond\{-\}\]_\{n\}\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R^1\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{R^2\}\\\) are each hydrogen or an alkyl or aryl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15303
name: polysulfonamide
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing sulfonamide linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-SO2-NR\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is hydrogen or an alkyl\, aryl\, or heteroaryl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15304
name: polysulfone
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing sulfonyl linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-SO2\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15305
name: polythioester
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing thiocarboxylate \(thioester\) linkages in the backbone\:\\\(\\ce\{-S-CO\\bond\{-\}\}\\\) or \\\(\\ce\{-O-CS\\bond\{-\}\}\\\) or \\\(\\ce\{-S-CS\\bond\{-\}\}\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15306
name: polythioether
def: "Polymers composed of macromolecules containing thioether \(sulfide\) linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-S\\bond\{-\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15307
name: polytriazine
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing triazine rings in the backbone\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15308
name: polyurea
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing ureylene or N-substituted ureylene linkages in the backbone\: \\\[\\ce\{-NR-CO-NR\\bond\{-\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\) is hydrogen\, alkyl\, aryl\, heteroaryl\, substituted alkyl\, substituted aryl\, or substituted heteroaryl."
synonym: "polyureylene" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15309
name: cardo polymer
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules consisting of a single-strand main chain and many ring units\, each having one atom in common with the main chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15310
name: catenane polymer
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules consisting of physically catenated macrorings that have no atom in common and cannot be separated unless chemical bonds are broken."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15311
name: helical polymer
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules that adopt partly or completely a helical conformation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15312
name: activity coefficient at infinite dilution
def: "For a substance \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)\, activity coefficient \\\(f_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\) extrapolated to infinite dilution\: \\\[\\ln \(f_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\infty\}\) = \\lim \\limits_\{x_\{\\ce\{B\}\} \\to 0\} \(\\frac\{\\mu_\{\\ce\{B\}\} - \\mu_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\ast\}\}\{RT\} - \\ln x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15313
name: analytical method
def: "Class of experimental procedures for solubility determination in which a saturated solution is prepared and then analyzed to determine composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15314
name: aquamolality
def: "For a solute \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) in a system containing solvent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and a reference solvent \\\(\\ce\{C\}\\\)\, \\\[m_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\(\\ce\{C\}\)\} = m_\{\\ce\{B\}\} \\overline M/M_\{\\ce\{C\}\}\\\] where \\\(m_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\) is the molality of \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)\, \\\(M_\{\\ce\{C\}\}\\\) is the molar mass of a reference component\, and \\\(\\overline\{M\}\\\) is the average molar mass of a mixed solvent\, defined for a system containing two solvents as \\\[\\overline M = x_\{\nu\,\\ce\{A\}\} M_\{\\ce\{A\}\} + \(1 - x_\{\nu\,\\ce\{A\}\}\) M_\{\\ce\{C\}\}\\\] the solvent amount fraction of solvent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\)."
synonym: "solvomolality" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15315
name: azeotropic point
def: "For a mixture\, temperature and pressure \(the azeotropic temperature and pressure\) at which the compositions of the liquid and vapor phases become equal \(the azeotropic composition\)\, but the intensive properties of the two phases \(such as molar volume\) are different."
synonym: "azeotrope" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15316
name: binary system
def: "System containing two components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15317
name: Bunsen coefficient
def: "Volume \\\(V^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) of an amount \\\(n_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) of a gas dissolved at a given standard temperature \\\(T^\{^⦵\\!\}\\\) \(usually \\\(\\pu\{273.15 K\}\\\)\) and given standard \(partial\) pressure \\\(p_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{_\{^⦵\\!\}\}\\\) \(\\\(\\pu\{1 bar\} = \\pu\{0.1 MPa\}\\\) or\, in older literature\, \\\(\\pu\{1 atm\}\\\)\) divided by the volume of the solvent \\\(V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) containing an amount \\\(n_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\\\) of solvent at temperature \\\(T\\\) and the given total pressure \\\(p^\{_\{^⦵\\!\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15318
name: Clarke–Glew equation
def: "Semi-empirical equation describing the temperature dependence of the standard Gibbs energy of solution\, \\\[\\Delta_\{\\rm\{sln\}\}G^\{^⦵\\!\}\(T\, p^\{_\{^⦵\\!\}\}\)/RT = A_\{0\} + A_\{1\}\(T_\{\\rm\{ref\}\}/T\) + A_\{2\}\\ln\(T/T_\{\\rm\{ref\}\}\) + A_\{3\}\(T/T_\{\\rm\{ref\}\}\) + A_\{4\}\(T/T_\{\\rm\{ref\}\}\)^\{2\} + \\ldots\\\] where the \\\(A_\{i\}\\\) are constants that can be related to thermodynamic quantities \(but subject to uncertainty because of statistical correlations among the fitting coefficients\) and \\\(T_\{\\rm\{ref\}\}\\\) is a reference temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15319
name: Clarke–Glew–Weiss equation
def: "Clarke–Glew equation with \\\(T_\{\\rm\{ref\}\} = \\pu\{100 K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15320
name: coexistence curve
def: "Boundary of stable phase separation \(limits of solubility\) in a liquid or solid system of two or more components."
synonym: "binodal curve" []
synonym: "conodal curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15321
name: conjugate phases
def: "Two phases of variable composition in mutual thermodynamic equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15322
name: critical index
def: "Index in the basic equation describing a coexistence curve \\\[\\beta = \\lim \\limits_\{T \\to T_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\} \\left\(\\frac\{\\partial \\ln \(x'' - x'\)\}\{\\partial \\ln \\vert T - T_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\vert\} \\right\)\\\] where \\\(x''\\\)\, \\\(x'\\\) are the mole fractions of one component in the respective concentrated and dilute phases and \\\(T_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) is the critical solution temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15323
name: data
def: "Experimental results\, often numerical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15324
name: dystectic reaction
def: "Isobaric\, reversible melting or dissolution with either complete or partial dissociation on heating of a solid compound\, \\\(\\ce\{A_\{\\rm\{a\}\}B_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\}\\\)\, formed by components \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) \\\[\\ce\{A_\{\\rm\{a\}\}B_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\(s\)  A_\{\\rm\{a\}\}B_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\(l\)  \{\\rm\{a\}\}A\(l\) + \{\\rm\{b\}\}B\(l\)\}\\\] where the forward arrows indicate the direction of heating."
synonym: "congruent melting" []
synonym: "indifferent melting" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15325
name: dystonic reaction
def: "Reversible dissolution in an isothermal\, isobaric system of three or more components characterized by dissolution and saturation with a stoichiometric compound consisting of two or more of these components. The equilibrium process is\, for example\, \\\[\\ce\{AB.p H2O  A\(aq\, sat\) + B\(aq\, sat\) + p H2O\(l\)\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15326
name: eutectoid reaction
def: "Isothermal reversible reaction of a solid mixture phase \\\(\\upgamma\\\) which is transformed into two \(or more\) different solid \(pure\, binary compound or mixture\) phases \\\(\\upalpha\\\) and \\\(\\upbeta\\\) during the cooling of a system. In a binary system\, \\\[\\ce\{\\upgamma  \\upalpha + \\upbeta\}\\\] where the forward arrow indicates the direction of cooling. The equilibrium reaction occurs along the eutectoid line at the eutectoid temperature. At the eutectoid composition\, the compositions of the high temperature solid phase and the mechanical mixture of the low-temperature solid phases are equal\, and intermediate to the compositions of the pure solid phases \\\(\\upalpha\\\) and \\\(\\upbeta\\\). This composition and the eutectoid temperature\, which are isobaric invariants of the system\, define the eutectoid point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15327
name: eutonic reaction
def: "Reversible dissolution in a system of three or more components characterized by the composition of a solution that is simultaneously saturated with respect to all \(at least two or more\) dissolved solutes. The two simultaneous equilibrium processes are\, for example\, \\\[\\begin\{array\}\{l\}\\ce\{A.p H2O  A\{\(aq\, sat\)\} + p H2O\(l\)\} \\\\ \\ce\{B.q H2O  B\{\(aq\, sat\)\} + q H2O\(l\)\}\\end\{array\}\\\]"
synonym: "(isothermally and isobarically invariant) reaction of double saturation" []
synonym: "isothermally invariant reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15328
name: fitting equation
def: "Theoretically based or empirical equation for interpolation of data over a range of temperature\, pressure\, composition\, or other variable."
synonym: "smoothing equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15329
name: Gibbs–Duhem equation
def: "Equation relating the intensive variables \\\(T\\\)\, \\\(p\\\) and the \\\(C\\\) chemical potentials \\\(\\mu_\{i\}\\\) in a phase \\\[S\{\\rm\{d\}\}T - V\{\\rm\{d\}\}p + \\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{C\} n_\{i\} \{\\rm\{d\}\}\\mu_\{i\} = 0\\\] where \\\(C\\\) is the total number of components\, \\\(i\\\) in a phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15330
name: Gibbs energy of transfer
def: "Change in Gibbs energy at a given temperature and pressure for transfer of a substance between two different phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15331
name: Gibbs–Konovalov equations
def: "Pair of equations for a binary mixture of components \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) that relate the variables \\\(T\\\)\, \\\(p\\\)\, in one phase of variable composition\, \\\(\\upalpha\\\)\, to the variables \\\(T\\\)\, \\\(p\\\)\, in a coexisting equilibrium phase of variable composition\, \\\(\\upbeta\\\)\: \\\[\\begin\{array\}\{c\} -\\frac\{\\left\(1 - x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\beta\} \\right\)\\Delta_\{\\alpha\}^\{\\beta\} H_\{\\ce\{A\}\} + x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\beta\} \\Delta_\{\\alpha\}^\{\\beta\} H_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\}\{T\}\{\\rm\{d\}\}T + \\left\[\\left\(1 - x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\beta\} \\right\)\\Delta_\{\\alpha\}^\{\\beta\}V_\{\\ce\{A\}\} + x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\beta\}\\Delta_\{\\alpha\}^\{\\beta\}V_\{\\ce\{B\}\} \\right\]\{\\rm\{d\}\}p +\\left\(x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\alpha\} - x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\beta\} \\right\)\\left\(\\frac\{\\partial^\{2\} G_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\alpha\}\}\{\\partial x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\alpha 2\}\} \\right\)_\{T\,p\} \{\\rm\{d\}\}x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\alpha\} = 0 \\\\ -\\frac\{\\left\(1 - x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\alpha\} \\right\)\\Delta_\{\\alpha\}^\{\\beta\} H_\{\\ce\{A\}\} + x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\alpha\} \\Delta_\{\\alpha\}^\{\\beta\} H_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{T\}\{\\rm\{d\}\}T + \\left\[\\left\(1 - x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\alpha\} \\right\)\\Delta_\{\\alpha\}^\{\\beta\}V_\{\\ce\{A\}\} + x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\alpha\}\\Delta_\{\\alpha\}^\{\\beta\}V_\{\\ce\{B\}\} \\right\]\{\\rm\{d\}\}p +\\left\(x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\alpha\} - x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\beta\} \\right\)\\left\(\\frac\{\\partial^\{2\} G_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\alpha\}\}\{\\partial x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\beta 2\}\} \\right\)_\{T\,p\} \{\\rm\{d\}\}x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\beta\} = 0 \\end\{array\}\\\] where \\\(\\Delta_\{\\upalpha\}^\{\\upbeta\} H_\{\\ce\{A\}\} = H_\{\\ce\{A\}\}^\{\\upbeta\} - H_\{\\ce\{A\}\}^\{\\upalpha\}\\\)\, \\\(\\Delta_\{\\upalpha\}^\{\\upbeta\} V_\{\\ce\{A\}\} = V_\{\\ce\{A\}\}^\{\\upbeta\} - V_\{\\ce\{A\}\}^\{\\upalpha\}\\\) are the enthalpy and volume of transfer of component \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) from phase \\\(\\upalpha\\\) to phase \\\(\\upbeta\\\)\, and similarly for component \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)."
synonym: "Gibbs–Konovalow equations" []
synonym: "van der Waals’ equations" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15332
name: higher-order system
def: "System containing more than three components."
synonym: "multicomponent system" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15333
name: infinite dilution
def: "Limiting composition or other variable in a solution obtained by extrapolating to a value of zero for the variable describing the composition of the solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15334
name: infinite miscibility
def: "Property of a system of forming a single phase at all relative proportions of its components."
synonym: "miscibility in all proportions" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15335
name: ionic mole fraction
def: "For an ionized salt \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) in solution\, \\\[x_\{+\\rm\{B\}\} = \\frac\{\nu_\{+\\ce\{B\}\}x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\}\{1 + \\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{s\} \\left\(\nu_\{i\} - 1 \\right\)x_\{i\}\}\,x_\{-\\ce\{B\}\} = \\frac\{\nu_\{-\\ce\{B\}\}x_\{+\\ce\{B\}\}\}\{\nu_\{+\\ce\{B\}\}\}\\\] where the summation is over all $s$ solute components \\\(i\\\) and \\\(\nu_\{i\} = \nu_\{+i\} + \nu_\{-i\}\\\) is the sum of the stoichiometric numbers of the ions formed from salt \\\(i\\\).\nFor a single solvent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) in an ionic solution\, \\\[x'_\{\\ce\{A\}\} = \\frac\{x_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\}\{1 + \\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{s\} \\left\(\nu_\{i\} - 1 \\right\)x_\{i\}\}\\\] Note 1\: These are generalizations of terms and are used in formulating fitting equations for solubility of salts\, in defining activity coefficients on the mole fraction scale\, and in discussing salt effects on solubilities of gases.Note 2\: Note that \\\[\\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{s\} \\left\(x_\{+i\} - x_\{-i\} \\right\) + x'_\{\\ce\{A\}\} = 1\\\]"
synonym: "ionic amount fraction" []
synonym: "species amount fraction" []
synonym: "species mole fraction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15336
name: isopleth
def: "Line joining points of equal composition on a phase diagram."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15337
name: mass ratio
def: "Mass of one constituent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) divided by the mass of a second constituent \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) in the same system. \\\[\\zeta_\{\\ce\{A\,B\}\} = m_\{\\ce\{A\}\}/m_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15338
name: mass solubility
def: "Obsolete term for molality of a saturated solution of a gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15339
name: metatectic reaction
def: "Isothermal reversible reaction of a solid mixture phase \\\(\\upbeta\\\) which is transformed into a different solid phase \\\(\\upalpha\\\) plus a liquid phase \\\(\\ce\{l\}\\\) during cooling of a system. For a binary system\, \\\[\\ce\{\\upbeta  \\upalpha + 1\}\\\] where the forward arrow indicates the direction of cooling. The equilibrium transformation occurs along the metatectic line characterized by the metatectic temperature. The metatectic composition and temperature\, isobaric invariants of the system\, define the metatectic point\, which lies between the compositions of liquid \\\(\\ce\{l\}\\\) and solid phase \\\(\\upalpha\\\)."
synonym: "catatectic reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15340
name: mole percent
def: "Deprecated term that includes both a quantity and a unit. Replace by mole fraction expressed as percent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15341
name: mole ratio
def: "Amount of one constituent divided by the amount of a second constituent in the same system. For constituents 1 and 2\, \\\[r_\{12\} = n_\{1\}/n_\{2\}\\\]"
synonym: "amount ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15342
name: mutual solubility
def: "In a system of two or more liquid or solid components\, solubility of all components in all phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15343
name: non-saturating solute
def: "Solute which forms an unsaturated solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15344
name: peritonic reaction
def: "Isothermal\, isobaric reversible reaction between two phases\, a saturated liquid and a solid\, that results\, on removal of the solvent component of a ternary system in one new solid phase. For example\, \\\[\\ce\{l + \\upalpha  \\upbeta\}\\\] where the forward arrow indicates the direction of removal of the solvent component. The system is in equilibrium along the peritonic line\, on which is found the peritonic composition or point\, an isothermal\, isobaric invariant of the system which lies between the compositions of phases \\\(\\ce\{l\}\\\) and \\\(\\upalpha\\\)."
synonym: "transition reaction (in phase equilibria)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15345
name: permil
def: "One part in a thousand parts."
synonym: "per mille" []
synonym: "permille" []
synonym: "promille" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15346
name: phase diagram
def: "Graphical representation\, by use of points\, lines\, and surfaces\, of the phases present at chemical equilibrium in unary\, binary\, ternary\, and higher-order systems containing two or more phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15347
name: Poynting correction
def: "Factor used to convert experimentally determined Henry’s law constants at the saturation vapor pressure of the solvent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\)\, \\\(p_\{\\ce\{A\}\}^\{\\ast\}\\\)\, to a reference pressure \\\(p\\\) \\\[\\begin\{array\}\{c\} I_\{\\rm\{p\}\} = \\int_\{p_\{\\ce\{A\}\}^\{\\ast\}\}^p \\frac\{V_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\infty \{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\}\{RT\}\{\\rm\{d\}\}p \\\\ k_\{\\rm\{H\}\,\\ce\{B\}\}\(p\) = k_\{\\rm\{H\}\,\\ce\{B\}\}\(p^\{\\ast\}\) \\exp \\left\[I_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\(p\) \\right\]\\end\{array\}\\\] is the partial molar volume of liquid \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) at infinite dilution in solvent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\). The second equation gives the relation between the two Henry’s law constants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15348
name: primary data
def: "Data reported in peer-reviewed scientific reports of original research which allow an assessment of data quality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15349
name: Raoult’s law
def: "Fugacity \(\\\(\\tilde\{p\}_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\)\) of a gaseous component \(\\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)\) in equilibrium with a liquid or solid mixture containing \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) is directly proportional to the activity \(\\\(a_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\)\) of the component in the mixture \\\[\\tilde\{p\}_\{\\ce\{B\}\} = \\tilde\{p\}_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\ast\} f_\{\\ce\{B\}\} x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\] where \\\(f_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\) is the activity coefficient of \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\) referenced to Raoult’s law at mole fraction \\\(x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\tilde\{p\}_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\) is the fugacity of pure \\\(\\ce\{B\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15350
name: reciprocal salt system
def: "System containing \\\(n\\\) ionic species with or without a single solvent. Because of electrical neutrality\, the system has \\\(n\\\) components in an aqueous system with a single solvent and \\\(n - 1\\\) components in a molten salt system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15351
name: reduced pressure
def: "Pressure divided by the critical pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15352
name: reduced temperature
def: "Temperature divided by the critical temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15353
name: reference data
def: "Data that are critically evaluated and verified\, obtained from an identified source\, and related to a property of a phenomenon\, body\, or substance\, or a system of components of known composition and structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15354
name: retrograde solubility
def: "Solubility that decreases with an increase in conditions such as temperature or added component\, where an increase is expected as the usual case.\nIn ternary liquid systems with coexisting phases\, where the critical solution point is not a maximum on a ternary diagram\, passing from a homogeneous mixture in which the composition of a given component is greater than that of the critical mixture to a heterogeneous mixture and back to a homogeneous mixture by altering the relative amounts of the two other components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15355
name: saturating solute
def: "Solute that forms a saturated solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15356
name: scaling equation
def: "Dimensionless equation representing mutual solubilities in a number of chemically related liquid systems. Each mutual solubility is subtracted from the solubility at the critical solution point\, and the temperature is divided by the critical solution temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15357
name: Sechenov equation
def: "Expression of the salt effect that relates the change in solubility of a nonelectrolyte \(e.g.\, gas or organic liquid\) to changing ionic strength of aqueous solutions\, \\\[\\lg \(s_\{\\rm\{o\}\}/s\) = K_\{s\} I_\{s\}\\\] where \\\(s_\{o\}\\\)\, \\\(s\\\) are the solubilities of nonelectrolyte in pure water and saline solution\, respectively\, \\\(K_\{s\}\\\) the Sechenov parameter \(an empirical proportionality constant\)\, and \\\(I_\{s\}\\\) the ionic strength of the saline solution."
synonym: "Setschenow equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15358
name: Sieverts’ law
def: "Solubility of a diatomic gas in a molten metal is proportional to the square root of the partial pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15359
name: solubility constant
def: "Generalization of solubility product to include\, for example\, undissociated salt or uncharged cation-anion pair as solute\, reaction between solution species and the cation or anion of the solid salt\, and gas-solution equilibria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15360
name: solubilisation
def: "Process by which an agent increases the solubility or the rate of dissolution of a solid or liquid solute.Modified from entry 'micellar solubilization' in."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15361
name: solvent mole fraction
def: "Mole or mass fraction of a solvent in a solution containing \\\(s\\\) solute constituents \(\\\(i = 1\, 2\, \\ldots\, s\\\)\) and \\\(p\\\) solvent constituents \(\\\(i = s + 1\, s + 2\, \\ldots\, p\\\)\). For a solvent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\)\, \\\[x_\{\{\\rm\{v\}\}\,\{\\ce\{A\}\}\} = x_\{\\ce\{A\}\}/\\sum \\limits_\{i=s+1\}^\{p\} x_\{i\}\\\] where \\\[\\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{p\} x_\{i\} = \\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{s\} x_\{i\} + \\sum \\limits_\{i=s+1\}^\{p\} x_\{i\} = 1\\\] and analogously for \\\(w_\{\{\\rm\{v\}\}\,\{\\ce\{A\}\}\}\\\)\, where the first and last summations are over all solvent constituents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15362
name: solvent volume fraction
def: "For a solvent \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\)\, volume fraction of \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) divided by the sum of the volume fractions of solvent in a solution containing both \\\(s\\\) solutes \(\\\(i = 1\, 2\, \\ldots\, s\\\)\) and \\\(p\\\) solvents \(\\\(i = s + 1\, s + 2\, \\ldots\, p\\\)\) \\\[\\varphi_\{\{\\rm\{v\}\}\,\{\\ce\{A\}\}\} = \\varphi_\{\\ce\{A\}\}/\\sum \\limits_\{i=s+1\}^\{p\} \\varphi_\{i\}\\\] where the summation is over the \\\(p\\\) solvent components only."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15363
name: standard amount concentration
def: "Chosen value of amount concentration."
synonym: "standard concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15364
name: synthetic method
def: "Class of experimental procedures for solubility determination in which a solution of known composition is prepared. Two major subclasses are recognized. In the isothermal method\, one liquid component is titrated with a second liquid component at constant temperature until persistent turbidity is observed \(i.e.\, to the cloud point\). In the polythermal method\, a mixture of known composition is heated above its solution temperature and monitored visually during cooling until turbidity \(the cloud point\) is observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15365
name: ternary diagram
def: "Triangular plot \(usually an equilateral or right-angled triangle\) whose vertices represent the pure components of a ternary system at constant temperature and pressure. Any point within the triangle represents the composition in terms of the two independent mole \(or mass\) fractions. Lines parallel to the sides of the triangle represent constant proportions of the respective components \(isopleths\)."
synonym: "Gibbs’ triangular representation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15366
name: ternary system
def: "System containing three components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15367
name: tie-line
def: "Straight line connecting compositions of independent coexisting phases in equilibrium on a phase diagram."
synonym: "connodal" []
synonym: "conodal" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15368
name: unary system
def: "System containing one component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15369
name: unsaturated solution
def: "Solution that has a lower proportion of a solute than one that is in equilibrium with undissolved solute at specified values of temperature and pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15370
name: unsaturation
def: "State of an unsaturated solution."
synonym: "undersaturation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15371
name: wet residue method
def: "Method for determining composition of a solid phase in a ternary system at constant temperature and pressure by analysis of the total mixture of solid and liquid in equilibrium."
synonym: "Schreinemakers’ method" []
synonym: "initial complex method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15372
name: Ising model
def: "Theory of coexistence curves or other discontinuities in the properties of phases \(such as order-disorder or magnetic transitions\) in binary systems\, based on a one-\, two-\, or three-dimensional statistical mechanical nearest-neighbor lattice theory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15373
name: Jänecke coordinates
def: "Coordinates used mainly in representation of multicomponent phase diagrams that distinguish solutes and a single solvent. The mole or mass fractions of the \\\(s\\\) solutes are chosen as the primary composition variables and the mole or mass fraction of solvent as a secondary variable\, so that the solute amount \(mass\) fractions may be represented\, for example\, in ternary and quaternary systems\, as linear \(ternary\) or planar triangular or square planar \(quaternary\) diagrams\, with the solvent amount or mass fraction along an axis perpendicular to the chosen diagram of solute variables. Quantitatively\, \\\[x_\{\{\\rm\{s\}\}\,\{\\ce\{B\}\}\} = x_\{\\ce\{B\}\}/\\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{s\}x_\{i\}\\\] where the summation is over the \\\(s\\\) solute substances. Note that \\\(\\sum \\limits_\{i=1\}^\{s\}x_\{\{\\rm\{s\}\}\,\{\\ce\{B\}\}\} = 1\\\)."
synonym: "solute mole (or mass) fraction coordinates" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15374
name: Kuenen coefficient
def: "Volume \\\(V^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) of an amount \\\(n_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) of a dissolved gas at a given standard temperature \\\(T^\{^⦵\\!\}\\\) \(usually \\\(\\pu\{273.15 K\}\\\)\) and given standard pressure \\\(p^\{_\{^⦵\\!\}\}\\\) \(\\\(\\pu\{1 bar\} = \\pu\{0.1 MPa\}\\\) or\, in older literature\, \\\(\\pu\{1 atm\}\\\)\) divided by the mass \\\(m^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) of the dissolving liquid containing an amount $n_\{\\ce\{A\}\}$ of solvent at temperature $T$ and the given pressure \\\(p^\{_\{^⦵\\!\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15375
name: mass percent
def: "Deprecated term that includes both a quantity and a unit. Replace by mass fraction expressed as percent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15377
name: Ostwald coefficient
def: "Volume \\\(V^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) of an amount \\\(n_\{\\ce\{B\}\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) of a dissolved gas calculated at given temperature \\\(T\\\) and pressure \\\(p\\\) divided by the volume of the dissolving liquid of volume \\\(V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) and containing an amount \\\(n_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\\\) of solvent at the same temperature \\\(T\\\) and pressure \\\(p\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15378
name: acyclic diene metathesis polymerisation
def: "Metathesis polymerization of an acylic diene monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15379
name: apparent rate constant of polymerisation
def: "Measured rate of polymerization divided by the concentration of the reactant \(if there is only one\) or the product of concentrations of the presumed reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15380
name: auto-acceleration
def: "Increase in the rate of polymerization with increasing conversion of the reactant\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15381
name: azeotropic copolymerisation
def: "Binary copolymerization in which the two monomer reactivity ratios\, \\\(r_\{12\}\\\) and \\\(r_\{21\}\\\) are not both equal to unity yet the copolymerization forms a copolymer with its molar ratio of monomer units equal to the molar ratio of monomers in the monomer feed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15382
name: backbiting
def: "Chain transfer from an active center on a macromolecule to another part of the same macromolecule."
synonym: "intramolecular chain transfer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15383
name: binary copolymerisation
def: "Copolymerization involving two species of monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15384
name: bulk polymerisation
def: "Polymerization of an undiluted liquid monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15385
name: ceiling temperature
def: "Temperature above which\, in a given chain polymerization\, polymer of high molar mass is not formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15386
name: chain copolymerisation
def: "Chain polymerization in which a copolymer is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15387
name: chain depropagation
def: "Chemical reaction that results in the formation of a monomer molecule at an active center on the terminal unit of a chain carrier and decreases the degree of polymerization of the chain carrier by one."
synonym: "depropagation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15388
name: chain-end reactivity ratio
def: "In a binary copolymerization\, the ratio of two rate constants defining the relative activity of an active center on a given type of terminal unit of a chain carrier toward one type of monomer\, in the two cases where the penultimate unit of the chain carrier is identical to and different from the terminal unit."
synonym: "radical reactivity ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15389
name: chain-transfer constant
def: "In a homopolymerization\, rate constant for chain transfer\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{tr\}\}\\\)\, divided by the rate constant for chain propagation\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, i.e.\, \\\(C_\{\\rm\{tr\}\} = k_\{\\rm\{tr\}\}/k_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15390
name: comonomer
def: "Monomer used in copolymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15391
name: copolymerisation composition equation
def: "Equation relating the instantaneous composition of a binary copolymer to the monomer reactivity ratios and the ratio of instantaneous monomer concentrations."
synonym: "Mayo–Lewis equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15392
name: coupling reaction
def: "Linking of two macromolecules through chemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15393
name: cross-propagation
def: "Propagation reaction in a copolymerization in which a chain carrier\, bearing at its active end a monomer unit of one kind\, reacts with a monomer molecule of another kind."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15394
name: cross-termination
def: "Termination reaction in a copolymerization between two chain carriers with different chemical structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15395
name: dead-end polymerisation
def: "Radical polymerization that stops\, as a consequence of the depletion of the initiator\, before the monomer has reacted fully."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15396
name: degradative chain transfer
def: "Chain transfer reaction that generates a new chain carrier of much lower reactivity than that of the original chain carrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15397
name: diffusion-controlled termination
def: "Chain termination in a chain polymerization in which the rate-determining step is a diffusion process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15398
name: end-capping
def: "Reaction in which end-groups of a desired structure are intentionally formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15399
name: enthalpy of polymerisation
def: "Change of enthalpy\, in a chain polymerization forming a homopolymer\, associated with the conversion of one mole of monomer into polymer under isobaric and isothermal conditions."
synonym: "molar enthalpy of polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15400
name: entropy of polymerisation
def: "Change of entropy\, in a chain polymerization forming a homopolymer\, associated with the conversion of one mole of monomer into polymer under isobaric and isothermal conditions."
synonym: "molar entropy of polymerization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15401
name: floor temperature
def: "Temperature below which\, in a given chain polymerization\, polymer of high molar mass is not formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15402
name: free ion
def: "Ion whose behavior is independent of the influence of a counterion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15403
name: heat of polymerisation
def: "Heat absorbed or given off from the conversion of a specified amount of monomer into polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15404
name: homopropagation
def: "Addition of a monomer to a chain carrier bearing a terminal group derived from the same monomer species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15405
name: ideal binary copolymerisation
def: "Binary copolymerization in which the relative rates of incorporation of the two types of monomer into the copolymer are independent of the nature of the monomer unit at the end of a propagating chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15406
name: ideal copolymerisation
def: "Copolymerization in which all types of propagating species show the same relative reactivity toward the monomers present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15407
name: interfacial polycondensation
def: "Polycondensation involving two monomers separately dissolved in immiscible solvents\, forming a twophase system\, with polycondensation taking place at \(or near\) an interface between the two phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15408
name: intermolecular chain transfer
def: "Chain transfer from an active center on a macromolecule to a different macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15409
name: kinetic-chain length
def: "In a chain polymerization\, the rate of chain propagation divided by the sum of the rates of all of the chain-termination processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15410
name: monomer reactivity ratios
def: "In binary copolymerization\, \(a\) the ratio \(\\\(r_\{12\}\\\)\) of the rate constant \(\\\(k_\{11\}\\\)\) for the homopropagation of monomer \\\(\\rm\{M\}_\{1\}\\\) to the rate constant \(\\\(k_\{12\}\\\)\) for the cross-propagation of the chain carrier \\\(\\ldots -\{\\rm\{m\}\}_\{1\}^\{\\ast\}\\\) with the monomer \\\(\\rm\{M\}_\{2\}\\\) and \(b\) the ratio \(\\\(r_\{21\}\\\)\) of the rate constant \(\\\(k_\{22\}\\\)\) for the homopropagation of monomer \\\(\\rm\{M\}_\{2\}\\\) to the rate constant for the cross-propagation \(\\\(k_\{21\}\\\)\) of the chain carrier \\\(\\ldots -\{\\rm\{m\}\}_\{2\}^\{\\ast\}\\\) with monomer \\\(\\rm\{M\}_\{1\}\\\). Thus\, \\\(r_\{12\} = k_\{11\}/k_\{12\}\, r_\{21\} = k_\{22\}/k_\{21\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15411
name: monomer reactivity scale
def: "Relative scale defined by the values of the rate constants for the addition of monomers to a reference active center."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15412
name: patterns of reactivity scheme
def: "Method for the prediction of monomer reactivity ratios in binary copolymerization\, based exclusively on experimentally determined parameters."
synonym: "Jenkins’ scheme" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15413
name: penultimate-unit effect
def: "Phenomenon that the penultimate monomer unit preceding the active center on the terminal unit of an active polymer chain affects the reactivity of that active center."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15414
name: primary-radical termination
def: "Termination reaction involving a radical chain carrier and a primary radical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15415
name: pulsed-laser polymerisation
def: "Polymerization initiated by successive pulses of laser light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15416
name: <i>Q-e</i> scheme
def: "Empirical equations expressing the monomer reactivity ratios in a binary radical copolymerization\, \\\(r_\{12\}\\\) and \\\(r_\{21\}\\\)\, in terms of the empirical parameters \\\(Q\\\) and \\\(e\\\) for the two monomers\, namely\, \\\(\\rm\{Q\}_\{1\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{Q\}_\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{e\}_\{1\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\rm\{e\}_\{2\}\\\)\, with\\\(r_\{12\} = \(Q_\{1\}/Q_\{2\}\)\\exp \[-\{e_\{1\}\}\(e_\{1\} - e_\{2\}\)\]\\\) and \\\(r_\{21\} = \(Q_\{2\}/Q_\{1\}\) \\exp \[-\{e_\{2\}\}\(e_\{2\} - e_\{1\}\)\]\\\)"
synonym: "Alfrey–Price scheme" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15417
name: radical reactivity scale
def: "Scale of the values of the rate constants for the reaction of radicals with a reference monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15418
name: rate of polymerisation
def: "Rate of consumption of monomers in a chain polymerization\, or the rate of consumption of the functional groups in the reaction mixture of a polycondensation or polyaddition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15419
name: secondary radical
def: "Radical formed by the rearrangement or fragmentation of a primary radical that is capable of initiating a polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15420
name: self-initiated polymerisation
def: "Polymerization in which initiating species are formed exclusively from the monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15421
name: solid-state polymerisation
def: "Polymerization of a crystalline monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15422
name: spontaneous termination
def: "Chain termination proceeding as a unimolecular process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15423
name: topochemical polymerisation
def: "Solid-state polymerization\, resulting in the formation of a polymer having essentially the same crystal structure as that of the monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15424
name: transamidation
def: "Interchange reaction involving amide groups."
synonym: "amide interchange" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15425
name: transesterification
def: "Interchange reaction involving ester groups."
synonym: "ester interchange" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15427
name: zwitterionic copolymerisation
def: "Copolymerization that is a zwitterionic polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15428
name: zwitterionic polymerisation
def: "Chain polymerization in which a growing macromolecule bears two ionic chain carriers of opposite signs\, at one or both of its ends."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15429
name: cyclic macromolecule
def: "Macromolecule in which the termini of the backbone are connected to form a ring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15430
name: main ring
def: "Macrocycle in a bridged polycyclic macromolecule or polycyclic macromolecular portion that contains the subunits of highest seniority."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15431
name: path length
def: "Number of polymer main-chain \(backbone\) atoms between two subunits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15432
name: polycyclic macromolecule
def: "Molecule that includes more than one macrocycle\, all of which have at least one atom in common with another macrocycle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15433
name: abiotic degradation
def: "Process in which a substance is converted to simpler products by physical or chemical mechanisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15434
name: abortifacient
def: "Substance that causes pregnancy to end prematurely and causes an abortion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15435
name: abuse
def: "Improper use of drugs or other substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15436
name: acaricide
def: "Substance intended to kill mites\, ticks\, or other Acaridae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15437
name: acceptable residue level
def: "Acceptable concentration of a residue that has been established for an antibiotic found in human or animal foods."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15438
name: acceptable risk
def: "Probability of suffering disease or injury that is considered to be sufficiently small to be 'negligible'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15439
name: accepted risk
def: "Probability of suffering disease or injury that is accepted by an individual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15440
name: accidental exposure
def: "Unintended contact with a substance or change in the physical environment \(including\, e.g.\, radiation\) resulting from an accident."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15441
name: acid dissociation constant
def: "Equilibrium constant for the following reaction of an acid \\\(\\ce\{HB\}\\\)\: \\\[\\begin\{gathered\} \\ce\{HB\(aq\)  H^\{+\}\(aq\) + B^\{-\}\(aq\)\} \\\\ K_\{\\rm\{a\}\} = \[\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}\]\[\\ce\{B^\{-\}\}\]/\[\\ce\{HB\}\]c^\{\\rm\{o\}\} \\end\{gathered\}\\\] where \\\(c^\{\\rm\{o\}\} = \\pu\{1 mol L-1\}\\\) is the standard amount concentration and activity coefficients have been neglected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15442
name: action level
def: "Concentration of a substance in air\, soil\, water\, or other defined medium at which specified emergency counter-measures\, such as the seizure and destruction of contaminated materials\, evacuation of the local population or closing down the sources of pollution\, are to be taken.\nConcentration of a pollutant in air\, soil\, water\, or other defined medium at which some kind of preventive action \(not necessarily of an emergency nature\) is to be taken."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15443
name: active ingredient
def: "Component of a mixture responsible for the biological effects of the mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15444
name: active metabolite
def: "Metabolite causing biological and \(or\) toxicological effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15445
name: acute toxicity test
def: "Study in which organisms are observed during only a short part of the life span and in which there is often only a single exposure to the test agent at the beginning of the study."
synonym: "short-term toxicity test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15446
name: adaptation
def: "Change in an organism\, in response to changing conditions of the environment \(specifically chemical\)\, which takes place without any irreversible disruptions of the given biological system and without exceeding normal \(homeostatic\) capacities of its response.\nProcess by which an organism stabilizes its physiological condition after an environmental change.Note\: If this process exceeds the homeostatic range\, it becomes pathological and results in symptoms of disease \(adverse effects\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15447
name: added risk
def: "Difference between the incidence of an adverse effect in a treated group \(of organisms or a group of exposed humans\) and a control group \(of the same organisms or the spontaneous incidence in humans\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15448
name: adverse event
def: "Occurrence that causes an adverse effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15449
name: aerodynamic diameter
def: "Diameter of a spherical particle with relative density equal to unity that has the same settling velocity in air as the particle in question."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15450
name: after-effect of a poison
def: "Ability of a poison to produce a change in an organism after cessation of contact."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15451
name: age sensitivity
def: "Quantitative and qualitative age dependence of an effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15452
name: air pollution control system
def: "Network of organizations that monitor air pollution.\nGroup of measures or processes used to minimize or prevent air pollution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15453
name: albuminuria
def: "Presence of albumin\, derived from plasma\, in the urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15454
name: alkylating agent
def: "Substance that introduces an alkyl substituent into a compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15455
name: allometric growth
def: "Regular and systematic pattern of growth such that the mass or size of any organ or part of a body can be expressed in relation to the total mass or size of the entire organism according to the allometric equation\: \\\[Y = b\{x^\{\\alpha\}\}\\\] where \\\(Y\\\) = mass of the organ\, \\\(x\\\) = mass of the organism\, \\\(\\alpha\\\) = growth coefficient of the organ\, and \\\(b\\\) is a constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15456
name: allometric scaling
def: "Adjustment of data to allow for change in proportion between an organ or organs and other body parts during the growth of an organism.\nAdjustment of data to allow for differences and make comparisons between species having dissimilar characteristics \(e.g.\, in size\, shape\, and metabolism\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15457
name: allometry
def: "Measurement of the rate of growth of a part or parts of an organism relative to the growth of the whole organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15458
name: allomone
def: "Semiochemical that is produced by an organism inducing a response in an organism of another species that is favorable to the emitter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15459
name: analytic study
def: "Study designed to examine associations\, commonly putative or hypothesized causal relationships."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15460
name: anaplasia
def: "Loss of normal cell differentiation\, a feature characteristic of most malignancies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15461
name: anthracosis
def: "Form of pneumoconiosis caused by accumulation of anthracite carbon deposits in the lungs due to inhalation of smoke or coal dust."
synonym: "coal miners’ pneumoconiosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15462
name: anticoagulant
def: "Substance that prevents blood clotting \(e.g.\, warfarin\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15463
name: antidote
def: "Substance capable of specifically counteracting or reducing the effect of a potentially toxic substance in an organism by a relatively specific chemical or pharmacological action."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15464
name: antihistamine
def: "Substance that blocks or counteracts the action of histamine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15465
name: antimuscarinic
def: "n.\, Substance inhibiting or preventing the actions of muscarine and muscarine-like agents \(e.g.\, atropine\) on the muscarinic acetylcholine receptors.\nadj.\, Inhibiting or preventing the actions of muscarine and muscarine-like agents on the muscarinic acetylcholine receptors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15466
name: antinicotinic
def: "n.\, Substance inhibiting or preventing the actions of nicotine and nicotine-like agents \(e.g.\, suxamethonium chloride\) on the nicotinic acetylcholine receptors.\nadj.\, Inhibiting or preventing the actions of nicotine and nicotine-like agents on the nicotinic acetylcholine receptors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15467
name: antipyretic
def: "Substance that relieves or reduces fever."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15468
name: aphid
def: "Common name for a harmful plant parasite in the family Aphididae\, some species of which are vectors of plant virus diseases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15469
name: aplasia
def: "Lack of development of an organ or tissue\, or of the cellular products from an organ or tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15470
name: arboricide
def: "Substance intended to kill trees and shrubs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15471
name: area source
def: "Widespread origin of emissions."
synonym: "distributed source" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15472
name: area under the moment curve
def: "Area between a curve and the abscissa \(horizontal axis\) in a plot of \(concentration \\\(\\times\\\) time\) vs. time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15473
name: argyria
def: "Pathological condition characterized by gray-bluish or black pigmentation of tissues \(such as skin\, retina\, mucous membranes\, internal organs\) caused by the accumulation of metallic silver\, due to reduction of a silver compound which has entered the organism during \(prolonged\) administration or exposure."
synonym: "argyrosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15474
name: arseniasis
def: "Chronic arsenical poisoning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15476
name: arteriosclerosis
def: "Hardening and thickening of the walls of the arteries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15477
name: arthralgia
def: "Pain in a joint."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15478
name: arthropathy
def: "Disease of a joint."
synonym: "arthrosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15479
name: arthrosis
def: "Joint or articulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15480
name: asbestosis
def: "Form of pneumoconiosis caused by inhalation of asbestos fibers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15481
name: ascaricide
def: "Substance intended to kill roundworms \(Ascaridae\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15482
name: asphyxia
def: "Condition resulting from insufficient intake of oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15483
name: asphyxiant
def: "Substance that blocks the transport or use of oxygen by living organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15484
name: assimilation
def: "Uptake and incorporation of substances by a living organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15485
name: asthenia
def: "Weakness; lack or loss of strength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15486
name: astringent
def: "adj.\, Causing contraction\, usually locally after topical application.\nn.\, Substance causing cells to shrink\, thus causing tissue contraction or stoppage of secretions and discharges; such substances may be applied to skin to harden and protect it."
synonym: "adstringent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15487
name: atherosclerosis
def: "Pathological condition in which there is thickening\, hardening\, and loss of elasticity of the walls of blood vessels\, characterized by a variable combination of changes of the innermost layer consisting of local accumulation of lipids\, complex carbohydrates\, blood and blood components\, fibrous tissue\, and calcium deposits. In addition\, the outer layer becomes thickened and there is fatty degeneration of the middle layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15488
name: attractant
def: "Substance that attracts animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15489
name: autophagosome
def: "Membrane-bound body \(secondary lysosome\) in which parts of the cell are digested."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15490
name: autoxidation
def: "Reaction with dioxygen at moderate temperatures."
synonym: "autooxidation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15491
name: autoxidation
def: "Apparently spontaneous\, usually slow reaction of foodstuff components with dioxygen in an aerobic environment at moderate temperatures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15492
name: auxotroph
def: "Organism unable to synthesize an organic molecule which is required for its growth\: When the compound is given to the organism with the other nutrients it requires\, growth of the organism may occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15493
name: avicide
def: "Substance intended to kill birds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15494
name: back-mutation
def: "Process that reverses the effect of a mutation which had inactivated a gene; thus\, it restores the wild phenotype."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15495
name: bacterial artificial chromosome
def: "DNA vector into which large DNA fragments can be inserted and cloned in a bacterial host."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15496
name: bactericide
def: "Substance intended to kill bacteria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15497
name: bagassosis
def: "Lung disease caused by the inhalation of dust from sugar-cane residues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15498
name: basal lamina
def: "Triple-layered structure on which epithelium sits\: It consists of an electron-dense layer \(lamina densa\) between two electron-transparent layers \(lamina lucida\). The lamina densa is composed of type IV collagen\, and the lamina lucida contains the glycoprotein laminin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15499
name: Bateman function
def: "Equation expressing the build up and decay in concentration of a substance \(usually in plasma\) based on first-order uptake and elimination in a one-compartment model\, having the form \\\[C = \[fDk_\{\\rm\{a\}\}/V\(k_\{\\rm\{a\}\} - k_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\)\] \[\\exp\(-k_\{\\rm\{e\}\}t\) - \\exp\(-k_\{\\rm\{a\}\}t\)\] \\\]where \\\(C\\\) is the concentration and \\\(D\\\) the dose of the substance\, \\\(f\\\) the fraction absorbed\, and \\\(V\\\) the volume of distribution. \\\(k_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) are the first-order rate constants of uptake and elimination\, respectively\, and \\\(t\\\) is time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15500
name: benchmark guidance value
def: "Biological monitoring guidance value set at the \\\(90^\{\\rm\{th\}\}\\\) percentile of available biological monitoring results collected from a representative sample of workplaces with good occupational hygiene practices."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15501
name: benchmark response
def: "Response\, expressed as an excess of background\, at which a benchmark dose or benchmark concentration is set."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15502
name: benign
def: "Of a disease\, producing no persisting harmful effects.\nTumor that does not invade other tissues \(see metastasis \)\, having lost growth control but not positional control.Note\: Such a tumor is not carcinogenic but may cause mechanical damage to adjacent tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15503
name: beryllium disease
def: "Serious and usually permanent lung damage resulting from chronic inhalation of beryllium."
synonym: "berylliosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15504
name: biased sample
def: "Any sample that is not a random sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15505
name: bilirubin
def: "Orange-yellow pigment\, a breakdown product of heme-containing proteins \(hemoglobin\, myoglobin\, cytochromes\)\, which circulates in the blood plasma bound to albumin or as water-soluble glucuronide conjugates\, and is excreted in the bile by the liver."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15506
name: bioaccumulation potential
def: "Ability of living organisms to concentrate a substance obtained either directly from the environment or indirectly through its food."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15507
name: biochemical mechanism
def: "Reaction or series of reactions\, usually enzyme-catalyzed\, associated with a specific physiological event in a living organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15508
name: bioelimination
def: "Removal\, usually from the aqueous phase\, of a test substance in the presence of living organisms by biological processes supplemented by physicochemical reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15509
name: bioinactivation
def: "Metabolic conversion of a xenobiotic to a less toxic derivative."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15510
name: biokinetics
def: "Science of the movements involved in the distribution of substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15511
name: biological cycle
def: "Complete circulatory process through which a substance passes in the biosphere. It may involve transport through the various media \(air\, water\, soil\)\, followed by environmental transformation\, and carriage through various ecosystems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15512
name: biological preparation
def: "Compound derived from living organisms and their products for use in medicine or as a pesticide\, etc."
synonym: "biopreparation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15513
name: biological specimen
def: "Organ\, tissue \(including blood\)\, secretion\, or excretion product taken from an organism as a sample reflecting the state of the whole organism.\nOrganism taken as a sample reflecting the state of a population or their environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15514
name: biological warfare
def: "Military operations using any organism \(bacteria\, virus\, or other disease-causing organism\) or toxin found in nature\, to kill\, injure\, or incapacitate human beings\, animals\, or plants."
synonym: "germ warfare" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15515
name: biopsy
def: "Excision of a small piece of living tissue for microscopic or biochemical examination; usually performed to establish a diagnosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15516
name: biostatic
def: "Adjective applied to a substance that arrests the growth or multiplication of living organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15517
name: biota
def: "All living organisms as a totality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15518
name: 2\,3-bis\(sulfanyl\)propan-1-ol
def: "Metal chelator that has been used in the treatment of arsenic\, antimony\, gold\, mercury\, and lead poisoning."
synonym: "2" []
synonym: "3-dimercaptopropan-1-ol" []
synonym: "British anti-Lewisite" []
synonym: "dimercaprol" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15519
name: bolus
def: "Single dose of a substance\, originally a large pill.\nDose of a substance administered by a single rapid intravenous injection.\nConcentrated mass of food ready to be swallowed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15520
name: botanical pesticide
def: "Substance with activity against pests\, that is produced naturally within a plant and may act as a defense against predators."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15521
name: botulism
def: "Acute food poisoning caused by botulinum toxin produced in food by the bacterium Clostridium botulinum and characterized by muscle weakness and paralysis; disturbances of vision\, swallowing\, and speech; and a high mortality rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15522
name: brady-
def: "Prefix meaning slow as in bradycardia or bradypnoea."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15523
name: bradypnoea
def: "Abnormally slow breathing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15524
name: breathing zone
def: "Space within a radius of \\\(\\pu\{0.5 m\}\\\) from a person’s face."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15525
name: bronchoconstriction
def: "Narrowing of the air passages through the bronchi of the lungs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15526
name: bronchodilation
def: "Expansion of the air passages through the bronchi of the lungs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15527
name: bronchospasm
def: "Intermittent violent contraction of the air passages of the lungs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15528
name: builder
def: "Material that enhances or maintains the cleaning efficiency of a surfactant\, in a detergent\, principally by inactivating water hardness; complex phosphates \(especially sodium tripolyphosphate\, i.e.\, pentasodium triphosphate\)\, sodium carbonate\, and sodium silicate are the builders most commonly used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15529
name: byssinosis
def: "Pneumoconiosis caused by inhalation of dust and associated microbial contaminants and observed in cotton\, flax\, and hemp workers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15530
name: bystander exposure
def: "Liability of members of the general public to come in contact with substances arising from operations or processes carried out by other individuals in their vicinity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15531
name: cacosmia
def: "Imagined perception of vile odors\, including coprosmia \(smelling feces\) and necrosmia \(the smell of death\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15532
name: calcification
def: "Form of mineralization in which organic tissue becomes hardened by deposition of calcium salts within its substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15533
name: carbonylhemoglobin
def: "Compound that is formed between carbon monoxide and hemoglobin in the blood of animals and humans and is incapable of transporting oxygen."
synonym: "carboxyhemoglobin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15534
name: carcinogenicity test
def: "Long-term \(chronic\) test designed to detect any possible carcinogenic effect of a test substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15535
name: cardiotoxic
def: "Chemically harmful to the cells of the heart."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15536
name: carrier-linked prodrug
def: "Compound that contains a temporary linkage between a given active substance and a transient carrier group\, the latter producing improved physicochemical or pharmacokinetic properties and easily removable in vivo."
synonym: "carrier prodrug" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15537
name: carrier substance
def: "Substance that binds to another substance and transfers it from one site to another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15538
name: case cohort study
def: "Variant of the case control study in which the controls are drawn from the same cohort as the cases but are identified before the cases develop; some of the controls may later become cases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15539
name: case control study
def: "Study that starts with the identification of persons with the disease \(or other outcome variable\) of interest\, and a suitable control \(comparison\, reference\) group of persons without the disease. The relationship of an attribute to the disease is examined by comparing the diseased and non-diseased with regard to how frequently the attribute is present or\, if quantitative\, the levels of the attribute\, in the two groups."
synonym: "case comparison study" []
synonym: "case compeer study" []
synonym: "case history study" []
synonym: "case referent study" []
synonym: "retrospective study" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15540
name: catatonia
def: "Behavior marked by excessive and sometimes violent motor activity and excitement\, or by generalized inhibition or stupor\, that may occur in schizophrenia\, mood disorders\, or organic brain syndromes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15541
name: cell cycle
def: "Regulated biochemical steps that cells go through involving DNA replication and cell division\, usually depicted as a sequential cyclical series of events."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15542
name: cell-mediated hypersensitivity
def: "State in which an individual reacts with allergic effects caused by the reaction of antigen-specific T-lymphocytes following exposure to a certain substance \(allergen\) after having been exposed previously to the same substance or chemical group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15543
name: cell proliferation
def: "Rapid increase in cell number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15544
name: cell strain
def: "Cells having specific properties or markers derived from a primary culture or cell line."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15545
name: censored data
def: "Sample observations for which the complete distribution is not known."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15546
name: certified reference material
def: "Reference material\, accompanied by documentation issued by an authoritative body and referring to valid procedures used to obtain a specified property value with uncertainty and traceability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15547
name: chain of custody
def: "Sequence of responsibility for a substance from the manufacturer to the distributor\, to the user\, or to the person\(s\) ultimately responsible for waste disposal. This term is also used in controlled transmission of samples from collection to analysis\, especially of samples of materials used for medico-legal or forensic purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15548
name: chelation therapy
def: "Treatment with a chelating agent to enhance the elimination or reduce the toxicity of a metal ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15549
name: chemesthesis
def: "Sensations that arise when chemical compounds activate receptor mechanisms for other effectors\, such as light\, pain\, pressure\, and heat\, in the eye\, skin\, nose\, mouth\, and throat; for example\, the burning feeling from chili pepper\, the cooling from the menthol in mouthwash\, and the stinging feeling of carbonation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15550
name: chemical conversion
def: "Change from one chemical species to another."
synonym: "conversion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15551
name: chemical safety
def: "Practical certainty that there will be no exposure of organisms to toxic amounts of any substance or group of substances\: This implies attaining an acceptably low risk of exposure to potentially toxic substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15552
name: chemical warfare
def: "Military operations using the toxic properties of chemical agents to kill\, injure\, or incapacitate human beings\, animals\, or plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15553
name: chemophobia
def: "Irrational fear of chemicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15554
name: chemosis
def: "Chemically induced swelling around the eye caused by edema of the conjunctiva."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15555
name: chemosteriliser
def: "Substance used to sterilize mites\, insects\, rodents\, or other animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15556
name: cholinesterase inhibitor
def: "Substance that inhibits the action of acetylcholinesterase \(EC 3.1.1.7\) and related enzymes which catalyze the hydrolysis of choline esters\: Such a substance causes hyperactivity in parasympathetic nerves."
synonym: "anticholinesterase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15557
name: chromatin
def: "Stainable complex of DNA and proteins present in the nucleus of a eukaryotic cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15558
name: chromosomal aberration
def: "Abnormality of chromosome number or structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15559
name: chronic toxicity test
def: "Study in which organisms are observed during the greater part of the life span and in which exposure to the test agent takes place over the whole observation time or a substantial part thereof."
synonym: "long-term toxicity test" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15560
name: chronotoxicology
def: "Study of the influence of biological rhythms on the toxicity of substances or of the influence of a toxicant on biological rhythms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15561
name: ciguateratoxin poisoning
def: "Serious illness caused by eating carnivorous fish such as snappers and barracuda that have become contaminated by toxins produced by the microalga\, Gambierdiscus toxicus; gastrointestinal symptoms may accompany a wide variety of neurological symptoms\, including ataxia\, vertigo\, flaccid paralysis\, respiratory arrest\, and reversed perception of hot and cold; the neurological symptoms may persist for many years."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15562
name: circadian
def: "Relating to or exhibiting an approximately \\\(\\pu\{24\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) periodicity."
synonym: "nychthemeral" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15563
name: circulation of substances
def: "Movement of xenobiotic substances in the environment with air flow\, river current\, sediment flow\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15564
name: cirrhosis
def: "Liver disease defined by histological examination and characterized by increased fibrous tissue\, abnormal morphological and physiological changes such as loss of functional liver cells\, and increased resistance to blood flow through the liver \(portal hypertension\).\nChronic interstitial inflammation and fibrosis of an organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15565
name: cirrhotic
def: "Describing an organ showing cirrhosis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15566
name: clastogenesis
def: "Formation \(or generation\) of chromosomal breaks and \(or\) consequent gain\, loss\, or rearrangement of pieces of chromosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15567
name: clinical toxicology
def: "Scientific study involving research\, education\, prevention\, and treatment of diseases caused by substances such as drugs and toxins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15568
name: clonic
def: "Pertaining to alternate muscular contraction and relaxation in rapid succession."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15569
name: cluster sampling
def: "A method of sampling in which the population is divided into aggregates \(or clusters\) of items bound together in a certain manner. A sample of these clusters is taken at random\, and all the items which constitute them are included in the sample.\nA sampling method in which each unit selected is a group of persons \(all persons in a city block\, a family\, etc.\) rather than an individual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15570
name: cocarcinogen
def: "Chemical\, physical\, or biological factor that intensifies the effect of a carcinogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15571
name: Codex Alimentarius
def: "Collection of internationally adopted food standards drawn up by the Codex Alimentarius Commission\, the principal body implementing the joint FAO/WHO Food Standards Program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15572
name: cohort
def: "Component of the population born during a particular period and identified by period of birth so that its characteristics \(such as causes of death and numbers still living\) can be ascertained as it enters successive time and age periods. The term 'cohort' has broadened to describe any designated group of persons followed or traced over a period of time\, as in the term cohort study \(prospective study\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15573
name: cohort analysis
def: "Tabulation and analysis of morbidity or mortality rates in relationship to the ages of a specific group of people \(cohort\)\, identified by their birth period\, and followed as they pass through different ages during part or all of their life span. In certain circumstances such as studies of migrant populations\, cohort analysis may be performed according to duration of residence in a country rather than year of birth\, in order to relate health or mortality experience to duration of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15574
name: cohort study
def: "Analytic study in epidemiology in which subsets of a defined population can be identified who are\, have been\, or in the future may be exposed or not exposed\, or exposed in different degrees\, to a factor or factors hypothesized to influence the probability of occurrence of a given disease or other outcome. The main feature of the method is observation of a large population for a prolonged period \(years\)\, with comparison of incidence rates of the given disease in groups that differ in exposure levels."
synonym: "concurrent study" []
synonym: "follow-up study" []
synonym: "incidence study" []
synonym: "longitudinal study" []
synonym: "prospective study" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15575
name: combined effect of poisons
def: "Simultaneous or successive effect of two or more poisons on the organism by the same route of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15576
name: comet assay
def: "Means of measuring DNA damage\, particularly DNA strand breaks. A cell is embedded in agar and exposed to a DNA-damaging agent. The cell is then permeabilized with a detergent\, and an electric field is applied. If the cell’s genomic DNA has been broken into small fragments\, these fragments move out of the cell by electrophoresis and form a streak or 'tail' leading away from the cell which looks like a comet."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15577
name: comparative genomics
def: "Study of the relationship of genome structure and function across different biological species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15578
name: compartmentalise
def: "Separate into compartments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15579
name: competent authority
def: "In the context of European Community Council Directive 79/831/EEC\, the Sixth Amendment to the European Community Council Directive 67/548/EEC relating to the Classification\, Packaging and Labeling of Dangerous Substances\, official government organization or group receiving and evaluating notifications of new substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15580
name: competent bacteria
def: "Culture of bacteria \(or yeast\) treated in such a way that their ability to take up DNA molecules without transduction or conjugation has been enhanced."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15581
name: complete mineralisation
def: "Complete breakdown of a complex organic compound to carbon dioxide\, water\, oxides\, and oxidative inorganic products such as nitrate or sulfate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15582
name: comprehensive effect of poisons
def: "Simultaneous or successive effect made on an organism by poisons entering from different media\, air\, water\, or food or through the skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15583
name: computational toxicology
def: "Application of mathematical and computer models to predict adverse effects and to better understand the mechanism\(s\) through which a given chemical causes harm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15584
name: concentration–effect curve
def: "Graph of the relation between exposure concentration and the magnitude of the resultant biological change."
synonym: "exposure–effect curve" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15585
name: concentration–effect relationship
def: "Association between exposure concentration and the resultant magnitude of the continuously graded change produced\, either in an individual or in a population."
synonym: "exposure–effect relationship" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15586
name: concurrent validity
def: "Measurement and its criterion refer to the same point in time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15587
name: confounding
def: "Situation in which the effects of two processes are not distinguishable from one another\: The distortion of the apparent effect of an exposure on risk brought about by the association of other factors which can influence the outcome.\nRelationship between the effects of two or more causal factors as observed in a set of data\, such that it is not logically possible to separate the contribution which any single causal factor has made to an effect.\nSituation in which a measure of the effect of an exposure on risk is distorted because of the association of exposure with other factor\(s\) which influence the outcome under study."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15588
name: confounding variable
def: "Changing factor that can cause or prevent the outcome of interest\, is not an intermediate variable\, and is associated with the factor under investigation."
synonym: "confounder" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15589
name: conjunctiva
def: "Mucous membrane that covers the eyeball and lines the under-surface of the eyelid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15590
name: conjunctivitis
def: "Inflammation of the conjunctiva."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15591
name: conservative assessment of risk
def: "Assessment of risk that assumes the worst possible case scenario and therefore gives the highest possible value for risk\: Risk management decisions based on this value will maximize safety."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15592
name: construct validity
def: "Extent to which a measurement corresponds to theoretical concepts \(constructs\) concerning the phenomenon under study; for example\, if on theoretical grounds\, the phenomenon should change with age\, a measurement with construct validity would reflect such a change."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15593
name: contact poison
def: "Chemical that injures the target organism through physical contact and skin absorption rather than through ingestion or inhalation.\nPesticide \(herbicide\) that causes injury to only the plant tissue to which it is applied or which is not appreciably translocated within plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15594
name: containment
def: "Process by which possible release\, discharge\, or spill of a toxic substance during normal use or after an accident is prevented by appropriate action."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15595
name: content validity
def: "Extent to which the measurement incorporates the domain of the phenomenon under study; for example\, a measurement of functional health status should embrace activities of daily living\, occupational\, family\, and social functioning\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15596
name: contraindication
def: "Any condition that renders some particular line of treatment improper or undesirable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15597
name: control group
def: "Selected subjects of study\, identified as a rule before a study is done\, which comprises humans\, animals\, or other species who do not have the disease\, intervention\, procedure\, or whatever is being studied\, but in all other respects are as nearly identical to the test group as possible."
synonym: "comparison group" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15598
name: matched control
def: "Control \(individual or group or case\) selected to be similar to a study individual or group\, or case\, in specific characteristics\: some commonly used matching variables are age\, sex\, race\, and socioeconomic status."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15599
name: core grade
def: "Quality rating\, based on standard evaluation criteria established by the U.S. Office of Pesticide Programs regulatory agencies\, given to toxicological studies after submission by registrants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15600
name: count mean diameter
def: "Mean of the diameters of all particles in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15601
name: count median diameter
def: "Calculated diameter in a population of particles in a gas or liquid phase above which there are as many particles with larger diameters as there are particles below it with smaller diameters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15602
name: crepitations
def: "Abnormal respiratory sounds heard on auscultation of the chest\, produced by passage of air through passages which contain secretion or exudate or which are constricted by spasm or a thickening of their walls."
synonym: "crackles" []
synonym: "râles" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15603
name: criterion
def: "Validated set of data used as a basis for judgment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15604
name: criterion validity
def: "Extent to which the measurement correlates with an external criterion of the phenomenon under study."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15605
name: critical concentration
def: "Concentration of a substance at and above which adverse functional changes\, reversible or irreversible\, occur in a cell or an organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15606
name: critical dose
def: "Dose of a substance at and above which adverse functional changes\, reversible or irreversible\, occur in a cell or an organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15607
name: critical effect
def: "For deterministic effects\, the first adverse effect that appears when the threshold \(critical\) concentration or dose is reached in the critical organ\: Adverse effects with no defined threshold concentration are regarded as critical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15608
name: critical end-point
def: "Toxic effect used by the USEPA as the basis for a reference dose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15609
name: critical group
def: "Part of a target population most in need of protection because it is most susceptible to a given toxicant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15610
name: critical organ
def: "Organ that first attains the critical concentration of a substance and exhibits the critical effect under specified circumstances of exposure and for a given population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15611
name: critical organ concentration
def: "Mean concentration of a substance in the critical organ at the time the substance reaches its critical concentration in the most sensitive type of cell in the organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15612
name: cross-sectional study
def: "Study that examines the relationship between diseases \(or other health-related characteristics\) and other variables of interest as they exist in a defined population at one particular time."
synonym: "disease frequency survey" []
synonym: "prevalence study" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15613
name: cumulative death rate
def: "Proportion of a defined group that dies within the specified time period \(e.g.\, month\, year\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15614
name: cumulative effect
def: "Overall change that occurs after repeated doses of a substance or radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15615
name: cumulative incidence
def: "Number or proportion of individuals in a group who experience the onset of a health-related event during a specified time interval."
synonym: "incidence proportion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15616
name: cumulative incidence rate
def: "Proportion of the cumulative incidence to the total population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15617
name: cumulative incidence ratio
def: "Value obtained by dividing the cumulative incidence rate in the exposed population by the cumulative incidence rate in the unexposed population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15618
name: cumulative median lethal dose
def: "Estimate of the total administered amount of a substance that is associated with the death of half a population of animals when the substance is administered repeatedly in doses which are generally fractions of the median lethal dose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15619
name: cumulative risk
def: "Probability of a common harmful effect associated with concurrent exposure by all relevant pathways and routes of exposure to a group of substances that share a common chemical mechanism of toxicity.\nTotal probability of a harmful effect over time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15620
name: cyanotoxin
def: "Toxin produced by Cyanobacteria\, sometimes called blue–green algae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15621
name: cyclooxygenase specific inhibitors
def: "Substances\, such as aspirin and ibuprofen\, that block the activity of cyclooxygenase \(COX\)\, an enzyme that is responsible for the formation of prostanoids \(including prostaglandins\, prostacyclin\, and thromboxane\); inhibition of COX can provide relief from inflammation and pain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15622
name: cytochrome P420
def: "Inactive derivative of cytochrome P450 found in microsomal preparations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15623
name: cytochrome P448
def: "Obsolete term for cytochrome P450 I\, A1\, and A2\, one of the major families of the cytochromes P450 hemoproteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15624
name: cytoplasm
def: "Fundamental substance or matrix of the cell \(within the plasma membrane\) which surrounds the nucleus\, endoplasmic reticulum\, mitochondria\, and other organelles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15625
name: decipol
def: "Unit of perceived air quality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15626
name: decompensation
def: "Explicit pathophysiological changes following compensation for adverse effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15627
name: decontamination
def: "Process of rendering harmless \(by neutralization\, elimination\, removal\, etc.\) a potentially toxic substance in the natural environment\, laboratory areas\, the workplace\, other indoor areas\, clothes\, food\, water\, sewage\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15628
name: dehydrogenase
def: "Enzyme that catalyzes oxidation of compounds by removing hydrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15629
name: delayed effect
def: "Consequence occurring after a latent period following the end of exposure to a toxic substance or other harmful environmental factor."
synonym: "latent effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15630
name: dental fluorosis
def: "Tooth enamel malformations due to excessive fluoride exposure during dental development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15631
name: deoxyribonucleic acid cloning
def: "Replication of DNA sequences ligated into a suitable vector in an appropriate host organism."
synonym: "DNA cloning" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15632
name: deoxyribonucleic acid repair
def: "Restoration of the molecular structure of DNA after it has been damaged by a chemical or physical agent\: This may involve direct DNA damage reversal\, base excision repair\, nucleotide excision repair\, mismatch repair\, or double-strand break repair"
synonym: "DNA repair" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15633
name: dependence
def: "A psychic craving for a drug or other substance that may or may not be accompanied by a physical dependency.\nReliance on a drug or other substance to maintain health."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15634
name: depilatory
def: "Substance causing loss of hair."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15635
name: dermal
def: "Pertaining to the skin."
synonym: "cutaneous" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15636
name: dermal irritation
def: "Skin reaction resulting from a single or multiple exposure to a physical or chemical entity at the same site\, characterized by the presence of inflammation; it may result in cell death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15637
name: descriptive epidemiology
def: "Study of the occurrence of disease or other health-related characteristics in populations\, including general observations concerning the relationship of disease to basic characteristics such as age\, sex\, race\, occupation\, and social class; it may also be concerned with geographic location. The major characteristics in descriptive epidemiology can be classified under the headings\: individuals\, time\, and place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15638
name: desquamation
def: "Shedding of an outer layer of skin in scales or shreds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15639
name: detriment
def: "Estimated measure of the expected harm or loss associated with an adverse event\, usually in a manner chosen to facilitate meaningful addition over different events. It is generally the integrated product of arbitrary values of risk and hazard and is often expressed in terms such as costs in U.S. dollars\, loss in expected years of life or loss in productivity\, and is needed for numerical exercises such as cost-benefit analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15640
name: developmental toxicity
def: "Adverse effects on the developing organism \(including structural abnormality\, altered growth\, or functional deficiency or death\) resulting from exposure through conception\, gestation \(including organogenesis\)\, and postnatally up to the time of sexual maturation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15641
name: diaphoresis
def: "Profuse perspiration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15642
name: discharge standard
def: "Maximum amount of a pollutant released from a given source to a specified medium which is acceptable under specified circumstances."
synonym: "discharge release limit" []
synonym: "effluent standard" []
synonym: "emission standard" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15643
name: discordance
def: "Any difference in a character between individuals due to genetic differences such as may occur in dizygotic twins\, or between matched pairs in a case cohort study."
synonym: "genetic discordance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15644
name: disease
def: "Literally\, dis-ease\, lack of ease; pathological condition that presents a group of symptoms peculiar to it and which establishes the condition as an abnormal entity different from other normal or pathological body states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15645
name: disposition
def: "Natural tendency shown by an individual or group of individuals\, including any tendency to acquisition of specific diseases\, often due to hereditary factors.\nTotal of the processes of absorption of a chemical into the circulatory systems\, distribution throughout the body\, biotransformation\, and excretion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15646
name: dissipation
def: "Reduction in the amount of a pesticide or other compound that has been applied to plants\, soil\, etc. \(used when it is not clear whether this is by mineralization degradation\, binding\, or leaching\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15647
name: distribution volume
def: "Theoretical volume of a body compartment throughout which a substance is calculated to be distributed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15648
name: diuresis
def: "Excretion of urine\, especially in excess."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15649
name: diuretic
def: "Agent that increases urine production."
synonym: "micturitic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15650
name: dominant half life
def: "Half life of a fraction of a substance in a specific organ or compartment if it defines approximately the overall clearance rate for that substance at a specific time point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15651
name: dominant lethal mutation
def: "Genetic change occurring in a germ cell that does not cause dysfunction of the gamete but which is lethal to the fertilized egg or developing embryo which develops from it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15652
name: duplicate portion sampling method
def: "Study in which test persons consume their ordinary diet but\, for each meal\, they prepare for subsequent analysis a duplicate portion of all food as prepared\, served\, and consumed."
synonym: "duplicate diet study" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15653
name: duplicate samples
def: "Two samples taken under the same or comparable conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15654
name: dysfunction
def: "Abnormal\, impaired\, or incomplete functioning of an organism\, organ\, tissue\, or cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15655
name: ectoparasiticide
def: "Substance intended to kill parasites living on the exterior of the host."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15656
name: elimination half life
def: "Period taken for the plasma concentration of a substance to decrease by half."
synonym: "elimination half time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15657
name: eliminator
def: "Substance that contributes to the elimination of a poison from an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15658
name: embryotoxicity
def: "Production by a substance of toxic effects in progeny in the first period of pregnancy between conception and the fetal stage.\nAny toxic effect on the conceptus as a result of prenatal exposure during the embryonic stages of development\: These effects may include malformations and variations\, malfunctions\, altered growth\, prenatal death\, and altered postnatal function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15659
name: embryotropic effect
def: "Change in the embryo and the regulation of its development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15660
name: emission and exposure control
def: "Technical and administrative procedures and specifications applied for the monitoring\, reduction\, or elimination of emissions from a source or exposure to a target."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15661
name: endocon
def: "Portion of a conjugated metabolite that is derived from a natural product \(such as a sugar\, amino acid\, or other organic acid\) of the metabolizing organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15662
name: endoplasmic reticulum
def: "Intracellular complex of membranes in which proteins and lipids\, as well as molecules for export\, are synthesized and in which the biotransformation reactions of the monooxygenase enzyme systems occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15663
name: endothelial
def: "Pertaining to the layer of flat cells lining the inner surface of blood and lymphatic vessels\, and the surface lining of serous and synovial membranes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15664
name: enteritis
def: "Intestinal inflammation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15665
name: enterohepatic circulation
def: "Cyclical process involving intestinal reabsorption of a substance that has been excreted through the bile\, followed by transfer back to the liver\, making it available for biliary excretion again."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15666
name: environment
def: "Aggregate\, at a given moment\, of all external conditions and influences to which a system under study is subjected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15667
name: environmental damage
def: "Adverse effects to the natural environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15668
name: environmental exposure level
def: "Level \(concentration or amount or a time integral of either\) of a substance to which an organism or other component of the environment is exposed in its natural surroundings."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15669
name: environmental health
def: "Human welfare and its influence by the environment\, including technical and administrative measures for improving the human environment from a health point of view."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15670
name: environmental health impact assessment
def: "Estimate of the adverse effects to health or risks likely to follow from a proposed or expected environmental change or development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15671
name: environmental health criteria documents
def: "Critical publications of IPCS containing reviews of methodologies and existing knowledge—expressed\, if possible\, in quantitative terms—of selected substances \(or groups of substances\) on identifiable\, immediate\, and long-term effects on human health and welfare."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15672
name: environmental hygiene
def: "Practical control measures used to improve the basic environmental conditions affecting human health\, for example\, clean water supply\, human and animal waste disposal\, protection of food from biological contamination\, and housing conditions\, all of which are concerned with the quality of the human environment."
synonym: "environmental sanitation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15673
name: environmental medicine
def: "Specialty devoted to the prevention and management of environmentally induced injury\, illness\, and disability\, and the promotion of the health of individuals\, families\, and communities by ensuring a healthy environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15674
name: environmental protection
def: "Actions taken to prevent or minimize adverse effects to the natural environment.\nComplex of measures including monitoring of environmental pollution\, development and practice of environmental protection principles \(legal\, technical\, and hygienic\)\, including risk assessment\, risk management\, and risk communication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15675
name: epigastric
def: "Pertaining to the upper-middle region of the abdomen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15676
name: epileptiform
def: "Occurring in severe or sudden spasms\, as in convulsion or epilepsy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15677
name: epithelioma
def: "Any tumor derived from epithelium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15678
name: eschar
def: "Slough or dry scab on an area of skin that has been burnt."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15679
name: estimated daily intake
def: "Prediction of the daily intake of a residue of a potentially harmful agent based on the most realistic estimation of the residue levels in food and the best available food consumption data for a specific population\: Residue levels are estimated taking into account known uses of the agent\, the range of contaminated commodities\, the proportion of a commodity treated\, and the quantity of home-grown or imported commodities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15680
name: estimated environmental concentration
def: "Predicted concentration of a substance\, typically a pesticide\, within an environmental compartment based on estimates of quantities released\, discharge patterns\, and inherent disposition of the substance \(fate and distribution\) as well as the nature of the specific receiving ecosystems."
synonym: "predicted exposure concentration (PEC)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15681
name: estimated exposure concentration
def: "Measured or calculated amount or mass concentration of a substance to which an organism is likely to be exposed\, considering exposure by all sources and routes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15682
name: estimated exposure dose
def: "Measured or calculated dose of a substance to which an organism is likely to be exposed\, considering exposure by all sources and routes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15683
name: estimated maximum daily intake
def: "Prediction of the maximum daily intake of a residue of a potentially harmful agent based on assumptions of average food consumption per person and maximum residues in the edible portion of a commodity\, corrected for the reduction or increase in residues resulting from preparation\, cooking\, or commercial processing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15684
name: etiology
def: "Science dealing with the cause or origin of disease.\nIn individuals\, the cause or origin of disease."
synonym: "aetiology" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15685
name: eukaryote
def: "Cell or organism with the genetic material packed in a membrane-surrounded structurally discrete nucleus and with well-developed cell organelles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15686
name: European Inventory of Existing Chemical Substances
def: "List of all substances supplied either singly or as components in preparations to persons in a Member State of the European Community on any occasion between 1 January 1971 and 18 September 1981."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15687
name: excess lifetime risk
def: "Additional or excess risk incurred over the lifetime of an individual by exposure to a toxic substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15688
name: exchange transfusion
def: "Method of active artificial elimination of toxicity consisting in complete replacement of blood of the patient by donor blood."
synonym: "blood substitution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15689
name: excretion rate
def: "Amount of substance and \(or\) its metabolites that is excreted divided by time of excretion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15690
name: exocon
def: "Portion of a conjugated metabolite that is derived from the parent molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15691
name: exogenous
def: "Resulting from causes or derived from materials external to an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15692
name: exothelium
def: "Layer of flattened epithelial cells external to an organ or tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15693
name: expected environmental concentration
def: "Calculated concentrations of a substance\, typically a pesticide\, in various environmental compartments based on calculations using maximum-exposure scenarios."
synonym: "expected exposure concentration (EEC)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15694
name: explant
def: "Living tissue removed from its normal environment and transferred to an artificial medium for growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15695
name: exposed
def: "Subject to a factor that is under study in the environment\, for instance\, an environmental hazard."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15696
name: exposed group
def: "People \(or other organisms\) who have been exposed to a supposed cause of a disease or health state of interest\, or possess a characteristic that is a determinant of the health outcome of interest."
synonym: "exposed" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15697
name: exposure limit
def: "General term defining an administrative substance concentration or intensity of exposure that should not be exceeded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15698
name: exposure ratio
def: "In a case control study\, value obtained by dividing the rate at which persons in the case group are exposed to a risk factor \(or to a protective factor\) by the rate at which persons in the control group are exposed to the risk factor \(or to the protective factor\) of interest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15699
name: exposure surface
def: "Surface on a target where a substance \(e.g.\, a pesticide\) is present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15700
name: exposure test
def: "Determination of the level\, concentration\, or uptake of a potentially toxic compound and \(or\) its metabolite\(s\) in biological samples from an organism \(blood\, urine\, hair\, etc.\) and the interpretation of the results to estimate the absorbed dose or degree of environmental pollution; or the measuring of biochemical effects\, usually not direct adverse effects of the substance\, and relating them to the quantity of substance absorbed\, or to its concentration in the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15701
name: external validity
def: "Generalizability of the results of a particular study\, beyond the limits of the population actually studied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15702
name: extracellular space
def: "Volume within a tissue\, outside cells\, and excluding vascular and lymphatic space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15703
name: extracellular volume
def: "Volume of fluid outside the cells but within the outer surface of an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15704
name: extraction ratio
def: "Amount of substance extracted from a source divided by the total contained within the source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15705
name: extra risk
def: "Probability that an agent produces an observed response\, as distinguished from the probability that the response is caused by a spontaneous event unrelated to the agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15706
name: extraneous residue limit
def: "Refers to a pesticide residue or contaminant arising from environmental sources \(including former agricultural uses\) other than the use of a pesticide or contaminant substance directly or indirectly on the commodity. It is the maximum concentration of a pesticide residue or contaminant that is recommended by the Codex Alimentarius Commission to be legally permitted or recognized as acceptable in or on food\, agricultural commodity\, or animal feed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15707
name: extrapyramidal movement disorders
def: "Involuntary movement disorders mediated by signaling through neurons outside the pyramidal \(corticospinal\) tract\, often describing side effects of psychiatric medications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15708
name: fertility
def: "Ability to conceive and to produce offspring\: For litter-bearing species\, the number of offspring per litter is used as a measure of fertility."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15709
name: fertility toxicant
def: "Produces abnormalities of male or female reproductive functions or impairs reproductive capacity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15710
name: fetotoxicity
def: "Toxicity to the fetus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15711
name: fibrosis
def: "Abnormal formation of fibrous tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15712
name: fiducial limit
def: "Form of confidence limit given as a stated probability\, for example\, \\\(P = \\pu\{0.95\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15713
name: first-order chemical reaction
def: "Chemical reaction where the initial rate is directly proportional to the concentration of one of the reactants.\nAny process in which a variable decreases with time at a constant fractional amount."
synonym: "first-order reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15714
name: fixed dose procedure
def: "Acute toxicity test in which a substance is tested initially at a small number \(3 or 4\) predefined doses to identify which produces evident toxicity without lethality\: The test may be repeated at one or more higher or lower defined discriminating doses to satisfy the criteria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15715
name: fluorosis
def: "Adverse effects of fluoride\, as in dental or skeletal fluorosis."
synonym: "fluoridosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15716
name: foci
def: "Small groups of cells distinguishable\, in appearance or histochemically\, from the surrounding tissue\: indicative of an early stage of a lesion that may lead to the formation of a neoplastic nodule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15717
name: food additive
def: "Any substance\, not normally consumed as a food by itself and not normally used as a typical ingredient of a given food\, whether or not it has nutritive value\, that is added intentionally to food for a technological \(including organoleptic\) purpose in the manufacture\, processing\, preparation\, treatment\, packing\, packaging\, transport\, or holding of the food. Addition results\, or may be reasonably expected to result \(directly or indirectly\)\, in the substance or its byproducts becoming a component of\, or otherwise affecting\, the characteristics of the food to which it is added."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15718
name: food allergy
def: "Hypersensitivity reaction to substances in the diet to which an individual has previously been sensitized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15719
name: food intolerance
def: "Physiologically based reproducible\, unpleasant \(adverse\) reaction to a specific food or food ingredient that is not immunologically based."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15720
name: food web
def: "Network of food chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15721
name: forced diuresis
def: "Method of stimulating diuresis based on performing hydrational therapy\, sometimes with parallel introduction of diuretics\, with the aim of achieving increased clearance of a toxic substance in urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15722
name: founder effect
def: "Changes in allelic frequencies that occur when a small group is separated from a large population and establishes a colony in a new location."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15723
name: fumigant
def: "Substance that is vaporized in order to kill or repel pests."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15724
name: fungus preparation
def: "Substance obtained from fungi that has an insecticidal effect reflecting the pathogenicity of the fungi for insects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15725
name: gametocide
def: "Substance intended to kill gametes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15726
name: gastroenteritis
def: "Inflammation of the stomach and intestine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15727
name: gastrointestinal
def: "Pertaining to or communicating with the stomach and intestine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15728
name: gavage
def: "Administration of materials directly into the stomach by esophageal intubation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15729
name: gene map
def: "Map showing the positions in the genome of genes or other genetic markers\, either relative to each other or as a physical map of absolute distances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15730
name: generally regarded as safe
def: "Phrase used to describe the USFDA philosophy that justifies approval of food additives that may not meet the usual test criteria for safety but have been used extensively and have not demonstrated that they cause any harm to consumers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15731
name: genetically modified organism
def: "Bacterium\, plant\, or animal whose DNA has been deliberately altered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15732
name: genetic polymorphism
def: "Existence of inter-individual differences in DNA sequences coding for one specific gene\, giving rise to different functional and \(or\) morphological traits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15733
name: genetic susceptibility
def: "Predisposition to a particular disease or sensitivity to a substance due to the presence of a specific allele or combination of alleles in an individual’s genome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15734
name: genetic toxicology
def: "Study of chemically or physically induced changes to the structure of DNA\, including epigenetic phenomena or mutations that may or may not be heritable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15735
name: germ-free animal
def: "Animal grown under sterile conditions in the period of postnatal development\: Such animals are usually obtained by Caesarean operation and kept in special sterile boxes in which there are no viable microorganisms \(sterile air\, food\, and water are supplied\)."
synonym: "axenic animal" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15736
name: germinal aplasia
def: "Complete failure of gonad development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15737
name: glomerular
def: "Pertaining to a tuft or cluster\, as of a plexus of capillary blood vessels or nerve fibers\, especially referring to the capillaries of the glomerular of the kidney."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15738
name: glomerular filtration
def: "Formation of an ultrafiltrate of the blood occurring in the glomerulus of the kidney."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15739
name: glomerular filtration rate
def: "Volume of ultrafiltrate formed in the kidney tubules from the blood passing through the glomerular capillaries divided by time of filtration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15740
name: glue-sniffing
def: "Inhalation of solvent vapor from plastic cements or other adhesives in order to become intoxicated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15741
name: gnotobiota
def: "Specifically and entirely known microfauna and microflora of a specially reared laboratory animal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15742
name: gnotobiote
def: "Specially reared laboratory animal whose microflora and microfauna are specifically known in their entirety."
synonym: "gnotobiont" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15743
name: goiter
def: "Noncancerous enlargement of the thyroid gland\, visible as a swelling at the front of the neck\, that is often associated with iodine deficiency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15744
name: goitrogen
def: "Any substance \(such as thiouracil\) that induces the formation of a goiter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15745
name: gonadotropic
def: "Pertaining to effects on sex glands and on the systems that regulate them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15746
name: good agricultural practice
def: "Nationally authorized safe uses of pesticides under actual conditions necessary for effective and reliable pest control."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15747
name: graminicide
def: "Pesticide \(herbicide\) used for the control of weedy grasses \(Gramineae\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15748
name: ground treatment of plants
def: "Dusting or spraying of plants with pesticides by hand\, by special machines\, or by apparatus fixed to tractors or driven by them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15749
name: guideline for exposure limits
def: "Scientifically judged quantitative value \(a concentration or number\) of an environmental constituent that ensures aesthetically pleasing air\, water\, or food and from which no adverse effect is expected concerning noncarcinogenic endpoints\, or that gives an acceptably low estimate of lifetime cancer risk from those substances which are proven human carcinogens or carcinogens with at least limited evidence of human carcinogenicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15750
name: guideline value
def: "Quantitative measure \(a concentration or a number\) of a constituent of an environmental medium that ensures aesthetically pleasing air\, water\, or food and does not result in a significant risk to the user."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15751
name: guides to air quality
def: "Sets of atmospheric concentrations and exposure times that are associated with specific effects of varying degrees of pollution on humans\, animals\, vegetation\, and the environment in general."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15752
name: guides to environmental quality
def: "Sets of concentrations\, numbers and exposure times that are associated with the specific effects of factors in environmental media on humans\, animals\, vegetation\, and the environment in general."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15753
name: harmful occupational factor
def: "Component of the work environment\, the effect of which on a worker under certain conditions leads to ill health or reduction of working ability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15754
name: harmful substance
def: "Substance that\, following contact with an organism\, can cause ill health or adverse effects either at the time of exposure or later in the life of the present and future generations."
synonym: "noxious substance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15755
name: hazard communication standard
def: "U.S. OSHA standard requiring all employers to inform employees of the hazard of substances in the workplace and the steps necessary to avoid harm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15756
name: hazardous production factor
def: "Production factor the effect of which on a worker under certain conditions results in injury or some impairment of health."
synonym: "hazard at work" []
synonym: "hazardous occupational factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15757
name: health
def: "State of complete physical\, mental\, and social well-being\, and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity.\nState of dynamic balance in which an individual’s or a group’s capacity to cope with the circumstances of living is at an optimal level.\nState characterized by anatomical\, physiological\, and psychological integrity; ability to perform personally valued family\, work\, and community roles; ability to deal with physical\, biological\, psychological\, and social stress; a feeling of well-being; and freedom from the risk of disease and untimely death.\nIn ecology\, a sustainable steady state in which humans and other living organisms can coexist indefinitely."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15758
name: health advisory level
def: "In the United States\, nonregulatory health-based reference level of chemical traces \(usually in ppm\, i.e.\, \\\(\\pu\{mg L-1\}\\\)\) in drinking water at which there are no adverse health risks when ingested over various periods of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15759
name: health-based exposure limit
def: "Maximum concentration or intensity of exposure that can be tolerated without significant effect \(based on only scientific and not economic evidence concerning exposure levels and associated health effects\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15760
name: health hazard
def: "Any factor or exposure that may adversely affect health."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15761
name: healthy worker effect
def: "Epidemiological phenomenon observed initially in studies of occupational diseases\: Workers usually exhibit lower overall disease and death rates than the general population\, due to the fact that the old\, severely ill\, and disabled are ordinarily excluded from employment. Death rates in the general population may be inappropriate for comparison\, if this effect is not taken into account."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15762
name: hematemesis
def: "Vomiting of blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15763
name: hematotoxicity
def: "Adverse changes in blood caused by exposure to chemicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15764
name: hematuria
def: "Presence of blood in the urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15766
name: hemochromatosis
def: "Hereditary disorder affecting iron metabolism in which excessive amounts of iron accumulate in the body tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15767
name: hemoglobinuria
def: "Presence of free hemoglobin in the urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15768
name: hemolysin
def: "Substance that damages the membrane of erythrocytes causing the release of hemoglobin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15769
name: hemolysis
def: "Release of hemoglobin from erythrocytes\, and its appearance in the plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15770
name: hemoperfusion
def: "Passing blood through a column of charcoal or adsorbent resin for the removal of drugs or toxins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15771
name: hemosiderin
def: "Insoluble iron\(III\) hydroxide-based pigment deposited in cells in conditions of iron overload."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15772
name: hepatic
def: "Pertaining to the liver."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15773
name: hepatotoxic
def: "Poisonous to liver cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15774
name: herbicide
def: "Substance intended to kill plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15775
name: heterozygote
def: "Organism that has different allelic forms of a specified gene on each of a pair of homologous chromosomes or describing the genome of that organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15776
name: Hill plot
def: "Graphical method for analyzing binding of a molecule \\\(\\ce\{A\}\\\) to a macromolecule \\\(\\ce\{P\}\\\) with \\\(n\\\) binding sites. A Hill plot of \\\(\\log\[\\theta/\(1 - \\theta\)\]\\\) vs. \\\(\\log\[\\ce\{A\}\]\\\) has a slope of 1 if binding is noncooperative and \\\(\\gt 1\\\) for cooperative binding\, where \\\(\\theta = \[\\ce\{A\}\]_\{\\rm\{bound\}\}/n\[\\ce\{P\}\]_\{\\rm\{total\}\}\\\) is the fraction of sites occupied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15777
name: histogenic origin
def: "Germ cell layer of the embryo from which a given adult tissue develops."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15778
name: histology
def: "Study \(usually microscopic\) of the anatomy of tissues and their cellular and subcellular structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15779
name: histopathology
def: "Microscopic pathological study of the anatomy and cell structure of tissues in disease to reveal abnormal or adverse structural changes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15780
name: hit-and-run effect
def: "Toxicity that follows a single exposure to a substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15781
name: homology
def: "Similarity of anatomical structures in different species because of shared ancestry\nSimilarity of DNA or RNA nucleotide sequences\, or of protein amino-acid sequences\, supporting the hypothesis that they share a common ancestor.Note\: Homology among DNA or RNA nucleotide sequences\, or among protein amino-acid sequences\, is often concluded on the basis of sequence similarity. In general\, if there are almost identical sequences\, it is likely that they are homologous. However\, it is possible that highly similar sequences were not derived from a common ancestor\, i.e.\, they are similar but not homologous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15782
name: homozygote
def: "Organism that has the same allelic form of a specified gene on each of a pair of homologous chromosomes or describing the genome of that organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15783
name: human ecology
def: "Interrelationship between humans and the entire environment—physical\, biological\, socioeconomic\, and cultural\, including the interrelationships between individual humans or groups of humans and other human groups or groups of other species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15784
name: human equivalent dose
def: "Human dose of an agent that is believed to induce the same magnitude of a toxic effect that the known animal dose has induced."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15785
name: human exposure threshold
def: "Generic value of exposure to a substance\, or a group of substances falling within a defined structural class\, below which there is expected to be no appreciable risk to human health."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15786
name: hygiene
def: "Science of health and its preservation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15787
name: hyper-
def: "Prefix meaning above or excessive\: when used with the suffix '-emia' refers to blood and with the suffix '-uria' refers to urine\, for example\, 'hyperbilirubinemia'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15788
name: hyperemia
def: "Excessive amount of blood in any part of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15789
name: hyperalimentation
def: "Ingestion or administration of nutrients in excess of optimal amounts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15790
name: hyperbilirubinemia
def: "Excessive concentration of bilirubin in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15791
name: hypercalcemia
def: "Excessive concentration of calcium in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15792
name: hyperglycemia
def: "Excessive concentration of glucose in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15793
name: hyperkalemia
def: "Excessive concentration of potassium in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15794
name: hypernatremia
def: "Excessive concentration of sodium in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15795
name: hyperparathyroidism
def: "Abnormally increased parathyroid gland activity that affects\, and is affected by\, plasma calcium concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15796
name: hypertension
def: "Persistently high blood pressure in the arteries or in a circuit\, for example\, pulmonary hypertension or hepatic portal hypertension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15797
name: hypervitaminosis
def: "Condition resulting from the ingestion of an excess of one or more vitamins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15798
name: hypo-
def: "Prefix meaning under\, deficient\: when used with the suffix '-emia' refers to blood and with the suffix '-uria' refers to urine\, for example\, 'hypocalcemia'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15799
name: hypocalcemia
def: "Abnormally low calcium concentration in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15800
name: hypokalemia
def: "Abnormally low potassium concentration in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15801
name: hypomagnesemia
def: "Abnormally low magnesium concentration in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15802
name: hyponatremia
def: "Abnormally low sodium concentration in the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15803
name: hypovolemic
def: "Pertaining to an abnormally decreased volume of circulating fluid \(plasma\) in the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15804
name: iatrogenic
def: "Any adverse condition resulting from medical treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15805
name: icterus
def: "Excess of bile pigment in the blood and consequent deposition and retention of bile pigment in the skin and the sclera."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15806
name: idiosyncrasy
def: "Genetically based unusually high sensitivity of an organism to the effect of certain substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15807
name: immediately-dangerous-to-life-or-health-concentration
def: "According to the U.S. NIOSH\, the maximum exposure concentration from which one could escape within \\\(\\pu\{30 min\}\\\) without any escape-impairing symptoms or any irreversible health effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15808
name: in silico
def: "Phrase applied to data generated and analyzed using computer modeling and information technology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15809
name: incidence rate
def: "Measure of the frequency at which new events occur in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15810
name: indication
def: "Quantity value provided by a measuring instrument or a measuring system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15811
name: indirect exposure
def: "Exposure to a substance in a medium or vehicle other than the one originally receiving the substance.\nExposure of people to a substance by contact with a person directly exposed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15812
name: individual protective device
def: "Device for individual use for protection of the whole body\, eyes\, respiratory pathways\, or skin of workers against hazardous and harmful production factors."
synonym: "personal protective device (PPD)" []
synonym: "personal protective equipment (PPE)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15813
name: individual risk
def: "Probability that an individual person will experience an adverse effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15814
name: inert chemical
def: "Substance that is not generally reactive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15815
name: inert ingredient
def: "Any intentionally added ingredient of a mixture that does not contribute to the desired biological effect\: This definition does not include impurities and does not imply that the inert ingredient has no biological effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15816
name: infusion
def: "Therapeutic introduction of a fluid other than blood\, as a \(usually saline\) solution\, into a vein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15817
name: ingestion
def: "Process of taking food and drink into the body by mouth.\nProcess of taking in particles by a phagocytic cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15818
name: inhalation
def: "Act of drawing in of air\, vapor\, or gas and any suspended particulates into the lung."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15819
name: inherently biodegradable
def: "Class of compounds for which there is unequivocal evidence of biodegradation \(primary or ultimate\) in any test of biodegradability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15820
name: intake
def: "Amount of a substance that is taken into the body\, regardless of whether or not it is absorbed\: The total daily intake is the sum of the daily intake by an individual from food\, drinking-water\, and inhaled air."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15821
name: integral indicator of toxic effect
def: "Parameter \(such as body weight or temperature\) characterizing the overall changes in the general state of the organism exposed to a toxic substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15822
name: intermittent effect
def: "Biological change that comes and goes at intervals."
synonym: "discontinuous effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15823
name: internal validity
def: "Selection and comparison of index and comparison groups in such a manner that\, apart from sampling error\, the observed differences between these groups with respect to dependent variables under study may be attributed only to the hypothesized effect under investigation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15824
name: interpolation
def: "Estimation of a value between two known data points."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15825
name: interpretation
def: "Evaluation of the observations from an investigation or study in order to determine their significance for human health\, for the environment or for both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15826
name: interspecies dose conversion
def: "Process of extrapolating from the doses of one animal species to another\, for example\, from rodent dose to human equivalent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15827
name: interstitial fluid
def: "Aqueous solution filling the narrow spaces between cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15828
name: interstitial pneumonia
def: "Chronic form of pneumonia involving increase of the interstitial tissue and decrease of the functional lung tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15829
name: intervention study
def: "Epidemiological investigation designed to test a hypothesized cause–effect relationship by intentional change of a supposed causal factor in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15830
name: intestinal reabsorption
def: "Absorption further down the intestinal tract of a substance or substances that have been absorbed before and subsequently excreted into the intestinal tract\, usually through the bile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15831
name: intoxication
def: "Poisoning\: pathological process with clinical signs and symptoms caused by a substance of exogenous or endogenous origin.\nDrunkenness following consumption of beverages containing ethanol or other compounds affecting the central nervous system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15832
name: intrinsic activity
def: "Maximal stimulatory effect induced by a compound in relation to that of a given reference compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15833
name: intrinsic clearance
def: "Volume of plasma or blood from which a substance is completely removed in a period of time under unstressed conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15834
name: intrinsic factor
def: "Specific protein required for the absorption of \\\(\\ce\{vitamin B_\{12\}\}\\\) and secreted by cells in the gastric glands of the stomach."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15835
name: irreversible alteration
def: "Change from normal structure or function that persists or progresses after cessation of exposure of the organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15836
name: irritant
def: "n.\, Substance that causes inflammation following immediate\, prolonged\, or repeated contact with skin\, mucous membrane\, or other biological material.\nNote\: A substance capable of causing inflammation on first contact is called a primary irritant.\nadj.\, Causing inflammation following immediate\, prolonged\, or repeated contact with skin\, mucous membrane\, or other tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15837
name: isotonic
def: "Denoting a liquid exerting the same osmotic pressure or chemical potential of water \(water potential\) as another liquid with which it is being compared."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15838
name: itai-itai disease
def: "Illness \(renal osteomalacia\) observed in the Toyama prefecture of Japan\, resulting from the ingestion of cadmium-contaminated rice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15839
name: jaundice
def: "Pathological condition characterized by deposition of bile pigment in the skin and mucous membranes\, including the conjunctivae\, resulting in yellow appearance of the patient or animal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15840
name: joint effect
def: "Simultaneous or successive effect of factors of diverse types \(chemical\, physical\, biological\) on an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15841
name: kairomone
def: "Semiochemical that is produced by one organism inducing a response in an organism of another species that is unfavorable to the emitter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15842
name: kinetics
def: "Branch of chemistry concerned with measuring and studying rates of chemical reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15843
name: ketosis
def: "Pathological increase in the production of ketone bodies\, for example\, following blockage or failure of carbohydrate metabolism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15844
name: knock-down
def: "Technique used to decrease the expression of a particular gene in a cell or living organism in order to define its function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15845
name: knock-in
def: "Technique used to express an exogenous gene or to overexpress an endogenous gene in a living organism in order to define its function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15846
name: knock-out
def: "Technique used to inactivate a particular gene in a living organism in order to define its function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15847
name: lacrimator
def: "Substance that irritates the eyes and causes the production of tears or increases the flow of tears."
synonym: "lachrymator" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15848
name: larvicide
def: "Substance intended to kill larvae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15849
name: laryngospasm
def: "Reflex spasmodic closure of the sphincter of the larynx\, particularly the glottic sphincter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15850
name: lassitude
def: "Weakness; exhaustion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15851
name: lavage
def: "Irrigation or washing out of a hollow organ or cavity such as the stomach\, intestines\, or lungs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15852
name: laxative
def: "Substance that causes evacuation of the intestinal contents."
synonym: "cathartic" []
synonym: "purgative" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15853
name: lead colic
def: "Chronic intestinal pains and constipation caused by lead poisoning."
synonym: "painters’ colic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15854
name: lesion
def: "Area of pathologically altered tissue.\nInjury or wound.\nInfected patch of skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15856
name: life-long exposure
def: "Subjection to a potentially toxic substance during the whole lifetime."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15857
name: limacide
def: "Substance intended to kill mollusks including the gastropod mollusk\, Limax."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15858
name: limited evidence
def: "According to the USEPA’s Guidelines for Carcinogen Risk Assessment\, 'limited evidence' is a collection of facts and accepted scientific inferences that suggests that an agent may be causing an effect\, but this suggestion is not strong enough to be considered established fact."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15859
name: linearised multistage model
def: "Sequence of steps in which \(a\) a multistage model is fitted to tumor incidence data; \(b\) the maximum linear term consistent with the data is calculated; \(c\) the low-dose slope of the dose–response function is equated to the coefficient of the maximum linear term; and \(d\) the resulting slope is then equated to the upper bound of potency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15860
name: macroscopic pathology
def: "Study of changes associated with disease that are visible to the naked eye without the need for a microscope."
synonym: "gross pathology" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15861
name: mainstream smoke
def: "Smoke that is inhaled by the smoker."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15862
name: malaise
def: "Vague feeling of bodily discomfort."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15863
name: malignancy
def: "Population of cells showing both uncontrolled growth and a tendency to invade and destroy other tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15864
name: mania
def: "Emotional disorder \(mental illness\) characterized by an expansive and elated state \(euphoria\)\, rapid speech\, flight of ideas\, decreased need for sleep\, distractibility\, grandiosity\, poor judgment\, and increased motor activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15865
name: margin of exposure
def: "Ratio of the no-observed-adverse-effect level \(NOAEL\) to the theoretical or estimated exposure dose \(EED\) or concentration \(EEC\)."
synonym: "margin of safety (MOS)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15866
name: mass mean diameter
def: "Diameter of a spherical particle with a mass equal to the mean mass of all the particles in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15867
name: mass median diameter
def: "Diameter of a spherical particle with the median mass of all the particles in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15868
name: maximum average daily concentration of an atmospheric pollutant
def: "Highest of the average daily concentrations recorded at a definite point of measurement during a certain period of observation."
synonym: "peak daily average concentration of an air pollutant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15869
name: maximum contaminant level
def: "Under the Safe Drinking Water Act \(USA\)\, primary MCL is a regulatory concentration for drinking water which takes into account both adverse effects \(including sensitive populations\) and technological feasibility \(including natural background levels\)\: Secondary MCL is a regulatory concentration based on 'welfare'\, such as taste and staining\, rather than health\, but also takes into account technical feasibility."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15870
name: maximum exposure limit
def: "Occupational exposure limit legally defined in the United Kingdom under COSHH as the maximum concentration of an airborne substance\, averaged over a reference period\, to which employees may be exposed by inhalation under any circumstances\, and set on the advice of the HSC Advisory Committee on Toxic Substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15871
name: maximum residue limit
def: "Maximum contents of a pesticide residue \(expressed as \\\(\\pu\{mg kg-1\}\\\) fresh weight\) recommended by the Codex Alimentarius Commission to be legally permitted in or on food commodities and animal feeds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15872
name: maximum residue limit for veterinary drugs
def: "Maximum contents of a drug residue \(expressed as \\\(\\pu\{mg kg-1\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{\\upmu g kg-1\}\\\) fresh weight\) recommended by the Codex Alimentarius Commission to be legally permitted or recognized as acceptable in or on food commodities and animal feeds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15873
name: maximum velocity
def: "In Michaelis–Menten kinetics\, the maximum rate of conversion of a substrate when its concentration is not rate-limiting."
synonym: "maximum rate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15874
name: mercurialism
def: "Chronic poisoning caused by exposure to mercury\, often by breathing its vapor but also by skin absorption and\, less commonly\, by ingestion."
synonym: "Mad Hatter syndrome" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15875
name: mesothelioma
def: "Malignant tumor of the mesothelium of the pleura\, pericardium\, or peritoneum\, that may be caused by exposure to asbestos fibers and some other fibers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15876
name: metabolic enzymes
def: "Proteins that catalyze chemical transformations of body constituents and\, in more common usage\, of xenobiotics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15877
name: metabolic model
def: "Analysis and theoretical reconstruction of the way in which the body deals with a specific substance\, showing the proportion of the intake that is absorbed\, the proportion that is stored and in what tissues\, the rate of breakdown in the body and the subsequent fate of the metabolic products\, and the rate at which it is eliminated by different organs as unchanged substance or metabolites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15878
name: metabolic transformation
def: "Biotransformation of a substance that takes place within a living organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15879
name: metastasis
def: "Movement of bacteria or body cells\, especially cancer cells\, from one part of the body to another\, resulting in change in location of a disease or of its symptoms from one part of the body to another.\nGrowth of pathogenic microorganisms or of abnormal cells distant from the site of their origin in the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15880
name: methemoglobin
def: "Derivative of hemoglobin that is formed when the iron\(II\) in the heme porphyrin is oxidized to iron\(III\); this derivative cannot transport dioxygen."
synonym: "methaemoglobin" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15881
name: methemoglobinaemia
def: "Presence of methaemoglobin in the blood in greater than normal proportion."
synonym: "methaemoglobinaemia" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15882
name: methemoglobin-forming substance
def: "Substance capable of oxidizing directly or indirectly the iron\(II\) in hemoglobin to iron\(III\) to form methemoglobin."
synonym: "methaemoglobin-forming substance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15883
name: microalbuminuria
def: "Chronic presence of albumin in slight excess in urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15884
name: micromercurialism
def: "Early or subclinical effects of exposure to elemental mercury detected at the low exposure levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15885
name: microproteinaemia
def: "Chronic presence of microprotein \(alpha-1 and beta-2 microglobulin\) in blood\, indicating proximal renal tubule damage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15886
name: midstream sampling
def: "Taking an aliquot of a flowing liquid\, such as urine\, avoiding initial and terminal flow periods which are likely to be unrepresentative."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15887
name: minimal risk level
def: "Estimate of the daily human exposure to a hazardous substance that is likely to be without appreciable risk of adverse noncancer health effects over a specified duration of exposure\: This substance-specific estimate is used by ATSDR health assessors to identify contaminants and potential health effects that may be of concern at hazardous waste sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15888
name: miscible
def: "Liquid substances capable of mixing without separation into two phases; refers to liquid mixtures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15889
name: miticide
def: "Substance intended to kill mites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15890
name: mitochondrion
def: "Eukaryote cytoplasmic organelle that is bounded by an outer membrane and an inner membrane; the inner membrane has folds called cristae that are the center of ATP synthesis in oxidative phosphorylation in the animal cell and supplement ATP synthesis by the chloroplasts in photosynthetic cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15891
name: molluskicide
def: "Substance intended to kill mollusks."
synonym: "limacide" []
synonym: "molluscicide" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15892
name: monooxygenase
def: "Enzyme that catalyzes reactions between an organic compound and molecular oxygen in which one atom of the oxygen molecule is incorporated into the organic compound and one atom is reduced to water; involved in the metabolism of many natural and foreign compounds giving both unreactive products and products of different or increased toxicity from that of the parent compound."
synonym: "mixed-function oxidase (MFO)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15893
name: morbidity
def: "Any departure\, subjective or objective\, from a state of physiological or psychological well-being\: In this sense\, 'sickness'\, 'illness'\, and 'morbid condition' are similarly defined and synonymous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15894
name: morbidity rate
def: "Term \(to be avoided\) used loosely to refer to incidence or prevalence rates of disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15895
name: morbidity survey
def: "Method for the estimation of the prevalence and \(or\) incidence of a disease or diseases in a population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15896
name: mortality study
def: "Investigation dealing with death rates or proportion of deaths attributed to specific causes as a measure of response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15897
name: mucociliary transport
def: "Process of removal of particles from the bronchi of the lungs in a mucus stream moved by cilia\, thus contributing to uptake from the gastrointestinal tract."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15898
name: multicompartment model
def: "Product of a compartmental analysis requiring more than two compartments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15899
name: multifactorial disease
def: "Illness with pathogenesis dependent on complex interplay of genetic and \(or\) environmental factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15900
name: multiple screening
def: "Procedure that has evolved by combining single screening tests\, and is the logical corollary of mass screening."
synonym: "multiphasic screening" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15901
name: multipotent
def: "Of a cell\, capable of giving rise to several different kinds of structure or types of cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15902
name: multistage cluster sampling
def: "Cluster sampling with more than two stages\, each sampling being made on aggregates \(or clusters\) in which the clusters already obtained by the preceding sampling have been divided."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15903
name: multistage model
def: "Dose–response model for cancer death estimation of the form \\\[P = 1 - \\exp \[-\(q_\{0\} + q_\{1\}d_\{1\} + q_\{2\}d_\{2\} + \\ldots + q_\{k\}d_\{k\}\)\]\\\] where \\\(P\\\) is the probability of cancer death from a continuous dose rate\, \\\(d_\{i\}\\\) \, of group \(or stage\) \\\(i = 0\, 1\, 2 \\ldots\\\)\, the \\\(q\\\)’s are constants\, and \\\(k\\\) is the number of dose groups \(or\, if less than the number of dose groups\, \\\(k\\\) is the number of biological stages believed to be required in the carcinogenesis process\). With the multistage model\, it is assumed that cancer is initiated by cell mutations in a finite series of steps."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15904
name: murine
def: "Of or belonging to the family of rats and mice \(Muridae\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15905
name: myalgia
def: "Pain or tenderness in a muscle or group of muscles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15906
name: myasthenia
def: "Muscular weakness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15907
name: mycotoxin
def: "Toxin produced by a fungus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15908
name: nanotoxicology
def: "Scientific discipline involving the study of the actual or potential danger presented by the harmful effects of nanoparticles on living organisms and ecosystems\, of the relationship of such harmful effects to exposure\, and of the mechanisms of action\, diagnosis\, prevention\, and treatment of intoxications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15909
name: natriuretic
def: "Substance increasing the rate of excretion of sodium ion in the urine."
synonym: "saluretic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15910
name: natural occurrence
def: "Presence of a substance in nature\, as distinct from presence resulting from inputs from human activities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15911
name: negligible risk
def: "Probability of adverse effects occurring that can reasonably be described as trivial.\nProbability of adverse effects occurring that is so low that it cannot be reduced appreciably by increased regulation or investment of resources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15912
name: nematicide
def: "Substance intended to kill nematodes."
synonym: "nematocide" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15913
name: neoplasia
def: "New and abnormal formation of tissue as a tumor or growth by cell proliferation that is faster than normal and continues after the initial stimulus that initiated the proliferation has ceased."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15914
name: nephritis
def: "Inflammation of the kidney\, leading to kidney failure\, usually accompanied by proteinuria\, hematuria\, edema\, and hypertension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15915
name: nephropathy
def: "Any disease or abnormality of the kidney."
synonym: "renopathy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15916
name: nephrosis
def: "Disease of the kidneys marked by degeneration of renal tubular epithelium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15917
name: nephrotoxic
def: "Chemically harmful to the cells of the kidney."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15918
name: nitrosative stress
def: "Adverse effects occurring when the generation of reactive nitrogen species in a system exceeds the system’s ability to neutralize and eliminate them; nitrosative stress may lead to nitrosylation reactions that can alter protein structure\, thus inhibiting normal function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15919
name: no-acceptable-daily-intake-allocated
def: "This expression is applicable to a substance for which the available information is not sufficient to establish its safety\, or when the specifications for identity and purity are not adequate\, or when the available data show that the substance is hazardous and should not be used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15920
name: nodule
def: "Small node or boss that is solid and can be detected by touch."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15921
name: no-effect dose
def: "Amount of a substance that has no effect on the organism."
synonym: "subthreshold dose" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15922
name: no-effect level
def: "Maximum dose \(of a substance\) that produces no detectable changes under defined conditions of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15923
name: non-bioenvironmental transformation
def: "Change in the chemical or physical nature of a substance occurring as a result of physicochemical conditions and independent of any biological system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15924
name: nonionising radiation
def: "Electromagnetic radiation of low energy that is not capable of causing ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15925
name: nonoccupational exposure
def: "Environmental exposure outside the workplace to substances that are otherwise associated with particular work environments and \(or\) activities and processes that occur there."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15927
name: nosocomial
def: "Associated with a hospital or infirmary\, especially used of diseases that may result from treatment in such an institution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15928
name: nutritional table method
def: "Procedure for evaluating the dietary intake of a large number of people."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15929
name: nychthemeral
def: "Relating to or exhibiting a nychthemeron or \\\(\\pu\{24\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) period."
synonym: "circadian" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15930
name: objective environment
def: "Actual physical\, chemical\, and social environment as described by objective measurements\, such as noise levels in decibels and concentrations of air pollutants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15931
name: occupational environment
def: "Surrounding conditions at a workplace."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15932
name: occupational exposure
def: "Experience of substances\, intensities of radiation\, etc.\, or other conditions while at work."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15933
name: occupational exposure limit
def: "Regulatory level of exposure to substances\, intensities of radiation\, etc.\, or other conditions\, specified appropriately in relevant government legislation or related codes of practice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15934
name: occupational exposure standard
def: "Level of exposure to substances\, intensities of radiation\, etc.\, or other conditions considered to represent specified good practice and a realistic criterion for the control of exposure by appropriate plant design\, engineering controls\, and\, if necessary\, the addition and use of personal protective clothing.\nIn UK\, health-based exposure limit defined under COSHH Regulations as the concentration of any airborne substance\, averaged over a reference period\, at which\, according to current knowledge\, there is no evidence that it is likely to be injurious to employees\, if they are exposed by inhalation\, day after day\, to that concentration\, and set on the advice of the HSE Advisory Committee on Toxic Substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15935
name: occupational hygiene
def: "Identification\, assessment\, and control of physicochemical and biological factors in the workplace that may affect the health or well-being of those at work and in the surrounding community."
synonym: "industrial hygiene" []
synonym: "occupational safety and health" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15936
name: occupational medicine
def: "Specialty devoted to the prevention and management of occupational injury\, illness\, and disability\, and the promotion of the health of workers\, their families\, and their communities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15937
name: ocular
def: "Pertaining to the eye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15938
name: odds
def: "Ratio of the probability of occurrence of an event to that of non-occurrence\, or the ratio of the probability that something is so\, to the probability that it is not so."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15939
name: odds ratio
def: "Quotient obtained by dividing one set of odds by another. The terms 'odds' or 'odds ratio' are defined differently according to the situation under discussion. Consider the following notation for the distribution of a binary exposure and a disease in a population or a sample. \\\[\\begin\{array\}\{l c c\} & Exposed & Nonexposed \\\\ \\rm\{Disease\} & a & b \\\\ \\rm\{No\\ disease\} & c & d \\\\ \\end\{array\}\\\] The odds ratio \(cross-product ratio\) is \\\(ad/bc\\\)."
synonym: "cross-product ratio" []
synonym: "relative odds" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15940
name: odor threshold
def: "In principle\, the lowest concentration of an odorant in the air that can be detected by a human being."
synonym: "odor detection threshold" []
synonym: "odour threshold" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15941
name: olf
def: "Unit used to measure scent emission of people and objects; one olf is defined as the scent emission of an 'average person'\, a sitting adult who takes an average of \\\(\\pu\{0.7\}\\\) baths per day and whose skin has a total area of \\\(\\pu\{1.8 m2\}\\\); the scent emission of an object or person is measured by specially trained personnel comparing it to normed scents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15942
name: olfactometer
def: "Apparatus for testing the power of the sense of smell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15943
name: oligozoospermia
def: "Sperm concentration less than a reference value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15944
name: oliguria
def: "Excretion of a diminished amount of urine in relation to fluid intake."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15945
name: -omics\, -omes
def: "Neologism referring to the fields of study in biology ending in the suffix -omics\, such as genomics or proteomics\: The related neologism -omes are the objects of study of the field such as the genome or proteome\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15946
name: oncogene
def: "Gene that can cause neoplastic \(see neoplasia\) transformation of a cell; oncogenes are slightly changed equivalents of normal genes known as proto-oncogenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15947
name: oncogenesis
def: "Production or causation of tumors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15948
name: oncogenic
def: "Capable of producing tumors in animals\, either benign \(noncancerous\) or malignant \(cancerous\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15949
name: one-hit model
def: "Dose-response model of the form \\\[P = 1 - \{\\rm\{e\}\}^\{-bd\}\\\] where \\\(P\\\) is the probability of cancer death from a continuous dose rate\, \\\(d\\\)\, and \\\(b\\\) is a constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15950
name: onycholysis
def: "Loosening or detachment of the nail from the nail bed following some destructive process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15951
name: ophthalmic
def: "Pertaining to the eye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15952
name: organ dose
def: "Amount of a substance or physical agent \(radiation\) absorbed by an organ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15953
name: organelle
def: "Microstructure or separated compartment within a cell that has a specialized function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15954
name: organic carbon partition coefficient
def: "Measure of the tendency for organic substances to be adsorbed by soil or sediment\, expressed as\: \\\[K_\{\\rm\{oc\}\} = \\frac\{\(\\ce\{mass adsorbed substance\}\)/\(\\ce\{mass organic carbon\}\)\}\{\(\\ce\{mass concentration of absorbed substance\}\)\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:15955
name: osteo-
def: "Prefix meaning pertaining to bone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15956
name: osteodystrophy
def: "Abnormal development of bone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15957
name: osteomalacia
def: "Condition marked by softening of the bones \(due to impaired mineralization\, with excess accumulation of osteoid\)\, with pain\, tenderness\, muscular weakness\, anorexia\, and loss of weight\, resulting from deficiency of vitamin D and calcium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15958
name: osteoporosis
def: "Significant decrease in bone mass with increased porosity and increased tendency to fracture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15959
name: ovicide
def: "Substance intended to kill eggs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15960
name: para-occupational exposure
def: "Exposure of a worker’s family to substances carried from the workplace to the home.\nExposure of visitors to substances in the workplace."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15961
name: parasympathetic
def: "Of\, relating to\, or affecting the parasympathetic nervous system which stimulates digestive secretions\, slows the heart\, constricts the pupils of the eyes\, and dilates blood vessels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15962
name: parasympatholytic
def: "Producing effects resembling those caused by interruption of the parasympathetic nerve; also called anticholinergic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15963
name: parenteral dosage
def: "Method of introducing substances into an organism avoiding the gastrointestinal tract \(subcutaneously\, intravenously\, intramuscularly\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15964
name: passive smoking
def: "Inhalation of sidestream smoke by people who do not smoke themselves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15965
name: penetration
def: "Action of entering or passing through a cell membrane.\nAbility or power to enter or pass through a cell membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15966
name: percutaneous
def: "Through the skin following application on the skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15967
name: perfusion
def: "Act of pouring over or through\, especially the passage of a fluid through the vessels of a specific organ.\nLiquid poured over or through an organ or tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15968
name: peritoneal dialysis
def: "Method of artificial detoxication in which a toxic substance from the body is transferred into liquid that is instilled into the peritoneum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15969
name: permeability coefficient
def: "Quantity defining the permeability of molecules across a cell membrane and expressed as \\\[P = \\frac\{KD\}\{\\Delta x\}\\\] where \\\(K\\\) is the partition coefficient\, \\\(D\\\) is the diffusion coefficient\, and \\\(\\Delta x\\\) is the thickness of the cell membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15970
name: permeable
def: "Of a membrane\, allowing a given substance to pass through."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15971
name: permissible exposure limit
def: "Recommendation by U.S. OSHA for a time-weighted average concentration that must not be exceeded during any \\\(\\pu\{8\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) work shift of a \\\(\\pu\{40\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) working week."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15972
name: personal monitoring
def: "Type of environmental monitoring in which an individual’s exposure to a substance is measured and evaluated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15973
name: personal protective device
def: "Equipment \(clothing\, gloves\, hard hat\, respirator\, etc.\) worn by an individual to prevent exposure to a potentially toxic substance."
synonym: "individual protective device (IPD)" []
synonym: "personal protective equipment (PPE)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15974
name: personal sampler
def: "Compact\, portable instrument for individual air sampling\, measuring\, or both\, the content of a harmful substance in the respiration zone of a working person."
synonym: "individual monitor" []
synonym: "individual sampler" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15975
name: pest
def: "Organism that may harm public health\, attacks food and other materials essential to mankind\, or otherwise affects human beings adversely."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15976
name: phase III reaction
def: "Further metabolism of conjugated metabolites produced by phase II reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15977
name: phenome
def: "Complete phenotypic description of an organism \(by analogy with genome\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15978
name: photo-irritation
def: "Inflammation of the skin caused by exposure to light\, especially that due to metabolites formed in the skin by photolysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15979
name: phototoxicity
def: "Adverse effects produced by exposure to light energy\, especially those produced in the skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15980
name: physical map
def: "Map showing how much DNA\, measured in base pairs\, separates two genes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15981
name: piscicide
def: "Substance intended to kill fish."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15982
name: placentation
def: "Formation of a placenta in the uterus.\nType or structure of a placenta.\nIn botany\, arrangement of placentas within the plant ovary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15983
name: plumbism
def: "Chronic poisoning caused by absorption of lead or lead salts."
synonym: "saturnism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15984
name: pneumoconiosis
def: "Usually fibrosis of the lungs that develops owing to \(prolonged\) inhalation of inorganic or organic dusts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15985
name: pneumonitis
def: "Inflammation of the lung."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15986
name: point mutation
def: "Change in a single base pair in DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15987
name: poison-bearing
def: "Containing a poison."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15988
name: poisoning
def: "Morbid condition produced by a poison."
synonym: "intoxication" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15989
name: polyclonal antibody
def: "Antibody produced by a number of different cell types."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15990
name: polydipsia
def: "Chronic excessive thirst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15991
name: polyuria
def: "Excessive production and discharge of urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15992
name: population at risk
def: "Persons who can and may develop an adverse health effect and who are potentially exposed to a substance under study. People already having chronic disease are excluded from the population at risk in studies of the incidence of the adverse effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15993
name: population critical concentration
def: "Concentration of a substance in the critical organ at which a specified percentage of the exposed population has reached the individual critical organ concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15994
name: population effect
def: "Absolute number or incidence rate of cases occurring in a group of people."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15995
name: porphyria
def: "Disturbance of porphyrin metabolism characterized by increased formation\, accumulation\, and excretion of porphyrins and their precursors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15996
name: porphyrin
def: "Natural pigment containing a fundamental skeleton of four pyrrole nuclei united through the α-positions by four methine groups to form a macrocyclic structure."
synonym: "porphin" []
synonym: "porphine" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:15997
name: posology
def: "Pharmacological study of the choice of appropriate dose of a drug in relation to the physiological factors\, such as age\, that may influence its effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15998
name: post-translational modification
def: "Processes by which proteins are biochemically modified within a cell following their synthesis on the ribosomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:15999
name: practical certainty
def: "Numerically specified low risk of exposure to a potentially toxic substance \(e.g.\, 1 in \\\(\\pu\{1000\}\\\)\) or socially acceptable low risk of adverse effects from such an exposure applied to decision making in regard to chemical safety."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16000
name: precordial
def: "Pertaining to the region over the heart and lower thorax."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16001
name: predictive validity
def: "Reliability of a measurement expressed in terms of its ability to predict the criterion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16002
name: predictive value
def: "Percentage of positive results that are true positives or of negative results that are true negatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16003
name: premature ovarian failure
def: "Follicular depletion by the age of 35 years."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16004
name: preneoplastic
def: "Before the formation of a tumor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16005
name: prior informed consent
def: "Concept in law and medicine that states that before one is subjected to a risk\, especially a risk of bodily harm\, one is entitled to be fully informed well in advance of the nature of that risk in order to make an informed decision about whether to accept it or not."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16006
name: primary protection standard
def: "Accepted maximum level of a pollutant \(or its indicator\) in the target organism\, or some part thereof\, or an accepted maximum intake of a pollutant or nuisance into the target under specified circumstances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16007
name: procarcinogen
def: "Substance that has to be metabolized before it becomes a carcinogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16008
name: progression
def: "Increase in the size of a tumor or spread of cancer in the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16009
name: prokaryote
def: "Unicellular organism\, characterized by the absence of a membrane-enclosed nucleus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16010
name: pro-pesticide
def: "Substance applied in a form that is not active as a pesticide and which becomes active once it enters an organism and undergoes chemical modification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16011
name: proportional mortality rate
def: "Proportion of observed deaths from a specified condition in a defined population divided by the proportion of deaths expected from this condition in a standard population\, expressed either on an age-specific basis or after age adjustment."
synonym: "proportional mortality ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16012
name: proteinuria
def: "Excretion of excessive amounts of protein \(derived from blood plasma or kidney tubules\) in the urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16013
name: pseudo-acceptable daily intake
def: "Intake for a substance derived by applying a thousandfold uncertainty factor to the lowest low-effect level for noncarcinogenic endpoints."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16014
name: pseudoadaptation
def: "Apparent adaptation of an organism to changing conditions of the environment \(especially chemical\) associated with stresses in biochemical systems that exceed the limits of normal \(homeostatic\) mechanisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16015
name: public health impact assessment
def: "Applying risk assessment to a specific target population of known size\, giving as the end product a quantitative statement about the number of people likely to be affected in a particular population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16016
name: pyrexia
def: "Condition in which the temperature of a human being or mammal is above normal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16017
name: quantal
def: "Describing a condition that can be expressed only as 'occurring' or 'not occurring'\, such as death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16018
name: radiation toxicology
def: "Scientific study involving research\, education\, prevention\, and treatment of diseases caused by ionizing or nonionizing radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16019
name: rate difference
def: "Absolute difference between two rates."
synonym: "excess rate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16020
name: ratticide
def: "Substance intended to kill rats."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16021
name: reabsorption
def: "Absorption by a living organism of a substance which it has previously absorbed and then released \(e.g.\, the uptake of a substance from the proximal renal tubule following glomerular filtration\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16022
name: reactive nitrogen species
def: "Radical nitrogen-based molecules that can act to facilitate nitrosylation reactions; reactive nitrogen species include dioxidonitrogen\(\\\(\\ce\{^\{.\}\}\\\)\) \(nitrogen dioxide\, nitryl radical\) \\\(\\ce\{NO^\{.\}\}\\\)\, oxidonitrogen\(\\\(\\ce\{^\{.\}\}\\\)\) \(nitrogen monoxide\, nitrosyl radical\) \\\(\\ce\{NO^\{.\}\}\\\) oxidonitrogen\(1+\) \(nitrosyl cation\) \\\(\\ce\{NO^\{+\}\}\\\)\, hydroxyoxidonitrogen \(nitrous acid\) \\\(\\ce\{HNO2\}\\\)\, and oxidonitrate\(1-\) \\\(\\ce\{NO^\{-\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16023
name: readily biodegradable
def: "Arbitrary classification of substances that have passed certain specified screening tests for ultimate biodegradability; these tests are so stringent that such compounds will be rapidly and completely biodegraded in a wide variety of aerobic environments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16024
name: recalcitrance
def: "Ability of a substance to remain in a particular environment in an unchanged form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16025
name: receptor-mediated endocytosis
def: "Endocytosis of a substance and its receptor following receptor binding."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16026
name: recessive gene
def: "Allele which in the heterozygous state is expected to have no effect on the phenotype of the organism which carries it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16027
name: recommended exposure level
def: "Highest allowable regulatory airborne concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16028
name: reconstitution
def: "Restoration to original form of a substance previously altered for preservation and storage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16029
name: recycling
def: "Process or method allowing for the recovery of some value from a waste\, either as reusable material or as energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16030
name: reference concentration
def: "An estimate \(with uncertainty spanning perhaps an order of magnitude\) of a continuous inhalation exposure to the human population \(including sensitive subgroups which include children\, asthmatics\, and the elderly\) that is likely to be without an appreciable risk of deleterious effects during a lifetime."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16031
name: reference distribution
def: "Statistical distribution of reference values."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16032
name: reference individual
def: "Person selected with the use of defined criteria for comparative purposes in a clinical study."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16033
name: reference interval
def: "Area between and including two reference limits\, for example\, the percentiles \\\(\\pu\{2.5\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{97.5\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16034
name: reference limit
def: "Boundary value defined so that a stated fraction of the reference values is less than or exceeds that boundary value with a stated probability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16035
name: reference population
def: "Group of all reference individuals used to establish criteria against which a population that is being studied can be compared."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16036
name: reference sample group
def: "Selected reference individuals\, statistically adequate numerically to represent the reference population."
synonym: "reference group" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16037
name: reference value
def: "Quantity value\, generally accepted as having a suitably small measurement uncertainty\, to be used as a basis for comparison with values of quantities of the same kind."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16038
name: regulatory dose
def: "Term used by the USEPA to describe the expected dose resulting from human exposure to a substance at the level at which it is regulated in the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16039
name: regulatory sequence
def: "DNA sequence to which specific proteins bind to activate or repress the expression of a gene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16040
name: relative excess risk
def: "Measure that can be used in comparison of adverse reactions to drugs\, or other exposures\, based solely on the component of risk due to the exposure or drug under investigation\, removing the risk due to background exposure experienced by all in the population. The relative excess risk\, \\\(R\\\)\, is given by \\\[R = \(R_\{1\} - R_\{0\}\)/\(R_\{2\} - R_\{0\}\)\\\] where \\\(R_\{1\}\\\) is the rate in the population\, \\\(R_\{2\}\\\) is the rate in the comparison population\, and \\\(R_\{0\}\\\) is the rate in the general population."
synonym: "comparative risk" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16041
name: relative systemic availability
def: "Quantity of metabolizable substance divided by product of quantity of absorbed substance and exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16042
name: remedy
def: "Anything\, such as a medicine or therapy\, that relieves pain\, cures disease\, or corrects a disorder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16043
name: renal plasma flow
def: "Volume of plasma passing through the kidneys in unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16044
name: repellent
def: "Substance used mainly to repel blood-sucking insects in order to protect humans and animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16045
name: replicate sampling
def: "Act of taking multiple samples concurrently under comparable conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16046
name: reserve capacity
def: "Physiological or biochemical capacity that may be available to maintain homeostasis when the body or an organism is exposed to an environmental change."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16047
name: reservoir
def: "Storage compartment from which a substance may be released with subsequent biological effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16048
name: residual risk
def: "Health risk remaining after risk reduction actions are implemented."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16049
name: residue
def: "Contaminant remaining in an organism or in other material such as food or packaging\, following exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16050
name: resorptive effect
def: "Action of a substance after its reabsorption from the gut into the blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16051
name: respirable dust
def: "Mass fraction of dust \(particles\) that penetrates to the unciliated airways of the lung \(the alveolar region\)."
synonym: "respirable particles" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16052
name: retrospective study
def: "Research design used to test etiological hypotheses in which inferences about exposure to the putative causal factor\(s\) are derived from data relating to characteristics of the persons or organisms under study or to events or experiences in their past."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16053
name: returned effect of poisons
def: "Enhancement of the dose–effect relationship for a poison following repeated exposure to decreasing doses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16054
name: reverse mutation
def: "Mutation in a mutant allele which makes it capable of producing the nonmutant phenotype; this may result from restoration of the original DNA sequence of the gene or from production of a new DNA sequence which has the same effect."
synonym: "back mutation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16055
name: reverse transcription
def: "Process by which an RNA molecule is used as a template to make a single-stranded DNA copy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16056
name: reversible alteration
def: "Change from normal structure or function\, induced by a substance or other agent\(s\)\, that returns to normal status or within normal limits after cessation of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16057
name: rhabdomyolysis
def: "Acute\, fulminating\, potentially lethal disease of skeletal muscle that causes disintegration of striated muscle fibers as evidenced by myoglobin in the blood and urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16058
name: rhonchus
def: "Harsh crepitation in the throat\, often resembling snoring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16059
name: risk associated with a lifetime exposure
def: "Probability of the occurrence of a specified undesirable event following exposure of an individual person from a given population to a specified substance at a defined level for the expected lifetime of the average member of that population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16060
name: risk aversion
def: "Tendency of an individual person to avoid risk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16061
name: risk communication
def: "Interpretation and communication of risk assessments in terms that are comprehensible to the general public or to others without specialist knowledge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16062
name: risk <i>de minimis</i>
def: "Risk that is negligible and too small to be of societal concern \(usually assumed to be a probability below \\\(\\pu\{E-5\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{E-6\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "de minimis risk" []
synonym: "negligible risk" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16063
name: risk evaluation
def: "Establishment of a qualitative or quantitative relationship between risks and benefits\, involving the complex process of determining the significance of the identified hazards and estimated risks to those organisms or people concerned with or affected by them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16064
name: risk identification
def: "Recognition of a potential hazard and definition of the factors required to assess the probability of exposure of organisms or people to that hazard and of harm resulting from such exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16065
name: risk marker
def: "Attribute that is associated with an increased probability of occurrence of a disease or other specified outcome and that can be used as an indicator of this increased risk."
synonym: "risk indicator" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16066
name: risk monitoring
def: "Process of following up the decisions and actions within risk management in order to check whether the aims of reduced exposure and risk are achieved."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16067
name: risk perception
def: "Subjective perception of the gravity or importance of the risk based on a person’s knowledge of different risks and the moral\, economic\, and political judgment of their implications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16068
name: risk phrases
def: "Word groups identifying potential health or environmental hazards required under CPL Directives \(European Community\); may be incorporated into Safety Data Sheets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16069
name: risk-specific dose
def: "Amount of exposure corresponding to a specified level of risk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16070
name: rodenticide
def: "Substance intended to kill rodents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16071
name: route of exposure
def: "Means by which a toxic agent gains access to an organism by administration through the gastrointestinal tract \(ingestion\)\, lungs \(inhalation\)\, skin \(topical\)\, or by other routes such as intravenous\, subcutaneous\, intramuscular\, or intraperitoneal routes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16072
name: S9 fraction
def: "Supernatant fraction obtained from an organ \(usually liver\) homogenate by centrifuging at \\\(\\pu\{9000 g\}\\\) for \\\(\\pu\{20 min\}\\\) in a suitable medium; this fraction contains cytosol and microsomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16073
name: safety pharmacology
def: "Science directed to the discovery\, development\, and safe therapeutic use of biologically active substances as a result of the identification\, monitoring\, and characterization of potentially undesirable pharmacodynamic activities of these substances in nonclinical studies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16074
name: sarcoma
def: "Malignant tumor arising in a connective tissue and composed primarily of anaplastic cells resembling supportive tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16075
name: saturable elimination
def: "Elimination that becomes concentration-independent at a concentration at which the elimination process is functioning maximally."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16076
name: saturnia
def: "Pain in a joint resulting from lead poisoning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16077
name: saturnism
def: "Intoxication caused by lead."
synonym: "plumbism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16078
name: scotoma
def: "Area of diminished or lost vision within the visual field\, surrounded by an area of less affected or normal vision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16079
name: secretion
def: "Process by which a substance such as a hormone or enzyme produced in a cell is passed through a plasma membrane to the outside\, for example\, the intestinal lumen or the blood \(internal secretion\).\n\nSolid\, liquid\, or gaseous material passed from the inside of a cell through a plasma membrane to the outside as a result of cell activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16080
name: self-cleaning of water
def: "Water purification by natural biological and physicochemical processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16081
name: self-purification of the atmosphere
def: "Purification of the atmosphere from contaminants by natural biological and physicochemical processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16082
name: semiochemical
def: "Substance produced by plants or animals\, or a synthetic analogue thereof\, that evokes a behavioral response in individuals of the producing species or other species \(e.g.\, allomones\, kairomones\, pheromones\, and synomones\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16083
name: semipermeable membrane
def: "Membrane that will preferentially allow certain molecules or ions to pass through it while preventing the passage of others."
synonym: "differentially permeable membrane" []
synonym: "selectively membrane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16084
name: sensory effect level
def: "Intensity\, where the detection threshold level is defined as the lower limit of the perceived intensity range \(by convention\, the lowest concentration that can be detected in \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of the cases in which it is present\).\nQuality\, where the recognition threshold level is defined as the lowest concentration at which the sensory effect can be recognized correctly in \\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\) of the cases.\nAcceptability and annoyance\, where the nuisance threshold level is defined as the concentration at which not more than a small proportion of the population\, less than \\\(\\pu\{5\\%\}\\\)\, experiences annoyance for a small part of the time\, less than \\\(\\pu\{2\\%\}\\\).Note\: Since annoyance will be influenced by a number of factors\, a nuisance threshold level cannot be set on the basis of concentration alone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16085
name: short-term exposure limit
def: "Fifteen-minute time-weighted average \(TWA\) exposure recommended by ACGIH which should not be exceeded at any time during a workday\, even if the \\\(\\pu\{8 h\}\\\) TWA is within the threshold limit value\: time-weighted average\, TLV-TWA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16086
name: side-effect
def: "Action of a drug other than that desired for beneficial pharmacological effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16087
name: siderosis
def: "Pneumoconiosis resulting from the inhalation of iron dust.\nExcess of iron in the urine\, blood\, or tissues\, characterized by hemosiderin granules in urine and iron deposits in tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16088
name: sidestream smoke
def: "Cloud of small particles and gases that is given off from the end of a burning tobacco product \(cigarette\, pipe\, cigar\) between puffs and is not directly inhaled by the smoker."
synonym: "environmental tobacco smoke (ETS)" []
synonym: "secondhand smoke" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16089
name: sign
def: "Objective evidence of a disease\, deformity\, or an effect induced by an agent\, perceptible to an examining physician."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16090
name: silicosis
def: "Pneumoconiosis resulting from inhalation of silica dust."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16091
name: simulation test
def: "Procedure designed to predict the rate of biodegradation of a compound under relevant environmental conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16092
name: skeletal fluorosis
def: "Osteosclerosis due to fluoride."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16093
name: slimicide
def: "Substance intended to kill slime-producing organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16094
name: slope factor
def: "Value\, in inverse concentration or dose units\, derived from the slope of a dose-response curve; in practice\, limited to carcinogenic effects with the curve assumed to be linear at low concentrations or doses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16095
name: societal risk
def: "Total probability of harm to a human population including the probability of adverse effects to health of descendants and the probability of disruption resulting from loss of services such as industrial plant or loss of material goods and electricity."
synonym: "population risk" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16096
name: soil partition coefficient
def: "Experimental ratio of a substance’s concentration in the soil to that in the aqueous \(dissolved\) soil phase at equilibrium\: It is valid only for the specific concentration and solid/solution ratio of the test."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16097
name: solvent abuse
def: "Deliberate inhalation \(or drinking\) of volatile solvents\, in order to become intoxicated."
synonym: "solvent sniffing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16098
name: specific death rate
def: "Death rate computed for a subpopulation of individual organisms or people having a specified characteristic or attribute\, and named accordingly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16099
name: specific pathogen free
def: "Describing an animal removed from its mother under sterile conditions just prior to term and subsequently reared and kept under sterile conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16100
name: spreader
def: "Agent used in some pesticide formulations to extend the even disposition of the active ingredient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16101
name: stability half life
def: "Time required for the amount of a substance in a formulation to decrease\, for any reason\, by one-half \(\\\(\\pu\{50\\%\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "stability half time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16102
name: standard morbidity ratio
def: "Ratio of the number of patients with a particular disease observed in a study group or population to the total number of people in the group or population multiplied by \\\(\\pu\{100\}\\\)."
synonym: "standardized morbidity ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16103
name: standard mortality ratio
def: "Ratio of the number of deaths observed in the study group or population to the number of deaths that would be expected if the study population had the same specific rates as the standard population\, multiplied by \\\(\\pu\{100\}\\\)."
synonym: "standardized mortality ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16104
name: stannosis
def: "Pneumoconiosis resulting from inhalation of tin dust."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16105
name: subchronic
def: "Repeated over a short period\, usually about \\\(\\pu\{10\\%\}\\\) of the life span; an imprecise term used to describe exposures of intermediate duration."
synonym: "semichronic" []
synonym: "subacute" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16106
name: subchronic effect
def: "Biological change resulting from an environmental alteration lasting about \\\(\\pu\{10\\%\}\\\) of the lifetime of the test organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16107
name: subchronic toxicity test
def: "Animal experiment serving to study the effects produced by the test substance when administered in repeated doses \(or continually in food\, drinking water\, air\) over a period of up to about 90 days."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16108
name: subclinical effect
def: "Biological change with detectable symptoms following exposure to an agent known to cause disease either before symptoms of the disease occur or when they are absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16109
name: subfertility
def: "Fertility below the normal range for a given species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16110
name: subjective environment
def: "Surrounding conditions as perceived by persons living in these conditions."
synonym: "perceived environment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16111
name: sufficient evidence
def: "According to the USEPA’s Guidelines for Carcinogen Risk Assessment\, sufficient evidence is a collection of facts and scientific references that is definite enough to establish that an adverse effect is caused by the agent in question."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16112
name: suggested no-adverse-response level
def: "Maximum dose or concentration that on current understanding is likely to be tolerated by an exposed organism without producing any harm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16113
name: suicide reaction
def: "Formation of irreversible cleavage complexes \(also referred to as 'suicide complexes'\) leading to cell death."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16114
name: summary sheet
def: "Two-to-four page summary of a risk assessment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16115
name: summation
def: "Process of addition of separate postsynaptic responses caused by stimuli that are adjacent in time and space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16116
name: Superfund
def: "Federal authority\, established by the U.S. Comprehensive Environmental Response\, Compensation\, and Liability Act \(CERCLA\) in 1980\, to respond directly to releases or threatened releases \(such as from landfills or waste disposal areas\) of hazardous substances that may endanger health or welfare."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16117
name: superoxide dismutase
def: "Enzymatic antioxidant that removes the potentially toxic superoxide ion \(\\\(\\ce\{O2^\{-\}\}\\\)\) by disproportionating it to \\\(\\ce\{O2\}\\\) and hydrogen peroxide \(\\\(\\ce\{H2O2\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16118
name: surrogate
def: "Relatively well studied toxicant whose properties are assumed to apply to an entire chemically and toxicologically related class."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16119
name: susceptible
def: "Describing a group of organisms more vulnerable to a given exposure than the majority of the population to which they belong."
synonym: "vulnerable" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16120
name: sympatholytic
def: "adj.\, Blocking transmission of impulses from the adrenergic \(sympathetic\) postganglionic fibers to effector organs or tissues.\nn.\, Agent that blocks transmission of impulses from the adrenergic \(sympathetic\) postganglionic fibers to effector organs or tissues."
synonym: "anti-adrenergic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16121
name: symptom
def: "Any subjective evidence of a disease or an effect induced by a substance as perceived by the affected subject."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16122
name: symptomatology
def: "General description of all of the signs and symptoms of exposure to a toxicant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16123
name: synergist
def: "Substance that contributes more than additively to a mutual effect with another substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16124
name: synomone
def: "Semiochemical that is produced by one organism inducing a response in an organism of another species that is favorable to both the emitter and the responding organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16125
name: synteny
def: "Property of genes which reside on the same chromosome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16126
name: systematic sample
def: "Subset selected according to some simple rule such as specified date or alphabetic classification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16127
name: tachy-
def: "Prefix meaning rapid as in tachycardia and tachypnoea."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16128
name: tachypnoea
def: "Abnormally fast breathing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16129
name: taeniacide
def: "Substance intended to kill tapeworms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16130
name: target population
def: "Collection of individuals\, items\, measurements\, etc. about which inferences are required\: The term is sometimes used to indicate the population from which a sample is drawn and sometimes to denote any reference population about which inferences are needed.\nGroup of persons for whom an intervention is planned."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16131
name: temporary acceptable daily intake
def: "Value for the acceptable daily intake \(ADI\) proposed for guidance when data are sufficient to conclude that use of the substance is safe over the relatively short period of time required to generate and evaluate further safety data\, but are insufficient to conclude that use of the substance is safe over a lifetime."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16132
name: temporary maximum residue limit
def: "Regulatory value established for a specified\, limited time when only a temporary acceptable daily intake has been established for the pesticide concerned or\, with the existence of an agreed acceptable daily intake\, the available residue data are inadequate for firm maximum residue recommendations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16133
name: testing of chemicals
def: "In toxicology\, evaluation of the therapeutic and potentially toxic effects of substances by their application through relevant routes of exposure with appropriate organisms or biological systems so as to relate effects to dose following application.\nIn chemistry\, qualitative or quantitative analysis by the application of one or more fixed methods and comparison of the results with established standards."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16134
name: theoretical maximum daily intake
def: "Predicted maximum daily intake of a residue\, assuming that it is present at the maximum residue level and that average daily consumption of foods per person is represented by assessed regional diets\: It is expressed in milligrams of residue per person per day."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16135
name: therapeutic cloning
def: "Generation and manipulation of stem cells with the objective of deriving cells of a particular organ or tissue to treat a disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16136
name: threshold
def: "Dose or exposure concentration below which a defined effect will not occur."
synonym: "threshold concentration" []
synonym: "threshold dose" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16137
name: threshold limit value–ceiling
def: "As defined by ACGIH\, concentration of a potentially toxic substance that should not be exceeded during any part of the working exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16138
name: threshold limit value–short-term exposure limit
def: "As defined by ACGIH\, concentration to which it is believed that workers can be exposed continuously for a short period of time without suffering from \(1\) irritation\, \(2\) chronic or irreversible tissue damage\, or \(3\) narcosis of sufficient degree to increase the likelihood of accidental injury\, impair self-rescue or materially reduce work efficiency\, and provided that the daily TLV-TWA is not exceeded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16139
name: threshold limit value–time-weighted average
def: "As defined by ACGIH\, time-weighted average concentration for a conventional \\\(\\pu\{8\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) workday and a \\\(\\pu\{40\\!\\mbox\{-\}h\}\\\) workweek\, to which it is believed nearly all workers may be repeatedly exposed\, day after day\, without adverse effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16140
name: threshold of toxicological concern
def: "Human exposure threshold value for a group of chemicals below which there should be no appreciable risk to human health."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16141
name: thyrotoxicosis
def: "Condition resulting from excessive concentrations of thyroid hormones\, as in hyperthyroidism\, characterized by bulging eyes and rapid heart rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16142
name: tidal volume
def: "Quantity of air or test gas that is inhaled and exhaled during one respiratory cycle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16143
name: time-weighted-average-exposure
def: "Concentration in the exposure medium at each measured time interval multiplied by that time interval and divided by the total time of observation."
synonym: "time-weighted-average concentration (TWAC)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16144
name: tissue dose
def: "Amount of a substance or physical agent \(radiation\) absorbed by a tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16145
name: tolerable daily intake
def: "Estimate of the amount of a potentially harmful substance \(e.g.\, contaminant\) in food or drinking water that can be ingested daily over a lifetime without appreciable health risk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16146
name: tolerable risk
def: "Probability of suffering disease or injury that can\, for the time being\, be tolerated\, taking into account the associated benefits\, and assuming that the risk is minimized by appropriate control procedures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16147
name: tolerable weekly intake
def: "Estimate of the amount of a potentially harmful substance \(e.g.\, a contaminant\) in food or drinking water that can be ingested weekly over a lifetime without appreciable health risk."
synonym: "provisional tolerable weekly intake (PTWI)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16148
name: tonic
def: "Characterized by tension\, especially muscular tension.\nMedical preparation that increases or restores normal muscular tension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16149
name: topical
def: "Applied directly to the surface of the body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16150
name: torsade de pointes
def: "Potentially lethal form of ventricular tachycardia following chronic abuse of alcohol and mainly due to hypomagnesemia."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16151
name: total diet study
def: "Study designed to establish the pattern of pesticide residue intake by a person consuming a defined diet.\nStudy undertaken to show the range and amount of various foodstuffs in a typical diet or to estimate the total amount of a specific substance in a typical diet."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16152
name: total terminal residue
def: "Summation of levels of all the residues of a defined pesticide in a food."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16153
name: toxemia
def: "Condition in which the blood contains toxins produced by body cells at a local source of infection or derived from the growth of microorganisms.\nPregnancy-related condition characterized by high blood pressure\, swelling\, and fluid retention\, and proteins in the urine."
synonym: "blood poisoning" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16154
name: toxic dose
def: "Amount of a substance that produces intoxication without lethal outcome."
synonym: "superthreshold dose" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16155
name: toxicity test
def: "Experimental study of the adverse effects of exposure of a living organism to a substance for a defined duration under defined conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16156
name: toxicogenomics
def: "Scientific subdiscipline that combines toxicology with genomics to determine how an organism’s genetic make-up influences its response to a toxic substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16157
name: toxicological data sheet
def: "Document that gives in a uniform manner data relating to the toxicology of a substance\, its production and application\, properties and methods of identification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16158
name: toxicometry
def: "Term sometimes used to indicate a combination of investigative methods and techniques for making a quantitative assessment of toxicity and the hazards of potentially toxic substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16159
name: toxicophobia
def: "Morbid dread of poisons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16160
name: toxicophoric group
def: "Structural moiety that upon metabolic activation exerts toxic effects\: The presence of a toxicophoric group indicates only potential and not necessarily actual toxicity of a drug or other substances."
synonym: "toxogenic group" []
synonym: "toxophoric group" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16161
name: toxicovigilance
def: "Active process of identification\, investigation\, and evaluation of various toxic effects in the community with a view to taking measures to reduce or control exposure\(s\) involving the substance\(s\) which produces these effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16162
name: toxification
def: "Metabolic conversion of a potentially toxic substance to a product that is more toxic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16163
name: tracer substance
def: "Substance that can be tracked through one or more reactions or systems\, often by detecting an incorporated isotope."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16164
name: transcriptome
def: "Total mRNA expressed in a cell or tissue at a given point in time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16165
name: transcriptomics
def: "Global analysis of gene expression to identify and evaluate changes in synthesis of mRNA after chemical exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16166
name: transformed cell
def: "Cell that has become genetically altered spontaneously or by incorporation of foreign DNA to produce a cell with an extended lifetime in culture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16167
name: transgene
def: "Gene from one source that has been incorporated into the genome of another organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16168
name: treatability
def: "In relation to waste water\, the amenability of substances to removal without adversely affecting the normal operation of biological treatment processes \(such as a sewage treatment plant\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16169
name: triage
def: "Process for sorting people into groups based on their need for or likely benefit from immediate medical treatment.\nNote\: Triage is used in hospital emergency rooms\, on battlefields\, and at disaster sites when limited medical resources must be allocated.\nSystem used to allocate a scarce commodity\, such as food\, to those most likely to benefit from it.\nProcess in which things are ranked in terms of importance or priority."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16170
name: trueness
def: "Closeness of agreement between the average of a theoretically infinite number of replicate measured quantity values and a reference quantity value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16171
name: tubular reabsorption
def: "Transfer of solutes from the renal tubule lumen to the tubular epithelial cell and normally from there to the peritubular fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16172
name: tumorigenic
def: "Able to cause tumors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16173
name: tumor progression
def: "Sequence of changes by which a benign tumor develops from the initial lesion to a malignant stage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16174
name: tumor suppressor gene
def: "Gene that serves to protect cells from entering a cancerous state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16175
name: two-compartment model
def: "Product of compartmental analysis requiring two compartments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16176
name: ulcer
def: "Defect\, often associated with inflammation\, occurring locally or at the surface of an organ or tissue owing to sloughing of necrotic tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16177
name: uncertainty
def: "Parameter characterizing the dispersion of the quantity values being attributed to a measurand\, based on the information used."
synonym: "measurement uncertainty" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16178
name: unit risk
def: "Upper-bound excess lifetime cancer risk estimated to result from continuous exposure to an agent at a concentration of \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu g L-1\}\\\) in water\, or \\\(\\pu\{1 \\upmu g m-3\}\\\) in air."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16179
name: upper boundary
def: "Estimate of the plausible upper limit to the true value of a quantity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16180
name: urticaria
def: "Vascular reaction of the skin marked by the transient appearance of smooth\, slightly elevated patches \(wheals\, hives\) that are redder or paler than the surrounding skin and often attended by severe itching."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16181
name: vacuole
def: "Membrane-bound cavity within a cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16182
name: validity of a measurement
def: "Expression of the degree to which a measurement measures what it purports to measure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16183
name: validity
def: "Degree to which the inferences drawn\, especially generalizations extending beyond the study sample\, are warranted when account is taken of the study methods\, the representativeness of the study sample\, and the nature of the population from which it is drawn."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16184
name: vasodilation
def: "Increase in the caliber of the blood vessels\, leading to an increased blood flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16185
name: vehicle
def: "Substance\(s\) used to formulate active ingredients for administration or use."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16186
name: ventilation
def: "Process of supplying a building or room with fresh air.\nProcess of exchange of air between the ambient atmosphere and the lungs.\nIn physiology\, the amount of air inhaled per day.\nOxygenation of blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16187
name: ventricular fibrillation
def: "Irregular heartbeat characterized by uncoordinated contractions of the ventricle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16188
name: vermicide
def: "Substance intended to kill intestinal worms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16189
name: vesicant
def: "adj.\, Producing blisters on the skin.\nn.\, Substance that causes blisters on the skin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16190
name: virtually safe dose
def: "Human exposure over a lifetime to a carcinogen which has been estimated\, using mathematical modeling\, to result in a very low incidence of cancer\, somewhere between zero and a specified incidence \(e.g.\, 1 cancer in \\\(\\pu\{1000000\}\\\) exposed people\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16191
name: virucide
def: "Substance used to control viruses."
synonym: "antiviral" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16192
name: wasting syndrome
def: "Disease marked by weight loss and atrophy of muscular and other connective tissues that is not directly related to a decrease in food and water consumption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16193
name: water potential
def: "Difference in free energy or chemical potential \(per unit molal volume\) between pure water and water in cells and solutions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16194
name: weight-of-evidence for toxicity
def: "Extent to which the available biomedical data support the hypothesis that a substance causes a defined toxic effect such as cancer in humans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16195
name: working zone
def: "Space measuring up to \\\(\\pu\{2 m\}\\\) over the level of the floor or platform that contains a worker’s permanent or temporary station."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16196
name: x-disease
def: "Hyperkeratotic disease in cattle following exposure to chlorinated dibenzo-p-dioxins\, naphthalenes\, and related compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16197
name: zero-order kinetics
def: "Kinetics of a reaction in which the rate is independent of the concentration\(s\) of the reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16198
name: zoocide
def: "Substance intended to kill animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16199
name: caged compound
def: "Molecular species that can rapidly be converted from inactive into active form by light. Typically\, photocaged compounds have a covalently attached group that can be photocleaved by electromagnetic radiation of a specific wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16200
name: charge-transfer absorption
def: "Electronic absorption corresponding to a charge-transfer transition."
synonym: "CT absorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16201
name: cis/trans photoisomerisation
def: "Photoinduced geometric isomerization about a double bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16202
name: external quantum efficiency
def: "Efficiency of light emission for solid-state light-emitting devices\, such as organic light-emitting devices \(OLEDs\). Analogous to emission quantum yield."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16203
name: Franck–Condon factor
def: "Squared modulus of the overlap integral between the vibrational wave functions \(\\\(\\mathit\{\\Theta\}\\\)\) for the initial \(0\) and final \(\\\(\\rm\{e\}\\\)\) state of a given electronic transition. \\\[\\left\\vert \\int \\mathit\{\\Theta\} _\{\\rm\{v'\}\}^\{\(\\rm\{e\}\)\} \\mathit\{\\Theta\} _\{\\rm\{v\}\}^\{\(0\)\} dQ \\right\\vert^\{2\}\\\] where the integration is over all nuclear coordinates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16205
name: Lippert–Mataga equation
def: "Correlation of the Stokes-shift\, \\\(\\Delta \\tilde\{\nu\}\\\)\, between the absorption and emission maxima\, and the orientation polarizability \\\(f\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\,n\)\\\) of the solvent\,\\\[\\Delta \\tilde\{\nu\} = 2 \\frac\{\(\\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{\\rm\{exc\}\} - \\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{\\rm\{gr\}\}\)^\{2\}\}\{c h r^\{3\}\} f\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\,n\) + \{\\rm\{const.\}\}\\\]with \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{\\rm\{exc\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{\\mu\}_\{\\rm\{gr\}\}\\\) the excited- and ground-state dipole moments of the dissolved molecule\, respectively\, \\\(c\\\) the speed of light\, \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant\, \\\(r\\\) the radius of the cavity in which the solute resides\, and \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) the 'relative static permittivity' \(formerly called dielectric constant\). The expression of the Stokes shift depends only on the absolute magnitude of the difference between dipole moments and not on the angle between the dipoles. The above equation is sometimes called the Lippert equation.\nThe orientation polarizability is given by\\\[f\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\,n\) = \\frac\{\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\} - 1\)\}\{\(2\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\} + 1\)\} - \\frac\{\(n^\{2\} - 1\)\}\{\(2n^\{2\} + 1\)\}\\\]with \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) the relative static permittivity and \\\(n\\\) the refractive index of the solvent\, \\\(f\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\,n\)\\\) ranges from \\\(\\pu\{0.001\}\\\) in cyclohexane to \\\(\\pu\{0.320\}\\\) in water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16206
name: photocaged fluorescent molecules
def: "Caged fluorophores displaying a high fluorescent enhancement after photolysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16207
name: photochemical aromatic substitution
def: "Photoinduced substitution in an aromatic compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16208
name: aromatic substitution photochemical electrophile-olefin combination
def: "Photosubstitution in electron-rich aromatic compounds\, upon reaction with electron-deficient olefins\, occurring in polar solvents through an electron-transfer pathway."
synonym: "photo-EOCAS" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16209
name: photochemical nucleophile-olefin combination - aromatic substitution
def: "Photoinduced electron-transfer reaction of an aromatic electron-acceptor in the presence of an olefinic electron donor and a nucleophile\, leading to aromatic substitution."
synonym: "photo-NOCAS" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16210
name: aromatic substitution photochemical radical-olefin combination
def: "Regioselective combination of a radical\, an olefin\, and an arene via a photoinduced electron-transfer pathway."
synonym: "photo-ROCAS" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16211
name: photochirogenesis
def: "Photochemical induction of molecular chirality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16212
name: photoenolisation
def: "A special case of phototautomerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16213
name: photorefractive effect
def: "Photoinduced change in refractive index."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16214
name: photoremovable protecting group
def: "Release of a reactive species by photolysis of a molecular entity. The release may be induced by direct excitation\, by triplet sensitization\, or by photoinduced electron transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16215
name: photostimulated unimolecular radical nucleophilic substitution
def: "Nucleophilic substitution in a substrate through processes that involve electron transfer steps. The reaction occurs through chain steps. The most extensively used method of initiation is photoinitiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16216
name: photosubstitution
def: "Photochemical process leading to a substitution reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16217
name: phototautomerisation
def: "Photoisomerization of the general form \\\[\\ce\{G-X-Y=Z \[h\nu\] X=Y-Z-G\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:16218
name: polar driving force
def: "The negative of the standard Gibbs energy change \(i.e.\, \\\(-\\Delta G^\{\\circ\}\\\)\) for outer-sphere electron transfer in polar solvents\, where the electrostatic work terms in the expression used for the calculation of the Gibbs energy of photoinduced electron transfer are in general negligibly small."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16219
name: polarisation spectroscopy
def: "In absorption experiments\, polarization spectroscopy is performed with polarized light on anisotropic samples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16221
name: spectral fluence
def: "Derivative of fluence\, \\\(H_\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\)\, with respect to wavelength\, _010366_."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16224
name: genetic epidemiology
def: "Study of the correlations between phenotypic trends and genetic variation across population groups and the application of the results of such a study to control of health problems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16225
name: risk ratio
def: "Value obtained by dividing the probability of occurrence of a specific effect in one group by the probability of occurrence of the same effect in another group\, or the value obtained by dividing the probability of occurrence of one potentially hazardous event by the probability of occurrence of another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16226
name: toxicity exposure ratio
def: "Ratio of the measure of the effects \(e.g.\, \\\(\\pu\{LD_\{50\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\pu\{LC_\{50\}\}\\\)\, NOEC\) to the estimated exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16228
name: bowtie entanglement
def: "Chain entanglement with topology similar to that of a bowtie."
synonym: "butterfly entanglement" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16229
name: acute effect
def: "Effect of finite duration occurring rapidly \(usually in the first \\\(\\pu\{24 h\}\\\) or up to \\\(\\pu\{14 d\}\\\)\) following a single dose or short exposure to a substance or radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16230
name: acute exposure
def: "Exposure of short duration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16231
name: chronic
def: "Long-term \(in relation to exposure or effect\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16232
name: chronic exposure
def: "Continued exposures occurring over an extended period of time\, or a significant fraction of the test species’ or of the group of individuals’\, or of the population’s lifetime."
synonym: "long-term exposure" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16233
name: benchmark concentration
def: "Statistical lower CL on the concentration that produces a defined response \(called the benchmark response or BMR\, usually \\\(\\pu\{5\\%\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{100\\%\}\\\)\) for an adverse effect compared to background\, defined as \\\(\\pu\{0\\%\}\\\).Dose–response curve showing BMC and BMCL \(BMC 10 % lower CL\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16234
name: physiologically based pharmacokinetic modeling
def: "Mathematical modeling of kinetic behavior of a substance\, based on measured physiological parameters."
synonym: "toxicologically based pharmacokinetic modeling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16235
name: deterministic effect
def: "Phenomenon committed to a particular outcome determined by fundamental physical principles."
synonym: "deterministic process" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16236
name: stochastic
def: "Pertaining to or arising from chance and hence obeying the laws of probability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16237
name: effect
def: "Change in biochemistry\, morphology\, physiology\, growth\, development\, or lifespan of an organism which results in impairment of functional capacity or impairment of capacity to compensate for additional stress or increase in susceptibility to other environmental influences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16238
name: elimination rate
def: "Derivative with respect to time of the concentration or amount of a substance in the body\, or a part thereof\, resulting from elimination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16239
name: local effect
def: "Change occurring at the site of contact between an organism and a toxicant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16240
name: toxicant
def: "This is the preferred term for a substance that is considered to be toxic under circumstances which are thought likely to happen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16241
name: medicine
def: "Any drug or remedy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16242
name: suspension of solid charged particles
def: "Dispersion of solid electrically charged particles\, commonly larger in size range than colloidal particles \(of approximate diameter range \\\(\\pu\{\{1\}\{-\}\{1000\} nm\}\\\)\) \(dispersed phase\)\, in water or an aqueous solution \(continuous\, liquid phase\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16243
name: suspension potentiometric cell
def: "Galvanic cell containing\, as its essential component\, a uniform or combined suspension or one of its separated parts alone\, the eqs or sediment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16244
name: anomalous liquid junction potential
def: "Electrochemical potential that arises at the reference electrode–measurement solution interface when\, during a potentiometric measurement\, the filling solution of the salt bridge of the reference electrode contacts the charged particles of a suspension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16245
name: pIon
def: "General mathematical expression form of any ion X activity\: \\\[\\rm\{p\(ion\\ X\)\} = \{\\rm\{pX\}\} = -\\lg \{\\rm\{a_\{X\}\}\} = -\\lg \(\{\\rm\{m_\{X\}\} \\gamma_\{X\}/m^\{\\circ\}\}\)\\\]\, analogously to \\\[\\pu\{pH\} = -\\lg \{\\rm\{a_\{H\}\}\} = -\\lg \(\{\\rm\{m_\{H\}\} \\gamma_\{H\}/m^\{\\circ\}\}\)\\\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16246
name: suspension pH
def: "Readings on a pH-meter scale obtained for a suspension of soil \(in water or electrolyte\) by any method for potentiometric pH measurement\, performed in a manner analogous to the pH measurement in solutions."
synonym: "soil suspension pH" []
synonym: "suspension soil pH" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16247
name: bridgehead atom
def: "Atom that is part of two or more rings in a polycyclic molecule and that is separated from another bridgehead atom by bridges all of which contain at least one other atom."
synonym: "bridgehead" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16248
name: unimolecular
def: "Feature of a reaction in which only one molecular entity is involved."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16249
name: initialisation
def: "Process for converting a starting initiator or chain transfer agent into a macro-initiator or macro-chain-transfer agent during reversible-deactivation polymerization \(RDP\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16250
name: conjugate
def: "Molecule or compound designed to support\, transport\, and/or deliver one or several active species in order to take advantage of their specificity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16251
name: scattering coefficient
def: "Negative Napierian logarithm of the ratio of the intensity of radiation scattered \(\\\(I_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\) by a non-absorbing sample to the incident intensity \(\\\(I_\{0\}\\\)\)\, divided by the path-length\, \\\(l\\\). \\\[s = -\\ln\(I_\{s\}/I_\{0\}\)/l\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:16252
name: numerical aperture
def: "For an optical fibre\, \\\(n_\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\) sin \\\(\\theta_\{\\alpha\}\\\)\, where \\\(n_\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\) is the refractive index of the exterior medium and \\\(\\theta_\{\\alpha\}\\\) is the maximum input half angle that can support propagation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16253
name: molar activity \(material\)
def: "Activity of a specified radionuclide \(\\\(\\ce\{R\}\\\)\) in a material \(\\\(\\ce\{M\}\\\)\) divided by amount of substance of that material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16254
name: Nernstian response
def: "Response of an ion-selective electrode according to a local thermodynamic equilibrium\, over a given range of activity \(or concentration\) that follows the Nernst equation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16256
name: pre-column derivatisation
def: "Technique in chromatography in which the sample is chemically derivatized before entering the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16257
name: phase volume ratio
def: "Volume of the mobile phase divided by volume of the stationary phase in a column in counter-current chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16258
name: total mobile-phase volume in column
def: "Sum of the interparticle volume of the column and intraparticle volume of the column\: \\\(V_\{\\rm\{t\}\} = V_\{0\} + V_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:16259
name: retention time
def: "Retention time of a sample component\, the molecules of which are smaller than the largest pores of the particles of the stationary phase but larger than the smallest pores."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16260
name: total mobile phase time
def: "Retention time of a sample component the molecules of which are smaller than the smallest pores of the particles of the stationary phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16261
name: alveolar period
def: "Phase in lung development beginning in utero \(about \\\(\\pu\{32\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{36\}\\\) weeks in the human fetus\) and lasting until about eight years of age."
synonym: "alveolar phase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16262
name: osteoblast
def: "Fibroblast-derived bone-forming cell that produces a collagen type-I-rich matrix; this matrix calcifies to become bone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16263
name: umbilicus
def: "Feature marking the point in the abdominal wall where the umbilical cord entered the fetus."
synonym: "belly button" []
synonym: "navel" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16264
name: dyspnea
def: "Difficulty in breathing; shortness of breath."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16265
name: neuropeptide
def: "One of a group of small proteins \(peptides\) that serve as neurotransmitters in the brain\, autonomic nervous system and neuroendocrine system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16266
name: neurotoxic esterase assay
def: "Test in which delayed neuropathy resulting from hydrolytic cleavage of organophosphate esters is studied with measurement of the responsible enzyme activity\,"
synonym: "NTE assay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16267
name: photo-induced toxicity
def: "Toxicity of a chemical in the presence of light due to the production of toxic photolysis products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16268
name: component
def: "Constituent whose amount or concentration can be varied independently in a mixture."
synonym: "independent component" []
synonym: "thermodynamic component" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:16269
name: constituent
def: "Chemical species present in a system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:16270
name: mixture
def: "Gaseous\, liquid\, or solid phase containing more than one substance\, when all the substances are treated in the same way."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00001
name: α-addition
def: "A chemical reaction resulting in a single reaction product from two or three reacting chemical species\, with formation of two new chemical bonds to the same atom in one of the reactant molecular entities. The synonymous term 1/1/addition is also used. For example\:  \(This particular example can also be viewed as an insertion reaction.\). In inorganic chemistry such $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}addition\}$ reactions\, generally to a metallic central atom\, are known as 'oxidative additions'. $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}Addition\}$ is the reverse of α-elimination or 1/1/elimination."
synonym: "1/1/addition" []
synonym: "alpha-addition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00003
name: α\, β
def: "Stereodescriptors\, used in a number of different ways.Relative stereodescriptors used in carbohydrate nomenclature to describe the configuration at the anomeric carbon by relating it to the anomeric reference atom. For simple cases the anomeric reference atom is the same as the configurational reference atom. Thus in $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\{-ᴅ-glucopyranose\}\}$ the reference atom is $\\ce\{C-5\}$ and the $\\ce\{OH\}$ at $\\ce\{C-1\}$ is on the same side as the $\\ce\{OH\}$ at $\\ce\{C-5\}$ in the Fischer projection.Relative stereodescriptors used by Chemical Abstracts Service to describe the configuration of a cyclic molecule \(including suitable polycyclic systems\) with several stereogenic centres whereby the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ side of the reference plane is the side on which the substituent with CIP priority lies at the lowest numbered stereogenic centre. The other side is $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$. Absolute stereodescriptors originally devised for steroid nomenclature. However in this sense it is only meaningful if there is an agreed absolute configuration and orientation of the structure so as to define the plane and which way up the molecule is represented. Substituents above the plane of the steroid are described as $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$ and are shown as a solid line \( or \); those below the plane are described as $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ and are shown by a broken line \( or \). The extension of this system to tetrapyrroles has been documented and it has been widely used elsewhere."
synonym: "alpha, beta" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00004
name: α-cleavage
def: "The fission of a bond originating at an atom which is adjacent to one assumed to bear the charge; the definition of $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$\, $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ cleavage then follows automatically. The process\:  would thus be described as $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}fission\}$ of a ketone with expulsion of a radical $\\ce\{R^\{1.\}\}$ The carbon atoms of the radical $\\ce\{R^\{1.\}\}$ are called the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$\, $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$\, $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}carbons\}$\, starting with the atom nearest the functional group."
synonym: "alpha-cleavage" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00005
name: α-decay
def: "Radioactive decay in which an alpha particle is emitted."
synonym: "alpha-decay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00006
name: α-effect
def: "A positive deviation of an $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}nucleophile\}$ \(a nucleophile bearing an unshared pair of electrons on an atom adjacent to the nucleophilic site\) from a Brønsted-type plot of \\\(\\log k_\{\\rm\{nuc\}\}\\\) vs. \\\(\{\\rm\{p\}\}K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) constructed for a series of related normal nucleophiles. More generally\, it is the influence of the atom bearing a lone pair of electrons on the reactivity at the adjacent site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00007
name: α-elimination
def: "A transformation of the general type\:  where the central atom Z is commonly carbon. The reverse reaction is called α-addition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00009
name: α-oxo carbenes
def: "Synonymous with acyl carbenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00010
name: α-particle
def: "Nucleus of the $\\ce\{^\{4\}He\}$ atom."
synonym: "alpha-particle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00012
name: 'A' value
def: "The conformational preference of an equatorial compared to an axial substituent in a monosubstituted cyclohexane. This steric substituent parameter equals \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) in \\\(\\pu\{kcal mol-1\}\\\) for the equatorial to axial equilibration on cyclohexane. The values are also known as 'Winstein–Holness' A values."
synonym: "Winstein–Holness A value" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00013
name: abatement
def: "Action taken to reduce air pollution which involves the use of control equipment or some new process. This refers to a reduction or lessening as opposed to elimination of a type of discharge or pollutant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00014
name: <i>abeo-</i>
def: "Bond migration\, i.e. the formal transfer of one end of a skeletal bond to another position\, with a compensating transfer of a hydrogen atom\, can be indicated by a prefix of the form '\\\(x\(y\\rightarrow z\)abeo-\\\)'. The prefix is compiled as follows\: a numeral denoting the stationary \(unchanged\) end of the migrating bond \(x\) is followed by parentheses enclosing the locant denoting the original position \(y\) from which the other end of this bond has migrated\, an arrow\, and the locant \(z\) denoting the new position to which the bond has moved. The closing parenthesis is followed by the italicized prefix abeo- \(Latin\: I go away\) to indicate bond migration. The original numbering is retained for the new compound and is used for the numbers x\, y and z. It is always necessary to specify the resulting stereochemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00016
name: abiotic
def: "Not associated with living organisms. Synonymous with abiological."
synonym: "abiological" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00017
name: abiotic transformation
def: "Process in which a substance in the environment is modified by non-biological mechanisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00018
name: absolute activation analysis
def: "A kind of activation analysis in which the elemental concentrations in the material are calculated from known nuclear constants\, irradiation and measurement parameters\, rather than by comparing with known standards."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00019
name: absolute activity
def: "The exponential of the ratio of the chemical potential\, \\\(\\mu\\\)\, to \\\(R\\\,T\\\) where \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(\\lambda=\\rm\{e\}^\{\\\,\\mu/RT\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00020
name: absolute configuration
def: "The spatial arrangement of the atoms of a chiral molecular entity \(or group\) and its stereochemical description\, e.g.\, R or S."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00021
name: absolute counting
def: "A measurement under such well-defined conditions that the activity of a sample can be derived directly from the observed counting rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00022
name: absolute electrode potential
def: "The electrode potential of a metal measured with respect to a universal reference system \(not including any additional metal/solution interface\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00023
name: absolute full energy peak efficiency
def: "Of a radiation spectrometer\, the counting efficiency when considering only the events recorded in the full energy peak."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00024
name: absolute lethal concentration
def: "Lowest concentration of a substance in an environmental medium which kills 100% of test organisms or species under defined conditions. This value is dependent on the number of organisms used in its assessment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00025
name: absolute lethal dose
def: "Lowest amount of a substance which kills 100% of test animals under defined conditions. This value is dependent on the number of organisms used in its assessment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00026
name: absolute photopeak efficiency
def: "Of a γ-ray spectrometer\, the counting efficiency when only the events recorded in the photopeak are considered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00027
name: absolute preconcentration
def: "An operation \(process\) as the result of which microcomponents are transferred from the sample of larger mass into the sample of smaller mass\, so that the concentration of the microcomponents is increased. Examples include the decrease in solvent volume during distillation or evaporation\, and the transfer of microcomponents from an aqueous solution into a smaller volume of organic solvent by extraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00028
name: absorbance
def: "Logarithm of the ratio of incident to transmitted radiant power through a sample \(excluding the effects on cell walls\). Depending on the base of the logarithm a decadic and Napierian absorbance are used."
synonym: "extinction" []
synonym: "optical density" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00029
name: absorbance matching
def: "A procedure where the concentration of a known analyte may be determined by diluting the sample with solvent until the absorbance matches the absorbance of the analyte in the reference cell. This method is particularly useful if the Beer-Lambert law does not hold."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00030
name: absorbed dose
def: "Amount of a substance absorbed into an organism or into organs and tissues of interest."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00031
name: absorbed dose
def: "Energy imparted to matter by ionizing radiation in a suitable small element of volume divided by the mass of that element of volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00032
name: absorbed electron coefficient
def: "Number of absorbed electrons per primary electron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00033
name: absorbed electrons
def: "The \(excess\) electrons present in a specimen under electron bombardment which are led to ground and measured as specimen current. The number of absorbed electrons per unit time \(or the specimen current\) equals the number of primary electrons minus the number of back scattered\, secondary and transmitted electrons per unit time. Therefore the fraction of electrons being absorbed depends on many parameters\, including the composition and thickness of the specimen\, the primary electron energy\, the electron incidence angle and local electrostatic fields when present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00034
name: absorber
def: "A device used commonly for sampling by absorption in which a gaseous or liquid material is removed from another gas or liquid by selective absorption; these include\: scrubber\, impinger\, packed column\, spray chamber\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00035
name: absorptance
def: "Ratio of the absorbed to the incident radiant power. When \\\(\\alpha \\leq 1\\\)\, \\\(\\alpha \\approx A_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(A_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the Napierian absorbance."
synonym: "absorption factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00036
name: absorption
def: "The process of one material \(absorbate\) being retained by another \(absorbent\); this may be the physical solution of a gas\, liquid\, or solid in a liquid\, attachment of molecules of a gas\, vapour\, liquid\, or dissolved substance to a solid surface by physical forces\, etc. In spectrophotometry\, absorption of light at characteristic wavelengths or bands of wavelengths is used to identify the chemical nature of molecules\, atoms or ions and to measure the concentrations of these species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00037
name: absorption coefficient
def: "Linear decadic \(\\\(a\\\)\,\\\(K\\\)\) and Napierian absorption coefficients \(\\\(\\alpha \\\)\) are equal to the corresponding absorbances divided by the optical path length through the sample. The molar absorption coefficients \(decadic \\\(\\varepsilon\\\)\, Napierian \\\(\\kappa \\\)\) are the linear absorption coefficients divided by the amount concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00038
name: absorption cross-section
def: "Molar Napierian absorption coefficient divided by the Avogadro constant. When integrated against the logarithm of wavenumber \(or frequency\) it is called the integrated absorption cross-section."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00040
name: absorption intensity
def: "Differently defined physical quantities describing the absorption of radiation by a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00041
name: absorption line
def: "A narrow range of wavelengths in which a substance absorbs light; a series of discrete absorption lines can be used as an unambiguous identification for many relatively simple chemical species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00042
name: absorption pathlength
def: "The length of the radiation path through the absorbing medium; it is equal to the cell path length\, \\\(l\\\)\, in the case of single-pass cells at normal incidence of radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00043
name: absorption spectrum
def: "The wavelength dependence of the absorption cross-section \(or absorption coefficient\); usually represented as a plot of absorption cross-section versus wavelength \\\(\\lambda\\\) \(or \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{\\lambda\}\\\)\) of the light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00044
name: absorptivity
def: "Absorptance divided by the optical path length. For very low attenuance it approximates the absorption coefficient \[within the approximation \\\(\(1 - e^\{-A\}\) \\sim A\\\)\]. The use of this term is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00045
name: abstraction
def: "A chemical reaction or transformation\, the main feature of which is the bimolecular removal of an atom \(neutral or charged\) from a molecular entity. For example\:  \(proton abstraction from acetone\)  \(hydrogen abstraction from methane\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00046
name: abstraction process
def: "In abstraction and extraction processes\, an adsorptive or adsorbate species extracts an adsorbed atom or a lattice atom respectively. Abstraction process\: Extraction process\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00048
name: abundance sensitivity
def: "The ratio of the maximum ion current recorded at a mass \\\(m\\\) to the ion current arising from the same species recorded at an adjacent mass \(\\\(m\\pm 1\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00050
name: accelerating voltage scan
def: "This is an alternative method of producing a momentum \(mass\) spectrum in magnetic deflection instruments by varying the accelerating voltage. This scan can also be used\, in conjunction with a fixed radial electric field\, to produce an ion kinetic energy spectrum."
synonym: "high voltage scan" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00051
name: acceleration
def: "Vector quantity equal to the derivative of velocity with respect to time. For the acceleration of free fall the symbol \\\(g\\\) is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00053
name: acceleration of free fall
def: "Acceleration of free fall in vacuum due to gravity. Also called acceleration due to gravity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00056
name: acceptor number
def: "A quantitative measure of Lewis acidity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00057
name: accommodation coefficient
def: "A measure of the efficiency of capture of molecules or atoms which collide with aerosol particles\, cloud droplets\, etc. The accommodation coefficient is the fraction of the collisions which result in the capture of the molecules \(atoms\, radicals\, etc.\) by the particle\, cloud droplet\, etc.; fraction of colliding molecules which are not reflected but which enter the surface of an aqueous aerosol. Synonymous with sticking coefficient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00058
name: accretion
def: "The process by which aerosols grow in size by external addition of various chemical species; a form of agglomeration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00059
name: accuracy
def: "Ability of a measuring instrument to give responses close to a true value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00060
name: accuracy
def: "Closeness of the agreement between the result of a measurement and a true value of the measurand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00061
name: acenes
def: "Polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons consisting of fused benzene rings in a rectilinear arrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00062
name: acetals
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2C\(OR'\)2\}$ \($\\ce\{R'\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\) and thus diethers of geminal diols. Originally\, the term was confined to derivatives of aldehydes \(one $\\ce\{R = H\}$\)\, but it now applies equally to derivatives of ketones \(neither $\\ce\{R = H\}$\). Mixed acetals have different $\\ce\{R'\}$ groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00064
name: acetonides
def: "Cyclic acetals derived from acetone and diols\, usually vicinal diols\, or polyhydroxy compounds. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00065
name: acetylene black
def: "A special type of carbon black formed by an exothermic decomposition of acetylene. It is characterized by the highest degree of aggregation and crystalline orientation when compared with all types of carbon black."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00066
name: acetylenes
def: "Acyclic \(branched or unbranched\) and cyclic \(with or without side chain\) hydrocarbons having one or more carbon–carbon triple bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00067
name: acetylides
def: "Compounds arising by replacement of one or both hydrogen atoms of acetylene \(ethyne\) by a metal or other cationic group. E.g.\, $\\ce\{NaC#CH\}$ monosodium acetylide. By extension\, analogous compounds derived from terminal acetylenes\, $\\ce\{RC#CH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00068
name: Acheson graphite
def: "A synthetic graphite made by the Acheson process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00071
name: acid
def: "A molecular entity or chemical species capable of donating a hydron \(proton\) \(see Brønsted acid\) or capable of forming a covalent bond with an electron pair \(see Lewis acid\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00072
name: acid anhydrides
def: "Compounds consisting of two acyl groups bonded to the same oxygen atom acyl-O-acyl. Symmetric and mixed anhydrides have identical and different acyl groups\, respectively. Examples\,acetic anhydride  acetic benzenesulfonic anhydride  \(thiobenzoic\) anhydride"
synonym: "anhydrides" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00075
name: acid–base indicator
def: "An acid or base which exhibits a colour change on neutralization by the basic or acidic titrant at or near the equivalence point of a titration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00077
name: acid deposition
def: "The acids deposited by transfer from the atmosphere either by precipitation \(rain\, fog\, snow\, etc.\)\, called wet deposition\, or by direct transfer to the surface of the earth induced by the flow of acid-containing air masses over the earth's surface\, called dry deposition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00079
name: acidity
def: "Of a compound\: For Brønsted acids the tendency of a compound to act as a hydron donor. It can be quantitatively expressed by the acid dissociation constant of the compound in water or some other specified medium. For Lewis acids it relates to the association constants of Lewis adducts and π adducts.Of a medium\: The use of the term is mainly restricted to a medium containing Brønsted acids\, where it means the tendency of the medium to hydronate a specific reference base. It is quantitatively expressed by the appropriate acidity function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00080
name: acidity constant
def: "The equilibrium constant \\\(K_\{\\rm\{a\}n\}\\\) for splitting off the nth proton from a charged or uncharged acid\, to be defined. One may write \\\(K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) for \\\(K_\{\\rm\{a\}1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00081
name: acidity function
def: "Any function that measures the thermodynamic hydron-donating or -accepting ability of a solvent system\, or a closely related thermodynamic property\, such as the tendency of the lyate ion of the solvent system to form Lewis adduct. \(The term 'basicity function' is not in common use in connection with basic solutions.\) Acidity functions are not unique properties of the solvent system alone\, but depend on the solute \(or family of closely related solutes\) with respect to which the thermodynamic tendency is measured. Commonly used acidity functions refer to concentrated acidic or basic solutions. Acidity functions are usually established over a range of composition of such a system by UV/VIS spectrophotometric or NMR measurements of the degree of hydronation \(protonation or Lewis adduct formation\) for the members of a series of structurally similar indicator bases \(or acids\) of different strength\: the best known of these functions is the Hammett acidity function \\\(H_\{0\}\\\) \(for uncharged indicator bases that are primary aromatic amines\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00082
name: acidosis
def: "Pathological condition in which the hydrogen ion substance concentration of body fluids is above normal and hence the pH of blood falls below the reference interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00083
name: acid rain
def: "Rain with \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\) values &lt; about 5; commonly results from acids formed from pollutants. 'Pure' rain water equilibrated with atmospheric $\\ce\{CO2\}$ and naturally occurring acids in relatively clean air usually has a \\\(\\rm\{pH\} > 5\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00085
name: <i>aci</i>-nitro compounds
def: "A class name for hydrocarbylideneazinic acids\, $\\ce\{R2C=N^\{+\}\(–O^\{−\}\)OH\}$. The use of aci-nitro as a prefix in systematic nomenclature to name specific compounds is abandoned."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00086
name: actinic flux
def: "The quantity of light available to molecules at a particular point in the atmosphere and which\, on absorption\, drives photochemical processes in the atmosphere. It is calculated by integrating the spectral radiance \\\(L \\left\(\\lambda\,\\\,\\theta\,\\\,\\varphi\\\,\\right\)\\\) over all directions of incidence of the light\, \\\(S_\{\\lambda\} = \\int _\{\\theta\}\\\, \\int _\{\\phi\} \\\{L\\left \(\\lambda\,\\theta\,\\varphi \\right\)/\(hc/\\lambda\)\\\}\\\, \\rm\{sin\}\\\,\\theta\\\: \\rm\{d\}\\theta\\\: \\rm\{d\}\\varphi\\\). If the radiance is expressed in \\\(\\pu\{J m-2 s-1 st-1 nm-1\}\\\) and \\\(hc/\\lambda\\\) is the energy per quantum of light of wavelength \\\(\\lambda\\\)\, the actinic flux has units of \\\(\\rm\{quanta cm\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\). This important quantity is one of the terms required in the calculation of j-values\, the first order rate coefficients for photochemical processes in the sunlight-absorbing\, trace gases in the atmosphere. The actinic flux is determined by the solar radiation entering the atmosphere and by any changes in this due to atmospheric gases and particles \(e.g. Rayleigh scattering absorption by stratospheric ozone\, scattering and absorption by aerosols and clouds\)\, and reflections from the ground. It is therefore dependent on the wavelength of the light\, on the altitude and on specific local environmental conditions. The actinic flux has borne many names \(e.g. flux\, flux density\, beam irradiance\, actinic irradiance\, integrated intensity\) which has caused some confusion. It is important to distinguish the actinic flux from the spectral irradiance\, which refers to energy arrival on a flat surface having fixed spatial orientation \(\\\(\\pu\{J m-2 nm-1\}\\\)\) given by\: \\\[E_\{\\lambda\} = \\int _\{\\theta\}\\\, \\int _\{\\phi\} L\\left \(\\lambda\,\\theta\,\\varphi \\right \)\\\, \\rm\{cos\}\\\,\\theta  \\\: \\rm\{sin\}\\\,\\theta\\\: \\rm\{d\}\\theta\\\: \\rm\{d\}\\varphi\\\] The actinic flux does not refer to any specific orientation because molecules are oriented randomly in the atmosphere. This distinction is of practical relevance\: the actinic flux \(and therefore a j-value\) near a brightly reflecting surface \(e.g. over snow or above a thick cloud\) can be a factor of three higher than that near a non-reflecting surface. The more descriptive name of spectral spheradiance is suggested for the quantity herein called actinic flux."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00087
name: actinometer
def: "A chemical system or physical device which determines the number of photons in a beam integrally or per unit time. This name is commonly applied to devices used in the ultraviolet and visible wavelength ranges. For example\, solutions of iron\(III\) oxalate can be used as a chemical actinometer\, while bolometers\, thermopiles\, and photodiodes are physical devices giving a reading that can be correlated to the number of photons detected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00088
name: action spectrum
def: "A plot of a relative biological or chemical photoresponse \(= \\\(\\Delta y\\\)\) per number of incident photons against wavelength or energy of radiation under the same radiant power of light. This form of presentation is frequently used in the studies of biological or solid state systems\, where the nature of the absorbing species is unknown. This type of action spectrum is sometimes called spectral responsivity or sensitivity spectrum. The precise action spectrum is a plot of the spectral or quantum effectiveness. By contrast\, a plot of the biological or chemical change or response per absorbed photon \(quantum efficiency\) versus wavelength is the efficiency spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00089
name: activated adsorption process
def: "If the temperature coefficient of the rate of adsorption is substantial\, an adsorption process is said to be activated \(i.e. to have a significant activation energy\). In this case\, the sticking coefficient is small. In general\, the activation energy of activated adsorption is a function of coverage and it usually increases with increasing coverage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00090
name: activated carbon
def: "A porous carbon material\, a char which has been subjected to reaction with gases\, sometimes with the addition of chemicals\, e.g. $\\ce\{ZnCl2\}$\, before\, during or after carbonization in order to increase its adsorptive properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00091
name: activated charcoal
def: "A traditional term for activated carbon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00092
name: activated complex
def: "An activated complex\, often characterized by the superscript ‡\, is defined as that assembly of atoms which corresponds to an arbitrary infinitesimally small region at or near the col \(saddle point\) of a potential energy surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00093
name: activation
def: "This word is used in different senses\:   Input of external energy into a chemical system is said to bring about activation of the system.   An added substance that increases the rate of a catalysed reaction is known as an activator\, and the effect is called activation. The degree of activation \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) is defined by\: \\\[\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{a\}\}=\\frac\{v\\\,-\\\,v_\{0\}\}\{v_\{0\}\} = \\frac\{v\}\{v_\{0\}\} - 1\\\] where \\\(v_\{0\}\\\) is the rate of the catalysed reaction in the absence of the activator and \\\(v\\\) is the rate of the catalysed reaction in the presence of the activator.  When some of the energy required for a reaction to occur is provided by a previous exothermic chemical reaction there is said to be chemical activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00094
name: activation
def: "The process of transition from the passive to the active state by removal of the passivating film. A necessary condition for activation is an electrode potential negative to the equilibrium potential of formation of the passivating film. Activation is achieved by cathodic currents\, by a reduced substance in the adjacent solution\, or by contact with an electronic conductor having a suitably negative corrosion potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00095
name: activation
def: "The process of inducing radioactivity by irradiation. In general\, a specification is added of the type of incident radiation \(e.g. nuclear\, neutron\, photon\) or its energy \(e.g. thermal\, fast\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00096
name: activation analysis
def: "A kind of elemental or isotopic analysis based on the measurement of characteristic radiation from nuclides formed directly or indirectly by activation of the test portion. In general\, a specification is added of the type of the incident radiation \(e.g. neutron\, photon\) and its energy \(e.g. thermal\, fast\)."
synonym: "nuclear activation analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00101
name: activation cross-section
def: "The cross-section for the formation of a radionuclide by a specified reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00102
name: activation energy
def: "An empirical parameter characterizing the exponential temperature dependence of the rate coefficient\, \\\(k\\\)\, \\\(E_\{a\} = R\\\, T^\{2\}\\frac\{\\rm\{d\(ln\}k\\rm\{\)\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}T\}\\\)\, where \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature. The term is also used for threshold energies in electronic potential surfaces\, in which case the term requires careful definition."
synonym: "Arrhenius activation energy" []
synonym: "energy of activation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00103
name: activation reaction
def: "Process leading from the reactants to the formation of an activated complex or transition state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00104
name: activator
def: "A substance\, other than the catalyst or one of the substrates\, that increases the rate of a catalysed reaction without itself being consumed; the process is called activation. An activator of an enzyme-catalysed reaction may be called enzyme activator\, if it acts by binding to the enzyme."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00107
name: active metal
def: "A metal corroding in the active state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00108
name: active site
def: "The term is often applied to those sites for adsorption which are the effective sites for a particular heterogeneous catalytic reaction. The terms active site and active centre are often used as synonyms\, but active centre may also be used to describe an ensemble of sites at which a catalytic reaction takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00109
name: active solid
def: "A porous solid with adsorptive properties by means of which chromatographic separations may be achieved. The separations resulting from this action follow laws different from those deriving from the partitioning action of the liquid phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00111
name: active state
def: "Corrosion in the active state occurs by direct transfer \(which may involve one or several steps\) of metal ions from the metallic phase to the adjacent electrolyte. The corrosion current of an active metal usually increases when the electrode potential is made more positive\, other conditions remaining constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00112
name: active transport
def: "The carriage of a solute across a biological membrane from low to high concentration which requires the expenditure of \(metabolic\) energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00114
name: activity
def: "The number of nuclear decays occurring in a given quantity of material in a small time interval\, divided by that time interval. Often\, this term is referred to as absolute activity. Symbol \\\(A=- \\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}N\}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}\\\). Synonymous with disintegration rate or decay rate."
synonym: "decay rate" []
synonym: "disintegration rate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00115
name: activity
def: "Defined by the equation \\\[a = e^\{\\frac\{\\mu - \\mu^\{o\}\}\{R\\\,T\}\}\\\] where \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(\\mu\\\) the chemical potential and \\\(\\mu^\{o\}\\\) the standard chemical potential the definition of which depends on the choice of the standard state."
synonym: "relative activity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00116
name: activity coefficient
def: "The activity coefficient \\\(f_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) of a substance B in a liquid or solid mixture containing mole fractions \\\(x_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(x_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\)\, ... of the substances B\, C\, ... is a dimensionless quantity defined in terms of the chemical potential \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) of B in the mixture by\: \\\[R\\ T\\ \\ln \(x_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ f_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\)=\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{cd\}\,\\\,T\,\\\,P\,\\\,x\\right\)- \\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{*\}\\left\(\\rm\{cd\}\,\\\,T\,\\\,p\\right\)\\\] where \\\(x\\\) denotes the set of mole fractions \\\(x_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(x_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\)\, ... . The activity coefficient \\\(\\gamma _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) of a solute B in a solution \(especially a dilute liquid solution\) containing molalities \\\(m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\)\, ... of solutes B\, C\, ... in a solvent A is a dimensionless quantity defined in terms of the chemical potential \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) by\: \\\[R\\ T\\ \\ln \\left\(\\frac\{m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ \\gamma _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{m^\{\\ominus\}\}\\right\)=\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}- \\left\\\{\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}- R\\ T\\ \\ln \\left\(\\frac\{m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{m^\{\\ominus \}\}\\right\)\\right\\\}^\{\\infty \}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00119
name: acylals
def: "Diesters of geminal diols; i.e.  E.g. benzylidene diacetate\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00121
name: acyl carbenes
def: "Any compound . In organic chemistry\, an unspecified acyl carbene is commonly a carboxylic acyl carbene\, ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00123
name: acyl groups
def: "Groups formed by removing one or more hydroxy groups from oxoacids that have the general structure $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$ \($l \neq 0$\)\, and replacement analogues of such acyl groups. In organic chemistry an unspecified acyl group is commonly a carboxylic acyl group. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00124
name: acyl halides
def: "Compounds consisting of an acyl group bonded to halogen\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3S\(=O\)2Cl\}$\, methanesulfonyl chloride; $\\ce\{CH3COCl\}$\, acetyl chloride; cyclohexanecarboximidoyl chloride\,"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00126
name: acyloins
def: "$\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}Hydroxy\}$ ketones\, $\\ce\{RCH\(OH\)C\(=O\)R\}$. So named from the fact that they are formally derived from reductive coupling of carboxylic acyl groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00127
name: acyloxyl radicals
def: "Oxygen-centered radicals consisting of an acyl radical bonded to an oxygen atom. E.g. ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00129
name: acyl species
def: "Acyl intermediates include acyl anions\, acyl radicals and acyl cations \(synonym acylium ions\) which are formally derived from oxoacids $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$ \($l \neq 0$\) by removal of a hydroxyl cation $\\ce\{HO^\{+\}\}$\, a hydroxyl radical $\\ce\{HO^\{.\}\}$ or a hydroxyl anion $\\ce\{HO^\{-\}\}$\, respectively\, and replacement analogues of such intermediates. Acyl anions\, radicals and cations can formally be represented by canonical forms having a negative charge\, an unpaired electron or a positive charge on the acid-generating element of the oxoacid.Acyl anions\, e.g. Acyl radicals\, e.g. Acyl cations\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00132
name: addition
def: "Refers to addition reaction or addition transformation.Loosely\, the formation of an adduct. \(For an example\, see Lewis acid.\)Loosely\, any association or attachment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00133
name: addition reaction
def: "A chemical reaction of two or more reacting molecular entities\, resulting in a single reaction product containing all atoms of all components\, with formation of two chemical bonds and a net reduction in bond multiplicity in at least one of the reactants. The reverse process is called an elimination reaction. The addition may occur at only one site \(α-addition\, 1/1/addition\)\, at two adjacent sites \(1/2/addition\) or at two non-adjacent sites \(1/3/- or 1/4/addition\, etc.\). For example\:   If the reagent or the source of the addends of an addition are not specified\, then it is called an addition transformation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00134
name: additive
def: "A substance added to a sample for any of a variety of purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00135
name: additive name
def: "The formal assembly of names for the components of a compound without loss of atoms or groups of atoms from any componentThe addition or attachment of atoms or groups of atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00136
name: additivity
def: "The process by which each chemical species present in the ion source at a certain partial pressure makes a contribution to the total mass spectrum which is the same as that which it would give if that chemical species alone were present in the ion source at a pressure equal to this certain partial pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00137
name: additivity principle
def: "The hypothesis that each of several structural features of a molecular entity makes a separate and additive contribution to a property of the substance concerned. More specifically\, it is the hypothesis that each of the several substituent groups in a parent molecule makes a separate and additive contribution to the standard Gibbs energy change \(or Gibbs energy of activation\) corresponding to a particular equilibrium \(or rate of reaction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00138
name: adduct
def: "A new chemical species AB\, each molecular entity of which is formed by direct combination of two separate molecular entities A and B in such a way that there is change in connectivity\, but no loss\, of atoms within the moieties A and B. Stoichiometries other than 1\:1 are also possible\, e.g. a bis-adduct \(2\:1\). An intramolecular adduct can be formed when A and B are groups contained within the same molecular entity. This is a general term which\, whenever appropriate\, should be used in preference to the less explicit term complex. It is also used specifically for products of an addition reaction. For examples\, see Lewis adduct\, Meisenheimer adduct\, π-adduct."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00139
name: adduct ion
def: "An ion formed by interaction of two species\, usually an ion and a molecule\, and often within the ion source\, to form an ion containing all the constituent atoms of one species as well as an additional atom or atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00140
name: adhesional wetting
def: "A process in which an adhesional joint is formed between two phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00141
name: adiabatic
def: "This word is used with various different meanings\, and when it is used it should be defined. In thermodynamics 'adiabatic' is used in a macroscopic sense to refer to a process occurring in a thermally insulated system\, so that there is no flow of heat to or from the surroundings. In reaction dynamics\, the word has been used in a microscopic sense\, with a range of meanings which have only a tenuous relationship to the thermodynamic meaning or the etymology. Whereas the thermodynamic meaning relates to conditions imposed on a process by an observer\, the microscopic meaning relates to conditions under which the process occurs naturally. The microscopic meanings\, as used in reaction dynamics\, all have in common the feature that quantum states remain unchanged during the course of reaction. Different quantum states may be referred to\: A reaction that is not adiabatic is referred to as nonadiabatic or diabatic\, and some workers make a distinction between the two words.A reaction in which there is no change of electronic state or multiplicity has been called adiabatic\, or more specifically electronically adiabatic. A reaction in which there is no change of vibrational state during the course of reaction has been said to be vibrationally adiabatic. More loosely\, the expression has been applied to a process in which excess vibrational energy in the reactants appears as vibrational energy in the products\, or in which ground-state vibration in the reactants leads to ground state vibration in the products.A reaction in which excess rotational energy in the reactants appears as rotational energy in the products\, or in which ground-state rotation in the reactants leads to ground-state rotation in the products\, has been referred to as rotationally adiabatic.In the Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel–Marcus \(RRKM\) theory of unimolecular reactions\, a degree of freedom whose quantum number is more or less preserved during energization and subsequent reaction has been called 'adiabatic'; the word 'inactive' has also been applied to it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00142
name: adiabatic electron transfer
def: "Electron transfer process in which the reacting system remains on a single electronic surface in passing from reactants to products. For adiabatic electron transfer the electronic transmission factor is close to unity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00143
name: adiabatic ionization
def: "A process whereby an electron is either removed from or added to an atom or molecule producing an ion in its ground state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00144
name: adiabatic lapse rate
def: "The rate of decrease in temperature with increase in altitude of an air parcel which is expanding slowly to a lower atmospheric pressure without exchange of heat; for a descending parcel it is the rate of increase in temperature with decrease in altitude. Theory predicts that for dry air it is equal to the acceleration of gravity divided by the specific heat of dry air at constant pressure \(approximately \\\(9.8\\ ^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} \\rm\{C\}\\ \\rm\{km\}^\{-1\}\\\)\). The moist adiabatic lapse rate is less than the dry adiabatic lapse rate and depends on the moisture content of the air mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00145
name: adiabatic photoreaction
def: "Within the 'Born-Oppenheimer' approximation\, a reaction of an excited state species that occurs on a single 'potential-energy surface'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00146
name: adiabatic transition-state theory
def: "A form of transition-state theory in which the system is assumed to preserve its internal quantum states as it moves over the potential-energy surface. A detailed state-to-state scattering theory version of adiabatic transition-state theory has been referred to as the adiabatic channel model."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00147
name: adiabatic treatments of reaction rates
def: "These are treatments of reaction rates in which the system is assumed to remain on a single potential-energy surface during the entire course of reaction\, or to remain in a conserved internal state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00148
name: adjacent re-entry model
def: "A model of crystallinity in which chain folds regularly connect adjacent stems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00150
name: adjuvant
def: "In pharmacology\, a substance added to a drug to speed or increase the action of the main component.In immunology\, a substance \(such as aluminium hydroxide\) or an organism \(such as bovine tuberculosis bacillus\) which increases the response to an antigen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00152
name: adsorbate
def: "A molecular species of gas\, dissolved substance or liquid which adheres to or is adsorbed in an extremely thin surface layer of a solid substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00153
name: adsorbent
def: "A condensed phase at the surface of which adsorption may occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00154
name: adsorber
def: "Solid material used to capture either a gas or liquid; often activated carbon is employed as the solid because of its high surface area per unit mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00155
name: adsorption
def: "An increase in the concentration of a dissolved substance at the interface of a condensed and a liquid phase due to the operation of surface forces. Adsorption can also occur at the interface of a condensed and a gaseous phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00156
name: adsorption capacity
def: "For strongly adsorbed solutes of limited solubility\, the value of the amount of adsorbed substance reached in a saturated solution is called the adsorption capacity of the adsorbent for a specific solute; its value depends also\, in general\, on the nature and\, in the case of more than two components\, on the relative composition of the bulk liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00157
name: adsorption chromatography
def: "Chromatography in which separation is based mainly on differences between the adsorption affinities of the sample components for the surface of an active solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00158
name: adsorption complex
def: "The entity constituted by the adsorbate and the part of the adsorbent to which it is bound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00159
name: adsorption current
def: "A faradaic current whose magnitude depends on the applied potential\, and\, at any particular applied potential\, on the rate or extent of the adsorption of an electroactive substance \(or the product obtained from the reduction or oxidation of an electroactive substance\) onto the surface of the indicator or working electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00161
name: adsorption hysteresis
def: "Adsorption hysteresis is said to occur when adsorption and desorption values deviate from one another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00162
name: adsorption indicator
def: "A substance which is adsorbed or desorbed with concomitant colour change at or near the equivalence point of a titration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00163
name: adsorption isobar
def: "The function relating the amount\, mass\, or volume\, or corresponding excess of substance adsorbed by a given amount of solid\, to the temperature at constant pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00164
name: adsorption isostere
def: "The function relating the equilibrium pressure to the temperature at a constant value of the amount\, or excess amount\, of substance adsorbed by a given amount of solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00166
name: adsorptive
def: "The material that is present in one or other \(or both\) of the bulk phases and capable of being adsorbed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00168
name: advection
def: "The transport of air\, its properties \(such as heat\)\, trace materials\, fog\, cold air\, etc.\, solely by mass motion of the atmosphere\, generally in a horizontal direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00169
name: aeration
def: "The process by which a volume filled with a liquid becomes permeated with air or another gas; aeration is often accomplished by spraying the liquid into the air\, bubbling air through a liquid or agitating the liquid to promote surface absorption of air."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00170
name: aerobe
def: "An organism that needs oxygen for respiration and hence for growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00171
name: aerobic
def: "Requiring molecular oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00172
name: aerobic conditions
def: "Conditions for growth or metabolism in which the organism is sufficiently supplied with oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00173
name: aerogel
def: "Gel comprised of a microporous solid in which the dispersed phase is a gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00174
name: aerometer
def: "Instrument used to measure the density of gases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00175
name: aerometric measurement
def: "A measurement of the temperature\, pressure\, air motion \(velocity vectors\)\, or other physical measurement to characterize an air mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00176
name: aerosol
def: "Sol in which the dispersed phase is a solid\, a liquid or a mixture of both and the continuous phase is a gas \(usually air\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00177
name: affinity chromatography
def: "An expression characterizing the particular variant of chromatography in which the unique biological specificity of the analyte and ligand interaction is utilized for the separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00178
name: affinity of reaction
def: "Negative partial derivative of Gibbs energy with respect to extent of reaction at constant pressure and temperature. It is positive for spontaneous reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00179
name: after mass analysis
def: "The sum of all the separate ion currents carried by the different ions contributing to the spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00180
name: ageing of precipitate
def: "The time-dependent change of those properties of a precipitate\, e.g. loss of water\, growth of crystals\, recrystallization\, decrease of the specific surface\, loss of coprecipitated substances\, which generally improve the filtering properties. The process of ageing is very often promoted by maintaining the precipitate and precipitation medium together at elevated temperatures for a period of time. The terms chemical\, physical and thermal ageing may be used in cases in which some of the \(usually combined\) effects named above are to be emphasized specifically."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00182
name: agglomeration
def: "Process of contact and adhesion whereby dispersed particles are held together by weak physical interactions ultimately leading to phase separation by the formation of precipitates of larger than colloidal size."
synonym: "coagulation" []
synonym: "flocculation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00183
name: agglutination
def: "An immunochemical reaction leading to the aggregation of particulate matter such as bacteria\, erythrocytes or other cells\, or synthetic particles such as plastic beads coated with antigens or antibodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00184
name: aggregate
def: "Certain materials used as catalysts or supports consist of spheroids smaller than \\\(10\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) in diameter\, cemented into larger entities. A primary particle should be defined as the smallest discrete identifiable entity and the method of identification should be mentioned \(e.g. transmission electron microscopy\, scanning electron microscopy\). An assemblage of such primary particles exhibiting an identifiable collective behaviour \(e.g. chemical nature of the aggregated primary particles\, texture of the aggregate\, resistance to mechanical separation upon grinding\) constitutes an aggregate. When describing the aggregates the criterion of identification should be mentioned. Strongly bonded aggregates are called agglomerates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00185
name: aglycon
def: "The non-sugar compound remaining after replacement of the glycosyl group from a glycoside by a hydrogen atom."
synonym: "aglycone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00186
name: agonist
def: "Substance which binds to cell receptors normally responding to naturally occurring substances and which produces a response of its own."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00187
name: agostic
def: "The term designates structures in which a hydrogen atom is bonded to both a carbon atom and a metal atom. The term is also used to characterize the interaction between a $\\ce\{CH\}$ bond and an unsaturated metal centre\, and to describe similar bonding of a transition metal with $\\ce\{Si–H\}$ compounds. The expression '$\\mu$-hydrido-bridged' is also used to describe the bridging hydrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00188
name: agranular carbon
def: "A monogranular or monolithic carbon material with homogeneous microstructure which does not exhibit any structural components distinguishable by optical microscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00190
name: air contaminant
def: "A substance\, gaseous material or aerosol\, which is present in an air mass at levels greater than in clean air. An air contaminant has been added commonly by anthropogenic activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00191
name: air-lift bioreactor
def: "A bioreactor in which the reaction medium is kept mixed and gassed by introduction of air or another gas \(mixture\) at the base of a column-like reactor equipped either with a draught tube or another device \(e.g. external tube\) by which the reactor volume is separated into a gassed and an ungassed region thus generating a vertically circulating flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00192
name: air mass
def: "A qualitative term to describe a widespread body of air with approximately uniform characteristics which had been identified at a given time over a particular region of the earth's surface. Sometimes an air mass is marked by inert tracers such as $\\ce\{SF6\}$ which may be added to it. The composition of a given air mass undergoes alteration as it migrates\, chemical changes occur\, compounds are removed by dry and wet deposition and new impurities are added to the mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00193
name: air monitoring station
def: "A site at which monitoring of the concentration of one or more pollutants is carried out \(e.g. the BAPMoN stations\, Background Air Pollution Monitoring Network of the WMO\, World Meteorological Organization\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00194
name: air pollutant
def: "A substance\, gaseous material or aerosol which has been introduced into the air \(either by human activity or by natural processes\) in sufficient concentration to produce a measurable effect on humans\, animals\, vegetation or materials \(monuments\, etc.\)\: $\\ce\{SO2\}$\, $\\ce\{NO2\}$\, $\\ce\{H2S\}$\, $\\ce\{CO\}$\, hydrocarbons\, etc."
synonym: "pollutant" []
synonym: "pollution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00195
name: air pollution
def: "Usually the presence of substances in the atmosphere\, resulting either from human activity or natural processes\, present in sufficient concentration\, for a sufficient time and under circumstances such as to interfere with comfort\, health or welfare of persons or the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00196
name: air pollution index
def: "A mathematical combination of the concentrations of air pollutants \(weighted in some fashion to reflect the estimated health impact of the specific pollutant\) which gives an approximate numerical measure of the quality of the air at a given time. These indices have little scientific basis but have been used to inform the public \(in a qualitative fashion\) of the degree of pollution present at a given time. It is recommended that the actual measured pollutant concentrations be used by all information services when possible with simultaneous reference given to the corresponding concentrations which are considered by health authorities to be hazardous to human health."
synonym: "air quality index" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00197
name: air pollution survey
def: "A study of the concentrations and geographical distribution of specified air pollutants in a given area and an assessment of the damage\, if any\, which the pollutants have caused."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00198
name: air quality characteristic
def: "One of the quantifiable properties relating to an air sample\: concentration of a constituent\, wind speed\, temperature\, etc. The quantity of air quality characteristic is the true value of the characteristic being investigated; it is recognized that in practice\, this value can only be approximated by existing methods."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00199
name: air resource management
def: "The detailed planning and the implementation of air pollution control programs designed to preserve the health and welfare of the people in the region\, the plant and animal life\, physical property\, good visibility and other factors which determine the air quality and the maintenance of an aesthetically acceptable environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00200
name: air sampling network
def: "A number of air sampling stations which are established in a given geographical region at which measurements of both pollutant concentrations and meteorological quantities \(wind speed\, direction\, rain fall\, humidity\, etc.\) are made to determine the extent and the nature of the air pollution and to establish trends in the concentrations of the air pollutants with time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00201
name: Aitken particles
def: "Aerosol particles below \\\(0.1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) in diameter. These generally are the most numerous among all particles in the air. Their concentrations can be determined with the Aitken counter which measures total particle number density. Owing to their small size\, Aitken particles contribute little to the total mass concentration of all aerosol particles; this is determined primarily by particles of diameter \\\(> 0.1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00202
name: albedo
def: "The fraction of the energy of electromagnetic radiation reflected from a body \(or surface\) relative to the energy incident upon it. The reflection of light from a surface is\, of course\, dependent on the wavelength of the light\, the nature of the surface and its angle of incidence with the surface. The term albedo usually connotes a broad wavelength band \(visible\, ultraviolet or infrared\)\, whereas the terms reflectivity and spectral albedo are used to describe the reflection of monochromatic \(single wavelength or small band of wavelengths\) radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00203
name: alcoholates
def: "Synonymous with alkoxides. Alcoholate should not be used for solvates derived from an alcohol such as $\\ce\{CaCl2.nROH\}$\, for the ending -ate often occurs in names for anions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00204
name: alcohols
def: "Compounds in which a hydroxy group\, $\\ce\{-OH\}$\, is attached to a saturated carbon atom $\\ce\{R3COH\}$. The term 'hydroxyl' refers to the radical species\, $\\ce\{HO^\{.\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00206
name: aldaric acids
def: "Polyhydroxy dicarboxylic acids having the general formula $\\ce\{HOC\(=O\)\[CH\(OH\)\]_\{n\}C\(=O\)OH\}$\, formally derived from an aldose by oxidation of both terminal carbon atoms to carboxyl groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00207
name: aldazines
def: "Azines of aldehydes $\\ce\{RCH=NN=CHR\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00208
name: aldehydes
def: "Compounds $\\ce\{RC\(=O\)H\}$\, in which a carbonyl group is bonded to one hydrogen atom and to one $\\ce\{R\}$ group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00209
name: aldimines
def: "Imines derived from aldehydes $\\ce\{RCH=NR\}$. E.g. $\\ce\{EtCH=NH\}$\, $\\ce\{PhCH=NMe\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00210
name: alditols
def: "Acyclic polyols having the general formula $\\ce\{HOCH2\[CH\(OH\)\]nCH2OH\}$ \(formally derivable from an aldose by reduction of the carbonyl group\)."
synonym: "glycitols" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00211
name: aldoketoses
def: "A now less preferred synonym for ketoaldoses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00212
name: aldonic acids
def: "Polyhydroxy acids having the general formula $\\ce\{HOCH2\[CH\(OH\)\]_\{n\}C\(=O\)OH\}$ and therefore derived from an aldose by oxidation of the aldehyde function\, e.g. ᴅ-gluconic acid."
synonym: "glyconic acids" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00213
name: aldoses
def: "Aldehydic parent sugars \(polyhydroxyaldehydes $\\ce\{H\[CH\(OH\)\]_\{n\}C\(=O\)H\}$\, $n \\ge 2$\) and their intramolecular hemiacetals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00214
name: aldoximes
def: "Oximes of aldehydes $\\ce\{RCH=NOH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00215
name: alert levels
def: "Designated concentrations of certain key pollutants at which some degree of danger to public health is expected. In many areas in which a relatively high level of pollution is often encountered\, several levels of alert are often established. For example\, a first alert may signify a potential problem exists; a second alert becomes a signal for the curtailment of certain significant emission sources; the third alert signifies the need for some specified emergency action which must be taken to ensure the public safety."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00216
name: alicyclic compounds
def: "Aliphatic compounds having a carbocyclic ring structure which may be saturated or unsaturated\, but may not be a benzenoid or other aromatic system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00217
name: aliphatic compounds
def: "Acyclic or cyclic\, saturated or unsaturated carbon compounds\, excluding aromatic compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00218
name: aliquot
def: "A known amount of a homogeneous material\, assumed to be taken with negligible sampling error. The term is usually applied to fluids. The term 'aliquot' is usually used when the fractional part is an exact divisor of the whole; the term 'aliquant' has been used when the fractional part is not an exact divisor of the whole \(e.g. a \\\(15\\ \\rm\{ml\}\\\) portion is an aliquant of \\\(100\\ \\rm\{ml\}\\\)\). When a laboratory sample or test sample is 'aliquoted' or otherwise subdivided\, the portions have been called split samples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00220
name: alkaloids
def: "Basic nitrogen compounds \(mostly heterocyclic\) occurring mostly in the plant kingdom \(but not excluding those of animal origin\). Amino acids\, peptides\, proteins\, nucleotides\, nucleic acids\, amino sugars and antibiotics are not normally regarded as alkaloids. By extension\, certain neutral compounds biogenetically related to basic alkaloids are included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00221
name: alkalosis
def: "Pathological condition in which the hydrogen ion substance concentration of body fluids is below normal and hence the pH of blood rises above the reference interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00222
name: alkanes
def: "Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having the general formula $\\ce\{C_\{n\}H_\{2n+2\}\}$\, and therefore consisting entirely of hydrogen atoms and saturated carbon atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00223
name: alkanium ions
def: "Carbocations derived from alkanes by $C$-hydronation containing at least one pentacoordinate carbon atom\, e.g. $\\ce\{^\{+\}CH5\}$ methanium\, $\\ce\{\[C2H7\]^\{+\}$ ethanium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00224
name: alkenes
def: "Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having one carbon–carbon double bond and the general formula $\\ce\{C_\{n\}H_\{2n\}\}$. Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having more than one double bond are alkadienes\, alkatrienes\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00225
name: alkoxides
def: "Compounds\, $\\ce\{ROM\}$\, derivatives of alcohols\, $\\ce\{ROH\}$\, in which $\\ce\{R\}$ is saturated at the site of its attachment to oxygen and $\\ce\{M\}$ is a metal or other cationic species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00226
name: alkoxyamines
def: "O-Alkyl hydroxylamines \(with or without substitution on $\\ce\{N\}$\) $\\ce\{R'ONR2\}$ \($\\ce\{R'\neq H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00227
name: alkylenes
def: "An old term\, which is not recommended\, for alkenes\, especially those of low molecular weight.An old term for alkanediyl groups commonly but not necessarily having the free valencies on adjacent carbon atoms\, e.g. $\\ce\{–CH\(CH3\)CH2\\bond\{–\}\}$ propylene \(systematically called propane-1\,2-diyl\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00228
name: alkyl groups
def: "Univalent groups derived from alkanes by removal of a hydrogen atom from any carbon atom $\\ce\{–C_\{n\}H_\{2n+1\}\}$. The groups derived by removal of a hydrogen atom from a terminal carbon atom of unbranched alkanes form a subclass of normal alkyl \($n$-alkyl\) groups $\\ce\{H\(CH2\)_\{n\}\}$. The groups $\\ce\{RCH2\}$\, $\\ce\{R2CH\}$ \($\\ce\{R \neq H\}$\)\, and $\\ce\{R3C\}$ \($\\ce\{R \neq H\}$\) are primary\, secondary and tertiary alkyl groups\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00230
name: alkylideneaminoxyl radicals
def: "Radicals having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=N–O^\{.\}\}$. Synonymous with iminoxyl radicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00231
name: alkylideneaminyl radicals
def: "Radicals having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=N^\{.\}\}$. Synonymous with iminyl radicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00232
name: alkylidene groups
def: "The divalent groups formed from alkanes by removal of two hydrogen atoms from the same carbon atom\, the free valencies of which are part of a double bond\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(CH3\)2C=\}$ propan-2-ylidene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00233
name: alkylidenes
def: "Carbenes $\\ce\{R2C\{\:\}\}$ formed by mono or dialkyl substitution of methylene\, $\\ce\{H2C\{\:\}\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3CH2CH\{\:\}\}$ propylidene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00234
name: alkylidynes
def: "Carbenes $\\ce\{R2C…\}$ containing a univalent carbon atom\, atom\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3CH2C…\}$ propylidyne"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00235
name: alkyl radicals
def: "Carbon-centered radicals derived formally by removal of one hydrogen atom from an alkane\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3CH2C^\{.\}H2\}$ propyl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00236
name: alkynes
def: "Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having a carbon-carbon triple bond and the general formula $\\ce\{C_\{n\}H_\{2n-2\}\}$\, $\\ce\{RC#CR\}$. Acyclic branched or unbranched hydrocarbons having more than one triple bond are known as alkadiynes\, alkatriynes\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00237
name: allele
def: "One of several alternate forms of a gene which occur at the same locus on homologous chromosomes and which become separated during meiosis and can be recombined following fusion of gametes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00238
name: allenes
def: "Hydrocarbons \(and by extension\, derivatives formed by substitution\) having two double bonds from one carbon atom to two others $\\ce\{R2C=C=CR2\}$. \(The simplest member\, propadiene\, is known as allene\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00239
name: allo-
def: "Amino acids with two chiral centres were named in the past by allotting a name to the first diastereoisomer to be discovered. The second diastereoisomer\, when found or synthesized\, was then assigned the same name but with the prefix allo-. This method can be used only with trivial names but not with semisystematic or systematic names. It is now recommended that allo should be used only for alloisoleucine and allothreonine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00240
name: allosteric enzymes
def: "Enzymes which contain regions to which small\, regulatory molecules \(cf. effector\) may bind in addition to and separate from substrate binding sites. On binding the effector\, the catalytic activity of the enzyme towards the substrate may be enhanced\, in which case the effector is an activator\, or reduced\, in which case it is an inhibitor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00241
name: allostery
def: "A phenomenon whereby the conformation of an enzyme or other protein is altered by combination\, at a site other than the substrate-binding site\, with a small molecule\, referred to as an effector\, which results in either increased or decreased activity by the enzyme."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00243
name: allotropes
def: "Different structural modifications of an element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00244
name: allotropic transition
def: "A transition of a pure element\, at a defined temperature and pressure\, from one crystal structure to another which contains the same atoms but which has different properties. Examples\: The transition of graphite to diamond\, that of body-centred-cubic iron to face-centred-cubic iron\, and the transition of orthorhombic sulfur to monoclinic sulfur."
synonym: "allotriomorphic transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00245
name: allylic groups
def: "The group $\\ce\{CH2=CHCH2\}$ \(allyl\) and derivatives formed by substitution. The term 'allylic position' or 'allylic site' refers to the saturated carbon atom. A group\, such as $\\ce\{–OH\}$\, attached at an allylic site is sometimes described as 'allylic'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00246
name: allylic intermediates
def: "Carbanions\, carbenium ions or radicals\, formally derived by detachment of one hydron\, hydride or hydrogen from the $\\ce\{CH3\}$ group of propene or derivatives thereof. E.g. $\\ce\{H2C=CHCH2^\{+\}\}$ allyl cation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00247
name: allylic substitution reaction
def: "A substitution reaction occurring at position 1/ of an allylic system\, the double bond being between positions 2/ and 3/. The incoming group may be attached to the same atom 1/ as the leaving group\, or the incoming group becomes attached at the relative position 3/\, with movement of the double bond from 2/3 to 1/2. For example\:  \(written as a transformation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00249
name: alternant
def: "A conjugated system of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ is termed alternant if its atoms can be divided into two sets so that no atom of one set is directly linked to any other atom of the same set."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00250
name: alternating copolymer
def: "A copolymer consisting of macromolecules comprising two species of monomeric units in alternating sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00251
name: alternating copolymerization
def: "A copolymerization in which an alternating copolymer is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00252
name: alternating current
def: "Current with sinusoidal wave forms; all other wave forms should be termed 'periodic'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00254
name: alternating voltage
def: "This term should be applied only to sinusoidal phenomena; the term periodic voltage should be used for other wave forms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00256
name: altocumulus cloud
def: "A dappled layer of patch or cloud composed of flattened globules that may be arranged in groups\, lines or waves collectively known as billows; \\\(\\rm\{2000–6000 m\}\\\); vertical velocities of \\\(\\rm\{0.05–0.1 m s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00257
name: altostratus cloud
def: "A grey\, uniform\, striated or fibrous sheet but without halo phenomena\, and through which the sun is seen only as a diffuse\, bright patch or not at all; usually at elevations \\\(\\rm\{2000–6000 m\}\\\); vertical velocities of \\\(\\rm\{0.05–0.1 m s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00258
name: ambident
def: "A description applied to a chemical species whose molecular entities each possess two alternative and strongly interacting distinguishable reactive centres\, to either of which a bond may be made in a reaction\: the centres must be connected in such a way that reaction at either site stops or greatly retards subsequent attack at the second site. The term is most commonly applied to conjugated nucleophiles\, for example the enolate ion\:  \(which may react with electrophiles either at the $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}carbon atom\}$ or at oxygen\) or $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}pyridones\}$\, and also to the vicinally ambident cyanide ion\, cyanate ion\, thiocyanate ion\, sulfinate ion\, nitrite ion and unsymmetrical hydrazines. Ambident electrophiles are exemplified by carboxylic esters $\\ce\{RC\(=O\)OCR3\}$ which react with nucleophiles either at the carbonyl carbon or the alkoxy carbon. Molecular entities\, such as dianions of dicarboxylic acids\, containing two non-interacting \(or feebly interacting\) reactive centres\, are not generally considered to be ambident and are better described as 'bifunctional'. The Latin root of the word implies two reactive centres\, but the term has in the past also incorrectly been applied to chemical species with more than two reactive centres. For such species the existing term 'polydent' \(or\, better\, 'multident'\) is more appropriate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00259
name: ambient air
def: "The outdoor air in the particular location."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00260
name: ambient air quality
def: "A general term used to describe the quality of the outside air. Usually adjectives such as good\, fair\, bad\, etc. are used by the media to describe this; often some form of air pollution or air quality index is employed to determine the specific descriptive term to be used. These are very qualitative terms of little or no scientific value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00261
name: <i>ambo</i>
def: "A prefix used to indicate that a molecule with two \(or more\) chiral elements is present as a mixture of the two racemic diastereoisomers in unspecified proportions. For example\, the dipeptide formed from ʟ-alanine and ᴅʟ-leucine is ʟ-alanyl-ambo-leucine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00262
name: Ames/salmonella test
def: "A screening test employed in predicting the mutagenic and the potential carcinogenic activities of chemicals in the environment. It employs Ames test strains of salmonella bacteria \($\\ce\{his^\{-\}\}$\) which lack the ability to produce histidine. The compound to be tested\, the bacteria and a small amount of histidine \(insufficient to permit colony growth but enough to allow sufficient growth for expression of mutations\) are added to agar. The bacteria are allowed to incubate for about \\\(63\\ \\rm\{h\}\\\) at \\\(37\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\). If a significant increase in colonies above background is observed in the sample containing the test compound\, then it is concluded that the chemical tested is a direct mutagen for the particular Ames strain of bacteria. This is taken as a qualitative indication of the possible carcinogenic activity of this chemical in other biological systems. This procedure seems to be of qualitative value in a preliminary screening of potential carcinogens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00263
name: amic acids
def: "Carbamoyl carboxylic acids\, i.e. compounds containing a carboxy and a carboxamide group\, e.g. 5-carbamoylnicotinic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00265
name: amide oximes
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(NH2\)=NOH\}$ and derivatives formed by substitution; formally the oximes of carboxamides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00266
name: amides
def: "Derivatives of oxoacids $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}\\ \(l \neq 0\)$ in which an acidic hydroxy group has been replaced by an amino or substituted amino group. Chalcogen replacement analogues are called thio-\, seleno- and telluro-amides. Compounds having one\, two or three acyl groups on a given nitrogen are generically included and may be designated as primary\, secondary and tertiary amides\, respectively\, e.g.  benzamide\, N\,N-dimethylmethanesulfonamide\,  secondary amides \(see imides\)\,  tertiary amides\,  phenylphosphonamidic acid.Notes\:Amides with $\\ce\{NH2\}$\, $\\ce\{NHR\}$ and $\\ce\{NR2\}$ groups should not be distinguished by means of the terms primary\, secondary and tertiary.Derivatives of certain acidic compounds $\\ce\{R_\{n\}E\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$\, where $\\ce\{E\}$ is not carbon \(e.g. sulfenic acids\, $\\ce\{RSOH\}$\, phosphinous acids\, $\\ce\{R2POH\)\}$\, having the structure $\\ce\{R_\{n\}E\(NR2\)_\{m\}\}$ may be named as amides but do not belong to the class amides proper\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3CH2SNH2\}$ ethanesulfenamide or ethylsulfanylamine.The term applies also to metal derivatives of ammonia and amines\, in which a cation replaces a hydrogen atom on nitrogen. Such compounds are also called azanides\, e.g.  lithium diisopropylamide\, synonym lithium diisopropylazanide.See also\: carboxamides\, lactams\, peptides\, phosphoramides\, sulfonamides"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00267
name: amidines
def: "Derivatives of oxoacids $\\ce\{R_\{n\}E\(=O\)OH\}$ in which the hydroxy group is replaced by an amino group and the oxo group is replaced by $\\ce\{=NR\}$. Amidines include carboxamidines\, sulfinamidines and phosphinamidines\, $\\ce\{R2P\(=NR\)NR2\}$. In organic chemistry an unspecified amidine is commonly a carboxamidine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00268
name: amidium ions
def: "Cations formally derived by the addition of one hydron to the $\\ce\{N\}$ or $\\ce\{O\}$ atom of an amide and $N$-hydrocarbyl derivatives thereof. In organic chemistry an unspecified amidium ion is commonly a carboxamidium ion  or  The term does not imply knowledge concerning the position of the cationic centre\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhC\(=O\)N^\{+\}Me3\}$ N\,N\,N-trimethylbenzamidium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00269
name: amidrazones
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(=NH\)NHNH2\}$ or $\\ce\{RC\(NH2\)=NNH2\}$\, formally derived from carboxylic acids. These tautomers are named hydrazide imides and amide hydrazones\, respectively. Also included are $N$-hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00270
name: aminals
def: "Compounds having two amino groups bonded to the same carbon\, $\\ce\{R2C\(NR2\)2\}$. Also called geminal diamines. \[The term aminal has also been used\, with consequent ambiguity\, for $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}amino\}$ ethers \(hemiaminal ethers\); such use is discouraged.\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00271
name: amine imides
def: "Compounds formally derived from the attachment of an amine $\\ce\{R3N\}$ to a nitrene $\\ce\{RN\{\:\}\}$ . The structure $\\ce\{R3N^\{+\}–N−R\}$ expresses the 1\,2-dipolar character of amine imides. They may be named systematically as substituted diazan-2-ium-1-ides\, e.g. $\\ce\{Me3N^\{+\}–N−Me\}$\, 1\,2\,2\,2-tetramethyldiazan-2-ium-1-ide or trimethylamine $N$-methylimide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00273
name: amine oxides
def: "Compounds derived from tertiary amines by the attachment of one oxygen atom to the nitrogen atom $\\ce\{R3N^\{+\}–O^\{−\}\}$. By extension the term includes the analogous derivatives of primary and secondary amines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00274
name: amines
def: "Compounds formally derived from ammonia by replacing one\, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups\, and having the general structures $\\ce\{RNH2\}$ \(primary amines\)\, $\\ce\{R2NH\}$ \(secondary amines\)\, $\\ce\{R3N\}$ \(tertiary amines\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00275
name: amine ylides
def: "Synonymous with ammonium ylides"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00277
name: aminium ions
def: "Cations $\\ce\{HR3N^\{+\}\}$ formed by hydronation of an amine $\\ce\{R3N\}$. 'Non-quaternary ammonium ions' is a synonymous term\, e.g. prolinium\, $\\ce\{PhN^\{+\}HMe2\}$\, $N\,N$-dimethylanilinium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00278
name: aminiumyl radical ions
def: "Radicals cations\, $\\ce\{R3N^\{.+\}\}$\, derivable from aminium ions\, $\\ce\{R3NH^\{+\}\}$\, by removal of a hydrogen atom. Aminiumyl radical ions are\, except for $\\ce\{H3N^\{.+\}\}$\, synonymous with the ammoniumyl radical ions. As the term ammonium is well known\, ammoniumyl radical ions is the more desirable class name."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00279
name: amino-acid residue
def: "When two or more amino acids combine to form a peptide\, the elements of water are removed\, and what remains of each amino acid is called an amino-acid residue. $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}Amino-acid\}$ residues are therefore structures that lack a hydrogen atom of the amino group \($\\ce\{–NH–CHR–COOH\}$\)\, or the hydroxyl moiety of the carboxyl group \($\\ce\{NH2–CHR–CO\\bond\{–\}\}$\)\, or both \($\\ce\{–NH–CHR–COO\\bond\{–\}\}$\); all units of a peptide chain are therefore amino-acid residues. \(Residues of amino acids that contain two amino groups or two carboxyl groups may be joined by isopeptide bonds\, and so may not have the formulas shown.\) The residue in a peptide that has an amino group that is free\, or at least not acylated by another amino-acid residue \(it may\, for example\, be acylated or formylated\)\, is called N-terminal; it is at the N-terminus. The residue that has a free carboxyl group\, or at least does not acylate another amino-acid residue\, \(it may\, for example\, acylate ammonia to give $\\ce\{–NH–CHR–CO–NH2\}$\)\, is called C-terminal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00283
name: amino sugars
def: "Monosaccharides having one alcoholic hydroxy group \(commonly but not necessarily in position 2\) replaced by an amino group; systematically known as $x$-amino-$x$-deoxymonosaccharides. \(Glycosylamines are excluded.\) E.g. ᴅ-glucosamine or 2-amino-2-deoxy-ᴅ-glucopyranose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00284
name: aminoxides
def: "The anion $\\ce\{H2N–O^\{−\}\}$\, aminoxide and its $\\ce\{N\}$ hydrocarbyl derivatives $\\ce\{R2N–O^\{−\}\}$; formally derived from hydroxylamines\, $\\ce\{R2N–OH\}$\, by removing a hydron from the hydroxy group\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(CH3\)2N–O^\{−\}\}$ dimethylaminoxide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00285
name: aminoxyl radicals
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2N–O^\{.\}  R2N^\{.+\}–O^\{−\}\}$; they are radicals derived from hydroxylamines by removal of the hydrogen atom from the hydroxy group\, and are in many cases isolable. The synonymous terms 'nitroxyl radicals' and 'nitroxides' erroneously suggest the presence of a nitro group; their use is not desirable. Example\, $\\ce\{\(ClCH2\)2N–O^\{.\}\}$ bis\(chloromethyl\)aminoxyl."
synonym: "aminooxyl radicals" []
synonym: "iminooxy radicals" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00289
name: aminyl radicals
def: "The nitrogen-centered radical $\\ce\{H2N^\{.\}\}$\, formally derived by the removal of a hydrogen atom from ammonia\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives $\\ce\{R2N^\{.\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00292
name: ammonium ylides
def: "1\,2-Dipolar compounds of general structure $\\ce\{R3N^\{+\}–C^\{−\}R2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00293
name: ammoniumyl radical ions
def: "$\\ce\{H3N^\{.+\}\}$ and its hydrocarbyl derivatives\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(CH3\)3N^\{.+\}\}$ trimethylammoniumyl\, $\\ce\{PhN^\{.+\}H2\}$ phenylammoniumyl or benzenaminiumyl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00294
name: amorphous carbon
def: "A carbon material without long-range crystalline order. Short-range order exists\, but with deviations of the interatomic distances and/or interbonding angles with respect to the graphite lattice as well as to the diamond lattice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00295
name: amount concentration
def: "Amount of a constituent divided by the volume of the mixture. Also called amount-of-substance concentration\, substance concentration \(in clinical chemistry\) and in older literature molarity. For entities B it is often denoted by \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]\\\). The common unit is mole per cubic decimetre \(\\\(\\pu\{mol dm-3\}\\\)\) or mole per litre \(\\\(\\pu\{mol L-1\}\\\)\) sometimes denoted by \\\(M\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00296
name: amount fraction
def: "Amount of a constituent divided by the total amount of all constituents in the mixture. It is also called mole fraction. Amount fraction is equal to the 'number fraction'\: the number of entities of one constituent divided by the total number of entities in the mixture."
synonym: "mole fraction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00297
name: amount of substance
def: "The amount of substance\, symbol \\\(n\\\)\, of a system is a measure of the number of specified elementary entities. An elementary entity may be an atom\, a molecule\, an ion\, an electron\, any other particle or specified group of particles."
synonym: "chemical amount" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00298
name: amount-of-substance concentration
def: "Synonymous with amount concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00299
name: amount-of-substance fraction
def: "Synonymous with amount fraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00300
name: ampere
def: "The ampere\, symbol \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\)\, is the SI unit of electric current. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the elementary charge \\\(e\\\) to be \\\(1.602\\ 176\\ 634\\times10^\{-19\}\\\) when expressed in the unit \\\(\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, which is equal to \\\(\\rm\{A s\}\\\)\, where the second is defined in terms of ΔνCs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00301
name: amperometric detection method
def: "A detection method in which the current is proportional to the concentration of the species generating the current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00302
name: amphipathic
def: "The property of surface activity is usually due to the fact that the molecules of the substance are amphipathic or amphiphilic\, meaning that each contains both a hydrophilic and a hydrophobic \(lipophilic\) group. This assumes that one of the two phases is aqueous\, and the other non-aqueous. If both are non-aqueous \(e.g. oil/air\)\, molecules containing organophilic and organophobic groups may be amphipathic and surface active."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00303
name: amphiphilic
def: "A term used to describe a compound containing a large organic cation or anion which possesses a long unbranched hydrocarbon chain\, e.g. \n$\\ce\{CH3\(CH2\)_\{n\}CO2^\{−\}M^\{+\}\}$$\\ce\{CH3\(CH2\)_\{n\}N^\{+\}\(CH3\)3X^\{−\}\}$ \($n \\gt 7$\)$\\ce\{CH3\(CH2\)_\{n\}SO3^\{−\}M^\{+\}\}$.The existence of distinct polar \(hydrophilic\) and nonpolar \(hydrophobic\) regions in the molecule promotes the formation of micelles in dilute aqueous solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00304
name: amphiprotic solvent
def: "Self-ionizing solvent possessing both characteristics of Brønsted acids and bases\, for example $\\ce\{H2O\}$ and $\\ce\{CH3OH\}$\, in contrast to aprotic solvent."
synonym: "amphiprotic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00306
name: amphoteric
def: "A chemical species that behaves both as an acid and as a base is called amphoteric. This property depends upon the medium in which the species is investigated\: $\\ce\{H2SO4\}$ is an acid when studied in water\, but becomes amphoteric in superacids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00307
name: amplification reaction
def: "A reaction which replaces the conventional reaction used in a particular determination so that a more favourable measurement can be made. The sequence can be repeated to provide a further favourable increase in measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00310
name: amplitude of alternating current
def: "Half the peak-to-peak amplitude of a sinusoidal alternating current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00311
name: amplitude of alternating voltage
def: "This term should denote half of the peak-to-peak amplitude. Peak-to-peak and r.m.s. amplitudes should be so specified."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00312
name: AM 0 sunlight
def: "Solar irradiance in space just above the atmosphere of the earth on a plane perpendicular to the direction of the sun \(air mass\, AM\, zero\). Also called extraterrestrial irradiance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00313
name: AM 1 sunlight
def: "Solar irradiance at sea level\, i.e.\, traversing the atmosphere\, when the direction of the sun is perpendicular to the surface of the earth. Also called terrestrial global irradiance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00314
name: anabolism
def: "The processes of metabolism that result in the synthesis of cellular components from precursors of low molecular weight."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00315
name: anaerobe
def: "An organism that does not need free-form oxygen for growth. Many anaerobes are even sensitive to free oxygen. Obligate \(strict\) anaerobes grow only in the absence of oxygen. Facultative anaerobes can grow either in the presence or in the absence of molecular oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00316
name: anaerobic
def: "Not requiring molecular oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00317
name: anaesthetic
def: "Substance which produces loss of feeling or sensation\: general anaesthetic produces loss of consciousness; local or regional anaesthetic renders a specific area insensible to pain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00318
name: analgesic
def: "Substance which relieves pain\, without causing loss of consciousness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00319
name: analogue metabolism
def: "Process by which a normally non-biodegradable compound is biodegraded in the presence of a structurally similar compound which can induce the necessary enzymes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00320
name: analogue to digital converter \(pulse\)
def: "A pulse amplitude analyser which\, for each pulse processed\, produces an integer proportional to the height of that pulse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00331
name: analyte
def: "The component of a system to be analysed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00332
name: analytical function
def: "A function which relates the measured value \\\(\\hat\{C\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) to the instrument reading\, \\\(X\\\)\, with the value of all interferants\, \\\(C_\{i\}\\\)\, remaining constant. This function is expressed by the following regression of the calibration results. \\\[\\hat\{C\}_\{\\rm\{a\}\} = f\\left\(X\\right\)\\\] The analytical function is taken as equal to the inverse of the calibration function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00333
name: analytical instrument
def: "A device or a combination of devices used to carry out an analytical process. The analytical process is all or part of the analytical procedure that encompasses all steps from the introduction of the sample or the test portion to the production of the result. An analytical instrument may carry out single or multiple analytical procedures. In the latter case it may be selective\, i.e. designed to carry out any requested combination of procedures within the set\, on each specimen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00334
name: analytical intercomparison
def: "A procedure which gives insight to the accuracy of results of analytical procedures by comparing the results obtained in the analyses of identical samples at different laboratories and preferably with different analytical methods."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00336
name: analytical pyrolysis
def: "The characterization\, in an inert atmosphere\, of a material or a chemical process by a chemical degradation reaction\(s\) induced by thermal energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00337
name: analytical quality control
def: "Procedures which give insight into the precision and accuracy of analysis results."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00338
name: analytical radiochemistry
def: "That part of analytical chemistry in which the application of radioactivity is an essential step in the analytical procedures. Synonymous with radioanalytical chemistry."
synonym: "radioanalytical chemistry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00342
name: analytical unit
def: "An assembly of subunits comprising\: suitable apparatus permitting the introduction and removal of the gas\, liquid or solid to be analysed and/or calibration materials; a measuring cell or other apparatus which\, from the physical or chemical properties of the components of the material to be analysed\, gives signals allowing their identification and/or measurement; signal processing devices \(amplification\, recording\) or\, if need be\, data processing devices."
synonym: "analyser" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00344
name: aneroid barometer
def: "An instrument for monitoring the atmospheric pressure in which no liquid is employed\, but rather changes in pressure between the atmosphere and a closed vessel bend a diaphram which moves a pointer on a scale."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00345
name: Anger camera
def: "A camera in which a large diameter scintillator is coupled to an array of photomultiplier tubes by fibre optics. X-ray imaging may also be achieved in multi-crystal cameras where many small crystals individually scintillate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00346
name: angle
def: "The angle between two half-lines terminating at the same point is the ratio of the length of the included arc of the circle \(with its centre at that point\) to the radius of the circle. Symbols\: \\\(\\alpha \\\)\, \\\(\\beta \\\)\, \\\(\\gamma \\\) ... This is a quantity of dimension one with the SI unit radian \(\\\(\\pu\{rad\}\\\)\)\, but very often degrees of arc are used."
synonym: "plane angle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00348
name: angle of optical rotation
def: "Angle through which plane polarized light is rotated clockwise\, as seen when facing the light source\, in passing through an optically active medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00349
name: angle strain
def: "Strain due to a departure in bond angle from 'normal' values. The term is often used in the context of non-aromatic cyclic compounds in which the internal angles differ from the regular tetrahedral angle of \\\(109^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\,28^\{\\prime \}\\\); in this sense angle strain is also known as 'Baeyer strain'."
synonym: "Baeyer strain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00351
name: angular distribution
def: "With reference to the center of mass\, the products of a bimolecular reaction are scattered with respect to the initial velocity vector\, and the distribution of scattering angles is known as the angular distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00352
name: angular frequency
def: "Frequency multiplied by $2\\uppi\\!$. Also called pulsatance. It was formerly called circular frequency."
synonym: "circular frequency" []
synonym: "pulsatance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00353
name: angular momentum
def: "Angular momentum\, or moment of momentum of a particle about a point is a vector quantity equal to the vector product of the position vector of the particle and its momentum\, \\\(L=r\\times p\\\). For special angular momenta of particles in atomic and molecular physics different symbols are used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00355
name: anhydro bases
def: "Compounds resulting from internal acid–base neutralization \(with loss of water\) in iminium hydroxides containing an acidic site conjugated with the iminium function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00356
name: anilides
def: "Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}\\ \(l \neq 0\)$ by replacing an $\\ce\{OH\}$ group by the NHPh group or derivative formed by ring substitution; N-phenyl amides\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3C\(=O\)NHPh\}$ acetanilide.See\: amidesSalts formed by replacement of a nitrogen-bound hydron of aniline by a metal or other cation\, e.g. $\\ce\{NaNHPh\}$ sodium anilide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00357
name: anils
def: "A term for the subclass of Schiff bases $\\ce\{R2C=NR'\}$\, where $\\ce\{R'\}$ = phenyl or substituted phenyl group. Thus\, $N$-phenyl imines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00358
name: anion
def: "A monoatomic or polyatomic species having one or more elementary charges of the electron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00359
name: anion exchange
def: "The process of exchanging anions between a solution and an anion exchanger."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00360
name: anion exchanger
def: "Ion exchanger with anions as counter-ions. The term anion-exchange resin may be used in the case of solid organic polymers. The base form of an anion exchanger is the ionic form in which the counter-ions are hydroxide groups \($\\ce\{OH–\}$ form\) or the ionogenic groups form an uncharged base\, e.g. $\\ce\{-NH2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00361
name: anionic polymerization
def: "An ionic polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are anions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00362
name: anionotropic rearrangement
def: "A rearrangement in which the migrating group moves with its electron pair from one atom to another."
synonym: "anionotropy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00365
name: annelation
def: "Alternative\, but less desirable term for annulation. The term is widely used in the German and French languages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00366
name: annihilation
def: "An interaction between a particle and its antiparticle in which they both disappear."
synonym: "energy pooling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00367
name: annulation
def: "A transformation involving fusion of a new ring to a molecule via two new bonds. Some authors use the term 'annelation' for the fusion of an additional ring to an already existing one\, and 'annulation' for the formation of a ring from one or several acyclic precursors\, but this distinction is not made generally."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00368
name: annulenes
def: "Mancude monocyclic hydrocarbons without side chains of the general formula $\\ce\{C_\{n\}H_\{n\}\}$ \($n$ is an even number\) or $\\ce\{C_\{n\}H_\{n+1\}\}$ \($n$ is an odd number\). In systematic nomenclature an annulene with seven or more carbon atoms may be named \[$n$\]annulene\, where $n$ is the number of carbon atoms\, e.g. annulene for cyclonona-1\,3\,5\,7-tetraene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00369
name: annulenylidenes
def: "Carbenes\, derived by formal insertion of a divalent carbon atom into an even-membered annulene\, e.g. cycloheptatrienylidene\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00370
name: anode
def: "Electrode at which oxidation takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00371
name: anodic transfer coefficient
def: "Defined by analogy with cathodic transfer coefficient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00372
name: anomeric effect
def: "Originally the thermodynamic preference for polar groups bonded to C-1 \(the anomeric carbon of a glycopyranosyl derivative\) to take up an axial position.  This effect is now considered to be a special case of a general preference \(the generalized anomeric effect\) for synclinal \(gauche\) conformations about the bond C–Y in the system X–C–Y–C where X and Y are heteroatoms having nonbonding electron pairs\, commonly at least one of which is nitrogen\, oxygen or fluorine. For example in chloro\(methoxy\)methane the anomeric effect stabilizes the synclinal conformation.  In alkyl glycopyranosides the anomeric effect operates at two sites \(i\) along the endocyclic C-1 oxygen bond \(endo-anomeric effect\) and \(ii\) along the exocyclic C-1 oxygen bond \(exo-anomeric effect\). The opposite preference is claimed for some systems\, e.g.\, glycopyranosyltrialkylammonium salts\, and has been referred to as the reverse anomeric effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00373
name: anomers
def: "Diastereoisomers of glycosides\, hemiacetals or related cyclic forms of sugars\, or related molecules differing in configuration only at C-1 of an aldose\, C-2 of a 2-ketose\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00374
name: ansa compounds
def: "Benzene derivatives having para positions \(or meta\) bridged by a chain \(commonly 10 to 12 atoms long\) \(Latin ansa\, handle\). By extension\, any arene bridged by a chain constrained to lie over one of the two faces of the arene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00375
name: antagonism
def: "Combined effect of two or more factors which is smaller than the solitary effect of any one of those factors. In bioassays\, the term may be used when a specified response is produced by exposure to either of two factors but not by exposure to both together."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00376
name: antagonist
def: "Substance that reverses or reduces the effect induced by an agonist.Substance that attaches to and blocks cell receptors that normally bind naturally occurring substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00377
name: antarafacial
def: "When a part of a molecule \('molecular fragment'\) undergoes two changes in bonding \(bond-making or bond-breaking\)\, either to a common centre or to two related centres\, external to itself\, these bonding changes may be related in one of two spatially different ways. These are designated as 'antarafacial' if opposite faces of the molecular fragment are involved\, and 'suprafacial' if both changes occur at the same face. The concept of 'face' is clear from the diagrams in the cases of planar \(or approximately planar\) frameworks with isolated or interacting p-orbitals \[diagrams \(a\) and \(b\)\].  The terms antarafacial and suprafacial are\, however\, also employed in cases in which the essential part of the molecular fragment undergoing changes in bonding comprises two atoms linked only by a $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$. In these cases it is customary to refer to the phases of the local $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonding\}$ orbital\: occurrence of the two bonding changes at sites of like orbital phase is regarded as suprafacial\, whereas that at two sites of opposite phase is antarafacial. The possibilities are shown for $\\ce\{C–C\}$ and $\\ce\{C–H\}$ $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ in diagrams \(c\) and \(d\). There may be two distinct and alternative stereochemical outcomes of a suprafacial process involving a $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ between saturated carbon atoms\, i.e. either retention or inversion at both centres. The antarafacial process results in inversion at one centre and retention at the second. For examples of the use of these terms\, see cycloaddition\, sigmatropic rearrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00378
name: anthelmintic
def: "Substance intended to kill parasitic intestinal worms\, such as helminths."
synonym: "anthelminth" []
synonym: "anthelminthic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00379
name: anthocyanidins
def: "Aglycons of anthocyanins; they are oxygenated derivatives of flavylium \(2-phenylchromenylium\) salts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00380
name: anthocyanins
def: "Plant pigments of the flavonoid class; they are glycosides that on hydrolysis yield coloured aglycons called anthocyanidins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00381
name: anti
def: "In the representation of stereochemical relationships 'anti' means 'on opposite sides' of a reference plane\, in contrast to 'syn' which means 'on the same side'\, as in the following examples.Two substituents attached to atoms joined by a single bond are anti if the torsion angle \(dihedral angle\) between the bonds to the substituents is greater than $\\pu\{90\\!^\{\\circ\}\}$\, or syn if it is less than $\\pu\{90\\!^\{\\circ\}\}$. \(A further distinction is made between antiperiplanar\, synperiplanar\, anticlinal and synclinal.\)In the older literature the terms anti and syn were used to designate stereoisomers of oximes and related compounds. That usage was superseded by the terms 'trans' and 'cis' or E and Z\, respectively.When the terms are used in the context of chemical reactions or transformations\, they designate the relative orientation of substituents in the substrate or product\:Addition to a carbon-carbon double bond\:  Alkene-forming elimination\:  In the examples described under \(1\) and \(2\) anti processes are always antarafacial\, and syn processes are suprafacial."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00382
name: antiaromatic compounds
def: "Compounds that contain \\\(4\\ n\\\) \(\\\(n\neq 0\\\)\) $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ in a cyclic planar\, or nearly planar\, system of alternating single and double bonds\, e.g. cyclobuta-1\,3-diene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00383
name: antibiotic
def: "Substance produced by\, and obtained from\, certain living cells \(especially bacteria\, yeasts and moulds\)\, or an equivalent synthetic substance\, which is biostatic or biocidal at low concentrations to some other form of life\, especially pathogenic or noxious organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00384
name: antibody
def: "A protein \(immunoglobulin\) produced by the immune system of an organism in response to exposure to a foreign molecule \(antigen\) and characterized by its specific binding to a site of that molecule \(antigenic determinant or epitope\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00385
name: anticholinergic
def: "\(adjective\). Preventing transmission of parasympathetic nerve impulses.\(noun\). Substance which prevents transmission of parasympathetic nerve impulses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00386
name: anticircular elution
def: "The opposite of circular development. Here the sample as well as the mobile phase is applied at the periphery of a circle and both move towards the centre."
synonym: "anticircular development" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00389
name: anticodon
def: "A sequence of three nucleotides in the anticodon-loop of a tRNA\, which recognizes and binds the complementary triplet sequence \(codon\) of the mRNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00390
name: anti-Compton γ-ray spectrometer
def: "A gamma-ray spectrometer in which the effect of the Compton scattering is at least partly compensated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00391
name: anticyclone
def: "A large system of winds that rotate about a centre of high atmospheric pressure\, clockwise \(viewed from above\) in the northern hemisphere and counterclockwise in the southern hemisphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00394
name: antigen
def: "A substance that stimulates the immune system to produce a set of specific antibodies and that combines with the antibody through a specific binding site or epitope."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00398
name: antimetabolite
def: "Substance\, structurally similar to a metabolite\, which competes with it or replaces it\, and so prevents or reduces its normal utilization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00399
name: antimony–xenon lamp
def: "An intense source of ultraviolet\, visible\, and near infra-red radiation produced by an electrical discharge in a mixture of antimony vapour and xenon under high pressure. Its output in the ultraviolet region is higher than that of the mercury–xenon arc."
synonym: "antimony–xenon arc" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00400
name: antimycotic
def: "Substance used to kill a fungus or to inhibit its growth. Synonymous with fungicide."
synonym: "fungicide" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00401
name: antiparticle
def: "Of a given particle\, a particle with the same rest mass\, where the two can annihilate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00404
name: <i>antiprismo</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote eight atoms bound into a rectangular antiprism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00405
name: antiresistant
def: "Substance used as an additive to a pesticide formulation in order to reduce the resistance of insects to the pesticide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00406
name: antiserum
def: "Serum containing antibodies to a particular antigen either because of immunization or after an infectious disease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00408
name: anti-Stokes type radiation
def: "Fluorescence radiation occurring at shorter wavelengths than absorption."
synonym: "anti-Stokes type fluorescence" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00409
name: anti-thixotropy
def: "Opposite of thixotropy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00412
name: apex current
def: "In measurement of non-faradaic admittance \(or tensammetry\)\, a plot of alternating current against applied potential shows a minimum or maximum when a non-electroactive substance undergoes adsorption or desorption at the surface of the indicator electrode. Such a maximum or minimum may be called an 'apex' to emphasize its non-faradaic origin and distinguish it from a 'summit'\, which would result from a charge-transfer process. The highest value of the current on such an apex may be called an 'apex current'\, and the corresponding applied potential may be called an 'apex potential'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00414
name: aphicide
def: "Substance intended to kill aphids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00415
name: apical
def: "In trigonal bipyramidal structures \(e.g. a five-coordinate trigonal bipyramid with phosphorus as central atom\) the term apical refers to the two positions that are collinear with the central atom or to the bonds linking these positions to the central atom. The three equivalent bonds \(or positions\) in a plane passing through the central atom and perpendicular to the direction of the apical bonds are described as equatorial.  The term apical is also used for the bond pointing from the atom at or near the centre of the base to the apex of a pyramidal structure. The positions at or near the base of the pyramid\, or the bonds linking those positions to the central atom of the base are described as 'basal'. The apical bonds have also been called axial."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00416
name: apo-
def: "An unitalicized prefix\, preceded by a locant\, used to indicate that all of the molecule beyond the carbon atom corresponding to that locant has been replaced by hydrogen atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00417
name: apoenzyme
def: "The protein part of an enzyme without the cofactor necessary for catalysis. The cofactor can be a metal ion\, an organic molecule \(coenzyme\)\, or a combination of both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00418
name: apparent
def: "A term\, indicated by a superscript '\, which means that a process is not well known or that its value carries uncertainties which are not known\, e.g. \\\(\\Delta G^\{\\circ\}\{'\}\\\) is the apparent standard Gibbs energy change. In the context of partial molar quantities the word is used in a different sense; as a symbol for 'apparent' in this connection the use of subscript \\\(\\varphi \\\)\, as in \\\(Y_\{\\varphi\}\\\)\, is recommended. Other notations employed for this property include \\\(^\{\\varphi\}X\\\) and \\\(\\varphi_\{X\}\\\)."
synonym: "apparent quantity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00420
name: apparent viscosity
def: "The ratio of stress to rate of strain\, calculated from measurements of forces and velocities as though the liquid were Newtonian. If the liquid is actually non-Newtonian\, the apparent viscosity depends on the type and dimensions of the apparatus used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00421
name: appearance energy
def: "Refers to ionization of a molecule or atom by electron collision or photon absorption. In mass spectrometry it has often been reported as the voltage which corresponds to the minimum energy of the electrons in the ionizing beam necessary for the production of a given fragment ion. In photoionization it is the minimum energy of the quantum of light which produces ionization of the absorbing molecule."
synonym: "appearance potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00423
name: appearance temperature
def: "The temperature of the atomization surface at which the analyte signal/noise \(S/N\) ratio reaches a value of 3 when the quantity of analyte in the atomizer is one hundred times the characteristic mass for peak high absorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00424
name: applied potential
def: "The difference of potential measured between identical metallic leads to two electrodes of a cell. The applied potential is divided into two electrode potentials\, each of which is the difference of potential existing between the bulk of the solution and the interior of the conducting material of the electrode\, an \\\(i\\ R\\\) or ohmic potential drop through the solution\, and another ohmic potential drop through each electrode. In the electroanalytical literature this quantity has often been denoted by the term voltage\, whose continued use is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00425
name: aprotic solvent
def: "Non-protogenic \(in a given situation\). \(With extremely strong Brønsted acids or bases\, solvents that are normally aprotic may accept or lose a proton. For example\, acetonitrile is in most instances an aprotic solvent\, but it is protophilic in the presence of concentrated sulfuric acid and protogenic in the presence of potassium tert-butoxide. Similar considerations apply to benzene\, trichloromethane\, etc.\)"
synonym: "aprotic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00426
name: aquation
def: "The incorporation of one or more integral molecules of water into another species with or without displacement of one or more other atoms or groups. For example\, the incorporation of water into the inner ligand sphere of an inorganic complex is an aquation reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00427
name: <i>arachno</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to designate a boron structure intermediate between nido- and hypho- in degree of openness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00428
name: Archibald's method
def: "A sedimentation method based on the fact that at the meniscus and at the bottom of the centrifuge cell there is never a flux of the solute across a plane perpendicular to the radial direction and the equations characterizing the sedimentation equilibrium always apply there\, even though the system as a whole may be far from equilibrium. The use of the term 'approach to sedimentation equilibrium' for Archibald's method is discouraged\, since it has a more general meaning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00429
name: area
def: "This is understood to be the geometrical or projected area\, and to ignore surface roughness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00430
name: area of interface
def: "In all measurements it is desirable that quantities like the charge and the capacity be related to unit\, true\, surface area of the interface. While this is relatively simple for a liquid-liquid interface\, there are great difficulties when one phase is solid. In any report of these quantities it is essential to give a clear statement as to whether they refer to the true or the apparent \(geometric\) area and\, especially if the former is used\, precisely how it was measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00432
name: areic
def: "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by area. Areic charge is the charge on a surface divided by the surface area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00433
name: arene epoxides
def: "Epoxides derived from arenes by 1\,2-addition of an oxygen atom to a formal double bond\, e.g. 5\,6-epoxycyclohexa-1\,3-diene.  \(Common usage has extended the term to include examples with the epoxy group bridging nonadjacent atoms.\)"
synonym: "arene oxides" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00435
name: arenes
def: "Monocyclic and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00436
name: arenium ions
def: "Cations derived formally by the addition of a hydron or other cationic species to any position of an arene\, e.g. $\\ce\{C6H7^\{+\}\}$\, benzenium. The term includes\:Arenium σ-adduct \(sigma-adduct\) \(Wheland intermediates\) \(which are considered to be intermediates in electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions\) and other cyclohexadienyl cations. Arenium π-adduct \(pi-adduct\)\, such as\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00437
name: arenols
def: "Synonymous with phenols \(but rarely used\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00439
name: argon ion laser
def: "A CW or pulsed laser emitting lines from \\\(334\\\) to \\\(529\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) from singly ionized argon. Principal emissions are at \\\(488.0\\\) and \\\(514.5\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00440
name: arithmetic mean
def: "The sum of a series of observations divided by the number of observations. Symbol\: \\\(\\bar\{x\}\\\). It can be calculated by the formula\: \\\[\\bar\{x\}=\\frac\{\\sum x_\{i\}\}\{n\}\\\]"
synonym: "average" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00441
name: aromatic
def: "In the traditional sense\, 'having a chemistry typified by benzene'.A cyclically conjugated molecular entity with a stability \(due to delocalization\) significantly greater than that of a hypothetical localized structure \(e.g. Kekulé structure\) is said to possess aromatic character. If the structure is of higher energy \(less stable\) than such a hypothetical classical structure\, the molecular entity is 'antiaromatic'. The most widely used method for determining aromaticity is the observation of diatropicity in the $\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}$ NMR spectrum.See also\: Hückel \(4n + 2\) rule\, Möbius aromaticityThe terms aromatic and antiaromatic have been extended to describe the stabilization or destabilization of transition states of pericyclic reactions The hypothetical reference structure is here less clearly defined\, and use of the term is based on application of the Hückel \(4n + 2\) rule and on consideration of the topology of orbital overlap in the transition state. Reactions of molecules in the ground state involving antiaromatic transition states proceed\, if at all\, much less easily than those involving aromatic transition states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00442
name: aromaticity
def: "The concept of spatial and electronic structure of cyclic molecular systems displaying the effects of cyclic electron delocalization which provide for their enhanced thermodynamic stability \(relative to acyclic structural analogues\) and tendency to retain the structural type in the course of chemical transformations. A quantitative assessment of the degree of aromaticity is given by the value of the resonance energy. It may also be evaluated by the energies of relevant isodesmic and homodesmotic reactions. Along with energetic criteria of aromaticity\, important and complementary are also a structural criterion \(the lesser the alternation of bond lengths in the rings\, the greater is the aromaticity of the molecule\) and a magnetic criterion \(existence of the diamagnetic ring current induced in a conjugated cyclic molecule by an external magnetic field and manifested by an exaltation and anisotropy of magnetic susceptibility\). Although originally introduced for characterization of peculiar properties of cyclic conjugated hydrocarbons and their ions\, the concept of aromaticity has been extended to their homoderivatives \(see homoaromaticity\)\, conjugated heterocyclic compounds \(heteroaromaticity\)\, saturated cyclic compounds \($\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}aromaticity\}$\) as well as to three-dimensional organic and organometallic compounds \(three-dimensional aromaticity\). A common feature of the electronic structure inherent in all aromatic molecules is the close nature of their valence electron shells\, i.e.\, double electron occupation of all bonding MOs with all antibonding and delocalized nonbonding MOs unfilled. The notion of aromaticity is applied also to transition states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00443
name: arrester
def: "Equipment designed to remove particles from a gaseous medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00446
name: Arrhenius equation
def: "An equation that represents the dependence of the rate constant \\\(k\\\) of a reaction on the absolute temperature \\\(T\\\)\: \\\[k = A\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{\\frac\{-E_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}\{R\\ T\}\}\\\] In its original form the pre-exponential factor \\\(A\\\) and the activation energy \\\(E_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) are considered to be temperature-independent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00447
name: arsanes
def: "The saturated hydrides of tervalent arsenic\, having the general formula $\\ce\{As_\{n\}H_\{n+2\}\}$. Individual members having an unbranched arsenic chain are named arsane\, diarsane\, triarsane\, etc. The name of a saturated hydride of arsenic where one or more arsenic atoms have bonding number 5 is formed by prefixing locants and \\\(\\lambda ^\{5\}\\\) symbols to the name of the corresponding arsane\, e.g. $\\ce\{H2AsAsHAsH2\}$ triarsane\, $\\ce\{H4AsAsH3AsH4\}$ 1\\\(\\lambda ^\{5\}\\\)\,2\\\(\\lambda ^\{5\}\\\)\,3\\\(\\lambda ^\{5\}\\\)-triarsane. Hydrocarbyl derivatives of $\\ce\{AsH3\}$ belong to the class arsines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00448
name: arsanylidenes
def: "Recommended name for carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce\{R–As\{\:\}\}$ \(former IUPAC name is arsinediyls\). A common non-IUPAC synonym is arsinidenes."
synonym: "arsinidenes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00449
name: arsanylium ions
def: "The arsanyl cation\, $\\ce\{H2As^\{+\}\}$\, and derivatives by substitution. The name arsinylium \(systematically derived from arsine\) is not applied as it already designates $\\ce\{H2As\(=O\)^\{+\}\}$\, the acylium ion derived from arsinic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00450
name: arsenides
def: "Compounds obtained from arsines $\\ce\{AsR3\}$ by replacing one or more hydrogen atoms by a metal\, e.g. $\\ce\{CaAsPh\}$ calcium phenylarsenide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00451
name: arsine oxides
def: "$\\ce\{H3As=O\}$ and its hydrocarbyl derivatives \(analogously arsine imides and arsine sulfides\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(CH3\)3As=O\}$ trimethylarsine oxide or trimethylarsane oxide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00452
name: arsines
def: "AsH3 and compounds derived from it by substituting one\, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups\: $\\ce\{R3As\}$\, $\\ce\{RAsH2\}$\, $\\ce\{R2AsH\}$\, $\\ce\{R3As\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\) are called primary\, secondary and tertiary arsines\, respectively. A specific arsine is preferably named as a substituted arsane\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3CH2AsH2\}$ ethylarsane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00453
name: arsinic acids
def: "$\\ce\{H2As\(=O\)OH\}$ and its $\\ce\{As\}$-hydrocarbyl derivatives\, e.g. $\\ce\{Me2As\(=O\)OH\}$\, dimethylarsinic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00455
name: arsinous acids
def: "$\\ce\{H2AsOH\}$ and its As-hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00456
name: arsonic acids
def: "$\\ce\{HAs\(=O\)\(OH\)2\}$ and its $\\ce\{As\}$-hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00457
name: arsonium compounds
def: "Salts \(including hydroxides\) $\\ce\{\[R4As\]^\{+\}X^\{−\}\}$ containing tetracoordinate arsonium ion and the associated anion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00458
name: arsonous acids
def: "$\\ce\{HAs\(OH\)2\}$ and its As-hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00459
name: arsoranes
def: "The mononuclear hydride $\\ce\{AsH5\}$\, systematically named \\\(\\lambda ^\{5\}\\\)-arsane\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives. By extension the term also applies to arsonium ylides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00461
name: artificial radioactivity
def: "Synonymous with induced radioactivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00462
name: aryl cations
def: "Carbocations formally derived by removal of a hydride ion from a ring carbon atom of an arene. E.g. phenyl cation or phenylium\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00463
name: arylene groups
def: "Bivalent groups derived from arenes by removal of a hydrogen atom from two ring carbon atoms. A synonym is arenediyl groups. E.g. o-phenylene or benzene-1\,2-diyl\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00464
name: aryl groups
def: "Groups derived from arenes by removal of a hydrogen atom from a ring carbon atom. Groups similarly derived from heteroarenes are sometimes subsumed in this definition. E.g. o-tolyl\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00465
name: aryne
def: "A hydrocarbon derived from an arene by abstraction of two hydrogen atoms from adjacent carbon atoms; thus 1\,2-didehydroarene. Arynes are commonly represented with a formal triple bond. The analogous heterocyclic compounds are called heteroarynes or hetarynes. For example\, benzyne\:  Arynes are usually transient species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00466
name: ascending elution
def: "A mode of operation in which the paper or plate is in a vertical or slanted position and the mobile phase is supplied to its lower edge; the upward movement depends on capillary action."
synonym: "ascending development" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00467
name: ash
def: "The solid residue which remains after the combustion of a fuel such as coal. Ash consists largely of heat treated mineral matter\, but it may contain some products of the incomplete combustion of the fuel as well."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00468
name: ashing
def: "Dry or wet mineralization as a method of preconcentration of trace substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00469
name: aspirator
def: "Any apparatus that produces a movement of a fluid by suction \(e.g. a squeeze bulb\, pump\, Venturi\, etc.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00470
name: assay
def: "A set of operations having the object of determining the value of a quantity. In analytical chemistry\, this term is synonymous with measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00471
name: assay kit
def: "A set of components \(reagents and other necessary materials\) and procedural instructions packaged together and designed for the estimation in vitro of a value of a specified quantity\, when used according to the instructions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00472
name: association
def: "The assembling of separate molecular entities into any aggregate\, especially of oppositely charged free ions into ion pairs or larger and not necessarily well-defined clusters of ions held together by electrostatic attraction. The term signifies the reverse of dissociation\, but is not commonly used for the formation of definite adducts by colligation or coordination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00473
name: association reaction
def: "The reaction of a \(slow moving\) ion with a neutral species wherein the reactants combine to form a single ionized species."
synonym: "associative combination" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00475
name: associative ionization
def: "This occurs when two excited gaseous atoms or molecular moieties interact and the sum of their internal energies is sufficient to produce a single\, additive ionic product."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00477
name: <i>asym</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote asymmetrical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00478
name: asymmetric
def: "Lacking all symmetry elements \(other than the trivial one of a one-fold axis of symmetry\)\, i.e. belonging to the symmetry point group \\\(C_\{1\}\\\). The term has been used loosely \(and incorrectly\) to describe the absence of a rotation–reflection axis \(alternating axis\) in a molecule\, i.e. as meaning chiral\, and this usage persists in the traditional terms asymmetric carbon atom\, asymmetric synthesis\, asymmetric induction\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00479
name: asymmetric carbon atom
def: "The traditional name \(van 't Hoff\) for a carbon atom that is attached to four different entities \(atoms or groups\)\, e.g. Cabcd."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00482
name: asymmetric film
def: "A film bounded by two different bulk phases. When the bulk phases are identical the film is described as symmetric."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00483
name: asymmetric induction
def: "The traditional term describing the preferential formation in a chemical reaction of one enantiomer or diastereoisomer over the other as a result of the influence of a chiral feature present in the substrate\, reagent\, catalyst or environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00484
name: asymmetric synthesis
def: "A traditional term used for stereoselective synthesis of chiral compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00485
name: asymmetric transformation
def: "The conversion of a racemate into a pure enantiomer or into a mixture in which one enantiomer is present in excess\, or of a diastereoisomeric mixture into a single diastereoisomer or into a mixture in which one diastereoisomer predominates. This is sometimes called deracemization. If the two enantiomers of a chiral substrate A are freely interconvertible and if an equal amount or excess of a non-racemizing second enantiomerically pure chemical species\, say \($R$\)-B\, is added to a solution of racemic A\, then the resulting equilibrium mixture of adducts A·B will\, in general\, contain unequal amounts of the diastereoisomers \($R$\)-A$\\cdot$\($R$\)-B and \($S$\)-A$\\cdot$\($R$\)-B. The result of this equilibration is called asymmetric transformation of the first kind. If\, in such a system\, the two diastereoisomeric adducts differ considerably in solubility so that only one of them\, say \($R$\)-A$\\cdot$\($R$\)-B\, crystallizes from the solution\, then the equilibration of diastereoisomers in solution and concurrent crystallization will continue so that all \(or most\) of the substrate A can be isolated as the crystalline diastereoisomer \($R$\)-A$\\cdot$\($R$\)-B. Such a 'crystallization-induced asymmetric transformation' is called an asymmetric transformation of the second kind."
synonym: "deracemization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00486
name: asymmetry
def: "Denoting absence of any symmetry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00487
name: asymmetry potential
def: "The measured potential difference of a symmetrical cell with identical solutions and reference electrodes on each side of the glass membrane. There is rarely the need\, nor the possibility\, of measuring the asymmetry potential of commercial glass electrodes. Drifts in glass electrode potentials with time and variations from day to day in the potential measured in a standard buffer may be attributed to changes in asymmetry potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00488
name: atactic macromolecule
def: "A regular macromolecule in which the configurational \(base\) units are not all identical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00489
name: atactic polymer
def: "A polymer composed of atactic macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00490
name: atmosphere
def: "Non-SI unit of pressure\, \\\(1\\ \\rm\{atm\} = 101\\\,325\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00491
name: atmosphere
def: "The entire mass of air surrounding the earth which is composed largely of nitrogen\, oxygen\, water vapour\, clouds \(liquid or solid water\)\, carbon dioxide\, together with trace gases and aerosols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00492
name: atmospheric pressure ionization
def: "An ambiguous term; in essence\, it is used to describe chemical ionization at atmospheric pressure. It is recommended that use of the term be discontinued."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00493
name: atom
def: "Smallest particle still characterizing a chemical element. It consists of a nucleus of a positive charge \(\\\(Z\\\) is the proton number and \\\(e\\\) the elementary charge\) carrying almost all its mass \(more than 99.9%\) and \\\(Z\\\) electrons determining its size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00494
name: atomic fluorescence
def: "A combined process of photon absorption by an atom followed by spontaneous photon emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00495
name: atomic laser
def: "A gas laser which is pumped using energy transfer from other atoms or molecules. Examples are the helium-neon \($\\ce\{HeNe\}$\) and the copper vapour laser."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00496
name: atomic mass
def: "Rest mass of an atom in its ground state. The commonly used unit is the unified atomic mass unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00497
name: atomic mass constant
def: "One twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom in its nuclear and electronic ground state\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{u\}\} = \\pu\{1.6605402\(10\)E-27 kg\}\\\). It is equal to the unified atomic mass unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00499
name: atomic number
def: "The number of protons in the atomic nucleus."
synonym: "proton number" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00500
name: atomic orbital
def: "One-electron wavefunction obtained as a solution of the Schrödinger equation for an atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00502
name: atomic spectral lines
def: "Atomic and ionic spectral lines originate from specified electronic transitions between energy levels of atoms and ions\, respectively. In the past it has been common usage to denote atomic lines as arc lines and ionic lines as spark lines. This usage is now considered to be incorrect. The correct way to indicate that lines are due to atomic or ionic transitions is\: Element symbol I wavelength\, e.g.\, Cu I \\\(324.7\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\); and Element symbol II wavelength\, e.g.\, Cu II \\\(213.6\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\). Similarly for higher states of ionization\, the type of line is represented by III\, IV\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00503
name: atomic symbol
def: "One\, two or three letters used to represent the atom in chemical formulae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00504
name: atomic units
def: "The units designed to simplify the form of the fundamental equations of quantum mechanics by eliminating from them fundamental constants. The atomic unit of length is the Bohr radius\, \\\(a_\{\\rm\{o\}\}=\\frac\{h^\{2\}\}\{4\\ \\pi ^\{2\}\\ m\\ e^\{2\}\}=5.291\\ 77249\\times 10^\{-11\}\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\) \(\\\(0.529177249\\ \\mathring\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\). Energy is measured in hartrees\, where \\\(1\\ \\rm\{hartree\} = \\frac\{e^\{2\}\}\{a_\{\\rm\{o\}\}\}=4.359\\ 7482\\times 10^\{-18\}\\ \\rm\{J\}\\\). Masses are specified in terms of atomic mass unit\, \\\(\\pu\{amu\} = \\pu\{1.6605402E-27 kg\}\\\) and of the electron mass unit\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{e\}\} = \\pu\{0.910953E-30 kg\}\\\). The advantage of atomic units is that if all calculations are directly expressed in such units\, the results do not vary with any revision of the numerical values of the fundamental constants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00506
name: atomization
def: "The conversion of volatilized analyte into free atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00507
name: atomization surface temperature
def: "The temperature of the support from which the sample is atomized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00508
name: atomize
def: "To subdivide a liquid into very small particles; methods include\: impact with a jet of gas\, use of a spinning disk generator\, vibrating orifice generator\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00509
name: atomizer
def: "Any system which is capable of converting the analyte into atomic vapour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00510
name: atom–molecule complex mechanism
def: "A mechanism that sometimes applies to the combination of atoms\, but rarely of free radicals. In this mechanism the atom A first combines with a third body or chaperon\, $$\\ce\{A + M -> AM\}$$ and the complex AM then forms $\\ce\{A2 + M\}$ by collision with another atom $\\ce\{A\}$. Contrast the energy-transfer mechanism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00511
name: atropisomers
def: "A subclass of conformers which can be isolated as separate chemical species and which arise from restricted rotation about a single bond\, e.g. ortho-substituted biphenyl\, 1\,1\,2\,2-tetra-tert-butylethane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00512
name: attachment
def: "A transformation by which one molecular entity \(the substrate\) is converted into another by the formation of one \(and only one\) two-centre bond between the substrate and another molecular entity and which involves no other changes in connectivity in the substrate. For example\, the formation of an acyl cation by attachment of carbon monoxide to a carbenium ion \($\\ce\{R^\{+\}\}$\)\:  The product of an attachment may also be the adduct of the two reactants\, but not all adducts can be represented as the products of an attachment. \(For example\, the Diels–Alder cycloaddition\:  results in an adduct of buta-1\,3-diene and ethene\, but the reaction cannot be described as an attachment since bonds are formed between more than two centres.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00513
name: attenuance
def: "Analogous to absorbance\, but taking into account also the effects due to scattering and luminescence. It was formerly called extinction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00514
name: attenuance filter
def: "An optical device \(filter\) which reduces the radiant power of a light beam by a constant factor over all wavelengths within its operating range. Sometimes called attenuator or neutral density filter."
synonym: "neutral-density filter" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00515
name: attenuation
def: "The reduction of a radiation quantity upon passage of radiation through matter resulting from interactions of the radiation with the matter it traverses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00516
name: attenuation coefficient
def: "Analogous to absorption coefficient but taking into account also the effects due to scattering and luminescence. It was formerly called extinction coefficient."
synonym: "extinction coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00517
name: atto
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-18\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\rm\{a\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00518
name: attractive–mixed–repulsive classification
def: "A classification of potential-energy surfaces in which a highly attractive surface is at one extreme and a highly repulsive surface is at the other. The energy release in intermediate cases is referred to as mixed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00519
name: attractive potential-energy surface
def: "A potential-energy surface for a process A + B–C in which the initial descent of the system into the product valley is associated with a substantial decrease in the A–B distance and with little separation between the products A–B and C. In terms of a potential-energy profile\, the energy barrier occurs in the early stage of the reaction path. Attractive surfaces are also called early-downhill surfaces\, and the barrier in such a surface is called a Type-I barrier."
synonym: "early-downhill surface" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00520
name: Auger effect
def: "The emission of an electron from an atom accompanying the filling of a vacancy in an inner electron shell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00521
name: Auger electron
def: "Electron originating in the Auger effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00522
name: Auger electron spectroscopy
def: "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with \\\(\\mathrm\{keV\}\\\)-energy electrons or X-rays\, and the energy distribution of the electrons produced through radiationless de-excitation of the atoms in the sample \(Auger electrons\) is recorded. The derivative curve may also be recorded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00523
name: Auger electron yield
def: "The fraction of the atoms having a vacancy in an inner orbital which relax by emission of an Auger electron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00524
name: Auger yield
def: "For a given excited state of a specified atom the probability that the de-excitation occurs by the Auger effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00525
name: autocatalytic reaction
def: "A chemical reaction in which a product \(or a reaction intermediate\) also functions as a catalyst. In such a reaction the observed rate of reaction is often found to increase with time from its initial value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00526
name: auto-ionization
def: "This occurs when an internally supra-excited atom or molecular moiety loses an electron spontaneously without further interaction with an energy source. \(The state of the atom or molecular moiety is known as a pre-ionization state.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00527
name: automation
def: "Mechanization with process control\, where process means a sequence of manipulations. One or several functions in an analytical instrument may be automated. The corresponding adjective is automated and the verb is automate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00528
name: automerization
def: "Synonymous with degenerate rearrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00529
name: autophobicity
def: "If adsorption equilibrium and mutual saturation of the phases is not achieved instantly\, it is possible to distinguish the initial spreading tension\, \\\(\\sigma _\{\\rm\{i\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}\\\)\, from the final spreading tension\, \\\(\\sigma _\{\\rm\{i\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}\\\)\, when equilibrium has been reached. In the case in which \\\(\\sigma _\{\\rm\{i\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}\\\) is positive\, while \\\(\\sigma _\{\\rm\{i\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}\\\) is negative\, the system is said to exhibit autophobicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00531
name: autoprotolysis
def: "A proton \(hydron\) transfer reaction between two identical molecules \(usually a solvent\)\, one acting as a Brønsted acid and the other as a Brønsted base. For example\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00532
name: autoprotolysis constant
def: "The product of the activities \(or\, more approximately\, concentrations\) of the species produced as the result of autoprotolysis. For solvents in which no other ionization processes are significant the term is synonymous with 'ionic product'. The autoprotolysis constant for water\, \\\(K_\{\\rm\{w\}\}\\\)\, is equal to the product of activities\: \\\[a\\left\(\\rm\{H\}_\{3\}\\rm\{O\}^\{\\rm\{+\}\}\\right\)\\ a\\left\(\\rm\{OH\}^\{\\rm\{-\}\}\\right\) = 1.0\\times 10^\{-14\}\\\] at \\\(25\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00533
name: autoradiograph
def: "A radiograph of an object containing radioactive substance\, produced by placing the object adjacent to a photographic plate or film or a fluorescent screen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00534
name: autoradiolysis
def: "Radiolysis of a radioactive material resulting directly or indirectly from its radioactive decay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00535
name: auxiliary electrode
def: "Three-electrode cells comprise \(1\) an indicator \(or test\) electrode or a working electrode\, at the surface of which processes that are of interest may occur\, \(2\) a reference electrode and \(3\) a third electrode\, the auxiliary or counter electrode\, which serves merely to carry the current flowing through the cell\, and at the surface of which no processes of interest occur. If processes of interest occur at both the anode and the cathode of a cell \(as in differential amperometry or controlled current potentiometric titration with two indicator electrodes\)\, the cell should be said to comprise two indicator \(or test\) working electrodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00536
name: auxochrome
def: "An atom or group which\, when added to or introduced into a chromophore\, causes a bathochromic shift and/or a hyperchromic effect in a given band of the chromophore\, usually in that of lowest frequency. This term is obsolete."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00537
name: auxotrophy
def: "The inability of an organism to synthesize a particular organic compound required for its growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00539
name: average current density
def: "The average current density\, \\\(j\\\)\, is defined by \\\[j = A^\{-1\}\\ \\int _\{A\}j_\{x\}\\ \\rm\{d\}A\\\] where \\\(A\\\) is the electrode area\, \\\(j_\{x\}\\\) is the local current density and \\\(\\rm\{d\}A\\\) is an infinitesimal surface element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00540
name: average degree of polymerization
def: "Any average\, \\\(\\bar\{X\}_\{\\rm\{k\}\}\\\)\, of the degree of polymerization\, where \\\(\\rm\{k\}\\\) specifies the type of average."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00541
name: average life
def: "Defined for an atom or nuclear system in a specified state. For an exponentially decaying system\, it is the average time for the number of atoms or nuclei in a specified state to decrease by a factor e. Synonymous with mean lifetime."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00542
name: average rate of flow
def: "The ratio of the mass of a drop\, at the instant when it is detached from the tip of the capillary\, to the drop time \\\(t_\{1\}\\\); the average value of the instantaneous rate of flow over the entire life of the drop."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00543
name: Avogadro constant
def: "The Avogadro constant\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{A\}\} = \\pu\{6.02214076E23 mol-1\}\\\)\, is a proportionality constant between the quantity amount of substance \(\\\(n\\\)\, with unit mole\) and the quantity for counting entities \(\\\(N\\\) with unit one\, symbol 1\)\, \\\(N = N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\ n\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00544
name: avoided crossing
def: "Frequently\, two Born–Oppenheimer electronic states \(A\, B\) change their energy order as molecular geometry \(x\) is changed continuously along a path. In the process their energies may become equal at some points \(the surfaces are said to cross\, dotted lines in the figure\)\, or only come relatively close \(the crossing of the surfaces is said to be avoided\). If the electronic states are of the same symmetry\, the surface crossing is always avoided in diatomics and usually avoided in polyatomics.   Synonymous with intended crossing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00545
name: Avrami equation
def: "An equation\, describing crystallization kinetics\, of the form\: \\\[1 - \\varphi _\{c\} = \\rm\{e\}^\{K\\ t^\{n\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\varphi _\{c\}\\\) is the crystalline volume fraction developed at time \\\(t\\\) and constant temperature\, \\\(K\\\) and \\\(n\\\) are suitable parameters. \\\(K\\\) is temperature dependent. According to the original theory\, \\\(n\\\) should be an integer from 1 to 4\, the value of which should depend only on the type of the statistical model; however\, it has become customary to regard it as an adjustable parameter that may be non-integral."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00546
name: axial
def: "In the chair form of cyclohexane ring bonds to ring atoms \(and molecular entities attached to such bonds\) are termed axial or equatorial according to whether the bonds make a relatively large or small angle\, respectively\, with the plane containing or passing closest to a majority of the ring atoms. Thus the axial bonds are approximately parallel to the $C_\{3\}$ axis and the equatorial bonds approximately parallel to two of the ring bonds. These terms are also used for the chair form of other saturated six-membered rings. The corresponding bonds occurring at the allylic positions in mono-unsaturated six-membered rings are termed pseudo-axial \(or quasi-axial\) and pseudo-equatorial \(or quasi-equatorial\). The terms axial and equatorial have similarly been used in relation to the puckered conformation of cyclobutane\, crown conformer of cyclooctane\, etc. and the terms pseudo-axial and pseudo-equatorial in the context of the non-planar structures of cyclopentane and cycloheptane.  See apical for an alternative use of axial and equatorial with bipyramidal structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00547
name: axial chirality
def: "Term used to refer to stereoisomerism resulting from the non-planar arrangement of four groups in pairs about a chirality axis. It is exemplified by allenes $\\ce\{abC=C=Ccd\}$ \(or $\\ce\{abC=C=Cab\}$\) and by the atropisomerism of ortho-substituted biphenyls. The configuration in molecular entities possessing axial chirality is specified by the stereodescriptors $R_\{\\rm\{a\}\}$ and $S_\{\\rm\{a\}\}$ \(or by $P$ or $M$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00548
name: axialite
def: "A multilayer aggregate\, consisting of lamellar crystals splaying out from a common edge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00553
name: azanes
def: "Saturated acyclic nitrogen hydrides having the general formula $\\ce\{N_\{n\}H_\{n+2\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00555
name: azides
def: "Compounds bearing the group $\\ce\{N3\}$\, viz. $\\ce\{–N=N^\{+\}=N^\{-\}\}$; usually attached to carbon\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhN3\}$ phenyl azide or azidobenzene.Salts of hydrazoic acid\, $\\ce\{HN3\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{NaN3\}$ sodium azide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00557
name: azines
def: "Condensation products\, $\\ce\{R2C=NN=CR2\}$\, of two moles of a carbonyl compound with one mole of hydrazine  \(this term should not be confused with the ending -azine appearing in Hantzsch–Widman names for some heterocycles\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00558
name: azinic acids
def: "Derivatives of the parent structure $\\ce\{H2N^\{+\}\(O^\{−\}\)OH\}$\, of which the alkylidene derivatives\, $\\ce\{R2C=N^\{+\}\(O^\{−\}\)OH\}$ \(tautomers of nitroalkanes\)\, are the most commonly encountered. The alkylideneazinic acids are known as nitronic acids\, or synonymously as aci-nitro compounds\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH2=N^\{+\}\(O^\{−\}\)OH\}$ methylideneazinic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00559
name: azlactones
def: "Oxazol-5\(4$H$\)-ones I\, compounds derived by cyclization of $N$-acyl $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}amino\}$ carboxylic acids\, $\\ce\{RC\(=O\)NHCR2C\(=O\)OH\}$\, through formal loss of the elements of water. 4-Hydrocarbylideneazlactones II are often referred to as 'unsaturated azlactones'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00560
name: azo compounds
def: "Derivatives of diazene \(diimide\)\, $\\ce\{HN=NH\}$\, wherein both hydrogens are substituted by hydrocarbyl groups\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhN=NPh\}$ azobenzene or diphenyldiazene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00561
name: azo imides
def: "N-Imides of azo compounds\, analogous to azoxy compounds\, having a delocalized structure  commonly but undesirably referred to as 'azimines'."
synonym: "azimines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00562
name: azomethine imides
def: "The 1\,3-dipolar N-imides of azomethines having the structure   The term azo ylides\, derived from the second resonance form\, has also been used."
synonym: "azo ylides" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00564
name: azomethines
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RN=CR2\}$ \($\\ce\{R\neq H\}$\). Many consider the term to include the compounds $\\ce\{RN=CRH\}$ \($\\ce\{R\neq H\}$\)\, thus making azomethines synonymous with Schiff bases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00565
name: azomethine ylides
def: "1\,3-Dipolar compounds having the structure"

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00566
name: azonic acids
def: "$N$-Hydrocarbyl derivatives of the parent structure $\\ce\{HN^\{+\}\(O^\{−\}\)\(OH\)2\}$. Cf. phosphonic acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A00567
name: azoxy compounds
def: "$N$-Oxides of azo compounds of structure $\\ce\{RN=N^\{+\}\(O^\{−\}\)R\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhN=N^\{+\}\(O^\{−\}\)Ph\}$ azoxybenzene or diphenyldiazene oxide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:A04388
name: π-adduct
def: "An adduct formed by electron-pair donation from a $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$ into a $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$\, or from a $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$ into a $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$\, or from a $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$ into a $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$. For example\:  Such an adduct has commonly been known as a '$\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}complex\}$'\, but\, as the bonding is not necessarily weak\, it is better to avoid the term complex."
synonym: "π-complex" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:A05436
name: σ-adduct
def: "The product formed by the attachment of an electrophilic or nucleophilic entering group or of a radical to a ring carbon of an aromatic species so that a new $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ is formed and the original conjugation is disrupted. \(This has generally been called a '$\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}complex\}$'\, but adduct is more appropriate than complex according to the definitions given.\) The term may also be used for analogous adducts to unsaturated \(and conjugated\) systems in general."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06761
name: albumin
def: "A type of protein\, especially a protein of blood plasma which transports various substances\, including metal ions\, drugs and xenobiotics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06766
name: anation
def: "Replacement of the ligand water by an anion in a coordination entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06767
name: apoprotein
def: "A protein without its characteristic prosthetic group or metal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06776
name: anisotropy
def: "The property of molecules and materials to exhibit variations in physical properties along different molecular axes of the substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06794
name: acid-labile sulfur
def: "Refers to sulfido ligands\, e.g.\, the bridging ligands in iron-sulfur proteins\, which are released as $\\ce\{H2S\}$ at acid pH."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06796
name: autacoid
def: "A biological substance secreted by various cells whose physiological activity is restricted to the vicinity of its release; it is often referred to as a local hormone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06797
name: absorption coefficient
def: "Ratio of the absorbed quantity \(uptake\) of a substance to the administered quantity \(intake\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06799
name: acceptable daily intake
def: "Estimate by JECFA of the amount of a food additive\, expressed on a body-weight basis\, that can be ingested daily over a lifetime without appreciable health risk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06800
name: acute toxicity
def: "Adverse effects of finite duration occurring within a short time \(up to $\\pu\{14 d\}$\) after administration of a single dose \(or exposure to a given concentration\) of a test substance or after multiple doses \(exposures\)\, usually within $\\pu\{24 h\}$ of a starting point \(which may be exposure to the toxicant\, or loss of reserve capacity\, or developmental change\, etc.\)Ability of a substance to cause adverse effects within a short time of dosing or exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06803
name: allometric
def: "Pertaining to a systematic relationship between growth rates of different parts of an organism and its overall growth rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06804
name: apoptosis
def: "Active process of programmed cell death requiring metabolic energy\, often characterised by fragmentation of DNA\, and without associated inflammation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06805
name: attributable risk
def: "Part of a risk that is identified as due to exposure to a defined substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06809
name: adverse effect
def: "Change in biochemistry\, morphology\, physiology\, growth\, development\, or lifespan of an organism which results in impairment of functional capacity or impairment of capacity to compensate for additional stress or increase in susceptibility to other environmental influences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06849
name: aging
def: "Processes that occur in a polymeric material during a specified period of time\, and that usually result in changes in physical and/or chemical structure and the values of the properties of the material."
synonym: "ageing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06862
name: asymmetric membrane
def: "Membrane constituted of two or more structural planes of non-identical morphologies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06934
name: adsorption isotherm
def: "Isotherm describing adsorption of the sample component on the surface of the stationary phase from the mobile phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06947
name: artificial neural networks
def: "Artificial neural networks \(ANN\) are algorithms simulating the functioning of human neurons and may be used for pattern recognition problems\, e.g.\, to establish quantitative structure-activity relationships."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06983
name: <i>ab initio</i> quantum mechanical methods
def: "Methods of quantum mechanical calculations independent of any experiment other than the determination of fundamental constants. The methods are based on the use of the full Schrödinger equation to treat all the electrons of a chemical system. In practice\, approximations are necessary to restrict the complexity of the electronic wavefunction and to make its calculation possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06984
name: agostic interaction
def: "The manner of interaction \(termed according to the Greek 'to hold or clasp to oneself as a shield'\) of a coordinatively unsaturated metal atom with a ligand\, when the metal atom draws the ligand towards itself. An important type of agostic interaction is the C–H–Metal coordination providing for the activation of the C–H bond in transition metal complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06985
name: alternancy symmetry
def: "A topological property of the molecular graphs of alternant hydrocarbons which allows the carbon atoms to be divided into two subsets in such a way that no two atoms of the same subset are adjacent. A consequence of this property is the symmetrical arrangement of the energy levels of bonding and antibonding Hückel MOs relative to the level of a nonbonding orbital \(energy level of the p AO of a carbon atom\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06986
name: angular overlap model
def: "A method of description of transition metal–ligand interactions and main group stereochemistry\, whose basic assumption is that the strength of a bond formed using atomic orbitals on two atoms is related to the magnitude of overlap of the two orbitals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06987
name: antiaromaticity
def: "Those cyclic molecules for which cyclic electron delocalization provides for the reduction \(in some cases\, loss\) of thermodynamic stability compared to acyclic structural analogues are classified as antiaromatic species. In contrast to aromatic compounds\, antiaromatic ones are prone to reactions causing changes in their structural type\, and display tendency to alternation of bond lengths and fluxional behavior \(see fluxional molecules\) both in solution and in the solid. Antiaromatic molecules possess negative \(or very low positive\) values of resonance energy and a small energy gap between their highest occupied and lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals. In antiaromatic molecules\, an external magnetic field induces a paramagnetic electron current. Whereas benzene represents the prototypical aromatic compound\, cyclobuta-1\,3-diene exemplifies the compound with most clearly defined antiaromatic properties."
synonym: "antithetical to aromaticity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06988
name: antibonding molecular orbital
def: "The molecular orbital whose occupation by electrons decreases the total bonding \(as usual\, increases the total energy\) of a molecule. The energy level of an antibonding MO lies higher than the average of the valence atomic orbitals of the atoms constituting the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06989
name: antisymmetry principle
def: "The postulate that electrons must be described by wavefunctions which are antisymmetric with respect to interchange of the coordinates \(including spin\) of a pair of electrons. A corollary of the principle is the Pauli exclusion principle. All particles with half-integral spin \(fermions\) are described by antisymmetric wavefunctions\, and all particles with zero or integral spin \(bosons\) are described by symmetric wavefunctions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06990
name: apicophilicity
def: "In trigonal bipyramidal structures with a five-coordinate central atom\, the stabilization achieved through a ligand changing its position from equatorial to apical \(axial\). The apicophilicity of an atom or a group is evaluated by either the energy difference between the stereoisomers \(permutational isomers\) containing the ligand in apical and equatorial positions or the energy barrier to permutational isomerization \(see also Berry pseudorotation\). In general\, the greater the electronegativity and the stronger the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$-withdrawing properties of a ligand \(as for $\\ce\{Cl\}$\, $\\ce\{F\}$\, $\\ce\{CN\}$\)\, the higher is its apicophilicity. The notion of apicophilicity has been extended to four-coordinate bisphenoidal and three-coordinate T-shaped structures\, which can be viewed as trigonal bipyramidal species where\, respectively\, one or two vertices are occupied by phantom ligands \(lone electron pairs\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06992
name: atom-atom polarizability
def: "A quantity used in perturbation HMO theory as a measure of the change in electron density\, \\\(q\\\)\, of atom \\\(s\\\) caused by a change in the electronegativity \(or coulomb integral\) of atom \\\(r\\\)\: \\\[\\pi _\{\\rm\{sr\}\}=\\frac\{\\partial q_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\{\\partial a_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06993
name: atom-bond polarizability
def: "A quantity used in perturbation HMO theory as a measure of the change in electron density\, \\\(q\\\)\, of atom \\\(m\\\) caused by a change in the resonance integral\, \\\(\\beta \\\)\, of bond \\\(rs\\\)\: \\\[\\pi _\{\\rm\{m\,rs\}\}=\\frac\{\\partial q_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\}\{\\partial \\beta _\{\\rm\{rs\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06994
name: atomic charge
def: "The charge attributed to an atom \\\(A\\\) within a molecule defined as \\\(\\zeta =Z_\{\\rm\{A\}\}- q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(Z_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) is the atomic number of \\\(A\\\) and \\\(q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) is the electron density assigned to \\\(A\\\). The method of calculation of \\\(q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) depends on the choice of the scheme of partitioning electron density. In the framework of the Mulliken population analysis \\\(q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) is associated with the so-called gross atomic population\: \\\(q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}=\\sum q_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(q_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\}\\\) is a gross population for an orbital \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\\) in the basis set employed defined according to \\\[q_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\} = P_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\}+\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\n\nu \neq \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\} \n\\end\{array\}\}P_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\nu\}\\ S_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\nu\}\\\] where \\\(P_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\} \}\\\) and \\\(S_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\}\}\\\) are the elements of density matrix and overlap matrix\, respectively \(see overlap integral\). In the Hückel molecular orbital theory \(where \\\(S_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\nu\} = \\delta _\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\nu\}\\\)\)\, \\\(q_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\} = n_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\}\\ P_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(n_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\}\\\) is the number of electrons in the \\\(\\rm\{MO\}\\\,\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT06996
name: aufbau principle
def: "A rule for building up the electronic configuration of atoms and molecules. It states that a maximum of two electrons are put into orbitals in the order of increasing orbital energy\: the lowest-energy orbitals are filled before electrons are placed in higher-energy orbitals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07196
name: ampholytic polymer
def: "Polyelectrolyte composed of macromolecules containing both cationic and anionic groups\, or corresponding ionizable groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07198
name: anionic polymer
def: "Polymer composed of negatively charged macromolecules and an equivalent amount of counter-cations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07312
name: absorbed photon flux density
def: "Number of photons of a particular wavelength per time interval \(spectral photon flux\, number basis\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{p\}\,\\lambda \}\\\)\, or spectral photon flux\, amount basis\, \\\(q_\{n\,\\rm\{p\}\,\\lambda \}\\\)\) absorbed by a system per volume\, \\\(V\\\). On number basis\, SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-4\}\\\)\, and the  common unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{cm\}^\{-3\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\). On amount basis\, SI unit is \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1 m-4\}\\\)\, and a common unit is \\\(\\pu\{einstein s-1 cm-3 nm-1\}\\\)."
synonym: "absorbed spectral photon flux density" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07313
name: absorbed radiant power density
def: "Spectral radiant energy per time interval \(spectral radiant power\, \\\(P_\{\\lambda\}\\\)\) absorbed by a system per volume\, \\\(V\\\). SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-4\}\\\); common unit is \\\(\\rm\{W cm\}^\{-3\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\)."
synonym: "absorbed spectral radiant power density" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07314
name: actinic
def: "Applied or referred to actinism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07315
name: actinism
def: "Chemical changes on living and non-living materials caused by optical radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07317
name: acyl shift
def: "Photochemical process with skeletal isomerization by shift of an acyl group from position 1 to position 2 or 3."
synonym: "1" []
synonym: "1" []
synonym: "2-acyl shift" []
synonym: "3-acyl shift" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07318
name: alkene photocycloaddition
def: "Photochemical process leading to formation of a cyclobutane upon addition of an electronically excited alkene to another ground-state alkene or of an electronically excited $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\,\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}unsaturated\}$ carbonyl compound to a ground-state alkene. The latter is called a de Mayo reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07319
name: alkene photodimerization
def: "Photochemical process leading to the formation of a cyclobutane from two identical alkenes\, i.e.\, from the photocycloaddition of an electronically excited alkene to a ground-state alkene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07320
name: alkene photoisomerization
def: "Photochemical process with geometrical isomerization of a carbon–carbon double bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07321
name: alkene photorearrangement
def: "Photochemical process leading to the skeletal rearrangement of an alkene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07326
name: amalgam lamp
def: "Intense source of ultraviolet \(\\\(185\\\) and \\\(253.7\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\) radiation produced by an electrical discharge in a lamp with the inner side covered by an amalgam of mercury with another element such as indium or gallium to control the vapour pressure of the mercury. These lamp have 2-3 times the UV output for the same wavelength as the standard low-pressure mercury lamp."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07327
name: aromatic photocycloaddition
def: "Inter- and intramolecular photochemical processes involving the addition of a $\\ce\{C–C\}$ double \(or triple\) bond \(i\) to the 1\,2-positions of an arene in which case it is called an ortho photocycloaddition\, with formation of a benzocyclobutene \(or a benzocyclobutadiene\) derivative\, \(ii\) to the 1\,3-positions of an arene in which case it is called a meta photocycloaddition\, with formation of tricyclo\[3.3.0.02\,8\]oct-3-ene \(or octa-3\,6-diene\) derivatives\, or \(iii\) to the 1\,4-positions of an arene in which case it is called a para photocycloaddition\, with formation of bicyclo\[2\,2\,2\]oct-2-ene \(or octa-2\,5-diene\) derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07328
name: asymmetric photochemistry
def: "Photochemical process leading to a chiral substance from an achiral precursor such that one enantiomer predominates over the other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07329
name: aza-di-π-methane rearrangement
def: "Photochemical reaction of a 1-aza-1\,4-diene or a 2-aza-1\,4-diene in the triplet excited state to form the corresponding cyclopropylimine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07502
name: agglomerate
def: "Cluster of molecules or particles that results from agglomeration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07516
name: alcogel
def: "Gel in which the swelling agent consists predominantly of an alcohol or a mixture of alcohols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07517
name: aquagel
def: "Hydrogel in which the network component is a colloidal network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07591
name: affine chain behaviour
def: "Behaviour of a polymer network in which the junction points deform uniformly with the macroscopic deformation of the network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07606
name: aerosol hydrolysis
def: "Hydrolysis of the dispersed component of an aerosol."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07607
name: agglomeration
def: "Process in which dispersed molecules or particles assemble rather than remain as isolated single molecules or particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:AT07608
name: aggregation
def: "Process whereby dispersed molecules or particles form aggregates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00571
name: β-cleavage
def: "Fission next but one to a heteroatom or functional group producing a radical and an ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00572
name: β-decay
def: "Nuclear decay in which a β-particle is emitted or in which orbital electron capture occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00573
name: β-particle
def: "Electron ejected from a radioactive nucleus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00575
name: back electron transfer
def: "A term often used to indicate thermal reversal of excited state electron transfer restoring the donor and acceptor in their original oxidation level. In using this term one should also specify the resulting electronic state of the donor and acceptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00576
name: back extraction
def: "Synonymous with stripping \(by extraction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00577
name: background
def: "The term employed to designate the value indicated by a radiation measuring device in the absence of the source whose radiation is to be measured\, when the device is placed under its normal conditions of operation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00578
name: background concentration
def: "The concentration of a given species in a pristine air mass in which anthropogenic impurities of a relatively short lifetime are not present. The background concentrations of relatively long-lived molecules\, methane\, carbon dioxide\, halocarbons \($\\ce\{CF3Cl\}$\, $\\ce\{CF2Cl2\}$\, etc.\) and some other species continue to rise due to anthropogenic input\, so the composition of background air is undergoing continual change. Background concentration of a given species is sometimes considered to be the concentration of that impurity in a given air mass when the contribution from anthropogenic sources under study is absent. Synonymous with baseline concentration."
synonym: "background level" []
synonym: "baseline concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00579
name: background mass spectrum
def: "The mass spectrum observed when no sample is intentionally introduced into the mass spectrometer or spectrograph."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00580
name: background radiation
def: "Radiation from any source other than the one it is desired to detect or measure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00581
name: backscatter
def: "Scattering of radiation in a generally backward direction. In the assay of radioactivity\, it applies to the scattering of radiation into the radiation detector from any material except the sample and the detector. In light scattering\, it is said to occur when the scattering angle\, \\\(\\theta \\\)\, is \\\(180^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\). Conversely\, forward scattering occurs when \\\(\\theta \\\) is \\\(0^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00582
name: back scatter coefficient
def: "Number of back scattered electrons \(BSEs\) generated per primary electron for a given specimen and experimental condition. It depends on \(mean\) atomic number of the excited area of the sample\, angle between electron beam and sample surface\, primary electron energy and thickness of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00583
name: back scattered electrons
def: "All primary electrons which are scattered out of the original direction and retransmitted through the surface of the solid. In practice\, electrons emitted from the surface of a solid under electron bombardment which have a kinetic energy in the range between \\\(50\\ \\rm\{eV\}\\\) and excitation energy \(\\\(E_\{0\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00587
name: baffle chamber
def: "A chamber used in incinerator design to promote the settling of fly ash and coarse particulate matter by changing the direction and/or reducing the velocity of the gases produced by the combustion of the refuse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00588
name: bag filter
def: "A large bag constructed of a suitable fabric which is often tubular in shape\, into which a particle-containing air stream flows. Modern bags are constructed of a fabric which is capable of collecting all but very fine particles in the gas stream. The efficiency of the removal of particles of various size ranges changes with the amount of particles captured by the filter and the filtering time. The bag operates on the same principle as the one on a household vacuum cleaner."
synonym: "fabric filter" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00589
name: baghouse
def: "An installation which contains many bag filters in parallel so that the resistance to air flow in a large installation is not seriously increased by the addition of these controls."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00590
name: Bainite transition
def: "A term that is sometimes used in metallurgy for the martensitic transition that describes the transition between a face-centred-cubic lattice and a body-centred-tetragonal lattice. Example\: The transition between the face-centred-cubic lattice of austenite and the body-centred-tetragonal lattice of a martensite."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00591
name: baking
def: "The process in which the carbonaceous binder\, usually coal tar pitch or petroleum pitch\, as part of a shaped carbon mix is converted to carbon yielding a rigid carbon body by the slow application of heat. The process can take as little as \\\(14\\ \\rm\{days\}\\\) in coarse-grained\, electrothermic grades \(low binder level\) and as long as \\\(36\\ \\rm\{days\}\\\) in ultra-fine-grained\, specialty grades \(high binder level\). The final baking temperature can be in the range of \\\(1100\{-\}1500\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\, depending on the grade."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00592
name: Baldwin's rules
def: "A set of empirical rules for certain formations of 3- to 7-membered rings. The predicted pathways are those in which the length and nature of the linking chain enables the terminal atoms to achieve the proper geometries for reaction. The disfavoured cases are subject to severe distortions of bond angles and bond distances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00593
name: bandgap energy
def: "The energy difference between the bottom of the conduction band and the top of the valence band in a semiconductor or an insulator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00594
name: bandpass filter
def: "An optical device which permits the transmission of radiation within a specified wavelength range and does not permit transmission of radiation at higher or lower wavelengths. It can be an interference filter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00595
name: band spectra
def: "The combination of many different spectral lines resulting from vibrational\, rotational and electronic transitions. Unresolved band spectra may appear as a spectral continuum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00596
name: bar
def: "Non-SI unit of pressure\, \\\(\\rm\{bar\} = 10^\{5\}\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00597
name: barbiturates
def: "Pyrimidine-2\,4\,6\(1H\,3H\,5H\)-trione \(trivial name barbituric acid\) and derivatives\:  \(keto tautomeric form\)Salts of barbituric acid and its derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00598
name: barn
def: "Non-SI unit of area\, \\\(\\rm\{b\} = 10^\{-28\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{2\}\\\)\, used to express cross sections in nuclear reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00599
name: Barton reaction
def: "Photolysis of a nitrite to form a $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\\mbox\{-\}nitroso alcohol\}$. The mechanism is believed to involve a homolytic $\\ce\{RO–NO\}$ cleavage\, followed by $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\\mbox\{-\}hydrogen\}$ abstraction and radical coupling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00601
name: base
def: "A chemical species or molecular entity having an available pair of electrons capable of forming a covalent bond with a hydron \(proton\) \(see Brønsted base\) or with the vacant orbital of some other species \(see Lewis base\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00602
name: base electrolyte
def: "Synonymous with supporting electrolyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00603
name: base kind of quantity
def: "A kind of quantity considered dimensionally independent of other kinds of quantities\, i.e. it is not defined by an equation containing other kinds of quantities\, e.g. length\, mass\, amount of substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00604
name: baseline
def: "The portion of the chromatogram recording the detector response when only the mobile phase emerges from the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00606
name: baseline error
def: "An error which arises if the two beams in a double-beam spectrometer are not fully equivalent in transmitted power nor are corrected to be so \(e.g. due to unmatched cells\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00607
name: base pairing
def: "The specific association between two complementary strands of nucleic acids that results from the formation of hydrogen bonds between the base components of the nucleotides of each strand\: A=T and G=C in DNA\, A=U and G=C \(and sometimes G=U\) in RNA \(the lines indicate the number of hydrogen bonds\). Single-stranded nucleic acid molecules can adopt a partially double-stranded structure through intrastrand base pairing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00608
name: base peak
def: "The peak in a mass spectrum corresponding to the separated ion beam which has the greatest intensity. This term may be applied to the spectra of pure substances or mixtures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00609
name: base quantity
def: "One of the quantities that\, in a system of quantities\, are conventionally accepted as functionally independent of one another. In the SI\, these are\: length\, mass\, time\, electric current\, temperature\, amount of substance\, and luminous intensity. All other physical quantities \(and units\) are regarded as being derived from these base quantities \(and base units\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00610
name: base unit
def: "Unit of measurement of a base quantity in a given system of quantities. By international agreement\, a set of seven dimensionally independent units form the SI base units\: the metre\, kilogram\, second\, ampere\, kelvin\, mole and candela."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00611
name: basicity
def: "For Brønsted bases\, the tendency of a compound to act as hydron \(proton\) acceptor. The basicity of a chemical species is normally expressed by the acidity of the conjugate acid \(see conjugate acid–base pair\). For Lewis bases it relates to the association constants of Lewis adducts and π-adducts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00613
name: batch
def: "A quantity of material which is known or assumed to be produced under uniform conditions. Some vocabularies assume 'lot' and 'batch' to be synonymous. The distinction made here with respect to knowledge of production history permits a lot to consist of one or more batches and is useful in interpreting the results of analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00615
name: batch operation
def: "The operation of an analytical instrument in such a way that one or more analytical procedures must be completed for a sequence of samples before the next sequence can be started. This term batch usually implies a sequence of a variably sized group of samples\, the size of which is not related to a particular type of instrument."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00616
name: batch reactor
def: "In a batch reactor the reactants and the catalyst are placed in the reactor which is then closed to transport of matter and the reaction is allowed to proceed for a given time whereupon the mixture of unreacted material together with the products is withdrawn. Provision for mixing may be required."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00618
name: bathochromic shift
def: "Shift of a spectral band to lower frequencies \(longer wavelengths\) owing to the influence of substitution or a change in environment. It is informally referred to as a red shift and is opposite to hypsochromic shift \(blue shift\)."
synonym: "bathochromic effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00619
name: bead-rod model
def: "A model simulating the hydrodynamic properties of a chain macromolecule consisting of a sequence of beads\, each of which offers hydrodynamic resistance to the flow of the surrounding medium and is connected to the next bead by a rigid rod which does not. The mutual orientation of the rods is random."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00620
name: bead-spring model
def: "A model simulating the hydrodynamic properties of a chain macromolecule consisting of a sequence of beads\, each of which offers hydrodynamic resistance to the flow of the surrounding medium and is connected to the next bead by a spring which does not contribute to the frictional interaction but which is responsible for the elastic and deformational properties of the chain. The mutual orientation of the springs is random."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00621
name: beam current
def: "The number of primary electrons reaching the surface of the specimen per unit time expressed as electrical current. Recommended symbol\: \\\(i_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\); unit\: \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\); typical range\: \\\(\\mathrm\{nA\}\\\). Recommended measurement technique\: Faraday cage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00622
name: beam diameter
def: "Diameter of the beam between which 68% of the electrons fall. For Gaussian beam shapes this corresponds to the 2\\\(\\sigma \\\)-value of the intensity distribution of the beam. Measurement can be carried out by scanning a beam across a sufficiently sharp edge and recording the transmitted electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00624
name: becquerel
def: "SI derived unit of radioactivity equal to one disintegration per second; admitted for reasons of safeguarding human health\, symbol\: \\\(\\rm\{Bq\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00625
name: bed volume
def: "Synonymous with column volume for a packed column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00626
name: Beer–Lambert law
def: "The absorbance of a beam of collimated monochromatic radiation in a homogeneous isotropic medium is proportional to the absorption path length\, \\\(l\\\)\, and to the concentration\, \\\(c\\\)\, or — in the gas phase — to the pressure of the absorbing species. The law can be expressed as\: \\\[A = \\rm\{log\}_\{10\}\\left \( \\frac\{P_\{\\lambda \}^\{0\}\}\{P_\{\\lambda \}\} \\right \) = \\varepsilon \\\:c\\\:l\\\] or \\\[P_\{\\lambda \}=P_\{\\lambda \}^\{0\}10^\{-\\varepsilon \\\: c\\\: l\}\\\] where the proportionality constant\, \\\(\\varepsilon\\\)\, is called the molar \(decadic\) absorption coefficient. For \\\(l\\\) in \\\(\\pu\{cm\}\\\) and \\\(c\\\) in \\\(\\pu\{mol dm-3\}\\\) or \\\(\\rm\{M\}\\\)\, \\\(\\varepsilon\\\) will result in \\\(\\pu\{dm3 mol-1 cm-1\}\\\) or \\\(\\rm\{M\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{cm\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, which is a commonly used unit. The SI unit of \\\(\\varepsilon\\\) is \\\(\\pu\{m2 mol-1\}\\\). Note that spectral radiant power must be used because the Beer–Lambert law holds only if the spectral bandwidth of the light is narrow compared to spectral linewidths in the spectrum."
synonym: "Beer–Lambert–Bouguer law" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00627
name: before mass analysis
def: "The sum of all the separate ion currents for ions of the same sign \(usually positive\) before mass analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00628
name: Bell–Evans–Polanyi principle
def: "The linear relation between energy of activation \\\(\(E_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\)\\\) and enthalpy of reaction \\\(\(\\Delta H_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\)\\\) sometimes observed within a series of closely related reactions. \\\[E_\{\\rm\{a\}\} = A + B\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x394\}H_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00629
name: bending of energy bands
def: "The distribution of potential in the space charge region of a semiconductor results in a change in the electron energy levels with distance from the interface. This is usually described as 'bending of the energy bands'. Thus the bands are bent\, upwards if \\\(\\sigma >0\\\) and downwards if \\\(\\sigma"

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00630
name: benzenium ions
def: "Arenium ions\, derived from benzene or substituted derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00632
name: benzylic groups
def: "Arylmethyl groups and derivatives formed by substitution\: $\\ce\{ArCR2^\{–\}\}$\, Benzyl\, $\\ce\{C6H5CH2^\{–\}\}$\, is the prototype. The term 'benzylic position' or 'benzylic site' refers to the saturated carbon atom. A group\, such as $\\ce\{–OH\}$\, attached at a benzylic site is sometimes referred to as 'benzylic'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00633
name: benzylic intermediates
def: "Carbanions\, carbenium ions or radicals\, derived formally by detachment of one hydron\, hydride or hydrogen\, respectively\, from the $\\ce\{CH3\}$ group of toluene or substitution derivatives thereof\, e.g. $\\ce\{C6H5CH2^\{.\}\}$\, benzyl radical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00634
name: benzynes
def: "1\,2-Didehydrobenzene \(the aryne derived from benzene\) and its derivatives formed by substitution; see arynes\, dehydroarenes. The terms m- and p-benzyne are occasionally\, but erroneously\, used for 1\,3- and 1\,4-didehydrobenzene\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00637
name: betaines
def: "Originally\, the compound betaine\, $\\ce\{\(CH3\)3N^\{+\}–CH2C\(=O\)O^\{−\}\}$ $N\,N\,N$-trimethylammonioacetate\, and similar zwitterionic compounds derived from other amino acids. By extension\, neutral molecules having charge-separated forms with an onium atom which bears no hydrogen atoms and that is not adjacent to the anionic atom. Betaines cannot be represented without formal charges. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00638
name: betweenanenes
def: "Bicyclic alkenes having a double bond between the bridgehead atoms and a trans attachment of each branch to the double bond. Thus trans-bicyclo\[m.n.0\]alk-1 \(m+2\)-enes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00640
name: biased linear pulse amplifier
def: "A pulse amplifier which\, within the limits of its normal operating characteristics\, has a constant gain for that portion of an input pulse that exceeds the threshold value and that produces no output for pulses whose amplitude is below the threshold."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00642
name: bifunctional catalysis
def: "Catalysis \(usually for hydron transfer\) by a bifunctional chemical species involving a mechanism in which both functional groups are implicated in the rate-controlling step\, so that the corresponding catalytic coefficient is larger than that expected for catalysis by chemical species containing only one of these functional groups. The term should not be used to describe the concerted action of two different catalysts \('concerted catalysis'\)."
synonym: "polyfunctional catalysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00644
name: bimodal distribution
def: "The occurrence of two maxima in a frequency distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00646
name: binder
def: "Usually a coal tar pitch or petroleum pitch \(but may include thermosetting resins or mesophase pitch powders\) which\, when mixed with a binder coke or a filler\, constitutes a carbon mix. This is used in preparation of the formation of shaped green bodies and subsequently carbon artifacts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00647
name: binder
def: "An additive used to hold the solid stationary phase to the inactive plate or sheet in thin-layer chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00648
name: binder coke
def: "A constituent of a carbon \(or ceramic\) artifact resulting from carbonization of the binder during baking."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00649
name: binding site
def: "A specific region \(or atom\) in a molecular entity that is capable of entering into a stabilizing interaction with another molecular entity. An example of such an interaction is that of an active site in an enzyme with its substrate. Typical forms of interaction are by hydrogen bonding\, coordination and ion pair formation. Two binding sites in different molecular entities are said to be complementary if their interaction is stabilizing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00650
name: Bingham flow
def: "Many colloidal dispersions show Bingham flow which is characterized by a $\\upsigma\\!$-D diagram as shown. At rates of shear greater than that at point A\, the following relation applies\: \\\[\\sigma - \\sigma _\{\\rm\{B\}\}=\\eta _\{\\Delta \}\\ D\\\] where \\\(\\sigma _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) \(or \\\(\\tau _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\) is called the Bingham yield stress\, \\\(\\eta _\{\\Delta \}\\\) is the differential viscosity\, \\\(D\\\) is the shear rate\, and \\\(\\sigma \\\) is the average of three normal stress components if the deformation is purely dilatational."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00651
name: bioassay
def: "A procedure for determining the concentration or biological activity of a substance \(e.g. vitamin\, hormone\, plant growth factor\, antibiotic\) by measuring its effect on an organism or tissue compared to a standard preparation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00652
name: biocatalyst
def: "An enzyme or enzyme complex consisting of\, or derived from\, an organism or cell culture \(in cell-free or whole-cell forms\) that catalyses metabolic reactions in living organisms and/or substrate conversions in various chemical reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00653
name: biochemical oxygen demand
def: "The amount of oxygen\, divided by the volume of the system\, taken up through the respiratory activity of microorganisms growing on the organic compounds present in the sample \(e.g. water or sludge\) when incubated at a specified temperature \(usually \\\(\\pu\{20^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)\) for a fixed period \(usually \\\(\\rm\{5\\ days\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{BOD\}_\{5\}\\\)\). It is a measure of that organic pollution of water which can be degraded biologically. In practice\, it is usually expressed in milligrams \\\(\\ce\{O2\}\\\) per litre."
synonym: "biological oxygen demand" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00654
name: biochip
def: "An integrated circuit whose electrical and logical functions are performed by protein molecules appropriately manipulated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00656
name: biodegradation
def: "Breakdown of a substance catalysed by enzymes in vitro or in vivo. This may be characterized for purposes of hazard assessment as\:Primary. Alteration of the chemical structure of a substance resulting in loss of a specific property of that substance.Environmentally acceptable. Biodegradation to such an extent as to remove undesirable properties of the compound. This often corresponds to primary biodegradation but it depends on the circumstances under which the products are discharged into the environment.Ultimate. Complete breakdown of a compound to either fully oxidized or reduced simple molecules \(such as carbon dioxide/methane\, nitrate/ammonium and water\).It should be noted that the products of biodegradation can be more harmful than the substance degraded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00657
name: bioelectronics
def: "The application of biomolecular principles to microelectronics such as in biosensors and biochips."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00658
name: biological half life
def: "For a substance the time required for the amount of that substance in a biological system to be reduced to one half of its value by biological processes\, when the rate of removal is approximately exponential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00659
name: bioluminescence
def: "Luminescence produced by living systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00660
name: biomass
def: "Material produced by the growth of microorganisms\, plants or animals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00661
name: biopolymers
def: "Macromolecules \(including proteins\, nucleic acids and polysaccharides\) formed by living organisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00662
name: bioreactor
def: "An apparatus used to carry out any kind of bioprocess; examples include fermenter or enzyme reactor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00663
name: biosensor
def: "A device that uses specific biochemical reactions mediated by isolated enzymes\, immunosystems\, tissues\, organelles or whole cells to detect chemical compounds usually by electrical\, thermal or optical signals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00664
name: biosphere
def: "That part of the globe that encompasses all forms of life on the earth. It extends from the ocean depths to a few thousand metres of altitude in the atmosphere\, and includes life forms at the earth's surface\, in soils and constituents which exchange materials with atmospheres\, oceans or surfaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00665
name: biosynthesis
def: "The production of a chemical compound by a living organism \(cf. biotransformation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00666
name: biotechnology
def: "The integration of natural sciences and engineering sciences in order to achieve the application of organisms\, cells\, parts thereof and molecular analogues for products and services."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00667
name: biotransformation
def: "Any chemical conversion of substances that is mediated by living organisms or enzyme preparations derived therefrom."
synonym: "bioconversion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00668
name: biphotonic excitation
def: "Simultaneous \(coherent\) absorption of two photons \(either same or different wavelength\)\, the energy of excitation being the sum of the energies of the two photons. Also called two-photon excitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00669
name: biphotonic process
def: "Resulting from biphotonic excitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00671
name: biradical
def: "An even-electron molecular entity with two \(possibly delocalized\) radical centres which act nearly independently of each other\, e.g.  Species in which the two radical centres interact significantly are often referred to as 'biradicaloids'. If the two radical centres are located on the same atom\, the species are more properly referred to by their generic names\: carbenes\, nitrenes\, etc. The lowest-energy triplet state of a biradical lies below or at most only a little above its lowest singlet state \(usually judged relative to \\\(k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\\\)\, the product of the Boltzmann constant \\\(k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) and the absolute temperature \\\(T\\\)\). The states of those biradicals whose radical centres interact particularly weakly are most easily understood in terms of a pair of local doublets. Theoretical descriptions of low-energy states of biradicals display the presence of two unsaturated valences \(biradicals contain one fewer bond than permitted by the rules of valence\)\: the dominant valence bond structures have two dots\, the low energy molecular orbital configurations have only two electrons in two approximately nonbonding molecular orbitals\, two of the natural orbitals have occupancies close to one\, etc. Although this term has been recommended in the past for diradicals\, specialists working in the field prefer the latter term."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00673
name: bisecting conformation
def: "For a structure containing the grouping $\\ce\{R3C–C\(Y\)=X\}$ \(with identical or different groups $\\ce\{R\}$\) the conformation in which the torsion angle is such that $\\ce\{X\}$ is antiperiplanar to one of the groups $\\ce\{R\}$\, and\, in a Newman projection\, the double bond $\\ce\{C=X\}$ bisects one of the $\\ce\{R–C–R\}$ angles. In this conformation the bond $\\ce\{C–Y\}$ eclipses one of the $\\ce\{C–R\}$ bonds. The other conformation\, in which $\\ce\{X\}$ is synperiplanar to one of the groups $\\ce\{R\}$\, is called an eclipsing conformation."
synonym: "eclipsing conformation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00674
name: bismuthanes
def: "The saturated hydrides of tervalent bismuth\, having the general formula $\\ce\{Bi_\{n\}H_\{n+2\}$. Hydrocarbyl derivatives of $\\ce\{BiH3\}$ belong to the class bismuthines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00675
name: bismuthines
def: "$\\ce\{BiH3\}$ and compounds derived from it by substituting one\, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups\: $\\ce\{R3Bi\}$\, $\\ce\{RBiH2\}$\, $\\ce\{R2BiH\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{RH\}$\) are called primary\, secondary and tertiary bismuthines\, respectively. A specific bismuthine is preferably named as a substituted bismuthane\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(CH3\)3Bi\}$ trimethylbismuthane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00676
name: bisphenols
def: "By usage\, the methylenediphenols\, $\\ce\{HOC6H4CH2C6H4OH\}$\, commonly $p\,p$-methylenediphenol\, and their substitution products \(generally derived from condensation of two equivalent amounts of a phenol with an aldehyde or ketone\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00677
name: bivane
def: "A wind vane used in turbulence studies to obtain horizontal and vertical components of the wind vector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00678
name: black film
def: "A film thinner than about \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{4\}\\\) wavelength of visible light. Black films are often equilibrium film\, but equilibrium films may be considerably thicker under some conditions. In soap films\, two types of equilibrium film are often observed\: the one characterized by thickness of the order of \\\(7\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) or more which varies significantly with minor changes in composition such as ionic strength is called 'common black film'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00679
name: blank value
def: "A reading or result originating from the matrix\, reagents and any residual bias in the measurement device or process\, which contributes to the value obtained for the quantity in the analytical procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00680
name: blaze-angle
def: "The angle \(\\\(\\beta \\\)\) between the operating facet of the grooves and the overall plane of a grating."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00681
name: bleaching
def: "The loss of absorption or emission intensity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00682
name: block
def: "A portion of a macromolecule\, comprising many constitutional units that has at least one feature which is not present in the adjacent portions. Where appropriate\, definitions relating to 'macromolecule' may also be applied to 'block'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00683
name: block copolymer
def: "A copolymer that is a block polymer. In the constituent macromolecules of a block copolymer\, adjacent blocks are constitutionally different\, i.e. adjacent blocks comprise constitutional units derived from different species of monomer or from the same species of monomer but with a different composition or sequence distribution of constitutional units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00684
name: block macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule which is composed of blocks in linear sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00685
name: block polymer
def: "A polymer composed of block macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00686
name: blotting
def: "A technique used for transferring DNA\, RNA or protein from gels to a suitable binding matrix\, such as nitrocellulose or Nylon paper\, while maintaining the same physical separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00687
name: blowdown
def: "Hydrocarbons purged during refinery shutdowns and startups which should be piped to storage systems for safe venting\, flaring or recovery. This term also applies to the purging of water in boiler operation\, and serves in the control of dissolved solids in the boiler water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00688
name: blue shift
def: "Informal expression for hypsochromic shift."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00691
name: bohr
def: "Atomic unit of length\, \\\[a_\{0\}\\approx 5.291\\ 772\\ 49\(24\)\\times 10^\{-11\}\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00692
name: Bohr magneton
def: "Electromagnetic fundamental physical constant\: \\\[\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\} = \\frac\{e\\ \\mathrm\{\\hslash \}\}\{2\\ m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\} = 9.274\\hspace\{0.5em\}0154\\hspace\{0.25em\}\\left\(31\\right\)\\times 10^\{-24\}\\ \\mathrm\{J\}\\ \\mathrm\{T\}^\{-1\}\\\] where \\\(e\\\) is the elementary charge\, \\\(\\mathrm\{\\hslash\}\\\) the Planck constant divided by \\\(2\\ \\pi \\\) and \\\(m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) the electron rest mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00693
name: Bohr radius
def: "Atomic fundamental physical constant used as atomic unit of length\: \\\[a_\{0\}=\\frac\{4\\ \\pi \\ \\varepsilon_\{0\}\\ \\mathrm\{\\hslash \}^\{2\}\}\{m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\ e^\{2\}\}=5.291 \\ 772\\ 49\\ \(24\)\\times 10^\{-11\}\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\] where \\\(e\\\) is the elementary charge\, \\\(\\mathrm\{\\hslash\}\\\) is the Planck constant divided by \\\(2\\ \\pi \\\) and \\\(m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the electron rest mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00694
name: bolometer
def: "A detector constructed from a material having a large temperature coefficient of resistance. Absorption of radiation gives rise to a change in resistance. A bolometer is named according to its active component\, e.g. thermistor bolometer\, semiconductor bolometer\, superconductor bolometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00695
name: Boltzmann constant
def: "The Boltzmann constant\, \\\(k\\\)\, is one of seven fixed constants defining the International System of Units\, the SI\, with \\\(k = \\pu\{1.380649E-23 J K-1\}\\\). The Boltzmann constant is a proportionality constant between the quantities temperature \(with unit kelvin\) and energy \(with unit joule\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00696
name: bomb-digestion
def: "Materials which are not fully dissolved by acid-digestion at atmospheric pressure may require a more vigorous treatment in pressure vessels lined with polytetrafluoroethylene \(PTFE\) glass\, silica or vitreous \(glassy\) carbon or in sealed silica tubes; this treatment is called bomb-digestion. The test sample and acids are heated in such a closed vessel\, so that the digestion is carried out at higher temperature and pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00697
name: bond
def: "There is a chemical bond between two atoms or groups of atoms in the case that the forces acting between them are such as to lead to the formation of an aggregate with sufficient stability to make it convenient for the chemist to consider it as an independent 'molecular species'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00698
name: bond dissociation
def: "\(In ordinary usage the term refers to homolysis only.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00699
name: bond-dissociation energy
def: "The enthalpy \(per mole\) required to break a given bond of some specific molecular entity by homolysis\, e.g. for \, symbolized as \\\(D\\left\(\\rm\{CH\}_\{3\}\\\,-\\rm\{H\}\\right\)\\\) \(cf. heterolytic bond dissociation energy\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00700
name: bonded phase
def: "A stationary phase which is covalently bonded to the support particles or to the inside wall of the column tubing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00701
name: bond energy
def: "The average value of the gas-phase bond dissociation energies \(usually at a temperature of \\\(298\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\) for all bonds of the same type within the same chemical species. The mean bond energy for methane\, for example\, is one-fourth the enthalpy of reaction for\:  Tabulated bond energies are generally values of bond energies averaged over a number of selected typical chemical species containing that type of bond."
synonym: "mean bond energy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00702
name: bond-energy-bond-order method
def: "An empirical procedure for estimating activation energies\, involving empirical relationships between bond length\, bond dissociation energy\, and bond order."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00703
name: bonding number
def: "The bonding number \\\(n\\\) of a skeletal atom is the sum of the total number of bonding equivalents \(valence bonds\) of that skeletal atom to adjacent skeletal atoms in a parent hydride\, if any\, and the number of attached hydrogen atoms\, if any. Examples\: $\\ce\{SH2\}$\: for $\\ce\{S\}$\, \\\(n=2\\\); $\\ce\{SH6\}$\: for $\\ce\{S\}$\, \\\(n=6\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00705
name: bond number
def: "The number of electron-pair bonds between two nuclei in any given Lewis formula. For example\, in ethene the bond number between the carbon atoms is two\, and between the carbon and hydrogen atoms it is one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00707
name: bond order
def: "Theoretical index of the degree of bonding between two atoms relative to that of a single bond\, i.e. the bond provided by one localized electron pair. In molecular orbital theory it is the sum of the products of the corresponding atomic orbital coefficients \(weights\) over all the occupied molecular spin-orbitals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00709
name: boranes
def: "The molecular hydrides of boron\, e.g. $\\ce\{B5H9\}$ pentaborane\(9\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00710
name: boranylidenes
def: "The species $\\ce\{RB\{\:\}\}$ containing an electrically neutral univalent boron atom with two formally non-bonding electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00711
name: borderline mechanism
def: "A mechanism intermediate between two extremes\, for example a nucleophilic substitution intermediate between \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{\\rm\{N\}\}1\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{\\rm\{N\}\}2\\\)\, or intermediate between electron transfer and \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{\\rm\{N\}\}2\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00712
name: borenes
def: "A traditional term for boranylidenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00713
name: borinic acids
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2BOH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00714
name: boronic acids
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RB\(OH\)2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00715
name: borylenes
def: "A traditional term for boranylidenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00716
name: boson
def: "Particle of integer spin quantum number following Bose–Einstein statistics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00717
name: boundary layer
def: "That well-mixed region of the lower atmosphere in which the turbulence is maintained largely by convective buoyancy induced by the upward heat flux originating from the solar-heated surface of the earth. During the afternoon this often extends from \\\(1\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\) to \\\(5\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\) in height. The surface boundary layer is that region of the lower atmosphere where the shearing stress is constant. It is separated by the Ekman layer from the free atmosphere\, where the behaviour of the atmosphere approaches that of an ideal fluid in approximate geostrophic equilibrium \(horizontal coriolis force balances the horizontal pressure force at all points in the field\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00718
name: bound fraction
def: "The fraction of the incubation mixture which\, after separation\, contains the analyte bound to the binding reagent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00719
name: bowsprit
def: "In the boat form of cyclohexane and related structures there are two ring atoms lying out of the plane of the other four; exocyclic bonds to these two atoms pointing in a direction roughly parallel to that plane are called bowsprit\, the other two are called flagpole."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00720
name: branch
def: "An oligomeric or polymeric offshoot from a macromolecular chain."
synonym: "pendant chain" []
synonym: "side chain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00721
name: branched chain
def: "A chain with at least one branch point intermediate between the boundary units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00722
name: branched polymer
def: "A polymer the molecules of which are branched chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00723
name: branching chain reaction
def: "A chain reaction which includes a propagation step or steps in which there is an increase in the number of active intermediates; for example\,  is a branching reaction since one active intermediate \($\\ce\{H\}$\) gives rise to two \($\\ce\{OH\}$ and $\\ce\{O\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00724
name: branching decay
def: "Nuclear decay which can proceed in two or more different ways."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00725
name: branching fraction
def: "In branching decay the fraction of nuclei which decay in a specified way."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00726
name: branching index
def: "A parameter\, \\\(g\\\)\, characterizing the effect of long-chain branches on the size of a branched macromolecule in solution and defined as the ratio of the mean-square radius of gyration of a branched molecule\, \\\(< s_\{\\rm\{b\}\}^\{2\} >\\\)\, to that of an otherwise identical linear molecule\, \\\(< s _\{\\rm\{l\}\}^\{2\} >\\\)\, with the same relative molecular mass in the same solvent and at the same temperature\, i.e. \\\(g = \\frac\{< s_\{\\rm\{b\}\}^\{2\} >\}\{< s_\{\\rm\{l\}\}^\{2\} >\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00727
name: branching ratio
def: "The ratio of alternative products formed in a reaction \(e.g. the $\\ce\{HF\}$/$\\ce\{DF\}$ ratio in the reaction between $\\ce\{F\}$ and $\\ce\{HD\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00728
name: branch point
def: "Point on a polymer chain at which a branch is attached."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00729
name: branch unit
def: "A constitutional unit containing a branch point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00730
name: break
def: "The process involving the coalescence of gas bubbles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00731
name: break
def: "The flocculation of an emulsion\, viz. the formation of aggregates may be followed by coalescence. If coalescence is extensive it leads to the formation of a macrophase and the emulsion is said to break."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00732
name: Bredt's rule
def: "A double bond cannot be placed with one terminus at the bridgehead of a bridged ring system unless the rings are large enough to accommodate the double bond without excessive strain. For example\, while bicyclo\[2.2.1\]hept-1-ene is only capable of existence as a transient\, its higher homologues having a double bond at the bridgehead position have been isolated\: e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00733
name: breeching
def: "The passage or conduit through which the exhaust products of combustion are carried to the stack or chimney."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00734
name: breeze
def: "A term sometimes used to describe very fine particles of coke. Also a meteorological term for a gentle wind."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00735
name: Bremsstrahlung
def: "X-radiation emitted as a result of the retardation of high energy particles by matter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00736
name: bridge
def: "A valence bond or an atom or an unbranched chain of atoms connecting two different parts of a molecule. The two tertiary carbon atoms connected through the bridge are termed bridgeheads."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00737
name: bridged carbocation
def: "A carbocation \(real or hypothetical\) in which there are two \(or more\) carbon atoms that could in alternative Lewis formulae be designated as carbenium centres but which is instead represented by a structure in which a group \(a hydrogen atom or a hydrocarbon residue\, possibly with substituents in non-involved positions\) bridges these potential carbenium centres. One may distinguish 'electron-sufficient bridged carbocations' and 'electron-deficient bridged carbocations'. Examples of the former are phenyl-bridged ions \(for which the trivial name 'phenonium ion' has been used\)\, such as \(A\). These ions are straightforwardly classified as carbenium ions. The latter type of ion necessarily involves three-centre bonding. Structures \(C\) and \(D\) contain five-coordinate carbon atoms. The 'hydrogen-bridged carbocation' \(B\) contains a two-coordinate hydrogen atom. hypercoordination\, which includes two-coordination for hydrogen and five- but also higher coordination for carbon is generally observed in bridged carbocations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00739
name: bridge index
def: "In a coordination entity\, the number of central atoms linked by a particular bridging ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00740
name: bridge solution
def: "Solution of high concentration of inert salt\, preferably comprising cations and anions of equal mobility\, optionally interposed between the reference electrode filling and both the test and standard solution\, when the test solution and filling solution are chemically incompatible. This procedure introduces into the operational cell a second liquid junction formed usually in a similar way to the first. \(This definition refers to a bridge solution of a double junction reference electrode.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00741
name: bridging ligand
def: "A ligand attached to two or more\, usually metallic\, central atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00744
name: Brønsted acid
def: "A molecular entity capable of donating a hydron \(proton\) to a base\, \(i.e. a 'hydron donor'\) or the corresponding chemical species. For example\: $\\ce\{H2O\}$\, $\\ce\{H3O^\{+\}\}$\, $\\ce\{CH3CO2H\}$\, $\\ce\{H2SO4\}$\, $\\ce\{HSO4^\{−\}\}$\, $\\ce\{HCl\}$\, $\\ce\{CH3OH\}$\, $\\ce\{NH3\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00745
name: Brønsted base
def: "A molecular entity capable of accepting a hydron \(proton\) from an acid \(i.e. a 'hydron acceptor'\) or the corresponding chemical species. For example\: $\\ce\{OH−\}$\, $\\ce\{H2O\}$\, $\\ce\{CH3CO2^\{−\}\}$\, $\\ce\{HSO4^\{−\}\}$\, $\\ce\{SO4^\{2−\}\}$\, $\\ce\{Cl^\{−\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00746
name: Br&#xF8;nsted relation
def: "The term applies to either of the equations\: \\\[\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{HA\}\}\}\{p\} = G\\left \( \\frac\{q\\ K_\{\\rm\{HA\}\}\}\{p\} \\right \)^\{\\alpha\}\\\] \\\[\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{q\} = G\\left \( \\frac\{q\\ K_\{\\rm\{HA\}\}\}\{p\} \\right \)^\{-\\beta\} \\\] \(or their logarithmic forms\) where \\\(\\alpha\\\)\, \\\(\\beta\\\) and \\\(G\\\) are constants for a given reaction series \(\\\(\\alpha\\\) and \\\(\\beta\\\) are called 'Brønsted exponents'\)\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{HA\}\}\\\) and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) are catalytic coefficients \(or rate coefficients\) of reactions whose rates depend on the concentrations of $\\ce\{HA\}$ and/or of $\\ce\{A^\{-\}\}$. \\\(K_\{\\rm\{HA\}\}\\\) is the acid dissociation constant of the acid $\\ce\{HA\}$\, \\\(p\\\) is the number of equivalent acidic protons in the acid $\\ce\{HA\}$\, and \\\(q\\\) is the number of equivalent basic sites in its conjugate base $\\ce\{A^\{-\}\}$. The chosen values of \\\(p\\\) and \\\(q\\\) should always be specified. \(The charge designations of $\\ce\{H\}$ and $\\ce\{A\}$ are only illustrative.\) The Brønsted relation is often termed the 'Brønsted catalysis law' \(or the 'Catalysis law'\). Although justifiable on historical grounds\, this name is not recommended\, since Brønsted relations are known to apply to many uncatalysed and pseudo-catalysed reactions \(such as simple proton \(hydron\) transfer reactions\). The term 'pseudo-Brønsted relation' is sometimes used for reactions which involve nucleophilic catalysis instead of acid–base catalysis. Various types of Brønsted parameters have been proposed such as \\\(\\beta_\{\\rm\{lg\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\beta_\{\\rm\{nuc\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\beta_\{\\rm\{eq\}\}\\\) for leaving group\, nucleophile and equilibrium constants\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00747
name: Brooks and Taylor structure
def: "The structure of the anisotropic spheres which precipitate from isotropic pitch during pyrolysis. The structure of the spheres consists of a lamellar arrangement of aromatic molecules in parallel layers which are perpendicular to the polar axis of the sphere and which are perpendicular to the mesophase-isotropic phase interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00748
name: Brownian motion
def: "The movement of particles in a colloidal system such as an aerosol caused by collision with the molecules in the fluid in which the particles are imbedded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00750
name: bubble column
def: "A bioreactor\, in the shape of a column\, in which the reaction medium is kept mixed and aerated by introduction of air into the bottom \(cf. air lift bioreactor\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00751
name: bubbler
def: "An apparatus used to absorb certain water soluble components in a gas stream for later analysis. Usually it involves the use of a glass fritted tube which forces the air into small bubbles of high surface area during operation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00752
name: buffer-addition technique
def: "A technique in which an additive \(called a spectrochemical buffer\) is added to both the sample and reference solutions for the purpose of making the measure of the analyte less sensitive to variations in interferent concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00753
name: bulk concentration
def: "In any technique that involves the establishment of a concentration gradient\, either within the material from which an electrode is made or in the solution that is in contact with an electrode\, the bulk concentration of a substance B is the total or analytical concentration of B at points so remote from the electrode-solution interface that the concentration gradient for B is indistinguishable from zero at the instant under consideration. In common practice the bulk concentration of B is taken to be the total or analytical concentration of B that would be present throughout the electrode or solution if there were no current flowing through the cell and if the electrode and solution did not interact in any way. In the absence of any homogeneous reaction or other process that produces or consumes B\, the bulk concentration of B is the total or analytical concentration of B that is present before the excitation signal is applied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00754
name: bulk mesophase
def: "A continuous anisotropic phase formed by coalescence of mesophase spheres. Bulk mesophase retains fluidity and is deformable in the temperature range up to about \\\(770\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\, and transforms into green coke by further loss of hydrogen or low-molecular-weight species. This bulk mesophase can sometimes be formed directly from the isotropic pitch without observation of intermediate spheres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00755
name: bulk rheology
def: "Rheology may be conveniently divided into bulk rheology\, in which effects due to the surface of the system can be neglected\, and surface rheology\, in which such effects are predominant. It should be noted that in surface rheology the neglect of bulk behaviour is permissible only in exceptional circumstances\, such as for very thin films surrounded by a gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00756
name: bulk sample
def: "The sample resulting from the planned aggregation or combination of sample units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00758
name: Bunnett–Olsen equations
def: "The equations for the relation between \\\(\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{\\rm\{\[SH\}^\{+\}\\rm\{\]\}\}\{\[S\]\}\)+\\rm\{H\}_\{0\}\\\) and \\\(\\log _\{10\}\\rm\{\[H\}^\{+\}\\rm\{\]\}+\\rm\{H\}_\{0\}\\\) for base S in aqueous mineral acid solution\, where \\\(\\rm\{H\}_\{0\}\\\) is Hammett's acidity function and \\\(\\log _\{10\}\\rm\{\[H\}^\{+\}\\rm\{\]\}+\\rm\{H\}_\{0\}\\\) represents the activity function \\\(\\frac\{\\log _\{10\}\(\\gamma _\{S\}\\ \\gamma _\{H^\{+\}\}\)\}\{\\gamma _\{\\rm\{SH\}^\{+\}\}\}\\\) for the nitroaniline reference bases to build \\\(\\rm\{H\}_\{0\}\\\). \\\[\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{\[\\rm\{SH\}^\{+\}\]\}\{\[\\rm\{S\}\]\}\)- \\log _\{10\}\[\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\]=\(\\varPhi - 1\)\\ \(\\log _\{10\}\\rm\{\[H\}^\{+\}\\rm\{\]\}+\\rm\{H\}_\{0\}\)+pK_\{\\rm\{SH\}^\{+\}\}\\\] \\\[\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{\[\\rm\{SH\}^\{+\}\]\}\{\[\\rm\{S\}\]\}\)+H_\{0\}=\\varPhi \\ \(\\log _\{10\}\[\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\]+\\rm\{H\}_\{0\}\)+pK_\{\\rm\{SH\}^\{+\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00759
name: Bunsen burner
def: "Premix burners are distinguished as Bunsen-\, Meker-\, or slot-burners according to whether they have one large hole\, a number of small holes\, or a slot as outlet port\(s\) for the gas mixture\, respectively. When several parallel slots are present\, they are identified as multislot burners \(e.g. a three-slot burner\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00760
name: Bunte salts
def: "Salts \(usually sodium salts\) of $S$-alkylthiosulfuric acid\, of general structure $\\ce\{RSS\(=O\)2O^\{−\}M^\{+\}\}$. Use of this term is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00762
name: burning velocity
def: "The mean velocity of the flame front \(in \\\(\\rm\{mm s\}^\{-1\}\\\)\) towards the unburnt gas mixture \(usually vertically downwards\). The quantity applies to gas mixtures and not to injection burners\, and depends on the flame temperature and the solvent nebulized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00763
name: burn-up
def: "Induced nuclear transformation of atoms during reactor operation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00764
name: burn-up fraction
def: "The fraction of an initial quantity of a given nuclide that has undergone burn-up."

[Term]
id: goldbook:B00766
name: bypass injector
def: "A sample injector by means of which the eluent \(carrier gas\) may be temporarily diverted through a sample chamber so that the sample is carried to the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06756
name: bioconjugate
def: "A molecular species produced by living systems of biological origin when it is composed of two parts of different origins\, e.g. a conjugate of a xenobiotic with some groups\, e.g. glutathione\, sulfate or glucuronic acid\, to make it soluble in water or compartmentalized within the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06768
name: biomimetic
def: "Refers to a laboratory procedure designed to imitate a natural chemical process. Also refers to a compound that mimics a biological material in its structure or function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06798
name: bioisostere
def: "A compound resulting from the exchange of an atom or group of atoms with another\, broadly similar\, atom or group of atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06813
name: bioavailability
def: "Ratio of the systemic exposure from extravascular \(ev\) exposure to that following intravenous \(iv\) exposure as described by the equation\: \\\[F=\\frac\{A_\{\\rm\{ev\}\}\\ D_\{\\rm\{iv\}\}\}\{B_\{\\rm\{iv\}\}\\ D_\{\\rm\{ev\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(F\\\) is the bioavailabilty\, \\\(A\\\) and \\\(B\\\) are areas under the \(plasma\) concentration-time curve following extravascular and intravenous administration\, respectively\, and \\\(D_\{\\rm\{ev\}\}\\\) and \\\(D_\{\\rm\{iv\}\}\\\) are the administered extravascular and intravenous doses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06847
name: bioavailability
def: "Extent of absorption of a substance by a living organism compared to a standard system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06885
name: backflush
def: "Temporary reversal of the direction of the permeable flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06899
name: benchmark dose
def: "The statistical lower bound on a dose corresponding to a specified level of risk."
synonym: "lower effective dose" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06900
name: biological effect monitoring
def: "Continuous or repeated measurement of early biological effects of exposure to a substance to evaluate ambient exposure and health risk by comparison with appropriate reference values based on knowledge of the probable relationship between ambient exposure and biological effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06901
name: biological exposure index
def: "Reference values intended as guidelines for the evaluation of potential health hazards in the practice of industrial hygiene. BEIs represent the levels of determinants that are most likely to be observed in specimens collected from a healthy worker who has been exposed to chemicals to the same extent as a worker with inhalation exposure to the TLV."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06902
name: biological monitoring
def: "Continuous or repeated measurement of potentially toxic substances or their metabolites in tissues\, secreta\, excreta\, expired air\, or any combination of these to evaluate occupational or environmental exposure and health risk by comparison with appropriate reference values based on knowledge of the probable relationship between ambient exposure and resultant health effects. Sometimes also called 'biomonitoring'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06903
name: biological tolerance values for working materials
def: "The maximum permissible quantity of a chemical compound or its metabolites\, or any deviation from the norm of biological parameters induced by those substances in exposed humans."
synonym: "Biologische Arbeitsstoff Toleranz Werte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06904
name: biomarker
def: "A parameter that can be used to identify a toxic effect in an individual organism and can be used in extrapolation between species\, or as an indicator signalling an event or condition in a biological system or sample and giving a measure of exposure\, effect\, or susceptibility. The term 'biomarker' is generally used in scientific publications. In occupational hygiene and health\, 'biological monitoring' and 'biological effect monitoring' are used with the meaning 'monitoring biomarkers'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06905
name: body burden
def: "Total amount of substance of a chemical present in an organism at a given time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06997
name: back donation
def: "A description of the bonding of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}conjugated\}$ ligands to a transition metal which involves a synergic process with donation of electrons from the filled $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$ or lone electron pair orbital of the ligand into an empty orbital of the metal \(donor–acceptor bond\)\, together with release \(back donation\) of electrons from an nd orbital of the metal \(which is of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}symmetry\}$ with respect to the metal–ligand axis\) into the empty $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!^\{\\ast\}\\mbox\{-\}antibonding orbital\}$ $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}complex\}$ of the ligand."
synonym: "Chatt–Dewar–Duncanson model" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06998
name: basis function
def: "A one-electron function used in the expansion of the molecular orbital function. Basis functions are commonly represented by atomic orbitals \(see also Slater-type orbital or Gaussian-type orbital\) centered on each atom of the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT06999
name: basis set
def: "A set of basis functions employed for the representation of molecular orbitals. One may distinguish the minimal basis set \(includes one basis function for each SCF occupied atomic orbital with distinct principal and angular momentum quantum numbers\); split valence basis set \(includes two or more sizes of basis function for each valence orbital\); double zeta \(DZ\) basis set \(a split valence basis set that includes exactly twice as many functions as the minimal basis set\); extended basis set \(the set larger than the double zeta basis set\); polarized basis set \(incorporates basis functions of higher angular quantum number beyond what is required by the atom in its electronic ground state; allows orbitals to change not only a size\, but also a shape\); basis set with diffuse functions and others."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07000
name: Bent's rule
def: "In a molecule\, smaller bond angles are formed between electronegative ligands since the central atom\, to which the ligands are attached\, tends to direct bonding hybrid orbitals of greater p character towards its more electronegative substituents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07001
name: binding energy
def: "The difference between the total energy of a molecular system and the sum of the energies of its isolated $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$. The value of binding energy depends upon the geometrical arrangement of the isolated subunits \(molecules\). According to another definition\, the term to be subtracted from the total energy is the sum of the energies of the separate atoms in the corresponding valence states\, which compose the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07002
name: bond energy
def: "The energy required to break a given type of bond between atoms in certain valence states. An averaged bond energy is commonly derived by dissecting the heat of atomization of a molecule into contributions of individual bonds. For molecules with localized bonds\, the heats of atomization \(formation\) are usually well approximated by the sum of pertinent averaged bond energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07003
name: bond length
def: "The distance between atomic centers involved in a chemical bond. The notion of bond length is defined differently in various experimental methods of determination of molecular geometry; this leads to small \(usually \\\(0.01\{-\}0.02\\ \\mathring\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\) differences in bond lengths obtained by different techniques. For example\, in gas-phase electron-diffraction experiments\, the bond length is the interatomic distance averaged over all occupied vibrational states at a given temperature. In an X-ray crystal structural method\, the bond length is associated with the distance between the centroids of electron densities around the nuclei. In gas-phase microwave spectroscopy\, the bond length is an effective interatomic distance derived from measurements on a number of isotopic molecules\, etc. A number of empirical relationships between bond lengths and bond orders in polyatomic molecules were suggested\, see\, for example\, fractional bond number \(the Pauling's bond order\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07004
name: bond orbital
def: "A localized molecular orbital related to a certain $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$\, $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$\, or $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07005
name: bond order
def: "The electron population in the region between atoms A and B of a molecular entity at the expense of electron density in the immediate vicinity of the individual atomic centers. Different schemes of partitioning electron density give rise to different definitions of bond orders. In the framework of the Mulliken population analysis\, bond order is associated with the total overlap population\, \\\[\\rm\{q\}_\{\\rm\{AB\}\}=2\\ \\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\n\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\} \n\\end\{array\}\}^\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\n\nu \n\\end\{array\}\}^\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\rm\{P\}_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\} \}\\ \\rm\{S\}_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\} \}\\\] where \\\(\\rm\{P\}_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BD\}\}\\\) are respectively the elements of the density matrix and overlap matrix \(see overlap integral\). A large positive value of bond order signifies strong bonding between the atoms of the molecular entity\, whereas negative values of \\\(\\rm\{q\}_\{\\rm\{AB\}\}\\\) imply that electrons are displaced away from the inter-atomic region and point to an anti-bonding interaction. In valence bond theory\, bond order is given by a weighted average of the formal bond orders \(i.e. by the number of electron pairs in a given Lewis structure\) between the atoms in the resonance structures \(see resonance hybrid\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07006
name: bond-dissociation energy
def: "For a diatomic molecule\, the maximum vibrational energy that a molecule can have prior to its decomposition into the ground electronic states of the constituent atoms \(spectroscopic bond-dissociation energy\, \\\(D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\). The \\\(D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) value is related to the chemical dissociation energy\: \\\(D_\{\\rm\{0\}\}=D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}- \\mathrm\{E\}_\{\\rm\{vib\}\}\\left\(0\\right\)\\\)\, where \\\(\\mathrm\{E\}_\{\\rm\{vib\}\}\\left\(0\\right\)\\\) is zero-point vibrational energy. This definition is usually extended to the dissociation of polyatomic molecules into certain molecular fragments through homolytic or heterolytic bond cleavages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07007
name: bonding molecular orbital
def: "The molecular orbital whose occupation by electrons increases the total bonding \(usually\, lowers the total energy\) of a molecule. Generally\, the energy level of a bonding MO lies lower than the average of the valence orbitals of the atoms constituting the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07008
name: Born–Oppenheimer approximation
def: "Representation of the complete wavefunction as a product of an electronic and a nuclear part\, \\\[\\Psi \\left\(\\rm\{r\,R\}\\right\)=\\Psi _\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{r\,R\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{\\rm\{N\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{R\}\\right\)\\\] where the two wave-functions may be determined separately by solving two different Schroedinger equations. The validity of the Born–Oppenheimer approximation is founded on the fact that the ratio of electronic to nuclear mass \(\\\(\\frac\{m\}\{M\}\\cong 5\\times 10^\{-4\}\\\)\) is sufficiently small and the nuclei\, as compared to the rapidly moving electrons\, appear to be fixed. The approximation breaks down near a point where two electronic states acquire the same energy \(see Jahn–Teller effect\). The BO approximation is often considered as being synonymous with the adiabatic approximation. More precisely\, the latter term denotes the case when \\\(\\Psi _\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) diagonalize the electronic Hamiltonian. Thus\, the adiabatic approximation is an application of the BO approximation."
synonym: "BO approximation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07169
name: biodegradable polymer
def: "Polymer susceptible to degradation by biological activity\, with the degradation accompanied by a lowering of its molar mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07330
name: bicycle-pedal mechanism
def: "Volume-conserving mechanism proposed for the photoisomerization of conjugated double bonds. Two alternating double bonds rotate concertedly with only the two $\\ce\{CH\}$ units turning in and out of the plane of the molecule."
synonym: "BP mechanism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07331
name: bicycle rearrangement
def: "Photochemical rearrangement of unsaturated substrates by group migration over a $\\ce\{\\uppi\\! perimeter\}$ following the movement of a bicycle pedal. An example is\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07334
name: bipolarons
def: "Bound pairs of polarons mutually attracted by the lattice distortion in a solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07335
name: branching plane
def: "At a conical intersection point\, the plane spanned by the gradient difference vector \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{x_\{1\}\}\\\)\) and the gradient of the interstate coupling vector \(\\\(\\boldsymbol\{x_\{2\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\[x_\{1\} = \\frac\{\\delta\(E_\{2\}-E_\{1\}\)\}\{\\delta Q\}\\boldsymbol\{q\}\\\] \\\[x_\{2\} = \\boldsymbol\{q\}\\\] where \\\(\\boldsymbol\{C_\{1\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{C_\{2\}\}\\\) are the configuration interaction eigenvectors \(i.e.\, the excited and ground-state adiabatic wavefunctions\) in a conical intersection problem\, \\\(H\\\) is the conical intersection Hamiltonian\, \\\(\\textbf\{Q\}\\\) represents the nuclear configuration vector of the system\, and thus \\\(\\textbf\{q\}\\\) is a unit vector in the direction of vector \\\(\\textbf\{q\}\\\). \\\(E_\{1\}\\\) and \\\(E_\{2\}\\\) are the energies of the lower and upper states\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07336
name: Brewster angle
def: "When an unpolarized planar electromagnetic wavefront impinges on a flat dielectric surface\, there is a unique angle \(\\\(\\theta_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\)\, commonly referred to as Brewster angle\, at which the reflected waves are all polarized into a single plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07337
name: brightness
def: "Obsolete term. This term is reserved for non-quantitative reference to physiological perception of light and is not recommended as a quantitative measure of the radiance of an emitting device\, e.g.\, a lamp."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07338
name: brightness
def: "Product of the fluorescence quantum yield \(\\\(\\varPhi _\{\\rm\{f\}\}\\\)\) of a dye and the molar decadic absorption coefficient at the excitation wavelength\, \\\(\\varepsilon\\left\(\\lambda \\right\)\\\)\, i.e.\, \\\(\\varPhi _\{\\rm\{f\}\}\\ \\varepsilon\\left\(\\lambda \\right\)\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:BT07564
name: bimodal network
def: "Polymer network comprising polymer chains having two significantly different molar-mass distributions between adjacent junction points."
synonym: "bimodal polymer network" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00767
name: χ-parameter
def: "A numerical parameter\, $\\chi$\, employed in the Flory–Huggins theory\, which accounts in the main for the contribution of the non-combinatorial entropy of mixing and for the enthalpy of mixing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00769
name: cage
def: "An aggregate of molecules\, generally in the condensed phase\, that surrounds the fragments formed\, for example\, by thermal or photochemical dissociation. Because the cage hinders the separation of the fragments by diffusion\, they may preferentially react with one another \('cage effect'\) but not necessarily to reform the precursor species. For example\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00770
name: cage compound
def: "A polycyclic compound having the shape of a cage. The term is also used for inclusion compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00771
name: cage effect
def: "When in a condensed phase\, or in a dense gas\, reactant molecules come together\, or species are formed in proximity to one another\, and are caged in by surrounding molecules\, they may undergo a set of collisions known as an encounter; the term 'cage effect' is then applied.The cage effect is also known as the Franck–Rabinowitch effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00773
name: calcination
def: "Heating to high temperatures in air or oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00774
name: calcined coke
def: "A petroleum coke or coal-derived pitch coke obtained by heat treatment of green coke to about \\\(1600\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\). It will normally have a hydrogen content of less than \\\(0.1\\ \\rm\{wt.\}\\%\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00775
name: calibration
def: "The set of operations which establish\, under specified conditions\, the relationship between values indicated by the analytical instrument and the corresponding known values of an analyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00776
name: calibration component
def: "A component of a calibration mixture\, present in the gaseous or vapour state\, quantitatively and qualitatively defined\, and used directly for testing and for calibration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00778
name: calibration function
def: "The functional \(not statistical\) relationship for the chemical measurement process\, relating the expected value of the observed \(gross\) signal or response variable \\\(E\(y\)\\\) to the analyte amount \\\(x\\\). The corresponding graphical display for a single analyte is referred to as the calibration curve. When extended to additional variables or analytes which occur in multicomponent analysis\, the 'curve' becomes a calibration surface or hypersurface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00779
name: calibration gas mixture
def: "A gas mixture of known composition\, generally comprising one or more calibration components and a complementary gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00780
name: calibration material
def: "A material of known composition or properties which can be presented to the analytical instrument for calibration purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00781
name: calibration mixture
def: "A gaseous or liquid mixture of known composition\, generally comprising one or more calibration components and an inert diluent\, used directly for testing and calibration of analytical instruments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00782
name: calibration sample
def: "The test portion or test solution used for calibration of an analytical procedure. The calibration sample is normally of known weight or volume and is prepared according to specifications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00783
name: calixarenes
def: "Originally macrocyclic compounds capable of assuming a basket \(or 'calix'\) shaped conformation. They are formed from p-hydrocarbyl phenols and formaldehyde. The term now applies to a variety of derivatives by substitution of the hydrocarbon cyclo\{oligo\[\(1\,3-phenylene\)methylene\]\}."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00784
name: calorie
def: "Non-SI unit of energy. There are three differently defined calories\: thermochemical calorie \(\\\(\\rm\{cal\}_\{\\rm\{th\}\} = 4.184\\ \\rm\{J\}\\\)\)\, international steam table calorie \(\\\(\\rm\{cal\}_\{\\rm\{IT\}\} = 4.1868\\ \\rm\{J\}\\\)\)\, \\\(15\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\) calorie \(\\\(\\rm\{cal\}_\{15\} = 4.1855\\ \\rm\{J\}\\\)\)\, however only the symbol \\\(\\rm\{cal\}\\\) is usually used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00785
name: calorimetric titration
def: "A titration performed in a calorimeter which produces a plot of heat change versus volume of titrant. This is the preferred term for experiments in which the main goal is the measurement of thermodynamic parameters. Because such experiments may be performed in any of a variety of types of calorimeters with varying degrees of calorimetric accuracy\, it is important that the reporting of such results include an assessment of accuracy and precision. The term stands in contrast to the main emphasis in enthalpimetric analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00786
name: calorimetry
def: "A general term describing any experiment in which heat is measured as some chemical reaction or physical process occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00787
name: candela
def: "The candela\, symbol \\\(\\pu\{cd\}\\\)\, is the SI unit of luminous intensity in a given direction. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the luminous efficacy of monochromatic radiation of frequency \\\(\\pu\{540E12 Hz\}\\\)\, \\\(K_\{\\rm\{cd\}\}\\\)\, to be \\\(683\\\) when expressed in the unit \\\(\\pu\{lm W^\{-1\}\}\\\)\, which is equal to \\\(\\pu\{cd sr W^\{-1\}\}\\\)\, or \\\(\\pu\{cd sr kg-1 m-2 s3\}\\\)\, where the kilogram\, metre and second are defined in terms of h\, c and ΔνCs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00789
name: canonical rate constant
def: "The rate constant for a system in which the reactants are in thermal equilibrium at a given temperature. In statistical mechanics the expression 'canonical ensemble' is used to refer to a closed system in thermal equilibrium\, the species being present in a statistical distribution. By contrast\, a microcanonical ensemble is composed of systems all having the same energy\: a canonical ensemble therefore consists of a statistical distribution of microcanonical ensembles. The canonical or thermal rate constant can be obtained from the microcanonical rate constant by summing over the energy\, taking into account the statistical distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00790
name: canonical variational transition-state theory
def: "A development of transition-state theory in which the position of the dividing surface is varied so as to minimize the rate constant at a given temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00791
name: capacitance
def: "The charge on a plate divided by the potential difference between the plates."
synonym: "electric capacitance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00792
name: capacitance hygrometer
def: "Hygrometer using the capacitance variations of a capacitor whose dielectric medium consists of the gas or of a material in contact with this gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00793
name: capillary column
def: "A general term for columns having a small diameter. A capillary column may contain a packing or have the stationary phase supported on its inside wall. The former case corresponds to packed capillary column while the latter case corresponds to an open-tabular column. Due to the ambiguity of this term its use without an adjective is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00794
name: capillary condensation
def: "Capillary condensation is said to occur when\, in porous solids\, multilayer adsorption from a vapour proceeds to the point at which pore spaces are filled with liquid separated from the gas phase by menisci. The concept loses its sense when the dimensions of the pores are so small that the term meniscus ceases to have a physical significance. Capillary condensation is often accompanied by hysteresis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00795
name: captodative effect
def: "Effect on the stability of a carbon-centred radical determined by the combined action of a captor \(electron withdrawing\) and a dative \(electron releasing\) substituent\, both attached to the radical centre. The term is also used for certain unsaturated compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00796
name: capture
def: "A process in which an atomic or nuclear system acquires an additional particle. In general\, a specification is added of the type of the captured particle or its energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00798
name: capture cross-section
def: "The cross-section for capture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00800
name: capture γ-radiation
def: "The γ-radiation emitted in radiative capture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00802
name: carbaboranes
def: "A class of boron compound of general formula $\\ce\{\[\(CH\)_\{a\}\(BH\)_\{m\}H_\{b\}\]^\{c\}\}$ where $c$ can be positive\, negative or zero. The $\\ce\{CH\}$ groups occupy polyhedron vertices\, and other hydrogen atoms are either bridging or terminal."
synonym: "carboranes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00803
name: carbamates
def: "Salts or esters of carbamic acid\, $\\ce\{H2NC\(=O\)OH\}$\, or of $N$-substituted carbamic acids $\\ce\{R2NC\(=O\)OR'\}$\, \($\\ce\{R'\}$ = hydrocarbyl or a cation\). The esters are often called urethanes or urethans\, a usage that is strictly correct only for the ethyl esters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00804
name: carbanion
def: "Generic name for anions containing an even number of electrons and having an unshared pair of electrons on a tervalent carbon atom \(e.g. $\\ce\{Cl3C^\{−\}\}$ or $\\ce\{HC#C^\{−\}\}$\) or — if the ion is mesomeric \(see mesomerism\) — having at least one significant contributing structure with an unshared pair of electrons on a tervalent carbon atom\, for example\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00806
name: carbenes
def: "The electrically neutral species $\\ce\{H2C\{\:\}\}$ \(methylene\) and its derivatives\, in which the carbon is covalently bonded to two univalent groups of any kind or a divalent group and bears two nonbonding electrons\, which may be spin-paired \(singlet state\) or spin-non-paired \(triplet state\). In systematic name formation\, carbene is the name of the parent hydride $\\ce\{C\{\:\}H2\}$ hence\, the name dichlorocarbene for $\\ce\{C\{\:\}Cl2\}$. However\, names for acyclic and cyclic hydrocarbons containing one or more divalent carbon atoms are derived from the name of the corresponding all-\\\(\\lambda ^\{4\}\\\)-hydrocarbon using the suffix -ylidene. E.g. prop-2-en-1-ylidene\, $\\ce\{H2C=CHCH\{\:\}\}$ ethenylidene\, $\\ce\{H2C=C\{\:\}\}$; cyclohexylidene\,Subclasses of carbenes include acyl carbenes \, imidoyl carbenes\,  and vinyl carbenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00807
name: carbene analogues
def: "The electrically neutral mononuclear hydrides of group 14 having two non-bonding electrons\, the electrically neutral mononuclear hydrides of group 15 having four non-bonding electrons\, and also the compound $\\ce\{HB\{\:\}\}$ . The names of a number of these are shown below.\n\n$\\ce\{RB\{\:\}\}$boranylidenes$\\ce\{RAs\{\:\}\}$arsanylidenes\n$\\ce\{RN\{\:\}\}$nitrenes$\\ce\{R2Sn\{\:\}\}$stannylidenes\n$\\ce\{R2Si\{\:\}\}$silylenes$\\ce\{RSb\{\:\}\}$stibanylidenes\n$\\ce\{RP\{\:\}\}$phosphanylidenes$\\ce\{R2Pb\{\:\}\}$plumbylidenes\n$\\ce\{R2Ge\{\:\}\}$germylidenes\n\nNote that if $\\ce\{R\} = \\ce\{H\}$\, these compounds are parent hydrides; derivatives formed by substitution are named accordingly. However\, if the substituent's first atom\, bearing the free valence\, is of the same element as the atom of the carbene analogue\, other parent compounds may be required \(see carbenes\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{HN\{\:\}\}$ nitrene; $\\ce\{CH3N\{\:\}\}$ methylnitrene; $\\ce\{H2NN\{\:\}\}$ diazanylidene \(synonym isodiazene\, not aminonitrene\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00809
name: carbene radical anions
def: "Species $\\ce\{R2C^\{_\{\\vdots\}−\}\}$\, having three non-bonding electrons\, formally derived by addition of an electron to a carbene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00810
name: carbene radical cations
def: "Species $\\ce\{R2C^\{.+\}\}$ having one non-bonding electron\, formally derived by subtraction of an electron from a carbene\, e.g. $\\ce\{H2C^\{.+\}\}$ methyliumyl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00811
name: carbenium centre
def: "The three-coordinate carbon atom in a carbenium ion to which the excess positive charge of the ion \(other than that located on heteroatoms\) may be formally considered to be largely attributed\, i.e. which has one vacant p-orbital. \(N.B. It is not always possible to uniquely identify such an atom.\) This formal attribution of charge often does not express the real charge distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00812
name: carbenium ion
def: "A generic name for carbocations\, real or hypothetical\, that have at least one important contributing structure containing a tervalent carbon atom with a vacant p-orbital. \(The name implies a protonated carbene or a substitution derivative thereof.\) The term was proposed \(and rejected\) as a replacement for the traditional usage of the name carbonium ion. To avoid ambiguity\, the name should not be used as the root for the systematic nomenclature of carbocations. The corresponding difficulty confused carbonium ion nomenclature for many years. For example\, the term 'ethylcarbonium ion' has at times been used to refer either to $\\ce\{CH3CH2^\{+\}\}$ \(ethyl cation\) or \(correctly\) to $\\ce\{CH3CH2CH2^\{+\}\}$ \(propyl cation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00813
name: carbenoids
def: "Complexed carbene-like entities that display the reactivity characteristics of carbenes\, either directly or by acting as sources of carbenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00817
name: carbocation
def: "A cation containing an even number of electrons with a significant portion of the excess positive charge located on one or more carbon atoms. This is a general term embracing carbenium ions\, all types of carbonium ions vinyl cations\, etc. Carbocations may be named by adding the word 'cation' to the name of the corresponding radical. Such names do not imply structure \(e.g. whether three-coordinated or five-coordinated carbon atoms are present\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00818
name: carbocyclic compounds
def: "Cyclic compounds in which all of the ring members are carbon atoms\, e.g. 1\,2-dihydronaphthalene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00819
name: carbodiimides
def: "Carbodiimide\, $\\ce\{HN=C=NH\}$\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00820
name: carbohydrates
def: "Originally\, compounds such as aldoses and ketoses having the stoichiometric formula $\\ce\{C_\{n\}\(H2O\)_\{n\}\}$ hence 'hydrates of carbon'. The generic term carbohydrate includes monosaccharides\, oligosaccharides and polysaccharides as well as substances derived from monosaccharides by reduction of the carbonyl group \(alditols\)\, by oxidation of one or more terminal groups to carboxylic acids\, or by replacement of one or more hydroxy group\(s\) by a hydrogen atom\, an amino group\, thiol group or similar groups. It also includes derivatives of these compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00821
name: carbon
def: "Element number 6 of the periodic table of elements \(electronic ground state \\\(1\\rm\{s\}^\{2\}2\\rm\{s\}^\{2\}2\\rm\{p\}^\{2\}\\\)\). For a description of the various types of carbon as a solid the term carbon should be used only in combination with an additional noun or a clarifying adjective."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00822
name: carbonaceous mesophase
def: "A liquid-crystalline state of pitch which shows the optical birefringence of disc-like \(discotic\) nematic liquid crystals. It can be formed as an intermediate phase during thermolysis \(pyrolysis\) of an isotropic molten pitch or by precipitation from pitch fractions prepared by selective extraction. Generally\, the spherical mesophase precipitated from a pyrolysing pitch has the Brooks and Taylor structure. With continuous heat treatment the carbonaceous mesophase coalesces to a state of bulk mesophase before solidification to green coke with further loss of hydrogen or low-molecular-weight compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00823
name: carbon artifact
def: "An 'artificially produced' solid body which consists mainly of carbonaceous material in a distinct shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00825
name: carbon–carbon composite
def: "A carbon fibre-reinforced carbon matrix material. The carbon matrix phase is typically formed by solid\, liquid or gaseous pyrolysis of an organic precursor material. The matrix is either a graphitizable carbon or non-graphitizable carbon\, and the carbonaceous reinforcement is fibrous in form. The composite may also contain other components in particulate or fibrous forms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00826
name: carbon cenospheres
def: "Porous or hollow carbonaceous sphere-like particles \(frequently in the size range of a few to several hundreds of µm diameter\) formed during pyrolysis\, also in the course of combustion\, of carbonaceous liquid droplets \(e.g. heavy fuel\) or solid particles \(e.g. coal\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00827
name: carbon cloth
def: "A textile material consisting of carbon fibres oriented at least in two directions. Carbon cloth is not necessarily woven."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00828
name: carbon dioxide laser
def: "A continuous or pulsed source of coherent radiation normally tunable through the $\\ce\{CO2\}$ vibration–rotation band centred near \\\(10.6\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)."
synonym: "CO<sub>2</sub> laser" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00829
name: carbon electrode
def: "An electrode for an electrical application. In its green state it comprises granular carbon material bound with pitch. The granular carbon material may be either needle coke\, fine-grained or isotropic coke or reclaimed graphite powder. Electrodes for use in steel production can only be manufactured from needle coke\, and the green electrodes are fired at temperatures above around \\\(2800\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) to produce highly graphite electrodes. The other granular carbon materials may be used for aluminium electrodes where the duty is not so severe\, and the green electrodes are generally fired to lower temperatures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00830
name: carbon felt
def: "A textile material consisting of\, in approximation\, randomly oriented and intertwined carbon fibres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00831
name: carbon fibre
def: "Fibres \(filaments\, tows\, yarns\, rovings\) consisting of at least $\\pu\{92\\%\}$ \(mass fraction\) carbon\, usually in the non-graphitic state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00832
name: carbon fibre fabrics
def: "Woven textile materials made of carbon fibres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00833
name: carbon fibres type HM
def: "Carbon fibres type HM \(high modulus\) are carbon fibres with a value of Young's modulus \(tensile modulus\) larger than \\\(300\\ \\rm\{GPa\}\\\) \(nearly $\\pu\{30\\%\}$ of the \\\(C_\{11\}\\\) elastic constant of a graphite single crystal\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00834
name: carbon fibres type HT
def: "Carbon fibres type HT are carbon fibres with values of Young's modulus between \\\(150\\\) and \\\(275\\\) to \\\(300\\ \\rm\{GPa\}\\\). The term HT\, referring to high tensile strength\, was early applied because fibres of this type display the highest tensile strengths."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00835
name: carbon fibres type IM
def: "Carbon fibres type IM \(intermediate modulus\) are related to carbon fibres type HT because of the comparable values of tensile strength\, but are characterized by greater stiffness \(Young's modulus up to approximately $\\pu\{35\\%\}$ of the theoretical \\\(C_\{11\}\\\) value\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00836
name: carbon fibres type LM
def: "Carbon fibres type LM \(low modulus\) are carbon fibres with isotropic structure\, tensile modulus values as low as $\\pu\{10\\%\}$ of the \\\(C_\{11\}\\\) values of the graphite single crystal\, and low strength values."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00837
name: carbon fibres type UHM
def: "Carbon fibres type UHM \(ultra-high modulus\) designates a class of carbon fibres having very high values of Young's modulus larger than \\\(600\\ \\rm\{GPa\}\\\) \(i.e. greater than $\\pu\{55\\%\}$ of the theoretical \\\(C_\{11\}\\\) value of graphite\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00838
name: carbonitriles
def: "In systematic nomenclature\, the suffix -carbonitrile is used to name compounds $\\ce\{RC#N\}$ where the suffix includes the carbon atom of the $\\ce\{–CN\}$. However\, carbonitrile is not a class name for nitriles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00839
name: carbonium ion
def: "The term should be used with great care since several incompatible meanings are currently in use. It is not acceptable as the root for systematic nomenclature for carbocation.In most of the existing literature the term is used in its traditional sense for what is here defined as carbenium ion.A carbocation\, real or hypothetical\, that contains at least one five-coordinate carbon atom.A carbocation\, real or hypothetical\, whose structure cannot adequately be described by two-electron two-centre bonds only. \(The structure may involve carbon atoms with a coordination number greater than five.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00840
name: carbonization
def: "A process by which solid residues with increasing content of the element carbon are formed from organic material usually by pyrolysis in an inert atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00841
name: carbon material
def: "A solid high in content of the element carbon and structurally in a non-graphitic state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00842
name: carbon mix
def: "A mixture of filler coke\, e.g. grains and/or powders of solid carbon materials and a carbonaceous binder and selected additives\, prepared in heated mixers at temperatures in the range of \\\(410\{-\}445\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) as a preliminary step for the formation of shaped green bodies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00844
name: carbonyl compounds
def: "Compounds containing the carbonyl group\, $\\ce\{C=O\}$. The term is commonly used in the restricted sense of aldehydes and ketones\, although it actually includes carboxylic acids and derivatives.Metal carbonyls\, in which carbon monoxide is a formal ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00845
name: carbonyl imides
def: "1\,3-dipolar compounds having the structure\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00847
name: carbonyl oxides
def: "1\,3 dipolar compounds having the structure  Also called peroxo compounds."
synonym: "peroxo compounds" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00848
name: carbonyl ylides
def: "1\,3-Dipolar compounds having the structure"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00850
name: carboxamides
def: "Amides of carboxylic acids\, having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(=O\)NR2\}$. The term is used as a suffix in systematic name formation to denote the $\\ce\{–C\(=O\)NH2\}$ group including its carbon atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00851
name: carboxamidines
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(=NR\)NR2\}$. The term is used as a suffix in systematic nomenclature to denote the $\\ce\{–C\(=NH\)NH2\}$ group including its carbon atom\, e.g. acetamidine\, $\\ce\{CH3C\(=NH\)NH2\}$; 2-butyl-4\,5-dihydroimidazole\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00852
name: carboxylic acids
def: "Oxoacids having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(=O\)OH\}$. The term is used as a suffix in systematic name formation to denote the $\\ce\{–C\(=O\)OH\}$ group including its carbon atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00854
name: carbynes
def: "The neutral species $\\ce\{HC_\{^\\vdots\}\}$ \(methylidyne\) and its derivatives formed by substitution in which a univalent carbon atom is covalently bonded to one group and also bears three nonbonding electrons. \(This term carries no implication about spin-pairing\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00856
name: carbynium ions
def: "The cationic species $\\ce\{H2C^\{.+\}\}$ or substitution derivatives thereof\, formally derived by adding a hydron to a carbyne or subtracting an electron from a carbene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00857
name: carotenes
def: "Hydrocarbon carotenoids \(a subclass of tetraterpenes\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00858
name: carotenoids
def: "Tetraterpenoids \($\\ce\{C40\}$\)\, formally derived from the acyclic parent\, $\\ce\{\\uppsi\\!\,\\!\\uppsi\\!-carotene\}$ I by hydrogenation\, dehydrogenation\, cyclization\, oxidation\, or combination of these processes. This class includes carotenes\, xanthophylls and certain compounds that arise from rearrangement of the skeleton of I or by loss of part of this structure. retinoids are excluded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00859
name: carrier
def: "A substance in appreciable amount which\, when associated with a trace of a specified substance\, will carry the trace with it through a chemical or physical process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00860
name: carrier
def: "A substance in appreciable amount which\, when associated with a tracer of a specified substance\, will carry the tracer with it through a chemical or physical process\, or prevent the tracer from undergoing nonspecific processes due to its low concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00861
name: carrier atom
def: "A participant atom which is neither core nor peripheral and which carries other groups to and from the molecule containing the core atom\(s\) but which does not itself become covalently attached to that molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00863
name: carrier gas
def: "A gas introduced in order to transport a sample for analytical purposes. In gas chromatography it is the gas which is passed continuously through the column and whose passage promotes the elution of the components of the sample. The carrier gas together with the portions of the sample present in this phase constitutes the mobile phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00866
name: carry-over
def: "A process by which materials are carried into a reaction mixture to which they do not belong. These materials can be either parts of a specimen\, or reagents including the diluent or wash solution. In such cases\, carry-over means the transfer of material \(specimen or reagents\) from one container\, or from one reaction mixture\, to another one. It can be either unidirectional or bidirectional in a series of specimens or assays. The term carry-over effect is used for carry-over from specimen to specimen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00869
name: cascade impactor
def: "An instrument used for the classification of aerosols according to size and for possible subsequent chemical analysis. Air is drawn through a series of orifices of decreasing size; the air flow is normal to collecting surfaces on which aerosols are collected by inertial impaction. The particles\, separated stepwise by their momentum differences into a number of size ranges\, are collected simultaneously."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00870
name: catabolism
def: "Reactions involving the oxidation of organic substrates to provide chemically available energy \(e.g. ATP\) and to generate metabolic intermediates.Generally\, the process of breakdown of complex molecules into simpler ones\, often providing biologically available energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00871
name: catabolite repression
def: "A decrease in the activity of certain auxiliary catabolic enzymes when a surplus of an \(easily metabolizable\) substrate is available. Commonly this effect is caused by glucose \(glucose repression\) or by metabolites produced from glucose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00872
name: catalymetric titration
def: "A titration process\, which involves a catalyst\, where the end-point is detected by the sudden increase or decrease of the rate of a reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00873
name: catalysed rate of reaction
def: "The observed rate of reaction\, \\\(\\xi \\\)\, minus the spontaneous rate of reaction\, \\\(\\xi _\{0\}\\\)\, observed under otherwise similar conditions in the absence of added catalytic material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00874
name: catalysis
def: "The action of a catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00876
name: catalyst
def: "A substance that increases the rate of a reaction without modifying the overall standard Gibbs energy change in the reaction; the process is called catalysis. The catalyst is both a reactant and product of the reaction. The words catalyst and catalysis should not be used when the added substance reduces the rate of reaction \(see inhibitor\). Catalysis can be classified as 'homogeneous catalysis'\, in which only one phase is involved\, and 'heterogeneous catalysis'\, in which the reaction occurs at or near an interface between phases. Catalysis brought about by one of the products of a reaction is called autocatalysis. Catalysis brought about by a group on a reactant molecule itself is called intramolecular catalysis. The term catalysis is also often used when the substance is consumed in the reaction \(for example\: base-catalysed hydrolysis of esters\). Strictly\, such a substance should be called an activator."
synonym: "accelerator" []
synonym: "kinetic synergist" []
synonym: "surface catalysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00877
name: catalyst
def: "A substance included in the solvent to increase the rate of transfer without affecting the position of equilibrium. The term accelerator may also be used but kinetic synergist is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00878
name: catalyst ageing
def: "Catalyst deactivation resulting from changes in the structure or in the texture of the catalyst. Changes of this kind are usually irreversible and the catalyst cannot be regenerated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00880
name: catalyst decay
def: "The conversion in a catalytic reaction performed under constant conditions of reaction often decreases with time of run or time on stream. This phenomenon is called catalyst deactivation or catalyst decay."
synonym: "catalyst deactivation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00881
name: catalytic activity
def: "Increase in the rate of reaction of a specified chemical reaction that an enzyme produces in a specific assay system."
synonym: "enzyme activity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00882
name: catalytic activity concentration
def: "Catalytic activity of the component divided by the volume of the system."
synonym: "catalytic concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00883
name: catalytic activity content
def: "Catalytic activity of the component divided by the mass of the system."
synonym: "catalytic content" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00884
name: catalytic activity fraction
def: "Quotient of the catalytic activity of the isozyme and the catalytic activity of all the isozymes of the same enzyme in the system."
synonym: "catalytic fraction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00885
name: catalytic coefficient
def: "If the rate of reaction\, \\\(\nu\\\)\, is expressible in the form\: \\\[\nu = \(k_\{0\}+\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}k_\{i\}\\ \[C_\{i\}\]^\{n_\{i\}\}\)\\ \[A\]^\{\\alpha \}\\ \[B\]^\{\\beta \}\\ ...\\\] where $\\ce\{A\}$\, $\\ce\{B\}$\, ... are reactants and \\\(C_\{i\}\\\) represents one of a set of catalysts\, then the proportionality factor \\\(k_\{i\}\\\) is the catalytic coefficient of the particular catalyst \\\(C_\{i\}\\\). Normally the partial order of reaction \\\(n_\{i\}\\\) with respect to a catalyst is unity\, so that \\\(k_\{i\}\\\) is an \\\(\\left \( \\mathit\{\\alpha \} + \\mathit\{\\beta \} + ... + 1 \\right \)\\\)th order rate coefficient. The proportionality factor \\\(k_\{0\}\\\) is the \\\(\\left \( \\mathit\{\\alpha \} + \\mathit\{\\beta \} + ...\\right \)\\\)th order rate coefficient of the uncatalysed component of the total reaction. For example\, if there is catalysis by hydrogen and hydroxide ions\, and the order of reaction can be expressed in the form\: \\\[k = k_\{0\}+k_\{\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\}\\ \[\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\]+k_\{\\rm\{OH\}^\{-\}\}\\ \[\\rm\{OH\}^\{-\}\]\\\] then \\\(k_\{H^\{+\}\}\\\) and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{OH\}^\{-\}\}\\\) are the catalytic coefficients for $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}$\, respectively. The constant \\\(k_\{0\}\\\) relates to the uncatalysed reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00889
name: catalytic current
def: "The faradaic current that is obtained with a solution containing two substances B and A may exceed the sum of the faradaic currents that would be obtained with B and A separately\, but at the same concentrations and under the same experimental conditions. In either of the two following situations the increase is termed a catalytic current. B is reduced or oxidized at the electrode-solution interface to give a product B' that then reduces or oxidizes A chemically. The reaction of B with A may yield either B or an intermediate in the overall half-reaction by which B' was obtained from B. In this situation the increase of current that results from the addition of A to a solution of B may be termed a regeneration current. The presence at the electrode-solution interface of one substance\, which may be either A or the product A' of its reduction or oxidation\, decreases the over-potential for the reduction or oxidation of B. In either case the magnitude of the catalytic current depends on the applied potential. If the current observed with a mixture of A and B is smaller than the sum of the separate currents\, the term non-additive current should be used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00891
name: catalytic dehydrocyclization
def: "A reaction in which an alkane is converted into an aromatic hydrocarbon and hydrogen. For example\,"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00892
name: catalytic domain of a polypeptide
def: "Any part of a polypeptide chain that possesses a catalytic function. It may contain more than one structural domain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00894
name: catalytic graphitization
def: "The transformation of non-graphitic carbon into graphite by heat treatment in the presence of certain metals or minerals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00895
name: catalytic hydrocracking
def: "A process similar to catalytic cracking in its industrial purpose but effected under hydrogen pressure and on a catalyst containing an ingredient with a hydrogenating function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00896
name: catalytic hydrodesulfurization
def: "A process in which\, in the presence of hydrogen\, sulfur is removed as hydrogen sulfide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00897
name: catalytic hydrogenolysis
def: "Ordinarily\, a reaction in which $\\ce\{A + H2\}$ gives $\\ce\{B\}$.   For example\,    The term may also be used for cleavage of bonds other than $\\ce\{A\}$\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00898
name: catalytic methanation
def: "A process for removing carbon monoxide from gas streams or for producing methane by the reaction"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00899
name: catalytic reforming
def: "A process for increasing the octane number of naphthas. It involves isomerization of alkanes\, dehydrogenation of cyclohexanes to aromatic hydrocarbons\, isomerization and dehydrogenation of alkylcyclopentanes\, and dehydrocyclization of alkanes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00902
name: catecholamines
def: "4-\(2-Aminoethyl\)pyrocatechol \[4-\(2-aminoethyl\)benzene-1\,2-diol\] and derivatives formed by substitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00903
name: <i>catena</i>-
def: "An affix used in inorganic nomenclature to denote a chain structure; often used to designate linear polymeric substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00904
name: catenanes
def: "Hydrocarbons having two or more rings connected in the manner of links of a chain\, without a covalent bond. More generally\, the class catena compounds embraces functional derivatives and hetero analogues\, e.g\:"
synonym: "catena compounds" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00905
name: cathode
def: "Electrode at which reduction takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00906
name: cathodic transfer coefficient
def: "For a reaction with a single rate-determining step \\\[\\frac\{\\alpha _\{\\rm\{c\}\}\}\{\nu \} = -\\frac\{R\\ T\}\{n\\ F\}\\ \(\\frac\{\\partial \(\\ln \(|I_\{\\rm\{c\}\}|\)\)\}\{\\partial E\}\)_\{T\,p\,c_\{i\}\,...\}\\\] where \\\(\\alpha _\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) is the cathodic transfer coefficient \(number\)\, \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, and \\\(\nu \\\) the stoichiometric number giving the number of identical activated complexes formed and destroyed in the completion of the overall reaction as formulated with the transfer of \\\(n\\\) electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00907
name: cation
def: "A monoatomic or polyatomic species having one or more elementary charges of the proton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00908
name: cation exchange
def: "The process of exchanging cations between a solution and a cation exchanger."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00909
name: cation exchanger
def: "Ion exchanger with cations as counter-ions. The term cation-exchange resin may be used in the case of solid organic polymers. The acid form of a cation exchanger is the ionic form of a cation exchanger in which counter-ions are hydrogen ions \(H-form\) or the ionogenic groups have added a proton forming an undissociated acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00910
name: cationic polymerization
def: "An ionic polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are cations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00914
name: cavitands
def: "Compounds constrained by structure to having or accommodating a cavity large enough to host other molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00915
name: cavity dumping
def: "Periodic removal of coherent radiation from a laser cavity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00917
name: ceilometer
def: "An automatic\, recording instrument for reading the height of the cloud-base."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00918
name: cell constant
def: "Defined as\: \\\[\\kappa _\{\\rm\{cell\}\} = \\kappa \\ R\\\] where \\\(R\\\) is the measured resistance of the cell and \\\(\\kappa \\\) is the conductivity \(formerly called the specific conductance\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00922
name: cell error
def: "The error which results if the incident beam does not fall perpendicularly to the windows of the cell\, or if the cell windows are contaminated or have some other imperfection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00928
name: Celsius temperature
def: "Thermodynamic temperature minus \\\(273.15\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\, invariably expressed in the SI unit degree Celsius \(\\\(\\pu\{^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)\) which is equal to the kelvin. Sometimes the misnomer centigrade temperature is used for Celsius temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00929
name: centi
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-2\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\rm\{c\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00930
name: central atom
def: "The atom in a coordination entity which binds other atoms or groups of atoms \(ligands\) to itself\, thereby occupying a central position in the coordination entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00931
name: centre
def: "The centre of a Mössbauer spectrum is defined as the Doppler-velocity at which the resonance maximum is \(or would be\) observed when all magnetic dipole\, electric quadrupole\, etc. hyperfine interactions are \(or would be\) absent. The contribution of the second order Doppler shift \(\\\(\\delta _\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\)\) should be indicated\, if possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00933
name: centrifugal acceleration
def: "The acceleration of a component as a result of a uniform rotational motion. Centrifugal acceleration is a vector quantity."
synonym: "centripetal acceleration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00934
name: centrifugal barrier
def: "In a reaction without an electronic energy barrier\, or its reverse\, the rotational energy of the transition state gives rise to a reaction barrier\, which is known as the centrifugal barrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00936
name: centrifugal force
def: "Fictitious force acting on a body as a result of centripetal acceleration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00937
name: centrifugal radius
def: "The radius at which a component is spinning at the end of the period of centrifugation. For a component sedimented from a dilute suspension\, it can be equated with radius of rotation at the bottom of the centrifuge tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00938
name: cephalins
def: "Compounds derived from glycerol in which a primary and the secondary hydroxy groups are esterified with long-chain fatty acids\, and the remaining primary one with the mono\(2-aminoethyl\) ester of phosphoric acid\, or with the monoserine ester of phosphoric acid. The term is not recommended. These compounds are preferably designated as \(3-phosphatidyl\)ethanolamines and \(3-phosphatidyl\)serines respectively."
synonym: "kephalins" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00939
name: cephalosporins
def: "Cephems having the basic structure shown."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00940
name: cephams
def: "Natural and synthetic antibiotics containing the 5-thia-1-azabicyclo\[4.2.0\]octan-8-one nucleus; generally assumed to have the 6R configuration\, unless otherwise specified. The numbering used differs from that of the von Baeyer named bicyclic system. Where they differ\, the von Baeyer numbering is shown in parentheses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00941
name: cephems
def: "2\,3-Didehydrocephams\: 5-thia-1-azabicyclo\[4.2.0\]oct-2-en-8-ones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00942
name: ceramic filter
def: "A component of a stack sampling system which is suitable for high temperature use; also known as a ceramic thimble."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00943
name: Cerenkov detector
def: "A charged particle detector based on the Cerenkov effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00944
name: Cerenkov effect
def: "Emission of radiation in the visible and ultraviolet spectrum arising when a charged particle crosses a medium with a velocity greater than that of light in the same medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00945
name: Cerenkov radiation
def: "Radiation resulting from the Cerenkov effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00946
name: chain
def: "The whole or part of a macromolecule\, an oligomer molecule or a block\, comprising a linear or branched sequence of constitutional units between two boundary constitutional units\, each of which may be either an end-group\, a branch point or an otherwise-designated characteristic feature of the macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00947
name: chain axis
def: "The straight line parallel to the direction of chain extension\, connecting the centres of mass of successive blocks of chain units\, each of which is contained within an identity period."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00948
name: chain branching
def: "When in a chain reaction there is a net increase in the number of chain carrier there is said to be chain branching. A simple example of a chain-propagating reaction leading to chain branching is\:  in which there is one chain carrier \(an oxygen atom\) on the left and two chain carriers \(a hydrogen atom and a hydroxyl radical\) on the right."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00949
name: chain carrier
def: "A species\, such as an atom or free radical\, which is involved in chain-propagating reactions is known as a chain carrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00950
name: chain conformational repeating unit
def: "The smallest structural unit of a polymer chain with a given conformation that is repeated along that chain through symmetry operations."
synonym: "conformational repeating unit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00952
name: chain fission yield
def: "The fraction of fissions giving rise to nuclei of a particular mass number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00953
name: chain folding
def: "The conformational feature in which a loop connects two parallel stems belonging to the same crystal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00955
name: chain initiation
def: "The process in a chain reaction that is responsible for the formation of a chain carrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00956
name: chain length
def: "In a chain reaction\, the average number of times the closed cycle of reactions \(involving the chain-propagating reactions\) is repeated. It is equal to the rate of the overall reaction divided by the rate of the initiation reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00957
name: chain-orientational disorder
def: "Structural disorder resulting from the statistical coexistence within the crystals of identical chains with opposite orientations. A typical example is provided by the up-down statistical coexistence of anticlined chains in the same crystal structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00958
name: chain polymerization
def: "A chain reaction in which the growth of a polymer chain proceeds exclusively by reaction\(s\) between monomer\(s\) and reactive site\(s\) on the polymer chain with regeneration of the reactive site\(s\) at the end of each growth step."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00959
name: chain-propagating reaction
def: "A chain-propagating reaction\, or more simply a propagating reaction\, is an elementary step in a chain reaction in which one chain carrier is converted into another. The conversion can be a unimolecular reaction or a bimolecular reaction with a reactant molecule."
synonym: "propagating reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00960
name: chain reaction
def: "A reaction in which one or more reactive reaction intermediates \(frequently radicals\) are continuously regenerated\, usually through a repetitive cycle of elementary steps \(the 'propagation step'\). For example\, in the chlorination of methane by a radical mechanism\, $\\ce\{Cl^\{.\}\}$ is continuously regenerated in the chain propagation steps\:   In chain polymerization reactions\, reactive intermediates of the same types\, generated in successive steps or cycles of steps\, differ in relative molecular mass\, as in\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00961
name: chain scission
def: "A chemical reaction resulting in the breaking of skeletal bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00963
name: chain transfer
def: "The abstraction\, by the radical end of a growing chain-polymer\, of an atom from another molecule. The growth of the polymer chain is thereby terminated but a new radical\, capable of chain propagation and polymerization\, is simultaneously created. For the example of alkene polymerization cited for a chain reaction\, the reaction\:  represents a chain transfer\, the radical $\\ce\{Cl3C^\{.\}\}$ inducing further polymerization\:   The phenomenon occurs also in other chain reactions such as cationic polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00964
name: chair\, boat\, twist
def: "If carbon atoms 1\, 2\, 4 and 5 of cyclohexane occupy coplanar positions and when carbon atoms 3 and 6 are on opposite sides of the plane the conformation \(of symmetry group $D_\{\\rm\{3d\}\}$\) is called a chair form. The same term is applied to similar conformations of analogous saturated six-membered ring structures containing hetero-atoms and/or bearing substituent groups\, but these conformations may be distorted from the exact $D_\{\\rm\{3d\}\}$ symmetry. For cyclohexane and most such analogues\, the chair form is the most stable conformation. If the cyclohexane conformation has no centre of symmetry but possesses two planes of symmetry\, one of them bisecting the bonds between atoms 1 and 2 and between 4 and 5 and the other plane passing through atoms 3 and 6 \(which lie out of the plane and on the same side of the plane containing 1\, 2\, 4 and 5\)\, that conformation \(of symmetry group $C_\{\\rm\{2v\}\}$\) is called a boat form and it is generally not a stable form. Again\, this term is also applied to structural analogues. The conformation of $D_\{2\}$ symmetry passed through in the interconversion of two boat forms of cyclohexane is called the twist form \(also known as skew boat\, skew form and stretched form\).  In a five-membered ring a conformation in which two adjacent atoms are maximally displaced\, in opposite directions\, relative to the plane containing the other three carbon atoms has been called a half-chair but is better called a twist conformation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00966
name: chalcones
def: "1\,3-Diphenylpropenone \(benzylideneacetophenone\)\, $\\ce\{ArCH=CH\(=O\)Ar\}$\, and its derivatives formed by substitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00967
name: chamber saturation
def: "Uniform distribution of the eluent vapour throughout the chamber prior to chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00968
name: change of a quantity
def: "The increment of the value of a quantity \\\(Q\\\) with time. The change may be expressed either infinitesimally at time \\\(t\\\) by the differential \\\(\\rm\{d\}Q\\\) or \\\(\\rm\{d\}Q\(t\)\\\)\, or in practice it may be expressed by a finite increment over the time interval \\\(\(t_\{1\};t_\{2\}\)\\\)\, i.e. \\\(Q\(t_\{2\}\)- Q\(t_\{1\}\)\\\)\, which may be written \\\(\\Delta Q\\\) or \\\(\\Delta Q\(t_\{1\};t_\{2\}\)\\\) \\\[\\Delta Q = \\Delta Q\(t_\{1\};t_\{2\}\) = Q\(t_\{2\}\)- Q\(t_\{1\}\)\\\] Examples are\: mass change\, \\\(\\Delta m\\\); amount of substance change\, \\\(\\Delta n\\\); volume change\, \\\(\\Delta V\\\); substance concentration change\, \\\(\\Delta c\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00969
name: change ratio of a quantity
def: "A term which may be expressed infinitesimally at time \\\(t\\\) by a ratio of differentials \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}Q_\{1\}\(t\)\}\{\\rm\{d\}Q_\{2\}\(t\)\}\\\) where the kind of quantities are the same but for different components in the same system. In practice\, the ratio for a finite interval is\: \\\[\\frac\{\\Delta Q_\{1\}\(t_\{1\};\\\,t_\{2\}\)\}\{\\Delta Q_\{2\}\(t_\{1\};t_\{2\}\)\}\\\] Examples are\: mass change ratio\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}m_\{1\}\(t\)\}\{\\rm\{d\}m_\{2\}\(t\)\}\\\); amount of substance change ratio\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{1\}\(t\)\}\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{2\}\(t\)\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00970
name: channel
def: "Regions of potential-energy surfaces where there are valleys are sometimes referred to as arrangement channels. The reactant channel or entrance channel is that corresponding to configurations similar to those of the reactants; the product channel or exit channel relates to configurations similar to those of the products. In collision theory\, the specification of a complete set of quantum numbers in a given arrangement channel is called a channel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00973
name: chaperon
def: "A species that facilitates a combination reaction between atoms or radicals\, or the reverse process\, is known as a chaperon. It is a special case of a third body."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00974
name: char
def: "A solid decompostion product of a natural or synthetic organic material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00975
name: characteristic
def: "A property or attribute of a material that is measured\, compared or noted. Attributes are ordinarily qualitative characteristics\, but quantitative characteristics \(variables\) may be converted into attributes by assigning items to certain categories on the basis of the measured values. The value of the characteristic determined as a result of an observation or test is the observed value; when determined by a specified test method\, it is called the test result. The concentration or quantity of an analyte as estimated by use of a sample is usually the characteristic of interest in analytical chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00976
name: characteristic group
def: "A single heteroatom\, for example\, $\\ce\{–Cl\}$ and $\\ce\{=O\}$; a heteroatom bearing one or more hydrogen atoms or other heteroatoms\, for example\, $\\ce\{–NH2\}$\, $\\ce\{–OH\}$\, $\\ce\{–SO3H\}$\, $\\ce\{–PO3H2\}$ and $\\ce\{-IO2\}$; or a heteroatomic group attached to or containing a single carbon atom\, for example\, $\\ce\{–CHO\}$\, $\\ce\{–C#N\}$\, $\\ce\{–COOH\}$ and $\\ce\{–NCO\}$\, attached to a parent hydride."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00977
name: characteristic length
def: "The term characteristic length or scale refers\, in general\, to the parameter which characterizes the density profile \(of a given physical quantity\). The static \(equilibrium\) or dynamic character of a characteristic length must be specified. The terms out of plane and in plane refer to characteristic lengths normal or parallel to the interface\, respectively. Since interfacial layer 'thickness' and characteristic length correspond to various concepts\, the current usage where an out of plane characteristic length is referred to as the interfacial layer thickness\, is confusing and should be abandoned."
synonym: "characteristic scale" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00978
name: characteristic mass for integrated absorbance
def: "That mass of analyte which produces an integrated absorbance signal whose net area is equal to \\\(0.0044\\ \\rm\{s\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00979
name: characteristic mass for peak absorption
def: "That mass of analyte which produces a defined peak absorbance of 0.0044 \(or 1% absorbance\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00980
name: characteristic mass
def: "The mass of analyte which\, when atomized electrothermally\, produces the uniquely defined absorption signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00981
name: characteristic potential
def: "An applied potential that is characteristic of a charge-transfer process and the experimental conditions \(such as the composition of the solvent and supporting electrolyte and the temperature\) under which it is investigated\, and whose nature depends on the technique that is employed. Some typical characteristic potentials are the half-wave potential in polarography\, the quarter-transition-time potential in chronopotentiometry\, the peak potential and the half-peak potential in linear-sweep voltammetry\, and the summit potential in ac polarography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00982
name: characteristic ratio
def: "The ratio of the mean-square end-to-end distance\, \\\(< r^\{2\} >_\{0\}\\\)\, of a linear polymer chain in a theta state to \\\(N\\cdot L^\{2\}\\\)\, where \\\(N\\\) is the number of rigid sections in the main chain\, each of length \\\(L\\\); if all of the rigid sections are not of equal length\, the mean-square value of \\\(L\\\) is used\, i.e. \\\[L^\{2\}=\\sum _\{i\}\\frac\{L_\{i\}^\{\\bar\{2\}\}\}\{N\}\\\] In simple single-strand chains\, the bonds are taken as the rigid sections. The recommended symbol is\: \\\(C_\{N\}\\\) \(\\\(C_\{\\infty \}\\\) when \\\(N\\rightarrow \\infty \\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00985
name: characteristic X-ray emission
def: "X-ray emission originates from the radiative decay of electronically highly excited states of matter. Excitation by electrons is called primary excitation and by photons\, secondary or fluorescence excitation. Particle induced X-ray emission \(PIXE\) is produced by the excitation of heavier particles such as protons\, deuterons or heavy atoms in varying degrees of ionization. Emission of photons in the X-ray wavelength region also occurs from ionized gases or plasmas at high temperatures\, from nuclear processes \(low-energy end of the gamma-ray spectrum\) and from radiative transitions between muonic states. Characteristic X-ray emission consists of a series of X-ray spectral lines with discrete frequencies\, characteristic of the emitting atom. Other features are emission bands from transitions to valence levels. In a spectrum obtained with electron or photon excitation the most intense lines are called diagram lines or normal X-ray lines. They are dipole allowed transitions between normal X-ray diagram levels."
synonym: "characteristic X-radiation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00986
name: charcoal
def: "A traditional term for a char obtained from wood\, peat\, coal or some related natural organic materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00988
name: charge density
def: "Electric charge divided by the volume it occupies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00989
name: charge-exchange ionization
def: "This occurs when an ion/atom or ion/molecule reaction takes place in which the charge on the ion is transferred to the neutral species without any dissociation of either. Synonymous with charge-transfer ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00990
name: charge-exchange reaction
def: "Synonymous with charge-transfer reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00991
name: charge hopping
def: "Electron or hole transfer between equivalent sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00992
name: charge-inversion mass spectrum
def: "Charge inversion processes of the types\:  or   or respectively\, occuring in a collision cell \(containing a gas\, X\) located in a field-free region preceding a magnetic and electric sector combination placed in either order\, may be detected as follows. If the instrument slits are wide\, and if the connections to the two sectors\, appropriate to transmission of either positive or negative main-beam ions\, are simply reversed\, the negative or positive product ions of the two processes\, respectively\, will be transmitted. If the magnetic field is scanned\, a spectrum of such product ions will be obtained\, and this spectrum is called a charge-inversion mass spectrum. These spectra are sometimes referred to as −E and +E spectra\, respectively. The terms '2E\, E/2\, −E or +E mass spectrum' should not be used without prior explanation of the meaning 2E\, E\, +E or −E."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00993
name: charge number
def: "Ratio of the charge of a particle to the elementary charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00994
name: charge number
def: "The magnitude of the charge on an ion\, given in arabic numerals followed by the sign of the charge\, in parentheses without a space\, immediately after the name of an ion\, e.g. iron\(2+\)\, hexacyanoferrate\(4−\)."
synonym: "Ewens–Bassett number" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00995
name: charge number
def: "Number of electrons transferred according to the cell reaction equation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00996
name: charge-permutation reaction
def: "A general term to describe an ion/neutral species reaction where there is a change in the magnitude and/or sign of the charges of the reactant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00997
name: charge population
def: "The net electric charge on a specified atom in a molecular entity\, as determined by some prescribed definition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00998
name: charge recombination
def: "Reverse of charge separation. In using this term it is important to specify the resulting electronic state of the donor and acceptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C00999
name: charge separation
def: "A process in which\, under the influence of a suitable driving force \(e.g. provided by photoexcitation\)\, electronic charge moves in a direction that increases the difference in local charges between donor and acceptor sites. electron transfer between neutral species is one of the most important examples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01000
name: charge shift
def: "A process in which under the influence of a suitable driving force \(e.g. provided by photoexcitation\) electronic charge moves without changing the difference in local charges between donor and acceptor sites. Electron transfer reversing the charges in a system composed of a neutral donor and a cationic acceptor or of a neutral acceptor and an anionic donor provide prominent examples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01001
name: charge-stripping reaction
def: "An ion/neutral species reaction wherein the charge on the reactant ion is made more positive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01002
name: charge-transfer adsorption
def: "Oxidative or reductive chemisorption where 'reductive' and 'oxidative' refer to electron gain or loss on species in the solid. In simple cases it is non-dissociative\, i.e. there is a mere transfer of charge between adsorptive and adsorbent in forming the adsorbate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01003
name: charge-transfer complex
def: "An electron-donor–electron-acceptor complex\, characterized by electronic transition\(s\) to an excited state in which there is a partial transfer of electronic charge from the donor to the acceptor moiety."
synonym: "electron-donor-acceptor complex" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01004
name: charge-transfer device
def: "A charge-transfer device has a metal oxide semiconductor \(MOS\) structure that is composed of many independent pixels where charge is stored in such a way that the charge pattern corresponds to the irradiation pattern. These devices can be linear or two-dimensional. According to the method used to detect the charge pattern\, two types of charge-transfer devices can be distinguished\: charge-coupled devices \(CCDs\) and charge-injection devices \(CIDs\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01005
name: charge-transfer reaction
def: "An ion/neutral species reaction wherein the total charge on the reactant ion is transferred initially to the reactant neutral species so that the reactant ion becomes a neutral entity. Considering some of the possible reactions of ions $\\ce\{M^\{2+\}\}$\, $\\ce\{M^\{+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{M^\{−\}\}$ with a neutral species $\\ce\{X\}$\, these would be further categorized as follows\:    All are ion/neutral species reactions and also charge permutation reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01006
name: charge-transfer state
def: "A state related to the ground state by a charge-transfer transition."
synonym: "CT state" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01007
name: charge-transfer step
def: "An elementary step in which charge is transferred from one phase to the other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01008
name: charge-transfer transition
def: "An electronic transition in which a large fraction of an electronic charge is transferred from one region of a molecular entity\, called the electron donor\, to another\, called the electron acceptor \(intramolecular CT\) or from one molecular entity to another \(intermolecular CT\). Typical for donor-acceptor complexes or multichromophoric molecular entities. In some cases the charge-transfer absorption band may be obscured by the absorption of the partners."
synonym: "CT transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01009
name: charge-transfer transition to solvent
def: "Electronic transition which is adequately described by single electron transfer between a solute and the solvent\, as opposed to excitation followed by electron transfer to solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01010
name: charring
def: "Pyrolysis of samples containing organic matter. In the presence of oxygen this is referred to as ashing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01012
name: chelation
def: "The formation or presence of bonds \(or other attractive interactions\) between two or more separate binding sites within the same ligand and a single central atom. A molecular entity in which there is chelation \(and the corresponding chemical species\) is called a 'chelate'. The terms bidentate \(or didentate\)\, tridentate\, tetradentate\, ... multidentate are used to indicate the number of potential binding sites of the ligand\, at least two of which must be used by the ligand in forming a 'chelate'. For example\, the bidentate ethylenediamine forms a chelate with $\\ce\{CuI\}$ in which both nitrogen atoms of ethylenediamine are bonded to copper. \(The use of the term is often restricted to metallic central atoms.\) The phrase 'separate binding sites' is intended to exclude cases such as $\\ce\{\[PtCl3\(CH2=CH2\)\]^\{−\}\}$\, ferrocene and \(benzene\)tricarbonylchromium in which ethene\, the cyclopentadienyl group and benzene\, respectively\, are considered to present single binding sites to the respective metal atom\, and which are not normally thought of as chelates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01014
name: cheletropic reaction
def: "A form of cycloaddition across the terminal atoms of a fully conjugated system with formation of two new $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ to a single atom of the \('monocentric'\) reagent. There is formal loss of one $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ in the substrate and an increase in coordination number of the relevant atom of the reagent. An example is the addition of sulfur dioxide to butadiene\:  The reverse of this type of reaction is designated 'cheletropic elimination'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01015
name: chelotropic reaction
def: "Alternative \(and etymologically more correct\) name for cheletropic reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01016
name: chemical actinometer
def: "A detector in which the amount of a chemical formed is proportional to the numbers of photons absorbed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01017
name: chemical activation
def: "When some of the energy required for a reaction is provided by a preceding exothermic chemical reaction there is said to be chemical activation. For example\, in the scheme\:   some or all of the energy required for X to decompose is provided by the first reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01020
name: chemical decomposition
def: "The breakdown of a single entity \(normal molecule\, reaction intermediate\, etc.\) into two or more fragments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01021
name: chemical dosimeter
def: "A dosimeter in which the dose is measured by observing the extent\, under specified conditions\, of a chemical reaction caused by the ionizing radiation to be measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01022
name: chemical element
def: "A species of atoms; all atoms with the same number of protons in the atomic nucleus.A pure chemical substance composed of atoms with the same number of protons in the atomic nucleus. Sometimes this concept is called the elementary substance as distinct from the chemical element as defined under \(1\)\, but mostly the term chemical element is used for both concepts."
synonym: "element" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01023
name: chemical equilibrium
def: "Reversible processes \[processes which may be made to proceed in the forward or reverse direction by the \(infinitesimal\) change of one variable\]\, ultimately reach a point where the rates in both directions are identical\, so that the system gives the appearance of having a static composition at which the Gibbs energy\, \\\(G\\\)\, is a minimum. At equilibrium the sum of the chemical potentials of the reactants equals that of the products\, so that\: \\\[\\Delta G_\{\\rm\{r\}\} = \\Delta G_\{\\rm\{r\}\}^\{o\}+R\\ T\\ \\ln K=0\\\] \\\[\\Delta G_\{\\rm\{r\}\}^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} = -R\\ T\\ \\ln K\\\] The equilibrium constant\, \\\(K\\\)\, is given by the mass-law effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01024
name: chemical flux
def: "A concept related to rate of reaction\, particularly applicable to the progress in one direction only of component reaction steps in a complex system or to the progress in one direction of reactions in a system at dynamic equilibrium \(in which there are no observable concentration changes with time\). Chemical flux is a derivative with respect to time\, and has the dimensions of amount of substance per unit volume transformed per unit time. The sum of all the chemical fluxes leading to destruction of B is designated the 'total chemical flux out of B' \(symbol \\\(\\sum \\varphi_\{-\\ce\{B\}\}\\\)\); the corresponding formation of B by concurrent elementary reactions is the 'total chemical flux into B or A' \(symbol \\\(\\sum \\varphi_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\\\)\). For the mechanism\:  the total chemical flux into C is caused by the single reaction \(1\)\: \\\[\\sum \\varphi_\{\\ce\{C\}\} = \\varphi_\{1\}\\\] whereas the chemical flux out of C is a sum over all reactions that remove C\: \\\[\\sum\\varphi_\{-\\ce\{C\}\} = \\varphi_\{-1\} + \\varphi_\{2\}\\\] where \\\(\\varphi_\{-1\}\\\) is the 'chemical flux out of C into B \(and/or A\)' and \\\(\\varphi_\{2\}\\\) is the 'chemical flux out of C into E'. The rate of reaction of C is then given by\: \\\[\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{C\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = \\sum\\varphi_\{\\ce\{C\}\} - \\sum\\varphi_\{-\\ce\{C\}\}\\\] In this system \\\(\\varphi_\{1\}\\\) \(or \\\(\\sum \\varphi_\{-\\ce\{A\}\}\\\)\) can be regarded as the hypothetical rate of decrease in the concentration of A due to the single \(unidirectional\) reaction \(1\) proceeding in the assumed absence of all other reactions. For a non-reversible reaction\: \\\[\\ce\{A ->\[1\] P\}\\\] \\\[-\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{A\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = \\varphi_\{1\}\\\] If two substances A and P are in chemical equilibrium\: \\\[\\ce\{A  P\}\\\] then\: \\\[\\sum\\varphi_\{\\ce\{A\}\} = \\sum\\varphi_\{-\\ce\{A\}\} = \\sum\\varphi_\{\\ce\{P\}\} = \\sum\\varphi_\{-\\ce\{P\}\}\\\] and \\\[-\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{A\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{P\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = 0\\\]"
synonym: "total chemiflux" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01025
name: chemical induction
def: "When in a chemical system one reaction accelerates another there is said to be chemical induction or coupling. It is due to an intermediate or product of the inducing reaction participating in the second reaction. Chemical induction is often observed in oxidation–reduction reactions\, chain reactions\, and biological reactions. Sometimes a reaction having \\\(\\Delta G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} > 0\\\) is induced by a simultaneous process having \\\(\\Delta G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} < 0\\\)."
synonym: "chemical coupling" []
synonym: "coupling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01026
name: chemical ionization
def: "This concerns the process whereby new ionization species are formed when gaseous molecules interact with ions. The process may involve transfer of an electron\, proton or other charged species between the reactants. When a positive ion results from chemical ionization\, the term may be used without qualification. When a negative ion is involved\, the term negative ion chemical ionization should be used; note that negative ion formation by attachment of a free electron does not fall within this definition. Chemical ionization and chemi-ionization are two terms which should not be used interchangeably."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01027
name: chemical isotope exchange
def: "Exchange of isotopes between different types of molecules or ions in the course of a chemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01028
name: chemical laser
def: "A continuous wave or pulsed laser in which the excitation and population inversion of the emitting species result from a chemical reaction. Typical examples are $\\ce\{HF\}$ and $\\ce\{DF\}$ lasers emitting many lines in the IR region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01030
name: chemical measurement process
def: "An analytical method of defined structure that has been brought into a state of statistical control\, such that its imprecision and bias are fixed\, given the measurement conditions. This is prerequisite for the evaluation of the performing characteristics of the method\, or the development of meaningful uncertainty statements concerning analytical results."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01031
name: chemical oxygen demand
def: "A measure of the amount of oxygen\, divided by the volume of the system\, required to oxidize the organic \(and inorganic\) matter in wastewater using a chemically oxidizing agent. In practice\, it is usually expressed in milligrams $\\ce\{O2\}$ per litre."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01032
name: chemical potential
def: "The chemical potential of a substance B in a mixture of substances B\, C ... is related to the Gibbs energy \\\(G\\\) of the mixture by\: \\\[\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial G\}\{\\partial n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\)_\{T\,p\,n_\{\\rm\{C\}\neq \\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(p\\\) is the pressure and \\\(n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(n_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\)\, ... are the amounts of substance of B\, C\, ... . For a pure substance B\, the chemical potential \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{*\}\\\) is given by\: \\\[\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{*\}=\\frac\{G^\{*\}\}\{n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}=G_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{*\}\\\] where \\\(G_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{*\}\\\) is the molar Gibbs energy\, and where the superscript * attached to a symbol denotes the property of the pure substance. The superscript \\\(^\{⦵\}\\\) or \\\(^\{\\circ\}\\\) attached to a symbol may be used to denote a standard thermodynamic quantity."
synonym: "partial molar Gibbs energy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01033
name: chemical reaction
def: "A process that results in the interconversion of chemical species. Chemical reactions may be elementary reactions or stepwise reactions. \(It should be noted that this definition includes experimentally observable interconversions of conformers.\) Detectable chemical reactions normally involve sets of molecular entities as indicated by this definition\, but it is often conceptually convenient to use the term also for changes involving single molecular entities \(i.e. 'microscopic chemical events'\)."
synonym: "reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01034
name: chemical reaction equation
def: "Symbolic representation of a chemical reaction where the reactant entities are given on the left hand side and the product entities on the right hand side. The coefficients next to the symbols and formulae of entities are the absolute values of the stoichiometric numbers. Different symbols are used to connect the reactants and products with the following meanings\: \\\(=\\\) for a stoichiometric relation; \\\(\\rightarrow \\\) for a net forward reaction; \\\(\\leftrightarrows \\\) for a reaction in both directions; \\\(\\leftrightharpoons \\\) for equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01035
name: chemical relaxation
def: "If the equilibrium mixture of a chemical reaction is disturbed by a sudden change\, especially of some external parameter \(such as temperature\, pressure or electrical field strength\)\, the system will readjust itself to a new position of the chemical equilibrium or return to the original position\, if the perturbation is temporary. The readjustment is known as chemical relaxation. In many cases\, and in particular when the displacement from equilibrium is slight\, the progress of the system towards equilibrium can be expressed as a first-order law\: \\\[C_\{t\}- \\left\(C_\{\\rm\{eq\}\}\\right\)_\{2\} = \[\\left\(C_\{\\rm\{eq\}\}\\right\)_\{1\}- \\left\(C_\{\\rm\{eq\}\}\\right\)_\{2\}\]\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{\\frac\{-t\}\{\\tau \}\}\\\] where \\\(\\left\(C_\{\\rm\{eq\}\}\\right\)_\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\left\(C_\{\\rm\{eq\}\}\\right\)_\{2\}\\\) are the equilibrium concentrations of one of the chemical species involved in the reaction before and after the change in the external parameter\, and \\\(C_\{t\}\\\) is its concentration at time \\\(t\\\). The time parameter \\\(\\tau \\\)\, named relaxation time\, is related to the rate constants of the chemical reaction involved. Measurements of the relaxation times by relaxation methods \[involving a temperature jump \(T-jump\)\, pressure jump\, electric field jump or a periodic disturbance of an external parameter\, as in ultrasonic techniques\] are commonly used to follow the kinetics of very fast reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01036
name: chemical shift
def: "The fractional variation of the resonance frequency of a nucleus in nuclear magnetic resonance \(NMR\) spectroscopy in consequence of its magnetic environment. The chemical shift of a nucleus\, \\\(\\delta \\\)\, is expressed as a ratio involving its frequency\, \\\(\nu _\{\\mathrm\{cpd\}\}\\\)\, relative to that of a standard\, \\\(\nu _\{\\mathrm\{ref\}\}\\\)\, and defined as\: \\\[\\delta = \\frac\{\nu _\{\\rm\{cpd\}\}- \nu _\{\\rm\{ref\}\}\}\{\nu _\{ref\}\}\\\] \\\(\\delta \\\)-values are normally expressed in \\\(\\mathrm\{ppm\}\\\). For $\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}$ and $\\ce\{^\{13\}C\}$ NMR the reference signal is usually that of tetramethylsilane \(TMS\)\, strictly speaking in dilute solution in $\\ce\{CDCl3\}$. Other references are used in the older literature and for other solvents\, such as $\\ce\{D2O\}$. Resonance lines to high frequency from the TMS signal have positive\, and resonance lines to low frequency from TMS have negative\, \\\(\\delta \\\)-values \(arising from relative deshielding and shielding respectively\). The archaic terms 'downfield' and 'upfield' should no longer be used. For nuclei other than $\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}$\, chemical shifts are expressed either in the same manner relative to an agreed substance containing the relevant nucleus or relative to the $\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}$ resonance of TMS as \\\(\\mathit\{\\Xi\}\\\) values\, defined in the references below."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01037
name: chemical shift
def: "The displacement of photoelectron or Auger peak energies originating from changes in electron binding energies as a consequence of differences in the chemical environment of the atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01039
name: chemical substance
def: "Matter of constant composition best characterized by the entities \(molecules\, formula units\, atoms\) it is composed of. Physical properties such as density\, refractive index\, electric conductivity\, melting point etc. characterize the chemical substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01040
name: chemical vapour generation
def: "A system in which the analyte is separated from the sample matrix by the generation of gaseous species as a result of a chemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01041
name: chemical yield
def: "The fraction of the amount of an element or chemical compound following a specified chemical reaction or separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01042
name: chemiexcitation
def: "Generation\, by a chemical reaction\, of electronically excited molecular entities from reactants in their ground electronic states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01044
name: chemi-ionization
def: "This refers to a process whereby gaseous molecules are ionized when they interact with other internally excited gaseous molecules or molecular moieties. Chemi-ionization and chemical ionization are two terms which should not be used interchangeably."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01045
name: chemiluminescence
def: "Emission of radiation resulting from a chemical reaction. The emitting species may be a reaction product or a species excited by energy transfer from an excited reaction product. The excitation may be electronic\, vibrational or rotational; if the luminescence occurs in the infrared the expression infrared chemiluminescence is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01046
name: chemiluminescence analyser
def: "An instrument consisting of a reaction chamber with separate inlets for the sample and reagent gas\, an optical filter\, a photomultiplier and a signal processing device. The reactive gas is introduced in excess. The quantity of light produced is proportional to the sample flow rate and the concentration of the measured substance in the sample under specified temperature and pressure conditions. The filter limits the wavelength to the region of interest and helps to eliminate interferences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01047
name: chemiluminescent methods of detection
def: "Methods which utilize gas or liquid phase reactions between two molecules which produce a third molecule in an excited state. The wavelength distribution of the light emission from the excited molecule is characteristic of the species; in some circumstances this may be used to identify and to determine the concentration of one of the reacting species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01048
name: chemisorption
def: "Adsorption which results from chemical bond formation \(strong interaction\) between the adsorbent and the adsorbate in a monolayer on the surface."
synonym: "chemical adsorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01051
name: chemoselectivity
def: "Chemoselectivity is the preferential reaction of a chemical reagent with one of two or more different functional groups. A reagent has a high chemoselectivity if reaction occurs with only a limited number of different functional groups. For example\, sodium tetrahydroborate is a more chemoselective reducing agent than is lithium tetrahydroaluminate. The concept has not been defined in more quantitative terms. The term is also applied to reacting molecules or intermediates which exhibit selectivity towards chemically different reagents. Some authors use the term 'chemospecificity' for 100% chemoselectivity. However\, this usage is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01053
name: chemostat
def: "A bioreactor in which constant growth conditions for microorganisms are maintained over prolonged periods of time by supplying the reactor with a continuous input of nutrients and continuous removal of medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01055
name: chimney effect
def: "A vertical movement of a localized mass of air or other gases which occurs due to local temperature differences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01057
name: chiral
def: "Having the property of chirality. As applied to a molecule the term has been used differently by different workers. Some apply it exclusively to the whole molecule\, whereas others apply it to parts of a molecule. For example\, according to the latter view\, a meso-compound is considered to be composed of two chiral parts of opposite chirality sense; this usage is to be discouraged. In its application to an assembly of molecules\, some restrict the term to an assembly in which all of the molecules have the same chirality sense\, which is better called enantiopure. Others extend it to a racemic assembly\, which is better just called a racemate. Use of the term to describe molecular assemblies should be avoided."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01058
name: chirality
def: "The geometric property of a rigid object \(or spatial arrangement of points or atoms\) of being non-superposable on its mirror image; such an object has no symmetry elements of the second kind \(a mirror plane\, \\\(\\sigma =S_\{1\}\\\)\, a centre of inversion\, \\\(i=S_\{2\}\\\)\, a rotation-reflection axis\, \\\(S_\{2n\}\\\)\). If the object is superposable on its mirror image the object is described as being achiral."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01059
name: chirality axis
def: "An axis about which a set of ligands is held so that it results in a spatial arrangement which is not superposable on its mirror image. For example with an allene $\\ce\{abC=C=Ccd\}$ the chiral axis is defined by the $\\ce\{C=C=C\}$ bonds; and with an ortho-substituted biphenyl the atoms $\\ce\{C-1\}$\, $\\ce\{C-1'\}$\, $\\ce\{C-4\}$ and $\\ce\{C-4'\}$ lie on the chiral axis."
synonym: "axis of chirality" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01060
name: chirality centre
def: "An atom holding a set of ligands in a spatial arrangement which is not superposable on its mirror image. A chirality centre is thus a generalized extension of the concept of the asymmetric carbon atom to central atoms of any element\, for example $\\ce\{N^\{+\}abcd\}$\, $\\ce\{Pabc\}$ as well as $\\ce\{Cabcd\}$ ."
synonym: "asymmetric centre" []
synonym: "centre of chirality" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01061
name: chirality element
def: "General name for a chirality axis\, chirality centre or chirality plane. Also referred to as an element of chirality."
synonym: "element of chirality" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01062
name: chirality plane
def: "A planar unit connected to an adjacent part of the structure by a bond which results in restricted torsion so that the plane cannot lie in a symmetry plane. For example with \(E\)-cyclooctene the chiral plane includes the double bond carbon atoms and all four atoms attached to the double bond; with a monosubstituted paracyclophane the chiral plane includes the monosubstituted benzene ring with its three hydrogen atoms and the three other atoms linked to the ring \(i.e. from the substituent and the two chains linking the two benzene rings\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01063
name: chirality sense
def: "The property that distinguishes enantiomorphs. The specification of two enantiomorphic forms by reference to an oriented space e.g. of a screw\, a right threaded one or a left threaded one. The expression 'opposite chirality' is short for 'opposite chirality sense'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01064
name: chiroptic
def: "A term referring to the optical techniques \(using refraction\, absorption or emission of anisotropic radiation\) for investigating chiral substances \[e.g. measurements of optical rotation at a fixed wavelength\, optical rotatory dispersion \(ORD\)\, circular dichroism \(CD\)\, and circular polarization of luminescence \(CPL\)\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01065
name: chirotopic
def: "The description of an atom \(or point\, group\, face\, etc. in a molecular model\) that resides within a chiral environment. One that resides within an achiral environment has been called achirotopic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01066
name: chloramines
def: "Amines substituted at nitrogen with one or two chlorine atoms \(a contracted form of N-chloroamines\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01067
name: chlorocarbons
def: "Compounds consisting wholly of chlorine and carbon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01071
name: chromatogram
def: "A graphical or other presentation of detector response\, concentration of analyte in the effluent or other quantity used as a measure of effluent concentration versus effluent volume or time. In planar chromatography 'chromatogram' may refer to the paper or layer with the separated zones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01072
name: chromatograph
def: "The assembly of apparatus for carrying out chromatographic separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01073
name: chromatograph
def: "To separate by chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01074
name: chromatographic detector
def: "A device that measures the change of composition of the effluent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01075
name: chromatography
def: "A physical method of separation in which the components to be separated are distributed between two phases\, one of which is stationary \(stationary phase\) while the other \(the mobile phase\) moves in a definite direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01076
name: chromophore
def: "The part \(atom or group of atoms\) of a molecular entity in which the electronic transition responsible for a given spectral band is approximately localized. The term arose in the dyestuff industry\, referring originally to the groupings in the molecule that are responsible for the dye's colour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01077
name: chromosome
def: "A self-replicating structure consisting of DNA complexed with various proteins and involved in the storage and transmission of genetic information; the physical structure that contains genes \(cf. plasmid\). Eukaryotic cells have a characteristic number of chromosomes per cell \(cf. ploidy\) and contain DNA as linear duplexes. The chromosomes of bacteria consist of double-standed\, circular DNA molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01079
name: chemically induced dynamic nuclear polarization
def: "Non-Boltzmann nuclear spin state distribution produced in thermal or photochemical reactions\, usually from colligation and diffusion\, or disproportionation of radical pairs\, and detected by NMR spectroscopy by enhanced absorption or emission signals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01081
name: cine-substitution
def: "A substitution reaction \(generally aromatic\) in which the entering group takes up a position adjacent to that occupied by the leaving group. For example\,"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01082
name: CIP priority
def: "In the CIP rules the conventional order of ligands established for the purpose of unambiguous designation of stereoisomers. It is deduced by application of 'sequence rules'\, the authoritative statement of which appears in R.S. Cahn\, C.K. Ingold and V. Prelog\, Angew. Chem.78\, 413-447 \(1966\)\, Angew. Chem. Internat. Ed. Eng. 5\, 385-415\, 511 \(1966\); and V. Prelog and G. Helmchen\, Angew. Chem. 94\, 614-631 \(1982\)\, Angew. Chem. Internat. Ed. Eng. 21\, 567-583 \(1982\)."
synonym: "priority" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01083
name: C. I. P. system
def: "Short for Cahn–Ingold–Prelog system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01087
name: <i>cis</i>-
def: "A prefix designating two groups occupying adjacent positions in a coordination sphere\, not now generally recommended for precise nomenclature purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01088
name: <i>cis</i> conformation
def: "Conformations referring to torsion angles $\\uptheta\\!$ \($\\ce\{A\}$\, $\\ce\{B\}$\, $\\ce\{C\}$\, $\\ce\{D\}$\)\, where $\\ce\{A\}$\, $\\ce\{B\}$\, $\\ce\{C\}$\, $\\ce\{D\}$ are main-chain atoms\, can be described as\: cis or synperiplanar \(\\\(\\rm\{C\}\\\)\); gauche or synclinal \(\\\(\\rm\{G\}\\\)\); anticlinal \(\\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\)\); and trans or antiperiplanar \(\\\(\\rm\{T\}\\\)\)\, corresponding to torsion angles within $\\pu\{\\pm 30\\!^\{\\circ\}\}$ of\, respectively\, $\\pu\{\\pm 0\\!^\{\\circ\}\}$\, $\\pu\{\\pm 120\\!^\{\\circ\}\}$ and $\\pu\{\\pm 180\\!^\{\\circ\}\}$. The letters shown in parentheses \(upper case \\\(\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{G\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{T\}\\\)\) are the recommended abbreviations. The symbols \\\(\\rm\{G\}^\{+\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{G\}^\{-\}\\\) \(or \\\(\\rm\{A\}^\{+\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{G\}^\{-\}\\\)\, for example\) refer to torsion angles of similar type but opposite known sign\, i.e. $\\pu\{\\sim +60^\{\\circ\}\}$\, $\\pu\{\\sim -60^\{\\circ\}\}$ \(or $\\pu\{\\sim +120^\{\\circ\}\}$\, $\\pu\{\\sim -120^\{\\circ\}\}$\). The notation \\\(\\rm\{G\}\\\)\, \\\(\\bar\{\\rm\{G\}\}\\\) ; \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\)\, \\\(\\bar\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) \(and \\\(\\rm\{T\}\\\)\, \\\(\\bar\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\) ; \\\(\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, \\\(\\bar\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\) - whenever the torsion angles are not exactly equal to $\\pu\{180\\!^\{\\circ\}\}$ and $\\pu\{0\\!^\{\\circ\}\}$\, respectively\) is reserved for the designation of enantiomorph conformations\, i.e. conformations of opposite but unspecified sign. Where necessary\, a deviation from the proper value of the torsion angle can be indicated by the sign \($\\sim$\)\, as in the following examples\: \\\(\\rm\{G\(\\sim\)\}\\\); \\\(\\rm\{G\}^\{+\}\(\\sim\)\\\); \\\(\\rm\{G\}^\{-\}\\rm\{\(\\sim\)\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01089
name: <i>cis</i>-fused
def: "Steric relations at saturated bridgeheads common to two rings are denoted by cis or trans\, followed by a hyphen and placed before the name of the ring system\, according to the relative positions of the exocyclic atoms or groups attached to the bridgeheads. Such rings are said to be cis-fused or trans-fused."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01091
name: cistactic polymer
def: "A tactic polymer in which the main-chain double bonds of the configurational base units are entirely in the cis arrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01092
name: <i>cis</i>\, <i>trans</i>
def: "Descriptors which show the relationship between two ligands attached to separate atoms that are connected by a double bond or are contained in a ring. The two ligands are said to be located cis to each other if they lie on the same side of a plane. If they are on opposite sides\, their relative position is described as trans. The appropriate reference plane of a double bond is perpendicular to that of the relevant $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ and passes through the double bond. For a ring \(the ring being in a conformation\, real or assumed\, without re-entrant angles at the two substituted atoms\) it is the mean plane of the ring\(s\). For alkenes the terms cis and trans may be ambiguous and have therefore largely been replaced by the E\, Z convention for the nomenclature of organic compounds. If there are more than two entities attached to the ring the use of cis and trans requires the definition of a reference substituent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01093
name: <i>cis</i>-<i>trans</i> isomers
def: "Stereoisomeric olefins or cycloalkanes \(or hetero-analogues\) which differ in the positions of atoms \(or groups\) relative to a reference plane\: in the cis-isomer the atoms are on the same side\, in the trans-isomer they are on opposite sides."
synonym: "geometric isomerism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01094
name: class \(a\) metal ion
def: "A metal ion that combines preferentially with ligands containing ligating atoms that are the lightest of their Periodic Group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01095
name: class \(b\) metal ion
def: "A metal ion that combines preferentially with ligands containing ligating atoms other than the lightest of their Periodic Group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01096
name: class of helix
def: "The number of skeletal chain atoms contained within the helix residue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01097
name: clathrates
def: "Inclusion compounds in which the guest molecule is in a cage formed by the host molecule or by a lattice of host molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01098
name: clausius
def: "Non-SI unit of entropy\, \\\(\\rm\{Cl\} = 4.184\\ \\rm\{J K\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01099
name: clean surface
def: "An experimental surface having no surface contamination observable by means of the used method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01100
name: clean up
def: "A process caused by sputtering\, trapping \(or adsorbing\) metallic atoms\, gas atoms or molecules on the walls of the vessel or anode. This has the effect of reducing the gas pressure in sealed lamps\, and may be useful for cleaning up or gettering unwanted gases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01103
name: clone
def: "A population of genetically identical cells produced from a common ancestor. Sometimes\, 'clone' is also used for a number of recombinant DNA molecules all carrying the same inserted sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01106
name: <i>closo</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote a cage or closed structure\, especially a boron skeleton that is a polyhedron having all triangular faces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01107
name: cloud
def: "An aerosol of the atmosphere which is dense enough to be perceptible to the eye. Usually refers to an assembly of water droplet aerosols suspended in the atmosphere\, although dust clouds are also recognized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01115
name: cluster ion
def: "An ion formed by the combination of more ions or atoms or molecules of a chemical species often in association with a second species. For example\, $\\ce\{\[\(H2O\)_\{n\}H\]^\{+\}\}$ is a cluster ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01116
name: coacervation
def: "The separation into two liquid phases in colloidal systems. The phase more concentrated in colloid component is the coacervate\, and the other phase is the equilibrium solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01117
name: coagulation
def: "When a sol is colloidally unstable \(i.e. the rate of aggregation is not negligible\) the formation of aggregates is called coagulation or flocculation."
synonym: "flocculation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01118
name: coal-derived pitch coke
def: "The primary industrial solid carbonization product obtained from coal tar pitch mainly produced in chamber or delayed coking processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01119
name: coalescence
def: "The disappearance of the boundary between two particles \(usually droplets or bubbles\) in contact\, or between one of these and a bulk phase followed by changes of shape leading to a reduction of the total surface area. The flocculation of an emulsion\, viz. the formation of aggregates\, may be followed by coalescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01120
name: coalification
def: "A geological process of formation of materials with increasing content of the element carbon from organic materials that occurs in a first\, biological stage into peats\, followed by a gradual transformation into coal by action of moderate temperature \(about \\\(500\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\) and high pressure in a geochemical stage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01121
name: coal tar pitch
def: "A residue produced by distillation or heat treatment of coal tar. It is a solid at room temperature\, consists of a complex mixture of numerous predominantly aromatic hydrocarbons and heterocyclics\, and exhibits a broad softening range instead of a defined melting temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01123
name: codon
def: "The sequence of three consecutive nucleotides that occurs in mRNA which directs the incorporation of a specific amino acid into a protein and includes also the starting or termination signals of protein synthesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01124
name: coefficient
def: "Proportionality constant between two quantities of different dimension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01125
name: coefficient of haze
def: "One technique of measurement of the amount of filterable particulate matter suspended in air which has been used in the past depends upon drawing a measured sample of air \(usually 1 000 linear feet\) through a paper or membrane filter. A measurement is made of the intensity of light transmitted through the dust spot formed relative to that transmitted through an identical clean filter. The dirtiness of the air is reported in terms of the \\\(\\rm\{COH\}\\\) unit. This relates to the quantity of particulate material which produces an optical density\, \\\[\\log_\{10\}\(\\frac\{I_\{0\}\}\{I\}\)\\\] of 0.01 when measured by light transmission at \\\(\\pu\{400 nm\}\\\) and relative to the transmission of an identical dust-free filter taken as 100%. Thus a filter which transmitted 50% relative to the clean filter has an absorbance of 0.301 or 30.1 \\\(\\rm\{COH\}\\\) units. This is not a recommended measure of filterable particulate matter since the size\, colour and other properties of the aerosol and the air in which it is suspended affect the results."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01126
name: coenzyme
def: "The dissociable\, low-relative-molecular-mass active group of an enzyme which transfers chemical groups\, hydrogen or electrons. A coenzyme binds with its associated protein \(apoenzyme\) to form the active enzyme \(holoenzyme\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01127
name: coextraction
def: "Formation of mixed-species aggregates in a low-polarity organic phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01128
name: cofactors
def: "Organic molecules \(cf. coenzymes\) or ions \(usually metal ions\) that are required by an enzyme of its activity. They may be attached either loosely or tightly prosthetic group\) to the enzyme. A cofactor binds with its associated protein \(apoenzymes\)\, which is functionally inactive\, to form the active enzyme \(holoenzyme\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01129
name: coherence length
def: "The distance over which order is maintained. As an example\, there can be long-range atomic or molecular order\, i.e. coherence. Coherence lengths are significantly larger than molecular size. Normally\, coherence length is estimated from electron\, neutron or X-ray scattering and scales the size of ordered domains in material where long range ordering occurs \(as in liquid crystals\, for example\). The term coherence length is also used for the scale characterizing the profile of molecular axis orientation in the distorted\, so-called\, transition layers formed at a solid/liquid-crystal boundary when an appropriate external field is applied \(e.g. when the orientation of anchored surface molecules of a nematic liquid crystal is unaffected by an external field -- magnetic or electric -- whereas the bulk molecules reorient freely\). This scale is referred to as electric coherence length\, \\\(\\zeta _\{\\rm\{E\}\}\\\) or magnetic coherence length\, \\\(\\zeta _\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\)\, depending on the nature of the applied field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01130
name: coherent radiation
def: "Radiation emitted by a source when all the elementary waves emitted have a phase difference constant in space and time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01131
name: coherent scattering
def: "Scattering is coherent whenever the phases of the signals arising from different scattering centres are correlated and incoherent whenever these phases are uncorrelated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01132
name: coherent source
def: "Those sources where the radiation has a constant phase relationship between waves spatially as well as temporally\, e.g. lasers. Non-coherent optical sources emit radiation which is randomly distributed in phase\, spatially as well as temporally. Most sources which are used in spectroscopy and for spectrochemical analysis conform to this latter group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01133
name: coherent structure
def: "If the net of an ordered adsorbed phase is in registry with the lattice of the adsorbent the structure is called coherent\, if not it is called incoherent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01134
name: coherent system of units of measurement
def: "System of units of measurement in which all of the derived units are coherent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01135
name: coherent unit
def: "Derived unit of measurement that may be expressed as a product of powers of base units with the proportionality factor one."
synonym: "coherent derived unit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01136
name: coincidence circuit
def: "An electronic circuit which produces a usable output pulse only when each of its inputs receives a pulse within a specified time interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01139
name: coincidence resolving time
def: "The greatest time interval that can elapse between the occurrence of two or more consecutive signal pulses\, in order that the measuring device processes them as a coincidence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01141
name: co-ions
def: "Ions of low relative molecular mass with a charge of the same sign as that of a given colloidal ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01142
name: coke
def: "A solid high in content of the element carbon and structurally in the non-graphitic state. It is produced by pyrolysis of organic material which has passed\, at least in part\, through a liquid or liquid-crystalline state during the carbonization process. Coke can contain mineral matter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01143
name: coke breeze
def: "A by-product of coke manufacture; it is the residue from the screening of heat-treated coke. The particle size is less than \\\(10\\ \\rm\{mm\}\\\). Generally\, coke breeze has a volatile matter content of \\\("

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01144
name: coking
def: "Many reactions involving hydrocarbons and particularly those run at higher temperatures lead to the deposition on the catalyst of high molecular weight compounds of carbon and hydrogen which deactivate the catalyst. This phenomenon is called coking or fouling. Catalysts so deactivated can often be regenerated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01145
name: col
def: "A mountain-pass in a potential-energy surface is known as a col or saddle point. It is a point at which the gradient is zero along all coordinates\, and the curvature is positive along all but one coordinate\, which is the reaction coordinate\, along which the curvature is negative."
synonym: "saddle point" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01147
name: cold neutrons
def: "Neutrons with a neutron temperature considerably lower than normal room temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01148
name: collection
def: "The planned removal from solution of a micro- or macro-component by the intentional formation of a contaminated host precipitate\, or by the adsorption or entrapment of the component by an added solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01149
name: collection efficiency
def: "A term which characterizes an entire sampling and sample pretreatment procedure\, usually represented as a percentage of the original amount of the analyte which is left for measurement \(signal formation\) after having passed through this procedure. This term also applies to the efficiency of collection of an air pollutant by an arrestment plant. For particulates\, the collection efficiency is size related."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01150
name: collection gas flow system
def: "A system in which the analyte is carried to a collecting device called the collector which may be an amalgamator for mercury\, a cold trap for some of the more stable hydrides\, an absorbing solution in which the analyte is absorbed by some chemical reaction\, an electrothermal atomizer or a reservoir."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01151
name: collector
def: "A device for removing and retaining contaminants from air or other gases; a term which is often applied to cleaning devices in exhaust systems. Also used to designate a device for removing and retaining samples from media in different environmental compartments to be investigated. A collector is sometimes used to describe a scavenger."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01152
name: collector
def: "A solid substance added to or formed within a solution to collect a micro- or macro-component."
synonym: "scavenger" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01153
name: colligation
def: "The formation of a covalent bond by the combination or recombination of two radicals \(the reverse of unimolecular homolysis\)."
synonym: "radical combination" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01154
name: collimation
def: "The limiting of a beam of radiation to the required dimensions and angular spread."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01155
name: collimator
def: "An arrangement of absorbers used for collimation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01156
name: collinear reaction
def: "This is a reaction assumed to occur via an activated complex in which all of the atoms directly involved in the process lie along a straight line."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01157
name: collisional activation
def: "An ion/neutral species process wherein excitation of a projectile ion of high translational energy is brought about by the same mechanism as in collision-induced dissociation. \(The ion may decompose subsequently.\)"
synonym: "collisionally activated dissociation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01158
name: collisional broadening
def: "Collisions of the emitting or absorbing particle with other particles cause collisional broadening as well as collisional shift of the spectral line. When collisions occur between unlike\, neutral particles the term foreign-gas broadening is used\, or van der Waals' broadening when both collision partners are neutral. When the colliding particles are of the same species\, the term resonance broadening is employed. The term Lorentz broadening was previously used for neutral particle collision broadening\, and Holtsmark broadening for cases of van der Waals' broadening when collisions took place with like particles. Both terms are now discouraged. Stark broadening refers to collisions with charged particles or particles with a strong permanent electrical dipole moment. Whereas a strong chaotic electrical field causes Stark broadening\, an applied static electrical field induces a Stark shift."
synonym: "Holtsmark broadening" []
synonym: "Lorentz broadening" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01159
name: collisional excitation
def: "An ion/neutral species process wherein there is an increase in the reactant ion's internal energy at the expense of the translational energy of either \(or both\) of the reacting species. The scattering angle may be large."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01160
name: collision complex
def: "An ensemble formed by two reaction partners for which the distance is the sum of their van der Waals radii. As such it constitutes a subclass of the species indicated as encounter complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01161
name: collision cross-section
def: "In simple hard sphere collision theory the area of the circle with radius equal to the collision diameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01162
name: collision density
def: "Total collision frequency for all molecules within a volume divided by that volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01163
name: collision diameter
def: "In simple hard sphere collision theory the sum of radii of two colliding spherical particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01164
name: collision efficiency
def: "The collision efficiency\, or de-energization efficiency\, is defined by\: \\\[k_\{\\rm\{-1\}\} = B_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\ k_\{\\rm\{-1\}\}^\{\\rm\{sc\}\}\\\] where \\\(k_\{-1\}\\\) is the rate constant for a particular substance \\\(\\rm\{M\}\\\) when it brings about the de-energization process  and \\\(k_\{-1\}^\{\\rm\{sc\}\}\\\) is the corresponding rate constant for a reference molecule \\\(\\rm\{M\}_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) that de-energizes \\\(\\rm\{A\}^\{*\}\\\) on every collision; that is\, the reference molecule \\\(\\rm\{M\}_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) undergoes strong collisions\, and by definition has a collision efficiency \\\(B_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) of unity. The species \\\(\\rm\{A\}^\{*\}\\\) is usually in a vibrationally-excited state\, and \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\) has energy less than that required for reaction to occur."
synonym: "de-energization efficiency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01166
name: collision frequency
def: "Average number of collisions experienced by a molecule A with other molecules A \(or B\) in a time interval divided by that time interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01167
name: collision-induced dissociation
def: "An ion/neutral species process wherein the projectile ion is dissociated as a result of interaction with a target neutral species. This is brought about by conversion of part of the translational energy of the ion to internal energy in the ion during the collision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01169
name: collision number
def: "Now called collision density."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01170
name: collision theory
def: "Various collision theories\, dealing with the frequency of collision between reactant molecules\, have been put forward. In the earliest theories reactant molecules were regarded as hard spheres\, and a collision was considered to occur when the distance \\\(d\\\) between the centres of two molecules was equal to the sum of their radii. For a gas containing only one type of molecule\, A\, the collision density is given by simple collision theory as\: \\\[Z_\{\\mathrm\{AA\}\}=\\frac\{\\sqrt\{2\}\\ \\pi \\ \\sigma ^\{2\}\\ u\\ N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{2\}\}\{2\}\\\] Here \\\(N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) is the number density of molecules and \\\(u\\\) is the mean molecular speed\, given by kinetic theory to be \\\(\\sqrt\{\\frac\{8\\ k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\}\{\\pi \\ m\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(m\\\) is the molecular mass\, and \\\(\\sigma =\\pi \\ d_\{\\mathrm\{AA\}\}^\{2\}\\\). Thus\: \\\[Z_\{\\mathrm\{AA\}\}=2\\ N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{2\}\\ \\sigma ^\{2\}\\ \\sqrt\{\\frac\{\\pi \\ k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\}\{m\}\}\\\] The corresponding expression for the collision density \\\(Z_\{\\mathrm\{AB\}\}\\\) for two unlike molecules A and B\, of masses \\\(m_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) and \\\(m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is\: \\\[Z_\{\\mathrm\{AB\}\}=N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\ N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ \\sigma ^\{2\}\\ \\sqrt\{\\frac\{\\pi \\ k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\}\{\\mu \}\}\\\] where \\\(\\mu \\\) is the reduced mass \\\(\\frac\{m_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\ m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{m_\{\\rm\{A\}\}+m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\sigma =\\pi \\ d_\{\\mathrm\{AB\}\}^\{2\}\\\). For the collision frequency factor these formulations lead to the following expression\: \\\[z_\{\\mathrm\{AA\}\}\\quad \\rm\{or\}\\quad z_\{\\mathrm\{AB\}\}=L\\ \\sigma ^\{2\}\\ \\sqrt\{\\frac\{8\\ \\pi \\ k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\}\{\\mu \}\}\\\] where \\\(L\\\) is the Avogadro constant. More advanced collision theories\, not involving the assumption that molecules behave as hard spheres\, are known as generalized kinetic theories."
synonym: "kinetic theory of collisions" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01171
name: colloid
def: "A short synonym for colloidal system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01172
name: colloidal
def: "The term refers to a state of subdivision\, implying that the molecules or polymolecular particles dispersed in a medium have at least in one direction a dimension roughly between \\\(1\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) and \\\(1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\, or that in a system discontinuities are found at distances of that order."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01173
name: colloidal carbon
def: "A particulate carbon with particle sizes below ca.\\\(1000\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) in at least one dimension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01174
name: colloidal dispersion
def: "A system in which particles of colloidal size of any nature \(e.g. solid\, liquid or gas\) are dispersed in a continuous phase of a different composition \(or state\). The name dispersed phase for the particles should be used only if they have essentially the properties of a bulk phase of the same composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01175
name: colloidal electrolyte
def: "An electrolyte which gives ions of which at least one is of colloidal size. This term therefore includes hydrophobic sols\, ionic association colloids\, and polyelectrolytes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01176
name: colloidally stable
def: "Particles which do not aggregate at a significant rate\: the precise connotation depends on the type of aggregation under consideration. For example\, a concentrated paint is called stable by some people because oil and pigment do not separate out at a measurable rate\, and unstable by others because the pigment particles aggregate into a continuous network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01177
name: colloidal suspension
def: "A suspension in which the size of the particles lies in the colloidal range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01178
name: colloid osmotic pressure
def: "The pressure difference which has to be established between a colloidal system and its equilibrium liquid to prevent material transfer between the two phases when they are separated by a membrane\, permeable to all components of the system\, except the colloidal ones."
synonym: "Donnan pressure" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01179
name: colorimeter
def: "An instrument used for colour measurement based on optical comparison with standard colours."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01180
name: colour indicator
def: "An indicator which is classified as one- or two-colour\, depending on whether it is colourless on one side of the transition interval or possesses a different colour on each side of this range."
synonym: "color indicator" []
synonym: "indicator" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01181
name: column
def: "The tube and the stationary phase contained within\, through which the mobile phase passes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01182
name: column chromatography
def: "A separation technique in which the stationary bed is within a tube. The particles of the solid stationary phase or support coated with a liquid stationary phase may fill the whole inside volume of the tube \(packed column\) or be concentrated on or along the inside tube wall leaving an open\, unrestricted path for the mobile phase in the middle part of the tube \(open-tubular column\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01183
name: column volume
def: "The geometric volume of the part of the tube that contains the packing\: \\\[V_\{\\rm\{c\}\} = A_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\ L\\\] where \\\(A_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) is the internal cross-sectional area of the tube and \\\(L\\\) the length of the packed part of the column. In the case of wall-coated open-tubular columns the column volume corresponds to the geometric volume of the whole tube having a liquid or a solid stationary phase on its wall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01184
name: combination electrode
def: "An ion-selective electrode and an external reference electrode combined in a single unit\, thereby avoiding the need for a separate external reference electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01185
name: combined sample
def: "A sample obtained by removing specific fractions by separation or selection techniques \(e.g. heavy liquid\, magnetic\, sieving\, etc.\)\, analysing the fractions separately\, and combining the results mathematically. When not combined\, the sample is a modified sample. This term should not be confused with composite sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01186
name: comb macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule comprising a main chain with multiple trifunctional branch points from each of which a linear side-chain emanates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01187
name: comb polymer
def: "A polymer composed of comb macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01188
name: combustion gas
def: "A gas or vapour produced in furnaces\, combustion chambers or in open burning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01189
name: commensurate–incommensurate transition
def: "A transition between two states having magnetic or crystallographic structures with a basic lattice and an imposed super-periodicity. In the commensurate \(lock-in\) state this super-periodicity is a simple rational multiple of the basic unit cell. In the incommensurate state the ratio of the super-periodicity repeat distance to the basic lattice repeat distance is irrational and may show continuous variation with variation in some degree of freedom \(e.g. temperature\, pressure\, composition\) of the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01191
name: common-ion effect
def: "A reduction in the rate of reaction of certain reactions of a substrate RX in solution \[by a path that involves a pre-equilibrium with formation of $\\ce\{R^\{+\}\}$ \(or $\\ce\{R^\{−\}\}$\) ions as reaction intermediates\] caused by the addition to the reaction mixture of an electrolyte solute containing the 'common ion' $\\ce\{X^\{−\}\}$ \(or $\\ce\{X+\}$\). For example\, the rate of solvolysis of diphenylmethyl chloride in acetone-water is reduced by the addition of salts of the common ion $\\ce\{Cl^\{−\}\}$ which causes a decrease in the quasi-equilibrium concentration of the diphenylmethyl cation in the scheme\:   This phenomenon is a direct consequence of the mass-law effect on ionization equilibria in electrolytic solution. More generally\, the common-ion effect is the influence of the 'common ion' on the reactivity due to the shift of the dissociation equilibrium. It may also lead to an enhancement of the rate of reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01192
name: communities
def: "Naturally occuring groups of different \(micro\)organisms inhabiting a common environment\, interacting with each other\, especially through food relationships and relatively independent of other groups. Communities may be of varying sizes\, and larger communities may contain smaller ones."
synonym: "consortia" []
synonym: "synergistic associations" []
synonym: "syntrophic associations" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01194
name: comparator
def: "A known amount of element that is simultaneously irradiated with the test portion in the context of activation analysis. If one comparator is used \(single comparator method\)\, it is essentially identical to a flux monitor \(except that this term is not necessarily linked to activation analysis\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01195
name: compensation
def: "For the process\: \\\[k = A\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{\\frac\{-E\}\{R\\ T\}\}\\\] where \\\(k\\\) is a rate constant\, \\\(A\\\) the frequency factor and \\\(E\\\) the activation energy\, \\\(A\\\) and \\\(E\\\) sometimes exhibit compensation\, i.e. they change in the same direction with change in catalyst for a given reaction or with change in reaction for a given catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01196
name: compensation
def: "For internal compensation\, see compound.See\: external compensation"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01197
name: compensation effect
def: "In a considerable number of cases plots of \\\(T\\ \\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}S\\\) vs. \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H\\\)\, for a series of reactions\, e.g. for a reaction in a range of different solvents\, are straight lines of approximately unit slope. Therefore\, the terms \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H\\\) and \\\(T\\ \\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}S\\\) in the expression partially compensate\, and \\\[\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}G = \\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H - T\\ \\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}S\\\] often is a much simpler function of solvent \(or other\) variation than \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H\\\) or \\\(T\\ \\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}S\\\) separately."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01199
name: competitive binding assay
def: "An assay based on the competition between a labelled and an unlabelled ligand in the reaction with a receptor binding agent \(e.g. antibody\, receptor\, transport protein\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01200
name: competitive inhibition
def: "The reduction in rate due to reduction or loss of activity of a catalyst caused by adsorption of poison which is not greatly preferred to adsorption of reactant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01202
name: complementary DNA
def: "A single-stranded DNA molecule with a nucleotide sequence that is complementary to an RNA molecule; cDNA is formed by the action of the enzyme reverse transcriptase on an RNA template. After conversion to the double-stranded form\, cDNA is used for molecular cloning or for hybridization studies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01203
name: complex
def: "A molecular entity formed by loose association involving two or more component molecular entities \(ionic or uncharged\)\, or the corresponding chemical species. The bonding between the components is normally weaker than in a covalent bond. The term has also been used with a variety of shades of meaning in different contexts\: it is therefore best avoided when a more explicit alternative is applicable. In inorganic chemistry the term 'coordination entity' is recommended instead of 'complex'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01204
name: complex coacervation
def: "Coacervation caused by the interaction of two oppositely charged colloids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01205
name: complex mechanism
def: "This term is sometimes applied to an elementary reaction which proceeds via an intermediate species having a lifetime longer than a few rotational periods\, in contrast to a direct reaction. However\, in view of the danger of confusion with a reaction occurring in more than one step \(a composite reaction\) this usage is not to be recommended; it is better to use the terms complex-mode mechanism or complex-mode reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01206
name: complex-mode reaction
def: "An elementary reaction which proceeds via an intermediate species having a lifetime longer than a few rotational periods is known as a complex-mode reaction\, or as an indirect reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01209
name: component
def: "Constituent of a mixture the amount or concentration of which can be varied independently. The number of components in a given system is the minimum number of independent species necessary to define the composition in all the phases of a system. It may vary with external conditions since additional chemical equilibria reduce the number of components. The term component is also often used in the more general sense as defined here under constituent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01210
name: composite mechanism
def: "A reaction that involves more than one elementary reaction is said to occur by a composite mechanism. The terms complex mechanism\, indirect mechanism\, and step-wise mechanism are also commonly used. There are two main kinds of evidence for a composite mechanism\: The kinetic equation for the reaction does not correspond to its stoichiometry.There is experimental evidence\, direct or indirect\, for intermediates of such a nature that it is necessary to conclude that more than one elementary reaction is involved. There are many types of composite mechanisms\, for example\: Reactions occurring in parallel\, such as\:  are called parallel reactions or simultaneous reactions. When there are simultaneous reactions there is sometimes  competition\, as in the scheme\:  where B and C compete with one another for A.Reactions occurring in forward and reverse directions are called opposing reactions\: Reactions occurring in sequence\, such as  are known as consecutive reactions.Reactions are said to exhibit feedback if a substance formed in one step affects the rate of a previous step. For example\, in the scheme\: The intermediate Y may catalyse the reaction  \(positive feedback\) or it may inhibit it \(negative feedback\).Chain reactions"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01211
name: composite reaction
def: "A chemical reaction for which the expression for the rate of disappearance of a reactant \(or rate of appearance of a product\) involves rate constants of more than a single elementary reaction. Examples are 'opposing reactions' \(where rate constants of two opposed chemical reactions are involved\)\, 'parallel reactions' \(for which the rate of disappearance of any reactant is governed by the rate constants relating to several simultaneous reactions to form different respective products from a single set of reactants\)\, and stepwise reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01212
name: composite sample
def: "Often prepared as a representative mixture of several different \(usually bulk\) samples\, and from which the laboratory sample is taken."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01213
name: compositional heterogeneity
def: "The variation in elemental composition from molecule to molecule usually found in copolymers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01214
name: composition of pure air
def: "The composition of air is variable with respect to several of its components \(e.g. $\\ce\{CH4\}$\, $\\ce\{CO2\}$\, $\\ce\{H2O\}$\) so 'pure' air has no precise meaning; it is commonly considered to be air which is free of dust\, aerosols and reactive gaseous contaminants of anthropogenic origin. The composition of the major components in dry air is relatively constant \(percent by volume given\)\: nitrogen\, 78.084; oxygen\, 20.946; argon\, 0.934; carbon dioxide\, 0.033; neon\, 0.0018; helium\, 0.000524; methane\, 0.00016; krypton\, 0.000114; hydrogen 0.00005; nitrous oxide\, 0.00003; xenon\, 0.0000087. The concentrations of carbon dioxide\, methane\, nitrous oxide\, the chlorofluorocarbons and some other species of anthropogenic origin are increasing measurably with time. Relative clean air which is free of most reactive anthropogenic pollution \($\\ce\{NO\}$\, $\\ce\{NO2\}$\, $\\ce\{SO2\}$\, non-methane hydrocarbons\, etc.\)\, often used as a reference sample in the calibration and operation of instruments\, is purchased under the designation of zero air."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01216
name: compression factor
def: "Product of pressure and molar volume divided by the gas constant and thermodynamic temperature. For an ideal gas it is equal to 1."
synonym: "compressibility factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01217
name: comproportionation
def: "The reverse of disproportionation. The term 'symproportionation' is also used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01218
name: Compton effect
def: "The elastic scattering of a photon by an electron which afterwards occurs as a free electron. Part of the energy and momentum of the incident photon is transferred to the electron and the remaining part is carried away by the scattered photon. Synonymous with Compton scattering"
synonym: "Compton scattering" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01219
name: Compton electron
def: "The energetic electron resulting from the Compton effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01222
name: concentration
def: "Group of four quantities characterizing the composition of a mixture with respect to the volume of the mixture \(mass\, amount\, volume and number concentration\).Short form for amount-of-substance concentration \('substance concentration' in clinical chemistry\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01224
name: concentration-cell corrosion
def: "The local variation of the corrosion rate due to the action of a corrosion cell resulting from inhomogeneous composition of the environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01225
name: concentration distribution ratio
def: "The ratio of the analytical concentration of a component in the stationary phase to its analytical concentration in the mobile phase. \\\[D_\{\\rm\{c\}\}=\\frac\{\\rm\{amount of component\}/\\rm\{cm\}^\{3\}\\rm\{of stationary phase\}\}\{\\rm\{amount of component\}/\\rm\{cm\}^\{3\}\\rm\{of mobile phase\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01227
name: concentration gradient
def: "Differential change in concentration of a component in a given direction in a small distance divided by the distance in that direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01229
name: concentration in experimental surface
def: "The amount of the material of interest divided by the total amount of substances in the volume of interest. Concentration may be defined in terms of numbers of atoms \(particles\) \(\\\(\\rm\{ppma\}\\\)\) or in terms of weight \(\\\(\\frac\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{g\}\}\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "surface concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01230
name: concentration overpotential
def: "The concentration overpotential of an electrode reaction at a given electrode current density \(\\\(\\rm\{c.d.\}\\\)\) is basically the difference in equilibrium potentials across the diffusion layer. More precisely\, it is the potential of a reference electrode \(of the same electrode reaction as the working electrode\) with the interfacial concentrations which establish themselves at \\\(\\rm\{c.d.\}\\\)\, relative to the potential of a similar reference electrode with the concentrations of the bulk solution. From such a measured potential difference\, with \\\(\\rm\{c.d.\}\\\) flowing\, one needs to subtract the ohmic potential drop prevailing between the two electrodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01232
name: concentration-sensitive detector
def: "A device the response of which is proportional to the concentration of a sample component in the eluent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01234
name: concerted process
def: "Two or more primitive changes are said to be concerted \(or to constitute a concerted process\) if they occur within the same elementary reaction. Such changes will normally \(though perhaps not inevitably\) be 'energetically coupled'. \(In the present context the term 'energetically coupled' means that the simultaneous progress of the primitive changes involves a transition state of lower energy than that for their successive occurrence.\) In a concerted process the primitive changes may be synchronous or asynchronous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01235
name: condensation
def: "The physical process of converting a material from a gaseous or vapour phase to a liquid or solid phase; this commonly results when the temperature is lowered and/or the vapour pressure of the material is increased. The tendency exists for condensation to occur when the partial pressure of a given component of a gaseous mixture at a given temperature exceeds the vapour pressure of the liquid or solid form of that component at the given temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01236
name: condensation nuclei
def: "Particles\, either liquid or solid\, or ions upon which condensation of water vapour \(or other substances\) begins in the atmosphere. Condensation nuclei are usually very small hygroscopic aerosols \(\\\(\\pu\{0.001 \\upmu m\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{0.1 \\upmu m\}\\\) in diameter\)\, but these are not as abundant as the smaller particles. The number of CN which are active \(initiate condensation\) in a given air mass may be a function of the relative humidity. Usually CN are counted as the active nuclei at about $\\ce\{300\\%\}$ relative humidity\, while cloud condensation nuclei \(CCN\) are counted as the number of active nuclei at relative humidity less than or equal to $\\ce\{102\\%\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01237
name: condensation polymerization
def: "Polymerization by a repeated condensation process \(i.e. with elimination of simple molecules\)."
synonym: "polycondensation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01238
name: condensation reaction
def: "A \(usually stepwise\) reaction in which two or more reactants \(or remote reactive sites within the same molecular entity\) yield a single main product with accompanying formation of water or of some other small molecule\, e.g. ammonia\, ethanol\, acetic acid\, hydrogen sulfide. The mechanism of many condensation reactions has been shown to comprise consecutive addition and elimination reactions\, as in the base-catalysed formation of \(E\)-but-2-enal \(crotonaldehyde\) from acetaldehyde\, via 3-hydroxybutanal \(aldol\). The overall reaction in this example is known as the aldol condensation. The term is sometimes also applied to cases where the formation of water or another simple molecule does not occur\, as in 'benzoin condensation'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01240
name: conditional potential
def: "Conditional \(formal\) potential\, \\\(E^\{0'\}\\\)\, is related to the potential of a cell reaction\, \\\(E_\{\\mathrm\{cell\}\}\\\)\, by an equation analogous to that relating \\\(E_\{\\mathrm\{cell\}\}\\\) to the standard potential \\\(E_\{0\}\\\)\, with the activity\, \\\(a_\{i\}\\\)\, replaced by any composition variable\, \\\(c_\{i\}\\\) \(to be indicated by a subscript\). For example\: \\\[E_\{\\mathrm\{cell\}\}=E_\{\\rm\{c\}\}^\{0'\}- \\frac\{R\\ T\}\{n\\ F\}\\ \\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}\nu _\{i\}\\ \\ln c_\{i\}\\\]"
synonym: "formal potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01242
name: conditioning
def: "Synonymous with pre-equilibration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01244
name: conduction band
def: "A vacant or only partially occupied set of many closely spaced electronic levels resulting from an array of a large number of atoms forming a system in which the electrons can move freely or nearly so. This term is usually used to describe the properties of metals and semiconductors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01245
name: conductivity
def: "Reciprocal of resistivity. This quantity is a tensor in an anisotropic medium. It was formerly called 'specific conductance'."
synonym: "electric conductivity" []
synonym: "electrical conductivity" []
synonym: "specific conductance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01246
name: confidence level
def: "The probability of covering the expected value of an estimated parameter with an interval estimated for the parameter \(symbol \\\(1 - \\alpha\\\)\). The confidence level can be expressed as a number between 0 and 1\, or in percent. The complementary quantity is known as the significance level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01247
name: confidence limits
def: "Symmetric confidence limits \(\\\(\\pm C\\\)\) about the estimated mean\, which cover the population mean with probability \\\(1 - \\alpha \\\). The quantity \\\(C\\\) is calculated by the formula\: \\\[C = \\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{p\}\,v^\{S\}\}\}\{\\sqrt\{n\}\}\\\] Here \\\(t_\{\\rm\{p\}\,v\}\\\)\, is the critical value from the \\\(t\\\)- \(or Student\) distribution function corresponding to the confidence level \\\(1 - \\alpha \\\) and degrees of freedom \\\(v\\\). The symbol \\\(p\\\) represents the percentile \(or percentage point\) of the \\\(t\\\)-distribution. For 1-sided intervals\, \\\(p=1- \\alpha \\\); for 2-sided intervals\, \\\(p=1- \\frac\{\\alpha \}\{2\}\\\). In each case\, the confidence level is \\\(1 - \\alpha \\\). The confidence interval is given as \\\(\\overline\{x\}\\ \\pm \\ C\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01248
name: configuration
def: "A distribution of the electrons of an atom or a molecular entity over a set of one-electron wavefunctions called orbitals\, according to the Pauli principle. From one configuration several states with different multiplicities may result. For example\, the ground electronic configuration of the oxygen molecule \($\\ce\{O2\}$\) is\: \\\(1\\sigma _\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(1\\sigma _\{\\rm\{u\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(2\\sigma _\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(2\\sigma _\{\\rm\{u\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(1\\pi _\{\\rm\{u\}\}^\{4\}\\\)\, \\\(3\\sigma _\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(1\\pi _\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{2\}\\\) resulting in the \\\(^\{3\}\\Sigma_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\)\, \\\(^\{1\}\\Delta_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) and \\\(^\{1\}\\Sigma_\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{+\}\\\) multiplets."
synonym: "electron configuration" []
synonym: "electronic configuration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01249
name: configuration
def: "In the context of stereochemistry\, the term is restricted to the arrangements of atoms of a molecular entity in space that distinguishes stereoisomers\, the isomerism between which is not due to conformation differences."
synonym: "stereochemical configuration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01250
name: configurational base unit
def: "A constitutional repeating unit in a regular macromolecule\, a regular oligomer molecule\, regular block or a regular chain\, the configuration of which is defined at least at one site of stereoisomerism in the main chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01251
name: configurational disorder
def: "Structural disorder resulting from the statistical co-crystallization of different configurational repeating units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01252
name: configurational homosequence
def: "A constitutional homosequence in which the relative or absolute configuration is defined at one or more sites of stereoisomerism in each constitutional unit in the main chain of a polymer molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01253
name: configurational repeating unit
def: "The smallest set of successive configurational base units that prescribes configurational repetition at one or more sites of stereoisomerism in the main chain of a regular macromolecule\, a regular oligomer molecule\, a regular block or a regular chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01254
name: configurational sequence
def: "The whole or part of a chain comprising one or more species of configurational unit\(s\) in defined sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01255
name: configurational unit
def: "A constitutional unit having at least one site of defined stereoisomerism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01256
name: configuration interaction
def: "The mixing of many-electron wavefunctions constructed from different electronic configurations to obtain an improved many-electron state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01258
name: conformation
def: "The spatial arrangement of the atoms affording distinction between stereoisomers which can be interconverted by rotations about formally single bonds. Some authorities extend the term to include inversion at trigonal pyramidal centres and other polytopal rearrangements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01259
name: conformational analysis
def: "The assessment of the relative energies \(or thermodynamic stabilities\)\, reactivities\, and physical properties of alternative conformations of a molecular entity\, usually by the application of qualitative or semi-quantitative rules or by semi-empirical calculations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01260
name: conformational disorder
def: "Structural disorder resulting from the statistical coexistence within the crystals of identical configurational units with different conformations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01262
name: conformer
def: "One of a set of stereoisomers\, each of which is characterized by a conformation corresponding to a distinct potential energy minimum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01264
name: congruent transition
def: "A transition in which the two-phase equilibrium of melting\, vaporization or allotropism of a compound involves phases of the same composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01265
name: coning and quartering
def: "The reduction in size of a granular or powdered sample by forming a conical heap which is spread out into a circular\, flat cake. The cake is divided radially into quarters and two opposite quarters are combined. The other two quarters are discarded. The process is repeated as many times as necessary to obtain the quantity desired for some final use \(e.g. as the laboratory sample or as the test sample\). If the process is performed only once\, coning and quartering is no more efficient than taking alternate portions and discarding the others."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01266
name: conjugate acid–base pair
def: "The Brønsted acid $\\ce\{BH^\{+\}\}$ formed on protonation of a base $\\ce\{B\}$ is called the conjugate acid of $\\ce\{B\}$\, and $\\ce\{B\}$ is the conjugate base of $\\ce\{BH^\{+\}\}$. \(The conjugate acid always carries one unit of positive charge more than the base\, but the absolute charges of the species are immaterial to the definition.\) For example\: the Brønsted acid $\\ce\{HCl\}$ and its conjugate base $\\ce\{Cl^\{−\}\}$ constitute a conjugate acid–base pair."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01267
name: conjugated system
def: "In the original meaning a conjugated system is a molecular entity whose structure may be represented as a system of alternating single and multiple bonds\: e.g. $\\ce\{CH2=CH–CH=CH2\}$\, $\\ce\{CH2=CH–C#N\}$. In such systems\, conjugation is the interaction of one p-orbital with another across an intervening $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ in such structures. \(In appropriate molecular entities d-orbitals may be involved.\) The term is also extended to the analogous interaction involving a p-orbital containing an unshared electron pair\, e.g. $\\ce\{\{\:\}Cl–CH=CH2\}$."
synonym: "conjugation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01268
name: conjugate solutions
def: "Two solutions that co-exist in equilibrium at a given temperature and pressure and\, at constant pressure \(temperature\)\, change their compositions and relative proportions with a variation of temperature \(pressure\). The term usually refers to two immiscible liquids\, but it is also applicable to two immiscible solid solutions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01270
name: conjugation
def: "The contact-dependent transfer of a part or all of its genome from one bacterial cell \(donor\) to another \(recipient\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01271
name: conjugation labelling
def: "Labelling of a substance by conjugation with a labelled molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01273
name: conjunctive name
def: "A name for assemblies of functionalized acyclic parent hydrides and cyclic systems implying the loss of an appropriate number of hydrogen atoms from each."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01274
name: connectivity
def: "In a chemical context\, the information content of a line formula\, but omitting any indication of bond multiplicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01276
name: consecutive reactions
def: "Reactions occurring in sequence\, such as\:  The overall process is said to occur by consecutive steps."
synonym: "consecutive steps" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01278
name: consignment
def: "A quantity of material transferred on one occasion and covered by a single set of shipping documents. It may consist of one or more lots or portions of lots. The presence of different lots in a consignment is important from the point of view of the sampling plan and the interpretation of the results of analysis. The term 'population' is used as the general term for the quantity of parent material being sampled when it is immaterial if the parent body is a consignment\, lot\, batch\, entity\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01279
name: consistency
def: "A general term to describe the property of a material by which it resists permanent change of shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01281
name: constituent
def: "Chemical species present in a system; often called a component\, although the term component has a more restricted meaning in physical chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01282
name: constitution
def: "The description of the identity and connectivity \(and corresponding bond multiplicities\) of the atoms in a molecular entity \(omitting any distinction arising from their spatial arrangement\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01283
name: constitutional heterogeneity
def: "The variation in constitution from molecule to molecule in polymers with molecules uniform with respect to elemental composition. An example is a polymer composed of linear and branched molecules; another example is a statistical copolymer comprising two isomeric constitutional units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01284
name: constitutional homosequence
def: "A constitutional sequence which contains constitutional units of only one species and in one sequential arrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01285
name: constitutional isomerism
def: "Isomerism between structures differing in constitution and described by different line formulae e.g. $\\ce\{CH3OCH3\}$ and $\\ce\{CH3CH2OH\}$ ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01286
name: constitutional repeating unit
def: "The smallest constitutional unit the repetition of which constitutes a regular macromolecule\, a regular oligomer molecule\, a regular block or a regular chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01287
name: constitutional sequence
def: "The whole or part of a chain comprising one or more species of constitutional unit\(s\) in defined sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01288
name: constitutional unit
def: "An atom or group of atoms \(with pendant atoms or groups\, if any\) comprising a part of the essential structure of a macromolecule\, an oligomer molecule\, a block or a chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01289
name: constitutive enzymes
def: "Enzymes which are produced constitutively by the cell under all physiological conditions. Therefore\, they are not controlled by induction or repression."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01290
name: contact angle
def: "When a liquid does not spread on a substrate \(usually a solid\)\, a contact angle \\\(\\left\(\\theta\\right\) \\\) is formed which is defined as the angle between two of the interfaces at the three-phase line of contact. It must always be stated which interfaces are used to define \\\(\\theta\\\). It is often necessary to distinguish between the 'advancing contact angle' \\\(\(\\theta _\{\\rm\{a\}\}\) \\\)\, the 'receding contact angle' \\\(\(\\theta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}\) \\\) and the 'equilibrium contact angle' \\\(\(\\theta _\{\\rm\{e\}\}\) \\\). When \\\(\(\\theta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}\) \neq \(\\theta _\{\\rm\{a\}\}\) \\\) the system is said to exhibit contact angle hysteresis"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01291
name: contact corrosion
def: "The enhancement of the corrosion rate of the material with the more negative corrosion cell resulting from the contact between different electron-conducting phases."
synonym: "galvanic corrosion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01293
name: contact potential difference
def: "The electric potential difference between one point in the vacuum close to the surface of M1 and another point in the vacuum close to the surface of M2\, where M1 and M2 are two uncharged metals brought into contact."
synonym: "Volta potential difference" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01295
name: contamination
def: "Carry-over from a preceding sample probe into a following specimen cup\, which will influence not just one result\, but all assays on that specimen\, or with that reagent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01297
name: continuity inversion
def: "A change in the mutual dispersion of two phases in contact."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01298
name: continuous analyser
def: "An analyser in which subassemblies operate continuously."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01300
name: continuous-flow cell
def: "A cell which allows liquid \(or gaseous\) samples to pass through the cell continuously while absorption measurements are made."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01301
name: continuous flow enthalpimetry
def: "A term used to describe methods wherein a reagent is continuously fed into a flowing analyte stream and the temperature difference is measured before and after a reactor \(mixing chamber\). Alternatively\, the temperature may be measured in a differential manner between a reference and a reactor chamber. The analyte concentration is directly proportional to the measured temperature difference. Synonymous with continuous flow enthalpimetric analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01302
name: continuous indication analyser
def: "An analysis with a permanent indication related to the sample concentration. To obtain a continuous indication\, the sampling and measuring cell need to be continuous. A time lag may exist between sampling and indication of measured concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01304
name: continuous measuring cell
def: "A measuring cell which operates continuously."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01305
name: continuous operation
def: "The operation of an analytical instrument in such a way that no analytical procedure needs to be completed on any sample before the next procedure can be started."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01306
name: continuous precipitation
def: "A diffusional reaction in a multi-component system in which atoms are transported to the growing nuclei by diffusion over relatively large distances in the parent phase and during which the mean composition of the parent phase changes continuously towards its equilibrium value. Synonymous with nucleation and growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01308
name: contour length
def: "The maximum end-to-end distance of a linear polymer chain. For a single-strand polymer molecule\, this usually means the end-to-end distance of the chain extended to the all-transconformation. For chains with complex structure\, only an approximate value of the contour length may be accessible. The sum of the lengths of all skeletal bonds of a single-strand polymer molecule is occasionally termed 'contour length'. This use of this term is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01309
name: contributing structure
def: "The definition is based on the valence-bond formulation of the quantum mechanical idea of the wavefunction of a molecule as composed of a linear combination of wavefunctions\, each representative of a formula containing bonds that are only single\, double or triple with a particular pairing of electron spins. Each such formula represents a contributing structure\, also called 'resonance structure' to the total wavefunction\, and the degree to which each contributes is indicated by the square of its coefficient in the linear combination. The contributing structures\, also called 'canonical forms'\, themselves thus have a purely formal significance\: they are the components from which wavefunctions can be built. Structures may be covalent \(or non-polar\) or ionic \(or polar\). The representation is frequently kept qualitative so that we speak of important or major contributing structures and minor contributing structures."
synonym: "canonical form" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01310
name: controlled atmosphere
def: "A synthetic gaseous sample of pure air which may contain carefully determined amounts of certain contaminants; this may be used as a standard for the calibration of analytical techniques\, as a simulated environment for the study of biological responses\, or for other purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01311
name: control material
def: "A material to be used for the assessment of the performance of an analytical procedure or part thereof."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01312
name: control sample
def: "The test portion or test solution used for assessment of the performance of an analytical procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01313
name: convection
def: "Vertical motion of the air induced by the expansion of the air heated by the earth's surface and its resulting buoyancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01314
name: convenience sample
def: "A sample chosen on the basis of accessibility\, expediency\, cost\, efficiency or other reason not directly concerned with sampling parameters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01316
name: conventional true value
def: "Value attributed to a particular quantity and accepted\, sometimes by convention\, as having an uncertainty appropriate for a given purpose. Examples are the CODATA recommended values of fundamental physical constants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01317
name: conversion electron
def: "Electron ejected from the atom in the process of internal conversion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01319
name: conversion spectrum
def: "A plot of a quantity related to the absorption \(absorbance\, cross section\, etc.\) multiplied by the quantum yield for the considered process against a suitable measure of photon energy\, such as frequency\, \\\(\nu \\\)\, wavenumber\, \\\(\\sigma \\\)\, or wavelength\, \\\(\\lambda \\\)\, e.g. the conversion cross section\, \\\(\\sigma \\varphi \\\)\, has the SI unit \\\(\\mathrm\{m\}^\{2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01321
name: co-oligomer
def: "An oligomer derived from more than one species of monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01322
name: co-oligomerization
def: "Oligomerization in which a co-oligomer is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01324
name: cooperative transition
def: "A transition that involves a simultaneous\, collective displacement or change of state of the atoms and/or electrons in the entire system. Examples\: An order–disorder transition of atoms or electrons\, as in an alloy\, a ferromagnet or superconductor; a Jahn–Teller or ferroic transition; a martensitic transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01325
name: cooperativity
def: "Interaction between the substrate binding sites of an allosteric enzymes. Binding of a substrate molecule to one binding site changes the affinity of the binding sites on the other subunits \(cf. allosteric enzymes\) to the substrate by induction of a conformation change at the other binding sites. Cooperative enzymes typically display a sigmoid \(S-shaped\) plot of the reaction rate against substrate concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01329
name: coordination
def: "The formation of a covalent bond\, the two shared electrons of which have come from only one of the two parts of the molecular entity linked by it\, as in the reaction of a Lewis acid and a Lewis base to form a Lewis adduct; alternatively\, the bonding formed in this way. In the former sense\, it is the reverse of unimolecular heterolysis. 'Coordinate covalence' and 'coordinate link' are synonymous \(obsolescent\) terms. The synonym 'dative bond' is obsolete. \(The origin of the bonding electrons has by itself no bearing on the character of the bond formed. Thus\, the formation of methyl chloride from a methyl cation and a chloride ion involves coordination; the resultant bond obviously differs in no way from the $\\ce\{C–Cl\}$ bond in methyl chloride formed by any other path\, e.g. by colligation of a methyl radical and a chlorine atom.\) The term is also used to describe the number of ligands around a central atom without necessarily implying two-electron bonds."
synonym: "coordinate covalence" []
synonym: "coordinate link" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01330
name: coordination entity
def: "An assembly consisting of a central atom \(usually metallic\) to which is attached a surrounding array of other groups of atoms \(ligands\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01331
name: coordination number
def: "The coordination number of a specified atom in a chemical species is the number of other atoms directly linked to that specified atom. For example\, the coordination number of carbon in methane is four\, and it is five in protonated methane\, $\\ce\{CH5^\{+\}$. \(The term is used in a different sense in the crystallographic description of ionic crystals.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01332
name: coordination polyhedron
def: "In a coordination entity\, the solid figure defined by the positions of the ligand atoms directly attached to the central atom."
synonym: "polygon" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01333
name: coordinatively saturated complex
def: "A transition metal complex that has formally 18 outer shell electrons at the central metal atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01334
name: coordinatively unsaturated complex
def: "A transition metal complex that possesses fewer ligands than exist in the coordinatively saturated complex. These complexes usually have fewer than 18 outer shell electrons at the central metal atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01335
name: copolymer
def: "A polymer derived from more than one species of monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01336
name: copolymerization
def: "Polymerization in which a copolymer is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01337
name: copolymer micelle
def: "A micelle formed by one or more block or graft copolymer molecules in a selective solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01338
name: copper vapour laser
def: "A pulsed source of coherent radiation emitting at \\\(578.2\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) and \\\(510.5\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) from excited copper atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01339
name: coprecipitation
def: "The simultaneous precipitation of a normally soluble component with a macro-component from the same solution by the formation of mixed crystals\, by adsorption\, occlusion or mechanical entrapment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01340
name: copy number
def: "The number of copies of a plasmid or a gene within a cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01341
name: core atom
def: "The primary reference atom in a mechanistic change; usually either of the two atoms of a transformed multiple bond or the single atom at which substitution occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01343
name: corrected emission spectrum
def: "Obtained after correcting for instrumental and sample effects and usually represented by a graph of \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} _\{\\lambda\}\\\) against wavelength\, where \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} _\{\\lambda\}\\\) is the spectral \(radiant\) energy flux."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01344
name: corrected excitation spectrum
def: "Obtained if the photon flux incident on the sample is held constant. If the solution is sufficiently dilute that the fraction of the exciting radiation absorbed is proportional to the absorption coefficient of the analyte\, and if the quantum yield is independent of the exciting wavelength\, the corrected excitation spectrum will be identical in shape to the absorption spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01345
name: corrected retention volume
def: "The corrected retention volume\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{0\}\\\)\, is given by \\\[V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{0\}=j\\ V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\] where \\\(j\\\) is the pressure gradient correction factor for a homogeneously filled column of constant diameter and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\) is the retention volume. This quantity is of limited use because it is influenced by the volumes of the sample injector and detector\, as well as the interstitial volume of the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01346
name: correlation analysis
def: "The use of empirical correlations relating one body of experimental data to another\, with the objective of finding quantitative estimates of the factors underlying the phenomena involved. Correlation analysis in organic chemistry often uses linear free-energy relations for rates or equilibria of reactions\, but the term also embraces similar analysis of physical \(most commonly spectroscopic\) properties and of biological activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01347
name: correlation coefficient
def: "A measure of the degree of interrelationship which exists between two measured quantities\, \\\(x\\\) and \\\(y\\\); the correlation coefficient \(\\\(r\\\)\) is defined by the following relation\: \\\[r=\\frac\{\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni=1\n\\end\{array\}\}^\{n\}\(x_\{i\}- \\overline\{x\}\)\\ \(y_\{i\}- \\overline\{y\}\)\}\{\\sqrt\{\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni=1\n\\end\{array\}\}^\{n\}\(x_\{i\}- \\overline\{x\}\)^\{2\}\\ \\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni=1\n\\end\{array\}\}^\{n\}\(y_\{i\}- \\overline\{y\}\)^\{2\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(x_\{i\}\\\) and \\\(y_\{i\}\\\) are the measured values in the \\\(i\\\)th experiment of \\\(n\\\) total experiments\, \\\(\\overline\{x\}\\\) and \\\(\\overline\{y\}\\\) are the arithmetic means of \\\(x_\{i\}\\\) and \\\(y_\{i\}\\\)\: \\\[\\overline\{x\}=\\frac\{\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni=1\n\\end\{array\}\}^\{n\}x_\{i\}\}\{n\}\\\] \(similar expression for \\\(\\overline\{y\}\\\)\). The linear correlation coefficient indicates the degree to which two quantities are linearly related. If \\\(x=a\\ y\\\) is followed then \\\(r=1\\\)\, and departures from this relationship decrease \\\(r\\\); if interpretations of data based on the linear correlation coefficient are to be made\, one should consult a book on statistics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01348
name: correlation diagram
def: "A diagram which shows the relative energies of orbitals\, configurations\, valence bond structures\, or states of reactants and products of a reaction\, as a function of the molecular geometry\, or another suitable parameter. An example involves the interpolation between the energies obtained for the united atoms and the values for the separated atoms limits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01349
name: correlation energy
def: "The difference between the Hartree–Fock energy calculated for a system and the exact non-relativistic energy of that system. The correlation energy arises from the approximate representation of the electron-electron repulsions in the Hartree–Fock method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01350
name: corrinoids
def: "Derivatives of the corrin nucleus\, which contains four reduced or partly reduced pyrrole rings joined in a macrocycle by three  groups and one direct carbon–carbon bond linking $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ positions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01351
name: corrosion
def: "An irreversible interfacial reaction of a material \(metal\, ceramic\, polymer\) with its environment which results in consumption of the material or in dissolution into the material of a component of the environment. Often\, but not necessarily\, corrosion results in effects detrimental to the usage of the material considered. Exclusively physical or mechanical processes such as melting or evaporation\, abrasion or mechanical fracture are not included in the term corrosion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01352
name: corrosion cell
def: "A galvanic cell resulting from inhomogeneities in the material or in its environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01357
name: corrosion potential
def: "The electrode potential spontaneously acquired by a corroding material in a particular environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01358
name: corrosion rate
def: "The amount of substance transferred per unit time at a specified surface. Using Faraday's law\, the corrosion rate\, \\\(\nu_\{\\mathrm\{cor\}\}\\\)\, can be formally expressed as an electric current which at the corrosion potential is called the corrosion current\, \\\(I_\{\\mathrm\{cor\}\}\\\)\, e.g. for the anodic dissolution of one component of a material with \\\(\nu_\{\\mathrm\{cor\}\}\\\) in \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1\}\\\) and \\\(I_\{\\mathrm\{cor\}\}\\\) in \\\(\\mathrm\{A\}\\\) one obtains \\\(I_\{\\mathrm\{cor\}\}=n\\ F\\ \nu_\{\\mathrm\{cor\}\}\\\)\, \\\(n\\\) being the charge number of the electrode reaction and \\\(F\\\) the Faraday constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01361
name: co-solvency
def: "The dissolution of a polymer in a solvent comprising more than one component\, each component of which by itself is a non-solvent for the polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01364
name: cotectic
def: "The conditions of pressure\, temperature and composition under which two or more solid phases crystallize at the same time from a single liquid over a finite range of decreasing temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01365
name: coulomb
def: "SI derived unit of electric charge\, \\\(\\rm\{C\} = \\rm\{A\}\\cdot \\rm\{s\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01366
name: coulomb integral
def: "Integral over space of the type \\\(\\int \\psi _\{r\}\\ast \\ \\hat\{H\}\\ \\psi _\{r\}\\ \\rm\{d\}\\tau \\\) where \\\(\\hat\{H\}\\\) is the hamiltonian operator containing electrostatic potential energy terms and \\\(\\psi \\\) is a one-electron wavefunction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01367
name: coulometric detection method
def: "A detection method in which the current is directly proportional to the flow rate of the substance involved in the electrochemical reaction\, and the amount of charge which flows is proportional to the amount of substances taking part in the reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01369
name: coumarins
def: "2H-Chromen-2-one \(older name 1\,2-benzopyrone\)\, trivially named coumarin\, and its derivatives formed by substitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01370
name: count
def: "1. Information corresponding to a pulse processed for counting.2. Number of pulses recorded during a measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01371
name: counter-ions
def: "The mobile exchangeable ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01373
name: counter tube
def: "Radiation detector consisting of a gas-filled tube or valve whose gas amplification is much greater than one\, and in which the individual ionizing events give rise to discrete electrical pulses. Often an expression is added indicating the geometry \(e.g. end window\)\, composition of the gas \(e.g. helium\) or the physical process for its operation \(e.g. proton recoil\, fission\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01377
name: counting efficiency
def: "The ratio between the number of particles or photons counted with a radiation counter and the number of particles or photons of the same type and energy emitted by the radiation source."
synonym: "efficiency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01378
name: counting loss
def: "A reduction of the counting rate resulting from phenomena such as the resolving time or the dead time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01379
name: counting rate
def: "The number of counts occuring in unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01380
name: coupled reaction
def: "A reaction which follows the \(slower\) reaction of kinetic interest\, so as to provide means of monitoring the formation of a reaction product. This reaction is sometimes referred to as the indicator reaction."
synonym: "indicator reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01381
name: coupled simultaneous techniques
def: "The application of two or more techniques to the same sample when the two instruments involved are connected through an interface\, e.g. simultaneous thermal analysis and mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01383
name: coupling constant
def: "A quantitative measure for nuclear spin-spin\, nuclear-electron \(hyperfine coupling\) and electron-electron \(fine coupling in EPR\) coupling in magnetic resonance spectroscopy. The 'indirect' or scalar NMR coupling constants are in a first approximation independent of the external magnetic field and are expressed in \\\(\\mathrm\{Hz\}\\\)."
synonym: "spin-spin coupling constant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01384
name: covalent bond
def: "A region of relatively high electron density between nuclei which arises at least partly from sharing of electrons and gives rise to an attractive force and characteristic internuclear distance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01386
name: Cox–Yates equation
def: "A modification of the Bunnett–Olsen equation of the form\: \\\[\\log_\{10\}\(\\frac\{\[\\ce\{SH^\{+\}\}\]\}\{\[\\ce\{S\}\]\}\) - \\log_\{10\}\[\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}\] = m^\{*\}X + \\pu\{p\}K_\{\\ce\{SH\}^\{+\}\}\\\] is the activity function \\\[\\log_\{10\}\(\\frac\{\\gamma_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\ \\gamma_\{\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\}\}\{\\gamma_\{\\rm\{SH\}^\{+\}\}\}\)\\\] for an arbitrary reference base. The function \\\(X\\\) is called the excess acidity because it gives a measure of the difference between the acidity of a solution and that of an ideal solution of the same concentration. In practice \\\[X = -\(\\rm\{H\}_\{0\} + \\log_\{10\}\[\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\]\)\\\] and \\\[m^\{*\} = 1 - \\mathit\{\\Phi\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01387
name: cracking
def: "The thermal or catalytic decomposition of a compound such as a hydrocarbon into chemical species of smaller molecular weight."
synonym: "catalytic cracking" []
synonym: "hydrocarbon cracking" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01388
name: cream
def: "A highly concentrated emulsion formed by creaming of a dilute emulsion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01389
name: creaming
def: "The macroscopic separation of a dilute emulsion into a highly concentrated emulsion\, in which interglobular contact is important\, and a continuous phase under the action of gravity or a centrifugal field. This separation usually occurs upward\, but the term may still be applied if the relative densities of the dispersed and continuous phases are such that the concentrated emulsion settles downward."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01390
name: cream volume
def: "The volume of cream formed in an emulsion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01391
name: cresols
def: "The monomethylphenols and their derivatives formed by substitution on the ring with substituents other than $\\ce\{–OH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01393
name: critical energy
def: "A chemical reaction cannot occur\, except by quantum-mechanical tunnelling unless the total energy available is greater than a certain energy\, which is known as the critical energy\, or the threshold energy."
synonym: "threshold energy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01394
name: critical excitation energy
def: "Minimum energy to excite a specific analytical signal. The term is most frequently used in connection with the generation of X-ray spectra\, designating the ionization \(binding\) energy of the orbital on which the transition ends."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01395
name: critical micelle concentration
def: "There is a relatively small range of concentrations separating the limit below which virtually no micelle are detected and the limit above which virtually all additional surfactant molecules form micelles. Many properties of surfactant solutions\, if plotted against the concentration\, appear to change at a different rate above and below this range. By extrapolating the loci of such a property above and below this range until they intersect\, a value may be obtained known as the critical micellization concentration \(critical micelle concentration\)\, symbol \\\(c_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\)\, abbreviation cmc \(or c.m.c.\). As values obtained using different properties are not quite identical\, the method by which the cmc is determined should be clearly stated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01396
name: critical point
def: "The temperature and pressure at which the liquid and vapour intensive properties \(density\, heat capacity\, etc.\) become equal. It is the highest temperature \(critical temperature\) and pressure \(critical pressure\) at which both a gaseous and a liquid phase of a given compound can coexist."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01397
name: critical pressure
def: "The minimum pressure which would suffice to liquefy a substance at its critical temperature. Above the critical pressure\, increasing the temperature will not cause a fluid to vaporize to give a two-phase system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01400
name: critical solution point
def: "The point\, with coordinates critical solution temperatures and critical composition\, on a temperature-composition phase diagram at which the distinction between co-existent phases vanishes. In solid-solid\, solid-liquid and liquid-liquid systems both upper and lower critical solution temperatures and corresponding 'critical solution compositions' can occur. Synonymous with consolute point."
synonym: "consolute point" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01402
name: critical temperature
def: "That temperature\, characteristic of each gas\, above which it is not possible to liquefy a given gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01403
name: critical thickness of a film
def: "A film often thins gradually to a thickness at which it either ruptures or converts abruptly to an equilibrium film. This thickness is sometimes well enough defined statistically to be considered a critical thickness\, \\\(t_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) or \\\(h_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01404
name: cross-conjugation
def: "In a system $\\ce\{XC6H4GY\}$ this is conjugation involving the substituent $\\ce\{X\}$\, the benzene ring and the side-chain connective-plus-reaction site $\\ce\{GY\}$\, i.e. either $\\ce\{X\}$ is a $+R$ group and $\\ce\{GY\}$ is a $-R$ group\, or $\\ce\{X\}$ is a $-R$ group and $\\ce\{GY\}$ is a $+R$ group. In Hammett correlations this situation can lead to the need to apply exalted substituent constants \\\(\\sigma ^\{+\}\\\) or \\\(\\sigma ^\{-\}\\\)\, respectively\, as in electrophilic or nucleophilic aromatic substitution\, respectively. The term 'through resonance' is synonymous. Cross conjugation has also been used to describe the interactions occurring in 2-phenylallyl and similar systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01405
name: crossed electric and magnetic fields
def: "Electric and magnetic fields with the electric field direction at right angles to the magnetic field direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01407
name: cross-flow filtration
def: "Method of operating a filtration device where retained fluid is circulated over the membrane \(filter\) surface thus preventing undue build-up of filtered material on membrane \(filter\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01408
name: crossing over
def: "The usually reciprocal exchange of genetic material between chromosomes; part of natural genetic recombination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01409
name: crosslink
def: "A small region in a macromolecule from which at least four chains emanate\, and formed by reactions involving sites or groups on existing macromolecules or by interactions between existing macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01410
name: cross-over concentration
def: "The concentration range at which the sum of the volumes of the domains occupied by the solute molecules or particles in solution is approximately equal to the total volume of that solution. The term 'domain' refers to the smallest convex body that contains the molecule or particle in its average shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01411
name: cross reaction
def: "The ability of substances other than the analyte to bind to the binding reagent and the ability of substances other than the binding reagent to bind the analyte in competitive binding assays."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01420
name: crowding
def: "The displacement of an impurity from an extract phase by contact with a solution containing the main extractable solute. The main solute need not be present in a pure solution but should have a higher distribution ratio than the impurities present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01421
name: crown
def: "A molecular entity comprising a monocyclic ligand assembly that contains three or more binding sites held together by covalent bonds and capable of binding a guest in a central \(or nearly central\) position. The adducts formed are sometimes known as 'coronates'. The best known members of this group are macrocyclic polyethers\, such as '18-crown-6'\, containing several repeating units $\\ce\{–CR2–CR2O–\}$ \(where $\\ce\{R\}$ is most commonly $\\ce\{H\}$\)\, and known as crown ethers."
synonym: "coronands" []
synonym: "coronates" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01422
name: crown conformation
def: "A conformation of a saturated cyclic molecular entity\, containing an even number \(≥8\) of atoms in the ring\, in which these atoms lie alternately in each of two parallel planes and are symmetrically equivalent \(\\\(D_\{4\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) for cyclooctane\, \\\(D_\{5\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) for cyclodecane etc\). It is analogous to the chair conformation of cyclohexane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01423
name: crud
def: "A deposit or emulsion at the interface between two partially settled phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01424
name: cryogenic
def: "Low temperature processes\, apparatus\, etc.; usually applied to systems operated at the temperature of liquid nitrogen\, helium or other condensed gas which boils at a very low temperature \(at atmospheric pressure\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01425
name: cryogenic sampling
def: "The collection of trace compounds from gaseous media by co-condensation with a major constituent \(e.g. water vapour\, $\\ce\{CO2\}$\, $\\ce\{N2\}$\, $\\ce\{Ar\}$\) of the matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01426
name: cryptand
def: "A molecular entity comprising a cyclic or polycyclic assembly of binding sites that contains three or more binding sites held together by covalent bonds\, and which defines a molecular cavity in such a way as to bind \(and thus 'hide' in the cavity\) another molecular entity\, the guest \(a cation\, an anion or a neutral species\)\, more strongly than do the separate parts of the assembly \(at the same total concentration of binding sites\). The adduct thus formed is called a 'cryptate'. The term is usually restricted to bicyclic or oligocyclic molecular entities. Example\:  Corresponding monocyclic ligand assemblies crowns are sometimes included in this group\, if they can be considered to define a cavity in which a guest can hide. The terms 'podand' and 'spherand' are used for certain specific ligand assemblies. Coplanar cyclic polydentate ligands\, such as porphyrins\, are not normally regarded as cryptands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01428
name: crystal diffraction spectrometer
def: "A spectrometer\, in which diffraction by a crystal is used to obtain the energy spectra of electromagnetic radiation as well as of slow neutrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01429
name: crystal field splitting
def: "The removal of a degeneracy of the energy levels of molecular entities or ions due to the lower site symmetry created by a crystalline environment. This term is sometimes incorrectly used synonymously with the term ligand field splitting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01430
name: crystal laser
def: "A solid state laser in which the active medium is composed of ionic species supported in a crystalline host. The first laser used a ruby crystal in which chromium ions as the active medium were supported in an aluminium oxide \(sapphire\) crystal. One of the most common crystal lasers involves neodymium atoms in a yttrium aluminium garnet host crystal \(the YAG\:Nd laser\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01431
name: crystalline electrodes
def: "Electrodes which contain mobile ions of one sign and fixed sites of opposite sign. They may be homogeneous or heterogeneous.  Homogeneous membrane electrodes are ion-selective electrodes in which the membrane is a crystalline material prepared from either a single compound or a homogeneous mixture of compounds \(i.e. $\\ce\{Ag2S\}$\, $\\ce\{AgI\}$/$\\ce\{Ag2S\}$\).Heterogeneous membrane electrodes are formed when an active substance\, or mixture of active substances\, is mixed with an inert matrix\, such as silicone rubber or PVC\, or placed on hydrophobized graphite or conducting epoxy\, to form the heterogeneous sensing membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01432
name: crystalline polymer
def: "A polymer showing crystallinity. One or two-dimensional order leads to mesophase structure. The range of order may be as small as about \\\(2\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) in one \(or more\) crystallographic direction\(s\) and is usually below \\\(50\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) in at least one direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01433
name: crystallinity
def: "The presence of three-dimensional order on the level of atomic dimensions. Crystallinity may be detected by diffraction techniques\, heat-of-fusion measurements\, etc. The amount of disorder within the crystalline region is not incompatible with this concept."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01434
name: crystallization
def: "The formation of a crystalline solid from a solution\, melt vapour or a different solid phase\, generally by the lowering of the temperature or by evaporation of a solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01435
name: CT
def: "Abbreviation for charge-transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01437
name: cumulative double bonds
def: "Those present in a chain in which at least three contiguous carbon atoms are joined by double bonds; non-cumulative double bonds comprise every other arrangement of two or more double bonds in a single structure. The generic name 'cumulene' is given to compounds containing three or more cumulative double bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01438
name: cumulative fission yield
def: "The fraction of fissions which have resulted in the production of a nuclide either directly or indirectly\, up to a specified time. If no time is specified\, the yield is considered to be the asymptotic value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01439
name: cumulative sample
def: "A sample collected over an extended period of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01440
name: cumulenes
def: "Hydrocarbons \(and by extension\, derivatives formed by substitution\) having three or more cumulative double bonds\, e.g. $\\ce\{R2C=C=C=CR2\}$ ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01441
name: cumulonimbus cloud
def: "Heavy masses of dense cloud whose cumuliform summits rise in the forms of towers\, the upper parts having a fibrous texture and often spreading out into the shape of an anvil; these clouds generally produce showers of rain and sometimes of snow\, hail or soft hail\, and often develop into thunderstorms; up to \\\(12\\ 000\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\); summits may be as cold as \\\(\\pu\{-50 ^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\); strong convective motions with vertical velocities of \\\(3\\\) to greater than \\\(30\\ \\rm\{m s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01442
name: cumulus cloud
def: "Detached\, dense clouds with a dome-shaped upper surface with sharp-edged\, rounded protuberances and a nearly horizontal base; \\\(600\{-\}6000\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\) or more; convective motion with rising\, large bubbles of warm air with vertical speeds of \\\(1\{-\}5\\ \\rm\{m s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01443
name: cupola
def: "A vertical shaft furnace used for melting metals; the melting of ores is accomplished in a blast furnace."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01444
name: curie
def: "Non-SI unit of radioactivity\, \\\(\\rm\{Ci\}\\approx 3.7\\times 10^\{10\}\\ \\rm\{Bq\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01452
name: current density
def: "The current density \\\(j_\{\\mathbf\{B\}\}\\\) of a species B in a given point of the solution is obtained by multiplying the flux density of that species at the given point by the Faraday constant \\\(F\\\) and by the charge number \\\(z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) of the species\: \\\[j_\{\\mathbf\{B\}\} = z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ F\\ N_\{\\mathbf\{B\}\}\\\] where \\\(j_\{\\mathbf\{B\}\}\\\) is a vector which indicates the direction in which the charges transported by the species B flow and which gives the number of these charges going through a plane oriented perpendicular to the vector\, divided by time and by area\, and \\\(N_\{\\mathbf\{B\}\}\\\) is the flux density of a minor constituent of the solution with respect to a fixed frame of reference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01456
name: current distribution
def: "The ratio of current density at a point X on an interface to the average current density \(\\\(\\frac\{j_\{\\rm\{x\}\}\}\{j\}\\\)\) is called the 'relative local current density'. The current distribution is described by the function \\\(\\frac\{j_\{\\rm\{x\}\}\}\{j\} = f\(x\)\\\) \(or more generally\, \\\(\\frac\{j_\{\\rm\{x\}\}\}\{j\} = f\(x\,\\\,y\,\\\,z\)\\\) where \\\(x\\\) or \\\(\(x\,\\\,y\,\\\,z\)\\\) are the coordinates of the points of the electrode-solution interface. The 'primary current distribution' is that which establishes itself when the influence of overpotential is negligible. The 'secondary current distribution' is that which establishes itself when the influence of the overpotential cannot be neglected but concentration overpotential is negligible. The secondary distribution is often described in terms of dimensionless numbers of the form \\\[\\mathrm\{Wa\}=\\frac\{\\kappa \}\{l\}\\ \\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}\\eta \}\{\\mathrm\{d\}j\}\\\] where \\\(\\kappa \\\) is the conductivity of the solution\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}\\eta \}\{\\rm\{d\}j\}\\\) the slope of the overpotential-current curve under the above conditions and \\\(l\\\) a characteristic length of the system\, for instance the radius of a disc electrode. \\\(\\mathrm\{Wa\}\\\) is the 'Wagner number'. It is a quantity which determines the throwing power and characterizes the equalizing influence of overpotential on the current distribution. In electroplating the 'throwing power' is qualitatively defined as 'the ability of a solution to deposit metal uniformly upon a cathode of irregular shape'. The 'tertiary current distribution' is that which establishes itself when the influence of the overpotential \(including concentration overpotential\) cannot be neglected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01458
name: current efficiency
def: "If several reactions take place simultaneously at the electrode a partial electrode current density \(c.d.\) \\\(j_\{k\}\\\) can be assigned to each reaction. It is given by the stoichiometry of the reaction and by the amount of substance of B reacting \(per unit time and per unit electrode area\) in the reaction considered. The current efficiency of reaction \\\(k\\\)\, \\\(\\varepsilon_\{k\}\\\) is defined as the ratio of \\\(j_\{k\}\\\) to the total c.d.\: \\\[\\varepsilon_\{k\}=\\frac\{j_\{k\}\}\{\\sum_\{m\} j_\{m\}\}\\\] Note that \\\(\\varepsilon_\{k\}\\\) may be larger than one if cathodic and anodic reactions take place simultaneously at the same electrode. However\, \\\(\\varepsilon_\{k\}\\\) still gives correctly the product yield\, which is the quantity of industrial interest. The product yield is the amount of substance of B produced per unit charge and is equal to \\\(\\frac\{\\varepsilon_\{k\}\\ \nu _\{B\,k\}\}\{n_\{k\}\\ F\}\\\) \(in the absence of a chemical reaction which is consecutive to the electrode reaction and which consumes or produces species B\). \\\(n_\{k\}\\\) is the charge number of electrode reaction \\\(k\\\). Note that in the case of simultaneous electrode reactions the distribution of the partial c.d. \\\(j_\{k\}\\\) may be different from that of the total c.d.\, i.e. the function \\\(\\frac\{\\left\(j_\{k\}\\right\)_\{\\rm\{x\}\}\}\{j\}=f_\{k\}\\left\(x\\right\)\\\) may be different from \\\(\\frac\{j_\{\\rm\{x\}\}\}\{j\} = f\(x\)\\\). In electroplating the term 'metal distribution' is sometimes used to designate the distribution \\\(f_\{k\}\\left\(x\\right\)\\\) of the partial c.d. for metal deposition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01480
name: Curtin–Hammett principle
def: "In a chemical reaction that yields one product \(X\) from one conformational isomer \(A'\) and a different product \(Y\) from another conformational isomer \(A''\) \(and provided these two isomers are rapidly interconvertible relative to the rate of product formation\, whereas the products do not undergo interconversion\) the product composition is not in direct proportion to the relative concentrations of the conformational isomers in the substrate; it is controlled only by the difference in standard free energies \(\\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}G\\\)\) of the respective transition states.  It is also true that the product composition is formally related to the relative concentrations of the conformational isomers A' and A'' \(i.e. the conformational equilibrium constant\) and the respective rate constants of their reactions; these parameters are generally — though not invariably — unknown. The diagram below represents the energetic situation for transformation of interconverting isomers A and A' into products X and Y."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01481
name: cut off
def: "The size of particles at which the retention efficiency of an instrument device drops below a specified value under defined conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01482
name: cut-off filter
def: "An optical device which only permits the transmission of radiation of wavelengths that are longer than or shorter than a specified wavelength. Usually\, the term refers to devices which transmit radiation of wavelengths longer than the specified wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01484
name: CW
def: "Abbreviation for continuous wave\, i.e. non-pulsed source of electromagnetic radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01485
name: cyanates
def: "Salts and esters of cyanic acid\, $\\ce\{HOC#N\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{KOCN\}$ potassium cyanate\, $\\ce\{PhOCN\}$ phenyl cyanate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01486
name: cyanides
def: "Salts and $C$-organyl derivatives of hydrogen cyanide\, $\\ce\{HC#N\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3C#N\}$ methyl cyanide \(acetonitrile\)\, $\\ce\{NaCN\}$ sodium cyanide\, $\\ce\{PhC\(=O\)CN\}$ benzoyl cyanide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01487
name: cyanine dyes
def: "Synthetic dyes with the general formula $\\ce\{R2N\[CH=CH\]_\{n\}CH=N^\{+\}R2 \}$ $\\ce\{R2N^\{+\}=CH\[CH=CH\]_\{n\}NR2\}$ \($n$ is a small number\) in which the nitrogen and part of the conjugated chain usually form part of a heterocyclic system\, such as imidazole\, pyridine\, pyrrole\, quinoline and thiazole\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01488
name: cyanogenic
def: "Compounds able to produce cyanide; examples are the cyanogenic glycosides such as amygdalin in peach and apricot stones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01489
name: cyanohydrins
def: "Alcohols substituted by a cyano group\, most commonly\, but not limited to\, examples having a cyano and a hydroxy group attached to the same carbon atom\, formally derived from aldehydes or ketones by the addition of hydrogen cyanide. An individual cyanohydrin can systematically be named as a hydroxy nitrile\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(CH3\)2C\(OH\)C#N\}$ 'acetone cyanohydrin' \(2-hydroxy-2-methylpropanenitrile\)\, $\\ce\{HOCH2CH2C#N\}$ 'ethylene cyanohydrin' \(3-hydroxypropanenitrile\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01490
name: cybotactic region
def: "That part of a solution in the vicinity of a solute molecule in which the ordering of the solvent molecules is modified by the presence of the solute molecule. The term solvent 'cosphere' of the solute has also been used."
synonym: "cosphere" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01492
name: cyclic acid anhydrides
def: "Acid anhydrides derived by loss of water between two oxoacid functions $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$ \($l \neq 0$\) \(carboxylic\, sulfonic\, etc.\) in the same molecule so as to close a ring. Oxo oxygen replacement analogues are included\, e.g."
synonym: "cyclic anhydrides" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01493
name: cyclitols
def: "Hydroxylated cycloalkanes containing at least three hydroxy groups\, each attached to a different ring carbon atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01494
name: cyclization
def: "Formation of a ring compound from a chain by formation of a new bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01495
name: cyclo-
def: "A prefix used in names to designate a ring structure\, usually italicized in inorganic\, but not in organic nomenclature. Examples\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01496
name: cycloaddition
def: "A reaction in which two or more unsaturated molecules \(or parts of the same molecule\) combine with the formation of a cyclic adduct in which there is a net reduction of the bond multiplicity. The following two systems of notations have been used for the more detailed specification of cycloadditions\, of which the second\, more recent system \[described under \(2\)\] is preferred\:   A\(i + j + ...\) cycloaddition is a reaction in which two or more molecules \(or parts of the same molecule\)\, respectively\, provide units of i\, j\, ... linearly connected atoms\: these units become joined at their respective termini by new $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ so as to form a cycle containing \(i + j + ...\) atoms. In this notation\, \(a\) a Diels–Alder reaction is a \(4+2\) cycloaddition\, \(b\) the initial reaction of ozone with an alkene is a \(3+2\) cycloaddition\, and \(c\) the reaction shown below is a \(2+2+2\) cycloaddition. \(N.B.\: parentheses \(...\) are used in the description based on numbers of atoms.\)   The symbolism \[i + j + ...\] for a cycloaddition identifies the numbers i\, j\, ... of electrons in the interacting units that participate in the transformation of reactants to products. In this notation the reaction \(a\) and \(b\) of the preceding paragraph would both be described as \[2+4\] cycloadditions\, and \(c\) as a \[2+2+2\] cycloaddition. The symbol a or s \(a = antarafacial\, s = suprafacial\) is often added \(usually as a subscript after the number to designate the stereochemistry of addition to each fragment. A subscript specifying the orbitals\, viz. $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\}$ \(sigma\, pi\) with their usual significance\) or n \(for an orbital associated with a single atom only\)\, may be added as a subscript before the number. Thus the normal Diels–Alder reaction is a \\\(\\left \[4_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\: +\\\: 2_\{\\rm\{s\}\} \\right \]\\\) or \\\(\\left \[_\{\\pi \}4_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\: +\\\: _\{\\pi \}2_\{\\rm\{s\}\} \\right \]\\\) cycloaddition\, whilst the reaction\:  would be a \\\(\\left \[14_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\:  +\\\: 2_\{\\rm\{s\}\} \\right \]\\\) or \\\(\\left \[_\{\\pi \}14_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\:  +\\\: _\{\\pi \}2_\{\\rm\{s\}\} \\right \]\\\) cycloaddition. \(N.B. Square brackets are used in the descriptions based on numbers of electrons.\) Cycloadditions may be pericyclic reactions or \(non-concerted stepwise reactions. The term 'dipolar cycloaddition' is used for cycloadditions of 1\,3-dipolar compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01497
name: cycloalkanes
def: "Saturated monocyclic hydrocarbons \(with or without side chains\)\, e.g. cyclobutane. Unsaturated monocyclic hydrocarbons having one endocyclic double or one triple bond are called cycloalkenes and cycloalkynes\, respectively. Those having more than one such multiple bond are cycloalkadienes\, cycloalkatrienes\, etc. The inclusive terms for any cyclic hydrocarbons having any number of such multiple bonds are cyclic olefins or cyclic acetylenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01498
name: cycloalkyl groups
def: "Univalent groups derived from cycloalkanes by removal of a hydrogen atom from a ring carbon atom\, e.g. 2-methylcyclopropyl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01500
name: cyclodextrins
def: "Cyclic oligoglucosides containing five to ca. ten glucose residues in which an enclosed tubular space allows reception of a guest molecule to form a clathrate. The synonymous term Schardinger dextrins is not recommended. \($\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}Cyclodextrin\}$ has six glucose residues; $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}Cyclodextrin\}$ has seven.\) Semi-systematically $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}cyclodextrin\}$ is called cyclomaltohexaose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01501
name: cycloelimination
def: "The reverse of cycloaddition. The term is preferred to the synonyms 'cycloreversion'\, 'retro-addition' and 'retrocycloaddition'."
synonym: "cycloreversion" []
synonym: "retro-addition" []
synonym: "retroaddition" []
synonym: "retrocycloaddition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01502
name: cyclohexadienyl cations
def: "A subclass of arenium ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01503
name: cyclone collector
def: "A dust\, grit or droplet separator utilizing essentially the centrifugal force derived from the motion of the gas. The flow of gases containing suspended particles into the device is transformed into a confined vortex from which centrifugal forces tend to drive the suspended particles to the wall of the cyclone body. The agglomerated particles are subsequently removed from the cyclone by gravitational action."
synonym: "cyclone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01504
name: cyclophanes
def: "The term originally applied to compounds having two p-phenylene groups held face to face by $\\ce\{–\[CH2\]_\{n\}\\bond\{–\}\}$ bridges. It now designates compounds having \(i\) mancude-ring systems\, or assemblies of mancude-ring systems\, and \(ii\) atoms and/or saturated or unsaturated chains as alternate components of a large ring. E.g.\(1\,4\)\(1\,4\)cyclophane \[or 1\(1\,4\)\,4\(1\,4\)-dibenzenacyclohexaphane\].  Many chemists include in this class any bridged aromatic system\, irrespective of the attachment positions of the bridge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01505
name: cyclopolymerization
def: "A polymerization in which the number of cyclic structures in the constitutional units of the resulting macromolecules is larger than in the monomer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01507
name: cyclosilazanes
def: "Compounds having rings of alternating silicon and nitrogen atoms\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01508
name: cyclosiloxanes
def: "Compounds having rings of alternating silicon and oxygen atoms\, e.g. 2\,2-dimethylcyclotrisiloxane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01509
name: cyclotron
def: "A particle accelerator in which the particles travel in a succession of semicircular orbits of increasing radii under the influence of a magnetic field and are accelerated at the beginning of each such orbit by traversing an electric field produced by a high-frequency generator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C01510
name: cytochromes
def: "Conjugated proteins containing haem as the prosthetic group and associated with electron transport and with redox processes. The terminal electron transport chain of oxidative respiration contains at least five different cytochromes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:C05438
name: σ-constant
def: "Specifically the substituent constant for meta- and for para-substituents in benzene derivatives as defined by Hammett on the basis of the ionization constant of a substituted benzoic acid in water at \\\(25\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, i.e. \\\(\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}\{K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}^\{0\}\}\)\\\)\, where \\\(K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) is the ionization constant of a m- or p-substituted benzoic acid and \\\(K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}^\{0\}\\\) that of benzoic acid itself. The term is also used as a collective description for related electronic substituent constants based on other standard reaction series\, of which\, \\\(\\sigma ^\{+\}\\\)\, \\\(\\sigma ^\{-\}\\\) and \\\(\\sigma ^\{0\}\\\) are typical; also constants which represent dissected electronic effects such as \\\(\\sigma _\{I\}\\\) and \\\(\\sigma _\{R\}\\\). For this purpose it might be better always to spell out the term in full\, i.e. as 'Hammett sigma constant'\, and restrict \\\(\\sigma \\\)-constants to the scale of substituent constants which is based on benzoic acid. A large positive \\\(\\sigma \\\)-value implies high electron-withdrawing power by inductive and/or resonance effect\, relative to H; a large negative \\\(\\sigma \\\)-value implies high electron-releasing power relative to H."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT01038
name: chemical species
def: "An ensemble of chemically identical molecular entities that can explore the same set of molecular energy levels on the time scale of the experiment. The term is applied equally to a set of chemically identical atomic or molecular structural units in a solid array. For example\, two conformational isomers may be interconverted sufficiently slowly to be detectable by separate NMR spectra and hence to be considered to be separate chemical species on a time scale governed by the radiofrequency of the spectrometer used. On the other hand\, in a slow chemical reaction the same mixture of conformers may behave as a single chemical species\, i.e. there is virtually complete equilibrium population of the total set of molecular energy levels belonging to the two conformers. Except where the context requires otherwise\, the term is taken to refer to a set of molecular entities containing isotopes in their natural abundance. The wording of the definition given in the first paragraph is intended to embrace both cases such as graphite\, sodium chloride or a surface oxide\, where the basic structural units may not be capable of isolated existence\, as well as those cases where they are. In common chemical usage generic and specific chemical names \(such as radical or hydroxide ion\) or chemical formulae refer either to a chemical species or to a molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT01101
name: clearance
def: "Product of the concentration of a component in an output system and the volume flow rate of that output system divided by the concentration of this component in the input system."
synonym: "volume rate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06757
name: chemical diffusion
def: "Diffusion under the influence of a gradient in chemical composition. In concentrated solid solutions\, e.g. $\\ce\{A_\{1-x\}B_\{x\}\}$\, or in diffusion couples\, the motion of one constituent causes a counterflow of the other constituent\(s\) or vacancies. In this case one can define a diffusion coefficient for the intermixing\, which is called the chemical diffusion coefficient or interdiffusion coefficient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06762
name: catalase
def: "A hemeprotein which catalyses the disproportionation of dihydrogen peroxide to $\\ce\{O2\}$\, and water; it also catalyses the oxidation of other compounds\, such as ethanol\, by dihydrogen peroxide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06769
name: cluster
def: "A number of metal centres grouped close together which can have direct metal bonding interactions or interactions through a bridging ligand\, but are not necessarily held together by these interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06770
name: crystal field
def: "The average static electric field experienced by an ion\, molecule or atom in a crystal generated by all the other surrounding atoms\, molecules\, or ions. The concept introduced by becquerel and developed by Bethe has been extended to refer to the field generated by the ligands \(anions or neutral species\) surrounding a transition metal ion in a coordination compound. With the understatement that some covalency may exist in the metal-ligand bond\, it is often referred to as ligand field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06777
name: circular dichroism
def: "A spectroscopic method which measures the difference in absorbance of left- and right-handed circularly polarised light by a material\, as a function of the wavelength. Most biological molecules\, including proteins and nucleic acids\, are chiral and show circular dichroism in their ultraviolet absorption bands\, which may be used as an indication of secondary structure. Metal centres that are bound to such molecules\, even if they have no inherent chirality\, usually exhibit CD in absorption bands associated with ligand-based or ligand-metal charge-transfer transitions. CD is frequently used in combination with absorption and MCD studies to assign electronic transitions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06814
name: carrier protein
def: "Protein to which a specific ligand or hapten is conjugated.Unlabeled protein introduced into an assay at relatively high concentrations which distributes in a fractionation process in the same manner as labeled protein analyte\, present in very low concentrations.Protein added to prevent non-specific interaction of reagents with surfaces\, sample components\, and each other.Protein found in cell membranes\, which facilitates transport of a ligand across the membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06815
name: ceiling value
def: "Airborne concentration of a potentially toxic substance which should never be exceeded in a worker’s breathing zone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06816
name: cell line
def: "Defined unique population of cells obtained by culture from a primary source through numerous generations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06817
name: chronic toxicity
def: "Adverse effects following chronic exposure.Effects which persist over a long period of time whether or not they occur immediately upon exposure or are delayed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06818
name: compartmental analysis
def: "Mathematical process leading to a model of transport of a substance in terms of compartments and rate constants\, usually taking the form \\\[C=A\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{-\\alpha\\ t\}+B\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{-\\beta \\ t\}\\rm\{...\}\\\] where each exponential term represents one experiment. \\\(C\\\) is the substance concentration; \\\(A\\\)\, \\\(B\\\)\,… are proportionality constants; \\\(\\alpha \\\)\, \\\(\\beta \\\)\,… are rate constants; and \\\(t\\\) is time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06819
name: congener
def: "One of two or more substances related to each other by origin\, structure\, or function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06820
name: critical study
def: "Investigation yielding the no observed adverse effect level that is used by the USEPA as the basis of the reference dose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06821
name: cytochrome P450
def: "Member of a superfamily of heme-containing monooxygenases involved in xenobiotic metabolism\, cholesterol biosynthesis\, and steroidogenesis\, in eukaryotic organisms found mainly in the endoplasmic reticulum and inner mitochondrial membrane of cells. ‘P450’ refers to a feature in the carbon monoxide absorption difference spectrum at \\\(450\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) caused by the presence of a thiolate in the axial position of the heme opposite to the carbon monoxide ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06850
name: crazing
def: "Formation of cavities\, when a polymer is stressed\, which contain load-bearing fibrils spanning the gap between the surfaces of each cavity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06857
name: clearing point
def: "The temperature at which the transition between the mesophase with the highest temperature range and the isotropic phase occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06859
name: chemical species
def: "Specific form of an element defined as to isotopic composition\, electronic or oxidation state\, and/or complex or molecular structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06860
name: clearance
def: "Volume of blood or plasma or mass of an organ effectively cleared of a substance by elimination \(metabolism and excretion\) divided by time of elimination.Note\:Total clearance is the sum of the clearances of each eliminating organ or tissue for a given substance.\(in pulmonary toxicology\) Volume or mass of lung cleared divided by time of elimination; used qualitatively to describe removal of any inhaled substance which deposits on the lining surface of the lung.\(in renal toxicology\) Quantification of the removal of a substance by the kidneys by the processes of filtration and secretion; clearance is calculated by relating the rate of renal excretion to the plasma concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06863
name: composite membrane
def: "Membrane having chemically or structurally distinct layers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06864
name: counter-current flow
def: "Flow through a membrane module in which the fluids on the upstream and downstream sides of the membrane move parallel to the membrane surface but in opposite directions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06916
name: continuous bed packing
def: "A column packing\, which is a single entity\, rather than being composed of individual particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06917
name: carbon loading
def: "Mass fraction of the packing material which is carbon. Usually taken as a guide to the extent of alkyl substitution on the surface. Usually reported as percentage carbon determined using elemental analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06920
name: chiral stationary phase
def: "A stationary phase which incorporates a chiral selector. If not a constituent of the stationary phase as a whole\, the chiral selector can be chemically bonded to \(chiral bonded stationary phase \) or immobilised onto the surface of a solid support or column wall \(chiral coated stationary phase\)\, or simply dissolved in the liquid stationary phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06930
name: chiral mobile phase
def: "A mobile phase containing a chiral selector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06931
name: chiral selector
def: "The chiral component of the separation system capable of interacting enantioselectively with the enantiomers to be separated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06937
name: convex isotherm
def: "Distribution isotherm\, the slope of which is continuously decreasing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06938
name: concave isotherm
def: "Distribution isotherm\, the slope of which is continuously increasing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06948
name: chemometrics
def: "Chemometrics is the application of statistics to the analysis of chemical data \(from organic\, analytical or medicinal chemistry\) and design of chemical experiments and simulations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06949
name: CLOGP values
def: "CLOGP values are calculated 1-octanol/water partition coefficients\, frequently used in structure-property correlation or quantitative structure-activity relationship \(SPC/QSAR\) studies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06950
name: cluster analysis
def: "Cluster analysis is the clustering\, or grouping\, of large data sets \(e.g.\, chemical and/or pharmacological data sets\) on the basis of similarity criteria for appropriately scaled variables that represent the data of interest. Similarity criteria \(distance based\, associative\, correlative\, probabilistic\) among the several clusters facilitate the recognition of patterns and reveal otherwise hidden structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06951
name: comparative molecular field analysis
def: "Comparative Molecular Field Analysis \(CoMFA\) is a 3D-QSAR method that uses statistical correlation techniques for the analysis of the quantitative relationship between the biological activity of a set of compounds with a special alignment\, and their three-dimensional electronic and steric properties. Other properties\, such as hydrophobicity and H-bonding\, can also be incorporated into the analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06952
name: computational chemistry
def: "Computational chemistry is a discipline using mathematical methods for the calculation of molecular properties or for the simulation of molecular behaviour. It also includes\, e.g.\, synthesis planning\, database searching\, combinatorial library manipulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06953
name: computer-assisted drug design
def: "Computer-assisted drug design involves all computer-assisted techniques used to discover\, design and optimize biologically active compounds with a putative use as drugs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06954
name: computer-assisted molecular design
def: "Computer-assisted molecular design involves all computer-assisted techniques used to discover\, design and optimize compounds with desired structure and properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06955
name: computer-assisted molecular modeling
def: "Computer-assisted molecular modeling is the investigation of molecular structures and properties using computational chemistry and graphical visualization techniques."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT06956
name: conformational analysis
def: "Conformational analysis consists of the exploration of energetically favourable spatial arrangements \(shapes\) of a molecule \(conformations\) using molecular mechanics\, molecular dynamics\, quantum chemical calculations or analysis of experimentally-determined structural data\, e.g\, NMR or crystal structures.Molecular mechanics and quantum chemical methods are employed to compute conformational energies\, whereas systematic and random searches\, Monte Carlo\, molecular dynamics\, and distance geometry are methods \(often combined with energy minimization procedures\) used to explore the conformational space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07009
name: chemical bond
def: "When forces acting between two atoms or groups of atoms lead to the formation of a stable independent molecular entity\, a chemical bond is considered to exist between these atoms or groups. The principal characteristic of a bond in a molecule is the existence of a region between the nuclei of constant potential contours that allows the potential energy to improve substantially by atomic contraction at the expense of only a small increase in kinetic energy. Not only directed covalent bonds characteristic of organic compounds\, but also bonds such as those existing between sodium cations and chloride anions in a crystal of sodium chloride or the bonds binding aluminium to six molecules of water in its environment\, and even weak bonds that link two molecules of $\\ce\{O2\}$ into $\\ce\{O4\}$\, are to be attributed to chemical bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07010
name: closed shell molecular systems
def: "Even-electron atomic or molecular systems whose electron configurations consist of doubly occupied orbitals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07011
name: concerted reaction
def: "A single-step reaction through which reactants are directly transformed into products\, i.e.\, without involvement of any intermediates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07012
name: conservation of orbital symmetry
def: "The orbital symmetry control of concerted reactions; this requires transformation of the molecular orbitals of reactants into those of products to proceed continuously by following a reaction path along which the symmetry of these orbitals remains unchanged. Reactions which adhere to this requirement are classified as symmetry-allowed reactions\, and those which do not as symmetry-forbidden reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07013
name: Coulomb repulsion
def: "The potential energy component corresponding to the electrostatic interaction between each pair of charged particles\: \\\[V=\\frac\{1\}\{4\\\: \\pi\\\: \\varepsilon_\{0\}\}\\ \\sum _\{i\}\\sum _\{j \\lt i\}e_\{i\}\\ e_\{j\}\\ \\Delta r_\{ij\}\\\] where \\\(\\varepsilon _\{0\}\\\) is the permittivity of a vacuum\, \\\(\\Delta r_\{ij\}\\\) is the distance between the two particles\, and \\\(e_\{i\}\\\) and \\\(e_\{j\}\\\) are the charges on particles \\\(i\\\) and \\\(j\\\). In molecular orbital theory\, the electrostatic repulsion between the two electrons occupying the orbitals \\\(\\Psi _\{i\}\\\) and \\\(\\Psi _\{j\}\\\). In the Hartree–Fock method\, the mean Coulomb repulsion is determined by the value of the coulomb integral \\\[J_\{ij\}=\\int \\int \\Psi _\{i\\rm\{*\}\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{1\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{i\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{1\}\\right\)\\ \\left\(\\frac\{e^\{2\}\}\{r_\{12\}\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{j\\rm\{*\}\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{2\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{j\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{2\}\\right\)  \\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\mathbf\{r\}_\{1\} \\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\mathbf\{r\}_\{2\} = \\lt ij|ij \\gt\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07014
name: coupled cluster method
def: "A method employed in ab initio quantum mechanical calculations providing for a higher-level treatment of electron correlation beyond MP4."
synonym: "CC method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07016
name: curve-crossing model
def: "Model of organic reactivity that generates a reaction profile from curves that describe the energies of reactant\, product\, and intermediate electronic configurations \(or\, alternatively\, reactant\, product and intermediate electronic states\) as a function of the reaction coordinate. The crossing reflects the electronic reorganization that accompanies the transformation of reactants and products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07134
name: chemical amplification
def: "Process consisting of a chemical reaction that generates a species that catalyzes another reaction and also the succeeding catalyzed reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07135
name: chemical modification
def: "Process by which at least one feature of the chemical constitution of a polymer is changed by chemical reaction\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07136
name: crosslinking
def: "Reaction involving sites or groups on existing macromolecules or an interaction between existing macromolecules that results in the formation of a small region in a macromolecule from which at least four chains emanate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07137
name: curing
def: "Chemical process of converting a prepolymer or a polymer into a polymer of higher molar mass and then into a network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07154
name: chelating polymer
def: "Polymer containing ligand groups capable of forming bonds \(or other attractive interactions\) between two or more separate binding sites within the same ligand group and a single atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07170
name: conducting polymer
def: "Polymeric material that exhibits bulk electric conductivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07200
name: cationic polymer
def: "Polymer composed of positively charged macromolecules and an equivalent amount of counter-anions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07201
name: conducting polymer composite
def: "Electrically-conducting composite comprising a non-conducting polymer matrix and an electrically-conducting material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07202
name: critical ion-concentration in an ionomer
def: "Concentration of ionic groups in an ionomer matrix above which ionic aggregation occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07339
name: chemically induced dynamic electron polarization
def: "Non-Boltzmann electron spin-state population produced in thermal or photochemical reactions\, either from a combination of radical pairs \(called radical-pair mechanism\)\, or directly from the triplet state \(called triplet mechanism\) and detectable by ESR spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07340
name: chemically initiated electron exchange luminescence
def: "Type of luminescence resulting from a thermal electron-transfer reaction. Also called catalyzed chemiluminescence."
synonym: "catalyzed chemiluminescence" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07341
name: colourability
def: "Ability of a colourless or slightly coloured photochromic material to develop colour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07342
name: color scale
def: "Series of ordered numbers that represents observable gradations of a given attribute or gradations of a combination of attributes of color perception."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07343
name: complete active space self-consistent field
def: "Computational scheme employed in multiconfigurational SCF theory especially suitable for studies of reactivity of excited states. The wavefunction is defined by selecting the set of active orbitals involved in the excitation or chemical reaction under investigation and is constructed as a linear expansion in the set of configuration functions that can be generated by occupying the active orbitals in all ways consistent with an overall spin and space symmetry \(full configuration interaction\, CI\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07344
name: complete active space self-consistent field second-order perturbation theory
def: "Theoretical scheme suitable for computations of accurate excitation energies and reaction barriers. In the first step\, electron correlation is taken into account only to a certain extent by using a CASSCF formalism \(the so called non-dynamic correlation\)\, whereas the remaining electron correlation \(the so called dynamic correlation\) is included through the use of second-order perturbation theory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07345
name: computational photochemistry
def: "Aspects of research that address the study of photochemical events by means of computer simulations using specialized software tools and strategies in order to get an understanding of the process at a microscopic level. The aim is to uncover the mechanism of known photochemical processes\, design new photochemical systems\, and predict molecular properties that are experimentally inaccessible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07346
name: concentration depolarization
def: "Loss of emission anisotropy due to transfer of electronic excitation from photo-selected molecules to other molecules with different orientations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07347
name: conical intersection
def: "Point of crossing between two electronic states of the same spin multiplicity \(most commonly singlets or triplets\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07348
name: continuous wave laser
def: "CW operation of a laser means that the laser is continuously pumped and continuously emits electromagnetic radiation. The emission can occur in a single cavity mode or on multiple modes."
synonym: "CW laser" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07349
name: conversion cross-section
def: "Product of the quantum yield by the absorption cross-section\, \\\(\\sigma \\ \\varPhi \\\). SI unit is \\\(\\pu\{m2\}\\\); commonly expressed in \\\(\\pu\{cm2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07350
name: crystal photochemistry
def: "Photochemical process in the crystal lattice of a photoactive compound or of a chromophore in the crystal lattice of host molecules. Often asymmetric photochemistry takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07351
name: cut-on filter
def: "Optical device that only permits the transmission of radiation of wavelength shorter than a specified wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07503
name: chemical functionality
def: "Ability of functional groups present within a polymer or polymer network to participate in chemical reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07504
name: connectivity
def: "Number of covalent bonds emanating from a constitutional unit of an oligomer molecule or a macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07518
name: colloidal gel
def: "Gel in which the network component comprises particles of colloidal dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07539
name: ceramer
def: "Chemically bonded hybrid material which is a crosslinked organic–inorganic polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07540
name: ceramic
def: "Rigid material that consists of an infinite three-dimensional network of sintered crystalline grains comprising metals bonded to carbon\, nitrogen or oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07541
name: ceramic precursor
def: "Material that is converted to a ceramic through pyrolysis."
synonym: "pre-ceramic" []
synonym: "pre-ceramic material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07542
name: ceramic-reinforced polymer
def: "Polymer composite consisting of a polymer continuous phase and disperse phase domains of microscopic ceramic particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07543
name: ceramic yield
def: "Mass of ceramic expressed as a percentage of the mass of the ceramic precursor used in the ceramization process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07545
name: composite
def: "Multicomponent material comprising multiple\, different \(non-gaseous\) phase domains in which at least one type of phase domain is a continuous phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07546
name: creep
def: "Time-dependent change of the dimensions of a material under a constant load."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07554
name: chemically bonded hybrid
def: "Hybrid material in which the different components are bonded to each other by covalent or partially covalent bonds."
synonym: "chemically bonded hybrid material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07555
name: clay hybrid
def: "Organic–inorganic composite material in which one of the components is a clay\, the particles of which are dispersed in a polymer."
synonym: "polymer-clay composite" []
synonym: "polymer-clay hybrid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07563
name: colloidal network
def: "Network comprising particles of colloidal dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07581
name: compatible polymer blend
def: "Immiscible polymer blend that exhibits macroscopically uniform physical properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07593
name: chain entanglement
def: "Interlocking of polymer chains in a polymer material forming a transient or permanent network junction over the time-scale of the measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07596
name: crosslink density
def: "Number of crosslinks per unit volume in a polymer network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07597
name: crosslinking site
def: "Site on a macromolecule or region in a polymer material that takes part in the formation of chemical or physical crosslinks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07609
name: carbo-reduction
def: "Process in which a metal oxide is reduced in the presence of carbon or a carbon-containing compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07610
name: ceramization
def: "Process in which a ceramic precursor is converted into a ceramic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:CT07612
name: colloidal processing
def: "Sol-gel processing in which a network of precipitated colloidal particles is treated by a conventional processing technique\, such as cold pressing\, hot pressing or sintering\, in order to produce a ceramic article."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01511
name: Δ\, Λ
def: "Designations of stereoisomers of tris\(didentate\) metal complexes and other octahedral complexes."
synonym: "delta, lambda" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01513
name: d\, l\, dl
def: "Obsolete \(usage strongly discouraged\) alternatives for\, respectively\, the prefixes \(+\)- and \(−\)- \[used to designate the sign of optical rotation of enantiomers under specified condition \(and hence to distinguish the enantiomers\)\] and \(±\)- for a racemate."
synonym: "+" []
synonym: "-" []
synonym: "minus" []
synonym: "plus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01514
name: dalton
def: "Non-SI unit of mass \(symbol \\\(\\mathrm\{Da\}\\\)\)\, equal to the unified atomic mass unit \(atomic mass constant\). It is often used in biochemistry and molecular biology although it was never approved by the Conférence Général des Poids et Mesures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01517
name: dark photochemistry
def: "Chemical reactions involving electronically excited molecular entities which are generated thermally rather than by absorption of electromagnetic radiation. The use of this term is discouraged."
synonym: "photochemistry without light" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01518
name: dark reaction
def: "A chemical reaction that does not require or depend on the presence of light. Contrasts with a photochemical reaction which is initiated by light absorption by one or more of the reactants."
synonym: "darkness reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01521
name: data reduction
def: "The process of transforming the initial representation of a spectrometer output into a form which is amenable to interpretation; for example\, a bar graph or a table of ion currents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01523
name: dative bond
def: "The coordination bond formed upon interaction between molecular species\, one of which serves as a donor and the other as an acceptor of the electron pair to be shared in the complex formed\, e.g\, the $\\ce\{N\\!\\rightarrow\\!B\}$ bond in $\\ce\{H3N\\!\\rightarrow\\!BH3\}$. In spite of the analogy of dative bonds with covalent bonds\, in that both types imply sharing a common electron pair between two vicinal atoms\, the former are distinguished by their significant polarity\, lesser strength\, and greater length. The distinctive feature of dative bonds is that their minimum-energy rupture in the gas phase or in inert solvent follows the heterolytic bond cleavage path."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01524
name: daughter ion
def: "An electrically charged product of reaction of a particular parent ion. In general such ions have a direct relationship to a particular precursor ion and indeed may relate to a unique state of the precursor ion. The reaction need not necessarily involve fragmentation. It could\, for example\, involve a change in the number of charges carried. Thus\, all fragment ions are daughter ions\, but not all daughter ions are necessarily fragment ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01525
name: daughter product
def: "Any nuclide which follows a specified radionuclide in a decay chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01526
name: Davydov splitting
def: "The splitting of bands in the electronic or vibrational spectra of crystals due to the presence of more than one \(interacting\) equivalent molecular entity in the unit cell."
synonym: "factor-group splitting" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01527
name: day
def: "Non-SI unit of time\, \\\(\\rm\{d\}=86\\ 400\\ \\rm\{s\}\\\). The day is not exactly defined in terms of the second since so-called leap seconds are added or subtracted from the day semiannually in order to keep the annual average occurrence of midnight at 24\:00 on the clock."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01528
name: deactivation
def: "Any loss of energy by an excited molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01529
name: dead time
def: "Of a radiation counter\, the constant and known value imposed on the resolving time\, usually in order to make the correction for resolving time losses more accurate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01530
name: dead time
def: "The time which elapses between the moment at which a sudden change in concentration \(or a new sample\) is introduced and the moment at which the detector response indication reaches the measurement threshold of the analyser\, a value conventionally fixed at 10% of the final change in indication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01531
name: dead time correction
def: "Correction to be applied to the observed number of pulses in order to take into account the number of pulses lost during the resolving or dead time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01532
name: dead-volume
def: "This term is used to express the extra-column volume. Strictly speaking\, the term 'dead-volume' refers to volumes within the chromatographic system which are not swept by the mobile phase. On the other hand\, mobile phase is flowing through most of the extra-column volumes. Due to this ambiguity the use of term 'dead-volume' is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01533
name: debye
def: "Non-SI unit of electric dipole moment. It is equal to the electric dipole moment for two charges of \\\(10^\{-10\}\\ \\rm\{franklin\}\\\) separated by \\\(1\\ \\rm\{ångström\}\\\)\, \\\(\\pu\{D\} = \\pu\{E-18 Fr cm\} \\approx \\pu\{3.33564E-30 C m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01534
name: Debye–Hückel equation
def: "The chemical potential or activity of ions cannot be determined on a purely thermodynamic basis. This is due to the fact that the effects of an ion cannot be separated from the effects of the accompanying counter-ion\, or in other terms\, the electrochemical potential of the ion cannot be separated into the chemical and the electrical component. Such a separation must necessarily be based on a non-thermodynamic convention. The present convention is based on the assumption that the molal activity coefficient of the chloride ion in dilute aqueous solutions \(\\\(\\pu\{I \\lt 0.10 mol kg-1\}\\\)\) can be estimated by means of the Debye–Hückel equation\: \\\[-\\log_\{10\}\\gamma_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=\\frac\{z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{2\}\\ A\\ \\sqrt\{I\}\}\{1 + \\mathring\{a\}\\ B\\ \\sqrt\{I\}\}\\\] where \\\(I\\\) is ionic strength\, \\\(z\\\) is the charge number of the ion\, \\\(\\mathring\{a\}\\\) is ion size parameter and \\\(A\\\) and \\\(B\\\) are temperature-dependent constants. According to the Bates–Guggenheim convention \\\(\\mathring\{a\}\\ B\\\) is taken to be \\\(1.5 \\sqrt\{\\pu\{mol kg-1\}\}\\\) at all temperatures and for all compositions of the solutions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01535
name: deca
def: "SI prefix for 10 \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{da\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01536
name: decadic absorbance
def: "The negative decadic logarithm of one minus absorptance as measured on a uniform sample\: \\\[A=- \\log _\{10\}\(1- \\alpha \)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01537
name: decay chain
def: "A series of nuclides in which each member transforms into the next through nuclear decay until a stable nuclide has been formed. Synonymous with radioactive chain and radioactive series."
synonym: "radioactive chain" []
synonym: "radioactive series" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01538
name: decay constant
def: "Proportionality constant relating the activity of a radioactive substance\, A\, to the number of decaying particles B\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(A=\\lambda \\ N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\). probability of a nuclear decay within a time interval divided by that time interval. Synonymous with 'disintegration constant'."
synonym: "disintegration constant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01539
name: decay curve
def: "A graph showing the relative amount of radioactive substance remaining after any time interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01541
name: decay rate
def: "The rate at which a pollutant is removed from the atmosphere either by reaction with reactive transient species such as the $\\ce\{HO\}$ radical\, $\\ce\{O3\}$\, etc.\, by photodecomposition initiated by light absorption by the impurity\, or by loss at the surface of aerosols\, the earth\, etc. The decay rate as applied to radioactive materials is related to the radioactive half-life \(\\\(t_\{1/2\}\\\)\) of the particular isotopic species A and its concentration \\\(\[A\]_\{t\}\\\)\, at the given time \(\\\(t\\\)\)\: \\\[\\rm\{Rate\}=\\frac\{\[A\]_\{t\}\\ \\ln 2\}\{t_\{1/2\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01543
name: decay scheme
def: "A graphical representation of the energy levels of the members of a decay chain showing the way along which nuclear decay occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01544
name: decay time
def: "The time on stream during which the rate constant for the catalytic reaction\, \\\(k\\\)\, falls to a specified fraction of its original value\, often 0.5."
synonym: "time of deactivation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01545
name: deci
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-1\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\rm\{d\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01546
name: decibel
def: "Non-SI unit for expressing power levels\, \\\(\\mathrm\{dB\}=0.1151293\\ \\mathrm\{Np\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01547
name: decomposition
def: "The breakdown of a single phase into two or more phases. The term applies also to other chemical entities such as a normal molecule and a reaction intermediate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01549
name: decontamination factor
def: "The ratio of the proportion of contaminant to product before treatment to the proportion after treatment. It is the reciprocal of the enrichment factor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01553
name: definitive method
def: "A method of exceptional scientific status which is sufficiently accurate to stand alone in the determination of a given property for the certification of a reference material. Such a method must have a firm theoretical foundation so that systematic error is negligible relative to the intended use. Analyte masses \(amounts\) or concentrations must be measured directly in terms of the base units of measurements\, or indirectly related through sound theoretical equations. Definitive methods\, together with certified reference materials\, are primary means for transferring accuracy\, i.e. establishing traceability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01554
name: deflection
def: "\(In terms of divisions of the pointer scale\). The other point of reversal of an ideal \(undamped\) swing starting at the zero point of the pointer scale. Since the points of reversal of an ideal swing are located symmetrically about the rest point\, the deflection is equal to twice the rest point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01555
name: deflocculation
def: "The reversal of coagulation or flocculation\, i.e. the dispersion of aggregates to form a colloidally stable suspension or emulsion."
synonym: "peptization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01556
name: degeneracy
def: "Number of states having the same energy. Symbols\: \\\(g\\\)\, \\\(d\\\)\, \\\(W\\\)\, \\\(\\omega \\\)\, \\\(\\beta \\\). Also called statistical weight."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01557
name: degenerate chain branching
def: "Sometimes chain branching is brought about by an intermediate which has a long lifetime compared with an ordinary free radical. If this intermediate can break down in different ways\, only one of which leads to branching\, there may be a relatively slow increase in the number of radicals\, and there is said to be degenerate chain branching."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01559
name: degenerate rearrangement
def: "A molecular rearrangement in which the principal product is indistinguishable \(in the absence of isotopic labelling\) from the principal reactant. The term includes both 'degenerate intramolecular rearrangements' and reactions that involve intermolecular transfer of atoms or groups \('degenerate intermolecular rearrangements'\)\: both are degenerate isomerizations. The occurrence of degenerate rearrangements may be detectable by isotopic labelling or by dynamic NMR techniques. For example\: thesigmatropic rearrangement of hexa-1\,5-diene \(Cope rearrangement\)\:  Synonymous but less preferable terms are 'automerization'\, 'permutational isomerism'\, 'isodynamic transformation'\, 'topomerization'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01560
name: degree
def: "Non-SI unit of plane angle\, \\\(1\\ ^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} = \\frac\{\\pi \}\{180\}\\ \\rm\{rad\}\\approx 0.017\\ 453\\ 29\\\,...\\\,\\rm\{rad\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01561
name: degree Celsius
def: "SI derived unit of Celsius temperature equal to the kelvin \(symbol\: \\\(\\pu\{^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01562
name: degree Fahrenheit
def: "Non-SI unit of Fahrenheit temperature\, \\\(\\pu\{^\{\\circ\}F = \\frac\{5\}\{9\} K\}\\\). The Fahrenheit temperature is related to the Celsius temperature by \\\(\\frac\{\\theta_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\}\{^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} \\rm\{F\}\} = \\frac\{9\}\{5\}\\ \\frac\{\\theta\}\{^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} \\rm\{C\}\}\\\,+\\\,32\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01564
name: degree of association
def: "The number of surfactant ions in the micelle. It does not concern the location of the counterions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01565
name: degree of crystallinity
def: "The fractional amount of crystallinity in the polymer sample \(\\\(w_\{c\}\\\) for mass fraction; \\\(\\psi_\{c\}\\\) for volume fraction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01566
name: degree of dissociation
def: "Degree of reaction for a dissociation reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01568
name: degree of ionization
def: "Degree of reaction for an ionization reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01569
name: degree of polymerization
def: "The number of monomeric units in a macromolecule or oligomer molecule\, a block or a chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01570
name: degree of reaction
def: "Extent of reaction divided by the maximum extent of reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01571
name: degrees of cistacticity and transtacticity
def: "For a regular polymer containing double bonds in the main chain of the constitutional repeating units these are the fractions of such double bonds that are in the cis and trans configurations\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01572
name: degrees of freedom
def: "A statistical quantity indicating the number of values which could be arbitrarily assigned within the specification of a system of observations. For simple replication\, with \\\(n\\\) measurements and one estimated parameter \(the mean\)\, \\\(\nu =n- 1\\\). More generally\, for multivariable computations\, the number of degrees of freedom equals the number of observations minus the number of fitted parameters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01573
name: degrees of triad isotacticity\, syndiotacticity\, and heterotacticity
def: "The fractions of triads in a regular vinyl polymer that are of the mm\, rr and mr = rm types\, respectively. In cases where triad analysis is not attainable\, the diad isotacticity and diad syndiotacticity may be defined as the fractions of diads in a regular vinyl polymer that are of the m and r types\, respectively. \(m = meso; r = racemic\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01574
name: dehydroarenes
def: "Species\, usually transient\, derived formally by the abstraction of a hydrogen atom from each of two ring atoms of an arene. The name for specific compounds requires the numerical prefix di-. E.g. 1\,8-didehydronaphthalene or naphthalene-1\,8-diyl.  1\,2-Didehydroarenes are called arynes and are commonly represented with a formal triple bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01575
name: dehydrobenzenes
def: "A subclass of dehydroarenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01576
name: delayed coincidence
def: "The occurrence of two or more events separated by a short but measurable time interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01577
name: delayed coke
def: "A commonly used term for a primary carbonization product \(green or raw coke\) from high-boiling hydrocarbon fractions \(heavy residues of petroleum or coal processing\) produced by the delayed coking process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01578
name: delayed coking process
def: "A thermal process which increases the molecular aggregation or association in petroleum-based residues or coal tar pitches leading to extended mesophase domains. This is achieved by holding them at an elevated temperature \(usually \\\(750\{-\}765\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\) over a period of time \(\\\(12\\ \\rm\{to\}\\ 36\\ \\rm\{h\}\\\)\). It is performed in a coking drum and is designed to ultimately produce delayed coke. The feed is rapidly pre-heated in a tubular furnace to about \\\(760\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01579
name: delayed fluorescence
def: "Three types of delayed fluorescence are known\:   E-type delayed fluorescence\: The process in which the first excited singlet state becomes populated by a thermally activated radiationless transition from the first excited triplet state. Since in this case the populations of the singlet and triplet states are in thermal equilibrium\, the lifetimes of delayed fluorescence and the concomitant phosphorescence are equal.   P-type delayed fluorescence\: The process in which the first excited singlet state is populated by interaction of two molecules in the triplet state \(triplet-triplet annihilation\) thus producing one molecule in the excited singlet state. In this biphotonic process the lifetime of delayed fluorescence is half the value of the concomitant phosphorescence.   Recombination fluorescence\: The first excited singlet state becomes populated by recombination of radical ions with electrons or by recombination of radical ions of opposite charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01580
name: delayed luminescence
def: "Luminescence decaying more slowly than that expected from the rate of decay of the emitting state. The following mechanisms of luminescence provide examples\:triplet-triplet or singlet-singlet annihilation to form one molecular entity in its excited singlet state and another molecular entity in its electronic ground state \(sometime referred to as P type\)\,  thermally activated delayed fluorescence involving reversible intersystem crossing \(sometimes referred to as E type\)\, and   combination of oppositely charged ions or of an electron and a cation. For emission to be referred to in this case as delayed luminescence at least one of the two reaction partners must be generated in a photochemical process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01581
name: delayed neutrons
def: "Neutrons emitted by fission products formed by nuclear decay \(the observed delay is due to the preceding nuclear decay or decays\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01582
name: deliquescence
def: "The process that occurs when the vapour pressure of the saturated aqueous solution of a substance is less than the vapour pressure of water in the ambient air. When water vapour is collected by the pure solid compound\, a mixture of the solid and liquid or an aqueous solution of the compound forms until the substance is dissolved and is in equilibrium with its environment; at this time the vapour pressure of water over the aqueous solution will equal the partial pressure of water in the atmosphere in contact with it. A crystalline salt aerosol particle will deliquesce in the atmosphere when the relative humidity surpasses a characteristic value\, the so-called deliquescence point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01583
name: delocalization
def: "A quantum mechanical concept most usually applied in organic chemistry to describe the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonding\}$ in a conjugated system. This bonding is not localized between two atoms\: instead\, each link has a 'fractional double bond character' or bond order. There is a corresponding 'delocalization energy'\, identifiable with the stabilization of the system compared with a hypothetical alternative in which formal \(localized\) single and double bonds are present. Some degree of delocalization is always present and can be estimated by quantum mechanical calculations. The effects are particularly evident in aromatic systems and in symmetrical molecular entities in which a lone pair of electrons or a vacant p-orbital is conjugated with a double bond \(e.g. carboxylate ions\, nitro compounds\, enamines\, the allyl cation\). Delocalization in such species may be represented by partial bonds or as resonance \(here symbolized by a two-headed arrow\) between contributing structures.   These examples also illustrate the concomitant delocalization of charge in ionic conjugated systems. Analogously\, delocalization of the spin of an unpaired electron occurs in conjugated radicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01585
name: demister
def: "Apparatus made of wire mesh or glass fibre which is used to help remove acid mist as in the manufacture of sulfuric acid. Demisters are also components of wet arrestment plants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01586
name: denaturation
def: "The process of partial or total alteration of the native structure of a macromolecule resulting from the loss of tertiary or tertiary and secondary structure that is a consequence of the disruption of stabilizing weak bonds. Denaturation can occur when proteins and nucleic acids are subjected to elevated temperature or to extremes of pH\, or to non-physiological concentrations of salt\, organic solvents\, urea or other chemical agents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01587
name: denaturation
def: "Addition of methanol or acetone to alcohol to make it unfit for drinking."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01588
name: dendrite
def: "A crystalline morphology produced by skeletal growth\, leading to a 'tree-like' appearance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01589
name: denitrification
def: "The reduction of nitrates to nitrites\, dinitrogen oxide \(nitrous oxide\) or dinitrogen catalysed by microoganisms\, e.g. facultative aerobic soil bacteria under anaerobic conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01590
name: density
def: "Mass of a sample or body divided by its volume."
synonym: "mass density" []
synonym: "specific weight" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01591
name: density inversion
def: "The interchange of the denser and less dense phases due to changes in solute concentration. phase inversion is often used in this context but is ambiguous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01593
name: density of states
def: "Number of states within an energy interval divided by volume and that energy interval. Symbol\: \\\(N_\{E\}\\\).Number of states within an energy interval divided by that energy interval. Symbol\: \\\(\\rho\(E\)\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01594
name: denticity
def: "In a coordination entity the number of donor groups from a given ligand attached to the same central atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01595
name: denuder system
def: "An apparatus used to separate gases and aerosols \(over a given diameter\) which is based upon the difference in diffusion velocity between gases and aerosol particles. Usually a tube containing a selective internal wall coating which removes the gaseous compounds at the wall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01596
name: deodorizer
def: "Equipment for the removal of noxious gases and odours\, which may consist of combustion\, absorption\, or adsorption units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01597
name: deoxyribonucleic acids
def: "High molecular weight\, linear polymers\, composed of nucleotides containing deoxyribose and linked by phosphodiester bonds; DNA contain the genetic information of organisms. The double-stranded form consists of a double helix of two complementary chains that run in opposite directions and are held together by hydrogen bonds between pairs of the complementary nucleotides and Hoogsteen \(stacking\) forces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01598
name: depolarization of scattered light
def: "The phenomenon\, due primarily to the anisotropy of the polarizability of the scattering medium\, resulting from the fact that the electric vectors of the incident and scattered beams are not coplanar and that\, therefore\, light scattered from a vertically \(horizontally\) polarized incident beam contains a horizontal \(vertical\) component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01600
name: depolymerization
def: "The process of converting a polymer into a monomer or a mixture of monomers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01601
name: deposition
def: "Deposition is normally considered to be one of two types\: dry deposition is the process by which aerosols and gases in the air are deposited on the surface of the earth \(soil\, water\, rock\, plants\, etc.\); this is termed 'dry' deposition even when the receptor surface is moist. Wet deposition is that process which involves the transport of chemicals to the surface of the earth by water droplets or snow crystals which scavenge pollutants as they form and fall through the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01602
name: deposition velocity
def: "The ratio of flux density \(often given in units of \\\(\\pu\{g cm-2 s-1\}\\\)\) of a substance at a sink surface to its concentration in the atmosphere \(corresponding units of \\\(\\pu\{g cm-3\}\\\)\). While the units of this ratio are clearly those of velocity \(in this case \\\(\\pu\{cm s-1\}\\\)\)\, the ratio is not a flow velocity in the normal sense of the word."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01603
name: depsides
def: "Intermolecular esters formed from two or more molecules of the same or different phenolic benzoic acids. Depending on the number of the component units\, di-\, tri-\, etc. depsides may result\, e.g. 4-O-galloylgallic acid\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01604
name: depsipeptides
def: "Natural or synthetic compounds having sequences of amino and hydroxy carboxylic acid residues \(usually $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}amino\}$ and \\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}hydroxy acids\}\)\, commonly but not necessarily regularly alternating. In cyclodepsipeptides\, the residues are connected in a ring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01605
name: depth of penetration
def: "The inverse of the absorption coefficient. If the decadic absorption coefficient\, \\\(a\\\)\, is used\, the depth of penetration \(\\\(\\frac\{1\}\{a\}\\\)\) is the distance at which the radiant power\, \\\(P_\{\\lambda \}\\\)\, decreases to one tenth of its incident value\, \\\(P_\{\\lambda \}^\{0\}\\\). If the Naperian absorption coefficient\, \\\(\\alpha \\\)\, is used\, the depth of penetration \(\\\(\\frac\{1\}\{\\alpha \} = \\beta \\\) in this case\) is the distance at which the radiant power decreases to \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) of its incident value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01606
name: depth profile
def: "Dependence of concentration on depth perpendicular to the surface in a solid sample. It can be obtained by a simultaneous or sequential process of erosion and surface analysis or by measurement of the energy loss of primary backscattered particles produced by nuclear reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01607
name: depth resolution
def: "The distance between the 84 and 16 per cent levels of the depth profile of an element in a perfect sandwich sample with an infinitesimally small overlap of the components. These limits correspond to the 2\\\(\\sigma \\\)-value of the Gaussian distribution of the signal at the interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01609
name: derivative potentiometric titration
def: "A titration that involves measuring\, recording\, or computing the first derivative of the potential of a single indicator electrode with respect to the volume or otherwise added amount of reagent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01610
name: derivative spectroscopy
def: "The first \(second\, ...\) derivative absorption spectrum of a molecule is defined as the first \(second\, ...\) derivative\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}A\\left\(\\overset\{˜\}\{\nu \}\\right\)\}\{\\rm\{d\}\\overset\{˜\}\{\nu \}\}\\\)\[\\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}^\{2\}A\\left\(\\overset\{˜\}\{\nu \}\\right\)\}\{\\rm\{d\}\\overset\{˜\}\{\nu \}^\{2\}\}\\\)\, ... \] of the absorbance \\\(A\\\) as a function of wavenumber\, \\\(\\overset\{˜\}\{\nu \}\\\). Wavelengths may be used in place of wavenumbers but the shape of the derivative spectra will be slightly different. When derivative spectra are obtained at low temperature\, they are called first \(second\, ...\) derivative low temperature absorption spectra \(specifying the solvent\, temperature and solute concentration\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01611
name: derived coherent unit
def: "A unit constructed exclusively from base units\, e.g. \\\(\\pu\{mol kg-1\}\\\)\, \\\(\\pu\{kg m-3\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01612
name: derived kind of quantity
def: "A kind of quantity characterized by an equation between base kinds of quantities\, e.g. \\\(\\rm\{mass concentration\} = \\rm\{mass\}\\ \\left\(\\rm\{length\}\\right\)^\{-3\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01613
name: derived non-coherent unit
def: "A unit constructed from base units and numerical factors\, which may be named and symbolized\, e.g. \\\(\\mathrm\{mg\}\\\)\, \\\(\\pu\{mol l-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01614
name: derived quantity
def: "Quantity defined\, in a system of quantities\, as a function of base quantities of that system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01615
name: derived unit
def: "Unit of measurement of a derived quantity in a given system of quantities. Derived units are expressed algebraically in terms of base units by means of the mathematical symbols of multiplication and division."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01616
name: descending elution/development
def: "A mode of operation in which the mobile phase is supplied to the upper edge of the paper or plate and the downward movement is governed mainly by gravity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01618
name: desiccant
def: "Drying agent.In agriculture\, a substance used for drying up plants and facilitating their mechanical harvesting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01619
name: desolvation
def: "The process of evaporation of the solvent from an aerosol of fine droplets \(the droplets being the result of nebulizing a solution\). Desolvation converts the aerosol into a dry aerosol consisting of a suspension of solid or molten particles of the solute."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01620
name: desorption
def: "The converse of adsorption\, i.e. the decrease in the amount of adsorbed substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01621
name: desorption by displacement
def: "The process in which expulsion of a previously adsorbed component from the interfacial layer is effected by subsequent stronger adsorption of another component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01622
name: desulfurization
def: "The process by which sulfur is removed from a material such as coal or oil. It may involve one of many techniques including elutriation\, froth flotation\, laundering\, magnetic separation\, chemical treatment\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01623
name: desymmetrization
def: "The modification of an object which results in the loss of one or more symmetry elements\, such as those which preclude chirality \(mirror plane\, centre of inversion\, rotation-reflection axis\)\, as in the conversion of a prochiral molecular entity into a chiral one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01624
name: desymmetrization step
def: "The removal of the smallest possible number of symmetry elements from a molecule with or without the restriction to pointwise substitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01625
name: detachment
def: "The reverse of an attachment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01626
name: detailed balancing
def: "When equilibrium is reached in a reaction system \(containing an arbitrary number of components and reaction paths\)\, as many atoms\, in their respective molecular entities will pass forward\, as well as backwards\, along each individual path in a given finite time interval. Accordingly\, the reaction path in the reverse direction must in every detail be the reverse of the reaction path in the forward direction \(provided always that the system is at equilibrium\). The principle of detailed balancing is a consequence for macroscopic systems of the principle of microscopic reversibility."
synonym: "principle of detailed balancing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01627
name: detection efficiency
def: "The ratio between the number of particles or photons detected and the number of similar particles or photons emitted by the radiation source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01629
name: detection limit
def: "The minimum single result which\, with a stated probability\, can be distinguished from a suitable blank value. The limit defines the point at which the analysis becomes possible and this may be different from the lower limit of the determinable analytical range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01643
name: detergent
def: "A surfactant \(or a mixture containing one or more surfactants\) having cleaning properties in dilute solutions \(soaps are surfactants and detergents\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01644
name: detoxification
def: "Process\, or processes\, of chemical modification which make a toxic molecule less toxic.Treatment of patients suffering from poisoning in such a way as to promote physiological processes which reduce the probability or severity of harmful effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01645
name: deuteriation
def: "A term used to represent replacement of protium by deuterium. The use of the word deuteration is also acceptable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01648
name: deuterium
def: "A specific name for the atom $\\ce\{^\{2\}H\}$. The cation $\\ce\{2H^\{+\}\}$ is a deuteron the species $\\ce\{2H^\{−\}\}$ is a 'deuteride' anion and $\\ce\{^\{2\}H\}$ is the 'deuterio' group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01649
name: deuteron
def: "Nucleus of the $\\ce\{^\{2\}H\}$ \(deuterium\) atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01650
name: deviation
def: "The difference between an observed value and the arithmetic mean of the set to which it belongs. It can be calculated by the formula\: @D01650-1@"

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01651
name: devolatilizer
def: "Material added to a sample to decrease its volatilization or that of some component of it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01652
name: dew point
def: "That temperature at which water vapour present in the atmosphere is just sufficient to saturate it. When air is cooled below the dew point\, the excess of water vapour appears as tiny droplets or crystals of ice depending on the temperature of the air mass. This term is also applicable to acid gases as in the problem of acid corrosion and acid smut formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01653
name: dew point hygrometer
def: "Instrument in which the sample is passed over a cooled surface. The temperature at which dew forms on the cooled surface is a function of the water content of the gas passing over the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01654
name: Dexter excitation transfer
def: "Excitation transfer occurring as a result of an electron exchange mechanism. It requires an overlap of the wavefunctions of the energy donor and the energy acceptor. It is the dominant mechanism in triplet-triplet energy transfer. The transfer rate constant\, \\\(k_\{\\mathrm\{ET\}\}\\\)\, is given by\: \\\[k_\{\\rm\{ET\}\}\\propto \\frac\{h\}\{2\\ \\pi \}\\ P^\{2\}\\ J\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{\\frac\{-2\\ r\}\{L\}\}\\\] where \\\(r\\\) is the distance between donor \(D\) and acceptor \(A\)\, \\\(L\\\) and \\\(P\\\) are constants not easily related to experimentally determinable quantities\, and \\\(J\\\) is the spectral overlap integral. For this mechanism the spin conservation rules are obeyed."
synonym: "electron exchange excitation transfer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01655
name: dextrans
def: "Branched poly-$\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$-ᴅ-glucosides of microbial origin having glycosidic bonds predominantly $\\ce\{C-1\\!\\rightarrow\\!C-6\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01656
name: dextrins
def: "Poly-$\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$-ᴅ-glucosides of intermediate chain length derived from starch components \(amylopectins\) by the action of amylases \(starch hydrolysing enzymes\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01658
name: diabatic coupling
def: "Energy coupling between two potential-energy surfaces is sometimes known as diabatic coupling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01659
name: diabatic electron transfer
def: "Electron transfer process in which the reacting system has to cross over between different electronic surfaces in passing from reactants to products. For diabatic electron transfer the electronic transmission factor is ≪ 1 \(see Marcus equation\). The term 'non-adiabatic electron transfer' has also been used and is in fact more widespread\, but should be discouraged because it contains a double negation."
synonym: "non-adiabatic electron transfer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01660
name: diabatic photoreaction
def: "Within the Born–Oppenheimer approximation\, a reaction beginning on one excited state 'potential-energy surface' and ending\, as a result of radiationless transition\, on another surface\, usually that of the ground state. Also called non-adiabatic."
synonym: "nonadiabatic photoreaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01661
name: diacylamines
def: "Compounds having two acyl groups substituted on ammonia or a primary amine acyl-NR-acyl. They are also known as secondary amides and\, especially the cyclic examples derived from diacids\, as imides\, e.g. $\\ce\{\[RC\(=O\)\]2NR\}$\, $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)2NHC\(=O\)R\}$ ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01663
name: diagram level
def: "A level described by the removal of one electron from the configuration of the neutral ground state. These levels form a spectrum similar to that of a one-electron or hydrogen-like atom but\, being single-valency levels\, have the energy scale reversed relative to that of single-electron levels. Diagram levels may be divided into valence levels and core levels according to the nature of the electron vacancy. Diagram levels with orbital angular momentum different from zero occur in pairs and form spin doublets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01665
name: dialysate
def: "After \(complete or incomplete\) dialysis two solutions are obtained. The one free from colloidal material is called dialysate; the other one\, containing the colloidal particles may be called retentate\, dialysis residue\, or simply residue\, but should not be called dialysate."
synonym: "dialysis residue" []
synonym: "residue" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01666
name: dialysis
def: "The process of separating a colloidal sol from a colloid-free solution by a membrane permeable to all components of the system except the colloidal ones\, and allowing the exchange of the components of small molar mass to proceed for a certain time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01668
name: diamagnetic
def: "Substances having a negative magnetic susceptibility are diamagnetic. They are repelled out of a magnetic field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01670
name: diamidides
def: "Analogues of acyclic carboxylic acid anhydrides in which $\\ce\{=O\}$ has been replaced by $\\ce\{=NR\}$ and $\\ce\{–O–\}$ by $\\ce\{–NR–\}$\: $\\ce\{RC\(=NR\)NRC\(=NR\)R\}$\, N-imidoyl amidines. The name 1\,3\,5-triazapentadienes is sometimes applied as a class name to diamidides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01671
name: diamond
def: "An allotropic form of the element carbon with cubic structure \(space group \\\(O_\{h\}^\{7\}- Fd3m\\\)\) which is thermodynamically stable at pressures above \\\(6\\ \\rm\{GPa\}\\\) at room temperature and metastable at atmospheric pressure. At low pressures diamond converts rapidly to graphite at temperatures above \\\(1900\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) in an inert atmosphere. The chemical bonding between the carbon atoms is covalent with \\\(\\mathrm\{sp\}^\{3\}\\\) hybridization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01672
name: diamond by CVD
def: "Diamond by CVD \(chemical vapour deposition\) is formed as crystals or as films from various gaseous hydrocarbons or other organic molecules in the presence of activated\, atomic hydrogen. It consists of \\\(\\rm\{sp\}^\{3\}\\\)-hybridized carbon atoms with the three-dimensional crystalline structure of the diamond lattice."
synonym: "CVD diamond" []
synonym: "low-pressure diamond" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01673
name: diamond-like carbon films
def: "Diamond-like carbon \(DLC\) films are hard\, amorphous films with a significant fraction of \\\(\\mathrm\{sp\}^\{3\}\\\)-hybridized carbon atoms and which can contain a significant amount of hydrogen. Depending on the deposition conditions\, these films can be fully amorphous or contain diamond crystallites. These materials are not called diamond unless a full three-dimensional crystalline lattice of diamond is proven."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01674
name: dianions
def: "Molecular entities bearing two negative charges\, which may be located on a single atom or on different atoms or may be delocalized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01675
name: diastereoisomer excess
def: "This is defined by analogy with enantiomeric excess\, as \\\(D_\{1\}- D_\{2\}\\\) \[and the percent diastereoisomer excess as \\\(100\\ \(D_\{1\}-D_\{2\}\)\\\)\]\, where the mole fractions of two diastereoisomers in a mixture or the fractional yields of two diastereoisomers formed in a reaction are \\\(D_\{1\}\\\) and \\\(D_\{2\}\\\) \(\\\(D_\{1\}+D_\{2\}=1\\\)\). The term is not applicable if more than two diastereoisomers are present. Frequently this term is abbreviated to \\\(\\rm\{d.e.\}\\\)"
synonym: "diastereoisomeric excess" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01677
name: diastereoisomeric units
def: "Two non-superposable configurational units that correspond to the same constitutional unit are considered to be diastereomeric if they are not mirror images."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01678
name: diastereoisomerization
def: "The interconversion of diastereoisomers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01679
name: diastereoisomerism
def: "Stereoisomerism other than enantiomerism. Diastereoisomers \(or diastereomers\) are stereoisomers not related as mirror images. Diastereoisomers are characterized by differences in physical properties\, and by some differences in chemical behaviour towards achiral as well as chiral reagents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01681
name: diastereomeric ratio
def: "This is defined by analogy with enantiomeric ratio as the ratio of the percentage of one diastereoisomer in a mixture to that of the other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01683
name: diastereomorphism
def: "The relationship between objects \(or models\) analogous to that between diastereoisomeric molecular entities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01685
name: diastereotopic
def: "Constitutionally equivalent atoms or groups of a molecule which are not symmetry related. Replacement of one of two diastereotopic atoms or groups results in the formation of one of a pair of diastereoisomers. In the example below the two hydrogen atoms of the methylene group C-3 are diastereotopic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01688
name: diazenyl radicals
def: "Radicals of structure $\\ce\{RN=N^\{.\}\}$ ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01689
name: diazoamino compounds
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RN=N–NR2\}$ \(not all $\\ce\{R = H\}$\, and one $\\ce\{R\}$ commonly aryl\). In systematic nomenclature\, diazoamino prefixed to the name of $\\ce\{RH\}$ names the compound $\\ce\{RN=NNHR'\}$ \($\\ce\{R = R'\}$\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhN=N–NPhMe\}$\, N-methyldiazoaminobenzene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01690
name: diazoates
def: "Salts $\\ce\{RN=NO−M^\{+\}\}$ \($\\ce\{R\}$ commonly aryl\) of the compounds $\\ce\{RN=NOH\}$ \(hydrocarbon\)diazohydroxides\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhN=NO−K^\{+\}\}$ potassium benzenediazoate. In the IUPAC 'Blue Book' these compounds are named as hydrocarbyldiazenolates and hydrocarbyldiazenols\, respectively. E.g. $\\ce\{PhN=NOH\}$ phenyldiazenol. $\\ce\{RN=NO−M^\{+\}\}$ are also known by the non-IUPAC name diazotates."
synonym: "diazotates" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01691
name: diazo compounds
def: "Compounds having the divalent diazo group\, $\\ce\{=N^\{+\}=N^\{−\}\}$\, attached to a carbon atom\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH2=N2\}$ diazomethane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01692
name: diazonium salts
def: "Compounds of structure $\\ce\{RN2^\{+\}Y^\{−\}\}$\, in which $\\ce\{R\}$ is generally but not necessarily aryl\, and the cations of which are usually formulated as $\\ce\{RN^\{+\}\\!#N\}$\, E.g. $\\ce\{PhN^\{+\}\\!#N\}$ benzenediazonium chloride. They may also be named\, from the canonical form $\\ce\{RN=N^\{+\}\}$\, hydrocarbyldiazenylium salts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01693
name: diazooxides
def: "Diazocyclohexadienones\, which may also be considered as dipolar diazonio phenoxides\, obtained by diazotizing aromatic primary amines having a hydroxy group in an ortho or para position. Also known as diazophenols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01695
name: dicarbenium ions
def: "Species carrying two positive charges\, formally located on tervalent carbon atoms\, e.g. $\\ce\{^\{+\}CH2–\\!^\{+\}CH2\}$ ethane-1\,2-diyl dication\, $\\ce\{^\{+\}CH2CH2–\\!^\{+\}CH2\}$ propane-1\,3-bis\(ylium\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01696
name: dichotomous sampler
def: "Device for dividing a polydispersed aerosol particle population into two size fractions during sampling. The fractionation is based on the momentum differences of the particles which allow the larger particles to pass through a zone of stagnant gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01698
name: dielectric polarization
def: "The difference between the electric displacement vector and the product of the electric field strength with the permittivity of vacuum\, \\\(P = D - \\varepsilon_\{0\}\\ E\\\)."
synonym: "dipole moment per volume" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01699
name: dienes
def: "Compounds that contain two fixed double bonds \(usually assumed to be between carbon atoms\). Dienes in which the two double-bond units are linked by one single bond are termed conjugated\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH2=CH–CH=CH2\}$ buta-1\,3-diene. Dienes in which the double bonds are adjacent are called cumulative\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3–CH=C=CH2\}$ buta-1\,2-diene. Those in which one or more of the unsaturated carbon atoms is replaced by a heteroatom may be called heterodienes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01700
name: dienophile
def: "The olefin component of a Diels–Alder reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01701
name: difference absorption spectroscopy
def: "A highly concentrated analyte in an analytical sample can be determined with better precision by replacing the blank \(reference\) cell by one containing a solution of the analyte or other absorber of known concentration; this is known as difference absorption spectroscopy. Difference spectra can also be obtained by computer or other subtraction methods."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01702
name: differential capacitance
def: "Differential capacitance \(per unit area of interphase or electrode; SI unit \\\(\\pu\{F m-2\}\\\)\) is given by \\\[C=\(\\frac\{\\partial Q\}\{\\partial E\}\)_\{T\,p\,\\mu _\{i\}\,...\}\\\] where \\\(E\\\) is the potential of the electrode with respect to a reference electrode\, \\\(\\mu _\{i\}\\\) is a set of chemical potentials which are held constant\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(Q\\\) is the electric charge \(per unit area of interphase or electrode\)\, and \\\(p\\\) is the pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01703
name: differential detector
def: "A device which measures the instantaneous difference in the composition of the column effluent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01704
name: differential diffusion coefficient
def: "Defined by \\\[D_\{i\} = \\frac\{-J_\{i\}\}\{\nabla c_\{i\}\}\\\] where \\\(J_\{i\}\\\) is the amount of species \\\(i\\\) flowing through unit area in unit time and \\\(\nabla c_\{i\}\\\) is the concentration gradient of species \\\(i\\\). Different diffusion coefficients may be defined depending on the choice of the frame of reference used for \\\(J_\{i\}\\\) and \\\(\nabla c_\{i\}\\\). For systems with more than two components\, the flow of any component and hence its diffusion coefficient depends on the concentration distribution of all components. The limiting differential diffusion coefficient is the value of \\\(D_\{i\}\\\) extrapolated to zero concentration of the diffusing species\: \\\[\[D_\{i\}\]=\\lim _\{c_\{i\}\\rightarrow 0\}D_\{i\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01705
name: differential molar energy of adsorption
def: "When the addition of a differential amount \\\(\\mathrm\{d\}n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\\\) or \\\(\\mathrm\{d\}n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is effected at constant gas volume\, the differential molar energy of adsorption of component \\\(i\\\)\, \\\(\\Delta _\{a\}U_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\\\) or \\\(\\Delta _\{a\}U_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, is defined as\: \\\[\\Delta _\{a\}U_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}=U_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}- U_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\] or \\\[\\Delta _\{a\}U_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\} = U_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}- U_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\] where the differential molar surface excess energy\, \\\(U_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\\\)\, is given by \\\[U_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}=\(\\frac\{\\partial U^\{\\sigma \}\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\)_\{T\,m\,n_\{j\}^\{\\sigma \}\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial U\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\}\)_\{T\,m\,V^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\,p_\{i\}\,n_\{j\}^\{\\sigma \}\}\\\] and the differential molar interfacial energy\, \\\(U_\{i\}^\{s\}\\\)\, by \\\[U_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial U\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\)_\{T\,m\,V^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\,p_\{i\}\,n_\{j\}^\{\\sigma \}\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial U\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\)_\{T\,m\,V^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\,V^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\,p_\{i\}\,n_\{j\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\\\] \\\(U_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) is the differential molar energy of component \\\(i\\\) in the gas phase\, i.e. \\\[\(\\frac\{\\partial U\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\}\)_\{T\,V\,n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01708
name: differential scanning calorimetry
def: "A technique in which the difference in energy inputs into a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) and a reference material is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance and reference material are subjected to a controlled temperature programme."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01709
name: differential thermal analysis
def: "A technique in which the temperature difference between a substance and a reference material is measured as a function of temperature\, while the substance and reference material are subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01710
name: differential viscosity
def: "The derivative \\\(\\eta _\{\\Delta \}\\\) of stress with respect to the rate of shear at a given shear rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01711
name: diffraction
def: "A modification which light \(or electron\, neutron beams\, etc.\) undergoes in passing by the edges of opaque bodies or through narrow slits or in being reflected from ruled surfaces \(or crystalline materials\). The light waves\, owing to their wave-like nature appear to be deflected and produce fringes of parallel light and dark bands corresponding to regions of constructive reinforcement or destructive interference\, respectively\, of the waves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01712
name: diffraction analysis
def: "The application of diffraction techniques \(X-rays\, electrons\, neutrons\) which are sometimes used to identify the presence of certain solid aerosols and dust particles through the characteristic diffraction patterns which result from each unique crystal structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01713
name: diffused junction semiconductor detector
def: "A semiconductor detector in which the p–n or n–p junction is produced by diffusion of donor or acceptor impurities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01714
name: diffuse layer
def: "The region in which non-specifically adsorbed ions are accumulated and distributed by the contrasting action of the electric field and thermal motion. Counter and co-ions in immediate contact with the surface are said to be located in the Stern layer. Ions farther away from the surface form the diffuse layer or Gouy layer."
synonym: "Gouy layer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01715
name: diffuser
def: "A porous plate or tube\, commonly made of carborundum\, alundum or silica sand\, through which air is forced and divided into minute bubbles for diffusion in liquids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01716
name: diffusion
def: "The spreading or scattering of a gaseous or liquid material. eddy diffusion in the atmosphere is the process of transport of gases due to turbulent mixing in the presence of a composition gradient. Molecular diffusion is the net transport of molecules which results from their molecular motions alone in the absence of turbulent mixing; it occurs when the concentration gradient of a particular gas in a mixture differs from its equilibrium value. eddy diffusion is the most important mixing process in the lower atmosphere\, while molecular diffusion becomes significant at the lower pressures of the upper atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01717
name: diffusional transition
def: "A transition that requires the rearrangement of atoms\, ions or molecules in a manner that cannot be accomplished by a cooperative atomic displacement; it may require the movement of atoms\, ions or molecules over distances significantly larger than a unit cell. Example\: The transition of graphite \(hexagonal sheets of three-coordinated carbon atoms\) to diamond \(infinite three-dimensional framework of four-coordinated carbon atoms\) at high temperature and pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01718
name: diffusion battery
def: "An aerosol sizing instrument for particles with diameters below \\\(0.2\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\). The fractionation is based on different diffusivities of the small particles and their deposition on the walls of the long parallel or circular channels\, formed by equally spaced plates\, bundles of small bore parallel tubes or sets of stainless wire screens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01719
name: diffusion coefficient
def: "Proportionality constant \\\(D\\\)\, relating the flux of amount of entities B to their concentration gradient \\\(J_\{n\}=- D\\ \nabla c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01722
name: diffusion current
def: "A faradaic current whose magnitude is controlled by the rate at which a reactant in an electrochemical process diffuses toward an electrode-solution interface \(and\, sometimes\, by the rate at which a product diffuses away from that interface\). For the reaction mechanism  there are two common situations in which a diffusion current can be observed. In one\, the rate of formation of B from electroinactive C is small and the current is governed by the rate of diffusion of B toward the electrode surface. In the other\, C predominates at equilibrium in the bulk of the solution\, but its transformation into B is fast; C diffuses to the vicinity of the electrode surface and is there rapidly converted into B\, which is reduced."
synonym: "diffusion-controlled current" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01723
name: diffusion current constant
def: "The empirical quantity defined by \\\[i=\\frac\{i_\{\\rm\{d\}\,l\}\}\{c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ m^\{\\frac\{2\}\{3\}\}\\ t_\{1\}^\{\\frac\{1\}\{6\}\}\}\\\] the equation where \\\(i_\{\\rm\{d\}\,l\}\\\) = limiting diffusion current\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) = bulk concentration of the substance B whose reduction or oxidation is responsible for the wave in question\, \\\(m\\\) = average rate of \(mass\) flow of mercury \(or other liquid metal\) and \\\(t_\{1\}\\\) = drop time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01725
name: diffusion layer
def: "The region in the vicinity of an electrode where the concentrations are different from their value in the bulk solution. The definition of the thickness of the diffusion layer is arbitrary because the concentration approaches asymptotically the value \\\(c_\{0\}\\\) in the bulk solution \(see diagram\)."
synonym: "concentration boundary layer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01726
name: diffusion layer thickness
def: "Defined by the equation\: \\\[\\delta = \\frac\{D\}\{k_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\}=\\frac\{n\\ F\\ D\\ c\\ A\}\{l_\{i\}\}\\\] where \\\(D\\\) is the diffusion coefficient\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) is the heterogeneous diffusion rate constant\, \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant\, \\\(c\\\) is the concentration\, \\\(A\\\) is the geometric area of the electrode\, \\\(l_\{i\}\\\) is the limiting current and \\\(n\\\) is the charge number of the cell reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01728
name: diffusionless transition
def: "A transition that does not involve long-range diffusion of atomic species over distances significantly greater than a typical interatomic distance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01729
name: diffusion potential
def: "For an ideal dilute solution\, \\\(\\Delta \\mathit\{\\Phi \}_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) is the integral of \\\(\nabla \\mathit\{\\Phi \}\\\) \(given by the following equation\) across the boundary between two regions of different concentrations. \\\[\nabla \\mathit\{\\Phi \}=\\frac\{R\\ T\\ \\sum D_\{i\}\\ z_\{i\}\\ \nabla c_\{i\}\}\{F\\ \\sum s_\{i\}^\{2\}\\ D_\{i\}\\ c_\{i\}\}\\\] where \\\(D_\{i\}\\\) is the diffusion coefficient of species \\\(i\\\)\, \\\(z_\{i\}\\\) is the charge number of species \\\(i\\\)\, \\\(c_\{i\}\\\) is the concentration of species \\\(i\\\)\, \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, and \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01730
name: dihedral angle
def: "The angle between two intersecting planes on a third plane normal to the intersection of the two planes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01731
name: diisotactic polymer
def: "An isotactic polymer that contains two chiral or prochiral atoms with defined stereochemistry in the main chain of the configurational base unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01732
name: dilatancy
def: "An increase in volume of the system which usually accompanies shear thickening. This term has formerly been used for the phenomenon of shear thickening."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01733
name: dilational transition
def: "A transition in which the crystal structure is dilated \(or compressed\) along one \(or more\) crystallographic direction\(s\) while the symmetry about that direction is retained. Examples\:The transition of a $\\ce\{CsCl\}$-type structure to a rock salt structure in which dilation occurs along the three-fold axis.The transition at \\\(T_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\) in quenched $\\ce\{NiS\}$ in which volume expansion occurs without change of symmetry on going from a metallic state \(\\\(T > T_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\)\) to a semiconducting state \(\\\(T < T_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "dilatational transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01735
name: diluent
def: "The liquid or homogeneous mixture of liquids in which extractant\(s\) and possible modifier\(s\) may be dissolved to form the solvent phase."
synonym: "cosolvent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01736
name: diluent gas
def: "A gas of known quality introduced for analytical purposes so that it quantitatively lowers the concentration of the components of a gaseous sample; this may also be the complementary gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01738
name: diluter
def: "A device used to add a measured volume or amount of the sample to a measured volume of a diluent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01739
name: dilute solution
def: "Solution in which the sum of amount fractions of all the solutes is small compared to 1."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01740
name: dilution rate
def: "The ratio of the ingoing volume flow rate \(\\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}V\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\)\) and the culture volume \(\\\(V\\\)\). In continuous fermentation\, a measure of a rate at which the existing medium is replaced with fresh medium\, \\\(D\\\) is the reciprocal of the hydraulic retention time \(\\\(\\mathrm\{HRT\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01741
name: dimension
def: "Product of powers of the base dimensions \(the dimensions attributed to the base quantities of a system\) which parallels the expression of the quantity in terms of the base quantities. When all the exponents of the base dimensions are zero the quantity has the dimension one and is often called dimensionless."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01742
name: dimensionless quantities
def: "Quantities of dimension one\, or numerical quantities\, are numbers which may be integers \(or exceptionally half-integers\) when obtained by counting \(e.g. number of molecules\, quantum numbers\, ...\)\, rational numbers such as ratios\, factors and fractions when obtained as ratios of two quantities of the same kind or real numbers when obtained by taking logarithms \(e.g. absorbance\, power levels\, ...\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01743
name: dimeric ion
def: "An ion formed either when a chemical species exists in the vapour as a dimer and can be detected as such\, or when a molecular ion can attach to a neutral molecule within the ion source to form an ion such as $\\ce\{\[M2\]^\{+.\}\}$ where $\\ce\{M\}$ represents the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01744
name: dimerization
def: "The transformation of a molecular entity $\\ce\{A\}$ to give a molecular entity $\\ce\{A2\}$ . For example\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01745
name: di-π-methane rearrangement
def: "Photochemical reaction of a molecular entity comprising two $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}systems\}$\, separated by a saturated carbon atom \(a 1\,4-diene or an allyl-substituted aromatic analogue\)\, to form an ene- \(or aryl-\) substituted cyclopropane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01746
name: Dimroth–Reichardt <em>E</em><sub>T</sub> parameter
def: "A measure of the ionizing power \(loosely polarity\) of a solvent\, based on the maximum wavenumber of the longest wavelength electronic absorption band of\:  in a given solvent. \\\(E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\)\, called \\\(E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\(30\)\\\) by its originators\, is given by\: \\\[E_\{\\rm\{T\}\} = 2.859\\times 10^\{-3\}\\ \nu = 2.859\\times 10^\{4\}\\ \\lambda ^\{-1\}\\\] where \\\(E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\) is in \\\(\\pu\{kcal mol-1\}\\\)\, \\\(\nu \\\) is in \\\(\\pu\{cm-1\}\\\) and \\\(\\lambda \\\) is in \\\(\\rm\{nm\}\\\). The so-called normalized \\\(E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}^\{\\rm\{N\}\}\\\) scale is defined as\: \\\[E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}^\{\\rm\{N\}\}=\\frac\{E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{solvent\}\\right\)- E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{Si\}\\rm\{Me\}_\{4\}\\right\)\}\{E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{water\}\\right\)- E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{Si\}\\rm\{Me\}_\{4\}\\right\)\}=\\frac\{E_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{solvent\}\\right\)- 30.7\}\{32.4\}\\\]"
synonym: "ET-value" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01747
name: diode laser
def: "A solid state laser in which the active medium is the p-n junction between p-type and n-type semiconductor host materials. This term is preferred over the sometimes-used term 'semiconductor laser'."
synonym: "semiconductor laser" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01748
name: diols
def: "Compounds that contain two hydroxy groups\, generally assumed to be\, but not necessarily\, alcoholic. Aliphatic diols are also called glycols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01749
name: diosphenols
def: "Cyclic $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}diketones\}$\, which exist predominantly in an enolic form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01750
name: dioxin
def: "Colloquial \(short\) name of a toxic by-product \(and sometimes contaminant\) of herbicides; the full name of this species is\: 2\,3\,7\,8-tetrachlorodibenzo\[b\,e\]\[1\,4\]-dioxin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01751
name: dipolar aprotic solvent
def: "A solvent with a comparatively high relative permittivity \(or dielectric constant\)\, greater than ca. 15\, and a sizable permanent dipole moment\, that cannot donate suitably labile hydrogen atoms to form strong hydrogen bonds\, e.g. dimethyl sulfoxide. The term \(and its alternative 'polar aprotic solvent'\) is a misnomer and is therefore discouraged. Such solvents are usually not aprotic but protophilic \(and at most weakly protogenic\). In describing a solvent it is better to be explicit about its essential properties\, e.g. dipolar and non-protogenic."
synonym: "polar aprotic solvent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01752
name: dipolar bond
def: "A bond formed \(actually or conceptually\) by coordination of two neutral moieties\, the combination of which results in charge-separated structures\, e.g.  The term is preferred to the obsolescent synonyms 'coordinate link'\, 'coordinate covalence'\, 'dative bond'\, 'semipolar bond'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01753
name: dipolar compounds
def: "Electrically neutral molecules carrying a positive and a negative charge in one of their major canonical descriptions. In most dipolar compounds the charges are delocalized; however the term is also applied to species where this is not the case. 1\,2-Dipolar compounds have the opposite charges on adjacent atoms. The term 1\,3-dipolar compounds is used for those in which a significant canonical resonance form can be represented by a separation of charge over three atoms \(in connection with 1\,3-dipolar cycloadditions\). Subclasses of 1\,3-dipolar compounds include\:\nAllyl type $\\ce\{X=Y^\{+\}-Z^\{−\}\}$$\\ce\{\}$$\\ce\{X^\{−\}-Y^\{+\}=Z\}$$\\ce\{\}$$\\ce\{X^\{+\}-Y-Z^\{−\}\}$$\\ce\{\}$$\\ce\{X^\{−\}=Y-Z^\{+\}\}$ $\(\\ce\{X\, Z = C\, N\, or O; Y = N or O\}\)$See\: azo imides\, azomethine imides\, azomethine ylides\, azoxy compounds\, carbonyl imides\, carbonyl oxides\, carbonyl ylides\, nitro compounds\, nitrones\nPropargyl type $\\ce\{X#N^\{+\}-Z^\{−\}\}$$\\ce\{\}$$\\ce\{X^\{−\}=N^\{+\}=Z\}$$\\ce\{\}$$\\ce\{X^\{−\}=N-Z^\{+\}\}$$\\ce\{\}$$\\ce\{X-N=Z\}$ $\(\\ce\{X = C or O\, Z = C\, N\, or O\}\)$See\: azides\, diazo compounds\, nitrile imides\, nitrile oxides\, nitrile ylides\, nitrilium betaines\nCarbene type $\\ce\{X\{\:\}-C=Z\}$$\\ce\{\}$$\\ce\{X^\{+\}=C-Z^\{−\}\}$ $\(\\ce\{X = C or N; Z = C\, N\, or O\}\)$See\: acyl carbenes\, betaines\, imidoyl carbenes\, vinyl carbenes"

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01756
name: dipolar mechanism
def: "Synonymous with Förster excitation transfer"

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01758
name: dipole–dipole interaction
def: "Intermolecular or intramolecular interaction between molecules or groups having a permanent electric dipole moment. The strength of the interaction depends on the distance and relative orientation of the dipoles. The term applies also to intramolecular interactions between bonds having permanent dipole moments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01760
name: dipole length
def: "Electric dipole moment divided by the elementary charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01763
name: dipyrrins
def: "Compounds containing two pyrrole rings linked through a methine\, $\\ce\{–CH=\}$\, group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01765
name: diradicals
def: "Molecular species having two unpaired electrons\, in which at least two different electronic states with different multiplicities \[electron-paired \(singlet state\) or electron-unpaired \(triplet state\)\] can be identified\, e.g. $\\ce\{H2C^\{.\}–CH2–C^\{.\}H2\}$ propane-1\,3-diyl \(trimethylene\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01766
name: direct amplification
def: "A type of amplification reaction where the constituent to be determined is amplified directly and it is this constituent which is finally measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01767
name: direct current
def: "This term and its symbol\, \\\(i_\{\\mathrm\{dc\}\}\\\) or \\\(I_\{\\mathrm\{dc\}\}\\\)\, should be used \(in preference to 'current' alone\) only to denote the steady \(time-dependent\) component of a current that also has a periodic component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01769
name: direct fission yield
def: "The fraction of fissions giving rise to a particular nuclide before any nuclear decay has occurred."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01770
name: direct-injection burner
def: "A burner in which oxidant and fuel emerge from separate ports and are mixed above the burner orifices through their turbulent motion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01771
name: direct-injection enthalpimetry
def: "An analytical method in which a reactant is injected into a calorimetric vessel containing another reagent. The enthalpy change of the ensuing reaction is measured and directly related to the amount of the limiting reagent \(usually the analyte\). If the experiment determines information other than amounts of analytes\, an acceptable synonym is 'batch injection calorimetry'. Other terms which have been used in the literature but are not recommended include the 'concentration thermometric technique' and the 'direct thermometric method'."
synonym: "batch injection calorimetry" []
synonym: "concentration thermometric technique" []
synonym: "direct thermometric method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01772
name: direct isotope dilution analysis
def: "Isotope dilution analysis used for the determination of a non-radioactive element with the aid of one of its radionuclides."
synonym: "direct radiochemical isotope dilution analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01773
name: direct reaction
def: "A chemical process in which the reactive complex has a lifetime that is shorter than its period of rotation. In a molecular-beam experiment the reaction products of a direct reaction are scattered\, with reference to the centre of mass of the system\, in preferred directions rather than at random. Some direct reactions are impulsive which means that there is an energy exchange that is very fast compared to the vibrational time scale. A direct reaction is to be contrasted with an indirect reaction\, also known as a complex-mode reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01775
name: direct transfer gas flow system
def: "A system in which the purge gas transports the analyte directly to the sampling or excitation sources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01776
name: disaccharides
def: "Compounds in which two monosaccharides are joined by a glycosidic bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01777
name: discomfort threshold
def: "The lowest value \(e.g. concentration of an impurity\, etc.\) at which a sensation of discomfort is perceived; a measure which varies from person to person."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01778
name: discontinuous analyser
def: "An analyser with at least one discontinuous subassembly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01779
name: discontinuous indication
def: "Indication related to the concentration during intervals of time which are not continuous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01780
name: discontinuous measuring cell
def: "A measuring cell which operates intermittently and not necessarily at fixed time intervals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01782
name: discontinuous precipitation
def: "A diffusional reaction in a multi-component system in which structural and compositional changes occur in regions immediately adjacent to the advancing interface. The parent phase remains unchanged until swept over by the interface; the transition is complete in regions over which the interface has passed. Synonymous with nucleation and growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01783
name: discontinuous simultaneous techniques
def: "The application of coupled techniques to the same sample when sampling for the second technique is discontinuous\, e.g. discontinuous thermal analysis and gas chromatography\, when discrete portions of evolved volatile\(s\) are collected from the sample situation in the instrument used for the first technique."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01785
name: discriminator
def: "A basic function unit comprising an electronic circuit which gives an output pulse for each input pulse whose amplitude lies above a given threshold value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01787
name: disintegration energy
def: "Energy liberated in a decay process and carried as kinetic energy of the emitted alpha or beta particle and the recoil energy of the product atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01790
name: disjoining pressure
def: "The force \\\(\\mathit\{\\Pi\} _\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) per unit area which can be obtained as the derivative of \\\(-g_\{\\rm\{t\}\}\\\) with respect to the distance\, where \\\(g_\{\\rm\{t\}\}\\\) is the total Gibbs energy of interaction per unit area of each of the two flat and parallel plates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01792
name: dispenser
def: "A device used to deliver a measured amount of material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01793
name: dispersion
def: "Dispersion of a material \\\(= \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}n\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\lambda\}\\\) where \\\(n\\\) = refractive index and \\\(\\lambda \\\) wavelength; angular dispersion \\\(= \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\mathit\{\\varPhi\}\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\lambda\}\\\) where \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\\\) = angle; and linear dispersion \\\(= \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}x\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\lambda\}\\\) where \\\(x\\\) = separation of spectral lines. The reciprocal of the last-named quantity is more frequently used \\\(\\left\(\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\lambda\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}x\}\\right\)\\\)\, and is commonly expressed in \\\(\\pu\{\\unicode\{x212B\} mm-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01794
name: dispersion
def: "The dilution of a pollutant by spreading in the atmosphere due to diffusion or turbulent action \(eddy diffusion\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01797
name: displacement chromatography
def: "A procedure in which the mobile phase contains a compound \(the displacer\) more strongly retained than the components of the sample under examination. The sample is fed into the system as a finite slug."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01798
name: displacive transition
def: "A transition in which a displacement of one or more kinds of atoms or ions in a crystal structure changes the lengths and/or directions of bonds\, without severing the primary bonds. Examples\: The transitions of the low-temperature polymorphs of $\\ce\{SiO2\}$ \(quartz\, tridymite and cristobalite\) to their respective high-temperature polymorphs\, which involve distortions or rotations of the $\\ce\{SiO4\}$ tetrahedra. Also Jahn–Teller and ferroic transitions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01799
name: disproportionation
def: "Any chemical reaction of the type \, where A\, A' and A'' are different chemical species. For example\:  The reverse of disproportionation is called comproportionation. A special case of disproportionation \(or 'dismutation'\) is 'radical disproportionation'\, exemplified by\:  Reactions of the more general type\:  are also loosely described as 'radical disproportionations'."
synonym: "dismutation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01801
name: dissociation
def: "The separation of a molecular entity into two or more molecular entities \(or any similar separation within a polyatomic molecular entity\). Examples include unimolecular heterolysis and homolysis\, and the separation of the constituents of an ion pair into free ions.   The separation of the constituents of any aggregate of molecular entities.   In both senses dissociation is the reverse of association."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01802
name: dissociation energy
def: "Energy required to dissociate a molecule into two parts. Subscripts 0 and e\, i.e. \\\(D_\{0\}\\\) and \\\(D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\, are used to denote the dissociation from the ground state and potential energy minimum\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01803
name: dissociative adsorption
def: "Adsorption with dissociation into two or more fragments\, both or all of which are bound to the surface of the adsorbent. The process may be homolytic\, as in the chemisorption of hydrogen\: \\\[\\rm\{H\}_\{2\}+2\\rm\{*\}\\rightarrow 2\\rm\{H\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\] where * represents a surface site\, or heterolytic\, as in\: \\\[\\rm\{H\}_\{2\}+\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}+\\rm\{O\}^\{2-\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{H\}^\{-\}\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}+\\rm\{HO\}^\{-\}\\\] where \\\(\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{O\}^\{2- \}\\\) are surface sites in which the ions are of lower coordination than the ions in the bulk phase. Associative desorption is the reverse of dissociative adsorption."
synonym: "dissociative chemisorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01804
name: dissociative ionization
def: "An ionization process in which a gaseous molecule decomposes to form products\, one of which is an ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01805
name: dissociative surface reaction
def: "Dissociative surface reaction and its reverse\, associative surface reaction\, are defined by\: \\\[2^\{\\rm\{*\}\}+\\rm\{C\}_\{2\}\\rm\{H\}_\{5\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm\{H\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}+\\\,^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\{\\rm\{CH\}_\{2\}\\rm\{CH\}_\{2\}\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\] where * represents a surface site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01806
name: dissolution
def: "The mixing of two phases with the formation of one new homogeneous phase \(i.e. the solution\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01808
name: dissymmetry of scattering
def: "The ratio of two Rayleigh ratios for different angles of observation\, i.e. \\\[z\(\\theta _\{1\}\,\\\, \\theta _\{2\}\) = \\frac\{R\(\\theta _\{1\}\)\}\{R\(\\theta _\{2\}\)\}\\\] \\\[\\theta _\{1\} < \\theta _\{2\}\\\] The angles must be specified; in light scattering it is customary to let \\\(\\theta = 1 - \\theta _\{1\}\\\) and\, most frequently\, \\\(\\theta _\{1\} = 45 ^\{\\circ\}\\\) and \\\(\\theta _\{2\} = 135 ^\{\\circ\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01809
name: distonic radical cation
def: "A radical cation in which charge and radical sites are separated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01810
name: distribuend
def: "The substance that is distributed between two immiscible liquids or liquid phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01811
name: distribution
def: "The apportionment of a solute between two phases. The term partition or extraction may also be used in this sense where appropriate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01814
name: distribution constant
def: "The concentration of a component in or on the stationary phase divided by the concentration of the component in the mobile phase. Since in chromatography a component may be present in more than one form \(e.g. associated and dissociated forms\)\, the analytical condition used here refers to the total amount present without regard to the existence of various forms. These terms are also called the distribution coefficients. However\, the present term conforms more closely to the general usage in science."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01815
name: distribution function
def: "A normalized function giving the relative amount of a portion of a polymeric substance with a specific value\, or a range of values\, of a random variable or variables."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01817
name: distribution ratio
def: "The ratio of the total analytical concentration of a solute in the extract \(regardless of its chemical form\) to its total analytical concentration in the other phase."
synonym: "distribution coefficient" []
synonym: "extraction coefficient" []
synonym: "scrubbing coefficient" []
synonym: "stripping coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01818
name: disyndiotactic polymer
def: "A syndiotactic polymer that contains two chiral or prochiral atoms with defined stereochemistry in the main chain of the configurational base unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01819
name: ditactic polymer
def: "A tactic polymer that contains two sites of defined stereoisomerism in the main chain of the configurational base unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01820
name: diterpenoids
def: "Terpenoids having a $\\ce\{C20\}$ skeleton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01822
name: diurnal variation
def: "Indicates variations which follow a distinctive pattern which recurs with a daily cycle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01823
name: dividing surface
def: "A surface\, usually taken to be a hyperplane\, constructed at right angles to the minimum-energy path on a potential-energy surface. In conventional transition-state theory it passes through the highest point on the minimum-energy path. In generalized versions of transition-state theory the dividing surface can be at other positions; in variational transition-state theory the position of the dividing surface is varied so as to get a better estimate of the rate constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01824
name: DM-interference
def: "Interference in which the analytical signal is influenced by a different mechanism than the analyte e.g. the formation of non-dissociating species in emission spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01826
name: DNA probe
def: "A short sequence of DNA labelled isotopically or chemically that is used for the detection of a complementary nucleotide sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01827
name: Dobson unit
def: "Unit sometimes used in the description of the total ozone in a column of air overhead. It is given as the thickness \(in units of \\\(10^\{-3\}\\ \\rm\{cm\}\\\) or \\\(10^\{-5\}\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\)\) of that layer which would be formed by the total ozone in a column reduced to the pressure of \\\(760\\ \\rm\{Torr\}\\\) and temperature of \\\(0\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01828
name: <i>dodecahedro</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote eight atoms bound into a dodecahedron with triangular faces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01829
name: dolichols
def: "A group of prenol derivatives in which \\\(n\\\) in the general formula $\\ce\{H–\[CH2–C\(CH3\)=CH–CH2\]_\{n\}–OH\}$ is greater than four and in which the residue that carries the hydroxy group is saturated\, i.e. 2\,3-dihydropolyprenols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01830
name: Donnan emf
def: "The potential difference \\\(E_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\) at zero electric current between two identical salt bridges\, usually saturated $\\ce\{KCl\}$ bridges \(conveniently measured by linking them to two identical electrodes\) inserted into two solutions in Donnan equilibrium."
synonym: "Donnan potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01831
name: Donnan equilibrium
def: "The equilibrium characterized by an unequal distribution of diffusible ions between two ionic solutions \(one or both of the solutions may be gelled\) separated by a membrane which is impermeable to at least one of the ionic species present\, e.g. because they are too large to pass through the pores of the membrane. The membrane may be replaced by other kinds of restraint\, such as gelation\, the field of gravity\, etc.\, which prevent some ionic components from moving from one phase to the other\, but allow other components to do so."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01833
name: donor number
def: "A quantitative measure of Lewis basicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01834
name: doping
def: "The action of adding a small amount of foreign atoms to form a solid solution in the lattice of a non-metallic catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01835
name: Doppler broadening
def: "Broadening due to the random motion of the emitting or absorbing atoms. A Doppler broadened line has a Gaussian shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01837
name: dosage
def: "As applied to an air pollutant in an exposure chamber\, dosage is commonly defined as the concentration of the pollutant times the duration of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01839
name: dose
def: "A general term denoting the quantity of radiation or energy absorbed. For special purposes\, it must be appropriately qualified e.g. absorbed\, maximum permissible\, mean lethal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01841
name: dose equivalent
def: "Product of the absorbed dose of ionizing radiation\, a quality factor and a modifying factor to take into account the effect of radiation on different biological tissues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01842
name: double-beam spectrometer
def: "Double \(spectral\) beam spectrometers are used where two samples are to be excited by two different wavelengths. A double- \(synchronous\) beam spectrometer is a luminescence spectrometer in which both the excitation and emission monochromators scan the excitation and emission spectra simultaneously\, usually with a fixed wavelength difference between excitation and emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01843
name: double escape peak
def: "In a gamma-ray spectrum\, the peak due to pair production in the detector and escape\, from the sensitive part of the detector\, of two photons of \\\(511\\ \\rm\{keV\}\\\) resulting from annihilation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01844
name: double-focusing mass spectrograph
def: "An instrument which uses both direction and velocity focusing\, and therefore an ion beam initially diverging in direction and containing ions of different kinetic energies is separated into beams according to the quotient mass/charge\, these beams being focused on to a photographic plate or film."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01845
name: double-focusing mass spectrometer
def: "An instrument which uses both direction and velocity focusing\, and therefore an ion beam of a given mass/charge is brought to a focus when the ion beam is initially diverging and contains ions of the same mass and charge with different translational energies. The ion beam is measured electrically."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01847
name: double-layer current
def: "The non-faradaic current associated with the charging of the electrical double layer at an electrode-solution interface\, given by\: \\\[i_\{\\mathrm\{DL\}\}=\\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}\(\\sigma \\ A\)\}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}\\\] where \\\(\\sigma \\\) = surface charge density of the double layer\, \\\(A\\\) = area of the electrode-solution interface and \\\(t\\\) = time. Capital letters should be used as subscripts to avoid the possibility of confusing this symbol with that for the limiting diffusion current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01849
name: double-strand chain
def: "A chain that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through three or four atoms\, two on one side and either one or two on the other side of each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01850
name: double-strand copolymer
def: "A copolymer the macromolecules of which are double-strand macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01851
name: double-strand macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through three or four atoms\, two on one side and either one or two on the other side of each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01852
name: double-strand polymer
def: "A polymer the macromolecules of which are double-strand macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01853
name: doublet state
def: "A state having a total electron spin quantum number equal to \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01854
name: double-wavelength spectroscopy
def: "The effect of spectral background due to impurities\, solvent or radiation scattering may be reduced if the difference in the absorbances of a sample measured at two selected wavelengths is obtained. This is often achieved by repetitively switching from one wavelength to the other. Double-wavelength spectroscopy does this automatically by allowing two beams of radiation of different wavelengths to pass through the cell. One beam is fixed at a longer wavelength and the other measures absorbance while being scanned over a limited wavelength range at shorter wavelengths."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01855
name: doubling time
def: "The time \(\\\(\\min \\\)\, \\\(\\mathrm\{h\}\\\)\) required for a cell population to double either the number of cells or the active cell mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01857
name: down-time
def: "The loss of time that should be available for analysis. This might be due to breakdown\, maintenance or other factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01858
name: downwash
def: "As applied to the action of chimney gases\, it is the downward motion of the chimney gases brought on by eddies which form in the lee of a chimney when the wind is blowing. It may result in bringing the flue gases to the ground prematurely."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01859
name: drift
def: "A slow non-random change in signal with time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01860
name: driving force
def: "The decrease in Gibbs energy on going from the reactants to the products of a chemical reaction \(\\\(-\\Delta G\\\)\)."
synonym: "affinity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01861
name: droplet
def: "A small liquid particle. The size of droplets encountered in the atmosphere extends over a wide range; e.g. liquid aerosol solutions which make up the fine particle fraction of continental tropospheric aerosol are usually \\\(2\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) in diameter. Cloud water droplets usually have diameters in the range of \\\(5\{-\}70\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\, while rain droplets commonly have diameters ranging from \\\(0.1\{-\}3\\ \\rm\{mm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01862
name: drop time
def: "The time that elapses between the instants at which two successive drops of liquid metal are detached from the tip of the capillary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01863
name: drug
def: "Any substance which when absorbed into a living organism may modify one or more of its functions. The term is generally accepted for a substance taken for a therapeutic purpose\, but is also commonly used for abused substances. Synonymous with medicine\, pharmaceutical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01864
name: dry bulb temperature
def: "In psychrometry\, the temperature of the gas measured by a dry bulb thermometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01865
name: drying agent
def: "A substance which removes water \(liquid or gaseous\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01866
name: dual substituent-parameter equation
def: "In a general sense\, any equation which expresses substituent effects in terms of two parameters. However\, in practice the term is used more specifically for an equation for summarizing the effects of meta- or para- substituents \(i = m or p\) $\\ce\{\\textbf\{X\}\}$ on chemical reactivity\, spectroscopic properties\, etc. of a probe site $\\ce\{\\textbf\{Y\}\}$ in benzene or other aromatic system. \\\[P^\{\\rm\{i\}\} = \\rho_\{I\}^\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\ \\sigma_\{I\} + \\rho_\{R\}^\{\\rm\{i\}\} \\sigma_\{R\}\\\] \\\(P\\\) is the magnitude of the property $\\ce\{\\textbf\{Y\}\}$ for substituent $\\ce\{\\textbf\{X\}\}$\, expressed relative to the property for $\\ce\{X\} = \\ce\{H\}$; \\\(\\sigma _\{I\}\\\) and \\\(\\sigma _\{R\}\\\) are inductive or polar and resonance substituent constants\, respectively\, there being various scales for \\\(\\sigma _\{R\};\\\,\\rho _\{I\}\\\) and \\\(\\rho _\{R\}\\\) are the corresponding regression coefficients."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01867
name: dust
def: "Small\, dry\, solid particles projected into the air by natural forces\, such as wind\, volcanic eruption and by mechanical or man-made processes such as crushing\, grinding\, milling\, drilling\, demolition\, shoveling\, conveying\, screening\, bagging and sweeping. Dust particles are usually in the size range from about \\\(1\{-\}100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) in diameter and they settle slowly under the influence of gravity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01868
name: dust collector
def: "A device for monitoring dust emissions. Also the equipment used to remove and collect dust from process exhaust gases; this may employ simply sedimentation \(dustfall jars\, coated slides\, papers\, etc.\)\, inertial separation \(cyclones\, impactors\, impingers\, etc.\)\, precipitation \(thermal and electrostatic\) or filtration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01869
name: dust fall
def: "Solid particles in the air which fall to the ground under the influence of gravity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01870
name: dye laser
def: "A CW or pulsed source of coherent radiation in which the active medium is usually a solution of a fluorescent organic molecule \(the dye\) pumped with a suitable pump laser or with a flash lamp. These lasers can be tuned over a large part of the fluorescence band of the dye."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01871
name: dynamic fields mass spectrometer
def: "A mass spectrometer in which the separation of a selected ion beam depends essentially on the use of fields\, or a field\, varying with time. These fields are generally electric."
synonym: "dynamic field mass spectrometer" []
synonym: "dynamic fields mass spectrometer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01874
name: dynamic range
def: "The ratio between the maximum usable indication and the minimum usable indication \(detection limit\). A distinction may be made between the linear dynamic range\, where the response is directly proportional to concentration\, and the dynamic range where the response may be non-linear\, especially at higher concentrations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01875
name: dynamic surface tension
def: "In certain circumstances\, one may measure surface tensions that are different from the equilibrium value. Such a surface tension is called the dynamic surface \(or interfacial\) tension \(\\\(\\gamma ^\{\\rm\{dyn\}\}\\\) or \\\(\\sigma ^\{\\mathrm\{dyn\}\}\\\)\). The equilibrium value is then called the static surface \(or interfacial\) tension \(\\\(\\gamma ^\{\\rm\{st\}\}\\\) or \\\(\\sigma ^\{\\mathrm\{st\}\}\\\)\). The modifying signs may be omitted if there is no danger of ambiguity."
synonym: "dynamic interfacial tension" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01876
name: dynamic thermomechanometry
def: "A technique in which the dynamic modulus and/or damping of a substance under oscillatory load is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01877
name: dynamic viscosity
def: "For a laminar flow of a fluid the ratio of the shear stress to the velocity gradient perpendicular to the plane of shear."
synonym: "viscosity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01878
name: dyne
def: "cgs unit of force\, \\\(\\rm\{dyn\} = 10^\{-5\}\\ \\rm\{N\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01879
name: dyotropic rearrangement
def: "An uncatalysed process in which two $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ simultaneously migrate intramolecularly\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:D01880
name: dypnones
def: "1\,3-Diphenylbut-2-en-1-one\, $\\ce\{PhC\(=O\)CH=C\(CH3\)Ph\}$\, and its ring-substituted derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06810
name: dose
def: "Total quantity of a substance administered to\, taken up\, or absorbed by an organism\, organ\, or tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06866
name: disruptor
def: "A chemical group used to disrupt the linearity of the backbone of molecules MCPLCs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06874
name: downstream
def: "Side of a membrane from which permeate emerges."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06875
name: dynamic membrane formation
def: "Process in which an active layer is formed on the membrane surface by the deposition of substances contained in the fluid being treated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06889
name: Donnan exclusion
def: "Reduction in concentration of mobile ions within an ion exchange membrane due to the presence of fixed ions of the same sign as the mobile ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06906
name: dose-response and dose-effect relationships
def: "The graph of the relation between dose and the proportion of individuals responding with an all-or-none effect; it is essentially the graph of the probability of an occurrence \(or the proportion of a population exhibiting an effect\) against dose. Typical examples of such all-or-none effects are mortality or the incidence of cancer. The dose-effect curve is the graph of the relation between dose and the magnitude of the biological change produced measured in appropriate units. It applies to measurable changes giving a graded response to increasing doses of a drug or xenobiotic. It represents the effect on an individual animal or person\, when biological variation is taken into account. An example is the increased effect of lead on the haem synthesis\, e.g.\, on activity of the enzyme 6-amino laevulinic acid dehydratase in blood serum or coproporphyrin levels in urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06932
name: distribution isotherm
def: "The equilibrium relation between the concentration of a sample component in the stationary phase \\\(c_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\)\, and in the mobile phase \\\(c_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\)\, expressed as a function \\\(c_\{\\rm\{S\}\}=f\(c_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\)\\\). In some versions of chromatography\, a distribution isotherm can be seen as a partition isotherm\, an adsorption isotherm\, or a combination of these\, depending on the mechanism of separation."
synonym: "extraction isotherm" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06957
name: discriminant analysis
def: "Discriminant analysis is a statistical technique to find a set of descriptors which can be used to detect and rationalize separation between activity classes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06958
name: docking studies
def: "Docking studies are computational techniques for the exploration of the possible binding modes of a substrate to a given receptor\, enzyme or other binding site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06959
name: three-dimensional quantitative structure–activity relationships
def: "Three-dimensional quantitative structure-activity relationships \(3D-QSAR\) involve the analysis of the quantitative relationship between the biological activity of a set of compounds and their three-dimensional properties using statistical correlation methods."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT06960
name: drug design
def: "Drug design includes not only ligand design\, but also pharmacokinetics and toxicity\, which are mostly beyond the possibilities of structure- and/or computer-aided design. Nevertheless\, appropriate chemometric tools\, including experimental design and multivariate statistics\, can be of value in the planning and evaluation of pharmacokinetics and toxicological experiments and results. Drug design is most often used instead of the correct term 'Ligand Design'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07018
name: degenerate orbitals
def: "Orbitals whose energy levels are equal in the absence of external fields. Any linear combination of the functions corresponding to a given set of degenerate orbitals serves as an equivalent representation of this set of orbitals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07019
name: delocalization
def: "Redistribution of the valence-shell electron density throughout a molecular entity as compared with some localized models \(individual atoms in their valence states\, separated bonds or fragments\). Different topological modes of the electron delocalization include\: ribbon delocalization of either $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ or $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ \(i.e.\, electrons occupying respectively $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}$\)surface delocalization of $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ occurring through an overlap of radially oriented $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}$ of a cyclic molecule\, as is the case of cyclopropane; andvolume delocalization of $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ through an overlap of $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}$ directed inside a molecular polyhedron\, as is the case in tetrahedrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07020
name: delocalization energy
def: "The difference between the actual $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ energy of a molecular entity and the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ energy of a hypothetical species with a localized form of the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ system. These energies are normally evaluated within Hückel molecular orbital theory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07021
name: dynamic reaction path
def: "A classical trajectory method based on molecular orbital calculations which determines atomic accelerations\, velocities and positions using the energy gradient\, and does not require prior knowledge of the potential energy surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07203
name: dopant
def: "Charge-transfer agent used to generate\, by oxidation or reduction\, positive or negative charges in an intrinsically conducting polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07204
name: doping
def: "Oxidation or reduction process brought about by a dopant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07352
name: DAS
def: "Acronym for decay-associated spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07353
name: Dauben–Salem–Turro rules
def: "Set of rules proposed in 1975 to classify photochemical reactions based on correlation diagrams and under the assumption that all photochemical processes are controlled by the generation of primary products with characteristics of diradicals\, i.e.\, considering the physical fact that the initial single excitation separates two electrons of a pair into two distinct orbitals. The reactions are classified according to their topicity\, i.e.\, the total number and nature of available radical sites generated in the primary photochemical product."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07354
name: de Mayo reaction
def: "Photochemical process leading to the formation of cyclobutanols by cycloaddition of an alkene to a 1\,3-dicarbonyl-enol. The enol originates in most cases from the tautomerization of a 1\,3-dicarbonyl compound. The products of the de Mayo reaction are susceptible to undergo a retro-aldol ring opening resulting in the formation of 1\,5-dicarbonyl compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07356
name: dichroic mirror
def: "A mirror used to reflect light selectively according to its wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07357
name: dichroism
def: "Dependence of absorbance of a sample on the type of polarization of the measuring beam. This polarization may be linear\, corresponding to linear dichroism \(LD\) in which the difference in absorption for two perpendicularly linearly polarized beams is measured\, \\\(\\Delta A_\{\\rm\{l\}\} = A_\{\\rm\{Z\}\} - A_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\\\)\, or circular dichroism \(CD\) in which the difference in absorption for left minus right circularly polarized beams is measured\, \\\(\\Delta A_\{\\rm\{C\}\} = A_\{\\rm\{L\}\} - A_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07358
name: diradicaloid
def: "Diradical-like. Often used to refer to a species in which the two radical centres interact significantly."
synonym: "biradicaloid" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07360
name: di-π-silane rearrangement
def: "Silicon version of the di-π-methane rearrangement with a $\\ce\{Si\}$ atom at position 3 of the basic 1\,4-diene chromophore."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07361
name: dose
def: "Energy or amount of photons absorbed per volume \(or per mass\) by an irradiated object during a particular exposure time. SI units are \\\(\\pu\{J m-3\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{J g-1\}\\\) and \\\(\\pu\{mol m-3\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{mol g-1\}\\\)\, respectively. Common units are \\\(\\pu\{einstein m-3\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{einstein g-1\}\\\)\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07362
name: downconversion
def: "Process by which a photon with frequency \\\(\nu _\{1\}\\\) interacts with a non-linear medium and splits into two simultaneously emitted photons with frequencies \\\(\nu _\{2\}\\\) and \\\(\nu _\{3\}\\\) so that the energy is conserved\: \\\[\nu _\{1\}=\nu _\{2\}+\nu _\{3\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07363
name: driving force
def: "Term widely used to indicate the negative of the standard Gibbs energy change for outer-sphere electron transfer \(\\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{ET\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07364
name: Drude–Nernst equation
def: "Equation describing the contraction \(\\\(\\Delta V_\{\\rm\{el\}\}\\\)\) taking place in a dielectric medium of relative static permittivity \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) \(formerly called dielectric constant\) upon introduction of an ion of charge number \\\(z\\\) and radius \\\(r\\\)\: \\\[\\Delta V_\{\\rm\{el\}\} = -\\frac\{\\left \( z\\\,e \\right \)^\{2\}\}\{2\\\,r\\\,\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}\\frac\{\\partial\\left \( \\rm\{ln\}\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\right\)\}\{\\partial\\\,p\}\\\] with \\\(e\\\) the elementary charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07365
name: dual fluorescence
def: "Systems or molecular species showing two fluorescence bands. Several non-trivial molecular properties may be at the basis of a dual fluorescence\, such as conformational equilibrium in the ground state and a mixture of excited states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07366
name: dual-mode photochromism
def: "Photochromism occurring in complex systems and triggered alternatively by two different external stimuli\, such as light and an electric current. In such a case photochromism and 'electrochromism' are mutually regulated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07534
name: drying control chemical additive
def: "Co-solvent included to facilitate the rapid drying of gels without cracking."

[Term]
id: goldbook:DT07618
name: densification
def: "Removal of impurities and the elimination of pores from a xerogel to give a material of as near bulk density as possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E00008
name: α-expulsion
def: "A general reaction by which a group attached to the alpha carbon of an excited chromophore is expelled either as an odd electron species or as an anionic species. This reaction should be distinguished from an α-\(alpha-\)cleavage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01882
name: <i>E</i>\, <i>Z</i>
def: "The approved stereodescriptors of stereoisomeric alkenes $\\ce\{R1R2C=CR3R4\}$ \($\\ce\{R1\} \neq \\ce\{R2\} \, \\ce\{R3\} \neq \\ce\{R4\}$\); neither $\\ce\{R1\}$ nor $\\ce\{R2\}$ need be different from $\\ce\{R3\}$ or $\\ce\{R4\}$\)\, cumulenes $\\ce\{R1R2C\(=C=C\)_\{n\}=CR3R4\}$ and related systems e.g. $\\ce\{R1R2C=NOH\}$\, $\\ce\{HON=C\\\{\[CH2\]_\{n\}\\\}2C=NOH\}$. The group of highest CIP priority attached to one of the terminal doubly bonded atoms of the alkene\, oxime\, etc. or cumulene \(i.e. $\\ce\{R1\}$ or $\\ce\{R2\}$\) is compared with the group of highest precedence attached to the other \(i.e. $\\ce\{R3\}$ or $\\ce\{R4\}$\). The stereoisomer is designated as $Z$ \(zusammen = together\) if the groups lie on the same side of a reference plane passing through the double bond and perpendicular to the plane containing the bonds linking the groups to the double-bonded atoms; the other stereoisomer is designated as $E$ \(entgegen = opposite\). The descriptors may be applied to structures with a fractional bond order between one and two; and to double bonds involving elements other than carbon. They are not used to describe ring substitution relationships."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01884
name: eclipsed
def: "Two atoms or groups attached to adjacent atoms are said to be eclipsed if the torsion angle between the three bonds is zero \(or approximately so\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01886
name: eclipsing strain
def: "Intramolecular strain present due to nonbonded interactions between two eclipsed atoms or groups. It is\, for example\, one of the interactions responsible for the existence of a rotational barrier and hence restricted rotation about the $\\ce\{C–C\}$ bond in ethane. It has also been called Pitzer strain\, torsional strain and bond opposition strain."
synonym: "Pitzer strain" []
synonym: "bond opposition strain" []
synonym: "torsional strain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01888
name: eddy
def: "In turbulent fluid motion\, a blob of the fluid that has some definitive character and moves in some way differently from the main flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01889
name: eddy dispersion
def: "The process by which substances are mixed in the atmosphere or in any fluid system due to eddy motion."
synonym: "diffusion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01890
name: educt
def: "Term used mainly in the German literature for starting material \(reactant\). It should be avoided in English\, because there it means 'something that comes out' and not 'something that goes in'. The German use of the term is in fact also incorrect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01891
name: effective cadmium cut-off energy
def: "In a given experimental configuration\, the energy value determined by the condition that the detector response would be unchanged if the cadmium cover surrounding the detector was replaced by a fictitious cover opaque to neutrons with energy below this value and transparent to neutrons with energy above this value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01892
name: effective charge
def: "Change in effective charge is a quantity obtained by comparison of the polar effect of substituents on the free energies of rate or equilibrium processes with that on a standard ionization equilibrium. Provided the effective charges on the states in the standard equilibrium are defined\, then it is possible to measure effective charges for states in the reaction or equilibrium under consideration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01893
name: effective chimney height
def: "A height used for the purposes of calculating the dispersion of emitted gases from a chimney\, and which differs from the real chimney height by an amount which depends on such factors as the exit velocity\, buoyancy effects and wind speed; it may also be affected by the local topography. It denotes the maximum height of the centre of a plume path above the level of the ground. The effective height may be above or below the actual chimney height\, although the former case is most common."
synonym: "stack height" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01894
name: effective half life
def: "For a radioactive substance\, the time required for the amount of that substance in a biological system to be reduced to one half of its value by both radioactive decay and biological processes\, when the rate of removal is approximately exponential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01895
name: effectively infinite thickness
def: "Solid or liquid samples of one gram or more are generally used in X-ray spectroscopy. If the sample is thick enough so that the intensity of characteristic fluorescent radiation is not significantly modified by an increase in thickness\, the sample is described as a bulk sample of effectively infinite thickness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01896
name: effective molarity
def: "The ratio of the first-order rate constant of an intramolecular reaction involving two functional groups within the same molecular entity to the second-order rate constant of an analogous intermolecular elementary reaction. This ratio has the dimension of concentration. The term can also apply to an equilibrium constant."
synonym: "effective concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01898
name: effective theoretical plate number
def: "A number indicative of column performance when resolution is taken into account\: \\\[N=\\frac\{16\\ R_\{\\rm\{s\}\}^\{2\}\}\{\(1- \\alpha \)^\{2\}\}\\\] where \\\(R_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the peak resolution and \\\(\\alpha \\\) is the separation factor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01899
name: effective thermal cross-section
def: "A fictitious cross-section for a specified reaction\, which\, when multiplied by the 2200 metre-per-second flux density\, gives the correct reaction rate for thermal neutrons."
synonym: "Westcott cross-section" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01901
name: effector
def: "A small molecule which increases \(activator\) or decreases \(inhibitor\) the activity of an \(allosteric\) protein by binding to the protein at the regulatory site \(which is different from the substrate-binding catalytic site\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01902
name: efficiency
def: "The ratio between the useful energy delivered or bound and the energy supplied\, i.e. energy output/energy input. It is also used in the sense of a quantitative measure of the relative rate of a given step involving a species with respect to the sum of the rates of all of the parallel steps which depopulate that species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01906
name: efficiency of atomization
def: "The ratio of the amount of analyte that passes through the flame cross-section at the observation height\, as free neutral \(or ionized\) atoms\, to the amount of analyte aspirated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01907
name: efficiency of nebulization
def: "The substance fraction of component entering the flame in the amount of component consumed. The efficiency of nebulization is related to the amount of component and not to the amount of solvent. It cannot be calculated directly from the ratio\: volume rate of sample drained from the spray chamber divided by volume rate of fluid consumed. Corrections usually have to be made to take account of differences in component concentrations in the drained and consumed solutions\, respectively\, because of evaporation of some solvent from mist droplets deposited on the walls."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01908
name: efficiency spectrum
def: "A plot of the efficiency of a step \(\\\(\\eta \\\)\) against wavelength or photon energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01909
name: efflorescence
def: "The reverse of deliquescence\: the drying of a salt solution when the vapour pressure of water in the saturated solution of a substance is greater than the partial pressure of water in the ambient air. Also refers to the loss of water of crystallization from a solid salt such as $\\ce\{Na2CO3.10H2O\}$ ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01910
name: effluent
def: "Any spent liquors or other waste materials which are emitted by a source \(waste from plating shops\, pickling tanks\, sewage treatment plants\, chemical manufacturing plants\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01911
name: effluent
def: "The mobile phase leaving the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01913
name: eighteen-electron rule
def: "An electron-counting rule to which an overwhelming majority of stable diamagnetic transition metal complexes adhere. The number of nonbonded electrons at the metal plus the number of electrons in the metal-ligand bonds should be 18. The 18 electron rule in transition metal chemistry is a full analogue of the 'Lewis octet rule'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01914
name: einstein
def: "One mole of photons. Although widely used\, it is not an IUPAC sanctioned unit. It is sometimes defined as the energy of one mole of photons. This use is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01915
name: elastic collision
def: "A particle \(atom or molecule\) can undergo a change in its state of excitation as a result of collisions with other particles. In an elastic collision an exchange only of kinetic energy takes place between the colliding species; in an inelastic collision there is an interchange between the kinetic energy and the internal energy of the particle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01916
name: elastic scattering
def: "When in a molecular collision there is no transfer of energy among different degrees of freedom\, there is said to be elastic scattering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01917
name: elastic scattering
def: "Radiation may be scattered by its transmission through a medium containing particles. If the scatter results in no significant change in the wavelength relative to the primary radiation it is called elastic scattering. In cases where the scattering centres are small compared to the wavelength of the radiation the elastic scattering is called Rayleigh scattering and Mie scattering if this condition is not fulfilled."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01918
name: electrical arc
def: "A self-sustaining electrical discharge between at least two electrodes\, characterized by a comparatively small cathode fall voltage\, a low burning voltage and a relatively high current density. The burning voltage of an arc is the voltage across the electrode gap during an arc discharge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01921
name: electrical hygrometer
def: "A hygrometer whose sensitive element has electrical properties which vary with the humidity of the gas which traverses the hygrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01923
name: electric charge
def: "Integral of the electric current over time. The smallest electric charge found on its own is the elementary charge\, \\\(e\\\)\, the charge of a proton."
synonym: "charge" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01925
name: electric conductance
def: "Reciprocal of the electric resistance."
synonym: "conductance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01927
name: electric current
def: "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01928
name: electric current density
def: "A vector quantity the scalar product of which with the cross sectional area vector is equal to the electric current. By magnitude it is the electric current divided by the cross sectional area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01929
name: electric dipole moment
def: "Vector quantity\, the vector product of which with the electric field strength\, \\\(E\\\)\, of a homogeneous field is equal to the torque. \\\(T=p\\times E\\\). The direction of the dipole moment is from the negative to the positive charge."
synonym: "dipole moment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01930
name: electric displacement
def: "Product of the permittivity and electric field strength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01931
name: electric field
def: "Force acting on a charge divided by the charge."
synonym: "electric field strength" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01932
name: electric mobility
def: "Speed of ions divided by the electric field strength in the phase concerned."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01933
name: electric polarizability
def: "Tensor quantity relating the induced electric dipole moment\, \\\(p_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)\, to the applied electric field strength\, \\\(E\\\)\, \\\(p_\{\\rm\{i\}\}=\\alpha \\ E\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01934
name: electric potential difference
def: "Difference in the potentials of electrodes on the right and left of a galvanic cell. When \\\(\\Delta V\\\) is positive\, positive charge flows from left to right through the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01935
name: electric potential
def: "At a point\, the work required to bring a charge from infinity to that point in the electric field divided by the charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01936
name: electric resistance
def: "To direct current\, the potential difference divided by the current when there is no electromotive force in the conductor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01938
name: electric sector
def: "An arrangement of two conducting sheets forming a capactor and giving a radial electrostatic field which is used to deflect and focus ion beams of different energies. The capacitor may\, for example\, be cylindrical\, spherical or toroidal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01939
name: electrified interphase
def: "An interphase between phases containing free charged components which are usually accumulated or depleted in the surface region thus giving rise to net charges on the phase. This definition includes the special case when the net charge on each of the phases reduces to zero. Charged components may or may not cross the interface between two phases\, so that interphases may be divided into the limiting types unpolarizable and polarizable\, respectively. Ideally unpolarizable interphases are those for which the exchange of common charged particles between the phases proceeds unhindered. Ideally polarizable interphases are those for which there are no common components between the phases or the exchange of these is hindered. This condition may arise as a result of the equilibrium conditions or from the kinetics of charge transfer and leads to an interphase which is impermeable to electric charge. Real interphases may approach more or less well one of these idealized cases. Polarisibility or non-polarisibility is not an absolute property of an interphase but depends on a number of conditions\, e.g. time scale of the experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01940
name: electroactive substance
def: "In voltammetry and related techniques\, a substance that undergoes a change of oxidation state\, or the breaking or formation of chemical bonds\, in a charge-transfer step. If an electroactive substance B is formed\, in the solution or electrode\, by a chemical reaction from another substance C\, the substance C should be called the precursor of B. In potentiometry with ion-selective electrodes\, a material containing\, or in ion-exchange equilibrium with\, the sensed ion. The electroactive substance is often incorporated in an inert matrix such as poly\(vinyl chloride\) or silicone rubber."
synonym: "depolarizer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01941
name: electrocapillarity
def: "The dependence of the interfacial tension on the electrical state of the interphase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01942
name: electrocapillary equation
def: "A form of the Gibbs adsorption equation which includes an expression of the phenomenon of electrocapillarity\: \\\[s\\ \\mathrm\{d\}T- \\tau \\ \\mathrm\{d\}p+\\mathrm\{d\}\\gamma +\\sigma ^\{\\alpha \}\\ \\mathrm\{d\}E+\\sum \\mathit\{\\Gamma \}_\{j\}\\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\mu _\{j\}=0\\\] where \\\(s\\\) is the surface excess of entropy of unit area of interphase\, \\\(T\\\) is the temperature\, \\\(\\tau \\\) is the thickness or excess volume of unit area of the interphase\, \\\(p\\\) is the external pressure\, \\\(\\gamma \\\) is the interfacial tension\, \\\(\\sigma ^\{\\alpha \}\\\) is the free surface charge density on phase \\\(\\alpha \\\) \(areal amount of charge on the surface of phase \\\(\\alpha \\\)\)\, \\\(E\\\) is the generalized potential\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{j\}\\\) is the surface excess\, \\\(\\mu _\{j\}\\\) is the chemical potential and \\\(j\\\) is an electrically neutral component of one or other of the phases; the sum is over all the components but one in each phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01943
name: electrochemical detector
def: "An electrochemical cell which responds to certain substances in the carrier gas eluting from the column. The electrochemical process may be an oxidation\, reduction or a change in conductivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01944
name: electrochemical method of detection
def: "A method in which either current or potential is measured during an electrochemical reaction. The gas or liquid containing the trace impurity to be analysed is sent through an electrochemical cell containing a liquid or solid electrolyte and in which an electrochemical reaction specific to the impurity takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01945
name: electrochemical potential
def: "Of a substance in a specified phase\, the partial molar Gibbs energy of the substance at the specified electric potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01948
name: electrocyclic reaction
def: "A molecular rearrangement that involves the formation of a $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ between the termini of a fully conjugated linear $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ system \(or a linear fragment of a $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ system\) and a decrease by one in the number of \, or the reverse of that process. For example\:  The stereochemistry of such a process is termed 'conrotatory' or antarafacial if the substituents at the interacting termini of the conjugated system both rotate in the same sense\, e.g.  or 'disrotatory' \(or suprafacial\) if one terminus rotates in a clockwise and the other in a counter-clockwise sense\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01950
name: electrodecantation
def: "Electrodialysis may lead to local differences in concentration and density. Under the influence of gravity these density differences lead to large scale separation of sols of high and of low \(often vanishingly low\) concentrations. This process is called electrodecantation."
synonym: "electrophoresis convection" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01952
name: electrode current density
def: "If the charging current is negligible\, in the case of a single electrode reaction\, the electrode current density \(\\\(\\rm\{c.d.\}\\\)\) of the electric current flowing through the electrode is related to the flux density of a species B by the equation\: \\\[j = n\\ \nu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{-1\}\\ F\\ \\left\(N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\right\)_\{e\}\\\] where \\\(\\left\(N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\right\)_\{e\}\\\) is the normal component of the vector \\\(N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) at the electrode-solution interface\, \\\(n\\\) is the charge number of the electrode reaction and \\\(\nu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the stoichiometric number of species B. The ratio \\\(\\frac\{n\}\{\nu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\) is to be taken as positive if the species B is consumed in a cathodic reaction or produced in an anodic reaction. Otherwise it is to be taken as negative. With the convention that the normal distance vector points into the electrolytic solution\, a cathodic current is then negative\, an anodic current positive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01955
name: electrodeposition
def: "The deposition of dissolved or suspended material by an electric field on an electrode. \(Includes electro-crystallization\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01956
name: electrode potential
def: "Electromotive force of a cell in which the electrode on the left is a standard hydrogen electrode and the electrode on the right is the electrode in question."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01959
name: electrode process
def: "The totality of changes occurring at or near a single electrode during the passage of current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01960
name: electrode reaction
def: "For an electrode process at a given potential the current is controlled by the kinetics of a number of steps which include the transport of reactants to and from the interface and the interfacial reaction itself. The latter\, which is called the electrode reaction\, must always include at least one elementary step in which charge is transferred from one phase to the other\, but may also involve purely chemical steps within the interfacial region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01961
name: electrode reaction rate constants
def: "The electrode reaction rate constants are related to the partial currents by \\\[k_\{\\mathrm\{ox\}\}=\\frac\{l_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}\{n\\ F\\ A\\ \\Pi\\\: c_\{i\}^\{\nu _\{i\}\}\}\\\] \\\[k_\{\\mathrm\{red\}\}=\\frac\{l_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\}\{n\\ F\\ A\\ \\Pi\\\:c_\{i\}^\{\nu _\{i\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(k_\{\\mathrm\{ox\}\}\\\) and \\\(k_\{\\mathrm\{red\}\}\\\) are the rate constants for the oxidizing \(anodic\) and reducing \(cathodic\) reactions respectively\, \\\(n\\\) is the charge number of the cell reaction\, \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant and \\\(A\\\) is the geometric area of the electrode\, the product \\\(\\Pi\\\:c_\{i\}^\{\nu _\{i\}\}\\\) includes all the species \\\(i\\\) which take part in the partial reaction\, \\\(c_\{i\}\\\) is the volume concentration of species \\\(i\\\) and \\\(\nu _\{i\}\\\) is the order of the reaction with respect to species \\\(i\\\). The conditional rate constant of an electrode reaction is the value of the electrode reaction rate constant at the conditional \(formal\) potential of the electrode reaction. When \\\(\\alpha \\\) the transfer coefficient is independent of potential\, \\\[k_\{\\rm\{c\}\} = \\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{ox\}\}\}\{\\rm\{e\}^\{\\frac\{\\alpha _\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\ \(E- E_\{\\rm\{c\}\}^\{0'\}\)\\ n\\ F\}\{\nu \\ R\\ T\}\}\} = \\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{red\}\}\}\{\\rm\{e\}^\{\\frac\{-\\alpha _\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\ \(E- E_\{\\rm\{c\}\}^\{0'\}\)\\ n\\ F\}\{\nu \\ R\\ T\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\alpha _\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\alpha _\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) are the anodic and cathodic transfer coefficients respectively\, \\\(E\\\) is the electric potential difference\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) is the conditional \(formal\) potential\, \\\(\nu \\\) is the stoichiometric number\, \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant and \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature. Similar rate constants can be defined using activities in place of concentrations in the first two equations\, and the standard electrode potential in place of the conditional potential in the latter two equations. This type of rate constant is called the standard rate constant of the electrode reaction. The observable electrode rate constant is the constant of proportionality expressing the dependence of the rate of the electrode reaction on the interfacial concentration of the chemical species involved in the reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01965
name: electrofuge
def: "A leaving group that does not carry away the bonding electron pair. For example\, in the nitration of benzene by $\\ce\{NO2^\{+\}\}$\, $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ is the electrofuge. The adjective of electrofuge is electrofugal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01966
name: electrogenerated chemiluminescence
def: "Luminescence produced by electrode reactions. Also called electroluminescence or electrochemiluminescence."
synonym: "electrochemiluminescence" []
synonym: "electroluminescence" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01967
name: electrographite
def: "A synthetic graphite made by electrical heating of graphitizable carbon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01968
name: electrokinetic potential
def: "Potential drop across the mobile part of the double layer\, that is responsible for electrokinetic phenomena. \\\(\\zeta \\\) is positive if the potential increases from the bulk of the liquid phase towards the interface. In calculating the electrokinetic potential from electrokinetic phenomena it is often asumed that the liquid adhering to the solid wall and the mobile liquid are separated by a sharp shear plane. As long as there is no reliable information on the values of the permittivity and the viscosity in the electrical double layer close to the interface\, the calculation of the electrokinetic potential from electrokinetic experiments remains open to criticism. It is therefore essential to indicate in all cases which equations have been used in the calculation of \\\(\\zeta \\\). It can be shown\, however\, that for the same assumptions about the permittivity and viscosity all electrokinetic phenomena must give the same value for the electrokinetic potential."
synonym: "ζ-potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01972
name: electrolytic hygrometer
def: "Hygrometer using a hygroscopic substance \(for example\, diphosphorus pentaoxide\, $\\ce\{P2O5\}$\) which is transformed into an electrolyte \(phosphoric acid\, $\\ce\{H3PO4\}$\) in contact with the moisture in the gas. The electrolyte \(phosphoric acid\) is electrolysed continuously and the electrolysis current is measured. At a constant flow of the gas to be analysed\, the electrolysis current is a linear function of the water concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01973
name: electromeric effect
def: "A molecular polarizability effect occurring by an intramolecular electron displacement \(sometimes called the 'conjugative mechanism' and\, previously\, the 'tautomeric mechanism'\) characterized by the substitution of one electron pair for another within the same atomic octet of electrons. It can be indicated by curved arrow symbolizing the displacement of electron pairs\, as in\:  which represents the hypothetical electron shift  The term has been deemed obsolescent or even obsolete \(see mesomeric effect\, resonance effect\). It has long been custom to use phrases such as 'enhanced substituent resonance effect' which imply the operation of the electromeric effect\, without using the term\, and various modern theoretical treatments parametrize the response of substituents to 'electronic demand'\, which amounts to considering the electromeric effect together with the inductomeric effect."
synonym: "tautomeric effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01974
name: electromotive force
def: "Energy supplied by a source divided by the electric charge transported through the source. For a galvanic cell it is equal to the electric potential difference for zero current through the cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01975
name: electron
def: "Elementary particle not affected by the strong force having a spin quantum number \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\\)\, a negative elementary charge and a rest mass of \\\(0.000\\ 548\\ 579\\ 903\(13\)\\ \\rm\{u\}\\\)."
synonym: "negaton" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01976
name: electron acceptor
def: "A substance to which an electron may be transferred; for example 1\,4-dinitrobenzene or the dication 1\,1'-dimethyl-4\,4'-bipyridyldiium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01977
name: electron affinity
def: "Energy required to detach an electron from the singly charged negative ion \(energy for the process \). The equivalent more common definition is the energy released \(\\\(E_\{\\rm\{initial\}\} - E_\{\\rm\{final\}\}\\\)\) when an additional electron is attached to a neutral atom or molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01979
name: electron attachment
def: "The transfer of an electron to a molecular entity\, resulting in a molecular entity of \(algebraically\) increased negative charge. It is not an attachment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01980
name: electron capture
def: "A nuclear transformation in which the nucleus captures an orbital electron. Often the shell\, from which the electron is captured\, is indicated. \(K-\, L-\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01981
name: electron capture detector
def: "A small radioactive source containing $\\ce\{^\{3\}H\}$ or $\\ce\{^\{63\}Ni\}$ ionizes the molecules of the carrier gas \(nitrogen or argon–methane\)\, and a potential difference creates a small current. This current is reduced when an electronegative substance \(such as a halocarbon\) is introduced. The reduction in current is a measure of the concentration of the electronegative substance. The detection limit \(threshold\) varies greatly according to the substances to be analysed and can reach a mixing ratio of \\\(10^\{-12\}\\\). The linear dynamic range may be \\\(10^\{4\}\\\) but the maximum measuring value generally lies below \\\(1\\ \\rm\{ppmv\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01982
name: electron charge
def: "The negative electric charge which appears on the electron or univalent ions \[ \\\(1.602\\times 10^\{-19\}\\\) coulombs or \\\(4.803\\times 10^\{-10\}\\\) electrostatic units \(\\\(\\mathrm\{esu\}\\\)\)\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01984
name: electron correlation
def: "The adjustment of electron motion to the instantaneous \(as opposed to time-averaged\) positions of all the electrons in a molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01985
name: electron-deficient bond
def: "A single bond between adjacent atoms that is formed by less than two electrons\, as in $\\ce\{B2H6\}$\:  The $\\ce\{B–H–B\}$ bonds are called 'two-electron three-centre bonds'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01986
name: electron density
def: "If \\\(P\(x\,\\\,y\,\\\,z\)\\ \\rm\{d\}x\\ \\rm\{d\}y\\ \\rm\{d\}z\\\) is the probability of finding an electron in the volume element \\\(\\rm\{d\}x\\ \\rm\{d\}y\\ \\rm\{d\}z\\\) at the point of a molecular entity with coordinates \\\(x\,\\\,y\,\\\,z\\\)\, then \\\(P\(x\,\\\,y\,\\\,z\)\\\) is the electron density at this point. For many purposes \(e.g. X-ray scattering\, forces on atoms\) the system behaves exactly as if the electrons were spread out into a continuously distributed charge. The term has frequently been wrongly applied to negative charge population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01987
name: electron detachment
def: "The reverse of an electron attachment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01988
name: electron donor
def: "A molecular entity that can transfer an electron to another molecular entity\, or to the corresponding chemical species.\[obsolete\] A Lewis base. This use is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01990
name: electronegativity
def: "Concept introduced by L. Pauling as the power of an atom to attract electrons to itself. There are several definitions of this quantity. According to Mulliken it is the average of the ionization energy and electron affinity of an atom\, but more frequently a relative scale due to Pauling is used where dimensionless relative electronegativity differences are defined on the basis of bond dissociation energies\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\)\, expressed in electronvolts\: \\\[\\chi _\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)- \\chi _\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{B\}\\right\)=\\left\(\\rm\{eV\}\\right\)^\{-\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\}\\ \\sqrt\{E_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{AB\}\\right\)- \\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \[E_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{AA\}\\right\)+E_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{BB\}\\right\)\]\}\\\] The scale is chosen so as to make the relative electronegativity of hydrogen \\\(\\chi _\{\\rm\{r\}\} = 2.1\\\). The sign of the square root was chosen intuitively by Pauling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01991
name: electron energy
def: "The potential difference through which electrons are accelerated before they are used to bring about electron ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01992
name: electroneutrality principle
def: "The principle expresses the fact that all pure substances carry a net charge of zero."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01993
name: electron exchange excitation transfer
def: "Synonymous with Dexter excitation transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01994
name: electronically excited state
def: "A state of an atom or molecular entity which has greater electronic energy than the ground state of the same entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01996
name: electronic effect of substituents\: symbols and signs
def: "The inductive effect has universally been represented by the symbol \\\(\\rm\{I\}\\\). This is now commonly taken to include both through-bonds and through-space transmission\, but \\\(\\rm\{I\}\\\) is also used specifically for through-bonds transmission; through-space transmission is then symbolized as \\\(\\rm\{F\}\\\) \(for field effect\). The symbols for the influence of substituents exerted through electron delocalization have variously been \\\(\\rm\{M\}\\\) \(mesomeric\)\, \\\(\\rm\{E\}\\\) \(electromeric\)\, \\\(\\rm\{T\}\\\) \(tautomeric\)\, \\\(\\rm\{C\}\\\) \(conjugative\)\, K \(konjugativ\) and \\\(\\rm\{R\}\\\) \(resonance\). Since the present fashion is to use the term resonance effect\, \\\(\\rm\{R\}\\\) is the most commonly used symbol\, although \\\(\\rm\{M\}\\\) is still seen quite often. Both the possible sign conventions are in use. The Ingold sign convention associates electronegativity \(relative to hydrogen atom\) with a negative sign\, electropositivity with a positive sign. Thus the nitro group is described as electronwithdrawing by virtue of its \\\(-\\rm\{I\}\\\) and \\\(-\\rm\{M\}\\\) effects; chloro is described as a \\\(-\\rm\{I\}\\\)\, \\\(+\\\,\\rm\{M\}\\\) substituent\, etc. For correlation analysis and linear free-energy relationships this convention has been found inconvenient\, for it is in contradiction to the sign convention for polar substituent constants \($\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}constants\}$\). Authors concerned with these fields often avoid this contradiction by adopting the opposite sign convention originally associated with Robinson\, for electronic effects. This practice is almost always associated with the use of R for the electron delocalization effect\: thus the nitro group is a \\\(+\\\,\\rm\{I\}\\\)\, \\\(+\\\,\\rm\{R\}\\\) substituent; chloro a \\\(+\\\,\\rm\{I\}\\\)\, \\\(-\\rm\{R\}\\\) substituent\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01997
name: electronic energy migration
def: "The movement of electronic excitation energy from one molecular entity to another of the same species\, or from one part of a molecular entity to another of the same kind \(e.g. excitation migration between the chromophores of an aromatic polymer\). The migration can happen via radiative or radiationless processes."
synonym: "electronic energy hopping" []
synonym: "energy migration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E01999
name: electron ionization
def: "This is the term used to describe ionization of any species by electrons. The process may\, for example\, be written\:   for atoms or molecules\: \\\[\\rm\{M\} + e \\rightarrow \\rm\{M\}^\{+\\cdot \} + 2e\\\]   for radicals\: \\\[\\rm\{M\}^\{\\cdot\}  + e \\rightarrow \\rm\{M\}^\{+\} + 2e\\\]   The term 'electron impact' should not be used."
synonym: "electron impact" []
synonym: "electron impact ionization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02000
name: electron jump
def: "The term can be applied to a reaction in which there is an electron transfer with the formation of an ionic intermediate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02001
name: electron kinetic energy
def: "The difference between the energy of the exciting photon and the ionization energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02003
name: electron-pair acceptor
def: "A synonym for Lewis acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02004
name: electron-pair donor
def: "A synonym for Lewis base."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02005
name: electron paramagnetic resonance
def: "Electron paramagnetic resonance \(EPR\) and/or electron spin resonance \(ESR\) is defined as the form of spectroscopy concerned with microwave-induced transitions between magnetic energy levels of electrons having a net spin and orbital angular momentum. The term electron paramagnetic resonance and the symbol EPR are preferred and should be used for primary indexing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02006
name: electron probe microanalysis
def: "General term for methods using bombardment of a solid specimen by electrons which generate a variety of signals providing the basis for a number of different analytical techniques."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02007
name: electron probe X-ray microanalysis
def: "Any analytical technique which uses the excitation and evaluation of the characteristic X-ray spectrum of a solid specimen by a focused electron beam \(typically of a diameter of less than \\\(1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\). Qualitative and quantitative X-ray analysis is the main feature of EPXMA. For obtaining supplementary information the secondary\, back scattered and absorbed electrons are also frequently observed. Qualitative and quantitative X-ray analysis is\, however\, the main feature of EPXMA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02008
name: electron rest mass
def: "Atomic fundamental physical constant used as atomic unit of mass\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{e\}\} = \\pu\{9.1093897\(54\)E-31 kg\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02009
name: electron spectroscopy for chemical analysis
def: "Type of spectroscopy involving the measurement of the kinetic energy of electrons emitted by chemical substances usually as a result of excitation by monochromatic X-rays."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02010
name: electron stopping power
def: "When a beam of electrons strikes a target or specimen there are three ways in which the electrons may lose energy \(low energy collisions\, X-ray production\, and formation of a spectral continuum\). The average energy loss per unit distance travelled along the electron path is called electron stopping power\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}E\}\{\\rm\{d\}x\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02011
name: electron transfer
def: "The transfer of an electron from one molecular entity to another\, or between two localized sites in the same molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02012
name: electron-transfer catalysis
def: "The term indicates a sequence of reactions such as shown in equations \(1\)–\(3\)\, leading from $\\ce\{A\}$ to $\\ce\{B\}$\:  \\\[\\rm\{A\} + \\rm\{e\}^\{-\} \\rightarrow \\rm\{A\}^\{\\cdot- \}\\\]$\\quad$\(1\)\\\[\\rm\{A\}^\{\\cdot- \} \\rightarrow \\rm\{B\}^\{\\cdot- \}\\\]$\\quad$\(2\)\\\[\\rm\{B\}^\{\\cdot- \} + \\rm\{A\} \\rightarrow \\rm\{B\} + \\rm\{A\}^\{\\cdot- \}\\\]$\\quad$\(3\) An analogous sequence involving radical cations \($\\ce\{A^\{+.\}\}$\, $\\ce\{B^\{+.\}\}$\) is also observed. The most notable example of electron-transfer catalysis is the \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{\\rm\{RN\}\}1\\\) \(or \\\(\\rm\{T\}+\\rm\{D\}_\{\\rm\{N\}\}+\\rm\{A\}_\{\\rm\{N\}\}\\\)\) reaction of aromatic halides. The term has its origin in a suggested analogy to acid-base catalysis\, with the electron instead of the proton. However\, there is a difference between the two catalytic mechanisms\, since the electron is not a true catalyst\, but rather behaves as the initiator of a chain reaction. 'Electron-transfer induced chain reaction' is a more appropriate term for the situation described by equations \(1\)–\(3\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02013
name: electron transfer photosensitization
def: "Photochemical process in which a reaction of a non-absorbing substrate is induced by electron transfer \(not energy transfer\) with an excited light-absorbing sensitizer. The overall process must be such that the sensitizer is recycled. Depending on the action of the excited sensitizer as electron donor or acceptor the sensitization is called reductive or oxidative."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02014
name: electronvolt
def: "Non-SI unit of energy\, \\\(\\rm\{eV\}\\approx 1.602\\ 176\\ 487\(40\)\\times 10^\{-19\}\\ \\rm\{J\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02015
name: electron work function
def: "The minimum work needed to extract electrons from the Fermi level of a metal M across a surface carrying no net charge. It is equal to the sum of the potential energy and the kinetic Fermi energy taken with the reverse sign\: \\\[\\mathit\{\\Phi\}^\{M\} = -\(V_\{\\rm\{e\}\} + \\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{e\}\}^\{F\}\)\\\] where \\\(V_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the potential energy for electrons in metals and \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{e\}\}^\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\) is the kinetic energy of electrons at the Fermi level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02017
name: electro-osmotic pressure
def: "The pressure difference \\\(\\Delta p\\\) across the membrane\, plug\, etc.\, needed just to stop electro-osmotic volume flow. \\\(\\Delta p\\\) is positive if the higher pressure is on the high potential side."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02019
name: electro-osmotic volume flow
def: "The volume flow per unit time per unit field strength through the whole plug."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02020
name: electrophile
def: "An electrophile \(or electrophilic reagent\) is a reagent that forms a bond to its reaction partner \(the nucleophile\) by accepting both bonding electrons from that reaction partner. An 'electrophilic substitution reaction' is a heterolytic reaction in which the reagent supplying the entering group acts as an electrophile. For example\:  Electrophilic reagents are Lewis acids. 'Electrophilic catalysis' is catalysis by Lewis acids. The term 'electrophilic' is also used to designate the apparent polar character of certain radicals as inferred from their higher relative reactivities with reaction sites of higher electron density."
synonym: "electrophilic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02021
name: electrophilicity
def: "The property of being electrophilic \(see electrophile\).The relative reactivity of an electrophilic reagent. \(It is also sometimes referred to as 'electrophilic power'.\) Qualitatively\, the concept is related to Lewis acidity. However\, whereas Lewis acidity is measured by relative equilibrium constants\, electrophilicity is measured by relative rate constants for reactions of different electrophilic reagents towards a common substrate \(usually involving attack at a carbon atom\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02022
name: electrophoresis
def: "The motion of colloidal particles in an electric field. The term 'cataphoresis' should be abandoned."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02024
name: electrophoretic mobility
def: "The observed rate of migration of a component \(\\\(v\\\)\) divided by electric field strength \(\\\(E\\\)\) in a given medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02025
name: electrophotography
def: "Processes of photoimaging which are based on photo-induced changes of electric fields \(photo-conductive or photo-electrostatic effects\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02027
name: electrostatic filter
def: "Filters for which an electrostatic charge is applied to the filter element. A fibrous filter material is often pleated in between V-shaped supports consisting of electrostatically charged\, metal rods which are insulated from the supporting frame."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02028
name: electrostatic precipitator
def: "A device which separates particles from a gas stream by passing the carrier gas between pairs of electrodes across which a unidirectional\, high-voltage potential is placed. The particles are charged before passing through the field and migrate to an oppositely charged electrode. These devices are very efficient collectors of small particles\, and their use in removing particles from power plant plumes and in other industrial applications is widespread."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02029
name: electrothermal atomizer
def: "A device which is heated to the temperature required for analyte atomization by the passage of electrical current through its body. In open atomizers the sample vapour\, together with analyte atoms\, leaving the atomizer surface may move freely into the surrounding space. In confined atomizers the sample vapour is restricted by the atomizer wall except for the openings where the optical beam enters and leaves the atomizer and the sample introduction hole."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02030
name: electroviscous effects
def: "For dispersions of charged particles\, these are those components of the viscosity connected with the charge of the particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02032
name: elementary charge
def: "The elementary charge\, \\\(e\\\)\, is one of seven fixed constants defining the International System of Units\, the SI\, with \\\(e = \\pu\{1.602176634E-19 C\}\\\). The elementary charge \\\(e\\\) corresponds to a coupling strength of the electromagnetic force via the fine-structure constant and is considered as a fundamental constant of nature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02033
name: elementary entity
def: "Any countable object or event\, but usually a molecule\, an ion or a specified group of atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02034
name: elementary particle
def: "A particle in which\, at the present\, no structure can be observed at moderate energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02035
name: elementary reaction
def: "A reaction for which no reaction intermediates have been detected or need to be postulated in order to describe the chemical reaction on a molecular scale. An elementary reaction is assumed to occur in a single step and to pass through a single transition state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02036
name: element effect
def: "The ratio of the rate constants of two reactions that differ only in the identity of the element of the atom in the leaving group\, e.g. \\\(\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{Br\}\}\}\{k_\{\\rm\{Cl\}\}\}\\\). As for isotope effects\, a ratio of unity is regarded as a 'null effect'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02038
name: elimination
def: "The reverse of an addition reaction or transformation. In an elimination two groups \(called eliminands\) are lost most often from two different centres \(1/2/elimination or 1/3/elimination\, etc.\) with concomitant formation of an unsaturation in the molecule \(double bond\, triple bond\) or formation of a new ring. If the groups are lost from a single centre \($\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}elimination\}$\, 1/1/elimination\) the resulting product is a carbene or a 'carbene analogue'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02039
name: eluate
def: "The effluent from a chromatographic bed emerging when elution is carried out."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02040
name: eluent
def: "The liquid or gas entering a chromatographic bed and used to effect a separation by elution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02041
name: elute
def: "To chromatograph by elution chromatography. This term is preferred to the term 'develop'\, which has been used in paper chromatography and in thin-layer chromatography. The process of elution may continue until the components have left the chromatographic bed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02045
name: elution band
def: "Synonymous with chromatographic peak."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02046
name: elution chromatography
def: "A procedure for chromatographic separation in which the mobile phase is passed through the chromatographic bed after the application of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02047
name: elution curve
def: "A chromatogram or part of a chromatogram recorded when elution techniques are used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02053
name: elutriation
def: "The process of separating the lighter particles of a powder from the heavier ones by means of an upward directed stream of fluid \(gas or liquid\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02054
name: emanation thermal analysis
def: "A thermoanalytical technique in which the release of radioactive emanation from a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature programme."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02055
name: embryo
def: "An aggregate of a small number of atoms\, molecules or ions. An embryo of such a size that the Gibbs energy at constant pressure and temperature is a maximum is called a critical embryo. An embryo larger than a critical embryo is called a homogeneous nucleus. Colloidal sols may be formed by condensation methods. When a condensation method is applied\, molecules \(or ions\) are deposited on nuclei which may be of the same chemical species as the colloid \(homogeneous nucleation\) or different \(heterogeneous nucleation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02056
name: emission
def: "Radiative deactivation of an excited state; transfer of energy from a molecular entity to an electromagnetic field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02057
name: emission
def: "The total rate at which a solid\, liquid or gaseous pollutant is emitted into the atmosphere from a given source; usually expressed as mass per unit time. Primary emissions are those substances which are emitted directly to the atmosphere \(e.g. $\\ce\{NO\}$\, $\\ce\{SO2\}$\, etc.\)\, while secondary emissions are formed from the primary emissions through thermal or photochemical reactions \(e.g. ozone\, aldehydes\, ketones\, sulfuric acid\, nitric acid\, etc.\). The point or area from which the discharge takes place is called the source; the area in which the emission or its transformed products \(e.g. in the case of aerosols\, acidic deposition\, etc.\) may be deposited is called the receptor area or sink. Emission may be applied to noise\, heat\, etc.\, as well as pollutants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02058
name: emission control equipment
def: "Air pollution control equipment which either converts the pollutant chemically to a non-polluting substance or collects the pollutant by some means including gravity settling chambers\, inertial separators\, cyclonic separators\, filters\, electrical precipitators\, scrubbers\, \(spray towers\, jet scrubbers\, Venturi scrubbers\, inertial scrubbers\, mechanical scrubbers and packed scrubbers\). Certain gases and odoriferous compounds are controlled by combustion\, absorption \(spray chambers\, mechanical contactors\, bubble cap or sieve plate contactors and packed towers\) and adsorption units \(packed beds or fluidized beds\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02059
name: emission flux
def: "The emission per unit area of the appropriate surface of an emitting source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02060
name: emission spectrum
def: "Plot of the emitted spectral radiant power \(spectral radiant exitance\) or of the emitted spectral photon irradiance \(spectral photon exitance\) against a quantity related to photon energy\, such as frequency\, \\\(\nu \\\)\, wavenumber\, \\\(\\sigma \\\)\, or wavelength\, \\\(\\lambda \\\). When corrected for wavelength-dependent variations in the equipment response\, it is called a corrected emission spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02062
name: emittance
def: "Ratio of radiant exitance of a thermal radiator to that of a full radiator \(black body\) at the same temperature. In ISO this quantity is called emissivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02063
name: empirical formula
def: "Formed by juxtaposition of the atomic symbols with their appropriate subscripts to give the simplest possible formula expressing the composition of a compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02064
name: emulsifier
def: "A surfactant which when present in small amounts facilitates the formation of an emulsion\, or enhances its colloidal stability by decreasing either or both of the rates of aggregation and coalescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02065
name: emulsion
def: "A fluid colloidal system in which liquid droplets and/or liquid crystals are dispersed in a liquid. The droplets often exceed the usual limits for colloids in size. An emulsion is denoted by the symbol O/W if the continuous phase is an aqueous solution and by W/O if the continuous phase is an organic liquid \(an 'oil'\). More complicated emulsions such as O/W/O \(i.e. oil droplets contained within aqueous droplets dispersed in a continuous oil phase\) are also possible. Photographic emulsions\, although colloidal systems\, are not emulsions in the sense of this nomenclature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02066
name: enamines
def: "Alkenylamines; by usage the term refers specifically to vinylic amines\, which have the structure $\\ce\{R2NCR=CR2\}$ ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02069
name: enantiomer
def: "One of a pair of molecular entities which are mirror images of each other and non-superposable."
synonym: "optical isomer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02070
name: enantiomeric excess
def: "For a mixture of \\\(\\left\(+\\right\)\\\)- and \\\(\\left\(-\\right\)\\\)-enantiomers\, with composition given as the mole or weight fractions \\\(F_\{\\left\(\\!+\\!\\right\)\}\\\) and \\\(F_\{\\left\(\\!-\\!\\right\)\}\\\) \(where \\\(F_\{\\left\(\\!+\\!\\right\)\}\\\,+\\\,F_\{\\left\(\\!-\\!\\right\)\}=1\\\)\) the enantiomer excess is defined as \\\(|F_\{\\left\(\\!+\\!\\right\)\} - F_\{\\left\(\\!-\\!\\right\)\}|\\\) \(and the percent enantiomer excess by \\\(100\\ |F_\{\\left\(\\!+\\!\\right\)\} - F_\{\\left\(\\!-\\!\\right\)\}|\\\)\). Frequently this term is abbreviated e.e."
synonym: "enantiomer excess" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02071
name: enantiomerically enriched
def: "A sample of a chiral substance whose enantiomeric ratio is greater than \\\(50\\ \\colon\\ 50\\\) but less than \\\(100\\ \\colon\\ 0\\\)."
synonym: "enantioenriched" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02072
name: enantiomerically pure
def: "A sample all of whose molecules have \(within limits of detection\) the same chirality sense. Use of homochiral as a synonym is strongly discouraged."
synonym: "enantiopure" []
synonym: "homochiral" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02073
name: enantiomeric groups
def: "Chiral groups that are mirror images of one another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02075
name: enantiomeric ratio
def: "The ratio of the percentage of one enantiomer in a mixture to that of the other e.g. \\\(70\\left\(\\!+\\!\\right\)\\\,\\colon\\\,30\\left\(\\!-\\!\\right\)\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02076
name: enantiomeric units
def: "Two configurational units that correspond to the same constitutional unit are considered to be enantiomeric if they are non-superposable mirror images."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02077
name: enantiomerization
def: "The interconversion of enantiomers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02078
name: enantiomerism
def: "The isomerism of enantiomers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02079
name: enantiomorph
def: "One of a pair of chiral objects or models that are non-superposable mirror images of each other. The adjective enantiomorphic is also applied to mirror-image related groups within a molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02083
name: enantiotopic
def: "Constitutionally identical atoms or groups in molecules which are related by symmetry elements of the second kind only \(mirror plane\, inversion centre or rotation–reflection axis\). For example the two groups c in a grouping Cabcc are enantiotopic. Replacement of one of a pair of enantiotopic groups forms one of a pair of enantiomers. Analogously\, if complexation or addition to one of the two faces defined by a double bond or other molecular plane gives rise to a chiral species\, the two faces are called enantiotopic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02085
name: encapsulation
def: "Enzymes or cells which are of relatively large size may be entrapped in a maze of polymeric molecules \(a gel\). This procedure is called immobilization by inclusion. When the biocatalyst is enclosed inside a semipermeable membrane\, usually approximately spherical\, the method is known as encapsulation. In the process of reticulation the primary biocatalyst particles \(individual enzyme molecules\, cofactors or individual cells\) are covalently attached to each other by organic chains\, into a three-dimensional network. The term grafting is also used in this context. Attachment to the support by adsorption forces is called immobilization by adsorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02086
name: encounter
def: "A set of collisions between reactant molecules in solids\, liquids or dense gases\, occurring in rapid succession as a result of the cage effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02087
name: encounter complex
def: "A complex of molecular entities produced at an encounter-controlled rate\, and which occurs as an intermediate in a reaction mechanism. When the complex is formed from two molecular entities it is called an 'encounter pair'. A distinction between encounter pairs and \(larger\) encounter complexes may be relevant in some cases\, e.g. for mechanisms involving pre-association."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02089
name: encounter-controlled rate
def: "A rate of reaction corresponding to the rate of encounter of the reacting molecular entities. This is also known as 'diffusion-controlled rate' since rates of encounter are themselves controlled by diffusion rates \(which in turn depend on the viscosity of the medium and the dimensions of the reactant molecular entities\). For a bimolecular reaction between solutes in water at \\\(\\pu\{25^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\) an encounter-controlled rate is calculated to have a second-order rate constant of about \\\(\\pu\{E10 dm3 mol-1 s-1\}\\\)."
synonym: "diffusion-controlled rate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02091
name: endergonic reaction
def: "This is usually taken to be a reaction for which the overall standard Gibbs energy change \\\(\\Delta G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is positive. Some workers use this term with respect to a positive value of \\\(\\Delta H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) at the absolute zero of temperature."
synonym: "endoergic reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02092
name: end-group
def: "A constitutional unit that is an extremity of a macromolecule or oligomer molecule. An end-group is attached to only one constitutional unit of a macromolecule or oligomer molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02093
name: endoenzymes
def: "Enzymes that cut internal bonds of a polymer. Endonucleases are able to cleave phosphodiester bonds within a nucleic acid chain by hydrolysis either randomly or at specific base sequences \(cf. restriction enzymes\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02094
name: <i>endo</i>\, <i>exo</i>\, <i>syn</i>\, <i>anti</i>
def: "Descriptors of the relative orientation of groups attached to non-bridgehead atoms in a bicyclo\[x.y.z\]alkane \(x ≥ y &gt; z &gt; 0\).  If the group is orientated towards the highest numbered bridge \(z bridge\, e.g. C-7 in example below\) it is given the description exo; if it is orientated away from the highest numbered bridge it is given the description endo. If the group is attached to the highest numbered bridge and is orientated towards the lowest numbered bridge \(x bridge\, e.g. C-2 in example below\) it is given the description syn; if the group is orientated away from the lowest numbered bridge it is given the description anti."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02095
name: endothermic reaction
def: "A reaction for which the overall standard enthalpy change \\\(\\Delta H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is positive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02097
name: end-to-end distance
def: "The length\, \\\(r\\\)\, of the end-to-end vector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02098
name: end-to-end vector
def: "The vector\, \\\(r\\\)\, connecting the two ends of a linear polymer chain in a particular conformation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02099
name: ene reaction
def: "The addition of a compound with a double bond having an allylic hydrogen \(the 'ene'\) to a compound with a multiple bond \(the 'enophile'\) with transfer of the allylic hydrogen and a concomitant reorganization of the bonding\, as illustrated below for propene \(the 'ene'\) and ethene \(the 'enophile'\). The reverse is a 'retro-ene' reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02100
name: energized species
def: "In an elementary process\, a species having sufficient energy to become an activated complex\, but which does not have the structure of the activated complex. If it is not inactivated by collisions it can become an activated complex and pass at once into products\, after undergoing vibration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02101
name: energy
def: "In mechanics the sum of potential energy and kinetic energy. In thermodynamics the internal energy or internal energy increase\, \\\(\\Delta U\\\)\, is the sum of heat and work brought to the system. Only changes in energy are measurable. For photons \\\[E=h\\ \nu \\\]where \\\(h\\\) is the Planck constant and \\\(\nu \\\) the frequency of radiation. In relativistic physics \\\[E=m\\ c^\{2\}\\\] where \\\(c\\\) is the speed of light and \\\(m\\\) the mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02102
name: energy
def: "Energy of the individual particles or photons of which a radiation consists."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02103
name: energy dispersion
def: "The separation of characteristic photons according to their energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02105
name: energy dispersive X-ray fluorescence analysis
def: "A method of X-ray fluorescence analysis where element specificity is obtained by measuring the energy spectrum of the induced X-radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02106
name: energy flux density
def: "For mono-directional radiation\, the energy traversing in a time interval over a small area perpendicular to the direction of the energy flow\, divided by that time interval and by that area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02109
name: energy of activation
def: "This is defined by the equation\: \\\[U^\{\\ddagger \} = -R\\ T\\ \(\\frac\{\\partial \(\\ln I_\{0\}\)\}\{\\partial T^\{-1\}\}\)_\{p\,c_\{j\}\,...\}\\\] where \\\(I_\{0\}\\\) is the exchange current. At any overpotential \\\(\\eta \\\) it is defined by the equation\: \\\[U^\{\\ddagger \}\\left\(\\eta \\right\) = -R\\ \(\\frac\{\\partial \(\\ln \(|I|\)\)\}\{\\partial T^\{-1\}\}\)_\{p\,\\eta \,c_\{j\}\,...\}\\\] where \\\(I\\\) is the current passing from the electrode into the electrolyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02113
name: energy resolution
def: "A measurement\, at given energy\, of the smallest difference between the energies of two particles or photons capable of being distinguished by a radiation spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02114
name: energy storage efficiency
def: "The rate of the Gibbs energy storage in an endothermic photochemical reaction divided by the incident irradiance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02115
name: energy threshold
def: "The limiting kinetic energy of a incident particle or energy of an incident photon below which a specified process cannot take place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02116
name: energy transfer
def: "From a phenomenological point of view\, the term is used to describe the process by which a molecular entity absorbs light and a phenomenon originates from the excited state of another molecular entity. In mechanistic photochemistry the term has been reserved for the photophysical process in which an excited state of one molecular entity \(the donor\) is deactivated to a lower-lying state by transferring energy to a second molecular entity \(the acceptor\) which is thereby raised to a higher energy state. The excitation may be electronic\, vibrational\, rotational or translational. The donor and acceptor may be two parts of the same molecular entity\, in which case the process is called intramolecular energy transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02117
name: energy transfer plot
def: "A plot of the quenching rate constant of an excited molecular entity by a series of quenchers versus the excited state energy of the quenchers. Alternatively\, a plot of the rate constant for the sensitization of a reaction versus the excited state energy of different sensitizers. This type of plot is used to estimate the energy of the excited molecular entity quenched \(in the former case\) or produced \(in the latter case\)."
synonym: "Hammond–Herkstroeter plot" []
synonym: "Herkstroeter plot" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02118
name: energy yield of luminescence
def: "The ratio of the energy emitted as luminescence to the energy absorbed by a species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02119
name: enforced concerted mechanism
def: "Variation of reaction parameters in a series of reactions proceeding in non-concerted steps may lead to a situation where the putative intermediate will possess a lifetime shorter than a bond vibration\, so that the steps become concerted. The transition state structure will lie on the coordinate of the More O'Ferrall–Jencks diagram leading to that of the putative intermediate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02120
name: enhanced phosphorescence analysis
def: "A use of luminescence quenching effects to enhance sensitivity. The strong depopulation of the fluorescing singlet excited state by external heavy atom perturbers can lead to a large population of the phosphorescing triplet excited state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02121
name: enhancement reaction
def: "A reaction which enables a favourable measurement to be made in which the constituent which is finally measured has not been magnified in any way \(cf. amplification reaction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02122
name: enhancer
def: "A fluorescent compound which accepts energy and thus enhances or promotes the emission from a sample containing a chemically or enzymatically generated excited molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02123
name: enolates
def: "Salts of enols \(or of the tautomeric aldehydes or ketones\)\, in which the anionic charge is delocalized over oxygen and carbon\, or similar covalent metal derivatives in which the metal is bound to oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02124
name: enols
def: "Alkenols; the term refers specifically to vinylic alcohols\, which have the structure $\\ce\{HOCR'=CR2\}$. Enols are tautomeric with aldehydes \($\\ce\{R'\} = \\ce\{H\}$\) or ketones \($\\ce\{R'\\!\neq H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02126
name: enoses
def: "Monosaccharides having a carbon–carbon double bond anywhere in the backbone chain. Glycals \(term not recommended\) designates the enoses that are generated by formal elimination of the hemiacetal hydroxy group and an adjacent hydrogen atom. Thus glycals are cyclic enol ethers. Unsaturated hexoses\, for example\, are called hexenoses\, e.g. the hex-1-enopyranose derived from ᴅ-glucopyranose\)\:"
synonym: "glycals" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02127
name: enrichment factor
def: "The factor by which the ratio of two substances in the feed must be multiplied to give their ratio after treatment. \\\[\\frac\{Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{Q_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\} = S_\{\\rm\{A\,B\}\}\\ \\frac\{Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{'\}\}\{Q_\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{'\}\}\\\] where \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) and \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{'\}\\\) are the final and initial amounts of species A and \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) and \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{'\}\\\) are the final and initial amounts of species B. Hence \\\(S_\{\\rm\{A\,B\}\}=\\frac\{E_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{E_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\) where \\\(E\\\) is the fraction extracted. In terms of \\\(D\\\)\,\\\(n\\\)\,\\\(r\\\) \(where \\\(n\\\) is the number of stages and \\\(r\\\) the phase ratio\)\, \\\[S_\{\\rm\{A\,B\}\} = \\frac\{1- \(1\\\,+\\\,r\\ D_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\)^\{-n\}\}\{1- \(1\\\,+\\\,r\\ D_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\)^\{-n\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02129
name: <i>ent</i>
def: "A prefix used to indicate the enantiomer for natural products and related molecules where the trivial name only refers to one enantiomer\, e.g. ent-kaurene is the enantiomer of kaurene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02130
name: entering group
def: "An atom or group that forms a bond to what is considered to be the main part of the substrate during a reaction. For example\: the attacking nucleophile in a bimolecular nucleophilic substitution reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02131
name: enthalpimetric analysis
def: "The generic designation for a family of analytical methods in which the enthalpy change of a chemical reaction is measured\, directly or indirectly\, in order to perform a quantitative determination of a reactant or catalyst. In general\, at least one reactant is a liquid or solution. It is a subset of calorimetry in which the system is maintained at constant \(usually atmospheric pressure\)\, and the emphasis is on a quantitative determination in a reasonable length of time. Analytical solution calorimetry is a synonymous designation that may be used but is not preferred. 'Thermochemical analysis' has been used but is not recommended because of potential confusion and overlap with the methods of thermal analysis. The adjective 'thermometric' is also associated with the names of some of these methods\, but is not recommended as a general designation."
synonym: "analytical solution calorimetry" []
synonym: "enthalpimetry" []
synonym: "thermochemical analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02140
name: enthalpogram
def: "A plot of temperature versus time or heat change versus time which is produced in direct-injection enthalpimetry. Use of the term 'thermogram' is not recommended because it is too general and overlaps with other fields."
synonym: "thermogram" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02141
name: enthalpy
def: "Internal energy of a system plus the product of pressure and volume. Its change in a system is equal to the heat brought to the system at constant pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02142
name: enthalpy of activation
def: "The standard enthalpy of activation \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is the enthalpy change that appears in the thermodynamic form of the rate equation obtained from conventional transition state theory. This equation is only correct for a first order reaction\, for which the rate constant has the dimension reciprocal time. For a second order reaction\, for which the rate constant has the dimension \(reciprocal time\) × \(reciprocal concentration\)\, the left hand side should be read as \\\(k\\ c^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(c^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) denotes the standard concentration \(usually \\\(\\pu\{1 mol dm-3\}\\\)\). \\\[k = \\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\}\{h\}\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{\\frac\{\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}S^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\}\{R\}\}\\ \\mathrm\{e\}^\{\\frac\{-\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\}\{R\\ T\}\}\\\] The quantity \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}S^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is the standard entropy of activation\, and care must be taken with standard states. In this equation \\\(k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the Boltzmann constant\, \\\(T\\\) the absolute temperature\, \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant\, and \\\(R\\\) the gas constant. The enthalpy of activation is approximately equal to the activation energy; the conversion of one into the other depends on the molecularity. The enthalpy of activation is always the standard quantity\, although the word standard and the superscript \\\(^\{\\circ\}\\\) on the symbol are often omitted. The symbol is frequently \(but incorrectly\) written \\\(\\Delta H^\{\\ddagger \}\\\)\, where the standard symbol is omitted and the \\\(\\ddagger \\\) is placed after the \\\(H\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02143
name: enthalpy of immersion
def: "Synonymous with enthalpy of wetting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02144
name: enthalpy of wetting
def: "Referred to unit of mass of the solid\, the difference \(at constant temperature\) between the enthalpy of a solid completely immersed in a wetting liquid\, and that of the solid and the liquid taken separately. It must be specified whether the solid in the initial state is in contact with vacuum or with the vapour of the liquid at a given partial pressure. Measurements of the enthalpy of wetting of a solid equilibrated with varying relative pressures of the vapour of a pure wetting liquid may be used to derive the differential enthalpy of adsorption of the vapour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02145
name: entitic
def: "Modifier used to denote divided by number of entities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02146
name: entitic quantity
def: "One which refers to a single entity \(particle\) of a system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02147
name: entrainment
def: "The act of forming a mist or fog\: droplets of a liquid carried off by the vapours of a boiling liquid or from a liquid through which bubbles of gas or vapour are passing rapidly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02148
name: entrance channel
def: "The region of a potential-energy surface or hypersurface that corresponds to molecular configurations that are closer in geometry to those of the reactants than to those of the products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02149
name: entropy
def: "Quantity the change in which is equal to the heat brought to the system in a reversible process at constant temperature divided by that temperature. Entropy is zero for an ideally ordered crystal at 0 \\\(\\rm\{K\}\\\). In statistical thermodynamics \\\[S=k\\ \\ln W\\\] where \\\(k\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(W\\\) the number of possible arrangements of the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02150
name: entropy of activation
def: "The entropy change that appears in the thermodynamic form of the rate equation obtained from conventional transition-state theory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02151
name: entropy unit
def: "Non-SI unit of molar entropy\, \\\(\\rm\{e.u.\} = \\pu\{4.184 J K-1 mol-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02152
name: envelope conformation
def: "The conformation \(of symmetry group \\\(C_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\) of a five-membered ring in which four atoms are coplanar\, and one atom \(the flap\) projects out of the plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02158
name: enzyme repression
def: "The mode by which the synthesis of an enzyme is prevented by repressor molecules. In many cases\, the end product of a synthesis chain \(e.g. an amino acid\) acts as a feed-back corepressor by combining with an intracellular aporepressor protein\, so that this complex is able to block the function of an operator. As a result\, the whole operon is prevented from being transcribed into mRNA\, and the expression of all enzymes necessary for the synthesis of the end product amino acid is abolished."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02159
name: enzymes
def: "Macromolecules\, mostly of protein nature\, that function as \(bio\)catalysts by increasing the reaction rates. In general\, an enzyme catalyses only one reaction type \(reaction specificity\) and operates on only one type of substrate \(substrate specificity\). Substrate molecules are attacked at the same site \(regiospecificity\) and only one or preferentially one of the enantiomers of chiral substrates or of racemic mixtures is attacked \(stereospecificity\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02160
name: enzyme substrate electrode
def: "A sensor in which an ion-selective electrode is covered with a coating containing an enzyme which causes the reaction of an organic or inorganic substance \(substrate\) to produce a species to which the electrode responds. Alternatively\, the sensor could be covered with a layer of substrate which reacts with the enzyme\, co-factor or inhibitor to be assayed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02162
name: enzymic decomposition
def: "Decomposition of organic materials \(e.g. starch\, sugars\, proteins\, etc.\) can be achieved by enzymic decomposition\, in which the enzyme converts a high molecular mass compound into lower molecular mass species. The process can be regarded as an example of enzymic degradation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02163
name: epicadmium neutrons
def: "Neutrons of kinetic energy greater than the effective cadmium cut-off energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02164
name: epigenetic
def: "Descriptive term for processes that change the phenotype without altering the genotype."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02165
name: epihalohydrins
def: "Compounds having the \(halomethyl\)oxirane skeleton\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02166
name: epimerization
def: "Interconversion of epimers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02167
name: epimers
def: "Diastereoisomers that have the opposite configuration at only one of two or more tetrahedral stereogenic centres present in the respective molecular entities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02168
name: epi-phase
def: "The less dense phase in a distribution system. The term is often used when two non-aqueous phases are present or when the solvent is an aqueous solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02169
name: episulfonium ions
def: "Ions derived from thiiranes\, in which a trivalent sulfur atom bears a positive charge\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02170
name: epithermal neutrons
def: "Neutrons of kinetic energy greater than that of thermal agitation. The term is often restricted to energies just above thermal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02171
name: epitope
def: "Any part of a molecule that acts as an antigenic determinant. A macromolecule can contain many different epitopes each capable of stimulating production of a different specific antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02173
name: epoxy compounds
def: "Compounds in which an oxygen atom is directly attached to two adjacent or non-adjacent carbon atoms of a carbon chain or ring system; thus cyclic ethers. The term epoxides represents a subclass of epoxy compounds containing a saturated three-membered cyclic ether; thus oxirane derivatives\, e.g. 1\,2-epoxypropane\, or 2-methyloxirane \(an epoxide\); 9\,10-epoxy-9\,10-dihydroanthracene \(an epoxy compound\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02174
name: equatorial
def: "For the use of this term in the context of cyclohexane see axial\, equatorial. For the use of this term in the context of a bipyramidal structure see apical\, basal\, equatorial."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02175
name: equilibration
def: "The operation by which a system of two or more phases is brought to a condition where further changes with time do not occur. This term is not synonymous with pre-equilibrium and should not be used in that sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02177
name: equilibrium constant
def: "Quantity characterizing the equilibrium of a chemical reaction and defined by an expression of the type \\\(K_\{x\}=\\underset\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\n\\rm\{B\}\n\\end\{array\}\}\{\\Pi \}x_\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\nu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(\nu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the stoichiometric number of a reactant \(negative\) or product \(positive\) for the reaction and \\\(x\\\) stands for a quantity which can be the equilibrium value either of pressure\, fugacity\, amount concentration\, amount fraction\, molality\, relative activity or reciprocal absolute activity defining the pressure based\, fugacity based\, concentration based\, amount fraction based\, molality based\, relative activity based or standard equilibrium constant \(then denoted \\\(K^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\)\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02179
name: equilibrium dialysate
def: "The colloid-free solution obtained at equilibrium in dialysis. Its composition approaches that of the dispersion medium \(more precisely\, the limit to which the composition of the dispersion medium tends at large distances from the particles\). In the dialysis equilibrium an osmotic pressure difference exists between sol and equilibrium dialysate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02180
name: equilibrium distance
def: "Internuclear distance at the minimum of the electronic \(or potential\) energy surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02181
name: equilibrium film
def: "A liquid film of thickness\, or thicknesses\, at which it is stable or metastable with respect to small thickness changes. Unless the area of the film is small\, its composition may not be the same over its area and the \(metastable\) equilibrium thickness may be characteristic of the local condition only."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02184
name: equilibrium reaction
def: "When the reactants in a chemical reaction are initially in a Boltzmann distribution the reaction is referred to as an equilibrium reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02186
name: equilibrium sedimentation
def: "A method by which the distribution of the concentration of the solute or dispersed component in a dilute solution or dispersion along the centrifuge cell is measured at sedimentation equilibrium and the results are interpreted in terms of molar masses or their distribution\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02189
name: equivalence postulate
def: "The working hypothesis that the chain monomeric units are geometrically equivalent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02190
name: equivalent chain
def: "A hypothetical freely jointed chain with the same mean-square end-to-end distance and contour length as an actual polymer chain in a theta state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02191
name: equivalent diameter
def: "The diameter of a spherical particle which will give identical geometric\, optical\, electrical or aerodynamic behaviour to that of the particle \(non-spherical\) being examined; sometimes referred to as the Stokes diameter for particles in non-turbulent flows."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02192
name: equivalent entity
def: "Entity corresponding to the transfer of a $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ ion in a neutralization reaction\, of an electron in a redox reaction\, or to a magnitude of charge number equal to 1 in ions. Examples\: \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\\) $\\ce\{H2SO4\}$\, \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{5\}\\\) $\\ce\{KMnO4\}$\, \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{3\}\\\) $\\ce\{Fe^\{3+\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02193
name: erg
def: "cgs unit of energy\, \\\(\\rm\{erg\} = 10^\{-7\}\\ \\rm\{J\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02194
name: error
def: "Result of a measurement minus the true value of the measurand. Since a true value cannot be determined\, in practice the conventional true value is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02211
name: <i>erythro</i> structures
def: "The relative configuration at two contiguous carbon atoms in the main chain bearing\, respectively\, substituents $\\ce\{a\}$ and $\\ce\{b\}$ \($\\ce\{a\} \neq \\ce\{b\}$\)\, is designated by the prefix erythro or erythro or threo\, as appropriate\, by analogy with the terminology for carbohydrate systems in which the substituents are $\\ce\{OH\}$. Examples\:  Similar systems in which a higher level of substitution exists may be treated analogously if the erythro or threo designation is employed to denote the relative placements of those two substituents\, one for each backbone carbon atom\, which rank highest according to the Sequence Rule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02212
name: <i>erythro</i>\, <i>threo</i>
def: "Descriptors of the diastereoisomers of an acyclic structure or partial structure having two stereogenic centres. This notation is derived from carbohydrate nomenclature. The extension of this system has given rise to conflicting interpretations of these prefixes. It is recommended that for such cases the l\, u or R*\, S* system should be used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02214
name: escape depth
def: "The distance into the sample measured from the physical surface from which all but a fraction \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{e\}\\\) of the particles or radiation detected have originated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02218
name: Esin and Markov coefficient
def: "The left-hand side of one of the various cross-differential relationships that can be obtained from the Gibbs adsorption equation when only one chemical potential \(\\\(\\mu \\\)\) is considered as variable\, viz. \\\[\(\\frac\{\\partial E\}\{\\partial \\mu \}\)_\{T\,p\,\\sigma \}=- \(\\frac\{\\partial \\mathit\{\\Gamma \}\}\{\\partial \\sigma \}\)_\{T\,p\,\\mu \}\\\] where \\\(E\\\) is the potential difference\, \\\(T\\\) is the temperature\, \\\(p\\\) is the pressure\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}\\\) is the surface excess and \\\(\\sigma \\\) is the charge density."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02219
name: esters
def: "Compounds formally derived from an oxoacid $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$\, \($l \neq 0$\) and an alcohol\, phenol\, heteroarenol\, or enol by linking with formal loss of water from an acidic hydroxy group of the former and a hydroxy group of the latter. By extension acyl derivatives of alcohols\, etc. Acyl derivatives of chalcogen analogues of alcohols \(thiols\, selenols\, tellurols\) etc. are included. E.g. $\\ce\{R'C\(=O\)\(OR\)\}$\, $\\ce\{R'C\(=S\)\(OR\)\}$\, $\\ce\{R'C\(=O\)\(SR\)\}$\, $\\ce\{R'S\(=O\)2\(OR\)\}$\, $\\ce\{\(HO\)2P\(=O\)\(OR\)\}$\, $\\ce\{\(R'S\)2C\(=O\)\}$\, $\\ce\{ROCN\}$ \(but not $\\ce\{R–NCO\}$\) \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02221
name: ethers
def: "Compounds ROR \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). \(Compounds $\\ce\{R3SiOR\}$\, silicon analogues of ethers\, are trialkylsilyloxy compounds\). E.g. $\\ce\{CH3CH2OCH2CH3\}$ ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02223
name: euatmotic reaction
def: "An isothermal\, reversible reaction between two \(or more\) solid phases during the heating of a system as a result of which a single vapour phase is produced."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02225
name: eutectic reaction
def: "An isothermal\, reversible reaction between two \(or more\) solid phases during the heating of a system\, as a result of which a single liquid phase is produced."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02226
name: evaluation function
def: "The inverse of the calibration function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02227
name: evaporation
def: "The physical process by which a liquid substance is converted to a gas or vapour. This may occur at or below the normal boiling point of the liquid \(the temperature at which a liquid boils at 1 atmosphere pressure\) and the process is endothermic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02228
name: even-electron ion
def: "An ion containing no unpaired electrons\, for example $\\ce\{CH3^\{+\}\}$\, in its ground state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02229
name: evolved gas analysis
def: "A technique in which the nature and/or amount of volatile product\(s\) released by a substance subjected to a controlled temperature program is \(are\) determined. The method of analysis should always be clearly stated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02230
name: evolved gas detection
def: "A technique in which the evolution of gas from a substance is detected as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02232
name: exa
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{18\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{E\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02236
name: excess Rayleigh ratio
def: "The difference between the Rayleigh ratio for a dilute solution and for pure solvent. If the scattering intensity is not reduced to the Rayleigh ratio\, the difference between the scattering intensities for a dilute solution and that for pure solvent is named 'excess scattering'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02237
name: excess volume
def: "For a pure liquid\, despite its low compressibility\, the variation of density near a solid surface can be detected and measured. The total volume \\\(V\\\) of a system consisting of solid and pure liquid is different from \(usually less than\) that calculated assuming a constant liquid density. If the densities of bulk solid \(\\\(\\rho ^\{\\mathrm\{sol\}\}\\\)\) and liquid \(\\\(\\rho ^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\)\) are known then an excess volume \(usually negative\) can be defined as\: \\\[V^\{\\sigma \}=V- V^\{\\mathrm\{sol\}\}- V^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}=V- \\frac\{m^\{\\mathrm\{sol\}\}\}\{\\rho ^\{\\mathrm\{sol\}\}\}- \\frac\{m^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{\\rho ^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(m^\{\\mathrm\{sol\}\}\\\) is the mass of solid\, \\\(V^\{\\mathrm\{sol\}\}\\\) its volume calculated from the bulk density\, \\\(V^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is the initial volume of liquid and \\\(m^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is the mass of liquid. The excess mass is given by\: \\\[m^\{\\sigma \}=m^\{\\rm\{l\}\}- \(V- V^\{\\mathrm\{sol\}\}\)\\ \\rho ^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02238
name: exchange current
def: "The common value \\\(I_\{0\}\\\) of the anodic and cathodic partial currents when the reaction is at equilibrium \\\[I_\{0\}=I_\{\\rm\{a\}\}=- I_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\] For an electrode at equilibrium at which only one reaction is significant \\\(I=0\\\). When more than one reaction is significant at a given electrode\, subscripts to \\\(I_\{0\}\\\) may be used to distinguish exchange currents. \\\(I\\\) is not usually zero when only one of these reactions is at equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02239
name: exchange extraction
def: "An extraction operation or process in which a metal from one phase is exchanged with the equivalent amount of a second metal from the other phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02240
name: exchange-inversion transition
def: "A transition between antiferromagnetic and ferromagnetic coupling between two subarrays of magnetic atoms. Example\: Ordered $\\ce\{FeRh\}$ changes from antiferromagnetic to ferromagnetic coupling with the simple cubic $\\ce\{Fe\}$ array in a first-order transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02241
name: exchange labelling
def: "Labelling of a substance by isotope exchange."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02242
name: excimer
def: "An electronically excited dimer\, 'non-bonding' in the ground state. For example\, a complex formed by the interaction of an excited molecular entity with a ground state partner of the same structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02243
name: excimer laser
def: "A source of pulsed coherent radiation obtained from an exciplex. The proper name is exciplex laser. Typical lasing species are noble gas halides \($\\ce\{XeCl\}$\, $\\ce\{KrF\}$\, etc.\) emitting in the UV domain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02244
name: excimer-luminescence
def: "An effect accompanying concentration quenching in some cases\, namely the formation of a new bimolecular species which is capable of emission \(excimer- and exciplex-luminescence\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02245
name: excipient
def: "Any more or less inert substance added to a drug to give suitable consistency or form to the drug."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02246
name: exciplex
def: "An electronically excited complex of definite stoichiometry\, 'non-bonding' in the ground state. For example\, a complex formed by the interaction of an excited molecular entity with a ground state counterpart of a different structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02248
name: excitation
def: "Process causing the transition of a system from one state to another of higher energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02249
name: excitation-emission spectrum
def: "The three-dimensional spectrum generated by scanning the emission spectrum at incremental steps of excitation wavelength \(\\\(x\\\) axis = emission wavelength\, \\\(y\\\) axis = excitation wavelength\, \\\(z\\\) axis = emission flux\) is called a \(fluorescence\, phosphorescence\) excitation-emission spectrum \(or EES\). The spectra are particularly useful for investigating samples containing more than one emitting species. Corrected EES are obtained if \(a\) the emission is corrected for instrumental response with wavelength\, and \(b\) the exciting radiation flux in photons \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\) is held constant for all excitation wavelengths."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02250
name: excitation energy
def: "The minimum energy required to bring a system to a specified higher energy level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02251
name: excitation energy
def: "Kinetic energy of bombarding particles at the surface of the solid."
synonym: "acceleration energy" []
synonym: "initial energy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02252
name: excitation level
def: "X-ray levels have various degrees of ionization -- single\, double or higher -- and may in some cases also be electrically neutral. An excitation level \(exciton\) is an electrically neutral X-ray level with an expelled electron bound in the field of a core electron vacancy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02253
name: excitation spectrum
def: "A plot of the spectral radiant exitance or of the spectral photon exitance against the frequency \(or wavenumber\, or wavelength\) of excitation. When corrected for wavelength dependent variations in the excitation radiant power this is called a corrected excitation spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02256
name: excitation transfer
def: "Synonymous with energy transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02257
name: excited state
def: "State of a system with energy higher than that of the ground state. This term is most commonly used to characterize a molecule in one of its electronically excited states\, but can also refer to vibrational and/or rotational excitation in the electronic ground state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02258
name: exciton
def: "In some applications it is useful to consider electronic excitation as if a quasi-particle\, capable of migrating\, were involved. This is termed an exciton. In organic materials two models are used\: the band or wave model \(low temperature\, high crystalline order\) and the hopping model \(higher temperature\, low crystalline order or amorphous state\). Energy transfer in the hopping limit is identical with energy migration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02259
name: excluded volume of a macromolecule
def: "The volume from which a macromolecule in a dilute solution of a macromolecule effectively excludes all other macromolecules. The excluded volume of a macromolecule depends on the Gibbs and Helmholtz energies of mixing of solvent and polymer\, i.e. on the thermodynamic quality of the solvent\, and is not a measure of the geometrical volume of that macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02260
name: excluded volume of a segment
def: "The volume from which a segment of a macromolecule in solution effectively excludes all other segments\, i.e. those belonging to the same macromolecule as well as those belonging to other macromolecules. The excluded volume of a segment depends on the Gibbs and Helmholtz energies of mixing of solvent and polymer\, i.e. on the thermodynamic quality of the solvent\, and is not a measure of the geometrical volume of that segment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02261
name: exclusion chromatography
def: "Chromatography in which separation is based mainly on exclusion effects\, such as differences in molecular size and/or shape or in charge. The term size-exclusion chromatography may also be used when separation is based on molecular size. The terms gel filtration and gel-permeation chromatography \(GPC\) were used earlier to describe this process when the stationary phase is a swollen gel. The term ion-exclusion chromatography is specifically used for the separation of ions in an aqueous phase."
synonym: "gel-filtration chromatography" []
synonym: "gel-permeation chromatography" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02262
name: exergonic reaction
def: "This expression is often applied to a reaction for which the overall standard Gibbs energy change \\\(\\Delta G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is negative. Some workers use this term with reference to a negative value of \\\(\\Delta H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) at the absolute zero of temperature."
synonym: "exoergic reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02263
name: exfoliated graphite
def: "The product of very rapid heating \(or flash heating\) of graphite intercalation compounds\, such as graphite hydrogensulfate of relatively large particle diameter \(flakes\). The vaporizing intercalated substances force the graphite layers apart. The exfoliated graphite assumes an accordion-like shape with an apparent volume often hundreds of times that of the original graphite flakes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02265
name: exit channel
def: "This expression refers to the region of a potential-energy surface or hypersurface that corresponds to molecular configurations that are closer to those of the products than to those of the reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02267
name: exoenzymes
def: "Enzymes that cleave monomers \(sometimes also di- or oligomers\) from one end of a polymer chain. Exonucleases are able to cleave nucleotides\, one by one\, from either the 5- or the 3- \(or both\) ends of nucleic acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02268
name: exon
def: "A section of DNA which carries the coding sequence for a protein or part of it. Exons are separated by intervening\, non-coding sequences \(cf. intron\). In eukaryotes most genes consist of a number of exons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02269
name: exothermic reaction
def: "A reaction for which the overall standard enthalpy change \\\(\\Delta H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is negative."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02270
name: expansion factor
def: "The ratio of a dimensional characteristic of a macromolecule in a given solvent at a given temperature to the same dimensional characteristic in the \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B8\}\\\) state at the same temperature. The most frequently used expansion factors are\: expansion factor of the mean-square end-to-end distance\, \\\(\\alpha _\{r\} = \\sqrt\{\\frac\{ < r^\{2\} > \}\{< r^\{2\} >_\{0\}\}\}\\\); expansion factor of the radius of gyration \\\(\\alpha _\{s\} = \\sqrt\{\\frac\{ < s^\{2\} > \}\{ < s^\{2\} >_\{0\}\}\}\\\); viscosity expansion factor \\\(\\alpha _\{\\eta \} = \(\\frac\{\\left\[\\eta \\right\]\}\{\\left\[\\eta \\right\]_\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B8\} \}\}\)^\{\\frac\{1\}\{3\}\}\\\) where \\\(\[\\eta\]\\\) and \\\(\[\\eta\]_\{\\uptheta\}\\\) are the intrinsic viscosity in a given solvent and in the \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B8\}\\\) state at the same temperature\, respectively. Expansion factors defined by different dimensional characteristics are not exactly equal\, nor need they have a constant ratio as a function of relative molecular mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02273
name: experimental perturbational calculation
def: "To assess the uncertainty of derived final results from a least-squares procedure one can investigate the sensitivity of output data for errors in the input data by means of this calculation. One performs the calculations many times with perturbed input data and studies the relation between the changes \(perturbations\) in the input data and the changes in the output data. The perturbations are preferably generated using random numbers with a distribution function chosen to mimic the distribution of uncertainties in the primary results."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02274
name: explosivity limits
def: "The concentration limits\, usually of a substance in air\, between which combustion will be self-sustaining."
synonym: "explosion limits" []
synonym: "flammable limits" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02275
name: exponential decay
def: "Variation of a quantity \(generally the activity of a quantity of a radionuclide\) according to the law \\\(A=A_\{0\}\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{-\\lambda\\ t\}\\\) where \\\(A\\\) and \\\(A_\{0\}\\\) are the values of the quantity being considered at time \\\(t\\\) and zero respectively\, and \\\(\\lambda\\\) is an appropriate constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02277
name: expression
def: "The cellular production of the protein encoded by a particular gene. The process includes transcription of DNA\, processing of the resulting mRNA product and its translation into an active protein.A recombinant gene inserted into a host cell by means of a vector is said to be expressed if the synthesis of the encoded protein can be demonstrated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02278
name: extended-chain crystal
def: "A polymer crystal in which the chains are in an essentially fully extended conformation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02279
name: extended Hammett equation
def: "This term applies in a general way to any multiparametric extension of the Hammett equation. It is sometimes used specifically for a form of dual substituent-parameter equation in which the actual value of the correlated property \\\(P\\\) under the influence of the substituent $\\ce\{X\}$ is used\, rather than the value relative to that for $\\ce\{X\}$ = $\\ce\{H\}$. An intercept term \\\(h\\\) corresponding to the value of \\\(P\\\) for $\\ce\{X\}$ = $\\ce\{H\}$ is introduced\, e.g. \\\[P=\\alpha \\ \\sigma _\{I\}+\\beta \\ \\sigma _\{R\}+h\\\] The equation may be applied to systems for which the inclusion of further terms to represent other effects\, e.g. steric\, is appropriate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02280
name: extended X-ray absorption fine structure
def: "Structure in X-ray absorption spectra remote from the edge and extending to higher energies\, due mainly to the scattering of the expelled electron by neighbouring atoms. Structure closer to the absorption edge is sometimes called near-edge X-ray absorption fine structure \(NEXAFS\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02281
name: extensive quantity
def: "Physical quantity whose magnitude is additive for subsystems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02282
name: extent
def: "A quantity measured by its area\, \\\(A\\\) \(or \\\(S\\\)\). For solids\, a real \(true\, actual\) and a geometric surface \(interface\) area\, \\\(A_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) and \\\(A_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\)\, respectively\, may be defined if asperities are present whose height is orders of magnitude greater than the atomic or molecular size. The geometric surface is the projection of the real surface on a plane parallel to the macroscopic\, visible phase boundary. If asperities are of the order of the atomic size\, the surface of the solid may be described as stepped. \(High index faces of crystals are stepped surfaces but may be ideally smooth in the sense of the roughness factor.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02283
name: extent of reaction
def: "Extensive quantity describing the progress of a chemical reaction equal to the number of chemical transformations\, as indicated by the reaction equation on a molecular scale\, divided by the Avogadro constant \(it is essentially the amount of chemical transformations\). The change in the extent of reaction is given by \\\(\\mathrm\{d\}\\xi =\\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\nu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(\nu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the stoichiometric number of any reaction entity B \(reactant or product\) and \\\(\\mathrm\{d\}n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the corresponding amount."
synonym: "advancement" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02290
name: external standard
def: "A compound present in a standard sample of known concentration and volume which is analysed separately from the unknown sample under identical conditions. It is used to facilitate the qualitative indentification and/or quantitative determination of the sample components. The volume of the external standard \(standard sample\) need not to be known if it is identical to that of the unknown sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02291
name: external surface
def: "When a porous solid consists of discrete particles it is inconvenient to describe the outer boundary of the particles as the external surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02292
name: exterplex
def: "Termolecular analogue of an exciplex. Use of this term is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02295
name: extra-column volume
def: "The volume between the effective injection point and the effective detection point\, excluding the part of the column containing the stationary phase. It is composed of the volumes of the injector\, connecting lines and detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02296
name: extract
def: "The separated phase \(often but not necessarily organic\) that contains the material extracted from the other phase. Where appropriate the term 'loaded solvent' may be used\, but is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02297
name: extract
def: "To transfer a solute from a liquid phase to another immiscible or partially miscible liquid phase in contact with it. The term is also applied to the dissolution of material from a solid phase with a liquid in which it is not wholly soluble \(i.e. leaching\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02298
name: extractability
def: "A property which qualitatively indicates the degree to which a substance is extracted. The term is imprecise and generally used in a qualitative sense. It is not a synonym for fraction extracted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02299
name: extractant
def: "The active component\(s\) primarily responsible for transfer of a solute from one phase to the other."
synonym: "extracting agent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02301
name: extraction
def: "Distribution and partition are often used as synonyms for the general phenomenon of extraction where appropriate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02302
name: extraction
def: "In connection with processes\, this term often refers to the initial transfer step whereby the main solute\, often together with impurities\, is transferred from feed to solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02304
name: extraction constant
def: "The extraction constant at zero ionic strength\, \\\(K_\{\\rm\{ex\}\}^\{\\rm\{0\}\}\\\)\, is the equilibrium constant of the distribution reaction expressed in terms of the reacting species. Thus\, for the gross reaction\: \\\[\\rm\{M\}_\{\\rm\{aq\}\}^\{n+\} + n\\rm\{HL\}_\{\\rm\{org\}\} \\rightleftharpoons  \\rm\{ML\}_\{n\,\\rm\{org\}\} + n\\rm\{H\}_\{\\rm\{aq\}\}^\{+\}\\\] in which the reagent $\\ce\{HL\}$ initially dissolved in an organic phase reacts with a metal ion $\\ce\{M^\{n\}\}$ in aqueous solution to form a product $\\ce\{ML_\{n\}\}$ which is more soluble in the organic phase than in water\, \\\[K_\{\\rm\{ex\}\}^\{\\rm\{0\}\} = \\frac\{a_\{\\rm\{ML \} n\,\\rm\{org\}\}\\hspace\{2pt\}a_\{\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\,\\rm\{aq\}\}^\{n\}\}\{a_\{\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}\,\\rm\{aq\}\}\\hspace\{2pt\}a_\{\\rm\{HL\}\,\\rm\{org\}\}^\{n\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "extraction equilibrium constant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02305
name: extraction factor
def: "The ratio of the total mass of a solute in the extract to that in the other phase."
synonym: "concentration factor" []
synonym: "mass distribution ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02306
name: extraction fractionation
def: "A process in which a polymeric material\, consisting of macromolecules differing in some characteristic affecting their solubility\, is separated from a polymer-rich phase into fractions by successively increasing the solution power of the solvent\, resulting in the repeated formation of a two-phase system in which the more soluble components concentrate in the polymer-poor phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02309
name: extrapolated range
def: "The distance from a radiation source\, calculated by extrapolation to zero of the flux density\, of the tangent to the flux density versus distance curve\, taken at the point where the flux density has decreased to one half of its initial value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02310
name: extremophiles
def: "Organisms which require extreme physico-chemical conditions for their optimum growth and proliferation. Extremophilic microorganisms are e.g. thermophiles or psychrophiles\, halophiles\, alkalophiles or acidophiles\, osmophiles and barophiles\, based on their growth at extremes of temperature\, salt concentration\, pH\, osmolarity or pressure\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E02311
name: extrusion transformation
def: "A transformation in which an atom or group $\\ce\{Y\}$ connected to two other atoms or groups $\\ce\{X\}$ and $\\ce\{Z\}$ is lost from a molecule\, leading to a product in which $\\ce\{X\}$ is bonded to $\\ce\{Z\}$\, e.g.  or  The reverse of an extrusion is called insertion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:E04391
name: π-electron acceptor/donor group
def: "A substituent capable of a $\\ce\{+R\}$ \(e.g.$\\ce\{NO2\}$\) or $\\ce\{-R\}$ \(e.g. $\\ce\{OCH3\}$\) effect\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET02156
name: enzyme induction
def: "The process whereby an \(inducible\) enzyme is synthesized in response to a specific molecule \(cf. inducer\).The inducer molecule \(often a substrate that needs the catalytic activity of the inducible enzyme for its metabolism\) combines with a repressor and thereby prevents the blocking of an operator by the repressor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET02276
name: exposure
def: "For X- or gamma-radiation in air\: the sum of the electrical charges of all the ions of one sign produced when all electronss liberated by photonss in a suitably small element of volume of air completely stopped\, divided by the mass of the air in the volume element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06758
name: electromigration
def: "The transport of particles under the influence of an electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06759
name: Einstein equation
def: "In its most general form\, the Einstein equation relates the atomic mobility \\\(b_\{i\}\\\) of a species \\\(i\\\) to the diffusion coefficient \\\(D_\{i\}\\\)\: i.e.\, \\\(D_\{i\}=k\\ T\\ b_\{i\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06763
name: entatic state
def: "A state of an atom or group which\, due to its binding in a protein\, has its geometric or electronic condition adapted for function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06811
name: exposure
def: "Concentration\, amount or intensity of a particular physical or chemical agent or environmental agent that reaches the target population\, organism\, organ\, tissue\, or cell\, usually expressed in numerical terms of concentration\, duration\, and frequency \(for chemical agents and micro-organisms\) or intensity \(for physical agents\).Note\: This differs from the definition of exposure in radiation chemistry.Process by which a substance becomes available for absorption by the target population\, organism\, organ\, tissue or cell\, by any route."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06822
name: endocytosis
def: "Uptake of material into a cell by invagination of the plasma membrane and its internalisation in a membrane-bounded vesicle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06823
name: enzyme induction
def: "Process whereby an enzyme is synthesised in response to the presence of a specific substance or to other agents such as heat or a metal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06851
name: environmental stress cracking
def: "Cracking caused by the combined actions of \(i\) mechanical stress and \(ii\) chemical agents or radiation or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06890
name: electro-dialysis
def: "Membrane-based separation process in which ions are driven through an ion-selective membrane under the influence of an electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06891
name: electro-osmosis
def: "Process in which water is transported across the thickness of an anion- exchange or cation-exchange membrane under an applied electric field."
synonym: "electroosmosis" []
synonym: "electrosmosis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06895
name: enantiotropic mesophase
def: "A mesophase that is thermodynamically stable over a definite temperature or pressure range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06907
name: environmental monitoring
def: "Continuous or repeated measurement of agents in the \(working\) environment to evaluate environmental exposure and possible damage by comparison with appropriate reference values based on knowledge of the probable relationship between ambient exposure and resultant health effects."
synonym: "ambient monitoring" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06922
name: electro-osmotic hold-up time
def: "Time required for a liquid in a capillary to move due to electro-osmosis through the effective length of the capillary\, \\\(L_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}\\\). This time is usually measured as the migration time of a neutral compound\, called an electro-osmotic flow marker which is assumed to have an electro-osmotic mobility that is negligible compared to that of the analyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06923
name: electro-osmotic mobility
def: "Electro-osmotic velocity divided by electric field \(strength\) \\\[\\mu _\{\\rm\{eo\}\}=\\frac\{\nu _\{\\rm\{eo\}\}\}\{E\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET06925
name: electrophoretic velocity
def: "The velocity of a charged analyte under the influence of an electric field relative to the background electrolyte. Also called rate of migration \(in electrophoresis\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07022
name: electron-counting rules
def: "Rules establishing correspondence between the topology of a molecular structure and the number of electrons which may be placed into its bonding molecular orbitals. In the origin of various electron-counting rules lies a general assumption that the completeness of the valence electron shell of a molecular entity belonging to a certain structural type serves as the major criterion of structural stability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07023
name: electron-deficient compounds
def: "Molecules or ions that contain too few electrons to allow their bonding to be described exclusively in terms of two-center\, two-electron\, i.e.\, covalent\, bonds. Such molecules or certain fragments in these are often held together by the multicenter bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07024
name: electron density function
def: "The electron probability distribution function\, \\\(\\rho \\\)\, defined as \\\[\\rho \(\\mathbf\{r\}\) = n\\ \\int \\Psi ^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\left\[\\mathbf\{r\}\(1\)\,\\mathbf\{r\}\(2\)\\\,\\rm\{...\}\\\,\\mathbf\{r\}\(n\)\\right\]\\ \\Psi \\left\[\\mathbf\{r\}\(1\)\,\\mathbf\{r\}\(2\)\\\,\\rm\{...\}\\\,\\mathbf\{r\}\(n\)\\right\]\\rm\{d\}\\mathbf\{r\}\(2\)\\\,\\rm\{...\}\\\,\\rm\{d\}\\mathbf\{r\}\(n\)\\\] where \\\(\\Psi \\\) is an electronic wave-function and integration is made over the coordinates of all but the first electron of \\\(n\\\). The physical interpretation of the electron density function is that \\\(\\rho \\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\mathbf\{\\mathbf\{r\}\}\\\) gives the probability of finding an electron in a volume element \\\(\\mathrm\{d\}\\mathbf\{\\mathbf\{r\}\}\\\)\, i.e.\, electron density in this volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07025
name: electronic chemical potential
def: "The quantity that measures the escaping tendency of electrons from a species in its ground state. It is the negative of the absolute electronegativity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07026
name: electronic state
def: "An arrangement allowed by the laws of quantum mechanics of electrons within an atom\, molecule \(or system of molecules\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07027
name: electronic stability
def: "Unavailability of another electronic structure \(different electronic state\) of lower energy with the same number of electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07028
name: energy gradient
def: "First derivatives of the total energy with respect to nuclear coordinates\, i.e.\, negative values of forces on the nuclei. Evaluation of energy gradient plays a central role in searching potential energy surfaces for stationary points. A widespread technique is based on the minimization of the gradient norm \(the square of energy gradient\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07029
name: energy hypersurface
def: "The notion of hypersurface is used to stress the multidimensionality of PESs. In a molecular system consisting of \\\(N\\\) atomic nuclei\, the number of the independent coordinates that fully determine the PES is equal to \\\(3\\ N-6\\\) \(or \\\(3\\ N-5\\\) if the system is linear\)."
synonym: "potential energy surface" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07030
name: equalization of electronegativity\, principle of
def: "The postulate that in a molecule all the constituent atoms should have the same electronegativity value\, which would be the geometric mean of the electronegativities of isolated atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07031
name: equilibrium geometry
def: "Molecular geometry that corresponds to the true minimum on the respective potential energy surface. While information relating to the equilibrium geometry is provided by calculations within the adiabatic approximation \(minimization of the total energy with respect to any independent geometrical parameter\)\, various experiments yield some effective geometries for the molecule which are averaged over molecular vibrations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07032
name: extended Hückel MO method
def: "A semi-empirical all-valence electron quantum mechanical method which uses the same approximations\, apart from $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}approximation\}$ and neglect of overlap integrals\, as those of the Hückel molecular orbital theory. The method reproduces relatively well the shapes and the order of energy levels of molecular orbitals. The account for overlap makes it possible to describe the net destabilization caused by interaction of two doubly occupied orbitals\, which effect is not reproduced by HMO theory."
synonym: "EHMO method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07034
name: exchange repulsion
def: "The correction to the Coulomb repulsion between two electrons in orbitals \\\(\\Psi _\{i\}\\\) and \\\(\\Psi _\{j\}\\\) for the case when the electrons possess parallel spins. It is to be subtracted from the Coulomb repulsion to give the total energy of the electron–electron interaction. In the Hartree–Fock theory the magnitude of the exchange repulsion is given by the exchange integral \\\[K_\{ij\}=\\int \\int \\Psi _\{i\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{1\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{j\}^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{1\}\\right\)\\ \\left\(\\frac\{e^\{2\}\}\{r_\{12\}\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{i\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{2\}\\right\)\\ \\Psi _\{j\}\\left\(\\mathbf\{r\}_\{2\}\\right\)  \\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\mathbf\{r\}_\{1\}  \\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\mathbf\{r\}_\{2\}=\\lt ij|ji \\gt\\\] For the case of electrons with opposite spins \\\(K_\{ij\}\\\) vanishes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07171
name: electroluminescent polymer
def: "Polymeric material that shows luminescence when an electric current passes through it such that charge carriers can combine at luminescent sites to give rise to electronically excited states of luminescent groups or molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07205
name: electrically conducting polymer
def: "Polymeric material that exhibits bulk electric conductivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07249
name: exfoliation
def: "Process by which the layers of a multi-layered structure separate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07267
name: extender
def: "Substance\, especially a diluent or modifier\, added to a polymer to increase its volume without substantially altering the desirable properties of the polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07367
name: electron back-transfer
def: "Thermal inversion of excited-state electron transfer restoring the donor and acceptor in their original oxidation state. In using this term one should also specify the resulting electronic state of the donor and acceptor. This term is preferred to back electron-transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07368
name: electrostriction
def: "Contraction of a dielectric in an electric-field gradient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07369
name: El-Sayed rules
def: "The rate of intersystem crossing is relatively large if the radiationless transition involves a change of orbital type."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07370
name: emission anisotropy
def: "Used to characterize luminescence \(fluorescence\, phosphorescence\) polarization resulting from photoselection. Defined as\: \\\[r = \\frac\{I_\{\\parallel\} - I_\{\\perp \}\}\{I_\{\\parallel\} + 2I_\{\\perp \}\}\\\] where \\\(I_\{\\parallel\}\\\) and \\\(I_\{\\perp \}\\\) are the intensities measured with the linear polarizer for emission parallel and perpendicular\, respectively\, to the electric vector of linearly polarized incident electromagnetic radiation \(which is often vertical\). The quantity \\\(I_\{\\parallel\} + 2I_\{\\perp \}\\\) is proportional to the total fluorescence intensity \\\(I\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07371
name: entrainment
def: "Term used in photostimulated radical nucleophilic substitution to designate the induction of the reaction of a less reactive \(or non-reactive\) nucleophile with a substrate by the addition of catalytic amounts of another nucleophile\, more reactive at initiation. Often\, the non-reactive nucleophile at initiation is\, however\, quite reactive at propagation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07372
name: excimer lamp
def: "Non-coherent source of ultraviolet radiation capable of producing quasi-monochromatic radiation from the near UV \(\\\(\\lambda = 354\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\) to the vacuum UV \(\\\(\\lambda = 126\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\). The operation of the excimer lamps relies on the radiative decomposition of excimers or exciplexes created by various types of discharges."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07373
name: exposure
def: "Term generally applied to the time-integrated radiation incident from all upward directions on a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07374
name: <i>E</i>/<i>Z</i> photoisomerization
def: "General designation of a photoinduced geometrical isomerization of a C–C double bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07547
name: elastomer
def: "Polymer that displays rubber-like elasticity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07565
name: entanglement network
def: "Polymer network with junction points or zones formed by physically entangled chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07598
name: elastically active network chain
def: "Segment of a chain between two successive crosslinks in a polymer network that is long enough to show entropic elasticity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ET07615
name: electron beam curing
def: "Curing induced by electron beam irradiation."
synonym: "EB curing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02313
name: <i>fac</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to designate three groups occupying the corners of the same face of an octahedral coordination sphere\, not now generally recommended for precise nomenclature purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02314
name: factor
def: "Proportionality constant between two quantities of the same dimension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02316
name: fallout
def: "A measurement of air contamination consisting of the mass rate at which solid particles deposit from the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02319
name: fanning
def: "In blast furnace operation\, the idling period between the blowing periods when the blast pressure is reduced to a minimum. This also applies to plume behaviour under inversion conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02320
name: farad
def: "SI derived unit of electric capacitance\, \\\(\\pu\{F\} = \\pu\{C V-1\} = \\pu\{m-2 kg-1 s4 A2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02321
name: faradaic current
def: "A current corresponding to the reduction or oxidation of some chemical substance. The net faradaic current is the algebraic sum of all the faradaic currents flowing through an indicator or working electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02322
name: faradaic current density
def: "Measures the rate of the interfacial reaction\, the proportionality constant being the charge number of the reaction multiplied by the Faraday constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02323
name: faradaic demodulation current
def: "Component of the current that is due to the demodulation associated with an electrode reaction and that appears if an indicator or working electrode is subjected to the action of two intermodulated applied potentials of different frequency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02324
name: faradaic rectification current
def: "A component of the current that is due to the rectifying properties of an electrode reaction and that appears if an indicator or working electrode is subjected to any periodically varying applied potential while the mean value of the applied potential is controlled."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02325
name: Faraday constant
def: "Fundamental physical constant representing molar elementary charge\: \\\(F = \\pu\{9.64853399\(24\)E4 C mol-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02326
name: Faraday cup collector
def: "A hollow collector\, open at one end and closed at the other\, used to collect beams of ions."
synonym: "cylinder collector" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02327
name: fast atom bombardment ionization
def: "This term is used to describe ionization of any species by causing interaction of the sample \(which may be dissolved in a solvent matrix\) and a beam of neutral atoms having high translational energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02328
name: fast-atom bombardment mass spectrometry
def: "A method in which ions are produced in a mass spectrometer from non-volatile or thermally fragile organic molecules by bombarding the compound in the condensed phase with energy-rich neutral particles."
synonym: "FAB mass spectrometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02329
name: fast neutrons
def: "Neutrons of kinetic energy greater than some specified value. This value may vary over a wide range and will be dependent upon the application."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02330
name: fatty acids
def: "Aliphatic monocarboxylic acids derived from or contained in esterified form in an animal or vegetable fat\, oil or wax. Natural fatty acids commonly have a chain of 4 to 28 carbons \(usually unbranched and even-numbered\)\, which may be saturated or unsaturated. By extension\, the term is sometimes used to embrace all acyclic aliphatic carboxylic acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02331
name: feedback
def: "The process whereby the output of a device is used to modify the operation of an analytical instrument."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02333
name: feed-back inhibition
def: "A metabolic control mechanism in which the end product of a biochemical sequence is able to inhibit the activity of an early enzyme in the sequence\, thereby controlling the metabolic flux through this pathway. As an example\, isoleucine controls its own synthesis by inhibiting threonine deaminase; adenosine 5-triphosphate \(ATP\) and citrate control glycolysis by inhibiting phosphofructokinase."
synonym: "end product inhibition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02334
name: feed rate
def: "The amount of reactant fed per unit time to the inlet of the reactor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02335
name: femto
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-15\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{f\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02336
name: fenestranes
def: "Compounds of the general formula shown above. They may be considered to be spiro compounds having bridges of carbon atoms connecting the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!'\}$ positions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02337
name: fermentation
def: "In metabolism\, the dehydrogenating degradation of organic substance by organisms or cells under anaerobic conditions in which electrons are transferred to metabolites which accumulate and are excreted in reduced form. Fermentation is only possible if the organism is able to gain energy by this process.In microbiology\, the process in which cells \(microorganisms\, plant or animal cells\) are cultured in a bioreactor in liquid or solid medium to convert organic substances into biomass \(growth\) or into products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02338
name: fermenter
def: "A bioreactor which enables optimal fermentation conditions to be maintained\, allowing addition of nutrients\, removal of products and insertion of measuring and/or control probes as well as other necessary equipment \(e.g. for heating\, cooling\, aeration\, agitation\, sterilization\, etc.\) under sterile conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02339
name: fermi
def: "Non-SI unit of length\, \\\(\\pu\{f\} = \\pu\{E-15 m\}\\\)\, used in nuclear physics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02340
name: Fermi energy
def: "The total energy of an electron in an uncharged metal at the Fermi level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02341
name: Fermi level
def: "The chemical potential of electrons in a solid \(metals\, semiconductors or insulators\) or in an electrolyte solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02342
name: fermion
def: "Particle of half-integer spin quantum number following Fermi–Dirac statistics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02345
name: ferrocenophanes
def: "Compounds in which the two ring components of ferrocene are linked by one or more bridging chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02346
name: ferroelastic transition
def: "A transition in which a crystal switches from one stable orientation state into another that is equally stable by the application of a mechanical stress along an appropriate direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02347
name: ferroelectric transition
def: "A transition from a ferroelectric to either another ferroelectric\, or a paraelectric\, or an antiferroelectric state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02348
name: ferroic transition
def: "A general term for ferroelastic\, ferroelectric\, antiferroelectric\, ferromagnetic\, antiferromagnetic and ferrimagnetic transitions in which a suitable driving force switches a ferroic crystal from one orientation state\, or domain state\, to another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02350
name: fertile
def: "Of a nuclide\, capable of being transformed\, directly or indirectly\, into a fissile nuclide by neutron captureOf a material\: containing one or more fertile nuclides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02351
name: <i>f</i>-functional branch point
def: "Branch point from which f linear chains emanate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02353
name: fibrous activated carbon
def: "An activated carbon in the form of fibres\, filaments\, yarns or rovings and fabrics or felts. Such fibres differ from carbon fibres used for reinforcement purposes in composites by their high surface area\, high porosity and low mechanical strength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02355
name: fibrous crystal in polymers
def: "A type of crystal significantly longer in one dimension than in either of the other two. Fibrous crystals may comprise essentially extended chains parallel to the fibre axis; however\, macroscopic polymer fibres containing chain-folded crystals are also known."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02357
name: field desorption
def: "A term used to describe the formation of ions in the gas phase from a material deposited on a solid surface \(known as an 'emitter'\) in the presence of a high electrical field. 'Field desorption' is an ambiguous term because it implies that the electric field desorbs a material as an ion from some kind of emitter on which the material is deposited. There is growing evidence that some of the ions formed are due to thermal ionization and some to field ionization of vapour evaporated from material on the emitter. Because there is generally little or no ionization unless the emitter is heated by an electric current\, 'field desorption' is a misnomer. The term is\, however\, firmly implanted in the literature and most users undertand what is going on regardless of the implications of the term. In addition\, no better simple term has been suggested to take its place and so\, reluctantly\, it is recommended that it be retained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02358
name: field effect
def: "An experimentally observable effect symbolized by \\\(F\\\) \(on reaction rates\, etc.\) of intramolecular coulombic interaction between the centre of interest and a remote unipole or dipole\, by direct action through space rather than through bonds. The magnitude of the field effect \(or 'direct effect'\) depends on the unipolar charge/dipole moment\, orientation of dipole\, shortest distance between the centre of interest and the remote unipole or dipole\, and on the effective dielectric constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02360
name: field ionization
def: "The removal of electrons from any species by interaction with a high electrical field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02361
name: field level
def: "Logarithm of the ratio of two values of a field quantity\, usually amplitude. Levels are expressed in different ways\: \\\(L_\{F\}=\\ln \(\\frac\{F\}\{F_\{0\}\}\)\\ N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) is the symbol for the unit neper coherent with the SI\, or \\\(L_\{F\}=20\\ \\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{F\}\{F_\{0\}\}\)\\ \\mathrm\{dB\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\mathrm\{dB\}\\\) is the symbol for the unit decibel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02362
name: filamentous carbon
def: "A carbonaceous deposit from gaseous carbon compounds\, consisting of filaments grown by the catalytic action of metal particles."
synonym: "fibrous carbon" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02363
name: filler
def: "Filler \(also called grist\) is a petroleum- or coal-based coke fraction of a green\, carbon mix or formulation. Coarse particles\, \\\(>0.425\\ \\mathrm\{mm\}\\\)\, are sometimes referred to as tailings; fine particles\, \\\("
synonym: "grist" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02364
name: filler coke
def: "The main constituent of a carbon artifact\, introduced as solid component \(predominantly in the form of particulate carbon\) into the 'carbon mix' from which polygranular carbon and graphite materials are obtained by heat treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02365
name: filling solution
def: "Solution containing the anion to which the reference electrode of the operational pH cell is reversible\, e.g. chloride for silver-silver chloride electrode. In the absence of a bridge solution\, a high concentration of filling solution comprising cations and anions of almost equal mobility is employed as a means of maintaining the liquid junction potential small and approximately constant on substitution of test solution for standard solution\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02366
name: film
def: "A generic term referring to condensed matter restricted in one dimension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02368
name: film badge
def: "A package containing one or more small photographic films for approximate measurement of radiation exposure\, used for the purpose of monitoring personnel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02370
name: film element
def: "A small homogeneous part of a film including the two interfaces and any fluid between them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02381
name: film tension
def: "The contractile force per unit length\, \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3A3\}_\{\\rm\{f\}\}\\\)\, exerted by an equilibrium film in contact with the bulk phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02384
name: filter
def: "A porous material on which solid particles present in air or other fluid which flows through it are largely caught and retained. Filters are made with a variety of materials\: cellulose and derivatives\, glass fibre\, ceramic\, synthetic plastics and fibres. Filters may be naturally porous or be made so by mechanical or other means. membrane/ceramic filters are prepared with highly controlled pore size in a sheet of suitable material such as polyfluoroethylene\, polycarbonate or cellulose esters. Nylon mesh is sometimes used for reinforcement. The pores constitute 80–85% of the filter volume commonly and several pore sizes are available for air sampling \(\\\(0.45\{-\}0.8\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) are commonly employed\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02385
name: filter
def: "Material interposed in the path of radiation to modify the spectral distribution of the radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02387
name: filter spectrometer
def: "A spectrometer which has one or more spectral filters for isolating one or more spectral bands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02388
name: filtration
def: "The process of segregation of phases; e.g. the separation of suspended solids from a liquid or gas\, usually by forcing a carrier gas or liquid through a porous medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02389
name: fine structure constant
def: "Atomic fundamental physical constant\, \\\(\\alpha =\\frac\{\\mu _\{0\}\\ e^\{2\}\\ c_\{0\}\}\{2\\ h\} = 7.297\\ 353\\ 08\\ \\left\(33\\right\)\\times 10^\{-3\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\mu _\{0\}\\\) is the permeability of vacuum\, \\\(e\\\) the elementary charge\, \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant\, and \\\(c_\{0\}\\\) the speed of light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02390
name: first-order phase transition
def: "A transition in which the molar Gibbs energies or molar Helmholtz energies of the two phases \(or chemical potentials of all components in the two phases\) are equal at the transition temperature\, but their first derivatives with respect to temperature and pressure \(for example\, specific enthalpy of transition and specific volume\) are discontinuous at the transition point\, as for two dissimilar phases that coexist and that can be transformed into one another by a change in a field variable such as pressure\, temperature\, magnetic or electric field. Example\: The transition on heating $\\ce\{CsCl\}$ to \\\(752\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) at which it changes from the low-temperature\, $\\ce\{CsCl\}$-type structure to the high-temperature $\\ce\{NaCl\}$-type structure. Synonymous with discontinuous phase transition."
synonym: "discontinuous phase transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02391
name: Fischer projection
def: "A projection formula in which vertically drawn bonds are considered to lie below the projection plane and horizontal bonds to lie above that plane. Thus for the molecule Cabcd\:"
synonym: "Fischer–Tollens projection" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02392
name: Fischer–Rosanoff convention
def: "An arbitrary convention according to which \(+\)-glyceraldehyde\, now known to be \(R\)-2\,3-dihydroxypropanal\, was named ᴅ-glyceraldehyde \(with the enantiomer ʟ-glyceraldehyde and its racemate ᴅʟ-glyceraldehyde\) and taken to have the absolute configuration represented by the Fischer projection formula shown in the diagram.  The atom numbered 1 according to normal nomenclature rules is conventionally placed at the top of the main chain\, which is drawn vertically and other groups are drawn on either side of that main chain. The convention is still in use for $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}amino acids\}$ and for sugars."
synonym: "Rosanoff convention" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02394
name: fissile
def: "Of a nuclide\: capable of undergoing fission by interaction with \(slow\) neutrons.Of a material\: containing one or more fissile nuclides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02395
name: fissionable
def: "Synonymous with fissile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02397
name: fission fragments
def: "Nuclei resulting from fission and possessing kinetic energy acquired from the fission process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02398
name: fission neutrons
def: "Neutrons originating in the fission process which have retained their original energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02400
name: fission products
def: "Nuclides produced by fission and the daughter products of these nuclides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02401
name: fission yield
def: "The fraction of fissions leading to fission products of a given type."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02402
name: fixed ions
def: "In an ion exchanger\, the non-exchangeable ions which have a charge opposite to that of the counter-ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02403
name: fixed neutral loss scan
def: "A scan which determines\, in a single experiment\, all the parent ion mass-to-charge ratios which react to the loss or gain of a selected neutral mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02404
name: fixed neutral loss spectrum
def: "A spectrum obtained when data are acquired that determine all the parent ion mass-to-charge ratios that react by the loss \(gain\) of a selected neutral mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02405
name: fixed precursor ion scan
def: "A scan which determines\, in a single experiment\, all the daughter ion mass-to-charge ratios that are produced by the reaction of a selected parent ion mass-to-charge ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02406
name: fixed product ion scan
def: "A scan which determines\, in a single experiment\, all the parent ion mass-to-charge ratios that react to produce a selected daughter ion mass-to-charge ratio \(sometimes called a 'parent ion scan'\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02407
name: fixed product ion spectrum
def: "A spectrum obtained when data are acquired that determine all the parent ion mass-to-charge ratios that react to produce a selected daughter ion mass-to-charge ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02409
name: flame-in-tube atomizer
def: "An atomizer in which hydrogen\, used as carrier gas\, is partially combusted in the inlet arm of a T-shaped tube furnace by introducing a limited amount of oxygen or air."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02410
name: flame ionization detector
def: "The gases emerging from the column are fed into a hydrogen flame across which an electrical potential is placed. Certain molecules ionize easily in the flame and the current produced is proportional to the instantaneous flow rate of the eluted component. The detector is relatively insensitive to inorganic molecules and is most used for organic compounds. Concentrations below \\\(1\\ \\rm\{ppmv\}\\\) are easily detected. The linear dynamic range is between \\\(10^\{3\}\\\) and \\\(10^\{5\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02411
name: flame photometric detector
def: "The eluent from the column is fed into a hydrogen-rich flame and produces light emission. Optical filters are used to select the wavelength range of the emission which is characteristic of specific atoms \(usually sulfur or phosphorus\). The detector is very specific\, depending on the choice of optical filters. It can detect the S- and P-containing compounds down to \\\(10^\{-3\}\\ \\rm\{ppmv\}\\\)\, but the detector is non-linear."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02412
name: flame photometry
def: "The use of emission spectroscopy in the ultraviolet and visible regions to identify and estimate the amounts of various elements which are excited in a flame\, an arc or high voltage spark."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02416
name: flash fluorimetry
def: "The term used when decay times luminescence are measured using a pulsed source of radiation. It is often necessary to separate the signal due to the light flash from luminescence emission signal by a deconvolution technique in order to obtain the correct decay curve for emission. Decay times corrected for this effect are termed corrected decay times of fluorescence or phosphorescence."
synonym: "phosphorimetry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02417
name: flash lamps
def: "Lamps which contain an inert gas which can be rapidly pulsed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02418
name: flash photolysis
def: "A technique of transient spectroscopy and transient kinetic studies in which a light pulse is used to produce transient species. Commonly\, an intense pulse of short duration is used to produce a sufficient concentration of a transient species suitable for spectroscopic observation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02419
name: flash point
def: "The lowest temperature at which a substance\, e.g. fuel oil\, will give off a vapour that will flash or burn momentarily when ignited."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02420
name: flash vacuum pyrolysis
def: "Thermal reaction of a molecule by exposing it to a short thermal shock at high temperature\, usually in the gas phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02421
name: flat band potential
def: "If effects of surface states are negligible\, \\\(\\Delta \\varphi ^\{\\rm\{sc\}\}\\\)\, the potential drop associated with the space charge in the semiconductor\, vanishes as the charge on the semiconductor becomes zero. The potential of the semiconductor corresponding to this condition is the flat band potential\, which plays the same role as the potential of zero charge for metals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02423
name: flavins
def: "Derivatives of the dimethylisoalloxazine \(7\,8-dimethylbenzo\[g\]pteridine- 2\,4\(3H\,10H\)-dione\) skeleton\, with a substituent on the 10 position. \(Riboflavin\, having a 10-ᴅ-ribityl group\, is the most prominent member\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02424
name: flavonoids
def: "Flavonoids\, isoflavonoids and neoflavonoids are natural products derived from 2-phenylchromen-4-one \(flavone\)\, 3-phenylchromen-4-one and 4-phenylcoumarin\, respectively. Derivations include reduction of the 2\(3\) carbon-carbon double bond \(flavanones\)\, reduction of the keto group \(flavanols\)\, and hydroxylation at various positions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02425
name: flavoproteins
def: "Flavins tightly bound or covalently attached to a protein chain\, commonly through the 8-methyl carbon atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02428
name: floc
def: "When a sol is colloidally unstable the formation of aggregates is called coagulation or flocculation. Some authors distinguish between coagulation and flocculation\, the latter implying the formation of a loose or open network which may or may not separate macroscopically. The loose structure formed in this way is called a floc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02430
name: floccule
def: "A small loosely aggregated mass of material suspended in or precipitated from a liquid; a cluster of particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02431
name: Flory–Huggins theory
def: "A thermodynamic theory of polymer solutions\, first formulated independently by Flory and by Huggins\, in which the thermodynamic quantities of the solution are derived from a simple concept of combinatorial entropy of mixing and a reduced Gibbs energy parameter\, the 'χ parameter'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02432
name: flotation
def: "The removal of matter by entrainment at an interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02433
name: flow analysis
def: "The generic name for all analytical methods that are based on the introduction and processing of test samples in flowing media."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02436
name: flow injection
def: "The introduction of a sample or reagent into a continuous stream by use of a rapid delivery device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02437
name: flow injection enthalpimetry
def: "A term used to describe several related methods in which a transient temperature change in a flowing liquid stream\, caused by a chemical reaction\, is used to quantitatively determine an analyte. The analyte is introduced as a discrete liquid sample \(i.e. test portion\) into the flowing stream. This definition limits the scope to experiments with primarily an analytical emphasis\, performed in a flow injection calorimeter. This maintains a clear distinction from classical flow calorimetric methods where reagents are combined continuously in flowing streams\, or where the heat effect is measured via the flowing of a fluid over a reaction vessel. An acceptable synonym is enthalpimetric flow injection analysis. Other non-recommended terms which have been used in the literature include 'peak enthalpimetry' and 'enzyme thermistor'. Flow enthalpimetry has been used to describe the flow injection technique\, but its use for this purpose is discouraged."
synonym: "enthalpimetric flow injection analysis" []
synonym: "flow enthalpimetry" []
synonym: "peak enthalpimetry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02438
name: flow-programmed chromatography
def: "A procedure in which the rate of flow of the mobile phase is changed systematically during a part or the whole of the separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02439
name: flow rate
def: "The volume of mobile phase passing through the column in unit time. The flow rate is usually measured at column outlet\, at ambient pressure \(\\\(p_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\) and temperature \(\\\(T_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\, in \\\(\\rm\{K\}\\\)\); this value is indicated with the symbol \\\(F\\\). If a water-containing flowmeter was used for the measurement \(e.g. the so-called soap bubble flowmeter\) then \\\(F\\\) must be corrected to dry gas conditions in order to obtain the mobile phase flow rate at ambient temperature \(\\\(F_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\[F_\{\\rm\{a\}\}=F\\ \(1- \\frac\{p_\{\\rm\{w\}\}\}\{p_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}\)\\\] where \\\(p_\{\\rm\{w\}\}\\\) is the partial pressure of water vapour at ambient temperature. In order to specify chromatographic conditions in column chromatography\, the flow-rate \(mobile phase flow rate at column temperature\, \\\(F_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\)\) must be expressed at \\\(T_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) \(kelvin\)\, the column temperature\: \\\[F_\{\\rm\{c\}\}=F_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\ \\frac\{T_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\}\{T_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02440
name: flow rate
def: "The volume rate of one component\, $\\ce\{\\textbf\{X\}\}$ \(e.g. air\, $\\ce\{O2\}$\)\, of the unburnt gas mixture \(such as $\\ce\{C2H2\}$\, $\\ce\{O2\}$\, etc.\) in the burner tip. Ambient temperature and pressure must be stated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02441
name: flow rate
def: "Quantity \\\(X\\\) \(e.g. heat\, amount\, mass\, volume\, ...\) transferred in a time interval divided by that time interval. General symbols\: \\\(q_\{X\}\\\)\, \\\(\\overset\{.\}\{X\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02442
name: flow rate of unburnt gas mixture
def: "The volume rate of the gas mixture of the burner tip. It depends on operating conditions such as gas pressure; the ambient temperature and pressure should also be stated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02443
name: flow reactor
def: "A reactor through which the reactants pass while catalysis is in progress."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02444
name: flow resistance parameter
def: "A term used to compare packing density and permeability of columns packed with different particles; it is dimensionless. \\\[\\mathit\{\\Phi\} = \\frac\{d_\{\\rm\{p\}\}^\{2\}\}\{B_\{0\}\}\\\] where \\\(d_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) is the average particle diameter. In open-tubular columns \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} = 32\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02446
name: flue gas
def: "Waste gas from the combustion process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02447
name: flue gas scrubber
def: "Equipment for removing fly ash and other objectionable materials from the products of combustion by means of sprays or wet baffles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02448
name: fluence
def: "At a given point in space\, the radiant energy incident on a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere. It is used in photochemistry to specify the energy delivered in a given time interval \(for instance by a laser pulse\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02449
name: fluid coke
def: "The carbonization product of high-boiling hydrocarbon fractions \(heavy residues of petroleum or coal processing\) produced by the fluid coking process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02450
name: fluidity
def: "Reciprocal of dynamic viscosity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02451
name: fluidized bed
def: "A catalyst bed in which the flow of gases is sufficient to cause the finely divided particles to behave like a fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02453
name: fluorescence
def: "Luminescence which occurs essentially only during the irradiation of a substance by electromagnetic radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02455
name: fluorescence error
def: "Some samples are excited to fluorescence by the incident radiation beam. When added to the transmitted radiation beam this fluorescence results in an erroneous reading called the fluorescence error. It is particularly noticeable if the photodetector is situated close to the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02457
name: fluorescence yield
def: "For a given transition from an excited state of a specified atom\, the ratio of the number of excited atoms which emit a photon to the total number of excited atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02458
name: fluorimeter
def: "An instrument used to measure the intensity and the wavelength distribution of the light emitted as fluorescence from a molecule excited at a specific wavelength or wavelengths within the absorption band of a particular compound. Characteristic fluorescence bands may be used to identify specific pollutants such as the polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons. Excitation spectra of impurities can be observed by scanning the wavelength of the excitation light which is incident on the sample over a range of wavelengths and observing the relative intensity of the fluorescence emitted at a given wavelength. These spectra are also characteristic of the impurity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02459
name: fluorocarbons
def: "Compounds consisting wholly of fluorine and carbon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02461
name: flux
def: "Flow rate of X through a cross-section perpendicular to the flow divided by the cross-sectional area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02462
name: flux depression
def: "The lowering of the particle \(or photon\) flux density in the neighbourhood of an object due to absorption of particles \(or photons\) in the object."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02463
name: fluxional
def: "A chemical species is said to be fluxional if it undergoes rapid degenerate rearrangements \(generally detectable by methods which allow the observation of the behaviour of individual nuclei in a rearranged chemical species\, e.g. NMR\, X-ray\). Example\: bullvalene \(1 209 600 interconvertible arrangements of the ten CH groups\).  The term is also used to designate positional change among ligands of complex compounds and organometallics. In these cases\, the change is not necessarily degenerate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02465
name: fly ash
def: "Finely divided particles of ash entrained in flue gases resulting from the combustion of fuel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02467
name: foam
def: "A dispersion in which a large proportion of gas by volume in the form of gas bubbles\, is dispersed in a liquid\, solid or gel. The diameter of the bubbles is usually larger than \\\(1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\, but the thickness of the lamellae between the bubbles is often in the usual colloidal size range. The term froth has been used interchangeably with foam. In particular cases froth may be distinguished from foam by the fact that the former is stabilized by solid particles \(as in froth flotation q.v.\) and the latter by soluble substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02468
name: foam fractionation
def: "A method of separation in which a component of the bulk liquid is preferentially adsorbed at the liquid/vapour \(L/V\) interface and is removed by foaming."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02469
name: foaming agent
def: "A surfactant which when present in small amounts facilitates the formation of a foam\, or enhances its colloidal stability by inhibiting the coalescence of bubbles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02470
name: fog
def: "A general term applied to a suspension of droplets in a gas. In meteorology\, it refers to a suspension of water droplets resulting in a visibility of less than \\\(1\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02471
name: fog horizon
def: "The top of a fog layer which is confined by a low-level temperature inversion and gives the appearance of the horizon \(which it actually obscures\) when viewed from above\, against the sky."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02472
name: folates
def: "A group of heterocyclic compounds based on the pteroic acid skeleton \(below\) conjugated with one or more ʟ-glutamate units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02473
name: fold
def: "The loop connecting two different stems in a folded chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02474
name: fold domain
def: "A portion of a polymer crystal wherein the fold planes have the same orientation. The sectors of lamellar crystals frequently represent fold domains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02475
name: folded-chain crystal
def: "A polymer crystal consisting predominantly of chains that traverse the crystal repeatedly by folding as they emerge at its external surfaces. The re-entry of the chain into the crystal is assumed to be adjacent or near-adjacent within the lattice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02476
name: fold plane
def: "A crystallographic plane defined by a large number of stems that are connected by chain folds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02477
name: fold surface
def: "A surface approximately tangential to the folds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02478
name: foot
def: "Non-SI unit of length\, \\\(\\pu\{ft\} = \\pu\{12 in\} = \\pu\{0.3048 m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02480
name: force
def: "Derivative of momentum with respect to time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02481
name: force constants
def: "The coefficients in an expansion of the intramolecular potential function in terms of a definite set of coordinates\, the values of which define the deformation of the molecule away from its equilibrium configuration. In order to define force constants\, one has to indicate both the definition of each coordinate and the expansion of the potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02484
name: formamidine disulfides
def: "The compound $\\ce\{H2NC\(=NH\)SSC\(=NH\)NH2\}$ and its derivatives formed by substitution at nitrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02485
name: formation constant
def: "For a mononuclear binary complex\, if a central atom \(central group\) $\\ce\{M\}$ \(the 'metal'\) and a ligand $\\ce\{L\}$ have been defined\, then in the following expressions \\\(K_\{n\}\\\) is the stepwise formation constant\, and \\\(\\beta_\{n\}\\\) is the cumulative formation constant for the complex $\\ce\{ML_\{n\}\}$. They can both be referred to as stability constants \(stepwise and cumulative\). \\\[K_\{n\}=K\(\\rm\{ML\}_\{n- 1\}+\\rm\{L\}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm\{ML\}_\{n\}\)\\\] \\\[\\beta_\{n\} = K\(\\rm\{M\}+n\\rm\{L\}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm\{ML\}_\{n\}\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02486
name: formazans
def: "The parent compound $\\ce\{H2NN=CHN=NH\}$ and its derivatives formed by substitution at carbon and/or nitrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02487
name: Förster cycle
def: "An indirect method of determination of excited state equilibria\, such as \\\(\\rm\{p\}K^\{\\rm\{*\,a\}\}\\\) values\, based on ground state thermodynamics and electronic transition energies. This cycle considers only the difference in molar enthalpy change \(\\\(\\Delta \\Delta H\\\)\) of reaction of ground and excited states\, neglecting the difference in molar entropy change of reaction of those states \(\\\(\\Delta \\Delta S\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02488
name: Förster excitation transfer
def: "A mechanism of excitation transfer which can occur between molecular entities separated by distances considerably exceeding the sum of their van der Waals radii. It is described in terms of an interaction between the transition dipole moments \(a dipolar mechanism\). The transfer rate constant \\\(k_\{D \\rightarrow  A\}\\\) is given by\: \\\[k_\{\\ce\{D -> A\}\} = \\frac\{K^\{2\}J \\pu\{8.8E-28 mol\}\}\{n^\{4\} \\tau_\{0\} r^\{6\}\}\\\] where \\\(K\\\) is an orientation factor\, \\\(n\\\) the refractive index of the medium\, \\\(\\tau _\{0\}\\\) the radiative lifetime of the donor\, \\\(r\\\) the distance \(cm\) between donor \(D\) and acceptor \(A\)\, and \\\(J\\\) the spectral overlap \(in coherent units \\\(\\pu\{cm6 mol-1\}\\\)\) between the absorption spectrum of the acceptor and the fluorescence spectrum of the donor. The critical quenching radius\, \\\(r_\{0\}\\\)\, is that distance at which \\\(k_\{D \\rightarrow  A\}\\\) is equal to the inverse of the radiative lifetime."
synonym: "dipole–dipole excitation transfer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02490
name: fossil fuel
def: "A fuel such as coal\, oil and natural gas which has formed over many years through the decomposition of deposited vegetation which was under extreme pressure of an overburden of earth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02491
name: fouling agent
def: "Fouling agents \(or mechanical inhibitors\) are inhibitory substances bound by neither covalent nor other strong bonds to the active centres\: the interaction is usually of the van der Waals\, H-bond or sometimes ionic\, type. They form protective layers or block pores\, thus physically impeding access of reactants to the active centres. The fouling agents which cause real problems are those which have a long standing effect and do not disappear spontaneously. Carbon deposits act\, partially or totally\, this way \(see coking\). Other examples are vanadium and nickel sulfide deposits in hydrotreating catalysts."
synonym: "mechanical inhibitor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02492
name: Fourier transform ion cyclotron resonance mass spectrometer
def: "A high-frequency mass spectrometer in which the cyclotron motion of ions\, having different mass/charge ratios\, in a constant magnetic field is excited essentially simultaneously and coherently by a pulse or a radio-frequency electric field applied perpendicular to the magnetic field. The excited cyclotron motion of the ions is subsequently detected on so-called receiver plates as a time domain signal that contains all the cyclotron frequencies that have been excited. Fourier transformation of the time domain signal results in the frequency domain FT-ICR signal which\, on the basis of the inverse proportionality between frequency and the mass/charge ratio\, can be converted into a mass spectrum. The term is sometimes contracted to Fourier transform mass spectrometer \(FT-MS\)."
synonym: "FT-ICR mass spectrometer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02493
name: Fourier transform spectrometer
def: "A scanning interferometer\, containing no principal dispersive element\, which first splits a beam into two or more components\, then recombines these with a phase difference. The spectrum is obtained by a Fourier transformation of the output of the interferometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02494
name: fraction
def: "In general\, a ratio of two quantities of the same kind\, the numerator quantity applying to one constituent \(or part\) of the system and the denominator to the sum of quantities for all constituents \(parts\) of the system. When applied to mixtures fractions represent a group of three quantities\: mass fraction\, volume fraction\, and amount fraction \(or amount fraction equal to the number fraction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02495
name: fractional change of a quantity
def: "A term which may be expressed infinitesimally at time \\\(t\\\) by the differential \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}Q\(t\)\}\{Q\(t\)\}\\\). For a finite time interval the quotient is \\\[\\frac\{\\Delta Q\\left\(t_\{1\};t_\{2\}\\right\)\}\{Q\\left\(t_\{1\}\\right\)\} = \\frac\{\\left\[Q\\left\(t_\{2\}\\right\)\\\,-\\\,Q\\left\(t_\{1\}\\right\)\\right\]\}\{Q\\left\(t_\{1\}\\right\)\}\\\] The quantities \\\(Q\(t_\{1\}\)\\\) and \\\(Q\(t_\{2\}\)\\\) are of the same kind and have the same type of component. Fractional change has dimension one. Examples are\: mass fractional change\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}m\(t\)\}\{m\(t\)\}\\\); amount of substance fractional change\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n\(t\)\}\{n\(t\)\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02496
name: fractional selectivity
def: "The term selectivity \(\\\(S\\\)\) is used to describe the relative rates of two or more competing reactions on a catalyst. Such competition includes cases of different reactants undergoing simultaneous reactions or of a single reactant taking part in two or more reactions. For the latter case\, \\\(S\\\) may be defined in two ways. The first of these defines a fractional selectivity\, \\\(S_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\)\, for each product by the equation \\\[S_\{\\rm\{F\}\}=\\frac\{\\xi _\{i\}\}\{\\sum \\xi _\{i\}\}\\\] The second defines relative selectivities\, \\\(S_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\)\, for each pair of products by \\\[S_\{\\rm\{R\}\}=\\frac\{\\xi _\{i\}\}\{\\xi _\{j\}\}\\\] In each case\, \\\(\\xi _\{i\}\\\) and \\\(\\xi _\{j\}\\\) are the rates of increase of the extent of reactions i and j respectively\, i.e. \\\[\\xi _\{i\}=\\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}\\xi _\{i\}\}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}\\\] and \\\[\\xi _\{j\}=\\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}\\xi _\{j\}\}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}\\\] where \\\(\\xi _\{i\}\\\) and \\\(\\xi _\{j\}\\\) are the extents of reactions \\\(i\\\) and \\\(j\\\) respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02497
name: fractionation
def: "A process by means of which macromolecular species differing in some characteristic \(chemical composition\, relative molecular mass\, branching\, stereoregularity\, etc.\) are separated from each other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02501
name: fraction collector
def: "A device for recovering fractional volumes of the column effluent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02503
name: fraction extracted
def: "The fraction of the total quantity of a substance extracted \(usually by the solvent\) under specified conditions\, i.e. \\\(E_\{\\rm\{A\}\} = \\frac\{Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{'\}\}\\\) where \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) is the mass of A extracted and \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{'\}\\\) is the total mass of A present at the start."
synonym: "recovery factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02506
name: fragmentation
def: "The heterolytic cleavage of a molecule according to the general reaction\:  where $\\ce\{\{a\}-b\}$ is an electrofuge and $\\ce\{X\}$ is a nucleofuge \(which may emerge from the reaction in combined form\)\, and the middle group affords the unsaturated fragment $\\ce\{c=d\}$. For example\, The breakdown of a radical into a diamagnetic molecule or ion and a smaller radical\, e.g. The breakdown of a radical ion in a mass spectrometer or in solution\, forming an ion of lower molar mass and a radical\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02507
name: fragmentation reaction
def: "A fragmentation reaction may be written\: \\\[\\rm\{M\}_\{1\}^\{+\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{M\}_\{2\}^\{+\}+\\rm\{M\}_\{3\}\\\] The decomposition of a metastable ion of mass-to-charge ratio \\\(\\frac\{m_\{1\}\}\{z_\{1\}\}\\\) into an ion of mass-to-charge ratio \\\(\\frac\{m_\{2\}\}\{z_\{2\}\}\\\) after electric acceleration of the metastable ion and before magnetic deflection gives rise to a peak in the mass spectrum at an apparent mass\, \\\(\\frac\{m_\{2\}^\{2\}\\ z_\{1\}\}\{m_\{1\}\\ z_\{2\}^\{2\}\}\\\). The symbol \\\(m^\{*\}\\\) should be used to indicate the apparent mass of the product ions giving rise to this peak. Traditionally\, the peak itself has been called a metastable peak and this should preferably be expressed as metastable ion peak. It is nevertheless recommended that the former term should be retained. The word metastable should never be used as a noun. Its use as an adjective should be limited to such terms as metastable ion\, where it is used correctly\, and to the special case of metastable peak discussed above. It should never be used in such terms as metastable reaction\, metastable decomposition\, metastable studies\, etc. It should be clear that the metastable ion is the ion that undergoes fragmentation; it is not detected. When a reaction is written with an asterisk above the arrow as shown\: \\\[\\rm\{M\}_\{1\}^\{+\}\\overset\{\\rm\{*\}\}\{\\rightarrow \}\\rm\{M\}_\{2\}^\{+\}+\\rm\{M\}_\{3\}\\\] this means that the reaction has been confirmed by the observation of a metastable peak. The textual description of such a process may be written as\, for example\, \(\\\(m^\{*\}\\\)\, \\\(43\\rightarrow 28\\\)\, calcd. 18.2\, obsd. 18.3\) meaning 'for the fragmentation \\\(\\frac\{m\}\{z\}\\ 43\\rightarrow \\frac\{m\}\{z\}\\ 28\\\) a metastable peak at \\\(\\frac\{m\}\{z\}\\\) 18.3 \(calculated 18.2\) has been observed'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02508
name: fragment ion
def: "An electrically charged dissociation product of an ionic fragmentation. Such an ion may dissociate further to form other electrically charged molecular or atomic moieties of successively lower formula weight."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02509
name: frame-shift mutation
def: "A point mutation involving either the deletion or insertion of a nucleotide in a gene. By the frame-shift mutation\, the normal reading frame used when decoding the nucleotide triplets in the gene is altered to another reading frame."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02510
name: Franck–Condon principle
def: "Classically\, the Franck–Condon principle is the approximation that an electronic transition is most likely to occur without changes in the positions of the nuclei in the molecular entity and its environment. The resulting state is called a Franck–Condon state\, and the transition involved\, a vertical transition. The quantum mechanical formulation of this principle is that the intensity of a vibronic transition is proportional to the square of the overlap integral between the vibrational wavefunctions of the two states that are involved in the transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02512
name: franklin
def: "esu unit of electric charge\, \\\(\\rm\{Fr\}\\approx 3.335\\ 64\\times 10^\{-10\}\\ \\rm\{C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02513
name: free charge density on the interface
def: "The physical charge density believed to occur on either side of the electrical double-layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02514
name: free electron laser
def: "Source of coherent radiation in which the active medium is an electron beam moving at speeds close to the speed of light in the spatially periodic magnetic field produced by an array of magnets \(the wiggler\). The emitted wavelength\, \\\(\\lambda _\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\)\, is approximately given by \\\[\\frac\{\\lambda _\{\\omega \}\}\{4\\ E^\{2\}\}\\\] with \\\(\\lambda _\{\\omega \}\\\) being the wiggler period and \\\(E\\\) the electron's energy in \\\(\\mathrm\{MeV\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02516
name: freely draining
def: "An adjective referring to a chain macromolecule the segments of which produce such small frictional effects when moving in a medium that the hydrodynamic field in the vicinity of a given segment is not affected by the presence of other segments. Thus\, the solvent can flow virtually undisturbed through the domain occupied by a freely draining macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02517
name: freely jointed chain
def: "A hypothetical linear chain molecule consisting of infinitely thin rectilinear segments uniform in length; each segment can take all orientations in space with equal probability\, independently of its neighbours. For models in which the segments are not all uniform in length\, the name 'random-walk chain' has been used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02518
name: freely rotating chain
def: "A hypothetical linear chain molecule\, free from short-range and long-range interactions\, consisting of infinitely thin rectilinear segments \(bonds\) of fixed length\, jointed at fixed bond angles; the torsion angles of the bonds can assume all values with equal probability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02520
name: free rotation
def: "In a stereochemical context the rotation about a bond is called 'free' when the rotational barrier is so low that different conformations are not perceptible as different chemical species on the time scale of the experiment. The inhibition of rotation of groups about a bond due to the presence of a sufficiently large rotational barrier to make the phenomenon observable on the time scale of the experiment is termed hindered rotation or restricted rotation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02521
name: free-running laser
def: "A term applied to a pulsed laser\, meaning that the laser emission lasts as long as the pumping process is sufficient to sustain lasing conditions. Typical pulse durations are in the \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{s\}\\\)-\\\(\\rm\{ms\}\\\) range\, depending on the pumping source. When the operation mode of a pulsed laser is not specified as mode-locked\, Q-switched\, or anything else\, it must be considered as free-running."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02523
name: freezing
def: "The conversion of a liquid to a solid by lowering the temperature and/or the application of pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02524
name: freezing out
def: "A term used in combustion for the analysis of incinerator flue gas components \(largely the organic fraction\) in which a series of traps at progressively lower temperatures is employed. In more general use\, the term implies the removal of a condensable gas or liquid by condensation in a trap at low temperatures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02525
name: frequency
def: "Reciprocal of the period."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02526
name: frequency distribution
def: "If the range of observed measurement values is subdivided into a regular sequence of smaller intervals\, this distribution is a plot of the frequency of occurrence of values falling into each interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02528
name: frictional coefficient
def: "A tensor correlating the frictional force\, \\\(F\\\)\, opposing the motion of a particle in a viscous fluid and the velocity \\\(u\\\) of this particle relative to the fluid. In the case of an isolated spherical particle in a viscous isotropic fluid\, \\\(f\\\) is a constant and \\\(F=f\\ u\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02530
name: friction factor
def: "Quotient of frictional force and normal force\, for a sliding body. Formerly called frictional coefficient or coefficient of friction."
synonym: "friction coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02531
name: fringed-micelle model
def: "A model of crystallinity in which the crystallized segments of a macromolecule belong predominantly to different crystals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02532
name: frontal chromatography
def: "A procedure in which the sample \(liquid or gas\) is fed continuously into the chromatographic bed. In frontal chromatography no additional mobile phase is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02533
name: frontier orbitals
def: "The Highest-energy Occupied Molecular Orbital \(HOMO\) \(filled or partly filled\) and Lowest-energy Unoccupied Molecular Orbital \(LUMO\) \(completely or partly vacant\) of a molecular entity. Examination of the mixing of frontier molecular orbitals of reacting molecular entities affords an approach to the interpretation of reaction behaviour; this constitutes a simplified perturbation molecular orbital theory of chemical behaviour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02534
name: fronting
def: "Asymmetry of a peak such that\, relative to the baseline\, the front is less steep than the rear. In paper chromatography and thin-layer chromatography\, it refers to the distortion of a spot showing a diffuse region in front of the spot in the direction of flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02535
name: front surface geometry
def: "A system where excitation and observation are from the same face of the sample \(\\\(\\alpha\\ \\angle\\ 90^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\beta\\ \\angle\\ 180^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) where \\\(\\alpha \\\) = angle of incidence of the exciting beam on the plane surface of the sample\, and \\\(\\beta \\\) = angle between the exciting direction and observation direction.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02536
name: frost point hygrometer
def: "Instrument in which the sample is passed over a cooled surface. The temperature at which frost forms on it is a function of the water content of the gas passing over the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02537
name: froth flotation
def: "The removal of particulate matter by foaming."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02538
name: Frumkin effect
def: "In an electrode reaction\, when reactants or intermediates are adsorbed\, the rate of reaction may no longer be related to the concentration by a simple law. The deviation may be due to either entropic or energetic effects or both. The situation best understood is that where a reactant is non-specifically adsorbed in the outer Helmholtz plane \(inner boundary of the diffuse layer\). The effect of such adsorption on electrode kinetics is usually termed the Frumkin effect. Rate constants\, transfer coefficients etc. corrected for this effect are frequently called 'true' rate constants etc. It would be preferable to describe them as 'corrected for the Frumkin effect'\, but in any case\, if such a correction is carried out\, the basis on which it is made should be clearly described."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02540
name: fuel cycle
def: "The sequence of steps\, such as utilization\, reprocessing\, and refabrication\, through which nuclear fuel may pass."
synonym: "nuclear fuel cycle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02541
name: fuel element
def: "The smallest structurally discrete part of a reactor which has fuel as its principal constituent."
synonym: "nuclear fuel element" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02542
name: fuel reprocessing
def: "The processing of nuclear fuel\, after its use in a reactor\, to remove fission products and recover fissile and fertile material."
synonym: "nuclear fuel reprocessing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02543
name: fugacity
def: "Of a substance B\, \\\(f_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) or \\\(\\tilde\{p\}_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, in a gaseous mixture is defined by \\\(f_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=\\lambda _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ \\lim _\{p\\rightarrow 0\}\\frac\{p_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\lambda _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(p_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the partial pressure of B and \\\(λ_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) its absolute activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02544
name: fugacity coefficient
def: "Ratio of fugacity to the partial pressure of a gaseous constituent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02545
name: fulgides
def: "Dialkylidenesuccinic anhydrides \(generally photochromic\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02546
name: full energy peak
def: "Of a spectrum of radiation\, the part of the spectral response curve corresponding to the total absorption in a detecting material of the energy of the detected radiation."
synonym: "total absorption peak" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02547
name: fullerenes
def: "Compounds composed solely of an even number of carbon atoms\, which form a cage-like fused-ring polycyclic system with twelve five-membered rings and the rest six-membered rings. The archetypal example isfullerene\, where the atoms and bonds delineate a truncated icosahedron. The term has been broadened to include any closed cage structure consisting entirely of three-coordinate carbon atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02548
name: fulminates
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RON=C\{\:\}\}$. So called because fulminic acid \(actually $\\ce\{HC#N^\{+\}–O^\{−\}\}$ formonitrile oxide\) was previously considered to be $\\ce\{HON=C\{\:\}\}$.Salts of fulminic acid\, e.g. $\\ce\{Na^\{+\}\[–C#N^\{+\}O^\{−\}\]\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02549
name: fulvalenes
def: "The hydrocarbon fulvalene and its derivatives formed by substitution \(and by extension\, analogues formed by replacement of one or more carbon atoms of the fulvalene skeleton by a heteroatom\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02550
name: fulvenes
def: "The hydrocarbon fulvene and its derivatives formed by substitution \(and by extension\, analogues formed by replacement of one or more carbon atoms of the fulvene skeleton by a heteroatom\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02551
name: fume
def: "Fine solid particles \(aerosol\)\, predominantly less than \\\(1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) in diameter\, which result from the condensation of vapour from some types of chemical reaction. Usually this is formed from the gaseous state generally after volatilization from melted substances and often accompanied by chemical reactions such as oxidation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02552
name: fumes
def: "In popular usage\, a term often taken to mean airborne effluents\, unpleasant and malodorous\, which might arise from chemical processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02553
name: fumigation
def: "An atmospheric phenomenon in which pollution\, retained by an inversion layer near its level of emission\, is brought rapidly to ground level as the inversion breaks up. This term also applies to the exposure of material \(e.g. grain\) to chemicals to kill insects\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02554
name: functional class name
def: "A name that expresses the characteristic group as a class term written as a separate word following the name of a parent structure or a name derived from a parent structure. In the latter case\, when the derived name is that for a substituent group \(formerly called a 'radical'\)\, the method has been called 'radicofunctional nomenclature'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02555
name: functional group
def: "Organic compounds are thought of as consisting of a relatively unreactive backbone\, for example a chain of \\\(\\rm\{sp\}^\{3\}\\\) hybridized carbon atoms\, and one or several functional groups. The functional group is an atom\, or a group of atoms that has similar chemical properties whenever it occurs in different compounds. It defines the characteristic physical and chemical properties of families of organic compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02556
name: functional parent
def: "A structure the name of which implies the presence of one or more characteristic groups and which has one or more hydrogen atoms attached to at least one of its skeletal atoms or one of its characteristic groups\, or in which at least one of its characteristic groups can form at least one kind of functional modification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02558
name: furanocoumarins
def: "An alternative name for furocoumarins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02559
name: furanoses
def: "Cyclic hemiacetal forms of monosaccharides in which the ring is five-membered \(i.e. a tetrahydrofuran skeleton\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02561
name: furnace pyrolysis
def: "A flowing stream of gas \(hydrogen\, oxygen\, nitrogen\, chlorine\, etc.\) required to produce volatile species of the elements being determined\, is passed over the test sample in a heated furnace. The analytes leave the furnace in the gas stream\, or are entrained by a carrier gas. The analytes in the gas stream may be collected in an absorbing solution\, on a carbon or other filter or by condensation on a cool surface. In the case of mercury this can also be done by amalgamation with a noble metal. The analytes may then be swept and released from the trap\, by heating\, into a sampling source for analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02562
name: furocoumarins
def: "Derivatives \(mostly of natural origin\) of the linear furocoumarin skeleton\, psoralen\, or its angular isomer\, angelicin\, variously substituted with hydroxy\, methoxy\, alkyl\, or hydroxymethyl groups\, among others."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02563
name: fusion
def: "The amalgamation of two distinct cells or macromolecules into a single integrated unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:F02564
name: fusion name
def: "A composite name for a polycyclic parent structure having the maximum number of non-cumulative double bonds and at least one ortho fusion. Name formation involves the dissection of the structure into contiguous components having recognized trivial or semisystematic names\, one of which is selected as the 'base component'. attachment of the other components is described by prefixes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT06771
name: ferredoxin
def: "A protein containing more than one iron and acid-labile sulfur\, that displays electron-transfer activity but not classical enzyme function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT06786
name: Fenton reaction
def: "$\\ce\{Fe^\{2+\} + H2O2 -> Fe^\{3+\} + OH^\{.\} + OH^\{−\}\}$ . This is the iron-salt-dependent decomposition of dihydrogen peroxide\, generating the highly reactive hydroxyl radical\, possibly via an oxoiron\(IV\) intermediate. Addition of a reducing agent\, such as ascorbate\, leads to a cycle which increases the damage to biological molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT06824
name: first-pass effect
def: "Biotransformation and\, in some cases\, elimination of a substance in the liver after absorption from the intestine and before it reaches the systemic circulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT06825
name: fractionation
def: "Process of classification of an analyte or a group of analytes from a sample according to physical \(e.g.\, size\, solubility\) or chemical \(e.g.\, bonding\, reactivity\) properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT06876
name: fouling
def: "Process resulting in loss of performance of a membrane due to the deposition of suspended or dissolved substances on its external surfaces\, at its pore openings\, or within its pores."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07172
name: ferroelectric polymer
def: "Polymer in which spontaneous polarization arises when dipoles become arranged parallel to each other by electric fields."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07173
name: ferromagnetic polymer
def: "Polymer that exhibits magnetic properties because it has unpaired electron spins aligned parallel to each other or electron spins that can easily be so aligned."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07174
name: functional polymer
def: "Polymer that bears specified chemical groups.Polymer that has specified physical\, chemical\, biological\, pharmacological\, or other uses which depend on specific chemical groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07301
name: fibrillar morphology
def: "Morphology in which phase domains have shapes with one dimension much larger than the other two dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07375
name: fatigue
def: "Loss of performance over time\, due to chemical degradation. The major cause of fatigue is oxidation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07376
name: fluence rate
def: "Total radiant power\, \\\(P\\\)\, incident from all directions onto a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-2\}\\\)."
synonym: "radiant energy fluence rate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07377
name: fluorescence lifetime
def: "Parameter describing the time evolution of the decay of the fluorescent radiant intensity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07378
name: fluorescence resonance energy transfer
def: "Term frequently and inappropriately applied to resonance energy transfer in the sense of Förster-resonance-energy transfer \(FRET\)\, which does not involve the emission of radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07379
name: fluorogenic
def: "Property of a material in which fluorescence is induced or enhanced by the addition of another molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07380
name: fluorophore
def: "Molecular entity \(often organic\) that emits fluorescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07381
name: Förster-resonance-energy transfer
def: "Non-radiative excitation transfer between two molecular entities separated by distances considerably exceeding the sum of their van der Waals radii. It describes the transfer in terms of the interaction between the transition \(dipole\) moments of the entities in the very weak dipole-dipole coupling limit. It is a Coulombic interaction frequently called a dipole-dipole coupling. The transfer rate constant from donor to acceptor\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\)\, is given by \\\[k_\{\\rm\{T\}\} = k_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\left \( \\frac\{R_\{0\}\}\{r\} \\right \)^\{6\}=\\frac\{1\}\{\\tau _\{D\}^\{0\}\}\\left \( \\frac\{R_\{0\}\}\{r\}\\right \)^\{6\}\\\] where \\\(k_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\tau_\{\\rm\{D\}\}^\{0\}\\\) are the emission rate constant and the lifetime of the excited donor in the absence of transfer\, respectively\, \\\(r\\\) is the distance between the donor and the acceptor and \\\(R_\{0\}\\\) is the critical quenching radius or Förster radius\, i.e.\, the distance at which transfer and spontaneous decay of the excited donor are equally probable \(\\\(k_\{\\rm\{T\}\} = k_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\)\) \(see Note 3\). \\\(R_\{0\}\\\) is given by \\\[R_\{0\} = Const.\\left \( \\frac\{\\kappa^\{2\}\\mathit\{\\Phi\}_\{D\}^\{0\}J \}\{n^\{4\}\} \\right \)^\{1/6\}\\\] where \\\(\\kappa\\\) is the orientation factor\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} _\{D\}^\{0\}\\\) is the fluorescence quantum yield of the donor in the absence of transfer\, \\\(n\\\) is the average refractive index of the medium in the wavelength range where spectral overlap is significant\, \\\(J\\\) is the spectral overlap integral reflecting the degree of overlap of the donor emission spectrum with the acceptor absorption spectrum and given by \\\[J = \\int _\{\\lambda \}I_\{\\lambda\}^\{D\}\(\\lambda\)\\epsilon _\{A\}\\left \( \\lambda \\right \)\\lambda^\{4\}\\rm\{d\}\\lambda\\\] where \\\(I_\{\\lambda\}^\{D\}\(\\lambda\)\\\) is the normalized spectral radiant intensity of the donor so that \\\(\\int_\{\\lambda\}I_\{\\lambda\}^\{D\}\(\\lambda\)\\rm\{d\}\\lambda = 1\\\). \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\(\{\\lambda\}\)\\\) is the molar decadic absorption coefficient of the acceptor. See Note 3 for the value of \\\(Const.\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07382
name: Fourier-transform spectroscopy
def: "Measurement technique whereby spectra are collected based on measurements of the temporal coherence of a radiative source\, using time-domain measurements of the electromagnetic radiation or other type of radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07383
name: frequency
def: "The number of waveperiods per time interval. The linear frequency\, \\\(\nu\\\)\, is the number of cycles per time interval. SI unit \\\(\\rm\{Hz =  s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07384
name: frequency-domain fluorometry
def: "Technique that permits recovery of the parameters characterizing a fluorescence decay. Instead of using an exciting visible\, UV\, or infrared radiation pulse \(see single-photon timing\)\, the sample is excited by sinusoidally modulated radiation at high frequency. The fluorescence response is sinusoidally modulated at the same frequency as\, but delayed in phase and partially demodulated with respect to the excitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07385
name: FRET
def: "Acronym for Förster-resonance-energy transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07505
name: functionality
def: "Number of covalent bonds that a monomer molecule or monomeric unit in a macromolecule or oligomer molecule can form with other reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07549
name: fractal agglomerate
def: "Agglomerate having the same fractal dimension as the constituent particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:FT07550
name: fractal dimension
def: "Parameter that provides a mathematical description of the fractal structure of a polymer network\, an aggregated particulate sol\, or of the particles that comprise them."
synonym: "Hausdorff dimension" []
synonym: "mass fractal dimension" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02566
name: γ-cascade
def: "Two or more different gamma rays emitted successively from one nucleus when it passes through one or more energy levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02571
name: <em>G</em> value
def: "The number of specified chemical events in an irradiated substance produced per \\\(100\\ \\rm\{eV\}\\\) of energy absorbed from ionizing radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02572
name: gain
def: "The gain of a photomultiplier is given by the equation \\\(G=k\\ \\sigma ^\{n\}\\\)\, where \\\(k\\\) is the efficiency of collection of photoelectrons on the first dynode\, \\\(\\sigma \\\) is the secondary emission ratio\, i.e. the number of secondary electrons emitted for each electron incident on the dynode and \\\(n\\\) is the number of dynodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02574
name: Galvani potential difference
def: "An electric potential difference between points in the bulk of two phases. It is measurable only when the two phases have identical composition \(e.g. two copper wires\). It is the difference of inner electric potentials in two phases. Symbol\: \\\(\\Delta _\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\phi \\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02576
name: gas analysis installation
def: "An assembly for the purpose of determining one or more components in a gaseous mixture. It generally comprises the following elements\: sample probe\, region of primary treatment of the gases\, region of secondary treatment of gases\, points for venting to the atmosphere and for calibration sample injection\, transfer line and the analytical unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02577
name: gas black
def: "Finely divided carbon \(graphite\) produced by incomplete combustion or thermal decomposition of natural gas."
synonym: "carbon black" []
synonym: "channel black" []
synonym: "furnace black" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02578
name: gas chromatography
def: "A separation technique in which the mobile phase is a gas. Gas chromatography is always carried out in a column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02579
name: gas constant
def: "Fundamental physical constant\, \\\(R = \\pu\{8.314472\(15\) J K-1 mol-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02580
name: gaseous diffusion separator
def: "Instrumentation to separate a gas mixture into its components by means of differences in the diffusion rates of the component molecules. This method has been used in separation of various isotopes of uranium \(by means of $\\ce\{UF6\}$\) and hydrogen \($\\ce\{H2O\}$\, $\\ce\{HDO\}$\, $\\ce\{D2O\}$\, $\\ce\{HTO\}$\, etc.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02581
name: gas-filled phototube
def: "Similar in construction to a vacuum phototube except that it is filled with a noble gas \(usually $\\ce\{Ar\}$\) at a pressure of about \\\(10\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\). Photoelectrons accelerated by the anode electric potential ionize gas atoms. The additional electrons provide a substantial intrinsic gain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02582
name: gas-filled X-ray detector
def: "A cylindrical cathode with a window\, an axial wire anode and an ionizable gas. The gas may be continuously replenished giving a flow-through detector or the detector may be sealed. Following an original ionizing event\, electron multiplication occurs through a process of gas amplification in the high electric field surrounding the anode wire. The gain of this process is defined as the number of electrons collected on the anode wire for each primary electron produced. For X-rays having energies higher than the excitation potential of the detector gas\, the spectral responsivity function has a second peak in addition to the main peak that is called the escape peak. The escape peak has a mean pulse height proportional to the difference between the photon energy of the incident X-rays and of the spectral characteristic line of the detector gas. A quenching gas\, a molecular gas\, is added to the detector gas in order to neutralize the detector gas ions and to absorb secondary electrons as well as UV radiation resulting from neutralization of detector gas ions. According to the potential applied to the anode\, the detector can work as an ionization chamber\, proportional counter\, or Geiger counter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02585
name: gas laser
def: "A laser in which the active medium is a gas containing a laser active species. It is typically composed of a plasma discharge tube containing a gas that can be excited with an electric discharge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02586
name: gas-liquid chromatography
def: "Comprises all gas-chromatographic methods in which the stationary phase is a liquid dispersed on a solid support. Separation is achieved by partition of the components of a sample between the phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02587
name: gas-phase acidity
def: "The negative of the Gibbs energy \(\\\(\\Delta G_\{\\rm\{r\}\}^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\) change for the reaction\:  in the gas phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02588
name: gas-phase basicity
def: "The negative of the Gibbs energy \(\\\(\\Delta G_\{\\rm\{r\}\}^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\) change associated with the reaction\: in the gas phase.  Also called absolute or intrinsic basicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02589
name: gas-phase-grown carbon fibres
def: "Carbon fibres grown in an atmosphere of hydrocarbons with the aid of fine particulate solid catalysts such as iron or other transition metals and consisting of graphitizable carbon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02590
name: gas sensing electrode
def: "A sensor composed of an indicator and a reference electrode in contact with a thin film of solution which is separated from the bulk of the sample solution by a gas-permeable membrane or an air gap. This intermediate solution interacts with the gaseous species \(penetrated through the membrane or an air gap\) in such a way as to produce a change in a measured constituent \(e.g. the $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ activity\) of the intermediate solution. This change is then sensed by the ion-selective electrode and is related to the partial pressure of the gaseous species in the sample. \[Note\: In electrochemical literature the term gas electrode is used for the classical\, redox-equilibrium-based gas electrodes as well\, such as the hydrogen or the chlorine gas electrodes \($\\ce\{Pt\(s\)\}$|$\\ce\{H2\(g\)\}$|$\\ce\{H^\{+\}\(aq\)\}$ or $\\ce\{Pt\(s\)\}$|$\\ce\{Cl2\(g\)\}$|$\\ce\{Cl^\{-\}\(aq\)\}$\]. These electrodes respond both to the partial pressure of the gas \($\\ce\{H2\}$ or $\\ce\{Cl2\}$\) and to the ionic activities \($\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ or $\\ce\{Cl^\{-\}\}$\). The Clark oxygen electrode fits under this classification although\, in contrast to other gas sensors\, it is an amperometric and not a potentiometric device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02591
name: gas-solid chromatography
def: "Comprises all gas chromatographic methods in which the stationary phase is an active solid \(e.g. charcoal\, molecular sieves\). Separation is achieved by adsorption of the components of a sample. In gas chromatography the distinction between gas-liquid and gas-solid may be obscure because liquids are used to modify solid stationary phases\, and because the solid supports for liquid stationary phases affect the chromatographic process. For classification by the phases used\, the term relating to the predominant effect should be chosen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02592
name: gated photodetector
def: "Where photodetectors are switched on \(or off\) usually in a repetitive manner employing electronic switches\, they are described as gated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02593
name: gauche
def: "Synonymous with a synclinal alignment of groups attached to adjacent atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02595
name: gauche effect
def: "The stabilization of the gauche \(synclinal\) conformation in a two carbon unit bonded vicinally to electronegative elements e.g. 1\,2-difluoroethane.The destabilization of the gauche \(synclinal\) conformation in a two carbon unit bonded vicinally to large\, soft and polarizable elements such as sulfur and bromine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02596
name: gauss
def: "emu unit of magnetic flux density\, \\\(\\rm\{G\}=10^\{-4\}\\ \\rm\{T\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02597
name: Gaussian band shape
def: "A band shape described by the Gaussian function\: \\\[F\\left\(\nu - \nu _\{0\}\\right\) = \\frac\{a\}\{\\sqrt\{\\pi \}\}\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{-a^\{2\}\\ \(\nu - \nu _\{0\}\)^\{2\}\}\\\] In this equation\, \\\(a^\{-1\}\\\) is proportional to the width of the band\, and \\\(\nu _\{0\}\\\) is the frequency of the band maximum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02598
name: Geiger counter
def: "A gas-filled X-ray detector in which gas amplification reaches saturation and proportionality no longer exists. The output signal does not depend on the incident energy. The time taken for the counter to recover from the saturation is called dead time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02599
name: Geiger–Muller counter tube
def: "A counter tube operated under such conditions that the magnitude of each pulse is independent of the amount of energy deposited in it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02600
name: gel
def: "Non-fluid colloidal network or polymer network that is expanded throughout its whole volume by a fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02602
name: geminate pair
def: "Pair of molecular entities in close proximity in solution within a solvent cage and resulting from reaction \(e.g. bond scission\, electron transfer\, group transfer\) of a precursor that constitutes a single kinetic entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02603
name: geminate recombination
def: "This expression refers to the reaction\, with each other\, of two transient species produced from a common precursor in solution. If reaction occurs before any separation by diffusion has occurred\, this is termed primary geminate recombination. If the mutually reactive entities have been separated\, and come together by diffusion\, this is termed secondary geminate recombination. This is illustrated in the reaction diagram below\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02604
name: gene
def: "Structurally\, a basic unit of hereditary material; an ordered sequence of nucleotide bases that encodes one polypeptide chain \(via mRNA\). The gene includes\, however\, regions preceding and following the coding region \(leader and trailer\) as well as \(in eukaryotes\) intervening sequences \(introns\) between individual coding segments \(exons\). Functionally\, the gene is defined by the cis-trans test that determines whether independent mutations of the same phenotype occur within a single gene or in several genes involved in the same function."
synonym: "cistron" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02605
name: gene amplification
def: "An increase in the number of copies of a specific gene in an organism. This can lead to the production of a corresponding protein at elevated levels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02606
name: gene library
def: "A collection of cloned DNA fragments from a variety of species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02607
name: gene manipulation
def: "The use of in vitro techniques to produce DNA molecules containing novel combinations of genes or altered sequences\, and the insertion of these into vectors that can be used for their incorporation into host organisms or cells in which they are capable of continued propagation of the modified genes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02608
name: general acid–base catalysis
def: "Catalysis by acids or bases in solution is said to be general when it is possible to detect catalysis brought about by species other than the ions formed from the solvent itself \(e.g. when water is the solvent\, by species other than $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{OH^\{−\}\}$ ions\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02609
name: general acid catalysis
def: "The catalysis of a chemical reaction by a series of Brønsted acids \(which may include the solvated hydrogen ion\) so that the rate of the catalysed part of the reaction is given by \\\(\\sum k_\{\\rm\{HA\}\}\\ \[HA\]\\\) multiplied by some function of substrate concentrations. \(The acids $\\ce\{HA\}$ are unchanged by the overall reaction.\) General catalysis by acids can be experimentally distinguished from specific catalysis by hydrogen cations \(hydrons\) by observation of the rate of reaction as a function of buffer concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02610
name: general base catalysis
def: "The catalysis of a chemical reaction by a series of Brønsted bases \(which may include the lyate ion\) so that the rate of the catalysed part of the reaction is given by \\\(\\sum k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ \[\\rm\{B\}\]\\\) multiplied by some function of substrate concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02611
name: general force field
def: "A force field expressed in terms of \\\(3\\ N-6\\\) basis coordinates\: \\\[V=\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\\, \\sum f_\{ij\}\\ S_\{i\}\\ S_\{j\}\\\] \\\[V=\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\\, \\sum F_\{ij\}\\ S_\{i\}\\ S_\{j\}\\\] where \\\(f_\{ij\}\\\) \(or \\\(F_\{ij\}\\\)\) are force constants and the basis coordinates \\\(S\\\) \(or sometimes \\\(s\\\)\) may be internal symmetry coordinates\, local symmetry coordinates or any others suitable to the problem\, but the number of the coordinates has to be reduced to \\\(3\\\, N-6\\\) \(\\\(3\\\, N-5\\\) for linear molecules\)\, \\\(N\\\) being the number of atoms in the molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02612
name: generalized transition-state theory
def: "Any form of transition-state theory \(TST\)\, such as microcanonical variational TST\, canonical variational TST\, and improved canonical variational TST\, in which the transition state is not necessarily located at the saddle point\, is referred to as generalized transition-state theory."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02613
name: generally labelled tracer
def: "A tracer in which the position of the label is not defined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02614
name: generation time
def: "The average time required by a cell for the completion of one cell cycle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02615
name: genetic code
def: "The set of rules which governs the relationship between the linear order of the nucleotides in an mRNA molecule and the sequence of the amino acids in the protein which it encodes. The genetic code is a triplet code\, nearly universal. It is not overlapping\: a mutation which alters only a single nucleotide in a gene can change only one amino acid in the encoded protein. It is degenerate\: it assigns each of the 20 amino acids to one or more of 61 of the 64 possible nucleotide triplets \(cf. codon\) that can be constructed from four nucleotides. The remaining three triplets are used to signal the termination of a genetic message."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02616
name: genome
def: "The complete set of chromosomal and extrachromosomal genes of an organism\, a cell\, an organelle or a virus; the complete DNA component of an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02617
name: genotype
def: "The genetic constitution of an organism as revealed by genetic or molecular analysis\, i.e. the complete set of genes\, both dominant and recessive\, possessed by a particular cell or organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02618
name: geometrical equivalence
def: "The symmetry correspondence among units belonging to the same chain. The symmetry elements always bear a special relationship to the chain axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02619
name: geometric attenuation
def: "The reduction of a radiation quantity due to the effect only of the distance between the point of interest and the source\, excluding the effect of any matter present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02621
name: geometric mean
def: "The nth root of the product of the absolute values of the observations\, taken with the proper sign. It can be calculated with the formula\: \\\[\\bar\{x\}_\{\\rm\{g\}\} = \(\\Pi |x_\{i\}|\)^\{\\frac\{1\}\{n\}\}\\\] The \\\(\\mathit\{\\Pi\}\\\) product is taken from i = 1 to n."
synonym: "logarithmic mean" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02623
name: geometry
def: "A term used colloquially to signify the arrangement in space of the various components in an experiment\, particularly the source and the detector in radiation measurements."
synonym: "counting geometry" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02624
name: geometry factor
def: "The average solid angle in steradians at source subtended by the aperture or sensitive volume of the detector\, divided by $4\\uppi\\!$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02626
name: germylidenes
def: "Carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce\{R2Ge\{\:\}\}$. The older synonym germylenes is no longer recommended."
synonym: "germylenes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02627
name: Gibbs adsorption
def: "The surface excess amount or Gibbs adsorption of component \\\(i\\\)\, \\\(n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\\\)\, which may be positive or negative\, is defined as the excess of the amount of this component actually present in the system over that present in a reference system of the same volume as the real system and in which the bulk concentrations in the two phases remain uniform up to the Gibbs dividing surface. That is \\\[n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}=n_\{i\}- V^\{\\alpha \}\\ c_\{i\}^\{\\alpha \}- V^\{\\beta \}\\ c_\{i\}^\{\\beta \}\\\] where \\\(n_\{i\}\\\) is the total amount of the component \\\(i\\\) in the system\, \\\(c_\{i\}^\{\\alpha \}\\\) and \\\(c_\{i\}^\{\\beta \}\\\) are the concentrations in the two bulk phases \\\(\\alpha \\\) and \\\(\\beta \\\)\, and \\\(V^\{\\alpha \}\\\) and \\\(V^\{\\beta \}\\\) are the volumes of the two phases defined by the Gibbs surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02629
name: Gibbs energy
def: "Enthalpy minus the product of thermodynamic temperature and entropy. It was formerly called free energy or free enthalpy."
synonym: "Gibbs energy function" []
synonym: "free energy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02630
name: Gibbs energy diagram
def: "A diagram showing the relative standard Gibbs energies of reactants\, transition state\, reaction intermediates and products\, in the same sequence as they occur in a chemical reaction. These points are often connected by a smooth curve \(a 'Gibbs energy profile'\, commonly still referred to as a 'free energy profile'\) but experimental observation can provide information on relative standard Gibbs energies only at the maxima and minima and not at the configurations between them. The abscissa expresses the sequence of reactants\, products\, reaction intermediates and transition states and is usually undefined or only vaguely defined by the reaction coordinate \(extent of bond breaking or bond making\). In some adaptations the abscissas are however explicitly defined as bond orders\, Brønsted exponents\, etc. Contrary to statements in many text books\, the highest point on a Gibbs energy diagram does not necessarily correspond to the transition state of the rate-limiting step. For example\, in a stepwise reaction consisting of two reaction steps\:  one of the transition states of the two reaction steps must \(in general\) have a higher standard Gibbs energy than the other\, whatever the concentration of D in the system. However\, the value of that concentration will determine which of the reaction steps is rate-limiting. If the particular concentrations of interest\, which may vary\, are chosen as the standard state\, then the rate-limiting step is the one of highest Gibbs energy."
synonym: "Gibbs energy profile" []
synonym: "energy profile" []
synonym: "free energy profile" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02631
name: Gibbs energy of activation
def: "The standard Gibbs energy difference between the transition state of a reaction \(either an elementary reaction or a stepwise reaction\) and the ground state of the reactants. It is calculated from the experimental rate constant \\\(k\\\) via the conventional form of the absolute rate equation\: \\\[\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}G = R\\ T\\ \\left\[\\ln \(\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{h\}\) - \\ln \(\\frac\{k\}\{T\}\)\\right\]\\\] where \\\(k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant \(\\\(\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{h\} = 2.083\\ 58\\times 10^\{10\}\\ \\rm\{K\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\)\). The values of the rate constants\, and hence Gibbs energies of activation\, depend upon the choice of concentration units \(or of the thermodynamic standard state\)."
synonym: "standard free energy of activation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02632
name: Gibbs energy of repulsion
def: "Indicated by \\\(G_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) or \\\(G_\{\\rm\{el\}\}\\\) if the repulsion is due to electric effects \(\\\(g_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) or \\\(g_\{\\rm\{el\}\}\\\) is taken for unit area of each of two flat and parallel surfaces\). \\\(G_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) \(or \\\(G_\{\\rm\{el\}\}\\\)\) is defined as \\\[G_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\,\\rm\{\(\}\\rm\{or\}\\\,G_\{\\rm\{el\}\}\\rm\{\)\} = \\left\[\\int _\{\\rm\{final distance\}\}^\{\\infty \}\\rm\{Force\}  \\;\\rm\{d \(distance\)\}\\right\]_\{T\,p\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02634
name: Gibbs film elasticity
def: "Pertains to a film element of a soap film changing in area at constant mass and is the differential change of its surface tension with relative change in area \\\(A\\\)\, \\\[E=A\\ \(\\frac\{\\partial \\sigma \}\{\\partial A\}\)_\{T\,p\,n_\{i\}\}\\\] where \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(p\\\) is the pressure\, and \\\(n_\{i\}\\\) is the amount of substance of the species \\\(i\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02635
name: Gibbs surface
def: "A geometrical surface chosen parallel to the interface and used to define the volumes of the bulk phases in the calculation of the extent of adsorption\, and of other surface excess properties. Also referred to as Gibbs dividing surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02636
name: giga
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{9\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{G\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02637
name: glass electrode error
def: "Deviation of a glass electrode from the hydrogen-ion response function. An example often encountered is the error due to sodium ions at alkaline \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\) values\, which by convention is regarded as positive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02638
name: glass laser
def: "A solid state laser in which the active medium is a glass host that supports the active species. The neodymium-doped phosphate glass laser is one example."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02639
name: glass-like carbon
def: "An agranular non-graphitizable carbon with a very high isotropy of its structural and physical properties and with a very low permeability for liquids and gases. The original surfaces and the fracture surfaces have a pseudo-glassy appearance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02640
name: glass transition
def: "A second-order transition in which a supercooled melt yields\, on cooling\, a glassy structure. Below the glass-transition temperature the physical properties vary in a manner similar to those of the crystalline phase. Example\: Lithium disilicate crystals melt at \\\(1305\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\); the melt can be supercooled to the glass-transition temperature at approximately \\\(773\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) below which the viscous liquid freezes to a rigid amorphous glass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02641
name: glass-transition temperature
def: "The temperature at which the viscosity of the glass is \\\(10^\{13\}\\ \\rm\{dPa\}\\ \\rm\{s\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02642
name: globular-chain crystal
def: "A type of crystal comprised of macromolecules having globular conformations. Globular-chain crystals usually occur with globular proteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02643
name: glove box
def: "An enclosure in which material may be manipulated in isolation from the operator's environment. This manipulation is effected by gauntlet gloves or flexible plastic devices fixed to ports in the walls of the box."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02645
name: glycans
def: "Synonymous with polysaccharides. Glycans composed of a single type of monosaccharide residue \(homopolysaccharide\, synonym homoglycan\) are named by replacing the ending '-ose' of the sugar by '-an'\, e.g. mannans\, fructans\, xylans\, arabinans. dextrans and dextrins belong to the class glucans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02647
name: glycerides
def: "Esters of glycerol \(propane-1\,2\,3-triol\) with fatty acids\, widely distributed in nature. They are by long-established custom subdivided into triglycerides\, 1\,2- or 1\,3-diglycerides\, and 1- or 2-monoglycerides\, according to the number and position of acyl groups \(not\, as one might suppose\, the number of glycerol residues\). The recommended method for naming individual glycerides is mono-\, di- or tri-O-acylglycerol\, as appropriate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02648
name: glycerophospholipid
def: "Any derivative of glycerophosphoric acid that contains at least one O-acyl\, or O-alkyl\, or O-\(1-alkenyl\) group attached to the glycerol residue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02650
name: glyco-amino-acid
def: "A saccharide attached to a single amino acid by any kind of covalent bond. A glycosyl-amino-acid is a compound consisting of saccharide linked through a glycosyl linkage \(O-\, N-\, or S-\) to an amino acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02651
name: glycoconjugate
def: "A type of compound consisting of carbohydrate units covalently linked with other types of chemical constituent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02653
name: glycolipids
def: "Naturally occurring 1\,2-di-O-acylglycerols joined at oxygen 3 by a glycosidic linkage to a carbohydrate part \(usually a mono-\, di- or tri-saccharide\). Some substances classified as bacterial glycolipids have the sugar part acylated by one or more fatty acids and the glycerol part may be absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02654
name: glycols
def: "Dihydric alcohols\, also known as diols\, in which the two hydroxy groups are on different carbon atoms\, usually but not necessarily adjacent. E.g. $\\ce\{HOCH2CH2OH\}$ 'ethylene glycol' \(ethane-1\,2-diol\)\, $\\ce\{HO\[CH2\]4OH butane-1\,4-diol\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02656
name: glycopeptides
def: "Compounds in which a carbohydrate component is linked to a peptide/protein component."
synonym: "glycoproteins" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02658
name: glycosamines
def: "A term synonymous with amino sugars; now restricted to some trivial names."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02659
name: glycosaminoglycan
def: "The carbohydrate units in proteoglycans are polysaccharides that contain amino sugars; these polysaccharides are known as glycosaminoglycans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02660
name: glycoses
def: "A less frequently used term for monosaccharides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02661
name: glycosides
def: "Originally mixed acetals resulting from the attachment of a glycosyl group to a non-acyl group $\\ce\{RO– \(which itself may be derived from a saccharide and chalcogen replacements thereof \($\\ce\{RS\\bond\{–\}\}$\, $\\ce\{RSe\\bond\{–\}\}$\).  The bond between the glycosyl group and the $\\ce\{OR\}$ group is called a glycosidic bond. By extension\, the terms N-glycosides and C-glycosides are used as class names for glycosylamines and for compounds having a glycosyl group attached to a hydrocarbyl group respectively.   These terms are misnomers and should not be used. The preferred terms are glycosylamines and C-glycosyl compounds\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02663
name: glycosylamines
def: "Compounds having a glycosyl group attached to an amino group\, $\\ce\{NR2\}$; less elegantly called N-glycosides\, e.g. N\,N-dimethyl-$\\upbeta\\!$-ᴅ-glucopyranosylamine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02665
name: glycosyl group
def: "The structure obtained by removing the hydroxy group from the hemiacetal function of a monosaccharide and\, by extension\, of a lower oligosaccharide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02668
name: grab sampling
def: "The taking of a sample \(often in an evacuated bulb\) in a very short time; preferred terms are instantaneous sampling or instantaneous sampling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02669
name: gradient
def: "A differential ratio; the change in a quantity such as the mixing ratio of an impurity in air\, the temperature of the air\, etc.\, with height or distance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02670
name: gradient elution
def: "A procedure in which the composition of the mobile phase is changed continuously or stepwise during the elution process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02671
name: gradient layer
def: "The chromatographic bed used in thin-layer chromatography in which there is a gradual transition in some property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02672
name: gradientless reactor
def: "Stirred flow or recirculation reactors\, characterized ideally by very small concentration and temperature gradients within the catalyst region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02673
name: gradient packing
def: "Synonymous with gradient layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02674
name: gradual potential-energy surface
def: "These terms relate to the steepness of the energy rise along the reaction path of a potential-energy surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02675
name: graft copolymer
def: "A copolymer that is a graft polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02676
name: graft copolymerization
def: "Polymerization in which a graft copolymer is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02678
name: graft macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule with one or more species of block connected to the main chain as side chains\, these side chains having constitutional or configurational features that differ from those in the main chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02679
name: graft polymer
def: "A polymer composed of graft macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02680
name: gram
def: "cgs base unit of mass\, \\\(\\pu\{g\} = \\pu\{E-3 kg\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02681
name: Gram stain
def: "A set of two stains that are used to stain \(crystal violet and iodine\) and counterstain \(fuchsin\) bacteria. gram-negative bacteria are completely decolorized after treating the stained bacteria with ethanol or acetone and can be visualized by the counterstain. They have a complex cell wall in which the peptidoglycan layer is covered by lipopolysaccharides in an outer membrane structure. gram-positive bacteria resist decolorization by ethanol or acetone\, they retain the initial gram stain. They have a cell wall consisting predominantly of peptidoglycan not overlayed by an outer membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02682
name: granular carbon
def: "The term granular carbon is equivalent to coarse particulate carbon. This is a carbon material consisting of separate particles or grains which are monolithic\, on the average larger than about \\\(100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) in diameter\, but smaller than about \\\(1\\ \\rm\{cm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02683
name: graphene layer
def: "A single carbon layer of the graphite structure\, describing its nature by analogy to a polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon of quasi infinite size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02684
name: graphite
def: "An allotropic form of the element carbon consisting of layers of hexagonally arranged carbon atoms in a planar condensed ring system graphene layers The layers are stacked parallel to each other in a three-dimensional crystalline long-range order. There are two allotropic forms with different stacking arrangements\, hexagonal and rhombohedral. The chemical bonds within the layers are covalent with \\\(\\rm\{sp\}^\{2\}\\\) hybridization and with a C–C distance of \\\(141.7\\ \\rm\{pm\}\\\). The weak bonds between the layers are metallic with a strength comparable to van der Waals bonding only."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02686
name: graphite fibres
def: "Carbon fibres consisting mostly of synthetic graphite for which three-dimensional crystalline order is confirmed by X-ray diffraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02687
name: graphite material
def: "A material consisting essentially of graphitic carbon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02688
name: graphite whiskers
def: "Thin\, approximately cylindrical filaments in which graphene layers are arranged in a scroll-like manner. There is\, at least in part\, a regular stacking of the layers as in the graphite lattice\, giving rise to h\,k\,l\, X-ray reflections. The physical properties of graphite whiskers approach\, along the cylinder axis\, those of graphite."
synonym: "carbon whiskers" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02689
name: graphitic carbon
def: "All varieties of substances consisting of the element carbon in the allotropic form of graphite irrespective of the presence of structural defects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02690
name: graphitizable carbon
def: "A non-graphitic carbon which upon graphitization heat treatment converts into graphitic carbon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02691
name: graphitization
def: "A solid-state transformation of thermodynamically unstable non-graphitic carbon into graphite by means of heat treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02692
name: graphitization heat treatment
def: "A process of heat treatment of a non-graphitic carbon industrially performed at temperatures in the range between \\\(2500\\\) and \\\(3300\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\, to achieve transformation into graphitic carbon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02693
name: graphitized carbon
def: "A graphitic carbon with more or less perfect three-dimensional hexagonal crystalline order prepared from non-graphitic carbon by graphitization heat treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02694
name: gravimetric method
def: "A determination by weight; e.g. in the older method of $\\ce\{Cl^\{−\}\}$ ion determination\, a weighed amount of sample is dissolved in water\, $\\ce\{AgNO3\}$ solution added\, $\\ce\{AgCl\}$ is precipitated\, dried and weighed. From the known mass fraction of silver in $\\ce\{AgCl\}$\, the weights of the initial sample and that of the $\\ce\{AgCl\}$ precipitated\, the percentage of chlorine in the sample can be calculated readily."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02695
name: gravitational constant
def: "Universal fundamental physical constant in Newton's law of gravitation\, \\\(G = \\pu\{6.67259\(85\)E-11 m3 kg-1 s-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02696
name: gray
def: "SI derived unit of energy imparted to an element of matter by ionizing radiation divided by the mass of that element \(absorbed dose of radiation\) equal to one joule per kilogram and admitted for reasons of safeguarding human health\, \\\(\\pu\{Gy\} = \\pu\{J kg-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02697
name: green coke
def: "Green coke \(raw coke\) is the primary solid carbonization product from high boiling hydrocarbon fractions obtained at temperatures below \\\(900\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\). It contains a fraction of matter that can be released as volatiles during subsequent heat treatment at temperatures up to approximately \\\(1600\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\). This mass fraction\, the so-called volatile matter\, is in the case of green coke between 4 and 15 wt.%\, but it depends also on the heating rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02698
name: greenhouse effect
def: "Heating effect produced by certain gases \(e.g. $\\ce\{CO2\}$\, $\\ce\{O3\}$\, etc.\)\, which by virtue of their characteristic infrared absorption\, lower the earth to space transmission of long wavelength radiation but allow transmission of shorter wavelength radiation inward from the sun to the earth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02699
name: Grignard reagents
def: "Organomagnesium halides\, $\\ce\{RMgX\, having a carbon–magnesium bond \(or their equilibrium mixtures in solution with $\\ce\{R2Mg\}$ + $\\ce\{MgX\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02701
name: grit
def: "Airborne solid particles in the atmosphere which are of natural or manmade origin and which remain in suspension for some time; in the United Kingdom the size of the grit particles is defined as greater than \\\(75\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) in diameter \(retained on a 200 mesh British Standard sieve\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02702
name: ground level concentration
def: "The concentration of a chemical species\, normally a pollutant\, in air; usually measured at a specific height above the ground."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02703
name: ground level inversion
def: "The inversion of the normal temperature gradient in the atmosphere; the temperature of the air increases with increasing height of the air above the ground. This leads to poor mixing of gases released below the inversion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02704
name: ground state
def: "The state of lowest Gibbs energy of a system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02705
name: group
def: "A defined linked collection of atoms or a single atom within a molecular entity. This use of the term in physical organic and general chemistry is less restrictive than the definition adopted for the purpose of nomenclature of organic compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02706
name: group electronegativity
def: "Synonymous with substituent electronegativity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02707
name: group preconcentration
def: "An operation \(process\) as a result of which several microcomponents are isolated in one step. This may be achieved in one of two ways\, by transportation either of the matrix or of the microcomponents into a second phase. The first method is widely used in the analysis of relatively simple substances and materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02708
name: growth curve
def: "Curve giving the activity of a radioactive nuclide as a function of time and showing the increase of the activity through the decay of the precursor or as a result of activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02709
name: growth rate
def: "The measure \(\\\(\\rm\{h\}^\{-1\}\\\)\,\\\(\\rm\{d\}^\{-1\}\\\)\) of the rate of growth or multiplication of an organism or a culture\, usually expressed as specific growth rate \(the increase of mass or cell number per time unit referred to the unit of mass\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}\(\\ln X\)\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02710
name: Grunwald–Winstein equation
def: "The linear free energy relation\: \\\[\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\{k_\{0\}\}\) = m\\ Y\\\] expressing the dependence of the rate of solvolysis of a substrate on ionizing power of the solvent. The rate constant \\\(k_\{0\}\\\) applies to the reference solvent \(ethanol–water\, 80\:20\, v/v\) and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) to the solvent s\, both at \\\(25\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\). The parameter \\\(m\\\) is characteristic of the substrate and is assigned the value unity for tert-butyl chloride. The value \\\(Y\\\) is intended to be a quantitative measure of the ionizing power of the solvents. The equation was later extended to the form\: \\\[\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\{k_\{0\}\}\) = m\\ Y+l\\ N\\\] where \\\(N\\\) is the nucleophilicity of the solvent and \\\(l\\\) its susceptibility parameter. The equation has also been applied to reactions other than solvolysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02711
name: guest
def: "An organic or inorganic ion or molecule that occupies a cavity\, cleft or pocket within the molecular structure of a host molecular entity and forms a complex with it or that is trapped in a cavity within the crystal structure of a host."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02712
name: Guinier plot
def: "A diagrammatic representation of data on scattering from large particles\, obtained at different angles but at the same concentration\, constructed by plotting \\\(\\log _\{10\}\\Delta R\\left\(\\theta \\right\)\\\) or \\\(\\log _\{10\}P\\left\(\\theta \\right\)\\\) versus \\\(\(\\sin \\frac\{\\theta\}\{2\}\)^\{2\}\\\) or \\\(q^\{2\}\\\) and \(usually\) used for the evaluation of the radius of gyration. \\\(\\Delta R\(\\theta\)\\\) is the excess Rayleigh ratio\, \\\(P\\left\(\\theta \\right\)\\\) the particle scattering function\, \\\(\\theta \\\) the scattering angle and \\\(q\\\) the length of the scattering vector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:G02713
name: gustiness
def: "Intensity of turbulence; the ratio of the root mean square of wind velocity fluctuations to the mean wind velocity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT06826
name: genomics
def: "Science of using DNA- and RNA-based technologies to demonstrate alterations in gene expression.Method providing information on the consequences for gene expression of interactions of the organism with environmental stress\, xenobiotics\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT06961
name: genetic algorithm
def: "A genetic algorithm is an optimization algorithm based on the mechanisms of Darwinian evolution which uses random mutation\, crossover and selection procedures to breed better models or solutions from an originally random starting population or sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07138
name: grafting
def: "Reaction in which one or more species of block are connected to the main chain of a macromolecule as side-chains having constitutional or configurational features that differ from those in the main chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07386
name: gated photochromism
def: "Special form of photochromism in which one or both forms of a photochromic system are transformed reversibly\, either chemically or electrochemically\, into a non-photochromic form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07387
name: geminate ion pair
def: "Ion pair formed from a precursor that constitutes a single kinetic entity\, i.e.\, by electron transfer or ion transfer in an encounter complex \(cf. collision complex\) or by ionic dissociation of a single molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07388
name: Gibbs energy of photoinduced electron transfer
def: "For photoinduced electron transfer between an acceptor \(\\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\)\) and a donor \(\\\(\\rm\{D\}\\\)\) \(either one of them may be the electronically excited molecular entity\) of any charge type\, \\\(z\(\\rm\{A\}\)\\\) and \\\(z\(\\rm\{D\}\)\\\)\, the change in standard Gibbs energy can be approximated as \(the notation is for the case of neutral species \\\(\\rm\{D\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\)\) \\\[\\Delta _\{\\rm\{ET\}\}G^\{\\rm\{o\}\} = N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\left \\\{e\\\, \[E^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\\bullet \}/\\rm\{D\}\)- E^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\(\\rm\{A\}/\\rm\{A\}^\{-\\bullet \}\) \]+w\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\\bullet \}\\rm\{A\}^\{-\\bullet \}\)-w\(\\rm\{DA\}\)\\right \\\} - \\Delta E_\{0\,0\}\\\] where \\\(e = 1.602\\ 176\\ 487 \\times 10^\{-19\}\\\, \\rm\{C\}\\\) is the elementary charge\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{A\}\} = \\pu\{6.02214076E23 mol-1\}\\\) is the Avogadro constant\, \\\(E^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\\bullet\}/\\rm\{D\}\)/\\rm\{V\}\\\) is the standard electrode potential of the donor cation radical resulting from the electron transfer\, \\\(E^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\(\\rm\{A\}/\\rm\{A\}^\{-\\bullet\}\)/\\rm\{V\}\\\) is the standard electrode potential of the acceptor \(both relative to the same reference electrode\) and \\\(\\Delta E_\{0\,0\}/\\pu\{J mol-1\}\\\) is the vibrational zero electronic energy of the excited partner \(provided that a vibrationally equilibrated excited state at energy \\\(E_\{0\,0\}\\\) takes part in the reaction\)\, all data referring to the same solvent. \\\(w\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\\bullet \}\\rm\{A\}^\{-\\bullet \}\)\\\) and \\\(w\(\\rm\{DA\}\)\\\) are the electrostatic work terms that account for the effect of Coulombic attraction in the products and reactants\, respectively \\\[w\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\\bullet \}\\rm\{A\}^\{-\\bullet \}\)/J=\\frac\{z\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\\bullet \}\)z\(\\rm\{A\}^\{-\\bullet \}\)e^\{2\}\}\{4\\pi \\epsilon _\{0\}\\epsilon _\{r\}a\}\\\] \\\[w\(\\rm\{DA\}\)/J =\\frac\{z\(\\rm\{D\}\)z\(\\rm\{A\}\)e^\{2\}\}\{4\\pi \\epsilon _\{0\}\\epsilon _\{r\}a\}\\\] where \\\(a\\\) is the distance of the charged species after electron transfer\, \\\(\\varepsilon_\{r\}\\\) is the relative medium static permittivity \(formerly called dielectric constant\)\, \\\(\\varepsilon_\{0\} \\approx 8.854 \\times 10^\{-12\}\\ \\rm\{C\}^\{2\}\\ \\rm\{J\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-1\}\\\) is the electric constant \(vacuum permittivity\)\, and \\\(z\(\\rm\{X\}\)\\\) the charge of the species \\\(X\\\).  In SI units the factor \\\(\\frac\{e^\{2\}\}\{4\\pi\\varepsilon _\{0\}\} = 2.307 \\times 10^\{-28\}\\ \\rm\{J m\}\\\). For the case of neutral species \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{D\}\\\)\, \\\(z\(\\rm\{D\}\)=z\(\\rm\{A\}\)=0\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07389
name: global analysis
def: "Method for kinetic analysis of time-resolved emission or absorption data."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07535
name: gel point
def: "Point of incipient network formation in a process forming a chemical or physical polymer network."
synonym: "gelation point" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07536
name: gel time
def: "Time interval from the start of a network-forming process to the gel point."
synonym: "gelation time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07537
name: gelation temperature
def: "Temperature threshold for the formation of a thermoreversible gel."
synonym: "gel temperature" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07551
name: gel fraction
def: "Mass fraction of the network material resulting from a network-forming polymerization or crosslinking process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07552
name: green body
def: "Object formed from a preceramic material prior to pyrolysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07553
name: hybrid material
def: "Material composed of an intimate mixture of inorganic components\, organic components\, or both types of component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07620
name: gel aging
def: "Time-dependent changes in the chemical or the physical structure and the properties of a gel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:GT07621
name: gelation
def: "Process of passing through the gel point to form a gel or network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H01881
name: η
def: "An affix giving a topological indication of the bonding between a $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ ligand and the central atom in a coordination entity. A right superscript numerical index indicates the number of ligating atoms in the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ system of the ligand which bind to the central atom."
synonym: "eta" []
synonym: "hapto" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02714
name: haem
def: "An alternative spelling for heme."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02715
name: half-chair
def: "The conformation of a six-membered ring structure in which four contiguous atoms are in a plane and the other two atoms lie on opposite sides of the plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02716
name: half life
def: "For a given reaction the half life \\\(t_\{1/2\}\\\) of a reactant is the time required for its concentration to reach a value that is the arithmetic mean of its initial and final \(equilibrium\) values. For a reactant that is entirely consumed it is the time taken for the reactant concentration to fall to one half its initial value\: The half life of a reaction has meaning only in special cases\:  For a first-order reaction\, the half life of the reactant may be called the half life of the reaction.  For a reaction involving more than one reactant\, with the concentrations of the reactants in their stoichiometric ratios\, the half life of each reactant is the same\, and may be called the half life of the reaction.   If the concentrations of reactants are not in their stoichiometric ratios\, there are different half lives for different reactants\, and one cannot speak of the half life of the reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02717
name: half life
def: "For a single radioactive decay process\, the time required for the activity to decrease to half its value by that process."
synonym: "radioactive half life" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02720
name: half-peak potential
def: "In linear-sweep voltammetry\, triangular-wave voltammetry\, cyclic triangular-wave voltammetry\, and similar techniques\, the potential of the indicator electrode at which the difference between the total current and the residual current is equal to one-half of the peak current. This potential is attained in the interval in which the rate of the charge-transfer process\, and hence the \(absolute value of the\) current\, increase monotonically with time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02721
name: half thickness
def: "The thickness of a specified substance which\, when introduced into the path of a given beam of radiation\, reduces the value of a specified radiation quantity by one half."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02722
name: half-wave potential
def: "The potential of a polarographic or voltammetric indicator electrode at the point\, on the rising part of a polarographic or voltammetric wave\, where the difference between the total current and the residual current is equal to one-half of the limiting current. The quarter-wave potential\, the three-quarter-wave potential\, etc.\, may be similarly defined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02723
name: half-width
def: "The full width of a spectral band at a height equal to half of the height at the band maximum. Also known as full width at half maximum \(FWHM\). The dimension of band width should be either inverse length \(wavenumbers\) or inverse time \(frequencies\) so that the values give an indication of the energies. Note the hyphen in half-width. Half bandwidth has the meaning of half-width at half maximum."
synonym: "full width at half maximum" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02724
name: halirenium ions
def: "Cyclic cations having the structure\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02725
name: halochromism
def: "The colour change which occurs on addition of acid \(or base\, or a salt\) to a solution of a compound. A chemical reaction \(e.g. ion formation\) transforms a colourless compound into a coloured one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02726
name: haloforms
def: "Trihalomethanes $\\ce\{CHX3\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02727
name: halohydrins
def: "A traditional term for alcohols substituted by a halogen atom at a saturated carbon atom otherwise bearing only hydrogen or hydrocarbyl groups \(usually used to mean $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}halo alcohols\}$\). E.g. $\\ce\{BrCH2CH2OH\}$ 'ethylene bromohydrin' \(2-bromoethanol\)\, $\\ce\{ClCH2CH2CH2OH\}$ 'trimethylene chlorohydrin' \(3-chloro-propan-1-ol\)\, $\\ce\{PhCH\(OH\)CH2Cl\}$ 'styrene chlorohydrin' \(2-chloro-1-phenylethanol\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02728
name: halonium ions
def: "Ions of the form $\\ce\{R2X^\{+\}\}$\, where $\\ce\{X\}$ is any halogen \($\\ce\{X\} = \\ce\{Br^\{+\}\}$\, bromonium ions; $\\ce\{X\} = \\ce\{Cl^\{+\}\}$\, chloronium ions; $\\ce\{X\} = \\ce\{F^\{+\}\}$\, fluoronium ions; $\\ce\{X\} = \\ce\{I^\{+\}\}$\, iodonium ions\). They may be open-chain or cyclic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02729
name: halophiles
def: "Organisms which require a minimum concentration of sodium chloride in their environment \(cf. extremophiles\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02730
name: hamiltonian operator
def: "Quantum mechanical operator of total energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02732
name: Hammett equation
def: "The equation in the form\: \\\[\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{k\}\{k_\{0\}\}\) = \\rho \\ \\sigma \\\] or \\\[\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{K\}\{K_\{0\}\}\) = \\rho \\ \\sigma \\\] applied to the influence of meta- or para-substituents $\\ce\{X\}$ on the reactivity of the functional group $\\ce\{Y\}$ in the benzene derivative m- or $\\ce\{p-XC6H4Y\}$. \\\(k\\\) or \\\(K\\\) is the rate or equilibrium constant\, respectively\, for the given reaction of m- or $\\ce\{p-XC6H4Y\}$; \\\(k_\{0\}\\\) or \\\(K_\{0\}\\\) refers to the reaction of $\\ce\{C6H5Y\}$\, i.e. $\\ce\{X\} = \\ce\{H\}$; is the substituent constant characteristic of m- or $\\ce\{p-X\}$\: is the reaction constant characteristic of the given reaction of $\\ce\{Y\}$. The equation is often encountered in a form with \\\(\\log _\{10\}k_\{0\}\\\) or \\\(\\log _\{10\}K_\{0\}\\\) written as a separate term on the right hand side\, e.g. \\\[\\log _\{10\}k = \\rho \\ \\sigma +\\log _\{10\}k_\{0\}\\\] or \\\[\\log _\{10\}K = \\rho \\ \\sigma +\\log _\{10\}K_\{0\}\\\] It then signifies the intercept corresponding to $\\ce\{X\} = \\ce\{H\}$ in a regression of \\\(\\log _\{10\}k\\\) or \\\(\\log _\{10\}K\\\) on \\\(\\sigma \\\)."
synonym: "Hammett relation" []
synonym: "ρσ-equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02734
name: Hammond principle
def: "The hypothesis that\, when a transition state leading to an unstable reaction intermediate \(or product\) has nearly the same energy as that intermediate\, the two are interconverted with only a small reorganization of molecular structure. Essentially the same idea is sometimes referred to as 'Leffler's assumption'\, namely\, that the transition state bears the greater resemblance to the less stable species \(reactant or reaction intermediate/product\). Many text books and physical organic chemists\, however\, express the idea in Leffler's form\, but attribute it to Hammond. As a corollary\, it follows that a factor stabilizing a reaction intermediate will also stabilize the transition state leading to that intermediate. The acronym 'Bemahapothle' \(Bell\, Marcus\, Hammond\, Polanyi\, Thornton\, Leffler\) is sometimes used in recognition of the principal contributors towards expansion of the original idea of the Hammond postulate."
synonym: "Hammond postulate" []
synonym: "Leffler's assumption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02735
name: handedness
def: "This term has been used in two ways\, either chirality or chirality sense"

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02736
name: Hansch constant
def: "A measure of the capability of a solute for hydrophobic lipophilic interaction based on the partition coefficient \\\(P\\\) for distribution of the solute between octan-1-ol and water. The most general way of applying \\\(P\\\) in correlation analysis\, QSAR\, etc. is as log \\\(P\\\)\, but the behaviour of substituted benzene derivatives may be quantified by a substituent constant scale\, \\\(\\pi \\\)\, which is defined in a way analogous to the Hammett $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\}$ scale. There are various scales\, depending on the substrate series used as reference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02737
name: Hantzsch–Widman name
def: "A name for a heteromonocyclic parent hydride having no more than ten ring members formed by the citation of 'a' prefixes denoting the heteroatoms followed by an ending \(the 'stem'\) defining the size of the ring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02738
name: hapten
def: "A low-molecular weight molecule which contains an antigenic determinant but which is not itself antigenic unless complexed with an antigenic carrier. Examples of haptens are dinitrophenols\, phosphorylcholine and dextran."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02740
name: hard acid
def: "A Lewis acid with an acceptor centre of low polarizability. Other things being approximately equal\, complexes of hard acids and bases or soft acids and bases have an added stabilization \(sometimes called 'HSAB' rule\). For example the hard O- \(or N-\) bases are preferred to their S- \(or P-\) analogues by hard acids. Conversely a soft acid possesses an acceptor centre of high polarizability and exhibits the reverse preference for coordination of a soft base. These preferences are not defined in a quantitative sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02741
name: hard amorphous carbon films
def: "A synonym for diamond-like carbon films"

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02742
name: hard base
def: "A Lewis base with a donor centre \(e.g. an oxygen atom\) of low polarizability the converse applies to soft bases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02744
name: harmonic frequency generation
def: "Production of coherent radiation of frequency \\\(k\\ \nu \\\) \(\\\(k=2\,3\,...\\\)\) from coherent radiation of frequency. In general\, this effect is obtained through the interaction of laser light with a suitable optical medium with non-linear polarizability. The case \\\(k=2\\\) is referred to as frequency doubling\, \\\(k=3\\\) is frequency tripling and \\\(k=4\\\) is frequency quadrupling. Even higher integer values of \\\(k\\\) are possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02745
name: harmonic mean
def: "The number of observations\, divided by the sum of reciprocals of the observations. Symbol\: \\\(\\bar\{x\}_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\\\). It can be calculated with the formula\: \\\[\\bar\{x\}_\{\\rm\{h\}\} = \\frac\{n\}\{\\sum x_\{j\}^\{-1\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02746
name: harpoon mechanism
def: "Reaction sequence \(thermal or photoinduced\) between neutral molecular or atomic entities in which long-range electron transfer is followed by a considerable reduction of the distance between donor and acceptor sites as a result of the electrostatic attraction in the ion pair created."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02747
name: hartree
def: "Atomic unit of energy\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\\approx 4.359\\ 7482\(26\)\\times 10^\{-18\}\\ \\rm\{J\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02748
name: Hartree energy
def: "Atomic fundamental physical constant used as atomic unit of energy\: \\\(E_\{\\rm\{h\}\}=\\frac\{\\mathrm\{\\hslash \}^\{2\}\}\{m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\ a_\{0\}^\{2\}\} = 4.359\\ 7482\(26\)\\times 10^\{-18\}\\ \\rm\{J\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\mathrm\{\\hslash\}\\\) is the Planck constant divided by \\\(2\\ \\pi \\\)\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) the electron rest mass and \\\(a_\{0\}\\\) the Bohr radius."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02749
name: Haworth representation
def: "The Haworth representation of the cyclic forms of monosaccharides can be derived from the Fischer projection\, as follows. The monosaccharide is depicted with the carbon-chain horizontal and in the plane of the paper\, the potential carbonyl group being to the right. The oxygen bridge is then depicted as being formed behind the plane of the paper. The heterocyclic ring is therefore located in a plane approximately perpendicular to the plane of the paper and the groups attached to the carbon atoms of that ring are above and below the ring. The carbon atoms of the ring are not shown. Groups that appear to the right of the vertical chain in the Fischer projection \(structures $\\ce\{A\}$\, $\\ce\{D\}$\) then appear below the plane of the ring in the Haworth representation \(structures $\\ce\{B\}$\, $\\ce\{C\}$\, $\\ce\{E\}$\). However\, at the asymmetric carbon atom \($\\ce\{C-5\}$ in $\\ce\{A\}$; $\\ce\{C-4\}$ in $\\ce\{D\}$\) involved via oxygen in ring formation with the carbon atom of the carbonyl group a formal double inversion must be envisaged to obtain the correct Haworth representation. In the pyranose forms of ᴅ-aldohexoses $\\ce\{C-6\}$ will always be above the plane\, In the furanose forms of ᴅ-aldohexoses the position of $\\ce\{C-6\}$ will depend on the configuration at $\\ce\{C-4\}$; it will\, for example\, be above the plane in ᴅ-glucofuranoses \(e.g. $\\ce\{C\}$\) but below the plane in ᴅ-galactofuranoses \(e.g. $\\ce\{E\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02750
name: haze
def: "A state of reduced visibility \(\\\(1\{-\}2\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\)\) resulting from the increased light scatter due to the presence of fine dust or aerosol particles \($\\ce\{H2SO4\}$\, $\\ce\{NH4HSO4\}$\, products of the ozone-terpene reactions\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02751
name: haze horizon
def: "The top of a haze layer which is confined by a low-level temperature inversion so that it gives the appearance of the horizon which it may obscure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02752
name: heat
def: "Energy transferred from a hotter to a cooler body due to a temperature gradient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02753
name: heat capacity
def: "Heat brought to a system to increase its temperature divided by that temperature increase. At constant volume \\\(C_\{V\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial U\}\{\\partial T\}\)_\{V\}\\\) at constant pressure \\\(C_\{p\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial H\}\{\\partial T\}\)_\{p\}\\\)\, where \\\(U\\\) is the internal energy and \\\(H\\\) the enthalpy of the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02754
name: heat capacity of activation
def: "A quantity related to the temperature coefficient of \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H\\\) \(enthalpy of activation\) and \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}S\\\) \(entropy of activation\) according to the equations\: \\\[\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}C_\{p\} = \(\\frac\{\\partial \\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}H\}\{\\partial T\}\)_\{p\}=T\\ \(\\frac\{\\partial \\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}S\}\{\\partial T\}\)_\{p\}\\\] If the rate constant is expressible in the form \\\[\\ln k = \\frac\{a\}\{T\}+b+c\\ \\ln T + \\rm\{d\}T\,\\\] then\: \\\[\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}C_\{p\} = \(c - 1\)\\ R+2\\ \\rm\{d\}\(R\\ T\)\\\] SI unit\: \\\(\\pu\{J mol-1 K-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02755
name: heat flux
def: "Heat transferred through a cross section perpendicular to the flow in a small time interval divided by that time interval and the cross sectional area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02756
name: heavy atom effect
def: "The enhancement of the rate of a spin-forbidden process by the presence of an atom of high atomic number\, which is either part of\, or external to\, the excited molecular entity. Mechanistically\, it responds to a spin–orbit coupling enhancement produced by a heavy atom."
synonym: "external heavy atom effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02757
name: heavy atom isotope effect
def: "An isotope effect due to isotopes other than those of hydrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02758
name: heavy water
def: "Water containing a significant fraction \(up to 100 per cent\) of deuterium in the form of $\\ce\{D2O\}$ or $\\ce\{HDO\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02759
name: hecto
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{2\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{h\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02760
name: height equivalent to an effective theoretical plate
def: "The column length divided by the effective theoretical plate number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02761
name: height equivalent to a theoretical plate
def: "The column length divided by the theoretical plate number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02762
name: helicenes
def: "ortho-Fused polycyclic aromatic or heteroaromatic compounds in which all rings \(minimum five\) are angularly arranged so as to give helically shaped molecules\, which are thus chiral\, e.g. hexahelicene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02763
name: helicity
def: "The chirality of a helical\, propeller or screw-shaped molecular entity. A right-handed helix is described as P \(or plus\)\, a left-handed one as M \(or minus\).    The application of this system to the description of conformations considers the torsion angle between two specified \(fiducial\) groups that are attached to the atoms linked by that bond. The sign of the smaller torsion angle between the fiducial groups defines the chirality sense of the helix. Rules for the selection of fiducial groups according to priority are given by R.S. Cahn\, C.K. Ingold and V. Prelog\, Angew. Chem. 78\, 413-447 \(1966\)\, Angew. Chem. Internat. Ed. Eng. 5\, 385-415\, 511 \(1966\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02764
name: helion
def: "Nucleus of the $\\ce\{^\{3\}He\}$ atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02765
name: helium–cadmium laser
def: "A continuous wave laser emitting mainly at \\\(325.0\\\) and \\\(441.6\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) from singly ionized cadmium."
synonym: "cadium–helium laser" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02766
name: helium dead-space
def: "The position of the Gibbs surface is often defined experimentally as that surface which encloses the volume of space from which the solid excludes helium gas \(the so-called helium dead-space\)\, and is associated with the assumptions that the volume of the solid is unaffected by the adsorption of component i\, and that helium is not adsorbed by the solid. This requires that the measurement of the helium dead-space be made at a sufficiently high temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02767
name: helium ionization detector
def: "A weak beta source and a high potential raise the helium atom of the carrier gas to a metastable state. All other substances having an ionization potential lower than \\\(18\\ \\rm\{eV\}\\\) are ionized\, and the current that results is used to measure the components. The detector is usually employed to measure inorganic compounds at concentrations between \\\(0.1\\\) and \\\(10\\ \\rm\{ppmv\}\\\). It has a linear range of about \\\(10^\{2\}\\\) but is somewhat unstable and requires great care to ensure the helium purity and to eliminate all leaks in the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02768
name: helium–neon laser
def: "A continuous wave laser emitting mainly at \\\(632.8\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\, \\\(1152.3\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\, and \\\(3391.3\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) from excited neutral $\\ce\{Ne\}$ atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02769
name: helix
def: "The molecular conformation of a spiral nature\, generated by regularly repeating rotations around the backbone bonds of a macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02770
name: helix residue
def: "The smallest set of one or more successive configurational base units that generates the whole chain through helical symmetry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02771
name: helix sense
def: "The right-handed sense of a helix traces out a clockwise rotation moving away from the observer; the left-handed sense of a helix traces out a counterclockwise rotation moving away from the observer\, e.g. the ...\\\(\\mathrm\{TG\}^\{+\}\\\) \\\(\\mathrm\{TG\}^\{+\}\\\) \\\(\\mathrm\{TG\}^\{+\}\\\) … helix of isotactic poly\(propylene\) is left-handed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02772
name: Helmholtz energy
def: "Internal energy minus the product of thermodynamic temperature and entropy. It was formerly called free energy."
synonym: "Helmholtz energy function" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02773
name: hemes
def: "Complexes consisting of an iron ion coordinated to a porphyrin acting as a tetradentate ligand\, and to one or two axial ligands."
synonym: "heme derivatives" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02774
name: hemiacetals
def: "Compounds having the general formula $\\ce\{R2C\(OH\)OR'\}$ \($\\ce\{R'\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02775
name: hemiaminals
def: "$\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}Amino\}$ alcohols\, improperly called carbinolamines \(the adducts of ammonia\, or of primary or secondary amines to the carbonyl group of aldehydes and ketones\) $\\ce\{R2C\(OH\)NR2\}$. Compounds of structure $\\ce\{R2C\(OR'\)NR2\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\) are hemiaminal ethers\, or $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}amino\}$ ethers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02776
name: hemiketals
def: "Hemiacetals having the structure $\\ce\{R2C\(OH\)OR\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, derived from ketones by formal addition of an alcohol to the carbonyl group. This term\, once abandoned\, has been reinstated as a subclass of hemiacetals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02778
name: hemins
def: "Chloro\(porphyrinato\)iron\(III\) complexes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02779
name: hemochromes
def: "Iron–porphyrin complexes\, having one or two basic ligands \(e.g. piperidine\, amines\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02780
name: hemoglobins
def: "Heme derivatives having a protein chain as axial ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02781
name: Henderson–Hasselbalch equation
def: "An equation of the form\: \\\[\\rm\{pH\} = \\rm\{p\}K_\{\\rm\{a\}\}- \\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{\\left\[\\rm\{HA\}\\right\]\}\{\\left\[\\rm\{A\}^\{-\}\\right\]\}\)\\\] for the calculation of the \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\) of solutions where the ratio \\\(\\frac\{\\left\[\\rm\{HA\}\\right\]\}\{\\left\[\\rm\{A\}^\{-\}\\right\]\}\\\) is known."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02782
name: henry
def: "SI derived unit of inductance\, \\\(\\pu\{H\} = \\pu\{V A-1 s\} = \\pu\{m2 kg s-2 A-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02783
name: Henry's law
def: "The partial pressure \(fugacity\) of a solute \(B\) in a solution is directly proportional to the rational chemical activity \(\\\(a_\{x\}\\\)\) of the solute; this relationship is called Henry's law\: \\\[p_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=\\frac\{a_\{x\,\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\alpha _\{x\,\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\infty \}\}\\\] where \\\(\\alpha _\{x\,\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\infty \}\\\) is the rational solubility coefficient for infinite dilution\, i.e. for pure solvent. For the solvent \(A\) the relationship is called Raoult's law\, and the proportionality factor is the fugacity of the pure solvent\, \\\(\\overset\{˜\}\{p\}_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{\\ast \}\\\)\: \\\[p_\{\\rm\{A\}\}=\\overset\{˜\}\{p\}_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{\\ast \}\\ a_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02785
name: hertz
def: "SI derived unit of frequency equal to one cycle per second\, \\\(\\rm\{Hz\} = \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."
synonym: "cycles per second" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02786
name: Herz compounds
def: "1\,2$\\uplambda\\!$4\,3-Benzodithiazolium chlorides \(formed in the reaction of aniline and derivatives thereof with disulfur dichloride\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02787
name: hetarenes
def: "Synonymous with heteroarenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02788
name: hetaryl groups
def: "Synonymous with heteroaryl groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02789
name: hetarynes
def: "Synonymous with heteroarynes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02790
name: heteroalkenes
def: "Analogues of alkenes in which a doubly bonded carbon atom is replaced by a heteroatom\, e.g. $\\ce\{H2Si=CH2\}$ methylidenesilane \(silene less preferred\)\, $\\ce\{MeN=CH2\}$ N-methylmethanimine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02791
name: heteroarenes
def: "Heterocyclic compounds formally derived from arenes by replacement of one or more methine \($\\ce\{–C=\}$\) and/or vinylene \($\\ce\{–CH=CH\\bond\{–\}\}$\) groups by trivalent or divalent heteroatoms\, respectively\, in such a way as to maintain the continuous $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ system characteristic of aromatic systems and a number of out-of-plane $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ corresponding to the Hückel rule \(\\\(4\\ n+2\\\)\); an alternative term is hetarenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02792
name: heteroaryl groups
def: "The class of heterocyclyl groups derived from heteroarenes by removal of a hydrogen atom from any ring atom; an alternative term is hetaryl. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02793
name: heteroarynes
def: "Compounds derived from heteroarenes by replacement of a formal carbon-carbon double bond by a formal triple bond \(with loss of two hydrogen atoms\). Also known as hetarynes and as 1\,2-didehydroheteroarenes. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02794
name: heterobimetallic complex
def: "A metal complex having two different metal atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02795
name: heterochain polymer
def: "A class of polymer in which the main chain is constructed from atoms of two or more elements. Heterochain polymers are named by placing the names or symbols of all the elements in the main chain\, immediately before the expresssion '-chain polymer'\, e.g. \(oxygen\, carbon\)-chain polymer or \(O\, C\)-chain polymer; \(oxygen\, nitrogen\, carbon\)-chain polymer or \(O\, N\, C\)-chain polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02796
name: heteroconjugation
def: "\[obsolete\] Association between a base and the conjugate acid of a different base through a hydrogen bond \($\\ce\{B'\\bond\{...\}HB^\{+\}\}$ or $\\ce\{A'H\\bond\{...\}A^\{-\}\}$\). The term has its origin in the conjugate acid–base pair and is in no way related to conjugated systems of orbitals. Heteroassociation is a more appropriate term.\n\[obsolete\] Some authors refer to conjugated systems containing a heteroatom\, e.g. pyridine\, as 'heteroconjugated systems'. This usage is discouraged since it inappropriately suggests an analogy to homoconjugation\, and conflicts with the currently accepted definition of that term."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02797
name: heterocumulenes
def: "Cumulenes in which one or more carbon atoms of the cumulative bond system have been replaced by heteroatoms. E.g. $\\ce\{O=C=C=C=O\}$\, but not $\\ce\{CH2=C=O\}$\, ketene\, nor $\\ce\{O=C=O\}$\, carbon dioxide\, which are heteroallenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02798
name: heterocyclic compounds
def: "Cyclic compounds having as ring members atoms of at least two different elements\, e.g. quinoline\, 1\,2-thiazole\, bicyclo\[3.3.1\]tetrasiloxane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02799
name: heterocyclyl groups
def: "Univalent groups formed by removing a hydrogen atom from any ring atom of a heterocyclic compound. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02800
name: heterodetic cyclic peptide
def: "A peptide consisting only of amino-acid residues\, but in which the linkages forming the ring are not solely eupeptide bonds; one or more is an isopeptide\, disulfide\, ester\, or other bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02801
name: heterodisperse
def: "Describes a colloidal system in which all the particles are of different sizes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02802
name: heteroexcimer
def: "Synonymous with exciplex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02805
name: heterogeneous diffusion rate constant
def: "Defined by the equation\: \\\[k_\{\\rm\{d\}\}=\\frac\{I_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{n\\ F\\ c\\ A\}\\\] where the limiting current \\\(I_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is assumed to be due to the diffusion species of concentration \\\(c\\\) and of diffusion coefficient \\\(D\\\). \\\(n\\\) is the charge number of the cell reaction written so that the stoichiometric coefficient of this species is unity. \\\(A\\\) is usually taken as the geometric area of the electrode\, and \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02808
name: heteroleptic
def: "Transition metal or Main Group compounds having more than one type of ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02809
name: heterolysis
def: "The cleavage of a covalent bond so that both bonding electrons remain with one of the two fragments between which the bond is broken\, e.g.  Heterolytic bond fission is a feature of many bimolecular reactions in solution \(e.g.electrophilic substitution\, nucleophilic substitution\)."
synonym: "heterolytic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02810
name: heterolytic bond-dissociation energy
def: "The energy required to break a given bond of some specific compound by heterolysis. For the dissociation of a neutral molecule $\\ce\{AB\}$ in the gas phase into $\\ce\{A^\{+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{B^\{−\}\}$ the heterolytic bond-dissociation energy $D\(\\ce\{A^\{+\}B^\{−\}\}\)$ is the sum of the bond dissociation energy\, $D\(\\ce\{A–B\}\)$\, and the adiabatic ionization energy of the radical $\\ce\{A^\{.\}\}$; minus the electron affinity of the radical $\\ce\{B^\{.\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02811
name: heterolytic dissociative adsorption
def: "Relates in the usual sense to the formal nature of the cleavage of a single bond. If in the bond of the adsorptive $\\ce\{A\:B\}$\, $\\ce\{A\}$ or $\\ce\{B\}$ retains the electron pair\, the adsorption is heterolytic dissociative adsorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02812
name: heteropolysaccharide
def: "The class name for polysaccharides composed of two or more different kinds of monomeric units \(i.e. monosaccharides\)."
synonym: "heteroglycan" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02816
name: heterotrophic
def: "Descriptive of organisms which are not able to synthesize cell components from carbon dioxide as sole carbon source. Heterotrophic organisms use preformed oxidizable organic substrates such as glucose as carbon and energy sources."
synonym: "heterotrophic organisms" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02818
name: hexagonal graphite
def: "The thermodynamically stable form of graphite with an ABAB stacking sequence of the graphene layers. The exact crystallographic description of this allotropic form is given by the space group \\\(d_\{6h\}^\{4\} - P6_\{3\}/\\rm\{mmc\}\\\) \(unit cell constants\: \\\(a=245.6\\ \\mathrm\{pm\}\\\)\, \\\(c=670.8\\ \\mathrm\{pm\}\\\)\). Hexagonal graphite is thermodynamically stable below approximately \\\(2600\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) and \\\(6\\ \\rm\{GPa\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02819
name: <i>hexahedro</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote eight atoms bound into a hexahedron \(e.g. cube\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02820
name: <em>hexaprismo</em>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote twelve atoms bound into a hexagonal prism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02822
name: higher-order transition
def: "A general term used to describe a transition in which the first and second derivatives of the molar Gibbs energy or molar Helmholtz energy \(or chemical potential\) with respect to temperature and pressure are continuous\, but derivatives of some higher order are discontinuous at the transition point. Synonymous with smooth transition."
synonym: "smooth transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02823
name: highly oriented pyrolytic graphite
def: "Highly oriented pyrolytic graphite \(HOPG\) is a pyrolytic graphite with an angular spread of the c-axes of the crystallites of less than 1 degree."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02824
name: high-pressure graphitization
def: "A term which refers to the solid-state transformation of non-graphitic carbon into graphite by heat treatment under elevated pressure \(e.g. \\\(100\\\) to \\\(1000\\ \\rm\{MPa\}\\\)\) so that a definitely higher degree of graphitization is achieved at lower temperature and/or for a shorter heat treatment time than in heat treatment of the same non-graphitic material at atmospheric pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02825
name: high-pressure mercury lamp
def: "Radiation source containing mercury at a pressure of ca. \\\(8\\ \\rm\{MPa\}\\\) \(ca. \\\(80\\ \\rm\{bar\}\\\)\) or higher which emits lines over a background continuum between about \\\(200\\\) and \\\(1400\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)."
synonym: "high-pressure mercury arc" []
synonym: "hot quartz lamp" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02827
name: Hildebrand parameter
def: "A parameter measuring the cohesion of a solvent \(energy required to create a cavity in the solvent\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02829
name: histones
def: "A class of basic proteins associated with DNA in the chromosomes of eukaryotic cells forming the nucleosome as the basic subunit of chromatin. They contain an unusually large proportion of the basic amino acids arginine and lysine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02830
name: Hofmann rule
def: "'The principal olefin formed in the decomposition of quaternary ammonium hydroxides that contain different primary alkyl groups is always ethylene\, if an ethyl group is present.' Originally given in this limited form by A. W. Hofmann\, the rule has since been extended and modified as follows\: 'When two or more alkenes can be produced in a $\\upbeta\\!$-elimination reaction\, the alkene having the smallest number of alkyl groups attached to the double bond carbon atoms will be the predominant product.' This orientation described by the Hofmann rule is observed in elimination reactions of quaternary ammonium salts and tertiary sulfonium salts\, and in certain other cases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02831
name: hold-back carrier
def: "A carrier used to prevent a particular species from following other species in a chemical or physical operation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02832
name: hold-up volume
def: "The volume of eluent required to elute a component\, the concentration of which in the stationary phase is negligible compared to that in the mobile phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02833
name: hold-up volume
def: "The volume of the mobile phase \(or the corresponding time\) required to elute a component the concentration of which in the stationary phase is negligible compared to that in the mobile phase. In other words\, this component is not retained at all by the stationary phase. Thus\, the hold-up volume \(time\) is equal to the retention volume \(time\) of an unretained compound. The hold-up volume \(time\) includes any volumes contributed by the sample injector\, the detector\, and connectors. \\\[t_\{\\rm\{M\}\}=\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}\{F_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\}\\\] In gas chromatography this term is also called the gas hold-up volume \(time\). The corrected gas hold-up volume \(\\\(V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\)\) is the gas hold-up volume multiplied by the compression \(compressibility\) correction factor \(\\\(j\\\)\)\: \\\[V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}=V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\ j\\\] Assuming that the influence of extra column volume on \\\(V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\) is negligible\, \\\[V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}=V_\{\\rm\{G\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "hold-up time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02834
name: hole burning
def: "The photo bleaching of a feature\, normally a narrow range\, within an inhomogeneous broader absorption or emission band. The holes are produced by the disappearance of resonantly excited molecules as a result of photophysical or photochemical processes. The resulting spectroscopic technique is site-selection spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02835
name: hole transfer
def: "Charge migration process in which the majority carriers are positively charged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02836
name: holoenzyme
def: "An active enzyme consisting of the apoenzyme and coenzyme."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02838
name: homo
def: "a\) An acronym for Highest Occupied Molecular Orbital \(HOMO\). See\: frontier orbitals.b\) A prefix \(consisting of lower case letters\, homo\)\, used to indicate a higher homologue of a compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02839
name: homoaromatic
def: "Whereas in an aromatic molecule there is continuous overlap of p-orbitals over a cyclic array of atoms\, in a homoaromatic molecule there is a formal discontinuity in this overlap resulting from the presence of a single \\\(\\rm\{sp\}^\{3\}\\\) hybridized atom at one or several positions within the ring; p-orbital overlap apparently bridges these \\\(\\rm\{sp\}^\{3\}\\\) centres\, and features associated with aromaticity are manifest in the properties of the compound. Pronounced homoaromaticity is not normally associated with neutral molecules\, but mainly with species bearing an electrical charge\, e.g. the 'homotropylium' cation\, \($\\ce\{C8H9^\{+\}\}$\)\:  In bis\, tris\, \(etc.\) homoaromatic species\, two\, three\, \(etc.\) single \\\(\\rm\{sp\}^\{3\}\\\) centres separately interrupt the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02840
name: homochain polymer
def: "A class of polymer in which the main chain is constructed from atoms of a single element. Homochain polymers are named by placing the name or symbol of the element in the main chain immediately before the expression '-chain polymer'\, e.g. carbon-chain polymer or C-chain polymer; sulfur-chain polymer or S-chain polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02842
name: homoconjugation
def: "\[obsolete\] Association between a base and its conjugate acid through a hydrogen bond \($\\ce\{B\\bond\{...\}HB^\{+\}\}$ or $\\ce\{AH\\bond\{...\}A^\{-\}\}$\). Homoassociation is a more appropriate term for this phenomenon.The orbital overlap of two $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}systems\}$ separated by a non-conjugating group\, such as $\\ce\{CH2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02843
name: homocyclic compounds
def: "Cyclic compounds having as ring members atoms of the same element only\, e.g. benzene\, pentazole\, cyclohexasilane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02844
name: homodetic cyclic peptide
def: "A cyclic peptide in which the ring consists solely of amino-acid residues in eupeptide linkage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02845
name: homogeneity
def: "The degree to which a property or a constituent is uniformly distributed throughout a quantity of material. A material may be homogeneous with respect to one analyte or property but heterogeneous with respect to another. The degree of heterogeneity \(the opposite of homogeneity\) is the determining factor of sampling error."
synonym: "material heterogeneity" []
synonym: "material homogeneity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02850
name: homoleptic
def: "Transition metal or Main Group compounds having only one type of ligand are said to be homoleptic\, e.g. $\\ce\{TaMe5\}$."
synonym: "hemileptic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02851
name: homolysis
def: "The cleavage of a bond \('homolytic cleavage' or 'homolytic fission'\) so that each of the molecular fragments between which the bond is broken retains one of the bonding electrons. A unimolecular reaction involving homolysis of a bond \(not forming part of a cyclic structure\) in a molecular entity containing an even number of \(paired\) electrons results in the formation of two radicals\:  It is the reverse of colligation. Homolysis is also commonly a feature of bimolecular substitution reactions \(and of other reactions\) involving radicals and molecules."
synonym: "homolytic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02852
name: homolytic dissociative adsorption
def: "Relates in the usual sense to the formal nature of the cleavage of a single bond. If the electron pair in the bond of the adsorptive $\\ce\{A\{\:\}B\}$ is divided in the course of its dissociative adsorption\, the adsorption is homolytic dissociative adsorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02853
name: homomorphic
def: "Superposable ligands are called homomorphic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02854
name: homopolymer
def: "A polymer derived from one species of \(real\, implicit or hypothetical\) monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02855
name: homopolymerization
def: "Polymerization in which a homopolymer is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02856
name: homopolysaccharide
def: "The class name for polysaccharides composed of only one kind of monosaccharide."
synonym: "homoglycan" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02857
name: homotopic
def: "Atoms or groups of a molecule which are related by an n-fold rotation axis \(n = 2\, 3\, etc.\) are called homotopic. For example\, chiral tartaric acid \(\\\(C_\{2\}\\\) axis\)\, chloroform \(\\\(C_\{3\}\\\) axis\) and cyclohexaamylose \($\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}cyclodextrin\}$\, \\\(C_\{6\}\\\) axis\) have respectively two homotopic carboxyl groups\, three homotopic chlorine atoms and six homotopic ᴅ-glucose residues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02858
name: horizontal elution
def: "A mode of operation in which the paper or plate is in a horizontal position and the mobile-phase movement along the plane depends on capillary action."
synonym: "horizontal development" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02859
name: host
def: "A molecular entity that forms complexes with organic or inorganic guests\, or a chemical species that can accommodate guests within cavities of its crystal structure. Examples include cryptands and crowns \(where there are ion-dipole attractions between heteroatoms and positive ions\)\, hydrogen-bonded molecules that form 'clathrates' \(e.g. hydroquinone and water\)\, and host molecules of inclusion compounds \(e.g. urea or thiourea\). van der Waals forces and hydrophobic interactions bind the guest to the host molecule in clathrates and inclusion compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02860
name: host-vector system
def: "A compatible combination of host \(e.g. bacteria\) and vector \(e.g. plasmid\) that allows propagation of DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02861
name: hot atom
def: "An atom in an excited energy state or having kinetic energy above the ambient thermal level\, usually as a result of nuclear processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02862
name: hot cell
def: "A heavily shielded enclosure for highly radioactive materials. It may be used for their handling or processing by remote means or for their storage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02863
name: hot ground state reaction
def: "A hot state reaction of the ground electronic state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02865
name: hot state reaction
def: "A reaction proceeding from an ensemble of molecular entities possessing a higher average vibrational\, rotational or translational energy than they would at thermal equilibrium with the surrounding medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02866
name: hour
def: "Non-SI unit of time\, \\\(\\rm\{h\} = 3600\\ \\rm\{s\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02867
name: Hückel \(4<em>n</em> + 2\) rule
def: "Monocyclic planar \(or almost planar\) systems of trigonally \(or sometimes digonally\) hybridized atoms that contain $\\ce\{\(4n + 2\) \\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ \(where $n$ is a non-negative integer\) will exhibit aromatic character. The rule is generally limited to $n = 0 – 5$. This rule is derived from the Hückel MO calculation on planar monocyclic conjugated hydrocarbons $\\ce\{\(CH\)_\{m\}\}$ where $m$ is an integer equal to or greater than 3 according to which $\\ce\{\(4n + 2\) \\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ are contained in a closed-shell system. Examples of systems that obey the Hückel rule include\:  Systems containing $\\ce\{4n\\;\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ \(such as cyclobutadiene and the cyclopentadienyl cation\) are 'antiaromatic'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02868
name: Huggins coefficient
def: "A parameter in the Huggins equation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02869
name: Huggins equation
def: "The equation describing the dependence of the reduced viscosity\, \\\(\\frac\{\\eta _\{\\rm\{i\}\}\}\{c\}\\\)\, on the mass concentration of a polymer\, \\\(c\\\)\, for dilute polymer solutions of the form\: \\\[\\frac\{\\eta _\{\\rm\{i\}\}\}\{c\} = \\left\[\\eta \\right\]+k_\{\\rm\{H\}\}\\ \\left\[\\eta \\right\]^\{2\}\\ c\\\] where \\\(k_\{\\rm\{H\}\}\\\) is the Huggins coefficient and \\\(\[\\eta\]\\\) is the intrinsic viscosity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02870
name: humidity
def: "A general term referring to the water content of a gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02871
name: Hund rules
def: "Of the different multiplets resulting from different configurations of electrons in degenerate orbitals of an atom those with greatest multiplicity have the lowest energy \(multiplicity rule\).Among multiplets having the same multiplicity\, the lowest-energy one is that with the largest total orbital angular momentum \(angular momentum rule\) \(valid if the total orbital angular momentum is a constant of motion\).In configurations containing shells less than half full of electrons\, the term having the lowest total angular momentum \\\(J\\\) lies lowest in energy\, whereas in those with shells more than half filled\, the term having the largest value of \\\(J\\\) lies lowest \(fine structure rule\). Hund rules apply if the 'Russell–Saunders' coupling scheme is valid. Sometimes the first rule is applied with questionable validity to molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02874
name: hybridization
def: "Linear combination of atomic orbitals on an atom. Hybrid orbitals are often used in organic chemistry to describe the bonding molecules containing tetrahedral \(\\\(\\mathrm\{sp\}^\{3\}\\\)\)\, trigonal \(\\\(\\mathrm\{sp\}^\{2\}\\\)\) and digonal \(\\\(\\mathrm\{sp\}\\\)\) atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02875
name: hybridoma
def: "A hybrid cell line resulting from the fusion of a specific antibody-producing spleen cell \(lymphocyte\) with a myeloma cell\, which has the growth characteristics of the myeloma component and the antibody-secreting characteristics of the lymphocyte\, and will multiply to become a source of pure monoclonal antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02876
name: hydration
def: "Addition of water or of the elements of water \(i.e. H and OH\) to a molecular entity. For example\, hydration of ethene\:  The term is also used in a more restricted sense for the process\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02879
name: hydrazides
def: "Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$ \($l \neq 0$\) by replacing $\\ce\{–OH\}$ by $\\ce\{–NRNR2\}$ \($\\ce\{R\}$ groups are commonly $\\ce\{H\}$\)\, as in carbohydrazides\, $\\ce\{RC\(=O\)NHNH2\}$\, sulfonohydrazides\, $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)2NHNH2\}$\, and phosphonic dihydrazides\, $\\ce\{RP\(=O\)\(NHNH2\)2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02880
name: hydrazidines
def: "Compounds\, $\\ce\{RC\(NHNH2\)2=NNH2\}$\, derived from carboxylic acids by replacing $\\ce\{–OH\}$ by $\\ce\{–NHNH2\}$ \(or N-substituted analogues\) and $\\ce\{=O\}$ by $\\ce\{=NNH2\}$ \(or substituted analogues\). A specific hydrazidine is named as a hydrazide hydrazone\, e.g. hexanohydrazide hydrazone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02881
name: hydrazines
def: "Hydrazine \(diazane\)\, $\\ce\{H2NNH2\}$\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives. When one or more substituents are acyl groups\, the compound is a hydrazide. N-Alkylidene derivatives are hydrazones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02883
name: hydrazo compounds
def: "Compounds containing the divalent hydrazo group\, $\\ce\{–NHNH\\bond\{–\}\}$\, such as hydrazoarenes \(1\,2-diarylhydrazines or 1\,2-diaryldiazanes\, usually with both aryl groups the same\) and their N-substituted derivatives $\\ce\{ArNRNRAr\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02884
name: hydrazones
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=NNR2\}$\, formally derived from aldehydes or ketones by replacing $\\ce\{=O\}$ by $\\ce\{=NNH2\}$ \(or substituted analogues\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02885
name: hydrazonic acids
def: "Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$ \($l \neq 0$\) by replacing a double-bonded oxygen atom by $\\ce\{=NNR2\}$\, as in carbohydrazonic acids\, $\\ce\{RC\(OH\)=NNH2\}$ and sulfonohydrazonic acids\, $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)\(=NNH2\)OH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02889
name: hydrocarbons
def: "Compounds consisting of carbon and hydrogen only."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02890
name: hydrocarbylene groups
def: "Divalent groups formed by removing two hydrogen atoms from a hydrocarbon\, the free valencies of which are not engaged in a double bond\, e.g. 1\,3-phenylene\, $\\ce\{–CH2CH2CH2–\}$ propane-1\,3-diyl\, $\\ce\{–CH2–\}$ methylene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02891
name: hydrocarbyl groups
def: "Univalent groups formed by removing a hydrogen atom from a hydrocarbon\, e.g. ethyl\, phenyl."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02892
name: hydrocarbylidene groups
def: "Divalent groups\, $\\ce\{R2C=\}$\, formed by removing two hydrogen atoms from the same carbon atom of a hydrocarbon\, the free valencies of which are engaged in a double bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02893
name: hydrocarbylidyne groups
def: "Trivalent groups\, $\\ce\{RC#\}$\, formed by removing three hydrogen atoms from the same carbon atom of a hydrocarbon\, the free valencies of which are engaged in a triple bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02894
name: hydrocarbylsulfanyl nitrenes
def: "Nitrenes substituted with hydrocarbyl sulfanyl groups\, . Sulfenyl nitrenes is an older synonymous term. E.g. MeSN methylsulfanylnitrene or methylthionitrene. The synonymous term 'thiazynes' \(from the third canonical form; confusable with the hetarynes derivable from 1\,2- and 1\,4-thiazine\) is best avoided."
synonym: "sulfenyl nitrenes" []
synonym: "thiazynes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02895
name: hydrocracking unit
def: "Used in the thermal decomposition of heavy \(high molecular weight\) hydrocarbons to smaller \(low molecular weight\) hydrocarbons; high pressures of hydrogen and a special catalyst are employed. Sulfur compounds in the fuel are reduced to $\\ce\{H2S\}$\, and the final hydrocarbon product can be obtained relatively sulfur-free."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02896
name: hydrodynamically equivalent sphere
def: "A hypothetical sphere\, impenetrable to the surrounding medium\, displaying in a hydrodynamic field the same frictional effect as an actual polymer molecule. The size of a hydrodynamically equivalent sphere may be different for different types of motion of the macromolecule\, e.g. for diffusion and for viscous flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02897
name: hydrodynamic volume
def: "The volume of a hydrodynamically equivalent sphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02898
name: hydrogen
def: "The general name for the atom $\\ce\{H\}$\, without regard for its nuclear mass\, either for hydrogen in its natural abundance or where it is not desired to distinguish between the isotopes. The systematic name for atomic hydrogen is monohydrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02899
name: hydrogen bond
def: "A form of association between an electronegative atom and a hydrogen atom attached to a second\, relatively electronegative atom. It is best considered as an electrostatic interaction\, heightened by the small size of hydrogen\, which permits proximity of the interacting dipoles or charges. Both electronegative atoms are usually \(but not necessarily\) from the first row of the Periodic Table\, i.e. $\\ce\{N\}$\, $\\ce\{O\}$ or $\\ce\{F\}$. Hydrogen bonds may be inter-molecular or intramolecular. With a few exceptions\, usually involving fluorine\, the associated energies are less than \\\(\\pu\{20\\!-\\! 25 kJ mol-1\}\\\) \(\\\(\\pu\{5\\!\{-\}\\! 6 kcal mol-1\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02900
name: hydrogen gas electrode
def: "A thin foil of platinum electrolytically coated with a finely divided deposit of platinum or palladium metal\, which catalyses the electrode reaction\: \\\[\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}+\\rm\{e\}\\rightarrow \\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\rm\{H\}_\{2\}\\\] in solutions saturated with hydrogen gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02901
name: hydrolases
def: "Enzymes that catalyse the cleavage of $\\ce\{C–O\}$\, $\\ce\{C–N\}$\, $\\ce\{C–C\}$ and other bonds by reactions involving the addition or removal of water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02902
name: hydrolysis
def: "Solvolysis by water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02903
name: hydrometeor
def: "Any condensed water particle in the atmosphere of sufficient size to be potentially capable of undergoing precipitation \(in fogs\, clouds\, some hazes\, raindrops\, snowflakes\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02904
name: hydron
def: "The general name for the cation $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$; the species $\\ce\{H^\{−\}\}$ is the hydride anion and $\\ce\{H\}$ is the hydro group. These are general names to be used without regard to the nuclear mass of the hydrogen entity\, either for hydrogen in its natural abundance or where it is not desired to distinguish between the isotopes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02905
name: hydroperoxides
def: "Monosubstitution products of hydrogen peroxide \(dioxidane\)\, $\\ce\{HOOH\}$\, having the skeleton $\\ce\{ROOH\}$\, in which $\\ce\{R\}$ is any organyl group. Compounds in which $\\ce\{R\}$ is acyl are known as peroxy acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02906
name: hydrophilic
def: "'Water loving'. The capacity of a molecular entity or of a substituent to interact with polar solvents\, in particular with water\, or with other polar groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02907
name: hydrophobic interaction
def: "The tendency of hydrocarbons \(or of lipophilic hydrocarbon-like groups in solutes\) to form intermolecular aggregates in an aqueous medium\, and analogous intramolecular interactions. The name arises from the attribution of the phenomenon to the apparent repulsion between water and hydrocarbons. However\, the phenomenon ought to be attributed to the effect of the hydrocarbon-like groups on the water-water interaction. The misleading alternative term 'hydrophobic bond' is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02908
name: hydropolysulfides
def: "Compounds having the structures $\\ce\{RS2H\}$\, $\\ce\{RS3H\}$ ... $\\ce\{RSnH\}$\, in which $\\ce\{Sn\}$ is a chain of sulfur atoms\, and $\\ce\{R\}$ is hydrocarbyl. Some people exclude hydrodisulfides from the class hydropolysulfides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02909
name: hydrosphere
def: "The gaseous\, liquid and solid water of the earth \(oceans\, ice caps\, lakes\, rivers\, etc.\) as distinguished from the lithosphere and the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02910
name: hydrosulfides
def: "Term used in radicofunctional nomenclature of thiols. Hydrosulfides is not commonly used as a class name for thiols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02911
name: hydroxamic acids
def: "Compounds\, $\\ce\{RC\(=O\)NHOH\}$\, derived from oxoacids $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$ \($l \neq 0$\) by replacing $\\ce\{–OH\}$ by $\\ce\{–NHOH\}$\, and hydrocarbyl derivatives thereof. Specific examples are preferably named as N-hydroxy amides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02912
name: hydroximic acids
def: "Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$ \($l \neq 0$\) by replacing $\\ce\{=O\}$ by $\\ce\{=NOH\}$ \($\\ce\{=NOR\}$\)\, as in carbohydroximic acids\, $\\ce\{RC\(OH\)=NOH\}$\, and sulfonohydroximic acids\, $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)\(=NOH\)OH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02913
name: hydroxylamines
def: "Hydroxylamine\, $\\ce\{H2N–OH\}$\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02914
name: hygrometer
def: "Instrument used to measure the water vapour content of the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02922
name: hygrometry
def: "The measurement or indication of the water content of the ambient air or of a sample of gas."
synonym: "moisture analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02923
name: hyperchromic effect
def: "The increase in the intensity of a spectral band due to substituents or interactions with the molecular environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02924
name: hyperconjugation
def: "In the formalism that separates bonds into $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\uppi\\! types\}$\, hyperconjugation is the interaction of $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ \(e.g. $\\ce\{C–H\}$\, $\\ce\{C–C\}$\, etc.\) with a $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\}$ network. This interaction is customarily illustrated by contributing structure\, e.g. for toluene \(below\)\, sometimes said to be an example of 'heterovalent' or 'sacrificial hyperconjugation'\, so named because the contributing structure contains one two-electron bond less than the normal Lewis formula for toluene\:  At present\, there is no evidence for sacrificial hyperconjugation in neutral hydrocarbons. The concept of hyperconjugation is also applied to carbenium ions and radicals\, where the interaction is now between $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ and an unfilled or partially filled $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ or p-orbital. A contributing structure illustrating this for the tert-butyl cation is\:  This latter example is sometimes called an example of 'isovalent hyper-conjugation' \(the contributing structure containing the same number of two-electron bonds as the normal Lewis formula\). Both structures shown on the right hand side are also examples of 'double bond-no-bond resonance'. The interaction between filled $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ or p-orbitals and adjacent antibonding  $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!^\{\\ast\}\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}$ is referred to as 'negative hyperconjugation'\, as for example in the fluoroethyl anion\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02926
name: <i>hypho</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to designate an open structure\, especially a boron skeleton\, more closed than a klado structure but more open than an arachno structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02927
name: hypochromic effect
def: "The opposite of hyperchromic effect \(i.e. a decrease in intensity\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02928
name: hypo-phase
def: "The denser phase in an extraction system. This term is often used when two non-aqueous phases are present or when the solvent is an aqueous phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02929
name: hypsochromic shift
def: "Shift of a spectral band to higher frequency or shorter wavelength upon substitution or change in medium. It is informally referred to as blue shift."

[Term]
id: goldbook:H02930
name: hysteresis
def: "Regarding a material quantity or instrument's reading; the dependence of a value on the direction of change from a previous characteristic value. It may be quantified by the difference between the upscale and downscale variation starting from fixed lower and upper measurement points \(inversion\)."
synonym: "electrode memory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT06787
name: Haber–Weiss reaction
def: "The Haber–Weiss cycle consists of the following two reactions\: $\\ce\{H2O2 + OH^\{.\} -> H2O + O2^\{−\} + H^\{+\}\}$and$\\ce\{H2O2 + O2^\{-\} -> O2 + OH^\{-\} + OH^\{.\}\}$ The second reaction achieved notoriety as a possible source of hydroxyl radicals. However\, it has a negligible rate constant. It is believed that iron\(III\) complexes can catalyse this reaction\: first\, Fe\(III\) is reduced by superoxide\, followed by oxidation by dihydrogen peroxide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT06827
name: hazard
def: "Set of inherent properties of a substance\, mixture of substances\, or a process involving substances that\, under production\, usage\, or disposal conditions\, make it capable of causing adverse effects to organisms or the environment\, depending on the degree of exposure; in other words\, it is a source of danger."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT06886
name: hemodialysis
def: "Dialysis process in which undesired metabolites and toxic by-products\, such as urea and creatine\, are removed from blood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT06908
name: health surveillance
def: "The procedure for effects of work on the health of employees."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT06962
name: Hansch analysis
def: "Hansch analysis is the investigation of the quantitative relationship between the biological activity of a series of compounds and their physicochemical substituent or global parameters representing hydrophobic\, electronic\, steric and other effects using multiple regression correlation methodology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT06963
name: hydrophilicity
def: "Hydrophilicity is the tendency of a molecule to be solvated by water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT06964
name: hydrophobicity
def: "Hydrophobicity is the association of non-polar groups or molecules in an aqueous environment which arises from the tendency of water to exclude non-polar molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07035
name: Hückel molecular orbital theory
def: "The simplest molecular orbital theory of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}conjugated\}$ molecular systems. It uses the following approximations\: $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ approximation; LCAO representation of the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}molecular orbitals\}$; neglect of electron-electron and nuclear-nuclear repulsions \(in fact\, the assumption that these cancel\). The diagonal elements of the effective Hamiltonian\, coulombic integrals\, and the off-diagonal elements\, resonance integrals\, \(accounted for only directly bonded atoms\) are chosen as empirical parameters\, all overlap integrals being neglected."
synonym: "HMO theory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07041
name: harmonic approximation
def: "The approximation of the full nuclear potential of a molecular system in its equilibrium geometry and in the vicinity of the respective minimum on the potential energy surface by the function \\\[V=\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\,j=1\n\\end\{array\}\}^\{3\\ N\}\\frac\{\\partial ^\{2\}V\}\{\\partial q_\{i\}\\partial q_\{j\}\}\\\] where \\\(q_\{i\}\\\) are mass-weighted cartesian displacements of nuclei relative to their equilibrium positions. The approximation allows one to describe vibrational motion in terms of independent vibrational modes \(normal modes\) each of which is governed by a simple one-dimensional harmonic potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07048
name: homodesmotic reaction
def: "A subclass of isodesmic reactions in which reactants and products contain equal numbers of carbon atoms in corresponding states of hybridization; moreover\, there is matching of the carbon-hydrogen bonds in terms of the number of hydrogen atoms joined to the individual carbon atoms. To achieve all this matching\, one should significantly extend the number and types of reference molecules. In the aliphatic series of hydrocarbons these are propane\, isobutane and neopentanes as well as propene and isobutene; for aromatics—buta-1\,3-diene\, 2-vinylbutadiene \(3-methylidenehexa-1\,5-diene\) and 2\,3-divinylbutadiene \(3\,4-bismethylidenehexa-1\,5-diene\). Thus to assess strain energy of cyclopropane and aromatic stabilization of benzene the following homodesmotic reactions are to be respectively analysed.$\\ce\{c-\(CH2\)3 + 3CH3–CH3 -> 3CH3CH2CH3\}$\\\(\\Delta H_\{\\exp\}^\{\\circ\} = \\pu\{-26.5 kcal/mol\}\\\) \(\\\(\\pu\{110.9 kJ/mol\}\\\)\).$\\ce\{C6H6 + 3CH2=CH2 -> 3CH2=CH–CH=CH2\}$\(trans\)\\\(\\Delta H_\{\\rm\{calc\}\}^\{\\circ\} \\rm\{\(MP2/6\{-\}31G^\{**\}\)\} = \\pu\{23.9 kcal/mol\}\\\) \(\\\(\\pu\{100.0 kJ/mol\}\\\)\) Due to closer matching of the hybridization states of the atoms of reactants and products as compared to isodesmic reactions\, the homodesmotic reactions give more accurate estimates of the intrinsic strain and the cyclic delocalization. The definition may be extended to molecules with heteroatoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07049
name: hybrid orbital
def: "An atomic orbital derived through hybridization of atomic orbitals with different angular momentum quantum numbers located at a given atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07050
name: hydrogen bond
def: "A particular type of multicenter \(three center - four electron\) $\\ce\{X–H...Y\}$ in which the central hydrogen atom covalently linked to an electronegative atom $\\ce\{X\}$ \($\\ce\{C\}$\, $\\ce\{N\}$\, $\\ce\{O\}$\, $\\ce\{S\}$...\) forms an additional weaker bond with atom $\\ce\{Y\}$ \($\\ce\{N\}$\, $\\ce\{O\}$\, $\\ce\{S\}$...\) in the direction of its lone electron pair orbital. The energy of hydrogen bonds\, which is usually in the range of \\\(\\pu\{3\\!\{-\}\\! 15 kcal/mol\}\\\) \(\\\(\\pu\{12\\!\{-\}\\! 65 kJ/mol\}\\\)\)\, results from the electrostatic interaction and also from the orbital interaction of the antibonding $\\upsigma^\{\\ast\}\(\\ce\{XH\}\)MO$ of the molecule acting as the hydrogen donor and the non-bonding lone electron pair $MO\{\\rm\{n\}\}_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}$ of the hydrogen acceptor molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07051
name: hypercoordination
def: "A property of main-group atoms in molecular entities to acquire coordination numbers greater than four \(which would comply with the Lewis octet rule\). Hypercoordination may be associated with hypervalency\, but usually is referred to peculiar atomic centres in the electron-deficient species with multicentre $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonding\}$\, in which the bonding power of a pair of electrons is spread over more than two atoms. An example of a hypercoordinated atom is the five-coordinate carbon atom in the methanium cation\, where three $\\ce\{C–H\}$ bonds may be regarded as normal two center - two electron bonds and the bonding in the remaining $\\ce\{CH2\}$ fragment is governed by the three-centre\, two-electron bond. A particular case of a hypercoordinated atom is the hydrogen atom included into a hydrogen bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07052
name: hyperfine coupling
def: "The interaction between the spin magnetic moment of an unpaired electron and the nuclear spin magnetic moments resulting in the splitting of the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ \(spin up\) and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$ \(spin down\) energy levels in an external magnetic field and\, thus\, in the multiplet pattern of the ESR spectra of radical-like species and transition metal compounds. Two main contributions to the hyperfine coupling are usually considered\, Fermi contact and dipolar interactions. The contact interaction is isotropic and related to the unpaired spin density at the nucleus\, \\\(|\\psi _\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\}\}|^\{2\}\\\). The dipolar interaction is anisotropic\, and related to \\\(r^\{-3\}\\\)\, where \\\(r\\\) is the distance between the atom holding the unpaired electron and the nucleus with non-zero spin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07053
name: hyperpolarizability
def: "The energy of a molecule in an external electrostatic field can be expanded as \\\[E=E^\{\\rm\{o\}\}- \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}_\{i\}\\ F_\{i\}- \\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} _\{ij\}\\ F_\{i\}\\ F_\{j\}- \\frac\{1\}\{6\}\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} _\{ijk\}\\ F_\{i\}\\ F_\{j\}\\ F_\{k\}- \\frac\{1\}\{24\}\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B3\} _\{ijkl\}\\ F_\{i\}\\ F_\{j\}\\ F_\{k\}\\ F_\{l\}- \\rm\{...\}\\\] where \\\(E^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\) is the unperturbed energy\, \\\(F_\{i\}\\\) is the component of the field in the i direction\, \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}_\{i\}\\\) is the permanent dipole moment\, \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\}_\{ij\}\\\) is the polarizability tensor\, and \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\}_\{ijk\}\\\) and \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B3\}_\{ijkl\}\\\) are the first and second hyperpolarizability tensors\, respectively. \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\}\\\) is a third order symmetric tensor that measures the second order response of the molecular electric dipole moment to the action of an external electric field and is thus often referred to as dipole hyperpolarizability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07054
name: hypervalency
def: "The ability of an atom in a molecular entity to expand its valence shell beyond the limits of the Lewis octet rule. Hypervalent compounds are common for the second and subsequent row elements in groups 15–18 of the periodic table. A description of the hypervalent bonding implies a transfer of the electrons from the central \(hypervalent\) atom to the nonbonding molecular orbitals which it forms with \(usually more electronegative\) ligands. A typical example of the hypervalent bond is a linear three-centre\, four-electron bond\, e.g. that of $\\ce\{F_\{ap\}–P–F_\{ap\}\}$ fragment of $\\ce\{PF5\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07207
name: halato-telechelic polymer
def: "Polymer composed of linear macromolecules having ionic or ionizable end-groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07232
name: homologous polymer blend
def: "Mixture of two or more fractions of the same polymer\, each of which has a different molar-mass distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07293
name: hard-segment phase domain
def: "Phase domain of microscopic or smaller size\, usually in a block\, graft\, or segmented copolymer\, comprising essentially those segments of the polymer that are rigid and capable of forming strong intermolecular interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07390
name: half-life
def: "Time necessary for thermal bleaching to half of the absorbance of the coloured form of a photochromic compound at a specific wavelength during one cycle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07391
name: half-life
def: "Time needed for a concentration of the entity to decrease to \\\(1/2\\\) of its original value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07392
name: heliochromism
def: "Applied to photochromic compounds possessing a high efficiency for colouring with near-ultraviolet radiation and a low efficiency for bleaching with visible radiation\, but a moderate efficiency for thermal fading at ambient temperature. These compounds are activated by unfiltered sunlight and deactivated under diffuse daylight conditions\, therefore being suitable for sun-lens applications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07393
name: hula-twist mechanism
def: "Volume-conserving mechanism of photoisomerization of a double bond in a conjugated system involving simultaneous configurational and conformational isomerization\, e.g.\, the photoinitiated concerted rotation of two adjacent double and single bonds."
synonym: "HT mechanism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07394
name: hyperfine
def: "Interaction between the electron spin S and the nuclear spin I."
synonym: "hyperfine interaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07519
name: hydrogel
def: "Gel in which the swelling agent is water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07556
name: hybrid polymer
def: "Polymer or polymer network comprised of inorganic and organic components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07582
name: homogeneous polymer blend
def: "Polymer blend that is a single-phase structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07622
name: hipping
def: "Isostatic pressing process carried out at elevated temperatures."
synonym: "hot isostatic pressing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:HT07623
name: hydrolysis ratio
def: "Mole ratio of water to alkoxy groups used in sol-gel processing of metal alkoxides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I00954
name: identity period
def: "The shortest distance along the chain axis for translational repetition of the chain structure. The chain identity period is usually denoted by \\\(c\\\). Synonymous with \(chain\) repeating distance."
synonym: "chain identity period" []
synonym: "chain repeating distance" []
synonym: "repeating distance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02931
name: <i>icosahedro</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote twelve atoms bound into a triangular icosahedron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02932
name: icosanoids
def: "Unsaturated $\\ce\{C20\}$ fatty acids and skeletally related compounds. The spelling icosanoids is preferred over the spelling eicosanoids for consistency with icosanoic acid. Specialists working in this field commonly use eicosanoids\, however."
synonym: "eicosanoids" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02933
name: ideal adsorbed state
def: "The adsorbed state in a system following Langmuir's isotherm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02934
name: ideal dilute solution
def: "Dilute solution in which the solute may be regarded as obeying Henry's law\, so that all the solute activity coefficients may be approximated to 1."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02935
name: ideal gas
def: "Gas which obeys the equation of state \\\(p\\\, V=n\\\, R\\\, T\\\) \(the ideal gas law; \\\(p\\\) is the pressure\, \\\(V\\\) the volume\, \\\(n\\\) the amount of molecules\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature\). For an ideal gas the fugacity\, \\\(f\\\)\, of each constituent B is equal to its partial pressure\, \\\(p_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(f_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=p_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=x_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ p\\\)\, where \\\(x_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the amount fraction of B."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02938
name: ideal mixture
def: "A mixture of substances B\, C\, ... is called an ideal mixture when \\\(a_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=x_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(a_\{\\rm\{C\}\}=x_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\)\, ... or \\\(f_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=1\\\)\, \\\(f_\{\\rm\{C\}\}=1\\\) where \\\(a_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) and \\\(f_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) are the relative activity and the activity coefficient\, respectively\, of a substance B in a liquid or solid mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02940
name: identity reaction
def: "A chemical reaction whose products are chemically identical with the reactants\, for example the bimolecular self exchange reaction of $\\ce\{CH3I\}$ with $\\ce\{I^\{-\}\}$."
synonym: "degenerate chemical reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02941
name: illuminance
def: "Luminous flux received by a surface divided by the area of that surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02942
name: image converter tube
def: "An electron tube that produces on its fluorescent screen an image of the irradiation pattern of its photosensitive input surface. An image converter which produces an image with enhanced radiance is sometimes called an image intensifier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02943
name: image dissection tube
def: "A two-dimensional radiation detector in which the electron image produced by a photo-emitting surface\, usually a photocathode\, is focused in the plane of a defining aperture. Magnetic or electric fields scan this image across the defining aperture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02944
name: imaging
def: "The use of a photosensitive system for the capture\, recording and retrieval of information associated with an object using electromagnetic energy."
synonym: "photoimaging" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02945
name: imbalance
def: "The situation in which reaction parameters that characterize different bond forming or bond breaking processes in the same reaction have developed to different extents as the transition state is approached along some arbitrarily defined reaction coordinate. For example\, in the nitroalkane anomaly\, the Brønsted \\\(\\beta \\\) exponent for proton removal is smaller than the Brønsted \\\(\\alpha \\\) for the nitroalkane\, because of imbalance between the amount of bond breaking and resonance delocalization in the transition state. Imbalance is common in reactions such as elimination\, addition and other complex reactions that involve proton \(hydron\) transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02946
name: imbibition
def: "The uptake of a liquid by a gel or porous substance. It may or may not be accompanied by swelling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02948
name: imides
def: "Diacyl derivatives of ammonia or primary amines\, especially those cyclic compounds derived from diacids\, e.g. \nIn additive nomenclature\, in which imide is analogous to oxide\, the term is used to name compounds of the type $\\ce\{R3Y^\{+\}-N^\{-\}R\}$ \($\\ce\{Y = N\, P\}$\) and $\\ce\{R2Z^\{+\}-N^\{-\}R\}$ \($\\ce\{Z = O\, S\, Se\, Te\}$\)\, which are the products of formal attachment of an $\\ce\{RN=\}$ group to $\\ce\{N\}$\, $\\ce\{P\}$\, $\\ce\{O\}$\, $\\ce\{S\}$\, $\\ce\{Se\}$\, $\\ce\{Te\}$. E.g. amine imides\, azomethine imides.Salts having the anion $\\ce\{RN^\{2−\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02949
name: imidic acids
def: "Compounds derived from oxoacids $\\ce\{R_\{k\}E\(=O\)_\{l\}\(OH\)_\{m\}\}$ \($l \neq 0$\) by replacing $\\ce\{=O\}$ by $\\ce\{=NR\}$; thus tautomers of amides. In organic chemistry an unspecified imidic acid is generally a carboximidic acid\, $\\ce\{RC\(=NR\)\(OH\)\}$. E.g. $\\ce\{RS\(=NH\)2\(OH\)\}$ a sulfonodiimidic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02950
name: imidines
def: "Analogues of cyclic acid anhydrides in which $\\ce\{=O\}$ has been replaced by $\\ce\{=NR\}$ and $\\ce\{–O\\bond\{–\}\}$ by $\\ce\{–NR\\bond\{–\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02953
name: imidoyl carbenes
def: "Carbenes having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(=NR\)C\{\:\}–R\}$. Imidoyl is a shortened but imprecise term for carboximidoyl\, $\\ce\{RC\(=NH\)\\bond\{–\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02954
name: imidoyl nitrenes
def: "Nitrenes having the structure ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02957
name: imines
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RN=CR2\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} = \\ce\{H\}$\, hydrocarbyl\). Thus analogues of aldehydes or ketones\, having NR doubly bonded to carbon; aldimines have the structure $\\ce\{RCH=NR\}$\, ketimines have the structure $\\ce\{R'2C=NR\}$ \($\\ce\{R'\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). Imines include azomethines and Schiff bases. Imine is used as a suffix in systematic nomenclature to denote the $\\ce\{C=NH\}$ group excluding the carbon atom.An obsolete term for azacycloalkanes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02958
name: iminium compounds
def: "Salts in which the cation has the structure $\\ce\{R2C=N^\{+\}R2\}$. Thus N-hydronated imines and their N-substituted derivatives. The synonymous terms imonium compounds and immonium compounds are irregularly formed and should not be used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02959
name: imino acids
def: "An obsolete term\, which should not be used\, for imidic acids.Any carboxylic acids having an imino substituent\, $\\ce\{HN=\}$\, replacing two hydrogens. A shortened form of imino carboxylic acid.Obsolescent term for azaalkanoic acids and azacycloalkane-2-carboxylic acids\, e.g. proline\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02960
name: imino carbenes
def: "This term systematically means carbenes bearing an imino or N-substituted imino group\, $\\ce\{RN=\}$\, somewhere in the molecule. E.g. $\\ce\{R–C\:–CH2C\(=NR\)R\}$. It is listed here in order to warn against its misuse for alkylideneamino carbenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02962
name: iminoxyl radicals
def: "Synonymous with alkylideneaminoxyl radicals. The term can be regarded as a contraction of iminyloxyl radicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02964
name: iminylium ions
def: "Cations having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=N^\{+\}\}$. A contraction of alkaniminylium ions. Alkylideneaminylium ions is a synonymous term. A subclass of nitrenium ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02965
name: iminyl radicals
def: "Radicals having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=N^\{.\}\}$. A contraction of alkaniminyl radicals\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3–CH=N^\{.\}\}$\, ethaniminyl. A synonymous term is alkylideneaminyl radicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02966
name: immersional wetting
def: "A process in which a solid or liquid\, $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\, is covered with a liquid\, $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$\, both of which were initially in contact with a gas or liquid\, $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\}$\, without changing the area of the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\updelta\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02967
name: immission
def: "A Germanic term\, pronounced in English\, 'eye-mission'; the transfer of pollutants from the atmosphere to a 'receptor'; for example\, pollutants retained by the lungs. It does not have the same meaning as ground level concentration\, but is the opposite in meaning to emission. This term has not been used commonly in the English language."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02968
name: immission dose
def: "The integral of the immission flow into the receptor over the exposure period."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02969
name: immission flux
def: "The immission rate divided by the unit surface area of the receptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02970
name: immission rate
def: "The mass \(or other physical quantity\) of pollutant transferring per unit time into a receptor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02971
name: immobile adsorption
def: "A situation in which the freedom of molecules of adsorbate to move about the surface is limited. Adsorption is immobile when \\\(k\\ T\\\) is small compared to \\\(\\Delta E\\\)\, the energy barrier separating adjacent sites. The adsorbate has little chance of migrating to neighbouring sites and such adsorption is necessarily localized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02972
name: immobilized phase
def: "A stationary phase in which is immobilized on the support particles\, or on the inner wall of the column tubing e.g. by in situ polymerization \(cross-linking\) after coating."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02973
name: immobilization
def: "The technique used for the physical or chemical fixation of cells\, organelles\, enzymes\, or other proteins \(e.g. monoclonal antibodies\) onto a solid support\, into a solid matrix or retained by a membrane\, in order to increase their stability and make possible their repeated or continued use. The principle is also used for affinity chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02976
name: immobilized enzyme
def: "A soluble enzyme bound to an insoluble organic or inorganic matrix\, or encapsulated within a membrane in order to increase its stability and make possible its repeated or continued use."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02977
name: immune response
def: "Selective reaction of the body to substances that are foreign to it\, or that the immune system identifies as foreign\, shown by the production of antibodies and antibody-bearing cells or by a cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02978
name: immunization
def: "The administration of an antigen to an animal in order to stimulate the production of antibodies by that organism. Also\, the administration of antigens\, antibodies or lymphocytes to an animal to generate the corresponding active\, passive or adaptive immunity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02979
name: immunoassay
def: "A ligand-binding assay that uses a specific antigen or antibody\, capable of binding to the analyte\, to identify and quantify substances. The antibody can be linked to a radiosotope \(radioimmunoassay\, RIA\)\, or to an enzyme which catalyses an easily monitored reaction \(enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay\, ELISA\)\, or to a highly fluorescent compound by which the location of an antigen can be visualized \(immunofluorescene\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02980
name: immunochemistry
def: "Study of biochemical and molecular aspects of immunology\, especially the nature of antibodies\, antigens and their interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02981
name: immunogen
def: "A substance that elicits a cellular immune response and/or antibody production \(cf. antigen\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02982
name: immunoglobulin
def: "A protein of the globulin-type found in serum or other body fluids that possesses antibody activity. An individual Ig molecule is built up from two light \(L\) and two heavy \(H\) polypeptide chains linked together by disulfide bonds. Igs are divided into five classes based on antigenic and structural differences in the H chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02983
name: immunoradiometric assay
def: "An assay based on the reversible and non-covalent binding of an antigen by a specific antibody labelled with a radioactive nuclide as a tracer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02984
name: immunosuppression
def: "Reduction in the functional capacity of the immune response; may be due to\:  Inhibition of the normal response of the immune system to an antigen.   Prevention\, by chemical or biological means\, of the production of an antibody to an antigen by inhibition of the processes of transcription\, translation or formation of tertiary structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02985
name: impaction
def: "A forcible contact of particles of matter with a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02987
name: impact parameter
def: "In simple hard sphere collision theory the distance of closest approach that would result for two particles in a collision if the particle trajectories were undeflected by the collision."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02988
name: impedance
def: "Complex representation of potential difference divided by the complex representation of the current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02989
name: impingement
def: "Equivalent to impaction; often refers to impaction on a liquid surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02990
name: impinger
def: "A sampling instrument employing impingement for the collection of particulate matter. Common types are\: the midget impinger employing impingement in \\\(1\{-\}10\\ \\rm\{cm\}^\{3\}\\\) water\,  the standard impinger employing impingement in \\\(75\\ \\rm\{cm\}^\{3\}\\\) water and  dry impingers.  Impingers are also suitable for sampling certain gases and vapours."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02991
name: imprecision
def: "Variation of the result in a set of replicate measurements. This can be expressed\, e.g. as the standard deviation or coefficient of variation \(relative standard deviation\). This term may have a more general meaning\, e.g. if the replicates constitute a batch or involve different instruments\, laboratories and analyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02992
name: impregnation
def: "The modification of the separation properties of the chromatographic bed used in planar chromatography by appropriate additives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02993
name: improved canonical variational transition-state theory
def: "A modification of canonical variational transition-state theory in which\, for energies below the threshold energy\, the position of the dividing surface is taken to be that of the microcanonical threshold energy. This forces the contributions to rate constant to be zero below the threshold energy. A compromise dividing surface is then chosen so as to minimize the contributions to the rate constant made by reactants having higher energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02995
name: inaccuracy
def: "A quantitative term to describe the \(lack of\) accuracy of a chemical measurement process; comprises the imprecision and the bias. Inaccuracy must be viewed as a two-component quantity \(vector\); imprecision and bias should never be combined to give a scalar measure for chemical measurement process inaccuracy. \(One or the other component may\, however\, be negligible under certain circumstances.\) Inaccuracy should not be confused with uncertainty. Inaccuracy \(imprecision\, bias\) is characteristic of the measurement process\, whereas error and uncertainty are characteristics of a result. \(The latter characteristic\, of course\, derives from the imprecision and bounds for bias of the chemical measurement process.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02996
name: inch
def: "Non-SI unit of length\, \\\(\\pu\{in\} = \\pu\{0.0254 m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02997
name: incinerator
def: "Equipment in which solid\, liquid or gaseous combustible wastes are ignited and burned. Types include flue-fed and multiple-chamber incinerators with several stages of combustion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02998
name: inclusion compound
def: "A complex in which one component \(the host\) forms a cavity or\, in the case of a crystal\, a crystal lattice containing spaces in the shape of long tunnels or channels in which molecular entities of a second chemical species \(the guest\) are located. There is no covalent bonding between guest and host\, the attraction being generally due to van der Waals forces. If the spaces in the host lattice are enclosed on all sides so that the guest species is 'trapped' as in a cage\, such compounds are known as clathrates or 'cage compounds'."
synonym: "inclusion complex" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I02999
name: incoherent radiation
def: "Not having the properties of coherent radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03003
name: increment
def: "An individual portion of material collected by a single operation of a sampling device. Increments may be reduced individually or tested either \(a\) individually or \(b\) combined with other increments with the resulting composite reduced in size and tested as a single unit. Increments are created by the sampling operation and are usually taken from parts of a lot separated in time or space. Increments of a bulk population correspond to units of a packaged population."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03004
name: indicated hydrogen
def: "Under certain circumstances it is necessary to indicate in the name of a ring\, or ring system\, containing the maximum number of non-cumulative double bonds\, one or more positions where no multiple bond is attached. This is done by specifying the presence of an 'extra' hydrogen atom at such positions by citation of the appropriate numbered locant followed by an italicized capital H.Example\:  A second type of indicated hydrogen \(sometimes referred to as 'added hydrogen'\) describes hydrogen atoms added to a specific structure as a consequence of the addition of a suffix or a prefix describing a structural modification. This type of indicated hydrogen is normally cited in parentheses after the locant of the additional feature.Example\:"
synonym: "added hydrogen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03006
name: indicator electrode
def: "An electrode that serves as a transducer responding to the excitation signal \(if any\) and to the composition of the solution being investigated\, but that does not affect an appreciable change of bulk composition within the ordinary duration of a measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03008
name: indifferent absorbing ion
def: "An ion which absorbs through coulomb forces only and which is hence only repelled by surfaces of like sign\, attracted by surfaces of opposite sign\, and is not absorbed on an uncharged surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03009
name: indifferent electrolyte
def: "Synonymous with supporting electrolyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03010
name: indirect amplification
def: "A type of amplification reaction where the constituent required is associated with some other constituent which is then amplified and measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03011
name: indirect reaction
def: "A chemical process in which the reactive complex has a lifetime that is longer than its period of rotation is called an indirect reaction \(in contrast to a direct reaction\). In a molecular-beam experiment the products of such reactions are scattered at random with reference to the centre of mass of the system. Indirect reactions are also called complex-mode reactions. \(They are sometimes called complex reactions\, but this usage is not recommended in view of the danger of confusion with composite reactions\, which occur in more than one step\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03014
name: individual perception threshold
def: "A term used in odour testing which signifies the lowest concentration of a particular species at which a subject indicates both an initial positive and repeated response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03015
name: induced radioactivity
def: "Radioactivity induced by irradiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03016
name: induced reaction
def: "A reaction which occurs or is accelerated only if another reaction is simultaneously occurring in the same system. The substance\, which causes an induced reaction\, is termed inductor. The inductor\, unlike the catalyst\, is used up irreversibly during the process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03017
name: inducer
def: "A small molecule that triggers gene transcription by binding to a regulator protein \(cf. enzyme induction\). It acts by combining with the corresponding repressor protein to bring about an allosteric change so that the repressor is made incapable of combining with the operator of the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03018
name: induction
def: "An increase in the rate of synthesis of an \(inducible\) enzyme in response to the action of an inducer or environmental conditions. An inducer is often the substrate of the inducible enzyme or a structurally similar substance \(gratuitous inducer\) that is not metabolized.The experimental elicitation of lytic growth by a prophage from lysogenic bacteria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03019
name: induction period
def: "The initial slow phase of a chemical reaction which later accelerates. Induction periods are often observed with radical reactions\, but they may also occur in other systems \(for example before steady-state concentration of the reactants is reached\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03021
name: inductive effect
def: "In strict definition\, an experimentally observable effect \(on rates of reaction\, etc.\) of the transmission of charge through a chain of atoms by electrostatic induction. A theoretical distinction may be made between the field effect\, and the inductive effect as models for the coulomb interaction between a given site within a molecular entity and a remote unipole or dipole within the same entity. The experimental distinction between the two effects has proved difficult\, except for molecules of peculiar geometry\, which may exhibit 'reversed field effects'. Ordinarily the inductive effect and the field effect are influenced in the same direction by structural changes in the molecule and the distinction between them is not clear. This situation has led many authors to include the field effect in the term 'inductive effect'. Thus the separation of values into inductive and resonance components does not imply the exclusive operation of a through-bonds route for the transmission of the non-conjugative part of the substituent effect. To indicate the all-inclusive use of the term inductive\, the phrase 'so-called inductive effect' is sometimes used. Certain modern theoretical approaches suggest that the 'so-called inductive effect' reflects a field effect rather than through-bonds transmission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03022
name: inductomeric effect
def: "A molecular polarizability effect occurring by the inductive mechanism of electron displacement. The consideration of such an effect and the descriptive term have been regarded as obsolescent or even obsolete\, but in recent years theoretical approaches have reintroduced substituent polarizability as a factor governing reactivity\, etc. and its parametrization has been proposed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03025
name: inelastic scattering
def: "If in a molecular collision there is transfer of energy among degrees of freedom\, but no chemical reaction occurs\, there is said to be inelastic scattering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03026
name: inert
def: "Stable and unreactive under specified conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03027
name: inert gas
def: "A non-reactive gas under particular conditions. For example\, nitrogen at ordinary temperatures and the noble gases \(helium\, argon\, krypton\, xenon and radon\) are unreactive toward most species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03028
name: inertial defect
def: "Difference of principal moments of inertia \\\(\\mathit\{\\Delta\} = I_\{C\} - I_\{A\} - I_\{B\}\\\). For planar molecules it is close to zero and for non-planar molecules it is negative."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03029
name: inertial separator
def: "Any dry type collector which utilizes the relatively greater inertia of particles to effect their removal from a gas stream; e.g. cyclonic and impingement separators\, gravity settling chambers and high-velocity gas reversal chambers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03030
name: infinite source thickness
def: "For a specified radiation\, the minimum thickness of a flat preparation of a radioactive material where the intensity of the specified radiation at the surface does not increase when the thickness is increased by adding more of similar radioactive material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03032
name: information theory
def: "As applied in reaction dynamics\, information theory analyses the deviations of experimentally-determined energy distributions from predictions made on the basis of equal a priori probabilities for all possible outcomes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03033
name: inherent viscosity
def: "The ratio of the natural logarithm of the relative viscosity\, \\\(\\eta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\)\, to the mass concentration of the polymer\, \\\(c\\\)\, i.e. \\\[\\eta _\{\\rm\{inh\}\}\\equiv \\eta _\{\\ln\} = \\frac\{\\ln \\eta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}\{c\}\\\] The quantity \\\(\\eta _\{\\ln \}\\\)\, with which the inherent viscosity is synonymous\, is the logarithmic viscosity number."
synonym: "logarithmic viscosity number" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03034
name: inhibition
def: "The decrease in rate of reaction brought about by the addition of a substance inhibitor\)\, by virtue of its effect on the concentration of a reactant\, catalyst or reaction intermediate. For example\, molecular oxygen and p-benzoquinone can react as 'inhibitors' in many reactions involving radicals as intermediates by virtue of their ability to act as scavengers toward these radicals. If the rate of a reaction in the absence of inhibitor is \\\(v_\{0\}\\\) and that in the presence of a certain amount of inhibitor is \\\(v\\\)\, the degree of inhibition \(\\\(i\\\)\) is given by\: \\\[i=\\frac\{v_\{0\}- v\}\{v_\{0\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03035
name: inhibitor
def: "A substance that diminishes the rate of a chemical reaction; the process is called inhibition. Inhibitors are sometimes called negative catalysts\, but since the action of an inhibitor is fundamentally different from that of a catalyst\, this terminology is discouraged. In contrast to a catalyst\, an inhibitor may be consumed during the course of a reaction. In enzyme-catalysed reactions an inhibitor frequently acts by binding to the enzyme\, in which case it may be called an enzyme inhibitor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03036
name: inhibitory concentration
def: "Concentration of a substance that causes a defined inhibition of a given system\: \\\(\\rm\{IC\}_\{50\}\\\) is the median concentration that causes 50% inhibition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03037
name: inhibitory dose
def: "Dose of a substance that causes a defined inhibition of a given system\: \\\(\\rm\{ID\}_\{50\}\\\) is the median dose that causes 50% inhibition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03038
name: inhomogeneity error
def: "An error due to inhomogenous distribution of absorbing species in the analytical sample. It may be observed by probing the sample volume with an incident beam of radiation of reduced cross-section."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03040
name: initial rate method
def: "A mode of measurement in a kinetic method of analysis\, in which the initial reaction rate is determined \(usually by the slope method\) and utilized for the measurement of concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03041
name: initial state correlations
def: "These are correlations between the states of the products of a reaction and the initial states of the reactants. The correlations may relate to energy levels\, quantum numbers\, and symmetries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03042
name: initiation
def: "A reaction or process generating free radicals \(or some other reactive reaction intermediates\) which then induce a chain reaction. For example\, in the chlorination of alkanes by a radical mechanism the initiation step is the dissociation of molecular chlorine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03043
name: initiator
def: "A substance introduced into a reaction system in order to bring about an initiation reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03044
name: injection temperature
def: "The temperature at the point of injection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03045
name: in-laboratory processing
def: "The selection\, removal and preparation of the test \(or analytical\) portions from the laboratory sample. The processing may include a reduction in the size of the unit\(s\) \(division\) and particle size \(reduction\)\, as well as mixing to achieve homogeneity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03046
name: inner electric potential
def: "Quantity the gradient of which is equal to the negative of the electric field strength within the phase concerned. It can be calculated from the model of the phase. It is sometimes called the Galvani potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03047
name: inner filter effect
def: "In an emission experiment\, an apparent decrease in emission quantum yield and/or distortion of bandshape as a result of reabsorption of emitted radiation.During a light irradiation experiment\, absorption of incident radiation by a species other than the intended primary absorber."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03048
name: inner Helmholtz plane
def: "The locus of the electrical centres of specifically adsorbed ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03049
name: inner layer
def: "The region between the outer Helmholtz plane and the interface."
synonym: "compact layer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03050
name: inner orbital X-ray emission spectra
def: "X-ray spectral lines generated by the transition of electrons between 'inner orbitals'. The term 'inner orbital' serves to designate the sharp\, not degenerate electronic levels of atoms in a solid and therefore is a general term for all orbitals except the valence band."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03052
name: inner-sphere electron transfer
def: "Historically an electron transfer between two metal centres sharing a ligand or atom in their respective coordination shells. The definition has more recently been extended to any situation in which the interaction between the donor and acceptor centres in the transition state is significant \(\\\(\\pu\{\\gt 20 kJ mol-1\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03053
name: inoculation
def: "The introduction of a small sample of organisms \(microbial\, plant or animal cells\) into a culture medium to act as a seed for the production of large numbers of the same organism by growth and propagation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03054
name: inositols
def: "Cyclohexane-1\,2\,3\,4\,5\,6-hexols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03055
name: in-out isomerism
def: "Isomerism found in bicyclic systems having long enough bridges to allow the bridgehead exocyclic bond or lone pair of electrons to point either inside the structure or outside."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03056
name: input rate
def: "The number of samples that are processed by the instrument divided by time of operation. It should be stated if the residence time is included or not."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03057
name: insert
def: "A sequence of foreign DNA introduced into a restriction site \(insertion site\, cloning site\) of a vector DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03058
name: insertion
def: "A chemical reaction or transformation of the general type\: $$\\ce\{X-Z + Y -> X-Y-Z\}$$ in which the connecting atom or group $\\ce\{Y\}$ replaces the bond joining the parts $\\ce\{X\}$ and $\\ce\{Z\}$ of the reactant $\\ce\{XZ\}$. An example is the carbene insertion reaction\:  The reverse of an insertion is called an extrusion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03059
name: <i>in situ</i> microanalysis
def: "Term used for direct analytical investigation of the microstructural domains of a solid by focused beams of particles and radiation. Analytical characterization includes obtaining information about type\, quantity and distribution of the elements\, their state of chemical bonding\, morphology and crystalline \(geometric\) and electronic structure of the individual phases. The combination of this information serves as a basis for the property-relevant characterization of solids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03060
name: <i>in situ</i> micro-X-ray diffraction
def: "Any technique which utilizes the diffraction of X-rays generated in a microstructural domain of a solid under bombardment with a finely focused electron beam\, thus providing an X-ray diffraction pattern of this microstructural domain. The pattern can be recorded with a film either on the reflection or transmission side of the specimen \(in the latter case the crystalline sample has to be a thin film or a small particle\)."
synonym: "Kossel-technique" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03061
name: instability
def: "A change which takes place in an instrument reading over a stated period of unattended operation for a given value of the air quality characteristic. It can be characterized by the variation with time of its mean\, specifying the drift and by the dispersion. Span instability is the change which takes place in instrument span over a stated period of unattended operation. Zero instability is the change in instrument reading in response to a zero sample over a stated period of unattended operation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03062
name: instantaneous current
def: "At a dropping electrode\, the total current that flows at the instant when a time \\\(t\\\) has elapsed since the fall of the preceding drop. At any other electrode\, the total current that flows at the instant when a time \\\(t\\\) has elapsed since the beginning of an electrolysis. The instantaneous current is usually time-dependent and may have the character of an adsorption\, catalytic\, diffusion\, double-layer\, or kinetic current\, and may include a migration current. A plot of the dependence of instantaneous current on time is commonly called an 'i-t curve'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03063
name: instantaneous rate of flow
def: "The rate of increase of the mass of a drop at a particular instant \\\(t\\\) seconds after it has begun to form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03064
name: instantaneous sampling
def: "Obtaining a sample of the atmosphere in a period which is short compared with the duration of the sampling exercise. Such samples are often called 'grab' samples\, a term not recommended. These are useful for the analysis of hydrocarbons and other complicated mixtures of trace gases which are relatively stable in a stainless steel canister or tank and can be transported back to the laboratory for chromatographic or other analysis which cannot be done satisfactorily in the field."
synonym: "spot sampling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03065
name: instrumental activation analysis
def: "A kind of activation analysis in which element specificity is obtained by using appropriate irradiation conditions\, radiation measurement techniques and mathematical techniques for the interpretation of the measurement results."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03066
name: instrumental dependability
def: "This relates to the frequency of failures which interrupt the operation of an instrument. It can be quantified\, e.g. by the infrequency of breakdowns or by the availability of the instrument for use when required."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03067
name: instrumental indication
def: "The observed deflection or rest point multiplied by the value of the division for the load in question."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03068
name: integral capacitance
def: "Integral capacitance \(per unit area of electrode\) is given by \\\[K=\\frac\{Q\}\{E- E_\{Q=0\}\}\\\] where \\\(Q\\\) is the electric charge \(per unit area of electrode\)\, \\\(E\\\) is the potential of the electrode with respect to a reference electrode\, and \\\(E_\{Q=0\}\\\) is the potential at the point of zero charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03069
name: integral detector
def: "A device which measures the accumulated quantity of sample component\(s\) reaching the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03070
name: integrating sphere
def: "A hollow sphere having a highly reflecting inside surface used as a device to collect\, with very high efficiency\, light scattered or emitted from a sample contained in it or located outside and near one of the ports. Small ports allow the entrance of light and access to a detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03071
name: intended crossing
def: "Synonymous with avoided crossing. The term 'intended' should not be used in this context since it is an anthropomorphic term."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03072
name: intensity
def: "The traditional term for photon flux\, fluence rate\, irradiance or radiant power \(radiant flux\). In terms of an object exposed to radiation\, the term should now be used only for qualitative descriptions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03073
name: intensity
def: "The ratio of intensity of a separated ion beam to the intensity of the separated ion beam which has the greatest intensity. This ratio is generally equated to the normalized ratio of the heights of the respective peaks in the mass spectrum\, with the height of the base peak being taken as 100."
synonym: "intensity relative to base peak" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03074
name: intensive quantity
def: "Physical quantity whose magnitude is independent of the extent of the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03075
name: interaction distance
def: "The farthest distance of approach of two particles at which it is discernible that they will not pass at the impact parameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03076
name: intercalation compounds
def: "Compounds resulting from reversible inclusion\, without covalent bonding\, of one kind of molecule in a solid matrix of another compound\, which has a laminar structure. The host compound\, a solid\, may be macromolecular\, crystalline or amorphous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03077
name: intercalation reaction
def: "Reaction\, generally reversible\, that involves the penetration of a host material by guest species without causing a major structural modification of the host."
synonym: "insertion reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03080
name: interconal region
def: "The region of a Bunsen flame where the combustion zone has the form of a cone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03081
name: interconvertible enzyme
def: "An enzyme existing in at least two well defined\, reversibly convertible forms\, produced by covalent modifications of amino acid side chains under biological conditions. Covalent modifications that occur as intermediates in the catalytic process are not included in this definition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03082
name: interface
def: "The plane ideally marking the boundary between two phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03083
name: interfacial concentration
def: "The interfacial concentration \\\(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\,\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) \(or simply \\\(c_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\) of a species B is the concentration of that species at the boundary of the electrical double-layer facing the solution\, i.e. just outside the region where the departures from the electroneutrality of the solution are significant. This concept is mainly used in the usual case where the thickness of the electric double layer is very small as compared to that of the diffusion layer. It is often calculated from theory or derived from measurements of the limiting current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03084
name: interfacial double-layer
def: "The Coulombic interaction of interfacial charges \(e.g. ions\) and the magnetic or electrostatic interaction of interfacial molecules lead to particularly complex interfacial structures. Complex interfacial profiles that can be approximated by two distinct sub-layers with different physical properties \(e.g. structure and/or nature and/or composition\)\, are referred to as interfacial double-layers. Examples of such approximated complex profiles are\: the electrical double-layer consisting of a surface charge layer \(i.e. a two dimensional distribution of one type of ions\) and a diffuse charge layer \(counter-ions distributed over the space region next to the surface\); the approximated profile of the orientation angle of anisotropic liquid molecules within a 'double-layer' consisting of a distribution of so-called anchored molecules which are perturbed \(strongly bound and orientated\) by the surface\, and the adjacent\, so-called\, transition layer\, i.e. the region where the surface perturbation is damped."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03085
name: interfacial layer
def: "The inhomogeneous space region intermediate between two bulk phases in contact\, and where properties are significantly different from\, but related to\, the properties of the bulk phases. Examples of such properties are\: compositions\, molecular density\, orientation or conformation\, charge density\, pressure tensor\, electron density\, etc. The interfacial properties vary in the direction normal to the surface. Complex profiles of interfacial properties occur in the case of multicomponent systems with coexisting bulk phases where attractive/repulsive molecular interactions involve adsorption or depletion of one or several components. This interfacial region may also be regarded as a distinct\, though not autonomous\, phase and be called the interphase."
synonym: "interfacial region" []
synonym: "interphase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03086
name: interfacial layer width
def: "The characteristic length defined by the variance of the Gaussian function fitted to the gradient of the electron density profile established by X-ray specular reflectivity studies of the liquid interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03089
name: interference
def: "A systematic error in the measure of a signal caused by the presence of concomitants in a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03092
name: interfering lines
def: "Radiation from nearby spectral lines may perturb the measurement of the intensity of the line wanted. These lines are termed interfering lines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03093
name: interfering substance
def: "Any substance\, other than the ion being measured\, whose presence in the sample solution affects the measured emf of a cell. Interfering substances fall into two classes\: 'electrode/electrochemical' interferences and 'chemical' interferences. Examples of the first class include\:Those substances which give a similar response to the ion being measured and whose presence generally results in an apparent increase in the activity \(or concentration\) of the ion to be determined \(e.g. $\\ce\{Na^\{+\}\}$ for the $\\ce\{Ca^\{2+\}\}$ electrode\).Electrolytes present at a high concentration that give rise to appreciable liquid junction potential differences or results in a significant activity coefficient decrease\, or incipient Donnan exclusion failure. The second class of substances that should be recognized as chemical interferences includes\: Species that interact with the ion being measured so as to decrease its activity or apparent concentration. The electrode continues to report the true activity \(e.g. $\\ce\{CN^\{−\}\}$ present in the measurement of $\\ce\{Ag^\{+\}\}$\)\, but a considerable gap will occur between the activity and concentration of the ions even in very dilute solutions. Under these circumstances the determination of ionic concentration may be problematic. Substances interacting with the membrane itself\, blocking the surface or changing its chemical composition \[i.e. organic solvents for the liquid or poly\(vinyl chloride\) \(PVC\) membrane electrodes\] are grouped as interferences or electrode poisons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03095
name: interferons
def: "A class of glycoproteins \(with sugar groups attached at specific locations\) important in immune function. They are able to inhibit the multiplication of viruses in cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03096
name: intermediate
def: "A molecular entity with a lifetime appreciably longer than a molecular vibration \(corresponding to a local potential energy minimum of depth greater than \\\(R\\ T\\\)\) that is formed \(directly or indirectly\) from the reactants and reacts further to give \(either directly or indirectly\) the products of a chemical reaction; also the corresponding chemical species."
synonym: "reaction intermediate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03097
name: intermediate neutrons
def: "Neutrons of kinetic energy between the energies of slow and fast neutrons. In reactor physics\, the range might be \\\(1\\ \\rm\{eV\}\\\) to \\\(0.1\\ \\rm\{MeV\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03098
name: intermolecular
def: "Descriptive of any process that involves a transfer \(of atoms\, groups\, electrons\, etc.\) or interactions between two or more molecular entities.Relating to a comparison between different molecular entities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03100
name: internal absorptance
def: "Absorptance when surface effects and effects of the cuvette such as reflection losses are excluded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03102
name: internal conversion
def: "A photophysical process. An isoenenergetic radiationless transition between two electronic states of the same multiplicity. When the transition results in a vibrationally excited molecular entity in the lower electronic state\, this usually undergoes deactivation to its lowest vibrational level\, provided the final state is not unstable to dissociation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03103
name: internal energy
def: "Quantity the change in which is equal to the sum of heat\, \\\(q\\\)\, brought to the system and work\, \\\(w\\\)\, performed on it\, \\\(\\Delta U=q+w\\\). Also called thermodynamic energy."
synonym: "thermodynamic energy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03105
name: internal filling solution
def: "Aqueous electrolyte solution\, which may be gelled\, containing fixed concentration of hydrogen ions\, e.g. $\\ce\{HCl\}$ or a buffer solution\, and a fixed concentration of the ion to which the internal reference electrode is reversible\, e.g. chloride ion in the case of silver-silver chloride\, or calomel electrodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03106
name: internal reference electrode
def: "An 'inner' reference electrode which is contained inside an ion-selective electrode assembly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03108
name: internal standard
def: "A compound added to a sample in known concentration to facilitate the qualitative indentification and/or quantitative determination of the sample components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03109
name: internal surface
def: "With respect to porous solids\, the surface associated with pores communicating with the outside space. Since the accessibility of pores may depend on the size of the fluid molecules\, the extent of the accessible internal surface may depend on the size of the molecules comprising the fluid\, and may be different for the various components of a fluid mixture \(molecular sieve effect\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03111
name: internal valence force field
def: "A force field expressed in terms of bond-stretching\, angle-bending\, torsional and other displacements directly connected to the structural parameters of the molecule\: \\\[V=\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \\sum k_\{ij\}\\ R_\{i\}\\ R_\{j\}\\\] or \\\[V=\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \\sum K_\{ij\}\\ R_\{i\}\\ R_\{j\}\\\] where \\\(k\\\) \(or \\\(K\\\)\) are the force constants and \\\(R\\\) the internal valence coordinates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03114
name: international unit
def: "The unit of enzyme activity proposed by the International Union of Biochemistry in 1964. Specifically\, it is the amount of enzyme that catalyses the conversion of one micromole of substrate per minute under the specified conditions of the assay method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03115
name: interparticle porosity
def: "The interparticle volume of a packed column per unit column volume\: \\\[\\varepsilon = \\frac\{V_\{0\}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\}\\\] It is also called the interstitial fraction of the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03116
name: interparticle volume of the column
def: "The volume occupied by the mobile phase between the particles in the packed section of a column. It is also called the interstitial volume or the void volume of the column. In liquid chromatography\, the interparticle volume is equal to the mobile-phase hold-up volume \(\\\(V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\)\) in the ideal case\, neglecting any extra-column volume. In gas chromatography\, the symbol \\\(V_\{\\rm\{G\}\}\\\) may be used for the interparticle volume of the column. In the ideal case\, neglecting any extra-column volume\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{G\}\}\\\) is equal to the corrected gas hold-up volume \(\\\(V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\)\). \\\[V_\{\\rm\{G\}\}=V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}=V_\{\\rm\{M\}j\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03117
name: interpenetrating polymer network
def: "Polymer comprising two or more networks that are at least partially interlaced on a molecular scale but not covalently bonded to each other and cannot be separated unless chemical bonds are broken."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03119
name: interphase transition
def: "A transition that occurs at boundaries between phases. Example\: The precipitation of second phases or the initiation of new phase growth at an interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03120
name: interstitial fraction
def: "The interstitial volume per unit volume of a packed column. Defined by\: \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{I\}\}=\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{I\}\}\}\{X\}\\\) where \\\(V_\{\\rm\{I\}\}\\\) is the interstitial volume and \\\(X\\\) the column volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03121
name: interstitial velocity
def: "The linear velocity of the mobile phase inside a packed column calculated as the average over the entire cross section. This quantity can\, under idealized conditions\, be calculated from the equation\: \\\[u=\\frac\{F\}\{\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{I\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(F\\\) is the nominal linear flow and \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{I\}\}\\\) the interstitial fraction"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03122
name: interstitial volume
def: "The volume \\\(V_\{\\rm\{G\}\}\\\) of the column not occupied by the liquid phase and its solid support\, or by the active solid. It does not include any volume external to the column\, such as the volume of the sample injector or of the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03123
name: intersystem crossing
def: "A photophysical process. An isoenergetic radiationless transition between two electronic states having different multiplicities. It often results in a vibrationally excited molecular entity in the lower electronic state\, which then usually deactivates to its lowest vibrational level."
synonym: "state crossing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03124
name: interval analysis
def: "A method to estimate uncertainties in the results of a least-squares procedure. The input data are primary values plus limits of intervals expressing their uncertainty. The output consists of derived values plus limits of intervals expressing a pessimistic estimate of maximal uncertainty."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03125
name: intervalence charge transfer
def: "Electron transfer \(thermal or photoinduced\) between two metal sites differing only in oxidation state. Quite often such electron transfer reverses the oxidation states of the sites. The term is frequently extended to the case of metal-to-metal charge transfer between non-equivalent metal centres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03126
name: interzonal region
def: "The region of the Bunsen flame confined by the inner and outer zones\, where in many instances the conditions for flame analysis are optimum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03128
name: intra-
def: "The prefix for an elementary reaction or some part thereof indicating that the bond\(s\) undergoing primitive changes form part of a ring in the transition state of the elementary reaction. This prefix is used for nonpericyclic processes only and is replaced by 'cyclo' for pericyclic processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03130
name: intramolecular
def: "Descriptive of any process that involves a transfer \(of atoms\, groups\, electrons\, etc.\) or interactions between different parts of the same molecular entity.Relating to a comparison between atoms or groups within the same molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03131
name: intramolecular catalysis
def: "The acceleration of a chemical transformation at one site of a molecular entity through the involvement of another functional \('catalytic'\) group in the same molecular entity\, without that group appearing to have undergone change in the reaction product. The use of the term should be restricted to cases for which analogous intermolecular catalysis by chemical species bearing that catalytic group is observable. intramolecular catalysis can be detected and expressed in quantitative form by a comparison of the reaction rate with that of a comparable model compound in which the catalytic group is absent\, or by measurement of the effective molarity of the catalytic group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03132
name: intramolecular isotope effect
def: "A kinetic isotopic effect observed when a single substrate\, in which the isotopic atoms occupy equivalent reactive positions\, reacts to produce a non-statistical distribution of isotopomeric products. In such a case the isotope effect will favour the pathway with lower force constants for displacement of the isotopic nuclei in the transition state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03133
name: intraphase transition
def: "A transition that occurs within a phase. Example\: initiation of phase growth at an intragranular feature\, such as twin planes and dislocations in steels."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03134
name: intrinsic activation energy
def: "If \\\(E_\{\\rm\{a\,1\}\}\\\) and \\\(E_\{\\rm\{a\,- 1\}\}\\\) are the activation energies for a reaction in forward and reverse directions\, the lesser of the two is sometimes called the intrinsic activation energy. It is the activation energy for the reaction in the exothermic direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03135
name: intrinsic barrier
def: "The Gibbs energy of activation \(\\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}G\\\)\) in the limiting case where \\\(\\Delta G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} = 0\\\)\, i.e. when the effect of thermodynamic driving force is eliminated. According to the Marcus equation\, the intrinsic barrier is related to the reorganization energy\, \\\(\\lambda \\\)\, of the reaction by the equation\: \\\[\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}G = \\frac\{\\lambda \}\{4\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03136
name: intrinsic detector efficiency
def: "The ratio of the number of particles or photons detected to the number of similar particles or photons which have struck the envelope limiting the sensitive volume of a radiation detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03138
name: intrinsic full energy peak efficiency
def: "The detector efficiency when considering only events where the total energy of the radiation is absorbed in the sensitive volume of the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03139
name: intrinsic photopeak efficiency
def: "The detector efficiency when only counts resulting from the photoelectrical effect are considered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03140
name: intrinsic viscosity
def: "The limiting value of the reduced viscosity\, \\\(\\frac\{\\eta _\{\\rm\{i\}\}\}\{c\}\\\) or the inherent viscosity\, \\\(\\eta _\{\\rm\{inh\}\}\\\)\, at infinite dilution of the polymer\, i.e. \\\[\[\\eta\] = \\lim _\{c\\rightarrow 0\}\\frac\{\\eta _\{\\rm\{i\}\}\}\{c\}=\\lim _\{c\\rightarrow 0\}\\eta _\{\\rm\{inh\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "limiting viscosity number" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03141
name: intron
def: "An intervening section of DNA occuring almost exclusively within a eukaryotic gene but which is not translated to amino acid sequences in the gene product. The introns are removed from the premature mRNA through a process called splicing to form an active mRNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03142
name: inverse isotope effect
def: "A kinetic isotope effect in which \\\(\\frac\{k^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{k^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\}1\\\). The isotope effect will normally be 'normal' when the frequency differences between the isotopic transition state are smaller than in the reactants. Conversely\, an inverse isotope effect can be taken as evidence for an increase in the corresponding force constants on passing from the reactant to the transition state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03145
name: inverse square law
def: "The intensity of radiation from a point source in free space is inversely proportional to the square of the distance from the source. Presence of absorbers necessitates corrections to this law."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03146
name: inversion
def: "A symmetry operation involving a centre of inversion \(\\\(i\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03147
name: inversion height
def: "The height above ground level at which there is change in sign of the normal temperature 'lapse rate'\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}T\}\{\\rm\{d\}z\}\\\) \(the rate of change of the temperature with height\). Several temperature inversions may be present in the air over a given site at different altitudes as result of various meteorological factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03149
name: inversion point
def: "The temperature \(pressure\) at which one polymorph of a solid phase changes into another at constant pressure \(temperature\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03151
name: inverted micelle
def: "The reversible formation of association colloids from surfactants in non-polar solvents leads to aggregates termed inverted \(or inverse\, reverse or reversed\) micelle. Such association is often of the type\: \\\[\\rm\{Monomer\}\\rightleftarrows \\rm\{Dimer\}\\rightleftarrows \\rm\{Trimer\}\\rightleftarrows \\rm\{ ... \}n\\rm\{-mer\}\\\] and the phenomenon of critical micelle concentration \(or an analogous effect\) is consequently not observed. In an inverted micelle the polar groups of the surfactants are concentrated in the interior and the lipophilic groups extend towards and into the non-polar solvent."
synonym: "inverse micelle" []
synonym: "reverse micelle (or reversed micelle)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03152
name: inverted region
def: "In plots relating rate constants to charges in standard Gibbs energy \(\\\(\\Delta G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\) for electron transfer a region may occur in which the rate constants decrease as the exergonicity of the reaction increases. This region is often referred to as the inverted region and its presence is predicted by the theory developed for outer sphere electron transfer for the case \\\(-\\Delta G^\{\\circ\} \\gt \\lambda \\\) in the Marcus equation\, \\\(\\lambda\\\) being the reorganization energy. Note the similarity to the energy gap law for radiationless conversion of an excited state."
synonym: "Marcus inverted region" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03153
name: <i>in vitro</i>
def: "In glass\, referring to a study in the laboratory usually involving isolated organ\, tissue\, cell\, or biochemical systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03154
name: <i>in vivo</i>
def: "In the living body\, referring to a study performed on a living organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03158
name: ion
def: "An atomic or molecular particle having a net electric charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03161
name: ion collector
def: "Device for the capture of selected ions such as a Faraday cup collector \(with a d.c. amplifier\) or an electron multiplier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03162
name: ion cyclotron resonance mass spectrometer
def: "A high-frequency mass spectrometer in which the ions to be detected\, with a selected value of the quotient mass/charge\, absorb maximum energy through the effect of a high-frequency electric field and a constant magnetic field perpendicular to the electric field. Maximum energy is gained by the ions which satisfy the cyclotron resonance condition and as a result they are separated from ions of different mass/charge."
synonym: "ICR mass spectrometer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03165
name: ion energy loss spectra
def: "Spectra that show the loss of translational energy of ions involved in ion/neutral species reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03167
name: ion exchange
def: "In surface chemistry\, if the adsorption of one or several ionic species is accompanied by the simultaneous desorption \(displacement\) of an equivalent amount of one or more other ionic species\, this process is called ion exchange."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03168
name: ion-exchange chromatography
def: "Chromatography in which separation is based mainly on differences in the ion-exchange affinities of the sample components. Present day ion-exchange chromatography on small particle high efficiency columns\, and usually utilizing conductometric or spectroscopic detectors\, is often referred to as ion chromatography \(IC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03169
name: ion-exchange isotherm
def: "The concentration of a counter-ion in the ion exchanger expressed as a function of its concentration in the external solution under specified conditions and at constant temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03171
name: ion exchanger
def: "A solid or liquid\, inorganic or organic substance containing exchangeable ions with others of the same charge\, present in a solution in which the ion exchanger is considered to be insoluble. It is recognized that there are cases where liquid exchangers are employed and where it may be difficult to distinguish between the separation process as belonging to ion exchange or liquid-liquid distribution\, but the broad definition given here is regarded as that which is most appropriate. A monofunctional ion exchanger contains only one type of ionogenic group\, a bifunctional ion exchanger two types and a polyfunctional ion exchanger more than one type. In a macroporous ion exchanger the pores are large compared to atomic dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03173
name: ion-free layer
def: "An interphase in which only non-specifically adsorbed ions are present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03175
name: ionic conductivity
def: "Defined for ionic species $\\ce\{B\}$ by \\\(\\lambda = |z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}|\\ F\\ u_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) where \\\(z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the charge number of the ionic species $\\ce\{B\}$\, \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant\, and \\\(u_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the electric mobility of species $\\ce\{B\}$. In most current practice \\\(z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is taken as unity\, i.e. ionic conductivity is taken as that of species such as $\\ce\{Na^\{+\}\}$\, $\\ce\{Ca^\{2+\}\}$/2\, $\\ce\{La^\{3+\}\}$/3 etc. To avoid ambiguity the species considered should be clearly stated\, e.g. as \\\(\\lambda \(\\rm\{Ca\}^\{\\rm\{2+\}\}/2\)\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03176
name: ionic copolymerization
def: "A copolymerization which is an ionic polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03177
name: ionic dissociation
def: "The decomposition of an ion into another ion of lower mass and one or more neutral species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03178
name: ionic polymerization
def: "A chain polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are ions or ion pairs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03180
name: ionic strength
def: "On a molality basis\, \\\[I_\{m\} = \\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \\sum m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{2\}\\\] where the sum goes over all the ions B. \\\(z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the charge number of ion B. The ionic strength on a concentration basis is defined analogously \\\[I_\{c\} = \\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \\sum c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{2\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03181
name: ionic transport number
def: "Quotient of the current carried by an ionic component and the total current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03183
name: ionization
def: "The generation of one or more ions. It may occur\, e.g. by loss of an electron from a neutral molecular entity\, by the unimolecular heterolysis of such an entity into two or more ions\, or by a heterolytic substitution reaction involving neutral molecules\, such as\:    The loss of an electron from a singly\, doubly\, etc. charged cation is called second\, third\, etc. ionization. This terminology is used especially in mass spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03188
name: ionization buffer
def: "A buffer added to increase the free-electron concentration by the flame gases\, thus repressing and stabilizing the degree of ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03189
name: ionization by sputtering
def: "Ionization by bombardment of a solid specimen with accelerated ions or electrons or fast neutrals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03190
name: ionization chamber
def: "A radiation detector which employs an electric field for the collection at the electrodes of charges associated with the ions produced in the sensitive volume by ionizing radiation\, without charge multiplication. The solid angle \($\\ce\{2\\Uppi\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{4\\uppi\\!\}$\) subtended when using the detector\, is sometimes specified."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03194
name: ionization cross-section
def: "A measure of the probability that a given ionization process will occur when an atom or molecule interacts with an electron or a photon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03196
name: ionization efficiency
def: "The ratio of the number of ions formed to the number of electrons or photons used in an ionization process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03197
name: ionization efficiency curve
def: "The number of ions produced as a function of the energy of the electrons or photons used to produce ionization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03199
name: ionization energy
def: "Minimum energy required to eject an electron out of a neutral atom or molecule in its ground state. The adiabatic ionization energy refers to the formation of the molecular ion in its ground vibrational state and the vertical ionization energy applies to the transition to the molecular ion without change in geometry. This quantity was formerly called 'ionization potential'. The second ionization energy of an atom is the energy required to eject the second electron from the atom \(energy for the process $\\ce\{X^\{+\} -> X^\{2+\} + e^\{-\}\}$\)."
synonym: "ionization potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03214
name: ionizing collision
def: "An ion/neutral species interaction in which an electron or electrons are stripped from the ion and/or the neutral species in the collision. Generally\, this term has come to be used to describe collisions of fast-moving ions with a neutral species in which the neutral species is ionized with no change in the number of charges carried by the ion. Care should be taken when this term is used to emphasize if charge stripping of the ion has taken place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03215
name: ionizing power
def: "A term to denote the tendency of a particular solvent to promote ionization of an uncharged or\, less often\, charged solute. The term has been used both in a kinetic and in a thermodynamic context."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03216
name: ionizing radiation
def: "Any radiation consisting of directly or indirectly ionizing particles or a mixture of both\, or photons with energy higher than the energy of photons of ultraviolet light or a mixture of both such particles and photons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03217
name: ionizing voltage
def: "The voltage difference through which electrons are accelerated before they are used to bring about electron impact ionization. To obtain the true ionizing voltage\, corrections for any contact or surface potentials must be made."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03218
name: ion kinetic energy spectrum
def: "A spectrum obtained when a beam of ions is separated according to the translational energy-to-charge ratios of the ionic species contained within it. A radial electric field \(E\) achieves separation of the various ionic species in this way."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03219
name: ion laser
def: "A gas laser in which the active species is an ion formed in the electric discharge. Most practically important lasers of this type have continuous wave \(CW\) output. Examples are the argon ion\, krypton ion and helium-cadmium \(HeCd\) lasers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03221
name: ion microscopy
def: "Use of the secondary ion mass spectrometry \(SIMS\) technique to obtain micrographs of the elemental \(or isotopic\) distribution at the surface of a sample with a spatial resolution of \\\(2\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) or better."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03223
name: ion/molecule reaction
def: "An ion/neutral species reaction in which the neutral species is a molecule. The use of ion-molecule reaction is not recommended; the hyphen suggests a reaction of a species that is both an ion and a molecule and is not the intended meaning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03225
name: ion/neutral species exchange reaction
def: "In this reaction an association reaction is accompanied by the subsequent or simultaneous liberation of a different neutral species as product."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03226
name: ion/neutral species reaction
def: "A process wherein a charged species interacts with a neutral reactant to produce either chemically different species or changes in the internal energy of one or both of the reactants. The contrasting expression ion/neutral reaction is not ideal\, simply because the word neutral is not a noun. However\, any alternatives such as ion/neutral species reaction are so clumsy as to mitigate against their general acceptance"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03228
name: ionogenic groups
def: "The fixed groupings in an ion exchanger which are either ionized or capable of dissociation into fixed ions and mobile counter-ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03229
name: ionomer
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules in which a small but significant proportion of the constitutional units has ionic or ionizable groups\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03230
name: ionomer molecule
def: "A macromolecule in which a small but significant proportion of the constitutional units have ionizable or ionic groups\, or both. Some protein molecules may be classified as ionomer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03231
name: ion pair
def: "A pair of oppositely charged ions held together by coulomb attraction without formation of a covalent bond. Experimentally\, an ion pair behaves as one unit in determining conductivity\, kinetic behaviour\, osmotic properties\, etc. Following Bjerrum\, oppositely charged ions with their centres closer together than a distance\: \\\[q = \\frac\{8.36\\times 10^\{6\}\\ z^\{+\}\\ z^\{-\}\}\{\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\ T\}\\ \\rm\{pm\}\\\] are considered to constitute an ion pair \('Bjerrum ion pair'\). \[\\\(z^\{+\}\\\) and \\\(z^\{-\}\\\) are the charge numbers of the ions\, and \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) is the relative permittivity \(or dielectric constant\) of the medium.\] An ion pair\, the constituent ions of which are in direct contact \(and not separated by an intervening solvent or other neutral molecule\) is designated as a 'tight ion pair' \(or 'intimate' or 'contact ion pair'\). A tight ion pair of $\\ce\{X^\{+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{Y^\{−\}\}$ is symbolically represented as $\\ce\{X^\{+\}Y^\{−\}\}$. By contrast\, an ion pair whose constituent ions are separated by one or several solvent or other neutral molecules is described as a 'loose ion pair'\, symbolically represented as $\\ce\{X^\{+\}\}\\\,\\|\\\,\\ce\{Y^\{−\}\}$. The members of a loose ion pair can readily interchange with other free or loosely paired ions in the solution. This interchange may be detectable \(e.g. by isotopic labelling\) and thus afford an experimental distinction between tight and loose ion pairs. A further conceptual distinction has sometimes been made between two types of loose ion pairs. In 'solvent-shared ion pairs' the ionic constituents of the pair are separated by only a single solvent molecule\, whereas in 'solvent-separated ion pairs' more than one solvent molecule intervenes. However\, the term 'solvent-separated ion pair' must be used and interpreted with care since it has also widely been used as a less specific term for 'loose' ion pair."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03232
name: ion-pair formation
def: "Involves an ionization process in which a positive fragment ion and a negative fragment ion are among the products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03233
name: ion pair return
def: "The recombination of a pair of ions $\\ce\{R^\{+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{Z^\{−\}\}$ formed from ionization of $\\ce\{RZ\}$. If the ions are paired as a tight ion pair and recombine without prior separation into a loose ion pair this is called 'internal ion-pair return'\:   It is a special case of 'primary geminate recombination'. If the ions are paired as a loose ion pair and form the covalent chemical species via a tight ion pair\, this is called 'external ion-pair return'\:  It is a special case of 'secondary geminate recombination'. When the covalent molecule RZ is reformed without direct evidence of prior partial racemization or without other direct evidence of prior formation of a tight ion pair\, \(e.g. without partial racemization if the group R is suitably chiral\) the internal ion-pair return is sometimes called a 'hidden return'. External \(unimolecular\) ion-pair return is to be distinguished from 'external \(bimolecular\) ion return'\, the \(reversible\) process whereby dissociated ions are converted into loose ion pairs\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03238
name: ion probe microanalysis
def: "Any technique in which the specimen is bombarded by a focused beam of \(primary\) ions \(diameter less than \\\(10\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\) and the \(secondary\) ions ejected from the specimen are detected after passage through a mass spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03243
name: ion scattering spectrometry
def: "Any technique using low energy \(\\\(10\\ \\rm\{keV\}\\\)\) ions in which the bombarding particles scattered by the sample are detected and recorded as a function of energy and/or angle. This technique is used mainly for determining the composition and structure of the first few atomic layers of a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03244
name: ion-selective electrode
def: "An electrochemical sensor\, based on thin films or selective membranes as recognition elements\, and an electrochemical half-cell equivalent to other half-cells of the zeroth \(inert metal in a redox electrolyte\)\, 1st\, 2nd and 3rd kinds. These devices are distinct from systems that involve redox reactions \(electrodes of zeroth\, 1st\, 2nd and 3rd kinds\)\, although they often contain a 2nd kind electrode as the 'inner' or 'internal' reference electrode. The potential difference response has\, as its principal component\, the Gibbs energy change associated with permselective mass transfer \(by ion-exchange\, solvent extraction or some other mechanism\) across a phase boundary. The ion-selective electrode must be used in conjunction with a reference electrode \(i.e. 'outer' or 'external' reference electrode\) to form a complete electrochemical cell. The measured potential differences \(ion-selective electrode vs. outer reference electrode potentials\) are linearly dependent on the logarithm of the activity of a given ion in solution. Comment\: The term 'ion-specific electrode' is not recommended. The term 'specific' implies that the electrode does not respond to additional ions. Since no electrode is truly specific for one ion\, the term 'ion-selective' is recommended as more appropriate. 'Selective ion-sensitive electrode' is a little-used term to describe an ion-selective electrode. 'Principal' or 'primary' ions are those which an electrode is designed to measure. It is never certain that the 'principal' ion is most sensitively measured\, e.g. nitrate ion-selective electrodes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03245
name: ion-selective electrode cell
def: "An ion-selective electrode in conjunction with a reference electrode. Generally\, the cell contains two reference electrodes\, internal and external\, and the thin film or membrane recognition-transduction element. However\, besides this conventional type of cell \(with solution contact on both sides of the membrane\) there are cell arrangements with wire contact to one side of the membrane \(all solid state and coated wire types\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03247
name: ion source
def: "Generally an assembly composed of\: \(i\) an ionization chamber in which a stream of electrons flows from a hot filament across a stream of gas to collector \(the potential between filament and collector is usually between 50 and 70 \\\(\\rm\{V\}\\\)\); \(ii\) a device for the acceleration of these ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03249
name: ion trap mass spectrometer
def: "An arrangement in which ions with a desired range of quotients mass/charge are first made to describe stable paths under the effect of a high-frequency electric quadrupole field\, and are then separated and presented to a detector by adjusting the field so as to selectivity induce path instability according to their respective mass/charge ratios."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03251
name: <i>ipso</i>-attack
def: "The attachment of an entering group to a position in an aromatic compound already carrying a substituent group \(other than hydrogen\). The entering group may displace that substituent group but may also itself be expelled or migrate to a different position in a subsequent step. The term 'ipso-substitution' is not used\, since it is synonymous with substitution. For example\:   where E + is an electrophile and Z is a substituent \(other than hydrogen\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03253
name: iridoids
def: "Cyclic monoterpenoids having the iridane skeleton \(1-isopropyl-2\,3-di-methylcyclopentane\)\, the traditional\, but not undisputed\, numbering of which is shown in the diagram."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03254
name: irradiance
def: "Radiant power\, \\\(P\\\)\, of all wavelengths incident from all upward directions on a small element of surface containing the point under consideration divided by the area of the element. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03255
name: irradiation
def: "Exposure to ionizing radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03256
name: irregular macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule the structure of which essentially comprises the repetition of more than one type of constitutional unit or a macromolecule the structure of which comprises constitutional units not all connected identically with respect to directional sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03257
name: irregular polymer
def: "A polymer composed of irregular macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03258
name: irreversible transition
def: "A transition that changes the state of a system which cannot be readily reversed to restore the system to its original state. Example\: The conversion of $\\ce\{TiO2\}$ \(anatase\) to $\\ce\{TiO2\}$ \(rutile\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03260
name: isobar
def: "Lines on a plot joining points of equal barometric pressure in the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03261
name: isobaric mass-change determination
def: "A technique in which the equilibrium mass of a substance at constant partial pressure of the volatile product\(s\) is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program. The record is the isobaric mass-change curve. The mass should be plotted on the ordinate decreasing downwards and temperature on the abscissa increasing from left to right."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03262
name: isobaric separation
def: "Chromatographic separation carried out using constant inlet and outlet pressure conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03263
name: isobars
def: "Different nuclides of equal mass number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03265
name: isoclinic point
def: "A wavelength\, wavenumber\, or frequency at which the first derivative of an absorption spectrum of a sample does not change upon a chemical reaction or physical change of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03267
name: isocoumarins
def: "Isocoumarin \(1H-isochromen-1-one\) and its derivatives formed by substitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03268
name: isocratic analysis
def: "A procedure in which the composition of the mobile phase remains constant during the elution process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03269
name: isocyanates
def: "The isocyanic acid tautomer\, $\\ce\{HN=C=O\}$\, of cyanic acid\, $\\ce\{HOC≡N\}$ and its hydrocarbyl derivatives RN=C=O."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03270
name: isocyanides
def: "The isomer $\\ce\{HN+\\!#C^\{-\}\}$ of hydrocyanic acid\, $\\ce\{HC#N\}$\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives $\\ce\{RNC\}$ \($\\ce\{RN^\{+\}\\!#C^\{-\}\}$\)."
synonym: "isonitriles" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03271
name: isocyclic compounds
def: "A less preferred synonym for homocyclic compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03272
name: isodesmic reaction
def: "A reaction \(actual or hypothetical\) in which the types of bonds that are made in forming the products are the same as those which are broken in the reactants\, e.g.   Such processes have advantages for theoretical treatment. The Hammett equation as applied to equilibria \[cf. \(a\)\] essentially deals with isodesmic processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03273
name: isodiazenes
def: "Compounds having the structure . These compounds can also be called diazanylidenes or hydrazinylidenes. They have also been called by the trivial name azamines and the incorrectly formed name 1\,1-diazenes. They should not be called 'aminonitrenes'."
synonym: "azamines" []
synonym: "diazanylidenes" []
synonym: "hydrazinylidenes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03274
name: isoelectric
def: "A macro-ion of a polyampholyte \(in particular a protein\) is said to be isoelectric if it exhibits no electrophoresis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03275
name: isoelectric point
def: "The \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\) value at which the net electric charge of an elementary entity is zero. \\\(\\rm\{pI\}\\\) is a commonly used symbol for this kind-of-quantity. It should be replaced by \\\(\\rm\{pH\(I\)\}\\\) because it is a \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\) determined under that particular condition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03276
name: isoelectronic
def: "Two or more molecular entities are described as isoelectronic if they have the same number of valence electrons and the same structure\, i.e. number and connectivity of atoms\, but differ in some of the elements involved. Thus\: $\\ce\{CO\}$\, $\\ce\{N2\}$ and $\\ce\{NO^\{+\}\}$ are isoelectronic. $\\ce\{CH2=C=O\}$ and $\\ce\{CH2=N=N\}$ are isoelectronic. $\\ce\{CH3COCH3\}$ and $\\ce\{CH3N=NCH3\}$ have the same number of electrons\, but have different structures\, hence they are not described as isoelectronic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03277
name: isoemissive point
def: "Wavelength\, wavenumber or frequency at which the total intensity of emission of light by a sample does not change during a chemical reaction or physical change. The terms isolampsic and isostilbic are sometimes used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03278
name: isoentropic
def: "A reaction series is said to be isoentropic if the individual reactions of the series have the same standard entropy of activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03279
name: isoenzyme
def: "One of a group of related enzymes catalysing the same reaction but having different molecular structures and characterized by varying physical\, biochemical and immunological properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03280
name: isoequilibrium relationship
def: "A relationship analogous to the isokinetic relationship but applied to equilibrium data. The equation defining the isoequilibrium temperature \\\(\\beta \\\) is\: \\\[\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}H - \\beta\\ \\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}S = \\rm\{constant\}\\\] where \\\(\\Delta H\\\) and \\\(\\Delta S\\\) are enthalpy and entropy of reaction\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03282
name: isoionic
def: "A macro-ion of a polyampholyte \(in particular a protein\) is said to be isoionic if besides the polyampholyte and $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ or $\\ce\{OH^\{−\}\}$ ions \(in general ions of the solvent\) no other ions are present in the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03283
name: isoionic point
def: "The \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\) value at which the net electric charge of an elementary entity in pure water equals zero."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03284
name: isokinetic line
def: "A line in a given surface connecting points with equal wind speed; also called isotach or isovel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03285
name: isokinetic relationship
def: "When a series of structurally related substrates undergo the same general reaction or when the reaction conditions for a single substrate are changed in a systematic way\, the enthalpies and entropies of activation sometimes satisfy the relation\: \\\[\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H - \\beta \\ \\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}S = \\rm\{constant\}\\\] where the parameter \\\(\\beta \\\) is independent of temperature. This equation \(or some equivalent form\) is said to represent an 'isokinetic relationship'. The temperature \\\(T=\\beta \\\) \(at which all members of a series obeying the isokinetic relationship react at the same rate\) is termed the 'isokinetic temperature'. Supposed isokinetic relationships as established by direct correlation of \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H\\\) with \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}S\\\) are often spurious and the calculated value of \\\(\\beta \\\) is meaningless\, because errors in \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H\\\) lead to compensating errors in \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}S\\\). Satisfactory methods of establishing such relationships have been devised."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03286
name: isokinetic sampling
def: "A technique for collecting airborne particulate in which the sampling device has a collection efficiency of unity for all sizes of particles in sampled air\, regardless of wind velocity and direction of the instrument. The air stream entering the collector has a velocity \(speed and direction\) equal to that of the air in the gas stream just ahead of the sampling port of the collector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03287
name: isolated double bonds
def: "Those bonds which are neither conjugated nor cumulative as in  or the B ring of"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03288
name: isolobal
def: "The term is used to compare molecular fragments with each other and with familiar species from organic chemistry. Two fragments are isolobal if the number\, symmetry properties\, approximate energy and shape of the frontier orbitals and the number of electrons in them are similar."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03289
name: isomer
def: "One of several species \(or molecular entities\) that have the same atomic composition \(molecular formula\) but different line formulae or different stereochemical formulae and hence different physical and/or chemical properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03290
name: isomerases
def: "Enzymes that catalyse intramolecular rearrangements. Isomerases are classified into racemases and epimerases\, cis-trans isomerases\, intramolecular lyases and other isomerases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03291
name: isomeric
def: "Adjective derived from isomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03292
name: isomeric state
def: "A nuclear state having a mean lifetime long enough to be observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03293
name: isomeric transition
def: "A spontaneous transition between two isomeric states of a nucleus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03294
name: isomerism
def: "The relationship between isomers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03295
name: isomerization
def: "A chemical reaction\, the principal product of which is isomeric with the principal reactant. An intramolecular isomerization that involves the breaking or making of bonds is a special case of a molecular rearrangement. Isomerization does not necessarily imply molecular rearrangement \(e.g. in the case of the interconversion of conformational isomers\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03296
name: isomer shift
def: "Measure of the energy difference between the source \(\\\(E_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\) and the absorber \(\\\(E_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\) transition. The measured Doppler velocity shift\, \\\(\\delta \\\)\, is related to the energy difference by \\\[E_\{\\rm\{a\}\}- E_\{\\rm\{s\}\}=\\frac\{\\delta \\ E_\{\\gamma \}\}\{c\}\\\] where \\\(E_\{\\gamma \}\\\) is the Mössbauer gamma energy and \\\(c\\\) is the speed of light in vacuum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03298
name: isometric
def: "Two molecular entities that are superposable or can be made superposable by reflection of one of them in a mirror are isometric; otherwise they are anisometric."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03299
name: isomorphous structures
def: "In the crystalline state\, polymer chains are generally parallel to one another but neighbouring chains of equivalent conformation may differ in chirality and/or orientation. Chains of identical chirality and conformation are isomorphous. Chains of opposite chirality but equivalent conformation are enantiomorphous. For example\, two ...$\\ce\{TG^\{+\}TG^\{+\}TG^\{+\}\}$... helices of isotactic poly\(propylene\) are isomorphous. Isotactic poly\(propylene\) chains of the ...$\\ce\{TG^\{+\}TG^\{+\}TG^\{+\}\}$... and ...$\\ce\{G^\{+\}TG^\{+\}TG^\{+\}T\}$... types are mutually enantiomorphous. With regard to orientation\, consider a repeating side group originating at atom \\\(A_\{1\}^\{i\}\\\)\, the first atom of the side group being \\\(B_\{\\alpha \}^\{i\}\\\). For certain chain symmetries \(helical\, for instance\) the bond vectors \\\(\\overset\{\\rightarrow \}\{\\mathbf\{b\}\}\(A_\{1\}^\{i\}\,B_\{\\alpha \}^\{i\}\)\\\) have the same components \(positive or negative\) \\\(\\frac\{\\overset\{\\rightarrow \}\{\\mathbf\{b\}\}\\cdot \\overset\{\\rightarrow \}\{\\mathbf\{c\}\}\}\{|\\overset\{\\rightarrow \}\{\\mathbf\{c\}\}|\}\\\) along the c axis for every \\\(i\\\).  Two equivalent \(isomorphous or enantiomorphous\) chains in the crystal lattice\, having identical components of the bond vectors along $c$\, both positive or both negative\, are designated isoclined; two equivalent chains having bond vectors along $c$ of the same magnitude but opposite sign are designated anticlined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03302
name: isooptoacoustic point
def: "Wavelength\, wavenumber or frequency at which the total energy emitted by a sample as heat does not change upon a chemical reaction in or a physical change of the sample. Its position depends on the experimental conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03304
name: isopotential point
def: "For an ion-selective electrode cell\, there is often a particular activity of the measured ion for which the emf of the cell is independent of temperature. That activity\, and the corresponding potential difference\, defines the isopotential point. The specification of the ion-selective electrode and outer reference electrode must be described. Comment\: When an isothermal cell is used with identical reference electrodes\, the isopotential point is the activity of sensed species that gives zero net membrane potential\, e.g. sensed activity is the same in the inner and outer \(test\) solution. Calibration lines for different cell temperatures have different slopes\, but intersect at a common activity point. Cells with temperature gradients are not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03306
name: isoprenoids
def: "Compounds formally derived from isoprene \(2-methylbuta-1\,3-diene\)\, the skeleton of which can generally be discerned in repeated occurrence in the molecule. The skeleton of isoprenoids may differ from strict additivity of isoprene units by loss or shift of a fragment\, commonly a methyl group. The class includes both hydrocarbons and oxygenated derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03307
name: isopycnic
def: "An adjective describing components of a multicomponent system with equal partial specific volumes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03308
name: isopycnic separation
def: "Chromatographic separations carried out using constant density conditions. The temperature and pressure may be altered during the run \(originally the term 'isoconfertic separation' was used but this term is not recommended\)."
synonym: "isoconfertic separation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03309
name: isorefractive
def: "An adjective describing components of a multicomponent system having zero refractive index increments with respect to each other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03310
name: isosbestic point
def: "Wavelength\, wavenumber or frequency at which the total absorbance of a sample does not change during a chemical reaction or a physical change of the sample."
synonym: "isoabsorption point" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03311
name: isoselective relationship
def: "A relationship analogous to the isokinetic relationship\, but applied to selectivity data of reactions. At the isoselective temperature\, the selectivities of the series of reactions following the relationship are identical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03312
name: isoselenocyanates
def: "Selenium analogues of isocyanates $\\ce\{RN=C=Se\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03313
name: isosteric enthalpy of adsorption
def: "When the addition of the differential amount of component \\\(i\\\) \\\(\\mathrm\{d\}n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\\\) or \\\(\\mathrm\{d\}n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is effected at constant pressure \\\(p\\\)\, the differential molar enthalpy of adsorption\, \\\(\\Delta _\{a\}H_\{i\}^\{\\sigma\}\\\) or \\\(\\Delta _\{a\}H_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) also called the isosteric enthalpy of adsorption \(\\\(q^\{\\rm\{st\}\}\\\)\) is defined as\: \\\[\\Delta _\{a\}H_\{i\}^\{\\sigma\} = -q^\{\\rm\{st\}\,\\sigma\} = U_\{i\}^\{\\sigma\} - H_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\] \\\[\\Delta _\{a\}H_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\} = -q^\{\\rm\{st\}\,\\sigma\} = H_\{i\}^\{\\sigma\} - H_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\] where \\\(H_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial H^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\)_\{T\,p\,m\,n_\{j\}^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\\\) and \\\(H_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) is the partial molar enthalpy of component \\\(i\\\) in the gas phase\, i.e. \\\(\(\\frac\{\\partial H^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\}\)_\{T\,p\,n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{g\}\}\}\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03314
name: isostilbic point
def: "Same as isoemissive point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03315
name: isotactic macromolecule
def: "A tactic macromolecule\, essentially comprising only one species of configurational base unit\, which has chiral or prochiral atoms in the main chain in a unique arrangement with respect to its adjacent constitutional units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03316
name: isotactic polymer
def: "A polymer composed of isotactic macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03318
name: isotherm
def: "Lines joining points of equal temperature in the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03319
name: isothermal chromatography
def: "A procedure in which the temperature of the column is kept constant during the separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03320
name: isothiocyanates
def: "Sulfur analogues of isocyanates $\\ce\{RN=C=S\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03321
name: isotones
def: "Nuclides having the same neutron number but different atomic numbers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03322
name: isotope dilution
def: "Mixing of a given nuclide with one or more of its isotopes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03323
name: isotope dilution analysis
def: "A kind of quantitative analysis based on the measurement of the isotopic abundance of a nuclide after isotope dilution with the test portion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03327
name: isotope effect
def: "The effect on the rate or equilibrium constant of two reactions that differ only in the isotopic composition of one or more of their otherwise chemically identical components is referred to as a kinetic isotope effect or a thermodynamic \(or equilibrium\) isotope effect\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03328
name: isotope exchange
def: "A chemical reaction in which the reactant and product chemical species are chemically identical but have different isotopic composition. In such a reaction the isotope distribution tends towards equilibrium \(as expressed by fractionation factors\) as a result of transfers of isotopically different atoms or groups. For example\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03329
name: isotope exchange analysis
def: "A kind of quantitative analysis based on the isotope exchange between isotopes of the element to be determined and other isotopes of this element in different valency states or in different molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03330
name: isotope pattern
def: "Set of peaks related to ions with the same chemical formula but containing different isotopes; e.g. the 16 and 17 mass/charge peaks in a $\\ce\{CH4\}$ sample arising from $\\ce\{^\{12\}CH4^\{+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{^\{13\}CH4^\{+\}\}$ ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03331
name: isotopes
def: "Nuclides having the same atomic number but different mass numbers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03332
name: isotopic abundance
def: "The relative number of atoms of a particular isotopes in a mixture of the isotopes of an element\, expressed as a fraction of all the atoms of the element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03334
name: isotopically deficient
def: "An isotopically labelled compound may be designated as isotopically deficient when the isotopic content of one or more elements has been depleted\, i.e. when one or more nuclide\(s\) is \(are\) present in less than a natural ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03335
name: isotopically enriched ions
def: "When the abundance of a particular nuclide is increased above the level at which it occurs in nature and is incorporated in a molecule\, the term 'isotopically enriched ion' is used to describe any ion enriched in the isotope."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03336
name: isotopically labelled
def: "Describes a mixture of an isotopically unmodified compound with one or more analogous isotopically substituted compound\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03337
name: isotopically modified
def: "Describes a compound that has a macroscopic composition such that the isotopic ratio of nuclides for at least one element deviates measurably from that occurring in nature. It is either an isotopically substituted compound or an isotopically labelled compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03338
name: isotopically substituted
def: "Describes a compound that has a composition such that essentially all the molecules of the compound have only the indicated nuclide at each designated position. For all other positions\, the absence of nuclide indication means that the nuclide composition is the natural one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03339
name: isotopically unmodified
def: "Describes a compound that has a macroscopic composition such that its constituent nuclides are present in the proportions occurring in nature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03340
name: isotopic carrier
def: "A carrier which differs only in isotopic composition from the trace it has to carry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03341
name: isotopic enrichment
def: "Any process by which the isotopic abundance of a specified isotopes in a mixture of isotopes of an element is increased."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03342
name: isotopic enrichment factor
def: "For a material enriched in a specified isotope\, the ratio between the isotopic abundance and the natural abundance of that isotope."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03343
name: isotopic fractionation factor
def: "The ratio \\\[\\frac\{\(x_\{1\}/x_\{2\}\)_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{\(x_\{1\}/x_\{2\}\)_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(x\\\) is the abundance\, expressed as the atom fraction of the isotope distinguished by the subscript numeral\, when the two isotopes are equilibrated between two different chemical species $\\ce\{A\}$ and $\\ce\{B\}$ \(or between specific sites A and B in the same or different chemical species\). The term is most commonly met in connection with deuterium solvent isotope effects\, when the fractionation factor \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\\\) expresses the ratio\: \\\[\\varPhi = \\frac\{\(x_\{\\rm\{D\}\}/x_\{\\rm\{H\}\}\)_\{\\rm\{solute\}\}\}\{\(x_\{\\rm\{D\}\}/x_\{\\rm\{H\}\}\)_\{\\rm\{solvent\}\}\}\n\\\] for the exchangeable hydrogen atoms in the chemical species \(or sites\) concerned. The concept is also applicable to transition states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03344
name: isotopic ion
def: "Any ion containing one or more of the less abundant naturally occurring isotopes of the elements that make up its structure\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH2D^\{+\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03345
name: isotopic labelling
def: "Labelling in which the resulting product is only different from the initial one by its isotopic composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03346
name: isotopic molecular ion
def: "A molecular ion containing one or more of the less abundant naturally occurring isotopes of the atoms that make up the molecular structure. Thus\, for ethyl bromide there exist isotopic molecular ions such as\: $\\ce\{^\{13\}CCH5Br^\{+.\}\}$\, $\\ce\{C2H4DBr^\{+.\}\}$\, $\\ce\{C2H5^\{81\}Br^\{+.\}\}$\, $\\ce\{^\{13\}C2H5^\{81\}Br^\{+.\}\}$\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03348
name: isotopic scrambling
def: "The achievement\, or the process of achieving\, an equilibrium distribution of isotopes within a specified set of atoms in a chemical species or group of chemical species \(for an example\, see diagram\)."
synonym: "scrambling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03349
name: isotopic separation
def: "An operation for the purpose of modifying an isotopic abundance in a mixture of isotopes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03350
name: isotopic tracer
def: "A tracer which only differs in isotopic composition from the substance to be traced."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03351
name: isotopologue
def: "A molecular entity that differs only in isotopic composition \(number of isotopic substitutions\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH4\}$\, $\\ce\{CH3D\}$\, $\\ce\{CH2D2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03352
name: isotopomer
def: "Isomers having the same number of each isotopic atom but differing in their positions. The term is a contraction of 'isotopic isomer'. Isotopomers can be either constitutional isomers \(e.g. $\\ce\{CH2DCH=O\}$ and $\\ce\{CH3CD=O\}$\) or isotopic stereoisomers \[e.g. $\\ce\{\(\\textit\{R\}\)-\}$ and $\\ce\{\(\\textit\{S\}\)-CH3CHDOH\}$ or $\\ce\{\(\\textit\{Z\}\)-\}$ and $\\ce\{\(\\textit\{E\}\)-CH3CH=CHD\}$\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03353
name: isotropic
def: "A quantity which is independent of direction. Anisotropic and nonisotropic refer to quantities which are direction dependent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03354
name: isotropic carbon
def: "A monolithic carbon material without preferred crystallographic orientation of the microstructure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03355
name: isotropic pitch-based carbon fibres
def: "Carbon fibres obtained by carbonization of isotropic pitch fibres after these have been stabilized \(i.e. made non-fusible\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03356
name: isoureas
def: "The imidic acid tautomer of urea\, $\\ce\{H2NC\(=NH\)OH\}$\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:I03358
name: isozyme
def: "Synonymous with isoenzyme."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT06772
name: ionophore
def: "A compound which can carry specific ions through membranes of cells or organelles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT06773
name: ion pumps
def: "Ion pumps enable ions to flow through membranes in a thermodynamically uphill direction by the use of an energy source such as ATP or light. They consist of sugar-containing hetero-peptide assemblies\, which open and close upon the binding and subsequent hydrolysis of ATP\, usually transporting more than one ion towards the outside or the inside of the membrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT06779
name: iron-sulfur cluster
def: "A unit comprising two or more iron atoms and bridging sulfur ligands in an iron-sulfur protein. The recommended designation of a cluster consists of the iron and sulfur content\, in square brackets\, for example\, $\\ce\{\[2\\! Fe-2\\! S\]\}$\, $\\ce\{\[3\\! Fe-4\\! S\]\}$. The possible oxidation levels are indicated by the net charge excluding the ligands\, for example $\\ce\{\[4\\! Fe-4\\! S\]^\{2+\}\}$; $\\ce\{\[4\\! Fe-4\\! S\]^\{+\}\}$ or \($\\ce\{\[4\\! Fe-4\\! S\]^\{2+;1+\}\}$\) cluster."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT06780
name: iron-sulfur proteins
def: "Proteins in which non-haem iron is coordinated with cysteine sulfur and usually also with inorganic sulfur. Divided into three major categories\: rubredoxins; 'simple iron-sulfur proteins'\, containing only iron-sulfur clusters; and 'complex iron-sulfur proteins'\, containing additional active redox centres such as flavin\, molybdenum or haem. In most iron-sulfur proteins\, the clusters function as electron-transfer groups\, but in others they have other functions\, such as catalysis of hydratase/dehydratase reactions\, maintenance of protein structure\, or regulation of activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT06828
name: incidence
def: "Number of occurrences of illness commencing\, or of persons falling ill\, during a given period in a specific population\: usually expressed as a rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT06941
name: ideal chromatography
def: "Chromatographic process\, where no peak-broadening effects \(such as diffusion\, slow mass transfer\, etc.\) operate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT06944
name: ideal\, non-linear chromatography
def: "Chromatographic process\, where only the curvature of the distribution isotherm determines the shape of the peaks while other peak-broadening processes are neglected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07055
name: individual gauge for localized orbitals
def: "A method of calculation of nuclear shieldings\, in which localized molecular orbitals associated with inner shell\, bonding orbitals\, and lone pairs have unique origins for the calculation of diamagnetic and paramagnetic terms. With this method\, satisfactory estimates of NMR chemical shifts for elements in the first and second rows can be achieved in ab initio calculations with basis sets of moderate size provided sufficiently accurate molecular geometries are used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07056
name: instability of Hartree–Fock solution
def: "Existence of additional solutions to the equations of the Hartree–Fock method occurring usually in the case when potential energy surfaces of different electronic states are drawn close together. Within the spin-restricted Hartree–Fock method \(RHF\)\, singlet and triplet instabilities are distinguished. The former involves the existence of another solution with lower energy and the electron distribution of lower symmetry. It may be regarded as an indication that the initially assumed molecular shape needs correction. The triplet instability involves rejection of the condition of double occupancy of molecular orbitals and thus transition to the spin-unrestricted Hartree–Fock method \(UHF\). The triplet instability is a necessary\, but insufficient\, condition for the conclusion as to the biradical character of the ground state of a given system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07057
name: intrinsic reaction coordinate
def: "A minimum-energy reaction path on a potential energy surface in mass-weighted coordinates\, connecting reactants to products via the transition state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07058
name: ionic bond
def: "The bond between atoms with sharply different electronegativities. In strict terms\, an ionic bond refers to the electrostatic attraction experienced between the electric charges of a cation and an anion\, in contrast with a purely covalent bond. In practice\, it is preferable to consider the amount of ionic character of a bond rather than referring to purely ionic or purely covalent bonds. The relationship was proposed \(L. Pauling\) for the estimation of ionic character of a bond between atoms A and B\: \\\[\\rm\{Amount of ionic character\} = 1 - e^\{-1/4\}\\ \(\\chi _\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\,-\\\,\\chi_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\)\\\] where \\\(\\chi _\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\chi _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) are the Pauling electronegativities of atoms A and B. This type of bonding is realized mostly in solids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07059
name: isoconjugate systems
def: "Molecular entities with similar conjugate networks which have the same number of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$\, e.g. cyclopentadienide anion and pyrrole or benzene and pyridine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07060
name: isogyric reaction
def: "A reaction in which the number of electron pairs is conserved\, for example\: \n$$\\ce\{CH4\(^\{1\}\\textit\{A\}_\{1\}\) + H\(^\{2\}\\textit\{S\}\) -> CH3\(^\{2\}\\textit\{A\}^\{'\}_\{2\}\) + H2\(^\{1\}\\Sigma^\{+\}_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\)\}$$"

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07061
name: isostructural reaction
def: "A ligand exchange reaction\, where the structural type of the metal complex remains the same. $$\\ce\{ML_\{n\} + X -> ML_\{n-1\}X + L + \\Delta\\textit\{E\}\}$$ Although these types of reactions are not necessarily isodesmic reactions\, a substantial degree of error cancellation for the calculated energy\, $\\Delta E$\, is achieved because the coordination sphere of the metal atom $\\ce\{M\}$ is conserved."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07139
name: interchange reaction
def: "Reaction that results in an exchange of atoms or groups between a polymer and low-molar-mass molecules\, between polymer molecules\, or between sites within the same macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07155
name: ion-exchange polymer
def: "Polymer that is able to exchange ions \(cations or anions\) with ionic components in solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07175
name: impact-modified polymer
def: "Polymeric material whose impact resistance and toughness have been increased by the incorporation of phase microdomains of a rubbery material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07208
name: intrinsically conducting polymer
def: "Electrically conducting polymer composed of macromolecules having fully conjugated sequences of double bonds along the chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07210
name: ionene
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules in which ionized or ionic groups are parts of main chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07212
name: ionic aggregates in an ionomer
def: "Domains enriched with ionic groups within an ionomer matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07213
name: ionic polymer
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules containing ionic or ionizable groups\, or both\, irrespective of their nature\, content\, and location. Synonym\: ion-containing polymer."
synonym: "ion-containing polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07215
name: ionomer cluster
def: "Ionic aggregate\, in a polymer matrix of low polarity\, formed through interactions of ionomer multiplets."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07216
name: ionomer multiplet
def: "Ionic aggregate\, in a polymer matrix of low polarity\, formed through the association of ion-pairs in an ionomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07233
name: isomorphic polymer blend
def: "Polymer blend of two or more different semi-crystalline polymers that are miscible in the crystalline state as well as in the molten state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07239
name: immiscibility
def: "Inability of a mixture to form a single phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07240
name: immiscible polymer blend
def: "Polymer blend that exhibits immiscibility."
synonym: "heterogeneous polymer blend" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07246
name: impregnation
def: "Penetration of monomeric\, oligomeric\, or polymeric liquids into an assembly of fibres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07248
name: intercalation
def: "Process by which a substance becomes transferred into pre-existing spaces of molecular dimensions in a second substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07253
name: interfacial adhesion
def: "Adhesion in which interfaces between phases or components are maintained by intermolecular forces\, chain entanglements\, or both\, across the interfaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07396
name: IC
def: "Frequently used acronym for internal conversion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07397
name: ICT
def: "Acronym for intramolecular charge transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07398
name: intramolecular charge transfer emission
def: "Electronic emission from an ICT state."
synonym: "ICT emission" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07399
name: infrared
def: "Region of the electromagnetic spectrum extending from \\\(780\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) to about \\\(20\\\,000\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\).According to the CIE\, the range between \\\(780\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) and \\\(1\\ \\rm\{mm\}\\\) is subdivided into\:IR-A \\\(780\\\)–\\\(1400\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)IR-B \\\(1.4\\\)–\\\(3\\ \\unicode\[times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)IR-C \\\(3\\ \\unicode\[times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)–\\\(1\\ \\rm\{mm\}\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07400
name: intersection space
def: "\(3N − 8\)-dimensional subspace of the \(3N − 6\)-dimensional nuclear coordinate space consisting of an infinite number of conical intersection points \(N is the number of nuclei\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07401
name: intramolecular charge transfer
def: "Process that changes the overall charge distribution in a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07403
name: ISC
def: "Frequently used acronym for intersystem crossing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07404
name: isolampsic point
def: "Same as isoemissive point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07514
name: inorganic–organic polymer
def: "Polymer or polymer network with a skeletal structure comprising inorganic and organic units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07515
name: inorganic polymer
def: "Polymer or polymer network with a skeletal structure that does not include carbon atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07624
name: <i>in situ</i> composite formation
def: "Process for preparing a polymer composite by  forming the filler or reinforcement in an existing polymer or  polymerizing monomers in the presence of dispersed filler."

[Term]
id: goldbook:IT07625
name: isostatic pressing
def: "Application of a hydrostatic pressure through a liquid to achieve densification prior to the production of a uniform compact monolith through ceramization of the densified liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:J03359
name: <i>j</i>-value
def: "An effective first order rate constant for a photochemical reaction \(photodecomposition\, etc.\) of light-absorbing impurities in the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:J03360
name: Jablonski diagram
def: "Originally\, a diagram showing that the fluorescent state of a molecular entity is the lowest excited state from which the transition to the ground state is allowed\, whereas the phosphorescent state is a metastable state below the fluorescent state\, which is reached by radiationless transition. In the most typical cases the fluorescent state is the lowest singlet excited state and the phosphorescent state the lowest triplet state\, the ground state being a singlet. Presently\, modified Jablonski diagrams are frequently used and are actually state diagrams in which molecular electronic states\, represented by horizontal lines displaced vertically to indicate relative energies\, are grouped according to multiplicity into horizontally displaced columns. Excitation and relaxation processes that interconvert states are indicated in the diagram by arrows. Radiative transitions are generally indicated with straight arrows \(→\)\, while radiationless transitions are generally indicated with wavy arrows ⇝."

[Term]
id: goldbook:J03361
name: Jahn–Teller effect
def: "Molecular distortions due to an electronically degenerate @G02704-1@ state. For non-linear molecular entities in a geometry described by a point symmetry group possessing degenerate irreducible representations there always exists at least one non-totally symmetric vibration that makes electronically degenerate states unstable at this geometry. The nuclei are displaced to new equilibrium positions of lower symmetry causing a splitting of the originally degenerate states \(first-order Jahn–Teller effect\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:J03362
name: Jahn–Teller transition
def: "A displacive crystal distortion to lower symmetry that cooperatively removes a localized-electron orbital degeneracy at an array of like atoms so as to leave the atoms in the centre of symmetry of their distorted sites. Example\: The crystallographic distortion in $\\ce\{Mn3O4\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:J03363
name: joule
def: "SI derived unit of energy\, \\\(\\pu\{J\} = \\pu\{N m\} = \\pu\{kg m2 s-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:J03364
name: junction point
def: "Branch point in a polymer network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:J03365
name: junction unit
def: "A non-repeating atom or non-repeating group of atoms between blocks in a block macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:JT07602
name: junction-point density
def: "Number of junction points per unit volume in a polymer network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03366
name: κ
def: "An affix in the name of a polydentate chelate complex indicating single ligating atom attachments of a polyatomic ligand to a coordination centre. A right superscript numerical index indicates the number of such attachments."
synonym: "kappa" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03367
name: Kamlet–Taft solvent parameters
def: "Parameters of the Kamlet–Taft solvatochromic relationship which measure separately the hydrogen bond donor \($\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$\)\, hydrogen bond acceptor \($\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\)\, and dipolarity/polarizability \($\\ce\{\\uppi\\!^\{\\ast\}\}$\) properties of solvents as contributing to overall solvent polarity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03369
name: Kaptein–Closs rules
def: "Rules used to predict the sign of CIDNP effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03370
name: Kasha rule
def: "Polyatomic molecular entities luminesce with appreciable yield only from the lowest excited state of a given multiplicity. There are exceptions to this rule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03371
name: Kasha–Vavilov rule
def: "The quantum yield of luminescence is independent of the wavelength of exciting radiation. There are exceptions to this rule."
synonym: "Vavilov rule" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03372
name: katal
def: "Unit for catalytic activity coherent with the SI\, equal to the catalytic activity that catalyses a reaction rate of one mole per second in an assay system. \\\(\\pu\{1 kat\} = \\pu\{1 mol s-1\}\\\). The katal is recommended for use in the specific context of enzymes and clinical chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03373
name: Kekulé structure
def: "A representation of an aromatic molecular entity \(such as benzene\)\, with fixed alternating single and double bonds\, in which interactions between multiple bonds are assumed to be absent. For benzene\:  are the Kekulé structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03374
name: kelvin
def: "The kelvin\, symbol \\\(\\pu\{K\}\\\)\, is the SI unit of thermodynamic temperature. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the Boltzmann constant \\\(k\\\) to be \\\(\\pu\{1.380649E-23\}\\\) when expressed in the unit \\\(\\pu\{J K-1\}\\\)\, which is equal to \\\(\\pu\{kg m2 s-2 K-1\}\\\)\, where the kilogram\, metre and second are defined in terms of h\, c and ΔνCs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03376
name: ketals
def: "Acetals derived from ketones by replacement of the oxo group by two hydrocarbyloxy groups $\\ce\{R2C\(OR\)2\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). This term\, once abandoned\, has been reinstated as a subclass of acetals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03377
name: ketazines
def: "Azines of ketones $\\ce\{R2C=NN=CR2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03378
name: ketenes
def: "Compounds in which a carbonyl group is connected by a double bond to an alkylidene group $\\ce\{R2C=C=O\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03379
name: ketenimines
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=C=NR\}$. Thus imino analogues of ketenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03381
name: ketimines
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=NR'\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03383
name: ketoaldonic acids
def: "Monosaccharides in which a structure containing a keto group and a carboxylic acid group is in equilibrium with a hemiacetal structure. Specific compounds are named using the -ulosonic acid suffix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03384
name: ketoaldoses
def: "Monosaccharides which contain both an aldehydic and a ketonic carbonyl group in equilibrium with intramolecular hemiacetal forms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03385
name: keto carbenes
def: "The term has imprecisely been used to designate carboxylic acyl carbenes. keto carbenes are carbenes bearing an oxo function at an unspecified site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03386
name: ketones
def: "Compounds in which a carbonyl group is bonded to two carbon atoms $\\ce\{R2C=O\}$ \(neither $\\ce\{R\}$ may be $\\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03387
name: ketoses
def: "Ketonic parent sugars \(polyhydroxyketones $\\ce\{H–\[CHOH\]_\{n\}–C\(=O\)\[CHOH\]_\{m\}–H\}$ with three or more carbon atoms\) and their intramolecular hemiacetals. The oxo group is usually at $\\ce\{C-2\}$. E.g. ᴅ-fructose\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03388
name: ketoximes
def: "Oximes of ketones\, $\\ce\{R2C=NOH\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03389
name: ketyls
def: "Radical anions \(or the corresponding salts\) derived from ketones by addition of an electron $\\ce\{R2C^\{.\}–O^\{−\}  R2C^\{−\}–O^\{.\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03390
name: kilo
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{3\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\rm\{k\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03391
name: kilogram
def: "The kilogram\, symbol \\\(\\pu\{kg\}\\\)\, is the SI unit of mass. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the Planck constant \\\(h\\\) to be \\\(\\pu\{6.62607015E-34\}\\\) when expressed in the unit \\\(\\pu\{J s\}\\\)\, which is equal to \\\(\\pu\{kg m2 s-1\}\\\)\, where the metre and the second are defined in terms of c and ΔνCs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03392
name: kind-of-property
def: "An attribute of phenomena\, bodies or substances that may be distinguished qualitatively. Examples\: Colour \(value\: green\, blue\)\, transparency\, length \(value\: long\, short; \\\(2\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\)\, \\\(5\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\)\)\, amount-of-substance \(value\: \\\(\\pu\{2 mol\}\\\)\, \\\(\\pu\{5 mol\}\\\)\). Kind-of-property includes the concept kind-of-quantity. All kinds-of-property may be related to nominal \(e.g. green\, blue\) and ordinal scales \(e.g. small\, large\)\, but kinds-of-quantity are generally related to difference \[e.g. \\\(10\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\) \(i.e. \\\(10\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\) more than an arbitrary zero\)\] or ratio scales \(length \\\(2\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\) or \\\(5\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03393
name: kind-of-quantity
def: "Abstract property common to several real quantities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03394
name: kinematics
def: "The study of the properties of motion that are independent of the nature of the forces. In reaction dynamics the term is often applied in particular to effects that occur when atomic masses are changed\, the forces remaining the same."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03395
name: kinematic viscosity
def: "Dynamic viscosity divided by the density of the fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03396
name: kinetic activity factor
def: "A factor involving activity coefficients that appears as a multiplier in the rate equation. For a bimolecular reaction the factor is \\\[\\frac\{y_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\ y_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{y_\{\\ddagger\}\}\\\] where \\\(y_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\, \\\(y_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) and \\\(y_\{\\ddagger \}\\\) are the activity coefficients for the reactants A and B and the activated complex\, respectively. For a unimolecular reaction the factor is \\\[\\frac\{y_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{y_\{\\ddagger\}\}\\\] and for a trimolecular reaction it is \\\[\\frac\{y_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\ y_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ y_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\}\{y_\{\\ddagger\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03398
name: kinetic control
def: "The term characterizes conditions \(including reaction times\) that lead to reaction products in a proportion governed by the relative rates of the parallel \(forward\) reactions in which the products are formed\, rather than by the respective overall equilibrium constants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03399
name: kinetic current
def: "A faradaic current that corresponds to the reduction or oxidation of an electroactive substance formed by a prior chemical reaction from another substance that is not electroactive\, and that is partially or entirely controlled by the rate of that reaction. The reaction may be heterogeneous\, occurring at an electrode-solution interface \(surface reaction\)\, or it may be homogeneous\, occurring at some distance from the interface \(volume reaction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03401
name: kinetic electrolyte effect
def: "The general effect of an added electrolyte \(i.e. an effect other than\, or in addition to\, that due to its possible involvement as a reactant or catalyst\) on the observed rate constant of a reaction in solution. At low concentrations \(when only long-range coulombic forces need to be considered\) the effect on a given reaction is determined only by the ionic strength of the solution and not by the chemical identity of the ions. For practical purposes\, this concentration range is roughly the same as the region of validity of the Debye–Hückel limiting law for activity coefficients. At higher concentrations\, the effect of an added electrolyte depends also on the chemical identity of the ions. Such specific action can usually be interpreted as the incursion of a reaction path involving an ion of the electrolyte as reactant or catalyst\, in which case the action is not properly to be regarded just as a kinetic electrolyte effect. Kinetic electrolyte effects are usually \(too restrictively and therefore incorrectly\) referred to as 'kinetic salt effects'. A kinetic electrolyte effect ascribable solely to the influence of the ionic strength on activity coefficients of ionic reactants and transition states is called a 'primary kinetic electrolyte effect'. A kinetic electrolyte effect arising from the influence of the ionic strength of the solution upon the pre-equilibrium concentration of an ionic species that is involved in a subsequent rate-limiting step of a reaction is called a 'secondary kinetic electrolyte effect'. A common case encountered in practice is the effect on the concentration of hydrogen ion \(acting as catalyst\) produced from the ionization of a weak acid in a buffer solution. Synonymous with kinetic equivalence."
synonym: "kinetic ionic-strength effect" []
synonym: "kinetic salt effect" []
synonym: "salt effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03402
name: kinetic energy
def: "Energy of motion\: for a body of mass \\\(m\\\)\, \\\[E_\{\\rm\{k\}\}=\\frac\{m\\ v^\{2\}\}\{2\}\\\] where \\\(v\\\) is the speed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03403
name: kinetic equivalence
def: "Two reaction schemes are kinetically equivalent if they imply the same rate law. For example\, consider the two schemes \(i\) and \(ii\) for the formation of $\\ce\{C\}$ from $\\ce\{A\}$\: \\\[\(\\rm\{i\}\) \\qquad \\ce\{A \[k_\{1\}\,\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}\]\[k_\{-1\}\,\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}\] B ->\[k_\{2\}\] C\}\\\] Providing that $\\ce\{B\}$ does not accumulate as a reaction intermediate. \\\[\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}\[\\ce\{C\}\]\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\} = \\frac\{k_\{1\} k_\{2\}\[\\ce\{A\}\]\[\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}\]\}\{k_\{2\} + k_\{-1\} \[\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}\]\}\\\] \\\[\(\\rm\{ii\}\) \\qquad \\ce\{A \[k_\{1\}\]\[k_\{-1\}\] B ->\[k_\{2\}\]\[\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}\] C\}\\\] Providing that $\\ce\{B\}$ does not accumulate as a reaction intermediate\: \\\[\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}\[\\ce\{C\}\]\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\} = \\frac\{k_\{1\} k_\{2\}\[\\ce\{A\}\]\[\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}\]\}\{k_\{-1\} + k_\{2\} \[\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}\]\}\\\] Both equations for \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}\[\\ce\{C\}\]\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\) are of the form\: \\\[\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}\[\\ce\{C\}\]\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\} = \\frac\{r \[\\ce\{A\}\]\[\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}\]\}\{1 + s \[\\ce\{OH^\{-\}\}\]\}\\\] where \\\(r\\\) and \\\(s\\\) are constants \(sometimes called 'coefficients in the rate equation'\). The equations are identical in their dependence on concentrations and do not distinguish whether $\\ce\{OH^\{−\}\}$ catalyses the formation of $\\ce\{B\}$\, and necessarily also its reversion to $\\ce\{A\}$\, or is involved in its further transformation to $\\ce\{C\}$. The two schemes are therefore kinetically equivalent under conditions to which the stated provisos apply."
synonym: "kinetic ambiguity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03405
name: kinetic isotope effect
def: "The effect of isotopic substitution on a rate constant is referred to as a kinetic isotope effect. For example\, in the reaction\:  the effect of isotopic substitution in reactant $\\ce\{A\}$ is expressed as the ratio of rate constants \\\(\\frac\{k^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{k^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\}\\\)\, where the superscripts \\\(\\rm\{l\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{h\}\\\) represent reactions in which the molecules $\\ce\{A\}$ contain the light and heavy isotopes\, respectively. Within the framework of transition state theory in which the reaction is rewritten as\:  and with neglect of isotopic mass on tunnelling and the transmission coefficient\, \\\(\\frac\{k^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{k^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\}\\\) can be regarded as if it were the equilibrium constant for an isotope exchange reaction between the transition state $\\ce\{\[TS\]^\{\\ddagger\}\}$ and the isotopically substituted reactant $\\ce\{A\}$\, and calculated from their vibrational frequencies as in the case of a thermodynamic isotope effect. Isotope effects like the above\, involving a direct or indirect comparison of the rates of reaction of isotopologues\, are called 'intermolecular'\, in contrast to intramolecular isotope effects\, in which a single substrate reacts to produce a non-statistical distribution of isotopomeric product molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03406
name: kinetic method
def: "An analytical method in which the rate of a reaction or a related quantity is measured and utilized to determine concentrations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03407
name: kinetic resolution
def: "The achievement of partial or complete resolution by virtue of unequal rates of reaction of the enantiomers in a racemate with a chiral agent \(reagent\, catalyst\, solvent\, etc.\)."
synonym: "asymmetric destruction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03410
name: <i>klado</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to designate a very open polyboron structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03411
name: Koopmans' theorem
def: "Directly relates experimental ionization potentials with energy levels of molecular orbitals. The theorem states that the ionization potential required to remove an electron from the orbital \\\(\\Psi _\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) is given by the negative value of the energy of the orbital\, \\\(-\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)\, as calculated within the Hartree–Fock approximation. The theorem is not applied to localized molecular orbitals\, which are not eigenfunctions of the effective hamiltonian."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03412
name: Koppel–Palm solvent parameters
def: "Parameters to measure separately the ability of a solvent to enter into non-specific solvent–solute interactions \(permittivity \\\(\\varepsilon\\\) and refractive index \\\(\\eta _\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\)\) and specific solvent–solute interaction \(solvent basicity or nucleophilicity \\\(B\\\) and solvent acidity or electrophilicity \\\(E\\\)\) as contributing to overall solvent polarity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03415
name: Krafft point
def: "The temperature \(more precisely\, narrow temperature range\) above which the solubility of a surfactant rises sharply. At this temperature the solubility of the surfactant becomes equal to the critical micelle concentration. It is best determined by locating the abrupt change in slope of a graph of the logarithm of the solubility against \\\(t\\\) or \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{T\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03416
name: Kratky plot
def: "A diagrammatic representation of scattering data on large particles\, obtained at different angles but at the same concentration\, constructed by plotting \\\(\(\\sin \\frac\{\\theta \}\{2\}\)^\{2\}\\ \\Delta R\\left\(\\theta \\right\)\\\) vs. \\\(\(\\sin \\frac\{\\theta\}\{2\}\)^\{2\}\\\)\, or \\\(q^\{2\}\\ \\Delta R\\left\(\\theta \\right\)\\\) vs. \\\(q\\\) and used for the determination of molecular shape. \\\(\\Delta R\(\\theta\)\\\) is the excess Rayleigh ratio\, \\\(P\(\\theta\)\\\) the particle scattering function\, \\\(\\theta \\\) the scattering angle and \\\(q\\\) the length of the scattering vector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:K03417
name: krypton ion laser
def: "A CW or pulsed laser emitting lines from \\\(337\\\) to \\\(859\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) from singly ionized krypton. Principal emissions are at \\\(530.9\\\)\, \\\(568.2\\\)\, \\\(647.1\\\) and \\\(752.5\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03418
name: lambda
def: "Signifies\, with its superscript\, the bonding number\, i.e. the sum of the number of skeletal bonds to an atom and the number of hydrogen atoms associated with it in a parent hydride\, real or hypothetical. Its usage in names of compounds is generally restricted to non-standard valence states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03422
name: <i>l</i>
def: "An obsolete prefix for \(−\)-\,See\: d\, l\, dlFor like and unlike diastereoisomersSee\: l\, u"

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03423
name: <i>l</i>\, <i>u</i>
def: "Stereodescriptors of diastereoisomers with two chirality elements. When the two chirality elements are both R or both S the molecular entity is described as having an l relationship \(l = like\)\, when one chirality element is R and the other S the molecular entity is described as having a u relationship \(u = unlike\). The notation can be extended to molecules with more than two chirality elements whereby the l\, u relationships of the chiral elements are considered in pairs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03424
name: label
def: "A marker\, tag or indicator distinguishable by the observer but not by the system and used to identify a tracer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03425
name: labelling
def: "Providing a substance with a label."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03432
name: labile
def: "The term has loosely been used to describe a relatively unstable and transient chemical species or \(less commonly\) a relatively stable but reactive species. It must therefore not be used without explanation of the intended meaning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03433
name: laboratory sample
def: "The sample or subsample\(s\) sent to or received by the laboratory. When the laboratory sample is further prepared \(reduced\) by subdividing\, mixing\, grinding or by combinations of these operations\, the result is the test sample. When no preparation of the laboratory sample is required\, the laboratory sample is the test sample. A test portion is removed from the test sample for the performance of the test or for analysis. The laboratory sample is the final sample from the point of view of sample collection but it is the initial sample from the point of view of the laboratory. Several laboratory samples may be prepared and sent to different laboratories or to the same laboratory for different purposes. When sent to the same laboratory\, the set is generally considered as a single laboratory sample and is documented as a single sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03434
name: lachrymator
def: "A substance which produces a flow of tears in a person or animal \(e.g. acetyl chloride\, acrylaldehyde\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03435
name: lactams
def: "Cyclic amides of amino carboxylic acids\, having a 1-azacycloalkan-2-one structure\, or analogues having unsaturation or heteroatoms replacing one or more carbon atoms of the ring. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03436
name: lactides
def: "Cyclic esters derived by multiple esterification between two \(usually\) or more molecules of lactic acid or other hydroxy carboxylic acids.\n They are designated as dilactides\, trilactides\, etc. according to the number of hydroxy acid residues\, e.g. a dilactide \(a 1\,4-dioxane-2\,5-dione\)\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03437
name: lactims
def: "Tautomeric forms of lactams\, having an endocyclic carbon-nitrogen double bond. Thus\, cyclic carboximidic acids\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03438
name: lactols
def: "Cyclic hemiacetals formed by intramolecular addition of a hydroxy group to an aldehydic or ketonic carbonyl group. They are thus 1-oxacy-cycloalkan-2-ols or unsaturated analogues\, e.g. \[obsolete\] This term has also been used for hydroxy lactones\, but such use is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03439
name: lactones
def: "Cyclic esters of hydroxy carboxylic acids\, containing a 1-oxacycloalkan-2-one structure\, or analogues having unsaturation or heteroatoms replacing one or more carbon atoms of the ring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03440
name: ladder chain
def: "A double-strand chain\, consisting of an uninterrupted sequence of rings\, with adjacent rings having two or more atoms in common. Alternatively\, a ladder chain is a double-strand chain with adjacent constitutional unit joined to each other through four atoms\, two on one side and two on the other side of each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03441
name: ladder macromolecule
def: "A double-strand macromolecule consisting of an uninterrupted sequence of rings\, with adjacent rings having two or more atoms in common. Alternatively\, a ladder macromolecule is a double-strand macromolecule with constitutional unit joined to each other through four atoms\, two on one side and two on the other side of each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03443
name: lag phase
def: "The growth interval \(adaption phase\) between inoculation and start of the exponential phase during which there is little or no growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03445
name: Lambert law
def: "The fraction of light absorbed by a system is independent of the incident radiant power \(\\\(P_\{\\lambda \}^\{0\}\\\)\). This law holds only if \\\(P_\{\\lambda \}^\{0\}\\\) is small\, scattering is negligible\, and multiphoton processes\, excited state populations\, and photochemical reactions are negligible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03446
name: lamellar crystal
def: "A type of crystal with a large extension in two dimensions and a uniform thickness. A lamellar crystal is usually of a thickness in the \\\(5\{-\}50\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) range\, and it may be found individually or in aggregates. The parallel chain stems intersect the lamellar plane at an angle between \\\(45^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) and \\\(90^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\). The lamellae often have pyramidal shape owing to differences in the fold domains; as a result\, one can deduce different fold planes and fold surfaces from the lamellar morphology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03447
name: lamp
def: "A source of incoherent radiation."
synonym: "light source" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03448
name: lamp black
def: "A special type of carbon black produced by incomplete combustion of a fuel rich in aromatics that is burned in flat pans. lamp black is characterized by a relatively broad particle size distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03450
name: Landolt reaction
def: "A slow chemical reaction\, in which the formation of a product is delayed by a suitable reagent added for the purpose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03451
name: Langmuir–Hinshelwood mechanism
def: "A mechanism for surface catalysis in which the reaction occurs between species that are adsorbed on the surface is often known as a Langmuir–Hinshelwood mechanism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03453
name: Langmuir–Rideal mechanism
def: "A mechanism for surface catalysis in which the reaction occurs between a reactant molecule in the gas phase and one that is adsorbed on the surface."
synonym: "Rideal–Eley mechanism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03454
name: Laporte rule
def: "This rule states that for monophotonic radiative transitions in centrosymmetric systems\, the only non-vanishing electric-dipole transition moments are those which connect an even term \(g\) with an odd term \(u\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03455
name: lapse rate
def: "The variation of an atmospheric variable with height; unless otherwise stated the variable is temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03457
name: large particle
def: "A particle with dimensions comparable with the wavelength of the radiation in the medium or larger. In practice a particle must be treated as large if its largest dimension exceeds about one-twentieth of the wavelength employed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03458
name: lariat ethers
def: "Crown ethers having a side chain that holds one or more additional coordinating sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03459
name: laser
def: "A source of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation which produces light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation from which the acronym is derived. The light emitted is coherent except for superradiance emission. The essential elements of a laser are\: 1\, an active medium; 2\, a pumping process to make a population inversion; and 3\, suitable geometry of optical feedback elements. The active medium consists of a host material \(gas\, liquid or solid\) containing an active species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03461
name: laser beam ionization
def: "Ionization by irradiation of a specimen with a laser beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03469
name: laser ionization
def: "Occurs when a sample is irradiated with a laser beam. In the irradiation of gaseous samples\, ionization occurs via a single- or multi-photon process. In the case of solid samples\, ionization occurs via a thermal process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03473
name: laser micro emission spectroscopy
def: "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with a finely focused laser beam \(diameter less than \\\(10\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\) in the ultraviolet or visible range under conditions of vapourization and thermal excitation of electronic states of sample material and in which the photon emission spectrum is observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03474
name: laser micro mass spectrometry
def: "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with a finely focused laser beam \(diameter less than \\\(10\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\) in the ultraviolet or visible range under conditions of vapourization and ionization of sample material and in which the ions generated are recorded with a mass spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03477
name: laser Raman microanalysis
def: "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with a finely focused laser beam \(diameter less than \\\(10\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\) in the ultraviolet or visible range and the intensity versus wavelength function of the Raman radiation is recorded yielding information about vibrational states of the excited substance and therefore also about functional groups and chemical bonding."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03479
name: lasing
def: "The process of light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03481
name: latent image
def: "The primary result of radiation absorption in a photoimaging system which is susceptible to development."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03482
name: lateral order
def: "Order in the side-by-side packing of the molecules of a linear polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03483
name: lateral resolution
def: "For qualitative analysis\, this should be defined as the minimum distance of two points \(areas\) on the specimen corresponding to signal levels of 16 and 84%. For electron signals originating from top surface layers like secondary or Auger electrons and secondary ions the lateral resolution for qualitative purposes corresponds to the beam diameter. For signals originating in a greater depth of the sample \(back scattered electrons\, X-rays\) the lateral resolution is worse than the corresponding value of the beam diameter due to the diffusion of the primary electrons. The lateral resolution may be determined exactly with a sandwich specimen or a sharp edge specimen\, or approximately with a specimen showing a regular microstructure of known dimensions. Recommended abbreviation\: Lat. Res. \(qualitative\); unit\: \\\(\\rm\{m\}\\\); range\: \\\(\\rm\{nm\}\\\) or \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\} \\rm\{m\}\\\). For quantitative analysis\, this should be defined as the minimum distance of two points \(areas\) on the specimen corresponding to signal ratios of \\\(10^\{4\}\\\). This condition assures that the value for the lateral resolution defines the diameter of the analytical area which yields the total analytical signal. Due to the effect of electron diffusion in a solid and 'tail effects' in ion beam analysis the quantitative lateral resolution is significantly larger than the beam diameter. Recommended abbreviation\: Lat. Res. \(quantitative\); unit\: \\\(\\rm\{m\}\\\); range\: \\\(\\rm\{nm\}\\\) or \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\} \\rm\{m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03484
name: latex
def: "An emulsion or sol in which each colloidal particle contains a number of macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03485
name: lath crystal
def: "A lamellar crystal prevailingly extended along one lateral dimension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03486
name: lattice distortion
def: "Structural disorder resulting from misalignment of the unit cells within the crystals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03487
name: laws of distribution
def: "During the formation of a mixed crystal from a solution containing two components 'A' and 'B'\, the latter may be distributed according to the equation \\\[K_\{\\rm\{A\}\,\\rm\{B\}\}=\\frac\{b\\ \(a_\{0\}- a\)\}\{a\\ \(b_\{0\}- b\)\}\\\] In this homogeneous distribution\, \\\(a_\{0\}\\\) and \\\(b_\{0\}\\\) are the respective concentrations in the solution before crystallization and \\\(a\\\) and \\\(b\\\) are the respective concentrations in the solution after crystallization. \\\(K_\{\\rm\{A\}\,\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is usually called the separation factor. The term homogeneous distribution coefficient is not recommended. Alternatively the distribution of the micro-component may follow the equation of Doerner and Hoskins \\\[\\ln \(\\frac\{a_\{0\}\}\{a\}\) = \\lambda \\ \\ln \(\\frac\{b_\{0\}\}\{b\}\)\\\] \(logarithmic distribution\) where \\\(\\lambda \\\) is usually called the logarithmic distribution coefficient\, the meaning of the other symbols remaining the same. Exactly homogeneous or logarithmic distributions are extreme cases and very seldom encountered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03488
name: layer
def: "Any conceptual region of space restricted in one dimension\, within or at the surface of a condensed phase or a film. The usage of the term 'film' for an adsorption layer is confusing and is discouraged. The term double-layer applies to layers approximated by two 'distinct' sublayers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03489
name: layer equilibrium
def: "Saturation of the stationary phase with the mobile phase via the vapour phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03491
name: leader sequence
def: "A polynucleotide region between promoter and structural gene\, necessary for the correct transcription of DNA into mRNA.An N-terminal extension of polypeptides \(also known as the signal sequence\) which is necessary for the transport of the protein into or through the membrane or for its secretion into the extracellular medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03492
name: least-squares technique
def: "A procedure for replacing the discrete set of results obtained from an experiment by a continuous function. It is defined by the following. For the set of variables \\\(y\,x_\{0\}\,x_\{1\}\,\\\,...\\\) there are \\\(n\\\) measured values such as \\\(y_\{i\}\,x_\{0i\}\,x_\{1i\}\,\\\,...\\\) and it is decided to write a relation\: \\\[y = f\\left\(a_\{0\}\,a_\{1\}\,\\\,...\,a_\{K\};x_\{0\}\,x_\{1\}\,\\\,...\\right\)\\\] where \\\(a_\{0\}\,a_\{1\}\,\\\,...\,a_\{K\}\\\) are undetermined constants. If it is assumed that each measurement \\\(y_\{i\}\\\) of \\\(y\\\) has associated with it a number \\\(w_\{i\}^\{-1\}\\\) characteristic of the uncertainty\, then numerical estimates of the \\\(a_\{0\}\,a_\{1\}\,\\\,...\,a_\{K\}\\\) are found by constructing a variable \\\(S\\\)\, defined by \\\[S = \\sum_\{i\}\(w_\{i\}\\ \(y_\{i\}- f_\{i\}\)\)^\{2\}\\\] and solving the equations obtained by writing \\\[\\frac\{\\partial S\}\{\\partial a_\{j\}\}\\ \\overset\{˜\}\{a\}_\{j\}=0\\\] \\\(\\overset\{˜\}\{a\}_\{j\}=\\rm\{all\}\\\:\\\, a\\\) except \\\(a_\{j\}\\\). If the relations between the \\\(a\\\) and \\\(y\\\) are linear\, this is the familiar least-squares technique of fitting an equation to a number of experimental points. If the relations between the \\\(a\\\) and \\\(y\\\) are non-linear\, there is an increase in the difficulty of finding a solution\, but the problem is essentially unchanged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03493
name: leaving group
def: "An atom or group \(charged or uncharged\) that becomes detached from an atom in what is considered to be the residual or main part of the substrate in a specified reaction. For example\, in the heterolytic solvolysis of benzyl bromide in acetic acid\:  the leaving group is $\\ce\{Br^\{−\}\}$; in the reaction\:  the leaving group is $\\ce\{NMe3\}$ in the electrophilic nitration of benzene\, it is $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$. The term has meaning only in relation to a specified reaction. The leaving group is not\, in general\, the same as the substituent group present in the substrate \(e.g. bromo and trimethylammonio in the substrates of the first two examples above\). A slightly different usage of the term prevails in the \(non-mechanistic\) naming of transformations\, where the actual substituent group present in the substrate \(and also in the product\) is referred to as the leaving group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03494
name: lecithins
def: "Choline esters of phosphatidic acids. Specific compounds should be named systematically."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03495
name: lectins
def: "Glycoproteins isolated from plants but recently found also in animals and microorganisms that react specifically with terminal glycosidic residues of other molecules \(e.g. cell wall polysaccharides\); some causing cells to agglutinate \(cf. agglutination\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03497
name: left-to-right convention
def: "Arrangement of the structural formulae of the reactants so that the bonds to be made or broken form a linear array in which the electrons move from left to right."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03498
name: length
def: "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based. Special symbols are used for different types of lengths."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03499
name: lethal concentration
def: "Concentration of a potentially toxic substance in an environmental medium that causes death following a certain period of exposure \(denoted by LC\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03500
name: lethal dose
def: "Amount of a substance or physical agent \(radiation\) that causes death when taken into the body by a single absorption \(denoted by LD\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03501
name: lethal synthesis
def: "Metabolic formation of a highly toxic compound from one that is relatively non-toxic \(bioactivation\)\, often leading to death of affected cells. Synonymous with suicide metabolism."
synonym: "suicide metabolism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03502
name: leuco bases
def: "Colourless compounds formed by reduction of triphenylmethane dyes. Generally\, they are amino or hydroxy derivatives of triphenylmethane. Use of this class name\, which is more or less parochial to the technology of dyes\, is not endorsed. E.g. $\\ce\{\(p-Me2NC6H4\)2CHPh\}$\, the leuco base of Malachite Green."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03503
name: leuco compounds
def: "Dihydroxy polycyclic aromatic compounds which on oxidation form polycyclic quinone dyes \(intermediates in vat dyeing\). Use of this class name\, which is more or less parochial to the technology of dyes\, is not endorsed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03504
name: leukotrienes
def: "Linear $\\ce\{C20\}$ endogenous metabolites of arachidonic acid \(icosa-5\,8\,11\,14-tetraenoic acid\) containing a terminal carboxy function and four or more double bonds \(three or more of which are conjugated\) as well as other functional groups. A subclass of icosanoids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03505
name: level
def: "Logarithm of the ratio of a quantity and a reference value of that quantity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03506
name: levelling effect
def: "The tendency of a solvent to make all Brønsted acids whose acidity exceeds a certain value appear equally acidic. It is due to the complete transfer to a protophilic solvent of a hydron from a dissolved acid stronger than the conjugate acid of the solvent. The only acid present to any significant extent in all such solutions is the lyonium ion. For example\, the solvent water has a levelling effect on the acidities of $\\ce\{HClO4\}$\, $\\ce\{HCl\}$ and $\\ce\{HI\}$\: aqueous solutions of these acids at the same \(moderately low\) concentrations have the same acidities. A corresponding levelling effect applies to strong bases protogenic solvents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03507
name: level width
def: "Planck constant over \\\(2\\ \\pi\\\) divided by the mean lifetime."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03508
name: Lewis acid
def: "A molecular entity \(and the corresponding chemical species\) that is an electron-pair acceptor and therefore able to react with a Lewis base to form a Lewis adduct\, by sharing the electron pair furnished by the Lewis base. For example\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03509
name: Lewis acidity
def: "The thermodynamic tendency of a substrate to act as a Lewis acid. Comparative measures of this property are provided by the equilibrium constants for Lewis adduct formation of a series of Lewis acids with a common reference Lewis base."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03510
name: Lewis adduct
def: "The adduct formed between a Lewis acid and a Lewis base."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03511
name: Lewis base
def: "A molecular entity \(and the corresponding chemical species\) able to provide a pair of electrons and thus capable of coordination to a Lewis acid\, thereby producing a Lewis adduct."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03512
name: Lewis basicity
def: "The thermodynamic tendency of a substance to act as a Lewis base. Comparative measures of this property are provided by the equilibrium constants for Lewis adduct formation for a series of Lewis bases with a common reference Lewis acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03513
name: Lewis formula
def: "Molecular structure in which the valency electrons are shown as dots so placed between the bonded atoms that one pair of dots represents two electrons or one covalent \(single\) bond\, e.g.  A double bond is represented by two pairs of dots\, etc. Dots representing non-bonded outer-shell electrons are placed adjacent to the atoms with which they are associated\, but not between the atoms. Formal charges \(e.g. +\, −\, 2+\, etc.\) are attached to atoms to indicate the difference between the positive nuclear charge \(atomic number\) and the total number of electrons \(including those in the inner shells\)\, on the formal basis that bonding electrons are shared equally between atoms they join. \(Bonding pairs of electrons are usually denoted by lines\, representing covalent bonds\, as in line formulae.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03514
name: <span class=\"s\">LIDAR</span>
def: "An acronym for Light Detection And Ranging. It is a real time remote sensing technique for the determination of aerosol and trace gas concentrations in air by measurement of scattered laser radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03515
name: lifetime
def: "Lifetime of a molecular entity\, which decays by first-order kinetics\, is the time needed for a concentration of the entity to decrease to \\\(1/\\rm\{e\}\\\) of its original value\, i.e.\, \\\(c\(t = \\tau\) = \\frac\{c\(t\\\,=\\\,0\)\}\{e\}\\\). Statistically\, it represents the life expectation of the entity. It is equal to the reciprocal of the sum of the first-order rate constants of all processes causing the decay of the molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03516
name: lifetime
def: "The time required for the luminescence intensity to decay from some initial value to \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) of that value \(\\\(\\rm\{e\} = 2.718\\\,...\\\)\). Lifetimes can be measured by 'phase fluorimetry' \(phosphorimetry\) where the phase shift between the sinusoidally modulated exciting light and the emitted light is measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03517
name: ligand field splitting
def: "The removal of a degeneracy of atomic or molecular levels in a molecule or ion with a given symmetry\, induced by the attachment or removal of ligands to produce reduced symmetries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03518
name: ligands
def: "In an inorganic coordination entity\, the atoms or groups joined to the central atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03519
name: ligand to ligand charge transfer transition
def: "An electronic transition of a metal complex that corresponds to excitation populating an electronic state in which considerable electron transfer between two ligands has occurred."
synonym: "LLCT transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03520
name: ligand to metal charge transfer transition
def: "An electronic transition in a metal complex that corresponds to excitation populating an electronic state in which considerable electron transfer from a ligand to a metal centre has occurred."
synonym: "LMCT transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03521
name: ligases
def: "Enzymes that catalyse the ligation of two molecules with concomitant hydrolysis of the pyrophosphate bond in adenosine 5'-triphosphate \(ATP\) or a similar triphosphate\, forming $\\ce\{C–C\}$\, $\\ce\{C–O\}$\, $\\ce\{C–S\}$\, $\\ce\{P–O\}$ or $\\ce\{C–N\}$ bonds. In recombinant DNA technology\, ligases covalently join together two sequences of DNA \(e.g. host DNA and foreign DNA\) by a phosphodiester bond."
synonym: "synthetases" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03522
name: ligate
def: "To coordinate to a central atom as a ligand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03523
name: light-atom anomaly
def: "A dynamical effect that arises for a process $$\\ce\{A + B-C -> A-B + C\}$$ when the species $\\ce\{A\}$ is light \(e.g. a hydrogen atom\) compared to $\\ce\{B\}$ and $\\ce\{C\}$. The vibrational excitation of the product $\\ce\{A–B\}$ is low\, since the light atom $\\ce\{A\}$ approaches to within the bonding distance of $\\ce\{BC\}$ before the $\\ce\{C\}$ atom retreats. The energy of reaction is therefore released as repulsion between $\\ce\{A–B\}$ and $\\ce\{C\}$\, with the result that there is translational excitation of the products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03524
name: light polarization
def: "A light beam is said to be linearly polarized if the end-point of the electric vector moves in a straight line when viewed along the direction of propagation of the beam. If it moves along a circle the beam is circularly polarized and if it moves along an ellipse the beam is elliptically polarized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03525
name: light scattering
def: "The redirection of a light beam due to interactions with molecules \(Rayleigh and Raman scattering\) and aerosols \(Mie scattering\). Scattered light received at any point in the atmosphere \(sometimes called sky radiation\) is a very important component of the total radiation received from the sun; it is dependent on the solar zenith angle\, elevation aerosol concentration etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03527
name: lignans
def: "Plant products of low molecular weight formed primarily from oxidative coupling of two p-propylphenol moieties at their $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}carbon atoms\}$; products with units coupled in other ways are neolignans\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03528
name: lignins
def: "Macromolecular constituents of wood related to lignans\, composed of phenolic propylbenzene skeletal units\, linked at various sites and apparently randomly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03529
name: limiting adsorption current
def: "The potential-independent value that is approached by an adsorption current as the rate of reduction or oxidation of the electroactive substance is increased by varying the applied potential. The terms adsorption current and limiting adsorption current should not be applied to faradaic currents that have been increased or decreased by adding a non-electroactive surfactant to a solution containing an electroactive substance\, nor to apparent waves resulting from the effect of adsorption or desorption on double-layer currents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03530
name: limiting catalytic current
def: "The potential-independent value that is approached by a catalytic current as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the applied potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03531
name: limiting condition of operation
def: "Range of physical and operational parameters in which the method meets given values of performance characteristics with 95% probability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03532
name: limiting current
def: "The limiting value of a faradaic current that is approached as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the potential. It is independent of the applied potential over a finite range\, and is usually evaluated by subtracting the appropriate residual current from the measured total current. A limiting current may have the character of an adsorption\, catalytic\, diffusion\, or kinetic current\, and may include a migration current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03534
name: limiting diffusion current
def: "The potential-independent value that is approached by a diffusion current as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the applied potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03535
name: limiting kinetic current
def: "The potential-independent value that is approached by a kinetic current as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the applied potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03537
name: limiting migration current
def: "The limiting value of a migration current\, which is approached as the rate of the charge-transfer process is increased by varying the applied potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03540
name: limit of detection
def: "The limit of detection\, expressed as the concentration\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\)\, or the quantity\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\)\, is derived from the smallest measure\, \\\(x_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\)\, that can be detected with reasonable certainty for a given analytical procedure. The value of \\\(x_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\) is given by the equation \\\[x_\{\\rm\{L\}\}=\\overline\{x\}_\{\\rm\{bi\}\}+k\\ s_\{\\rm\{bi\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\overline\{x\}_\{\\rm\{bi\}\}\\\) is the mean of the blank measures\, \\\(s_\{\\rm\{bi\}\}\\\) is the standard deviation of the blank measures\, and \\\(k\\\) is a numerical factor chosen according to the confidence level desired."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03541
name: limit test
def: "Acute toxicity test in which\, if no ill-effects occur at a pre-selected maximum dose\, no further testing at greater exposure levels is required."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03546
name: linear chain
def: "A chain with no branch points intermediate between the boundary units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03547
name: linear copolymer
def: "A copolymer composed of linear macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03549
name: linear electron accelerator
def: "An evacuated metal tube in which electrons pass through a series of small gaps \(usually in the form of cavity resonators in the high frequency range\) so arranged and spaced that\, at a specific excitation frequency\, the stream of electrons on passing through successive gaps gains additional energy from the electric field in each gap."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03550
name: linear energy transfer
def: "The average energy locally imparted to a medium by a charged particle of specified energy\, per unit distance traversed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03551
name: linear free-energy relation
def: "A linear correlation between the logarithm of a rate constant or equilibrium constant for one series of reactions and the logarithm of the rate constant or equilibrium constant for a related series of reactions. Typical examples of such relations \(also known as linear Gibbs energy relations\) are the Brønsted relation\, and the Hammett equation.The name arises because the logarithm of an equilibrium constant \(at constant temperature and pressure\) is proportional to a standard free energy \(Gibbs energy\) change\, and the logarithm of a rate constant is a linear function of the free energy \(Gibbs energy\) of activation.It has been suggested that this name should be replaced by linear Gibbs energy relation\, but at present there is little sign of acceptance of this change. The area of physical organic chemistry which deals with such relations is commonly referred to as 'Linear Free-Energy Relationships'."
synonym: "linear Gibbs energy relation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03553
name: linearity of responsivity
def: "The extent to which the output of the detector is directly proportional to the incident radiant power at a given wavelength and at constant irradiation geometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03554
name: linear macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule the structure of which essentially comprises the multiple repetition in linear sequence of units derived\, actually or conceptually\, from molecules of low relative molecular mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03555
name: linear polarizer
def: "An optical device which allows the transmission of radiation of which the electric vector is restricted to one plane resulting in linearly polarized radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03556
name: linear polymer
def: "A polymer composed of linear macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03557
name: linear pulse amplifier
def: "A pulse amplifier which\, within the limits of its normal operating characteristics\, delivers an output pulse of amplitude proportional to that of the input pulse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03558
name: linear range
def: "Concentration range over which the intensity of the signal obtained is directly proportional to the concentration of the species producing the signal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03559
name: linear solvation energy relationships
def: "Equations involving the application of solvent parameters in linear or multiple \(linear\) regression expressing the solvent effect on the rate or equilibrium constant of a reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03560
name: linear strain
def: "Change of length divided by the original length."
synonym: "relative elongation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03561
name: line formula
def: "A two-dimensional representation of molecular entities in which atoms are shown joined by lines representing single or multiple bonds\, without any indication or implication concerning the spatial direction of bonds. For example\, methanol is represented as\:  \(The term should not be confused with the representation of chemical formulae by the 'Wiswesser line notation'\, a method of string notation. Formulae in this notation are also known as 'Wiswesser line formulae'.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03562
name: lineic
def: "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by length. Lineic mass of a thread is the mass of the thread divided by its length."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03563
name: line-of-centres model
def: "A form of the collision theory of chemical reactions in which the assumption is made that reaction can only occur if on collision the component of energy along the line of centres of the masses of the colliding species is greater than a particular threshold energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03564
name: line repetition groups
def: "The possible symmetries of arrays extending in one direction with a fixed repeating distance. linear polymer chains in the crystalline state must belong to one of the line repetition groups. Permitted symmetry elements are\: the identity operation \(symbol $\\rm\{l\}$\); the translation along the chain axis \(symbol t\); the mirror plane orthogonal to the chain axis \(symbol \\\(m\\\)\) and that containing the chain axis \(symbol \\\(d\\\)\); the glide plane containing the chain axis \(symbol \\\(c\\\)\); the inversion centre\, placed on the chain axis \(symbol \\\(i\\\)\); the two-fold axis orthogonal to the chain axis \(symbol 2\); the helical\, or screw\, symmetry where the axis of the helix coincides with the chain axis. In the latter case\, the symbol is \\\(\\rm\{s\}\\left\(A*M/N\\right\)\\\)\, where \\\(\\rm\{s\}\\\) stands for the screw axis\, \\\(A\\\) is the class of the helix\, * and / are separators\, and \\\(M\\\) is the integral number of residues contained in \\\(N\\\) turns\, corresponding to the identity period \(\\\(M\\\) and \\\(N\\\) must be prime to each other\). The class index \\\(A\\\) may be dropped if deemed unnecessary\, so that the helix may also be simply denoted as \\\(\\rm\{s\}\\left\(M/N\\right\)\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03565
name: line-shape analysis
def: "Determination of rate constants for a chemical exchange from the shapes of spectroscopic lines of dynamic processes. The method is most often used in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03566
name: Lineweaver–Burk plot
def: "A plot of the reciprocal of velocity of an enzyme-catalysed reaction \(ordinate\) versus the reciprocal of substrate concentration \(abscissa\). The plot is used to graphically define the maximum velocity of an enzyme-catalysed reaction and the Michaelis constant for the enzyme."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03567
name: line width
def: "Width of a spectral line in terms of wavelength\, frequency or wavenumber. Usually defined as full or half width of the line at half of the maximum intensity \(FWHM or HWHM\, respectively\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03568
name: line width
def: "The full width at half maximum peak-height. The natural line width is the theoretical value of the full width at half maximum of the nuclear transition\, usually calculated from lifetime data."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03569
name: linked scan
def: "A scan\, in an instrument comprising two or more analysers\, in which two or more of the analyser fields are scanned simultaneously so as to preserve a pre-determined relationship between parameters characterizing these fields. Often\, these parameters are the field strengths\, but they may also be the frequencies in the case of analysers in which alternating fields are employed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03570
name: lipid film
def: "Established term for W/O/W films \(i.e. oil film in water\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03571
name: lipids
def: "A loosely defined term for substances of biological origin that are soluble in nonpolar solvents. They consist of saponifiable lipids\, such as glycerides \(fats and oils\) and phospholipids\, as well as nonsaponifiable lipids\, principally steroids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03572
name: lipophilic
def: "Literally 'fat-loving'. Applied to molecular entities \(or parts of molecular entities\) having a tendency to dissolve in fat-like \(e.g. hydrocarbon\) solvents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03573
name: lipophobic
def: "Literally\, 'fat-rejecting'; the converse of lipophilic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03574
name: lipopolysaccharides
def: "Natural compounds consisting of a trisaccharide repeating unit \(two heptose units and octulosonic acid\) with oligosaccharide side chains and 3-hydroxytetradecanoic acid units \(they are a major constituent of the cell walls of gram-negative bacteria\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03575
name: lipoproteins
def: "Clathrate complexes consisting of a lipid enwrapped in a protein host without covalent binding in such a way that the complex has a hydrophilic outer surface consisting of all the protein and the polar ends of any phospholipids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03576
name: liposome
def: "Originally a lipid droplet in the endoplasmic reticulum of a fatty liver. Now applied to an artificially formed lipid droplet\, small enough to form a relatively stable suspension in aqueous media and with potential use in drug delivery."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03577
name: Lippman's equation
def: "An equation which gives the electric charge per unit area of an interface \(electrode\)\: \\\[\(\\frac\{\\partial \\gamma \}\{\\partial E_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\)_\{T\,p\,\\mu _\{i\}\neq \\mu \}=- Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\gamma \\\) is the interfacial tension\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) is the potential of a cell in which the reference electrode has an interfacial equilibrium with one of the ionic components of A\, \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) is the charge on unit area of the interface\, \\\(\\mu _\{i\}\\\) is the chemical potential of the combination of species \\\(i\\\) whose net charge is zero\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature and \\\(p\\\) is the external pressure. Since more than one type of reference electrode may be chosen\, more than one quantity \\\(Q\\\) may be obtained. Consequently \\\(Q\\\) cannot be considered as equivalent to the physical charge on a particular region of the interphase. It is in fact an alternative way of expressing a surface excess or combination of surface excess of charged species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03578
name: liquid chromatography
def: "A separation technique in which the mobile phase is a liquid. Liquid chromatography can be carried out either in a column or on a plane. Present-day liquid chromatography generally utilizing very small particles and a relatively high inlet pressure is often characterized by the term high-performance \(or high-pressure\) liquid chromatography\, and the acronym HPLC."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03579
name: liquid-crystal transitions
def: "A liquid crystal is a molecular crystal with properties that are both solid- and liquid-like. Liquid crystals are composed predominantly of rod-like or disc-like molecules\, that can exhibit one or more different\, ordered fluid phases as well as the isotropic fluid; the translational order is wholly or partially destroyed but a considerable degree of orientational order is retained on passing from the crystalline to the liquid phase in a mesomorphic transition.Transition to a nematic phase.A mesomorphic transition that occurs when a molecular crystal is heated to form a nematic phase in which the mean direction of the molecules is parallel or antiparallel to an axis known as the director.Transition to a cholesteric phase. A mesomorphic transition that occurs when a molecular crystal is heated to form a cholesteric phase in which there is simply a spiralling of the local orientational order perpendicular to the long axes of the molecules. Transition to a smectic state.A mesomorphic transition that occurs when a molecular crystal is heated to yield a  smectic state in which there is a one-dimensional density wave which produces very soft/disordered layers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03580
name: liquid excimer laser
def: "A liquid laser in which excimer species in liquids are used as the active medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03581
name: liquid-gel chromatography
def: "Includes gel-permeation and ion-exchange chromatography. Liquid is the mobile phase\, gel the stationary phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03582
name: liquid ion exchange
def: "A term used to describe a liquid-liquid extraction process that involves a transfer of ionic species from the extractant to the aqueous phase in exchange for ions from the aqueous phase. The term does not imply anything concerning the nature of the bonding in the extracted complex. The term solvent ion exchange is not recommended."
synonym: "solvent ion exchange" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03583
name: liquid ion laser
def: "A liquid laser in which rare earth ions in solution are used as the laser active medium. An example is $\\ce\{KrF\}$ in a liquid $\\ce\{Kr\}$ laser."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03584
name: liquid junction
def: "Any junction between two electrolyte solutions of different composition. Across such a junction there arises a potential difference\, called the liquid junction potential. In the operational pH cell the junction is between the test\, or pH standard\, solution and the filling solution or the bridge solution of the reference electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03585
name: liquid laser
def: "A laser in which a liquid solution is the active species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03586
name: liquid-liquid distribution
def: "The process of transferring a dissolved substance from one liquid phase to another \(immiscible or partially miscible\) liquid phase in contact with it. Although extraction\, partition and distribution are not synonymous\, extraction may replace distribution where appropriate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03587
name: liquid-liquid extraction
def: "This term may be used in place of liquid-liquid distribution when the emphasis is on the analyte\(s\) being distributed \(or extracted\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03588
name: liquid-phase loading
def: "A term used in partition chromatography to express the relative amount of the liquid stationary phase in the column packing. It is equal to the mass fraction \(%\) of liquid stationary phase in the total packing \(liquid stationary phase plus support\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03589
name: liquid scintillation detector
def: "A scintillation detector in which the test portion is mixed with a liquid scintillator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03590
name: liquidus
def: "A line on a binary phase diagram \(or surface on a ternary phase diagram\) that indicates the temperature at which solidification begins on cooling or at which melting is completed on heating under equilibrium conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03591
name: liquid volume
def: "That volume occupied by the liquid phase in the column. Defined by \\\[V_\{\\rm\{L\}\}=\\frac\{w_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\}\{\\rho _\{\\rm\{L\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(w_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\) is the weight of the liquid in the column\, and \\\(\\rho _\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\) is the density at the column temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03592
name: lithometeor
def: "A particle of dry substance in the atmosphere\, as contrasted to a hydrometeor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03593
name: lithosphere
def: "The crust of the earth\, usually thought of as discrete from and in contact with the hydrosphere and the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03594
name: litre
def: "Non-SI unit of volume\, \\\(\\pu\{L\} = \\pu\{E-3 m3\}\\\). The other allowed symbol is \\\(\\mathrm\{l\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03595
name: live time
def: "For a measurement\, the time during which a radiation measuring assembly is capable of processing events occurring in the radiation detector. It equals the clock time minus the integrated resolving or dead time \(to be distinguished from 'life time'\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03596
name: living copolymerization
def: "A copolymerization which is a living polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03597
name: living polymerization
def: "A chain polymerization from which chain transfer and chain termination are absent. In many cases\, the rate of chain initiation is fast compared with the rate of chain propagation\, so that the number of kinetic-chain carriers is essentially constant throughout the polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03598
name: load
def: "The total weight acting\, after counterbalancing\, upon the terminal bearing which carries the object being weighed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03599
name: loading capacity
def: "The maximum concentration of solute\(s\) that a solvent can contain under specified conditions."
synonym: "maximum loading" []
synonym: "saturation capacity" []
synonym: "saturation loading" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03600
name: local conformation
def: "The conformation of a macromolecule at the scale of the constitutional units. In the polymer literature\, local conformation is sometimes referred to as microconformation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03602
name: local efficiency of atomization
def: "The substance fraction of atomized component in the component consumed. The efficiency of atomization is measured in a given part of the flame\, usually the observation space; \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{a\}\} = \\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\ \\chi _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\ \\chi _\{\\rm\{v\}\}\\ \\chi _\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\). The signal is a function of the product \\\(q_\{\\rm\{v\}\}\\ \\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\, but \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) is also a function of \\\(q_\{\\rm\{v\}\}\\\)\, usually decreasing at high volume rates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03603
name: local flame temperature
def: "The effective thermodynamic temperature in the observation space as measured by a specific sensor for a specified element \(in \\\(\\rm\{K\}\\\)\). The temperature of a flame \(or other plasma\) is not homogeneous. It is usually lower at the borders of the flame. It is therefore appropriate to speak of an effective temperature which represents an average value of all temperatures throughout the observation space. The flame temperature depends on several factors such as\: kind of plasma\, kind of gas or gas mixture and concentration gradient of the thermometric species in the observation space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03604
name: local fraction atomized
def: "The substance fraction of the atomized component in the total volatilized component. This quantity is measured in a defined part of the flame\, usually the observation space. The fraction atomized is the result of chemical reactions in the gaseous state. It depends on the bond strength of the compounds that the component may form within the flame and on the composition and temperature of the flame. When analysing elements that tend to become oxidized in the flame\, it may be advisable to use as fuel gas mixtures with a reducing component such as $\\ce\{C2H2\}$ or $\\ce\{N2O\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03605
name: local fraction desolvated
def: "The amount-of-substance fraction of component in a desolvated state in the amount of component entering the flame. This quantity is measured in a defined part of the flame\, usually the observation space. Because it varies with height in the flame as a result of progressive evaporation of aerosol droplets\, it is appropriate to term the expression local. The fraction desolvated does not account for losses by incomplete volatilization of the dry aerosol \(which largely depends upon the nature and concentration of the component\). Such losses are described by local fraction volatilized\, which usually depends on the solute. Since \\\(\\chi _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) varies markedly with the height in the flame\, its observed value represents an average. Local fraction desolvated depends on the solvent\, the temperature of the flame and the time the component takes to travel from the tip of the burner to the height in the flame considered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03606
name: local fraction volatilized
def: "The substance fraction of the volatilized component in the total desolvated component. The gaseous state includes free atoms\, molecules and radicals. This quantity is measured in a defined part of the flame\, usually the observation space. The fraction volatilized varies inversely with the size of the desolvated particles. Since \\\(\\chi _\{\\rm\{v\}\}\\\) varies markedly with the height in the flame\, its observed value represents an average."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03608
name: localized-itinerant transition
def: "A transition of either of two types\: \(a\) a Mott transition \(single-valent systems\). \(b\) a small-polaron to itinerant-electron transition \(mixed-valent systems\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03611
name: logarithmic normal distribution
def: "A continuous distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form\: \\\[f_\{\\rm\{w\}\}\(x\)\\ \\rm\{d\}x = \\frac\{1\}\{a\\ \\sqrt\{\\pi \\ x\}\}\\ \\exp \(- \\frac\{1\}\{a^\{2\}\}\\ \\ln ^\{2\}\\frac\{x\}\{b\}\)\\ \\rm\{d\}x\\\] where \\\(x\\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length\, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization and \\\(a\\\) and \\\(b\\\) are positive adjustable parameters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03613
name: logit
def: "In competitive binding assays\, the logit-log dose relationship\, in which the response is defined by\: \\\(R=\\mathrm\{logit\}\\left\(y\\right\)=\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{y\}\{1- y\}\)\\\) where \\\(y=\\frac\{b\}\{b_\{0\}\}\\\) with \\\(b\\\) = fraction of tracer bound and \\\(b_\{0\}\\\) = value of \\\(b\\\) with no unlabelled ligand in the system. Logit transformed assay data frequently yield straight-line dose-response curves\, amenable to statistical analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03614
name: log-normal distribution
def: "A distribution function \\\(F\(y\)\\\)\, in which the logarithm of a quantity is normally distributed\, i.e. \\\(F\(y\)=f_\{\\rm\{gauss\}\}\(\\ln y\)\\\) where \\\(f_\{\\rm\{gauss\}\}\(x\)\\\) is a Gaussian distribution. The size distributions of atmospheric aerosols are often described using this distribution function\, although the term also applies to gaseous pollutants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03615
name: London–Eyring–Polanyi method
def: "A semiempirical method of calculating a potential-energy surface\, based on a simplified quantum-mechanical equation \(the London equation\)."
synonym: "LEP method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03616
name: London–Eyring–Polanyi–Sato method
def: "A modification of the London–Eyring–Polanyi proposed in 1955 by S. Sato."
synonym: "LEPS method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03617
name: London forces
def: "Attractive forces between apolar molecules\, due to their mutual polarizability. They are also components of the forces between polar molecules. Also called 'dispersion forces'."
synonym: "dispersion forces" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03618
name: lone pair
def: "Two paired electrons localized in the valence shell on a single atom. Lone pairs should be designated with two dots. The term 'nonbonding electron pair' is more appropriate\, and is found in many modern text books."
synonym: "electron pair" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03619
name: long chain
def: "A chain of high relative molecular mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03621
name: longitudinal order
def: "Order in the atomic positions along the chains of a linear polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03622
name: long-lived collision complex
def: "When a potential-energy surface is such that the system performs a number of vibrations and rotations in the region of a col\, the corresponding intermediate is known as a long-lived collision complex\, and the reaction is said to be indirect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03623
name: long-range intramolecular interaction
def: "An interaction between segments\, widely separated in sequence along the chain\, that occasionally approach one another during molecular flexing. This type of interaction is closely related to the excluded volume of a segment\, the latter quantity reflecting interactions involving segments and solvent molecules. If no confusion can occur\, the word 'intramolecular' may be omitted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03624
name: long spacing
def: "The average separation between stacked lamellar crystals\, usually measured by small-angle X-ray or neutron diffraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03625
name: loose end
def: "Polymer chain within a network which is connected by a junction-point at one end only."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03628
name: Lorentzian band shape
def: "This band shape is described by the function\: \\\[F\\left\(\nu - \nu _\{0\}\\right\) = \\frac\{1\}\{\\pi \}\\ \\gamma \\ \(\(\nu - \nu _\{0\}\)^\{2\}+\\gamma ^\{2\}\)^\{-1\}\\\] where \\\(\nu_\{0\}\\\) is the mean band position\, \\\(\\gamma \\\) is the half band width at half maximum\, and \\\(F\\ \(\nu - \nu _\{0\}\)\\\) is the frequency distribution function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03629
name: Lorenz–Mie theory
def: "Theory of light scattering by isotropic\, homogeneous spheres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03630
name: lot
def: "A quantity of material which is assumed to be a single population for sampling purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03631
name: low energy electron diffraction
def: "Any technique which measures the angular intensity distribution of electrons reflected from a crystalline surface under bombardment with low energy electrons \(\\\(E_\{0\} < 500\\ \\rm\{eV\}\\\)\) in larger angles of incidence. The diffraction pattern also provides very surface sensitive information on the atomic arrangement of the top layers of a solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03634
name: lowest-observed-effect-level
def: "Lowest concentration or amount of a substance\, found by experiment or observation\, that causes any alteration in morphology\, functional capacity\, growth\, development\, or life span of target organisms distinguishable from normal \(control\) organisms of the same species and strain under the same defined conditions of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03636
name: low pressure electrical discharge
def: "Low pressure electrical discharge sources are radiation sources in which radiation is produced by electrical discharges in gases at low pressures\, i.e. pressures from \\\(10^\{2\}\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\) to \\\(10^\{-3\}\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03637
name: low-pressure mercury lamp
def: "Resonance lamp that contains mercury vapour at pressures of about \\\(0.1\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\) \(\\\(0.75\\times 10^\{-3\}\\ \\rm\{Torr\}\\\); \\\(1\\ \\rm\{Torr\} = 133.33\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\)\). At \\\(\\pu\{25^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)\, such a lamp emits mainly at \\\(253.7\\\) and \\\(184.9\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\). They are also called germicidal lamps. There are cold- and hot-cathode as well as cooled electrode-less \(excited by microwaves\) low-pressure mercury lamps. The Wood lamp is a low-pressure mercury arc with an added fluorescent layer that emits in the UV-A spectral region \(\\\(315\\\)-\\\(400\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\)."
synonym: "low-pressure mercury arc" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03638
name: low temperature UV–VIS absorption spectroscopy
def: "The measurement of the absorption of UV–VIS radiation by a solute in a highly viscous or solid matrix at low temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03639
name: lumen
def: "SI derived unit of luminous flux\, \\\(\\rm\{lm\} = \\rm\{cd sr\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03640
name: luminance
def: "Photometric counterpart of radiance\, producing the visual sensation called brightness. Typical units are\: \\\(\\pu\{candela m-2\}\\\) \(\\\(\\mathrm\{nit\}\\\)\)\, \\\(\\pu\{candela cm-2\}\\\) \(\\\(\\mathrm\{stilb\}\\\)\)\, \\\(\\mathrm\{foot\}\\ \\mathrm\{lambert\}\\\) \(\\\(2.426\\ \\rm\{nit\}\\\)\). As with all photometric quantities\, luminance does not refer to a specific wavelength\, but applies to light emitted by a standard source \(formerly a 'standard international candle'\, now a blackbody radiator emitting at the temperature of solidifying platinum\, \\\(2042\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\). Conversion from photometric units to radiometric units \(e.g. \\\(\\rm\{J s\}^\{-1\}\\\)\) requires convolution over wavelength of the relative spectral response of the human eye \(photopic response tables\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03641
name: luminescence
def: "Spontaneous emission of radiation from an electronically or vibrationally excited species not in thermal equilibrium with its environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03644
name: luminescence quenching
def: "Radiationless redistribution of excitation energy via interaction \(electronic energy or charge transfer\) between an emitting species and the quencher."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03645
name: luminescence spectrometer
def: "The instrument used to measure luminescence emission spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03646
name: luminous flux
def: "Of a source of luminous intensity \\\(I\\\) in an element of solid angle \\\(\\rm\{d\}\\varOmega\\\) is given by \\\(\\rm\{d\}\\varPhi = I\\ \\rm\{d\}\\varOmega\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03647
name: luminous intensity
def: "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03648
name: luminous quantities
def: "Group of quantities characterizing visible light in terms of the appearance to the human eye. The symbols of luminous quantities may be distinguished by adding a subscript v. The units for luminous quantities are derived from the SI base unit candela."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03649
name: lumiphore
def: "A part of a molecular entity \(or atom or group of atoms\) in which electronic excitation associated with a given emission band is approximately localized. \(Analogous to chromophore for absorption spectra\)."
synonym: "luminophore" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03651
name: lux
def: "SI derived unit of illuminance\, \\\(\\rm\{lx\} = \\rm\{lm m\}^\{-2\}=\\rm\{cd sr m\}^\{-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03652
name: lyases
def: "Enzymes cleaving $\\ce\{C–C\}$\, $\\ce\{C–O\}$\, $\\ce\{C–N\}$ and other bonds by other reactions than by hydrolysis or oxidation. Lyases catalyse the addition of molecules to unsaturated compounds or the elimination of molecules creating an unsaturated residue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03653
name: lyate ion
def: "The anion produced by hydron removal from a solvent molecule. For example\, the hydroxide ion is the lyate ion of water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03654
name: lyonium ion
def: "The cation produced by hydronation of a solvent molecule. For example\, \($\\ce\{CH3OH2^\{+\}\}$\) is the lyonium ion of methanol."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03655
name: lyophilic
def: "The terms lyophilic \(hydrophilic\, lipophilic\, oleophilic\, etc.\) and lyophobic \(lipophobic\, etc.\) may be used to describe the character of interaction of a particular atomic group with the medium. In this usage the terms have the relative qualitative meaning of 'solvent preferring' \(water-preferring\, fat-preferring\, etc.\) and 'solvent rejecting' \(water-rejecting\, fat-rejecting\, etc.\) respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03656
name: lyophilic sols
def: "These comprise both association colloids in which aggregates of small molecules are formed reversibly and macromolecules in which the molecules themselves are of colloidal size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:L03658
name: lysimeter
def: "Laboratory column of selected representative soil or a protected monolith of undisturbed field soil with which it is possible to sample and monitor the movement of water and substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06764
name: ligand field
def: "Ligand field theory is a modified crystal field theory that assigns certain parameters as variables rather than taking them as equal to the values found for free ions\, thereby taking into account the potential covalent character of the metal-ligand bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06788
name: low-spin
def: "In any coordination entity with a particular $\\rm\{d^\{n\}\}$ \($1 \\lt \\pu\{n\} \\lt 9$\) configuration and a particular geometry\, if the n electrons are distributed so that they occupy the lowest possible energy levels\, the entity is a low-spin complex. If some of the higher energy d orbitals are occupied before all the lower energy ones are completely filled\, then the entity is a high-spin complex."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06829
name: latent period
def: "Delay between exposure to a harmful substance and the manifestations of a disease or other adverse effects.Period from disease initiation to disease detection."
synonym: "latency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06854
name: liquid-crystal state
def: "A mesomorphic state having long-range orientational order and either partial positional order or complete positional disorder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06855
name: liquid-crystalline phase
def: "A phase occurring over a definite temperature range within the liquid-crystalline state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06856
name: liquid crystal
def: "A substance in the liquid-crystalline state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06865
name: liquid-crystal dendrimer
def: "A highly-branched oligomer or polymer of dendritic structure containing mesogenic groups that can display mesophase behaviour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06867
name: liquid-crystal polymer
def: "A polymer material that\, under suitable conditions of temperature\, pressure\, and concentration\, exists as an LC mesophase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06877
name: Langmuir–Blodgett membrane
def: "Synthetic composite membrane formed by sequential depositing of one or more monolayers of surface-active component onto a porous or non-porous support."
synonym: "LB membrane" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06883
name: liquid membrane
def: "Liquid phase existing either in supported or unsupported form that serves as a membrane barrier between two phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06896
name: lyotropic mesophase
def: "A mesophase formed by dissolving an amphiphilic mesogen in a suitable solvent\, under appropriate conditions of concentration\, temperature\, and pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06909
name: lowest observed adverse effect level
def: "Lowest concentration or amount of a substance\, found by experiment or observation\, which causes an adverse alteration of morphology\, functional capacity\, growth\, development\, or life span of a target organism distinguishable from normal \(control\) organisms of the same species and strain under defined conditions of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06919
name: liquid-coated stationary phase
def: "A material in which a liquid stationary phase is coated on the surface of the solid support."
synonym: "liquid-coated stationary phase material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06935
name: linear distribution isotherm
def: "A @DT06932-1@ which can be approximated as \\\(c_\{\\rm\{s\}\} = K_\{\\rm\{C\}\}.c_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\) where \\\(K_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\) is a constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06939
name: linear chromatography
def: "Chromatographic process\, where the retention is governed by a linear distribution isotherm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT06965
name: lipophilicity
def: "Lipophilicity represents the affinity of a molecule or a moiety for a lipophilic environment. It is commonly measured by its distribution behaviour in a biphasic system\, either liquid-liquid \(e.g.\, partition coefficient in 1-octanol/water\) or solid-liquid \(retention on reversed-phase high-performance liquid chromatography \(RP-HPLC\) or thin-layer chromatography \(TLC\) system\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07064
name: least motion principle
def: "The statement that those elementary reactions are the most favoured which exhibit the fewest possible alterations in the positions of the atomic nuclei and in the electronic configuration. The most frequently used mathematical formulation of the principle rests on a mechanical model of a molecule in which the energy of structural deformation\, when reactants \(r\) turn into products \(p\)\, is assumed to be proportional to the sum of the squares of the changes in the positions of the nuclei common to both reactants and products \\\[E=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}f_\{i\}\\ \(q^\{\\rm\{p\}_\{i\}\}- q^\{\\rm\{r\}_\{i\}\}\)^\{2\}\\\] where \\\(f_\{i\}\\\) is the force constant \(in many applications set equal to unity\). The equation coincides with the relationship for the potential energy of small vibrations\, hence it is valid only at a very early stage of a reaction. This is one of the reasons why many reactions violate the principle of least motion."
synonym: "principle of least motion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07065
name: Lewis octet rule
def: "A classical rule for describing the electronic configuration of atoms in molecules\: the maximum number of electron pairs that can be accommodated in the valence shell of a first-row element is four. For the second and subsequent row elements there are many exceptions to this rule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07066
name: localized molecular orbitals
def: "The molecular orbitals located on certain fragments of a molecular system and spatially separated from each other as much as possible. The LMOs are derived from the electron occupied canonical molecular orbitals by subjecting them to a unitary transformation determined by an appropriate physical criterion\, e.g. by maximizing the sum of squares of the centroids of occupied MOs \(the Foster–Boys procedure\) or by minimizing the sum of the exchange \(or Coulomb\) repulsion integrals between the occupied MOs \(the Edmiston–Ruedenberg procedure\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07067
name: low-spin state
def: "When the separation between the highest occupied and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbitals is not large\, two alternative electronic states may be considered. The state with two electrons paired up in the HOMO is called a low-spin state. The low-spin state is the ground state when the one-electron energy needed to promote an electron to the LUMO is larger than the Coulomb and exchange repulsion energies required to pair up two electrons in the HOMO."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07156
name: living polymer
def: "Polymer with stable\, polymerization-active sites formed by a chain polymerization in which irreversible chain transfer and chain termination are absent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07176
name: liquid-crystalline polymer
def: "Polymeric material that\, under suitable conditions of temperature\, pressure\, and concentration\, exists as a liquid-crystalline mesophase."
synonym: "liquid crystal polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07408
name: LADS
def: "Acronym for lifetime-associated difference spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07409
name: Landau–Zener model
def: "Within the Born–Oppenheimer approximation\, a semi-classical model for the probability\, \\\(P\\\)\, of hopping from one electronic state to another of the same or different multiplicity \\\[P=\\exp \(- \\frac\{4\\ \\pi ^\{2\}\\ \\varepsilon_\{12\}^\{2\}\}\{h\\ \nu\\ |s_\{1\}-s_\{2\}|\}\)\\\] where \\\(\\varepsilon_\{12\}\\\) is the potential energy gap between the two electronic states at a surface crossing point\, \\\(|s_\{1\}- s_\{2\}|\\\) is the difference in slopes between the intersecting potential energy curves at this point and \\\(\nu\\\) is the nuclear relative velocity with which the system passes the point of closest approach."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07410
name: LAS
def: "Acronym for lifetime-associated spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07411
name: laser dye
def: "Organic dyes typically used dissolved in an organic solvent. The dye solution is circulated continuously through the laser chamber of the dye laser\, in which it is excited \(pumped\) by flash lamps or by another laser."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07412
name: laser-jet photochemical technique
def: "Technique by which high concentrations of excited states are generated in a jet\, permitting the readily determination of spectroscopic properties of those states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07413
name: LED
def: "Acronym for light-emitting diode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07414
name: light-emitting diode
def: "Semiconductor \(often a combination of gallium\, arsenic and phosphorous or gallium and nitrogen\) containing an n region \(where electrons are more numerous than positive charges\) separated from a p region \(where positive charges are more numerous than negative charges\). Upon application of a voltage\, charges move and emission of ultraviolet\, visible or infrared radiation is produced each time a charge recombination takes place. Although a LED emits incoherent monochromatic light\, normally a very narrow frequency range is obtained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:LT07415
name: linear dichroism
def: "For a uniaxial sample with sample axis \\\(Z\\\) is defined as\: \\\[\\Delta A_\{\\rm\{l\}\} = A_\{Z\} - A_\{Y\}\\\] where \\\(A_\{Z\}\\\) and \\\(A_\{Y\}\\\) are the absorption spectra measured with the electric vector of linearly polarized light along and perpendicular to the sample axis\, respectively. For an isotropic sample \\\(\\Delta A_\{\\rm\{l\}\} = 0\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03659
name: µ-
def: "An affix used in names to signify that a group so designated bridges two or more centres of coordination."
synonym: "mu-" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03662
name: macrocycle
def: "A cyclic macromolecule or a macromolecule cyclic portion of a macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03663
name: macrolides
def: "Macrocyclic lactones with a ring of twelve or more members."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03664
name: macrometeorology
def: "Study of the largest-scale aspects of the atmosphere\, e.g. general global circulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03666
name: macromolecular isomorphism
def: "Statistical co-crystallization of different constitutional repeating units which may either belong to the same copolymer chains \(copolymer isomorphism\) or originate from different homopolymer chains \(homopolymer isomorphism\). Isomorphism is a general term\: in the strict sense\, the crystal structure is essentially the same throughout the range of compositions; in isodimorphism or isopolymorphism\, there are two or more crystal structures\, respectively\, depending on composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03667
name: macromolecule
def: "A molecule of high relative molecular mass\, the structure of which essentially comprises the multiple repetition of units derived\, actually or conceptually\, from molecules of low relative molecular mass."
synonym: "macromolecular" []
synonym: "polymer molecule" []
synonym: "polymeric" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03668
name: macromonomer
def: "Polymer or oligomer the molecules of which each have one end-group that acts as a monomer molecule\, so that each polymer or oligomer molecule contributes only a single monomer unit to a chain of the product polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03669
name: macromonomeric unit
def: "The largest constitutional unit contributed by a single macromonomer molecule to the structure of a macromolecule."
synonym: "macromonomer unit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03670
name: macromonomer molecule
def: "A macromolecule that has one end-group which enables it to act as a monomer molecule\, contributing only a single monomeric unit to a chain of the final macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03672
name: macropore
def: "Pore with width exceeding about \\\(0.05\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) or \\\(50\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) \(\\\(500\\ \\rm\{Å\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03673
name: macroradical
def: "A macromolecule which is also a free radical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03674
name: macroscopic cross-section
def: "The cross-section per unit volume of a given material for a specified process. For a pure nuclide\, it is the product of the microscopic cross-section and the number of target nuclei per unit volume; for a mixture of nuclides\, it is the sum of such products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03676
name: macroscopic film
def: "A film which has lateral dimensions above \\\(100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03677
name: macroscopic kinetics
def: "A term applied to a kinetic study in which the interest is in the behaviour of bulk systems\, e.g. in changes in concentrations of reactants and products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03680
name: magnetic deflection
def: "The deflection of an ion beam as a result of the motion of the ions in a magnetic field \(magnetic sector\). Generally the direction of motion of the ions is at right angles to the direction of the magnetic field\, and the motion is uniform."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03681
name: magnetic equivalence
def: "Nuclei having the same resonance frequency in nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy and also identical spin-spin interactions with the nuclei of a neighbouring group are magnetically equivalent. The spin-spin interaction between magnetically equivalent nuclei does not appear\, and thus has no effect on the multiplicity of the respective NMR signals. Magnetically equivalent nuclei are necessarily also chemically equivalent\, but the reverse is not necessarily true."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03682
name: magnetic field scan
def: "The usual method of producing a momentum \(mass\) spectrum\, by varying the strength of the magnetic field."
synonym: "<em>B</em> scan" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03683
name: magnetic field strength
def: "Magnetic flux density divided by the permeability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03684
name: magnetic flux
def: "Scalar product of magnetic flux density and area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03685
name: magnetic flux density
def: "Magnetic flux density at the nucleus \(from experiment\) in those cases in which the magnetic hyperfine interaction can be described by an effective field. In other cases the vector components of the magnetic hyperfine interaction should be reported if possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03686
name: magnetic flux density
def: "Magnetic induction is a vector characterizing a magnetic field. The force acting on a moving charge in a magnetic field is the charge multiplied by the vector product of the velocity and magnetic flux density. This quantity is sometimes loosely called the magnetic field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03688
name: magnetic moment
def: "Vector quantity\, the vector product of which with the magnetic flux density of a homogeneous field is equal to the torque."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03689
name: magnetic susceptibility
def: "Relative permeability minus one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03690
name: magnetic transition
def: "A transition between disordered and ordered arrays of atomic magnetic moments. Where the ordered phase has a net spontaneous magnetization\, \\\(M_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\)\, the magnetic-ordering temperature is called a curie temperature\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\\\); where the net spontaneous magnetization of the ordered phase remains zero\, the ordering temperature is called a Néel temperature\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{N\}\}\\\). The temperature at which the two ferromagnetic subarrays of a ferrimagnet just cancel each other is called the compensation point. Materials exhibit ferromagnetic behaviour when unpaired electron spins are aligned in parallel\, antiferromagnetic behaviour when the alignment is antiparallel\, and ferrimagnetic behaviour if the alignment of the spins is antiparallel with unequal numbers in the two orientations or if the spins are canted. Therefore\, ferromagnetic\, ferrimagnetic and weak ferromagnetic transitions involve a net magnetic moment change\, whereas antiferromagnetic transitions have zero net magnetic moment change."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03691
name: magnetizability
def: "Tensor quantity relating the induced magnetic moment\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)\, to the applied magnetic flux density \\\(B\\\)\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{i\}\}=\\xi \\ B\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03692
name: magnetization transfer
def: "NMR method for determining kinetics of chemical exchange by perturbing the magnetization of nuclei in a particular site or sites and following the rate at which magnetic equilibrium is restored. The most common perturbations are saturation and inversion\, and the corresponding techniques are often called 'saturation transfer' and 'selective inversion-recovery'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03693
name: magnetogyric ratio
def: "Ratio of the magnetic moment to the angular momentum. It is often misleadingly called the gyromagnetic ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03694
name: main chain
def: "That linear chain to which all other chains\, long or short or both\, may be regarded as being pendant."
synonym: "backbone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03695
name: mancude-ring systems
def: "Rings having \(formally\) the maximum number of noncumulative double bonds\, e.g. benzene\, indene\, indole\, 4H-1\,3-dioxine. Also called mancunide-ring systems."
synonym: "mancunide-ring systems" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03697
name: manipulator
def: "A hand-operated or -controlled device for remotely handling radioactive materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03698
name: manual
def: "Refers to physical human intervention in an analytical procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03699
name: mapping
def: "The determination of the relative positions of genes within the chromosomes of restriction sites along a DNA molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03700
name: Marangoni effect
def: "Motions of the surface of a liquid are coupled with those of the subsurface fluid or fluids\, so that movements of the liquid normally produce stresses in the surface and vice versa. The movement of the surface and of the entrained fluid\(s\) caused by surface tension gradients is called the Marangoni effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03701
name: Marcus–Coltrin path
def: "A path trajectory over a potential-energy surface which corresponds to the outermost vibrational turning points for the molecular species involved. This path was suggested as a device for calculating the probability of quantum-mechanical tunnelling\: the trajectory for the system is considered to follow this path."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03702
name: Marcus equation
def: "Relation between the rate of outer-sphere electron transfer and the thermodynamics of this process. Essentially\, the rate constant within the encounter complex \(or the rate constant of intramolecular transfer\) is given by the Eyring equation\: \\\[k_\{\\mathrm\{ET\}\}=\\frac\{\\kappa _\{\\mathrm\{ET\}\}\\ k\\ T\}\{h\}\\ \\exp \(- \\frac\{\\Delta G^\{\\ddagger \}\}\{R\\ T\}\)\\\] where \\\(k\\\) is the Boltzmann constant\, \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant and \\\(\\kappa _\{\\rm\{ET\}\}\\\) the so-called electronic transmission factor \(\\\(\\kappa _\{\\rm\{ET\}\}\\sim 1\\\) for adiabatic and \\\("

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03703
name: Marcus–Hush relationship
def: "Relationship between the barrier \(\\\(\\Delta G^\{\\ddagger\}\\\)\) to thermal electron transfer\, the energy of a corresponding optical charge-transfer transition \(\\\(\\Delta E_\{\\rm\{op\}\}\\\)\)\, and the overall change in standard Gibbs energy accompanying thermal electron transfer \(\\\(\\Delta G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\). Assuming a quadratic relation between the energy of the system and its distortions from equilibrium \(harmonic oscillator model\) the expression obtained is\: \\\[\\Delta G^\{\\ddagger\} = \\frac\{\\Delta E_\{\\rm\{op\}\}^\{2\}\}\{4\\ \(\\Delta E_\{\\rm\{op\}\}\\\,-\\\,\\Delta G^\{o\}\)\}\\\] The simplest form of this expression obtains for degenerate electron transfer \(\\\(\\Delta G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\) in e.g. symmetrical mixed valence systems\: \\\[\\Delta G^\{\\ddagger\} = \\frac\{\\Delta E_\{\\rm\{op\}\}\}\{4\}\\\] Note that for this situation the Marcus equation reads\: \\\[\\Delta G^\{\\ddagger\} = \\frac\{\\lambda \}\{4\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03705
name: marker
def: "\(DNA\) A fragment of known size used as reference for analytical purposes.\(genetic\) A gene with known phenotype and mapped position.\(chromatography\) A reference substance co-chromatographed with the sample to assist in identifying the components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03706
name: Mark–Houwink equation
def: "The equation describing the dependence of the intrinsic viscosity of a polymer on its relative molecular mass \(molecular weight\) and having the form\: \\\[\[\\eta\] = K\\cdot M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}^\{a\}\\\] where \\\(\[\\eta\]\\\) is the intrinsic viscosity\, \\\(K\\\) and \\\(a\\\) are constants the values of which depend on the nature of the polymer and solvent as well as on temperature and \\\(M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) is usually one of the relative molecular mass averages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03707
name: Markownikoff rule
def: "'In the addition of hydrogen halides to unsymmetrically constituted \[unsaturated\] hydrocarbons\, the halogen atom becomes attached to the carbon bearing the lesser number of hydrogen atoms.' Originally formulated by Markownikoff \(Markovnikov\) to generalize the orientation in additions of hydrogen halides to simple alkenes\, this rule has been extended to polar addition reaction as follows. 'In the heterolytic addition of a polar molecule to an alkene or alkyne\, the more electronegative \(nucleophilic\) atom \(or part\) of the polar molecule becomes attached to the carbon atom bearing the smaller number of hydrogen atoms.' This is an indirect statement of the common mechanistic observation\, that the more electropositive \(electrophilic\) atom \(or part\) of the polar molecule becomes attached to the end of the multiple bond that would result in the more stable carbenium ion \(whether or not a carbenium ion is actually formed as a reaction intermediate in the addition reaction\). Addition in the opposite sense is commonly called 'anti-Markovnikov addition'."
synonym: "Markownikov rule" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03708
name: martensitic transition
def: "A diffusionless transition \(first studied in the steel alloy\, Martensite\)\, at constant composition\, generated by coordinated atomic\, ionic or molecular displacements over distances smaller than interatomic distances in the parent phase. The cooperative rearrangements of the crystal structure generally take place progressively by the movement of a two-dimensional interface through the solid. Examples\: The face-centred-cubic to body-centred-tetragonal transition of iron containing some carbon; the transition of tetragonal $\\ce\{ZrO2\}$ to monoclinic $\\ce\{ZrO2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03709
name: mass
def: "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03710
name: mass analysis
def: "A process by which a mixture of ionic or neutral species is identified according to the mass-to-charge \(\\\(\\frac\{m\}\{z\}\\\)\) ratios \(ions\) or their aggregate atomic masses \(neutrals\). The analysis may be qualitative and/or quantitative."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03711
name: mass-average velocity
def: "Mass-average velocity\: \\\[\nu_\{b\} = \\rho ^\{-1\}\\ \\sum C_\{i\}M_\{i\}\nu_\{i\}\\\] Cf. molar average velocity\: \\\[\nu_\{m\} = c_\{t\}^\{-1\}\\ \\sum c_\{i\}\nu_\{i\}\\\] with \\\(c_\{t\}=\\sum c_\{i\}\\\) where \\\(M_\{i\}\\\) = molar mass\, \\\(c_\{t\}\\\) = total concentration \(\\\(\\pu\{mol m-1\}\\\)\)\, \\\(\\rho \\\) = density of the solution \(\\\(\\pu\{kg m-3\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03712
name: mass balance
def: "Summation of the masses of a given element in its various compounds before and after reaction \(changes\) in the atmosphere; provides a test of the completeness of the accounting of the various reaction paths for this element which can be had through the compounds which have been analysed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03713
name: mass concentration
def: "Mass of a constituent divided by the volume of the mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03715
name: mass density gradient
def: "Change in mass density in a small distance divided by that distance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03716
name: mass-distribution function
def: "A distribution function in which the relative amount of a portion of a substance with a specific value\, or a weight distribution range of values\, of the random variable\(s\) is expressed in terms of mass fraction."
synonym: "weight-distribution function" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03718
name: mass distribution ratio
def: "The fraction \(\\\(1 - R\\\)\) of a component in the stationary phase divided by the fraction \(\\\(R\\\)\) in the mobile phase\: \\\[D_\{\\rm\{m\}\}=\\frac\{\\rm\{amount of substance in the stationary phase\}\}\{\\rm\{amount of component in the mobile phase\}\}\\\] This term is recommended in preference to the term capacity factor frequently used in the chromatographic literature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03719
name: mass excess
def: "Mass of an atom minus the product of mass number and atomic mass constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03720
name: mass flow rate
def: "Mass of a material crossing a surface divided by the time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03721
name: mass-flow sensitive detector
def: "A device the response of which is proportional to the amount of sample component reaching the detector in unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03722
name: mass fraction
def: "Mass of a constituent divided by the total mass of all constituents in the mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03723
name: massic
def: "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by mass; synonymous with specific."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03724
name: massive transition
def: "A transition in which a crystal structure is changed into a new structure during cooling or heating\, predominantly by interface-controlled reactions and the usual nucleation and growth characteristics associated with thermal diffusion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03725
name: mass-law effect
def: "At equilibrium\, the product of the activities \(or concentrations\) of the reacting species is constant. Thus for the equilibrium\: \\\[\\alpha\\\,\\rm\{A\}\\\,+\\\,\\beta\\\,\\rm\{B\}\\rightleftharpoons \\gamma\\\,\\rm\{C\}\\\,+\\\,\\delta\\\,\\rm\{D\}\\\] \\\[K=\\frac\{\\left\[\\rm\{C\}\\right\]^\{\\gamma \}\\ \\left\[\\rm\{D\}\\right\]^\{\\delta \}\}\{\\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]^\{\\alpha \}\\ \\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]^\{\\beta \}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03726
name: mass number
def: "Total number of heavy particles \(protons and neutrons jointly called nucleons\) in the atomic nucleus. Also called nucleon number. Symbol \\\(m\\\) in mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03727
name: mass of the stationary phase
def: "The mass \(weight\) of the liquid stationary phase or the active solid in the column. The mass \(weight\) of any solid support is not included. In the case of partition chromatography with a liquid stationary phase it is identical to the liquid phase mass \(weight\)."
synonym: "weight of the stationary phase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03728
name: mass peak
def: "A record of the ion current\, at a specific mass to charge ratio\, received by the collector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03729
name: mass range
def: "The range of mass numbers which can be characterized by a mass spectrometer with sufficient resolution to differentiate adjacent peaks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03730
name: mass resolving power
def: "Commonly and also acceptably defined in terms of the overlap \(or 'valley'\) between two peaks. Thus for two peaks of equal height\, masses \\\(m_\{1\}\\\) and \\\(m_\{2\}\\\)\, when there is overlap between the two peaks to a stated percentage of either peak height \(10% is recommended\)\, then the resolving power is defined as \\\(\\frac\{m_\{1\}\}\{m_\{1\} - m_\{2\}\}\\\). The percentage overlap \(or 'valley'\) concerned must always be stated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03731
name: mass spectrograph
def: "An instrument in which beams of ions are separated \(analysed\) according to the quotient mass/charge\, and in which the deflection and intensity of the beams are recorded directly on photographic plate or film."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03732
name: mass spectrometer
def: "An instrument in which beams of ions are separated \(analysed\) according to the quotient mass/charge\, and in which the ions are measured electrically. This term should also be used when a scintillation detector is employed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03733
name: mass spectrometer
def: "A mass spectrometer in which the ions to be separated absorb maximum energy through the effect of alternating electric fields which are parallel to the path of the ions. These ions are then separated from other ions with different mass/charge by an additional electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03736
name: mass spectrometer focusing system
def: "An assembly permitting the separation of ions according to their mass-to-charge ratio."
synonym: "mass spectrometer deflection system" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03745
name: mass spectrometric detector
def: "A mass spectrometer used as a detector to give qualitative and quantitative data on the various eluted substances. The mass spectrum of the eluted compounds provides evidence beyond the elution time as to the chemical nature of the species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03746
name: mass spectrometry
def: "The branch of science dealing with all aspects of mass spectroscopes and the results obtained with these instruments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03748
name: mass spectroscopy
def: "The study of systems by causing the formation of gaseous ions\, with or without fragmentation\, which are then characterized by their mass-to-charge ratios and relative abundances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03749
name: mass spectrum
def: "A spectrum which is obtained when a beam of ions is separated according to the mass-to-charge \(\\\(\\frac\{m\}\{z\}\\\)\) ratios of the ionic species contained within it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03751
name: E/2 mass spectrum
def: "Processes of the charge-stripping type\: \\\[\\rm\{M\}^\{+\}+\\rm\{X\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{M\}^\{2+\}+\\rm\{X\}+\\rm\{e\}\\\] occuring in a collision cell \(containing a gas\, X\) located in a field-free region preceding a magnetic and electric sector combination placed in either order\, may be detected as follows. If the instrument slits are wide and if the electric sector field \\\(E\\\) is set to half the value required to transmit the main ion-beam\, the only ions to be transmitted will be those with a kinetic energy/charge ratio half\, or almost exactly half\, that of the main ion beam. The product ions of the charge-stripping process fulfil this condition. If the magnetic field \\\(B\\\) is scanned\, a mass spectrum of such doubly-charged product ions\, and thus of their singly-charged precursors\, is obtained. Such a spectrum is called an \\\(\\frac\{E\}\{2\}\\\) mass spectrum. Interference from product ions from processes of the type\: \\\[\\rm\{M\}_\{1\}^\{+\}+\\rm\{X\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{M\}_\{2\}^\{+\}+\\rm\{X\}+\\rm\{M\}_\{3\}\\\] where \\\(m_\{1\}\\\)\, \\\(m_\{2\}\\\)\, and \(\\\(m_\{1\}- m_\{2\}\\\)\) are the masses of \\\(\\rm\{M\}_\{1\}^\{+\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{M\}_\{2\}^\{+\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{M\}_\{3\}\\\)\, respectively\, and where \\\(m_\{2\}=0.5\\ m_\{1\}\\\)\, can arise in \\\(\\frac\{E\}\{2\}\\\) mass spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03752
name: mass-to-charge ratio
def: "The abbreviation \\\(\\frac\{m\}\{z\}\\\) is used to denote the dimensionless quantity formed by dividing the mass number of an ion by its charge number. It has long been called the mass-to-charge ratio although \\\(m\\\) is not the ionic mass nor is \\\(z\\\) a multiple or the elementary \(electronic\) charge\, \\\(e\\\). The abbreviation \\\(\\frac\{m\}\{e\}\\\) is\, therefore\, not recommended. Thus\, for example\, for the ion \($\\ce\{C7H7^\{2+\}\}$\)\, \\\(\\frac\{m\}\{z\}\\\) equals 45.5."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03753
name: mass transfer
def: "Spontaneous \(irreversible\) process of transfer of mass across non-homogeneous fields. The driving force can be difference in concentration \(in liquids\) or partial pressure \(in gases\) of the component. In fluids\, mass transfer may be enhanced by turbulent flow. In biological systems\, mass transfer through membranes may result from normal diffusion\, facilitated diffusion or active transport."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03754
name: mass transfer coefficient
def: "The heterogeneous diffusion rate constant defined for the case of the limiting current. A more general definition in terms of the electrode current densities is\: \\\[k_\{\\rm\{d\}\}=\\frac\{j\\ \nu \}\{n\\ F\\ \(c_\{\\rm\{e\}\}- c_\{0\}\)\}\\\] or \\\[k_\{\\rm\{d\}\} = \\frac\{j\\ \nu \\ \(1- t_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ n\\ \nu ^\{-1\}\\ z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{-1\}\)\}\{n\\ F\\ \(c_\{\\rm\{e\}\}- c_\{0\}\)\}\\\] where \\\(j\\\) is the electrode current density\, \\\(\nu \\\) is the stoichiometric number\, \\\(n\\\) is the charge number of the electrode reaction\, \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the interfacial concentration\, \\\(c_\{0\}\\\) is the bulk concentration\, \\\(t_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the transport number of species B\, and \\\(z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the charge number of species B."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03755
name: mass-transfer-controlled electrolyte rate constant
def: "In controlled-potential coulometry and related techniques\, the empirically evaluated constant of proportionality defined by the equation \\\[s_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=- \\frac\{1\}\{c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\ \\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}\\\] where \\\(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the bulk concentration of the substance B\, and \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\) is the rate of change of that concentration\, resulting from the consumption of B by reduction or oxidation at the working electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03756
name: matched cells
def: "A pair of cells with closely similar optical properties are called matched cells. One cell is the sample cell while the other\, the reference \(or blank\) cell contains the solvent or a reference solution. In double beam spectrometers\, radiation is passed either simultaneously or alternately through the cells. In single beam instruments the cells are moved sequentially into the radiation beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03757
name: material safety data sheet
def: "Compilation of information required under the US OSHA Hazard Communication Standard on the identity of hazardous substances\, health and physical hazards\, exposure limits and precautions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03758
name: matrix
def: "The components of the sample other than the analyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03759
name: matrix effect
def: "The combined effect of all components of the sample other than the analyte on the measurement of the quantity. If a specific component can be identified as causing an effect then this is referred to as interference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03760
name: matrix isolation
def: "A term which refers to the isolation of a reactive or unstable species by dilution in an inert matrix \(argon\, nitrogen\, etc.\)\, usually condensed on a window or in an optical cell at low temperature\, to preserve its structure for identification by spectroscopic or other means."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03761
name: Mattauch–Herzog geometry
def: "An arrangement for a double-focusing mass spectrograph in which a deflection of \\\(\\frac\{\\pi\}\{4\\ \\sqrt\{2\}\}\\\) radians in a radial electrostatic field is followed by a magnetic deflection of \\\(\\frac\{\\pi\}\{2\}\\\) radians."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03762
name: maximum allowable concentration
def: "The maximum concentration of a pollutant which is considered harmless to healthy adults during their working hours\, assuming they breathe uncontaminated air at all other times."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03763
name: maximum emission concentration
def: "Standards for maximum concentration of air pollutant emission from stationary or mobile sources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03765
name: maximum permissible daily dose
def: "Maximum daily dose of substance whose penetration into a human body during a lifetime will not cause diseases or health hazards that can be detected by current investigation methods and will not adversely affect future generations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03766
name: maximum permissible level
def: "Level\, usually a combination of time and concentration\, beyond which any exposure of humans to a chemical or physical agent in their immediate environment is unsafe."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03767
name: maximum storage life
def: "Period during which there is no change in concentration in excess of the value of the uncertainty of the concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03768
name: maximum tolerable concentration
def: "Highest concentration of a substance in an environmental medium that does not cause death of test organisms or species \(denoted by \\\(\\rm\{LC\}_\{0\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03769
name: maximum tolerable dose
def: "Highest amount of a substance that\, when introduced into the body\, does not kill test animals \(denoted by \\\(\\rm\{LD\}_\{0\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03770
name: maximum tolerable exposure level
def: "Maximum amount or concentration of a substance to which an organism can be exposed without leading to an adverse effect after prolonged exposure time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03771
name: maximum tolerated dose
def: "High dose used in chronic toxicity testing that is expected on the basis of an adequate subchronic study to produce limited toxicity when administered for the duration of the test period. It should not induce\: \(a\) overt toxicity\, for example appreciable death of cells or organ dysfunction\, or \(b\) toxic manifestations that are predicted materially to reduce the life span of the animals except as the result of neoplastic development or \(c\) 10% or greater retardation of body weight gain as compared with control animals. In some studies\, toxicity that could interfere with a carcinogenic effect is specifically excluded from consideration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03772
name: McLafferty rearrangement
def: "$\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}Cleavage\}$ with concomitant specific transfer of a $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}hydrogen atom\}$ in a six-membered transition state in mono-unsaturated systems\, irrespective of whether the rearrangement is formulated by a radical or an ionic mechanism\, and irrespective of the position of the charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03773
name: mean
def: "The sum of a series of observations divided by the number of observations in the series."
synonym: "average" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03774
name: mean activity of an electrolyte in solution
def: "Defined by the equation\: \\\[a_\{\\pm \}=\\mathrm\{e\}^\{\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}- \\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\unicode\{x29B5\}\}\)\\ \nu \\ R\\ T\}\\\] where \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the chemical potential of the solute $\\ce\{B\}$ in a solution containing B and other species. The nature of $\\ce\{B\}$ must be clearly stated\: it is taken as a group of ions of two kinds carrying an equal number of positive and negative charges\, e.g. $\\ce\{Na^\{+\}\} + \\ce\{NO3^\{−\}\}$ or $\\ce\{Ba^\{2+\}\} + \\ce\{2Cl^\{−\}\}$ or $\\ce\{2Al^\{3+\}\} + \\ce\{3SO4^\{2−\}\}$. \\\(v\\\) is the total number of ions making up the group i.e. 2\, 3 and 5 respectively in the above examples. \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\unicode\{x29B5\}\}\\\) is the chemical potential of $\\ce\{B\}$ in its standard state\, usually the hypothetical ideal solution of concentration \\\(\\pu\{1 mol kg -1\}\\\) and at the same temperature and pressure as the solution under consideration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03775
name: mean catalytic activity rate
def: "The catalytic activity of a specified component in or moved from a system divided by the time during which the component was changed or moved. This kind of quantity should not be confused with rate of reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03776
name: mean current density
def: "Defined as \\\[j=\\frac\{I\}\{A\}\\\] where \\\(I\\\) is the electric current and \\\(A\\\) is usually taken as the geometric area of the electrode. However the nature of the area used in this calculation must be clearly stated. In interpreting the mean current density it is important to know whether the current is uniformly distributed over the electrode interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03777
name: mean exchange current density
def: "Defined by \\\[j_\{0\}=\\frac\{I_\{0\}\}\{A\}\\\] where \\\(I_\{0\}\\\) is the exchange current of the electrode reaction and \\\(A\\\) is usually taken as the geometric area of the electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03778
name: mean free path
def: "The average distance a molecule travels between collisions. For a molecule\, \\\(\\lambda = \(\\sqrt\{2\}\\ \\pi \\ n\\ d_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{2\}\)^\{-1\}\\\)\, where \\\(n\\\) is the number of molecules per unit volume and \\\(d_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) is their mean diameter. For $\\ce\{O2\}$ at one atmosphere and \\\(25\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, this distance is only \\\(9.7\\times 10^\{-6\}\\ \\rm\{cm\}\\\); at \\\(10^\{-6\}\\\) atmospheres and \\\(25\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\) it is \\\(9.7\\ \\rm\{cm\}\\\). For an aerosol particle\, the mean free path\, \\\(\\lambda _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) in the Stokes region \(see Stokes law\) is given by\: \\\(\\lambda _\{\\rm\{B\}\} = \\sqrt\{\\frac\{3\\ k\\ T\}\{m\}\}\\ m\\ B\\\) where \\\(m\\\) is the mass of the particle\, \\\(k\\\) is the Boltzmann constant \(\\\(1.381\\times 10^\{-23\}\\ \\rm\{J K\}^\{-1\}\\\)\)\, \\\(T\\\) is the temperature \(\\\(\\rm\{K\}\\\)\) and \\\(B\\\) is the mobility."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03779
name: mean interstitial velocity of the carrier gas
def: "The interstitial velocity\, \\\(u\\\) of the carrier gas multiplied by the pressure-gradient correction-factor\, \\\(j\\\)\: \\\[\\bar\{u\} = u\\ j\\\] \\\[\\bar\{u\} = \\frac\{F\\ j\}\{\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{I\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(F\\\) is the nominal linear flow\, \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{I\}\}\\\) is the interstitial fraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03782
name: mean linear range
def: "In a given material\, for specified charged particles of a specified energy\, the average displacement of the particles before they stop."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03783
name: mean mass range
def: "The mean linear range multiplied by the mass density of the material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03784
name: mean mass rate
def: "The mass of a specified component changed in or moved to or from a system divided by the time during which the component was changed or moved."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03785
name: mean residence time
def: "The mean time during which the molecules remain on the surface of the adsorbent\, i.e. the mean time interval between impact and desorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03786
name: mean substance rate
def: "The amount of substance of a specified component changed in or moved to or from a system divided by the time during which the component was changed or moved. This kind of quantity usually should be preferred to mean mass rate or mean volume rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03787
name: mean volume rate
def: "The volume of a specified component changed in or moved to or from a system divided by the time during which the component was changed or moved."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03788
name: measurable quantity
def: "An attribute of a substance which may be distinguished qualitatively and determined quantitatively. In the context of analytical chemistry\, the attribute may refer to a physical quantity such as X- or $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{ray\\ energy\}$\, or it may refer to a measure of amount such as mass or concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03789
name: measurand
def: "Particular quantity subject to measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03790
name: measured excitation spectrum
def: "The spectrum observed by measuring the variation of the luminescence flux from an analyte as a function of the excitation wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03792
name: measured spectrum
def: "Synonymous with spectrogram."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03793
name: measured value
def: "The observed value of weight\, volume\, meter-reading or other quantity\, found in the analysis of a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03794
name: measurement
def: "A description of a property of a system by means of a set of specified rules\, that maps the property onto a scale of specified values\, by direct or 'mathematical' comparison with specified reference\(s\). The demand for rules makes 'measurement' a scientific concept in contrast to the mere colloquial sense of 'description'. However\, in the present definition\, 'measurement' has a wider meaning than given in elementary physics. Even a very incomplete description of\, for instance\, a patient \(at a stated time\) has to be given by a set of measurements\, that are easier to manage and grasp."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03795
name: measurement resolution
def: "The minimum value above which the difference of two values of air quality characteristic can be distinguished with 95% probability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03796
name: measurement result
def: "The outcome of an analytical measurement \(application of the chemical measurement process\)\, or value attributed to a measurand. This may be the result of direct observation\, but more commonly it is given as a statistical estimate derived from a set of observations. The distribution of such estimates \(estimator distribution\) characterizes the chemical measurement process\, in contrast to a particular estimate\, which constitutes an experimental result. Additional characteristics become evident if we represent \\\(\\hat\{x\}\\\) as follows\: \\\[\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\n &&& e  & \\\\\n &&& ⎴ ⎴ ⎴ & \\\\\n\\hat\{x\}=\\tau +e = &\\tau& + &\\Delta& + &\\delta&= \\mu +\\delta \\\\\n & ⎵ ⎵ ⎵ &&& \\\\\n & \\mu &&&\n\\end\{array\}\\\] The true value\, \\\(\\tau \\\)\, is the value \\\(x\\\) that would result if the chemical measurement process were error-free. The error\, \\\(e\\\)\, is the difference between an observed \(estimated\) value and the true value; i.e. \\\(e=\\hat\{x\}- \\tau \\\) \(signed quantity\). The total error generally has two components\, bias \(\\\(\\mathit\{\\Delta\}\\\)\) and random error \(\\\(\\delta \\\)\)\, as indicated above. The limiting mean\, \\\(\\mu\\\)\, is the asymptotic value or population mean of the distribution that characterizes the measured quantity; the value that is approached as the number of observations approaches infinity. Modern statistical terminology labels this quantity the expectation value or expected value\, \\\(E\(\\hat\{x\}\)\\\). The bias\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Delta\}\\\)\, is the difference between the limiting mean and the true value; i.e. \\\(\\mathit\{\\Delta\} = \\mu - \\tau \\\) \(signed quantity\). The random error\, \\\(\\delta \\\)\, is the difference between an observed value and the limiting mean; i.e. \\\(\\delta = \\hat\{x\} - \\mu \\\) \(signed quantity\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03797
name: measurement solution
def: "The solution that is presented to the measuring device. It comprises the test solution\, or an aliquot thereof\, after it has undergone any treatment required prior to the presentation to the measuring device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03798
name: measurement threshold
def: "The minimum concentration of a substance which produces a quantifiable signal with a given uncertainty."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03800
name: mechanical entrapment
def: "The process of random incorporation of comparatively small quantities of other phases \(e.g. water\, dust\, particles\, etc.\) in the bulk of a precipitate during its formation.The deliberate capture of small quantities of such phases by the deliberate addition of solids to a liquid phase. \(The term inclusion is not recommended\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03801
name: mechanical hygrometer
def: "An apparatus containing an element \(hair\, goldbeater's skin\, carbon-film\) whose dimension or mass varies as a function of its water content."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03803
name: mechanization
def: "The use of devices to replace\, refine\, extend or supplement human effort. The corresponding verb is to mechanize."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03804
name: mechanism
def: "A detailed description of the process leading from the reactants to the products of a reaction\, including a characterization as complete as possible of the composition\, structure\, energy and other properties of reaction intermediates\, products and transition states. An acceptable mechanism of a specified reaction \(and there may be a number of such alternative mechanisms not excluded by the evidence\) must be consistent with the reaction stoichiometry\, the rate law and with all other available experimental data\, such as the stereochemical course of the reaction. Inferences concerning the electronic motions which dynamically interconvert successive species along the reaction path \(as represented by curved arrows\, for example\) are often included in the description of a mechanism. It should be noted that for many reactions all this information is not available and the suggested mechanism is based on incomplete experimental data. It is not appropriate to use the term mechanism to describe a statement of the probable sequence in a set of stepwise reactions. That should be referred to as a reaction sequence\, and not a mechanism."
synonym: "reaction mechanism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03805
name: mechanism-based inhibition
def: "Irreversible inhibition of an enzyme due to its catalysis of the reaction of an artificial substrate. Also called 'suicide inhibition'."
synonym: "suicide inhibition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03806
name: median
def: "Depending on whether the number of observations is even or odd\, the median can be estimated as follows\:   If \\\(n=2\\ m+1\\\)\: The middle value of a series of observations\, arranged in increasing or decreasing order.   If \\\(n=2\\ m\\\)\: The arithmetic mean of the two middle values of a series of observations\, arranged in increasing or decreasing order.   Comment\: The use of the median when reporting results of chemical analysis is generally not recommended\, because its statistical efficiency is less than that of the mean. In certain cases\, however\, especially when treating small sets of data\, the median may offer advantages because it is a so-called 'robust statistic'\, i.e. it offers considerable resistance to the effects of isolated outliers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03807
name: median effective concentration
def: "Statistically derived concentration of a substance in an environmental medium expected to produce a certain effect in 50% of test organisms in a given population under a defined set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03808
name: median effective dose
def: "Statistically derived dose of a chemical or physical agent \(radiation\) expected to produce a certain effect in 50% of test organisms in a given population or to produce a half-maximal effect in a biological system under a defined set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03809
name: median lethal concentration
def: "Statistically derived concentration of a substance in an environmental medium expected to kill 50% of organisms in a given population under a defined set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03810
name: median lethal dose
def: "Statistically derived dose of a chemical or physical agent \(radiation\) expected to kill 50% of organisms in a given population under a defined set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03811
name: median lethal time
def: "Statistically derived average time interval during which 50% of a given population may be expected to die following acute administration of a chemical or physical agent \(radiation\) at a given concentration under a defined set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03812
name: median narcotic concentration
def: "Statistically derived concentration of a substance in an environmental medium expected to cause narcotic conditions in 50% of a given population under a defined set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03813
name: median narcotic dose
def: "Statistically derived dose of a substance expected to cause narcosis in 50% of test animals under a defined set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03814
name: medium
def: "The phase \(and composition of the phase\) in which chemical species and their reactions are studied in a particular investigation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03815
name: medium effect
def: "The medium effect on ionic species B due to transfer from solvent \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{1\}\\\) to solvent \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{2\}\\\) \(number\) is defined by \\\[R\\ T\\ \\ln \\gamma _\{\\rm\{S\}_\{1\}\}^\{\\rm\{S\}_\{2\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{B\}\\right\)=\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\,\\rm\{S\}_\{2\}\}- \\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\,\\rm\{S\}_\{1\}\}\\\] where \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature and \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\,\\rm\{S\}_\{i\}\}\\\) is the standard chemical potential of B in solvent \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{i\}\\\) \(where \\\(i\\\) = 1 or 2\)\, the reference states being the same in both solvents. \\\(\\gamma _\{\\rm\{S\}_\{1\}\}^\{\\rm\{S\}_\{2\}\}\(\\rm\{B\}\)\\\) is not an exactly measurable quantity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03816
name: medium-pressure mercury lamp
def: "Non-coherent radiation source containing mercury vapour at pressures ranging from \\\(50\\\) to several hundred \\\(\\mathrm\{kPa\}\\\) \(\\\(1\\ \\rm\{atm\} = 101.325\\ \\rm\{kPa\}\\\)\). Emits mostly from \\\(200\\\) to \\\(1000\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) with the most intense lines approximately at \\\(218\\\)\, \\\(248\\\)\, \\\(254\\\)\, \\\(266\\\)\, \\\(280\\\)\, \\\(289\\\)\, \\\(297\\\)\, \\\(303\\\)\, \\\(313\\\)\, \\\(334\\\)\, \\\(366\\\)\, \\\(406\\\)\, \\\(408\\\)\, \\\(436\\\)\, \\\(546\\\) and \\\(578\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)."
synonym: "medium-pressure mercury arc" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03817
name: mega
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{6\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{M\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03818
name: meiosis
def: "The reductive cell division which results in daughter cells containing one copy of each of the chromosome of the parent. The entire meiotic process involves two separate divisions \(meiosis I and meiosis II\). The first division is a true reductive division with the chromosome number being halved\, whereas the second division resembles mitosis in many ways. Thus\, a diploid parental cell will give rise to haploid daughter cells \(cf. ploidy\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03819
name: Meisenheimer adduct
def: "A cyclohexadienyl derivative formed as Lewis adduct from a nucleophile \(Lewis base\) and an aromatic or heteroaromatic compound\, also called Jackson–Meisenheimer adduct. In earlier usage the term 'Meisenheimer complex' was restricted to the typical Meisenheimer alkoxide adduct of nitro-substituted aromatic ethers\, e.g.  Analogous cationic adducts\, such as\:  considered to be reaction intermediates in electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions\, are called 'Wheland intermediates'\, and sometimes\, inappropriately\, $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}complexes\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03821
name: melting
def: "The conversion of a solid to a liquid by the application of heat and/or pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03822
name: melting point corrected
def: "The term originally signified that a correction was made \(not made\) for the emergent stem of the thermometer. In current usage it often means that the accuracy of the thermometer was \(was not\) verified. This current usage is inappropriate and should be abandoned."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03823
name: membrane
def: "A general term which refers to a continuous layer\, usually consisting of a semi-permeable material\, with controlled permeability covering a structure\, such as carbon or an inert metal\, or separating two electrolyte solutions. This latter case is the most general form of an ion-selective electrode. The membrane separates the internal components of the ion-selective electrode from the test solution. The membrane of an ion-selective electrode is responsible for the EMF response and selectivity of the entire electrode. Comment\: Membranes of sensor electrodes are thought to be practically homogeneous\, but an actual membrane may contain inhomogeneous regions\, often at surfaces\, and connected with materials and preparation methods used. Inhomogeneous regions include low dielectric polymer regions with few charge sites and regions with high local site densities. Surface regions of plasticized liquid membranes often are low in sites and high in plasticizer or exuded impurities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03824
name: membrane emf
def: "The potential difference \\\(E_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) between two saturated $\\ce\{KCl\}$ bridges inserted into two solutions separated by a membrane. The solutions need not be in equilibrium with one another and need not contain any colloidal material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03825
name: membrane potential
def: "Synonymous with membrane emf."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03826
name: membrane sites
def: "Membranes frequently contain built-in 'fixed' charged sites \(e.g. immobile $\\ce\{–\(SO3^\{−\}\)\}$ in polystyrenesulfonate\)\, or intentionally added\, hydrophobically trapped\, 'mobile' sites \[e.g. tetraphenylborate in plasticized poly\(vinyl chloride\)\]. Such membranes with charged sites are named sited membranes. Ions of opposite sign in the membrane are 'counter-ions'. Ions of the same sign as sites are not present in significant quantities\, and are co-ions. Sited membranes are 'permselective' to counter-ions i.e. only counter-ions exchange into the membrane and therefore have some mobility in the membrane bulk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03827
name: memory effect
def: "In instruments used for atmospheric trace component analysis\, the dependence of an instrument reading on one or several previous sample\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03828
name: <i>mer</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote meridional; three groups occupying vertices of an octahedral coordination sphere in such a relationship that one is cis to the two others which are themselves trans\, not now recommended for precise nomenclature purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03834
name: mercury flow system
def: "For mercury released directly as atomic vapour\, different direct transfer systems are used. In an open dynamic system the liberated analyte is transported by a carrier gas through the sampling or excitation source and swept away. In an open static system the equilibrated gaseous phase is forced into the absorption cell by displacement\, e.g. by water. During measurement the gaseous phase is thus static. In a closed system the analyte and carrier gas are circulated through the absorption cell and the generator vessel until equilibrium between the liquid and gaseous phases is established."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03835
name: mercury–xenon lamp
def: "Non-coherent intense source of ultraviolet\, visible\, and near-infrared radiation produced by an electrical discharge in a mixture of mercury vapour and xenon under high pressure."
synonym: "mercury–xenon arc" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03836
name: mero
def: "A prefix meaning part\, partial or fragment\, used in formation of compound words\, e.g. merocyanines are compounds related to cyanines but have a nitrogen atom at only one end of the chromogenic system instead of at both ends."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03837
name: merry-go-round reactor
def: "An apparatus in which several samples are rotated around a radiation source in order to expose each to equal amounts of radiation."
synonym: "turntable reactor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03838
name: <i>meso</i>
def: "A prefix to indicate the presence of a symmetry element of the second kind \(see chirality\) in a chemical species which is usually one of a set of diastereoisomers that also includes a chiral member."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03839
name: <i>meso</i>-compound
def: "A term for the achiral member\(s\) of a set of diastereoisomers which also includes one or more chiral members. For example\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03840
name: mesogenic monomer
def: "A monomer which can impart the properties of liquid crystals to the polymers formed by its polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03841
name: mesogenic pitch
def: "A pitch with a complex mixture of numerous essentially aromatic hydrocarbons. It does not contain anisotropic particles detectable by optical microscopy. Mesogenic pitch is low in quinoline-insoluble fractions and capable of transforming into mesophase pitch during continuous heat treatment above \\\(750\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) by the formation of optically detectable carbonaceous mesophase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03842
name: mesoionic compounds
def: "Dipolar five- \(possibly six-\) membered heterocyclic compounds in which both the negative and the positive charge are delocalized\, for which a totally covalent structure cannot be written\, and which cannot be represented satisfactorily by any one polar structure. The formal positive charge is associated with the ring atoms\, and the formal negative charge is associated with ring atoms or an exocyclic nitrogen or chalcogen atom. Mesoionic compounds are a subclass of betaines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03843
name: mesolytic cleavage
def: "Cleavage of a bond in a radical ion whereby a radical and an ion are formed. The term reflects the mechanistic duality of the process\, which can be viewed as homolytic or heterolytic depending on how the electrons are attributed to the fragments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03844
name: mesomeric effect
def: "The effect \(on reaction rates\, ionization equilibria\, etc.\) attributed to a substituent due to overlap of its p- or $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}$ with the p- or $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}$ of the rest of the molecular entity. delocalization is thereby introduced or extended\, and electronic charge may flow to or from the substituent. The effect is symbolized by M. Strictly understood\, the mesomeric effect operates in the ground electronic state of the molecule. When the molecule undergoes electronic excitation or its energy is increased on the way to the transition state of a chemical reaction\, the mesomeric effect may be enhanced by the electromeric effect\, but this term is not much used\, and the mesomeric and electromeric effects tend to be subsumed in the term resonance effect of a substituent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03845
name: mesomerism
def: "Essentially synonymous with resonance. The term is particularly associated with the picture of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ as less localized in an actual molecule than in a Lewis formula. The term is intended to imply that the correct representation of a structure is intermediate between two or more Lewis formulae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03846
name: mesomorphic phase
def: "Often formed by concentrated systems of surfactants. These are states of matter in which anisometric molecules \(or particles\) are regularly arranged in one \(nematic state\) or two \(smectic state\) directions\, but randomly arranged in the remaining direction\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03847
name: mesomorphic transition
def: "A transition that occurs between a fully-ordered crystalline solid and an isotropic liquid. Mesomorphic transitions can occur \(i\) from a crystal to a liquid crystal \(ii\) from a liquid crystal to another liquid crystal and \(iii\) from a liquid crystal to an isotropic liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03848
name: mesopause
def: "That region of the atmosphere between the mesosphere and the thermosphere at which the temperature is a minimum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03849
name: mesophase
def: "A phase occurring over a definite range of temperature\, pressure\, or concentration within the mesomorphic state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03850
name: mesophase pitch
def: "A pitch with a complex mixture of numerous essentially aromatic hydrocarbons containing anisotropic liquid-crystalline particles \(carbonaceous mesophase\) detectable by optical microscopy and capable of coalescence into the bulk mesophase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03851
name: mesophase pitch-based carbon fibres
def: "Mesophase pitch-based carbon fibres \(MPP-based carbon fibres\) are carbon fibres obtained from mesogenic pitch after it has been transformed into mesophase pitch \(MPP\) at least during the process of spinning\, and after the spun mesophase pitch fibres have been made non-fusible \(stabilized\) and carbonized."
synonym: "MPP-based carbon fibres" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03852
name: mesophiles
def: "Microorganisms which grow at moderate temperatures in the range \\\(20\{-\}45^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, and which have an optimum growth temperature in the range \\\(30\{-\}39^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03853
name: mesopore
def: "Pore of intermediate size. \[Those with widths exceeding about \\\(0.05\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) or \\\(50\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) \(\\\(500\\ \\mathring\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\) are called macropores; those with widths not exceeding about \\\(2\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) \(\\\(20\\ \\mathring\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\) are called micropores\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03854
name: mesoscale
def: "In meteorology\, the size or scale of phenomena smaller than ordinary cyclones or weather systems but larger than such microscale phenomena as the thickness of the boundary layer\, the wakes of objects\, etc. Thunderstorms involve mesoscale processes\, and other meteorological events the size of cities are usually mesoscale processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03855
name: mesosphere
def: "That region of the atmosphere which lies above the stratopause \(about \\\(4752\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\)\) and below the mesopause \(about \\\(8090\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\)\) and in which temperature decreases with increasing height; this is the region in which the lowest temperatures of the atmosphere occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03856
name: meso structures
def: "Relative configurations of consecutive\, but not necessarily contiguous\, constitutionally equivalent carbon atoms that have a symmetrically-constituted connecting group \(if any\) are designated 'meso' or 'racemo'\, as appropriate. Examples\:  \(The symbol  represents a symmetrically-constituted connecting group\, such as $\\ce\{–CH2\\bond\{–\}\}$\, $\\ce\{–CH2–CH2\\bond\{–\}\}$\, or $\\ce\{–CR2–CH2–CR2\\bond\{–\}\}$ .\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03857
name: messenger RNA
def: "An RNA molecule that transfers the coding information for protein synthesis from the chromosomes to the ribosomes. mRNA is formed from a DNA template by transcription. It may be a copy of a single gene or of several adjacent genes \(polycistronic mRNA\). On the ribosome\, the sequence is converted into the programmed amino acid sequence through translation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03858
name: metabolism
def: "The entire physical and chemical processes involved in the maintenance and reproduction of life in which nutrients are broken down to generate energy and to give simpler molecules \(catabolism\) which by themselves may be used to form more complex molecules \(anabolism\). In case of heterotrophic organisms\, the energy evolving from catabolic processes is made available for use by the organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03859
name: metabolite
def: "Any intermediate or product resulting from metabolism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03860
name: metal–carbene complexes
def: "Metal complexes of the type $\\ce\{R2CML_\{n\}\}$ \($\\ce\{M\}$ = metal\, $\\ce\{L\}$ = ligand\) in which formally a carbene is coordinated to a metal\, e.g."
synonym: "carbene metal complexes" []
synonym: "carbene–metal complexes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03861
name: metal–carbyne complexes
def: "Metal complexes of the type $\\ce\{RCML_\{n\}\}$ \($\\ce\{M\}$ = metal\, $\\ce\{L\}$ = ligand\) in which formally a carbyne is coordinated to a metal\, e.g."
synonym: "carbyne metal complexes" []
synonym: "carbyne–metal complexes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03863
name: metal–insulator transition
def: "A transition characterized by a sudden change in electrical transport properties \(conductivity\) due to a reversible change from localized to itinerant behaviour of the electrons. Example\: The transition at \\\(339\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) in $\\ce\{VO2\}$ where it changes from a high-temperature metallic behaviour to a low-temperature semiconductor behaviour. Synonymous with metal–nonmetal transition."
synonym: "metal–nonmetal transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03864
name: metallacycloalkanes
def: "Monocyclic compounds containing a metal atom and saturated carbon atoms as ring members\, e.g.\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03865
name: metallocenes
def: "Organometallic coordination compounds in which one atom of a transition metal such as iron\, ruthenium or osmium is bonded to and only to the face of two cyclopentadienyl $\\ce\{\[\\upeta\\!^\{5\}\\mbox\{-\}\(C5H5\)\]\}$ ligands which lie in parallel planes. The term should not be used for analogues having rings other than cyclopentadienyl as ligands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03866
name: metallurgical coke
def: "Metallurgical coke is produced by carbonization of coals or coal blends at temperatures up to \\\(1400\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) to produce a macroporous carbon material of high strength and relatively large lump size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03868
name: metal to ligand charge transfer transition
def: "An electronic transition of a metal complex that corresponds to excitation populating an electronic state in which considerable electron transfer from the metal to a ligand has occurred."
synonym: "MLCT transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03869
name: metal to metal charge transfer transition
def: "An electronic transition of a bi- or poly-nuclear metal complex that corresponds to excitation populating an electronic state in which considerable electron transfer between two metal centres has occurred."
synonym: "MMCT transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03870
name: metamagnetic transition
def: "An antiferromagnetic to ferromagnetic transition for \\\(H_\{\\rm\{applied\}\}>H_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) and/or \\\(T>T_\{\\rm\{t\}\}\\\) where \\\(H_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) and \\\(T_\{\\rm\{t\}\}\\\) refer to the critical magnetic field and transition temperature\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03871
name: metastability
def: "A term that describes the state of a phase in which an energy barrier considerably higher than \\\(k\\ T\\\) must be surmounted before this phase can transform to a phase of lower molar Gibbs energy and molar Helmholtz energy\, where \\\(k\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03874
name: metastable ion
def: "An ion which is formed with sufficient excitation to dissociate spontaneously during its flight from the ion source to the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03875
name: metastable state
def: "An isomeric state in an energy state higher than the ground state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03876
name: metastable state
def: "Any excited state which in principle\, by virtue of the selection rules\, cannot radiatively combine with any lower state. It usually has a considerably longer lifetime than an ordinary excited state."
synonym: "metastable" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03878
name: metathesis
def: "A bimolecular process formally involving the exchange of a bond \(or bonds\) between similar interacting chemical species so that the bonding affiliations in the products are identical \(or closely similar\) to those in the reactants. For example\:  \(The term has its origin in inorganic chemistry with a different meaning\, but this older usage is not applicable in physical organic chemistry.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03879
name: methanogens
def: "Strictly anaerobic archaebacteria\, able to use only a very limited substrate spectrum \(e.g. molecular hydrogen\, formate\, methanol\, methylamine\, carbon monoxide or acetate\) as electron donors for the reduction of carbon dioxide to methane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03880
name: method of isotopic perturbation
def: "NMR shift difference measurement of the isotope effect on a fast \(degenerate\) equilibrium between two species which are equivalent except for isotopic substitution. This can be used to distinguish a rapidly equilibrating mixture with time-averaged symmetry from a single structure with higher symmetry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03883
name: methylotrophic microorganisms
def: "Organisms that use\, as substrates for growth\, $\\ce\{C\}$ compounds containing carbon in a lower oxidation state than in carbon dioxide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03884
name: metre
def: "The metre\, symbol \\\(\\pu\{m\}\\\)\, is the SI unit of length. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the speed of light in a vacuum \\\(c\\\) to be \\\(\\pu\{299792458\}\\\) when expressed in the unit \\\(\\pu\{m s-1\}\\\)\, where the second is defined in terms of the caesium frequency \\\(\\Delta \nu_\{\\ce\{Cs\}\}\\\)."
synonym: "meter" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03885
name: micellar catalysis
def: "The acceleration of a chemical reaction in solution by the addition of a surfactant at a concentration higher than its critical micelle concentration so that the reaction can proceed in the environment of surfactant aggregates \(micelles\). \(Rate enhancements may be due\, for example\, to higher concentration of the reactants in that environment\, more favourable orientation and solvation of the species\, or enhanced rate constants in the micellar pseudophase of the surfactant aggregate.\) Micelle formation can also lead to a decreased reaction rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03886
name: micellar mass
def: "The term usually refers to a neutral micelle and therefore includes an equivalent amount of counter-ions with the surfactant ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03887
name: micellar solubilization
def: "In a system formed by a solvent\, an association colloid and at least one other component \(the solubilizate\)\, the incorporation of this other component into or on the micelles is called micellar solubilization\, or\, briefly\, solubilization."
synonym: "solubilization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03889
name: micelle
def: "Surfactants in solution are often association colloids\, that is\, they tend to form aggregates of colloidal dimensions\, which exist in equilibrium with the molecules or ions from which they are formed. Such aggregates are termed micelles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03890
name: micelle charge
def: "Usually understood to include the net charge of the surfactant ions and the counterions bound to the micelle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03891
name: Michaelis constant
def: "Substance concentration of substrate at which the rate of reaction is equal to one half of the limiting rate \(maximum rate\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03892
name: Michaelis–Menten kinetics
def: "The dependence of an initial rate of reaction upon the concentration of a substrate S that is present in large excess over the concentration of an enzyme or other catalyst \(or reagent\) E with the appearance of saturation behaviour following the Michaelis-Menten equation\: \\\[\nu=\\frac\{V\\ \\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\}\{K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}+\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\}\\\] where \\\(\nu\\\) is the observed initial rate\, \\\(V\\\) is its limiting value at substrate saturation \(i.e. \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\\gg K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)\)\, and \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) the substrate concentration when \\\(\nu = \\frac\{V\}\{2\}\\\). The definition is experimental\, i.e. it applies to any reaction that follows an equation of this general form. The symbols \\\(V_\{\\max \}\\\) or \\\(\nu_\{\\max \}\\\) are sometimes used for \\\(V\\\). The parameters \\\(V\\\) and \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) \(the 'Michaelis constant'\) of the equation can be evaluated from the slope and intercept of a linear plot of \\\(\nu^\{-1\}\\\) vs. \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]^\{-1\}\\\) \(a 'Lineweaver–Burk plot'\) or from slope and intercept of a linear plot of \\\(\nu\\\) vs. \\\(\\frac\{\nu\}\{\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\}\\\) \('Eadie–Hofstee plot'\). A Michaelis–Menten equation is also applicable to the condition where E is present in large excess\, in which case the concentration \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{E\}\\right\]\\\) appears in the equation instead of \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{S\}\\right\]\\\). The term has sometimes been used to describe reactions that proceed according to the scheme\: \\\[\\rm\{E\}+\\rm\{S\}\\overset\{k_\{1\}\}\{\\underset\{k_\{-1\}\}\\rightleftarrows \}\\rm\{ES\}\\overset\{k_\{\\rm\{cat\}\}\}\{\\rightarrow \}\\rm\{Products\}\\\] in which case \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\} = \\frac\{k_\{-1\}+k_\{\\rm\{cat\}\}\}\{k_\{1\}\}\\\) \(Briggs–Haldane conditions\). It has more usually been applied only to the special case in which \\\(k_\{-1\}\\gg k_\{\\rm\{cat\}\}\\\) and \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\} = \\frac\{k_\{-1\}\}\{k_\{1\}\}=K_\{s\}\\\); in this case \\\(K_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) is a true dissociation constant \(Michaelis–Menten conditions\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03893
name: Michaelis–Menten mechanism
def: "The Michaelis–Menten mechanism is the simplest mechanism that will explain Michaelis–Menten kinetics. According to the mechanism\, a substrate A first combines with a molecule of enzyme E\, and this process is followed by a step in which the enzyme-substrate complex EA breaks down \(sometimes with the participation of the solvent\) into enzyme and reaction products\: \\\[\\rm\{E\}+\\rm\{A\}\\overset\{k_\{1\}\}\{\\underset\{k_\{-1\}\}\\rightleftharpoons \}\\rm\{EA\}\\overset\{k_\{2\}\}\{\\rightarrow \}\\rm\{E\}+\\rm\{Products\}\\\] If\, as is usual\, the substrate A is present in great excess of the enzyme it can be shown that steady-state conditions apply\, and that the rate equation is\: \\\[v = \\frac\{k_\{2\}\\ \\left\[\\rm\{E\}\\right\]_\{0\}\\ \\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]\}\{\\frac\{k_\{-1\}\\\,+\\\,k_\{2\}\}\{k_\{1\}\}\\\,+\\\,\\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]\}\\\] where \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{E\}\\right\]_\{0\}\\\) is the total concentration of enzyme. This equation is of the form of the Michaelis–Menten equation. Other\, more complicated\, mechanisms lead to the Michaelis–Menten equation\, adherence to which therefore does not require that the Michaelis– Menten mechanism applies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03894
name: micro
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-6\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03895
name: microbial leaching
def: "A process for the solubilization of metals\, mostly from poor ores\, by lithotrophic bacteria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03896
name: microcanonical rate constant
def: "A rate constant calculated by microcanonical variational transition-state theory and therefore a rate constant that relates to a fixed energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03897
name: microcanonical variational transition-state theory
def: "A development of transition-state theory in which the dividing surface is varied so as to minimize the rate calculated for a fixed energy. The rate expressions obtained in a microcanonical treatment can be integrated over the energy\, taking into account the statistical distribution over energy states\, so as to give the canonical\, or thermal\, rates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03898
name: microcarrier
def: "A small\, beaded material\, derived from silica\, glass\, dextran or similar materials\, used for the immobilization of biocatalysts or as a support for the culture of anchorage-dependent animal cell lines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03899
name: microclimatology
def: "The science that deals with the climate of restricted areas and investigates their phenomena and causes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03900
name: microelectrophoresis
def: "The electrophoresis technique involving the movement of a mass of particles on a small scale \(e.g. paper electrophoresis\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03901
name: microgel
def: "Particle of gel of any shape with an equivalent diameter of approximately \\\(0.1\\\) to \\\(100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)."
synonym: "gel microparticle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03902
name: microheterogeneity
def: "A particular glycoprotein may occur in forms that differ in the structure of one or more or its carbohydrate units\, a phenomenon known as microheterogeneity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03903
name: micrometeorology
def: "The study of the meteorological processes on scales from a millimeter or less up to tens or hundreds of metres; e.g. meteorology of a local site that is usually small and often is confined to a shallow layer of air next to the ground."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03904
name: micro-network
def: "Polymer network that has dimensions of the order of \\\(1\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) to \\\(1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03905
name: microphotometer
def: "The optical instrument used to measure transmittance in atomic emission spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03906
name: micropore
def: "Pore with width not exceeding about \\\(2.0\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) \(\\\(20\\ \\mathrm\{Å\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03907
name: micropore filling
def: "The process in which molecules are adsorbed in the adsorption space within micropores."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03908
name: micropore volume
def: "Conventionally measured by the volume of the adsorbed material which completely fills the micropores expressed in terms of bulk liquid at atmospheric pressure and at the temperature of measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03909
name: microporous carbon
def: "A porous carbon material\, usually a char or carbon fibres\, which may or may not have been subjected to an activation process to increase its adsorptive properties. A microporous carbon is considered to have a major part of its porosity in pores of less than \\\(2\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) width and to exhibit apparent surface areas usually higher than \\\(200\\ \\\) to \\\(300\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{2\}\\ \\rm\{g\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03911
name: microscopic cross-section
def: "A measure of the probability of a specified interaction or reaction between an incident radiation and a target particle or system of particles. It is the reaction rate per target particle for a specified process divided by the flux of the incident radiation. In general\, a specification is added of the type of radiation \(e.g. neutron\, photon\)\, the energy of the incident radiation \(e.g. thermal\, epithermal\, fast\) and the type of interaction of reaction \(e.g. activation\, fission\, scattering\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03912
name: microscopic diffusion control
def: "The observable consequence of the limitation that the rate of a bimolecular chemical reaction in a homogeneous medium cannot exceed the rate of encounter of the reacting molecular entities. If \(hypothetically\) a bimolecular reaction in a homogeneous medium occurred instantaneously when two reactant molecular entities made an encounter\, the rate of reaction would be an encounter-controlled rate\, determined solely by rates of diffusion of reactants. Such a hypothetical 'fully diffusion controlled rate' is also said to correspond to 'total microscopic diffusion control'\, and represents the asymptotic limit of the rate of reaction as the rate constant for the chemical conversion of the encounter pair into product \(or products\) becomes large relative to the rate constant for separation \(or dissociation\) of the encounter pair. 'Partial microscopic diffusion control' is said to operate in a homogeneous reaction when the rates of chemical conversion and of separation are comparable. \(The degree of microscopic diffusion control cannot usually be determined with any precision.\)"
synonym: "diffusion control" []
synonym: "encounter control" []
synonym: "transport control" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03913
name: microscopic electrophoresis
def: "A technique in which the electrophoresis of individual particles is observed with the aid of a microscope or ultra-microscope."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03914
name: microscopic film
def: "A film which has lateral dimensions in the range \\\(0.1\{-\}100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03915
name: microscopic kinetics
def: "This term applies to a kinetic study in which the emphasis is on the interpretation of the behaviour in terms of elementary reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03916
name: microscopic reversibility at equilibrium
def: "The principle of microscopic reversibility at equilibrium states that\, in a system at equilibrium\, any molecular process and the reverse of that process occur\, on the average\, at the same rate. This definition corresponds to the statement of the principle that was given by R.C. Tolman in 1924. However\, many workers have interchanged the meanings of microscopic reversibility and detailed balance\, and it seems best now to regard the two\, which are closely related\, as synonymous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03917
name: microsome
def: "Artefactual spherical particle\, not present in the living cell\, derived from pieces of the endoplasmic reticulum present in homogenates of tissues or cells\: microsomes sediment from such homogenates when centrifuged at \\\(10^\{6\}\\ \\rm\{g\}\\\) and higher\: the microsomal fraction obtained in this way is often used as a source of mono-oxygenase enzymes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03918
name: middle atmosphere
def: "The combined stratosphere and mesosphere in the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03919
name: Mie scattering
def: "The scattering of electromagnetic radiation by spherical particles of any size \\\(r\\\)\, relative to the wavelength\, \\\(\\lambda \\\). Since the cases \\\(r\\ll \\lambda \\\) and \\\(r\\gg \\lambda \\\) are covered by Rayleigh \(dipole\) scattering and geometric scattering theories\, respectively\, Mie scattering often refers to the case of \\\(r\\approx \\lambda \\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03920
name: migration
def: "The \(usually intramolecular\) transfer of an atom or group during the course of a molecular rearrangement.The movement of a bond to a new position\, within the same molecular entity is known as 'bond migration'. Allylic rearrangements\, e.g.  exemplify both types of migration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03921
name: migration current
def: "The difference between the current that is actually obtained\, at any particular value of the potential of the indicator or working electrode\, for the reduction or oxidation of an ionic electroactive substance and the current that would be obtained\, at the same potential\, if there were no transport of that substance due to the electric field between the electrodes. The sign convention regarding current is such that the migration current is negative for the reduction of a cation or for the oxidation of an anion\, and positive for the oxidation of a cation or the reduction of an anion. Hence the migration current may tend to either increase or decrease the total current observed. In any event the migration current approaches zero as the transport number of the electroactive substance is decreased by increasing the concentration of the supporting electrolyte\, and hence the conductivity"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03923
name: migratory aptitude
def: "The term is applied to characterize the relative tendency of a group to participate in a rearrangement. In nucleophilic rearrangements \( migration to an electron-deficient centre\)\, the migratory aptitude of a group is loosely related to its capacity to stabilize a partial positive charge\, but exceptions are known\, and the position of hydrogen in the series is often unpredictable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03924
name: migratory insertion
def: "A combination of migration and insertion. The term is mainly used in organometallic chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03925
name: milli
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-3\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{m\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03926
name: milligram equivalent of readability
def: "The product of readability and the value of the scale division \(in \\\(\\rm\{mg\}\\\) per division\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03927
name: millimetre of mercury
def: "Non-SI unit for pressure; \\\(\\rm\{mmHg\}\\approx 133.322\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\). This old unit is not recognized by CIPM or ISO\, but it is used for blood pressure."
synonym: "millimeter of mercury" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03928
name: milling
def: "The mechanical reduction of the particle size of a sample by attribution \(friction\)\, impact or cutting. The required particle size of a sample is related to the size of the test portion and the number of particles required to ensure homogeneity among test portions. The reduction in particle size may sometimes result in particles of different hardness and density\, which produces inhomogeneity during the preparation of the test sample or during the withdrawal of the test portion."
synonym: "grinding" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03929
name: minimum consumption time
def: "The time during which nebulization must be carried out in order to perform one analysis with a given precision. The minimum consumption time is equal to the minimum volume consumed of the prepared sample divided by the rate of fluid consumption in order to obtain a given precision. It can be reduced by means of integrating the signal or measuring the signal increments \(kinetic methods\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03930
name: minimum density of states criterion
def: "This is a criterion used in Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel–Marcus \(RRKM\) theory for locating the transition state. The reaction coordinate \\\(r\\\) at the transition state\, according to this criterion\, is given by \\\(\\frac\{\\partial \(N\\left\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\right\)\)\}\{\\partial r\}=0\\\)\, where \\\(N\\left\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\right\)\\\) is the density of states at an internal energy \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03931
name: minimum-energy reaction path
def: "Path orthogonal to the equipotential contours of a potential energy surface that connects the energy minima through a saddle point \(transition state\) from which it slopes downwards along the steepest descending lines in \\\(3N-6\\\) configurational space \(\\\(N\\\) is the number of nuclei in the reacting system\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03932
name: minimum lethal concentration
def: "Lowest concentration of a toxic substance in an environmental medium that kills individual organisms or test species under a defined set of conditions. Synonymous with lowest lethal concentration found."
synonym: "lowest lethal concentration found" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03933
name: minimum lethal dose
def: "Lowest amount of a substance that\, when introduced into the body\, may cause death to individual species of test animals under a defined set of conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03935
name: minute
def: "Non-SI unit of plane angle. \\\[1^\{\\prime\} = \(1/60\)^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} = 2.908\\ 882\\\,...\\\,\\times 10^\{-4\}\\rm\{rad\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03936
name: mist
def: "A qualitative term applied to a suspension of droplets in a gas. In the atmosphere a mist produces a generally thin\, greyish veil over the landscape. It reduces visibility to a lesser extent than fog but somewhat more than haze \(visibility of less than \\\(2\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\) but greater than \\\(1\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03937
name: mitochondria
def: "Organelles appearing in all eukaryotic cells which produce ATP as useful energy for the cell by oxidative phosphorylation. The proteins for the adenosine 5'-triphosphate \(ATP\)-generating electron transport of the respiration chain are located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. Mitochondria contain many enzymes of the citric acid cycle and for fatty acid $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}oxidation\}$. Many of them are coded for by nuclear DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03938
name: mitosis
def: "The process whereby a cell nucleus divides into two daughter nuclei\, each having the same genetic complement as the parent cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03940
name: mixed crystal
def: "A crystal containing a second constituent which fits into and is distributed in the lattice of the host crystal. \(The use of 'solid solution' for amorphous materials is not recommended.\)"
synonym: "solid solution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03941
name: mixed energy release
def: "This is an effect found in a process A + BC when the attacking species A is heavy. Because A is heavy the A–B distance continues to decrease while the repulsive energy of B–C is being released."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03942
name: mixed indicator
def: "An indicator containing a supplementary dye selected to heighten the overall colour change."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03943
name: mixed labelled
def: "A specifically labelled compound is referred to as mixed labelled when the isotopically substituted compound has more than one kind of modified atom e.g. $\\ce\{CH3–CH2–\[^\{18\}O\]\[^\{2\}H\]\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03944
name: mixed potential
def: "The value of the potential of a given electrode with respect to a suitable reference electrode when appreciable contributions to the total anodic and/or cathodic partial currents are made by species belonging to two or more different couples\, but the total current is zero\, i.e. \\\[I=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}I_\{i\}=0\\qquad \(I_\{i\}\neq 0\)\\\] where \\\(I_\{i\}\\\) is the partial current of reaction \\\(i\\\) \\\[I_\{i\}=I_\{i\,a\}+I_\{i\,c\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03945
name: mixing
def: "The combining of components\, particles or layers into a more homogeneous state. The mixing may be achieved manually or mechanically by shifting the material with stirrers or pumps or by revolving or shaking the container. The process must not permit segregation of particles of different size or properties. homogeneity may be considered to have been achieved in a practical sense when the sampling error of the processed portion is negligible compared to the total error of the measurement system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03946
name: mixing control
def: "The experimental limitation of the rate of reaction in solution by the rate of mixing of solutions of the two reactants. It can occur even when the reaction rate constant is several powers of 10 less than that for an encounter-controlled rate. Analogous \(and even more important\) effects of the limitation of reaction rates by the speed of mixing are encountered in heterogeneous \(solid/liquid\, solid/gas\, liquid/gas\) systems."
synonym: "macroscopic diffusion control" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03947
name: mixing height
def: "The height to which significant mixing of added pollutants occurs within the atmosphere. In reference to stack gases\, it is considered the height at which stack effluent begins mixing with the atmosphere as it leaves the stack."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03948
name: mixing ratio
def: "In meteorology\, the dimensionless ratio of the mass of a substance \(such as water vapour\) in an air parcel to the mass of the remaining substances in the air parcel. For trace substances\, this is approximated by the ratio of the mass of the substance to the mass of air. However\, in the case of water vapour the mass of dry air is used. In atmospheric chemistry\, mixing ratios \(molecular\, molar\, by volume\, as well as by weight\) are used to describe relative concentrations of atmospheric trace gases and impurities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03949
name: mixture
def: "Portion of matter consisting of two or more chemical substances called constituents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03950
name: MLCT
def: "Abbreviation for Metal to Ligand Charge Transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03951
name: mobile adsorption
def: "A situation in which molecules of adsorbate are free to move about the surface. Mobility of the adsorbate will increase with temperature and mobile adsorption may be either localized or non-localized. In localized mobile adsorption the adsorbate spends most of the time on the adsorption sites but can migrate or be desorbed and readsorbed elsewhere. In non-localized adsorption the mobility is so great that a small fraction of the adsorbed species is on the desorption sites and a large fraction at other positions on the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03952
name: mobile phase
def: "A fluid which percolates through or along the stationary bed\, in a definite direction. It may be a liquid \(liquid chromatography\) or a gas \(gas chromatography\) or a supercritical fluid \(supercritical-fluid chromatography\). In gas chromatography the expression carrier gas may be used for the mobile phase. In elution chromatography the expression 'eluent' is also used for the mobile phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03954
name: mobile-phase velocity
def: "The linear velocity of the mobile phase across the average cross-section of the chromatographic bed or column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03955
name: mobility
def: "Drift velocity of electrons or holes divided by the electric field strength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03956
name: mobility
def: "The velocity of a particle per unit applied force."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03957
name: Möbius aromaticity
def: "A monocyclic array of orbitals in which there is a single out-of-phase overlap \(or\, more generally\, an odd number of out-of-phase overlaps\) reveals the opposite pattern of aromatic character to Hückel systems; with \\\(4\\\, n\\\) electrons it is stabilized \(aromatic\)\, whereas with \\\(4\\\, n+2\\\) it is destabilized \(antiaromatic\). In the excited state \\\(4\\\, n+2\\\) Möbius $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ systems are stabilized\, and \\\(4\\\, n\\\) systems are destabilized. No examples of ground-state Möbius $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ systems are known\, but the concept has been applied to transition states of pericyclic reactions. The name is derived from the topological analogy of such an arrangement of orbitals to a Möbius strip."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03958
name: mode
def: "The value of the variable occurring with the greatest frequency in the series of observations. A bimodal distribution is one which contains two maxima. The use of mode when reporting results of chemical analysis is generally not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03959
name: mode-locked laser
def: "A laser in which many resonant modes are coupled in phase\, to yield a train of very short pulses \(e.g. ps pulses\). The coupling of the modes is obtained by modulation of the gain in the resonator\, and can be active \(electro-optic modulation of the losses or of the pump intensity\)\, or passive \(with a saturable absorber\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03960
name: moderation
def: "Reduction of the neutron energy by scattering without appreciable capture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03961
name: moderator
def: "A material used to reduce the neutron energy by scattering without appreciable capture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03962
name: modified active solid
def: "An active solid the sorptive properties of which have been changed by some treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03963
name: modified Arrhenius equation
def: "This is an extension of the simple Arrhenius equation in which the pre-exponential factor is proportional to \\\(T^\{n\}\\\) where \\\(T\\\) is the temperature and \\\(n\\\) a constant\: \\\[k = B\\ T^\{n\}\\ \\exp\(-E_\{\\rm\{a\}\}/RT\)\\\] \\\(B\\\) is a temperature-independent constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03964
name: modified sample
def: "A sample or a known fraction of the parent population in which the analyte has been isolated or \(usually\) concentrated before being submitted to the laboratory. If the isolation or concentration occurs in the laboratory\, the procedure is usually considered part of the preparation of the test sample from the laboratory sample \(in-laboratory processing\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03965
name: modifier
def: "A substance added to a solvent to improve its properties e.g. by increasing the solubility of an extractant\, changing interfacial parameters or reducing adsorption losses. Additives used to enhance extraction rates should be called accelerators or catalysts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03966
name: modulus of elasticity
def: "The normal stress divided by linear strain."
synonym: "Young's modulus" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03967
name: Mohr amplification process
def: "The process in which iodide is oxidized to iodate to achieve a more favourable measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03968
name: moiety
def: "In physical organic chemistry moiety is generally used to signify part of a molecule\, e.g. in an ester $\\ce\{R1COOR2\}$ the alcohol moiety is $\\ce\{R2O\}$. The term should not be used for a small fragment of a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03970
name: molality
def: "Amount of entities of a solute divided by the mass of the solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03971
name: molar
def: "Adjective before the name of an extensive quantity generally meaning division by amount of substance to make it intensive. Molar volume is volume divided by amount of substance. In a few special cases molar is used to denote division by amount concentration \(e.g. molar absorption coefficient\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03972
name: molar absorption coefficient
def: "Absorbance divided by the absorption pathlength\, \\\(l\\\)\, and the amount concentration\, \\\(c\\\)\: \\\[\\varepsilon\(\\lambda\) = \\frac\{1\}\{c\\\, l\}\\\, \\rm\{lg\}\\! \\left \( \\frac\{P_\{\\lambda\}^\{0\}\}\{P_\{\\lambda\}\}\\right \) = \\frac\{A\(\\lambda\)\}\{c\\\, l\}\\\] where \\\(P_\{\\lambda\}^\{0\}\\\) and \\\(P_\{\\lambda\}\\\) are\, respectively\, the incident and transmitted spectral radiant power."
synonym: "molar decadic absorption coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03974
name: molar activity
def: "For a specified isotope\, the activity of the compound divided by the amount of the material in moles\: \\\(A_\{\\rm\{m\}\}=\\frac\{A\}\{n\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03977
name: molarity
def: "Term sometimes used for amount concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03978
name: molar-mass exclusion limit
def: "The maximum value of the molar mass or molecular weight of molecules or particles\, in a specific polymer solvent system\, that can enter into the pores of the porous non-adsorbing material used in size-exclusion chromatography. For particles with molar mass or molecular weight larger than the exclusion limit the separation effect of the size-exclusion chromatography vanishes."
synonym: "molecular-weight exclusion limit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03979
name: molar refraction
def: "Function of the refractive index\, \\\(n\\\)\, given by \\\(R = V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\ \\frac\{n^\{2\}\\\,-\\\,1\}\{n^\{2\}\\\,+\\\,2\}\\\)\, where \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) is the molar volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03980
name: mole
def: "The mole\, symbol mol\, is the SI unit of amount of substance. One mole contains exactly \\\(\\pu\{6.02214076E23\}\\\) elementary entities. This number is the fixed numerical value of the Avogadro constant\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\, when expressed in the unit \\\(\\pu\{mol-1\}\\\) and is called the Avogadro number.\nThe amount of substance\, symbol \\\(n\\\)\, of a system is a measure of the number of specified elementary entities. An elementary entity may be an atom\, a molecule\, an ion\, an electron\, any other particle or specified group of particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03982
name: molecular beams
def: "A molecular beam is produced by allowing a gas at higher pressure to expand through a small orifice into a container at lower pressure. The result is a beam of particles \(atoms\, free radicals\, molecules or ions\) moving at approximately equal velocities\, with few collisions occurring between them. In a crossed molecular-beam experiment a reaction is studied using collimated beams of reactant molecules. For a bimolecular reaction\, beams of the two reactants are caused to impinge on one another\, often at an angle of 90°. In a beam-gas scattering experiment a collimated beam is introduced into a gas\, and the scattering patterns are observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03984
name: molecular conformation
def: "The conformation of the macromolecule as a whole. In the polymer literature\, molecular conformation is sometimes referred to as macroconformation. In molecular conformations involving parallel stems\, the latter may be confined to the same crystal or may also extend over several crystals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03985
name: molecular dynamics
def: "Synonymous with reaction dynamics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03986
name: molecular entity
def: "Any constitutionally or isotopically distinct atom\, molecule\, ion\, ion pair\, radical\, radical ion\, complex\, conformer etc.\, identifiable as a separately distinguishable entity. Molecular entity is used in this Compendium as a general term for singular entities\, irrespective of their nature\, while chemical species stands for sets or ensembles of molecular entities. Note that the name of a compound may refer to the respective molecular entity or to the chemical species\, e.g. methane\, may mean a single molecule of $\\ce\{CH4\}$ \(molecular entity\) or a molar amount\, specified or not \(chemical species\)\, participating in a reaction. The degree of precision necessary to describe a molecular entity depends on the context. For example 'hydrogen molecule' is an adequate definition of a certain molecular entity for some purposes\, whereas for others it is necessary to distinguish the electronic state and/or vibrational state and/or nuclear spin\, etc. of the hydrogen molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03987
name: molecular formula
def: "For compounds consisting of discrete molecules\, a formula according with the relative molecular mass \(or the structure\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03988
name: molecular ion
def: "An ion formed by the removal from \(positive ions\) or addition to \(negative ions\) a molecule of one or more electrons without fragmentation of the molecular structure. The mass of this ion corresponds to the sum of the masses of the most abundant naturally occurring isotopes of the various atoms that make up the molecule \(with a correction for the masses of the electron\(s\) lost or gained\). For example\, the mass of the molecular ion of ethyl bromide $\\ce\{C2H5^\{79\}Br\}$ will be \\\(2\\times 12\\\) plus \\\(5\\times 1.0078246\\\) plus 78.91839 minus the mass of the electron \(\\\(m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\). This is equal to \\\(107.95751\\ \\rm\{u\}-m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\)\, \\\(u\\\) being the unified atomic mass unit based on the standard that the mass of the isotope $\\ce\{^\{12\}C\}$ = \\\(12\\ \\rm\{u\}\\\) exactly."
synonym: "molecular anion" []
synonym: "molecular cation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03989
name: molecularity
def: "The number of reactant molecular entities that are involved in the 'microscopic chemical event' constituting an elementary reaction. \(For reactions in solution this number is always taken to exclude molecular entities that form part of the medium and which are involved solely by virtue of their solvation of solutes.\) A reaction with a molecularity of one is called 'unimolecular'\, one with a molecularity of two 'bimolecular' and of three 'termolecular'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03990
name: molecular kinetics
def: "This term is applied to a kinetic study in which there is emphasis on the behaviour of the system at the molecular level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03991
name: molecular laser
def: "A gas laser which makes use of electronic\, vibrational or rotational energy levels in molecules. The molecules can be excited by an electric discharge\, as in the case of the $\\ce\{N2\}$ or $\\ce\{CO2\}$ laser\, by chemical reactions\, as in the $\\ce\{HF\}$ or $\\ce\{DF\}$ lasers\, or by another laser\, as in the $\\ce\{CH3F\}$ far IR laser."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03993
name: molecular mechanics calculation
def: "An empirical calculational method intended to give estimates of structures and energies for conformations of molecules. The method is based on the assumption of 'natural' bond lengths and angles\, deviation from which leads to strain\, and the existence of torsional interactions and attractive and/or repulsive van der Waals and dipolar forces between non-bonded atoms. The method is also called '\(empirical\) force-field calculations'."
synonym: "force-field calculation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03994
name: molecular metal
def: "A non-metallic material whose properties resemble those of metals\, usually following oxidative doping; e.g. polyacetylene following oxidative doping with iodine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03995
name: molecular nucleation
def: "The initial crystallization of a small portion of a macromolecule\, after which further crystallization is thermodynamically favoured. Molecular nucleation may give rise to a new crystal or increase the size of a pre-existing one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03996
name: molecular orbital
def: "A one-electron wavefunction describing an electron moving in the effective field provided by the nuclei and all other electrons of a molecular entity of more than one atom. Such molecular orbitals can be transformed in prescribed ways into component functions to give 'localized molecular orbitals'. Molecular orbitals can also be described\, in terms of the number of nuclei \(or 'centres'\) encompassed\, as two-centre\, multi-centre\, etc. molecular orbitals\, and are often expressed as a linear combination of atomic orbitals. An orbital is usually depicted by sketching contours on which the wavefunction has a constant value \(contour map\) or by indicating schematically the envelope of the region of space in which there is an arbitrarily fixed high \(say 96%\) probability of finding the electron occupying the orbital\, giving also the algebraic sign \(+ or −\) of the wavefunction in each part of that region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03997
name: molecular rearrangement
def: "The term is traditionally applied to any reaction that involves a change of connectivity \(sometimes including hydrogen\)\, and violates the so-called 'principle of minimum structural change'. According to this oversimplified principle\, chemical species do not isomerize in the course of a transformation\, e.g. substitution\, or the change of a functional group of a chemical species into a different functional group is not expected to involve the making or breaking of more than the minimum number of bonds required to effect that transformation. For example\, any new substituents are expected to enter the precise positions previously occupied by displaced groups. The simplest type of rearrangement is an intramolecular reaction in which the product is isomeric with the reactant \(one type of 'intramolecular isomerization'\). An example is the first step of the Claisen rearrangement\:  The definition of molecular rearrangement includes changes in which there is a migration of an atom or bond \(unexpected on the basis of the principle of minimum structural change\)\, as in the reaction\:  where the rearrangement stage can formally be represented as the '1\,2-shift' of hydride between adjacent carbon atoms in the carbocation\:  Such migrations occur also in radicals\, e.g.\:  The definition also includes reactions in which an entering group takes up a different position from the leaving group\, with accompanying migration. An example of the latter type is the 'allylic rearrangement'\:  A distinction is made between 'intramolecular rearrangements' \(or 'true molecular rearrangements'\) and 'intermolecular rearrangements' \(or 'apparent rearrangements'\). In the former case the atoms and groups that are common to a reactant and a product never separate into independent fragments during the rearrangement stage \(i.e. the change is intramolecular\)\, whereas in an 'intermolecular rearrangement' a migrating group is completely free from the parent molecule and is re-attached to a different position in a subsequent step\, as in the Orton reaction\:"
synonym: "rearrangement" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03998
name: molecular sieve effect
def: "With respect to porous solids\, the surface associated with pores communicating with the outside space may be called the internal surface. Because the accessibility of ores may depend on the size of the fluid molecules\, the extent of the internal surface may depend on the size of the molecules comprising the fluid\, and may be different for the various components of a fluid mixture. This effect is known as the molecular sieve effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M03999
name: molecular spectra
def: "Spectra formed by bands consisting of rotational lines originating from rotational\, vibrational and electronic transitions of molecules. These may be emission or absorption spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04002
name: molecule
def: "An electrically neutral entity consisting of more than one atom \(\\\(n>1\\\)\). Rigorously\, a molecule\, in which \\\(n>1\\\) must correspond to a depression on the potential energy surface that is deep enough to confine at least one vibrational state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04004
name: molozonides
def: "1\,2\,3-Trioxolanes\, the primary products of the reaction of ozone at a carbon–carbon double bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04005
name: moment of a force
def: "About a point is the vector product of the radius vector from this point to a point on the line of action of the force and the force\, \\\(M=r\\times F\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04006
name: moment of inertia
def: "Of a body about an axis is the sum of the products of its mass elements and the squares of their distances to the axis. principal moments of inertia of a molecule are chosen so that \\\(I_\{A\}\\leq I_\{B\}\\leq I_\{C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04007
name: momentum
def: "Vector quantity equal to the product of mass and velocity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04008
name: momentum spectrum
def: "A spectrum which is obtained when a beam of ions is separated according to the momentum-to-charge ratios of the ionic species contained within it. A sector magnetic field achieves separation of the various ionic species in this way. If the ion beam is homogeneous in translational energy\, as is the case with sector instruments\, separation according to the \\\(\\frac\{m\}\{z\}\\\) ratios is also achieved and a mass spectrum is obtained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04009
name: monochromator
def: "Optical device that transmits a mechanically selectable narrow band of wavelengths of electromagnetic radiation chosen from a wider range of wavelengths available at the input."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04010
name: monoclonal antibodies
def: "A single species of immunoglobulin molecules produced by culturing a single clone of a hybridoma cell. MAbs recognize only one chemical structure\, i.e. they are directed against a single epitope of the antigenic substance used to raise the antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04011
name: monodisperse medium
def: "A colloidal system in which all particles are of \(nearly\) the same size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04013
name: mono-energetic radiation
def: "Radiation consisting of particles of a single kinetic energy or photons of a single energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04014
name: monoisotopic mass spectrum
def: "A spectrum containing only ions made up of the principal isotopes of atoms making up the original molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04015
name: monolayer
def: "A single\, closely packed layer of atoms or molecules. The term floating monolayer is used for certain spread monolayers or films which are in the condensed — often solid — state. The term 'Langmuir monolayer' has recently been coined for spread monolayers. This usage is not recommended."
synonym: "Langmuir monolayer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04016
name: monolayer capacity
def: "For chemisorption the amount of adsorbate which is needed to occupy all adsorption sites as determined by the structure of the adsorbent and by the chemical nature of the absorptive. For physisorption\, the amount needed to cover the surface with a complete monolayer of atoms or molecules in close-packed array\, the kind of close packing having to be stated explicitly when necessary."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04017
name: monomer
def: "A substance composed of monomer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04018
name: monomeric unit
def: "The largest constitutional unit contributed by a single monomer molecule to the structure of a macromolecule or oligomer molecule."
synonym: "mer" []
synonym: "monomer unit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04019
name: monomer molecule
def: "A molecule which can undergo polymerization thereby contributing constitutional units to the essential structure of a macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04021
name: monosaccharides
def: "A term which includes aldoses\, ketoses and a wide variety of derivatives. Derivation includes oxidation\, deoxygenation\, introduction of other substituents\, alkylation and acylation of hydroxy groups\, and chain branching. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04022
name: monotectic reaction
def: "The reversible transition\, on cooling\, of a liquid to a mixture of a second liquid and a solid\: \\\[\\rm\{liquid\}_\{1\}\\rightleftarrows \\rm\{liquid\}_\{2\}+\\rm\{solid\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04023
name: monotectoid reaction
def: "A reaction in a system containing two solid solution phases\, \\\(\\alpha\\mathit\{'\}\\\) and \\\(\\alpha\\mathit\{''\}\\\) in which \\\(\\alpha\\mathit\{'\}\\\) decomposes into \\\(\\alpha\\mathit\{''\}\\\) and a new phase \\\(\\beta \\\)\: \\\[\\alpha\\mathit\{'\}\\rightleftharpoons \\alpha\\mathit\{''\}+\\beta \\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04024
name: monotectoid temperature
def: "The maximum temperature at which a monotectoid reaction can occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04026
name: monoterpenoids
def: "Terpenoids having a $\\ce\{C10\}$ skeleton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04028
name: monotropic transition
def: "The irreversible transition from a metastable polymorphic form to the stable polymorph. Example\: The transition of metastable $\\ce\{CaCO3\}$ \(aragonite-type\) to the stable $\\ce\{CaCO3\}$ \(calcite-type\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04029
name: mordant
def: "Substance that fixes a dyestuff in or on a material by combining with the dye to form an insoluble compound\, used to fix or intensify stains in a tissue or cell preparation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04030
name: More O'Ferrall–Jencks diagram
def: "Visualization of the potential energy surfaces for a reacting system\, as a function of two chosen coordinates. It is particularly useful to discuss structural effects on the transition state geometry for processes occurring either by stepwise or concerted routes. The use of such diagrams\, first suggested for elimination reactions\, was later extended to acid–base catalysis and to certain other reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04031
name: Morin transition
def: "A transition specific to $\\ce\{\\upalpha-Fe2O3\}$ in which there is a change in the direction of the atomic magnetic moments in the antiferromagnetic state from parallel to perpendicular to the c-axis. Synonymous with spin-flop transition."
synonym: "spin-flop transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04032
name: morphotropic transition
def: "An abrupt change in the structure of a solid solution with variation in composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04033
name: Mössbauer effect
def: "Resonance absorption of γ-radiation by nuclei arranged in a crystal lattice in such a way that the recoil momentum is shared by many atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04034
name: Mössbauer thickness
def: "The effective thickness of a source \(\\\(t_\{s\}\\\)\) or absorber \(\\\(t_\{a\}\\\)\) in the optical path."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04035
name: most probable distribution
def: "A discrete distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form\: \\\[f_\{\\rm\{w\}\}\(x\) = a^\{2\}\\ x\\ \(1- a\)^\{x- 1\}\\\] where \\\(x\\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length\, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization and \\\(a\\\) is a positive adjustable parameter. For large values of \\\(x\\\)\, the most probable distribution converges to the particular case of the Schulz–Zimm distribution with \\\(b=1\\\). In the literature\, this distribution is sometimes referred to as the Flory distribution or the Schulz–Flory distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04037
name: Mott transition
def: "A transition occurring only in 'single-valent' systems from strongly correlated \(\\\(U > W\\\)\) to weakly correlated \(\\\(U < W\\\)\) electrons as a result of a change of bandwidth \\\(W\\\). \\\(W\\\) reflects the strength of the interatomic interactions in a periodic array of like atoms and \\\(U\\\) is a measure of the intra-atomic interactions\, i.e. the electrostatic energy involved in the creation of polar states by transferring an electron from one atom to the next\, as shown schematically by the disproportionation reaction\: \\\[\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}+\\rm\{M\}^\{n+\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{M\}^\{\(n+1\)+\}+\\rm\{M\}^\{\(n- 1\)+\}\\\] A \\\(U > W\\\) produces a magnetic semiconductor; a \\\(U < W\\\) gives rise to a metal \(normally nonmagnetic\). Note also that \\\(U\\\) and \\\(W\\\) can be altered by changes in interatomic distances\, brought about through temperature or pressure variations or by introducing an alloying element. Synonymous with Mott–Hubbard transition."
synonym: "Mott–Hubbard transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04041
name: mucopolysaccharides
def: "Polysaccharides composed of alternating units from uronic acids and glycosamines\, and commonly partially esterified with sulfuric acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04042
name: multi-centre bond
def: "Representation of some molecular entities solely by localized two-electron two-centre bonds appears to be unsatisfactory. Instead\, multi-centre bonds have to be considered in which electron pairs occupy orbitals encompassing three or more atomic centres. Examples include the three-centre bonds in diborane\, the delocalized $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonding\}$ of benzene and bridged carbocations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04043
name: multi-centre reaction
def: "A synonym for pericyclic reaction. The number of 'centres' is the number of atoms not bonded initially\, between which single bonds are breaking or new bonds are formed in the transition state. This number does not necessarily correspond to the ring size of the transition state for the pericyclic reaction. Thus\, a Diels–Alder reaction is a 'four-centre reaction'. This terminology has largely been superseded by the more detailed one developed for the various pericyclic reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04044
name: multi-channel pulse height analyser
def: "A pulse amplitude analyser which includes a storage function to record the number of pulses received per channel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04046
name: multienzyme
def: "A protein possessing more than one catalytic function contributed by distinct parts of a polypeptide chain \('domains'\)\, or by distinct subunits\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04047
name: multienzyme complex
def: "A multienzyme with catalytic domains on more than one type of polypeptide chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04048
name: multienzyme polypeptide
def: "A polypeptide chain containing at least two types of catalytic domains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04049
name: multilayer
def: "A system of adjacent layers or monolayers. The term bilayer applies to the particular case of a multilayer two monolayers thick. Monolayers and multilayers may be alternatively named 'films' provided boundaries can be defined for them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04050
name: multilayer adsorption
def: "Adsorption such that the adsorption space accommodates more than one layer of molecules\, and not all adsorbed molecules are in contact with the surface layer of the adsorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04051
name: multilayer aggregate
def: "A stack of lamellar crystals generated by spiral growth at one or more screw dislocations. The axial displacement over a full turn of the screw \(Burgers vector\) is usually equal to one lamellar thickness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04053
name: multiphoton ionization
def: "Occurs when an atom or molecule and their concomitant ions have energy states whereby the energy in two or more photons is absorbed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04054
name: multiphoton process
def: "A process involving interaction of two or more photons with a molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04056
name: multiple-pass cell
def: "Permits multiple passage of radiation to increase the absorption pathlength. It is constructed in such a way that mirrors either form part of the cell or are mounted in the sample cell holder."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04057
name: multiple peak scanning
def: "An alternative to multiple ion detection which emphasizes that monitoring of peaks rather than detection of ions is the important feature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04058
name: multiple scattering
def: "Successive rescattering of radiation within the scattering medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04059
name: multiplex spectrometer
def: "A spectrometer in which a single photodetector simultaneously receives signals from different spectral bands which are specifically encoded. In the case of frequency multiplexing\, each spectral band is modulated at a specific frequency. Decoding is achieved by filtering out\, by electronic means\, the corresponding signals. Frequency multiplexing may be realized by changing the path difference between the two interfering beams at a uniform rate. Fourier transform of the interferogram so obtained yields the spectrum. This method is called Fourier transform spectrometry \(FTS\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04061
name: multiplicative name
def: "A name that expresses the multiple occurrence of identical parent structures\, two or more of which are connected by a symmetrical structure expressible by means of a multivalent simple or composite prefix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04062
name: multiplicity
def: "The number of possible orientations\, calculated as \\\(2\\ S+1\\\)\, of the spin angular momentum corresponding to a given total spin quantum number \(\\\(S\\\)\)\, for the same spatial electronic wavefunction. A state of singlet multiplicity has \\\(S=0\\\) and \\\(2\\ S+1=1\\\). A doublet state has \\\(S=\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(2\\ S+1=2\\\)\, etc. Note that when \\\(S>L\\\) \(the total orbital angular momentum possible\) there are only \\\(2\\ L+1\\\) orientations of total angular momentum possible."
synonym: "spin multiplicity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04063
name: multiply labelled
def: "A selectively labelled compound may be multiply labelled when in the unmodified compound there is more than one atom of the same element at the position where the isotopic modification occurs\, e.g. $\\ce\{H\}$ in $\\ce\{CH4\}$\, or there are several atoms of the same element at different positions where the isotopic modification occurs\, e.g. $\\ce\{C\}$ in $\\ce\{C4H8O\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04065
name: multistage sampling
def: "Samples taken in a series of steps with the sampling portions constituting the sample \(units or increments\) at each step being selected from the larger or greater number of portions of the previous step\, or from a primary or composite sample. The first set of portions \(units or increments\) taken from the population available for sampling is the primary sample. The subsequent samples \(secondary\, tertiary\, etc.\) are the sets of subsamples\, units\, items\, individuals or increments taken from the preceding step. The units may be different steps of multistage sampling \(e.g. pallets\, cases\, packages\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04066
name: multi-strand chain
def: "A chain that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through more than four atoms\, more than two on at least one side of each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04067
name: multi-strand macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through more than four atoms\, more than two on at least one side of each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04068
name: munchnones
def: "Mesoionic compounds having a 1\,3-oxazole skeleton bearing an oxygen atom attached to the 5-position with the following delocalized structure\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04069
name: muonium
def: "Atom-like particle consisting of a positive muon and an electron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04071
name: mustards
def: "Compounds having two $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}haloalkyl groups\}$ bound to a sulfur atom\, as in $\\ce\{\(XC2H2C2H2\)2S\}$\, and their analogues\, the nitrogen \(and phosphorus\) mustards\, $\\ce\{\(XC2H2C2H2\)2NR\}$. Compounds having one $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}haloalkyl group\}$ and one $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}hydroxyalkyl group\}$ on $\\ce\{S\}$ are termed hemi- or semi-mustards by some chemists."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04072
name: mutagenesis
def: "The introduction of permanant heritable changes i.e. mutations into the DNA of an organism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04073
name: mutarotation
def: "The change in optical rotation accompanying epimerization. In sugar chemistry this term usually refers to epimerization at the hemiacetal carbon atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04074
name: mutation
def: "A heritable change in the nucleotide sequence of genomic DNA \(or RNA in RNA viruses\)\, or in the number of genes or chromosomes in a cell\, which may occur spontaneously or be brought about by chemical mutagens or by radiation \(induced mutation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04075
name: mutation rate
def: "The frequency \(\\\(\\rm\{h\}^\{-1\}\\\)\) with which a mutation occurs within a organism or gene. In general\, rates of spontaneous mutation vary between one in \\\(10^\{4\}\\\) and one in \\\(10^\{8\}\\\) per gene per generation\, and can be considerably increased by mutagens."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04076
name: mutual inductance
def: "For two thin conducting loops\, the magnetic flux through one loop\, caused by an electric current in the other loop\, divided by that current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:M04077
name: Myelin cylinders
def: "Birefringent cylinders which form spontaneously from lipid-containing material in contact with water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06760
name: mobility
def: "Mass transport is described by a mobility \\\(b\\\)\, defined by\: \\\[b_\{i\} = \\frac\{\nu _\{i\}\}\{\\boldsymbol\{F\}_\{i\}\}\\\] where \\\(\nu _\{i\}\\\) is the average drift velocity and \\\(\\boldsymbol\{F\}_\{i\}\\\) is the force on particle \\\(i\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06765
name: metalloenzyme
def: "An enzyme that\, in the active state\, contains one or more metal ions which are essential for its biological function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06778
name: magnetic circular dichroism
def: "Magnetic circular dichroism is observed when a sample differentially absorbs left- and right- circularly polarized light in a magnetic field parallel to the light beam."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06781
name: magnetic susceptibility
def: "For paramagnetic materials\, the magnetic susceptibility may be measured experimentally and used to give information on the molecular magnetic dipole moment\, and hence on the electronic structure of the molecules in the material. The paramagnetic contribution to the molar magnetic susceptibility of a material\, \\\(\\chi \\\)\, is related to the molecular magnetic dipole moment \\\(m\\\) by the curie relation\: \\\(\\chi = \\frac\{\\rm\{constant x\}\\ m^\{2\}\}\{T\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06795
name: magnetic resonance imaging
def: "The visualisation of the distribution of nuclear spins \(usually water\) in a body by using a magnetic field gradient \(NMR imaging\). A similar technique\, but less widely used\, is to visualise the distribution of paramagnetic centres \(EPR imaging\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06830
name: metabolic half-life
def: "Time required for one-half of the quantity of a substance in the body to be metabolised."
synonym: "metabolic half-time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06831
name: monitoring
def: "Continuous or repeated observation\, measurement\, and evaluation of health and/or environmental or technical data for defined purposes\, according to prearranged schedules in space and time\, using comparable methods for sensing and data collection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06832
name: Monte Carlo study
def: "Simulation and analysis of a sequence of events using random numbers to generate possible outcomes in an iterative process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06833
name: mutagen
def: "Agent that can induce heritable changes \(mutations\) of the genotype in a cell as a consequence of alterations or loss of genetic material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06853
name: mesomorphic state
def: "A state of matter in which the degree of molecular order is intermediate between the perfect three-dimensional\, long-range positional and orientational order found in solid crystals and the absence of long-range order found in isotropic liquids\, gases\, and amorphous solids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06858
name: mesogenic group
def: "A part of a molecule or macromolecule endowed with sufficient anisotropy in both attractive and repulsive forces to contribute strongly to mesophase or\, in particular\, liquid-crystal mesophase formation in low-molar-mass and polymeric substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06869
name: main-chain polymer liquid crystal
def: "A polymer\, whose molecules have mesogenic units only in their main chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06870
name: mesogen
def: "A compound that under suitable conditions of temperature\, pressure\, and concentration can exist as a mesophase or\, in particular\, as a liquid-crystal \(LC\) phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06878
name: membrane
def: "Structure\, having lateral dimensions much greater than its thickness\, through which transfer may occur under a variety of driving forces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06887
name: microfiltration
def: "Pressure-driven membrane-based separation process in which particles and dissolved macromolecules larger than \\\(0.1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) are rejected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06910
name: maximum latent period
def: "The length of time after which no causal relationship can reasonably be established. This length of time is the period from the last exposure to the point of time at which an exposed person has demonstrated the initial signs or symptoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06924
name: migration time
def: "The time required for the analyte to move through the effective length of the capillary\, \\\(L_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}\\\)\, that is\, from the sample introduction point to the detection point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06927
name: migration time of micelles
def: "Experimentally determined as the migration time of a compound that is completely retained in the micellar phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06928
name: mass distribution ratio
def: "Defined as\: \\\[k_\{\\rm\{MEKC\}\} = \\frac\{n_\{\\rm\{mc\}\}\}\{n_\{\\rm\{aq\}\}\} = K\\cdot \\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{mc\}\}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{aq\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(n_\{\\rm\{mc\}\}\\\) and \\\(n_\{\\rm\{aq\}\}\\\) are the chemical amounts of the analyte in the micellar and aqueous phases\, respectively\, \\\(K\\\) is the distribution constant and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{mc\}\}\\\) and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{aq\}\}\\\) are the corresponding volumes of the phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06929
name: mass distribution ratio
def: "Defined analogously to mass distribution ratio \(in micellar electrokinetic chromatography\)\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{MEEKC\}\}\\\) by replacing terms for micelles with corresponding terms for micro-emulsion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06966
name: MOL file format
def: "The MOL file format is used to encode chemical structures\, substructures and conformations as text-based connection tables. It is used by MDL Information Systems Inc. \(e.g.\, in their MACCS or ISIS programs\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06967
name: molecular connectivity index
def: "A molecular connectivity index is a numeric descriptor derived from molecular topology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06968
name: molecular design
def: "Molecular design is the application of all techniques leading to the discovery of new chemical entities with specific properties required for the intended application."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06969
name: molecular dynamics
def: "Molecular dynamics is a simulation procedure consisting of the computation of the motion of atoms in a molecule or of individual atoms or molecules in solids\, liquids and gases\, according to Newton's laws of motion. The forces acting on the atoms\, required to simulate their motions\, are generally calculated using molecular mechanics force fields."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06970
name: molecular graphics
def: "Molecular graphics is a technique for the visualization and manipulation of molecules on a graphical display device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06971
name: molecular modeling
def: "Molecular modeling is the investigation of molecular structures and properties using computational chemistry and graphical visualization techniques in order to provide a plausible three-dimensional representation under a given set of circumstances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06972
name: molecular shape
def: "The molecular shape is an attribute of a molecule dealing with spatial extension\, form\, framework\, or geometry. It is often described by\, e.g.\, principal axes\, ovality\, or connectivity indices."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06973
name: molfile
def: "A molfile is a table containing atom type\, connectivity and a more or less arbitrary 2D or 3D information about a molecule. Well-known file formats include the MOLfile used by MDL Information Systems Inc. \(e.g.\, in the database MACCS\)\, the MOL2 file used by Tripos Associates \(e.g.\, in the modeling package SYBYL\)\, or the CSSR format."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT06974
name: multivariate statistics
def: "Multivariate statistics is a set of statistical tools to analyze data \(e.g.\, chemical and biological\) matrices using regression and/or pattern recognition techniques."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07068
name: maximum hardness principle
def: "A chemical system at a given temperature will evolve to a configuration of maximum absolute hardness\, $\\upeta\\!$\, provided that the potential due to the nuclei\, plus any external potential and the electronic chemical potential\, remain constant. In terms of molecular orbital theory\, the highest value of $\\upeta\\!$ reflects the highest possible energy gap between the lowest unoccupied and highest occupied molecular orbitals; this value correlates with the stability of a system."
synonym: "principle of maximum hardness" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07069
name: molecular graph
def: "The graph with differently labelled \(coloured\) vertices \(chromatic graph\) which represent different kinds of atoms and differently labelled \(coloured\) edges related to different types of bonds. Within the topological electron distribution theory\, a complete network of the bond paths for a given nuclear configuration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07070
name: molecular graph theory
def: "The theory which deals with analyses of all consequences of connectivities inherent in molecular structure and chemical transformations. The theory does not produce numerical data but uses available data and searches for regularities that can be attributed to combinatorial and topological origins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07071
name: molecular Rydberg state
def: "An excited electronic state which is composed primarily of atomic orbitals with principal quantum numbers greater than that of the ground state and the valence excited states. Such electronic states typically have a large polarizability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07072
name: Monte Carlo method
def: "In mathematics\, a method originally used for calculating multiple integrals by means of a random sample. The method is used for numerical modelling of many-particle chemical systems\, in particular liquids; it is based on the equilibrium statistical mechanics theory. Observables A are calculated as mean values over a great number \(\\\(\\cong\\rm\{10\}^\{\\rm\{5\}\}-\\rm\{10\}^\{\\rm\{6\}\}\\\)\) of instant configurations as determined by coordinates of the particles. \\\[< A >\\ = \\frac\{1\}\{N\}\\sum _\{i=1\}^\{N\}A\\\{r_\{i\}\\\}\\\] where \\\(N\\\) is the number of configurations. At the first stage\, various configurations are randomly generated and then those energetically unrealizable eliminated. An efficient search for the most probable configurations to be entered into the above expression is provided by the Metropolis algorithm based on the principle of Markov's chain theory. While being elaborated for the study of equilibrium chemical systems\, MC method is also applied to studies of the dynamics of chemical reactions."
synonym: "method of Monte Carlo" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07073
name: Morse potential
def: "The empirical function relating the potential energy of a molecule to the inter-atomic distance r accounting for the anharmonicity of bond stretching\: \\\[E\\left\(r\\right\) = D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\ \(1- \\rm\{e\}^\{-a\\ \(r- r_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\)\}\)^\{2\}\\\] where \\\(D_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the bond-dissociation energy\, \\\(r_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the equilibrium bond length\, and \\\(a\\\) is a parameter characteristic of a given molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07074
name: Mulliken population analysis
def: "A partitioning scheme based on the use of density and overlap matrixes of allocating the electrons of a molecular entity in some fractional manner among its various parts \(atoms\, bonds\, orbitals\). As with other schemes of partitioning the electron density in molecules\, MPA is arbitrary and strongly dependent on the particular basis set employed. However\, comparison of population analyses for a series of molecules is useful for a quantitative description of intra-molecular interactions\, chemical reactivity and structural regularities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07075
name: multiconfiguration SCF method
def: "Configuration-interaction method in which simultaneous optimization of both the shapes of molecular orbitals and contributions from different electronic configurations is carried out by using the variational method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07140
name: main-chain scission
def: "Chemical reaction that results in the breaking of main-chain bonds of a polymer molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07141
name: Mechano-chemical reaction
def: "Chemical reaction that is induced by the direct absorption of mechanical energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07177
name: macroporous polymer
def: "Glass or rubbery polymer that includes a large number of macropores \(\\\(50\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)–\\\(1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) in diameter\) that persist when the polymer is immersed in solvents or in the dry state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07230
name: miscibility
def: "Capability of a mixture to form a single phase over certain ranges of temperature\, pressure\, and composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07270
name: miscibility gap
def: "Area within the coexistence curve of an isobaric phase diagram \(temperature vs composition\) or an isothermal phase diagram \(pressure vs composition\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07284
name: morphology coarsening
def: "Process by which phase domains increase in size during the aging of a multiphase material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07285
name: morphology
def: "Shape\, optical appearance\, or form of phase domains in substances\, such as high polymers\, polymer blends\, composites\, and crystals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07287
name: multiphase copolymer
def: "Copolymer comprising phase-separated microdomains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07303
name: microdomain morphology
def: "Morphology consisting of phase microdomains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07307
name: multicoat morphology
def: "Morphology observed in a blend of a block copolymer with the homopolymer of one of the blocks and characterized by alternating concentric shells of the copolymer and the homopolymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07309
name: multiple inclusion morphology
def: "Multiphase morphology in which dispersed phase domains of one polymer contain and completely encapsulate many phase domains of a second polymer that may have the same composition as the continuous phase domain."
synonym: "salami-like morphology" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07419
name: magic angle
def: "Upon excitation of an 'isotropic' sample \(assuming an ultra short excitation pulse\) the relationship between the fluorescence intensity detected at a time \\\(t\\\) and through a polarization analyser oriented at an angle \\\(\\beta\\\) with respect to the electric polarization of the exciting beam is given by \\\[I\(t\,\\beta \) \\propto  N\(t\)\\left \[ 1 + \(3\\\, \\rm\{cos\}^\{2\}\\\, \\beta - 1\)R\(t\) \\right \]\\\] where \\\(R\(t\)\\\) is the degree of alignment of the emitting transition dipole in the laboratory frame and \\\(N\(t\)\\\) is the excited-state population\, both at time \\\(t\\\). For \\\(\\beta = 54.7^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) \(the magic angle\)\, the dipole-alignment contribution vanishes and \\\(I\(t\,\\\,\\beta = 54.7^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} \) \\propto N\(t\)\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07422
name: molecular orientation
def: "Absorption probability \(referred to electric dipolar absorption\) for a molecular transition with its electric transition moment at an angle \\\(\\theta\\\) with the electric vector of the light is proportional to \\\(\\cos^\{2\}\\theta\\\). For the whole sample it is proportional to the orientation factor \\\(K_\{\\theta\} = \\\:< \\cos^\{2\}\\theta >\\\)\, averaged over all sample molecules. This average is \\\(1\\\) for a sample with all transition moments perfectly aligned along the electric vector of the light\, \\\(1/3\\\) for an isotropic sample and \\\(0\\\) for a sample where all transition moments are perpendicular to the electric vector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07424
name: multireference configuration interaction
def: "Configuration-interaction method in which the configurations are built by excitation out of a wavefunction obtained by using\, e.g.\, the multiconfiguration SCF method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07533
name: mixed ceramic
def: "Ceramic material consisting of co-continuous interpenetrating networks of two or more metal carbides\, nitrides or oxides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07559
name: monolith
def: "Shaped\, fabricated\, intractable article with a homogeneous microstructure which does not exhibit any structural components distinguishable by optical microscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07568
name: model network
def: "Polymer network synthesized using a reactant or reactants of known molar mass or masses and chemical structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:MT07637
name: microsyneresis
def: "Syneresis in which the exudation of the liquid is from microscopic regions within a network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N00350
name: ångström
def: "Non-SI unit of length\, \\\(\\rm\{Å\} = 10^\{-10\}\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\)\, widely used in molecular physics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04078
name: n → π* state
def: "An excited state related to the ground state by a n → π* transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04079
name: n → π* transition
def: "An electronic transition described approximately as promotion of an electron from a 'non-bonding' \(lone-pair\) n orbital to an 'antibonding' $\\ce\{\\uppi\\! orbital\}$ designated as $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!^\{\\ast\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04080
name: n-σ* delocalization
def: "Delocalization of a free electron pair \(n\) into an antibonding $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$ \($\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!^\{\\ast\}\}$\)."
synonym: "n-σ* no bond resonance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04081
name: n → σ* transition
def: "An electronic transition described approximately as promotion of an electron from a 'non-bonding' \(lone-pair\) n orbital to an 'antibonding' $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$ designated as $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!^\{\\ast\}\}$. Such transitions generally involve high transition energies and appear close to or mixed with Rydberg transitions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04082
name: nano
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-9\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\rm\{n\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04083
name: nanoscopic film
def: "A film which has lateral dimensions in the range of \\\(0.1\{-\}100\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04084
name: naphthenes
def: "Cycloalkanes especially cyclopentane\, cyclohexane and their alkyl derivatives. The term seems to be obsolescent\, except in the petrochemical industry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04085
name: naphthenic acids
def: "Acids chiefly monocarboxylic\, derived from naphthene. The term seems to be obsolescent\, except in the petrochemical industry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04086
name: narcissistic reaction
def: "A chemical reaction that can be described as the conversion of a reactant into its mirror image\, without rotation or translation of the product\, so that the product enantiomer actually coincides with the mirror image of the reactant molecule. Examples of such reactions are cited under the entries fluxional and degenerate rearrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04087
name: natural broadening
def: "Broadening which has its origin in the finite optical lifetime of one or both levels involved in the transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04088
name: natural graphite
def: "A mineral found in nature. It consists of graphitic carbon regardless of its crystalline perfection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04089
name: natural isotopic abundance
def: "Of a specified isotope of an element\, the isotopic abundance in the element as found in nature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04091
name: natural radiation
def: "Radiation originating from natural radioactivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04092
name: natural radioactivity
def: "Radioactivity of naturally occurring nuclide in materials where the isotopic abundance of that nuclide is natural."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04095
name: needle coke
def: "The commonly used term for a special type of coke with extremely high graphitizability resulting from a strong preferred parallel orientation of its turbostratic layer structure and a particular physical shape of the grains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04096
name: negative adsorption
def: "The depletion of one or more components in an interfacial layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04098
name: negative ion
def: "An atom\, radical\, molecule or molecular moiety in the vapour phase which has gained one or more electrons thereby acquiring an electrically negative charge. The use of the term anion as an alternative is not recommended because of its connotations in solution chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04100
name: neighbouring group participation
def: "The direct interaction of the reaction centre \(usually\, but not necessarily\, an incipient carbenium centre\) with a lone pair of electrons of an atom or with the electrons of a $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ or $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ contained within the parent molecule but not conjugated with the reaction centre. A distinction is sometimes made between n-\, $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}participation\}$. A rate increase due to neighbouring group participation is known as 'anchimeric assistance'. 'Synartetic acceleration' is the special case of anchimeric assistance ascribed to participation by electrons binding a substituent to a carbon atom in a $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}position\}$ relative to the leaving group attached to the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}carbon\}$ atom. According to the underlying model\, these electrons then provide a three-centre bond \(or 'bridge'\) 'fastening together' \(as the word 'synartetic' is intended to suggest\) the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}carbon\}$ atoms between which the charge is divided in the intermediate bridged ion formed \(and in the transition state preceding its formation\). The term synartetic acceleration is not widely used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04103
name: neodymium laser
def: "A CW pulsed laser emitting radiation from excited The $\\ce\{Nd^\{3+\}\}$ principally occurring around \\\(1.06\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) \(the precise position depends on the matrix\). The $\\ce\{Nd^\{3+\}\}$ is present as a dopant in suitable crystals \(e.g. yttrium–aluminium garnet\, YAG\) or in suitable glasses \(phosphate\, silicate\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04106
name: neper
def: "Unit\, coherent with the SI\, for expressing field levels\, \\\(\\rm\{Np\}=1\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04107
name: nephelometry
def: "Analytical methods which depend on the measurement of the intensity of scattered light emanating from an illuminated volume of an aerosol. The ratio of scattered intensity to illuminating intensity is compared with a standard of known properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04108
name: Nernst's diffusion layer
def: "A fictitious layer corresponding to the dotted straight lines of the diagram which shows the concentration profile along the direction perpendicular to an electrode surface. The thickness \\\(\\delta \\\) of this layer is called the effective \(or equivalent\) thickness of the diffusion layer. Its definition is apparent from the figure. It is the thickness which the diffusion layer would have if the concentration profile were a straight line coinciding with the tangent to the true concentration profile at the interface\, and that straight line were extended up to the point where the bulk concentration is reached. \\\(\\delta \\\) has a formal significance only. It is simply another way of writing the mass transfer coefficient \\\(k_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) defined in terms of a resistivity instead of a conductivity. N04108.png"

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04109
name: net
def: "A two-dimensional lattice \(in a particular range of surface coverage and temperature\) into which the adsorbate is ordered\, especially in some cases of localized adsorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04110
name: net current
def: "The sum of cathodic and anodic partial currents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04111
name: net electric charge
def: "The algebraic sum of the charges present at the surface of the particle divided by the elementary charge of the proton. The symbol \\\(z\\\) is also used for charge number of an ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04112
name: network
def: "A highly ramified macromolecule in which essentially each constitutional unit is connected to each other constitutional unit and to the macroscopic phase boundary by many permanent paths through the macromolecule\, the number of such paths increasing with the average number of intervening bonds; the paths must on the average be co-extensive with the macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04113
name: network polymer
def: "A polymer composed of one or more networks."
synonym: "polymer network" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04115
name: neutrino
def: "An elementary particle of negligible mass and zero electric charge. There are also a muon neutrino and tau neutrino."
synonym: "electron neutrino" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04116
name: neutron
def: "Nuclear particle of zero charge\, spin quantum number \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\\) and a mass of \\\(1.008\\ 664\\ 904\(14\)\\ \\rm\{u\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04117
name: neutron density
def: "The number of free neutrons divided by the containing volume. Partial densities may be defined for neutrons characterized by such parameters as energy and directions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04118
name: neutron multiplication
def: "The process in which a neutron produces on the average more than one neutron in a medium containing fissile material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04119
name: neutron number
def: "Number of neutrons in an atomic nucleus."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04120
name: neutron rest mass
def: "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\\(m_\{\\rm\{n\}\} = \\pu\{1.6749286\(10\)E-27 kg\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04132
name: neutron temperature
def: "The temperature assigned to a population of neutrons when this population is approximated by a Maxwellian distribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04133
name: neutrophilic organisms
def: "Organisms preferring a neutral medium for growth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04134
name: Newman projection
def: "A projection formula representing the spatial arrangement of bonds on two adjacent atoms in a molecular entity. The structure appears as viewed along the bond between these two atoms\, and the bonds from them to other groups are drawn as projections in the plane of the paper. The bonds from the atom nearer to the observer are drawn so as to meet at the centre of a circle representing that atom. Those from the further atom are drawn as if projecting from behind the circle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04135
name: newton
def: "SI derived unit of force\, \\\(\\pu\{N\} = \\pu\{kg m s-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04136
name: Newton black film
def: "In soap films\, two types of equilibrium film are often observed\, sometimes successively in the same system\: one characterized by thicknesses of the order of \\\(7\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) or more which varies significantly with minor changes in composition such as ionic strength\, and the other having a lesser thickness relatively independent of such changes. When a distinction is needed\, the former are designated as common black films\, and the latter as Newton black films. The current use of first or secondary for the common black film and of second\, primary or Perrin's for the Newton black film is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04137
name: Newton diagram
def: "A vector diagram used to show the relationship between initial and final velocities or momenta in a two-particle scattering process. It is commonly used to relate laboratory and centre-of-mass coordinates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04138
name: Newtonian fluid
def: "A fluid in which the components of the stress tensor are linear functions of the first spatial derivatives of the velocity components. These functions involve two material parameters \(taken as constants throughout the fluid\, although depending on ambient temperature and pressure\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04140
name: <i>nido</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote a nest-like structure\, especially a boron skeleton that is almost closed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04141
name: Nier–Johnson geometry
def: "An arrangement for a double-focusing mass spectrometer in which a deflection of \\\(\\frac\{\\pi\}\{2\}\\\) radians in a radial electrostatic field analyser is followed by a magnetic deflection of \\\(\\frac\{\\pi\}\{3\}\\\) radians. The electrostatic analyser uses a symmetrical object-image arrangement and the magnetic analyser is used asymmetrically."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04142
name: NIH shift
def: "The intramolecular hydrogen migration which can be observed in enzymatic and chemical hydroxylations of aromatic rings. It is evidenced by appropriate deuterium labelling\, i.e.  In enzymatic reactions the NIH shift is generally thought to derive from the rearrangement of arene oxide intermediates\, but other pathways have been suggested. \(NIH stands for U.S. National Institutes of Health where the shift was discovered.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04143
name: nimbostratus cloud
def: "An amorphous\, dark grey\, rainy cloud layer reaching almost to the ground; \\\(300\{-\}600\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\) vertical velocities of \\\(0.05\{-\}0.2\\ \\rm\{m s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04144
name: nitramines
def: "Amines substituted at N with a nitro group \(a contracted form of N-nitroamines\); they are thus amides of nitric acid\, and the class is composed of nitramide\, $\\ce\{O2NNH2\}$\, and its derivatives formed by substitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04145
name: nitrenes
def: "The neutral compound $\\ce\{HN\{\:\}\}$ having univalent nitrogen\, and its derivatives $\\ce\{RN\}$. Aminylenes is a recognized but less widely used synonym. Other names that have been used include aminediyls\, imidogens\, azenes. They may exist in either a singlet or a triplet electronic state \(four spin-paired electrons\, or two spin-paired and two with parallel spins\, respectively\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3N\{\:\}\}$ methylnitrene.Until the 1960s\, nitrenes had a totally different meaning\: analogues of nitrones in which the double-bonded oxygen is replaced by double-bonded carbon\, thus azomethine ylides."
synonym: "aminylenes" []
synonym: "azenes" []
synonym: "imidogens" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04146
name: nitrenium ions
def: "The cation \($\\ce\{H2N\{\:\}^\{+\}\}$\) and its N-hydrocarbyl derivatives \($\\ce\{R2N\{\:\}^\{+\}\}$\)\, in which the nitrogen has a positive charge\, and two unshared electrons. A synonymous but less widely used term is aminylium ions. The alkylidene derivatives of \($\\ce\{H2N\{\:\}^\{+\}\}$\)\, \($\\ce\{R2C=N\{\:\}^\{+\}\}$\)\, still belong to the class nitrenium ions\, but are more precisely designated by the term iminylium ions."
synonym: "aminylium ions" []
synonym: "imidonium ions" []
synonym: "imin" []
synonym: "imine radical" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04147
name: nitrification
def: "Sequential oxidation of ammonium salts to nitrite and nitrate by microorganisms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04148
name: nitrile imides
def: "Ylides having the structure . Also known as 'nitrile imines' or 'nitrilimines'\, especially in the German literature."
synonym: "nitrile imines" []
synonym: "nitrilimines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04150
name: nitrile oxides
def: "Ylides having the structure ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04151
name: nitriles
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC#N\}$; thus C-substituted derivatives of hydrocyanic acid\, $\\ce\{HC#N\}$. In systematic nomenclature\, the suffix nitrile denotes the triply bound $\\ce\{#N\}$ atom\, not the carbon atom attached to it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04152
name: nitrile sulfides
def: "Sulfur analogues of nitrile oxides ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04153
name: nitrile ylides
def: "1\,3-dipolar compounds having the structure\:  The uncharged canonical form  is called an alkylidene- \(or hydrocarbylidene-\) amino carbene; the name iminyl carbene is incorrect because iminyl is not a recognized prefix for $\\ce\{–N=CR2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04155
name: nitrilium betaines
def: "Derivatives of nitriles having the general structure $\\ce\{R–C#N^\{+\}–Y^\{−\}\}$. A subclass of 1\,3-dipolar compounds including nitrile imides\, nitrile oxides\, nitrile sulfides and nitrile ylides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04156
name: nitrilium ions
def: "Cations derived formally by attachment of one hydron to the nitrogen atom of a nitrile and hydrocarbyl derivatives thereof. E.g.  benzonitrilium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04157
name: nitrimines
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{O2NN=CR2\}$ \(also called N-nitroimines\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04158
name: nitro compounds
def: "Compounds having the nitro group\, $\\ce\{NO2\}$ \(free valence on nitrogen\)\, which may be attached to carbon\, nitrogen \(as in nitramines\)\, or oxygen \(as in nitrates\)\, among other elements \(in the absence of specification\, C-nitro compounds are usually implied\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04159
name: nitrogen fixation
def: "The assimilation of atmospheric nitrogen by microbial reduction to ammonia and conversion into organonitrogen compounds such as amino acids. Only a limited number of microorganisms are able to fix nitrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04160
name: nitrogen laser
def: "A source of pulsed semi-coherent superradiance mainly around \\\(337\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\). The lasing species is molecular nitrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04163
name: nitrolic acids
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(=NOH\)NO2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04164
name: nitrones
def: "The N-oxides of imines\, that have the structure $\\ce\{R2C=N+\(O−\)R'\}$ \($\\ce\{R'\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). Synonymous with azomethine oxides. N-Oxides $\\ce\{R2C=N+\(O−\)H\}$ may be included."
synonym: "azomethine oxides" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04166
name: nitrosamides
def: "Although this term has\, regrettably\, been used to mean the N-nitroso derivatives of amides\, logically it refers to amides of nitrous acid; the unstable parent compound $\\ce\{H2NNO\}$ is\, in fact\, named nitrosamide\, but its derivatives formed by substitution with hydrocarbyl groups are by long custom called nitrosamines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04167
name: nitrosamines
def: "N-Nitroso amines\, compounds of the structure $\\ce\{R2NNO\}$. Compounds $\\ce\{RNHNO\}$ are not ordinarily isolable\, but they\, too\, are nitrosamines. The name is a contraction of N-nitrosoamine and\, as such\, does not require the N locant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04168
name: nitrosimines
def: "N-Nitroso imines $\\ce\{O=NN=CR2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04169
name: nitroso compounds
def: "Compounds having the nitroso group\, $\\ce\{–NO\}$. attached to carbon\, or to another element\, most commonly nitrogen or oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04170
name: nitrosolic acids
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(=NOH\)NO\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04174
name: no carrier added
def: "A preparation of a radioactive isotope which is essentially free from stable isotopes of the element in question."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04175
name: noise
def: "The random fluctuations occurring in a signal that are inherent in the combination of instrument and method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04176
name: no-load indication
def: "The deflection or rest point \(no-load reading\) multiplied by the value of the division for zero load \(rider at zero\); symbol \\\(i_\{0\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04177
name: nominal linear flow
def: "The volumetric flowrate of the mobile phase divided by the area of the cross section of the column \(\\\(\\rm\{cm min\}^\{-1\}\\\)\) i.e. the linear flowrate in a part of the column not containing packing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04178
name: nominally labelled tracer
def: "A tracer in which the label is present mainly in a specified position."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04180
name: nonadiabatic coupling
def: "This is momentum coupling between two adiabatic potential-energy surfaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04183
name: nonbonded interactions
def: "Intramolecular attractions or repulsions between atoms that are not directly linked to each other\, affecting the thermodynamic stability of the chemical species concerned."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04184
name: non-calorimetric thermophysical measurements
def: "These include \(\\\(p\\\)\,\\\(V\\\)\, \\\(T\\\)\) measurements\, saturated density measurements\, and any other measurements which give information on the \(\\\(p\\\)\,\\\(V\\\)\, \\\(T\\\)\) surface of a fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04185
name: nonclassical carbocation
def: "A carbocation the ground state of which has delocalized \(bridged\) bonding $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ or $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$. \(N.B. Allylic and benzylic carbocations are not considered nonclassical.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04187
name: non-crystalline electrodes
def: "Electrodes in which a support matrix\, containing an ion exchanger \(either cationic or anionic\)\, a plasticizer solvent\, and possibly an uncharged\, selectivity-enhancing species\, forms the ion-selective membrane which is usually interposed between two aqueous solutions. The support used can be either macroporous \[e.g. poly\(propylene carbonate\) filter\, glass frit\, etc.\] or microporous \(e.g. 'thirsty' glass or inert polymeric material such as PVC\) yielding with the ion-exchanger and the solvent a 'solidified' homogeneous mixture. These electrodes exhibit a response due to the presence of the ion-exchange material in the membrane. The solvent-polymeric-membrane is an example."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04188
name: non-destructive activation analysis
def: "An activation analysis procedure in which\, after the irradiation\, no chemical and physical operations are applied which cause a change of any properties of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04191
name: non-draining
def: "An adjective describing a chain macromolecule that behaves in a hydrodynamic field as though the solvent within the domain of the macromolecule were virtually immobilized with respect to the macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04192
name: non-equilibrium reaction
def: "If the reactants in a chemical reaction are not initially present in a Boltzmann distribution the reaction is referred to as a non-equilibrium reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04193
name: non-graphitic carbon
def: "All varieties of solids consisting mainly of the element carbon with two-dimensional long-range order of the carbon atoms in planar hexagonal networks\, but without any measurable crystallographic order in the third direction \(c-direction\) apart from more or less parallel stacking."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04194
name: non-graphitizable carbon
def: "A non-graphitic carbon which cannot be transformed into graphitic carbon solely by high-temperature treatment up to \\\(3300\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) under atmospheric pressure or lower pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04195
name: non-isotopic labelling
def: "Labelling in which the resulting product has a different chemical composition from the initial one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04196
name: non-linearity error
def: "An error caused by any deviation from linearity of the response of the detector to the measured radiant power."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04197
name: non-linear optical effect
def: "An effect brought about by electromagnetic radiation the magnitude of which is not proportional to the irradiance. Non-linear optical effects of importance to photochemistry are harmonic frequency generation\, lasers\, Raman shifting\, upconversion\, and others."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04199
name: non-polarized interphases
def: "Interphases for which the exchange of common charged components between the phases proceeds unhindered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04200
name: non-radiative decay
def: "The disappearance of an excited species due to a radiationless transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04202
name: nonselectively labelled
def: "An isotopically labelled compound is designated as nonselectively labelled when the position\(s\) and the number of the labelling nuclide\(s\) are both undefined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04204
name: non-specific adsorption
def: "Ions approach an interface differently depending on the forces in play. Ions are non-specifically adsorbed \(positively or negatively\) when they are subjected in the interphase only to long-range coulombic interactions \(attraction or repulsion\). They are believed to retain their solvation shell\, and in the position of closest approach to the interface they are separated from it by one or more molecular layers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04205
name: non-uniform corrosion
def: "Corrosion is non-uniform if the time average of the corrosion current through a unit area depends on its position on the surface. Non-uniform corrosion can be due to inhomogeneities of structure or of composition of the corroding material\, or to inhomogeneities of the environment. Special cases of non-uniform corrosion such as pitting or intergranular corrosion are sometimes called localized corrosion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04206
name: non-uniform polymer
def: "A polymer comprising molecules non-uniform with respect to relative molecular mass or constitution or both."
synonym: "polydisperse polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04207
name: non-vertical energy transfer
def: "Energy transfer process with a low Franck–Condon factor because the nuclear geometries of the minima on the ground- and excited-state potential energy surfaces of the donor or of the acceptor are strongly displaced."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04209
name: no-observed-effect-level
def: "Greatest concentration or amount of a substance\, found by experiment or observation\, that causes no alterations of morphology\, functional capacity\, growth\, development\, or life span of target organisms distinguishable from those observed in normal \(control\) organisms of the same species and strain under the same defined conditions of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04210
name: nor-
def: "Affix used to denote the elimination of one methylene group from a side chain of a parent structure \(including a methyl group\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04211
name: normal
def: "The term 'normal' in e.g. 'normal boiling temperature' means the value at a pressure of \\\(101325\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04214
name: normal-phase chromatography
def: "An elution procedure in which the stationary phase is more polar then the mobile phase. This term is used in liquid chromatography to emphasize the contrast to reversed-phase chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04215
name: normal region
def: "Region for which the rate constants increase with increasing exergonicity of the reaction in plots relating rate constants for electron transfer\, or quantities related to it\, with the standard Gibbs energy for the reaction \(\\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{ET\}\}G^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\)\). This region is predicted by the Marcus theory for outer-sphere electron transfer for the case of \\\(-\\Delta_\{\\rm\{ET\}\} G^\{\\circ\} \\leq \\lambda \\\) in the Marcus equation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04216
name: normal stress
def: "Force acting normally to a surface divided by the area of the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04217
name: normal X-ray level
def: "Synonymous with diagram level."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04218
name: Norrish Type II photoreaction
def: "Intramolecular abstraction of a $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}hydrogen\}$ by an excited carbonyl compound to produce a 1\,4-diradical as a primary photoproduct."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04219
name: Norrish Type I photoreaction
def: "$\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}Cleavage\}$ of an excited carbonyl compound leading to an acyl-alkyl radical pair \(from an acyclic carbonyl compound\) or an acyl-alkyl diradical \(from a cyclic carbonyl compound\) as a primary photoproduct."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04224
name: <i>n</i>th order phase transition
def: "A transition in which the molar Gibbs energy \(or chemical potential\) and its \\\(\(n - 1\)\\\)th-order derivatives are continuous\, whereas the \\\(n\\\)th-order derivatives with respect to temperature and pressure are discontinuous at the transition point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04226
name: nuclear chemistry
def: "The part of chemistry which deals with the study of nuclei and nuclear reactions using chemical methods."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04227
name: nuclear decay
def: "A spontaneous nuclear transformation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04228
name: nuclear disintegration
def: "Nuclear decay involving a splitting into more nuclei or the emission of particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04229
name: nuclear fission
def: "The division of a nucleus into two or more parts with masses of equal order of magnitude\, usually accompanied by the emission of neutrons\, gamma radiation and\, rarely\, small charged nuclear fragments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04230
name: nuclear fuel
def: "Material containing fissile nuclides\, which when placed in a reactor\, enables a chain reaction to be achieved."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04232
name: nuclear fusion reaction
def: "A reaction between two light nuclei resulting in the production of a nuclear species heavier than either initial nucleus."
synonym: "nuclear fusion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04233
name: nuclear graphite
def: "A polygranular graphite material for use in nuclear reactor cores consisting of graphitic carbon of very high chemical purity. High purity is needed to avoid absorption of low-energy neutrons and the production of undesirable radioactive species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04234
name: nuclear isomers
def: "Nuclides having the same mass number and atomic number\, occupying different nuclear energy states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04235
name: nuclear level
def: "One of the energy values at which a nucleus can exist for an appreciable time \(\\\(> 10^\{-22\}\\ \\rm\{s\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04236
name: nuclear magneton
def: "Electromagnetic fundamental physical constant \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{N\}\}=\\frac\{m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\}\{m_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\}\\ \\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}=5.050\\ 7866\(17\)\\times 10^\{-27\}\\ \\rm\{J T\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, where \\\(m_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the electron rest mass\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) the proton rest mass and \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) the Bohr magneton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04237
name: nuclear particle
def: "A nucleus or any of its constituents in any of their energy states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04238
name: nuclear quadrupole moment
def: "A parameter which describes the effective shape of the equivalent ellipsoid of the nuclear charge distribution\, \\\(Q>0\\\) for prolate \(e.g. $\\ce\{^\{57\}Fe\}$\, $\\ce\{^\{19\}7Au\}$\); \\\(Q"

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04239
name: nuclear reactor
def: "A device in which a self-sustaining nuclear fission chain reaction can be maintained and controlled. The term is sometimes applied to a device in which a nuclear fusion reaction can be produced and controlled."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04240
name: nuclear transformation
def: "The change of one nuclide into another with a different proton number or nucleon number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04241
name: nuclear transition
def: "For a nucleus a change from one quantized energy state into another or a nuclear transformation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04242
name: nucleating agent
def: "A material either added to or present in a system\, which induces either homogeneous or heterogeneous nucleation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04243
name: nucleation
def: "The process by which nuclei are formed in solution. The condensation of a single chemical compound is called 'homogeneous nucleation'. The simultaneous condensation of more than one compound is called 'simultaneous nucleation'. The condensation of a compound on a foreign substance is called 'heterogeneous nucleation'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04244
name: nucleation and growth
def: "A process in a phase transition in which nuclei of a new phase are first formed\, followed by the propagation of the new phase at a faster rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04245
name: nucleic acids
def: "Macromolecules\, the major organic matter of the nuclei of biological cells\, made up of nucleotide units\, and hydrolysable into certain pyrimidine or purine bases \(usually adenine\, cytosine\, guanine\, thymine\, uracil\)\, ᴅ-ribose or 2-deoxy-ᴅ-ribose and phosphoric acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04246
name: nucleofuge
def: "A leaving group that carries away the bonding electron pair. For example\, in the hydrolysis of an alkyl chloride\, $\\ce\{Cl^\{-\}\}$ is the nucleofuge. The tendency of atoms or groups to depart with the bonding electron pair is called nucleofugality. The adjective is nucleofugal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04247
name: nucleon
def: "Heavy nuclear particle\: proton or neutron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04248
name: nucleon number
def: "Synonymous with mass number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04249
name: nucleophile
def: "A nucleophile \(or nucleophilic reagent\) is a reagent that forms a bond to its reaction partner \(the electrophile\) by donating both bonding electrons. A 'nucleophilic substitution reaction' is a heterolytic reaction in which the reagent supplying the entering group acts as a nucleophile. For example\:  The term 'nucleophilic' is also used to designate the apparent polar character of certain radicals\, as inferred from their higher relative reactivity with reaction sites of lower electron density. Nucleophilic reagents are Lewis bases."
synonym: "nucleophilic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04250
name: nucleophilic catalysis
def: "Catalysis by a Lewis base\, involving formation of a Lewis adduct as a reaction intermediate. For example\, the hydrolysis of acetic anhydride in aqueous solution catalysed by pyridine\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04251
name: nucleophilicity
def: "The property of being nucleophilic.The relative reactivity of a nucleophilic reagent. \(It is also sometimes referred to as 'nucleophilic power'.\) Qualitatively\, the concept is related to Lewis basicity. However\, whereas Lewis basicity is measured by relative equilibrium constants\:  nucleophilicity of a Lewis base is measured by relative rate constants of different nucleophilic reagents towards a common substrate\, most commonly involving formation of a bond to carbon\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04252
name: nucleoproteins
def: "Proteins having nucleic acids as prosthetic groups\, and thus yielding nucleic acids \(or their cleavage products\) as well as amino acids on hydrolytic cleavage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04253
name: nucleosides
def: "Ribosyl or deoxyribosyl derivatives \(rarely\, other glycosyl derivatives\) of certain pyrimidine or purine bases. They are thus glycosylamines or N-glycosides related to nucleotides by the lack of phosphorylation. It has also become customary to include among nucleosides analogous substances in which the glycosyl group is attached to carbon rather than nitrogen \('C-nucleosides'\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04254
name: nucleotide bases
def: "The heterocyclic pyrimidine and purine compounds which are constituents of all nucleic acids. Adenine \(A\)\, guanine \(G\) and cytosine \(C\) are found in both DNA and RNA\, thymine \(T\) is found \(primarily\) in DNA and uracil \(U\) only in RNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04255
name: nucleotides
def: "Compounds formally obtained by esterification of the 3 or 5 hydroxy group of nucleosides with phosphoric acid. They are the monomers of nucleic acids and are formed from them by hydrolytic cleavage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04256
name: nucleus
def: "The positively charged central portion of an atom\, excluding the orbital electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04257
name: nuclide
def: "A species of atom\, characterized by its mass number\, atomic number and nuclear energy state\, provided that the mean lifetime in that state is long enough to be observable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04258
name: nuclidic mass
def: "The rest mass of a nuclide expressed in atomic mass units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04259
name: nuisance threshold
def: "That concentration of an air pollutant that is considered objectionable. In the case of a substance with an objectionable odour\, it is the smallest concentration of the substance which can be detected by a human being \(nose\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04260
name: number concentration
def: "Number of entities of a constituent in a mixture divided by the volume of the mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04261
name: number content
def: "Number of defined particles\, or elementary entities\, of a component in a system divided by the mass of that system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04262
name: number density
def: "Number of particles divided by the volume they occupy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04263
name: number-distribution function
def: "A distribution function in which the relative amount of a portion of a substance with a specific value\, or a range of values\, of the random variable\(s\) is expressed in terms of mole fraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04264
name: number flow rate
def: "Number of defined particles\, or elementary entities of a defined component\, crossing a cross-section divided by the time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04266
name: number of entities
def: "Integer number obtained by counting of entities\, which are usually molecules\, atoms or ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:N04269
name: numerical value
def: "Quotient of the value of a quantity and the unit used in its expression."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT06774
name: nuclearity
def: "The number of central atoms joined in a single coordination entity by bridging ligands or metal-metal bonds is indicated by dinuclear\, trinuclear\, tetranuclear\, polynuclear\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT06834
name: necrosis
def: "Sum of morphological changes resulting from cell death by lysis and/or enzymatic degradation\, usually affecting groups of cells in a tissue."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT06888
name: nanofiltration
def: "Pressure-driven membrane-based separation process in which particles and dissolved macromolecules smaller than \\\(2\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) are rejected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT06911
name: no observed adverse effect level
def: "Greatest concentration or amount found by experiment or observation\, which causes no detectable adverse alteration of morphology\, functional capacity\, growth\, development\, or life span of the target organism under defined conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT06936
name: non-linear distribution isotherm
def: "A distribution isotherm which is not linear."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT06940
name: non-linear chromatography
def: "Chromatographic process\, where the retention is governed by a non-linear distribution isotherm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT06942
name: non-ideal chromatography
def: "Chromatographic process with normal peak-broadening effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT06943
name: non-ideal\, linear chromatography
def: "Chromatographic process\, where the retention is governed by a linear distribution isotherm and normal peak-broadening takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT06945
name: non-ideal\, non-linear chromatography
def: "Chromatographic process\, where both isotherm curvature and other peak-broadening processes \(such as diffusion\) contribute to the peak shape."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07076
name: natural atomic orbital
def: "A valence-shell atomic orbital whose derivation involves diagonalising the localized block of the full density matrix of a given molecule associated with basis functions \\\(\\chi _\{i\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)\\\) on that atom. A distinguishing feature of NAOs is that they meet the simultaneous requirement of orthonormality and maximum occupancy. For isolated atoms\, NAOs coincide with natural orbitals. In a polyatomic molecule the NAOs \(in contrast to natural orbitals that become delocalised over all nuclear centres\) mostly retain one-centre character\, and thus are optimal for describing the molecular electron density around each atomic centre."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07077
name: natural bond orbital
def: "The orbital which is formed from natural hybrid orbitals. For a localized $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ between atoms $\\ce\{A\}$ and $\\ce\{B\}$\, the NBO is\: \\\[\\sigma _\{\\rm\{AB\}\}=c_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\ \\rm\{h\}_\{\\rm\{A\}\}+c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ \\mathrm\{h\}_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\rm\{h\}_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{h\}_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) are the natural hybrids centered on atoms $\\ce\{A\}$ and $\\ce\{B\}$. NBOs closely correspond to the picture of localized bonds and lone pairs as basic units of molecular structure\, so that is possible to conveniently interpret ab initio wave-functions in terms of the classical Lewis structure concepts by transforming these functions to NBO form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07078
name: natural hybrid orbital
def: "Symmetrically orthogonalised directed hybrid orbital derived through unitary transformation of natural atomic orbitals centred on a particular atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07079
name: natural orbital
def: "The orbitals defined \(P. Lowdin\) as the eigenfunctions of the spinless one-particle electron density matrix. For a configuration interaction \(CI\) wave-function constructed from orbitals \\\(\\varPhi \\\)\, the electron density function\, \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C1\}\\\)\, is of the form\: \\\[\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C1\} = \\sum_\{i\}\\sum _\{j\}a_\{ij\}\\\,\\varPhi_\{i\}^\{*\}\\\,\\varPhi_\{j\}\\\] where the coefficients \\\(a_\{ij\}\\\) are a set of numbers which form the density matrix. The NOs reduce the density matrix \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C1\}\\\) to a diagonal form\: \\\[\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C1\} = \\sum _\{k\}b_\{k\}\\mathit\{\\Phi\}_\{k\}^\{*\}\\mathit\{\\Phi\}_\{k\}\\\] where the coefficients \\\(b_\{k\}\\\) are occupation numbers of each orbital. The importance of natural orbitals is in the fact that CI expansions based on these orbitals have generally the fastest convergence. If a CI calculation was carried out in terms of an arbitrary basis set and the subsequent diagonalisation of the density matrix \\\(\\rm\{a\}_\{ij\}\\\) gave the natural orbitals\, the same calculation repeated in terms of the natural orbitals thus obtained would lead to the wave-function for which only those configurations built up from natural orbitals with large occupation numbers were important."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07080
name: natural population analysis
def: "The analysis of the electron density distribution in a molecular system based on the orthonormal natural atomic orbitals. Natural populations\, \\\(n_\{i\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)\\\) are the occupancies of the natural atomic orbitals. These rigorously satisfy the Pauli exclusion principle\: \\\(0 < n_\{i\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\) < 2\\\). The population of an atom \\\(n\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)\\\) is the sum of natural populations \\\(n\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\n\\rm\{A\}\n\\end\{array\}\}n_\{i\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)\\\). A distinguished feature of the NPA method is that it largely resolves the basis set dependence problem encountered in the Mulliken population analysis method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07081
name: negative hyper-conjugation
def: "The effect of donation of electron density from filled $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\}$ or $\\ce\{n_\{\\uppi\\!\}\\mbox\{-\}orbitals\}$ to the symmetry adapted $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!^\{\\ast\}\\mbox\{-\}orbital\(s\)\}$ of $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!^\{\\ast\}\\mbox\{-\}bond\(s\)\}$ of a molecular entity resulting in building $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}character\}$ into bonds that nominally possess only $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}character\}$. The consequences of the effect are\, for example\, in elongation of the $\\ce\{C–F\}$ bond in the $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}fluoroethyl\}$ anion and stabilization of the anti-periplanar conformation of fluoromethylamine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07082
name: nodal plane
def: "A plane of a system in which the value of the orbital wave-function equals zero. This plane defines a region of zero electron density for the orbital."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07083
name: nonbonding molecular orbital
def: "A molecular orbital whose occupation by electrons does not contribute \(or contributes insignificantly\) to the binding energy of the molecule. Generally\, a nonbonding MO represents the highest occupied molecular orbital of a molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07084
name: nonclassical structure
def: "The structure of molecules or molecular ions that escapes description in terms of conventional rules of valency and stereochemistry. Nonclassical structures are characteristic of carbonium ions with hypercoordinated \(see hypercoordination\) carbon atoms\, e.g.\, methanium ion 1\, pyramidal dication $\\ce\{C6H6^\{2+\}\}$ 2 \(isomeric to benzene dication\)\, and the molecular species whose structure cannot be adequately represented by the equilibrium \(2-norbornyl cation\, 3\) or resonance of two or more classical structures. From the stereochemical point of view\, those structures are assigned to the nonclassical type for which all tetracoordinate carbon bonds extend into a single hemisphere\, i.e.\, the valence angle of a carbon atom is greater than $\\pu\{180\\!^\{\\circ\}\}$. A hypothetical example is pyramidane\, 4\, the structure of which corresponds to a local minimum on the $\\ce\{C5H4\}$ potential energy surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07085
name: non-Kekulé molecules
def: "Molecules that are fully conjugated\, but each of whose Kekulé structures contains at least two atoms that are not $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonded\}$. Examples of non-Kekule hydrocarbons are trimethylenemethane \(2-methylidenepropane-1\,3-diyl\) 1\, m-benzodiquinomethane \(benzene-1\,2-diyldimethyl\) 2\, and 1\,2\,4\,5 - tetramethylenebenzene \(benzene-1\,2\,4\,5-tetrayltetramethyl\) 3."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07086
name: normalization
def: "A mathematical procedure which ensures that the integral of the square of modulus of a wave-function over all space equals 1. The constant required to ensure that a wave-function is normalized is termed the normalization constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07178
name: non-linear optical polymer
def: "Polymer that exhibits an optical effect brought about by electromagnetic radiation such that the magnitude of the effect is not proportional to the irradiance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07243
name: nanocomposite
def: "Composite in which at least one of the phase domains has at least one dimension of the order of nanometres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07272
name: nucleation of phase separation
def: "Initiation of phase domain formation through the presence of heterogeneities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07304
name: nanodomain morphology
def: "Morphology consisting of phase nanodomains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07425
name: non-crossing rule
def: "Electronic states of the same symmetry cannot cross along a reaction coordinate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07426
name: non-linear optical techniques
def: "Collective name applied to techniques related to non-linear optical effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07427
name: Norrish–Yang reaction
def: "Photochemical process leading to the formation of cyclobutanols from excited carbonyl compounds by intramolecular $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}hydrogen\}$ abstraction \(Norrish Type II photoreaction\) and subsequent 1\,4-diradical combination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07428
name: nucleophilic aromatic photosubstitution
def: "Photosubstitution in aromatic compounds with the participation of nucleophiles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07521
name: nanogel
def: "Particle of gel of any shape with an equivalent diameter of approximately \\\(1\\\) to \\\(100\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)."
synonym: "gel nanoparticle" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07522
name: neutralized gel
def: "Gel containing acidic or basic groups that have been neutralized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07562
name: network
def: "Highly ramified structure in which essentially each constitutional unit is connected to each other constitutional unit and to the macroscopic phase boundary by many paths through the structure\, the number of such paths increasing with the average number of intervening constitutional units; the paths must on average be co-extensive with the structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07603
name: network-chain molar mass
def: "Number-average molar mass of polymer chains between two adjacent crosslinks or junction points in a polymer network."
synonym: "inter-junction molar mass" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07604
name: network defect
def: "Elastically ineffective chains in a polymer network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:NT07626
name: net shaping
def: "Production of an object in\, or as close as possible to\, its final shape prior to ceramization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04270
name: observation height
def: "The difference in height between the axis of the observed space \(optical axis\) and the burner tip \(in \\\(\\mathrm\{mm\}\\\)\). The optical axis of the instrument should be the same as the optical axis of the observation space. Other definitions which express the observation height as a fraction of the whole flame height are not recommended because the height to the tip of the flame is not well defined. The observation height should be stated in the method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04271
name: observation pathlength
def: "The intersection of the optical axis and the observation space \(in \\\(\\mathrm\{mm\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04272
name: observation space
def: "The intersection of the optical beam and that part of the flame where the net signal is at least half of the maximum net signal. The characteristics of the observation space depend on the temperature of the flame\, the stoichiometry of the gases and the properties of the processed fluid \(presence of proteins for instance\). The observation space in atomic absorption is analogous to the observation space of a cuvette in molecular absorption spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04273
name: observation volume
def: "The volume of that part of the flame that is observed through the optical device \(in \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{l\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04275
name: occlusion
def: "The process of incorporation of foreign substances as molecular species within precipitates as they are formed."
synonym: "molecular occlusion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04276
name: <i>octahedro</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote six atoms bound into an octahedron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04277
name: odd-electron ion
def: "Synonymous with radical ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04278
name: optically detected magnetic resonance
def: "A double resonance technique in which transitions between spin sublevels are detected by optical means. Usually these are sublevels of a triplet and the transitions are induced by microwaves. For different types of optical detection the following expressions are used\: ADMR \(absorption\)\, DEDMR \(delayed emission\, non-specified\)\, DFDMR \(delayed fluorescence\)\, FDMR \(fluorescence\)\, PDMR \(phosphorescence\). If a reaction yield is followed the expression RYDMR \(reaction yield detected magnetic resonance\) is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04279
name: odour threshold
def: "The concentration of a compound which produces an odour which is detectable by a human being \(nose\). For certain compounds this threshold is very low \(e.g. 1 part in \\\(10^\{9\}\\\) for certain sulfides\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04280
name: ohm
def: "SI derived unit of electrical resistance\, \\\(\\Omega = \\pu\{V A-1\} = \\pu\{m2 kg s-3 A-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04281
name: olefins
def: "Acyclic and cyclic hydrocarbons having one or more carbon–carbon double bonds\, apart from the formal ones in aromatic compounds. The class olefins subsumes alkenes and cycloalkenes and the corresponding polyenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04282
name: oligo
def: "A prefix meaning 'a few'\, and used for compounds with a number of repeating units intermediate between those in monomers and those in high polymers. The limits are not precisely defined\, and in practice vary with the type of structure being considered\, but are generally from 3 to 10\, e.g. oligopeptides\, oligosaccharides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04283
name: oligomer
def: "A substance composed of oligomer molecules. An oligomer obtained by telomerization is often termed a telomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04285
name: oligomerization
def: "The process of converting a monomer or a mixture of monomers into an oligomer. An oligomerization by chain reaction carried out in the presence of a large amount of chain transfer agent\, so that the end-groups are essentially fragments of the chain transfer agent\, is termed telomerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04286
name: oligomer molecule
def: "A molecule of intermediate relative molecular mass\, the structure of which essentially comprises a small plurality of units derived\, actually or conceptually\, from molecules of lower relative molecular mass."
synonym: "oligomeric" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04291
name: onium compounds
def: "Cations \(with their counter-ions\) derived by addition of a hydron to a mononuclear parent hydride of the nitrogen\, chalcogen and halogen families.$\\ce\{H4N^\{+\}\}$ ammonium$\\ce\{H3O^\{+\}\}$ oxonium$\\ce\{H2F^\{+\}\}$ fluoronium$\\ce\{H4P^\{+\}\}$ phosphonium$\\ce\{H3S^\{+\}\}$ sulfonium$\\ce\{H2Cl^\{+\}\}$ chloronium$\\ce\{H4As^\{+\}\}$ arsonium$\\ce\{H3Se^\{+\}\}$ selenonium$\\ce\{H2Br^\{+\}\}$ bromonium$\\ce\{H4Sb^\{+\}\}$ stibonium$\\ce\{H3Te^\{+\}\}$ telluronium$\\ce\{H2I^\{+\}\}$ iodonium$\\ce\{H4Bi^\{+\}\}$ bismuthoniumDerivatives formed by substitution of the above parent ions by univalent groups. The number of substituted hydrogen atoms is\, especially in the case of hydrocarbyl substituents\, indicated by the adjectives primary\, secondary\, tertiary or quaternary. E.g. \($\\ce\{Cl2F^\{+\}\}$\) dichlorofluoronium\, $\\ce\{\(CH3\)2S^\{+\}H\}$ dimethylsulfonium \(a secondary sulfonium ion\)\, $\\ce\{Cl\(CH3\)3P^\{+\}\}$ chlorotrimethylphosphonium\, $\\ce\{\(CH3CH2\)4N^\{+\}\}$ tetraethylammonium \(a quaternary ammonium ion\).See also\: arsonium compounds\, halonium ions\, oxonium ions\, phosphonium compounds\, quaternary ammonium compounds\, stibonium compounds\, sulfonium compoundsDerivatives formed by substitution of the above parent ions by groups having two or three free valencies on the same atom. Such derivatives are\, where possible\, designated by a specific class name. E.g. $\\ce\{RC#O^\{+\}\}$ hydrocarbylidyne oxonium ions\, $\\ce\{R2C=N^\{+\}H2X^\{−\}\}$ iminium compounds\, $\\ce\{RC#NH^\{+\}\}$ nitrilium ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04294
name: open film
def: "A film in which mass transfer can occur between the film and the coexisting bulk phases\, for all the components. The term partly open film applies to a film in which mass transfer can occur only for certain components. The term closed film applies to those films with fixed mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04295
name: open hearth furnace
def: "Reverberatory furnace\, containing a basin-shaped hearth\, for melting and refining suitable types of pig iron\, iron ore\, and scrap for steel production. A large amount of dust from ore and other materials and splashings from slag are carried away by the waste gases; a supplementary chamber is commonly used for collecting slag and dust."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04297
name: open-tubular column
def: "A column\, usually having a small diameter\, in which either the inner tube wall\, or a liquid or active solid held stationary on the tube wall acts as the stationary phase and there is an open\, unrestricted path for the mobile phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04298
name: operational pH cell
def: "An electrochemical cell which is the basis of practical pH measurement consisting of a hydrogen ion-responsive electrode \(hydrogen gas\, or glass\) and a reference electrode immersed in the test solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04299
name: operational pH standard
def: "Certain substances which meet the criteria of \(i\) preparation in highly pure state reproducibly and \(ii\) stability of solution over a reasonable period of time are designated as operational standards in aqueous solution of specified concentration. Their number is in principle unlimited but values are available now for 15 solutions. The values of operational pH standards\, i.e. \\\(\\mathrm\{pH\}\\left\(\\rm\{OS\}\\right\)\\\)\, are assigned by comparison with the reference value pH standard\, i.e. \\\(\\mathrm\{pH\}\\left\(\\rm\{RVS\}\\right\)\\\)\, in cells with liquid junction\, the operational cells\, where the liquid junctions are formed within vertical \\\(1\\ \\rm\{mm\}\\\) tubes."
synonym: "pH standard" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04301
name: operon
def: "A functional unit consisting of a promoter\, an operator and a number of structural genes\, found mainly in prokaryotes. The structural genes commonly code for several functionally related enzymes\, and although they are transcribed as one \(polycistronic\) mRNA each is independently translated. In the typical operon\, the operator region acts as a controlling element in switching on or off the synthesis of mRNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04302
name: opposing reactions
def: "Composite reactions\, occurring in forward and reverse directions\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04303
name: optical activity
def: "A sample of material able to rotate the plane of polarization of a beam of transmitted plane-polarized light is said to possess optical activity \(or to be optically active\). This 'optical rotation' is the classical distinguishing characteristic \(sufficient but not necessary\) of systems containing unequal amounts of corresponding enantiomers. An enantiomer causing rotation in a clockwise direction \(when viewed in the direction facing the oncoming light beam\) under specified conditions is called 'dextrorotatory' and its chemical name or formula is designated by the prefix \(+\)-; one causing rotation in the opposite sense is 'laevorotatory' and designated by the prefix \(−\)-. Materials with optical activity also exhibit other chiroptic phenomena."
synonym: "optically active" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04305
name: optical-beam error
def: "For wavelength-scanning spectrometers\, an error may be introduced if the image position on the photodetector changes. Reflection of the incident radiation\, e.g. between the cell walls\, is another source of error and results in a measured absorbance slightly higher than the true absorbance. These errors are collectively termed optical-beam errors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04307
name: optical filter
def: "A device which reduces the spectral range \(bandpass\, cut-off\, and interference filter\) or radiant power of incident radiation \(neutral density or attenuance filter\) upon transmission of radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04309
name: optically labile
def: "A term describing a system in which stereoisomerization results in a change of optical rotation with time. \(Usage strongly discouraged\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04310
name: optical purity
def: "The ratio of the observed optical rotation of a sample consisting of a mixture of enantiomers to the optical rotation of one pure enantiomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04313
name: optical rotatory power
def: "Angle of optical rotation divided by the optical path length through the medium and by either the mass concentration of the substance giving the specific optical rotatory power \\\(\\left\[\\alpha \\right\]_\{\\lambda \}^\{\\theta \}\\\) or by amount concentration\, giving the molar optical rotatory power\, \\\(\\alpha _\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04314
name: optical spectroscopy
def: "The study of systems by the electromagnetic radiation with which they interact or that they produce. By convention\, spectroscopic properties are expressed in terms of wavelength in vacuo\, not the wavelength within the medium being studied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04315
name: optical yield
def: "In a chemical reaction involving chiral reactants and products\, the ratio of the optical purity of the product to that of the precursor\, reactant or catalyst. This should not be confused with enantiomeric excess. The optical yield is in no way related to the chemical yield of the reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04316
name: optoacoustic spectroscopy
def: "Same as photoacoustic spectroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04317
name: orbital
def: "Wavefunction depending explicitly on the spatial coordinates of only one electron."
synonym: "atomic orbital" []
synonym: "molecular orbital" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04318
name: orbital energy
def: "Eigenvalue of a one-electron effective hamiltonian belonging to an orbital."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04319
name: orbital steering
def: "A concept expressing that the stereochemistry of approach of two reacting species is governed by the most favourable overlap of their appropriate orbitals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04320
name: orbital symmetry
def: "The behaviour of an atomic or localized molecular orbital under molecular symmetry operations characterizes its orbital symmetry. For example\, under a reflection in an appropriate symmetry plane\, the phase of the orbital may be unchanged \(symmetric\)\, or it may change sign \(antisymmetric\)\, i.e. the positive and negative lobes are interchanged. A principal context for the use of orbital symmetry is the discussion of chemical changes that involve 'conservation of orbital symmetry'. If a certain symmetry element \(e.g. the reflection plane\) is retained along a reaction pathway\, that pathway is 'allowed' by orbital symmetry conservation if each of the occupied orbitals of the reactant\(s\) is of the same symmetry type as a similarly \(e.g. singly or doubly\) occupied orbital of the product\(s\). This principle permits the qualitative construction of correlation diagrams to show how molecular orbitals transform \(and how their energies change\) during idealized chemical changes \(e.g. cycloadditions\). An idealized single bond is a $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$\, i.e. it has cylindrical symmetry\, whereas a p-orbital or $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$bond orbital has $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}symmetry\}$\, i.e. it is antisymmetric with respect to reflection in a plane passing through the atomic centres with which it is associated. In ethene\, the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonding orbital\}$ is symmetric with respect to reflection in a plane perpendicular to and bisecting the $\\ce\{C–C\}$ bond\, whereas the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!^\{\\ast\}\\mbox\{-\}antibonding orbital\}$ is antisymmetric with respect to this operation. Considerations of orbital symmetry are frequently grossly simplified in that\, for example\, the p-orbitals of a carbonyl group would be treated as having the same symmetry as those of ethene\, and the fact that the carbonyl group in\, for example\, camphor\, unlike that in formaldehyde\, has no mirror planes would be ignored. These simplified considerations nevertheless afford the basis of one approach to the understanding of the rules which indicate whether pericyclic reactions are likely to occur under thermal or photochemical conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04321
name: order–disorder transition
def: "A transition in which the degree of order of the system changes. Three principal types of disordering transitions may be distinguished\: \(i\) positional disordering in a solid\, \(ii\) orientational disordering which may be static or dynamic and \(iii\) disordering associated with electronic and nuclear spin states. Examples\:The transition of $\\ce\{LiFeO2\}$\, with a tetragonal unit cell\, in which the $\\ce\{Li+\}$ and $\\ce\{Fe3+\}$ cations are perfectly ordered on crystallographically non-equivalent octahedral sites to cubic $\\ce\{LiFeO2\}$ in which the $\\ce\{Li+\}$ and $\\ce\{Fe3+\}$ cations are distributed randomly over all the octahedral sites. The transition of orthorhombic $\\ce\{KCN\}$ to cubic $\\ce\{KCN\}$ in which the $\\ce\{CN−\}$ ions become oriented in any of the eight $\[111\]$ directions.A superconducting transition"

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04322
name: order of reaction
def: "If the macroscopic \(observed\, empirical or phenomenological\) rate of reaction \(v\) for any reaction can be expressed by an empirical differential rate equation \(or rate law\) which contains a factor of the form \\\(k\\ \\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]^\{\\alpha \}\\ \\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]^\{\\beta \}\\\) ... \(expressing in full the dependence of the rate of reaction on the concentrations \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]\\\)\, \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]\\\) ...\) where \\\(\\alpha \\\)\, \\\(\\beta \\\) are constant exponents \(independent of concentration and time\) and \\\(k\\\) is independent of \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]\\\) and \\\(\\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]\\\) etc. \(rate constant\, rate coefficient\)\, then the reaction is said to be of order \\\(\\alpha \\\) with respect to A\, of order \\\(\\beta \\\) with respect to B\, ... \, and of \(total or overall\) order \\\(n=\\alpha +\\beta\\\: +\\\,...\\\) The exponents \\\(\\alpha \\\)\, \\\(\\beta \\\)\, ... can be positive or negative integral or rational nonintegral numbers. They are the reaction orders with respect to A\, B\, ... and are sometimes called 'partial orders of reaction'. Orders of reaction deduced from the dependence of initial rates of reaction on concentration are called 'orders of reaction with respect to concentration'; orders of reaction deduced from the dependence of the rate of reaction on time of reaction are called 'orders of reaction with respect to time'. The concept of order of reaction is also applicable to chemical rate processes occurring in systems for which concentration changes \(and hence the rate of reaction\) are not themselves measurable\, provided it is possible to measure a chemical flux. For example\, if there is a dynamic equilibrium according to the equation\: \\\[a\\rm\{A\}\\rightleftharpoons p\\rm\{P\}\\\] and if a chemical flux is experimentally found\, \(e.g. by NMR line-shape analysis\) to be related to concentrations by the equation\: \\\[\\frac\{\\varphi _\{-\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{\\alpha \} = k\\ \\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]^\{\\alpha \}\\ \\left\[\\rm\{L\}\\right\]^\{\\lambda \}\\\] then the corresponding reaction is of order \\\(\\alpha \\\) with respect to A ... and of total \(or overall\) order \\\(n\(=\\alpha +\\lambda\\\: +\\\,...\)\\\). The proportionality factor \\\(k\\\) above is called the \(\\\(n\\\)th order\) 'rate coefficient'. Rate coefficients referring to \(or believed to refer to\) elementary reactions are called 'rate constants' or\, more appropriately 'microscopic' \(hypothetical\, mechanistic\) rate constants. The \(overall\) order of a reaction cannot be deduced from measurements of a 'rate of appearance' or 'rate of disappearance' at a single value of the concentration of a species whose concentration is constant \(or effectively constant\) during the course of the reaction. If the overall rate of reaction is\, for example\, given by\: \\\[v=k\\ \\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]^\{\\alpha \}\\ \\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]^\{\\beta \}\\\] but \[B\] stays constant\, then the order of the reaction \(with respect to time\)\, as observed from the concentration change of A with time\, will be \\\(\\alpha \\\)\, and the rate of disappearance of A can be expressed in the form\: \\\[v_\{\\rm\{A\}\}=k_\{\\rm\{obs\}\}\\ \\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]^\{\\alpha \}\\\] The proportionality factor \\\(k_\{\\rm\{obs\}\}\\\) deduced from such an experiment is called the 'observed rate coefficient' and it is related to the \\\(\(\\alpha + \\beta\)\\\)th order rate coefficient \\\(k\\\) by the equation\: \\\[k_\{\\rm\{obs\}\}=k\\ \\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]^\{\\beta \}\\\] For the common case when \\\(\\alpha = 1\\\)\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{obs\}\}\\\) is often referred to as a 'pseudo-first order rate coefficient' \(\\\(k_\{\\psi \}\\\)\). For a simple \(elementary\) reactions a partial order of reaction is the same as the stoichiometric number of the reactant concerned and must therefore be a positive integer \(see rate of reaction\). The overall order is then the same as the molecularity. For stepwise reaction there is no general connection between stoichiometric numbers and partial orders. Such reactions may have more complex rate laws\, so that an apparent order of reaction may vary with the concentrations of the chemical species involved and with the progress of the reaction\: in such cases it is not useful to speak of orders of reaction\, although apparent orders of reaction may be deducible from initial rates. In a stepwise reaction\, orders of reaction may in principle always be assigned to the elementary steps."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04323
name: order parameter
def: "A normalized parameter that indicates the degree of order of a system. An order parameter of 0 indicates disorder; the absolute value in the ordered state is 1."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04324
name: organelles
def: "Separated compartments within a cell with specialized functions\, e.g. nuclei \(containing most of the genetic material\)\, mitochondria \(respiratory energy supply for the cell\)\, chloroplasts \(location of photosynthesis\) etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04325
name: organic dye laser
def: "A liquid laser in which the active medium is an organic dye in a solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04327
name: organoheteryl groups
def: "Univalent groups containing carbon\, which are thus organic\, but which have their free valence at an atom other than carbon. This collective term is seldom used; specific subclasses are more frequently encountered \(organothio or organylthio\, organogermanium or organylgermanium groups\). The synonymous term organoelement groups is occasionally encountered. E.g. phenoxy\, acetamido\, pyridinio \($\\ce\{C5H5N^\{+\}\}$\)\, thiocyanato \($\\ce\{N#C–S−\}$\)\, trimethylsilyl; but not hydroxyphenyl\, aminoacetyl."
synonym: "organoelement groups" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04328
name: organometallic compounds
def: "Classically compounds having bonds between one or more metal atoms and one or more carbon atoms of an organyl group. Organometallic compounds are classified by prefixing the metal with organo-\, e.g. organopalladium compounds. In addition to the traditional metals and semimetals\, elements such as boron\, silicon\, arsenic and selenium are considered to form organometallic compounds\, e.g. organomagnesium compounds $\\ce\{MeMgI\}$ iodo\(methyl\)magnesium\, $\\ce\{Et2Mg\}$ diethylmagnesium; an organolithium compound $\\ce\{BuLi\}$ butyllithium; an organozinc compound $\\ce\{ClZnCH2C\(=O\)OEt\}$ chloro\(ethoxycarbonylmethyl\)zinc; an organocuprate $\\ce\{Li^\{+\}\[CuMe2\]^\{−\}\}$ lithium dimethylcuprate; an organoborane $\\ce\{Et3B\}$ triethylborane. The status of compounds in which the canonical anion has a delocalized structure in which the negative charge is shared with an atom more electronegative than carbon\, as in enolates\, may vary with the nature of the anionic moiety\, the metal ion\, and possibly the medium; in the absence of direct structural evidence for a carbon–metal bond\, such compounds are not considered to be organometallic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04329
name: organyl groups
def: "Any organic substituent group\, regardless of functional type\, having one free valence at a carbon atom\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3CH2–\}$\, $\\ce\{ClCH2–\}$\, $\\ce\{CH3C\(=O\)–\}$\, 4-pyridylmethyl. Organyl is also used in conjunction with other terms\, as in organylthio- \(e.g. $\\ce\{MeS-\}$\) and organyloxy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04330
name: origin of replication
def: "A sequence of DNA at which replication is initiated on a chromosome\, plasmid or virus."
synonym: "ori" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04331
name: ortho acids
def: "Hypothetical compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(OH\)3\}$. Thus\, hydrated forms of carboxylic acids. Orthocarbonic acid\, $\\ce\{C\(OH\)4\}$\, is generically included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04332
name: ortho amides
def: "Hypothetical compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(NH2\)3\}$\, and N-substituted derivatives thereof. $\\ce\{\(R2N\)4C\}$ are generically included\, but such use is obsolescent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04333
name: <i>ortho</i>- and <i>peri</i>-fused
def: "Polycyclic compounds in which one ring contains two\, and only two\, atoms in common with each of two or more rings of a contiguous series of rings. Such compounds have n common faces and less than 2n common atoms. Examples\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04334
name: ortho esters
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(OR'\)3\}$ \($\\ce\{R'\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, or the structure $\\ce\{C\(OR'\)4\}$ \($\\ce\{R'\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{HC\(OCH3\)3\}$ trimethyl orthoformate\, $\\ce\{C\(OCH3\)4\}$ tetramethyl orthocarbonate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04335
name: <i>ortho</i>-fused
def: "Polycyclic compounds in which two rings have two\, and only two\, atoms in common. Such compounds have n common faces and 2n common atoms. Examples\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04336
name: orthokinetic aggregation
def: "The rate of aggregation is in general determined by the frequency of collisions and the probability of cohesion during collision. If the collisions are caused by hydrodynamic motions \(e.g convection or sedimentation\) this is then referred to as orthokinetic aggregation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04337
name: osazones
def: "1\,2-Bis\(arylhydrazones\) of ketoaldoses \(aldoketoses\) formed from aldoses and 2-ketoses by reaction with excess arylhydrazine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04338
name: oscillating reaction
def: "A reaction is said to be oscillating if the concentration of one or more intermediates does not vary monotonically\, but passes through maximum and minimum values."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04339
name: oscillator strength
def: "Measure for integrated intensity of electronic transitions and related to the Einstein transition probability coefficient \\\(A_\{ij\}\\\) by\: \\\[f_\{ij\} = 1.4992\\times 10^\{-14\}\\ \\frac\{A_\{ij\}\}\{s^\{-1\}\}\\ \(\\frac\{\\lambda \}\{\\rm\{nm\}\}\)^\{2\}\\\] where \\\(\\lambda \\\) is the transition wavelength. There are\, however\, differing uses of \\\(f\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04340
name: osmolality
def: "Quotient of the negative natural logarithm of the rational activity of water and the molar mass of water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04342
name: osmotic coefficient
def: "Quantity characterizing the deviation of the solvent from ideal behaviour referenced to Raoult's law. The osmotic coefficient on a molality basis is defined by\: \\\[\\phi =\\frac\{\\mu _\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{*\}- \\mu _\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{R\\ T\\ M_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\ \\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}m_\{i\}\}\\\] and on an amount fraction basis by\: \\\[\\phi =\\frac\{\\mu _\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{*\}- \\mu _\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{R\\ T\\ \\ln x_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{*\}\\\) and \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) are the chemical potentials of the solvent as a pure substance and in solution\, respectively\, \\\(M_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) is its molar mass\, \\\(x_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) its amount fraction\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant and \\\(T\\\) the temperaure. The latter osmotic coefficient is sometimes called the rational osmotic coefficient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04343
name: osmotic concentration
def: "Product of the osmolality and the mass density of water. Formerly called osmolarity."
synonym: "osmolarity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04344
name: osmotic pressure
def: "Excess pressure required to maintain osmotic equilibrium between a solution and the pure solvent separated by a membrane permeable only to the solvent\: \\\[\\mathit\{\\Pi\} = -\\frac\{R\\ T\}\{V_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\\ \\ln a_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\] where \\\(V_\{\\mathrm\{A\}\}\\\)\, \\\(a_\{\\mathrm\{A\}\}\\\) are the partial molar volume and activity of solvent \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\\) for an incompressible fluid. For ideal dilute solutions\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Pi\} = c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ R\\ T=\\rho _\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ \\frac\{R\\ T\}\{M_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\)\, where entities \\\(\\rm\{B\}\\\) are individually moving solute molecules\, ions\, etc.\, regardless of their nature\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rho _\{\\mathrm\{B\}\}\\\) are the amount and mass concentration of the solutes\, and \\\(M_\{\\mathrm\{B\}\}\\\) is the mass average molar mass of the solutes. The amount is sometimes expressed in osmol \(meaning a mole of osmotically active entities\)\, but this usage and the corresponding term osmolarity are discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04346
name: osones
def: "An obsolescent and non-recommended term for 1\,2-ketoaldoses\, usually derived by hydrolysis of osazones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04347
name: osotriazoles
def: "The 1\,2\,3-triazoles formed on oxidizing osazones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04348
name: Ostwald ripening
def: "Dissolution of small crystals or sol particles and the redeposition of the dissolved species on the surfaces of larger crystals or sol particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04349
name: outer electric potential
def: "Electric potential outside the phase concerned. For a conducting sphere of excess charge \\\(Q\\\) and radius \\\(r\\\) in vacuum \\\(\\psi =\\frac\{Q\}\{4\\ \\pi \\ ɛ_\{0\}\\ r\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04350
name: outer Helmholtz plane
def: "At an electrified interface\, the locus of the electrical centres of non-specifically adsorbed ions in their position of closest approach."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04351
name: outer-sphere electron transfer
def: "An outer-sphere electron transfer is a reaction in which the electron transfer takes place with no or very weak \(\\\(\\pu\{4\\!\{-\}\\! 16 kJ mol-1\}\\\)\) electronic interaction between the reactants in the transition state. If instead the donor and the acceptor exhibit a strong electronic coupling\, the reaction is described as inner-sphere electron transfer. The two terms derive from studies concerning metal complexes and it has been suggested that for organic reactions the terms 'nonbonded' and 'bonded' electron transfer should be used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04352
name: outgassing
def: "A form of pretreatment in which a catalyst is heated in vacuo to remove adsorbed or dissolved gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04354
name: out-of-plane bending coordinate
def: "Given by \\\[\\Delta \\theta _\{i- jkl\}=\\frac\{\\Delta z_\{j\}\}\{r_\{eij\}\}\\ \\sin   \\mathit\{\\Phi \}_\{kil\}\\\] where the numbering of the atoms is given in the diagram. \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} _\{kil\}\\\) denotes  the angle between the bonds \\\(ik\\\) and \\\(il\\\)\, \\\(\\Delta z_\{j\}\\\) the perpendicular distance of the atom \\\(j\\\) from the instantaneous plane \\\(ikl\\\) and \\\(r_\{eij\}\\\) the equilibrium length of the bond \\\(ij\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04355
name: output rate
def: "The number of results that is produced by an instrument divided by time of operation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04357
name: overlap integral
def: "Integral over space of the type \\\(\\int \\psi _\{\\rm\{r\}\}^\{*\}\\ \\psi _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\ \\rm\{d\}\\tau \\\) where \\\(\\psi_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\psi_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) are different wavefunctions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04358
name: overpotential
def: "Deviation of the potential of an electrode from its equilibrium value required to cause a given current to flow through the electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04359
name: oxa-di-π-methane rearrangement
def: "A photochemical reaction of a $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\,\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}unsaturated ketone\}$ to form a saturated $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}cyclopropyl ketone\}$. The rearrangement formally amounts to a 1\,2-acyl shift and 'bond formation' between the former $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}carbon atoms\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04361
name: oxidant
def: "A very qualitative term which includes any and all trace gases which have a greater oxidation potential than oxygen \(for example\, ozone\, peroxyacetyl nitrate\, hydrogen peroxide\, organic peroxides\, $\\ce\{NO3\}$\, etc.\). It is recommended that alternative\, more definitive terms be used which define the specific oxidant of interest whenever possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04362
name: oxidation
def: "The complete\, net removal of one or more electrons from a molecular entity \(also called 'de-electronation'\).An increase in the oxidation number of any atom within any substrate.Gain of oxygen and/or loss of hydrogen of an organic substrate.All oxidations meet criteria 1 and 2\, and many meet criterion 3\, but this is not always easy to demonstrate. Alternatively\, an oxidation can be described as a transformation of an organic substrate that can be rationally dissected into steps or primitive changes. The latter consist in removal of one or several electrons from the substrate followed or preceded by gain or loss of water and/or hydrons or hydroxide ions\, or by nucleophilic substitution by water or its reverse and/or by an intramolecular molecular rearrangement. This formal definition allows the original idea of oxidation \(combination with oxygen\)\, together with its extension to removal of hydrogen\, as well as processes closely akin to this type of transformation transformation \(and generally regarded in current usage of the term in organic chemistry to be oxidations and to be effected by 'oxidizing agents'\) to be descriptively related to definition 1. For example the oxidation of methane to chloromethane may be considered as follows\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04363
name: oxidation number
def: "In English is largely synonymous with oxidation state\, and may be preferred when the value represents a mere parameter or number rather than being related to chemical systematics or a state of the atom in a compound. Etymologically\, it stems from the no-longer-used term 'Stock number' \(oxidation number of a central atom; the charge it would bear if all the ligands were removed along with the electron pairs that were shared with the central atom\) and the likewise obsolete term Ewens–Bassett number \(ion charge\)."
synonym: "Stock number" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04365
name: oxidation state
def: "Gives the degree of oxidation of an atom in terms of counting electrons. The higher the oxidation state \(OS\) of a given atom\, the greater is its degree of oxidation. Definition\: OS of an atom is the charge of this atom after ionic approximation of its heteronuclear bonds. The underlying principle is that the ionic sign in an AB molecule is deduced from the electron allegiance in a LCAO-MO model\: The bond&#x2019;s electrons are assigned to its main atomic contributor. Homonuclear AA bonds are divided equally. In practical use\, the ionic-approximation sign follows Allen electronegativities \(see Source\). There are two general algorithms to calculate OS\:   Algorithm of assigning bonds\, which works on a Lewis formula showing all valence electrons in a molecule\: OS equals the charge of an atom after its heteronuclear bonds have been assigned to the more electronegative partner \(except when that partner is a reversibly bonded Lewis-acid ligand\) and homonuclear bonds have been divided equally\:    Algorithm of summing bond orders\: Heteronuclear-bond orders are summed at the atom as positive if that atom is the electropositive partner in a particular bond and as negative if not\, and the atom&#x2019;s formal charge \(if any\) is added to that sum\, yielding the OS. This algorithm works on Lewis formulas and on bond graphs of atom connectivities for an extended solid\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04367
name: oxidative addition
def: "The insertion of a metal complex into a covalent bond involving formally an overall two-electron loss on one metal or a one-electron loss on each of two metals\, i.e. \\\[\\rm\{L\}_\{n\}\\rm\{M\}^\{m\}+\\rm\{XY\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{L\}_\{n\}\\rm\{M\}^\{m+2\}\\left\(\\rm\{X\}\\right\)\\left\(\\rm\{Y\}\\right\)\\\] or \\\[2\\rm\{L\}_\{n\}\\rm\{M\}^\{m\}+\\rm\{XY\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{L\}_\{n\}\\rm\{M\}^\{m+1\}\\left\(\\rm\{X\}\\right\)+\\rm\{L\}_\{n\}\\rm\{M\}^\{m+1\}\\left\(\\rm\{Y\}\\right\)\\\] In radical chemistry\, the term is used to indicate a radical addition to a carbon–carbon double bond\, under oxidative conditions. For example\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04368
name: oxidative coupling
def: "The coupling of two molecular entities through an oxidative process\, usually catalysed by a transition metal compound and involving dioxygen as the oxidant; e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04369
name: oxidized species
def: "A term used to characterize the degree of oxidation \(or reduction\) in atoms\, molecules and ions. It can be applied to an atom in a molecule or an ion which has a high oxidation state. An element or atom in a compound can be oxidized by reaction with oxygen\, while it can be reduced by reaction with hydrogen. An oxidized species may be formed also through the loss of electrons \(either to the positive electrode in a cell\, or through transfer to another atom or group of atoms\). For example\, the sulfur in $\\ce\{H2S\}$ is reduced sulfur relative to elementary sulfur\, while $\\ce\{SO2\}$ and $\\ce\{SO3\}$ are oxidized. Metallic iron \($\\ce\{Fe\}$\) is a reduced state of iron\, while the $\\ce\{Fe2+\}$ ion \(ferrous ion\) and $\\ce\{Fe^\{3+\}\}$ ion \(ferric ion\) are oxidized states of iron. $\\ce\{Fe^\{3+\}\}$ is in a higher oxidation state than $\\ce\{Fe^\{2+\}\}$ which is in a higher oxidation state than $\\ce\{Fe\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04370
name: oxidoreductases
def: "Enzymes that catalyse electron transfer in oxidation-reduction reactions. Oxidoreductases are classified into several groups according to their respective donors or acceptors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04371
name: oxime <i>O</i>-ethers
def: "O-Hydrocarbyl oximes $\\ce\{R2C=NOR'\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04372
name: oximes
def: "Compounds of structure $\\ce\{R2C=NOH\}$ derived from condensation of aldehydes or ketones with hydroxylamine. Oximes from aldehydes may be called aldoximes; those from ketones may be called ketoximes."
synonym: "isonitroso compounds" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04374
name: oxoacids
def: "Oxoacids \(and its variants oxyacids\, oxo acids\, oxy-acids\, oxiacids\, oxacids\) is a traditional name for any acid having oxygen in the acidic group. The term stands in contradistinction to `hydracids' \(e.g. $\\ce\{HCl\}$\) lacking oxygen. The term oxoacid now refers to a compound which contains oxygen\, at least one other element\, and at least one hydrogen bound to oxygen\, and which produces a conjugate base by loss of positive hydrogen ion\(s\) \(hydrons\). E.g. $\\ce\{P\(OH\)3\}$\, $\\ce\{RC\(=O\)OH\}$\, $\\ce\{HOSOH\}$\, $\\ce\{HOCl\}$\, $\\ce\{HON=O\}$\, $\\ce\{\(HO\)2SO2\}$\, $\\ce\{RP\(=O\)\(OH\)2\}$."
synonym: "oxyacid (oxy-acid)" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04375
name: oxocarbons
def: "Compounds consisting wholly of carbon and oxygen\, e.g. $\\ce\{CO\}$\, $\\ce\{O=C=O\}$\, $\\ce\{O=C=C=C=O\}$\,"

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04376
name: oxo carboxylic acids
def: "Compounds having a carboxy group as well as an aldehydic or ketonic group in the same molecule\, e.g. $\\ce\{HC\(=O\)CH2CH2CH2C\(=O\)OH\}$ 5-oxopentanoic acid. In an organic context the term is generally shortened to oxo acids. The full name should be used if confusion with oxoacids seems possible."
synonym: "oxo acids" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04377
name: oxo compounds
def: "Compounds containing an oxygen atom\, $\\ce\{=O\}$\, doubly bonded to carbon or another element. The term thus embraces aldehydes\, carboxylic acids\, ketones\, sulfonic acids\, amides and esters. Oxo used as an adjective \(and thus separated by a space\) modifying another class of compound\, as in oxo carboxylic acids\, indicates the presence of an oxo substituent at any position. To indicate a double-bonded oxygen that is part of a ketonic structure\, the term keto is sometimes used as a prefix\, but such use has been abandoned by IUPAC for naming specific compounds. A traditional use of keto is for indicating oxidation of $\\ce\{CHOH\}$ to $\\ce\{C=O\}$ in a parent compound that contains $\\ce\{OH\}$ groups\, such as carbohydrates\, e.g. 3-ketoglucose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04378
name: oxonium ions
def: "The parent ion $\\ce\{H3O^\{+\}\}$ and substitution derivatives thereof."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04379
name: oxonium ylides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2O^\{+\}-C^\{-\}R2\}$.See\: ylidesA class of 1\,3-dipolar compounds of general structure $\\ce\{R2C=O^\{+\}-Y^\{-\}\}$\, comprising carbonyl imides\, carbonyl oxides and carbonyl ylides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04381
name: oxygen-flask combustion
def: "In order to determine elements which are easily volatilized\, or are present as volatile species\, organic material may be ashed in oxygen in a closed oxygen combustion system. One such method is oxygen-flask combustion by which the test sample is burned in a closed flask containing oxygen and an absorbing solution in which the analytes are subsequently determined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04382
name: oxylium ions
def: "Species of the form $\\ce\{RO^\{+\}\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3O^\{+\}\}$ methoxylium\, $\\ce\{HO^\{+\}\}$ hydroxylium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04383
name: ozone hole
def: "A region of the stratosphere over Antarctica in which a marked decrease in the concentration of ozone has been observed in the Antarctic spring in recent years. The origin of this phenomenon is not yet established\, but several theories based on both physical \(transport related\) and chemical processes \(involvement of the halocarbons and their products of oxidation\) have been suggested. The latter explanation appears to be in better accord with recent findings."

[Term]
id: goldbook:O04384
name: ozonides
def: "The 1\,2\,4-trioxolanes formed by the reaction of ozone at a carbon–carbon double bond\, or the analogous compounds derived from acetylenic compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07087
name: open-shell systems
def: "Atomic or molecular systems in which the electrons are not completely assigned to orbitals in pairs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07179
name: optically active polymer
def: "Polymer capable of rotating the polarization plane of a transmitted beam of linear-polarized light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07305
name: onion morphology
def: "Multiphase morphology of roughly spherical shape that comprises alternating layers of different polymers arranged concentrically\, all layers being of similar thickness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07306
name: ordered co-continuous double gyroid morphology
def: "Co-continuous morphology in which a set of two gyroid-based phase domains exhibits a highly regular\, three-dimensional lattice-like morphology with Ia3d space group symmetry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07430
name: OLED
def: "Acronym for organic light-emitting device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07432
name: one-bond-flip
def: "Photoisomerization of one double bond in a conjugated double bond system. It is a regular E/Z photoisomerization.  Compare with bicycle-pedal mechanism\, hula-twist mechanism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07433
name: one-photon photochromism
def: "Photochromic process involving a one-photon mechanism. In this case\, the photoproduct B is formed from the singlet or from the triplet state of the thermodynamically-stable molecular entity A."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07436
name: optical multi-channel analyser
def: "Detection system for fast acquisition of spectra. It consists of a 'polychromator' fitted with a detector that enables spatial resolution\, e.g.\, a 'vidicon tube'\, a 'charge-coupled device' or a 'silicon photodiode array'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07437
name: optical parametric amplification
def: "Process of signal amplification by a parametric process and a pump wave."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07438
name: optical parametric oscillator
def: "Light source similar to a laser\, but based on 'non-linear optical gain' from parametric amplification rather than on stimulated emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07569
name: oxide network
def: "Network comprising only metal–oxygen linkages."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07577
name: organic–inorganic polymer
def: "Polymer or polymer network with a skeletal structure comprised only of carbon but which has side-groups that include inorganic components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07578
name: organically-modified ceramic
def: "Chemically bonded hybrid material which is a crosslinked inorganic–organic polymer."
synonym: "organomodified ceramic" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:OT07579
name: organically modified silica
def: "Silica modified by organic groups."
synonym: "organically modified silicate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04385
name: π – π* state
def: "An excited state related to the ground state by a π → π* transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04386
name: π → π* transition
def: "An electronic transition described approximately as a promotion of an electron from a 'bonding' $\\ce\{\\uppi\\! orbital\}$ to an 'antibonding' $\\ce\{\\uppi\\! orbital\}$ designated as $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!^\{\\ast\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04393
name: packed column
def: "A tube containing a solid packing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04394
name: packing
def: "The active solid\, stationary liquid plus solid support\, or swollen gel put in the column. The term packing refers to the conditions existing before the chromatographic run is started \(i.e. to the material introduced into the column\)\, whereas the stationary phase refers to the conditions during the run."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04395
name: paddlanes
def: "Tricyclic saturated hydrocarbons having two bridgehead carbon atoms joined through four bridges\, systematically named tricyclo \[\\\(m.n.o.p^\{\\rm\{1\,\}\(m+2\)\}\\\)\] alkanes\, have been referred to as \[\\\(m.n.o.p\\\)\] paddlanes \(when \\\(p=0\\\)\, the compounds are propellanes\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04396
name: pair attenuation coefficient
def: "The attenuation coefficient when only the pair production process is taken into account."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04397
name: pair correlation length
def: "A quantity which approximates to the characteristic length associated with certain number density profiles of diffuse layers. \\\(\\xi \\\) is defined as the separation between two interacting entities \(molecules\, macromolecular segments\, ions\, spins\)\, either in bulk or interfacial regions\, beyond which an appropriate function\, referred to as pair correlation function\, either decays \(e.g. to \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{e\}\\\) of an 'initial' value\) or vanishes. A characteristic length must be confused neither with the effective thickness of the interfacial layer\, nor with the range \(effective distance\) of the intermolecular \(pair\) potential. This range is defined by the distance over which this potential and the corresponding correlation function both vanish."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04398
name: pair production
def: "The simultaneous formation of an electron and a positron as a result of the interaction of a photon of sufficient energy \(&gt; \\\(1.02\\ \\rm\{MeV\}\\\)\) with the field of a particle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04399
name: Pallmann effect
def: "Synonymous with suspension effect  in an ion-selective electrode"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04400
name: PAN-based carbon fibres
def: "Carbon fibres obtained from polyacrylonitrile \(PAN\) precursor fibres by stabilization treatment\, carbonization and final heat treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04401
name: paraffin
def: "Obsolescent term for saturated hydrocarbons\, commonly but not necessarily acyclic. Still widely used in the petrochemical industry\, where the term designates acyclic saturated hydrocarbons\, and stands in contradistinction to naphthenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04402
name: parallel-chain crystal
def: "A type of crystal resulting from parallel packing of stems\, irrespective of the stems' directional sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04404
name: paramagnetic
def: "Substances having a magnetic susceptibility greater than 0 are paramagnetic. They are drawn into a magnetic field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04405
name: parent hydride
def: "An unbranched acyclic or cyclic structure or an acyclic/cyclic structure having a semisystematic or trivial name to which only hydrogen atoms are attached."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04406
name: parent ion
def: "An electrically charged molecular moiety which may dissociate to form fragments\, one or more of which may be electrically charged\, and one or more neutral species. A parent ion may be a molecular ion or an electrically charged fragment of a molecular ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04407
name: partial anodic current
def: "When a single electrochemical reaction occurs at an electrode \(i.e. all other electrochemical reactions may be neglected\)\: \\\[I=I_\{\\rm\{a\}\}+I_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\] where \\\(I\\\) is the total current\, \\\(I_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) is the positive partial anodic current and \\\(I_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) is the negative partial cathodic current. When more than one reaction is significant the reactions may be numbered and the numbers used as subscripts\: \\\(I_\{\\rm\{1\,a\}\}\\\)\, \\\(I_\{\\rm\{1\,c\}\}\\\)\, \\\(I_\{\\rm\{2\,a\}\}\\\)\, etc. Then\: \\\[I=\\sum I_\{i\,\\rm\{a\}\}+\\sum I_\{i\,\\rm\{c\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04408
name: partial charge exchange reaction
def: "Synonymous with partial charge transfer reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04409
name: partial charge transfer reaction
def: "An ion/neutral species reaction wherein the charge on a multiply-charged reactant ion is reduced."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04410
name: partial decay constant
def: "For a radionuclide\: the probability in unit time for the decay of one of its nuclei by one of several modes of decay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04411
name: partial digestion
def: "In partial digestion and/or selective digestion procedures only part or some of the analytes present are brought into solution. This may be preferred to total decomposition if relative concentrations of the analyte in the test samples provide sufficient information \(e.g. materials for geochemical exploration\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04412
name: partial isotherm
def: "The function relating\, at constant temperature and pressure\, the amount of a particular component in the interfacial layer per unit area \(or per unit mass of adsorbent\) with its mole fraction \(or concentration\) in the liquid phase. This function can be evaluated only when the location and the thickness of the interfacial layer have been defined."
synonym: "individual isotherm" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04413
name: partial kinetic current
def: "The current which would flow if mass transport were infinitely fast."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04414
name: partially draining
def: "An adjective describing a chain macromolecule that behaves in a hydrodynamic field as though the solvent within the domain of the macromolecule were progressively more immobilized with respect to the macromolecule in the direction from its outer fringes inward. A freely draining macromolecule and a non-draining macromolecule are two extremes of the concept of a partially draining macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04415
name: partial mass density
def: "Change in mass due to addition of a small amount of component to a system divided by the change in volume of the system. The systematic name is partial volumic mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04418
name: partial molar quantity
def: "Change in the considered extensive quantity when an infinitesimal amount of substance is added to the system at constant temperature\, pressure and amounts of all other constituents of the system divided by the added amount."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04421
name: partial rate factor
def: "The rate of substitution at one specific site in an aromatic compound relative to the rate of substitution at one position in benzene. For example\, the partial rate factor \\\(f_\{p\}^\{\\rm\{Z\}\}\\\) for para-substitution in a monosubstituted benzene $\\ce\{C6H5Z\}$ is related to the rate constants \\\(k\\left\(\\rm\{C\}_\{6\}\\rm\{H\}_\{5\}\\rm\{Z\}\\right\)\\\) and \\\(k\\left\(\\rm\{C\}_\{6\}\\rm\{H\}_\{6\}\\right\)\\\) for the total reaction \(i.e. at all positions\) of $\\ce\{C6H5Z\}$ and benzene\, respectively\, and % para \(the percentage para-substitution in the total product formed from $\\ce\{C6H5Z\}$\) by the relation\: \\\[f_\{p\}^\{\\rm\{Z\}\} = \\frac\{6\\ k\\left\(\\rm\{C\}_\{6\}\\rm\{H\}_\{5\}\\rm\{Z\}\\right\)\}\{k\\left\(\\rm\{C\}_\{6\}\\rm\{H\}_\{6\}\\right\)\}\\ \\frac\{\\%\\ \\rm\{para\}\}\{100\}\\\] Similarly for meta-substitution\: \\\[f_\{m\}^\{\\rm\{Z\}\} = \\frac\{6\\ k\\left\(\\rm\{C\}_\{6\}\\rm\{H\}_\{5\}\\rm\{Z\}\\right\)\}\{2\\ k\\left\(\\rm\{C\}_\{6\}\\rm\{H\}_\{6\}\\right\)\}\\ \\frac\{\\%\\ \\rm\{meta\}\}\{100\}\\\] \(The symbols \\\(p_\{f\}^\{\\rm\{Z\}\}\\\)\, \\\(m_\{f\}^\{\\rm\{Z\}\}\\\)\, \\\(o_\{f\}^\{\\rm\{Z\}\}\\\) are also in use.\) The term applies equally to the ipso position\, and it can be extended to other substituted substrates undergoing parallel reactions at different sites with the same reagent according to the same rate law."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04422
name: partial specific volume
def: "Change in volume of a system when a small amount of component is added divided by the mass of the added component. The systematic name is partial massic volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04423
name: particle concentration
def: "Commonly expressed in several ways\: mass concentration \(usually as \\\(\\unicode\[times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{g\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-3\}\\\)\) or number concentration \(number of particles \\\(\\pu\{cm-3\}\\\)\); modern instrumentation allows measurement of the number of particles as a function of size as well as the total number present in a given air volume. For atmospheric aerosols\, this is a complex distribution for which diameters range from below \\\(0.01\\\) to above \\\(100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\); the particles making the highest contribution to the total number density are in the size range below \\\(0.1\\ \\unicode\[times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\, those contributing most to the total surface area are in the \\\(0.1\\\) to \\\(1.0\\ \\unicode\[times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) range\, while those with the highest contribution to the volume or mass of the aerosol come from both the \\\(0.1\\\) to \\\(1.0\\ \\unicode\[times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) and \\\(1.0\\\) to \\\(100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) ranges."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04424
name: particle density
def: "The number of particles \(nuclear or elementary\) divided by the containing volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04426
name: particle induced X-ray emission analysis
def: "A kind of analysis based on the measurement of the energies and intensities of characteristic X-radiation emitted by a test portion during irradiation with charged particles other than electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04429
name: particle scattering function
def: "The ratio of the intensity of radiation scattered at an angle of observation \\\(\\theta \\\) to the intensity of radiation scattered at an angle zero\, i.e. \\\(P\\left\(\\theta \\right\)\\equiv \\frac\{R\\left\(\\theta \\right\)\}\{R\\left\(0\\right\)\}\\\). An alternative recommended symbol is \\\(P_\{\\theta \}\\\). Synonymous with particle scattering factor."
synonym: "particle scattering factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04430
name: particle size
def: "To describe the size of liquid or solid particles \(aerosol\) the average or equivalent diameter is used. For non-spherical particles collected in an impactor\, for example\, the aerodynamic diameter of a particle of arbitrary shape and density refers to the size of a spherical particle of unit density that would deposit on a given impactor surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04431
name: particle size distribution
def: "The size of the liquid or solid particles in the atmosphere usually extends from \\\(>0.01\\\) to \\\(< 100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) in diameter. In the earth's atmosphere the distribution function which describes the number of particles as a function of diameter\, mass or surface area of the aerosol can be determined reasonably well with modern instrumentation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04432
name: particular property
def: "A property of a given object \(phenomenon\, body or substance\). 'Particular property' includes the concept of particular quantity. The adjective 'particular' may be omitted\, if no ambiguity is caused."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04433
name: particulate carbon
def: "A carbon material consisting of separated monolithic particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04434
name: particulate matter
def: "A general term used to describe airborne solid or liquid particles of all sizes. The term aerosol is recommended for general use in describing airborne particulate matter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04435
name: partition
def: "This term is often used as a synonym for distribution and extraction. However\, an essential difference exists by definition between distribution constant or partition ratio and partition constant. The term partition should be\, but is not invariably\, applied to the distribution of a single definite chemical species between the two phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04436
name: partition chromatography
def: "Chromatography in which separation is based mainly on differences between the solubility of the sample components in the stationary phase \( gas chromatography\)\, or on differences between the solubilities of the components in the mobile and stationary phases \(liquid chromatography\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04437
name: partition coefficient
def: "This term is not recommended and should not be used as a synonym for partition constant\, partition ratio or distribution ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04438
name: partition constant
def: "The ratio of activity of a given species A in the extract to its activity in the other phase with which it is in equilibrium\, thus\: \\\[\\left\(K_\{\\rm\{D\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\right\)_\{\\rm\{A\}\} = \\frac\{a_\{\\rm\{A\,org\}\}\}\{a_\{\\rm\{A\,aq\}\}\}\\\] Its value should not vary with composition but depends on the choice of standard states and on the temperature \(and eventually the pressure\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04439
name: partition function
def: "For a molecule the ratio of the total number of molecules to their number in the non-degenerate ground state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04440
name: partition ratio
def: "The ratio of the concentration of a substance in a single definite form\, A\, in the extract to its concentration in the same form in the other phase at equilibrium\, e.g. for an aqueous/organic system\: \\\[\\left \(K_\{\\rm\{D\}\} \\right \)_\{A\} = \\frac\{\\left \[ A \\right \]_\{\\rm\{org\}\}\}\{\\left \[ A \\right \]_\{\\rm\{aq\}\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "distribution constant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04442
name: pascal
def: "SI derived unit of pressure\, \\\(\\pu\{Pa\} = \\pu\{N m-2\} = \\pu\{kg m-1 s-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04443
name: passivation
def: "The process of transition from the active to the passive state by formation of the passivating film. Passivation is achieved by an anodic current which at the respective electrode potential must be larger than the maximum current\, or by the presence of an oxidized substance in the neighbouring solution which passivates by being reduced \(passivator\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04444
name: passivation potential
def: "The most negative electrode potential at which the passivating film is formed. The passivation potential is equal to or more positive than the equilibrium potential of formation of the phase constituting the film. Usually\, the corrosion current goes through a maximum at the passivation potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04445
name: passive metal
def: "A metal corroding in the passive state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04446
name: passive sampler
def: "A device for preconcentration of trace substances from gaseous media based on molecular diffusion without controlled conveyance of the gas to be investigated \(e.g. work place air\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04447
name: passive state
def: "The passive state is characterized by a contiguous 'passivating film' of solid corrosion products separating the metallic phase from the adjacent electrolyte. Corrosion in the passive state involves growth of the passivating film and/or transfer of metal ions through the film into the electrolyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04448
name: Paterno–Büchi reaction
def: "The photocycloaddition of an electronically excited carbonyl group to a ground state olefin yielding an oxetane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04449
name: paucidisperse system
def: "A colloidal system in which only a few particle-sizes occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04451
name: peak
def: "The portion of a differential chromatogram recording the detector response when a single component is eluted from the column. If separation is incomplete\, two or more components may be eluted as one 'unresolved peak'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04452
name: peak analysis
def: "The extraction of relevant peak parameters \(i.e. position\, area\) from a measured spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04453
name: peak area
def: "The area enclosed between the peak and the baseline."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04454
name: peak area method
def: "A kind of peak analysis in which a peak area is calculated by subtracting an estimate of the underlying continuum in a relevant part of a measured spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04455
name: peak base
def: "The interpolation\, in a differential chromatogram\, of the baseline between the extremities of the peak."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04456
name: peak concentration
def: "The highest concentration of a given trace component which was measured with a continuous analyser during a specified sampling period."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04457
name: peak current
def: "In linear-sweep voltammetry\, triangular-wave voltammetry\, cyclic triangular-wave voltammetry\, and similar techniques\, the maximum value of the faradaic current due to the reduction or oxidation of a substance B during a single sweep. This maximum value is attained after an interval during which the concentration of B at the electrode-solution interface decreases monotonically\, while the faradaic current due to the reduction or oxidation of B increases monotonically\, with time. It is attained before an interval during which this current decreases monotonically with time because the rate of transport of B toward the electrode-solution interface is smaller than the rate at which it is removed from the interface by electrolysis. The term has also been used to denote the maximum value of the faradaic current attributable to the reduction or oxidation of an electroactive substance in techniques such as ac polarography\, differential pulse polarography\, and derivative polarography. However\, these techniques give curves that arise in ways different from that cited above\, and the terms summit\, summit current\, and summit potential are therefore recommended for use in connection with such techniques."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04458
name: peak elution volume
def: "The volume of mobile phase entering the column between the start of the elution and the emergence of the peak maximum\, or the corresponding time. In most of the cases\, this is equal to the total retention volume \(time\). There are\, however\, cases when the elution process does not start immediately at sample introduction. For example\, in liquid chromatography\, sometimes the column is washed with a liquid after the application of the sample to displace certain components which are of no interest and during this treatment the sample does not move along the column. In gas chromatography\, there are also cases when a liquid sample is applied to the top of the column but its elution starts only after a given period. This term is useful in such cases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04460
name: peak fitting
def: "A kind of peak analysis in which a relevant part of a spectrum is fitted with a theoretical response function."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04462
name: peak height
def: "The distance between the peak maximum and the peak base\, measured in a direction parallel to the axis representing the detector response."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04463
name: peak maximum
def: "The point on the peak at which the distance to the peak base\, measured in a direction parallel to the axis representing the detector response\, is a maximum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04464
name: peak potential
def: "In linear-sweep voltammetry\, triangular-wave voltammetry\, cyclic triangular-wave voltammetry\, and similar techniques\, the potential of the indicator electrode at which the peak current is attained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04465
name: peak resolution
def: "The separation of two peaks in terms of their average peak width at base \(\\\(t_\{\\rm\{R2\}\}>t_\{\\rm\{R1\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\[R_\{\\rm\{s\}\}=\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R2\}\}\\\,-\\\,t_\{\\rm\{R1\}\}\}\{\\frac\{w_\{\\rm\{b1\}\}\\\,+\\\,w_\{\\rm\{b2\}\}\}\{2\}\}=\\frac\{2\\ \(t_\{\\rm\{R2\}\}- t_\{\\rm\{R1\}\}\)\}\{w_\{\\rm\{b1\}\}\\\,+\\\,w_\{\\rm\{b2\}\}\}\\\] In the case of two adjacent peaks it may be assumed that \\\(w_\{\\rm\{b1\}\}\\approx w_\{\\rm\{b2\}\}\\\)\, and thus\, the width of the second peak may be substituted for the average value\: \\\[R_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\approx \\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R2\}\}\\\,-\\\,t_\{\\rm\{R1\}\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{b2\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04466
name: peak widths
def: "Peak widths represent retention dimensions \(time or volume\) parallel to the baseline. If the baseline is not parallel to the axis representing time or volume\, then the peak widths are to be drawn parallel to this axis. Three peak-width values are commonly used in chromatography. Peak width at base \(\\\(w_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\\\)\) is the segment of the peak base intercepted by the tangents drawn to the inflection points on either side of the peak. Peak width at half height \(\\\(w_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\\\)\) is the length of the line parallel to the peak base at 50% of the peak height that terminates at the intersection with the two limbs of the peak. Peak width at inflection points \(\\\(w_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)\) is the length of the line drawn between the inflection points parallel to the peak base. The peak width at base may be called the 'base width'. However\, the peak width at half height must never be called the 'half width' because that has a completely different meaning. Also\, the symbol \\\(w_\{1/2\}\\\) should never be used instead of \\\(w_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04467
name: pectins
def: "Polyuronic acids mostly derived from ᴅ-galacturonic acid \(abundant in some fruits\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04468
name: Peierls transition
def: "A metal-insulator transition in quasi one-dimensional solids that occurs as a result of a band gap opening up at the Fermi energy due to a displacive distortion of the regular array increasing the unit cell length\, usually a dimerization; the decrease in electronic energy outweighs the increase in lattice energy. Example\: The transition in methylethylmorpholinium tetracyanoquinodimethanide at \\\(335\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04469
name: pellicular packing
def: "In this case the stationary phase forms a porous outer shell on an impermeable particle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04470
name: penams
def: "Natural and synthetic antibiotics containing the 4-thia-1-azabicyclo\[3.2.0\]heptan-7-one structure\, generally assumed to have the 5R configuration unless otherwise specified. The numbering of the penam skeleton differs from that of the von Baeyer named bicyclic system. Where they differ the von Baeyer numbering is shown in parentheses in the example."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04472
name: pendant group
def: "An offshoot\, neither oligomeric nor polymeric from a chain."
synonym: "side group" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04473
name: penems
def: "2\,3-Didehydropenams."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04475
name: penicillins
def: "Substituted penams having the basic structure shown\, including the natural penicillins and synthetic analogues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04476
name: Penning gas mixture
def: "A Penning gas mixture consists of a rare gas containing impurity atoms possibly at very low concentrations. The impurity atoms have an ionization potential \\\(V_\{\\rm\{ion\}\}\\\) which is lower than or equal to the metastable potential \(\\\(V_\{\\rm\{meta\}\}\\\)\) of the parent noble gas. The Penning effect in a Penning gas mixture is the ionization by charge transfer \(charge exchange\) during collision between a metastable atom and a neutral atom which decreases the average energy to form an ion pair\, e.g.  In a glow discharge\, this results in an increase of the the ionization coefficient \(Townsend first coefficient\)\, a decrease in breakdown potential and a lowering of the cathode fall potential. The magnetic Penning effect describes the increase of the ionization probability of gas in a low pressure electrical discharge resulting from the helical \(spiral\) movement of electrons in a magnetic field placed normal to the anode-cathode electrical field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04478
name: <i>pentaprismo</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote ten atoms bound into a pentagonal prism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04479
name: peptides
def: "Amides derived from two or more amino carboxylic acid molecules \(the same or different\) by formation of a covalent bond from the carbonyl carbon of one to the nitrogen atom of another with formal loss of water. The term is usually applied to structures formed from $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}amino acids\}$\, but it includes those derived from any amino carboxylic acid.  \(R may be any organyl group\, commonly but not necessarily one found in natural amino acids\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04480
name: peptidoglycan
def: "A glycosaminoglycan formed by alternating residues of ᴅ-glucosamine and either muramic acid \{2-amino-3-O-\[\(S\)-1-carboxyethyl\]-2-deoxy-ᴅ-glucose\} or ʟ-talosaminuronic acid \(2-amino-2-deoxy-ʟ-taluronic acid\)\, which are usually N-acetylated or N-glycoloylated. The carboxyl group of the muramic acid is commonly substituted by a peptide containing residues of both ʟ- and ᴅ-amino acids\, whereas that of ʟ-talosaminuronic acid is substituted by a peptide consisting of ʟ-amino acids only."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04483
name: percent
def: "One part in a hundred\, \\\(\\rm\{%\} = 0.01\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04485
name: percentage exposed
def: "The accessibility of the atoms of metal in metallic catalysts\, supported or unsupported\, depends upon the percentage of the total atoms of metal which are surface atoms. It is recommended that the term percentage exposed be employed for this quantity rather than the term dispersion which has been frequently employed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04486
name: percentage relative error
def: "The relative error expressed in percent. It can be calculated from the relative error by multiplying by 100. Comment\: The term 'percentage relative error' should always be quoted in full\, rather than 'error' or 'percentage error' to avoid confusion."
synonym: "percentage error" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04487
name: percentage standard deviation
def: "The value of the relative standard deviation\, expressed in percent. It can be calculated from the relative standard deviation by multiplying by 100. Comment\: It is recommended that the 'relative standard deviation' be reported\, rather than the 'percentage standard deviation'\, in order to avoid confusion where results themselves are expressed as percentages. The term 'coefficient of variation' in place of 'relative standard deviation' is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04490
name: perfectly polarized interphase
def: "Differences from the behaviour of interphases containing only neutral species arise when the interphase may be described as perfectly polarized. This term is used when no charged component is common to both phases adjoining the interphase. This may arise as a result of the equilibrium conditions or from the kinetics of charge transfer and leads to an interphase impermeable to electric charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04491
name: pericyclic reaction
def: "A chemical reaction in which concerted reorganization of bonding takes place throughout a cyclic array of continuously bonded atoms. It may be viewed as a reaction proceeding through a fully conjugated cyclic transition state. The number of atoms in the cyclic array is usually six\, but other numbers are also possible. The term embraces a variety of processes\, including cycloadditions\, cheletropic reactions\, electrocyclic reactions\, sigmatropic rearrangements\, etc. \(provided they are concerted\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04492
name: perikinetic aggregation
def: "The rate of aggregation is in general determined by the frequency of collisions and the probability of cohesion during collision. If the collisions are caused by Brownian motion\, the process is called perikinetic aggregation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04493
name: period
def: "Time for one cycle of a periodic phenomenon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04494
name: periodic copolymer
def: "A copolymer consisting of macromolecules comprising more than two species of monomeric units in regular sequence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04495
name: periodic copolymerization
def: "A copolymerization in which a periodic copolymer is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04496
name: periodic voltage
def: "This general term is applicable to square\, triangular\, and other wave forms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04497
name: peripheral atom
def: "A secondary reference \(non-core\) atom which at some point in the mechanistic sequence is situated in the molecule containing the core atom\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04499
name: periselectivity
def: "The differentiation between two symmetry-allowed processes\, for example the \[2+4\] vs. \[4+6\] cycloaddition of cyclopentadiene to tropone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04500
name: peritectic reaction
def: "An isothermal\, reversible reaction between two phases\, a liquid and a solid\, that results\, on cooling of a binary\, ternary\, ... \, \\\(n\\\) system in one\, two\, ... \(\\\(n- 1\\\)\) new solid phases. Synonymous with incongruent reaction."
synonym: "incongruent reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04501
name: peritectoid reaction
def: "An isothermal\, reversible reaction in the solid state\, that\, on cooling of a binary\, ternary\, ... \, \\\(n\\\) system\, results in one\, two\, ... \(\\\(n-1\\\)\) new solid phases. For example\, in a binary system containing two solids \\\(\\alpha'\\\) and \\\(\\alpha''\\\) \\\[\\alpha' + \\alpha'' \\rightarrow \\beta\\\] Synonymous with metatectoid reaction."
synonym: "metatectoid reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04502
name: peritectoid temperature
def: "The maximum temperature at which a peritectoid reaction can occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04503
name: permeability
def: "Permeability of vacuum multiplied by the relative permeability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04504
name: permeability of vacuum
def: "Fundamental physical constant by definition equal to \\\(4\\ \\pi \\times 10^\{-7\}\\ \\rm\{H m\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, exactly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04505
name: permeation chromatography
def: "Separation based mainly upon exclusion effects\, such as differences in molecular size and/or shape \(e.g. molecular-sieve chromatography\) or in charge \(e.g. ion-exclusion chromatography\). The term gel-permeation chromatography is widely used for the process when the stationary phase is a gel. The term gel-filtration is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04506
name: permeation tube
def: "A device used for dynamic preparation of test gas mixtures by means of controlled permeation of a gaseous analyte out of a container through polymer material into a carrier gas stream. These devices containing certain condensable gases \(e.g. $\\ce\{NO2\}$\, $\\ce\{SO2\}$\, etc.\) when operated at closely controlled temperatures can be used as primary standards calibrated in terms of the weight loss per unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04507
name: permittivity
def: "Permittivity of vacuum multiplied by the relative permittivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04508
name: permittivity of vacuum
def: "Fundamental physical constant \\\(\\varepsilon_\{0\}=\\frac\{1\}\{\\mu _\{0\}\\ c_\{0\}^\{2\}\}\\\) exactly\, where \\\(\\mu_\{0\}\\\) is the permeability of vacuum and \\\(c_\{0\}\\\) the speed of light in vacuum. It is equal to \\\(8.854\\ 187\\ 817\\\,...\\times\\ 10^\{-12\}\\ \\rm\{F m\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04509
name: permselectivity
def: "A term used to define the preferential permeation of certain ionic species through ion-exchange membranes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04510
name: peroxides
def: "Compounds of structure $\\ce\{ROOR\}$ in which $\\ce\{R\}$ may be any organyl group \(The term is also used in an inorganic sense to denote salts of the anion $\\ce\{O2^\{2−\}\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04511
name: peroxisome
def: "Organelle\, similar to a lysosome\, characterized by its content of catalase \(EC 1.11.1.6\)\, peroxidase \(EC 1.11.1.7\) and other oxidative enzymes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04513
name: peroxy acids
def: "Acids in which an acidic $\\ce\{–OH\}$ group has been replaced by an $\\ce\{–OOH\}$ group\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3C\(=O\)OOH\}$ peroxyacetic acid\, $\\ce\{PhS\(=O\)2OOH\}$ benzeneperoxysulfonic acid."
synonym: "per acids" []
synonym: "peracids" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04515
name: persistence length
def: "The average projection of the end-to-end vector on the tangent to the chain contour at a chain end in the limit of infinite chain length. The persistence length \(symbol a\) is the basic characteristic of the worm-like chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04516
name: persistent
def: "A term used to characterize radicals which have lifetimes of several minutes or greater in dilute solution in inert solvents. Persistence is a kinetic or reactivity property. In contrast\, radical stability\, which is a thermodynamic property\, is expressed in terms of the $\\ce\{C–H\}$ bond strength of the appropriate hydrocarbon. The lifetime of a radical is profoundly influenced by steric shielding of the radical centre by bulky substituents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04517
name: perspective formula
def: "A geometric representation of stereochemical features of a molecule or model which appears as a view from an appropriate direction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04518
name: perturbed dimensions
def: "The dimensions of an actual polymer random coil not in a theta state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04519
name: pesticide
def: "Strictly\, a substance intended to kill pests\: in common usage\, any substance used for controlling\, preventing\, or destroying animal\, microbiological or plant pests."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04520
name: pesticide residue
def: "Any substance or mixture of substances in food for man or animals resulting from the use of a pesticide including any specified derivatives\, such as degradation and conversion products\, metabolites\, reaction products and impurities considered to be of toxicological significance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04521
name: peta
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{15\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{P\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04522
name: petroleum coke
def: "A carbonization product of high-boiling hydrocarbon fractions obtained in petroleum processing \(heavy residues\). It is the general term for all special petroleum coke products such as green\, calcined and needle petroleum coke."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04523
name: petroleum pitch
def: "A residue from heat treatment and distillation of petroleum fractions. It is solid at room temperature\, consists of a complex mixture of numerous predominantly aromatic and alkyl-substituted aromatic hydrocarbons\, and exhibits a broad softening range instead of a defined melting temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04524
name: pH
def: "The quantity pH is defined in terms of the activity of hydrogen\(1+\) ions \(hydrogen ions\) in solution\: \\\[\\rm\{pH\} = -\\rm\{lg\}\[a\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\] = -\\rm\{lg\}\[\\rm\{m\}\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\\gamma_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)/m^\{\\unicode\{x29B5\} \}\]\\\] where \\\(a\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\\\) is the activity of hydrogen ion \(hydrogen 1+\) in aqueous solution\, \\\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\\rm\{\(aq\)\}\\\)\, \\\(\\gamma_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\\\) is the activity coefficient of \\\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\\rm\{\(aq\)\}\\\) \(molality basis\) at molality \\\(m\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\)\\\)\, and \\\(m^\{⦵\} = \\pu\{1 mol kg-1\}\\\) is the standard molality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04525
name: pH<sub>0.5</sub> or pH<sub>1/2</sub>
def: "That value of pH in an aqueous phase at which the distribution ratio is unity at equilibrium. 50% of the solute is extracted \(\\\(E = 0.5\\\)\) only when the phase ratio is unity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04526
name: pharmacodynamics
def: "Study of pharmacological actions on living systems\, including the reactions with and binding to cell constituents\, and the biochemical and physiological consequences of these actions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04528
name: phase
def: "An entity of a material system which is uniform in chemical composition and physical state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04530
name: phase inversion
def: "A term used in two senses which should be specified\: density inversion or continuity inversion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04531
name: phase ratio
def: "The ratio of the volume of the mobile phase to that of the stationary phase in a column\: \\\[\\beta = \\frac\{V_\{0\}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\\\] In the case of open-tubular columns the geometric internal volume of the tube \(\\\(V_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\)\) is to be substituted for \\\(V_\{0\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04532
name: phase ratio
def: "The ratio of the quantity of the solvent to that of the other phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04533
name: phase rule
def: "The number of degrees of freedom\, \\\(F\\\)\, that a system containing \\\(C\\\) components can have when \\\(P\\\) phases are in equilibrium\, is given as\: \\\[F=C- P+2\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04534
name: phase separation
def: "The process by which a single solid \(liquid\) phase separates into two or more new phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04535
name: phase-space theory
def: "This is a theory applied to unimolecular or bimolecular reactions proceeding through long-lived complexes. The probability of reaction is assumed to be proportional to the number of states available to a particular product channel divided by the number of states corresponding to all product channels. The theory is used to predict rates\, product energy distributions\, product velocity distributions\, and product angular momentum distributions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04536
name: phase-transfer catalysis
def: "The phenomenon of rate enhancement of a reaction between chemical species located in different phases \(immiscible liquids or solid and liquid\) by addition of a small quantity of an agent \(called the 'phase-transfer catalyst'\) that extracts one of the reactants\, most commonly an anion\, across the interface into the other phase so that reaction can proceed. These catalysts are salts of 'onium ions' \(e.g. tetraalkylammonium salts\) or agents that complex inorganic cations \(e.g. crown ethers\). The catalyst cation is not consumed in the reaction although an anion exchange does occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04537
name: phase transition
def: "A change in the nature of a phase or in the number of phases as a result of some variation in externally imposed conditions\, such as temperature\, pressure\, activity of a component or a magnetic\, electric or stress field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04538
name: phenolates
def: "Synonymous with phenoxides. The term phenolate should not be used for solvates derived from a phenol\, for the ending -ate often occurs in names for anions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04539
name: phenols
def: "Compounds having one or more hydroxy groups attached to a benzene or other arene ring\, e.g. 2-naphthol\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04540
name: phenomenological equation
def: "In the following only media which are isotropic with respect to mass transport \(i.e. the transport coefficients are independent of direction\) are being considered. In the linear range \(not too far from equilibrium\)\, for uniform temperature and neglecting external fields such as the earth's gravitational field\, the flux density of species B is related to the gradients of the electrochemical potentials of all species by the phenomenological equation\: \\\[N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}- c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ v_\{\\rm\{A\}\}=- \\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\\\\\ni\neq A\n\\end\{array\}\}L_\{\\rm\{B\}i\}^\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\ \nabla \\mu _\{i\}\\\] with \\\[i=\\rm\{B\}\,\\rm\{C\}\,...\\\] where \\\(\nabla \\mu _\{i\}\\\) is the gradient of the electrochemical potential of species i. The proportionality factors \\\(L_\{\\rm\{B\}i\}^\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) are called phenomenological coefficients. Their values depend on the frame of reference. The latter is taken here to move with the velocity \\\(v_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) of species A\, and hence\: \\\[L_\{\\rm\{A\}i\}^\{\\rm\{A\}\}=0\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04541
name: phenonium ions
def: "The traditional generic name for those cyclohexadienyl cations that are spiro-annulated with a cyclopropane unit. Phenonium ions constitute a subclass of arenium ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04542
name: phenotype
def: "The observable structural and functional characteristics of an organism determined by its genotype and modulated by its environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04543
name: phenoxides
def: "Salts or analogous metal derivatives of phenols; synonymous with phenolates. A preferable general term\, however\, is aryloxides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04544
name: pheromone
def: "Substance used in olfactory communication between organisms of the same species eliciting a change in sexual or social behaviour. Synonymous with ectohormone\, feromone."
synonym: "ectohormone" []
synonym: "feromone" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04545
name: pH glass electrode
def: "A hydrogen-ion responsive electrode usually consisting of a bulb\, or other suitable form\, of special glass attached to a stem of high resistance glass complete with internal reference electrode and internal filling solution system. Other geometrical forms may be appropriate for special applications\, e.g. capillary electrode for measurement of blood pH."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04546
name: pH gradient
def: "The differential change of pH with distance \(\\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{dpH\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}I\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04547
name: phonon
def: "Elementary excitation in the quantum mechanical treatment of vibrations in a crystal lattice. An energy bundle that behaves as a particle of energy \\\(h\\ \nu \\\)\, with \\\(\nu \\\) the vibration frequency and \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04548
name: phosphanes
def: "The saturated hydrides of tervalent phosphorus having the general formula $\\ce\{P_\{n\}H_\{n+2\}\}$. Individual members having an unbranched phosphorus chain are named phosphane\, diphosphane\, triphosphane\, etc. The name of a saturated hydride of phosphorus wherein one or more phosphorus atoms have a bonding number of 5 is formed by prefixing locants and \\\(\\lambda ^\{5\}\\\) symbols to the name of the corresponding phosphane. Hydrocarbyl derivatives of $\\ce\{PH3\}$ belong to the class phosphines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04549
name: phosphanylidenes
def: "Recommended name for carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce\{RP\{\:\}\}$ \(former IUPAC name is phosphinediyls\). A common non-IUPAC synonym is phosphinidenes."
synonym: "phosphinediyls" []
synonym: "phosphinidenes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04550
name: phosphatidic acids
def: "Derivatives of glycerol in which one hydroxy group\, commonly but not necessarily primary\, is esterified with phosphoric acid and the other two are esterified with fatty acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04551
name: phosphazenes
def: "Compounds containing a phosphorus–nitrogen double bond\, i.e. derivatives of $\\ce\{H3P=NH\}$ and $\\ce\{HP=NH\}$. A multiplicity of such bonds is present in various well-established chain\, ring and cage compounds. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04552
name: phosphine oxides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R3P=O  R3P^\{+\}–O^\{−\}\}$ \(analogously\, phosphine imides and phosphine sulfides\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04553
name: phosphines
def: "$\\ce\{PH3\}$ and compounds derived from it by substituting one\, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups $\\ce\{R3P\}$. $\\ce\{RPH2\}$\, $\\ce\{R2PH\}$ and $\\ce\{R3P\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\) are called primary\, secondary and tertiary phosphines\, respectively. A specific phosphine is preferably named as a substituted phosphane\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3PH2\}$ methylphosphane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04554
name: phosphinic acids
def: "$\\ce\{H2P\(=O\)OH\}$ \(phosphinic acid\) and its P-hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04556
name: phosphinous acids
def: "$\\ce\{H2POH\}$ \(phosphinous acid\) and its P-hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04557
name: phospho
def: "A prefix used in biochemical nomenclature in place of phosphono to denote the $\\ce\{-P\(=O\)\(OH\)2\}$ group linked to a heteroatom\, e.g. $\\ce\{Me3N^\{+\}-CH2CH2–OP\(=O\)\(OH\)O^\{-\}\}$ phosphocholine.An infix used in biochemical nomenclature to name phosphoric diesters\, e.g. glycerophosphocholine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04558
name: phosphoglycerides
def: "Phosphoric diesters\, esters of phosphatidic acids\, generally having a polar head group \($\\ce\{OH\}$ or $\\ce\{N2\}$\) on the esterified alcohol which typically is 2-aminoethanol\, choline\, glycerol\, inositol\, serine. The term includes lecithins\, cephalins. E.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04559
name: phospholipids
def: "Lipids containing phosphoric acid as mono- or di-esters\, including phosphatidic acids and phosphoglycerides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04560
name: phosphonic acids
def: "$\\ce\{HP\(=O\)\(OH\)2\}$ \(phosphonic acid\) and its P-hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04561
name: phosphonitriles
def: "Compounds of stoichiometric composition $\\ce\{\[X2PN\]_\{n\}\}$\, in which $\\ce\{X\}$ is alkoxy\, halogen\, or other electronegative group\, and \\\(n\\\) is a variable integer\, the value of which may not be known."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04562
name: phosphonium compounds
def: "Salts \(and hydroxides\) $\\ce\{\[R4P^\{+\}\]X^\{−\}\}$ containing tetracoordinate phosphonium ion and the associated anion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04563
name: phosphonium ylides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R3P^\{+\}–C^\{−\}R2  R3P=CR2\}$. Also known as Wittig reagents."
synonym: "Wittig reagents" []
synonym: "phosphorus ylides" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04564
name: phosphono
def: "A prefix indicating the presence of the group $\\ce\{–P\(=O\)\(OH\)2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04565
name: phosphonous acids
def: "$\\ce\{HP\(OH\)2\}$ \(phosphonous acid\) and its P-hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04566
name: phosphoramides
def: "Compounds in which one or more of the OH groups of phosphoric acid have been replaced with an amino or substituted amino group; commonly confined to the phosphoric triamides\, $\\ce\{P\(=O\)\(NR2\)3\, since replacement of one or two $\\ce\{OH\}$ groups produces phosphoramidic acids $\\ce\{P\(=O\)\(OH\)\(NR2\)2\}$\, $\\ce\{P\(=O\)\(OH\)2\(NR2\)\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04567
name: phosphoranes
def: "The mononuclear hydride $\\ce\{PH5\}$\, systematically named \\\(\\lambda ^\{5\}\\\)-phosphane\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives. By extension\, the literature also applies the term to phosphonium ylides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04568
name: phosphoranyl radicals
def: "A tetracoordinate phosphorus species which has nine valence-shell electrons $\\ce\{R4P^\{.\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04569
name: phosphorescence
def: "From a phenomenological point of view\, the term has been used to describe long-lived luminescence. In mechanistic photochemistry\, the term designates luminescence involving change in spin multiplicity\, typically from triplet to singlet or vice versa. The luminescence from a quartet state to a doublet state is also phosphorescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04571
name: phosphoroscope
def: "A mechanical device used to separate phosphorescence from fluorescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04574
name: photoacoustic detector
def: "A pressure-sensitive detector used to detect intermittent radiation absorbed in a black body or in the sample concerned. The resulting rapid temperature change produces a transient pressure oscillation that is observed with the help of a microphone\, or a piezoelectric device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04575
name: photoacoustic effect
def: "Generation of heat after absorption of radiation\, due to radiationless deactivation or chemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04576
name: photoacoustic spectroscopy
def: "A spectroscopic technique based on the photoacoustic effect. A photoacoustic spectrum consists of a plot of the intensity of the acoustic signal detected by a microphone or a 'piezoelectric' detector\, against the excitation wavelength or another quantity related to the photon energy of the modulated excitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04577
name: photoadsorption
def: "Irradiation by light \(usually visible or ultraviolet\) may affect adsorption. In a system containing adsorptive and adsorbent exposure to light may lead to increased adsorption \(photoadsorption\) or it may lead to desorption of an adsorbate \(photodesorption\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04578
name: photoaffinity labelling
def: "Technique by which a photochemically reactive molecular entity\, specifically associated with a biomolecule\, is photo-excited in order to covalently attach a label to the biomolecule\, usually via intermediates. Identification of the resulting photo-crosslinked product provides structural information on the protein′s binding site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04579
name: photoassisted catalysis
def: "Catalytic reaction involving production of a catalyst by absorption of light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04580
name: photocatalysis
def: "Change in the rate of a chemical reaction or its initiation under the action of ultraviolet\, visible or infrared radiation in the presence of a substance—the photocatalyst—that absorbs light and is involved in the chemical transformation of the reaction partners."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04583
name: photochemical equivalence
def: "According to the principle of photochemical equivalence\, there is a one-to-one relationship between the number of photons absorbed by a system and the number of excited species produced. The principle often fails for light of high intensity \(e.g. laser beams\)\, when molecules may absorb more than one photon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04585
name: photochemical reaction
def: "Generally used to describe a chemical reaction caused by absorption of ultraviolet\, visible or infrared radiation. There are many ground-state reactions\, which have photochemical counterparts. Among these are photochemical nitrogen extrusions\, photocycloadditions\, photodecarbonylations\, photodecarboxylations\, photoenolizations\, photo-Fries rearrangement\, photoisomerizations\, photooxidations\, photorearrangements\, photoreductions\, photosubstitutions\, etc."
synonym: "photoreaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04586
name: photochemical smog
def: "Product of photochemical reactions caused by solar radiation and occurring in polluted air as well as in air contaminated by compounds emitted by natural sources\, such as those emitted by vegetation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04588
name: photochemistry
def: "The branch of chemistry concerned with the chemical effects of light \(far UV to IR\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04589
name: photochromism
def: "Reversible transformation of a molecular entity between two forms\, A and B\, having different absorption spectra\, induced in one or both directions by absorption of electromagnetic radiation. The spectral change produced is typically\, but not necessarily\, of visible colour and is accompanied by differences in other physical properties. The thermodynamically stable form A is transformed by irradiation into form B. The back reaction can occur thermally \(photochromism of type T\) or photochemically \(photochromism of type P\).  The terms 'switch-on' and 'switch-of' are sometimes used to denote the two directions of the photochromic reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04590
name: photoconductive detector
def: "In a photoconductive detector an electric potential is applied across the absorbing region and causes a current to flow in proportion to the irradiance if the photon energy exceeds the energy gap between the valence and the conduction band. Depending on their spectral responsivity function\, photoconductive detectors are divided into photoconductive detectors for the visible wavelength range e.g. cadmium sulfide or $\\ce\{CdS\}$ photoconductive detectors\, photoconductive detectors for the near infrared wavelength range e.g. lead sulfide or $\\ce\{PbS\}$ photoconductive detectors\, photoconductive detectors for the infrared wavelength range e.g. silicon doped with arsenide or $\\ce\{Si\:As\}$ photoconductive detectors\, and the mercury-cadmium-telluride or $\\ce\{HgCdTe\}$ photoconductive detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04591
name: photoconductivity
def: "Electrical conductivity resulting from photoproduction of charge carriers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04592
name: photocrosslinking
def: "The formation of a covalent linkage between two macromolecules or between two different parts of one macromolecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04593
name: photochemical curing
def: "Curing induced by photo-irradiation."
synonym: "photo-curing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04594
name: photocurrent yield
def: "The quantum efficiency of electron transport between the two electrodes of a photovoltaic cell or photoelectrochemical cell."
synonym: "current yield" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04595
name: photodegradation
def: "The photochemical transformation of a molecule into lower molecular weight fragments\, usually in an oxidation process. This term is widely used in the destruction \(oxidation\) of pollutants by UV-based processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04597
name: photodetachment
def: "Ejection of an electron from a negative ion upon photoexcitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04598
name: photodiode
def: "A two-electrode\, radiation-sensitive junction formed in a semiconductive material. A junction is formed by two successive regions of a semiconductive material having\, respectively\, an excess of electrons \(n-type\) or holes \(p-type\). A bias potential applied to the detector creates a region at the interface that is depleted of majority carriers. Each incident photon produces electron-hole pairs in the depletion region resulting in a measurable signal current. The photodiode can be operated either with zero bias in the photovoltaic mode where the photodiode is actually generating the electric potential supplied to the load. In a biased mode\, the photoconductive mode\, the reverse current is proportional to the irradiation. A Schottky-barrier photodiode is constructed by deposition of a metal film on a semiconductor surface in such a way that no interface layer is present. The barrier thickness depends on the impurity dopant concentration in the semiconductor layer. The incident radiation generates electon-hole pairs within the depletion region of the barrier where they are collected efficiently and rapidly by the built-in field. A PIN \(p-intrinsic-n\) diode is a planar diffused diode consisting of a single crystal having an intrinsic \(undoped or compensated\) region sandwiched between p- and n-type regions. A bias potential applied across the detector depletes the intrinsic region of charge carriers\, constituting the radiation-sensitive detector volume. The number of electron-hole pairs produced is dependent on the energy of the incident photons. An avalanche photodiode is a photodiode in which the photogenerated electron-hole pairs are accelerated by a bias potential near to breakdown potential so that further electron-hole pairs are formed leading to saturation of the photocurrent. This operational mode for photon counting is the so-called Geiger mode\, similar to that of the gas filled Geiger counter. Avalanche photodiodes can also be operated in the proportional mode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04599
name: photodiode array
def: "An arrangement of a number of photodiodes on a single chip."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04600
name: photodynamic effect
def: "A term used in photobiology to refer to photoinduced damage requiring the simultaneous presence of light\, photosensitizer and molecular oxygen. A sensitized photooxidation which involves molecular oxygen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04601
name: photoelectrical effect
def: "The ejection of an electron from a solid or a liquid by a photon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04602
name: photoelectric attenuation coefficient
def: "The attenuation coefficient when only the photoelectric process is taken into account."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04604
name: photoelectric peak
def: "Of a spectrum of γ-radiation\, the part of the spectral response curve corresponding to the absorption in the radiation detector by the photoelectrical effect of the detected $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{energy\}$. In most cases the peak also contains events caused by multiple processes and use of the expression full energy peak or full energy peak is to be preferred. Synonymous with photopeak."
synonym: "photopeak" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04605
name: photoelectrochemical cell
def: "An electrochemical cell in which current and a voltage are simultaneously produced upon absorption of light by one or more of the electrodes. Usually at least one of the electrodes is a semiconductor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04606
name: photoelectrochemical etching
def: "The dissolution of a semiconductor in an electrolytic solution upon exposure to light. Used in the photopatterning of semiconductor surfaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04607
name: photoelectrochemistry
def: "A term applied to a hybrid field of chemistry employing techniques which combine photochemical and electrochemical methods for the study of the oxidation-reduction chemistry of the ground or excited states of molecules or ions. In general\, it is the chemistry resulting from the interaction of light with electrochemical systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04608
name: photoelectrolytic cell
def: "A cell in which radiant energy causes a net chemical conversion in the cell\, e.g. so as to produce hydrogen as a useful fuel. These cells can be classified as photosynthetic or photocatalytic. In the former case\, radiant energy provides a Gibbs energy to drive a reaction such as \\\(\\rm\{H\}_\{2\}\\rm\{O\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{H\}_\{2\}+\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\rm\{O\}_\{2\}\\\) \, and electrical or thermal energy may be later recovered by allowing the reverse\, spontaneous reaction to proceed. In a photocatalytic cell the photon absorption promotes a reaction with \\\(\\Delta G < 0\\\) so there is no net storage of chemical energy\, but the radiant energy speeds up a slow reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04609
name: photoelectron spectroscopy
def: "A spectroscopic technique which measures the kinetic energy of electrons emitted upon the ionization of a substance by high energy monochromatic photons. A photoelectron spectrum is a plot of the number of electrons emitted versus their kinetic energy. The spectrum consists of bands due to transitions from the ground state of an atom or molecular entity to the ground and excited states of the corresponding radical cation. Approximate interpretations are usually based on 'Koopmans theorem' and yield orbital energies. PES and UPS \(UV photoelectron spectroscopy\) refer to the spectroscopy using vacuum ultraviolet sources\, while ESCA \(electron spectroscopy for chemical analysis\) and XPS use X-ray sources."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04611
name: photoelectron yield
def: "The number of photoelectrons emitted by the sample per incident photon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04612
name: photoemissive detector
def: "A detector in which a photon interacts with a solid surface\, which is called the photocathode\, or a gas\, releasing a photoelectron. This process is called the external photoelectric effect. The photoelectrons are collected by an electrode at positive electric potential\, i.e. the anode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04613
name: photoexcitation
def: "The production of an excited state by the absorption of ultraviolet\, visible\, or infrared radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04614
name: photo-Fries rearrangement
def: "A photorearrangement of aryl or acyl esters to give the-rearranged product \(as well as the-rearranged product\)\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04615
name: photogalvanic cell
def: "An electrochemical cell in which current or voltage changes result from photochemically generated changes in the relative concentrations of reactants in a solution phase oxidation-reduction couple."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04617
name: photoinduced electron transfer
def: "An electron transfer resulting from an electronic state produced by the resonant interaction of electromagnetic radiation with matter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04618
name: photoinduced polymerization
def: "The polymerization of a monomer by a free radical or ionic chain reaction initiated by photoexcitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04619
name: photoinitiation
def: "The photoproduction of a free radical or ion capable of initiating a chain reaction such as a polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04620
name: photoionization
def: "The term generally used to describe ionization of any species by photons. The process may\, for example\, be written  Electrons and photons do not 'impact' molecules or atoms; they interact with them in ways that result in various electronic excitations including ionization. For this reason it is recommended that in mass spectrometry the terms 'electron impact' and 'photon impact' not be used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04621
name: photoionization detector
def: "Radiation from an ultraviolet lamp ionizes certain species in the carrier gas. A potential difference is applied and the resulting ionization current is detected. The detector is only useful for substances with ionization potentials below about \\\(11\\ \\rm\{eV\}\\\). This makes it quite useful for detecting one component of a combined eluent when the other component\, for instance nitrogen\, has a high ionization potential. The detector has a small linear dynamic range and is capable of detecting substances below \\\(1\\ \\rm\{ppmv\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04622
name: photoisomerization
def: "Photochemical process leading to an isomerization of the substrate\, either by bond rotation\, skeletal rearrangement or atom- or group- transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04623
name: photoluminescence
def: "Luminescence from direct photoexcitation of the emitting species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04624
name: photolysis
def: "The cleavage of one or more covalent bonds in a molecular entity resulting from absorption of light\, or a photochemical process in which such cleavage is an essential part. For example\:  The term is used incorrectly to describe irradiation of a sample\, although in the combination flash photolysis this usage is accepted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04625
name: photometry
def: "The measurement of light over wavelengths which produce visual sensation \(approximately \\\(380\{-\}780\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\). Thus photometric quantities do not refer to a specific wavelength but to the light emitted by a standard source \(formerly a 'standard international candle'\, now a blackbody radiator emitting at the temperature of solidifying platinum\, \\\(2042\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\). luminance and illuminance are the photometric analogues of the radiometric quantities radiance and irradiance\, respectively\, but conversion from photometric units \(e.g. \\\(\\rm\{lm cm\}^\{-2\}\\\)\) to radiometric units \(e.g. \\\(\\pu\{J s-1 cm-2\}\\\)\) requires convolution over wavelength of the spectral radiation with the relative spectral response of the human eye. The standard response is called the 'spectral luminous efficiency of radiation'\, and is tabulated for daylight adapted vision in photopic response tables."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04626
name: photomultiplier tube
def: "A vacuum phototube with additional amplification by electron multiplication. It consists of a photocathode\, a series of dynodes\, called a dynode chain on which a secondary-electron multiplication process occurs\, and an anode. According to the desired response time\, transit time\, time spread\, gain\, or low dark current\, different types of dynode structures have been developed\, e.g. circular cage structure\, linear focused structure\, venetian blind structure\, box and grid structure. Some special dynode structures permit combination with additional electric or magnetic fields. A strip dynode photomultiplier tube consists of a photocathode followed by thin dynode material on an insulating substrate. In a continuous-strip photomultiplier\, two strip dynodes are arranged in parallel. A potential applied to the ends of the two strips produces an electric field across the continuous strip dynodes\, giving rise to electron multiplication along the dynodes. In a resistance-strip magnetic photomultiplier\, a uniform magnetic field is applied to the planes of the strips\, so that the electrons travel in the crossed electric and magnetic fields. A channel photomultiplier tube photocathode consists of a channel electron multiplier \(CEM\) system for the photoelectrons\, and an anode to collect the final electron current. The basic part of the CEM is a tube with a semiconducting inner surface. In general it is curved in order to inhibit the acceleration of positive ions towards the photocathode. A number of small channels called microchannels can be constructed in arrays for imaging applications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04627
name: photon
def: "Particle of zero charge\, zero rest mass\, spin quantum number 1\, energy \\\(h\\ \nu \\\) and momentum \\\(\\frac\{h\\ \nu \}\{c\}\\\)\(\\\(h\\\) is the Planck constant\, \\\(\nu \\\) the frequency of radiation and \\\(c\\\) the speed of light\)\, carrier of electromagnetic force."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04629
name: photon counting
def: "Recording of sequential single photons counted by way of recording and counting sequential electron pulses at the anode of the photomultiplier."
synonym: "single-photon counting" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04631
name: photon exitance
def: "Photon flux\, number basis\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, emitted by an element of the surface containing the source point under consideration divided by the area \\\(S\\\) of that element. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\\)."
synonym: "photon emittance" []
synonym: "specific photon emission" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04632
name: photon exposure
def: "Photon irradiance\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, integrated over the time of irradiation for a beam incident from all upward directions. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04633
name: photon flow
def: "The number of photons \(quanta\, \\\(N\\\)\) per unit time. \(\\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}N\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\)\, simplified expression\: \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} _\{\\rm\{p\}\} = \\frac\{N\}\{t\}\\\) when the number of photons is constant over the time considered\). The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\). Alternatively\, the term can be used with the amount of photons \(mol or its equivalent einstein\)\, the SI unit then being \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04634
name: photon fluence
def: "Amount of photons \(quanta of radiation\) incident on a small sphere from all directions\, divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere and integrated over time. Photons per surface area \(\\\(\\rm\{quanta m\}^\{-2\}\\\)\). SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04635
name: photon fluence rate
def: "Rate of photon fluence. Total number of photons \(\\\(N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\) incident from all directions on a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of the sphere and per time interval. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\). Same as photon fluence rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04636
name: photon flux
def: "Number of photons \(quanta of radiation\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\) per time interval. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04637
name: photon irradiance
def: "Number of photons \(quanta of radiation\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\) per time interval \(photon flux\)\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, incident from all upward directions on a small element of surface containing the point under consideration divided by the area of the element. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\). Equivalent definition\: Integral\, taken over the hemisphere visible from the given point\, of the expression \\\(L_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\, \\rm\{cos\}\\\:\\theta\\\, \\rm\{d\}\\varOmega\\\) the photon radiance at the given point in the various directions of the incident beam of solid angle \\\(\\varOmega\\\) and \\\(\\theta\\\) the angle between any of these beams and the normal to the surface at the given point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04638
name: photon quantities
def: "A group of quantities characterizing electromagnetic radiation in terms of the number of photons. The symbols of photon quantities may be distinguished by adding a subscript p."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04639
name: photon radiance
def: "Number of photons \(quanta of radiation\, \\\(N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\) per time interval \(photon flux\)\, \\\(q_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, leaving or passing through a small transparent element of surface in a given direction from the source about the solid angle \\\(\\varOmega\\\)\, divided by the solid angle and by the orthogonally projected area of the element in a plane normal to the given beam direction\, \\\(\\rm\{d\}S_\{\\perp \} = \\rm\{d\}S\\\, cos\\\,\\theta\\\)\, with \\\(\\theta\\\) the angle between the normal to the surface and the direction of the beam. Equivalent definition\: Integral taken over the hemisphere visible from the given point\, of the expression \\\(L_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\, cos\\\,\\theta\\\,\\rm\{d\}\\varOmega\\\)\, with \\\(L_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) the photon radiance at the given point in the various directions of the incident beam of solid angle \\\(\\varOmega\\\) and \\\(\\theta\\\) the angle between any of these beams and the normal to the surface at the given point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04640
name: photooxidation
def: "Oxidation reactions induced by light. Common processes are\: 1\, the loss of one or more electrons from a chemical species as a result of photoexcitation of that species; 2\, the reaction of a substance with oxygen under the influence of light. When oxygen remains in the product this latter process is also called photooxygenation. Reactions in which neither the substrate nor the oxygen are electronically excited are sometimes called photoinitiated oxidations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04641
name: photooxygenation
def: "The incorporation of molecular oxygen into a molecular entity. There are three common mechanisms\: Type I\: the reaction of triplet molecular oxygen with radicals formed photochemically. Type II\: the reaction of photochemically produced singlet molecular oxygen with molecular entities to give rise to oxygen containing molecular entities. The third mechanism proceeds by electron transfer producing superoxide anion as the reactive species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04645
name: photophoresis
def: "In aerosol physics\, the motion of particles due to the influence of light. In many cases\, this amounts to a special form of thermophoresis due to the heating of the particles by the light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04646
name: photophosphorylation
def: "The transformation of light \(solar\) energy for the reduction of $\\ce\{CO2\}$ and the generation of adenosine 5'-triphosphate\, ATP."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04647
name: photophysical processes
def: "Photoexcitation and subsequent events which lead from one to another state of a molecular entity through radiation and radiationless transitions. No chemical change results."
synonym: "primary photoprocess" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04648
name: photopolymerization
def: "Polymerization processes requiring a photon for the propagation step."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04650
name: photoreduction
def: "Reduction reactions induced by light. Common processes are\: 1\, addition of one or more electrons to a photoexcited species; 2\, the photochemical hydrogenation of a substance. Reactions in which the substrate is not electronically excited are sometimes called photoinitiated reductions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04651
name: photoresist
def: "A photoimaging material\, generally applied as a thin film\, whose local solubility properties can be altered photochemically. A subsequent development step produces an image which is useful for the fabrication of microelectronic devices \(e.g. integrated circuits\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04652
name: photosensitization
def: "The process by which a photochemical or photophysical alteration occurs in one molecular entity as a result of initial absorption of radiation by another molecular entity called a 'photosensitizer'. In mechanistic photochemistry the term is limited to cases in which the photosensitizer is not consumed in the reaction."
synonym: "sensitization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04654
name: photostationary state
def: "A steady state reached by a reacting chemical system when light has been absorbed by at least one of the components. At this state the rates of formation and disappearance are equal for each of the transient molecular entities formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04655
name: photosynthesis
def: "A metabolic process involving plants and some types of bacteria \(e.g. Chromatiaceae\, Rhodospirillaceae\, Chlorobiaceae\) in which light energy absorbed by chlorophyll and other photosynthetic pigments results in the reduction of $\\ce\{CO2\}$ followed by the formation of organic compounds. In plants the overall process involves the conversion of $\\ce\{CO2\}$ and $\\ce\{H2O\}$ to carbohydrates \(and other plant material\) and the release of $\\ce\{O2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04656
name: photothermal effect
def: "An effect produced by photoexcitation resulting partially or totally in the production of heat."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04657
name: photothermography
def: "A process utilizing both light and heat\, simultaneously or sequentially\, for image recording."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04658
name: phototransistor
def: "A bipolar transistor with its base-collector junction acting as a photodiode\, which\, if irradiated\, controls the response of the device. Due to the inherent current gain \(of the transistor\) the responsivity of the phototransistor is greater than that of photodiodes. A Darlington phototransistor consists of two separate transistors coupled in the high-impedance Darlington configuration with a phototransistor as the input transistor. A field effect phototransistor or photo-FET is a field effect transistor \(FET\) that employs photogeneration of carriers in the channel region \(the neutral region sandwiched between the insulator and the depletion region under the gate of the FET\). It is characterized by high responsivity due to the high current gain of the FET."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04659
name: photovoltaic cell
def: "A solid state device\, usually a semiconductor such as silicon\, which absorbs photons with energies higher than or equal to the bandgap energy and simultaneously produces electric power."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04660
name: pH-rate profile
def: "A plot of observed rate coefficient\, or more usually its decadic logarithm\, against pH of solution\, other variables being kept constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04662
name: phthaleins
def: "3\,3-Bis\(hydroxyaryl\)-2-benzofuran-1\(3H\)-ones generally derived from the condensation of phthalic anhydride with phenols. E.g. phenolphthalein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04663
name: phthalides
def: "3\,3-Di\(hydrocarbyl\) or 3-hydrocarbylidene-2-benzofuran-1\(3H\)-ones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04665
name: physical network
def: "Polymer network with junction points or zones formed by physically interacting chains which need not be permanent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04666
name: physical quantity
def: "An attribute of a phenomenon\, body or substance that may be distinguished qualitatively and determined quantitatively."
synonym: "measurable quantity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04667
name: physisorption
def: "Adsorption in which the forces involved are intermolecular forces \(van der Waals forces\) of the same kind as those responsible for the imperfection of real gases and the condensation vapours\, and which do not involve a significant change in the electronic orbital patterns of the species involved. The term 'van der Waals adsorption' is synonymous with physical adsorption\, but its use is not recommended."
synonym: "physical adsorption" []
synonym: "van der Waals adsorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04668
name: phytotoxicant
def: "An agent which produces a toxic effect in vegetation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04670
name: pico
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-12\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{p\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04671
name: picrates
def: "Salts or charge-transfer complexes of picric acid \(2\,4\,6-trinitrophenol\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04672
name: piezoluminescence
def: "Luminescence observed when certain solids are subjected to a change in pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04673
name: pile-up
def: "The processing by a radiation spectrometer of pulses resulting from the simultaneous adsorption of independent particles or photons in a radiation detector. As a result they are counted as one single particle or photon with energy between the individual energies and the sum of these energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04674
name: pinacols
def: "Tetra\(hydrocarbyl\)ethane-1\,2-diols\, $\\ce\{R2C\(OH\)–\(OH\)R2\}$\, of which the tetramethyl example is the simplest one and is itself commonly known as pinacol \(benzpinacol is the tetraphenyl analogue\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04676
name: PIN semiconductor detector
def: "A semiconductor detector consisting of a compensated region between a p and a n region. The compensated region is often referred to as intrinsic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04677
name: pitch
def: "A residue from pyrolysis of organic material or tar distillation which is solid at room temperature\, consisting of a complex mixture of numerous\, essentially aromatic hydrocarbons and heterocyclic compounds. It exhibits a broad softening range instead of a defined melting temperature. When cooled from the melt\, pitches solidify without crystallization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04678
name: pitch-based carbon fibres
def: "Carbon fibres obtained from pitch precursor fibres after stabilization treatment\, carbonization and final heat treatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04679
name: pitting corrosion
def: "A special type of non-uniform corrosion of passive metals\, resulting in the formation of pits\, which usually occurs in the presence of certain anions at electrode potentials positive to a critical pitting potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04681
name: planar chirality
def: "Term used by some authorities to refer to stereoisomerism resulting from the arrangement of out-of-plane groups with respect to a plane \(chirality plane\). It is exemplified by the atropisomerism of \(E\)-cyclooctene \(chiral plane = double bond and attached atoms\) or monosubstituted paracyclophane \(chiral plane = substituted ring\). The configuration of molecular entities possessing planar chirality is specified by the stereodescriptors \\\(R_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) and \\\(S_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) \(or \\\(P\\\) and \\\(M\\\)\)."
synonym: "planar stereoisomerism" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04682
name: planar chromatography
def: "A separation technique in which the stationary phase is present as or on a plane. The plane can be a paper\, serving as such or impregnated by a substrate as the stationary bed \(paper chromatography\, PC\) or a layer of solid particles spread on a support e.g. a glass plate \(thin layer chromatography\, TLC\). Sometimes planar chromatography is also termed open-bed chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04683
name: planar film
def: "A film whose boundaries are represented by ideal parallel planes. The planar film thickness is defined as the distance between these planes. The terms nanometre thick film\, micrometre thick film and millimetre thick film refer specifically to film thickness in the ranges \\\(0.1\{-\}100\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\, \\\(0.1\{-\}100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) and \\\(0.1\{-\}100\\ \\rm\{mm\}\\\)\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04685
name: Planck constant
def: "The Planck constant\, \\\(h\\\)\, is one of seven fixed constants defining the International System of Units\, the SI\, with \\\(h = \\pu\{6.62607015E-34 J s\}\\\). The Planck constant is considered as a fundamental constant of nature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04687
name: plasma
def: "A gas which is at least partly ionized and contains particles of various types\, viz. electrons\, atoms\, ions and molecules. The plasma as a whole is electrically neutral."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04688
name: plasma desorption ionization
def: "The ionization of any species by interaction with heavy particles \(which may be ions or neutral atoms\) formed as a result of the fission of a suitable nuclide adjacent to a target supporting the sample."
synonym: "fission fragment ionization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04689
name: plasmid
def: "An extrachromosomal genetic element consisting generally of a circular duplex of DNA which can replicate independently of chromosomal DNA. R-plasmids are responsible for the mutual transfer of antibiotic resistance among microbes. Plasmids are used as vectors for cloning DNA in bacteria or yeast host cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04690
name: plastic flow
def: "Steady flow occurring only above a certain finite stress."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04691
name: plastic transition
def: "A first-order transition that occurs when a low-temperature phase transforms to an orientationally-disordered high-temperature phase with a strong variation of enthalpy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04692
name: plateau border
def: "Except for free-floating bubbles\, films have to be supported by frames\, bulk surfaces or other films. The transition zone separating these from the film proper\, always containing some bulk liquid\, is called a plateau border."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04693
name: plate height
def: "The column length \(\\\(L\\\)\) divided by the plate number\: \\\[H=\\frac\{L\}\{N\}\\\] It is also called the height equivalent to one theoretical plate \(HETP\)."
synonym: "height equivalent to one theoretical plate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04694
name: plate number
def: "A number indicative of column performance\, calculated from the following equations which depend on the selection of the peak width expression\: \\\[N=\(\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\}\{\\sigma \}\)^\{2\}=\(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\}\{\\sigma \}\)^\{2\}\\\] \\\[N=16\\ \(\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\}\)^\{2\}=16\\ \(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\}\)^\{2\}\\\] \\\[N=5.545\\ \(\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\}\)^\{2\}=5.545\\ \(\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{h\}\}\}\)^\{2\}\\\] The value of 5.545 stands for \\\(8\\ \\ln 2\\\). These expressions assume a Gaussian \(symmetrical\) peak. In these expressions the units for the quantities inside the brackets must be consistent so that their ratio is dimensionless\: i.e. if the numerator is a volume\, then peak width must also be expressed in terms of volume. In former nomenclature the expressions 'number of theoretical plates' or 'theoretical plate number' were used for the same term. For simplification\, the present name is suggested."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04695
name: pleiotropic gene
def: "A gene affecting more than one \(apparently unrelated\) characteristic of the phenotype."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04696
name: ploidy
def: "A term indicating the number of sets of chromosomes present in an organism\, e.g. haploid \(one\) or diploid \(two\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04697
name: plug-flow
def: "A packed bed flow reactor is commonly called a fixed bed reactor and the term plug-flow is also used to indicate that no attempt is made to back-mix the reaction mixture as it passes through the catalyst bed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04699
name: plumbylidenes
def: "Carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce\{R2Pb\{\:\}\}$."
synonym: "plumbylenes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04700
name: plume
def: "The gaseous and aerosol effluents which are emitted from a chimney and the volume of space which they occupy \(often visible\). The shape of the plume and the concentrations of pollutants in it at various points along the path of the plume are sensitive functions of the meteorology\, local topography and the chemistry which occurs in the plume. Urban plumes are observed over many urban areas and downwind of these areas in which a combination of man sources of pollution are concentrated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04702
name: pneumatic detector
def: "A pressure-sensitive detector based on the pressure increase of a gas. A special type is the Golay cell where the pressure change is detected by observing the deflection off one of the chamber walls."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04703
name: point group
def: "The classification of the symmetry elements of an object. It is denoted in the Schoenflies notation by an italic symbol\, such as \\\(C_\{3\}\\\)\, \\\(D_\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\)\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04704
name: point of zero charge
def: "A surface charge is at its point of zero charge when the surface charge density is zero. It is a value of the negative logarithm of the activity in the bulk of the charge-determining ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04705
name: poise
def: "cgs unit of dynamic viscosity\, \\\(\\rm\{P\} = 0.1\\ \\rm\{Pa s\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04706
name: poison
def: "An inhibitory substance characterized by its propensity to attach very strongly\, by a true chemical bond \(e.g. covalent\) to the surface atoms or ions constituting the catalytically active sites. Poisons act in minute quantities. Typical poisons are $\\ce\{S\}$\, $\\ce\{As\}$\, etc. In most cases\, activity and/or selectivity cannot be recovered without a drastic change in operating conditions \(most often a regeneration\). recovery\, if at all\, takes place very slowly and/or only partially."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04707
name: Poisson distribution
def: "A discrete distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form\: \\\[f_\{\\rm\{w\}\}\(x\)=\\frac\{x\}\{a\\\,+\\\,1\}=\\frac\{\\rm\{e\}^\{-a\}\\ a^\{x-1\}\}\{\(x\\\,-\\\,1\)!\}\\\] where \\\(x\\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length\, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization and \\\(a\\\) is a positive adjustable parameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04709
name: polar effect
def: "For a reactant molecule RY\, the polar effect of the group R comprises all the processes whereby a substituent may modify the electrostatic forces operating at the reaction centre Y\, relative to the standard \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\rm\{Y\}\\\). These forces may be governed by charge separations arising from differences in the electronegativity of atoms \(leading to the presence of dipoles\)\, the presence of unipoles\, or electron delocalization. It is synonymous with electronic effect or 'electrical effect' of a substituent as distinguished from other substituent effects\, e.g. steric effects. Sometimes\, however\, the term 'polar effect' is taken to refer to the influence\, other than steric\, that non-conjugated substituents exert on reaction rates\, i.e. effects connected with electron delocalization between a substituent and the molecular framework to which it is attached are excluded. Polar effect is then not synonymous with electronic effect"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04710
name: polarity
def: "When applied to solvents\, this rather ill-defined term covers their overall solvation capability \(solvation power\) for solutes \(i.e. in chemical equilibria\: reactants and products; in reaction rates\: reactants and activated complex; in light absorptions\: ions or molecules in the ground and excited state\)\, which in turn depends on the action of all possible\, nonspecific and specific\, intermolecular interactions between solute ions or molecules and solvent molecules\, excluding such interactions leading to definite chemical alterations of the ions or molecules of the solute. Occasionally\, the term solvent polarity is restricted to nonspecific solute/solvent interactions only \(i.e. to van der Waals forces\)."
synonym: "polar solvent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04711
name: polarizability
def: "The ease of distortion of the electron cloud of a molecular entity by an electric field \(such as that due to the proximity of a charged reagent\). It is experimentally measured as the ratio of induced dipole moment \(\\\(\\mu _\{\\mathrm\{ind\}\}\\\)\) to the field \\\(E\\\) which induces it\: \\\[\\alpha =\\frac\{\\mu _\{\\rm\{ind\}\}\}\{E\}\\\] The units of \\\(\\alpha \\\) are \\\(\\rm\{C\}^\{2\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{2\}\\ \\rm\{V\}^\{-1\}\\\). In ordinary usage the term refers to the 'mean polarizability'\, i.e.\, the average over three rectilinear axes of the molecule. Polarizabilities in different directions \(e.g. along the bond in $\\ce\{Cl2\}$\, called 'longitudinal polarizability'\, and in the direction perpendicular to the bond\, called 'transverse polarizability'\) can be distinguished\, at least in principle. Polarizability along the bond joining a substituent to the rest of the molecule is seen in certain modern theoretical approaches as a factor influencing chemical reactivity\, etc.\, and parametrization thereof has been proposed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04712
name: polarization
def: "The relevant material property that couples with the radiation field. May be called optical or dielectric polarization. Optical spectroscopies may be classified according to the dielectric polarization power-law dependence on the external electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04713
name: polarization
def: "The difference of the electrode potential from the corrosion potential. The symbol should not be used for polarization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04714
name: polarization error
def: "An error which arises from the fact that the absorbance of a sample \(especially a solid\) could depend on the polarization of the incident radiation and from the polarization dependent response of the photodetector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04715
name: polarized interphases
def: "Ideally\, interphases for which there are no common components between the phases or the exchange of these is hindered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04716
name: polarography
def: "A measure of current as a function of potential when the working electrode is a dropping mercury \(or other liquid conductor\) electrode and unstirred solutions are used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04720
name: polyaddition
def: "A polymerization in which the growth of polymer chains proceeds by addition reactions between molecules of all degrees of polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04721
name: polychromator
def: "An extension of a monochromator\, a number of exit slits being placed in the focal plane\, so allowing a number of discrete bands to pass through \(to fall\, for example\, upon a number of photomultipliers\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04722
name: polycondensation
def: "A polymerization in which the growth of polymer chains proceeds by condensation reactions between molecules of all degrees of polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04723
name: polycrystalline graphite
def: "A graphite material with coherent crystallographic domains of limited size regardless of the perfection and preferred orientation \(texture\) of their crystalline structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04724
name: polycyclic system
def: "A molecular system regarded as containing a number of rings equal to the number of scissions required to convert it into an open-chain compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04726
name: polydisperse medium
def: "A colloidal system in which many particle sizes occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04728
name: polyelectrolyte
def: "Polymer composed of macromolecules in which a substantial portion of the constitutional units contains ionic or ionizable groups\, or both."
synonym: "polymer electrolyte" []
synonym: "polymeric electrolyte" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04730
name: polygranular carbon
def: "A carbon material composed of grains\, which can be clearly distinguished by means of optical microscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04731
name: polygranular graphite
def: "A graphite material composed of grains which can be clearly distinguished by means of optical microscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04732
name: polyhedranes
def: "Polycyclic hydrocarbons of the $\\ce\{\(CH\)_\{n\}\}$ series having skeletons corresponding to the regular and semiregular geometrical solids\, e.g. cubane\,"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04733
name: polyions
def: "Polycations or polyanions — multiply charged ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04734
name: polyketides
def: "Natural compounds containing alternating carbonyl and methylene groups \('$\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}polyketones\}$'\)\, biogenetically derived from repeated condensation of acetyl coenzyme A \(via malonyl coenzyme A\)\, and usually the compounds derived from them by further condensations. Considered by many to be synonymous with the less frequently used terms acetogenins and ketides."
synonym: "acetogenins" []
synonym: "ketides" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04735
name: polymer
def: "A substance composed of macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04736
name: polymer blend
def: "Macroscopically homogeneous mixture of two or more different species of polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04737
name: polymer crystal
def: "A crystalline domain usually limited by well-defined boundaries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04738
name: polymer crystallite
def: "A small crystalline domain. A crystallite may have irregular boundaries and parts of its constituent macromolecules may extend beyond its boundaries. This definition is not identical with that used in classical crystallography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04740
name: polymerization
def: "The process of converting a monomer mixture of monomers into a polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04743
name: polymer–polymer complex
def: "Complex\, at least two components of which are different polymers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04744
name: polymer-poor phase
def: "That phase of a two-phase equilibrium system\, consisting of a polymer and low molecular weight material\, in which the polymer concentration is lower. The use of the name 'sol phase' is discouraged. Synonymous with dilute phase."
synonym: "dilute phase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04745
name: polymer-rich phase
def: "That phase of a two-phase equilibrium system\, consisting of a polymer and low molecular weight material\, in which the polymer concentration is higher. The use of the name 'gel phase' is discouraged. Synonymous with concentrated phase."
synonym: "concentrated phase" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04746
name: polymer–solvent interaction
def: "The sum of the effects of all intermolecular interactions between polymer and solvent molecules in solution that are reflected in the Gibbs and Helmholtz energies of mixing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04747
name: polymolecularity correction
def: "A correction applied to relationships between a property and the molar mass or relative molecular mass\, obtained from polymers non-uniform with respect to relative molecular mass\, in order to obtain the corresponding relationship for polymers strictly uniform with respect to relative molecular mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04748
name: polymorphic transition
def: "A reversible transition of a solid crystalline phase at a certain temperature and pressure \(the inversion point\) to another phase of the same chemical composition with a different crystal structure."
synonym: "enantiotropic transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04749
name: polypeptides
def: "Peptides containing ten or more amino acid residues."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04750
name: polyprenols
def: "A subgroup of prenols in which \\\(n\\\) in the general formula $\\ce\{H–\[CH2–C\(CH3\)=CH–CH2\]_\{n\}–OH\}$ is greater than four."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04751
name: polyquinanes
def: "Saturated or unsaturated\, respectively\, polycyclic hydrocarbons consisting of fused five-membered rings\, commonly but not necessarily incorporating the skeleton of quinacene \(tricyclo\[5.2.1.04\,10\]deca-2\,5\,8-triene\)\: E.g.  bicyclo\[3.3.0\]octane\, dodecahedrane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04752
name: polysaccharides
def: "Compounds consisting of a large number of monosaccharides linked glycosidically. This term is commonly used only for those containing more than ten monosaccharide residues. Also called glycans."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04753
name: polysulfanes
def: "Compounds having an unbranched chain of sulfur atoms \($\\ce\{s2\}$ or higher\) terminating in H\: $\\ce\{HS_\{n\}H\}$. Some chemists exclude disulfane\, $\\ce\{HS2H\}$\, from the class polysulfanes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04754
name: polysulfides
def: "Compounds $\\ce\{R–\[S\]_\{n\}–R\}$\, with a chain of sulfur atoms \(\\\(n\\geq 2\\\)\) and $\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$. Some chemists exclude disulfides\, $\\ce\{RS2R\}$\, from the class polysulfides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04755
name: polytopal rearrangement
def: "Stereoisomerization interconverting different or equivalent spatial arrangements of ligands about a central atom or of a cage of atoms\, where the ligand or cage defines the vertices of a polyhedron. For example pyramidal inversion of amines\, Berry pseudorotation of $\\ce\{PF5\}$\, rearrangements of polyhedral boranes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04756
name: polytypic transition
def: "A transition of a crystalline structure into one or more forms which differ in the way identical layers of atoms are stacked. Example\: $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ consists of two identical close packings\, one of $\\ce\{Zn\}$ atoms\, the other $\\ce\{S\}$ atoms\, with the one displaced to the other along the c-axis through one-quarter of the layer spacing. In sphalerite-type $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ the layers have the face-centred-cubic \(ABC ABC\) sequence\, in wurtzite-type $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ they have the hexagonal-close-packed \(AB AB\) sequence. The transition of sphalerite-type $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ to wurtzite-type $\\ce\{ZnS\}$ occurs at \\\(1297\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04758
name: pooled standard deviation
def: "A problem often arises when the combination of several series of measurements performed under similar conditions is desired to achieve an improved estimate of the imprecision of the process. If it can be assumed that all the series are of the same precision although their means may differ\, the pooled standard deviations \\\(s_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) from \\\(k\\\) series of measurements can be calculated as \\\[s_\{\\rm\{p\}\}=\\sqrt\{\\frac\{\(n_\{1\}- 1\)\\ s_\{1\}^\{2\}+\(n_\{2\}- 1\)\\ s_\{2\}^\{2\}+...+\(n_\{k\}- 1\)\\ s_\{k\}^\{2\}\}\{n_\{1\}+n_\{2\}+...+n_\{k\}- k\}\}\\\] The suffices \\\(1\,2\,...\,k\\\) refer to the different series of measurements. In this case it is assumed that there exists a single underlying standard deviation \\\(\\sigma \\\) of which the pooled standard deviation \\\(s_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) is a better estimate than the individual calculated standard deviations \\\(s_\{1\}\,s_\{2\}\,...\,s_\{k\}\\\)\, For the special case where \\\(k\\\) sets of duplicate measurements are available\, the above equation reduces to \\\[s_\{\\rm\{p\}\}=\\sqrt\{\\frac\{\\sum \(x_\{i1\}- x_\{i2\}\)^\{2\}\}\{2\\ k\}\}\\\] Results from various series of measurements can be combined in the following way to give a pooled relative standard deviation \\\(s_\{\\rm\{r\,p\}\}\\\)\: \\\[s_\{\\rm\{r\,p\}\} = \\sqrt\{\\frac\{\\sum \(n_\{i\}-1\)\\ s_\{\\rm\{r\,\}i\}^\{2\}\}\{\\sum n_\{i\}- 1\}\} = \\sqrt\{\\frac\{\\sum \(n_\{i\}-1\)\\ s_\{i\}^\{2\}\\ x_\{i\}^\{-2\}\}\{\\sum n_\{i\}-1\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04759
name: population inversion
def: "A situation in which a higher energy state is more populated than a lower energy state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04760
name: pore size distribution
def: "The distribution of pore volume with respect to pore size; alternatively\, it may be defined by the related distribution of pore area with respect to pore size."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04762
name: porosity
def: "A concept related to texture\, referring to the pore space in a material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04763
name: porous-layer open-tabular column
def: "A column in which there is a porous layer on the inner wall. porosity can be achieved by either chemical means \(e.g. etching\) or by the deposition of porous particles on the wall from a suspension. The porous layer may serve as a support for a liquid stationary phase or as the stationary phase itself."
synonym: "PLOT column" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04764
name: porphyrinogens
def: "Hexahydroporphyrins in which the nitrogen atoms and four meso positions are saturated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04765
name: porphyrins
def: "Natural pigments containing a fundamental skeleton of four pyrrole nuclei united through the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\mbox\{-\}positions\}$ by four methine groups to form a macrocyclic structure \(porphyrin is designated porphine in Chemical Abstracts indexes\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04766
name: position-sensitive photomultiplier tube
def: "In position-sensitive photomultiplier tubes spatial resolution is obtained with the help of a partitioned photocathode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04768
name: positive ion
def: "This is an atom\, radical\, molecule or molecular moiety which has lost one of more electrons thereby attaining an electrically positive charge. The use of the term cation as an alternative is not recommended. The use of mass ion is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04769
name: positron
def: "A positively charged electron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04770
name: positronium
def: "Atom-like pair of particles consisting of an anti-electron \(positron\) and an electron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04771
name: post-column derivatization
def: "A version of reaction chromatography in which the separated components eluting from the column are derivatized prior to entering the detector. The derivatization process is generally carried out 'on-the-fly'\, i.e. during transfer of the sample components from the column to the detector. Derivatization may also be carried out before the sample enters the column or the planar medium; this is pre-column \(preliminary\) derivatization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04772
name: post-filter effect
def: "This effect arises when the exciting beam does not fill the cell completely and luminescence is absorbed by the analyte and interfering impurities in the non-illuminated region facing the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04773
name: postprecipitation
def: "The subsequent precipitation of a chemically different species upon the surface of an initial precipitate usually\, but not necessarily\, including a common ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04775
name: potential at the point of zero charge
def: "The value of the electric potential of an electrode at which one of the charges defined is zero. The reference electrode against which this is measured should always be clearly stated. The potential difference with respect to the potential of zero charge is defined by \\\[E_\{\\rm\{pzc\}\}=E- E_\{\\sigma =0\}\\\] where the potential at the point of zero charge is that for the given electrode in the absence of specific adsorption \(other than that of the solvent\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04776
name: potential-determining ions
def: "Those species which by virtue of their electron distribution between the solid and liquid phase \(or by their equilibrium with electrons in the solid\) determine the difference in Galvani potential between these phases. This definition requires that adsorbed p.d. ions are part of the adsorbent and belong to the category of surface ions."
synonym: "p.d. ions" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04778
name: potential energy
def: "Energy of position or orientation in a field of force."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04779
name: potential-energy profile
def: "A curve describing the variation of the potential energy of the system of atoms that make up the reactants and products of a reaction as a function of one geometric coordinate\, and corresponding to the 'energetically easiest passage' from reactants to products \(i.e. along the line produced by joining the paths of steepest descent from the transition state to the reactants and to the products\). For an elementary reaction the relevant geometric coordinate is the reaction coordinate ; for a stepwise reaction it is the succession of reaction coordinates for the successive individual reaction steps. \(The reaction coordinate is sometimes approximated by a quasi-chemical index of reaction progress\, such as 'degree of atom transfer' or bond order of some specified bond.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04780
name: potential-energy surface
def: "A geometric hypersurface on which the potential energy of a set of reactants is plotted as a function of the coordinates representing the molecular geometries of the system. For simple systems two such coordinates \(characterizing two variables that change during the progress from reactants to products\) can be selected\, and the potential energy plotted as a contour map. For simple elementary reactions\, e.g. \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\!-\\!\\rm\{B\}\\\,+\\\,\\rm\{C\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{A\}\\\,+\\\,\\rm\{B\}\\!-\\!\\rm\{C\}\\\) \, the surface can show the potential energy for all values of the A\, B\, C geometry\, providing that the ABC angle is fixed. For more complicated reactions a different choice of two coordinates is sometimes preferred\, e.g. the bond orders of two different bonds. Such a diagram is often arranged so that reactants are located at the bottom left corner and products at the top right. If the trace of the representative point characterizing the route from reactants to products follows two adjacent edges of the diagram\, the changes represented by the two coordinates take place in distinct succession; if the trace leaves the edges and crosses the interior of the diagram\, the two changes are concerted. In many qualitative applications it is convenient \(although not strictly equivalent\) for the third coordinate to represent the standard Gibbs energy rather than potential energy. Using bond orders is\, however\, an oversimplification\, since these are not well-defined\, even for the transition state. \(Some reservations concerning the diagrammatic use of Gibbs energies are noted under Gibbs energy diagram\). The energetically easiest route from reactants to products on the potential-energy contour map defines the potential-energy profile."
synonym: "potential-energy reaction surface" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04787
name: potential temperature
def: "The temperature that a dry air parcel would have if lowered or raised adiabatically to a level of 1000 \\\(\\rm\{mbar\}\\\) pressure \(or other arbitrary standard pressure\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04788
name: potentiation
def: "Dependent action in which a substance or physical agent at a concentration or dose that does not itself have an adverse effect enhances the harm done by another substance or physical agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04789
name: potentiometer
def: "In electronics\, a sensitive voltage measuring device based on a null technique which provides infinite impedance at null.In chemistry\, potentiometric methods involve the measurement with a potentiometer of voltage generated in a cell. A high impedance digital voltmeter is often used today as a convenient alternative to a potentiometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04790
name: potentiometric detection method
def: "A detection method in which the potential of a cell is related to the concentration \(activity\) of a reactant which is a component of the cell fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04791
name: potentiometric selectivity coefficient
def: "A coefficient which defines the ability of an ion-selective electrode to distinguish a particular ion from others. The selectivity coefficient\, \\\(K_\{\\rm\{A\,B\}\}^\{\\rm\{pot\}\}\\\)\, is evaluated by means of the emf response of the ion-selective electrode in mixed solutions of the primary ion\, A\, and interfering ion\, B \(fixed interference method\) or less desirable\, in separate solutions of A and B \(separate solution method\). The activities of the primary ion\, A\, and the interfering ion\, B\, at which \\\(K_\{\\rm\{A\,B\}\}^\{\\rm\{pot\}\}\\\) is determined should always be specified\, as the value of \\\(K_\{\\rm\{A\,B\}\}^\{\\rm\{pot\}\}\\\) is defined by the modified Nikolsky–Eisenman equation. The smaller the value of \\\(K_\{\\rm\{A\,B\}\}^\{\\rm\{pot\}\}\\\) the greater the electrode's preference for the principal ion\, A. Comment\: The terms selectivity constant and selectivity factor are frequently used instead of selectivity coefficient. However\, in order to standardize the terminology associated with ion-selective electrodes\, use of the term selectivity coefficient is recommended\, as is the fixed interference method for its evaluation. This selectivity coefficient is not identical to the similar term used in separation science."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04792
name: power
def: "Rate of energy transfer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04793
name: power level
def: "Logarithm of the ratio of a power to a reference power. Levels are expressed in different ways\: \\\(L_\{P\}=\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \\ln \(\\frac\{P\}\{P_\{0\}\}\)\\ \\mathrm\{Np\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\mathrm\{Np\}\\\) is the symbol for the unit neper coherent with the SI\, or \\\(L_\{P\}=10\\ \\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{P\}\{P_\{0\}\}\)\\ \\mathrm\{dB\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\mathrm\{dB\}\\\) is the symbol for the unit decibel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04794
name: pre-association
def: "A step on the reaction path of some stepwise reactions in which the molecular entity C is already present in an encounter pair or encounter complex with A during the formation of B from A\, e.g.  In this mechanism the chemical species C may but does not necessarily assist the formation of B from A\, which may itself be a bimolecular reaction with some other reagent. Pre-association is important when B is too short-lived to permit B and C to come together by diffusion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04795
name: precipitation
def: "The sedimentation of a solid material \(a precipitate\) from a liquid solution in which the material is present in amounts greater than its solubility in the liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04796
name: precipitation fractionation
def: "A process in which a macromolecules material\, consisting of macromolecules differing in some characteristic affecting their solubility\, is separated from solution into fractions by successively decreasing the solution power of the solvent\, resulting in the repeated formation of a two-phase system in which the less soluble components concentrate in the polymer-rich phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04797
name: precipitation from homogeneous solution
def: "The formation of a precipitate which is generated homogeneously and\, generally\, slowly by a precipitating agent within a solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04799
name: precision
def: "The closeness of agreement between independent test results obtained by applying the experimental procedure under stipulated conditions. The smaller the random part of the experimental errors which affect the results\, the more precise the procedure. A measure of precision \(or imprecision\) is the standard deviation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04800
name: precision
def: "The standard deviation of the instrument for the stated load \(e.g. \\\(l_\{20\}\\\) for a \\\(20\\ \\rm\{g\}\\\) load\). A statement of the procedure\, conditions\, and experience of the observer should be included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04801
name: precision
def: "This depends upon the method of weighing and upon the precision of indication\, \\\(l\\\)\, for the load in question."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04802
name: precision of indication
def: "The standard deviation of the instrumental indication\, \\\(i\\\)\, for a stated load."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04803
name: preconcentration
def: "An operation \(process\) as a result of which the ratio of the concentration or the amount of microcomponents \(trace constituents\) and macrocomponents \(matrix\) increases. The term enrichment is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04804
name: preconcentration coefficient
def: "This is defined as \\\[K = \\frac\{Q_\{\\rm\{T\}\}/Q_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}\{Q_\{\\rm\{T\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}/Q_\{\\rm\{M\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\) and \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{T\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\) are the quantities of the microcomponent in the concentrate and in the sample\, respectively \(mass units or concentration units\)\, and \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{M\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\) and \\\(Q_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\) and are the quantities of the matrix before and after preconcentration\, respectively. If the recovery is 100%\, \\\(K=\\frac\{Q_\{\\rm\{M\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\}\{Q_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}\\\). The terms enrichment coefficient and enrichment factor are not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04805
name: precursor
def: "Of a nuclide\, any radioactive nuclide which precedes that nuclide in a decay chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04806
name: precursor complex
def: "May either indicate an encounter complex or a collision complex\, but furthermore implies that this complex undergoes a reaction \(e.g. electron transfer\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04807
name: precursor ion
def: "Synonymous with parent ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04808
name: predissociation
def: "Dissociation occurring by tunnelling from a 'bound' to an 'unbound' vibronic state. In an absorption spectrum of a molecular entity\, the appearance of a diffuse band region within a series of sharp bands is called predissociation\, since irradiation with frequencies within the diffuse region leads to effective dissociation. The energy of the band is smaller than that of the dissociation continuum of the bound state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04809
name: pre-equilibrium
def: "Preliminary treatment of a solvent in order to convert the extractants into a suitable chemical form.Notes\:The use of equilibration in this sense is confusing and should be avoided.The term conditioning may be used as a synonym for pre-equilibration.Preliminary treatment of either phase with a suitable solution of the other phase \(in the absence of main extractable solute\(s\)\) so that when the subsequent equilibration is carried out changes in the \(volume\) phase ratio or in the concentrations of other components are minimized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04810
name: pre-equilibrium
def: "A rapidly reversible step preceding the rate-limiting step in a stepwise reaction. For example\:"
synonym: "prior equilibrium" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04811
name: pre-exponential factor
def: "Coefficient in front of the exponential factor expressing the empirical temperature dependence of the rate coefficient\, \\\(k\\\)\, on temperature\, \\\(T\\\)\, \\\(k=A\\ \\exp \(- E_\{\\rm\{a\}\}/R\\ T\)\\\)\, where \\\(E_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\) is the activation energy."
synonym: "<i>A</i>-factor" []
synonym: "Arrhenius A factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04812
name: preferential sorption
def: "An equilibrium phenomenon\, operative in polymer solutions in multicomponent solvents and in polymer networks swollen by multicomponent solvents\, that produces differences in solvent composition in the polymer-containing region and in the pure solvent which is in thermodynamic equilibrium with that region. Synonymous with selective sorption."
synonym: "selective sorption" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04813
name: pre-filter effect
def: "This effect arises when the luminescence detector does not see a portion of the luminescent volume where the excitation beam enters the sample. Thus the exciting beam flux is reduced by absorption by the analyte and interfering impurities before it enters the volume observed by the detection system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04814
name: premium coke
def: "An extremely well graphitizing carbon with a high degree of optical anisotropy \(isochromatic areas of optical texture above about \\\(100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)\) and which is characterized by a combination of the following properties which differ significantly from those of regular coke\: high real density\, low reversible thermal expansion and low ash content combined\, in most cases\, with low sulfur content."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04815
name: premix burner
def: "A burner in which fuel and oxidant are thoroughly mixed inside the burner housing before they leave the burner ports and enter the primary-combustion or inner zone of the flame. This type of burner usually produces an approximately laminar flame\, and is commonly combined with a separate unit for nebulizing the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04816
name: prenols
def: "Alcohols possessing the general formula $\\ce\{H–\[CH2C\(Me\)=CHCH2\]_\{n\}OH\}$ in which the carbon skeleton is composed of one or more isoprene units \(biogenetic precursors of the isoprenoids\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04817
name: pre-polymer
def: "A polymer or oligomer composed of pre-polymer molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04818
name: pre-polymer molecule
def: "A macromolecule or oligomer molecule capable of entering\, through reactive groups\, into further polymerization\, thereby contributing more than one monomeric unit to at least one chain of the final macromolecule. A pre-polymer molecule capable of entering into further polymerization through reactive end-groups\, often deliberately introduced\, is known as a telechelic molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04819
name: pressure
def: "Normal force acting on a surface divided by the area of that surface. For a mixture of gases the contribution by each constituent is called the 'partial pressure' \\\(p_\{i\} = x_\{i\} p\\\)\, where \\\(x_\{i\}\\\) is the amount fraction of the \\\(i\\\)th constituent and \\\(p\\\) is the total pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04821
name: pressure gradient correction factor
def: "A factor that corrects for the compressibility of the carrier gas. The values of the measured quantities obtained after multiplication by the factor \\\(j\\\) are independent of the pressure drop in the column. If \\\(p_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\)\, \\\(p_\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\) are respectively the pressures of the carrier gas at the inlet and outlet of the column\, then \\\(j\\\) is given by\: \\\[j=\\frac\{3\\ \[\(\\frac\{p_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\}\{p_\{\\rm\{o\}\}\}\)^\{2\}- 1\]\}\{2\\ \[\(\\frac\{p_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\}\{p_\{\\rm\{o\}\}\}\)^\{3\}- 1\]\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04822
name: pressure-induced transition
def: "A transition induced by a change of pressure. Example\: The room-temperature transition of $\\ce\{CdS\}$ with a wurtzite-type structure\, at \\\(1.74\\ \\rm\{GPa\}\\\)\, to $\\ce\{CdS\}$ with a $\\ce\{NaCl\}$-type structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04824
name: pressure-sensitive detector
def: "A detector which employs the pressure change which results from the absorption of radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04826
name: pretreatment
def: "Following the preparation of a catalyst or following its insertion into a catalytic reactor\, it is often subjected to various treatments before the start of a catalytic run. The term pretreatment may\, in general\, be applied to this set of treatments. In some cases the word activation is used. It implies that the material is converted into a catalyst or into a very much more effective one by the pretreatment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04827
name: primary crystallization
def: "The first stage of crystallization\, considered to be ended when most of the spherulite surfaces impinge on each other. In isothermal crystallization\, primary crystallization is often described by the Avrami equation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04829
name: primary electrons
def: "Electrons generated by a thermal or field emission source used to bombard the specimen for generation of the analytical signals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04831
name: primary isotope effect
def: "A kinetic isotope effect attributable to isotopic substitution of an atom to which a bond is made or broken in the rate-controlling step or in a pre-equilibrium step of a specified reaction. The corresponding isotope effect on the equilibrium constant of a reaction in which one or more bonds to isotopic atoms are broken\, is called a 'primary equilibrium isotope effect'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04834
name: primary mixture
def: "A mixture obtained directly from two or more components intended for the preparation of more dilute calibration mixtures \(called secondary or tertiary mixtures\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04836
name: primary photochemical process
def: "Any elementary chemical process undergone by an electronically excited molecular entity and yielding a primary photoproduct."
synonym: "primary photoreaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04837
name: primary pH standards
def: "Certain substances which meet the criteria of\:   reproducible preparation in a highly pure state;   stability of solution over a reasonable period of time;   having a low value of the residual liquid junction potential;   having pH betwen 3 and 11."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04838
name: primary pollutant
def: "A pollutant emitted directly into the air from identifiable sources \(e.g. $\\ce\{SO2\}$\, $\\ce\{NO\}$\, hydrocarbons\, etc.\). Secondary pollutants\, such as ozone\, are generated within the atmosphere through chemical changes which occur in primary pollutants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04840
name: primary photoproduct
def: "The first observable chemical entity which is produced in the primary photochemical process and which is chemically different from the reactant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04841
name: primary sample
def: "The collection of one or more increments or units initially taken from a population. The portions may be either combined \(composited or bulked sample\) or kept separate \(gross sample\). If combined and mixed to homogeneity\, it is a blended bulk sample. The term 'bulk sample' is commonly used in the sampling literature as the sample formed by combining increments. The term is ambiguous since it could also mean a sample from a bulk lot and it does not indicate whether the increments or units are kept separate or combined. Such use should be discouraged because less ambiguous alternative terms \(composite sample\, aggregate sample\) are available. 'Lot sample' and 'batch sample' have also been used for this concept\, but they are self limiting terms. The use of 'primary' in this sense is not meant to imply the necessity for multistage sampling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04843
name: primary structure
def: "In the context of macromolecules such as proteins\, constitutional formula\, usually abbreviated to a statement of the sequence and if appropriate cross-linking of chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04844
name: primary structure
def: "The amino acid sequence of the polypeptide chain\(s\)\, without regard to spatial arrangement \(apart from configuration at the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\mbox\{-\}carbon atom\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04845
name: primitive change
def: "One of the conceptually simpler molecular changes into which an elementary reaction can be notionally dissected. Such changes include bond rupture\, bond formation\, internal rotation\, change of bond length or bond angle\, bond migration\, redistribution of charge\, etc. The concept of primitive changes is helpful in the detailed verbal description of elementary reactions\, but a primitive change does not represent a process that is by itself necessarily observable as a component of an elementary reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04846
name: principal group
def: "The characteristic group chosen for citation at the end of a name by a suffix or a class name."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04847
name: principal ion
def: "A molecular or fragment ion which is made up of the most abundant isotopes of each of its atomic constituents. In the case of compounds that have been artifically isotopically enriched in one or more positions such as $\\ce\{CH3^\{13\}CH3\}$ or $\\ce\{CH2D2\}$ the principal ion may be defined by treating the heavy isotopes as new atomic species. Thus\, in the above two examples\, the principal ions would be of masses 31 and 18\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04848
name: principal moments of inertia
def: "Moments of inertia about the principal axes of the molecule chosen so as to make all the products of inertia equal to zero\, \\\(I_\{A\}\\leq I_\{B\}\\leq I_\{C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04849
name: principle of least nuclear motion
def: "The hypothesis that\, for given reactants\, the reactions involving the smallest change in nuclear positions will have the lowest energy of activation. \(It is also often simply referred to as principle of least motion.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04850
name: principle of microscopic reversibility
def: "In a reversible reaction\, the mechanism in one direction is exactly the reverse of the mechanism in the other direction. This does not apply to reactions that begin with a photochemical excitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04852
name: prior distribution
def: "A product state distribution calculated on the basis of some physical model for the reaction. The distribution usually referred to is that of the products at the instant of their formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04855
name: probability
def: "Number of favourable events divided by the total number of possible events. It is a number between 0 and 1."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04856
name: probability density
def: "Product of the wave function and its complex conjugate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04857
name: probe
def: "A specific DNA or RNA sequence which has been labelled by radioactivity\, fluorescence labels or chemiluminescence labels and which is used to detect complementary sequences by hybridization techniques such as blotting or colony hybridization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04858
name: process
def: "A phenomenon by which change takes place in a system. In physiological systems\, a process may be chemical\, physical or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04859
name: prochirality
def: "This term is used in different\, sometimes contradictory ways; four are listed below.The geometric property of an achiral object \(or spatial arrangement of points or atoms\) which is capable of becoming chiral in a single desymmetrization step. An achiral molecular entity\, or a part of it considered on its own\, is thus called prochiral if it can be made chiral by the replacement of an existing atom \(or achiral group\) by a different one. An achiral object which is capable of becoming chiral in two desymmetrization steps is sometimes described as proprochiral. For example the proprochiral $\\ce\{CH3CO2H\}$ becomes prochiral as $\\ce\{CH2DCO2H\}$ and chiral as $\\ce\{CHDTCO2H\}$.The term prochirality also applies to an achiral molecule or entity which contains a trigonal system and which can be made chiral by the addition to the trigonal system of a new atom or achiral group. For example addition of hydrogen to one of the enantiotopic faces of the prochiral ketone $\\ce\{CH3CH2COCH3\}$ gives one of the enantiomers of the chiral alcohol $\\ce\{CH3CH2CHOHCH3\}$; the addition of $\\ce\{CN^\{−\}\}$ to one of the diastereotopic faces of the chiral aldehyde shown below converts it into one of the diastereoisomers of the cyanohydrin. The two faces of the trigonal system may be described as Re and Si. The term prochiral also applies to a tetrahedral atom of an achiral or chiral molecule which is bonded to two stereoheterotopic groups. For example\, the prochiral molecule $\\ce\{CH3CH2OH\}$ can be converted into the chiral molecule $\\ce\{CH3CHDOH\}$ by the isotopic replacement of one of the two enantiotopic hydrogen atoms of the methylene group. The carbon atom of the methylene group is called prochiral. The prochiral molecule $\\ce\{HO2CCH2CHOHCH2CO2H\}$ can be converted into a chiral product by esterification of one of the two enantiotopic $\\ce\{–CH2CO2H\}$ groups. The carbon atom of the $\\ce\{CHOH\}$ group is called prochiral. The chiral molecule $\\ce\{CH3CHOHCH2CH3\}$ can be converted into one of the diastereoisomers of $\\ce\{CH3CHOHCHDCH3\}$ by the isotopic replacement of one of the two diastereotopic hydrogen atoms of the methylene group. The carbon atom of the methylene group is called prochiral. The stereoheterotopic groups in these cases may be described as pro-R or pro-S. Reference to the two stereoheterotopic groups themselves as prochiral\, although common\, is strongly discouraged.See\: chirality centreThe term prochirality is also applied to the enantiotopic faces of a trigonal system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04860
name: prochirality centre
def: "An atom of a molecule which becomes a chirality centre by replacing one of the two stereoheterotopic ligands attached to it by a different ligand\, e.g. C-1 of ethanol; C-3 of butan-2-ol."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04861
name: product
def: "A substance that is formed during a chemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04862
name: product-determining step
def: "The step of a stepwise reaction in which the product distribution is determined. The product-determining step may be identical to\, or occur later than\, the rate-controlling step on the reaction coordinate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04863
name: product development control
def: "The term is used for reactions under kinetic control where the selectivity parallels the relative \(thermodynamic\) stabilities of the products. Product development control is usually associated with a transition state occurring late on the reaction coordinate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04864
name: product ion
def: "Synonymous with daughter ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04865
name: productivity
def: "An economical figure denoting the mass of a product formed per unit reactor volume and unit time. Productivity is often referred to per unit of enzyme or biomass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04866
name: product state distribution
def: "The distribution of available energy among the vibrational\, rotational and translation degrees of freedom in the product molecules. The distribution usually referred to is that of the products at the instant of their formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04867
name: <i>pro-E\, pro-Z</i>
def: "One of a pair of identical groups $\\ce\{c\}$ attached to a double bond \(as in $\\ce\{abC=Cc2\}$\) is described as pro-E if\, when it is arbitrarily assigned CIP priority over the other group c\, the stereodescriptor of the molecule becomes E. The other group $\\ce\{c\}$ is then described as pro-Z."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04868
name: progenitor ion
def: "Synonymous with precursor ion  in mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04869
name: program
def: "Noun\: A set of instructions enabling a device to perform an action. Verb\: To provide a set of instructions enabling a device to perform an action."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04870
name: programmed-flow chromatography
def: "A procedure in which the rate of flow of the mobile phase is changed systematically during a part or whole of the separation."
synonym: "flow programming" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04871
name: programmed-pressure chromatography
def: "A procedure in which the inlet pressure of the mobile phase is changed systematically during a part or whole of the separation."
synonym: "pressure programming" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04872
name: programmed-temperature chromatography
def: "A procedure in which the temperature of the column is changed systematically during a part or the whole of the separation."
synonym: "temperature programming" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04873
name: projection formula
def: "A formal two-dimensional representation of a three-dimensional molecular structure obtained by projection of bonds \(symbolized as lines\) onto a plane with or without the designation of the positions of relevant atoms by their chemical symbols. A projection formula which indicates the spatial arrangement of bonds is called a stereochemical formula or stereoformula. Examples of stereoformulae are Fischer projection\, Newman projection\, sawhorse projection\, wedge projection and zig-zag projection."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04874
name: prolate trochoidal mass spectrometer
def: "A mass spectrometer in which ions of different mass/charge are separated by means of crossed electric and magnetic fields in such a way that the selected ions follow a prolate trochoidal path. The usual term 'cycloidal' used sometimes is incorrect because the path used is not cycloidal. A cycloid is a special case of a trochoid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04875
name: promoter
def: "The DNA region\, usually upsteam to the coding sequence of a gene or operon\, which binds and directs RNA polymerase to the correct transcriptional start site and thus permits the initation of transcription."
synonym: "promoter gene" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04876
name: promoter
def: "A relatively small quantity of one or more substances\, which when added to a catalyst improves the activity\, the selectivity\, or the useful lifetime of the catalyst. In general\, a promoter may either augment a desired reaction or suppress an undesired one."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04878
name: prompt coincidence
def: "The occurrence of two or more events separated by a time interval which is less than a specified small value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04879
name: prompt neutrons
def: "Neutrons accompanying the fission process without measurable delay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04882
name: propellanes
def: "Tricyclic saturated hydrocarbons\, systematically named tricyclo\[a.b.c.01\,\(a+2\)\]alkanes; have been referred to as \[a.b.c\]propellanes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04883
name: property
def: "A set of data elements \(system\, component\, kind-of-property\) common to a set of particular properties\, e.g. substance concentration of glucose in blood plasma. Information about identification\, time and result is not considered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04884
name: prophage
def: "The latent state of a phage genome in a lysogenic bacterium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04885
name: proportional counter
def: "A gas-filled X-ray detector in which the electric potential is high enough for the gain to reach a value in the range from \\\(10^\{2\}\\\) to \\\(10^\{5\}\\\). Each electron produced by the initial photo-ionization causes one avalanche. Since the number of avalanche events is proportional to the energy of the incident photons\, the charge collected by the anode is proportional to the X-ray photon energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04886
name: proportional counter tube
def: "A counter tube operated under such conditions that the magnitude of each pulse is proportional to the amount of energy deposited in it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04887
name: proportional gas-scintillation counter
def: "A proportional counter coupled to an ultraviolet sensitive photomultiplier tube. Initial electrons produced by the interaction of the high-energy photon with the counter fill-gas are accelerated by a high electric field where they acquire sufficient energy to excite the noble gas atoms. The resulting UV radiation is observed by a photomultiplier tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04889
name: <i>pro-R\, pro-S</i>
def: "A stereoheterotopic group $\\ce\{c\}$ \(as in tetrahedral $\\ce\{Xabc2\}$\) is described as pro-R if\, when it is arbitrarily assigned CIP priority over the other stereoheterotopic group $\\ce\{c\}$\, the configuration of the thus generated chiral centre is assigned the stereodescriptor R. The other group $\\ce\{c\}$ is then described as pro-S. This method for distinguishing between stereoheterotopic groups can be applied to other kinds of prochiral molecular entities or prochiral parts of molecular entities considered on their own."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04890
name: <i>pros</i>
def: "The nitrogen atoms of the imidazole ring of histidine are denoted by pros \('near'\, abbreviated $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\}$\) and tele \('far'\, abbreviated $\\ce\{\\uptau\\!\}$\) to show their position relative to the side chain. This recommendation arose from the fact that two different systems of numbering the atoms in the imidazole ring of histidine had both been used for a considerable time \(biochemists generally numbering as 1 the nitrogen atom adjacent to the side chain\, and organic chemists designating it as 3\). The carbon atom between the two ring nitrogen atoms is numbered 2 \(as in imidazole\)\, and the carbon atom next to the $\\ce\{\\uptau\\!\}$ nitrogen is numbered 5. The carbon atoms of the aliphatic chain are designated $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$. This numbering should also be used for the decarboxylation product histamine and for substituted histidine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04891
name: prostaglandins
def: "Naturally occurring compounds derived from the parent $\\ce\{C20\}$ acid\, prostanoic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04892
name: prostanoids
def: "The family of natural prostaglandins and prostaglandin-like compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04893
name: prosthetic group
def: "The non-amino acid portion of a conjugated protein. Examples include cofactors such as flavines or cytochromes as well as lipids and polysaccharides\, which are the prosthetic groups of lipoproteins and glycoproteins\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04894
name: proteases
def: "Enzymes that catalyse the hydrolysis of proteins. Usually several proteolytic enzymes are necessary for the complete breakdown of polypeptides to their amino acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04895
name: protected lyophobic colloid
def: "A colloidally stable mixture of a lyophobic and a lyophilic colloid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04896
name: protective action
def: "Addition of small amounts of hydrophilic colloid to a hydrophobic sol may make the latter more sensitive to flocculation by electrolyte. Higher concentrations of the same hydrophilic colloid usually protect the hydrophobic sol from flocculation. This phenomenon is called protective action."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04897
name: protein engineering
def: "A technique used to produce proteins with altered or novel amino acid sequences. The methods used are\: Transcription and translation systems from synthesized lengths of DNA or RNA with novel sequences.  Chemical modification of 'normal' proteins.   Solid-state polypeptide synthesis to form proteins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04898
name: proteins
def: "Naturally occurring and synthetic polypeptides having molecular weights greater than about 10000 \(the limit is not precise\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04899
name: proteoglycan
def: "A subclass of protein in which the carbohydrate units are polysaccharides that contain amino sugars. The protein is glycosylated by one or more \(up to about 100\) glycosaminoglycans \[linear polymers of up to about 200 repeating disaccharide units that consist of a hexosamine \(ᴅ-glucosamine or ᴅ-galactosamine\) alternating with a uronic acid \(ᴅ-glucuronic or ʟ-iduronic\) or a neutral sugar \(ᴅ-galactose\)\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04903
name: protium
def: "A specific name for the atom $\\ce\{^\{1\}H\}$. The cation $\\ce\{^\{1\}H^\{+\}\}$ is a proton\, the species $\\ce\{1H\}$ is a protide anion\, and $\\ce\{1H\}$ is the protio group. The word proton should not be used for $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ in its natural abundance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04904
name: protogenic
def: "Capable of acting as a proton donor \(strongly or weakly acidic as a Brønsted acid\). The term is preferred to the synonym 'protic' or the more ambiguous expression acidic by itself."
synonym: "protic" []
synonym: "protogenic solvent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04905
name: protolysis
def: "This term has been used synonymously with proton \(hydron\)-transfer reaction. Because of its misleading similarity to hydrolysis\, photolysis\, etc.\, its use is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04906
name: proton
def: "Nuclear particle of charge number +1\, spin quantum number \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\\) and rest mass of \\\(1.007\\ 276\\ 470\(12\)\\ \\rm\{u\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04907
name: proton affinity
def: "The negative of the enthalpy change in the gas phase reaction \(real or hypothetical\) between a proton \(more appropriately hydron\) and the chemical species concerned\, usually an electrically neutral species to give the conjugate acid of that species. Proton affinity is often\, but unofficially\, abbreviated as PA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04908
name: protonated molecule
def: "An ion formed by interaction of a molecule with a proton abstracted from an ion\, as often occurs in chemical ionization according to the reaction\: \\\(\\rm\{M\}+\\rm\{XH\}^\{+\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{MH\}^\{+\}+\\rm\{X\}\\\). The symbolism $\\ce\{\[M + H\]^\{+\}\}$ may also be used to represent the protonated molecule. The widely used term 'protonated molecular ion' to describe the $\\ce\{MH^\{+\}\}$ ion is not recommended\, since it suggests an associated product of a proton with a molecular ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04909
name: protonation constant
def: "The equilibrium constant \\\(K_\{\\rm\{H\}n\}\\\) for the addition of the \\\(n\\\)th proton to a charged or uncharged ligand. The cumulative protonation constant\, \\\(\\beta_\{\\rm\{H\}n\}\\\)\, is the equilibrium constant for the formation of $\\ce\{H_\{n\}L\}$ from \\\(n\\\) $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{L\}$. The same equilibrium constant may be described in several ways\, depending on how the ligand\, $\\ce\{L\}$\, or the acid\, has been defined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04911
name: proton magnetic moment
def: "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{p\}\} = 1.410\\ 607\\ 61\(47\)\\times 10^\{-26\}\\ \\rm\{J T\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04912
name: proton magnetogyric ratio
def: "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\\(\\gamma _\{\\rm\{p\}\} = 2.675\\ 221\\ 28\(81\)\\times 10^\{8\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{T\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04914
name: proton rest mass
def: "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\\(m_\{\\rm\{p\}\} = \\pu\{1.6726231\(10\)E-27 kg\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04915
name: proton transfer reaction
def: "A chemical reaction\, the main feature of which is the intermolecular or intramolecular transfer of a proton \(hydron\) from one binding site to another. For example\:  In the detailed description of proton transfer reactions\, especially of rapid proton transfers between electronegative atoms\, it should always be specified whether the term is used to refer to the overall process \(including the more-or-less encounter-controlled formation of a hydrogen bonded complex and the separation of the products; see microscopic diffusion control\) or just to the proton transfer event \(including solvent rearrangement\) by itself."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04916
name: protophilic
def: "Capable of acting as proton acceptor\, strongly or weakly basic \(as a Brønsted base\). Also called HBA \(hydrogen bond acceptor\) solvent."
synonym: "hydrogen bond acceptor" []
synonym: "protophilic solvent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04917
name: protoplast
def: "A spherical\, osmotically-sensitive cell without its cell wall but retaining an intact cell membrane. Protoplasts are used to create hybrid cells via protoplast fusion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04918
name: prototrophs
def: "Microoganisms capable of growing at or below a temperature optimum of \\\(15\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04920
name: pseudo acids
def: "Potentially acidic compounds that require some structural reorganization\, such as keto–enol tautomerization\, having a non-negligible activation energy\, in order to show normal acidic properties\, e.g. nitroalkanes\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04921
name: pseudo-asymmetric carbon atom
def: "The traditional name for a tetrahedrally coordinated carbon atom bonded to four different entities\, two and only two of which have the same constitution but opposite chirality sense. The r/s descriptors of pseudo-asymmetric carbon atoms are invariant on reflection in a mirror \(i.e. r remains r\, and s remains s\)\, but are reversed by the exchange of any two entities \(i.e. r becomes s\, and s becomes r\). An example is C-3 of ribaric \(C-3 is r\) or xylaric acid \(C-3 is s\) or hyoscyamine \(C-3 is r\). The hyphen in pseudo-asymmetric may be omitted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04923
name: pseudo bases
def: "Hydroxy compounds that give salts with acids by formation of water accompanied by a change of constitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04924
name: pseudo-catalysis
def: "If an acid or base is present in nearly constant concentration throughout a reaction in solution \(owing to buffering or the use of a large excess\)\, it may be found to increase the rate of that reaction and also to be consumed during the process. The acid or base is then not a catalyst and the phenomenon cannot be called catalysis according to the well-established meaning of these terms in chemical kinetics\, although the mechanism of such a process is often intimately related to that of a catalysed reaction. It is recommended that the term pseudo-catalysis be used in these and analogous cases \(not necessarily involving acids or bases\). For example\, if a Brønsted acid accelerates the hydrolysis of an ester to a carboxylic acid and an alcohol\, this is properly called acid catalysis\, whereas the acceleration\, by the same acid\, of hydrolysis of an amide should be described as pseudo-catalysis by the acid\: the 'acid pseudo-catalyst' is consumed during the reaction through formation of an ammonium ion. The terms 'general acid pseudo-catalysis' and 'general base pseudo-catalysis' may be used as the analogues of general acid catalysis and general base catalysis. The term 'base-promoted'\, 'base-accelerated' or 'base-induced' is sometimes used for reactions that are pseudo-catalysed by bases. However\, the term 'promotion' also has a different meaning in other chemical contexts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04925
name: pseudo-co-oligomer
def: "An irregular oligomer\, the molecules of which are derived from only one species of monomer but which display a variety of structural features more appropriate for description in co-oligomer terms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04926
name: pseudo-copolymer
def: "An irregular polymer\, the molecules of which are derived from only one species of monomer but which display a variety of structural features more appropriate for description in copolymer terms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04930
name: pseudohalogens
def: "Compounds that resemble the halogen elements\, $\\ce\{X2\}$\, in their chemistry\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(CN\)2\}$ cyanogen\, $\\ce\{\(SCN\)2\}$ thiocyanogen\, $\\ce\{ICN\}$ iodine cyanide. Certain ions that have sufficient resemblance to halide ions are sometimes referred to as pseudohalide ions\, e.g. $\\ce\{N3^\{−\}\}$\, $\\ce\{SCN^\{−\}\}$\, $\\ce\{CN^\{−\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04932
name: pseudopericyclic
def: "A concerted transformation is pseudopericyclic if the primary changes in bonding occur within a cyclic array of atoms at one \(or more\) of which nonbonding and bonding atomic orbitals interchange roles. A formal example is the enol \\\(\\rightarrow \\\) enol prototropy of pentane-2\,4-dione \(acetylacetone\).  Because the \\\(\\pi \\\)- and \\\(\\sigma \\\)-atomic orbitals that interchange roles are orthogonal\, such a reaction does not proceed through a fully conjugated transition state and is thus not a pericyclic reaction and therefore not governed by the rules that express orbital symmetry restrictions applicable to pericyclic reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04933
name: pseudo rate constant
def: "A term sometimes used for a rate coefficient. For example\, if the rate equation is\: \\\[v=k\\ \\rm\{\[A\]\}\\ \\rm\{\[B\]\}\\\] the function \\\(k\\ \\rm\{\[A\]\}\\\) is the pseudo first-order rate constant \(or first-order rate coefficient\) with respect to B. Similarly\, \\\(k\\ \\rm\{\[B\]\}\\\) is the pseudo first-order rate constant \(or first-order rate coefficient\) with respect to A. The term pseudo rate constant is particularly useful when the concentration of one reactant is much greater than that of another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04934
name: pseudorotation
def: "Stereoisomerization resulting in a structure that appears to have been produced by rotation of the entire initial molecule and is superposable on the initial one\, unless different positions are distinguished by substitution\, including isotopic substitution. One example of pseudorotation is a facile interconversion between the many envelope and twist conformers of a cyclopentane due to the out of plane motion of carbon atoms. Another example of pseudorotation \(Berry pseudorotation\) is a polytopal rearrangement that provides an intramolecular mechanism for the isomerization of trigonal bipyramidal compounds \(e.g. \\\(\\lambda ^\{5\}\\\)-phosphanes\)\, the five bonds to the central atom E being represented as \\\(\\rm\{e\}^\{1\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{e\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{e\}^\{3\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{a\}^\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{a\}^\{2\}\\\). Two equatorial bonds move apart and become apical bonds at the same time as the apical bonds move together to become equatorial.  A related conformational change of a trigonal bipyramidal structure is described as turnstile rotation. The process may be visualized as follows.  An apical and an equatorial bond rotate as a pair ca. 120° relative to the other three bonds. \(Doubts have been expressed about the distinct physical reality of this mechanism.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04939
name: psychrometric hygrometer
def: "Instrument by which the relative humidity of the atmosphere may be determined. It is generally composed of two temperature sensors\, one of which measures the temperature of the air; the other sensor is moistened with water and senses the cooling due to evaporation of water. The temperature difference between the two sensors is a function of relative humidity; sometimes referred to as a 'wet-and-dry bulb' thermometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04940
name: psychrometry
def: "The use of a wet-and-dry bulb thermometer for measurement of atmospheric humidity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04941
name: puffing
def: "An irreversible expansion of some carbon artifacts during graphitization heat treatment between \\\(1650\\\) and \\\(2700\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04942
name: puffing inhibitor
def: "Metals or metal compounds with a high chemical affinity for the heteroatoms in the carbons. They are distributed as fine particles within the carbon materials to be graphitized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04945
name: pulse amplitude analyser
def: "A sub-assembly for determining the distribution function of a set of pulses in terms of their amplitude. Synonymous with pulse height analyser."
synonym: "pulse height analyser" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04946
name: pulse amplitude selector
def: "A circuit which gives an output pulse for each input pulse whose amplitude lies within a chosen interval. Synonymous with single channel pulse height analyser."
synonym: "single channel pulse height analyser" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04947
name: pulse duration
def: "In pulse polarography\, differential pulse polarography\, Kalousek polarography\, and related techniques\, the duration of an interval during which the excitation signal deviates from the base line. This interval includes the sampling interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04951
name: pulse reactor
def: "In a pulse reactor\, a carrier gas\, which may be inert or possibly one of the reactants\, flows over the catalyst and small amounts of the other reactant or reactants are injected into the carrier gas at intervals. A pulse reactor is useful for exploratory work but kinetic results apply to a transient rather than to the steady state conditions of the catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04952
name: pump-probe technique
def: "Transient absorption spectroscopy using two pulses \(strong pump and weak probe\) and capable of achieving a high temporal resolution. A pump pulse excites the sample and triggers the process under investigation. A second delayed pulse\, the probe\, monitors an optical property. By varying the time delay between the pump and probe pulses\, it is possible to assemble measurements as a function of time. The probe pulse is typically a UV\, visible or infrared pulse in which case a snap-shot spectrum is taken as a function of the delay time. Often the probe pulse is generated from a portion of the excitation beam\, but it can also be an independently generated electromagnetic pulse."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04953
name: purine bases
def: "Purine and its substitution derivatives\, especially naturally occurring examples. The customary numbering shown below is not systematic.purine"

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04956
name: pyramidal inversion
def: "A polytopal rearrangement in which the change in bond directions to a three-coordinate central atom having a pyramidal arrangement of bonds \(tripodal arrangement\) causes the central atom \(apex of the pyramid\) to appear to move to an equivalent position on the other side of the base of the pyramid. If the three ligands to the central atom are different pyramidal inversion interconverts enantiomers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04957
name: pyranoses
def: "Cyclic hemiacetal forms of monosaccharides in which the ring is six-membered \(a tetrahydropyran\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04958
name: pyrimidine bases
def: "Pyrimidine and its substitution derivatives\, especially naturally occurring examples."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04959
name: pyro
def: "A prefix designating compounds formed by heating a compound\, usually with the elimination of water\, carbon dioxide\, or other simple molecule\, e.g. pyroglutamic acid from glutamic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04960
name: pyroelectric detector
def: "A detector \(based on the temperature dependence of ferroelectricity in some crystals\) which produces an electrical signal proportional to the energy flux on the collector surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04961
name: pyrolysis
def: "Thermolysis\, usually associated with exposure to a high temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04962
name: pyrolysis-gas chromatography
def: "A version of reaction chromatography in which a sample is thermally decomposed to simpler fragments before entering the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04963
name: pyrolytic carbon
def: "A carbon material deposited from gaseous hydrocarbon compounds on suitable underlying substrates \(carbon materials\, metals\, ceramics\) at temperatures ranging from \\\(1000\\\) to \\\(2500\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) \(chemical vapour deposition\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04964
name: pyrolytic graphite
def: "A graphite material with a high degree of preferred crystallographic orientation of the c-axes perpendicular to the surface of the substrate\, obtained by graphitization heat treatment of pyrolytic carbon or by chemical vapour deposition at temperatures above \\\(2500\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:P04965
name: pyrromethenes
def: "A less preferred term for dipyrrins. \(The term 'pyrromethane' has been used for the system with a $\\ce\{–CH2\\bond\{–\}\}$ linkage\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06782
name: photosystem
def: "A membrane-bound complex in plants and photosynthetic bacteria\, responsible for light-harvesting and primary electron transfer. Comprises light-harvesting pigments such as chlorophyll; a primary electron-transfer centre\, and secondary electron carriers. In green plant photosynthesis\, Photosystem I transfers electrons from plastocyanin to a \[2Fe-2S\] ferredoxin\, and contains iron-sulfur proteins. Photosystem II transfers electrons from the oxygen-evolving complex to plastoquinone\, and contains an iron centre."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06790
name: phosphorylation
def: "A process \(catalysed by enzymes\) involving the transfer of a phosphate group from a donor to a suitable acceptor; in general\, an ester linkage is formed\, for example\: $\\ce\{ATP + alcohol -> ADP + phosphate ester\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06791
name: polymerase chain reaction
def: "A laboratory technique to rapidly amplify pre-determined regions of double-stranded DNA. Generally involves the use of a heat-stable DNA polymerase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06792
name: polyhedral symbol
def: "The polyhedral symbol indicates the geometrical arrangements of the coordinating atoms about the central atom. It consists of one or more capital italic letters derived from common geometric terms \(tetrahedron\, square plane\, octahedron\, etc.\) which denote the idealised geometry of the ligands around the coordination centre\, and an arabic numeral that is the coordination number of the central atom. The polyhedral symbol is used as an affix\, enclosed in parentheses\, and separated from the name by a hyphen. Examples are T-4\, SP-4\, TBPY-5\, SPY-5\, OC-6\, and CU-8."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06835
name: pharmacokinetics
def: "Process of the uptake of drugs by the body\, the biotransformation they undergo\, the distribution of the drugs and their metabolites in the tissues\, and the elimination of the drugs and their metabolites from the body over a period of time.Study of such processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06836
name: phase I reaction
def: "Enzymic modification of a substance by oxidation\, reduction\, hydrolysis\, hydration\, dehydrochlorination\, or other reactions catalyzed by enzymes of the cytosol\, of the endoplasmic reticulum \(microsomal enzymes\) or of other cell organelles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06837
name: phase II reaction
def: "Binding of a substance\, or its metabolites from a phase I reaction\, with endogenous molecules \(conjugation\)\, making more water-soluble derivatives that may be excreted in the urine or bile."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06838
name: pinocytosis
def: "Type of endocytosis in which soluble materials are taken up by the cell and incorporated into vesicles for digestion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06839
name: plasma
def: "Fluid component of blood in which the blood cells and platelets are suspended. Synonym\: blood plasma.Fluid component of semen produced by the accessory glands\, the seminal vesicles\, the prostate\, and the bulbo-urethral glands.Cell substance outside the nucleus\, i.e.\, the cytoplasm."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06840
name: proteome
def: "Complete set of proteins encoded by the genome."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06879
name: penetrant
def: "Entity from a phase in contact with one of the membrane surfaces that passes though the membrane."
synonym: "permeant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06880
name: permeate
def: "Stream containing penetrants that leaves a membrane module."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06881
name: perstraction
def: "Separation process in which membrane permeation and extraction phenomena occur by contacting the downstream with an extracting solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06892
name: pervaporation
def: "Membrane-based process in which the feed and retentate streams are both liquid phases while permeant emerges at the downstream face of the membrane as a vapour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06918
name: polymeric stationary phase
def: "Stationary phase \(material\) based on particles of a cross-linked organic polymeric material. Typical materials are polystyrene divinylbenzene copolymers \(PS-DVB\) and modified PS-DVB materials."
synonym: "polymeric stationary phase material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06921
name: perfusion stationary phase
def: "Stationary phase in which the mobile phase primarily travels through the pores of the stationary phase."
synonym: "perfusion stationary phase material" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06933
name: partition isotherm
def: "Isotherm describing partition of the sample component between the bulk of a liquid stationary phase and a liquid\, gaseous or supercritical mobile phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06975
name: partial least squares
def: "Partial least squares projection to latent structures \(PLS\) is a robust multivariate generalized regression method using projections to summarize multitudes of potentially collinear variables."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT06976
name: pattern recognition
def: "Pattern recognition is the identification of patterns in large data sets\, using appropriate mathematical methodology. Examples are principal component analysis \(PCA\)\, SIMCA\, partial least squares \(PLS\) and artificial neural networks \(ANN\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07088
name: Pariser–Parr–Pople method
def: "A semi-empirical quantum mechanical method of calculation of the properties of conjugated molecules and ions from self-consistent-field theory and the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ approximation."
synonym: "PPP method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07089
name: Pauli exclusion principle
def: "A rule\, complementary to the aufbau principle\, of building up the electronic configuration of atoms and molecules\: a maximum of two electrons can occupy an orbital and then only providing that the spins of the electrons are paired\, i.e. opposed. The principle demands that the wavefunction for a many-electron system must be asymmetric with respect to the permutation of the space-spin coordinates for every pair of electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07090
name: perturbation theory
def: "Along with variational method\, the second major quantum-mechanical approximation method. The methods of perturbation theory are based on representation of the Hamiltonian of a system under study\, \\\(H\\\)\, through the Hamiltonian\, \\\(H^\{0\}\\\)\, of a system\, whose Schroedinger equation is solvable\, and its relatively small perturbation \\\(H^\{\\rm\{'\}\}\\\)\: \\\(H=H^\{0\}+H^\{\\rm\{'\}\}\\\). Numerous techniques are derived allowing one to relate the unknown eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of the perturbed system to the known eigenvalues and eigenfunctions of the unperturbed system. As distinct from the variational method\, the methods of perturbation theory are applicable to all the electronic states of an atom or molecule. When \\\(H^\{\\rm\{'\}\}\\\) is time-dependent\, the perturbed system does not have stationary states. In this case time-dependent perturbation theory\, which is the method of approximate calculation of the expansion of wave-functions of the perturbed system over wave-functions of stationary states of the unperturbed system\, must be employed. The applications of this method are associated mostly with studies of light emission and absorption by atoms and molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07091
name: pre-reactive complexes
def: "Weakly bound complexes with a potential minimum that precedes the activation barrier along the reaction path. In contrast with the van der Waals complexes\, which fall apart reversibly into their constituents\, the pre-reactive complexes may undergo a vigorous reaction to form different products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07142
name: polymer complexation
def: "Process that results in the formation of a polymer–polymer complex or a complex composed of a polymer and a low-molar-mass substance."
synonym: "polymer complex formation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07143
name: polymer cyclization
def: "Chemical reaction that leads to the formation of ring structures in or from polymer chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07144
name: polymer degradation
def: "Chemical changes in a polymeric material that usually result in undesirable changes in the in-use properties of the material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07145
name: polymer functionalization
def: "Introduction of desired chemical groups into polymer molecules to create specific chemical\, physical\, biological\, pharmacological\, or other properties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07146
name: polymer reaction
def: "Chemical reaction in which at least one of the reactants is a high-molar-mass substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07147
name: polymer-supported reaction
def: "Chemical reaction in which at least one reactant or a catalyst is bound through chemical bonds or weaker interactions\, such as hydrogen bonds or donor–acceptor interactions\, to a polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07148
name: protection of a reactive group
def: "Temporary chemical transformation of a reactive group into a group that does not react under conditions where the non-protected group reacts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07158
name: polymer catalyst
def: "Polymer that exhibits catalytic activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07159
name: polymer-metal complex
def: "Complex comprising a metal and one or more polymeric ligands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07160
name: polymer phase-transfer catalyst
def: "Polymer that acts as a phase-transfer catalyst and thereby causes a significant enhancement of the rate of a reaction between two reactants located in neighboring phases owing to its catalysis of the extraction of one of the reactants across the interface to the other phase where the reaction takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07161
name: polymer-supported catalyst
def: "Catalyst system comprising a polymer support in which catalytically active species are immobilized through chemical bonds or weaker interactions such as hydrogen bonds or donor–acceptor interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07162
name: polymer reactant
def: "Reactant \(reagent\) that is or is attached to a high-molar-mass linear polymer or a polymer network."
synonym: "polymer reagent" []
synonym: "polymer-supported reagent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07163
name: prepolymer
def: "Polymer or oligomer the molecules of which are capable of entering\, through reactive groups\, into further polymerization and thereby contributing more than one structural unit to at least one type of chain of the final polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07180
name: photo-elastic polymer
def: "Polymer that\, under stress\, exhibits birefringence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07181
name: photoluminescent polymer
def: "Polymer that exhibits luminescence \(i.e.\, fluorescence or phosphorescence arising from photo-excitation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07182
name: photosensitive polymer
def: "Polymer that responds to ultraviolet or visible light by exhibiting a change in its physical properties or its chemical constitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07183
name: piezoelectric polymer
def: "Polymer that exhibits a change in dielectric properties on application of pressure.Polymer that shows a change in its dimensions when subjected to an electric field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07185
name: polymer compatibilizer
def: "Polymeric additive that\, when added to a blend of immiscible polymers\, modifies their interfaces and stabilizes the blend."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07186
name: polymer drug
def: "Polymer that contains either chemically-bound drug molecules or pharmacologically active moieties."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07187
name: polymer gel
def: "Gel in which the network component is a polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07188
name: polymer membrane
def: "Thin layer of polymeric material that acts as a barrier permitting mass transport of selected species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07189
name: polymer solvent
def: "Polymer that acts like a solvent for compounds of low molar mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07190
name: polymer sorbent
def: "Polymer that adsorbs or absorbs a certain substance or certain substances from a liquid or a gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07191
name: polymer support
def: "Polymer to or in which a reagent or catalyst is chemically bound\, immobilized\, dispersed\, or associated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07192
name: polymer surfactant
def: "Polymer that lowers the surface tension of the medium in which it is dissolved\, or the interfacial tension with another phase\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07217
name: polyacid
def: "In polymer terminology\, polyelectrolyte composed of macromolecules containing acid groups on a substantial fraction of the constitutional units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07219
name: polybase
def: "In polymer terminology\, polyelectrolyte composed of macromolecules containing basic groups on a substantial fraction of the constitutional units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07220
name: polybetaine
def: "Ampholytic polymer in which pendant groups have a betaine-type structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07222
name: polyelectrolyte complex
def: "Neutral polymer-polymer complex composed of macromolecules carrying charges of opposite sign causing the macromolecules to be bound together by electrostatic interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07223
name: polyelectrolyte network
def: "Polymer network containing ionic or ionizable groups in a substantial fraction of its constitutional units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07242
name: polymer composite
def: "Composite in which at least one component is a polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07247
name: prepreg
def: "Sheets of a substrate that have been impregnated with a curable polymer\, curable polymers\, or liquid reactants\, or a thermoplastic\, and are ready for fabrication of laminates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07264
name: polymer alloy
def: "Polymeric material\, exhibiting macroscopically uniform physical properties throughout its whole volume\, that comprises a compatible polymer blend\, a miscible polymer blend or a multiphase copolymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07286
name: phase domain
def: "Region of a material that is uniform in chemical composition and physical state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07291
name: phase interaction
def: "Molecular interaction between the components present in the interphases of a multiphase mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07416
name: polaron
def: "Lattice distortion or polarization caused by photoexcitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07418
name: planar intramolecular charge transfer
def: "In a PICT state formed by intramolecular charge transfer in an electron donor \($\\ce\{D\}$\)/acceptor \($\\ce\{A\}$\) molecule\, the $\\ce\{D\}$ and $\\ce\{A\}$ subgroups have an overall planar configuration\, with substantial electronic coupling of $\\ce\{D\}$ and $\\ce\{A\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07440
name: parametric amplification
def: "Amplification of a signal based on parametric processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07441
name: parametric processes
def: "Interaction of photons in a non-linear medium satisfying the energy and momentum conservation laws. During the interaction the photon frequencies are mixed and photon\(s\) with different frequencies are created."
synonym: "optical parametric processes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07442
name: Peierls distortion
def: "Distortion of a regular one-dimensional structure with a partially occupied band to give bond alternation\, eventually leading to dimerization or oligomerization. The degree of oligomerization\, \\\(\\lambda \\\) depends on the electronic population of the conduction band indicated by the wave vector of the Fermi level \\\(k_\{\\mathrm\{F\}\}\\\) \\\[\\lambda = 2\\ \\pi /k_\{\\mathrm\{F\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07443
name: phosphorescence lifetime
def: "Parameter describing the time evolution of the decay of the phosphorescence radiant intensity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07444
name: photo-Bergman cyclization
def: "Intramolecular photoinduced cyclization of enediynes leading to aromatic compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07445
name: photobiology
def: "Branch of biology concerned with the biological and bioenvironmental effects of ultraviolet\, visible or infrared radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07446
name: photocatalyst
def: "Catalyst able to produce\, upon absorption of light\, chemical transformations of the reaction partners. The excited state of the photocatalyst repeatedly interacts with the reaction partners forming reaction intermediates and regenerates itself after each cycle of such interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07447
name: photochemical funnel
def: "Molecular structure where the excited-state reactant or intermediate is delivered to the ground state to initiate product formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07448
name: photochemical reaction path
def: "Sequence of geometries adopted by a molecule after initial electronic excitation\, combined with a specification of the electronic state at each geometry\, from radiation absorption to product formation. Theoretically\, this usually involves the computation of the MERP connecting the Franck–Condon point\, located on the potential-energy surface of the spectroscopic state\, to the final photoproducts located on the ground-state potential-energy surface. Experimentally\, a photochemical reaction path can be probed by detecting transient species or reaction intermediates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07449
name: photo-Claisen rearrangement
def: "Rearrangement closely related to the photo-Fries rearrangement; it is experienced by aryl ethers \(instead of aryl esters\) and follows an analogous mechanism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07450
name: photocyclization
def: "Intramolecular photochemical process leading to carbo- or hetero- cyclic ring systems by formation of one new single bond\, either by a concerted process \(e.g.\, electrocyclization\) or by multi-step processes such as Norrish–Yang reaction\, photoinduced electron transfer processes\, Yang photocyclization and others."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07451
name: photocycloaddition
def: "Intermolecular photochemical process leading to carbo- or hetero- cyclic ring systems by formation of two new single bonds\, either by a concerted process \(e.g.\, a concerted addition\) or by multi-step processes \(e.g.\, the Paternò–Büchi reaction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07452
name: photochemical nitrogen extrusion
def: "Photoinduced dinitrogen \($\\ce\{N2\}$\) elimination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07453
name: photodecarbonylation
def: "Photoinduced extrusion of carbon monoxide \($\\ce\{CO\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07454
name: photodecarboxylation
def: "Photoinduced extrusion of carbon dioxide \($\\ce\{CO2\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07455
name: photodeconjugation
def: "Photoinduced reaction of conjugated compounds \(e.g.\, enones and carboxylic acid derivatives\) leading to loss of conjugation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07456
name: photohydration
def: "Photochemical process leading to the addition of water across a $\\ce\{C–C\}$ double \(or triple\) bond or to a carbonyl \(or carbonyl analogue\) group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07458
name: photon echo
def: "Time-resolved optical spectroscopy in which the inhomogeneous broadening of absorbers is eliminated by the proper choice of geometry in a four-wave mixing experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07459
name: photon number
def: "Total number of photons calculated as \\\(N_\{\\rm\{p\}\} = n_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\, N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) with \\\(N_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) the amount of photons in einsteins and \\\(N_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) the Avogadro constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07460
name: photorearrangement
def: "Photoisomerization involving changes in the arrangement of the atoms within the molecular species. It may lead to unstable isomers that react further by\, e.g.\, dehydrogenation\, deprotonation\, or other reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07461
name: photoselection
def: "Selection of an aligned subset of sample molecules by an exciting light beam. The selected subset will have an orientation distribution that is determined by the electric vector of the beam. If the beam is linearly polarized a uniaxial sample with a known orientation distribution will be produced. Also\, the set of remaining molecules will be aligned with a known orientation distribution \(residual anisotropy\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07463
name: PLED
def: "Acronym for polymer light-emitting diode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07465
name: pump-dump-probe technique
def: "Transient absorption spectroscopy using three pulses \(a strong pump\, a dump pulse that can alter the course of the reaction photoinduced by the first pulse\, and a weak probe\). In general\, femtosecond laser pulses are used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07506
name: pre-gel regime
def: "Stage of a network-forming polymerization or crosslinking process extending up to\, but not beyond\, the gel point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07507
name: pre-gel state
def: "State of a network-forming polymerization or crosslinking process in the pre-gel regime."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07510
name: particulate sol
def: "Sol in which the dispersed phase consists of solid particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07511
name: polymeric sol
def: "Sol in which the dispersed phase consists of particles having a polymeric structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07523
name: particulate gel
def: "Gel in which the network component comprises solid particles."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07524
name: polyelectrolyte gel
def: "Polymer gel in which the polymer network contains ionic or ionizable groups in a significant fraction of its constitutional units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07570
name: perfect network
def: "Polymer network composed of chains all of which are connected at both of their ends to different junction points."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07583
name: polymer-derived ceramic
def: "Ceramic derived from a polymeric ceramic precursor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07594
name: permanent crosslink
def: "Crosslink formed by covalent bonds\, intermolecular or intramolecular interactions that are stable under the conditions of use of the material formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07605
name: phantom chain behaviour
def: "Hypothetical behaviour in which chains can move freely through one another when a network is deformed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07627
name: precipitation
def: "Sedimentation of a solid material \(a precipitate\) from a liquid solution in which the material is present in amounts greater than its solubility in the liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:PT07642
name: peptization
def: "Reversal of coagulation or flocculation\, i.e.\, the dispersion of agglomerates to form a colloidally stable suspension or emulsion."
synonym: "deflocculation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q02567
name: γ-quantum
def: "A photon of γ-radiation"

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04967
name: Q-switched laser
def: "A laser in which the state of the device introducing important losses in the resonant cavity and preventing lasing operation is suddenly switched to a state where the device introduces very low losses. This increases rapidly the Quality factor of the cavity\, allowing the build-up of a short and very intense laser pulse. Typical pulse durations are in the ns range. The Q-switching may be active \(a rotating mirror or electro-optic device\) or passive \(a saturable absorber\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04968
name: quadratic mean
def: "The square root of the expression in which the sum of squared observations is divided by the number \\\(n\\\). It can be calculated by the formula\: \\\[\\overline\{x\}_\{\\rm\{q\}\}=\\sqrt\{\\frac\{\\sum x_\{i\}^\{2\}\}\{n\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04969
name: <i>quadro</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote four atoms bound into a quadrangle \(e.g. square\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04970
name: quadrupole ion storage trap
def: "An arrangement in which ions with a desired range of quotients mass/charge are trapped by making them describe stable paths under the effect of a static and a high-frequency electric quadrupole field."
synonym: "Quistor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04971
name: quadrupole mass analyser
def: "An arrangement in which ions with a desired quotient mass/charge are made to describe a stable path under the effect of a static and a high-frequency electric quadrupole field\, and are then detected. Ions with a different mass/charge are separated from the detected ions because of their unstable paths."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04972
name: quadrupole splitting
def: "The measured Doppler velocity difference between the two peaks seen in quadrupole split spectra from nuclides such as $\\ce\{^\{57\}Fe\}$ and $\\ce\{^\{119\}Sn\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04973
name: qualitative analysis
def: "Analysis in which substances are identified or classified on the basis of their chemical or physical properties\, such as chemical reactivity\, solubility\, molecular weight\, melting point\, radiative properties \(emission\, absorption\)\, mass spectra\, nuclear half-life\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04974
name: qualitative elemental specificity
def: "Ability of a method to detect one element in the presence of another element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04975
name: quality assurance
def: "The guarantee that the quality of a product \(analytical data set\, etc.\) is actually what is claimed on the basis of the quality control applied in creating that product. Quality assurance is not synonymous with quality control. Quality assurance is meant to protect against failures of quality control."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04976
name: quality control
def: "The maintenance and statement of the quality of a product \(data set\, etc.\) specifically that it meets or exceeds some minimum standard based on known\, testable criteria."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04977
name: quality factor
def: "The linear-energy-transfer dependent factor by which absorbed dose is multiplied to obtain dose equivalent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04978
name: quality of solvent
def: "A qualitative characterization of the polymer–solvent interaction. A solution of a polymer in a 'better' solvent is characterized by a higher value of the second virial coefficient than a solution of the same polymer in a 'poorer' solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04979
name: quantized internal energy
def: "The quantized internal energy of a molecule in its electronic ground or excited state can be approximated with sufficient accuracy for analytical purposes by\: \\\[E_\{\\rm\{int\}\}=E_\{\\rm\{el\}\}+E_\{\\rm\{vib\}\}+E_\{\\rm\{rot\}\}\\\] where \\\(E_\{\\mathrm\{el\}\}\\\) is the electronic\, \\\(E_\{\\mathrm\{vib\}\}\\\) the vibrational and \\\(E_\{\\mathrm\{rot\}\}\\\) the rotational energy\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04980
name: quantitative analysis
def: "Analyses in which the amount or concentration of an analyte may be determined \(estimated\) and expressed as a numerical value in appropriate units. qualitative analysis may take place without quantitative analysis\, but quantitative analysis requires the identification \(qualification\) of the analytes for which numerical estimates are given."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04981
name: quantitative structure–activity relationships
def: "The building of structure–biological activity models by using regression analysis with physicochemical constants\, indicator variables or theoretical calculations. The term has been extended by some authors to include chemical reactivity\, i.e. activity is regarded as synonymous with reactivity. This extension is\, however\, discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04982
name: quantity
def: "Attribute of a phenomenon\, body or substance that may be distinguished qualitatively and determined quantitatively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04983
name: quantity calculus
def: "Algebra with quantities where the symbols of quantities represent products of numerical values and their units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04984
name: quantity of dimension one
def: "Quantity in the dimensional expression of which all the exponents of the dimensions of the base quantities are zero."
synonym: "dimensionless quantity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04986
name: quantum
def: "An elementary particle of electromagnetic energy in the sense of the wave-particle duality."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04987
name: quantum counter
def: "A medium emitting with a quantum yield independent of the excitation energy over a defined spectral range \(e.g\, concentrated rhodamine 6G solutions between \\\(300\\\) and \\\(600\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\). Also used for devices producing an electrical signal proportional to the photon flux absorbed in a medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04988
name: quantum efficiency
def: "For a primary photochemical process\, quantum efficiency is identical to quantum yield."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04991
name: quantum yield
def: "The integral quantum yield is \\\[\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\(\\lambda\) = \\frac\{\\rm\{number\\ of\\ events\}\}\{\\rm\{number\\ of\\ photons\\ absorbed\}\}\\\] For a photochemical reaction\, \\\[\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\(\\lambda\) = \\frac\{\\rm\{amount\\ of\\ reactant\\ consumed\\ or\\ product\\ formed\}\}\{\\rm\{number\\ of\\ photons\\ absorbed\}\}\\\] The differential quantum yield is \\\[\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\(\\lambda\) = \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}x/\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\}\{q_\{n\,\{\\rm\{p\}\}\}^\{0\}\[ 1 - 10^\{-A\(\\lambda\)\} \]\}\\\] where \\\(\{\\rm\{d\}\}x/\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\\\) is the rate of change of a measurable quantity \(spectral or any other property\)\, and \\\(q_\{n\\rm\{\,p\}\}^\{0\}\\\) the amount of photons \(\\\(\\pu\{mol\}\\\) or its equivalent \\\(\\rm\{einstein\}\\\)\) incident \(prior to absorption\) per time interval \(photon flux\, amount basis\). \\\(A\(\\lambda\)\\\) is the absorbance at the excitation wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04992
name: quarter-transition-time potential
def: "In chronopotentiometry \(at constant current density\)\, the potential of the indicator electrode at the instant when the time that has elapsed since the application of current is equal to one-fourth of the transition time. Appropriate correction for double-layer charging is needed in practice."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04993
name: quartet state
def: "A state having a total electron spin quantum number equal to \\\(\\frac\{3\}\{2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04994
name: quartz–iodine lamp
def: "Wolfram \(tungsten\) filament high-intensity incandescent lamp containing iodine in a quartz envelope. Used primarily as a source of visible radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04996
name: quasi-classical trajectory method
def: "A procedure for calculating trajectories in which the quantization of the reactants is taken into account\, but in which the course of the reaction is treated classically."
synonym: "QCT method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04997
name: quasi-enantiomers
def: "Constitutionally different yet closely related chemical species MX and MY having the opposite chirality sense of the large common chiral moiety M. For example \(R\)-2-bromobutane is a quasi-enantiomer of \(S\)-2-chlorobutane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q04999
name: quasi-equilibrium
def: "In conventional transition-state theory it is assumed that activated complex are formed in a state of equilibrium with the reactants. They are not in classical equilibrium with the reactants; if they were\, addition of more activated complexes to the system would cause the equilibrium to shift in favour of the reactants. This would not occur for an activated complex\, and the term quasi-equilibrium is used to denote this special type of equilibrium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05000
name: quasi-molecular ion
def: "A protonated molecule or an ion formed from a molecular ion by loss of a hydrogen atom. The use of the term 'pseudo-molecular ion' is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05001
name: quasi-racemic compound
def: "The crystalline product of a 1\:1 association between quasi-enantiomers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05002
name: quasi-single-strand polymer
def: "A regular linear polymer that can be described by a preferred constitutional repeating unit in which only one terminal constituent subunit is connected through a single atom to the other identical constitutional repeating units or to an end group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05003
name: quaternary ammonium compounds
def: "Derivatives of ammonium compounds\, $\\ce\{\(NH4^\{+\}\)Y^\{−\}\}$\, in which all four of the hydrogens bonded to nitrogen have been replaced with hydrocarbyl groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05004
name: quaternary structure
def: "The defined organization of two or more macromolecules with tertiary structure such as a protein that are held together by hydrogen bonds and van der Waals and coulombic forces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05006
name: quencher
def: "A molecular entity that deactivates \(quenches\) an excited state of another molecular entity\, either by energy transfer\, electron transfer\, or by a chemical mechanism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05007
name: quenching
def: "Deactivation of an excited molecular entity intermolecularly by an external environmental influence \(such as a quencher\) or intramolecularly by a substituent through a non-radiative process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05009
name: quenching correction
def: "Correction for errors due to different quenching for standards and test portions. When using liquid scintillation detectors these corrections can be based e.g. on the standard addition or sample channels ratio method or the use of automated external standardization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05010
name: quinarenes
def: "Mancude assemblies of three carbocyclic rings\, a six-membered quinonoid ring bonded at the 1\,4-positions to odd-membered rings which differ in ring size by two\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05011
name: quinhydrones
def: "Molecular complexes of one equivalent amount of a quinone with one equivalent amount of the corresponding hydroquinone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05012
name: quinomethanes
def: "Methylidenecyclohexadienones and dimethylidenecyclohexadienes\, formally derived from quinones by replacement of one or both of the quinone oxygens by methylidene groups. \(The diradical\, triplet state of quinodimethanes can also be called o- or p-xylylenes\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05014
name: quinone diazides
def: "A potentially confusing term for diazooxides; the presence of an azido group\, $\\ce\{–N3\}$\, is falsely suggested."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05015
name: quinones
def: "Compounds having a fully conjugated cyclic dione structure\, such as that of benzoquinones\, derived from aromatic compounds by conversion of an even number of number of $\\ce\{–CH=\}$ groups into $\\ce\{–C\(=O\)–\}$ groups with any necessary rearrangement of double bonds \(polycyclic and heterocyclic analogues are included\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05016
name: quinonimines
def: "Imines derived from quinones by replacement of one or both oxygens by $\\ce\{=NR\}$. The term may include both types unless the infix mono or di is included\, e.g. o-benzoquinone diimine\:"
synonym: "quinone imines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q05017
name: quinonoximes
def: "Mono- or di-oximes of quinones; quinone monooximes are tautomeric with nitrosophenols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Q06240
name: θ state
def: "The state of a polymer solution for which the second virial coefficient is zero."

[Term]
id: goldbook:QT06977
name: quantitative structure–activity relationship
def: "Quantitative structure–activity relationships \(QSAR\) are mathematical relationships linking chemical structure and pharmacological activity in a quantitative manner for a series of compounds. Methods which can be used in QSAR include various regression and pattern recognition techniques.QSAR is often taken to be equivalent to chemometrics or multivariate statistical data analysis. It is sometimes used in a more limited sense as equivalent to Hansch analysis. QSAR is a subset of the more general term SPC."

[Term]
id: goldbook:QT07469
name: quantum mechanics/molecular mechanics
def: "Hybrid procedure for the treatment of large molecular systems. A crucial part of the system \(e.g.\, the chromophore and its surroundings\) is treated explicitly by quantum mechanical \(QM\) techniques\, whereas the rest of the system is approximated by a classical or molecular mechanics \(MM\) force field treatment."
synonym: "QC/MM" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R00002
name: α-ray spectrometer
def: "A measuring assembly incorporating a radiation detector and a pulse amplitude\, used for determining the energy spectrum of $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ \($\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{\\upgamma\\!\}$\) radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R02568
name: γ-radiation
def: "Electromagnetic radiation emitted in the process of nuclear transformation or particle annihilation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R02570
name: γ-ray spectrometer
def: "A measuring assembly for determining the energy spectrum of $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{rays\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05022
name: RM value
def: "A logarithmic function of the \\\(R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\) value \(retardation factor\)\: \\\[R_\{\\rm\{M\}\}=\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{1\\\,-\\\,R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\}\{R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\}\)=\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{1\}\{R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\}\\\,-\\\,1\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05023
name: rabbit
def: "A small container propelled pneumatically or hydraulically through a tube leading from the laboratory to a location in a nuclear reactor or other device where irradiation of a sample can take place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05025
name: racemate
def: "An equimolar mixture of a pair of enantiomers. It does not exhibit optical activity. The chemical name or formula of a racemate is distinguished from those of the enantiomers by the prefix \(±\)- or rac- \(or racem-\) or by the symbols RS and SR."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05026
name: racemic
def: "Pertaining to a racemate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05027
name: racemic compound
def: "A crystalline racemate in which the two enantiomers are present in equal amounts in a well defined arrangement within the lattice of a homogeneous crystalline addition compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05028
name: racemic conglomerate
def: "An equimolar mechanical mixture of crystals each one of which contains only one of the two enantiomers present in a racemate. The process of its formation on crystallization of a racemate is called spontaneous resolution\, since pure or nearly pure enantiomers can often be obtained from the conglomerate by sorting."
synonym: "conglomerate" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05029
name: racemic mixture
def: "The term is confusing since it has been used as a synonym for both racemate and racemic conglomerate. Usage strongly discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05030
name: racemization
def: "The production of a racemate from a chiral starting material in which one enantiomer is present in excess."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05032
name: rad
def: "Non-SI unit of absorbed dose of radiation\, \\\(\\rm\{rad\} = 0.01\\ \\rm\{Gy\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05034
name: radial electrostatic field analyser
def: "An arrangement of two conducting sheets forming a capacitor and giving a radial electrostatic field which is used to deflect and focus ion beams of different energies. The capacitor may be cylindrical\, spherical\, or toroidal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05035
name: radial elution
def: "A mode of operation in which the sample is spotted at a point source at or near the middle of the plane and is carried outward in a circle by the mobile phase\, also applied at that place."
synonym: "circular development" []
synonym: "circular elution" []
synonym: "radial development" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05036
name: radian
def: "SI derived unit of plane angle\, \\\(\\rm\{rad\} = 1\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05037
name: radiance
def: "Radiant power\, \\\(P\\\)\, leaving or passing through a small transparent element of surface in a given direction from the source about the solid angle \\\(\\varOmega\\\)\, divided by the solid angle and by the orthogonally projected area of the element in a plane normal to the given beam direction\, \\\(\\rm\{d\}S_\{\\perp \} = \\rm\{d\}S\\\, \\rm\{cos\}\\\,\\theta\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05039
name: radiant energy
def: "The total energy emitted\, transferred or received as radiation in a defined period of time \(\\\(Q=\\int Q_\{\\lambda \}  \\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\lambda \\\)\). It is the product of radiant power\, \\\(P\\\)\, and time\, \\\(t\\\)\: \\\(Q=P\\ t\\\) when the radiant power is constant over the time considered."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05040
name: radiant energy density
def: "Radiant energy divided by the volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05041
name: radiant exitance
def: "Radiant power\, \\\(P\\\)\, emitted at all wavelengths by an element of surface containing the source point under consideration divided by the area of the source. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-2\}\\\)."
synonym: "exitance" []
synonym: "radiant emittance" []
synonym: "spherical radiant exitance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05042
name: radiant exposure
def: "Radiant energy\, \\\(Q\\\)\, incident from all upward directions on a small sphere divided by the cross-sectional area of that sphere. SI unit is \\\(\\pu\{J m-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05045
name: radiant intensity
def: "Radiant power\, \\\(P\\\)\, at all wavelengths per solid angle\, \\\(\\varOmega\\\). The @R05046-2@ emitted in a given direction by a source or an element of the source in a small cone containing the given direction divided by the solid angle of the cone. SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W sr\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05046
name: radiant power
def: "Power emitted\, transferred or received as radiation. SI unit is \\\(\\pu\{J s-1\} = \\pu\{W\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05047
name: radiant quantities
def: "Group of quantities characterizing electromagnetic radiation in terms of energy. The symbols of radiant quantities may be distinguished by adding a subscript e."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05048
name: radiation
def: "A term embracing electromagnetic waves as well as fast moving particles. In radioanalytical chemistry the term usually refers to radiation emitted during a nuclear process \(radioactive decay\, nuclear reaction\, nuclear fission\, accelerators\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05050
name: radiation chemistry
def: "The part of chemistry which deals with the chemical effects of ionizing radiation\, as distinguished from photochemistry associated with visible and ultraviolet electromagnetic radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05051
name: radiation constants
def: "Fundamental physical constants characterizing black body radiation. The first radiation constant is \\\(c_\{1\} = 2\\ \\pi \\ h\\ c_\{0\}^\{2\}=3.741\\\,7749\(22\)\\times 10^\{-16\}\\ \\rm\{W m\}^\{2\}\\\)\, the second is \\\(c_\{2\} = \\frac\{h\\ c_\{0\}\}\{k\}=1.438\\\,769\(12\)\\times 10^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{m K\}\\\)\, where \\\(h\\\) is the Planck constant \\\(c_\{0\}\\\) the speed of light and \\\(k\\\) the Boltzmann constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05052
name: radiation continuum
def: "Continuous \(in the wavelength\, not time sense\) radiation arising for example from non-quantized free-free transitions of electrons in the fields of the ions\, free-bound transitions or radiative recombinations of electrons and ions\, incandescent radiation emitted by hot solids \(when the radiation distribution conforms to that described by Planck's law\, it is considered black-body radiation\) and unresolvable band spectra\, i.e. where the spectral lines are wider than the spacings between them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05053
name: radiation counter
def: "Radiation measuring assembly comprising a radiation detector in which individual ionizing events cause electrical pulses and the associated equipment for processing and counting the pulses. Often an expression is added indicating the type of radiation detector \(e.g. scintillation\, semiconductor\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05054
name: radiation detector
def: "A device in which incident radiation produces a measurable effect. If this effect is a rise in temperature it is called a thermal detector. If it is a rise in pressure it is called a photoacoustic detector. In the case where an electrical signal is produced it is called a photoelectric detector. Photoelectric detectors can be classified as photo-emissive detectors and semiconductor detectors. Where the radiation produces a chemical reaction\, it is termed a photochemical detector. A detector yielding an output signal that is independent of the wavelength of the radiation over a specific region is called a nonselective detector. Where it is wavelength specific it is a selective detector. A detector having a quantum efficiency independent of the wavelength is a nonselective quantum counter. Certain detectors are able to distinguish between different quantum energies. This property is described by the energy resolution \\\(\\Delta E\\\) and the energy resolving power \\\(\\frac\{E\}\{\\Delta E\}\\\). These detectors are called energy dispersive detectors. In X-ray spectroscopy\, the reciprocal \\\(\\frac\{\\Delta E\}\{E\}\\\) is often used but this is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05055
name: radiation hazard
def: "Hazard that exists in a region where there is a radiation field\, other than what is considered to be normal background radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05056
name: radiationless deactivation
def: "Loss of electronic excitation energy without photon emission or chemical change. Often\, ultrafast radiationless decays are mediated by conical intersections."
synonym: "radiationless decay" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05057
name: radiationless transition
def: "A transition between two states of a system without photon emission or absorption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05059
name: radiation spectrum
def: "The components of radiation arranged in order of their wavelengths\, frequencies or quantum energies. For particle radiation they are arranged in order of their kinetic energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05060
name: radiative absorption
def: "A process by which a particle in the ground state or an excited state may undergo transition to a higher energy level by absorption of a photon. For a given particle in the lower state of probability per second of such a transition in a field with a continuous spectrum is proportional to the spectral radiant energy density of the absorption line. The proportionality constant is termed the transition probability for absorption. The transition probability for stimulated emission is defined in a similar way for the reverse radiative de-excitation process that is induced by the same radiation field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05061
name: radiative capture
def: "Capture of a particle by a nucleus followed by immediate emission of gamma radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05062
name: radiative de-excitation
def: "The change in the internal energy of a particle may be due to radiative processes\, i.e. the emission or absorption of a photon. A particle in an excited state may undergo a transition to a lower energy level by emission of a photon. This is known as radiative de-excitation. If such a transition occurs spontaneously its probability per second for a given excited particle is termed the transition probability for spontaneous emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05063
name: radiative energy transfer
def: "Transfer of excitation energy by radiative deactivation of a donor molecular entity and reabsorption of the emitted radiation by an acceptor molecular entity."
synonym: "trivial energy transfer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05064
name: radiative lifetime
def: "Lifetime of an excited molecular entity in the absence of radiationless transitions. The reciprocal of the first-order rate constant for the radiative step\, or the reciprocal of the sum of these rate constants if there is more than one such step. The equivalent term\, natural lifetime\, is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05065
name: radiative transition
def: "A transition between two states of a molecular entity\, the energy difference being emitted or absorbed as photons. In principle\, radiative and radiationless transitions can be distinguished in molecules. The first occur by absorption or emission of light quanta\, and the latter is the result of the transformation of electronic excitation energy into vibrational/rotational energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05066
name: radical
def: "A molecular entity such as $\\ce\{^\{.\}CH3\}$\, $\\ce\{^\{.\}SnH3\}$\, $\\ce\{Cl^\{.\}\}$ possessing an unpaired electron. \(In these formulae the dot\, symbolizing the unpaired electron\, should be placed so as to indicate the atom of highest spin density\, if this is possible.\) Paramagnetic metal ions are not normally regarded as radicals. However\, in the 'isolobal analogy' the similarity between certain paramagnetic metal ions and radicals becomes apparent. At least in the context of physical organic chemistry\, it seems desirable to cease using the adjective 'free' in the general name of this type of chemical species and molecular entity\, so that the term 'free radical' may in future be restricted to those radicals which do not form parts of radical pairs. Depending upon the core atom that possesses the unpaired electron\, the radicals can be described as carbon-\, oxygen-\, nitrogen-\, metal-centered radicals. If the unpaired electron occupies an orbital having considerable s or more or less pure p character\, the respective radicals are termed $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ or $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}radicals\}$. In the past\, the term 'radical' was used to designate a substituent group bound to a molecular entity\, as opposed to 'free radical'\, which nowadays is simply called radical. The bound entities may be called groups or substituents\, but should no longer be called radicals."
synonym: "free radical" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05069
name: radical centre
def: "The atom \(or group of atoms\) in a polyatomic radical on which an unpaired electron is largely localized. attachment of a monovalent atom to a radical centre gives a molecule for which it is possible to write a Lewis formula in which the normal stable valencies are assigned to all atoms."
synonym: "radical center" []
synonym: "radical centers" []
synonym: "radical centres" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05071
name: radical copolymerization
def: "A copolymerization which is a radical polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05073
name: radical ion
def: "A radical that carries an electric charge. A positively charged radical is called a 'radical cation' \(e.g. the benzene radical cation $\\ce\{C6H6^\{.+\}\}$\); a negatively charged radical is called a 'radical anion' \(e.g. the benzene radical anion $\\ce\{C6H^\{.−\}\}$ or the benzophenone radical anion $\\ce\{Ph2C–O^\{.−\}\}$\). Commonly\, but not necessarily\, the odd electron and the charge are associated with the same atom. Unless the positions of unpaired spin and charge can be associated with specific atoms\, superscript dot and charge designations should be placed in the order $\\ce\{^\{.+\}\}$ or $\\ce\{^\{.-\}\}$ suggested by the name 'radical ion'. \(e.g. $\\ce\{C3H6^\{.+\}\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05074
name: radical pair
def: "The term is used to identify two radicals in close proximity in solution\, within a solvent cage. They may be formed simultaneously by some unimolecular process\, e.g. peroxide decomposition\, or they may have come together by diffusion. While the radicals are together\, correlation of the unpaired electron spins of the two species cannot be ignored\: this correlation is responsible for the CIDNP phenomenon. A radical pair is called geminate provided that each radical partner is a descendant of the same parental pair."
synonym: "geminate pair" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05075
name: radical polymerization
def: "A chain polymerization in which the kinetic-chain carriers are radicals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05077
name: radioactive
def: "The property of a nuclide of undergoing spontaneous nuclear transformations with the emission of radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05078
name: radioactive age
def: "The time\, estimated from measurement of the isotopic composition\, during which the content of a radioactive species within an object has remained unchanged except for nuclear decay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05080
name: radioactive contamination
def: "A radioactive substance in a material or place where it is undesirable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05081
name: radioactive cooling
def: "Of a strongly radioactive material\, the decrease of its activity by nuclear decay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05082
name: radioactive dating
def: "The determination of the radioactive age of an object from its content of radioactive substances and of their daughter products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05083
name: radioactive decay
def: "Nuclear decay in which particles or electromagnetic radiation are emitted or the nucleus undergoes spontaneous fission or electron capture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05084
name: radioactive equilibrium
def: "Among the members of a decay chain\, the state which prevails when the ratios between the activities of successive members remain constant. \(This is not an equilibrium in the strict sense since radioactive decay is an irreversible process\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05085
name: radioactive fallout
def: "The deposition on the ground of radioactive substances from nuclear explosions and other injections of radioactive material into the atmosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05088
name: radioactive source
def: "Any quantity of radioactive material which is intended for use as a source of ionizing radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05089
name: radioactive tracer
def: "A tracer containing a radioactive label."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05090
name: radioactive tracer technique
def: "A technique for investigating recovery and loss of a microcomponent\, in which a radioactively-labelled element or compound chemically identical with the microcomponent is added to the sample before preconcentration and its behaviour is followed by sensitive\, rapid and selective radioactivity measurements."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05091
name: radioactive waste
def: "Unwanted radioactive materials obtained in the processing or handling of radioactive materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05092
name: radioactivity
def: "The property of certain nuclides showing radioactive decay."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05094
name: radiochemical activation analysis
def: "A kind of activation analysis in which\, after the irradiation\, chemical or physical separation is applied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05095
name: radiochemical purification
def: "Chemical separation applied to a radioactive preparation in order to improve the radiochemical purity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05096
name: radiochemical purity
def: "For a material\, the fraction of the stated isotope present in the stated chemical form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05097
name: radiochemical separation
def: "Separation by a chemical means of the radioactive isotopes of a specific element from a mixture of radionuclides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05098
name: radiochemical yield
def: "The yield of a radiochemical separation expressed as a fraction of the activity originally present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05099
name: radiochemistry
def: "That part of chemistry which deals with radioactive materials. It includes the production of radionuclides and their compounds by processing irradiated materials or naturally occurring radioactive materials\, the application of chemical techniques to nuclear studies\, and the application of radioactivity to the investigation of chemical\, biochemical or biomedical problems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05100
name: radiochromatograph
def: "A measuring assembly designed to measure the spatial or time distribution of the activity of a mixture of radioactive components after separation by a chromatographic method."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05101
name: radiocolloid
def: "A colloid in which some atoms are radioactive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05102
name: radioenzymatic assay
def: "An assay of the catalytic activity of an enzyme based on the use of a radioactive substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05103
name: radiograph
def: "A visual representation of an object produced by placing the object between a source of ionizing radiation and a photographic plate or film."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05104
name: radiogravimetric analysis
def: "A kind of quantitative analysis in which the activity of a precipitate is used as a measure of its mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05105
name: radioimmunoassay
def: "An assay based on the reversible and non-covalent binding of an antigen \(hapten\) by a specific antibody employing radioactivity labelled antigen \(hapten\) to measure the fraction of the antigen \(hapten\) bound to a substoichiometric amount of antibody."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05107
name: radioiodination
def: "The process of incorporating the radionuclides of iodine \(usually $\\ce\{^\{125\}I\}$\, $\\ce\{^\{131\}I\}$ or $\\ce\{^\{123\}I\}$\) into\, or of covalently linking a radioiodinated substance to\, a substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05108
name: radioisotope
def: "A radioactive isotope of a specified element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05109
name: radioisotope dilution analysis
def: "A kind of isotope dilution analysis making use of a radionuclide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05110
name: radioisotope induced X-ray emission analysis
def: "A kind of analysis based on the measurement of the energies and intensities of characteristic X-radiation emitted by a test portion during irradiation with a radioactive source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05111
name: radioluminescence
def: "Luminescence arising from excitation by high-energy particles or radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05112
name: radiolysis
def: "The cleavage of one or several bonds resulting from exposure to high-energy radiation. The term is also often used loosely to specify the method of irradiation \('pulse radiolysis'\) used in any radiochemical reaction\, not necessarily one involving bond cleavage."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05113
name: radiometric analysis
def: "A kind of quantitative analysis in which measurement of the activity is an essential step."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05114
name: radiometric titration
def: "A titration in which a radioactive indicator is used to monitor the end-point of the titration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05115
name: radiometry
def: "The measurement of quantities associated with radiant energy. The quantities may also describe the variation of the energy with respect to other variables such as wavelength\, time\, position\, direction \(solid angle\)\, area normal to the light or projected area of emitting or receiving surfaces. If the light is monochromatic\, it is sometimes convenient to replace the radiant energy by the corresponding number of photons \(or quanta\) which is obtained by dividing the energy by \\\(\\frac\{h\\ c\}\{\\lambda \}\\\) where \\\(h\\\) is the Planck constant\, \\\(c\\\) the velocity of light and \\\(\\lambda \\\) the wavelength of the light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05116
name: radionuclide
def: "A nuclide that is radioactive."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05117
name: radionuclidic purity
def: "For a material\, that fraction of the total activity which is present in the form of the stated radionuclide\, including daughter products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05118
name: radioreceptor assay
def: "An assay employing a radioactively labelled receptor protein as a tracer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05119
name: radiorelease analysis
def: "A kind of quantitative analysis based on the release of radioactivity from the reagent by reaction with the analyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05120
name: radiosonde
def: "A miniature radio transmitter with instruments in a package that is carried aloft \(e.g. by an unmanned balloon\) for broadcasting every few seconds by means of precise tone signals or other suitable method\, the humidity\, temperature\, pressure or other parameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05121
name: radius of gyration
def: "A parameter characterizing the size of a particle of any shape. For a rigid particle consisting of mass elements of mass \\\(m_\{i\}\\\)\, each located at a distance \\\(r_\{i\}\\\) from the centre of mass\, the radius of gyration\, \\\(s\\\)\, is defined as the square root of the mass-average of \\\(r_\{i\}^\{2\}\\\) for all the mass elements\, i.e. \\\[s = \\sqrt\{\\frac\{\\sum _\{i\}m_\{i\}\\ r_\{i\}^\{2\}\}\{\\sum _\{i\}m_\{i\}\}\}\\\] For a non-rigid particle\, an average over all conformations is considered\, i.e. \\\[\\sqrt\{ < s^\{2\} > \} = \\frac\{\\sqrt\{< \\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}m_\{i\}\\ r_\{i\}^\{2\} > \}\}\{\\sqrt\{\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}m_\{i\}\}\}\\\] The subscript zero is used to indicate unperturbed dimensions\, as in \\\(\\rm\{\}_\{0\}^\{1/2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05122
name: raffinate
def: "The phase remaining after extraction of some specified solute\(s\). When necessary it should be further specified\, e.g. scrub raffinate. The original meaning of raffinate as a 'refined product' has become extended and changed by common usage. The term should normally be applied only to waste streams but the latter may form the feed to a further extraction process for another solute."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05123
name: rain out
def: "The mechanism by which small particles in the clouds are removed by the formation of raindrops; this is a different mechanism from wash out which is a process which occurs below cloud level. Both the terms rain out and wash out have not always been used in accordance with these definitions. For clarity they should be replaced by the terms\, in-cloud scavenging and below-cloud scavenging\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05124
name: random coil
def: "The complete set of spatial arrangements of a chain molecule with a large number of segments that randomly change mutual orientation with time\, under conditions in which it is free from external constraints that would affect its conformation. If the solution of the chain molecules is not in a theta state\, the segments change mutual orientation only approximately randomly. Synonymous with statistical coil."
synonym: "statistical coil" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05125
name: random coincidence
def: "A coincidence of events occurring in not physically connected nuclei."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05126
name: random copolymer
def: "A copolymer consisting of macromolecules in which the probability of finding a given monomeric unit at any given site in the chain is independent of the nature of the adjacent units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05127
name: random copolymerization
def: "A copolymerization in which a random copolymer is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05128
name: random error
def: "Result of a measurement minus the mean that would result from an infinite number of measurements of the same measurand carried out under repeatability conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05129
name: random sample
def: "The sample so selected that any portion of the population has an equal \(or known\) chance of being chosen. Haphazard or arbitrary choice of units is generally insufficient to guarantee randomness."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05130
name: range
def: "The difference between the highest and lowest members of a series. This term is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05134
name: range of measurement
def: "The range of concentration between the measurement threshold and the maximum usable indication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05136
name: rate
def: "Derived quantity in which time is a denominator quantity. Rate of \\\(x\\\) is \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}x\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05139
name: rate-controlling step
def: "A rate-controlling \(rate-determining or rate-limiting\) step in a reaction occurring by a composite reaction sequence is an elementary reaction the rate constant for which exerts a strong effect — stronger than that of any other rate constant — on the overall rate. It is recommended that the expressions rate-controlling\, rate-determining and rate-limiting be regarded as synonymous\, but some special meanings sometimes given to the last two expressions are considered under a separate heading. A rate-controlling step can be formally defined on the basis of a control function \(or control factor\) CF\, identified for an elementary reaction having a rate constant \\\(k_\{i\}\\\) by\: \\\[\\rm\{CF\}=\\frac\{\\partial \(\\ln \nu\)\}\{\\partial \\ln k_\{i\}\}\\\] where \\\(\nu\\\) is the overall rate of reaction. In performing the partial differentiation all equilibrium constants \\\(K_\{j\}\\\) and all rate constants except \\\(k_\{i\}\\\) are held constant. The elementary reaction having the largest control factor exerts the strongest influence on the rate \\\(\nu\\\)\, and a step having a CF much larger than any other step may be said to be rate-controlling. A rate-controlling step defined in the way recommended here has the advantage that it is directly related to the interpretation of kinetic isotope effects. As formulated this implies that all rate constants are of the same dimensionality. Consider however the reaction of A and B to give an intermediate C\, which then reacts further with D to give products\:      \(1\)      \(2\)   Assuming that C reaches a steady state\, then the observed rate is given by\: \\\[\nu = \\frac\{k_\{1\}\\\,k_\{2\}\\\,\\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]\\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]\\left\[\\rm\{D\}\\right\]\}\{k_\{-1\}+k_\{2\}\\left\[\\rm\{D\}\\right\]\}\\\] Considering \\\(k_\{2\}\\left\[\\rm\{D\}\\right\]\\\) a pseudo-first order rate constant\, then \\\(k_\{2\}\\left\[\\rm\{D\}\\right\]\\gg k_\{-1\}\\\)\, and the observed rate \\\(\nu = k_\{1\}\\ \\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]\\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]\\\) and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{obs\}\}=k_\{1\}\\\). Step \(1\) is said to be the rate-controlling step. If \\\(k_\{2\}\\left\[\\rm\{D\}\\right\]\\ll k_\{-1\}\\\)\, then the observed rate\: \\\[\nu = \\frac\{k_\{1\}\\ k_\{2\}\}\{k_\{-1\}\}\\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]\\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]\\left\[\\rm\{D\}\\right\]=K\\ k_\{2\}\\left\[\\rm\{A\}\\right\]\\left\[\\rm\{B\}\\right\]\\left\[\\rm\{D\}\\right\]\\\] where \\\(K\\\) is the equilibrium constant for the pre-equilibrium \(1\) and is equal to \\\(\\frac\{k_\{1\}\}\{k_\{-1\}\}\\\)\, and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{obs\}\}=K\\ k_\{2\}\\\). Step \(2\) is said to be the rate-controlling step."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05140
name: rate-determining step
def: "These terms are best regarded as synonymous with rate-controlling step. However\, other meanings that have been given to them should be mentioned\, as it is necessary to be aware of them in order to avoid confusion. Sometimes the term rate-determining is used as a special case of rate-controlling\, being assigned only to an initial slow step which is followed by rapid steps. Such a step imposes an upper limit on the rate\, and has also been called rate-limiting. In view of the considerable danger of confusion when special meanings are applied to rate-determining and rate-limiting\, it is recommended that they be regarded as synonymous\, with the meaning explained under the entry rate-controlling step."
synonym: "rate-limiting step" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05141
name: rate law
def: "An expression for the rate of reaction of a particular reaction in terms of concentrations of chemical species and constant parameters \(normally rate coefficients and partial orders of reaction\) only. For examples of rate laws see equations \(1\)-\(3\) under kinetic equivalence\, and \(1\) under steady state."
synonym: "empirical differential rate equation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05142
name: ratemeter
def: "An electronic sub-assembly which gives a continuous indication proportional to the average counting rate over a predetermined time interval \(time constant\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05144
name: rate of change of a quantity
def: "The time derivative \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}Q\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\) of the value of the quantity. The differential quotient may also be called derivative \(or instantaneous\) rate of change. Examples are\: rate of change of mass\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}m\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\); rate of change of amount of substance\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05145
name: rate of change ratio
def: "The quotient of two rates where the quantities are of the same kind in the same system for different components\: \\\[\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}Q_\{1\}/\\rm\{d\}t\}\{\\rm\{d\}Q_\{2\}/\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] For finite time intervals\, mean rate of change ratio is\: \\\[\\frac\{\\Delta Q_\{1\}/\\Delta t\}\{\\Delta Q_\{2\}/\\Delta t\}=\\frac\{\\Delta Q_\{1\}\}\{\\Delta Q_\{2\}\}\\ \\Delta t\\\] Rate of change ratio has the dimension one. The denominator is often called the reference quantity. Examples are\: mass rate ratio\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}m_\{1\}/\\rm\{d\}t\}\{\\rm\{d\}m_\{2\}/\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\); amount of substance rate ratio\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{1\}/\\rm\{d\}t\}\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{2\}/\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05146
name: rate of consumption
def: "The rate of consumption of a specified reactant may be defined in two ways\:As the negative of the time derivative of the amount of reactant; thus for a reactant B\, present at any time in amount \\\(n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, the rate of its consumption may be defined as\: \\\[\nu \(n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\) = -\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] This definition is particularly appropriate for open systems. Here and elsewhere\, when a rate is defined in terms of a time derivative\, it must be understood that the definition relates to the process occurring in isolation. In a flow system there may be no actual changes with time\, and the time derivative must be inferred. Such an inference is also required for a reaction occurring by a composite mechanism.For kinetics in closed systems it is more usual to define a rate of consumption per unit volume; thus for a reactant B the rate of consumption\, \\\(\nu \(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\) \\\) is given by\: \\\[\nu \(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\) = -\\frac\{1\}\{V\}\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] When the volume \\\(V\\\) is constant this reduces to\: \\\[\nu \(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\) = -\\frac\{\\rm\{d\[B\]\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] When the volume is not constant the relationship \\\(n_\{\\rm\{B\}\} = \[\\rm\{B\}\]V\\\) may be differentiated to give\: \\\[\{\\rm\{d\}\}c_\{\\rm\{B\}\} = V\\rm\{d\[B\]\} + \\rm\{\[B\]d\}V\\\] and therefore\: \\\[\nu \(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\) = -\\frac\{\\rm\{d\[B\]\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\} - \\frac\{\\rm\{\[B\]\}\}\{V\}\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}V\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] In contrast to the rate of conversion and the rate of reaction\, the rate of consumption of a reactant may be specified even for a reaction of time-dependent stoichiometry or of unknown stoichiometry. The rate of consumption of a reactant is often called its rate of disappearance. However\, the former expression is to be preferred since the word disappearance is not appropriately translatable into certain languages. When English is used the word disappearance might be reserved for cases where the reactant is almost completely removed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05147
name: rate of conversion
def: "The rate of conversion for a reaction occurring in a closed system is defined as the time derivative of the extent of reaction\: \\\[\\overset\{\\rm\{.\}\}\{\\xi \}=\\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}\\xi \}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}\\\] In view of the definition of extent of reaction it follows that with reference to any species in a reaction showing time-independent stoichiometry \\\[\\overset\{\\rm\{.\}\}\{\\xi \}=\\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}\\xi \}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}=\\frac\{1\}\{\nu _\{i\}\}\\ \\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}n_\{i\}\}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}\\\] where \\\(n_\{i\}\\\) is the amount of the species at any time and \\\(\nu _\{i\}\\\) is its stoichiometric coefficient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05151
name: rate of fluid consumption
def: "The volume of fluid consumed by the nebulizer divided by time \(in \\\(\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{L\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\)\). Volume rate has also been called volume flow\, flux\, aspiration rate\, aspiration flow or aspiration flux. The fluid consumed is either aspirated or injected. The volume rate of fluid consumption by nebulizers is usually between \\\(10\\\) and \\\(100\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{L\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05152
name: rate of formation
def: "Like the rate of consumption\, the rate of formation of a specified product may be defined in two ways\:As the time derivative of the amount of a product. Thus for a product $\\ce\{Y\}$\, present at any time in amount \\\(n_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\\\). the rate of its formation may be given by\: \\\[\nu \(n_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\) = \\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] This definition is particularly appropriate for open systems.For kinetics in closed systems it is more usual to define a rate of formation per unit volume\, denoted \\\(\nu \(c_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\) \\\)\: \\\[\nu \(c_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\) = \\frac\{1\}\{V\}\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] When the volume is constant this reduces to\: \\\[\nu \(c_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\) = \\frac\{1\}\{V\}\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\} = \\frac\{\\rm\{d\[Y\]\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] When the volume is not constant the relationship \\\(n_\{\\rm\{Y\}\} = \[\\rm\{Y\}\]V\\\) may be differentiated to give\: \\\[\{\\rm\{d\}\}n_\{\\rm\{Y\}\} = V\\rm\{d\[Y\]\} + \\rm\{\[Y\]d\}V\\\] and the rate of formation becomes\: \\\[\nu \(c_\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\) = \\frac\{\\rm\{d\[Y\]\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\} + \\frac\{\\rm\{\[Y\]\}\}\{V\}\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}V\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] A rate of formation may be specified even for a reaction of time dependent stoichiometry or of unknown stoichiometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05153
name: rate of liquid consumption
def: "The volume of liquid sample that is consumed by the nebulizing system per unit of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05154
name: rate of migration
def: "The distance of migration divided by time\, sometimes called velocity of migration. The symbol \\\(v\\\) is also used for velocity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05155
name: rate of nucleation
def: "The number of nuclei formed in unit time per unit volume of the liquid phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05156
name: rate of reaction
def: "For the general chemical reaction\: \\\[\\ce\{aA + bB -> pP + qQ + \\dots\}\\\] occurring under constant-volume conditions\, without an appreciable build-up of reaction intermediates\, the rate of reaction \\\(\nu\\\) is defined as\: \\\[\nu = -\\frac\{1\}\{a\}\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{A\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = -\\frac\{1\}\{b\}\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{B\]\}\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = \\frac\{1\}\{p\}\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{P\]\}\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = \\frac\{1\}\{q\}\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{Q\]\}\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\}\\\] where symbols placed inside square brackets denote amount \(or amount of substance\) concentrations \(conventionally expressed in units of \\\(\\pu\{mol dm-3\}\\\)\). The symbols \\\(R\\\) and \\\(r\\\) are also commonly used in place of \\\(\nu\\\). It is recommended that the unit of time should always be the second. In such a case the rate of reaction differs from the rate of increase of concentration of a product P by a constant factor \(the reciprocal of its coefficient in the stoichiometric equation\, \\\(p\\\)\) and from the rate of decrease of concentration of the reactant A by \\\(\\alpha^\{-1\}\\\). The quantity\: \\\[\\dot\{\\xi\} = \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\xi\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\}\\\] defined by the equation\: \\\[\\dot\{\\xi\} = -\\frac\{1\}\{a\} \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}n_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} \n= -\\frac\{1\}\{b\} \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}n_\{\\ce\{B\}\}\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = \\frac\{1\}\{p\} \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}n_\{\\ce\{P\}\}\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = \\frac\{1\}\{q\} \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}n_\{\\ce\{Q\}\}\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\}\\\] \(where \\\(n_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\\\) designates the amount of substance A\, conventionally expressed in units of mole\) may be called the 'rate of conversion' and is appropriate when the use of concentrations is inconvenient\, e.g. under conditions of varying volume. In a system of constant volume\, the rate of reaction is equal to the rate of conversion per unit volume throughout the reaction. For a stepwise reaction this definition of 'rate of reaction' \(and 'extent of reaction'\, \\\(\\xi \\\)\) will apply only if there is no accumulation of intermediate or formation of side products. It is therefore recommended that the term 'rate of reaction' be used only in cases where it is experimentally established that these conditions apply. More generally\, it is recommended that\, instead\, the terms 'rate of disappearance' or 'rate of consumption' of A \(i.e. \\\(-\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{A\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\}\\\)\, the rate of decrease of concentration of A\) or 'rate of appearance' of P \(i.e. \\\(\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{P\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\}\\\)\, the rate of increase of concentration of product P\) be used\, depending on the concentration change of the particular chemical species that is actually observed. In some cases reference to the chemical flux observed may be more appropriate. The symbol \\\(\nu\\\) \(without lettered subscript\) should be used only for rate of reaction; \\\(\nu\\\) with a lettered subscript \(e.g. \\\(\nu_\{\\ce\{A\}\}\\\)\) refers to a rate of appearance or rate of disappearance \(e.g. of the chemical species A\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05157
name: ratio
def: "Quotient of quantities of the same kind for different components within the same system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05158
name: raw coke
def: "The term raw coke is equivalent to green coke although it is now used less frequently."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05159
name: Rayleigh ratio
def: "The quantity used to characterize the scattered intensity at the scattering angle \\\(\\theta\\\)\, defined as \\\(R\(\\theta\) = \\frac\{i_\{\\theta \}\\\,r^\{2\}\}\{I\\\,f\\\,V\}\\\)\, where \\\(I\\\) is the intensity of the incident radiation\, \\\(i_\{\\theta\}\\\) is the total intensity of scattered radiation observed at an angle \\\(\\theta\\\) and a distance \\\(r\\\) from the point of scattering and \\\(V\\\) is the scattering volume. The factor \\\(f\\\) takes account of polarization phenomena. It depends on the type of radiation employed.   For light scattering\, dependent on the polarization of the incident beam\, \\\(f=1\\\) for vertically polarized light\, \\\(f = 1 - \\cos^\{2\}\\theta\\\) for horizontally polarized light and \\\(f = 1 + \\frac\{\\cos^\{2\}\\theta\}\{2\}\\\) for unpolarized light.   For small-angle neutron scattering\, \\\(f=1\\\).   For small-angle X-ray scattering\, \\\(f \\approx 1\\\)\, if \\\(\\theta < \\rm\{ca.\}\\ 5\\\,°\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05160
name: Rayleigh scattering
def: "The scattering of light by particles and molecules which are much smaller than the wavelength of the light. In the ideal case\, the process is one of pure dipole and induced dipole interactions with the electric field of the light wave. The scattering cross section for light of wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\) is proportional to \\\(\\lambda ^\{-4\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05161
name: rayon-based carbon fibres
def: "Carbon fibres made from rayon \(cellulose\) precursor fibres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05162
name: reactance
def: "Imaginary part of the impedance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05163
name: reactant
def: "A substance that is consumed in the course of a chemical reaction. It is sometimes known\, especially in the older literature\, as a 'reagent'\, but this term is better used in a more specialized sense as a test substance that is added to a system in order to bring about a reaction or to see whether a reaction occurs \(e.g. an analytical reagent\)."
synonym: "reagent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05164
name: reacting bond rules
def: "For an internal motion of a molecular entity corresponding to progress over a transition state \(energy maximum\)\, any change that makes the motion more difficult will lead to a new molecular geometry at the energy maximum\, in which the motion has proceeded further. Changes that make the motion less difficult will have the opposite effect. \(This rule corresponds to the Hammond principle\).For an internal motion of a molecular entity that corresponds to a vibration\, any change that tends to modify the equilibrium point of the vibration in a particular direction will actually shift the equilibrium in that direction.Effects on reacting bonds \(bonds made or broken in the reaction\) are the most significant. The bonds nearest the site of structural change are those most strongly affected."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05166
name: reaction barrier
def: "The energy barrier to chemical reaction. In vibrationally adiabatic transition-state theory its height is the zero-point energy of the activated complex minus that of the reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05167
name: reaction chromatography
def: "A technique in which the identities of the sample components are intentionally changed between sample introduction and detection. The reaction can take place upstream of the column when the chemical identity of the individual components passing through the column differs from that of the original sample\, or between the column and the detector when the original sample components are separated in the column but their identity is changed prior to entering the detection device."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05168
name: reaction coordinate
def: "A geometric parameter that changes during the conversion of one \(or more\) reactant molecular entities into one \(or more\) product molecular entities and whose value can be taken for a measure of the progress of an elementary reaction \(for example\, a bond length or bond angle or a combination of bond lengths and/or bond angles; it is sometimes approximated by a non-geometric parameter\, such as the bond order of some specified bond\). In the formalism of 'transition-state theory'\, the reaction coordinate is that coordinate in a set of curvilinear coordinates obtained from the conventional ones for the reactants which\, for each reaction step\, leads smoothly from the configuration of the reactants through that of the transition state to the configuration of the products. The reaction coordinate is typically chosen to follow the path along the gradient \(path of shallowest ascent/deepest descent\) of potential energy from reactants to products. The term has also been used interchangeably with the term transition coordinate\, applicable to the coordinate in the immediate vicinity of the potential energy maximum. Being more specific\, the name transition coordinate is to be preferred in that context."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05169
name: reaction cross-section
def: "A quantity used in collision theories of reactions in order to interpret calculated or experimental rates. In some collision theories the reaction cross-section is considered to be related to \\\(b_\{\\max \}\\\)\, the maximum value of the impact parameter that allows reaction to occur\, by\: \\\[\\sigma _\{\\rm\{r\}\}=P_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\ \\pi \\ b_\{\\max \}^\{2\}\\\] where \\\(P_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) is the reaction probability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05170
name: reaction dynamics
def: "A branch of chemical kinetics that is concerned with the intermolecular and intramolecular motions that occur in the elementary act of chemical change\, and with the details of the relationships between the quantum states of the reactant molecules and those of the product molecules. It is also known as molecular dynamics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05174
name: reaction path
def: "A synonym for mechanism.A trajectory on the potential-energy surface.A sequence of synthetic steps."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05175
name: reaction path degeneracy
def: "A factor that is introduced into rate theory to take account of the fact that the process may be able to occur in different but equivalent ways. Thus the process\:  has a reaction path degeneracy of 2 since the chlorine atom can abstract either of the two hydrogen atoms in the hydrogen molecule. The reaction path degeneracy has also been referred to as the statistical factor for the reaction."
synonym: "statistical factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05176
name: reaction probability
def: "The probability that a reaction occurs when two particles undergo a collision. If \\\(N_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) is the number of collisions occurring in unit time\, and \\\(N_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) the number of reactions occurring in unit time\: \\\(P_\{\\rm\{r\}\}=\\frac\{N_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}\{N_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05177
name: reaction stage
def: "A set of one or more \(possibly experimentally inseparable\) reaction steps leading to and/or from a detectable or presumed reaction intermediate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05178
name: reaction step
def: "An elementary reaction\, constituting one of the stages of a stepwise reaction in which a reaction intermediate \(or\, for the first step\, the reactants\) is converted into the next reaction intermediate \(or\, for the last step\, the products\) in the sequence of intermediates between reactants and products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05179
name: reaction time
def: "The period of time that elapses between the start of the reaction and the attainment of a given extent of a reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05180
name: reactive
def: "As applied to a chemical species\, the term expresses a kinetic property. A species is said to be more reactive or to have a higher reactivity in some given context than some other \(reference\) species if it has a larger rate constant for a specified elementary reaction. The term has meaning only by reference to some explicitly stated or implicitly assumed set of conditions. It is not to be used for reactions or reaction patterns of compounds in general. The term is also more loosely used as a phenomenological description not restricted to elementary reactions. When applied in this sense the property under consideration may reflect not only rate\, but also equilibrium\, constants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05181
name: reactive adsorption
def: "Reactive adsorption \(and its reverse\, reactive desorption\) resembles dissociative adsorption \(and its reverse\, dissociative adsorption\) but one fragment adds to an adsorbate rather than to a surface site."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05182
name: reactive complex
def: "A species of very short life that occurs as an intermediate in a chemical reaction. An activated complex \(or transition state\) is a special case."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05184
name: reactive scattering
def: "If in a molecular-beam experiment a chemical reaction occurs there is said to be reactive scattering."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05185
name: reactivity index
def: "Any numerical index derived from quantum mechanical model calculations that permits the prediction of relative reactivities of different molecular sites. Many indices are in use\, based on a variety of theories and relating to various types of reaction. The more successful applications have been to the substitution reactions of conjugated systems where relative reactivities are determined largely by changes of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05186
name: reactivity–selectivity principle
def: "This idea may be expressed loosely as\: the more reactive a reagent is\, the less selective it is. Consider two substrates \\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{2\}\\\) undergoing the same type of reaction with two reagents \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{2\}\\\) being more reactive than \\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{1\}\\\)\, and \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\) more reactive than \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) in the given type of reaction. The relative reactivities \(in log units\) for the four possible reactions may notionally be represented as shown in the diagram.  With the positions of \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{1\}+\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\)\)\, \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{2\}+\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\)\) and \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{1\}+\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\)\) fixed\, there are three types of positions for \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}^\{2\}+\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\)\). In position \(i\) the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\) for the two substrates\, measured by \\\(a\\\)\, is the same as the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) for the two substrates\, also \\\(a\\\). In position \(ii\) the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\) for the two substrates\, measured by \\\(b\\\)\, is less than the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) for the two substrates\, i.e. \\\(b < a\\\). It is this situation which is in accord with the RSP. In position \(iii\) the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{2\}\\\) for the two substrates\, measured by \\\(c\\\)\, is greater than the selectivity of \\\(\\rm\{R\}^\{1\}\\\) for the two substrates\, i.e. \\\(c > a\\\). This situation may be described as anti-RSP. There are many examples in which the RSP is followed\, but there are also many examples corresponding to situations \(i\) and \(iii\). The RSP is in accord with intuitive feeling and certainly holds in the limiting case when reactivity is controlled by diffusion. However\, the validity of the RSP is a matter of great controversy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05188
name: readability
def: "The smallest fraction of a division to which the index scale can be read with ease either by estimation or by the use of a vernier. It should normally be expressed in divisions of the pointer scale."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05189
name: reading
def: "Datum provided by an instrument."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05192
name: real potential of a species in a phase
def: "Defined for species $\\ce\{B\}$ in phase $\\upbeta\\!$ as \\\[\\alpha _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\beta \} = \\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\beta \}\\\,-\\\,z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ F\\ \\psi ^\{\\beta \}\\\] where \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\beta \}\\\) is the electrochemical potential of species $\\ce\{B\}$ in phase $\\upbeta\\!$\, \\\(z_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the charge number of species $\\ce\{B\}$\, \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant\, and \\\(\\psi ^\{\\beta \}\\\) is the outer electric potential of phase $\\upbeta\\!$. Since \\\(\\psi ^\{\\beta \}\\\) is zero when the charge on the phase $\\upbeta\\!$ is zero\, the real potential may be regarded as the value of the electrochemical potential of the uncharged phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05195
name: rearrangement ion
def: "An electrically charged species\, involving a molecular or parent ion\, in which atoms or groups of atoms have transferred from one portion of a molecule or molecular moiety to another during the ionization fragmentation processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05196
name: rearrangement stage
def: "The elementary reaction or reaction stage \(of a molecular rearrangement\) in which there is both making and breaking of bonds between atoms common to a reactant and a reaction product or a reaction intermediate. If the rearrangement stage consists of a single elementary reaction\, this is a 'rearrangement step'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05197
name: rebound reaction
def: "If in a chemical process\, occurring in a molecular beam\, the reaction products are scattered backwards with respect to the moving centre of mass of the system\, the reaction is called a rebound reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05199
name: recognition site
def: "A nucleotide sequence to which a protein binds specifically.An amino acid sequence in an antibody molecule to which the specific antigen binds specifically."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05200
name: recoil
def: "The motion acquired by a particle through a collision with\, or the emission of\, another particle or electromagnetic radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05201
name: recoil-free fraction
def: "The fraction of all Mössbauer gamma rays of the transition which are emitted or absorbed without significant recoil energy loss."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05202
name: recoil labelling
def: "Labelling by a chemical reaction initiated by recoil."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05205
name: reconstructive transition
def: "A transition which involves a major reorganization of the crystal structure and a change of local topology\, during which primary bonds are broken and reformed so that there is no immediate relationship between the crystal structures of the parent and product phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05206
name: recovery
def: "In toxicology\, the process leading to partial or complete restoration of a cell\, tissue\, organ or organism following its damage from exposure to a harmful substance or agent.Term used in analytical and preparative chemistry to denote the fraction of the total quantity of a substance recoverable following a chemical procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05208
name: recrystallization
def: "\(chemical\)\: The repeated dissolution and precipitation of a solid from a liquid solvent.\(metallurgical\)\: The process in which a crystalline solid with a high degree of disorder develops a new microstructure by the nucleation of relatively imperfection-free regions\, and their propagation throughout the complete solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05209
name: redox ion exchangers
def: "Conventional ion exchangers in which reversible redox couples have been introduced as counter-ions either by sorption or complex formation. They closely resemble redox polymers in their behaviour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05210
name: redox polymer
def: "Polymer containing groups that can be reversibly reduced or oxidized."
synonym: "electron-exchange polymer" []
synonym: "oxidation-reduction polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05211
name: red shift
def: "Informal term for bathochromic shift."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05212
name: reduced adsorption
def: "Of component \\\(i\\\)\, defined by the equation \\\[\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\(n\)\} = \\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}- \\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}\\ \\frac\{c_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \} - c_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}- c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\] where \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma \}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}\\\)\, \\\(c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\}\}\\\) are\, respectively\, the total Gibbs surface concentration and the total concentrations in the bulk phases $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\: \\\[\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}\\mathit\{\\Gamma \}_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}\\\] \\\[c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}c_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\] \\\[c^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}c_\{i\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\}\}\\\] The reduced adsorption is invariant to the location of the Gibbs surface. Alternatively\, the reduced adsorption may be regarded as the Gibbs surface concentration of \\\(i\\\) when the Gibbs surface is chosen so that \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma \}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \}\\\) is zero\, i.e. the Gibbs surface is chosen so that the reference system has not only the same volume\, but also contains the same total amount of substance\(\\\(n\\\)\) as the real system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05214
name: reduced mass
def: "Effective mass in equations of motion for a many particle system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05215
name: reduced mobile phase velocity
def: "A term used mainly in liquid chromatography. It compares the mobile phase velocity with the velocity of diffusion into the pores of the particles \(the so-called diffusion velocity\, \\\(u_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\[\nu=\\frac\{\\overline\{u\}\}\{u_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\}=\\frac\{\\overline\{u\}\\ d_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\}\{D_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}\\\] In open-tubular chromatography\: \\\[\nu=\\frac\{\\overline\{u\}\\ d_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\}\{D_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05216
name: reduced osmotic pressure
def: "The osmotic pressure divided by mass concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05217
name: reduced sample
def: "A representative part of the primary \(composited or gross\) sample obtained by a division and reduction process. Typically the mass approximates that of the final laboratory sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05219
name: reduced species
def: "A term used to characterize the degree of reduction \(or oxidation\) in atoms\, molecules and ions. An atom in a molecule or an ion which has a low oxidation state. An element or atom in a compound can be reduced by the reaction of an element or compound with hydrogen\, while it can be oxidized by reaction with oxygen. A reduced species can be formed also through the gain of electrons \(either at the negative electrode in a cell or through transfer from another atom\, ion or group of atoms in a chemical reaction\). For example\, the carbon atom in $\\ce\{CH4\}$ and other hydrocarbons is in a reduced state\, while the carbon in $\\ce\{CO2\}$ is in an oxidized state. Similarly the sulfur atom in $\\ce\{H2S\}$ is in a reduced state while that in sulfuric acid \($\\ce\{H2SO4\}$\) is in an oxidized state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05220
name: reduced viscosity
def: "The ratio of the relative viscosity increment to the mass concentration of the polymer\, \\\(c\\\)\, i.e. \\\(\\frac\{\\eta _\{i\}\}\{c\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\eta _\{i\}\\\) is the relative viscosity increment."
synonym: "viscosity number" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05221
name: reducing
def: "Decreasing the size of the laboratory sample or individual particles\, or both. Division of the size of the laboratory sample may be accomplished manually by coning and quartering or by riffling or mechanically by rotary dividers. Reduction of particle size may be accomplished by milling or grinding. Simultaneous division and reduction may also be achieved with mills having stream diverters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05222
name: reduction
def: "The complete transfer of one or more electrons to a molecular entity \(also called 'electronation'\)\, and\, more generally\, the reverse of the processes described under oxidation \(2\) and \(3\)."
synonym: "electronation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05223
name: reductive elimination
def: "The reverse of oxidative addition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05224
name: reductones
def: "Compounds containing an enediol structure stabilized by conjugation and hydrogen bonding with an adjacent carbonyl group\, $\\ce\{RC\(OH\)=C\(OH\)C\(=O\)R\}$. They are strong reducing agents\, fairly strong acids and commonly derived from saccharides by oxidation at the carbon atom alpha to the carbonyl function\, e.g. ascorbic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05225
name: re-extraction
def: "Since the prefix 're' can signify 'back' as well as 'again' this term is ambiguous and should be avoided\, except where the process of extraction \(e.g. from aqueous solution to an organic phase\) in a single direction is repeated \(following stripping\). It should not be used as a synonym for stripping or back-extraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05227
name: reference atom
def: "The atom to which a primitive change is nucleophilic\, electrophilic\, nucleofugal or homolytic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05229
name: reference electrode
def: "An external electrode system which comprises an inner element\, usually mercury–mercury\(I\) chloride \(calomel\)\, silver–silver chloride or thallium amalgam–thallium\(I\) chloride\, a chamber containing the appropriate filling solution and a device for forming a liquid junction\, e.g. capillary\, ceramic plug\, fritted disc or ground glass sleeve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05230
name: reference material
def: "A substance or mixture of substances\, the composition of which is known within specified limits\, and one or more of the properties of which is sufficiently well established to be used for the calibration of an apparatus\, the assessment of a measuring method or for assigning values to materials. Reference materials are available from national laboratories in many countries \[e.g. National Institute for Standards and Technology \(NIST\)\, USA.; Community Bureau of Reference\, UK\]."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05231
name: reference method
def: "A method having small\, estimated inaccuracies relative to the end use requirement. The accuracy of a reference method must be demonstrated through direct comparison with a definitive method or with a primary reference material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05232
name: reference procedure
def: "Agreed method for determining one or more air quality characteristics where it is not practical to produce a reference material; the result obtained is defined as the measure of the air quality characteristic. For example\, a gas such as $\\ce\{NO2\}$ may be somewhat unstable when stored in a tank at low mixing ratios\, so that the use of a more stable $\\ce\{NO\}$ standard mixture in $\\ce\{N2\}$ may be oxidized to $\\ce\{NO2\}$ by $\\ce\{O3\}$ in a specified manner referred to as the $\\ce\{NO2\}$ reference procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05233
name: reference state
def: "The state in which the element is stable at a chosen standard-state pressure and for a given temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05234
name: reference value pH standard
def: "An aqueous solution of \\\(\\pu\{0.05 mol\}\\\) potassium hydrogen phthalate per kg water which is the reference value for the definition of pH\(RVS\) values in the temperature range \\\(\\pu\{0\\!\{-\}\\! 95 ^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05235
name: reflectance
def: "Fraction of incident radiation reflected by a surface or discontinuity\, \\\(\\rho\(\\lambda\) = \\frac\{P_\{\\lambda\} ^\{\\rm\{refl\}\}\}\{P_\{\\lambda\} ^\{\\rm\{0\}\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(P_\{\\lambda\}^\{\\rm\{0\}\}\\\) and \\\(P_\{\\lambda\}^\{\\rm\{refl\}\}\\\) are\, respectively\, the incident and reflected spectral radiant power."
synonym: "reflectance factor" []
synonym: "reflectivity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05236
name: reflection electron energy loss spectroscopy
def: "Any technique in which a specimen is bombarded with a focused low energy \(\\\(E_\{0\} < 10\\ \\rm\{eV\}\\\)\) electron beam and the energy distribution of the reflected electrons is measured. This energy distribution contains features corresponding to discrete losses of energy of the reflected electrons due to excitation of vibrational and plasmon states and provides information on the type and geometric structure of compounds at the surface of the specimen."
synonym: "high resolution energy loss spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05238
name: reflection high energy electron diffraction
def: "Any technique which measures the angular intensity distribution of electrons 'reflected' from a crystalline surface under bombardment with high energy electrons near grazing incidence. The diffraction pattern provides very surface sensitive information \(information depth \\\(1\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\) on the atomic arrangement of the top layers of a solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05239
name: refraction effects
def: "The luminescence flux emitted from the interior of a rectangular sample reaching a photodetector place at some distance from the sample is decreased by a factor of approximately \\\(n^\{2\}\\\) \(where \\\(n\\\) is the refractive index of the medium\) compared with a medium whose refractive index is 1.0. Such effects are termed refraction effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05240
name: refractive index
def: "The ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to that in a given medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05241
name: refractive index increment
def: "The change of the solution refractive index\, \\\(n\\\)\, with solute concentration\, \\\(C\\\)\, i.e. \\\(\\frac\{\\partial n\}\{\\partial C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05242
name: regeneration
def: "Reversal of catalytic deactivation with restoration of the original catalytic activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05243
name: regioselectivity
def: "A regioselective reaction is one in which one direction of bond making or breaking occurs preferentially over all other possible directions. Reactions are termed completely \(100%\) regioselective if the discrimination is complete\, or partially \(x%\)\, if the product of reaction at one site predominates over the product of reaction at other sites. The discrimination may also semi-quantitatively be referred to as high or low regioselectivity. \(Originally the term was restricted to addition reactions of unsymmetrical reagents to unsymmetrical alkenes.\) In the past\, the term 'regiospecificity' was proposed for 100% regioselectivity. This terminology is not recommended owing to inconsistency with the terms stereoselectivity and stereospecificity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05244
name: regular block
def: "A block that can be described by only one species of constitutional repeating unit in a single sequential arrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05245
name: regular coke
def: "A petroleum coke with good graphitizability\, characterized by a combination of properties which differ significantly from those of metallurgical coke but do not reach the quality level of premium coke. These properties are\: optical anisotropy\, medium reversible thermal expansion and low ash content. Regular coke is mainly used for the production of synthetic carbon and graphite materials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05247
name: regular macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule the structure of which essentially comprises the repetition of a single constitutional unit with all units connected identically with respect to directional sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05248
name: regular oligomer molecule
def: "An oligomer molecule the structure of which essentially comprises the repetition of a single constitutional unit with all units connected identically with respect to directional sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05249
name: regular polymer
def: "A polymer composed of regular macromolecules\, regular star macromolecules or regular comb macromolecules. A polymer consisting of star macromolecules with arms identical with respect to constitution and degree of polymerization is considered to be regular. Analogously\, a polymer consisting of comb macromolecules with the sub-chains between the branch points of the main chain and the terminal sub-chains of the main chain identical with respect to constitution and degree of polymerization and the side-chains identical with respect to constitution and degree of polymerization is considered to be regular."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05250
name: regular single-strand polymer
def: "A regular linear polymer that can be described by a preferred constitutional repeating unit in which both terminal constituent subunits are connected through single atoms to the other identical constitutional repeating units or to an end group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05252
name: regulator gene
def: "A gene which codes for a protein \(an activator of repressor\) having the ability to induce or repress the transcription of other genes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05253
name: Reissert compounds
def: "Compounds formed by formal addition of an acyl group and a cyano group to a nitrogen atom and carbon atom\, respectively\, of a nitrogen–carbon bond in quinolines\, isoquinolines\, and related nitrogen heterocycles\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05255
name: relative
def: "Quotient of quantities having the same kind-of-quantity in different systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05257
name: relative adsorption
def: "If \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\sigma\}\\\) and \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{1\}^\{\\sigma\}\\\) are the Gibbs surface concentrations of components i and 1\, respectively\, with reference to the same\, but arbitrarily chosen\, Gibbs surface\, then the relative adsorption of component i with respect to component 1\, is defined as \\\[\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(1\\right\)\} = \\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\sigma\}- \\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{1\}^\{\\sigma\}\\ \\frac\{c_\{i\}^\{\\alpha\}- c_\{i\}^\{\\beta\}\}\{c_\{1\}^\{\\alpha\}- c_\{1\}^\{\\beta\}\}\\\] and is invariant to the location of the Gibbs surface. Alternatively\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(1\\right\)\}\\\) may be regarded as the Gibbs surface concentration of \\\(i\\\) when the Gibbs surface is chosen so that \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\sigma\}\\\) is zero\, i.e. the Gibbs surface is chosen so that the reference system contains the same amount of component 1 as the real system. Hence \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{1\}^\{\\left\(1\\right\)\}\\equiv 0\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05258
name: relative atomic mass
def: "The ratio of the average mass of the atom to the unified atomic mass unit."
synonym: "atomic weight" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05259
name: relative biological effectiveness
def: "The ratio of the appropriate value of the biological effectiveness of the radiation in question to that of X-radiation with an average specific ionization of 100 ion pairs per micron of water\, for the particular biological effect under consideration and for the condition under which the radiation is received."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05260
name: relative configuration
def: "The configuration of any stereogenic \(asymmetric\) centre with respect to any other stereogenic centre contained within the same molecular entity. Unlike absolute configuration\, relative configuration is reflection-invariant. Relative configuration\, distinguishing diastereoisomers\, may be denoted by the configurational descriptors \\\(R^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\)\, \\\(R^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\) \(or l\) and \\\(R^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\)\, \\\(S^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\) \(or u\) meaning\, respectively\, that the two centres have identical or opposite configurations. For molecules with more than two asymmetric centres the prefix rel- may be used in front of the name of one enantiomer where R and S have been used. If any centres have known absolute configuration then only \\\(R^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\) and \\\(S^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\) can be used for the relative configuration.See also\: α \(alpha\)\, β \(beta\) \(Defs. 1 and 3\)Two different molecules Xabcd and Xabce\, may be said to have the same relative configurations if e takes the position of d in the tetrahedral arrangement of ligands around X \(i.e. the pyramidal fragments Xabc are superposable\). By the same token the enantiomer of Xabce may be said to have the opposite relative configuration to Xabcd. The terms may be applied to chiral molecular entities with central atoms other than carbon but are limited to cases where the two related molecules differ in a single ligand.Both definitions can be generalized to include stereogenic units other than asymmetric centres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05261
name: relative counting
def: "A measurement in which the activity of a test portion is derived from the ratio between the count rates observed for the test portion and for a radioactive source of known activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05262
name: relative density
def: "Ratio of density to a reference density\, usually the density of water at \\\(\\pu\{4^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\); in the older literature this is called specific gravity."
synonym: "specific gravity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05263
name: relative detection limit
def: "\(Often incorrectly referred to as sensitivity\). Smallest amount of material detectable \($3\\upsigma\\!$-criterion\) in a matrix relative to the amount of material analysed — given in atomic\, mole or weight fractions."
synonym: "detection limit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05264
name: relative electrode potential
def: "The electrode potential of a single electrode is not amenable to direct experimental determination and can only be measured against a reference system. The measured quantity\, \\\(E\\\)\, is the relative electrode potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05266
name: relative error
def: "Error of measurement divided by the true value of the measurand. Since a true value cannot be determined\, in practice the conventional true value is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05267
name: relative humidity
def: "The ratio\, often expressed as a percentage\, of the partial pressure of water in the atmosphere at some observed temperature\, to the saturation vapour pressure of pure water at this temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05269
name: relative micellar mass
def: "The relative molecular mass \(\\\(M_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\)\) of a micelle is called the relative micellar mass or micellar weight and is defined as the mass of a mole of micelles divided by the mass of \\\(\\frac\{1\}\{12\}\\\) mole of $\\ce\{^\{12\}C\}$."
synonym: "micellar weight" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05270
name: relative molar mass
def: "Molar mass divided by \\\(\\pu\{1 g mol-1\}\\\) \(the latter is sometimes called the standard molar mass\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05271
name: relative molecular mass
def: "Ratio of the mass of a molecule to the unified atomic mass unit. Sometimes called the 'molecular weight' or relative molar mass."
synonym: "molecular weight" []
synonym: "relative molar mass" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05272
name: relative permeability
def: "Ratio of the magnetic flux density in a given medium to that in vacuum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05273
name: relative permittivity
def: "Ratio of the electric field strength in vacuum to that in a given medium. It was formerly called the dielectric constant."
synonym: "dielectric constant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05274
name: relative preconcentration
def: "An operation \(process\) as a result of which the ratio of concentration or amounts of microcomponents and main macrocomponents increases. This is a particular example of a separation\, but at greatly differing concentrations of components. The ratio of the masses of the initial and final samples is not of great importance. Frequently\, the main purpose of relative preconcentration is the replacement of a matrix unsuitable for analysis by a suitable medium. In some cases\, it is difficult to trace a boundary between the absolute and the relative preconcentrations as\, for example\, in preconcentration by zone melting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05276
name: relative retardation
def: "A term which is equivalent to relative retention used in column chromatography\: the ratio of the \\\(R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\) value of a component to the \\\(R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\) value of a standard \(reference\) substance. Since the mobile phase front is common for the two components\, the \\\(R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\) value can be expressed directly as the ratio of the distances travelled by the spot of the compound of interest \(\\\(b_\{i\}\\\)\) and the reference substance \(\\\(b_\{\\rm\{st\}\}\\\)\) respectively\: \\\[R_\{\\rm\{rel\}\}=\\frac\{R_\{\\rm\{F\}\\left\(i\\right\)\}\}\{R_\{\\rm\{F\}\\left\(\\rm\{st\}\\right\)\}\}=\\frac\{b_\{i\}\}\{b_\{\\rm\{st\}\}\}\\\] In former nomenclatures the symbol \\\(R_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) was used to express relative retardation in planar chromatography. Because of its identity with the symbol for peak resolution the symbol \\\(R_\{\\rm\{rel\}\}\\\) is suggested for relative retardation in planar chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05277
name: relative retention
def: "The ratio of the adjusted or net retention volume \(time\) or retention factor of a component relative to that of a standard\, obtained under identical conditions\: \\\[r=\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}i\}^\{'\}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\\left\(\\rm\{st\}\\right\)\}^\{'\}\}=\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{N\}i\}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{N\}\\left\(\\rm\{st\}\\right\)\}\}=\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}i\}^\{'\}\}\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\\left\(\\rm\{st\}\\right\)\}^\{'\}\}=\\frac\{k_\{i\}\}\{k_\{\\rm\{st\}\}\}\\\] Depending on the relative position of the peak corresponding to the standard compound in the chromatogram\, the value of \\\(r\\\) may be smaller\, larger or identical to unity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05280
name: relative standard deviation
def: "The standard deviation divided by the mean of the series. It can be calculated with the formula\: \\\[s_\{\\rm\{r\}\}=\\frac\{s\}\{\\overline\{x\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05281
name: relative uncertainty
def: "Uncertainty divided by the mean value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05282
name: relative viscosity
def: "The ratio of the viscosity of the solution\, \\\(\\eta \\\)\, to the viscosity of the solvent\, \\\(\\eta _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, i.e. \\\(\\eta _\{\\rm\{r\}\} = \\frac\{\\eta\}\{\\eta _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\\\). Synonymous with viscosity ratio."
synonym: "viscosity ratio" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05283
name: relative viscosity increment
def: "The ratio of the difference between the viscosities of solution and solvent to the viscosity of the solvent\, i.e. \\\(\\eta _\{i\} = \\frac\{\\eta - \\eta _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\{\\eta _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\eta \\\) is the viscosity of the solution and \\\(\\eta _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the viscosity of the solvent. The use of the term 'specific viscosity' for this quantity is discouraged\, since the relative viscosity increment does not have the attributes of a specific quantity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05284
name: relative volumic mass
def: "Synonymous with relative density."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05285
name: relaxation
def: "If a system is disturbed from its state of equilibrium it relaxes to that state\, and the process is referred to as relaxation. The branch of kinetics concerned with such processes is known as 'relaxation kinetics'. Relaxation techniques include temperature jump and 'pressure jump'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05287
name: relaxation time
def: "In magnetic resonance spectroscopy the longitudinal relaxation time\, \\\(T_\{1\}\\\)\, is associated with spin-lattice relaxation\, and the transverse relaxation time\, \\\(T_\{2\}\\\)\, with spin-spin relaxation. The definitions are\: \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}M_\{z\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\} = -\\frac\{M_\{z\} - M_\{z\,\\rm\{e\}\}\}\{T_\{1\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}M_\{x\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\} = -\\frac\{M_\{x\}\}\{T_\{2\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(M_\{z\}\\\) and \\\(M_\{x\}\\\) are the components of magnetization parallel and perpendicular to the static field \\\(B\\\) and the subscript e denotes the equilibrium value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05288
name: releaser
def: "A substance which reduces solute-volatilization interferences by forming a compound preferentially with the interferent\, thus preventing the reaction of the analyte or interferent from entering a thermally stable compound."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05289
name: rem
def: "Non-SI unit of dose equivalent \(\\\(\\rm\{rem\} = 0.01\\ \\rm\{Sv\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05290
name: Renner–Teller effect
def: "Molecular distortion in linear molecular species with degenerate electronic states \(e.g. belonging to the \\\(C_\{\\infty\\rm\{v\}\}\\\) group\). The Renner–Teller effect is a particular case of the Jahn–Teller effect. From the point of view of the potential energy surfaces the Renner–Teller effect generates a surface touching \(see scheme below\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05291
name: reorganization
def: "The molecular process by which \(i\) amorphous or poorly ordered regions of a polymer specimen become incorporated into crystals\, or \(ii\) a change to a more stable crystal structure takes place\, or \(iii\) defects within the crystal decrease."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05292
name: reorganization energy
def: "Gibbs energy dissipated when a system that has undergone 'vertical' electron transfer \(i.e.\, electron transfer obeying the Franck–Condon principle\) relaxes to the equilibrium state for its new charge distribution. Commonly the total reorganization energy \(\\\(\\lambda\\\)\) is written as the sum of an inner contribution \(\\\(\\lambda_\{\\rm\{in\}\}\\\)\) and an outer contribution \(\\\(\\lambda_\{\\rm\{out\}\}\\\)\) attributed to nuclear reorganizations of the redox partners and their environment \(solvent\)\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05293
name: repeatability
def: "The closeness of agreement between independent results obtained with the same method on identical test material\, under the same conditions \(same operator\, same apparatus\, same laboratory and after short intervals of time\). The measure of repeatability is the standard deviation qualified with the term\: `repeatability' as repeatability standard deviation. In some contexts repeatability may be defined as the value below which the absolute difference between two single test results obtained under the above conditions\, may be expected to lie with a specified probability."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05297
name: replacement name
def: "A name in which the replacement of an atom or a group of a parent structure by another atom or group is indicated by affixes attached to or inserted into the name of the parent structure. There are two main types of replacement names.   Skeletal replacement name. A name in which the replacement of skeletal atoms and their associated hydrogen atoms is indicated by non-detachable prefixes. When carbon atoms are replaced by heteroatoms this method has been called 'a' nomenclature since the prefixes end in 'a'. Certain names in which the prefix 'thio-'\, 'seleno-' or 'telluro-' indicates replacement of a skeletal oxygen atom by a sulfur\, selenium or tellurium atom\, respectively\, are also skeletal replacement names.   Functional replacement name. A name containing prefixes or infixes which indicate the replacement of an oxygen atom or hydroxy group of a characteristic group\, functional parent or class name by other atoms or groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05298
name: replacement operation
def: "The exchange of one group of atoms or a single non-hydrogen atom for another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05299
name: replicate sample
def: "Multiple \(or two\) samples taken under comparable conditions. This selection may be accomplished by taking units adjacent in time or space. Although the replicate samples are expected to be identical\, often the only thing replicated is the act of taking the physical sample. A duplicate sample is a replicate sample consisting of two portions. The umpire sample is usually used to settle a dispute; the replicate sample is usually used to estimate sample variability."
synonym: "duplicate sample" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05300
name: replication
def: "The duplication of DNA as part of the reproductive cycle of a cell or virus. During replication the two DNA strands in the double helix separate\, and each strand then acts as a template specifying the base sequence of newly synthesized complementary strands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05301
name: report
def: "A combination of specimen information and results. The report should contain information about unequivocal identification of the source and type of material analysed and the requesting agency. It may contain such other information that is pertinent to the correct interpretation of a result \(e.g. confidence interval\, reference data and interpretative information\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05302
name: reprecipitation
def: "The planned repetition of a precipitation in order to remove chemically different species from a previous precipitate or to improve its stoichiometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05303
name: representative sample
def: "A sample resulting from a sampling plan that can be expected to adequately reflect the properties of interest of the parent population. A representative sample may be a random sample or\, for example\, a stratified sample\, depending upon the objective of sampling and the characteristics of the population. The degree of representativeness of the sample may be limited by cost or convenience."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05304
name: repression
def: "Binding of a protein \(repressor\) to the operator sequence in an operon thus preventing the transcription of the following structural gene\(s\) into mRNA and consequently synthesis of protein \(cf. enzyme repression\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05305
name: reproducibility
def: "The closeness of agreement between independent results obtained with the same method on identical test material but under different conditions \(different operators\, different apparatus\, different laboratories and/or after different intervals of time\). The measure of reproducibility is the standard deviation qualified with the term 'reproducibility' as reproducibility standard deviation. In some contexts reproducibility may be defined as the value below which the absolute difference between two single test results on identical material obtained under the above conditions\, may be expected to lie with a specified probability. Note that a complete statement of reproducibility requires specification of the experimental conditions which differ."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05306
name: repulsive potential-energy surface
def: "A surface for an exergonic reaction $\\ce\{A + BC\}$ in which the col corresponds to considerable separation between the products $\\ce\{A–B + C\}$. The energy barrier in the potential-energy profile is in the later stages of the reaction path. On such a surface most of the energy is released after $\\ce\{A–B\}$ is formed. Repulsive surfaces are also called late-downhill surfaces\, and the barrier in such a surface is called a Type-II barrier."
synonym: "late-downhill surface" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05308
name: <i>Re</i>\, <i>Si</i>
def: "A stereoheterotopic face of a trigonal atom is designated Re if the ligands of the trigonal atom appear in a clockwise sense in order of CIP priority when viewed from that side of the face. The opposite arrangement is termed Si."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05309
name: residence time
def: "\(instrumentation\)\: The time required for air or reagent parcel to pass from the entrance to the exit of an instrument. Often this is approximated as the ratio of the interior volume of the device to the flow rate.\(atmospheric\)\: The average time a molecule or aerosol spends in the atmosphere after it is released or generated there. For compounds with well defined sources and emission rates\, this is estimated by the ratio of the average global concentration of a substance to its production rate on a global scale. It is a function of not only the emission rates but the loss rates by chemical and physical removal processes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05310
name: residence time
def: "The average time a particle or volume element of the culture resides in a bioreactor \(or other device\) through which a liquid medium continously flows. residence time of the liquid is the reciprocal of dilution rate."
synonym: "hydraulic retention time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05311
name: residual current
def: "The current that flows\, at any particular value of the applied potential\, in the absence of the substance whose behaviour is being investigated \(i.e. in a blank solution\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05312
name: residual fuel/oil
def: "The liquid or semi-liquid\, high-boiling fraction of residue from the distillation of petroleum which is used as a fuel. After removal of the lower boiling fraction of crude oil\, sold as petroleum gas\, the somewhat higher boiling fraction becomes gasoline and diesel oil. A portion of the higher boiling fraction is 'cracked' to yield additional gasoline. Still heavier oils become lubricants. paraffin\, asphalt\, etc.\, are also extracted from the crude oil. It is the remaining residual oil\, not economically usable for other industrial purposes\, which is then sold as a relatively low cost fuel for burning."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05313
name: residual liquid junction error
def: "Error arising from breakdown in the assumption that the liquid junction potential remains constant when solution X is substituted for solution S in the operational cell."
synonym: "residual liquid junction potential error" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05314
name: residual spectrum/background spectrum
def: "Set of peaks recorded in the absence of a sample and due either to small air leaks or to the presence of molecules desorbed from the walls of the introduction device or the source\, or from the pump fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05315
name: resistance
def: "Electric potential difference divided by the electric current when there is no electromotive force in a conductor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05316
name: resistivity
def: "Electric field strength divided by the current density. This quantity is a tensor in an anisotropic medium."
synonym: "electric resistivity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05317
name: resolution
def: "A characteristic of the separation of two adjacent peaks. It may be expressed according to the equation\: \\\[R_\{\\rm\{AB\}\} = 2\\ \\frac\{|d_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{B\}\\right\)\\\,-\\\,d_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)|\}\{|w\\left\(\\rm\{B\}\\right\)\\\,+\\\,w\\left\(\\rm\{A\}\\right\)|\}\\\] where \\\(R_\{\\rm\{AB\}\}\\\) is the resolution\, \\\(d_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\(\\rm\{A\}\)\\\) and \\\(d_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\(\\rm\{B\}\)\\\) are the retention distances \(time or volume\) of each eluted component A and B\, and \\\(w\(\\rm\{A\}\)\\\) and \\\(w\(\\rm\{B\}\)\\\) are the respective widths of each peak at its base."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05318
name: resolution
def: "\(energy\)\: By analogy with the peak width definition for mass resolution\, a peak showing the number of ions as a function of their translational energy should be used to give a value for the energy resolution.\($\\pu\{10\\%\}$ valley definition\)\: Let two peaks of equal height in a mass spectrum at masses \\\(m\\\) and \\\(m- \\Delta m\\\) be separated by a valley which at its lowest point is just $\\pu\{10\\%\}$ of the height of either peak. For similar peaks at a mass exceeding \\\(m\\\)\, let the height of the valley at its lowest point be more \(by any amount\) than ten per cent of either peak height. Then the resolution \($\\pu\{10\\%\}$ valley definition\) is \\\(\\frac\{m\}\{\\Delta m\}\\\). It is usually a function of \\\(m\\\). The ratio \\\(\\frac\{m\}\{\\Delta m\}\\\) should be given for a number of values of \\\(m\\\).\(peak width definition\)\: For a single peak made up of singly charged ions at mass \\\(m\\\) in a mass spectrum\, the resolution may be expressed as \\\(\\frac\{m\}\{\\Delta m\}\\\) where \\\(\\Delta m\\\) is the width of the peak at a height which is a specified fraction of the maximum peak height. It is recommended that one of three values $\\pu\{50\\%\}$\, $\\pu\{5\\%\}$ or $\\pu\{0.5\\%\}$ should always be used. For an isolated symmetrical peak recorded with a system which is linear in the range between $\\pu\{5\\%\}$ and $\\pu\{10\\%\}$ levels of the peak\, the $\\pu\{5\\%\}$ peak width definition is technically equivalent to the $\\pu\{10\\%\}$ valley definition. A common standard is the definition of resolution based upon \\\(\\Delta m\\\) being Full Width of the peak at Half its Maximum height\, sometimes abbreviated 'FWHM'. This acronym should preferably be defined the first time it is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05319
name: resolution
def: "Wavenumber\, wavelength or frequency difference of two still distinguishable lines in a spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05320
name: resolution
def: "The separation of a racemate into the component enantiomers."
synonym: "optical resolution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05321
name: resolving power
def: "The ability to distinguish between ions differing in the quotient mass/charge by a small increment. It may be characterized by giving the peak width\, measured in mass units\, expressed as a function of mass\, for at least two points on the peak\, specifically at fifty percent and at five percent of the maximum peak height."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05322
name: resolving power
def: "Transition wavenumber \(or wavelength or frequency\) divided by the resolution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05323
name: resolving time
def: "The smallest time interval which must elapse between the occurrence of two consecutive ionizing events or signal pulses\, in order that the measuring device be capable of fulfilling its function of each of them separately."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05325
name: resolving time correction
def: "Correction to be applied to the observed number of pulses in order to take into account the number of pulses lost during the resolving time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05326
name: resonance
def: "In the context of chemistry\, the term refers to the representation of the electronic structure of a molecular entity in terms of contributing structures. Resonance among contributing structures means that the wavefunction is represented by 'mixing' the wavefunctions of the contributing structures. The concept is the basis of the quantum mechanical valence bond methods. The resulting stabilization is linked to the quantum mechanical concept of 'resonance energy'. The term resonance is also used to refer to the delocalization phenomenon itself."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05327
name: resonance absorption technique
def: "The monitoring of atoms or radicals generated in the gas phase by observing the attenuation of the radiation from a lamp emitting the characteristic resonance radiation of the observed species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05329
name: resonance cross-section
def: "The cross-section for resonant absorption of the Mössbauer transition $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{ray\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05331
name: resonance effect magnitude
def: "Ratio of the difference in the transmitted or scattered intensity at resonance maximum and off-resonance\, relative to the intensity off-resonance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05333
name: resonance energy
def: "The difference in potential energy between the actual molecular entity and the contributing structure of lowest potential energy. The resonance energy cannot be measured\, but only estimated\, since contributing structures are not observable molecular entities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05334
name: resonance energy
def: "The energy of a particle entering a nuclear reaction\, this energy being just sufficiently high to lead to the formation of reaction products in one of their excited states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05335
name: resonance fluorescence
def: "Fluorescence from the primary excited atomic or molecular species at the wavelength of the exciting radiation \(no relaxation within the excited manifold\). This term is also used to designate the radiation emitted by an atom of the same wavelength as the longest one capable of exciting its fluorescence\, e.g. \\\(122.6\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) in the case of the hydrogen atom\, and \\\(253.7\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) in the case of the mercury atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05336
name: resonance fluorescence technique
def: "The monitoring of atoms or radicals generated in the gas phase by observing the intensity of fluorescence \(exitance\) emitted by the species after excitation with radiation of the same wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05337
name: resonance integral
def: "In Hückel molecular orbital theory\, the integral over space of the type \\\(\\int \\psi _\{r\}^\{*\}\\ \\hat\{H\}\\ \\psi _\{s\}  \\ \\rm\{d\}\\tau \\\) where \\\(\\hat\{H\}\\\) is the hamiltonian operator and \\\(\\psi _\{r\}\\\) and \\\(\\psi _\{s\}\\\) are different wavefunctions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05338
name: resonance integral
def: "The integral\, over all or some specified portion of the resonance energy range\, of the cross-section divided by the energy of a radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05339
name: resonance lamp
def: "Lamp emitting resonance radiation of atoms and their ions. Depending on the requirements\, the lamp is filled either with pure vapour of the element or with a mixture of it and other gases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05340
name: resonance line
def: "The longest wavelength capable of exciting fluorescence in an atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05341
name: resonance line
def: "The radiative decay of an excitation level may proceed to the neutral ground state and would thus occur at the same energy as the corresponding line in the absorption spectrum. Such a line is called a resonance line and the process is called resonance emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05342
name: resonance neutrons
def: "Neutrons whose energy corresponds to the resonance energy of a specified nuclide or element. If the nuclide is not specified\, the term refers to resonance neutrons of $\\ce\{^\{239\}U\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05343
name: resonance radiation
def: "Synonymous with resonance fluorescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05344
name: response constant
def: "A quantity whose expression includes a current\, whose value is characteristic of a charge-transfer process and the experimental conditions under which it is investigated\, and whose nature depends on the technique that is employed. Some typical response constants are the diffusion current constant in polarography\, the voltammetric constant in linear-sweep voltammetry\, and the chronopotentiometric constant in chronopotentiometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05345
name: response time
def: "The time required for the detector output to go from the initial value to a percentage \(e.g. 99%\) of the final value. In the case of an exponential behaviour of the detector \\\(\\tau _\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\) can be related to the time constant \\\(\\tau _\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\). The rise time \\\(\\tau _\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) is the time required for the detector output to vary between given percentages \(e.g. from 10% to 90%\) of the final value. Similarly\, the fall time \\\(\\tau _\{\\rm\{f\}\}\\\) is the time required for the detector output to vary between given percentages \(e.g. from 90% to 10%\) of the initial value."
synonym: "rise time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05346
name: response time
def: "Time which elapses\, when there is a stepwise change in the quantity to be measured\, between the moment when this change is produced and the moment when the indication reaches a value conventionally fixed at 90% of the final change in indication."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05347
name: responsivity
def: "Detector input can be e.g. radiant power\, irradiation\, radiant energy. It produces a measurable detector output which may be e.g. an electrical charge\, an electrical current or potential or a change in pressure. The ratio of the detector output and the detector input is defined as the responsivity. It is given in e.g. ampere/watt\, volt/watt. The responsivity is a special case of the general term sensitivity. Dark current is the term for the electrical output of a detector in the absence of input. This is a special case of the general term dark output. For photoconductive detectors the term dark resistance is used. If the responsivity is normalized with regard to that obtained from a reference radiation the resulting ratio is called relative responsivity. For measurements with monochromatic radiation at a given wavelength the term spectral responsivity \\\(R\(\\lambda\)\\\) is used. In some cases the 'relative spectral responsivity'\, where the spectral responsivity is normalized with respect to the responsivity at some given wavelength\, is used. The dependence of the spectral responsivity on the wavelength is described by the spectral responsivity function. The useful spectral range of the detector should be given as the wavelength range where the relative responsivity does not fall below a specified value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05348
name: rest point
def: "The position of the pointer with respect to the pointer scale when the motion of the beam has ceased."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05350
name: restriction enzymes
def: "Endonucleases which recognize specific base sequences within a DNA helix\, creating a double-strand break of DNA. Type I restriction enzymes bind to these recognition sites but subsequently cut the DNA at different sites. Type II restriction enzymes both bind and cut within their recognition or target sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05351
name: result
def: "The final value reported for a measured or computed quantity\, after performing a measuring procedure including all subprocedures and evaluations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05352
name: retardation factor
def: "The fraction of the sample component in the mobile phase at equilibrium; it is related to the retention factor and other fundamental chromatography terms\: \\\[R = \\frac\{1\}\{k\\\,+\\\,1\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05353
name: retardation factor
def: "Ratio of the distance travelled by the centre of the spot to the distance simultaneously travelled by the mobile phase\: \\\[R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}=\\frac\{b\}\{a\}\\\] By definition the \\\(R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\) values are always less than unity. They are usually given to two decimal places. In order to simplify this presentation the \\\(hR_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\) values may be used\: they correspond to the \\\(R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\) values multiplied by 100. Ideally\, \\\(R_\{\\rm\{F\}\}\\\) values are identical to the \\\(R\\\) values used in column chromatography."
synonym: "RF value" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05355
name: retarder
def: "This term\, used mainly in polymerization kinetics\, refers to a substance that reduces the rate of reaction. Synonymous with inhibitor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05357
name: retention
def: "Of atoms undergoing a nuclear transformation\, that fraction which remains in or reverts to its initial chemical form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05358
name: retention efficiency
def: "The ratio of the quantity of particles retained by a separator to the quantity entering it \(generally expressed as a percentage\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05359
name: retention factor
def: "A measure of the time the sample component resides in the stationary phase relative to the time it resides in the mobile phase; it expresses how much longer a sample component is retarded by the stationary phase than it would take to travel through the column with the velocity of the mobile phase. Mathematically\, it is the ratio of the adjusted retention volume \(time\) and the hold-up volume \(time\)\: \\\[k=\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{'\}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}=\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{'\}\}\{t_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}\\\] If the distribution constant is independent of sample component concentration\, then the retention factor is also equal to the ratio of the amounts of a sample component in the stationary and mobile phases respectively\, at equilibrium\: \\\[k=\\frac\{\\rm\{amount of component in stationary phase\}\}\{\\rm\{amount of component in mobile phase\}\}\\\] If the fraction of the sample component in the mobile phase is \\\(R\\\)\, then the fraction in the stationary phase is \\\(1 - R\\\); thus \\\[k=\\frac\{1- R\}\{R\}\\\] In former nomenclatures and in the literature one may find the expressions partition ratio\, capacity ratio\, capacity factor or mass distribution ratio to describe this term. In the literature the symbol \\\(k^\{'\}\\\) is often used for the retention factor\, particularly in liquid chromatography. The original reason for this was to clearly distinguish it from the partition coefficient \(distribution constant\) for which the symbol K had been utilized. Since\, however\, the distribution constants are all identified with a subscript\, there is no reason to add the prime sign to this symbol. It should be emphasized that all the recognized nomenclatures \(IUPAC\, BS\, ASTM\) have always clearly identified the capacity factor with the symbol \\\(k\\\) and not \\\(k^\{'\}\\\). The logarithm of the retention factor is equivalent to the \\\(R_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\) value used in planar chromatography. The symbol \\\(\\kappa \\\) is suggested to express \\\[\\kappa =\\log _\{10\}k=\\log _\{10\}\\left\[\\frac\{1- R\}\{R\}\\right\]\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05360
name: retention index
def: "The retention index of a sample component is a number\, obtained by interpolation \(usually logarithmic\)\, relating the adjusted retention volume \(time\) or the retention factor of the sample component to the adjusted retention volumes \(times\) of two standards eluted before and after the peak of the sample component. In the Kováts index or Kováts retention index used in gas chromatography\, n-alkanes serve as the standards and logarithmic interpolation is utilized\: \\\[I = 100\\left\[\\frac\{\\log _\{10\}X_\{i\}- \\log _\{10\}X_\{z\}\}\{\\log _\{10\}X_\{z+1\}- \\log _\{10\}X_\{z\}\}+z\\right\]\\\] where \\\(X\\\) refers to the adjusted retention volumes or times\, \\\(z\\\) is the number of carbon atoms of the n-alkane eluting before and \\\(\\left\(z+1\\right\)\\\) is the number of carbon atoms of the n-alkane eluting after the peak of interest\: \\\[V_\{\\rm\{R\}z\}^\{'\} < V_\{\\rm\{R\}i\}^\{'\} < V_\{\\rm\{R\}\(z+1\)\}\\\] The Kováts \(retention\) index expresses the number of carbon atoms \(multiplied by 100\) of a hypothetical normal alkane which would have an adjusted retention volume \(time\) identical to that of the peak of interest when analysed under identical conditions. The Kováts retention index is always measured under isothermal conditions. In the case of temperature-programmed gas chromatography a similar value can be calculated utilizing direct numbers instead of their logarithm. Since both the numerator and denominator contain the difference of two values\, here we can use the total retention volumes \(times\). Sometimes this value is called the linear retention index. \\\[I^\{\\rm\{T\}\} = 100\\left\[\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}i\}^\{\\rm\{T\}\}- t_\{\\rm\{R\}z\}^\{\\rm\{T\}\}\}\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\(z+1\)\}^\{\\rm\{T\}\}- t_\{\\rm\{R\}z\}^\{\\rm\{T\}\}\}+z\\right\]\\\] where \\\(t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\) refers to the total retention times \(chart distances\) measured under the conditions of temperature programming. The value of \\\(I^\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\) will usually differ from the value of \\\(I\\\) measured for the same compound under isothermal conditions\, using the same two phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05363
name: retention temperature
def: "The column temperature when the peak maximum for a component has been reached in temperature-programmed chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05366
name: retention volumes
def: "Retention measurements \(and measurements of hold-up volume and peak width\) may be made in terms of times or chart distances as well as volumes. If flow and recorder speeds are constant\, the volumes are directly proportional to the times and chart distances. The following definitions are drawn up in terms of volume\, and it is recommended that theoretical discussion should be couched in the same terms wherever possible. The total retention volume\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\)\, is the volume of eluent carrier gas admitted to the column between the injection of the sample and the emergence of the peak maximum of the specified component. It includes the hold-up volume. In gas chromatography\, the volume of carrier gas is specified at the outlet pressure and temperature of the column. Note\: the word 'total' in this definition allows retention time to be used as a general term when specification of a particular quantity is not required. The adjusted retention volume\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{'\}\\\)\, is the total retention volume less the hold-up volume\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\\\)\, i.e. \\\[V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{'\}=V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}- V_\{\\rm\{M\}\}=\\overline\{V\}- V_\{\\rm\{I\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\overline\{V\}\\\) is the peak elution volume and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{I\}\}\\\) the interstitial volume. The net retention volume\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{N\}\}\\\)\, is the adjusted retention volume multiplied by the pressure-gradient correction factor\: \\\[V_\{\\rm\{N\}\}=j\\ V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}^\{'\}\\\] The specific retention volume\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\)\, is the net retention volume per gram of stationary liquid\, active solid or solvent-free gel. In liquid chromatography\, except when conducted at very high pressures\, the compression of the mobile phase is negligible\, and the adjusted and net retention volumes are identical. The specific retention volume is then the adjusted retention volume per gram of stationary liquid\, active solid\, or solvent-free gel. It is recommended that\, when appropriate\, authors specify the drying conditions. At \\\(0\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, \\\[V_\{\\rm\{g\}\}=273\\ \\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{N\}\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\ T\}\\\] where \\\(w_\{\\rm\{L\}\}\\\) is the mass of the stationary liquid phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05369
name: retinoids
def: "Oxygenated derivatives of 3\,7-dimethyl-1-\(2\,6\,6-trimethylcyclohex-1-enyl\)nona-1\,3\,5\,7-tetraene as shown and derivatives thereof."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05370
name: retro
def: "A prefix which indicates a shift by one position of all single and double bonds in a conjugated polyene system. It has been applied only to carotenoids and retinoids\, e.g. retro-carotenoids\, retro-retinoids. A prefix which indicates the reverse sequence of a peptide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05374
name: reversed direct-injection burner
def: "A direct-injection burner combines the function of nebulizer and burner. Most commonly the oxidant is also used for aspirating and nebulizing the sample. However\, when the fuel is used for this purpose\, the term reversed direct-injection burner is applied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05375
name: reversed isotope dilution analysis
def: "Isotope dilution analysis used for the determination of the isotopic carrier in a solution of a radionuclide with the aid of one of its stable isotopes."
synonym: "reversed radiochemical isotope dilution analysis" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05376
name: reversed-phase chromatography
def: "An elution procedure used in liquid chromatography in which the mobile phase is significantly more polar then the stationary phase\, e.g. a microporous silica-based material with chemically bonded alkyl chains. The term 'reverse phase' is an incorrect expression to be avoided."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05378
name: reverse transcriptases
def: "Enzymes found in retroviruses that can synthesize complementary single-strands of DNA from an mRNA sequence as template. They are used in genetic engineering to produce specific cDNA molecules from purified preparations of mRNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05379
name: reversible transition
def: "A transition that returns a system directly to its original state by reversing the process that changed it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05380
name: revolutions per minute
def: "Non-SI unit for rotational frequency."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05381
name: rheology
def: "The study of the flow and deformation of matter under the influence of a mechanical force. It is concerned particularly with material behaviour which cannot be described by the simple linear models of hydrodynamics and elasticity. Some of these departures are due to the presence of colloidal particles or to the influence of surfaces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05382
name: rheopexy
def: "A phenomenon in which the time of solidification\, after discontinuation of a relatively high shear rate\, is shortened by applying a small shear rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05384
name: rhodamine dyes
def: "Dyes derived from condensation of phthalic anhydride with m-dialkylaminophenols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05385
name: rhombohedral graphite
def: "A thermodynamically unstable allotropic form of graphite with an ABCABC stacking sequence of the layers. The exact crystallographic description of this allotropic form is given by the space group \\\(D_\{3\\rm\{d\}\}^\{5\}- R\\overline\{3\}m\\\)\, \(unit cell constants\: \\\(a=256.6\\ \\mathrm\{pm\}\\\)\, \\\(c=1006.2\\ \\mathrm\{pm\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05386
name: ribonucleic acids
def: "Naturally occurring polyribonucleotides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05387
name: ribonucleotides
def: "Nucleotides in which the glycosyl group is a ribosyl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05388
name: ribosomal RNA
def: "RNA molecules which are essential structural and functional components of ribosomes\, the subcellular units responsible for protein synthesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05389
name: ribosomes
def: "Subcellular units composed of specific RNA molecules and a large number of proteins that are responsible for protein synthesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05390
name: Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel theory
def: "A theory of unimolecular gas reactions in which the rate with which the energized reactant molecule breaks down is treated as a function of the energy \\\(\\varepsilon\\\) that it contains. The theory assumes that the rate is proportional to the number of ways of distributing \\\(\\varepsilon\\\) among the internal degrees of freedom of the reactant molecule\, in such a manner that the critical energy \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\) is localized in one particular degree of freedom."
synonym: "RRK theory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05391
name: Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel–Marcus theory
def: "An improved form of Rice–Ramsperger–Kassel \(RRK\) theory in which account is taken of the way in which the various normal-mode vibrations and rotations contribute to reaction\, and allowance is made for the zero-point energies. In this theory the energy \\\(\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\) in an energized molecule is classified as either active\, \\\(\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*active\}\}\\\)\, or inactive\, \\\(\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*inactive\}\}\\\). The rate depends upon \\\(\\frac\{P\(\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*active\}\}\)\}\{N\(\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\)\}\\\)\, where \\\(N\(\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\)\\\) is the density of states having energy between \\\(\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*\}\}+\\rm\{d\}\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\)\, and \\\(P\(\\varepsilon^\{\\rm\{*active\}\}\)\\\) is the sum of the active quantum states of the activated complex. This extension of RRK theory brings it in line with transition-state theory."
synonym: "RRKM theory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05392
name: riffling
def: "The separation of a free-flowing sample into \(usually\) equal parts by means of a mechanical device composed of diverter chutes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05393
name: ring assembly
def: "Two or more cyclic systems \(single rings or fused systems\) which are directly joined to each other by double or single bonds are named ring assemblies when the number of such direct ring junctions is one less than the number of cyclic systems involved. Examples\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05394
name: Ringelmann chart
def: "A chart which has been used in air pollution evaluation for assigning the degree of blackness of smoke emanating from a source. The observer compares the shades of grey \(white to black\) with a series of shade diagrams formed by horizontal and vertical black grid lines on a white background. A corresponding number\, the Ringelmann number\, is then assigned to the describe the best match; numbers range from 0 \(white\) to 5 \(black\). This method of pollution evaluation\, although relevant to the enforcement of legislation still in force in many countries\, is not recommended for use today since more quantitative indicators of the efficiency of the combustion are now available."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05395
name: ring-opening copolymerization
def: "A copolymerization which is a ring-opening polymerization with respect to at least one monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05396
name: ring-opening polymerization
def: "A polymerization in which a cyclic monomer yields a monomeric unit which is acyclic or contains fewer cycles than the monomer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05397
name: ring reversal
def: "The interconversion by rotation about single bonds \(coupled with angle deformation in the transition state\) of cyclic conformers having equivalent ring shapes \(e.g. chair → chair\) though not necessarily equivalent spatial positions of substituents \(e.g. equatorial → axial\)."
synonym: "chair–chair interconversion" []
synonym: "ring inversion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05398
name: ring-sector
def: "A sequence of ring atoms\, linked together as an unbranched chain and connecting two bridgehead or junctions\, but not itself containing any bridgehead or junction\, is termed an atomic ring-sector. A bond linking together directly two bridgeheads or junctions is termed a bond ring-sector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05400
name: rise time
def: "The difference between the response time and the dead time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05401
name: rise velocity
def: "The vertical component of the velocity of the volatilized component in the observation space \(in \\\(\\rm\{mm s\}^\{-1\}\\\)\). It depends on the flame temperature\, the solute nebulized\, the observation height and the gas flow rate."
synonym: "vertical rise velocity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05402
name: Ritchie equation
def: "The linear free-energy relation \\\[\\log _\{10\}k_\{\\rm\{N\}\} = \\log _\{10\}k_\{0\}+N_\{\\rm\{+\}\}\\\] applied to the reactions between nucleophiles and certain large and relatively stable organic cations\, e.g. arenediazonium\, triarylmethyl and aryltropylium cations in various solvents. \\\(k_\{\\rm\{N\}\}\\\) is the rate constant for reaction of a given cation with a given nucleophilic system \(i.e. given nucleophile in a given solvent\). \\\(k_\{0\}\\\) is the rate constant for the same cation with water in water\, and \\\(N_\{\\rm\{+\}\}\\\) is a parameter which is characteristic of the nucleophilic system and independent of the cation. A surprising feature of the equation is the absence of a coefficient of \\\(N_\{\\rm\{+\}\}\\\)\, characteristic of the substrate \(cf. the \\\(s\\\) in the Swain–Scott equation\)\, even though values of \\\(N_\{\\rm\{+\}\}\\\) vary over 13 log units. The equation thus involves a gigantic breakdown of the reactivity–selectivity principle. The equation has been extended both in form and in range of application."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05404
name: röntgen
def: "Non-SI unit of exposure to X- or $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{radiation\}$\: \\\(\\rm\{R\} = \\pu\{2.58E-4 C kg\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05405
name: root-mean-square end-to-end distance
def: "The square root of the mean-square end-to-end distance of a linear polymer chain averaged over all conformations of the chain. For a freely jointed chain consisting of \\\(N\\\) segments each of length \\\(L\\\)\, it is given by\: \\\[\\rm\{\}^\{1/2\} = \\sqrt\{N\}\\ L\\\] The subscript zero is used to indicate unperturbed dimensions\, as in \\\(\\rm\{\}_\{0\}^\{1/2\}\\\). If this term is used repeatedly\, and if it is not confusing\, the abbreviated name 'end-to-end distance' may be used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05407
name: rotamer
def: "One of a set of conformers arising from restricted rotation about one single bond."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05408
name: rotational barrier
def: "In a rotation of groups about a bond\, the potential energy barrier between two adjacent minima of the molecular entity as a function of the torsion angle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05409
name: rotational constants
def: "Coefficients of quantum numbers in the rotational term expression and inversely proportional to the principal moments of inertia. Symbols\: \\\(A\\\)\, \\\(B\\\)\, \\\(C\\\). \\\(\\overset\{˜\}\{A\}=\\frac\{h\}\{8\\ \\pi ^\{2\}\\ c\\ I_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\\\) \(dimension wavenumber\)\, \\\(A=\\frac\{h\}\{8\\ \\pi ^\{2\}\\ I_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\\\) \(dimension frequency\) where \\\(h\\\) is the Planck constant and \\\(c\\\) the speed of light in vacuum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05410
name: rotational diffusion
def: "A process by which the equilibrium statistical distribution of the overall orientation of molecules or particles is maintained or restored. Rotational diffusion may be compared to translational diffusion through which the equilibrium statistical distribution of position in space is maintained or restored."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05411
name: rotational diffusion coefficient
def: "Defined by the equation\: \\\[D_\{\\theta \}=\\frac\{t_\{\\theta \}\}\{\\frac\{\\partial \(f\(\\theta \,\\mathit\{\\Phi \}\)\)\}\{\\partial \\theta \}\\ \\sin\\\,\\theta \}\\\] where \\\(f\\left\(\\theta \,\\mathit\{\\Phi \}\\right\)\\\:\\sin \\\,\\theta \\ \\rm\{d\}\\theta \\ \\rm\{d\}\\mathit\{\\Phi \}\\\) is the fraction of particles whose axes make an angle between \\\(\\theta \\\) and \\\(\\theta +\\mathrm\{d\}\\theta \\\) with the direction \\\(\\theta =0\\\)\, and have an azimuth between \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\\\) and \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} + \\rm\{d\}\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\\\); \\\(t_\{\\theta \}\\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\mathit\{\\Phi \}\\\) is the fraction of particles having an azimuth between \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\\\) and \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} + \\rm\{d\}\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\\\) whose axis passes from values \\\(\\theta \\\) in unit time. The axis whose rotational diffusion is considered has to be clearly indicated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05412
name: rotational frequency
def: "The number of rotations divided by time\: \\\[f_\{\\rm\{rot\}\}=\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}N\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\] The synonyms rate of rotation\, rate of revolution\, centrifugal speed\, centrifugation speed\, and the traditional units of rotational frequency such as revolutions per minute\, r.p.m.\, ppm\, \\\(\\rm\{rev min\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{r min\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, are not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05413
name: rotational term
def: "Rotational energy divided by the product of the Planck constant and the speed of light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05414
name: rotator phase transition
def: "A transition from a fully-ordered crystal of globular or quasi-spherical molecules to a crystal that retains translational order but exhibits dynamic orientational disorder and is usually mechanically soft \(plastic phase\). Example\: The transition of $\\ce\{CBr4\}$ to a plastic phase which exists from \\\(319\\\) to \\\(365\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05415
name: rotatory power
def: "The quantitative measure of optical activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05416
name: rotaxanes
def: "Molecules in which a ring encloses another\, rod-like molecule having end groups too large to pass through the ring opening\, and thus holds the rod-like molecule in position without covalent bonding."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05417
name: rotenoids
def: "Naturally occurring substances containing a cis-fused tetrahydrochromeno\[3\,4-b\]chromene nucleus. Many rotenoids contain an additional ring\, e.g rotenone\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05418
name: rotometer
def: "A device\, based on Stokes law\, for measuring rate of fluid flow. It is a tapered vertical tube having a circular cross-section in which a float moves in a vertical path to a height dependent upon the rate of fluid flow upward through the tube."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05419
name: roughness factor
def: "The ratio\: \\\[f_\{\\rm\{r\}\}=\\frac\{A_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\}\{A_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(A_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) is the real \(true\, actual\) surface \(interface\) area and \\\(A_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) is the geometric surface \(interface\) area."
synonym: "rugosity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05420
name: rovibronic state
def: "A state corresponding to a particular rotational sublevel of a particular vibrational level of a particular electronic state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05423
name: <i>R</i>\, <i>S</i>
def: "The approved designations \(devised by Cahn\, Ingold and Prelog\) of absolute configuration at four-coordinate \(quadriligant\) and six-coordinate \(sexiligant\) stereogenic centres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05424
name: <i>r</i>\, <i>s</i>
def: "Stereodescriptor s of pseudo-asymmetric atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05427
name: ruby laser
def: "A pulsed source of coherent radiation emitting mainly at \\\(\\pu\{694.3 nm\}\\\) from chromium ions \($\\ce\{Cr^\{3+\}\}$\) in aluminium oxide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05428
name: rupture
def: "The formation of a hole which permits coalescence or direct contact of the two phases which it separates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05429
name: Rutherford backscattering
def: "Any technique using high energy particles directed toward a sample\, in which the bombarding particles are detected and recorded as function of energy and/or angle. The technique is mostly used for determining depth distributions of elements based on the energy of the backscattered particle. In general\, $\\ce\{He^\{+\}\}$ or $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ particles are used at energies in the order of \\\(100\\ \\rm\{keV\}\\\) to some \\\(\\mathrm\{MeV\}\\\). Also referred to as backscattering spectrometry \(BSS\)."
synonym: "backscattering spectrometry (BSS)" []
synonym: "backscattering spectroscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05430
name: Rydberg constant
def: "Atomic fundamental physical constant \\\(R_\{\\infty\} = \\frac\{E_\{h\}\}\{2\\ h\\ c_\{0\}\} = 1.097\\ 373\\ 1534\\ \(13\)\\times 10^\{7\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, where \\\(E_\{h\}\\\) is the Hartree energy\, \\\(h\\\) the Planck constant\, and \\\(c_\{0\}\\\) the speed of light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05431
name: Rydberg orbital
def: "For an atom\, an orbital with principal quantum number greater than that of any occupied orbital of the ground state. For a molecular entity\, a molecular orbital which correlates with a Rydberg atomic orbital in an atomic fragment produced by dissociation. Typically\, the extension of the Rydberg orbital is large compared to the size of the atom or molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:R05432
name: Rydberg transition
def: "An electronic transition described approximately as promotion of an electron from a 'bonding' orbital to a Rydberg orbital. Spectral bands corresponding to Rydberg transitions approximately fit the Rydberg formula\: \\\[\\sigma = I - \\frac\{R\}\{\(n\\\,-\\\,\\mathit\{\\Delta \}\)^\{2\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\sigma \\\) is the wavenumber\, \\\(I\\\) the ionization potential of the atom or molecular entity\, \\\(n\\\) a principal quantum number\, \\\(R\\\) the Rydberg constant\, and \\\(\\mathit\{\\Delta\}\\\) the quantum defect which differentiates between s\, p\, d\, etc.\, orbitals. The notation used is\, e.g. \\\(\\pi \\rightarrow \\mathrm\{ns\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06783
name: redox potential
def: "Any oxidation-reduction \(redox\) reaction can be divided into two half-reactions\: one in which a chemical species undergoes oxidation and one in which another chemical species undergoes reduction. If a half-reaction is written as a reduction\, the driving force is the reduction potential. If the half-reaction is written as oxidation\, the driving force is the oxidation potential related to the reduction potential by a sign change. So the redox potential is the reduction/oxidation potential of a compound measured under standards conditions against a standard reference half-cell. In biological systems the standard redox potential is defined at pH – 7.0 versus the hydrogen electrode and partial pressure of hydrogen = 1 bar."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06793
name: rubredoxin
def: "An iron-sulfur protein without acid-labile sulfur\, in which an iron centre is coordinated by four sulfur-containing ligands\, usually cysteine. The function\, where known\, is an electron carrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06807
name: risk
def: "Probability of adverse effects caused under specified circumstances by an agent in an organism\, a population\, or an ecological system.Expected frequency of occurrence of a harmful event arising from such an exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06808
name: risk assessment
def: "Identification and quantification of the risk resulting from a specific use or occurrence of an agent\, taking into account possible harmful effects on individuals exposed to the agent in the amount and manner proposed and all the possible routes of exposure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06841
name: receptor
def: "Molecular structure in or on a cell that specifically recognizes and binds to a compound and acts as a physiological signal transducer\, or mediator of\, an effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06871
name: rigid chain
def: "The rod-like chain of an MCPLC \(main-chain polymer liquid crystal\) with direct links between the mesogenic groups for which the persistence length is at least comparable with the contour length and much greater than the diameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06893
name: reverse osmosis
def: "Liquid-phase pressure-driven separation process in which applied transmembrane pressure causes selective movement of solvent against its osmotic pressure difference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06912
name: reference dose
def: "An estimate \(with uncertainty spanning perhaps an order of magnitude\) of daily exposure to the human population \(including sensitive subgroups\) that is likely to be without appreciable risk of deleterious effects during a lifetime."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06913
name: risk estimation
def: "Assessment with or without mathematical modeling\, of the probability and nature of effects of exposure to a substance based on quantification of dose-effect and dose-response relationships for that substance and the population\(s\) and environmental components likely to be exposed and on assessment of the levels of potential exposure of people\, organisms\, and environment at risk."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06978
name: receptor
def: "A receptor is a protein or a protein complex in or on a cell that specifically recognizes and binds to a compound acting as a molecular messenger \(neurotransmitter\, hormone\, lymphokine\, lectin\, drug\, etc\). In a broader sense\, the term receptor is often used as a synonym for any specific \(as opposed to non-specific such as binding to plasma proteins\) drug binding site\, also including nucleic acids such as DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT06979
name: regression analysis
def: "Regression analysis is the use of statistical methods for modeling a set of dependent variables\, Y\, in terms of combinations of predictions\, X. It includes methods such as multiple linear regression \(MLR\) and partial least squares \(PLS\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07092
name: relative hardness
def: "The difference between the values of the absolute hardnesses \(\\\(\\eta \\\)\) of a given molecule and the corresponding reference structure \(\\\(\\eta _\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\(\\eta _\{\\rm\{r\}\} = \\eta - \\eta _\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\). The values of relative hardnesses correlate well with the resonance energies of cyclic compounds and may be used as an index of aromaticity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07093
name: relativistic effects
def: "Corrections to exact non-relativistic energy from the fact that inner shell electrons in heavy atoms move with velocities comparable in order of magnitude to the velocity of light. The most important relativistic effect is spin–orbit coupling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07094
name: resonance hybrid
def: "A localized valence bond representation of a molecule. This describes pictorially an electronic wavefunction that makes some contribution to the state wavefunction of the system. resonance hybrids are needed to describe the state of a system when that system is not adequately described by a single Lewis structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07095
name: ribbon delocalisation
def: "Electron delocalization realized in both acyclic and cyclic $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}conjugated\}$\, 1\, and $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonded\}$\, 2\, ribbon-like systems.  For cyclic systems\, a variety of linkage fashions between several ribbons \(pericyclic\, spirocyclic\, longicyclic and laticyclic\) is possible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07096
name: Rydberg state
def: "An electronic state that arises by the excitation of a valence electron to a diffuse atomic-like orbital. For instance\, the lowest lying Rydberg state \(n\, 3S\) of $\\ce\{NH3\}$ can be described as resulting from the excitation of a non-bonding orbital to a 3S Rydberg orbital."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07149
name: radiation reaction
def: "Chemical reaction that is induced by ionizing radiation with $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{ray\}$\, X-ray\, electron\, or other high-energy beams."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07150
name: reactive blending
def: "Mixing process that is accompanied by the chemical reaction\(s\) of components of a polymer mixture."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07164
name: reactive polymer
def: "Polymer having reactive functional groups that can undergo chemical transformation under the conditions required for a given reaction or application."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07166
name: resin
def: "Soft solid or highly viscous substance\, usually containing prepolymers with reactive groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07193
name: resist polymer
def: "Polymeric material that\, when irradiated\, undergoes a marked change in solubility in a given solvent or is ablated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07308
name: rod-like morphology
def: "Morphology characterized by cylindrical phase domains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07470
name: radiation trapping
def: "Process by which the resonance radiation emitted in a lamp is absorbed and re-emitted many times in the plasma prior to striking the phosphor. This process is called radiation trapping or imprisonment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07471
name: radical photosubstitution
def: "Photo-induced substitution resulting from the reaction of radicals produced by photolysis of an appropriate reagent\, such as\, e.g.\, halogenated compounds."
synonym: "free-radical photosubstitution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07472
name: Rehm–Weller equation
def: "Empirical correlation found between the observed second-order rate constant\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{q\}\}\\\)\, for an intermolecular electron-transfer reaction and the Gibbs energy of the photoinduced electron transfer process within the encounter complex \(\\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{ET\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\[k_\{\\rm\{q\}\} = \\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\}\{1+ \\frac \{k_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\}\{K_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\, \\rm\{Z\}\}\\left \[ \\rm\{exp\}\\left \( \\frac\{\\Delta G^\{\\ddagger \}\}\{RT\} \\right \) + \\rm\{exp\}\\left \( \\frac\{\\Delta_\{\\rm\{ET\}\} G^\{o \}\}\{RT\} \\right \) \\right \]\}\\\] with \\\(k_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\\\) and \\\(k_\{\\rm\{-d\}\}\\\) the rate constant for the formation and separation\, respectively\, of the encounter \(precursor\) complex\, \\\(K_\{\\rm\{d\}\} = \\\: ^\{k_\{\\rm\{d\}\}\}\\! /_\{k_\{\\rm\{-d\}\}\}\\\)\, \\\(Z\\\) the universal collision frequency factor\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) the absolute temperature and \\\(\\Delta G^\{\\ddagger\}\\\) the activation Gibbs energy of the forward electron transfer reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07473
name: residual emission anisotropy
def: "Photoselected molecules hindered in their rotation \(e.g.\, in lipid bilayers or liquid crystals\) do not become randomly oriented even after long time periods. Thus\, the emission anisotropy does not decay to zero but to a steady value\, \\\(r_\{\\mathrm\{\\infty \}\}\\\)\, called residual emission anisotropy. In the case of a single rotational correlation time\, \\\(\\tau _\{\\mathrm\{c\}\}\\\) or \\\(\\theta \\\)\, the decay of emission anisotropy following $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\\mbox\{-\}pulse\}$ excitation is given by\: \\\[r\\left\(t\\right\)=\(r_\{0\}- r_\{\\mathrm\{\\infty \}\}\)\\ \\exp \(- \\frac\{t\}\{\\tau _\{\\mathrm\{c\}\}\}\)+r_\{\\mathrm\{\\infty \}\}\\\] where \\\(r_\{0\}\\\) is the fundamental emission anisotropy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07474
name: rotational correlation time
def: "Parameter describing the time dependence of the tumbling of a molecular entity in a medium of viscosity \\\(\\eta\\\). The rotational correlation time can be obtained from the decay of the fluorescence or phosphorescence anisotropy and is related to the average molecular rotational diffusion coefficient\, \\\(D_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\)\, in turn related to the hydrodynamic molecular volume of the fluorophore\, \\\(V\\\)\, and to \\\(\\eta\\\) \(see Note 3\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07475
name: rotational relaxation time
def: "Parameter describing the time-dependence of the tumbling of a molecular entity in a medium of viscosity \\\(\\eta\\\) as originally defined by Debye\, and used by Perrin in the original development of the theories of rotational motion of fluorophores."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07526
name: responsive gel
def: "Gel that responds to external electrical\, mechanical\, thermal\, light-induced or chemical stimulation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07527
name: rheopexic gel
def: "Gel for which the time of solidification after discontinuation of a relatively high shear rate\, is reduced by applying a small shear rate."
synonym: "rheotropic gel" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07529
name: ringing gel
def: "Gel with energy dissipation in the acoustic frequency range."
synonym: "humming gel" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07572
name: reversible network
def: "Polymer network that forms or breaks up as the temperature is changed or under the action of a force."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07628
name: reaction injection moulding
def: "Reactive polymer processing that produces polymer monoliths by low-pressure injection and mixing of low-viscosity precursors into moulds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07629
name: reinforced reaction injection moulding
def: "Reaction injection moulding within which glass fibres are included to increase the strength of the moulding."

[Term]
id: goldbook:RT07630
name: reactive polymer processing
def: "Process whereby a polymeric monolith is produced through an in-situpolymerization or polymer modification reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S04387
name: π → σ* transition
def: "An electronic transition described approximately as a promotion of an electron from a 'bonding' orbital $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\}$ to an 'antibonding' orbital $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\}$ designated as $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!^\{\\ast\}\}$. Such transitions generally involve high transition energies and appear close to or mixed with Rydberg transitions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05434
name: σ\, π
def: "The terms are symmetry designations\, $\\ce\{\\uppi\\! molecular orbitals\}$ being antisymmetric with respect to a defining plane containing at least one atom \(e.g. the molecular plane of ethene\) and $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\! molecular orbitals\}$ symmetric with respect to the same plane. In practice the terms are used both in this rigorous sense \(for orbitals encompassing the entire molecule\) and also for localized two-centre orbitals or bonds\, and it is necessary to make a clear distinction between the two usages. In the case of two-centre bonds\, a $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ has a nodal plane that includes the internuclear bond axis\, whereas a $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ has no such nodal plane. \(A $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ in organometallic or inorganic molecular species has two nodes.\) Radicals are classified by analogy into $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}radicals\}$. Such two-centre orbitals may take part in molecular orbitals of $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ or $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}symmetry\}$. For example\, the methyl group in propene contains three $\\ce\{C–H\}$ bonds\, each of which is of local $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}symmetry\}$ \(i.e. without a nodal plane including the internuclear axis\)\, but these three '$\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$' can in turn be combined to form a set of group orbitals one of which has $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}symmetry\}$ with respect to the principal molecular plane and can accordingly interact with the two-centre orbital of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}symmetry\}$ \($\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$\) of the double-bonded carbon atoms\, to form a molecular orbital of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}symmetry\}$. Such an interaction between the $\\ce\{CH3\}$ group and the double bond is an example of what is called hyperconjugation. This cannot rigorously be described as '$\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\\uppi\\!\}$ conjugation' since $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\}$ here refer to different defining planes\, and interaction between orbitals of different symmetries \(with respect to the same defining plane\) is forbidden."
synonym: "sigma, pi" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05435
name: σ → σ* transition
def: "An electronic transition described approximately as promotion of an electron from a 'bonding' $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$ to an 'antibonding' $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}orbital\}$ designated as \\\(\\sigma ^\{\\rm\{*\}\}\\\). Such transitions generally involve high transition energies\, and appear close to or mixed with Rydberg transitions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05441
name: saccharides
def: "The monosaccharides \(1\) and di- \(2\)\, oligo- \(3 - 10\) and polysaccharides \(many\)\, which are made up of $n$ monosaccharide units linked to each other by a glycosidic bond. Considered by some to be synonymous with carbohydrates."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05442
name: Sackur–Tetrode constant
def: "Fundamental physical constant representing the translational contribution to molar entropy; \\\(\\frac\{S_\{0\}\}\{R\} = -1.151\\ 693\(21\)\\\) at \\\(1\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) and standard pressure of \\\(100\\ \\rm\{kPa\}\\\)\, where \\\(R\\\) is the gas constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05443
name: sacrificial acceptor
def: "Molecular entity that acts as the electron acceptor in a photoinduced electron transfer process and is not restored in a subsequent oxidation process but is destroyed by irreversible chemical conversion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05444
name: sacrificial donor
def: "Molecular entity that acts as the electron donor in a photoinduced electron transfer process and is not restored in a subsequent reduction process but is destroyed by irreversible chemical conversion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05447
name: salt
def: "A chemical compound consisting of an assembly of cations and anions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05449
name: salt form of an ion exchanger
def: "The ionic form of an ion exchanger in which the counter-ions are neither hydrogen nor hydroxide ions. When only one valence is possible for the counter-ion\, or its exact form or charge is not known\, the symbol or the name of the counter-ion without charge is used\, e.g. sodium-form or $\\ce\{Na\}$-form\, tetramethylammonium-form\, orthophosphate-form. When one of two or more possible forms is exclusively present\, the oxidation state may be indicated by a Roman numeral\, e.g. $\\ce\{Fe^\{II\}\}$ form\, $\\ce\{Fe^\{III\}\}$ form."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05450
name: salting out
def: "The addition of particular electrolytes to an aqueous phase in order to increase the distribution ratio of a particular solute."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05451
name: sample
def: "A portion of material selected from a larger quantity of material. The term needs to be qualified\, e.g. bulk sample\, representative sample\, primary sample\, bulked sample\, test sample. The term 'sample' implies the existence of a sampling error\, i.e. the results obtained on the portions taken are only estimates of the concentration of a constituent or the quantity of a property present in the parent material. If there is no or negligible sampling error\, the portion removed is a test portion\, aliquot or specimen. The term 'specimen' is used to denote a portion taken under conditions such that the sampling variability cannot be assessed \(usually because the population is changing\)\, and is assumed\, for convenience\, to be zero. The manner of selection of the sample should be prescribed in a sampling plan."
synonym: "analytical sample" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05456
name: sample error
def: "An error which arises when the absorbance of some samples changes with time\, e.g. as a result of photochemical reaction\, formation of aggregates or adsorption on the cell wall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05457
name: sample handling
def: "Any action applied to the sample before the analytical procedure. Such actions include the addition of preservatives\, separation procedures\, storage at low temperature\, protection against light and irradiation\, loading\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05458
name: sample injector
def: "A device by which a liquid or gaseous sample is introduced into the apparatus. The sample can be introduced directly into the carrier-gas stream\, or into a chamber temporarily isolated from the system by values which can be changed so as to make an instantaneous switch of the gas stream through the chamber. The latter is a by-pass injector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05459
name: sampler
def: "A device used to withdraw and deliver a volume or an amount of a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05461
name: sample unit
def: "The discrete identifiable portion suitable for taking as a sample or as a portion of a sample. These units may be different at different stages of sampling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05463
name: sampling error
def: "That part of the total error \(the estimate from a sample minus the population value\) associated with using only a fraction of the population and extrapolating to the whole\, as distinct from analytical or test error. It arises from a lack of homogeneity in the parent population. In chemical analysis\, the final test result reflects the value only as it exists in the test portion. It is usually assumed that no sampling error is introduced in preparing the test sample from the laboratory sample. Therefore\, the sampling error is usually associated exclusively with the variability of the laboratory sample. Sampling error is determined by replication of the laboratory samples and their multiple analyses. Since sampling error is always associated with analytical error\, it must be isolated by the statistical procedure of analysis of variance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05465
name: sampling interval
def: "In Tast polarography\, square-wave polarography\, and similar techniques\, the interval during which the current is measured or recorded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05466
name: sampling plan
def: "A predetermined procedure for the selection\, withdrawal\, preservation\, transportation and preparation of the portions to be removed from a population as samples. Summarizing the test values or observations from the selected portions yields an estimate for the concentration of an analyte or a value for a property determined with a calculable degree of uncertainty at a specified confidence level. A sampling plan includes the designation of the number\, location and size of the portions\, and instructions for the extent of compositing and for the reduction \(in amount and fineness\) of the portions to a laboratory sample and to test portions. It may also contain acceptance criteria. Some sampling plans do not include more than instructions for the statistical selection of portions to be removed. Such plans should properly be designated as 'statistical sampling plans'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05467
name: sampling time
def: "In Tast polarography\, square-wave polarography\, and similar techniques\, the duration of the sampling interval."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05468
name: sandwich compounds
def: "Compounds in which a metal atom is located between the faces of two parallel and planar \(or nearly so\) ring structures\, e.g. bis\(\\\(\\eta ^\{5\}\\\)-cyclopentadienyl\)iron \(ferrocene\)\, dibenzenechromium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05469
name: sanitary land fill
def: "An engineered burial of refuse. The refuse is dumped into trenches and compacted by bulldozer\, where\, it is hoped\, aerobic metabolism by microorganisms decomposes the organic matter to stable compounds \($\\ce\{H2O\}$\, $\\ce\{CO2\}$\, etc.\). Moisture is essential for the biological degradation and groundwater assists the process except when it fills air voids and prevents the transport of oxygen to the refuse. Land fills of unsatisfactory design can be major sources of air\, water and soil pollution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05470
name: saprophyte
def: "A microorganism that feeds on dead and decaying organic matter. Saprophytes excrete enzymes that digest organic residues externally\, the low molecular weight compounds formed then being absorbed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05471
name: saturated solution
def: "A solution which has the same concentration of a solute as one that is in equilibrium with undissolved solute at specified values of the temperature and pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05472
name: saturation
def: "The state of a saturated solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05473
name: saturation
def: "Of an irradiated element for a specified isotope\, the steady state reached when the disintegration rate of the nuclide formed is equal to its production rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05474
name: saturation activity
def: "For a specified isotope\, the value of the activity of an irradiated element\, whence state of saturation is reached."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05476
name: saturation fraction
def: "Amount-of-substance of a component \(solute\) in a solution divided by the amount-of-substance of the component when it is saturating the system at constant temperature and pressure. Also referred to simply as saturation\, for example oxygen saturation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05478
name: saturation transfer
def: "A term used in nuclear magnetic resonance. When a nucleus is strongly irradiated\, its spin population may partly be transferred to another nucleus by an exchange process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05479
name: saturation vapour pressure
def: "The pressure exerted by a pure substance \(at a given temperature\) in a system containing only the vapour and condensed phase \(liquid or solid\) of the substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05480
name: sawhorse projection
def: "A perspective formula indicating the spatial arrangement of bonds on two adjacent carbon atoms. The bond between the two atoms is represented by a diagonal line\, the left-hand bottom end of which locates the atom nearer the observer and the right-hand top end the atom that is further away. In general a Newman or zig-zag projection is preferred."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05481
name: Saytzeff rule
def: "Dehydrohalogenation of secondary- and tertiary-alkyl halides proceeds by the preferential removal of the $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}hydrogen\}$ from the carbon that has the smallest number of hydrogens. Originally formulated by A. Saytzeff \(Zaitsev\) to generalize the orientation in $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$elimination reactions of alkyl halides\, this rule has been extended and modified\, as follows\: When two or more olefins can be produced in an elimination reaction\, the thermodynamically most stable alkene will predominate. Exceptions to the Saytzeff rule are exemplified by the Hofmann rule."
synonym: "Zaitsev rule" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05482
name: scaler
def: "A sub-assembly for counting electrical pulses and containing one or more scaling circuits."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05483
name: scaling circuit
def: "An electronic circuit which produces an output pulse for each time a specified number of pulses has been received at its input."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05484
name: scanning electron microscopy
def: "Any analytical technique which involves the generation and evaluation of secondary electrons \(and to a lesser extent back scattered electrons\) by a finely focused electron beam \(typically \\\(10\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) or less\) for high resolution and high depth of field imaging."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05485
name: scanning method
def: "This term refers to the sequence of control over operating parameters of a mass spectrometer that results in a spectrum of masses\, velocities\, momenta or energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05486
name: scanning transmission electron microscopy
def: "A special TEM-technique in which an electron transparent sample is bombarded with a finely focused electron beam \(typically of a diameter of less than $\\pu\{10 nm\}$\) which can be scanned across the specimen or rocked across the optical axis and transmitted\, secondary\, back scattered and diffracted electrons as well as the characteristic X-ray spectrum can be observed. STEM essentially provides high resolution imaging of the inner microstructure and the surface of a thin sample \(or small particles\)\, as well as the possibility of chemical and structural characterization of micrometer and sub-micrometer domains through evaluation of the X-ray spectra and the electron diffraction pattern."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05487
name: scattering
def: "A process in which a change in direction or energy of an incident radiation is caused by interaction with a particle\, a system of particles\, or a photon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05488
name: scattering angle
def: "The angle between the forward direction of the incident beam and a straight line connecting the scattering point and the detector. Synonymous with angle of observation."
synonym: "angle of observation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05490
name: scattering cross-section
def: "The scattering coefficient per particle \(\\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{cm\}^\{2\}\}\{\\rm\{particle\}\}\\\)\); \\\[b_\{\\rm\{scat\}\}=n\\ \\sigma _\{\\rm\{scat\}\}\\\] where \\\(n\\\) is the number concentration of particles \(\\\(\\rm\{particles cm\}^\{-3\}\\\)\) and \\\(\\sigma _\{\\rm\{scat\}\}\\\) is the scattering cross-section. \\\(b_\{\\rm\{scat\}\}\\\)\, the scattering component of extinction due to gas and particles\, is measured in the atmosphere using a nephelometer. For a homogeneous atmosphere it is related in theory to the meteorological range \(\\\(L_\{\\rm\{V\}\}\\\)\)\: \\\(L_\{\\rm\{V\}\}=\\frac\{3.9\}\{b_\{\\rm\{scat\}\}\}\\\); \\\(b_\{\\rm\{scat\}\}\\\) and \\\(b_\{\\rm\{abs\}\}\\\) represent the scattering and absorption coefficients per unit length for a light beam \(of path length \\\(L\\\)\) which has a spectral radiance \(intensity\) \\\(I_\{0\}\\\) incident on a sample of air and \\\(I\\\) is the transmitted spectral radiance \(intensity\)\, \\\(\\ln \(\\frac\{I_\{0\}\}\{I\}\) = L\\ \(b_\{\\rm\{scat\}\}+b_\{\\rm\{abs\}\}\)\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05491
name: scattering error
def: "The presence of particulate matter\, emulsions\, micelles\, etc. may cause radiation scattering which will result in further attenuation of the transmitted beam and the measured absorbance will be too high. This error is called the scattering error."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05492
name: scattering matrix
def: "The Stokes parameters of scattered light are given by the matrix equation \\\[\(s_\{0\}\,s_\{1\}\,s_\{2\}\,s_\{3\}\) = F\\ \(s_\{0\}^\{0\}\,s_\{1\}^\{0\}\,s_\{2\}^\{0\}\,s_\{3\}^\{0\}\)\\\] where the 4\, 4 scattering matrix \\\(F\\\) is comprised of 16 scattering matrix elements. These matrix elements\, which may originate in theory or experiment\, provide a complete description of the scattered radiation in terms of the incident radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05493
name: scattering plane
def: "The plane containing the incident light beam and the line from the centre of the scattering system to the observer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05494
name: scattering vector
def: "The vector difference between the wave propagation vectors of the incident and the scattered beam\, both of length \\\(\\frac\{2\\ \\pi \}\{\\lambda \}\\\)\, where \\\(\\lambda \\\) is the wavelength of the scattered radiation in the medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05495
name: scavenger
def: "A substance that reacts with \(or otherwise removes\) a trace component \(as in the scavenging of trace metal ions\) or traps a reactive reaction intermediate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05496
name: scavenging
def: "Binding radicals or free electrons with a receptive \(or reactive\) material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05497
name: Schenck-sensitization mechanism
def: "Chemical transformation of one molecular entity caused by photoexcitation of a sensitizer\, which undergoes temporary covalent bond formation with the molecular entity \(M\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05498
name: Schiff bases
def: "Imines bearing a hydrocarbyl group on the nitrogen atom $\\ce\{R2C=NR'\}$ \($\\ce\{R'\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). Considered by many to be synonymous with azomethines."
synonym: "Schiff's bases" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05499
name: Schiller layers
def: "In some systems\, sedimenting particles form layers separated by approximately equal distances of the order of the wavelength of light. This gives rise to strong colours when observed in reflected light and the system is said to form iridescent layers or Schiller layers."
synonym: "iridescent layers" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05501
name: Schulze–Hardy rule
def: "The generalization that the critical coagulation concentration for a typical lyophobic sol is extremely sensitive to the valence of the counter-ions \(high valence gives a low critical coagulation concentration\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05502
name: Schulz–Zimm distribution
def: "In an assembly of macromolecules\, a continuous distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form\: \\\[f_\{\\rm\{w\}\}\(x\)\\ \\rm\{d\}x = \\frac\{a^\{b\\\,+\\\,1\}\}\{\\varGamma\\left\(b\\\,+\\\,1\\right\)\}\\ x^\{b\}\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{-a\\ x\}\\ \\rm\{d\}x\\\] where \\\(x\\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length\, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization\, \\\(a\\\) and \\\(b\\\) are positive adjustable parameters\, and \(\\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}\\left\(b+1\\right\)\\\)\) is the gamma function of \(\\\(b+1\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05503
name: scintillation
def: "Burst of luminescence of short duration caused by an individual energetic particle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05504
name: scintillation counter
def: "A scintillator coupled to a photomultiplier tube. Incident X-ray photons are converted in the scintillator into bursts of visible light photons\, some of which fall on the photocathode and can be measured. For incident photons having energies higher than the absorption edge of the elements contained in the scintillator\, an escape peak can be observed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05505
name: scintillation detector
def: "A radiation detector using a medium in which a burst of luminescence radiation\, produced along the path of an ionizing particle\, is quantified."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05506
name: scintillation spectrometer
def: "A measuring assembly incorporating a scintillation detector and a pulse amplitude analyser used for determining the energy spectrum of certain types of radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05507
name: scintillators
def: "Materials used for the measurement of radioactivity\, by recording the radioluminescence. They contain compounds \(chromophores\) which combine a high fluorescence quantum efficiency\, a short fluorescence lifetime\, and a high solubility. These compounds are employed as solutes in aromatic liquids and polymers to form organic liquid and plastic scintillators\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05508
name: s-<i>cis</i>\, s-<i>trans</i>
def: "The spatial arrangement of two conjugated double bonds about the intervening single bond is described as s-cis if synperiplanar and s-trans if antiperiplanar. This term should not be applied to other systems such as N-alkyl amides \(use E/Z or sp/ap\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05510
name: scrubber
def: "An apparatus used in sampling and in flue gas cleaning. The gas is passed through a space containing wetted 'packing' or spray. In general\, particles are collected in scrubbers by one or a combination of the following\: impingement of particles on a liquid medium; diffusion of the particles onto a liquid medium; condensation of liquid medium vapours on the particles; partitioning of the gas into extremely small elements to allow collection of the particles by Brownian diffusion and gravitation settling on the gas-liquid interface. The devices include spray towers\, jet scrubbers\, Venturi scrubbers\, cyclonic scrubbers\, inertial scrubbers\, mechanical scrubbers and packed scrubbers. Normally the gas flow in the scrubber is counter to the liquid flow. Efficient scrubbers will collect particles as small as \\\(1\\\) to \\\(2\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\) in diameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05511
name: scrubbing
def: "The process of selectively removing contaminating solutes \(impurities\) from an extract that contains these as well as the main extractable solute by treatment with a new immiscible liquid phase. The term stripping has a different meaning and should not be used in this sense although this usage has been customary in certain industries."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05512
name: seco-
def: "Cleavage of a ring with addition of one or more hydrogen atoms at each terminal group thus created is indicated by the prefix 'seco'. Examples\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05513
name: second
def: "The second\, symbol \\\(\\pu\{s\}\\\)\, is the SI unit of time. It is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of the caesium frequency \\\(\\Delta \nu_\{\\ce\{Cs\}\}\\\)\, the unperturbed ground-state hyperfine transition frequency of the caesium 133 atom\, to be \\\(\\pu\{9192631770\}\\\) when expressed in the unit Hz\, which is equal to \\\(\\pu\{s-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05514
name: second
def: "Non-SI unit of plane angle\: \\\(1^\{\\prime\\prime\} = \\frac\{1\}\{3600\}\\ ^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} = 4.848\\ 137\\\,...\\\,\\times\\ 10^\{-6\}\\ \\rm\{rad\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05515
name: secondary crystallization
def: "Crystallization occurring after primary crystallization\, usually proceeding at a lower rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05517
name: secondary electron multiplier
def: "A device to multiply current in an electron beam \(or in a photon or particle beam by first conversion to electrons\) by incidence of accelerated electrons upon the surface of an electrode which yields a number of secondary electrons greater than the number of incident electrons. These electrons are then accelerated to another electrode \(or another part of the same electrode\)\, which in turn emits further secondary electrons so that the process can be repeated. It is recommended that one should refer to the abundance of an ion\, to the intensity of an ion beam\, and to the height or area of a peak."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05518
name: secondary electrons
def: "All electrons emitted from the surface of a solid except back scattered primary electrons. In practice\, electrons emitted from the surface of a solid under particle bombardment which have a kinetic energy of less than \\\(50\\ \\rm\{eV\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05519
name: secondary-electron yield
def: "The number of secondary electrons generated per primary electron for a given specimen and experimental conditions. It depends on the \(mean\) atomic number of the excited area of the sample\, the angle between electron beam and sample surface\, the primary electron energy\, thickness of the sample and sample potentials."
synonym: "secondary electron yield" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05521
name: secondary fluorescence
def: "Ionization of the analyte element in a sample by characteristic radiation from other elements in the sample resulting in an enhancement of the signal measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05522
name: secondary ionization
def: "The process in which ions are ejected from a sample surface \(which may be a solid or substrate dissolved in a solvent matrix\) as a result of bombardment by a primary beam of atoms or ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05523
name: secondary isotope effect
def: "A kinetic isotope effect that is attributable to isotopic substitution of an atom to which bonds are neither made nor broken in the rate-controlling step or in a pre-equilibrium step of a specified reaction\, and is therefore not a primary isotope effect. One speaks of $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\, \(etc.\) secondary isotope effects\, where $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\, \(etc.\) denote the position of isotopic substitution relative to the reaction centre. The corresponding isotope effect on the equilibrium constant of such a reaction is called a 'secondary equilibrium isotope effect'. Secondary isotope effects have been discussed in terms of the conventional electronic effects of physical organic chemistry\, e.g. induction\, hyperconjugation\, hybridization\, etc.\, since these properties are determined by the electron distribution\, that depends on vibrationally averaged bond lengths and angles which vary slightly with isotopic substitution. While this usage is legitimate\, the term 'electronic isotope effect' should be avoided\, because of the misleading implication that such an effect is electronic rather than vibrational in origin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05526
name: secondary metabolites
def: "Metabolites which are produced by routes other than the normal metabolic pathways\, mostly after the phase of active growth and under conditions of deficiency. The biological significance of many secondary metabolites is not exactly known."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05527
name: secondary pollution
def: "The products of the primary pollutants which form through photochemical and thermal reactions in the atmosphere \($\\ce\{O3\}$\, peroxyacetyl nitrate\, etc.\)."
synonym: "secondary emissions" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05528
name: secondary radiation
def: "Radiation emitted by any matter irradiated with electromagnetic or ionizing radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05530
name: secondary structure
def: "The conformational arrangement \($\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$-helix\, $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\mbox\{-\}pleated\}$ sheet\, etc.\) of the backbone segments of a macromolecule such as a polypeptide chain of a protein without regard to the conformation of the side chains or the relationship to other segments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05531
name: second-order transition
def: "A transition in which a crystal structure undergoes a continuous change and in which the first derivatives of the Gibbs energies \(or chemical potentials\) are continuous but the second derivatives with respect to temperature and pressure \(i.e. heat capacity\, thermal expansion\, compressibility\) are discontinuous. Example\: The order–disorder transition in metal alloys\, for example\, $\\ce\{CuZn\}$."
synonym: "continuous transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05532
name: secular equilibrium
def: "Radioactive equilibrium where the half life of the precursor isotope is so long that the change of its activity can be ignored during the period of interest and all activities remain constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05533
name: sediment
def: "The highly concentrated suspension which may be formed by the sedimentation of a dilute suspension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05534
name: sedimentation
def: "In the atmospheric sciences\, the process of removal of an air borne particle from the atmosphere due to the effect of gravity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05535
name: sedimentation coefficient
def: "Velocity of sedimentation divided by the acceleration of the force field \(gravitation or centrifugation\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05536
name: sedimentation equilibrium
def: "The equilibrium established in a centrifugal field when there is no net flux of any component across any plane perpendicular to the centrifugal force."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05537
name: sedimentation field strength
def: "The potential difference \\\(E_\{\\mathrm\{sed\}\}\\\) per unit length in a sedimentation or centrifugation cell. As the contributions of the interfacial potential differences at the electrodes are not included in \\\(E_\{\\mathrm\{sed\}\}\\\) this quantity\, although theoretically important\, is not accessible to measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05538
name: sedimentation potential difference
def: "The potential difference \\\(E_\{\\mathrm\{sed\}\}\\\) or \\\(E\\\) at zero current caused by the sedimentation of particles in the field of gravity or in a centrifuge\, between two identical electrodes at different levels \(or at different distances from the centre of rotation\). \\\(E\\\) is positive if the lower \(peripheral\) electrode is negative. This is also called the Dorn effect."
synonym: "Dorn effect" []
synonym: "sedimentation potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05539
name: sedimentation velocity
def: "Velocity of a component in a fluid relative to the fluid in the direction of gravitational or centrifugal acceleration. Also called sedimentation rate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05540
name: sedimentation velocity method
def: "A method by which the velocity of motion of solute component\(s\) or dispersed particles is measured and the result is expressed in terms of its \(their\) sedimentation coefficient\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05541
name: sedimentation volume
def: "The volume \\\(V_\{\\mathrm\{sed\}\}\\\) of sediment formed in a suspension. If the sediment is formed in a centrifugal field\, the strength of this field should be explicitly indicated\, otherwise normal gravity is understood."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05542
name: segment
def: "\(for bulk materials\)\: Each of the single\, large portions of material pre-existing either in space \(e.g. bags\, bales\, drums\) or accumulated during a fixed time \(e.g. discharge from a conveyor belt\) or formable as increments by a sampling device. Segments may be actual or conceptual."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05544
name: segregation
def: "The process that differentiates the composition at an interface or surface from the average or bulk composition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05545
name: segregation
def: "The rejection of a fraction of macromolecules\, or of impurities\, or both\, from growing crystals. The rejected macromolecules are usually those of insufficient relative molecular mass\, or differing in constitution or configuration \(e.g. branching\, tacticity\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05547
name: selected ion monitoring
def: "This term is used to describe the operation of the mass spectrometer in which the intensities of several specific ion beams are recorded rather than the entire mass spectrum. An alternative recommended term is multiple ion \(peak\) monitoring. The use of the terms multiple ion detection and mass fragmentography are not recommended\, because in the case of the former\, it is the monitoring of several peaks which should be emphasized rather than the detection of different ions\, and in the case of the latter it is often not fragments that are being monitored but peaks from molecular ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05548
name: selection
def: "A laboratory method applying a mixture of microorganisms to particular growth conditions under which only the cells with particular characteristics can survive and may be isolated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05549
name: selection rule
def: "A rule that states whether a given transition is allowed or forbidden\, on the basis of the symmetry or spin of the wavefunctions of the initial and final states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05550
name: selective
def: "A term which expresses qualitatively the extent to which other substances interfere with the determination of a substance according to a given procedure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05551
name: selective corrosion
def: "Corrosion of a single phase having more than one component\, when the ratio of the corrosion rates of the components differs from the ratio of bulk mole fractions. Selective corrosion changes the composition of the material in the interfacial region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05554
name: selective elution
def: "An elution procedure in which a specific eluent is used\, e.g. a complexing agent that forms stable non-sorbable complexes with one or a group of the compounds to be separated\, but affects the other components only to a negligible extent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05556
name: selectively labelled
def: "An isotopically labelled compound is designated as selectively labelled when a mixture of isotopically substituted compounds is formally added to the analogous isotopically unmodified compound in such a way that the position\(s\) but not necessarily the number of each labelling nuclide is defined. A selectively labelled compound may be considered as a mixture of specifically labelled compounds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05557
name: selective micro-sample
def: "A sample which results where a small portion has been separated from the lot or laboratory sample by selective means such as magnetic-\, density-\, or manual separation\, by micro-drilling\, or by centrifugation\, e.g. the separation of magnetic materials from a geological material\, or the separation of metal particles from a lubricating oil. If individual particles are analysed the term individual particle analysis is applied."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05558
name: selective poisoning
def: "In selective poisoning or selective inhibition\, a poison retards the rate of one catalysed reaction more than that of another or it may retard only one of the reactions."
synonym: "selective inhibition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05559
name: selective preconcentration
def: "An operation \(process\) as a result of which microcomponents are selectively isolated from a sample. It is used when the simultaneous presence of several components in the concentrate may distort the results of analysis. Selective preconcentration usually is achieved by isolation of the microcomponent to be determined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05560
name: selective sample
def: "A sample that is deliberately chosen by using a sampling plan that screens out materials with certain characteristics and/or selects only material with other relevant characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05561
name: selective solvent
def: "A medium that is a solvent for at least one component of a mixture of polymers\, or for at least one block of a block or graft polymer\, but a non-solvent for the other component\(s\) or block\(s\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05563
name: selectivity
def: "It sometimes refers to the discrimination shown by a given reactant A when it reacts with two alternative reactants B and C\, or in two different ways \(e.g. at two different sites\) with a reactant B."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05564
name: selectivity
def: "\(qualitative\)\: The extent to which other substances interfere with the determination of a substance according to a given procedure.\(quantitative\)\: A term used in conjunction with another substantive \(e.g. constant\, coefficient\, index\, factor\, number\) for the quantitative characterization of interferences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05566
name: selectivity coefficient
def: "The equilibrium coefficient obtained by application of the law of mass action to ion exchange and characterizing quantitatively the ability of an ion exchanger to select one of two ions present in the same solution. The ions involved in the exchange should be specified as subscripts.Examples\:Exchange\: $\\ce\{Mg^\{2+\}\}$ – $\\ce\{Ca^\{2+\}\}$ \\\[k_\{\\rm\{Mg\}/\\rm\{Ca\}\}=\\frac\{\\rm\{\[Mg\]\}_\{\\rm\{S\}\}/\\rm\{\[Ca\]\}_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\}\{\\rm\{\[Mg\]\}_\{\\rm\{M\}\}/\\rm\{\[Ca\]\}_\{\\rm\{M\}\}\}\\\] Exchange\: $\\ce\{SO4^\{2−\}\}$ – $\\ce\{Cl^\{−\}\}$ \\\[k_\{\\rm\{SO\}_\{4\}/\\rm\{Cl\}\} = \\frac\{\\rm\{\[\}\\rm\{SO\}_\{4\}\\rm\{\]\}_\{\\rm\{S\}\}/\\rm\{\[Cl\]\}_\{\\rm\{S\}\}^\{2\}\}\{\\rm\{\[\}\\rm\{SO\}_\{4\}\\rm\{\]\}_\{\\rm\{M\}\}/\\rm\{\[Cl\]\}_\{\\rm\{M\}\}^\{2\}\}\\\] In the above equations subscript S refers to the ion exchanger \('stationary phase'\) and M to the external solution \('mobile phase'\). For exchanges involving counter-ions differing in their charges\, the numerical value of \\\(k_\{\\rm\{A\}/\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) depends on the choice of the concentration scales in the ion exchanger and the external solution \(molal scale\, molar scale\, mole fraction scale\, etc.\). Concentration units must be clearly stated for an exchange of ions of differing charges. The corrected selectivity coefficient \(\\\(k_\{\\rm\{A\}/\\rm\{B\}\}^\{a\}\\\)\) is calculated in a way identical to the selectivity coefficient except that the concentrations in the external solutions are replaced by activities. This term should not be used as a synonym for separation factor."
synonym: "selectivity factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05567
name: selectivity factor
def: "A quantitative representation of selectivity in aromatic substitution reactions \(usually electrophilic\, for monosubstituted benzene derivatives\). If the partial rate factor\, \\\(f\\\)\, expresses the reactivity of a specified position in the aromatic compound $\\ce\{PhX\}$ relative to that of a single position in benzene\, then the selectivity factor \\\(S_\{f\}\\\) \(expressing discrimination between p- and m-positions in $\\ce\{PhX\}$\) is defined as\: \\\[S_\{f\}=\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{f_\{p\}^\{\\rm\{X\}\}\}\{f_\{m\}^\{\\rm\{X\}\}\}\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05569
name: selectivity ratio
def: "Synonymous with selectivity coefficient."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05570
name: selenenic acids
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RSeOH\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{ArSeOH\}$\, areneselenenic acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05571
name: selenides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RSeR\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). They are thus selenium analogues of ethers.Metal salts of selane\, $\\ce\{H2Se\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05572
name: seleninic acids
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RSe\(=O\)OH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05573
name: selenocyanates
def: "Salts and esters of selenocyanic acid\, $\\ce\{HSeCN\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3CH2SeCN\}$ ethyl selenocyanate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05574
name: selenols
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RSeH\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05575
name: selenones
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2Se\(=O\)2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05576
name: selenonic acids
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RSe\(=O\)2OH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05577
name: selenoxides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2Se=O\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05578
name: self-absorption
def: "Occurs in emission sources of finite thickness when radiant energy quanta emitted by atoms \(or molecules\) are absorbed by atoms of the same kind present in the same source. The absorbed energy is usually dissipated by collisional transfer of energy\, or through emission of radiant energy of the same or other frequencies. In consequence\, the observed radiant intensity of a spectral line \(or band component\) emitted by a source may be less than the radiant intensity would be from an optically thin source having the same number of emitting atoms. Self-absorption may occur in all emitting sources to some degree\, whether they are homogeneous or not."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05579
name: self-absorption broadening
def: "Photons emitted in one region of a source are partly absorbed in their passage through the plasma. Because of the fact that the absorption profile is of the same shape as the emission profile\, energy is selectively absorbed from the emission line\, i.e. the absorption coefficient is a maximum at the centre of the line or central wavelength. The actual line profile is changed as a result of the lowering of the maximum intensity accompanied by a corresponding increase in apparent halfwidth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05580
name: self-absorption effect
def: "The reabsorption of luminescence by the analyte and interfering impurities within the excitation volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05581
name: self-absorption factor
def: "The ratio between the quantity of the radiation emitted by the source and the quantity of the radiation as produced by the radioactive nuclei present in the source. Synonymous with source efficiency."
synonym: "source efficiency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05582
name: self-diffusion coefficient
def: "The diffusion coefficient \\\(D_\{i\}^\{*\}\\\) of species \\\(i\\\) in the absence of a chemical potential gradient. It is related to the diffusion coefficient \\\(D_\{i\}\\\) by \\\[D_\{i\}^\{*\}=D_\{i\}\\ \\frac\{\\partial \(\\ln c_\{i\}\)\}\{\\partial \\ln a_\{i\}\}\\\] where \\\(a_\{i\}\\\) is the activity of \\\(i\\\) in the solution\, and \\\(c_\{i\}\\\) is the concentration of \\\(i\\\). If an isotopically labelled species \(\\\(i^\{*\}\\\)\) is used to study diffusion\, the tracer diffusion coefficient\, \\\(D_\{i\}^\{*\}\\\)\, is practically identical to the self-diffusion coefficient provided that the isotope effect is sufficiently small."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05583
name: self inductance
def: "For a thin conducting loop\, the magnetic flux through the loop\, caused by an electric current in the loop\, divided by that current."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05584
name: self-poisoning
def: "A product of a reaction may cause poisoning or inhibition. The phenomenon is called self-poisoning or autopoisoning."
synonym: "autopoisoning" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05585
name: self-quenching
def: "Quenching of an excited atom or molecular entity by interaction with another atom or molecular entity of the same species in the ground state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05586
name: self-reversal
def: "A case of self-absorption\, when a line is self-absorbed to such an extent that the peak or central wavelength intensity is less than at the wings or non-central wavelengths."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05587
name: self-shielding
def: "The lowering of the flux density in the inner part of an object due to absorption in its outer layers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05588
name: selones
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=Se\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). They are thus selenium analogues of ketones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05589
name: semicarbazones
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=NNHC\(=O\)NH2\}$\, formally derived by condensation of aldehydes or ketones with semicarbazide $\\ce\{\[NH2NHC\(=O\)NH2\]\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05590
name: semicoke
def: "A carbonaceous material intermediate between a fusible mesophase pitch and a non-deformable green coke produced by incomplete carbonization at temperatures between the onset of fusion \(of coal\, ca. \\\(620\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\)\, and complete devolatilization. Semicoke still contains volatile matter\, therefore."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05591
name: semiconductor
def: "Material whose conductivity\, due to charges of both signs\, is normally in the range between that of metals and insulators and in which the electric charge carrier density can be changed by external means."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05592
name: semiconductor detector
def: "A radiation detector using a semiconductor\, in which free electric charges are produced along the path of an ionizing particle\, in combination with a high voltage and electrodes to collect the induced electric charges."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05597
name: semiconductor-metal transition
def: "Any transition from a semiconductor to a metallic state under the influence of a temperature or pressure change or both. Examples\:$\\ce\{Ti2O3\}$\: band-edge crossing in a semiconductor to semimetal transition.SmS\: localized level crossing a band edge. In SmS hydrostatic pressure above the critical pressure \\\(P_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\approx 0.65\\ \\mathrm\{GPa\}\\\) broadens the Sm-5d band to make the band edge cross the Sm-\\\(4\\rm\{f\}^\{6\}\\\) level.A localized-itinerant transition.A switching transition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05598
name: semi-interpenetrating polymer network
def: "Polymer comprising one or more polymer networks and one or more linear or branched polymers characterized by the penetration on a molecular scale of at least one of the networks by at least some of the linear or branched macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05599
name: semioxamazones
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=NNHC\(=O\)C\(=O\)NH2\}$\, formally derived from the condensation of aldehydes or ketones with semioxamazide \(the hydrazide of oxamic acid\)\, $\\ce\{H2NNHC\(=O\)C\(=O\)NH2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05600
name: semiquinones
def: "Radical anions having the structure $\\ce\{O^\{−\}–Z–O^\{.\}\}$ where $\\ce\{Z\}$ is an ortho- or para-arylene group or analogous heteroarylene group; they are formally generated by the addition of an electron to a quinone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05601
name: semisystematic name
def: "A name in which at least one part is used in a systematic sense."
synonym: "semitrivial name" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05602
name: seniority
def: "Terms used in reference to priority in a prescribed hierarchical order\, a senior feature being preferred."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05603
name: sensitive area
def: "That area of the detector where an incident radiant power results in a measurable output."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05604
name: sensitive volume
def: "That volume of the detector where an incident radiant power produces a measurable output."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05605
name: sensitivity
def: "Two different measures of sensitivity are recommended. The first\, which is suitable for relatively involatile materials as well as gases\, depends upon the observed change in ion current for a particular amount or change of flow rate of sample though the ion source. A second method of stating sensitivity\, that is most suitable for gases\, depends upon the change of ion current related to the change of partial pressure of the sample in the ion source. It is important that the relevant experimental conditions corresponding to sensitivity measurement should always be stated. These include in a typical case details of the instrument type\, bombarding electron current\, slit dimensions\, angular collimation\, gain of the multiplier detector\, scan speed and whether the measured signal corresponds to a single mass peak or to the ion beam integrated over all masses. Some indication of the time involved in the determination should be given\, e.g. counting time or band width. The sensitivity should be differentiated from the detection limit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05606
name: sensitivity
def: "The slope of the calibration curve. If the curve is in fact a 'curve'\, rather than a straight line\, then of course sensitivity will be a function of analyte concentration or amount. If sensitivity is to be a unique performance characteristic\, it must depend only on the chemical measurement process\, not upon scale factors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05608
name: sensitization
def: "Addition of small amounts of a hydrophilic colloid to a hydrophobic sol to possibly make the latter more sensitive to flocculation by electrolyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05609
name: sensitized luminescence
def: "The deactivation of the primarily excited emitter which can lead to the activation of the quencher followed by radiative deactivation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05611
name: separability assumption
def: "This expression refers to the assumption\, essential to conventional transition-state theory\, that the energy of the system may be expressed as the sum of components associated with different degrees of freedom. In transition-state theory it is assumed that the energy of the motion of the system through the dividing surface of the potential-energy surface is separable into various components. In many practical calculations it is assumed that the energy of the system is separable into electronic\, vibrational\, rotational and translational energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05612
name: separated flame
def: "In a premix burner\, sometimes provision is made to screen the observed portion of the flame gases from direct contact with the surrounding air. This may be done either mechanically by placing a tube on top of the burner around the flame\, which produces a zonal separation \(separated flame\)\, or aerodynamically by surrounding the flame with a sheath of inert gas that emerges from openings at the rim of the burner top \(shielded flame\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05614
name: separation factor
def: "The relative retention value calculated for two adjacent peaks \(\\\(V_\{\\rm\{R\}2\}^\{'\}>V_\{\\rm\{R\}1\}^\{'\}\\\)\)\: \\\[\\alpha =\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}2\}^\{'\}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}1\}^\{'\}\}=\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{N\}2\}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{N\}1\}\}=\\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}2\}^\{'\}\}\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}1\}^\{'\}\}=\\frac\{k_\{2\}\}\{k_\{1\}\}\\\] By definition\, the value of the separation factor is always greater than unity. The separation factor is also identical to the ratio of the corresponding distribution constants. The separation factor is sometimes also called the 'selectivity'. The use of this expression is discouraged."
synonym: "separation coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05615
name: separation factor
def: "The ratio of the respective distribution ratios of two extractable solutes measured under the same conditions. \\\[\\alpha_\{\\rm\{A\,B\}\} = \\frac\{D_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\{D_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05616
name: separation number
def: "This expresses the number of peaks which can be resolved in a given part of the chromatogram between the peaks of two consecutive n-alkanes with \\\(z\\\) and \(\\\(z+1\\\)\) carbon atoms in their molecules\: \\\[\\rm\{SN\} = \\frac\{t_\{\\rm\{R\}\\left\(z\\\,+\\\,1\\right\)\}- t_\{\\rm\{R\}z\}\}\{w_\{\\rm\{h\}z\}\\\,+\\\,w_\{\\rm\{h\}\\left\(z+1\\right\)\}\} - 1\\\] In the German literature the symbol \\\(\\rm\{TZ\}\\\) \(trennzahl\) is commonly used to express the separation number. As the separation number depends on the n-alkanes used for the calculation\, they always must be specified with any given \\\(\\rm\{SN\}\\\) value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05618
name: separation temperature
def: "The temperature of the chromatographic bed under isothermal operation. In column chromatography it is called the column temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05620
name: sequencing
def: "Analytical procedures for the determination of the order of amino acids in a polypeptide chain or of nucleotides in a DNA or RNA molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05621
name: sequential analyser
def: "A discontinuous analyser in which at least one sub-assembly operates sequentially."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05622
name: sequential indication
def: "An indication obtained following sequential sampling or received from a sequential cell or from data processing comprising a succession of predetermined repetitive operations \(or a combination of the three\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05623
name: sequential measuring cell
def: "A measuring cell which operates according to a succession of operations on the sample or on the sensitive elements \(or on both\)\, these operations being carried out according to one or more repetitive programs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05624
name: sequential sample
def: "Units\, increments or samples taken one at a time or in successive predetermined groups\, until the cumulative result of their measurements \(typically applied to attributes\)\, as assessed against predetermined limits\, permits a decision to accept or reject the population or to continue sampling. The number of observations required is not determined in advance\, but the decision to terminate the operation depends\, at each stage\, on the results of the previous observations. The plan may have a practical\, automatic termination after a certain number of units have been examined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05625
name: sequential spectrometer
def: "A spectrometer which enables the intensity of several spectral bands of radiation to be measured one after the other in time\, i.e. sequentially."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05626
name: series
def: "A number of measured values \(\\\(x_\{1\}\,\\\,x_\{2\}\,\\\,...\\ x_\{i\}\,\\\,...\\ x_\{n\}\\\)\) equivalent to each other with respect to statistical considerations\, e.g. the results of repeated analyses using only one analytical method on a substance that is presumed to be homogeneous."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05627
name: sesquiterpenoids
def: "Terpenoids having a $\\ce\{C15\}$ skeleton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05628
name: sesterterpenoids
def: "Terpenoids having a $\\ce\{C25\}$ skeleton. Sometimes erroneously referred to as sesterpenoids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05629
name: settling chamber
def: "Chamber designed to reduce the velocity of gases in order to permit the settling out of fly ash. It may be either part of\, adjacent to\, or external to an incinerator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05630
name: settling error
def: "An error caused by allowing insufficient time for the reading to settle or if an absorption peak is scanned too rapidly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05631
name: settling velocity
def: "The terminal rate of fall of a particle through a fluid as induced by gravity or other external force."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05632
name: shape selectivity
def: "Possibly observed in catalysts with very small pores. The selectivity is largely determined by the bulk or size of one or more reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05634
name: shear dependent viscosity
def: "For systems showing non-Newtonian behaviour when measured in steady simple shear\, a coefficient \\\(\\eta \\\) equal to \\\(\\frac\{\\sigma\}\{D\}\\\) at a given value of the shear rate \\\(D\\\)\, where \\\(\\sigma \\\) is the stress; \\\(\\eta _\{0\}\\\) is the limiting viscosity at zero shear rate\, and \\\(\\eta _\{\\infty \}\\\) the limiting viscosity at infinite shear rate; \\\(\\rm\{\[\}\\eta _\{0\}\\rm\{\]\}\\\) is the limit of intrinsic viscosity at zero shear."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05635
name: shear modulus
def: "Shear stress divided by shear strain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05636
name: shear rate
def: "The velocity gradient in a flowing fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05637
name: shear strain
def: "Displacement of one surface with respect to another divided by the distance between them."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05638
name: shear stress
def: "Force acting tangentially to a surface divided by the area of the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05640
name: shear thinning
def: "If viscosity is a univalued function of the rate of shear\, a decrease of the viscosity with increasing rate of shear is called shear thinning\, and an increase of the viscosity shear thickening."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05641
name: shear transition
def: "A diffusionless transition that involves a change of the shape of the unit cell by a process that can be described as shear."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05642
name: shear viscosity
def: "For a Newtonian fluid\, the shear viscosity is often termed simply viscosity since in most situations it is the only one considered. It relates the shear components of stress and those of rate of strain at a point in the fluid by\: \\\[\\sigma _\{xy\}=\\sigma _\{yx\}=\\eta \\ \(\\frac\{\\partial \nu_\{x\}\}\{\\partial y\}+\\frac\{\\partial \nu_\{y\}\}\{\\partial x\}\)=2\\ \\eta \\ \\overset\{\\rm\{.\}\}\{\\gamma \}_\{yx\}\\\] where \\\(\\overset\{\\rm\{.\}\}\{\\gamma \}_\{yx\}\\\)\, the shear component of rate of strain is defined as follows\: \\\[\\overset\{\\rm\{.\}\}\{\\gamma \}_\{yx\}=\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\ \(\\frac\{\\partial \nu_\{x\}\}\{\\partial y\}+\\frac\{\\partial \nu_\{y\}\}\{\\partial x\}\)\\\] Corresponding relations hold for \\\(\\sigma _\{xz\}\\\) and \\\(\\sigma _\{yz\}\\\); \\\(\\sigma _\{xy\}\\\) is the component of stress acting in the \\\(y\\\)-direction on a plate normal to the \\\(x\\\)-axis; \\\(\nu_\{x\}\\\)\, \\\(\nu_\{y\}\\\)\, \\\(\nu_\{z\}\\\) are the components of velocity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05644
name: shielding
def: "In the context of NMR spectroscopy shielding is the effect of the electron shells of the observed and the neighbouring nuclei on the external magnetic field. The external field induces circulations in the electron cloud. The resulting magnetic moment is oriented in the opposite direction to the external field\, so that the local field at the central nucleus is weakened\, although it may be strengthened at other nuclei \(deshielding\). The phenomenon is the origin of the structural dependence of the resonance frequencies of the nuclei."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05645
name: shielding constant
def: "In NMR the difference between the external magnetic flux density and the local magnetic flux density at a resonating nucleus affected by the neighbouring electrons divided by the external flux density\, \\\(\\sigma =\\frac\{B_\{0\}\\\,-\\\,B\}\{B_\{0\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05646
name: shish-kebab structure
def: "A polycrystalline morphology of double habit consisting of fibrous crystals overgrown epitaxially by lamellar crystals\, the stems of which are parallel to the fibre axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05647
name: short chain
def: "A chain of low relative molecular mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05649
name: short-range intramolecular interactions
def: "A steric or other interaction involving atoms or groups or both situated nearby in sequence along the chain. The interacting atoms or groups are typically separated by fewer than ten consecutive bonds in a chain. If no confusion can occur\, the word 'intramolecular' may be omitted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05650
name: shut-down state
def: "The condition of an instrument when it is switched off to conserve energy or reagents or to protect working parts. This term is of particular importance in clinical chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05651
name: shut-down time
def: "The time interval between production of the last result of an instrument and shut-down state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05652
name: shuttle vector
def: "A DNA molecule \(e.g. plasmid\) that is able to replicate in two different host organisms and can therefore be used to 'shuttle' or convey genes from one to the other."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05653
name: SI
def: "Système International d'Unités\, the international system of units established in 1960 and based on seven base units for the quantities\: length\, time\, mass\, electric current\, thermodynamic temperature\, amount of substance and luminous intensity."
synonym: "International system of units" []
synonym: "Système International d'Unités" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05657
name: siemens
def: "SI derived unit of electric conductance\, \\\(\\pu\{S\} = \\pu\{\\Omega^\{-1\}\} = \\pu\{m-2 kg-1 s3 A2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05658
name: sievert
def: "SI derived unit of dose equivalent \(the mean specific energy imparted to an element of matter corrected by a quality factor and a modifying factor to take into account the properties of irradiated matter\) equal to one joule per kilogram and admitted for reasons of safeguarding human health."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05660
name: sigmatropic rearrangement
def: "A molecular rearrangement that involves both the creation of a new $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$ between atoms previously not directly linked and the breaking of an existing $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bond\}$. There is normally a concurrent relocation of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ in the molecule concerned\, but the total number of $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$ does not change. The term was originally restricted to intramolecular pericyclic reactions\, and many authors use it with this connotation. It is\, however\, also applied in a more general\, purely structural\, sense. If such reactions are intramolecular\, their transition state may be visualized as an association of two fragments connected at their termini by two partial $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}bonds\}$\, one being broken and the other being formed as\, for example\, the two allyl fragments in \($\\ce\{a'\}$\). Considering only atoms within the \(real or hypothetical\) cyclic array undergoing reorganization\, if the numbers of these in the two fragments are designated \\\(i\\\) and \\\(j\\\)\, then the rearrangement is said to be a sigmatropic change of order \[\\\(i\\\)\, \\\(j\\\)\] \(conventionally \\\(\\rm\{\[\}i\\rm\{\]\}\\leq \\rm\{\[\}j\\rm\{\]\}\\\)\). Thus the rearrangement \(a\) is of order\, whilst reaction \(b\) is asigmatropic shift of hydrogen. \(N.B. By convention square brackets here refer to numbers of atoms\, in contrast with current usage in the context of cycloaddition.\)   The descriptors a and s \(antarafacial and antarafacial\) may also be annexed to the numbers \\\(i\\\) and \\\(j\\\); \(b\) is then described as a \[1s\,5s\] sigmatropic rearrangement\, since it is suprafacial with respect both to the hydrogen atom and to the pentadienyl system\:  The prefix 'homo' \(meaning one extra atom\, interrupting conjugation — cf. 'homoaromaticity'\) has frequently been applied to sigmatropic rearrangements\, but is misleading."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05661
name: signal
def: "A representation of a quantity within an analytical instrument."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05663
name: silanes
def: "Saturated silicon hydrides\, analogues of the alkanes; i.e. compounds of the general formula $\\ce\{SinH2_\{n+2\}\}$. Silanes may be subdivided into silane\, oligosilanes and polysilanes. Note hydrocarbyl derivatives and other derivatives are often referred to loosely as silanes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05664
name: silanols
def: "In a strict sense\, hydroxy derivatives of silanes $\\ce\{Si_\{n\}H_\{2n+1\}OH\}$.A name commonly applied to Si-hydrocarbyl derivatives\, $\\ce\{R3SiOH\}$\, of silanol\, $\\ce\{H3SiOH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05665
name: silasesquiazanes
def: "Compounds in which every silicon atom is linked to three nitrogen atoms and every nitrogen atom is linked to two silicon atoms\, thus consisting of $\\ce\{SiH\}$ and $\\ce\{NH\}$ units\, and having the general formula $\\ce\{\(SiH\)_\{2n\}\(NH\)_\{3n\}\}$. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05666
name: silasesquioxanes
def: "Compounds in which every silicon atom is linked to three oxygen atoms and every oxygen atom is linked to two silicon atoms\, and having the general formula $\\ce\{\(SiH\)_\{2n\}O_\{3n\}\}$. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05667
name: silasesquithianes
def: "Compounds in which every silicon atom is linked to three sulfur atoms and every sulfur atom is linked to two silicon atoms\, and having the general formula $\\ce\{\(SiH\)_\{2n\}S_\{3n\}\}$. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05668
name: silathianes
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{H3Si\[SSiH2\]_\{n\}SSiH3\}$ and branched-chain analogues. They are analogous in structure to siloxanes with $\\ce\{–S\\bond\{–\}\}$ replacing $\\ce\{–O\\bond\{–\}\}$. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05669
name: silazanes
def: "Saturated silicon-nitrogen hydrides\, having straight or branched chains. They are analogous in structure to siloxanes with $\\ce\{–NH\\bond\{–\}\}$ replacing $\\ce\{–O\\bond\{–\}\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{H3SiNHSiH2NHSiH3\}$ trisilazane. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05670
name: silicones
def: "Polymeric or oligomeric siloxanes\, usually considered unbranched\, of general formula $\\ce\{\[–OSiR2–\]_\{n\}\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05671
name: siloxanes
def: "Saturated silicon-oxygen hydrides with unbranched or branched chains of alternating silicon and oxygen atoms \(each silicon atom is separated from its nearest silicon neighbours by single oxygen atoms\). The general structure of unbranched siloxanes is $\\ce\{H3Si\[OSiH2\]_\{n\}OSiH3\}$. $\\ce\{H3Si\[OSiH2\]_\{n\}OSiH\[OSiH2OSiH3\]2\}$ is an example of a branched siloxane. By extension hydrocarbyl derivatives are commonly included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05672
name: silver film
def: "When viewed in reflected white light against a black background\, transparent films show the classical interference colours of thin plates which permit an estimate of their thickness to be made. When of the order of \\\(100\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) \(\\\(1000\\ \\unicode\{x212B\}\\\)\) in thickness they appear white \(silver film\) and when thinner they appear gradually less intensely white\, then grey and finally black."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05673
name: silylene
def: "Generic name for $\\ce\{H2Si\{\:\}\}$ and substitution derivatives thereof\, containing an electrically neutral bivalent silicon atom with two non-bonding electrons. \(The definition is analogous to that given for carbene.\)The silanediyl group \($\\ce\{H2Si\}$\)\, analogous to the methylene group \($\\ce\{H2C\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05674
name: silyl groups
def: "In a strict sense\, the silyl group is $\\ce\{H3Si\\bond\{–\}\}$.A name commonly applied to hydrocarbyl derivatives of the silyl group $\\ce\{R3Si\\bond\{–\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05675
name: silyl radicals
def: "In a strict sense\, the silyl radical is $\\ce\{H3Si^\{.\}\}$.A name commonly applied to the silicon-centered radicals $\\ce\{R3Si^\{.\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05676
name: simple shear
def: "An idealized treatment of a fluid between two large parallel plates \(to permit ignoring edge effects\) of area \\\(A\\\)\, separated by a distance \\\(h\\\). If one plate moves relative to the other with a constant velocity \\\(V\\\)\, requiring a force \\\(F\\\) acting in the direction of movement\, and the density\, pressure\, and viscosity throughout the fluid are constant\, the Newtonian equation can be coupled with the equations of motion and of continuity to show that the velocity gradient in the fluid is constant \(= \\\(\\frac\{V\}\{h\}\\\)\)\, and that \\\(\\frac\{F\}\{A\} = \\frac\{\\eta \\ V\}\{h\}\\\). This idealized case \(simple shear\) is sometimes used to define shear viscosity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05677
name: simulation technique
def: "A technique for reducing or eliminating analytical errors resulting from interferences\, using a reference solution sufficiently similar in quantitative composition to the sample solutions to be analysed that the interferences in the reference and sample solution are equivalent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05679
name: simultaneous pair transitions
def: "Simultaneous electronic transitions in two coupled absorbers or emitters. Because of the coupling\, transitions which are spin-forbidden in one of the centres might become spin-allowed \(spin flip\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05680
name: simultaneous reactions
def: "Composite reactions\, in which processes like \\\[\\rm\{A\}\\ \\overset\{1\}\{\\rightarrow \}\\ \\rm\{Y\}\\\] and \\\[\\rm\{B\}\\ \\overset\{2\}\{\\rightarrow \}\\ \\rm\{Z\}\\\] occur in parallel\, can be called simultaneous or parallel reactions. Sometimes there is competition involved\, like in the scheme\: \\\[\\rm\{A\}\\\,+\\\,\\rm\{B\}\\ \\overset\{1\}\{\\rightarrow \}\\ \\rm\{Y\}\\\] and \\\[\\rm\{A\}\\\,+\\\,\\rm\{C\}\\ \\overset\{2\}\{\\rightarrow \}\\ \\rm\{Z\}\\\] where B and C compete with one another for A."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05681
name: simultaneous spectrometer
def: "A spectrometer which has more than one detector and enables the intensities of several spectral bands to be measured at the same time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05682
name: single-beam spectrometer
def: "A luminescence spectrometer in which a single beam of radiation is used for excitation and a single beam of luminescence radiation is taken from the sample."
synonym: "single-beam luminescence spectrometer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05683
name: single cell protein
def: "Microbial biomass or proteins extracted therefrom obtained from processes in which bacteria\, yeasts\, other fungi or algae are cultivated in large quantities as human or animal protein supplement in animal feed or in human nutrition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05685
name: single-electron transfer mechanism
def: "A reaction mechanism characterized by the transfer of a single electron between the species occurring on the reaction coordinate of one of the elementary steps."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05686
name: single escape peak
def: "In a $\\upgamma\\!\\mbox\{-\}\\rm\{ray\\ spectrum\}$\, the peak due to pair production in the detector and escape\, from the sensitive part of the detector\, of one of the photons of \\\(511\\ \\rm\{keV\}\\\) resulting from annihilation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05687
name: single-focusing mass spectrometer
def: "An instrument in which an ion beam with a given value of mass/charge is brought to a focus although the initial directions of the ions diverge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05689
name: single-photon timing
def: "Technique that permits recovery of the parameters characterizing a fluorescence decay after pulse excitation \(in particular excited-states lifetimes\). It is based on the creation of a time histogram of many stochastic events involving the time delay between the electronic excitation of a molecule or material and its emission of a photon from an excited state. A key to the technique is that no more than one photon strike the detector per pulsed excitation. Excitation is commonly achieved with a flash from a repetitive nanosecond lamp or diode laser or a CW operated laser \(mode-locked laser\). The essential components of the hardware are a device to measure the excitation-emission delay time and another to determine the relative frequency of photons reaching the detector at each delay time. Delay times are usually measured with a time-to-amplitude-converter \(TAC\)\, using voltage to measure the delay between a start and a stop signal. The frequency of events with each delay is stored in a multi-channel analyser. This term is preferred to time-correlated single-photon counting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05690
name: single scattering
def: "Radiation scattering in which there is only one scattering encounter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05691
name: single-step reaction
def: "A reaction that proceeds through a single transition state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05692
name: single-strand chain
def: "A chain that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through two atoms\, one on each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05693
name: single-strand macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule that comprises constitutional unit connected in such a way that adjacent constitutional units are joined to each other through two atoms\, one on each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05694
name: single-strand polymer
def: "A polymer the macromolecules of which are single-strand macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05695
name: singlet molecular oxygen
def: "Oxygen molecule \(dioxygen\)\, $\\ce\{O2\}$\, in an excited singlet state. The ground state of $\\ce\{O2\}$ is a triplet \(\\\(^\{3\}\\Sigma_\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{-\}\\\)\). The two metastable singlet states derived from the ground-state configuration are \\\(^\{1\}\\Delta_\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\) and \\\(^\{1\}\\Sigma_\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{+\}\\\) \(the latter with the higher energy\)."
synonym: "singlet molecular dioxygen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05696
name: singlet-singlet absorption
def: "Absorption which results in the transition from the singlet ground state of the molecule into singlet excited states \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{0\}\\\) → \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\)\) and leads to the UV/visible absorption spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05698
name: singlet-singlet energy transfer
def: "Transfer of excitation from an electronically excited donor in a singlet state to produce an electronically excited acceptor in a singlet state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05699
name: singlet state
def: "A state having a total electron spin quantum number equal to 0."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05700
name: singlet-triplet absorption
def: "Absorption which takes place with the transition from the singlet ground state of the molecule to triplet states \(\\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{0\}\\\) → \\\(\\rm\{T\}_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\)\) and results in the singlet-triplet absorption spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05701
name: singlet-triplet energy transfer
def: "Transfer of excitation from an electronically excited donor in a singlet state to produce an electronically excited acceptor in a triplet state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05702
name: singly labelled
def: "A specifically labelled compound in which the isotopically substituted molecule has only one isotopically modified atom e.g. $\\ce\{CH3–CH\[^\{2\}H\]–OH\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05703
name: sink
def: "The receptor for material which is removed from the atmosphere. Because of long range transport of many pollutants such as $\\ce\{SO2\}$\, sulfuric acid and its salts\, the sink region can be many hundreds of kilometres from the source region of the pollutants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05704
name: sintering
def: "Temperature-induced coalescence and densification of porous solid particles below the melting points of their major components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05705
name: size-exclusion chromatography
def: "A separation technique in which separation mainly according to the hydrodynamic volume of the molecules or particles takes place in a porous non-adsorbing material with pores of approximately the same size as the effective dimensions in solution of the molecules to be separated."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05706
name: skeletal atom
def: "An atom of a skeletal structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05707
name: skeletal bond
def: "A bond connecting two skeletal atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05708
name: skeletal structure
def: "The sequence of atoms in the constitutional unit\(s\) of a macromolecule\, an oligomer molecule\, a block or a chain which defines the essential topological representation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05710
name: Slater-type orbital
def: "An approximate atomic orbital attempts to allow for electron-electron repulsion by scaling the nuclear charge for each orbital."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05712
name: slow neutrons
def: "Neutrons of kinetic energy less than some specified value. This value may vary over a wide range and depends on the application. In reactor physics\, the value is frequently chosen to be \\\(1\\ \\rm\{eV\}\\\); in dosimetry\, the effective cadmium cut-off is used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05713
name: small particle
def: "A particle much smaller than the wavelength of the radiation in the medium. In practice\, all dimensions of a particle considered small must be less than about one-twentieth of the wavelength employed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05715
name: SM-interference
def: "Interference by a substance that produces a signal by a similar mechanism to the analyte\, which in the given procedure \(including the apparatus used\) can not be distinguished from the signal given by the analyte. For example\, in a colour reaction with an organic ligand by also reacting with the ligand and producing a coloured compound absorbing at the same wavelength as the compound produced by the analyte or in an atomic spectrochemical analysis by absorbing or emitting at the same wavelength as the analyte."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05716
name: smog
def: "The term originated in Great Britain as a popular derivation of 'smoke-fog' and appears to have been in common use before World War 1. It originally referred to the heavy pollution derived largely from coal burning \(largely smoke filled air\, rich in sulfur dioxide\)\, and it probably was largely a reducing atmosphere. More common today in cities is an oxidizing atmosphere which contains ozone and other oxidants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05717
name: smog chamber
def: "A large confined volume in which sunlight or simulated sunlight is allowed to irradiate air mixtures of atmospheric trace gases \(hydrocarbons\, nitrogen oxides\, sulfur dioxide\, etc.\) which undergo oxidation. In theory these chambers allow the controlled study of complex reactions which occur in the atmosphere. However\, ill-defined wall reactions which generate some molecular and radical species \(e.g. $\\ce\{HONO\}$\, $\\ce\{CH2O\}$\, $\\ce\{HO\}$-radicals\, etc.\) and remove certain products \($\\ce\{H2O2\}$\, $\\ce\{HNO3\}$\, etc.\)\, the use of reactant concentrations well above those in the atmosphere\, ill-defined light intensities and wavelength distribution within the chamber\, and other factors peculiar to chamber experiments require that caution be exercised in the extrapolation of results obtained from them to atmospheric systems."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05718
name: smog index
def: "A mathematical correlation between smog and meteorological and/or pollutant concentrations associated with it. These are qualitative indices which are sometimes used in some urban communities to predict the degree of the air pollution which is expected for the coming day."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05719
name: smoke
def: "An aerosol originating from combustion\, thermal decomposition or thermal evaporation. Its particles may be solid \(magnesium oxide smoke\) or liquid \(tobacco smoke\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05721
name: soap
def: "A salt of a fatty acid\, saturated or unsaturated\, containing at least eight carbon atoms or a mixture of such salts. A neat soap is a lamellar structure containing much \(e.g. $\\pu\{75\\%\}$\) soap and little \(e.g. $\\pu\{25\\%\}$\) water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05722
name: soap curd
def: "A gel-like mixture of fibrous soap-crystals \('curd-fibres'\) and their saturated solution \(not a mesomorphic phase\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05723
name: soap film
def: "A term established by usage for A/W/A films stabilized by surfactants although it is not a film of soap\, nor is the stabilizing surfactant necessarily a soap. \(A/W/A means water film in air\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05726
name: soiling
def: "Visible damage to materials by deposition of air pollutants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05727
name: sol
def: "A fluid colloidal system of two or more components\, e.g. a protein sol\, a gold sol\, an emulsion\, a surfactant solution above the critical micelle concentration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05728
name: solar conversion efficiency
def: "The ratio of the Gibbs energy gain per unit time per \\\(\\mathrm\{m\}^\{2\}\\\) of surface exposed to the sun and the solar irradiance\, \\\(E\\\)\, integrated between \\\(\\lambda = 0\\\) and \\\(\\lambda = \\infty \\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05729
name: solar flare
def: "A bright eruption of energy from the sun's chromosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05730
name: solar radiation
def: "The electromagnetic radiation emitted by the sun. The total range of wavelengths of light emitted by the sun \(99.9% in the range from \\\(150\\ \\rm\{to\}\\ 4000\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\) is filtered on entering the earth's atmosphere\, largely through the absorption by oxygen\, ozone\, water vapour and carbon dioxide. Near sea level only light of wavelengths longer than about \\\(290\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) is present. The light from \\\(290\{-\}400\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) is effective in inducing important photochemical processes since absorption by the important trace gases\, ozone\, nitrogen dioxide\, aldehydes\, ketones\, etc.\, is significant in this region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05731
name: sol-gel transition
def: "Transition of a sol to a gel at the gel point."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05732
name: solid angle
def: "Of a cone\, the ratio of the area cut out on a spherical surface \(with its centre at the apex of that cone\) to the square of the radius of the sphere. It is a quantity of dimension one \(dimensionless quantity\) with unit steradian \(\\\(\\mathrm\{sr\}=1\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05733
name: solidification
def: "The transition of a liquid or gas into a solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05734
name: solid phase antibody radioimmunoassay
def: "A kind of radioimmunoassay employing an antibody bound to a solid phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05736
name: solid state lasers
def: "CW or pulsed lasers in which the active medium is a solid matrix \(crystal or glass\) doped with an ion \(e.g. $\\ce\{Nd^\{3+\}\}$\, $\\ce\{Cr^\{3+\}\}$\, $\\ce\{Eu^\{3+\}\}$\). The emitted wavelength depends on the active ion\, the selected optical transition\, and the matrix. Some of these lasers are tunable within a very broad range \(e.g. from \\\(700\\\) to \\\(1000\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) for $\\ce\{Ti^\{3+\}\}$ doped sapphire\). Pulsed lasers may be free-running\, Q-switched\, or mode-locked. Some CW lasers may be mode-locked."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05737
name: solid support
def: "Normally an inert porous solid\, which sorbs the liquid phase. The particle-size range of the support affects column efficiency and the pressure differential necessary to achieve a given flow rate. Modifications have been introduced for the achievement of special separations\, in which the solid support is not inert but is an active solid. In capillary columns the inner wall of the column serves as the solid support and obviates the use of additional porous solids for this purpose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05738
name: solidus
def: "A line on a binary phase diagram \(or a surface on a ternary phase diagram\) that indicates the temperature at which a system becomes completely solid on cooling or at which melting begins on heating under equilibrium conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05739
name: solid volume
def: "The volume occupied by the solid or the active solid in the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05740
name: solubility
def: "The analytical composition of a saturated solution\, expressed in terms of the proportion of a designated solute in a designated solvent\, is the solubility of that solute. The solubility may be expressed as a concentration\, molality\, mole fraction\, mole ratio\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05742
name: solubility product
def: "The product of the ion activities raised to appropriate powers of an ionic solute in its saturated solution expressed with due reference to the dissociation equilibria involved and the ions present."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05744
name: solute
def: "The minor component of a solution which is regarded as having been dissolved by the solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05745
name: solute-volatilization interference
def: "Interference due to changes in the volatilization rate of the dry aerosol particles in the case when volatilization of the analyte is incomplete in the presence and/or absence of the concomitant. This interference can either be specific\, if the analyte and interferent form a new phase of different thermostability\, as when $\\ce\{Mg\}$ and $\\ce\{Al\}$ form $\\ce\{MgAl2O4\}$ in an air–acetylene flame\, or non-specific\, if the analyte is simply dispersed in a large excess of the interferent\, as when $\\ce\{Ag\}$ is dispersed in $\\ce\{ThO2\}$. If the interferent has a high boiling point\, this latter is sometimes referred to as a blocking interference. It is often difficult to make sharp distinctions between the specific and non-specific solute-volatilization interferences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05746
name: solution
def: "A liquid or solid phase containing more than one substance\, when for convenience one \(or more\) substance\, which is called the solvent\, is treated differently from the other substances\, which are called solutes. When\, as is often but not necessarily the case\, the sum of the mole fractions of solutes is small compared with unity\, the solution is called a dilute solution. A superscript attached to the \\\(\\infty \\\) symbol for a property of a solution denotes the property in the limit of infinite dilution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05747
name: solvation
def: "Any stabilizing interaction of a solute \(or solute moiety\) and the solvent or a similar interaction of solvent with groups of an insoluble material \(i.e. the ionic groups of an ion-exchange resin\). Such interactions generally involve electrostatic forces and van der Waals forces\, as well as chemically more specific effects such as hydrogen bond formation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05748
name: solvatochromic relationship
def: "A linear free-energy relationship based on solvatochromism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05749
name: solvatochromism
def: "The \(pronounced\) change in position and sometimes intensity of an electronic absorption or emission band\, accompanying a change in the polarity of the medium. Negative \(positive\) solvatochromism corresponds to a hypsochromic \(bathochromic\) shift with increasing solvent polarity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05751
name: solvent
def: "The term applied to the whole initial liquid phase containing the extractant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05752
name: solvent extraction
def: "The process of transferring a substance from any matrix to an appropriate liquid phase. If the substance is initially present as a solute in an immiscible liquid phase the process is synonymous with liquid-liquid extraction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05753
name: solvent front
def: "The front line of the eluent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05755
name: solvent isotope effect
def: "A kinetic or equilibrium isotope effect resulting from change in the isotopic composition of the solvent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05756
name: solvent migration-distance
def: "The distance travelled by the solvent front."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05757
name: solvent parameter
def: "Quantitative measures of the capability of solvents for interaction with solutes. Such parameters have been based on numerous different physicochemical quantities\, e.g. rate constants\, solvatochromic shifts in ultraviolet/visible spectra\, solvent-induced shifts in infrared frequencies\, etc. Some solvent parameters are purely empirical in nature\, i.e. they are based directly on some experimental measurement. It may be possible to interpret such a parameter as measuring some particular aspect of solvent–solute interaction or it may be regarded simply as a measure of solvent polarity. Other solvent parameters are based on analysing experimental results. Such a parameter is considered to quantify some particular aspect of solvent capability for interaction with solutes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05758
name: solvent regeneration
def: "Treatment of the solvent for re-cycling\, e.g. by removal of degradation products or non-strippable solutes. The term solvent purification is synonymous\, but the terms scrubbing\, stripping and washing should not be used in this context."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05761
name: solvent shift
def: "A shift in the frequency of a spectral band of a chemical species arising from interaction with its solvent environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05762
name: solvolysis
def: "Generally\, reaction with a solvent\, or with a lyonium ion or lyate ion involving the rupture of one or more bonds in the reacting solute. More specifically the term is used for substitution elimination and fragmentation reactions in which a solvent species is the nucleophile \('alcoholysis' if the solvent is an alcohol\, etc.\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05763
name: solvophobicity parameter
def: "A solvent parameter defined by\: \\\[Sp = 1 - \\frac\{M\}\{M\\left\(\\rm\{hexadecane\}\\right\)\}\\\] derived from the Gibbs energy of transfer \(\\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{t\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\) of a series of solutes from water to numerous aqueous-organic mixtures and to pure solvents\: \\\[\\Delta _\{\\rm\{t\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{to solvent\}\\right\) = M\\ R_\{\\rm\{T\}\}+D\\\] where \\\(R_\{\\rm\{T\}\}\\\) is a solute parameter\, and \\\(M\\\) and \\\(D\\\) characterize the solvent. The \\\(M\\\) values are used to define a solvent solvophobic effect so that \\\(Sp\\\) values are scaled from unity \(water\) to zero \(hexadecane\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05764
name: solvus
def: "A line on a binary phase diagram \(or a surface on a ternary phase diagram\) that defines the limit of solid solubility under equilibrium conditions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05765
name: SOMO
def: "A Singly Occupied Molecular Orbital \(such as the half-filled HOMO of a radical\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05766
name: sonication
def: "Irradiation with \(often ultra\)sound waves\, e.g. to increase the rate of a reaction or to prepare vesicles in mixtures of surfactants and water."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05767
name: sonoluminescence
def: "Luminescence induced by sound waves."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05768
name: soot
def: "A randomly formed particulate carbon material and may be coarse\, fine and/or colloidal in proportions depending on its origin. Soot consists of variable quantities of carbonaceous and inorganic solids together with absorbed and occluded tars and resins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05769
name: sorption
def: "The process by which a substance \(sorbate\) is sorbed \(adsorbed or absorbed\) on or in another substance \(sorbent\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05770
name: sorption isotherm
def: "The concentration of a sorbed species in the ion exchanger\, expressed as a function of its concentration in the external solution under specified conditions and at constant temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05771
name: sorption techniques
def: "Techniques based on the distribution of components being separated between two phases\, one of which is stationary and the other mobile. The advantage of some chromatographic methods is the possibility of combining the preconcentration and determination steps\, as well as improving the speed of determination\, and the possibility of separation of components with similar properties\, and of achieving high values of the preconcentration coefficient. The methods enable analyses of microamounts of substances. ion-exchange chromatography is not widely used owing to the great volumes of solutions being treated and\, consequently\, to a great degree to the variation in the blank\, and to some diffusional limitations. Static ion-exchange\, which is much simpler and more readily carried out\, is practised quite widely."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05772
name: sorptive insertion
def: "This is analogous to the process of ligand insertion in coordination chemistry. $$\\ce\{H^\{\\ast\} + C2H4\(g\) -> ^\{\\ast\}C2H5\}$$ This reaction might also be imagined to proceed by adsorption of $\\ce\{C2H4\}$ followed by ligand migration \(an associative surface reaction\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05776
name: space charge
def: "At the surface region of a semiconductor the surface excess or deficiency of electrons constitutes a space charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05777
name: space formula
def: "Synonymous with stereochemical formula."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05778
name: space time
def: "The reciprocal of space velocity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05779
name: space velocity
def: "Defined as \\\(v_\{m\}\\\)\, \\\(v_\{v\}\\\)\, and \\\(v_\{a\}\\\) where the \\\(v_\{i\}\\\)s represent the rate of feed of the given reactant fed per unit mass\, volume or surface area of the catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05780
name: spark ionization
def: "Occurs when a solid sample is vaporized and partially ionized by an intermittent discharge. It is recommended that the word 'source' be dropped from this term."
synonym: "spark source ionization" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05781
name: spatial-distribution interference
def: "Interference which may occur when changes in concentration of concomitants affect the mass flow rates or mass flow patterns of the analyte species in the flame. If they are caused by changes in volume and rise velocity of the gases formed by combustion\, in extreme cases manifesting themselves by changes in the size and/or shape of the flame\, they are non-specific and are called flame-geometry interferences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05782
name: spatially resolving detector
def: "A detector for the measurement of the spatial distribution of the radiation. Spatially resolving detectors can be divided into two groups\:   the photosensitive area consists of a matrix of discrete photosensitive elements\, the pixels \(picture elements\) forming an array with the facility to separately read out the information\, simultaneously or sequentially.   the photosensitive area consists of a single photosensitive element that must be scanned \(e.g. image dissection tube.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05783
name: special salt effect
def: "The initial steep rate increase observed in the kinetic electrolyte effect on certain solvolysis reactions\, upon addition of some non-common ion salts\, especially $\\ce\{LiClO4\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05786
name: species
def: "A taxonomic subdivision of a genus; a group of closely related\, morphologically and physiologically similar individuals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05787
name: specific
def: "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by mass. Specific volume is the volume of a sample divided by its mass."
synonym: "specific quantity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05788
name: specific
def: "A term which expresses qualitatively the extent to which other substances interfere with the determination of a substance according to a given procedure. Specific is considered to be the ultimate of selective\, meaning that no interferences are supposed to occur."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05789
name: specific acid–base catalysis
def: "Catalysis by acids or bases in solution is said to be specific when the only observable catalytic effects are those due to the ions formed from the solvent itself \(e.g. if when water is the solvent the only observable catalysis is that due to the $\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{OH^\{−\}\}$ ions\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05790
name: specific activity
def: "For a specified isotopes\, or mixture of isotopes\, the activity of a material divided by the mass of the material."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05791
name: specific adsorption
def: "Ions become specifically adsorbed when short-range interactions between them and the interphase become important. They are believed then to penetrate into the inner layer and may \(but not necessarily\) come into contact with the surface. They are usually assumed to form a partial or complete monolayer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05792
name: specifically absorbing ion
def: "Ions which possess a chemical affinity for the surface in addition to the Coulomb interaction\, where chemical is a collective adjective\, embracing all interactions other than purely Coulombic. Examples are van der Waals or hydrophobic bonding\, pi-electron exchange and complex formation. Specifically adsorbing ions can adsorb on an initially uncharged surface and hence provide it with a charge. The term specifically adsorbed applies to the sorption of all other ions having an affinity to the surface in addition to the purely Coulombic contribution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05793
name: specifically labelled
def: "An isotopically labelled compound is designated as specifically labelled when a unique isotopically substituted compound is formally added to the analogous isotopically unmodified compound. In such a case\, both position\(s\) and number of each labelling nuclide are defined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05794
name: specifically labelled tracer
def: "A tracer in which the label is present in a specified position."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05795
name: specific burn-up
def: "The total energy released through induced nuclear transformations divided by the mass of a nuclear fuel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05796
name: specific catalysis
def: "The acceleration of a reaction by a unique catalyst\, rather than by a family of related substances. The term is most commonly used in connection with specific hydrogen-ion or hydroxide-ion \(lyonium ion or lyate ion\) catalysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05798
name: specific detector
def: "A detector which responds to a single sample component or to a limited number of components having similar chemical characteristics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05800
name: specific heat capacity
def: "Heat capacity divided by mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05801
name: specific ionization
def: "The number of ion pairs formed per unit distance along the track of an ionizing particle passing through matter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05802
name: specific permeability
def: "A term expressing the resistance of an empty tube or packed column to the flow of a fluid \(the mobile phase\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05804
name: specific pore volume
def: "The total internal void volume per unit mass of adsorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05806
name: specific surface area
def: "When the area of the interface between two phases is proportional to the mass of one of the phases \(e.g. for a solid adsorbent\, for an emulsion or for an aerosol\)\, the specific surface area \(\\\(a\\\)\,\\\(s\\\) or preferably \\\(a_\{s\}\\\)\) is defined as the surface area divided by the mass of the relevant phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05807
name: specific volume
def: "Volume of a substance divided by its mass. It is the reciprocal of mass density."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05809
name: specimen
def: "A specifically selected portion of a material taken from a dynamic system and assumed to be representative of the parent material at the time it is taken. Although the specimen may not be reproducible in time\, e.g. it may be taken from a flowing stream or a portion of blood\, no separable sampling error exists since this error is unavoidably included with the corresponding error of the estimate of the property\, function or analyte being studied. A specimen may be considered as a special type of sample\, taken primarily in time rather than in space. The term 'specimen' has been used both as a representative unit and as a nonrepresentative \(often better than most\) unit of a population\, usually in clinical\, biological and mineralogical collections. 'Collections' in this case is used as either a noun or verb. This usage is almost always self-evident\, and thus would not be confused with a time-type sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05810
name: spectator mechanism
def: "A pre-association mechanism in which one of the molecular entities\, C\, is already present in an encounter pair with A during formation of B from A\, but does not assist the formation of B\, e.g.  The formation of B from A may itself be a bimolecular reaction with some other reagent. Since C does not assist the formation of A\, it is described as being present as a spectator\, and hence such a mechanism is sometimes referred to as a spectator mechanism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05811
name: spectator-stripping reaction
def: "An extreme type of stripping reaction in which one reaction product has almost the same direction and momentum as one of the reactant molecules had before the reactive collision occurred."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05812
name: spectral bandwidth error
def: "To measure the true shape\, particularly the true maximum of an absorption band\, the spectral bandwidth \\\(\\Delta \\lambda \\\) of the instrument must be much less than the width of the absorption band. A spectral bandwidth error results from using too large a bandwidth relative to the absorption band being measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05813
name: spectral distribution
def: "The variation of the spectral radiance with wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05814
name: spectral effectiveness
def: "The reciprocal of the photon fluence rate\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{p\}\}^\{0\}\\\)\, at wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\)\, causing identical photoresponse\, \\\(\\Delta \\rm\{y\}\\\)\, per unit time \(\\\(\\frac\{\\Delta \\rm\{y\}\}\{\\Delta t\}\\\)\). The effectiveness spectrum is directly proportional to the conversion spectrum of the sensory pigment\, if spectral attenuance is negligible."
synonym: "effectiveness" []
synonym: "spectral photon effectiveness" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05816
name: spectral interference
def: "Interference due to the incomplete isolation of the radiation emitted or absorbed by the analyte from other radiation detected by the instrument. Its occurrence may be established by comparing the measures of the analyte-free blank solution and the solvent blank."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05817
name: spectral irradiance
def: "Irradiance\, \\\(E\\\)\, at wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-3\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05818
name: spectral overlap
def: "In the context of radiative energy transfer\, the integral\, \\\(J = \\int_\{0\}^\{\\infty\} f_\{\\rm\{D\}\}^\{\\prime\}\(\\sigma\)\\ \\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\(\\sigma\) \{\\rm\{d\}\}\\sigma \\\)\, which measures the overlap of the emission spectrum of the excited donor\, D\, and the absorption spectrum of the ground state acceptor\, A; \\\(f_\{\\rm\{D\}\}^\{'\}\\\) is the measured normalized emission of D\, \\\(f_\{\\rm\{D\}\}^\{'\}=\\frac\{f_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\left\(\\sigma \\right\)\}\{\\int _\{0\}^\{\\infty \}f_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\left\(\\sigma \\right\)  \\ \\mathrm\{d\}\\sigma \}\\\)\, \\\(f_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\(\\sigma\)\\\) is the photon exitance of the donor at wavenumber \\\(\\sigma \\\)\, and \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\(\\sigma\)\\\) is the decadic molar absorption coefficient of A at wavenumber \\\(\\sigma \\\). In the context of Förster excitation transfer\, \\\(J\\\) is given by\: \\\[J = \\int_\{0\}^\{\\infty\} \\frac\{f_\{\\rm\{D\}\}^\{^\\prime\}\(\\sigma\)\\ \\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\(\\sigma\)\}\{\\sigma^\{4\}\} \{\\rm\{d\}\}\\sigma \\\] In the context of Dexter excitation transfer\, \\\(J\\\) is given by\: \\\[J = \\int_\{0\}^\{\\infty\} f_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\(\\sigma\)\\ \\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\(\\sigma\) \{\\rm\{d\}\}\\sigma \\\] In this case \\\(f_\{\\rm\{D\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\)\, the emission spectrum of donor and absorption spectrum of acceptor\, respectively\, are both normalized to unity\, so that the rate constant for energy transfer\, \\\(k_\{\\rm\{ET\}\}\\\)\, is independent of the oscillator strength of both transitions \(contrast to Förster mechanism\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05819
name: spectral photon exitance
def: "The photon exitance\, \\\(M_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, at wavelength per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-3\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\). Alternatively\, the term can be used with the amount of photons \(mol or its equivalent einstein\) the SI unit then being \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1 m-3\}\\\) and the common unit \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1 m-2 nm-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05820
name: spectral photon flow
def: "The photon flow\, \\\(\\varPhi_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, at wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\). Alternatively\, the term can be used with the amount of photons \(mol or its equivalent einstein\)\, the SI unit then being \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1 m-1\}\\\) and the common unit \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1 nm-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05821
name: spectral photon flux
def: "The photon irradiance \\\(E_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, at wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-3\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\). Alternatively\, the term can be used with the amount of photons \(mol or its equivalent einstein\)\, the SI unit then being \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1 m-3\}\\\) and the common unit \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1 m-2 nm-1\}\\\)."
synonym: "photon irradiance" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05822
name: spectral photon radiance
def: "The photon radiance\, \\\(L_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\)\, at wavelength per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-3\}\\ \\rm\{sr\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{sr\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\). Alternatively\, the term can be used with the amount of photons \(mol or its equivalent einstein\) the SI unit then being \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1 m-3 sr-1\}\\\) and the common unit \\\(\\pu\{mol s-1 m-2 sr-1 nm-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05823
name: spectral quantities
def: "Quantities characterizing electromagnetic radiation \(radiant power\, energy\, energy density\, intensity\, exitance\, radiance\, irradiance\, etc.\) derived by differentiation with respect to wavelength\, frequency or wavenumber\, e.g. spectral \(concentration of\) irradiance is the derivative of the irradiance with respect to wavelength \(\\\(E_\{\\lambda \}\\\)\)\, frequency \(\\\(E_\{\nu \}\\\)\) or wavenumber \(\\\(E_\{\\stackrel\{˜\}\{\nu \}\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05824
name: spectral radiance
def: "The radiance\, \\\(L\\\)\, at wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-3\}\\ \\rm\{sr\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{sr\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05825
name: spectral radiant exitance
def: "The radiant exitance\, \\\(M\\\)\, at wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-3\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05826
name: spectral radiant flux
def: "Synonymous with spectral radiant power."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05827
name: spectral radiant intensity
def: "The radiation intensity\, \\\(I\\\) at wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{sr\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\rm\{W nm\}^\{-1\}\\ \\rm\{sr\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05828
name: spectral radiant power
def: "The radiant power at wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\) per unit wavelength interval. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\rm\{W nm\}^\{-1\}\\\)."
synonym: "total radiant power" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05829
name: spectral responsivity
def: "The spectral output quantity of a system such as a photomultiplier\, diode array\, photoimaging device\, or biological unit divided by the spectral irradiance \\\(s\(\\lambda\) = \\frac\{\\rm\{d\}y\\left\(\\lambda \\right\)\}\{\\rm\{d\}E\\left\(\\lambda \\right\)\}\\\)\, simplified expression\: \\\(s\(\\lambda\) = \\frac\{Y_\{\\lambda \}\}\{E_\{\\lambda \}\}\\\)\, where \\\(Y_\{\\lambda \}\\\) is the magnitude of the output signal for irradiation at wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\) and \\\(E_\{\\lambda \}\\\) is the spectral irradiance of the parallel and perpendicular incident beam at the same wavelength."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05831
name: spectral sensitization
def: "The process of increasing the spectral responsivity of a \(photoimaging\) system in a given wavelength region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05832
name: spectral spheradiance
def: "Alternative term suggested for actinic flux."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05833
name: spectrochemical buffer
def: "Buffer added to samples and reference samples with the intention of making the measure of the analytical element less sensitive to changes in concentration of an interferent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05834
name: spectrochemical carrier
def: "An additive which gives rise to a gas which can help to transport the vapour of the sample material into the excitation region of the source\, e.g. carbon in an air atmosphere when carbon dioxide is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05835
name: spectrogram
def: "A spectrum as recorded by a spectrometer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05836
name: spectrograph
def: "A combination of a spectral apparatus and a camera\, which enables an image of a spectrum to be recorded. Spectra are recorded by a photographic emulsion or other means\, e.g. two-dimensional electronic image sensors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05837
name: spectrometer
def: "A general term for describing a combination of spectral apparatus with one or more detectors to measure the intensity of one or more spectral bands."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05847
name: spectroscope
def: "A device which enables visual observation and evaluation of optical spectra \(usually confined to the visible spectral region\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05848
name: spectroscopy
def: "The study of physical systems by the electromagnetic radiation with which they interact or that they produce. Spectrometry is the measurement of such radiations as a means of obtaining information about the systems and their components. In certain types of optical spectroscopy\, the radiation originates from an external source and is modified by the system\, whereas in other types\, the radiation originates within the system itself."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05849
name: spectrum analysis
def: "The interpretation of the information present in an energy spectrum in terms of radiation energy and intensity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05851
name: specular reflectance
def: "Radiant power specularly reflected from the surface of a system\, \\\(P_\{\\mathrm\{refl\}\}\\\)\, divided by the incident power\, \\\(P_\{0\}\\\)\: \\\[\\rho =\\frac\{P_\{\\mathrm\{refl\}\}\}\{P_\{0\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "reflection factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05852
name: speed
def: "Scalar quantity equal to the absolute value of the velocity vector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05853
name: speed distribution function
def: "Probability density of finding a particle with speed within an interval between \\\(c\\\) and \\\(c+\\mathrm\{d\}c\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05854
name: speed of light in a vacuum
def: "The speed of light in vacuum\, \\\(c\\\)\, is one of seven fixed constants defining the International System of Units\, the SI\, with \\\(c = \\pu\{299792458 m s-1\}\\\). The speed of light is considered as a fundamental constant of nature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05856
name: spherical carbonaceous mesophase
def: "A term which describes the morphology of carbonaceous mesophase which is formed in the isotropic pitch matrix. The spherical carbonaceous mesophase usually has a lamellar structure consisting of flat aromatic molecules arranged in parallel layers which are perpendicular to the sphere/isotropic phase interface. On coalescence\, this spherical mesophase loses its characteristic morphology and is converted to the bulk mesophase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05857
name: spherical radiance
def: "Same as radiant exitance\, \\\(M\\\). It is the integration of the radiant power\, \\\(P\\\)\, leaving a source over the solid angle and over the whole wavelength range."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05858
name: spherical radiant exposure
def: "Synonymous with fluence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05859
name: spherulite
def: "A polycrystalline\, roughly spherical morphology consisting of lath\, fibrous or lamellar crystals emanating from a common centre. Space filling is achieved by branching\, bending or both\, of the constituent fibres or lamellae."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05861
name: spin-allowed electronic transition
def: "An electronic transition which does not involve a change in the spin part of the wavefunction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05862
name: spin conservation rule
def: "For both radiative and radiationless transitions\, the principle that transitions between terms of the same multiplicity are spin-allowed\, while transitions between terms of different multiplicity are spin-forbidden."
synonym: "Wigner rule" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05864
name: spin density
def: "The unpaired electron density at a position of interest\, usually at carbon\, in a radical. It is often measured experimentally by electron paramagnetic resonance \[EPR\, ESR \(electron spin resonance\)\] spectroscopy through hyperfine coupling constants of the atom or an attached hydrogen."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05865
name: spin-flip transition
def: "A rotation of electron spins\, above a critical magnetic field\, \\\(H_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\)\, in an antiferromagnet from parallel to largely perpendicular alignment\, relative to an applied magnetic field\, \\\(H_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\\\)\, for \\\(H_\{\\rm\{a\}\}>H_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05867
name: spin-glass transition
def: "A second-order transition from a paramagnetic or ferromagnetic state to a spin-glass state in which spins from moment-bearing solute atoms become ordered randomly in a non-magnetic host such that the net magnetization of any region is zero. Examples\: $\\ce\{Au–Fe\}$\, $\\ce\{Cu–Mn\}$ and $\\ce\{Mo–Fe\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05868
name: spin label
def: "A stable paramagnetic group \(typically a nitryl radical\) that is attached to a part of a molecular entity whose microscopic environment is of interest and may be revealed by the electron spin resonance \(ESR\) spectrum of the spin label. When a simple paramagnetic molecular entity is used in this way without covalent attachment to the molecular entity of interest it is frequently referred to as a 'spin probe'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05869
name: spinodal decomposition
def: "A clustering reaction in a homogeneous\, supersaturated solution \(solid or liquid\) which is unstable against infinitesimal fluctuations in density or composition. The solution therefore separates spontaneously into two phases\, starting with small fluctuations and proceeding with a decrease in the Gibbs energy without a nucleation barrier."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05870
name: spin–orbit coupling
def: "The interaction of the electron spin magnetic moment with the magnetic moment due to the orbital motion of the electron. One consequence of spin-orbit coupling is the mixing of zero-order states of different multiplicity. This effect may result in fine structure called spin-orbit splitting."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05871
name: spin–orbit coupling constant
def: "Coefficient in the spin–orbit coupling term in the hamiltonian."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05872
name: spin-orbit splitting
def: "Removal of state degeneracy by spin–orbit coupling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05873
name: spin-Peierls transition
def: "A magneto-elastic transition that occurs in quasi one-dimensional antiferromagnetic materials when the magnetic free energy decrease due to the formation of singlet spin pairs outweighs the increase in lattice free energy occurring as a result of the dimerization of the regular array. Example\: The transition in tetrathiafulvalenium bis\(dithiolene\)cuprate at \\\(12\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05875
name: spin–spin coupling
def: "The interaction between the spin magnetic moments of different electrons and/or nuclei. It causes\, e.g. the multiplet pattern in nuclear magnetic resonance spectra."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05876
name: spin–spin coupling constant
def: "Coefficient of the indirect spin–spin coupling between two nuclei \(A and B\) in a magnetic resonance hamiltonian."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05877
name: spin-state transition
def: "An electronic transition from a high-spin state to a low-spin state\, or vice versa. Example\: With an increase in temperature $\\ce\{Co^\{3+\}\}$ ions in $\\ce\{LaCoO3\}$ transform from a low-spin state \(\\\(\\rm\{t\}_\{\\rm\{2g\}\}^\{6\}\\rm\{e\}_\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{0\}\\\)\) to the high-spin state \(\\\(\\rm\{t\}_\{\\rm\{2g\}\}^\{4\}\\rm\{e\}_\{\\rm\{g\}\}^\{2\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05878
name: spin trapping
def: "In certain reactions in solution a transient radical will interact with a diamagnetic reagent to form a more persistent radical. The product radical accumulates to a concentration where detection and\, frequently\, identification are possible by EPR/ESR spectroscopy. The key reaction is usually one of attachment; the diamagnetic reagent is said to be a 'spin trap' and the persistent product radical is then the 'spin adduct'. The procedure is referred to as spin trapping\, and is used for monitoring reactions involving the intermediacy of reactive radicals at concentrations too low for direct observation. Typical spin traps are C-nitroso compounds and nitrones\, to which reactive radicals will rapidly add to form nitryl radicals. A quantitative development\, in which essentially all reactive radicals generated in a particular system are intercepted\, has been referred to as 'spin counting'. Spin trapping has also been adapted to the interception of radicals generated in both gaseous and solid phases. In these cases the spin adduct is in practice transferred to a liquid solution for observation in order to facilitate interpretation of the EPR/ESR spectra of the radicals obtained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05880
name: spiro chain
def: "A double-strand chain consisting of an uninterrupted sequence of rings\, with adjacent rings having only one atom in common. Alternatively\, a spiro chain is a double-strand chain with adjacent constitutional unit joined to each other through three atoms\, two on one side and one on the other side of each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05881
name: spiro compounds
def: "Compounds having one atom \(usually a quaternary carbon\) as the only common member of two rings\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05882
name: spiro macromolecule
def: "A double-strand macromolecule consisting of an uninterrupted sequence of rings\, with adjacent rings having only one atom in common. Alternatively\, a spiro macromolecule is a double-strand macromolecule with adjacent constitutional unit joined to each other through three atoms\, two on one side and one on the other side of each constitutional unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05884
name: spiro union
def: "A union formed by a single atom which is the only common member of two rings. The common atom is designated as the spiro atom. A free spiro union is one which constitutes the only union direct or indirect between the two rings."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05885
name: splicing
def: "of RNA\: The procedure by which introns are removed from eukaryotic precursor mRNA molecules and adjacent exon sequences are joined together \(spliced\).of DNA\: Manipulation for joining together double-stranded DNA fragments with protruding single-stranded 'sticky ends' by means of ligases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05886
name: spontaneous emission
def: "That mode of emission which occurs even in the absence of a perturbing external electromagnetic field. The transition between states\, \\\(n\\\) and \\\(m\\\)\, is governed by the einstein coefficient of spontaneous emission\, \\\(A_\{nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05887
name: spontaneous fission
def: "Nuclear fission which occurs without the addition of particles or energy to the nucleus"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05889
name: spot
def: "A zone in paper and thin-layer chromatography of approximately circular appearance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05891
name: sprayer
def: "The particular part of a nebulizer where the aspirated liquid is disrupted by the gas-jet into a spray."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05892
name: spreading function
def: "The normalized signal produced\, as a function of elution volume at the outlet of a size-exclusion chromatography set-up\, by an instantaneous injection of a uniform sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05893
name: spreading wetting
def: "A process in which a drop of liquid spreads over a solid or liquid substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05894
name: spread monolayer
def: "It may happen that a component is virtually insoluble in both of two adjoining phases\, but is present as a monolayer between them. Such a layer can be produced by spreading."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05895
name: sputtering
def: "Removal of surface material \(atoms\, clusters and molecules\) by particle bombardment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05896
name: sputter yield
def: "The number of particles sputtered from the surface of a target per primary ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05897
name: square-wave current
def: "In square-wave polarography\, the component of the current that is associated with the presence of a substance B. This component may be either faradaic \(if B is electroactive\) or non-faradaic \(if B is surface-active\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05900
name: stable
def: "As applied to chemical species\, the term expresses a thermodynamic property\, which is quantitatively measured by relative molar standard Gibbs energies. A chemical species A is more stable than its isomer B if \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} > 0\\\) for the \(real or hypothetical\) reaction \\\[\\rm\{A\}\\quad \\rightarrow \\quad \\rm\{B\}\\\] under standard conditions. If for the two reactions\: \\\[\\rm\{P\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{X\}+\\rm\{Y\}\\qquad \(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G_\{1\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\)\\\] \\\[\\rm\{Q\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{X\}+\\rm\{Z\}\\qquad \(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G_\{2\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\)\\\] \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G_\{1\}^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} > \\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G_\{2\}^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, P is more stable relative to the product Y than is Q relative to Z. Both in qualitative and quantitative usage the term stable is therefore always used in reference to some explicitly stated or implicitly assumed standard. The term should not be used as a synonym for unreactive or 'less reactive' since this confuses thermodynamics and kinetics. A relatively more stable chemical species may be more reactive than some reference species towards a given reaction partner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05901
name: stable film\, metastable film
def: "A film often thins gradually to a thickness at which it either ruptures or converts abruptly to an equilibrium film. Rupture under these conditions characterizes unstable films\, whereas transition to an equilibrium film characterizes \(meta\)stable films."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05902
name: stable ion
def: "An ion which is not sufficiently excited to dissociate spontaneously into a daughter ion and associated neutral fragment\(s\) or to react further in any other way within the time scale of the experiment\, e.g. until hitting the detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05903
name: staggered conformation
def: "The conformation of groups attached to two adjacent atoms if the torsion angles are such that the groups are as far away as possible from an eclipsed arrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05905
name: standard acceleration of free fall
def: "Acceleration defined as \\\(g_\{\\rm\{n\}\} = 9.806\\ 65\\ \\rm\{m s\}^\{-2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05906
name: standard atmosphere
def: "Pressure defined as \\\(101\\ 325\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\) and used as unit of pressure with the symbol \\\(\\rm\{atm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05907
name: standard atomic weights
def: "Recommended values of relative atomic masses of the elements revised biennially by the IUPAC Commission on Atomic Weights and Isotopic Abundances and applicable to elements in any normal sample with a high level of confidence. A normal sample is any reasonably possible source of the element or its compounds in commerce for industry and science and has not been subject to significant modification of isotopic composition within a geologically brief period."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05908
name: standard chemical potential
def: "Of substance B at temperature \\\(T\\\)\, \\\(\\mu _\{\\rm\{B\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\}\}\(T\)\\\)\, is the value of the chemical potential under specified standard conditions \(in the standard state\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05909
name: standard concentration
def: "Chosen value of amount concentration denoted by \\\(c^\{\\unicode\{x29B5\}\}\\\) or \\\(c^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) and usually equal to \\\(\\pu\{1 mol dm-3\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05910
name: standard conditions for gases
def: "Temperature\, \\\(273.15\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) \(\\\(0\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)\) and pressure of \\\(10^\{5\}\\\) pascals. IUPAC recommends that the former use of the pressure of 1 atm as standard pressure \(equivalent to \\\(1.01325\\times 10^\{5\}\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\)\) should be discontinued."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05911
name: standard deviation
def: "The positive square root of the sum of the squares of the deviations between the observations and the mean of the series\, divided by one less than the total number in the series. The standard deviation is the positive square root of the variance\, a more fundamental statistical quantity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05912
name: standard electrode potential
def: "The value of the standard emf of a cell in which molecular hydrogen under standard pressure is oxidized to solvated protons at the left-hand electrode."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05913
name: standard electromotive force
def: "Quantity defined by \\\(E^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} = -\\frac\{\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\}\{n\\ F\}=\\frac\{R\\ T\}\{n\\ F\}\\ \\ln K^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is the standard Gibbs energy of the cell reaction in the direction in which reduction occurs at the right-hand electrode in the diagram representing the cell \('reduction at right'\)\, \\\(K^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is the standard equilibrium constant for this reaction\, \\\(n\\\) its charge number\, \\\(F\\\) the Faraday constant\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05914
name: standard entropy of activation
def: "Entropy change associated with the activation reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05915
name: standard equilibrium constant
def: "Quantity defined by \\\(K^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} = \\rm\{e\}^\{-\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}/R\\ T\}\\\) where \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is the standard reaction Gibbs energy\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature. Some chemists prefer the name thermodynamic equilibrium constant and the symbol \\\(K\\\)."
synonym: "thermodynamic equilibrium constant" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05916
name: standard Gibbs energy of activation
def: "Standard Gibbs energy change associated with the activation reaction"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05917
name: standard hydrogen electrode
def: "For solutions in protic solvents\, the universal reference electrode for which\, under standard conditions\, the standard electrode potential \($\\ce\{H^\{+\}\}$/$\\ce\{H2\}$\) is zero at all temperatures. The absolute electrode potential of the hydrogen electrode under standard conditions can be expressed in terms of thermodynamic quantities by applying a suitable Born–Haber cycle\, thus\: \\\[E^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}/\\rm\{H\}_\{2\}\\right\)\\left\(\\rm\{abs\}\\right\)=\\Delta _\{\\rm\{at\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}+\\Delta _\{\\rm\{ion\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}+\\frac\{\\alpha _\{\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\,S\}\}\}\{F\}\\\] where \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{at\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{ion\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) are the atomization and ionization Gibbs energies of $\\ce\{H2\}$\, \\\(\\alpha _\{\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\}^\{\\rm\{o\,S\}\}\\\) is the real potential of $\\ce\{H2\}$ in solvent $\\ce\{S\}$ and \\\(F\\\) is the Faraday constant. The recommended absolute electrode potential of the hydrogen electrode is\: \\\[E^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}/\\rm\{H\}_\{2\}\\right\)^\{\\rm\{H\}_\{2\}\\rm\{O\}\}\\left\(\\rm\{abs\}\\right\)=\(4.44\\pm 0.02\)\\ \\rm\{V\}\\quad \\rm\{at\}\\quad 298.15\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05918
name: standard molality
def: "Chosen value of molality and usually equal to \\\(\\pu\{1 mol kg -1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05919
name: standard potential of an electrode reaction
def: "Synonymous with standard electrode potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05920
name: standard potential of the reaction in a chemical cell
def: "Synonymous with standard electromotive force."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05921
name: standard pressure
def: "Chosen value of pressure denoted by \\\(p^\{\\unicode\{x29B5\}\}\\\) or \\\(p^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\). In 1982 IUPAC recommended the value \\\(10^\{5\}\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\)\, but prior to 1982 the value \\\(101\\ 325\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\) \(= \\\(1\\ \\rm\{atm\}\\\)\) was usually used."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05923
name: standard reaction quantities
def: "Infinitesimal changes in thermodynamic functions with extent of reaction divided by the infinitesimal increase in the extent when all the reactants and products are in their standard states. For the quantity \\\(X\\\) they should be denoted by \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{r\}\}X^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, but usually only \\\(\\Delta X^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is used. For specific types of reactions the subscript r is replaced by\: f for formation\, c for combustion\, a for atomization and superscript ‡ for activation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05924
name: standard solution
def: "A solution of accurately known concentration\, prepared using standard substances in one of several ways. A primary standard is a substance of known high purity which may be dissolved in a known volume of solvent to give a primary standard solution. If stoichiometry is used to establish the strength of a titrant\, it is called a secondary standard solution. The term secondary standard can also be applied to a substance whose active agent contents have been found by comparison against a primary standard. Concentrations of standard solutions may be expressed in \\\(\\pu\{kmol m-3\}\\\)\, \\\(\\pu\{mol dm-3\}\\\)\, or in terms more closely related to those used in specific titrations \(as titres\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05925
name: standard state
def: "State of a system chosen as standard for reference by convention. Three standard states are recognized\: For a gas phase it is the \(hypothetical\) state of the pure substance in the gaseous phase at the standard pressure \\\(p=p^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, assuming ideal behaviour. For a pure phase\, or a mixture\, or a solvent in the liquid or solid state it is the state of the pure substance in the liquid or solid phase at the standard pressure \\\(p=p^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\). For a solute in solution it is the \(hypothetical\) state of solute at the standard molality \\\(m^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, standard pressure \\\(p^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) or standard concentration \\\(c^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) and exhibiting infinitely dilute solution behaviour. For a pure substance the concept of standard state applies to the substance in a well defined state of aggregation at a well defined but arbitrarily chosen standard pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05926
name: standard subtraction method
def: "A variation of the standard addition method. In this procedure changes in the potential resulting from the addition of a known amount of a species which reacts stoichiometrically with the ion of interest \(e.g. a complexing agent\) are employed to determine the original activity or concentration of the ion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05927
name: standard thermodynamic quantities
def: "Values of thermodynamic functions in the standard state characterized by a standard pressure\, molality or amount concentration\, but not by temperature. Standard quantities are denoted by adding the superscript \\\(^\{⦵\}\\\) or \\\(^\{\\circ\}\\\) to the symbol of the quantity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05928
name: standard uncertainty
def: "Uncertainty of measurement expressed as one standard deviation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05929
name: stand-by state
def: "The condition of an instrument where the analytical procedure can begin immediately."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05930
name: stannoxanes
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{H3S_\{n\}\[OSnH2\]_\{n\}OSnH3\}$. Thus\, tin analogues of siloxanes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05932
name: stannylidenes
def: "Carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce\{R2Sn\{\:\}\}$. The older synonymous term  stannylenes is no longer recommended."
synonym: "stannylenes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05933
name: star copolymer
def: "A copolymer where chains having different constitutional or configurational features are linked through a central moiety."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05934
name: Stark effect
def: "The splitting or shift of spectral lines in an electric field. Also called electrochromic effect."
synonym: "electrochromic effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05935
name: star macromolecule
def: "A macromolecule containing a single branch point from which linear chains \(arms\) emanate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05936
name: star polymer
def: "A polymer composed of star macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05937
name: starting line
def: "The point or line on a chromatographic layer where the substance to be chromatographed is applied."
synonym: "starting point" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05939
name: start-up time
def: "The time interval between turn-on of the instrument and its stand-by state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05943
name: state-to-state kinetics
def: "A branch of chemical kinetics concerned with the dynamics of reactions in which the reactant species are in known quantum states\, and in which the quantum states of the products are determined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05944
name: static fields mass spectrometer
def: "An instrument which can separate selected ion beams with fields which do not vary with time. The fields are generally both electric and magnetic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05945
name: static pressure
def: "The pressure of a fluid at rest or in motion exerted perpendicularly to the direction of flow."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05947
name: static stability
def: "In meteorology\, the stability of the atmosphere in the vertical direction to vertical displacements. If \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}\\\) is the dry adiabatic lapse rate\, dry air is stable if the lapse rate \\\(-\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}T\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}z\} \\lt \\Gamma\\\)\, unstable if \\\(-\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}T\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}z\} \\gt \\Gamma\\\) and neutral \\\(-\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}T\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}z\} = \\Gamma\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05948
name: stationary phase
def: "The phase of a culture of microorganisms or animal and plant cells cultured in vitro that follows the exponential growth phase and in which there is little or no growth. In some cases it is a phase of product formation\, e.g. formation of secondary metabolites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05949
name: stationary phase
def: "One of the two phases forming a chromatographic system. It may be a solid\, a gel or a liquid. If a liquid\, it may be distributed on a solid. This solid may or may not contribute to the separation process. The liquid may also be chemically bonded to the solid \(bonded phase\) or immobilized onto it \(immobilized phase\). The expression chromatographic bed or sorbent may be used as a general term to denote any of the different forms in which the stationary phase is used. Particularly in gas chromatography where the stationary phase is most often a liquid\, the term liquid phase is used for it as compared to the gas phase\, i.e. the mobile phase. However\, particularly in the early development of liquid chromatography\, the term 'liquid phase' had also been used to characterize the mobile phase as compared to the 'solid phase' i.e. the stationary phase. Due to this ambiguity\, the use of the term 'liquid phase' is discouraged. If the physical state of the stationary phase is to be expressed\, the use of the adjective forms such as liquid stationary phase and solid stationary phase\, bonded phase or immobilized phase is proposed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05950
name: stationary-phase fraction
def: "The volume of the stationary phase per unit volume of a packed column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05952
name: stationary phase volume
def: "The volume of the liquid stationary phase or the active solid in the column. The volume of any solid support is not included. In the case of partition chromatography with a liquid stationary phase\, it is identical to the liquid phase volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05953
name: stationary state
def: "\(in quantum mechanics\) A state that does not evolve with time.\(in kinetics\) See\: steady state"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05955
name: statistical copolymer
def: "A copolymer consisting of macromolecules in which the sequential distribution of the monomeric units obeys known statistical laws."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05956
name: statistical copolymerization
def: "A copolymerization in which a statistical copolymer is formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05959
name: statistical segment
def: "A segment of an actual polymer chain which behaves\, with respect to some property\, virtually as a segment of a freely jointed chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05961
name: steady state
def: "The state of a continuous process when it is operating in such a way that the concentration of solutes in exit streams remains constant with respect to time for constant feed concentrations\, even though the two phases are not necessarily in thermodynamic equilibrium in any part of the process. The term equilibrium should not be used to describe this situation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05962
name: steady state
def: "In a kinetic analysis of a complex reaction involving unstable intermediates in lconcentration\, the rate of change of each such intermediate is set equal to zero\, so that the rate equation can be expressed as a function of the concentrations of chemical species present in macroscopic amounts. For example\, assume that $\\ce\{\\textbf\{X\}\}$ is an unstable intermediate in the reaction sequence\:   Conservation of mass requires that\: \\\[\[\\ce\{A\}\] + \[\\ce\{X\}\] + \[\\ce\{D\}\] = \[\\ce\{A\}\]_\{0\}\\\] which\, since \\\(\[\\ce\{A\}\]_\{0\}\\\) is constant\, implies\: \\\[-\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{X\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{A\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} + \\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{D\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\}.\\\] Since \\\(\[\\ce\{X\}\]\\\) is negligibly small\, the rate of formation of $\\ce\{\\textbf\{D\}\}$ is essentially equal to the rate of disappearance of $\\ce\{\\textbf\{A\}\}$\, and the rate of change of \\\(\[\\ce\{X\}\]\\\) can be set equal to zero. Applying the steady state approximation \(\\\(\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{X\}\]/\{\\rm\{d\}\}t = 0\\\)\) allows the elimination of \\\(\[\\ce\{X\}\]\\\) from the kinetic equations\, whereupon the rate of reaction is expressed\: \\\[\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{D\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = -\\frac\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{A\}\]\}\{\{\\rm\{d\}\}t\} = \\frac\{k_\{1\}k_\{2\}\[\\ce\{A\}\]\[\\ce\{C\}\]\}\{k_\{-1\} + k_\{2\}\[\\ce\{C\}\]\}\\\]Note\: The steady-state approximation does not imply that \\\(\[\\ce\{X\}\]\\\) is even approximately constant\, only that its absolute rate of change is very much smaller than that of \\\(\[\\ce\{A\}\]\\\) and \\\(\[\\ce\{D\}\]\\\). Since according to the reaction scheme \\\(\{\\rm\{d\}\}\[\\ce\{X\}\]/\{\\rm\{d\}\}t = k_\{2\}\[\\ce\{X\}\]\[\\ce\{C\}\]\\\)\, the assumption that \\\(\[\\ce\{X\}\]\\\) is constant would lead\, for the case in which $\\ce\{\\textbf\{C\}\}$ is in large excess\, to the absurd conclusion that formation of the product $\\ce\{\\textbf\{D\}\}$ will continue at a constant rate even after the reactant $\\ce\{\\textbf\{A\}\}$ has been consumed.In a stirred flow reactor a steady state implies a regime so that all concentrations are independent of time."
synonym: "Bodenstein approximation" []
synonym: "stationary state" []
synonym: "steady state approximation" []
synonym: "steady state treatment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05964
name: Stefan–Boltzmann constant
def: "Fundamental physical constant \\\(\\sigma = \\frac\{2\\ \\pi ^\{5\}\\ k^\{4\}\}\{15\\ h^\{3\}\\ c_\{0\}^\{2\}\} = 5.670\\ 51\(19\)\\times 10^\{-8\}\\ \\rm\{W\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{K\}^\{-4\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05965
name: stem
def: "A crystallized\, rodlike portion of a polymer chain connected to non-rodlike portions\, or chain ends\, or both."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05966
name: step
def: "The portion of an integral chromatogram recording the amount of a component\, or the corresponding change in the signal from the detector as the component emerges from the column."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05967
name: step height
def: "The distance perpendicular to the time or volume axis\, through which the baseline moves as a result of a step on an integral chromatogram."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05969
name: stepwise elution
def: "An elution process in which the composition of the mobile phase is changed in steps during a single chromatographic run."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05970
name: stepwise reaction
def: "A chemical reaction with at least one reaction intermediate and involving at least two consecutive elementary reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05971
name: steradian
def: "SI derived unit of solid angle\, \\\(\\mathrm\{sr\}=1\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05972
name: stereoblock macromolecule
def: "A block macromolecule composed of stereoregular\, and possibly nonstereoregular\, blocks."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05973
name: stereoblock polymer
def: "A polymer composed of stereoblock macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05974
name: stereochemical formula
def: "A three-dimensional view of a molecule either as such or in a projection."
synonym: "stereoformula" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05975
name: stereoconvergence
def: "The predominant formation of the same stereoisomers or stereoisomer mixture of a reaction product when two different stereoisomers of the reactant are used in the same reaction. When that product involved in the reaction is one enantiomer the result has been called enantioconvergence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05976
name: stereodescriptor
def: "A prefix to specify configuration \(absolute or relative\) or conformation. For example R\, S; r\, s; P\, M; Re\, Si; E\, Z; ap\, sp; etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05977
name: stereoelectronic
def: "Pertaining to the dependence of the properties \(especially the energy\) of a molecular entity in a particular electronic state \(or of a transition state\) on relative nuclear geometry. The electronic ground state is usually considered\, but the term can apply to excited states as well. Stereoelectronic effects arise from the different alignment of electronic orbitals in different arrangements of nuclear geometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05978
name: stereoelectronic control
def: "Control of the nature of the products of a chemical reaction \(or of its rate\) by stereoelectronic factors. The term is usually applied in the framework of an orbital approximation. The variations of molecular orbital energies with relative nuclear geometry \(along a reaction coordinate\) are then seen as consequences of variations in basis-orbital overlaps."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05980
name: stereogenic unit
def: "A grouping within a molecular entity that may be considered a focus of stereoisomerism. At least one of these must be present in every enantiomer \(though the presence of stereogenic units does not conversely require the corresponding chemical species to be chiral\). Three basic types are recognized for molecular entities involving atoms having not more than four substituents\:  A grouping of atoms consisting of a central atom and distinguishable ligands\, such that the interchange of any two of the substituents leads to a stereoisomer. An asymmetric atom \(chirality centre\) is the traditional example of this stereogenic unit.   A chain of four non-coplanar atoms \(or rigid groups\) in a stable conformation\, such that an imaginary or real \(restricted\) rotation \(with a change of sign of the torsion angle\) about the central bond leads to a stereoisomer.   A grouping of atoms consisting of a double bond with substituents which give rise to cis-trans isomerism."
synonym: "stereoelement" []
synonym: "stereogen" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05981
name: stereoheterotopic
def: "Either enantiotopic or diastereotopic. \(In contrast the term constitutionally heterotopic has been used to describe identical groups located in constitutionally non-equivalent positions\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05982
name: stereohomosequence
def: "A configurational homosequence in which the relative or absolute configuration is defined at all sites of stereoisomerism in the main chain of a polymer molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05983
name: stereoisomerism
def: "Isomerism due to differences in the spatial arrangement of atoms without any differences in connectivity or bond multiplicity between the isomers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05984
name: stereoisomers
def: "Isomers that possess identical constitution\, but which differ in the arrangement of their atoms in space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05985
name: stereomutation
def: "A change of configuration at a stereogenic unit brought about by physical or chemical means."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05986
name: stereoregular macromolecule
def: "A regular macromolecule essentially comprising only one species of stereorepeating unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05987
name: stereoregular polymer
def: "A polymer composed of stereoregular macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05988
name: stereorepeating unit
def: "A configurational repeating unit having defined configuration at all sites of stereoisomerism in the main chain of a regular macromolecule\, a regular oligomer molecule\, a regular block or a regular chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05989
name: stereoselective polymerization
def: "Polymerization in which a polymer molecule is formed from a mixture of stereoisomeric monomer molecules by incorporation of only one stereoisomeric species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05990
name: stereoselective synthesis
def: "A chemical reaction \(or reaction sequence\) in which one or more new elements of chirality are formed in a substrate molecule and which produces the stereoisomeric \(enantiomeric or diastereoisomeric\) products in unequal amounts. Traditionally called asymmetric synthesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05991
name: stereoselectivity
def: "The preferential formation in a chemical reaction of one stereoisomer over another. When the stereoisomers are enantiomers\, the phenomenon is called 'enantioselectivity' and is quantitatively expressed by the enantiomeric excess; when they are diastereoisomers\, it is called 'diastereoselectivity' and is quantitatively expressed by the diastereoisomer excess."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05992
name: stereosequence
def: "A configurational sequence in which the relative or absolute configuration is defined at all sites of stereoisomerism in the main chain of a polymer molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05993
name: stereospecifically labelled tracer
def: "A tracer in which the label is present in a stereospecific position."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05994
name: stereospecificity
def: "A reaction is termed stereospecific if starting materials differing only in their configuration are converted into stereoisomeric products. According to this definition\, a stereospecific process is necessarily stereoselectivity but not all stereoselective processes are stereospecific. Stereospecificity may be total \(100%\) or partial. The term is also applied to situations where reaction can be performed with only one stereoisomer. For example\, the exclusive formation of trans-1\,2-dibromocyclohexane upon bromination of cyclohexene is a stereospecific process\, although the analogous reaction with \(E\)-cyclohexene has not been performed.The term has also been applied to describe a reaction of very high stereoselectivity\, but this usage is unnecessary and is discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05995
name: stereospecific polymerization
def: "Polymerization in which a tactic polymer is formed. However\, polymerization in which stereoisomerism present in the monomer is merely retained in the polymer is not to be regarded as stereospecific. For example\, the polymerization of a chiral monomer\, e.g. ᴅ-propylene oxide \(ᴅ-methyloxirane\)\, with retention of configuration is not considered to be a stereospecific reaction; however\, selective polymerization\, with retention\, of one of the enantiomers present in a mixture of ᴅ- and ʟ-propylene oxide molecules is so classified."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05996
name: steric-approach control
def: "Control of stereoselectivity of a reaction by steric hindrance towards attack of the reagent\, which is directed to the less hindered face of the molecule. Partial bond making is strong enough at the transition state for steric control to take place. This suggests that the transition state should not be close to products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05997
name: steric effect
def: "The effect on a chemical or physical property \(structure\, rate or equilibrium constant\) upon introduction of substituents having different steric requirements. The steric effect in a reaction is ascribed to the difference in steric energy between\, on the one hand\, reactants and\, on the other hand\, a transition state \(or products\). A steric effect on a rate process may result in a rate increase \('steric acceleration'\) or a decrease \('steric retardation'\). \(The adjective 'steric' is not to be confused with stereochemical.\) Steric effects arise from contributions ascribed to strain as the sum of \(1\) non-bonded repulsions\, \(2\) bond angle strain and \(3\) bond stretches or compressions. For the purpose of correlation analysis or linear free-energy relations various scales of steric parameters have been proposed\, notably A values\, Taft's \\\(E_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) and Charton's \\\(\nu \\\) scales. In a reactant molecule $\\ce\{RY\}$ and an appropriate reference molecule $\\ce\{R^\{\\circ\}Y\}$\, the 'primary steric effect' of $\\ce\{R\}$ is the direct result of differences in compressions which occur because $\\ce\{R\}$ differs from $\\ce\{R^\{\\circ\}\}$ in the vicinity of the reaction centre $\\ce\{Y\}$. A 'secondary steric effect' involves the differential moderation of electron delocalization by non-bonded compressions. Some authors make a distinction between 'steric' effects attributed to van der Waals repulsions alone\, and 'strain' effects\, attributed to deviations of bond angles from 'ideal' values."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05998
name: steric factor
def: "A factor introduced into simple versions of the collision theory of reactions to take care of the fact that the reaction probability depends on the certain mutual orientations of the reactant molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S05999
name: steric factor
def: "The ratio\, \\\(\\sigma \\\)\, of the root-mean-square end-to-end distance of a polymer chain with unperturbed dimensions\, \\\(< r^\{2\} >_\{0\}^\{1/2\}\\\)\, to that of a freely rotating chain with the same structure\, \\\(< r^\{2\} >_\{0\,f\}^\{1/2\}\\\)\, i.e. \\\(\\sqrt\{\\frac\{< r^\{2\} >_\{0\}\}\{< r^\{2\} >_\{0\,f\}\}\}\\\)\, in the limit of infinite chain length. The steric factor reflects the effect of hindrance to free rotation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06000
name: steric hindrance
def: "The original term for a steric effect arising from crowding of substituents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06001
name: steric isotope effect
def: "A secondary isotope effect attributed to the different vibrational amplitudes of isotopologues. For example\, both the mean and mean-square amplitudes of vibrations associated with $\\ce\{C–H\}$ bonds are greater than those of $\\ce\{C–D\}$ bonds. The greater effective bulk of molecules containing the former may be manifested by a steric effect on a rate or equilibrium constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06003
name: Stern layer
def: "Counter and co-ions in immediate contact with a surface are said to be located in the Stern layer\, and form with the fixed charge a molecular capacitor."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06004
name: Stern–Volmer kinetic relationships
def: "This term applies broadly to variations of quantum yield of photophysical processes \(e.g. fluorescence or phosphorescence\) or photochemical reaction \(usually reaction quantum yield\) with the concentration of a given reagent which may be a substrate or a quencher. In the simplest case\, a plot of \\\(\\frac\{\\mathit\{\\Phi\}^\{0\}\}\{\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\}\\\) \(or \\\(\\frac\{M^\{0\}\}\{M\}\\\) for emission\) vs. concentration of quencher\, \\\(\\ce\{\[Q\]\}\\\)\, is linear obeying the equation\: \\\[\\frac\{\\mathit\{\\Phi\}^\{0\}\}\{\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\} \\quad \{\\rm\{or\}\} \\quad \\frac\{M^\{0\}\}\{M\} = 1 + K_\{\\rm\{sv\}\} \[\\rm\{Q\}\]\\\] In equation \(1\) \\\(K_\{\\rm\{sv\}\}\\\) is referred to as the Stern–Volmer constant. Equation \(1\) applies when a quencher inhibits either a photochemical reaction or a photophysical process by a single reaction. \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\}^\{0\}\\\) and \\\(M^\{0\}\\\) are the quantum yield and emission intensity radiant exitance\, respectively\, in the absence of the quencher Q\, while \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\\\) and \\\(M\\\) are the same quantities in the presence of the different concentrations of Q. In the case of dynamic quenching the constant \\\(K_\{\\rm\{sv\}\}\\\) is the product of the true quenching constant \\\(k_\{\\rm\{q\}\}\\\) and the excited state lifetime\, \\\(\\tau^\{0\}\\\)\, in the absence of quencher. \\\(k_\{\\rm\{q\}\}\\\) is the bimolecular reaction rate constant for the elementary reaction of the excited state with the particular quencher Q. Equation \(1\) can therefore be replaced by the expression \(2\)\: \\\[\\frac\{\\mathit\{\\Phi\}^\{0\}\}\{\\mathit\{\\Phi\}\} \\quad \{\\rm\{or\}\} \\quad \\frac\{M^\{0\}\}\{M\} = 1 + k_\{\\rm\{q\}\} \\tau^\{0\} \[\{\\rm\{Q\}\}\]\\\] When an excited state undergoes a bimolecular reaction with rate constant \\\(k_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) to form a product\, a double-reciprocal relationship is observed according to the equation\: \\\[\\frac\{1\}\{\\mathit\{\\Phi\}_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\} = \\left\(1 + \\frac\{1\}\{k_\{\\rm\{r\}\} \\tau^\{0\} \[\{\\ce\{S\}\}\]\}\\right\) \\frac\{1\}\{A \\cdot B\}\\\] where \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\}_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) is the quantum efficiency of product formation\, \\\(A\\\) the efficiency of forming the reactive excited state\, \\\(B\\\) the fraction of reactions of the excited state with substrate S which leads to product\, and \\\(\\ce\{\[S\]\}\\\) is the concentration of reactive ground-state substrate. The intercept/slope ratio gives \\\(k_\{\\rm\{r\}\} \\tau ^\{0\}\\\). If \\\(\\ce\{\[S\]\} = \\ce\{\[Q\]\}\\\)\, and if a photophysical process is monitored\, plots of equations \(2\) and \(3\) should provide independent determinations of the product-forming rate constant \\\(k_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\). When the lifetime of an excited state is observed as a function of the concentration of S or Q\, a linear relationship should be observed according to the equation\: \\\[\\frac\{\\tau^\{0\}\}\{\\tau\} = 1 + k_\{\\rm\{q\}\} \\tau^\{0\} \[\\ce\{Q\}\]\\\] where \\\(\\tau^\{0\}\\\) is the lifetime of the excited state in the absence of the quencher Q."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06005
name: steroids
def: "Naturally occurring compounds and synthetic analogues\, based on the cyclopenta\[a\]phenanthrene carbon skeleton\, partially or completely hydrogenated; there are usually methyl groups at C-10 and C-13\, and often an alkyl group at C-17. By extension\, one or more bond scissions\, ring expansions and/or ring contractions of the skeleton may have occurred. Natural steroids are derived biogenetically from triterpenoids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06006
name: sterols
def: "Natural products derived from the steroid skeleton and containing a hydroxy group in the 3 position\, closely related to cholestan-3-ol."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06007
name: stibanes
def: "The saturated hydrides of tervalent antimony\, having the general formula $\\ce\{Sb_\{n\}H_\{n+2\}\}$. Hydrocarbyl derivatives of $\\ce\{SbH3\}$ belong to the class stibines."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06008
name: stibanylidenes
def: "Recommended name for carbene analogues having the structure $\\ce\{RSb\{\:\}\}$ \(former IUPAC name is stibinediyls\). A common non-IUPAC synonym is stibinidenes."
synonym: "stibinidenes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06009
name: stibines
def: "$\\ce\{SbH3\}$ and compounds derived from it by substituting one\, two or three hydrogen atoms by hydrocarbyl groups $\\ce\{R3Sb\}$. $\\ce\{RSbH2\}$\, $\\ce\{R2SbH\}$ and $\\ce\{R3Sb\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\) are called primary\, secondary and tertiary stibines\, respectively. A specific stibine is preferably named as a substituted stibane. E.g. $\\ce\{\(CH2=CH\)3Sb\}$ trivinylstibane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06011
name: stibonium compounds
def: "Salts \(including hydroxides\) containing an atom of tetracoordinate antimony\, of the form $\\ce\{\[R4Sb\]^\{+\}X^\{−\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06012
name: sticking coefficient
def: "The ratio of the rate of adsorption to the rate at which the adsorptive strikes the total surface\, i.e. covered and uncovered. It is usually a function of surface coverage\, of temperature and of the details of the surface structure of the adsorbent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06013
name: sticking probability
def: "Often used with the same meaning as sticking coefficient\, but in principle it is a microscopic quantity concerned with the individual collision process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06014
name: sticky ends
def: "The staggered ends of complementary sequences of DNA which result from cleavage by restriction enzymes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06015
name: stimulated emission
def: "That part of the emission which is induced by a resonant perturbing electromagnetic field. The transition between states\, n and m\, is governed by the einstein coefficient of stimulated emission\, \\\(B_\{nm\}\\\). CIDNP emission and lasing action are examples of processes which require stimulated emission."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06016
name: stirred flow reactor
def: "A reactor within which effective mixing is achieved\, often by placing the catalyst in a rapidly-rotating basket."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06017
name: stochastic sampling
def: "Synonymous with random sampling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06018
name: stochastic theories
def: "Theories that treat reaction rates in terms of the probabilities of transitions between the various energy levels in the reactant molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06019
name: Stockholm convention
def: "IUPAC convention on signs of electromotive forces and electrode potentials."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06021
name: stoichiometric
def: "Involving chemical combination in simple integral ratios. Characterized by having no excess of reactants or products over that required to satisfy the balanced chemical equation representing the given chemical reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06023
name: stoichiometric concentration
def: "The stoichiometric concentration\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\)\, of a component B in a system is given by the expression\: \\\[c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=\\frac\{n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{V\}\\\] where \\\(n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the stoichiometric amount of substance of component B in the system and \\\(V\\\) is the volume of the system. The component added to the system may dissociate or react with other components to form a series of derived components and only a fraction of the original component may actually exist in a free form in the system. It is therefore essential to distinguish between the stoichiometric concentration and the amount-of-substance concentration of the free form of the component in the system. Sometimes stoichiometric quantities are indicated by a subscript \(\\\(_\{\\circ\}\\\)\)\, e.g. \\\(n_\{\\rm\{o\,B\}\}\\\)\, \\\(c_\{\\rm\{o\,B\}\}\\\). In clinical chemistry\, the term stoichiometric concentration is rarely employed. Instead\, the name of the component is modified to indicate inclusion of the various derived forms\, e.g. mixtures of a defined chemical component and its derivatives may be denoted by the plural form of the name of the pure unchanged substance\, or to indicate the sum of components specified in individual quantities the specification 'total' may be employed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06024
name: stoichiometric mean molal
def: "The practical activity coefficient of electrolyte B is given by \\\[\\gamma _\{\\pm \}=\\frac\{a_\{\\pm \}\}\{\\sqrt\{\nu _\{\\rm\{+\}\}^\{\nu _\{\\rm\{+\}\}\}\\ \nu _\{\\rm\{-\}\}^\{\nu _\{\\rm\{-\}\}\}\}\\ \\frac\{m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{m^\{\\rm\{+\}\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(a_\{\\pm \}\\\) is the mean activity of B in solution\, \\\(m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the molality of B\, \\\(m^\{+\} = \\pu\{1 mol kg-1\}\\\)\, \\\(\nu _\{\\rm\{+\}\}\\\) is the number of cations and \\\(\nu _\{\\unicode\{x2013\}\}\\\) the number of anions in the chosen group B which is taken as the electrolyte. \\\[\nu = \nu _\{\\rm\{+\}\}+\nu _\{\\rm\{-\}\}\\\]"
synonym: "practical activity coefficient" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06025
name: stoichiometric number
def: "A chemical reaction of known stoichiometry can be written in general as\: \\\[a\\rm\{A\}\\\,+\\\,b\\rm\{B\}\\\,+\\\,...\\rightarrow\\\,...\\\,+y\\rm\{Y\}\\\,+\\\,z\\rm\{Z\}\\\] For the reaction products Y and Z the numbers \\\(y\\\) and \\\(z\\\) are known as the stoichiometric numbers\, \\\(\nu _\{\\rm\{Y\}\}\\\) and \\\(\nu _\{\\rm\{Z\}\}\\\)\, for Y and Z respectively. For the reactants the stoichiometric numbers are the negatives of the numbers appearing in the equation; for example the stoichiometric number \\\(\nu _\{\\rm\{A\}\}\\\) for the reactant A is \\\(-a\\\). In other words\, the stoichiometric numbers are positive for products and negative for reactants."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06026
name: stoichiometry
def: "The term refers to the relationship between the amounts of substances that react together in a particular chemical reaction\, and the amounts of products that are formed. The general stoichiometric equation\: \\\[a\\rm\{A\}\\\,+\\\,b\\rm\{B\}\\\,+\\\,...\\\,\\rightarrow\\\,...\\\,+\\\,y\\rm\{Y\}\\\,+\\\,z\\rm\{Z\}\\\] provides the information that a moles of A reacts with b moles of B to produce y moles of Y and z moles of Z. The stoichiometry of a reaction may be unknown\, or may be very complex. For example\, the thermal decomposition of acetaldehyde yields mainly methane and carbon monoxide\, but also a variety of minor products such as ethane\, acetone and diacetyl. The stoichiometric equation\:  is therefore only an approximate one. Even when the overall stoichiometry of a reaction is well defined\, it may be time-dependent in that it varies during the course of a reaction. Thus if a reaction occurs by the mechanism \\\(\\rm\{A\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{X\}\\rightarrow \\rm\{Y\}\\\)\, and X is formed in substantial amounts during the course of the process\, the relationship between the amounts of A\, X and Y will vary with time\, and no one stoichiometric equation can represent the reaction at all times."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06027
name: stokes
def: "cgs unit of kinematic viscosity\, \\\(\\rm\{St\} = 10^\{-4\}\\ \\rm\{m\}^\{2\}\\ \\rm\{s\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06028
name: Stokes law
def: "\\\(F=6\\ \\pi \\ \\eta \\ r\\ \nu\\\)\, where \\\(F\\\) is the force exerted on a sphere of radius \\\(r\\\) which is moving through a fluid of viscosity \\\(\\eta \\\) with a relative velocity \\\(\nu\\\); this equation holds at low velocities which are free from turbulence \(called the stokes region\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06029
name: Stokes number
def: "Sometimes referred to as the inertial parameter; it is an index of the impactability of an aerosol particle. It is defined by the equation\: \\\(St = 2\\ \\tau \\ \(V_\{\\rm\{t\}\}- \nu_\{\\rm\{t\}\}\)\\ D_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) where \\\(D_\{\\rm\{p\}\}\\\) is the diameter of a small drop\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{t\}\}- \nu_\{\\rm\{t\}\}\\\) is the difference in fall velocities of the drop and aerosol particles and \\\(\\tau \\\) is the characteristic relaxation time of a particle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06030
name: Stokes parameters
def: "Of elliptically polarized incident radiation\, these are given by \\\[s_\{0\}^\{0\}=E_\{1\}^\{0\}+E_\{2\}^\{0\}\\\] \\\[s_\{1\}^\{0\}=E_\{1\}^\{0\}- E_\{2\}^\{0\}\\\] \\\[s_\{2\}^\{0\}=2\\ \\sqrt\{E_\{1\}^\{0\}\\ E_\{2\}^\{0\}\}\\ \\cos   \\delta ^\{0\}\\\] \\\[s_\{3\}^\{0\}=2\\ \\sqrt\{E_\{1\}^\{0\}\\ E_\{2\}^\{0\}\}\\ \\sin   \\delta ^\{0\}\\\] where \\\(E_\{1\}^\{0\}\\\) and \\\(E_\{2\}^\{0\}\\\) specify the irradiances of the incident light polarized with their electric vectors vibrating perpendicular and parallel to the scattering\, respectively and \\\(\\delta ^\{0\}\\\) is the phase difference between these electric vectors."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06031
name: Stokes shift
def: "The difference \(usually in frequency units\) between the spectral positions of the band maxima \(or the band origin\) of the absorption and luminescence arising from the same electronic transition. Generally\, the luminescence occurring at a longer wavelength than the absorption is stronger than the opposite. The latter may be called an anti-Stokes shift."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06032
name: Stokes type radiation
def: "Fluorescence radiation occurring at wavelengths longer than absorption\, i.e. the normal case\, is said to be of the Stokes type."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06033
name: stopped flow
def: "A technique for following the kinetics of reactions in solution \(usually in the millisecond time range\) in which two reactant solutions are rapidly mixed by being forced through a mixing chamber. The flow of the mixed solution along a uniform tube is then suddenly arrested. At a fixed position along the tube the solution is monitored \(as a function of time following the stoppage of the flow\) by some method with a rapid response \(e.g. photometry\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06034
name: stopped-flow cell
def: "A small-volume absorption cell connected to a rapid mixing chamber."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06035
name: stopping power
def: "Of a substance\, for charged particles of specified energy\, the average energy loss in passing through a thin layer of that substance\, divided by the thickness of that layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06036
name: STP
def: "Abbreviation for standard temperature \(\\\(273.15\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) or \\\(0\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)\) and pressure \(\\\(10^\{5\}\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\)\); usually employed in reporting gas volumes. Note that flow meters calibrated in standard gas volumes per unit time often refer to volumes at \\\(25\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, not \\\(0\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06037
name: strain
def: "Strain is present in a molecular entity or transition structure if the energy is enhanced because of unfavourable bond lengths\, bond angles or dihedral angles \('torsional strain'\) relative to a standard. It is quantitatively defined as the standard enthalpy of a structure relative to a strainless structure \(real or hypothetical\) made up from the same atoms with the same types of bonding. \(The enthalpy of formation of cyclopropane is \\\(\\pu\{53.6 kJ mol-1\}\\\)\, whereas the enthalpy of formation based on three 'normal' methylene groups\, from acyclic models\, is \\\(\\pu\{-62 kJ mol-1\}\\\). On this basis cyclopropane is destabilized by ca. \\\(\\pu\{115 kJ mol-1\}\\\) of strain energy.\)"
synonym: "steric strain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06038
name: strain-induced transition
def: "A transition in a solid that is initiated by plastic strain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06039
name: stratified film
def: "A film in which more than two thicknesses coexist in a fixed configuration over significant periods of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06040
name: stratified sample
def: "A sample consisting of portions obtained from identified subparts \(strata\) of the parent population. Within each stratum\, the samples are taken randomly. The objective of taking stratified samples is to obtain a more representative sample than that which might otherwise be obtained by random sampling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06041
name: stratocumulus cloud
def: "A layer of patches composed of laminae or globular masses arranged in groups\, lines or waves and having a soft\, grey appearance; very often the rolls are so close together that their edges join and give the undersurface a wavy character; the process of formation \(cumulogenesis\) involves the spreading out of the tops of cumulus clouds\, the latter having disappeared; \\\("

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06042
name: stratopause
def: "That region of the atmosphere which lies between the stratosphere and the mesosphere and in which a maximum in the temperature occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06043
name: stratosphere
def: "The atmospheric shell lying just above the troposphere which is characterized by an increasing temperature with altitude. The stratosphere begins at the tropopause \(about \\\(10\{-\}15\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\) height\) and extends to a height of about \\\(50\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\)\, where the lapse rate changes sign at the stratopause and the beginning of the mesosphere."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06044
name: stratus cloud
def: "A uniform\, featureless layer of cloud resembling fog but not resting on the ground; when this very low layer is broken up into irregular shreds\, it is designated as fractostratus; \\\(300\{-\}600\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\); widespread irregular stirring and lifting of the shallow layer of cool\, damp air near the ground."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06045
name: stray radiation error
def: "A spectrometer set to pass radiation of a particular wavelength band always has a small amount of stray radiation of other wavelengths. Since the sample may absorb more \(or less\) of this stray radiation than of the radiation at the selected wavelength an error can occur. This error is called a stray radiation error."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06047
name: streak tube
def: "An image converter adapted to provide scanning or time-resolved images. If the image is recorded the whole device is an example of a streak camera."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06048
name: streaming birefringence
def: "The birefringence induced by flow in liquids\, solutions and dispersions of optically anisotropic\, anisometric or deformable flow molecules or particles due to a non-random orientation of the molecules or particles. Synonymous with flow birefringence."
synonym: "flow birefringence" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06049
name: streaming current
def: "The electric current \\\(I\\\) flowing in a streaming cell if the electrodes\, which are supposed to be ideally depolarized\, are short-circuited. \\\(I\\\) is positive if the current in the membrane\, plug\, etc.\, is from high to low pressure side \(and in the outside lead from low to high pressure side\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06050
name: streaming potential difference
def: "The potential difference \\\(E_\{\\mathrm\{st\}\}\\\) or \\\(E\\\) at zero current caused by the flow of liquid under a pressure gradient through a membrane\, plug or capillary. Identical electrodes must be used on both sides of the membrane\, plug\, etc. \\\(E\\\) is positive if the higher potential is on the high pressure side."
synonym: "streaming potential" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06051
name: stress-assisted transition
def: "A transition that takes place when an applied stress assists the transition to the new phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06052
name: stress graphitization
def: "The solid-state transformation of non-graphitic carbon into graphite by heat treatment combined with application of mechanical stress\, resulting in a defined degree of graphitization being obtained at a lower temperature and/or after a shorter time of heat treatment than in the absence of applied stress."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06054
name: stripping
def: "The process of removing solute\(s\) from a loaded solvent or extract. Generally this refers to the main solute\(s\) present."
synonym: "back extraction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06057
name: stripping reaction
def: "A chemical process\, studied in a molecular beam\, in which the reaction products are scattered forward with respect to the moving centre of mass of the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06058
name: stripping solution
def: "A solution \(usually aqueous\, sometimes water alone\) used for back-extracting the distribuend from the extract \(usually organic\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06059
name: strong collision
def: "A collision between two molecules in which the amount of energy transferred from one to the other is large compared with \\\(k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\\\)\, where \\\(k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(T\\\) the absolute temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06060
name: structural disorder
def: "Any deviation from the ideal three-dimensional regularity of the crystal structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06061
name: structural formula
def: "A formula which gives information about the way the atoms in a molecule are connected and arranged in space."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06062
name: structural transition
def: "A reversible or irreversible transition that involves a change of the crystal structure. Example\: The transition of $\\ce\{NH4Cl\}$ at $\\pu\{469 K\}$ from a $\\ce\{CsCl\}$-type structure to a $\\ce\{NaCl\}$-type structure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06063
name: structure
def: "The distribution in space of the atoms or ions in the material part of the catalyst and\, in particular\, the distribution at the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06064
name: styphnates
def: "Salts or charge-transfer complexes of styphnic acid \(2\,4\,6-trinitrobenzene-1\,3-diol\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06065
name: subchain
def: "An arbitrarily chosen contiguous sequence of constitutional units in a chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06066
name: subgroup-supergroup transition
def: "A transition in which the space-group symmetry of the lower symmetry phase is a subgroup of that of the higher symmetry phase. Example\: The transition of the low-temperature polymorph of quartz characterized by space-group symmetry \\\(P3_\{1\}2\\\) \(trigonal\)\, to the high temperature polymorph of quartz with space-group symmetry\, \\\(P6_\{2\}22\\\) \(hexagonal\). Synonymous with 'symmetry-breaking transition' \(note\: this term\, strictly speaking\, is no longer in use\)."
synonym: "symmetry-breaking transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06067
name: subjacent orbital
def: "The Next-to-Highest Occupied Molecular Orbital \('NHOMO'\, also called 'HOMO'\) and the Second Lowest Unoccupied Molecular Orbital \(SLUMO\). Subjacent orbitals are sometimes found to play an important role in the interpretation of molecular interactions according to the frontier orbital approach."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06068
name: sublation
def: "A flotation process in which the material of interest\, adsorbed on the surface of gas bubbles in a liquid\, is collected on an upper layer of immiscible liquid. There is no liquid-phase mixing in the bulk of the system; as a result recoveries can approach 100%."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06069
name: sublimation
def: "The direct transition of a solid to a vapour without passing through a liquid phase. Example\: The transition of solid $\\ce\{CO2\}$ to $\\ce\{CO2\}$ vapour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06071
name: subsample
def: "A subsample may be\: \(a\) a portion of the sample obtained by selection or division; \(b\) an individual unit of the lot taken as part of the sample; \(c\) the final unit of multistage sampling. The term 'subsample' is used either in the sense of a 'sample of a sample' or as a synonym for 'unit'. In practice\, the meaning is usually apparent from the context or is defined."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06073
name: substance content
def: "Amount-of-substance of a component divided by the mass of the system."
synonym: "content" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06074
name: substance flow rate
def: "Amount-of-substance of a component crossing a surface divided by the time. For clinical chemistry the term component is recommended. ISO uses 'substance'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06075
name: substance fraction
def: "Ratio of the amount-of-substance of the component to the total amount-of-substance in the system containing the component."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06076
name: substituent atom
def: "An atom \(group\) that replaces one or more hydrogen atoms attached to a parent structure or characteristic group except for hydrogen atoms attached to a chalcogen atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06078
name: substitution reaction
def: "A reaction\, elementary or stepwise\, in which one atom or group in a molecular entity is replaced by another atom or group. For example\,"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06079
name: substitutive name
def: "A name which indicates the exchange of one or more hydrogen atoms attached to a skeletal atom of a parent structure or to an atom of a characteristic group for another atom or group\, which may be expressed by a suffix or by prefixes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06080
name: substoichiometric extraction
def: "Solvent extraction in which the amount of reagent used is lower than that dictated by stoichiometry. If the constants of formation and extraction of the complexes are high\, the amount of extracted metal is dictated by the amount of extractant introduced."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06081
name: substoichiometric isotope dilution analysis
def: "A kind of isotope dilution analysis in which the final isotopic abundance is estimated from the amount of the nuclide present in a known quantity of the relevant element separated from the test portion\, where this quantity is smaller than the total amount of that element present in the test portion."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06082
name: substrate
def: "A chemical species\, the reaction of which with some other chemical reagent is under observation \(e.g. a compound that is transformed under the influence of a catalyst\). The term should be used with care. Either the context or a specific statement should always make it clear which chemical species in a reaction is regarded as the substrate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06083
name: substrate
def: "The chemical entity whose conversion to a product or products is catalysed by one or several enzymes.A solution or dry mixture containing all ingredients which are necessary for the growth of a microbial culture or for product formation.Component in the nutrient medium\, supplying the organisms with carbon \(C-substrate\)\, nitrogen \(N-substrate\)\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06084
name: substrate
def: "The terms substrate and subphase refer to a condensed phase that physically supports a film or layer. The term substrate should be used with care in the light of the existing double meaning of the term substrate in film science and technology\, on the one hand\, and in biochemistry\, on the other. The term subphase applies\, mainly\, to a liquid phase underlying an adsorption layer or a floating spread monolayer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06085
name: subtractive name
def: "A name for a modified parent structure in which prefixes and/or suffixes indicate the removal of atoms or groups and\, where required\, replacement by an appropriate number of hydrogen atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06086
name: successor complex
def: "The radical ion pair which forms by the transfer of an electron from the donor D to the acceptor A after these species have diffused together to form the precursor or encounter complex\: \\\[\\rm\{A\}+\\rm\{D\}\\rightarrow \(\\rm\{A D\}\)\\rightarrow \(\\rm\{A\}^\{\\cdot - \}\\rm\{D\}^\{\\cdot +\}\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06088
name: sugars
def: "A loose term applied to monosaccharides and lower oligosaccharides."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06091
name: sulfamic acids
def: "$\\ce\{H2NS\(=O\)2OH\}$ \(sulfamic acid\) and its N-hydrocarbyl derivatives. Sulfamic acid is called sulfamidic acid in inorganic chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06092
name: sulfanes
def: "A term including hydropolysulfides\, polysulfanes\, polysulfides. Use is discouraged because of confusion with the newer systematic name sulfane\, $\\ce\{H2S\}$\, and the numerous names derived therefrom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06093
name: sulfatides
def: "Hydrogen sulfate esters of glycosphingolipids. Specific compounds should be named as glycerosphingolipid derivatives\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06094
name: sulfenamides
def: "Compounds derived from sulfenic acids\, $\\ce\{RSOH\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, by replacement of $\\ce\{–OH\}$ by $\\ce\{–NR2\}$. May alternatively be considered as alkylsulfanylamines\, e.g. $\\ce\{C2H5SNH2\}$ ethanesulfenamide or ethylsulfanylamine."
synonym: "alkylsulfanylamines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06095
name: sulfenes
def: "S\,S-Dioxides of thioaldehydes and thioketones\, $\\ce\{R2C=SO2\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06096
name: sulfenic acids
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RSOH\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhSOH\}$ benzenesulfenic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06097
name: sulfenium ions
def: "A term sometimes erroneously used for sulfenylium ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06098
name: sulfenyl groups
def: "Groups having the structure $\\ce\{RS–\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). This term is derived from sulfenic acids. The synonymous term sulfanyl groups is derived from sulfane\, $\\ce\{H2S\}$. E.g. $\\ce\{CH3–S–\}$ methanesulfenyl\, methylthio or methylsulfanyl."
synonym: "sulfanyl groups" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06099
name: sulfenylium ions
def: "Cations having the structure $\\ce\{RS^\{+\}\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). This term is derived from sulfenic acids. The synonymous term sulfanylium ions is derived from sulfane\, $\\ce\{H2S\}$. E.g. $\\ce\{CH3S^\{+\}\}$ methylsulfanylium or methanesulfenylium ion."
synonym: "sulfanylium ions" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06101
name: sulfenyl radicals
def: "Sulfur-centered radicals having the structure $\\ce\{RS^\{.\}\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). This term is derived from sulfenic acids. The synonymous term sulfanyl radicals is derived from sulfane\, $\\ce\{H2S\}$. E.g. $\\ce\{CH3S^\{.\}\}$ methylsulfanyl or methanesulfenyl radical. An older synonymous term is alkylthio radical."
synonym: "alkylthio radical" []
synonym: "sulfanyl radicals" []
synonym: "thiyl radicals" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06102
name: sulfides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RSR\}$ \($\\ce\{R \neq H\}$\). Such compounds were once called thioethers.See also\: thioacetalsIn an inorganic sense\, salts or other derivatives of hydrogen sulfide.A term used in additive nomenclature\, see imides \(2\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06104
name: sulfimides
def: "$\\ce\{H2S=NH\}$\, sulfimide\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives. They are thus related to sulfoxides in the same way that imines are related to aldehydes or ketones\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(C2H5\)2S=NPh\}$ S\,S-diethyl-N-phenylsulfimide.A term used in Chemical Abstracts Service Index Nomenclature for sulfonylamines\, $\\ce\{RN=S\(=O\)2\}$."
synonym: "sulfilimines" []
synonym: "sulfimines" []
synonym: "sulfinimines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06106
name: sulfinamides
def: "Amides of sulfinic acids\, $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)OH\}$; thus $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)NR2\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhS\(=O\)NHCH3\}$ N-methylbenzenesulfinamide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06107
name: sulfinamidines
def: "Amidines of sulfinic acids\, $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)OH\}$; thus $\\ce\{RS\(=NR\)NR\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhS\(=NH\)NH2\}$ benzenesulfinamidine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06109
name: sulfinic acids
def: "$\\ce\{HS\(=O\)OH\}$\, sulfinic acid\, and its S-hydrocarbyl derivatives\, e.g. $\\ce\{\(CH3\)2CHS\(=O\)OH\}$ propane-2-sulfinic acid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06110
name: sulfinic anhydrides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)OS\(=O\)R\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06112
name: sulfinylamines
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RN=S=O\}$. Formerly called thionylamines."
synonym: "thionylamines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06113
name: sulfolipids
def: "Sulfate esters of glycolipids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06114
name: sulfonamides
def: "Amides of sulfonic acids $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)2NR'2\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhS\(=O\)2NHCH3\}$ N-methylbenzenesulfonamide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06115
name: sulfonamidines
def: "A term abandoned because of its ambiguous use to mean either $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)=NH2\}$ \(sulfonimidamide\) or $\\ce\{RS\(=NH\)2=NH2\}$ \(sulfonodiimidamide\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06116
name: sulfonediimines
def: "Compounds having the structure\, $\\ce\{RS\(=NR\)2R\}$\, formally derived from sulfones by replacing $\\ce\{\(=O\)2\}$ by $\\ce\{\(=NR\)2\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{Ph2S\(=NH\)2\}$ diphenyl sulfonediimine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06117
name: sulfones
def: "Compounds having the structure\, $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)2R\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{C2H5S\(=O\)2CH3\}$ ethyl methyl sulfone."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06118
name: sulfonic acids
def: "$\\ce\{HS\(=O\)2OH\}$\, sulfonic acid\, and its S-hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06119
name: sulfonic anhydrides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RS\(=O\)2OS\(=O\)2R'\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhS\(=O\)2OS\(=O\)2Ph\}$ benzenesulfonic anhydride."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06121
name: sulfonium compounds
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R3S+\}$ and associated anion \(generally\, but not necessarily\, all three $\\ce\{R\}$ groups are hydrocarbyl\). E.g. $\\ce\{\[\(CH3\)3S\]^\{+\}Cl^\{−\}\}$\, trimethylsulfonium chloride\, or"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06122
name: sulfonphthaleins
def: "3\,3-Bis\(hydroxyaryl\)-3H-2\,1-benzoxathiole S\,S-dioxides\, formed by condensation of o-sulfobenzoic acid with phenols or related compounds\, e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06123
name: sulfonylamines
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RN=S\(=O\)2\}$. \(Indexed by Chemical Abstracts Service at the heading sulfimide\). E.g. $\\ce\{CH3N=S\(=O\)2\}$ N-sulfonylmethylamine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06124
name: sulfoxides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2S=O\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{Ph2S=O\}$ diphenyl sulfoxide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06125
name: sulfoximides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2S\(=O\)=NR\}$. \(Indexed by Chemical Abstracts Service at the heading sulfoximines\). E.g. $\\ce\{\(CH3\)2S\(=O\)=NPh\}$ S\,S-dimethyl-N-phenylsulfoximide."
synonym: "sulfonimides" []
synonym: "sulfoximines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06127
name: sulfur diimides
def: "The parent compound $\\ce\{HN=S=NH\}$ and hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06128
name: sultams
def: "Sulfonamides in which the S-N bond is part of a ring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06129
name: sultims
def: "Tautomeric forms of sultams\, having a sulfur–nitrogen double bond as part of a ring."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06130
name: sultines
def: "Intramolecular cyclic esters of hydroxy sulfinic acids. \(Named by supposed analogy with lactones and sultones; but the -ine ending is unfortunate\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06131
name: sultones
def: "Intramolecular cyclic esters of hydroxy sulfonic acids\, analogous to lactones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06132
name: summit current
def: "In ac polarography\, differential pulse polarography\, derivative polarography\, square-wave polarography\, and similar techniques\, the maximum value of the component of the current that is associated with the presence of a substance B. Normally this component of the current is faradaic\, and the maximum arises because the rate of variation \(with applied potential\) of the rate of the charge-transfer process passes through a maximum. Similar maxima arise when this component is non-faradaic \(and when B is surface-active rather than electroactive\). In a case known to be of the latter type\, the term apex current is recommended as being more specific."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06133
name: summit potential
def: "In ac polarography\, differential pulse polarography\, derivative polarography\, and similar techniques\, the potential of the indicator electrode at which the summit current is attained."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06134
name: sum of states
def: "The total number of states for a system corresponding to an energy \\\(\\varepsilon\\\) less than or equal to a specified value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06135
name: superacid
def: "A medium having a high acidity\, generally greater than that of 100 wt.% sulfuric acid. The common superacids are made by dissolving a powerful Lewis acid \(e.g. $\\ce\{SbF5\}$\) in a suitable Brønsted acid such as $\\ce\{HF\}$ or $\\ce\{HSO3F\}$. \(An equimolar mixture of $\\ce\{HSO3F\}$ and $\\ce\{SbF5\}$ is known by the trade name 'magic acid'.\) In a biochemical context 'superacid catalysis' is sometimes used to denote catalysis by metal ions analogous to catalysis by hydrogen ions. By analogy\, a compound having a very high basicity\, such as lithium diisopropylamide\, is called a 'superbase'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06137
name: superconducting transition
def: "A transition at the critical temperature\, \\\(T_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\)\, below which the resistance of electrical conductors becomes zero and magnetic flux is excluded."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06138
name: supercritical fluid
def: "The defined state of a compound\, mixture or element above its critical pressure \(\\\(p_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\)\) and critical temperature \(\\\(T_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06139
name: supercritical fluid chromatography
def: "A separation technique in which the mobile phase is a fluid above and relatively close to its critical temperature and pressure. In general the terms and definitions used in gas or liquid chromatography are equally applicable to supercritical fluid chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06140
name: superequivalent adsorption
def: "This occurs when the specifically adsorbed amount of charge in the inner Helmholtz plane is higher than the charge on the metal phase\, taken with the reverse sign."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06141
name: superexchange interaction
def: "Electronic interaction between two molecular entities mediated by one or more different molecules or ions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06142
name: superficial work
def: "The reversible work of formation of unit area of new surface by cleavage. It has previously been called specific surface work\, surface energy\, surface tension or surface free energy. The symbol recommended is \\\(\\gamma _\{\\pi \}\\\) in preference to \\\(\\sigma \\\) because of the confusion with surface charge density. The superficial work \\\(\\gamma _\{\\pi \}\\\) is a scalar quantity and consequently is isotropic."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06143
name: superlattice
def: "A periodic multilayer which is synthetic and where a unit cell\, consisting of successive layers that are chemically different from their adjacent neighbours\, is repeated. The term quantum well multilayer applies to the superlattices with artificially created electronic band structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06144
name: superposability
def: "The ability to bring two particular stereochemical formulae \(or models\) into coincidence \(or to be exactly superposable in space\, and for the corresponding molecular entities or objects to become exact replicas of each other\) by no more than translation and rigid rotation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06145
name: superradiance
def: "Spontaneous emission amplified by a single pass through a population inverted medium. It is distinguished from true laser action by its lack of coherence. The term superradiance is frequently used in laser technology."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06146
name: supersaturation
def: "In chemistry\, an unstable system which has a greater concentration of a material in solution than would exist at equilibrium is said to be supersaturated.In meteorology\, supersaturation of an air mass with respect to $\\ce\{H2O\}$ vapour is of special interest. It is the saturation ratio minus one\, or the percent supersaturation is the percent relative humidity minus 100."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06147
name: support
def: "In multiphase catalysts\, the active catalytic material is often present as the minor component dispersed upon a support sometimes called a carrier. The support may be catalytically inert but it may contribute to the overall catalytic activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06148
name: support-coated open-tubular column
def: "A version of a porous-layer open-tabular \(PLOT\) column in which the porous layer consists of support particles and was deposited from a suspension."
synonym: "SCOT column" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06149
name: supporting electrolyte
def: "An electrolyte solution\, whose constituents are not electroactive in the range of applied potentials being studied\, and whose ionic strength \(and\, therefore\, contribution to the conductivity\) is usually much larger than the concentration of an electroactive substance to be dissolved in it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06150
name: support plate
def: "The plate that supports the thin layer in thin-layer chromatography."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06151
name: suppressor
def: "A substance which reduces emission\, absorption\, or light scattering by an interferent\, thus removing or lowering spectral interference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06153
name: supramolecule
def: "A system of two or more molecular entities held together and organized by means of intermolecular \(noncovalent\) binding interactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06154
name: surface
def: "The boundary between two phases. It is recommended that for the purpose of surface analysis a distinction be made between 'surface' in general\, 'physical surface' and 'experimental surface'\: Surface — The 'outer portion' of a sample of undefined depth; to be used in general discussions of the outside regions of the sample. Physical Surface — That atomic layer of a sample which\, if the sample were placed in a vacuum\, is the layer 'in contact with' the vacuum; the outermost atomic layer of a sample. Experimental Surface — That portion of the sample with which there is significant interaction with the particles or radiation used for excitation. It is the volume of sample required for analysis or the volume corresponding to the escape for the emitted radiation or particle\, whichever is larger."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06156
name: surface amount
def: "Amount of substance adsorbed on a surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06157
name: surface barrier semiconductor detector
def: "A semiconductor detector utilizing a junction due to a surface inversion layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06159
name: surface charge density
def: "Electric charge on a surface divided by the surface area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06161
name: surface chemical potential
def: "Defined by\: \\\[\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}=\(\\frac\{\\partial A^\{\\sigma \}\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\}\)_\{T\,A_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\,n_\{j\}^\{\\sigma \}\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial G^\{\\sigma \}\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\}\)_\{T\,p\,\\gamma \,n_\{j\}^\{\\sigma \}\}\\\] \\\[\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\rm\{S\}\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial A^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\}\)_\{T\,V^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\,A_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\,n_\{j\}^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\}=\(\\frac\{\\partial G^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\}\{\\partial n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\}\)_\{T\,p\,\\gamma \,n_\{j\}^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(A^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess Helmholtz energy\, \\\(G^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess Gibbs energy\, \\\(A^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\) is the interfacial Helmholtz energy\, \\\(G^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\) is the interfacial Gibbs energy\, and \\\(A_\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\) is the surface area. The quantities thus defined can be shown to be identical\, and the conditions of equilibrium of component \\\(i\\\) in the system to be \\\[\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\alpha \}=\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}=\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\rm\{S\}\}=\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\beta \}\\\] where \\\(\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\alpha \}\\\) and \\\(\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\beta \}\\\) are the chemical potentials of \\\(i\\\) in the bulk phases $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$. \(\\\(\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\alpha \}\\\) or \\\(\\mu _\{i\}^\{\\beta \}\\\) have to be omitted from this equlibrium condition if component \\\(i\\\) is not present in the respective bulk phase.\) The surface chemical potentials are related to the Gibbs energy functions by the equations \\\[G^\{\\sigma \}=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\\ \\mu _\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\\\] \\\[G^\{\\rm\{S\}\}=\\sum _\{\\begin\{array\}\{c\}\ni\n\\end\{array\}\}n_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\ \\mu _\{i\}^\{\\rm\{S\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06162
name: surface concentration
def: "Amount of substance adsorbed on a surface divided by the surface area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06163
name: surface excess conductivity
def: "The excess conductivity in a surface per unit length and width\, symbol \\\(\\kappa ^\{\\sigma \}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06164
name: surface contamination
def: "Material in the experimental surface which is either not characteristic of the sample or which would not be present if the sample had been prepared in an absolute vacuum by methods not contacting other substances to the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06165
name: surface coverage
def: "Number of adsorbed molecules on a surface divided by the number of molecules in a filled monolayer on that surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06166
name: surface crossing
def: "In a diagram of electronic energy versus molecular geometry\, the electronic energies of two states of different symmetry may be equal at certain geometrical parameters. At this point \(unidimensional representation\)\, line or surface \(more than one dimension\)\, the two potential-energy surfaces are said to cross one another."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06167
name: surface density
def: "Mass divided by area."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06169
name: surface dipole layer
def: "Particles in the surface region of a phase are subjected to orienting forces as a result of the anisotropic force field. Polar molecules \(e.g. permanent dipoles\) may thus be preferentially oriented in the surface region\, while polarizable molecules may be polarized \(induced dipoles\). The array of oriented polar and/or polarized molecules is called the surface dipole layer with which an electric potential drop is associated\, called the surface potential of the phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06170
name: surface electric potential
def: "Given by \\\[\\chi ^\{\\beta\} = \\mathit\{\\Phi \}^\{\\beta\} - \\psi ^\{\\beta\}\\\] where \\\(\\psi ^\{\\beta \}\\\) and \\\(\\mathit\{\\Phi\} ^\{\\beta\}\\\) are the outer and inner electric potentials of phase $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06171
name: surface excess
def: "For an interface\, the adsorption or surface excess of a given component is defined as the difference between the amount of component actually present in the system\, and that which would be present \(in a reference system\) if the bulk concentration in the adjoining phases were maintained up to a chosen geometrical dividing surface \(Gibbs dividing surface\). For a solid/liquid interface in which no component of the liquid phase penetrates into the solid\, the surface excess \(or adsorption\) of component i is defined as\: \\\[n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}=n_\{i\}- V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\ c_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\] where \\\(n_\{i\}\\\) is the total amount of i in the system\, \\\(V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is the volume of an arbitrarily chosen amount of bulk liquid \(in the framework of the so-called algebraic method\) and \\\(c_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is its bulk concentration in the liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06172
name: surface excess concentration
def: "A quantity defined by \\\[\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\} = \\frac\{n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma \}\}\{A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(n_\{i\}^\{\\sigma\}\\\) is the surface excess \(or adsorption\) of component \\\(i\\\) and \\\(A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the area of the dividing surface or interface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06173
name: surface excess energy
def: "Defined by\: \\\[U^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\} \} = U - U^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}- U^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}= U - V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\ \\frac\{U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\} - V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\ \\frac\{U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\] where \\\(V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) satisfy the condition \\\(V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \} + V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \} = V\\\)\, the total volume of the system. \(\\\(\\frac\{U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\\\)\) and \(\\\(\\frac\{U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\)\) are the energy densities in the two bulk phases where \\\(U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(U_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) are the mean molar energies and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) are the mean molar volumes of the two phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06174
name: surface excess enthalpy
def: "Defined by\: \\\[H^\{\\sigma \}=U^\{\\sigma \}- \\gamma \\ A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\] where \\\(U^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess energy\, \\\(\\gamma \\\) is the surface tension\, and \\\(A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the area relative to a Gibbs surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06175
name: surface excess entropy
def: "Defined by\: \\\[S^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3C3\}\} = S - S^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}- S^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}=S- V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\ \\frac\{S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}- V^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\ \\frac\{S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\] \(\\\(\\frac\{S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\}\\\)\) and \(\\\(\\frac\{S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\}\\\)\) are the entropy densities in the two bulk phases\, where \\\(S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(S_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) are the mean molar entropies and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) are the mean molar volumes of the two phases."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06176
name: surface excess Gibbs energy
def: "Defined by \\\[G^\{\\sigma \}=H^\{\\sigma \}- T\\ S^\{\\sigma \}=A^\{\\sigma \}- \\gamma \\ A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\] where \\\(H^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess enthalpy\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(S^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess entropy\, \\\(A^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess Helmholtz energy\, \\\(\\gamma \\\) is the surface tension\, and \\\(A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the area relative to a Gibbs surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06177
name: surface excess Helmholtz energy
def: "Defined by\: \\\[A^\{\\sigma \}=U^\{\\sigma \}- T\\ S^\{\\sigma \}\\\] where \\\(U^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess energy\, \\\(T\\\) is the thermodynamic temperature\, and \\\(S^\{\\sigma \}\\\) is the surface excess entropy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06178
name: surface excess isotherm
def: "The function relating\, at constant temperature and pressure\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{n\}\\right\)\}\\\)\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{l\}\\right\)\}\\\) or \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{v\}\\right\)\}\\\)\, or the respective specific quantities \\\(\\frac\{n^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\ \\Delta x_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{m\}\\\)\, \\\(\\frac\{A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\ \\mathit\{\\Gamma \}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{l\}\\right\)\}\}\{m\}\\\) or \\\(\\frac\{V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\ \\Delta c_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{m\}\\\) to the mole fraction \(or concentration\) of component \\\(i\\\) in the equilibrium liquid phase. With solutions of more than two components such isotherms are unequivocal functions only when the ratios of the mole fractions \(or concentrations\) of all other components except \\\(i\\\) are kept constant. \(\\\(A_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) is the area of interface\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{n\}\\right\)\}\\\)\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{l\}\\right\)\}\\\)\, \\\(\\mathit\{\\Gamma\}_\{i\}^\{\\left\(\\rm\{v\}\\right\)\}\\\) are relative adsorptions\, \\\(\\Delta x_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is the change in mole fraction of \\\(i\\\) resulting from bringing a specified mass \\\(m\\\) of solid into contact with a specified amount of solution \\\(n^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\Delta c_\{i\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\) is the change in concentration resulting from bringing a specified mass of solid into contact with a specified amount of solution of volume \\\(V^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\).\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06179
name: surface ionization
def: "Takes place when an atom or molecule is ionized when it interacts with a solid surface. ionization occurs only when the work function of the surface\, the temperature of the surface\, and the ionization energy of the atom or molecule have an appropriate relationship."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06180
name: surface ions
def: "Ions that are constituents of the surface or which have a particularly high affinity for the surface or surface sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06181
name: surface layer
def: "The region of space comprising and adjoining the phase boundary within which the properties of matter are significantly different from the values in the adjoining bulk phases."
synonym: "interfacial layer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06182
name: surface of a phase
def: "The plane ideally marking the boundary between a phase and its environment."
synonym: "free surface" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06183
name: surface of tension
def: "The mechanical properties of an interfacial layer between two fluid phases may be expressed in terms of those of a geometrical surface of uniform tension called the surface of tension\, whose location is dependent on the distribution of the stress tensor within the interfacial layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06185
name: surface pressure
def: "The change of interfacial tension caused by addition of a given species to a base solution. When an area of liquid covered with a spread substance is separated from a clean area of surface by a mechanical barrier\, the force acting on unit length of the barrier is called the surface pressure\, \\\(\\pi \\\) or \\\(\\pi _\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, and is equal to \\\(\\gamma ^\{0\} - \\gamma \\\) where \\\(\\gamma ^\{0\}\\\) is the surface tension of the clean surface and \\\(\\gamma \\\) that of the covered surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06187
name: surface region
def: "The tridimensional region\, extending from the free surface of a condensed phase towards the interior\, where the properties differ from the bulk. The term surface may also be used in this sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06189
name: surface shear viscosity
def: "For steady state deformations a surface shear viscosity \\\(\\eta ^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\, and an area viscosity or surface dilatational viscosity \\\(\\zeta ^\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\) can be defined. In a Cartesian system with the x-axis normal to the surface\, they are defined by the equations\: \\\[\\eta ^\{\\rm\{s\}\} = \\frac\{\\sigma _\{xy\}\}\{\\frac\{\\partial \nu_\{y\}\}\{\\partial \nu_\{x\}\}\}\\\] \\\[\\zeta ^\{\\rm\{s\}\}=\\frac\{\\Delta \\gamma \}\{\\frac\{\\mathrm\{d\}\(\\ln A\)\}\{\\mathrm\{d\}t\}\}\\\] where \\\(\\sigma _\{xy\}\\\) is the shear component of the surface stress tensor\, \\\(\nu_\{x\}\\\) and \\\(\nu_\{y\}\\\) are the \\\(x\\\) and \\\(y\\\) components of the surface velocity vector\, respectively\, \\\(A\\\) is the surface area\, \\\(t\\\) is the time\, and \\\(\\Delta \\gamma \\\) is the difference between the \(steady state\) dynamic surface tension and the equilibrium surface tension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06190
name: surface states
def: "Energy levels localized in the surface region of semiconductors\, which do not bear any direct relation to the bulk energy distribution\, but which can exchange electrons with the bulk. In such a case a space charge may arise even when \\\(\\sigma \\\) \(the free charge density\) is zero\, i.e. \\\(\\sigma =\\sigma _\{\\mathrm\{sc\}\}+\\sigma _\{\\mathrm\{ss\}\}\\\)\, where \\\(\\sigma _\{\\mathrm\{sc\}\}\\\) is the space charge density and \\\(\\sigma _\{\\mathrm\{ss\}\}\\\) is the charge density associated with the surface states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06191
name: surface stress
def: "The work required to form unit area of new surface by stretching under equilibrium conditions. It is numerically equal to the force acting in the jth direction per unit length of exposed edge\, the edge being normal to the ith direction\, that must be applied to a terminating surface to keep it in equilibrium\, the ith and jth directions lying in the plane of the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06192
name: surface tension
def: "Work required to increase a surface area divided by that area. When two phases are studied it is often called interfacial tension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06193
name: surface work
def: "Surface tension is the intensive factor in the differential expression for the work required to increase the area of the surface of tension. Measured under reversible conditions at constant temperature \(and normally constant pressure\) and referred to unit area\, this work\, the so-called \(differential\) surface work\, is equal to the static surface tension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06194
name: surfactant
def: "A substance which lowers the surface tension of the medium in which it is dissolved\, and/or the interfacial tension with other phases\, and\, accordingly\, is positively adsorbed at the liquid/vapour and/or at other interfaces."
synonym: "surface active agent" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06195
name: surprisal
def: "A function relating the prior distribution \\\(P_\{0\}\\\) of product states to an observed or calculated product distribution P. It is defined by\: \\\[s=- \\ln \(\\frac\{P\}\{P_\{0\}\}\)\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06196
name: surprisal analysis
def: "The study of product state distributions in terms of surprisal has been referred to as surprisal analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06197
name: suspended matter
def: "All particulate material which persists in the atmosphere or in a flue gas stream for lengthy periods because the particles are too small in size to have an appreciable falling velocity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06198
name: suspension
def: "A liquid in which solid particles are dispersed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06199
name: suspension effect
def: "An effect which occurs when ion-selective electrodes are used in concentrated\, space-filled suspensions while the external reference electrode remains in the supernatant \(suspension-free\) solution. The suspensions are specifically solvent-swollen ion exchangers or other materials\, like soils and clays\, that concentrate ions by adsorption and absorption. Space-filled\, gravity-packed suspensions act like a second phase and form apparently an interfacial potential difference \(PD\) with respect to the supernatant. The measured ion activity in the suspension differs from the value in the supernatant by the interfacial PD\, and corresponds to a higher value approximating the activity inside the ion exchanger gel. The effect nearly disappears when the outer reference electrode is placed in the same region of the suspension as the sensor electrode. There are some changes in the junction potential differences of the reference electrode\, between suspension and supernatant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06200
name: Swain–Lupton equation
def: "A dual parameter approach to the correlation analysis of substituent effects\, which involves a field constant \(\\\(F\\\)\) and a resonance constant \(\\\(R\\\)\). The original treatment was modified later. The procedure has been considerably applied\, but also much criticized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06201
name: Swain–Scott equation
def: "The linear free-energy relation of the form\: \\\[\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{k\}\{k_\{0\}\}\)=s\\ n\\\] applied to the variation of reactivity of a given electrophilic substrate towards a series of nucleophilic reagents. \\\(n\\\) is characteristic of the reagent \(i.e. a measure of its nucleophilicity\) and \\\(s\\\) is characteristic of the substrate \(i.e. a measure of its sensitivity to the nucleophilicity of the reagent\). A scale of \\\(n\\\) values is based on the rate coefficients \\\(k\\\) for the reaction of methyl bromide with nucleophiles in water at \\\(25\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, \\\(s\\\) being defined as 1.00 for these reactions and \\\(n\\\) being defined as 0.00 for the hydrolysis of methyl bromide. \(Other scales have been devised.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06202
name: swelling
def: "The increase in volume of a gel or solid associated with the uptake of a liquid or gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06203
name: swelling pressure
def: "That pressure difference \\\(\\mathit\{\\Pi\} _\{\\rm\{sw\}\}\\\) or \\\(\\mathit\{\\Pi\}\\\) which has to be established between a gel and its equilibrium liquid\, to prevent further swelling of the gel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06204
name: switchboard model
def: "A model of crystallinity in which the crystallized segments of a macromolecule belong to the same crystal\, although the stems are connected randomly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06205
name: switching transition
def: "A transition in semiconductive glasses in which\, beyond a critical applied voltage\, there is an avalanche breakdown of conducting electrons that causes local melting and hence local crystallization that gives metallic conductivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06206
name: sydnone imines
def: "Imines of sydnones \(i.e. having an imino group in place of the exocyclic oxygen atom\).  \(These structures represent only two canonical forms.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06207
name: sydnones
def: "Mesoionic compounds having the 1\,2\,3-oxadiazole skeleton bearing an oxygen atom attached to the 5 position.  \(These structures represent only two canonical forms\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06208
name: <i>sym</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote symmetrical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06209
name: symbiosis
def: "The term was originally applied to describe the maximum flocking of either hard or soft ligands in the same complexes. For hydrocarbon molecules\, symbiosis implies that those containing a maximum number of $\\ce\{C–H\}$ bonds \(e.g. $\\ce\{CH4\}$\) or $\\ce\{C–C\}$ bonds \(e.g. $\\ce\{Me4C\}$\) are the most stable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06210
name: symbol
def: "Conventional sign designating a quantity or a unit of measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06211
name: symmetrical films
def: "The properties of fluid films depend on the nature of the film phase and that of each of the two neighbouring bulk phases. These films should be described\, where appropriate\, by three capital letters such as A for air\, W for water\, O for oil\, and S for solid\, separated by solidi\, the middle letter indicating the film phase. For symmetrical films the first and last symbols are the same\, e.g. A/W/A\: water film in air\, or W/O/W\: oil film in water\, whereas for unsymmetrical films these are different\, e.g. W/O/A\: oil film between water and air."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06213
name: symmetry-conserving transition
def: "A transition in which the cell dimensions and/or angles of the one phase differ from those in the other phase\, but where the space-group symmetry is conserved. Example\: The transition of face-centred cubic $\\ce\{Ce\}$\, upon cooling\, to a face-centred cubic phase that is $\\pu\{10\\%\}$ denser. Upon cooling\, enough contraction takes place to allow an overlap of the \\\(\\rm\{fsp\}^\{2\}\\\) configuration and the change from an isolated non-bonding magnetic f electron to a bonding non-magnetic electron pair."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06214
name: symmetry number
def: "The symmetry number of a molecule is obtained by imagining all identical atoms to be labelled\, and then counting the number of different but equivalent arrangements that can be obtained by rotating \(but not reflecting\) the molecule. In the statistical-mechanical treatment of chemical equilibrium\, the partition function for each molecular species must be divided by its symmetry number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06216
name: <i>syn</i>
def: "See\: torsion angleSee\: endo\, exo\, syn\, antiSee\: antiFormerly used to describe the stereochemistry of oximes and related systems.See\: E\, Z"

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06218
name: synchronization
def: "This principle applies to reactions in which there is a lack of synchronization between bond formation or bond rupture and other primitive changes that affect the stability of products and reactants\, such as resonance\, solvation\, electrostatic\, hydrogen bonding and polarizability effects. The principle states that a product-stabilizing factor whose development lags behind bond changes at the transition state\, or a reactant-stabilizing factor whose loss is ahead of bond changes at the transition state\, increases the intrinsic barrier and decreases the 'intrinsic rate constant' of a reaction. For a product-stabilizing factor whose development is ahead of bond changes\, or reactant factors whose loss lags behind bond changes\, the opposite relations hold. The reverse effects are observable for factors that destabilize a reactant or product."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06219
name: synchronous
def: "A concerted process in which the primitive change concerned \(generally bond rupture and bond formation\) have progressed to the same extent at the transition state is said to be synchronous. The term figuratively implies a more or less synchronized progress of the changes. However\, the progress of the bonding change \(or other primitive change\) has not been defined quantitatively in terms of a single parameter applicable to different bonds or different bonding changes. The concept is therefore in general only qualitatively descriptive and does not admit an exact definition except in the case of concerted processes involving changes in two identical bonds."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06220
name: synchronously excited spectrum
def: "A two-dimensional spectrum obtained by varying both the excitation and emission wavelengths simultaneously and which corresponds to the curve where a plane\, parallel to the z-axis\, intersects the excitation-emission spectrum \(EES\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06221
name: synchrotron radiation
def: "X-Radiation which results from the acceleration of charged particles in circular orbits by strong electric and magnetic fields."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06223
name: syndet
def: "A synthetic detergent; a detergent other than soap."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06224
name: syndiotactic macromolecule
def: "A tactic macromolecule\, essentially comprising alternating enantiomeric configurational base units\, which have chiral or prochiral atoms in the main chain in a unique arrangement with respect to their adjacent constitutional units."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06225
name: syndiotactic polymer
def: "A polymer composed of syndiotactic macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06227
name: syneresis
def: "Spontaneous shrinking of a gel with exudation of liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06228
name: synergism
def: "A term describing the co-operative effect of two \(or more\) extractants where the distribution ratio for the combination is greater than the largest individual distribution ratio \(measured under comparable conditions\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06229
name: synergism
def: "Pharmacological or toxicological interaction in which the combined biological effect of two or more substances is greater than expected on the basis of the simple summation of the toxicity of each of the individual substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06230
name: synoptic scale
def: "In meteorology\, the size or scale of ordinary weather systems or cyclones; typically \\\(1000\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\) horizontally."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06232
name: syntectic reaction
def: "A reversible reaction that involves the conversion of two liquid phases\, \\\(\\rm\{l\}^\{'\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{l\}^\{''\}\\\)\, into a solid phase $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ on cooling\: \\\[\\rm\{l\}^\{'\}+\\rm\{l\}^\{''\}\\rightleftharpoons \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\\] The maximum temperature at which this reaction can occur is the congruent melting point of the solid phase. Example\: The conversion of co-existing $\\ce\{K\}$-rich and $\\ce\{Zn\}$-rich phases in the $\\ce\{K-Zn\}$ system to form an intermediate solid phase $\\ce\{KZn13\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06233
name: synthetic graphite
def: "A material consisting of graphitic carbon which has been obtained by graphitizing of non-graphitic carbon by chemical vapour deposition \(CVD\) from hydrobcarbons at temperatures above \\\(2500\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\, by decomposition of thermally unstable carbides or by crystallizing from metal melts supersaturated with carbon."
synonym: "artificial graphite" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06234
name: system
def: "Arbitrarily defined part of the universe\, regardless of form or size\, e.g. for clinical chemistry\, patient\, patient plasma\, patient urine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06235
name: systematic error
def: "Mean that would result from an infinite number of measurements of the same measurand carried out under repeatability conditions minus a true value of the measurand."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06236
name: systematic name
def: "A name composed wholly of specially coined or selected syllables\, with or without numerical prefixes; e.g. pentane\, oxazole."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06237
name: system of units
def: "Set of base units\, together with derived units\, defined in accordance with given rules\, for a given system of quantities\, e.g. Système International d'Unités \(SI\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:S06239
name: Szilard–Chalmers effect
def: "The rupture of the chemical bond between an atom and the molecule of which the atom is part\, as a result of a nuclear reaction of that atom."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06754
name: sialon
def: "A generic name for compounds or solid solutions in the system $\\ce\{M–Si–Al–O–N\}$ where $\\ce\{M\}$ is a metal. \(The use of capitals as in SiAlON is discouraged since they suggest a chemical composition.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06775
name: sequence
def: "The order of neighbouring amino acids in a protein or of the purine or pyrimidine bases in RNA or DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06784
name: Soret band
def: "A very strong absorption band in the blue region of the optical absorption spectrum of a haem protein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06785
name: stability constant
def: "An equilibrium constant that expresses the propensity of a substance to form from its component parts. The larger the stability constant\, the more stable is the species. The stability constant \(formation constant\) is the reciprocal of the instability constant \(dissociation constant\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06802
name: systemic effect
def: "Consequence that is either of a generalized nature or that occurs at a site distant from the point of entry of a substance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06842
name: second messenger
def: "Intracellular effector substance increasing or decreasing as a response to the stimulation of a receptor by an agonist\, considered as the 'first messenger'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06843
name: serum
def: "Watery proteinaceous portion of the blood that remains after clotting. Synonym\: blood serumClear watery fluid especially that moistening the surface of serous membranes or that exuded through inflammation of any of these membranes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06844
name: stem cell
def: "Multipotent cell with mitotic potential that may serve as a precursor for many kinds of differentiated cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06845
name: structure–activity relationship
def: "Association between specific aspects of molecular structure and defined biological action."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06848
name: speciation analysis
def: "Analytical activities of identifying and/or measuring the quantities of one or more individual chemical species in a sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06861
name: speciation
def: "Distribution of an element amongst defined chemical species in a system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06868
name: side group or side-chain polymer liquid crystal
def: "A polymer\, the molecules of which have mesogenic units only in side-group's side-chains."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06872
name: semi-rigid chain
def: "A chain for which the contour length is greater than the persistence length but for which their ratio is still below the Gaussian limit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06873
name: spacer
def: "A flexible segment used to link successive mesogenic units in the molecules of MCPLCs \(main-chain polymer liquid crystals\) or to attach mesogenic units as side-groups onto the polymer backbone of SGPLCs \(side-group polymer liquid crystals\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06914
name: stochastic effect
def: "Consequence for which the probability of occurrence depends on the absorbed dose; hereditary effects and cancer induced by radiation are considered to be stochastic effects. The term 'stochastic' indicates that the occurrence of effects so named would be random. This means that\, even for an individual\, there is no threshold of dose below which there is absolutely no probability of an effect occurring\, and the chance of experiencing the effect increases with increasing dose."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06980
name: simplified molecular input line entry system
def: "SMILES \(Simplified Molecular Input Line Entry System\) is a string notation used to describe the nature and topology of molecular structures."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06981
name: structure-based design
def: "Structure-based design is a design strategy for new chemical entities based on the three-dimensional \(3D\) structure of the target obtained by X-ray or nuclear magnetic resonance \(NMR\) studies\, or from protein homology models."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST06982
name: structure-property correlations
def: "Structure-property correlations \(SPC\) refer to all statistical mathematical methods used to correlate any molecular property \(intrinsic\, chemical or biological\) to any other property\, using statistical regression or pattern recognition techniques."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07097
name: scaling factor
def: "A variational parameter used as a multiplier of each nuclear Cartesian and electronic coordinate chosen to minimize the variational integral and to make a trial variation function to satisfy the virial theorem. In practical calculations\, the numeral factor to scale computed values\, e.g. vibrational frequencies\, to those found in experiments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07098
name: secular equation
def: "In molecular orbital theory\, the determinant of matrix elements whose solution leads to the set of energy levels of molecular orbitals for a given system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07099
name: semi-empirical quantum mechanical methods
def: "Methods which use parameters derived from experimental data to simplify computations. The simplification may occur at various levels\: simplification of the Hamiltonian \(e.g.\, as in the Extended Hückel method\)\, approximate evaluation of certain molecular integrals \(see\, for example\, zero differential overlap approximation\)\, simplification of the wavefunction \(for example\, use of a Pi \($\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\}$\) electron approximation as in Pariser–Parr–Pople method\)\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07100
name: Slater determinant
def: "The determinantal representation of many-electron wavefunction \\\(\\Psi \\\) which conforms to the requirement of the antisymmetry principle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07101
name: soliton
def: "Localized excitations propagating in a system with constant velocity and colliding with each other without change in their shapes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07102
name: solvation energy
def: "The change in Gibbs energy when an ion or molecule is transferred from a vacuum \(or the gas phase\) to a solvent. The main contributions to the solvation energy come from\:  the cavitation energy of formation of the hole which preserves the dissolved species in the solvent;  the orientation energy of partial orientation of the dipoles; the isotropic interaction energy of electrostatic and dispersion origin; and  the anisotropic energy of specific interactions\, e.g. hydrogen bonds\, donor-acceptor interactions etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07103
name: spin contamination
def: "In unrestricted Hartree–Fock method\, the wavefunctions obtained are eigenfunctions of the Hamiltonian and the spin projection \\\(S_\{\\rm\{z\}\}\\\) operators\, but not eigenfunctions of the \\\(S^\{2\}\\\) operator. As a result\, the wavefunctions of the doublet systems are spin-contaminated to some extent by admixtures of quartet\, sextet\, and higher spin states. The eigenvalues of the \\\(S^\{2\}\\\) operator are given as a measure of the spin contamination."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07104
name: spin crossover
def: "A type of molecular magnetism that is the result of electronic instability \(see electronic stability\) caused by external constraints \(temperature\, pressure\, or electromagnetic radiation\)\, which induce structural changes at molecular and lattice levels. The phenomenon is most characteristic of first-row transition metal complexes\, e.g. those of $\\ce\{Fe^\{2+\}\}$. An example of spin-crossover complexes \(the term of spin-state isomers is also used\) is $\\ce\{\[Fe\(2-pic\)3\]Cl2.EtOH\}$ \(2-pic = 2-picolylamine\). At the $\\ce\{Fe–N\}$ distance of \\\(203.2\\ \\rm\{pm\}\\\) \(\\\(115\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\)\, the complex has an electronic low-spin state \($^\{1\}\{\\rm\{A\}\}_\{1\}$\)\, whereas stretching the bond up to \\\(219.9\\ \\rm\{pm\}\\\) at \\\(227\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\) induces the transition to a high-spin state \($^\{5\}\{\\rm\{T\}\}_\{2\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07105
name: spin polarization
def: "Static and dynamic spin polarization effects are distinguished. The static polarization of an electron spin occurs in the $\\ce\{C-H\}$ bonds of aromatic radicals where the $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ closest to an unpaired $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$ tends to have its spin parallel to that of the $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electron\}$. Likewise\, static spin polarization arises in the $\\ce\{\\upsigma\\!\\mbox\{-\}system\}$ of conjugated radicals\: the electron of the doubly occupied molecular orbital prefers to take a closer spatial position to the odd one\, which spin is parallel to the latter. The effect reflects the energy unfavourable situation if an electron of opposite spin were to come nearby."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07106
name: spin projection
def: "A component\, \\\(M_\{S\}\\\)\, of the angular spin moment \\\(S\\\) along an arbitrary axis \(usually chosen as the z-direction\). \\\(M_\{S\}\\\) can take the values between \\\(-S\\\) and \\\(S\\\)\: \\\(-S\\\)\, \\\(-S + 1\\\)\,...\, \\\(S- 1\\\)\, \\\(S\\\). The term is also used to denote an operation \(spin-symmetry projection\) allowing one to eliminate spin contamination in the cases where wavefunctions are not eigenfunctions of the operator \\\(S^\{2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07107
name: stereochemical non-rigidity
def: "The capability of a molecule to undergo fast and reversible intramolecular isomerization\, the energy barrier to which is lower than that allowing for the preparative isolation of the individual isomers at room temperature. It is conventional to assign to the sterochemically non-rigid systems those compounds whose molecules rearrange rapidly enough to influence NMR line shapes at temperatures within the practical range \(from \\\(\\pu\{-100^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\) to \\\(\\pu\{200^\{\\circ\}C\}\\\)\) of experimentation. The energy barriers to thus defined rearrangements fall into the range of \\\(5\\\)–\\\(\\pu\{20 kcal/mol\}\\\) \(\\\(21\\\)–\\\(\\pu\{85 kJ/mol\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07108
name: strain energy
def: "The excess energy due to steric strain of a molecular entity or transition state structure\, i.e. distortions relative to a reference \(real or hypothetical\) 'strainless' structure with the standard bond lengths\, bond angles and dihedral angles. The strain energy components involve the following destabilizing terms\: non-bonded repulsions\, bond-angle distortions\, bond stretch or compression\, rotation around or twisting of double bonds\, and electrostatic strain. In general\, the contributions of these components are inseparable and interdependent. A quantitative assessment of strain and strain energies can be made by taking the difference between the heat of formation of the substance under consideration and that of a hypothetical strain-free model. Several approaches to the assessment of strain energies have been developed based on the use of energies of isodesmic and homodesmotic reactions and on the so-called 'strainless increments'\, i.e. heats of formation of certain groups \($\\ce\{CH3\}$\, $\\ce\{CH2\}$\, $\\ce\{CH\}$\, $\\ce\{C\}$ etc\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07109
name: structural stability
def: "Within the Born–Oppenheimer \(adiabatic\) approximation\, this is associated with an energy minimum on a potential energy surface. Structural stability implies that any change in the coordinates of the nuclei can only increase the total energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07110
name: sudden polarization
def: "The occurrence of a large intramolecular charge separation in the singlet excited state of polyenes and their derivatives twisted about a double bond. Non-symmetrical substitution or geometrical distortion is effective in polarizing the system. An example is the stabilization of the zwitterionic structure of 90° twisted ethene with one methylene group pyramidalized\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07111
name: supramolecular chemistry
def: "A field of chemistry related to species of greater complexity than molecules\, that are held together and organized by means of intermolecular interactions. The objects of supramolecular chemistry are supermolecules and other polymolecular entities that result from the spontaneous association of a large number of components into a specific phase \(membranes\, vesicles\, micelles\, solid state structures etc.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07151
name: sol-gel process
def: "Process through which a network is formed from solution by a progressive change of liquid precursor\(s\) into a sol\, to a gel\, and in most cases finally to a dry network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07152
name: surface grafting
def: "Process in which a polymer surface is chemically modified by grafting or by the generation of active sites that can lead to the initiation of a graft polymerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07194
name: shape-memory polymer
def: "Polymer that\, after heating and being subjected to a plastic deformation\, resumes its original shape when heated above its glass-transition or melting temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07195
name: superabsorbent polymer
def: "Polymer that can absorb and retain extremely large amounts of a liquid relative to its own mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07227
name: solid polymer electrolyte
def: "Electrically conducting solution of a salt in a polymer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07274
name: spinodal
def: "Curve defining the region of composition and temperature for a binary mixture across which a transition occurs from conditions where single-phase mixtures are metastable to conditions where single-phase mixtures are unstable and undergo phase separation by spinodal decomposition."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07294
name: soft-segment phase domain
def: "Phase domain of microscopic or smaller size\, usually in a block\, graft\, or segmented copolymer\, comprising essentially those segments of the polymer that have glass transition temperatures lower than the temperature of use."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07295
name: segmented copolymer
def: "Copolymer containing phase domains of microscopic or smaller size\, with the domains constituted principally of single types of structural unit."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07395
name: spectral sensitivity
def: "Referred to actinometer based on spectrophotometric measurements\, \\\(S_\{\\rm\{ac\}\}\\left\(\\lambda \\right\)=\\varPhi \\left\(\\lambda \\right\)\\ \\varepsilon\\left\(\\lambda _\{\\rm\{obs\}\}\\right\)=\\rm\{sensitivity or actinometric factor\}\\\). \\\(\\varPhi \\left\(\\lambda \\right\)\\\) is the quantum yield of the actinometer at the excitation wavelength and \\\(\\lambda _\{\\rm\{obs\}\}\\\) is the observation wavelength\, which may be the same as or differ from the former."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07417
name: self-localized excitations
def: "Physical and chemical properties of conjugated organic polymers with $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}electrons\}$ have been interpreted in terms of self-localized excitations\, which are quasi-particles with structural changes over several repeating units. These excitations can be classified into solitons\, polarons\, bipolarons and excitons according to their charge and spin."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07466
name: solubility parameter
def: "Parameter used in predicting the solubility of non-electrolytes \(including polymers\) in a given solvent.For a substance \\\(\\rm\{B\}\\\)\, \\\[\\delta _\{\\rm\{B\}\} = \\left \( \\frac\{\\Delta _\{\\rm\{vap\}\}E_\{\\rm\{m\,B\}\}\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\,B\}\}\} \\right \)^\{1/2\}\\\] where \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{vap\}\}E_\{\\rm\{m\,B\}\}\\\) is the molar energy of vaporization at zero pressure and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\,B\}\}\\\) is the molar volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07476
name: Schenck reaction
def: "The diastereoselective ene reaction of singlet molecular dioxygen with alkenes. An example is\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07477
name: singlet–triplet crossing
def: "Point of intersection between the potential energy surfaces of states of different multiplicity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07478
name: solvent-induced symmetry breaking
def: "Breaking of symmetry of a molecular species by interactions with the solvent that can modify the molecular charge distribution\, to favour asymmetrical configurations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07479
name: spectral fluence rate
def: "Derivative of fluence rate\, \\\(E_\{\\rm\{o\}\}\\\)\, with respect to wavelength\, \\\(\\lambda\\\). SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-3\}\\\); common unit is \\\(\\rm\{W m\}^\{-2\}\\ \\rm\{nm\}^\{-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07480
name: spectral radiant energy
def: "Derivative of radiant energy\, \\\(Q\\\)\, with respect to wavelength \\\(\\lambda \\\). SI unit is \\\(\\pu\{J m-1\}\\\); common unit is \\\(\\pu\{J nm-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07481
name: spin-flip method
def: "Quantum mechanical method for the calculation of open-shell excited states. The method accurately describes low-lying multi-configurational electronic states of diradicals and triradicals in an efficient and robust single-reference scheme. The target low-spin states \(e.g.\, S = 0 or S = 1/2\) are described as spin-flipping excitations \(S = −1\) from a well-behaved high-spin reference state \(e.g.\, S = 1 or S = 3/4\). By employing theoretical models of increasing complexity for the reference \(e.g.\, SCF\, MP2\, CCSD\)\, the accuracy in the target states’ description can be systematically improved. The SF methods result in multistate single-step computational schemes\, e.g.\, several low-lying states can be computed in a single calculation that includes both dynamical and non-dynamical correlation effects."
synonym: "SF method" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07482
name: spin-statistical factor
def: "From the possible encounter pairs between states of different spin multiplicity\, only those conserving multiplicity in going to products are expected to react."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07508
name: slip
def: "Ceramic precursor dispersed in a liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07512
name: sonosol
def: "Sol produced by the action of ultrasonically induced cavitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07513
name: sol fraction
def: "Mass fraction of the dissolved or dispersed material\, resulting from a network-forming polymerization or crosslinking process\, that is constituted of molecules of finite \(statistically definable\) relative molecular masses."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07530
name: sonogel
def: "Colloidal gel produced by the action of ultrasonically induced cavitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07538
name: swelling agent
def: "Fluid used to swell a gel\, network or solid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07566
name: sequential interpenetrating polymer network
def: "Interpenetrating polymer network prepared by a process in which the second component network is formed following the formation of the first component network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07567
name: simultaneous interpenetrating polymer network
def: "Interpenetrating polymer network prepared by a process in which the component networks are formed concurrently."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07574
name: sequential semi-interpenetrating polymer network
def: "Semi-interpenetrating polymer network prepared by a process in which the linear or branched components are formed following the completion of the reactions that lead to the formation of the network\(s\) or vice versa."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07575
name: simultaneous semi-interpenetrating polymer network
def: "Semi-interpenetrating polymer network prepared by a process in which the networks and the linear or branched components are formed concurrently."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07586
name: sol-gel material
def: "Material formed through a sol-gel process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07587
name: sol-gel coating
def: "Coating formed through a sol-gel process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07588
name: sol-gel metal oxide
def: "Metal oxide formed through a sol-gel process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07589
name: sol-gel silica
def: "Silica formed through a sol-gel process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07631
name: sedimentation
def: "Separation of a dispersed system under the action of a gravitational or centrifugal field according to the different densities of the components."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07632
name: shrinkage
def: "Decrease in volume of a network\, gel or solid associated with the exudation of a fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07633
name: slip casting
def: "Procedure in ceramic processing whereby slip is contained in a porous plaster mould prior to pyrolysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07634
name: sol-gel critical concentration
def: "Concentration of an added electrolyte above which a particulate sol undergoes coagulation instead of gelation."
synonym: "critical concentration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:ST07636
name: supercritical drying of a gel
def: "Drying of a gel using a supercritical fluid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T03420
name: λ-transition
def: "A second-order or higher-order transition\, in which the heat capacity shows either a discontinuity \(second-order\) or a vertex \(higher-order\) at the transition temperature. Example\: The transition at \\\(846\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)\, accompanied by a finite change in specific heat capacity at constant pressure \\\(C_\{p\}\\\)\, of the low-temperature polymorph of quartz to the high-temperature polymorph."
synonym: "lambda-transition" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06241
name: θ temperature
def: "The temperature at which a solution is in the θ state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06242
name: tactic block
def: "A regular block that can be described by only one species of configurational repeating unit in a single sequential arrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06243
name: tactic block polymer
def: "A polymer\, the molecules of which consist of tactic blocks connected linearly."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06244
name: tacticity
def: "The orderliness of the succession of configurational repeating units in the main chain of a regular macromolecule\, a regular oligomer molecule\, a regular block or a regular chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06245
name: tactic macromolecule
def: "A regular macromolecule in which essentially all the configurational repeating unit are identical."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06246
name: tactic polymer
def: "A polymer composed of tactic macromolecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06247
name: Taft equation
def: "Various equations are associated with R.W. Taft\, but the term is most often used to designate the family of equations that emerged from Taft's analysis of the reactivities of aliphatic esters\, and which involved the polar substituent constant \\\(\\sigma \\rm\{*\}\\\) and the steric substituent constant \\\(E_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\)\: \\\[\\log _\{10\}k = \\log _\{10\}k_\{0\}+\\rho \\rm\{*\}\\ \\sigma \\rm\{*\}+\\delta \\ E_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\] or the one-parameter forms applicable when the role of either the polar term or the steric term may be neglected. Nowadays \\\(\\sigma \\rm\{*\}\\\) is usually replaced by the related constant \\\(\\sigma _\{I\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06248
name: tagged
def: "Made identifiable by a label."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06249
name: tailing
def: "Asymmetry of a peak such that\, relative to the baseline\, the front is steeper than the rear. In paper chromatography and in thin-layer chromatography\, the distortion of a zone showing a diffuse region behind the zone in the direction of travel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06250
name: tandem mass spectrometer
def: "An arrangement in which ions are subjected to two or more sequential stages of analysis \(which may be separated spatially or temporally\) according to the quotient mass/charge. A hybrid mass spectometer is an instrument which combines analysers of different types\, e.g. magnetic plus electric sector combined with quadrupole. The study of ions involving two stages of mass analysis has been termed mass spectrometry/mass spectrometry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06252
name: tautomerism
def: "Isomerism of the general form\:  where the isomers \(called tautomers\) are readily interconvertible; the atoms connecting the groups $\\ce\{X\}$\, $\\ce\{Y\}$\, $\\ce\{Z\}$ are typically any of $\\ce\{C\}$\, $\\ce\{H\}$\, $\\ce\{O\}$ or $\\ce\{S\}$\, and $\\ce\{G\}$ is a group which becomes an electrofuge or nucleofuge during isomerization. The commonest case\, when the electrofuge is H+\, is also known as 'prototropy'. Examples\, written so as to illustrate the general pattern given above\, include\: keto-enol tautomerism\, such as\:   The grouping Y may itself be a three-atom \(or five-atom\) chain extending the conjugation\, as in\:  The double bond between $\\ce\{Y\}$ and $\\ce\{Z\}$ may be replaced by a ring\, when the phenomenon is called ring-chain tautomerism\, as in\:"
synonym: "cationotropic rearrangement" []
synonym: "cationotropy" []
synonym: "prototropic rearrangement" []
synonym: "prototropy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06253
name: tautomerization
def: "The isomerization by which tautomers are interconverted. It is a heterolytic molecular rearrangement and is frequently very rapid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06256
name: tele-substitution
def: "A substitution reaction in which the entering group takes up a position more than one atom away from the atom to which the leaving group was attached\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06257
name: tellurides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RTeR\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). Thus tellurium analogues of ethers.Metal salts of tellurane\, $\\ce\{H2Te\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06258
name: tellurones
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2Te\(=O\)2\}$. Thus tellurium analogues of sulfones."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06260
name: telomerization
def: "The formation of an addition oligomer\, having uniform end groups $\\ce\{X'\\ldots X^\{''\}\}$\, by a chain reaction in which a chain transfer limits the length of the polymer \('telomer'\) produced. An example is the polymerization of styrene in bromotrichloromethane solution \($\\ce\{X'\} = \\ce\{CCl3\}$\, $\\ce\{X^\{''\}\} = \\ce\{Br\}$\)\, where $\\ce\{Cl3C^\{.\}\}$ radicals are formed in the initiation step to produce $\\ce\{Cl3C\[CH2CHPh\]_\{n\}Br\}$\, with \\\(n\\\) greater than 1 and often less than ca. 10\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06261
name: temperature
def: "Short form for thermodynamic\, Celsius or Fahrenheit temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06262
name: temperature coefficient
def: "The dependence of a detector on temperature can be described by the temperature coefficient of responsivity and is expressed as percentage change in output per \\\(\\mathrm\{K\}\\\). In the case of a nonlinear dependence the temperature and the temperature range should also be stated for which the stated temperature coefficient of responsivity is applicable."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06263
name: temperature effect
def: "A term used for changes in the luminescence parameters caused by changes in temperature\, since the type of solvent and its temperature can effect the luminescence yield from an analyte as a result of quenching\, exciplex formation\, aggregation\, etc."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06264
name: temperature inversion
def: "A departure from the normal decrease of temperature with increasing altitude. A temperature inversion may be produced\, for example\, by the movement of a warm air mass over a cool one. Intense surface inversions may form over the land during nights with clear skies and low winds due to the radiative loss of heat from the surface of the earth. The temperature increases as a function of height in this case. Poor mixing of the pollutants generally occurs below the inversion\, since the normal convective process which drives the warmer and lighter air at ground level to higher altitudes is interrupted as the rising air parcels encounter the warmer air above. Temperature inversions near the surface are particularly effective in trapping ground level emissions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06265
name: temperature jump
def: "A relaxation technique in which the temperature of a chemical system is suddenly raised. The system then relaxes to a new state of equilibrium\, and analysis of the relaxation processes provides rate constants."
synonym: "T-jump" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06266
name: temperature lapse rate
def: "The rate of change of temperature with altitude \(\\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}T\}\{\\rm\{d\}z\}\\\)\). The rate of temperature decrease with increase in altitude which is expected to occur in an unperturbed dry air mass is \\\(9.8\\times 10^\{3\}\\ ^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C min\}^\{-1\}\\\). This is called the dry adiabatic lapse rate. The lapse rate is taken as positive when temperature decreases with increasing height. For air saturated with $\\ce\{H2O\}$\, the lapse rate is less because of the release of the latent heat of water as it condenses. The average tropospheric lapse rate is about \\\(6.5\\times 10^\{3\}\\ ^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\ \\min ^\{-1\}\\\). The lapse rate has a negative value within an inversion layer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06267
name: temperature-programmed chromatography
def: "A procedure in which the temperature of the column is changed systematically during a part or the whole of the separation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06269
name: template
def: "The nucleic acid single strand that is copied during replication or transcription."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06270
name: temporary poisoning
def: "Weak and reversible adsorption of a poison\, such that removal of the poison from the fluid phase results in restoration of the original catalytic activity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06271
name: tera
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{12\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{T\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06272
name: term
def: "Energy divided by the product of the Planck constant and the speed of light\, when of wavenumber dimension\, or energy divided by the Planck constant\, when of frequency dimension."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06273
name: term
def: "A set of levels which have the same electron configuration and the same value of the quantum numbers for total spin \\\(S\\\) and total orbital angular momentum \\\(L\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06274
name: termination
def: "The steps in a chain reaction in which reactive intermediates are destroyed or rendered inactive\, thus ending the chain."
synonym: "chain-ending step" []
synonym: "chain-termination reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06275
name: terminator
def: "A sequence of DNA lying beyond the 3' end of the coding segment of a gene which is recognized by RNA polymerase as a signal to stop synthesizing mRNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06277
name: term symbols
def: "Symbols characterizing the states of atoms and molecules in terms of multiplicity\, symmetry of the total electronic wavefunction\, and sometimes total \(orbital + spin\) angular momentum. Examples\: \\\(^\{2\}\\rm\{P\}_\{1/2\}\\\) denotes an atomic state of multiplicity 2 \(doublet\)\, electron orbital angular momentum quantum number \\\(L=1\\\)\, and orbital plus spin angular momentum quantum number \\\(J=\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06278
name: terpenes
def: "Hydrocarbons of biological origin having carbon skeletons formally derived from isoprene $\\ce\{\[CH2=C\(CH3\)CH=CH2\]\}$. This class is subdivided into the $\\ce\{C5\}$ hemiterpenes\, $\\ce\{C10\}$ monoterpenes\, $\\ce\{C15\}$ sesquiterpenes\, $\\ce\{C20\}$ diterpenes\, $\\ce\{C25\}$ sesterterpenes\, $\\ce\{C30\}$ triterpenes\, $\\ce\{C40\}$ tetraterpenes \(carotenoids\) and $\\ce\{C5_\{n\}\}$ polyterpenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06279
name: terpenoids
def: "Natural products and related compounds formally derived from isoprene units \(see isoprenoids\). They contain oxygen in various functional groups. This class is subdivided according to the number of carbon atoms in the same manner as are terpenes. The skeleton of terpenoids may differ from strict additivity of isoprene units by the loss or shift of a fragment\, generally a methyl group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06282
name: tertiary structure
def: "The spatial organization \(including conformation\) of an entire protein molecule or other macromolecule consisting of a single chain."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06283
name: tesla
def: "SI derived unit of magnetic flux density\, \\\(\\pu\{T\} = \\pu\{Wb m-2\} = \\pu\{kg s-2 A-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06284
name: test portion
def: "The amount or volume of the test sample taken for analysis\, usually of known weight or volume."
synonym: "analytical portion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06285
name: test sample
def: "The sample\, prepared from the laboratory sample\, from which test portions are removed for testing or for analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06286
name: test solution
def: "The solution prepared from the test portion for the analytical procedure. The proportions of test portion and solvent are normally known."
synonym: "analytical solution" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06287
name: tetracyclines
def: "A subclass of polyketides having an octahydrotetracene-2-carboxamide skeleton\, substituted with many hydroxy and other groups\, e.g. chlortetracycline\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06289
name: tetrahedral intermediate
def: "A reaction intermediate in which the bond arrangement around an initially double-bonded carbon atom \(typically a carbonyl carbon atom\) has been transformed from trigonal to tetrahedral. For example\, aldol in the condensation reaction of acetaldehyde \(but most tetrahedral intermediates have a more fleeting existence\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06290
name: <i>tetrahedro</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote four atoms bound into a tetrahedron."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06291
name: tetrapyrroles
def: "Natural pigments containing four pyrrole rings joined by one-carbon units linking position 2 of one pyrrole ring to position 5 of the next. porphyrins are macrocyclic tetrapyrroles. E.g. bilin \(a linear tetrapyrrole\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06292
name: tetraterpenoids
def: "Terpenoids having a $\\ce\{C40\}$ skeleton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06293
name: texture
def: "The detailed geometry of the void space in the particles of a catalyst."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06295
name: thermal analysis
def: "A group of techniques in which a physical property of a substance and/or its reaction product is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06296
name: thermal black
def: "A special type of carbon black produced by pyrolysis of gaseous hydrocarbons in a preheated chamber in the absence of air. Thermal black consists of relatively large individual spheres \(\\\(100\{-\}500\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) diameter\) and aggregates of a small number of pseudospherical particles. The preferred alignment of the layer planes is parallel to the surface of the spheres."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06297
name: thermal column
def: "A large body of moderator\, adjacent to or inside a reactor to provide thermal neutrons for experiments."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06298
name: thermal conductance
def: "Heat flow rate divided by the temperature difference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06299
name: thermal conductivity
def: "Tensor quantity relating the heat flux\, \\\(J_\{q\}\\\)\, to the temperature gradient\, \\\(J_\{q\}=- \\lambda \\ \nabla T\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06300
name: thermal conductivity detector
def: "In general\, two cells arranged in a bridge configuration detect the change in thermal conductivity of the gas at the output of the column. This detector is sensitive to any substance with thermal conductivity different from that of the carrier gas. The lowest detectable limit is between \\\(0.5\\\) and \\\(100\\ \\rm\{ppmv\}\\\). The linear dynamic range is of the order of \\\(10^\{3\}\\\). This type of detector is often used for measuring components at relatively high concentrations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06302
name: thermal fission
def: "Fission caused by thermal neutrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06303
name: thermal ionization
def: "Takes place when an atom or molecule interacts with a heated surface or is in a gaseous environment at high temperature. Examples of the latter may be a capillary arc plasma\, a microwave plasma or an inductively coupled plasma."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06304
name: thermal lensing
def: "A technique that determines the alteration in the refractive index of a medium as a result of the temperature rise in the path of a laser beam absorbed by the medium. The lens produced \(usually divergent\) causes a change \(usually a decrease\) in the irradiance measured along the laser beam axis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06306
name: thermally-induced transition
def: "A transition that is induced by a change in temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06307
name: thermal neutrons
def: "Neutrons in thermal equilibrium with the medium in which they exist\, in general at room temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06308
name: thermal resistance
def: "Reciprocal of the thermal conductance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06309
name: thermoacoustimetry
def: "A technique in which the characteristics of imposed acoustic waves are measured as a function of temperature after passing through a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06312
name: thermochromism
def: "A thermally induced transformation of a molecular structure or of a system \(e.g. of a solution\)\, thermally reversible\, that produces a spectral change\, typically\, but not necessarily\, of visible colour."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06313
name: thermocouple
def: "A device based on the thermoelectric effect\, by which two junctions between dissimilar conductors \(metallic or heavily doped semiconductors\) kept at different temperatures generate an electric potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06314
name: thermodilatometry
def: "A technique in which a dimension of a substance under negligible load is measured as a function of temperature while the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program. Linear thermodilatometry and volume thermodilatometry are distinguished on the basis of the dimensions measured."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06315
name: thermodynamically equivalent sphere
def: "A sphere\, impenetrable to other spheres\, displaying the same excluded volume as an actual polymer molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06316
name: thermodynamic control
def: "The term characterizes conditions that lead to reaction products in a proportion governed by the equilibrium constant for their interconversion and/or for the interconversion of reaction intermediates formed in or after the rate-limiting step. \(Some workers prefer to describe this phenomenon as 'equilibrium control'.\)"
synonym: "equilibrium control" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06319
name: thermodynamic isotope effect
def: "The effect of isotopic substitution on an equilibrium constant is referred to as a thermodynamic \(or equilibrium\) isotope effect. For example\, the effect of isotopic substitution in reactant A that participates in the equilibrium\: \\\[\\rm\{A\}+\\rm\{B\}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm\{C\}\\\] is the ratio \\\(\\frac\{K^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{K^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\}\\\) of the equilibrium constant for the reaction in which A contains the light isotope to that in which it contains the heavy isotope. The ratio can be expressed as the equilibrium constant for the isotopic exchange reaction\: \\\[\\rm\{A\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}+\\rm\{C\}^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\\rightleftharpoons \\rm\{A\}^\{\\rm\{h\}\}+\\rm\{C\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\] in which reactants such as B that are not isotopically substituted do not appear. The potential energy surfaces of isotopic molecules are identical to a high degree of approximation\, so thermodynamic isotope effects can only arise from the effect of isotopic mass on the nuclear motions of the reactants and products\, and can be expressed quantitatively in terms of partition function ratios for nuclear motion\: \\\[\\frac\{K^\{\\rm\{l\}\}\}\{K^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\} = \\frac\{\(Q_\{\\rm\{nuc\}\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}/Q_\{\\rm\{nuc\}\}^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\)_\{\\rm\{C\}\}\}\n\{\(Q_\{\\rm\{nuc\}\}^\{\\rm\{l\}\}/Q_\{\\rm\{nuc\}\}^\{\\rm\{h\}\}\)_\{\\rm\{A\}\}\}\\\] Although the nuclear partition function is a product of the translational\, rotational and vibrational partition functions\, the isotope effect is determined almost entirely by the last named\, specifically by vibrational modes involving motion of isotopically different atoms. In the case of light atoms \(i.e. protium vs. deuterium or tritium\) at moderate temperatures\, the isotope effect is dominated by zero-point energy differences."
synonym: "equilibrium isotope effect" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06321
name: thermodynamic temperature
def: "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06322
name: thermoelectrometry
def: "A technique in which an electrical characteristic of a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program. The most common measurements are of resistance\, conductance or capacitance."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06324
name: thermogravimetry
def: "A technique in which the mass of a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06325
name: thermoluminescence
def: "Luminescence arising from a reaction between species trapped in a rigid matrix and released as a result of an increase in temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06326
name: thermolysis
def: "The uncatalysed cleavage of one or more covalent bonds resulting from exposure of a compound to a raised temperature\, or a process in which such cleavage is an essential part."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06327
name: thermomagnetometry
def: "A technique in which a magnetic characteristic of a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06328
name: thermomechanical measurement
def: "A technique in which the deformation of a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) under non-oscillatory load is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06331
name: thermometric titration
def: "An analytical method in which one reactant \(the titrant\) is added continuously or stepwise to an adiabatic or isoperibol vessel containing another reactant. The enthalpy change\(s\) of the ensuing reaction\(s\) causes a temperature change which\, when plotted versus volume of titrant\, may be used to find the titration endpoint\(s\). This is the preferred term for experiments producing plots of temperature versus volume of titrant in which the main goal is a quantitative determination. Nonetheless\, when a calorimetric vessel is used\, such that the heat capacity is known\, thermodynamic parameters may also be estimated from such experiments. An acceptable synonym in that case is enthalpimetric titration. The use of the adjective thermometric is justified because of widespread historical and current usage\, and because a titration of necessity implies a chemical reaction. The term  'thermometric enthalpy titration' has been used\, but is not recommended. A method in which the titrant is a catalyst for an indicator reaction that occurs after the endpoint for the analyte reaction should be called a thermometric titration with catalytic endpoint detection\, not a 'catalytic thermometric titration'."
synonym: "enthalpimetric titration" []
synonym: "thermometric enthalpy titration" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06332
name: thermoparticulate analysis
def: "A thermoanalytical technique in which the release of particulate matter from a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06333
name: thermophile
def: "An organism that can tolerate high temperatures and that grows optimally at temperatures above \\\(45\\ ^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06335
name: thermopile
def: "Several thermocouples connected in series to increase the magnitude of the electric potential."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06336
name: thermoptometry
def: "A technique in which an optical characteristic of a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program. Measurements of total light\, light of specific wavelength\(s\)\, refractive index and luminescence lead to thermophotometry\, thermospectrometry\, thermorefractometry and thermoluminescence\, respectively; observation under the microscope leads to thermomicroscopy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06337
name: thermosonimetry
def: "A technique in which the sound emitted by a substance \(and/or its reaction product\(s\)\) is measured as a function of temperature whilst the substance is subjected to a controlled temperature program."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06338
name: thermosphere
def: "Atmospheric shell extending from the top of the mesosphere to outer space. It is a region of more or less steadily increasing temperature with height\, starting at \\\(70\\\) or \\\(80\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\). It includes the exosphere and most or all of the ionosphere \(not the D region\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06342
name: thickness of diffusion layer
def: "The distance from the electrode where the ratio \\\[\\frac\{c\\\,-\\\,c_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\}\{c_\{0\}\\\,-\\\,c_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\}\\\] reaches a given value. \(\\\(c_\{\\rm\{e\}\}\\\) is the interfacial concentration\, \\\(c\\\) is the concentration\, \\\(c_\{0\}\\\) is the value in the bulk solution.\) If this ratio is selected as\, say\, 0.99 the corresponding thickness of the diffusion layer is denoted by \\\(\\delta_\{0.99\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06343
name: thickness of electrical double layer
def: "The length characterizing the decrease with distance of the potential in the double layer = characteristic Debye length in the corresponding electrolyte solution = \\\(\\kappa^\{-1\}\\\)\:\\\[\\frac\{1\}\{\\kappa \} = \\sqrt\{\\frac\{\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\varepsilon_\{0\}RT\}\{F^\{2\}\\sum _\{i\}c_\{i\}z_\{i\}^\{2\} \}\}\\\]&nbsp;&nbsp;\\\[\\frac\{1\}\{\\kappa \} = \\sqrt\{\\frac\{\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}RT\}\{4\\pi F^\{2\}\\sum _\{i\}c_\{i\}z_\{i\}^\{2\} \}\}\\\]\(rationalized four-quantity system\)&nbsp;&nbsp;\(three-quantity electrostatic system\) where \\\(\\varepsilon\\\) = static permittivity = \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\varepsilon_\{0\}\\\)\, \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\) = relative static permittivity of solution; \\\(\\varepsilon_\{0\}\\\) = permittivity of vacuum\, \\\(R\\\) = gas constant\, \\\(T\\\) = thermodynamic temperature\, \\\(F\\\) = Faraday constant\, \\\(z_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) = concentration of species \\\(i\\\)\, \\\(z_\{\\rm\{i\}\}\\\) = ionic charge on species \\\(i\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06344
name: thickness of reaction layer
def: "When a kinetic current flows\, the concentrations of the electroactive substance B and its precursor C at very small distances from the electrode surface are influenced both by mass transfer and by the finite rate of establishment of the chemical equilibrium. As the distance from the electrode surface increases\, the chemical equilibrium is more and more nearly attained. The thickness of the reaction layer is the distance from the electrode surface beyond which deviations from the chemical equilibrium between C and B are taken to be negligibly small."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06345
name: thin film
def: "A film whose thickness is of the order of a characteristic scale or smaller. Since a film may 'look' operationally thin or thick\, according to the procedure applied\, it is also recommended that the measurement procedure employed be specified \(e.g. ellipsometrically thin film\, optically thin film\, etc.\). It is recommended that the physical specification of the film thickness be used\, whenever possible \(e.g. thick compared to the electron mean free path\, thin compared to the optical wavelength\, etc.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06346
name: thin-layer chromatography
def: "Chromatography carried out in a layer of adsorbent spread on a support e.g. a glass plate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06347
name: thio
def: "This prefix properly denotes replacement of an oxygen by a sulfur\, e.g. $\\ce\{PhC\(=S\)NH2\}$\, thiobenzamide. See also entries prefixed thio-."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06348
name: thioacetals
def: "A term including monothioacetals having the structure $\\ce\{R2C\(OR'\)\(SR'\)\}$ \(subclass monothioketals\, $\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\); and dithioacetals having the structure $\\ce\{R2C\(SR'\)2\}$ \(subclass dithioketals\, $\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}\, \\ce\{R'\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06349
name: thioaldehydes
def: "Compounds in which the oxygen of an aldehyde has been replaced by divalent sulfur\, $\\ce\{RC\(=S\)H\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3CH2C\(=S\)H propanethial\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06350
name: thioaldehyde <i>S</i>-oxides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RC\(=S=O\)H\}$. \(Also known by the disapproved term 'sulfines' which includes thioaldehyde S-oxides and thioketone S-oxides\)."
synonym: "sulfines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06351
name: thioanhydrides
def: "Compounds having the structure acyl-S-acyl. Also called diacylsulfanes\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3C\(=O\)SC\(=S\)CH2CH3\}$ acetic thiopropanoic thioanhydride."
synonym: "acid thioanhydrides" []
synonym: "diacylsulfanes" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06352
name: thiocarboxylic acids
def: "Compounds in which one or both oxygens of a carboxy group have been replaced by divalent sulfur $\\ce\{RC\(=O\)SH\}$ or $\\ce\{RC\(=S\)OH\}$ monothiocarboxylic acids\, $\\ce\{RC\(=S\)SH\}$ dithiocarboxylic acids."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06353
name: thiocyanates
def: "Salts and esters of thiocyanic acid $\\ce\{HSC#N\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3SC#N\}$ methyl thiocyanate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06354
name: thioethers
def: "Former name for sulfides $\\ce\{RSR\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06355
name: thiohemiacetals
def: "Compounds of structure $\\ce\{R2C\(SR'\)OH\}$ or $\\ce\{R2C\(OR'\)SH\}$ \(monothiohemiacetals\)\, or $\\ce\{R2C\(SR'\)SH\}$ \(dithiohemiacetals\)\, $\\ce\{R'\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06356
name: thioketones
def: "Compounds in which the oxygen of a ketone has been replaced by divalent sulfur $\\ce\{R2C=S\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3C\(=S\)CH2CH3\}$ butane-2-thione."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06357
name: thioketone <i>S</i>-oxides
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=S=O\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\). \(Also known by the disapproved term 'sulfines' which comprises thioaldehyde S-oxides and thioketone S-oxides.\)"
synonym: "sulfines" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06358
name: thiolates
def: "Derivatives of thiols\, in which a metal \(or other cation\) replaces the hydrogen attached to sulfur\, e.g. $\\ce\{CH3S^\{-\}Na^\{+\}\}$ sodium methanethiolate."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06359
name: thiols
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{RSH\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, e.g. $\\ce\{MeCH2SH\}$ ethanethiol. Also known by the term 'mercaptans' \(abandoned by IUPAC\)."
synonym: "mercaptans" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06361
name: third body
def: "A species\, other than the reactant itself\, which brings about the energization of a molecule that can undergo a unimolecular process\, or brings about a combination reaction between atoms or radicals. In the latter case a third body is also called a chaperon."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06366
name: threshold energy
def: "Potential energy gap between reactants and the transition state\, sometimes involving the zero point energies\, but usually not."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06367
name: through-bond electron transfer
def: "Intramolecular electron transfer for which the relevant electronic interaction between the donor and acceptor sites is mediated by through-bond interaction\, i.e. via the covalent bonds interconnecting these sites\, as opposed to through-space interaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06369
name: through-space electron transfer
def: "Electron transfer for which the relevant electronic interaction between the donor and acceptor sites is mediated either by direct orbital overlap or by superexchange interaction via intervening molecular entities not covalently bound to the donor or acceptor sites."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06371
name: TICT emission
def: "Electronic emission emanating from a TICT state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06372
name: TICT state
def: "The acronym derives from Twisted Internal Charge Transfer state\, proposed to be responsible for strongly Stokes-shifted fluorescence from certain aromatics\, particularly in a polar medium."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06373
name: tie molecule
def: "A molecule that connects at least two different crystals."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06375
name: time
def: "Base quantity in the system of quantities upon which SI is based."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06376
name: time constant
def: "If the output of a detector changes exponentially with time\, the time required for it to change from its initial value by the fraction \\\(1 - \\exp\(-t/\\tau _\{\\rm\{c\}\}\)\\\) \(for \\\(t=\\tau _\{\\rm\{c\}\}\\\)\) of the final value\, is called the time constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06377
name: time-correlated single photon counting
def: "A technique for the measurement of the time histogram of a sequence of photons with respect to a periodic event\, e.g. a flash from a repetitive nanosecond lamp or a CW operated laser \(mode locked laser\). The essential part is a time-to-amplitude-converter \(TAC\) which transforms the arrival time between a start and a stop pulse into a voltage. Sometimes called single photon timing."
synonym: "single photon timing" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06378
name: time-dependent stoichiometry
def: "A state in which if intermediates are formed in significant amounts during the course of a reaction\, the overall stoichiometric equation does not apply throughout."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06379
name: time-independent stoichiometry
def: "A state in which the stoichiometric equation applies throughout the course of the reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06380
name: time of centrifugation
def: "The time difference from switching on until switching off. The time for deceleration is not included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06382
name: time-of-flight mass spectrometer
def: "An arrangement using the fact that ions of different mass/charge need different times to travel through a certain distance in a field-free region after they have all been initially given the same translational energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06383
name: time
def: "The time in which a certain viscosity or yield stress is reached after discontinuation of a shear. These times depend on the values of viscosity or yield stress chosen by the experimenter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06384
name: time-resolved microwave conductivity
def: "Technique allowing the quantitative and qualitative detection of radiation-induced changes in the real\, \\\(\\Delta \\rm\{Re\}\\sigma\\\)\, and imaginary\, \\\(\\Delta \\rm\{Im\}\\sigma\\\)\, components of the conductivity of a medium by time-resolved measurement of changes in the microwave absorption resulting from the formation of mobile charges or from changes in the dipole moment or polarizability of molecules on excitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06385
name: time-resolved spectroscopy
def: "The recording of spectra at a series of time intervals after the excitation of the system with a light pulse \(or other perturbation\) of appropriately short duration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06386
name: titrant
def: "The solution containing the active agent with which a titration is made."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06387
name: titration
def: "The process of determining the quantity of a substance A by adding measured increments of substance B\, with which it reacts \(almost always as a standardized solution called the titrant\, but also by electrolytic generation\, as in coulometric titration\) with provision for some means of recognizing \(indicating\) the endpoint at which essentially all of A has reacted. If the endpoint coincides with the addition of the exact chemical equivalence\, it is called the equivalence point or stoichiometric or theoretical endpoint\, thus allowing the amount of A to be found from known amounts of B added up to this point\, the reacting weight ratio of A to B being known from stoichiometry or otherwise. Terms for varieties of titration can reflect the nature of the reaction between A and B. Thus\, there are acid–base\, complexometric\, chelatometric\, oxidation–reduction\, and precipitation titrations. Additionally\, the term can reflect the nature of the titrant\, such as acidimetric\, alkalimetric\, and iodometric titrations as well as coulometric titrations\, in which the titrant is generated electrolytically rather than being added as a standard solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06388
name: titration curve
def: "A plot of a variable related to a relevant concentration \(activity\) as the ordinate vs. some measure of the amount of titrant\, usually titration volume \(titre\) as the abscissa. If the variable is linearly related to concentrations\, such as the electrical conductance or the photometric absorbance\, the term linear titration curve is used. When a logarithmic expression of the concentration or activity is used\, such as the \\\(\\rm\{pH\}\\\)\, \\\(\\mathrm\{pM\}\\\)\, or the electrical potential in \\\(\\mathrm\{mV\}\\\)\, the curve is referred to as a logarithmic titration curve."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06389
name: titration error
def: "The difference in the amount of titrant\, or the corresponding difference in the amount of substance being titrated\, represented by the expression\: \(end-point value - equivalence-point value\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06391
name: titre
def: "The reacting strength of a standard solution\, usually expressed as the weight \(mass\) of the substance equivalent to \\\(1\\ \\rm\{cm\}^\{3\}\\\) of the solution."
synonym: "titer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06394
name: tonne
def: "Non-SI unit of mass\, \\\(\\pu\{t\} = \\pu\{10E3 kg\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06395
name: topochemical reaction
def: "A reversible or irreversible reaction that involves the introduction of a guest species into a host structure and that results in significant structural modifications to the host\, for example\, the breakage of bonds. Example\: The insertion of lithium at \\\(50\\\,^\{\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\rm\{C\}\\\) into the spinel $\\ce\{Li\[Mn2\]O4\}$ with symmetry \\\(F\\\,\\rm\{d3m\}\\\) to yield a layered structure with symmetry \\\(P\\bar\{3\}\\\,\\rm\{m1\}\\\)."
synonym: "insertion reaction" []
synonym: "topotactic reaction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06396
name: topomerization
def: "The identity reaction leading to exchange of the positions of identical ligands. The indistinguishable molecular entities involved are called topomers. For example in the reaction below the two identical ligating atoms $\\ce\{N_\{\\rm\{a\}\}\}$ and $\\ce\{N_\{\\rm\{b\}\}\}$ are interchanged by rotation about the $\\ce\{C-aryl\}$ bond but may be identified by NMR spectroscopy. This is a degenerate isomerization."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06399
name: topotactic transition
def: "A transition in which the crystal lattice of the product phase shows one or more crystallographically equivalent\, orientational relationships to the crystal lattice of the parent phase. Example\: Transitions in which the anionic array is unchanged during the transition but cation reorganization occurs\, as in\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06400
name: torque
def: "Sum of moments of forces not acting along the same line."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06401
name: torquoselectivity
def: "The preference for 'inward' or 'outward' rotation of substituents in conrotatory or disrotatory electrocyclic ring opening reactions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06402
name: torr
def: "Non-SI unit of pressure\, \\\(\\rm\{torr\}\\approx 133.322\\ \\rm\{Pa\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06403
name: torsional braid analysis
def: "A particular case of dynamic thermomechanometry in which the material is supported on a braid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06404
name: torsional stereoisomers
def: "Stereoisomers that can be interconverted \(actually or conceptually\) by torsion about a bond axis. This includes E\,Z-isomers of alkenes\, atropisomers and rotamers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06406
name: torsion angle
def: "In a chain of atoms A-B-C-D\, the dihedral angle between the plane containing the atoms A\,B\,C and that containing B\,C\,D. In a Newman projection the torsion angle is the angle \(having an absolute value between 0° and 180°\) between bonds to two specified \(fiducial\) groups\, one from the atom nearer \(proximal\) to the observer and the other from the further \(distal\) atom. The torsion angle between groups A and D is then considered to be positive if the bond A-B is rotated in a clockwise direction through less than 180° in order that it may eclipse the bond C-D\: a negative torsion angle requires rotation in the opposite sense. Stereochemical arrangements corresponding to torsion angles between 0° and ±90° are called syn \(s\)\, those corresponding to torsion angles between ±90° and 180° anti \(a\). Similarly\, arrangements corresponding to torsion angles between 30° and 150° or between -30° and -150° are called clinal \(c\) and those between 0° and 30° or 150° and 180° are called periplanar \(p\). The two types of terms can be combined so as to define four ranges of torsion angle; 0° to 30° synperiplanar \(sp\); 30° to 90° and -30° to -90° synclinal \(sc\); 90° to 150°\, and -90° to -150° anticlinal \(ac\); ±150° to 180° antiperiplanar \(ap\).  The synperiplanar conformation is also known as the syn- or cis-conformation; antiperiplanar as anti or trans and synclinal as gauche or skew. For macromolecular usage the symbols \\\(\\rm\{T\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{C\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{G\}^\{+\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{G\}^\{-\}\\\)\, \\\(\\rm\{A\}^\{+\}\\\) and \\\(\\rm\{A\}^\{-\}\\\) are recommended \(ap\, sp\, +sc\, -sc\, +ac and -ac respectively\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06409
name: total consumption time
def: "The time necessary to consume the prepared sample completely \(in \\\(\\mathrm\{s\}\\\)\). It is equal to the volume of the prepared sample divided by the rate of fluid consumption."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06410
name: total ion current
def: "\(after mass analysis\)\: The sum of the separate ion currents carried by the different ions contributing to the spectrum \(this is sometimes called the reconstructed ion current\).\(before mass analysis\)\: The sum of all the separate ion currents for ions of the same sign before mass analysis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06411
name: totally porous packing
def: "Packing in which the stationary phase permeates each porous particle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06413
name: total retention volume
def: "The volume of mobile phase entering the column between sample injection and the emergence of the peak maximum of the sample component of interest\, or the corresponding time. It includes the hold-up volume \(time\)\: \\\[t_\{\\rm\{R\}\}=\\frac\{V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\}\{F_\{\\rm\{c\}\}\}\\\] where \\\(F_\{c\}\\\) is the mobile phase flow rate at column temperature."
synonym: "retention time" []
synonym: "time" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06414
name: toxicity
def: "Capacity to cause injury to a living organism defined with reference to the quantity of substance administered or absorbed\, the way in which the substance is administered \(inhalation\, ingestion\, topical application\, injection\) and distributed in time \(single or repeated doses\)\, the type and severity of injury\, the time needed to produce the injury\, the nature of the organism\(s\) affected and other relevant conditions.Adverse effects of a substance on a living organism defined with reference to the quantity of substance administered or absorbed\, the way in which the substance is administered \(inhalation\, ingestion\, topical application\, injection\) and distributed in time \(single or repeated doses\)\, the type and severity of injury\, the time needed to produce the injury\, the nature of the organism\(s\) affected\, and other relevant conditions.Measure of incompatibility of a substance with life\: this quantity may be expressed as the reciprocal of the absolute value of median lethal dose \(\\\(\\frac\{1\}\{\\rm\{LD\}_\{50\}\}\\\)\) or concentration \(\\\(\\frac\{1\}\{\\rm\{LC\}_\{50\}\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06415
name: toxicodynamics
def: "Study of toxic actions on living systems\, including the reactions with and binding to cell constituents\, and the biochemical and physiological consequences of these actions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06416
name: toxicokinetics
def: "Process of the uptake of potentially toxic substances by the body\, the biotransformation they undergo\, the distribution of the substances and their metabolites in the tissues\, and the elimination of the substances and their metabolites from the body. Both the amounts and the concentrations of the substances and their metabolites are studied. The term has essentially the same meaning as pharmacokinetics\, but the latter term should be restricted to the study of pharmaceutical substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06417
name: toxicology
def: "Scientific discipline involving the study of the actual or potential danger presented by the harmful effects of substances \(poisons\) on living organisms and ecosystems\, of the relationship of such harmful effects to exposure\, and of the mechanisms of action\, diagnosis\, prevention and treatment of intoxications."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06418
name: toxin
def: "Poisonous substance produced by a biological organism such as a microbe\, animal or plant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06419
name: toxinology
def: "Scientific discipline involving the study of the chemistry\, biochemistry\, pharmacology and toxicology of toxins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06420
name: traceability
def: "The property of a result or measurement whereby it can be related to appropriate standards\, generally international or national standards\, through an unbroken chain of comparisons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06421
name: trace element
def: "Any element having an average concentration of less than about 100 parts per million atoms \(\\\(\\rm\{ppma\}\\\)\) or less than \\\(100\\ \\frac\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{g\}\}\{\\rm\{g\}\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06422
name: tracer
def: "A foreign substance mixed with or attached to a given substance to enable the distribution or location of the latter to be determined subsequently. There are several types of tracers which are used\: \(i\) A physical tracer is one that is attached by physical means to the object being traced; \(ii\) A chemical tracer is a chemical with properties similar to those of the substance being traced with which it is mixed homogeneously; \(iii\) An isotopic tracer is a unique isotope\, either radioactive or an enriched\, uncommon stable isotope\, of the element to be traced; \(iv\) A radioactive tracer is a physical or chemical tracer having radioactivity as its distinctive property which allows detection at small concentrations and hence after large transport distances. The composition of aerosols in the troposphere has been used as a qualitative tracer of air masses. The elemental analyses \(determined by neutron activation\, X-ray fluorescence\, etc.\) of the aerosols transported from various sources or source regions sometimes have characteristic patterns which are used to define qualitatively the origin of tropospheric aerosols collected in other geographical regions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06429
name: track
def: "The path of an ionizing particle as revealed by a track detector."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06430
name: track detector
def: "A detector which makes the paths of ionizing particles visible\, either directly \(e.g. cloud chamber\) or after suitable treatment \(photographic emulsion\, polymers\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06431
name: trajectory
def: "A path taken by a reaction system over potential-energy surface or a diagram or mathematical description that represents that path. A trajectory can also be called a reaction path."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06433
name: <i>trans-</i>
def: "A prefix designating two groups directly across a central atom from each other\, i.e. in the polar positions on a sphere\, not now generally recommended for precise nomenclature purposes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06434
name: transannular strain
def: "In medium-sized ring compounds\, the strain due to repulsive nonbonded interactions between substituents or hydrogen atoms attached to non-adjacent ring atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06436
name: transcription
def: "The process by which the genetic information encoded in a linear sequence of nucleotides in one strand of DNA is copied into an exactly complementary sequence of RNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06437
name: transducer
def: "An analytical instrument which provides an output quantity having a given relationship to the input quantity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06438
name: transduction
def: "The transfer of genetic information from one bacterium to another by means of a transducing bacteriophage. When the phage is grown on the first host\, a fragment of the host DNA can be incorporated into the phage particles. This foreign DNA can be transferred to the second host upon infection with progeny phage from the first experiment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06439
name: transfer
def: "Movement of a component within a system or across its boundary. It may be expressed using different kinds of quantities\, e.g rates of change \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}Q\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\) or \\\(\\frac\{\\Delta Q\}\{\\Delta t\}\\\). Examples are\: mass rate\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\) or \\\(\\frac\{\\Delta m_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\Delta t\}\\\); substance rate\, \\\(\\frac\{\\rm\{d\}n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\rm\{d\}t\}\\\) or \\\(\\frac\{\\Delta n_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\{\\Delta t\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06440
name: transfer
def: "The action of moving material between containers\, or containers between transport devices. This term is of particular importance in clinical chemistry."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06441
name: transferability
def: "Transferability assumes invariance of properties\, associated conceptually with an atom or a fragment present in a variety of molecules. The property\, such as electronegativity\, nucleophilicity\, NMR chemical shift\, etc. is held as retaining a similar value in all these occurrences."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06442
name: transfer activity coefficient
def: "A term used to quantify the difference in the free energy of a solute ion in two different standard states often in two different liquid phases. The relationship is \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{t\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\} = \nu \\ R\\ T\\ \\ln \\mathit\{\\gamma\}_\{\\rm\{t\}\}\\\) where \\\(\\Delta _\{\\rm\{t\}\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) is the transfer Gibbs energy and \\\(\nu \\\) is the number of ions in the solute."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06443
name: transferases
def: "Enzymes that catalyse reactions in which a group is transferred from one compound to another. Groups that are transferred are $\\ce\{C\}$\, aldehydic or ketonic residues\, acyl\, glycosyl\, alkyl\, nitrogenous\, phosphorus and sulfur containing groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06444
name: transfer line
def: "Line provided to carry the sample to be analysed from the sample point to the analytical unit without altering the composition of the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06445
name: transfer RNA
def: "A single-stranded RNA molecule containing about 70-90 nucleotides\, folded by intrastrand base pairing into a characteristic secondary \('cloverleaf'\) structure that carries a specific amino acid and matches it to its corresponding codon on an mRNA during protein synthesis."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06446
name: transformation
def: "The conversion of a substrate into a particular product\, irrespective of reagents or mechanisms involved. For example\, the transformation of aniline \($\\ce\{C6H5NH2\}$\) into N-phenylacetamide \($\\ce\{C6H5NHCOCH3\}$\) may be effected by use of acetyl chloride or acetic anhydride or ketene. A transformation is distinct from a reaction\, the full description of which would state or imply all the reactants and all the products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06447
name: transformation
def: "A process for genetic alteration of a cell following incorporation of foreign DNA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06450
name: transient phase
def: "This expression relates to the period of time that elapses prior to the establishment of a steady state. Initially the concentration of a reactive intermediate is zero\, and it rises to the steady-state concentration during the transient phase."
synonym: "induction period" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06451
name: transient species
def: "Relating to a short-lived reaction intermediate. It can be defined only in relation to a time scale fixed by the experimental conditions and the limitations of the technique employed in the detection of the intermediate. The term is a relative one. Transient species are sometimes also said to be 'metastable'. However\, this latter term should be avoided\, because it relates a thermodynamic term to a kinetic property\, although most transients are also thermodynamically unstable with respect to reactants and products."
synonym: "transient chemical species" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06453
name: transient spectroscopy
def: "A technique for the spectroscopic observation of transient species \(excited-state molecular entities or reactive intermediates\) generated by a pulse of short duration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06455
name: transition coordinate
def: "The reaction coordinate at the transition state corresponding to a vibration with an imaginary frequency. Motion along it in the two opposite senses leads towards the reactants or towards the products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06456
name: transition element
def: "An element whose atom has an incomplete d sub-shell\, or which can give rise to cations with an incomplete d sub-shell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06457
name: transition interval
def: "The range of concentration over which the eye is able to perceive change in hue\, colour intensity\, fluorescence or other property of a visual indicator arising from the varying ratio of the two relevant forms of the indicator."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06460
name: transition moment
def: "An electromagnetic wave may induce an oscillating electric moment in a molecule \(possibly leading to absorption if the oscillation frequency is equal to the light frequency\). The amplitude of this moment is the transition moment between the initial \(i\) and final \(f\) states \(here assumed to be non-degenerate\)\: \\\[M_\{\\rm\{if\}\} = \\\, < \\rm\{f\} \\mid M_\{\\rm\{op\}\}\\mid \\rm\{i\} >\\\] where \\\(M_\{\\rm\{op\}\}\\\) is the electric dipole moment operator\, a vector operator that is the sum of the position vectors of all charged particles weighted with their charge. The transition moment \\\(M_\{\\rm\{if\}\} \\\) is a vector in the molecular framework\, characterized both by its direction and its probability."
synonym: "electronic transition moment" []
synonym: "transition dipole moment" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06462
name: transition polarization
def: "The direction of the transition moment in the molecular framework."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06467
name: transition species
def: "Any intermediate species in an elementary reaction. An activated complex is an example of a transition species\, but the latter term also includes other intermediate species. Transition species have also been called transition states\, but there is then danger of confusion with a true activated complex\, which is also commonly known as a transition state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06468
name: transition state
def: "In theories describing elementary reaction it is usually assumed that there is a transition state of more positive molar Gibbs energy between the reactants and the products through which an assembly of atoms \(initially composing the molecular entities of the reactants\) must pass on going from reactants to products in either direction. In the formalism of 'transition state theory' the transition state of an elementary reaction is that set of states \(each characterized by its own geometry and energy\) in which an assembly of atoms\, when randomly placed there\, would have an equal probability of forming the reactants or of forming the products of that elementary reaction. The transition state is characterized by one and only one imaginary frequency. The assembly of atoms at the transition state has been called an activated complex. \(It is not a complex according to the definition in this Compendium.\) It may be noted that the calculations of reaction rates by the transition state method and based on calculated potential-energy surfaces refer to the potential energy maximum at the saddle point\, as this is the only point for which the requisite separability of transition state coordinates may be assumed. The ratio of the number of assemblies of atoms that pass through to the products to the number of those that reach the saddle point from the reactants can be less than unity\, and this fraction is the 'transmission coefficient' \\\(\\kappa \\\). \(There are also reactions\, such as the gas-phase colligation of simple radicals\, that do not require 'activation' and which therefore do not involve a transition state.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06469
name: transition state analogue
def: "A substrate designed to mimic the properties or the geometry of the transition state of reaction."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06470
name: transition state theory
def: "A theory of the rates of elementary reactions which assumes a special type of equilibrium\, having an equilibrium constant \\\(K^\{\\ddagger \}\\\)\, to exist between reactants and activated complexes. According to this theory the rate constant is given by\: \\\[k=\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\}\{h\}\\ K^\{\\ddagger \}\\\] where \\\(k_\{B\}\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(h\\\) is the Planck constant. The rate constant can also be expressed as\: \\\[k=\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\}\{h\}\\ \\exp \(\\frac\{\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}S^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\}\{R\}\)\\ \\exp \(- \\frac\{\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\}\{R\\ T\}\)\\\] where \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}S^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, the entropy of activation\, is the standard molar change of entropy when the activated complex is formed from reactants and \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, the enthalpy of activation\, is the corresponding standard molar change of enthalpy. The quantities \\\(E_\{a\}\\\) \(the energy of activation\) and \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) are not quite the same\, the relationship between them depending on the type of reaction. Also\: \\\[k=\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\}\{h\}\\ \\exp \(- \\frac\{\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\}\{R\\ T\}\)\\\] where \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, known as the Gibbs energy of activation\, is the standard molar Gibbs energy change for the conversion of reactants into activated complex. A plot of standard molar Gibbs energy against a reaction coordinate is known as a Gibbs-energy profile; such plots\, unlike potential-energy profiles\, are temperature-dependent. In principle the equations above must be multiplied by a transmission coefficient\, \\\(\\kappa \\\)\, which is the probability that an activated complex forms a particular set of products rather than reverting to reactants or forming alternative products. It is to be emphasized that \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}S^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\)\, \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}H^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) and \\\(\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}G^\{\\\,\\unicode\{x26ac\}\}\\\) occurring in the former three equations are not ordinary thermodynamic quantities\, since one degree of freedom in the activated complex is ignored. Transition-state theory has also been known as absolute rate theory\, and as activated-complex theory\, but these terms are no longer recommended."
synonym: "conventional transition-state theory" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06471
name: transition structure
def: "A saddle point on a potential-energy surface. It has one negative force constant in the harmonic force constant matrix."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06472
name: transition time
def: "In chronopotentiometry and related techniques\, the time that elapses between the instant at which current is applied and the instant at which the concentration of an electroactive substance B at the electrode-solution interface becomes indistinguishable from zero. In experimental practice the latter time is often taken to be the instant at which the rate of variation of the potential of the indicator electrode attains a maximum value."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06473
name: transition wavenumber
def: "Difference of term values for the corresponding states \(upper minus lower\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06474
name: transit time
def: "The time needed for the component to pass through the observation space \(in \\\(\\mathrm\{s\}\\\)\). It depends on the geometry of the observation space\, the flame temperature and the solute nebulized."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06475
name: translation
def: "The unidirectional process that takes place on the ribosomes whereby the genetic information present in an mRNA is converted into a corresponding sequence of amino acids in a protein."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06476
name: translational spectroscopy
def: "A technique to investigate the distribution of the velocity of product ions from reactions of ions and neutral species."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06478
name: transmission
def: "The ratio of the number of ions leaving a region of a mass spectrometer to the number entering that region."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06480
name: transmission electron energy loss spectroscopy
def: "Any technique in which an electron transparent specimen is bombarded with a finely focused electron beam and the energy distribution of the transmitted electrons is measured. This energy spectrum contains features corresponding to discrete losses of energy of the transmitted electrons due to excitation of electronic or plasmon states and provides information on the identity \(and in some cases\) chemical bonding of the elements in the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06481
name: transmission electron microscopy
def: "Any technique in which an electron transparent sample is bombarded with an electron beam and the intensity of the transmitted electrons which is determined by scattering phenomena \(electron absorption phenomena\) in the interior of the sample is recorded. TEM essentially provides a high resolution image of the microstructure of a thin sample. This technique is often just called 'electron microscopy'. The term transmission electron microscopy is however recommended for the sake of a clear distinction from other electron microscopic techniques."
synonym: "electron microscopy" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06483
name: transmission high energy electron diffraction
def: "Any technique which is based on the diffraction of high energy electrons \(\\\(E_\{0\} = 10\{-\}200\\ \\rm\{keV\}\\\)\) in crystalline materials and evaluation of the angular distribution of the transmitted electrons. The diffraction pattern represents an image of the reciprocal lattice and therefore contains information about crystal structure. This technique is also often called selected area electron diffraction \(SAED\). For consistency with other electron diffraction techniques\, however\, the term THEED is recommended."
synonym: "selected area electron diffraction" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06484
name: transmittance
def: "The ratio of the transmitted radiant power \(\\\(P_\{\\lambda \}\\\)\) to that incident on the sample \(\\\(P_\{\\lambda \}^\{0\}\\\)\)\: \\\[T=\\frac\{P_\{\\lambda \}\}\{P_\{\\lambda \}^\{0\}\}\\\] Internal transmittance refers to energy loss by absorption\, whereas the total transmittance is that due to absorption\, reflection\, scatter\, etc."
synonym: "transmission factor" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06486
name: transport
def: "The action of moving materials within the analytical instrument. Transport can involve any of several means including pressure flow\, where the materials are moved by fluid pressure\, either continuously or discontinuously; centrifugal flow\, where the materials are moved by centrifugal force; spontaneous motion\, where the materials are moved due to their intrinsic properties\, e.g. diffusion\, capillarity; and discrete transport\, where materials are enclosed within a moving container."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06488
name: transport interference
def: "Transport interferences affect the amount of desolvated sample passing through the horizontal flame cross-section per unit time at the observation height. They include factors affecting the rate of liquid consumption\, \\\(F_\{\\rm\{l\}\}\\\)\, the efficiency of nebulization\, \\\(\\varepsilon_\{\\rm\{n\}\}\\\)\, and the fraction desolvated \\\(\\beta_\{\\rm\{s\}\}\\\). They may be classified as non-specific \(and physical\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06489
name: transport number
def: "Of ions $\\ce\{B\}$\, the current density due to ions $\\ce\{B\}$ divided by the sum of current densities of all the ions in the electrolyte. ISO recommends the name current fraction of ions $\\ce\{B\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06490
name: transposon
def: "A movable DNA element that can be inserted at new sites into plasmids or chromosomes independently of the host cell recombination system. Prokaryotic transposons can carry genes that confer new phenotypic properties\, such as resistance to antibiotics on the host."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06491
name: transtactic polymer
def: "A tactic polymer in which the main-chain double bonds of the configurational base units are entirely in the trans-arrangement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06492
name: trapping
def: "The interception of a reactive molecule or reaction intermediate so that it is removed from the system or converted into a more stable form for study or identification."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06493
name: travel time
def: "The time needed for the component to be carried from the burner tip to the observation space \(in \\\(\\mathrm\{s\}\\\)\). Travel time depends on the observation height and the flame rise velocity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06494
name: treated solution
def: "The test solution that has been subjected to reaction or separation procedures prior to measurement of some property."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06496
name: <i>triangulo</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote three atoms bound into a triangle."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06497
name: triazanes
def: "Triazane\, $\\ce\{NH2NHNH2\}$\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06498
name: triazenes
def: "Triazene\, $\\ce\{NH2N=NH\}$\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06499
name: triboluminescence
def: "Luminescence resulting from the rubbing together of the surface of certain solids. It can be produced\, for example\, when solids are crushed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06500
name: trimethylenemethanes
def: "The diradical $\\ce\{CH2=C\(C^\{.\}H2\)2\}$\, 2-methylenepropane-1\,3-diyl\, for which no Kekulé structure can be written\, and its hydrocarbyl derivatives."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06501
name: trioxides
def: "Organic derivatives of trioxidane\, $\\ce\{HOOOH\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{ROOOR'\}$. When $\\ce\{R'\} = \\ce\{H\}$\, the compound is a hydrotrioxide."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06502
name: triple point
def: "The point in a one-component system at which the temperature and pressure of three phases are in equilibrium. If there are \\\(p\\\) possible phases\, there are \\\(\\frac\{p!\}\{\(p\\\,-\\\,3\)!\\ 3!\}\\\) triple points. Example\: In the sulfur system four possible triple points \(one metastable\) exist for the four phases comprising rhombic $\\ce\{S\}$ \(solid\)\, monoclinic $\\ce\{S\}$ \(solid\)\, $\\ce\{S\}$ \(liquid\) and $\\ce\{S\}$ \(vapour\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06503
name: triplet state
def: "A state having a total electron spin quantum number of 1."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06504
name: triplet-triplet absorption
def: "Absorption which takes place with the transition from the lowest triplet state of the molecule to higher triplet states \(T → \\\(T_\{n\}\\\)\) thus leading to the triplet-triplet absorption spectrum."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06505
name: triplet-triplet annihilation
def: "Two atoms or molecular entities both in a triplet state often interact \(usually upon collision\) to produce one atom or molecular entity in an excited singlet state and another in its ground singlet state. This is often\, but not always\, followed by delayed fluorescence."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06506
name: triplet-triplet energy transfer
def: "Energy transfer from an electronically excited triplet donor to produce an electronically excited acceptor in its triplet state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06507
name: triplet-triplet transitions
def: "Electronic transitions in which both the initial and final states are triplet states."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06508
name: <i>triprismo</i>-
def: "An affix used in names to denote six atoms bound into a triangular prism."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06509
name: tritactic polymer
def: "A tactic polymer that contains three sites of defined stereoisomerism in the main chain of the configurational base unit. Example\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06510
name: triterpenoids
def: "Terpenoids having a $\\ce\{C30\}$ skeleton."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06513
name: tritium
def: "A specific name for the atom $\\ce\{^\{3\}H\}$. The cation $\\ce\{^\{3\}H^\{+\}\}$ is a triton\, the species $\\ce\{^\{3\}H^\{−\}\}$ is a tritide anion and $\\ce\{^\{3\}H\}$ is the tritio group."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06516
name: trivial name
def: "A name having no part used in a systematic sense."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06517
name: Troe expression
def: "A semiempirical description of the rate coefficient for a specific three body reaction \[e.g. \] which represents well its pressure and temperature dependence in the region of transition between second and third order kinetics."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06518
name: tropilidenes
def: "This term has inconsistently been used to mean cyclohepta-1\,3\,5-trienes or to mean cyclohepta-1\,3\,5-trienes in dynamic equilibrium with bicyclo-\[4.1.0\]hepta-2\,4-dienes\, so that the structure of the bulk substance is indeterminate in the time scale of the method of observation. Use of this term is therefore discouraged."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06519
name: tropolones
def: "2-Hydroxycyclohepta-2\,4\,6-trienones and derivatives formed by substitution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06520
name: tropones
def: "Compounds that contain the cyclohepta-2\,4\,6-trienone ring system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06521
name: tropopause
def: "The region of the atmosphere which joins the troposphere and stratosphere\, and where the decreasing temperature with altitude\, characteristic of the troposphere ceases\, and the temperature increase with height which is characteristic of the stratosphere begins."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06522
name: troposphere
def: "The lowest layer of the atmosphere\, ranging from the ground to the base of the stratosphere \(tropopause\) at \\\(10\{-\}15\\ \\rm\{km\}\\\) of altitude depending on the latitude and meteorological conditions. About 70% of the mass of the atmosphere is in the troposphere. This is where most of the weather features occur and where the chemistry of the reactive anthropogenic species released into the atmosphere takes place."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06523
name: tropylium ions
def: "The delocalized carbenium ion\, cycloheptatrienylium\, $\\ce\{C7H7^\{+\}\}$\, derived formally by detachment of one hydride ion from the $\\ce\{CH2\}$ group of cyclohepta-1\,3\,5-triene and substitution derivatives thereof."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06524
name: tropyl radicals
def: "The delocalized radicals derived formally by abstraction of one hydrogen from the $\\ce\{CH2\}$ group of cyclohepta-1\,3\,5-triene and substitution derivatives thereof."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06525
name: true coincidence
def: "A coincidence of events occurring in the same atom or in physically connected atoms."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06527
name: true value
def: "The value that characterizes a quantity perfectly in the conditions that exist when that quantity is considered. It is an ideal value which could be arrived at only if all causes of measurement error were eliminated\, and the entire population was sampled."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06528
name: tub conformation
def: "A conformation \(of symmetry group \\\(D_\{\\rm\{2d\}\}\\\)\) of an eight-membered ring in which the four atoms forming one pair of diametrically opposite bonds in the ring lie in one plane and all other ring atoms lie to one side of that plane. It is analogous to the boat conformation of cyclohexane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06529
name: tunable laser
def: "A high intensity source with narrow spectral bandwidth\, with and without frequency doubling and/or Raman shifting. Its use may enable the spectral apparatus to be omitted. Tunable lasers may be either continuous \(CW\) or pulsed in nature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06530
name: Tung distribution
def: "A continuous distribution with the differential mass-distribution function of the form\: \\\[f_\{\\rm\{w\}\}\(x\)\\ \\rm\{d\}x = a\\ b\\ x^\{b-1\}\\ \\rm\{e\}^\{-a\\ x^\{b\}\}\\ \\rm\{d\}x\\\] where \\\(x\\\) is a parameter characterizing the chain length\, such as relative molecular mass or degree of polymerization and \\\(a\\\) and \\\(b\\\) are positive adjustable parameters."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06531
name: tunnelling
def: "The process by which a particle or a set of particles crosses a barrier on its potential-energy surface without having the energy required to surmount this barrier. Since the rate of tunnelling decreases with increasing reduced mass\, it is significant in the context of isotope effects of hydrogen isotopes."
synonym: "quantum-mechanical tunnelling" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06532
name: turbidimetric titration
def: "The process in which a precipitant is added incrementally to a highly dilute polymer solution and the intensity of light scattered by\, or the turbidity due to\, the finely dispersed particles of the polymer-rich phase is measured as a function of the amount of precipitant added."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06533
name: turbidity
def: "The apparent absorbance of the incident radiation due to scattering. For small particles\, direct proportionality exists between turbidity and the Rayleigh ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06534
name: turnover frequency
def: "Commonly called the turnover number\, \\\(N\\\)\, and defined\, as in enzyme catalysis\, as molecules reacting per active site in unit time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06537
name: twisted intramolecular charge transfer
def: "In a TICT state formed by intramolecular charge transfer in an electron donor \($\\ce\{D\}$\)/acceptor \($\\ce\{A\}$\) molecule\, the $\\ce\{D\}$ and $\\ce\{A\}$ subgroups have a mutually perpendicular configuration that leads to electronic decoupling of $\\ce\{D\}$ and $\\ce\{A\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06540
name: two-dimensional chromatography
def: "A procedure in which parts or all of the separated sample components are subjected to additional separation steps. This can be done e.g. by conducting a particular fraction eluting from the column into another column \(system\) having different separation characteristics. When combined with additional steps\, this may be described as multi-dimensional chromatography. In planar chromatography two-dimensional chromatography refers to the chromatographic process in which the components are caused to migrate first in one direction and subsequently in a direction at right angles to the first one; the two elutions are carried out with different eluents."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06541
name: two-photon excitation
def: "Excitation resulting from successive or simultaneous absorption of two photons by an atom or molecular entity. This term is used for successive absorption only if some of the excitation energy of the first photon remains in the atom or molecular entity before absorption of the second photon. The simultaneous two-photon absorption can also be called biphotonic excitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06542
name: two-photon process
def: "A photophysical or photochemical event triggered by a two-photon excitation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:T06543
name: two-site immunoradiometric assay
def: "Immunoradiometric assay involving two sets of antibodies\, one of which is labelled\, that combine with different immunoreactive sites of an antigen molecule."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT06812
name: target
def: "Any organism\, organ\, tissue\, cell\, or cell constituent that is subject to the action of an agent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT06846
name: topical effect
def: "Consequence of application of a substance to the surface of the body which occurs at the point of application."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT06897
name: thermotropic mesophase
def: "A mesophase formed by heating a solid or cooling an isotropic liquid\, or by heating or cooling a thermodynamically stable mesophase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT06898
name: transition temperature
def: "The temperature at which the transition from mesophase X to mesophase Y occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT06915
name: threshold limit value
def: "The concentration in air to which it is believed that most workers can be exposed daily without an adverse effect \(i.e.\, effectively\, the threshold between safe and dangerous concentrations\). The values were established \(and are revised annually\) by the ACGIH and are time-weighted concentrations \(TWA\) for a 7- or 8-h workday and 40-h workweek\, and thus are related to chronic effects. A short-term exposure limit \(STEL\) is defined as a 15-min TWA exposure\, which should not be exceeded at any time during a workday even if the 8-h TWA is within the TLV-TWA."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT06926
name: total velocity of the analyte
def: "The sum of electrophoretic velocity\, \\\(\nu_\{\\rm\{ep\}\}\\\)\, of an ion and the electroosmotic velocity\, \\\(\nu _\{\\rm\{eo\}\}\\\). \\\[\nu_\{\\rm\{tot\}\} = \nu_\{\\rm\{ep\}\}\\\,+\\\,\nu_\{\\rm\{eo\}\}\\\] This quantity can be measured experimentally as the effective length of the capillary divided by the migration time \(\\\(L_\{\\rm\{eff\}\}/t_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07167
name: telechelic polymer
def: "Prepolymer capable of entering into further polymerization or other reactions through its reactive end-groups."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07168
name: thermosetting polymer
def: "Prepolymer in a soft solid or viscous state that changes irreversibly into an infusible\, insoluble polymer network by curing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07268
name: thermoplastic elastomer
def: "Elastomer comprising a thermoreversible network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07484
name: time-dependent density functional theory
def: "Methods for computing accurate excitation energies at a low computational cost in large molecular species within the time-dependent scheme and the density functional theory. It is the only available DFT-based method for computing electronic excitation energies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07487
name: transient-stimulated emission pumping
def: "Transient-spectroscopy technique in which the excited state dynamics is probed via stimulating the molecular species from the photo-excited state \(produced by a short pump pulse\) back to the ground state by means of a short dump pulse at various pump–dump time delays."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07490
name: tungsten-halogen lamp
def: "Tungsten lamp."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07491
name: two-photon photochromism
def: "Photochromic process involving a two-photon process. The process might involve the simultaneous or the sequential absorption of two photons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07531
name: thermoreversible gel
def: "Swollen network in which the junction points are thermally reversible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07532
name: thixotropic gel
def: "Gel which has a reduced viscosity on the application of a finite shear but which recovers its original viscosity when the shear is discontinued."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07544
name: theoretical ceramic yield
def: "Ceramic yield based on the stoichiometry of the ceramization process."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07573
name: thermoreversible network
def: "Reversible network that forms or breaks up as the temperature is changed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07576
name: transient network
def: "Network that exists only transiently."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07595
name: transient crosslink
def: "Crosslink formed by intermolecular or intramolecular interactions that are unstable under the conditions of use of the material formed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07600
name: thermoreversible junction point
def: "Junction point in a polymer network that can be destroyed and formed reversibly by a change of temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07601
name: transient junction point
def: "Junction point in a polymer network that exists only for a finite period of time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:TT07616
name: thermal curing
def: "Curing induced by heating."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06546
name: ultimate capacity
def: "The theoretical maximum capacity of a solvent containing a given concentration of extractant for a solute under any conditions. Where appropriate the term stoichiometric capacity can be used."
synonym: "stoichiometric capacity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06547
name: ultrafiltrate
def: "The solution obtained by ultrafiltration\, in general not of the same composition as the equilibrium solution."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06548
name: ultrafiltration
def: "A separation process whereby a solution containing a solute of molecular size significantly greater than that of the solvent molecule is removed from the solvent by the application of a hydraulic pressure which forces only the solvent to flow through a suitable membrane\, usually having a pore size in the range \\\(0.001\{-\}0.1\\ \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3BC\}\\rm\{m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06549
name: ultrasonic detector
def: "Sound is generated in a reference cell and a measuring cell. An eluted component passing through the measuring cell changes the velocity of sound in the cell. This change is detected by a phase shift of acoustic signals between the two cells. This detector is usually employed for inorganic gases in the region where thermal conductivity detectors are not sufficiently sensitive. The detection threshold is of the order of \\\(0.1\\ \\rm\{ppmv\}\\\). The linear dynamic range is of the order of \\\(10^\{3\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06550
name: umpire sample
def: "A sample taken\, prepared and stored in an agreed upon manner for the purpose of settling a dispute. The agreement usually extends beyond the sample to the basis for reaching a decision \(e.g. quantity of material from which taken\, use of a third party and criteria serving as the basis for acceptance\, rejection or economic adjustment\). The term 'reference sample' has been used in this context but this term more properly should be used in conjunction with a 'reference material' or 'reference standard' which has a true or assigned value for a constituent or property. One of the characteristics of a reference material or reference standard is that it must have a negligible sampling error between test portions."
synonym: "reserve sample" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06551
name: umpolung
def: "Any process by which the normal alternating donor and acceptor reactivity pattern of a chain\, which is due to the presence of $\\ce\{O\}$ or $\\ce\{N\}$ heteroatoms\, is interchanged. Reactivity umpolung is most often achieved by temporary exchange of heteroatoms \($\\ce\{N\}$\, $\\ce\{O\}$\) by others\, such as $\\ce\{P\}$\, $\\ce\{S\}$ and $\\ce\{Se\}$. The original meaning of the term has been extended to the reversal of any commonly accepted reactivity pattern. For example\, reaction of $\\ce\{R–C#CX\}$ \($\\ce\{X\}$ = halide\) as a synthon for $\\ce\{'R–C#C^\{+\}\}$ \(i.e. electrophilic acetylene\) is an umpolung of the normal more common acetylide\, $\\ce\{R–C#C^\{−\}\}$ \(i.e. nucleophilic\) reactivity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06552
name: unactivated adsorption process
def: "If the temperature coefficient of the rate of adsorption is very small\, the adsorption process is said to be unactivated \(i.e. to have a negligible activation energy\). In this case the sticking coefficient at low coverages may be near unity particularly for smaller molecules."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06553
name: uncertainty of measurement
def: "Parameter\, associated with the result of a measurement\, that characterizes the dispersion of the values that could reasonably be attributed to the measurand \(the quantity being measured\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06554
name: unified atomic mass unit
def: "Non-SI unit of mass \(equal to the atomic mass constant\)\, defined as one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom in its ground state and used to express masses of atomic particles\, \\\(\\rm\{u\} \\approx \\pu\{1.6605402\(10\)E-27 kg\}\\\)."
synonym: "atomic mass unit" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06555
name: uniform corrosion
def: "Corrosion is uniform if the time average of the corrosion current through a unit area of any macroscopic dimension is independent of the position on the surface."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06556
name: uniformly labelled
def: "A selectively labelled compound where all atoms of a particular element are labelled in the same isotopic ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06557
name: uniformly labelled tracer
def: "A tracer in which the label is uniformly distributed over its possible positions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06558
name: uniform polymer
def: "A polymer composed of molecules uniform with respect to relative molecular mass and constitution."
synonym: "monodisperse polymer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06560
name: unit
def: "Each of the discrete\, identifiable portions of material suitable for removal from a population as a sample or as a portion of a sample\, and which can be individually considered\, examined or tested\, or combined. In the case of sampling bulk materials \(or large packages\)\, the units are increments\, created by a sampling device. In the case of packaged materials\, the unit may vary with the level of commercial distribution. For example\, an individual piece of candy is the sampling unit at the consumer level; a package of individual pieces is the sampling unit at the retail level; a carton of packages is the sampling unit at the wholesale level; a pallet of cartons is the shipping unit at the distribution centre level; and a truckload of pallets is the consignment unit at the manufacture level. Before packaging\, the bin containing the individual pieces would be the bulk lot \(or batch\) for sampling."
synonym: "individual" []
synonym: "item" []
synonym: "portion" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06561
name: unit
def: "Particular quantity\, defined and adopted by convention\, with which other quanitities of the same kind are compared in order to express their magnitudes relative to that quantity. Units have conventionally assigned names and symbols."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06562
name: unit cell
def: "The smallest\, regularly repeating material portion contained in a parallelepiped from which a crystal is formed by parallel displacements in three dimensions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06563
name: unit system
def: "Set of base units\, together with derived units\, defined in accordance with given rules\, for a given system of quantities."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06564
name: universal calibration
def: "A calibration of a size-exclusion chromatography set-up based on the finding that the retention volume of a molecular or particulate species is a single-valued function of an appropriate size parameter of this molecule or particle\, irrespective of its chemical nature and structure. The product of the intrinsic viscosity and molar mass\, \\\(\[\\eta\]M\\\)\, has been widely used as the size parameter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06565
name: universal detector
def: "A detector which responds to every component in the column effluent except the mobile phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06566
name: unperturbed dimensions
def: "The dimensions of an actual polymer random coil in a θ state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06567
name: unreactive
def: "Failing to react with a specified chemical species under specified conditions. The term should not be used in place of stable\, since a relatively more stable species may nevertheless be more reactive than some reference species towards a given reaction partner."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06569
name: unstable
def: "The opposite of stable\, i.e. the chemical species concerned has a higher molar Gibbs energy than some assumed standard. The term should not be used in place of reactive or transient\, although more reactive or transient species are frequently also more unstable. \(Very unstable chemical species tend to undergo exothermic unimolecular decompositions. Variations in the structure of the related chemical species of this kind generally affect the energy of the transition states for these decompositions less than they affect the stability of the decomposing chemical species. Low stability may therefore parallel a relatively high rate of unimolecular decomposition.\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06571
name: unstable ion
def: "An ion which is sufficiently excited to dissociate within the ion source."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06574
name: upconversion
def: "The process by which two photons with frequencies \\\(\nu _\{2\}\\\) and \\\(\nu _\{3\}\\\) combine in a non-linear medium to produce a higher energy photon with frequency \\\(\nu _\{1\}\\\) such that \\\(\nu _\{1\}=\nu _\{2\}+\nu _\{3\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06576
name: upper limit of measurement
def: "Highest value of the air quality characteristic which can be measured by an instrument; its variations\, caused for example by instability\, are expected to lie within specified limits. The difference between the lower detection limit and the upper limit of measurement constitutes the dynamic range of the instrument."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06577
name: ureides
def: "N-Acyl or N\,N'-diacyl ureas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06578
name: urethanes
def: "An alternative term for the compounds $\\ce\{R2NC\(=O\)OR'\}$ \($\\ce\{R\} \neq \\ce\{H\}$\)\, esters of carbamic acids\, $\\ce\{R2NC\(=O\)OH\}$\, in strict use limited to the ethyl esters\, but widely used in the general sense\, e.g. 'polyurethane resins'."
synonym: "urethans" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06579
name: uronic acids
def: "Monocarboxylic acids formally derived by oxidation to a carboxy group of the terminal $\\ce\{–CH2OH\}$ group of aldoses\, e.g. ᴅ-glucuronic acid."
synonym: "glycuronic acids" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06580
name: uronium salts
def: "Salts of O-hydronated urea\, having the structure $\\ce\{\[HOC\(=NH2\)NH2\]^\{+\}X^\{−\}\}$ \(and O- and N- hydrocarbyl derivatives\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06582
name: ultraviolet dose
def: "A dose of ultraviolet \(UV\) radiation."
synonym: "UV dose" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:U06584
name: ultraviolet stabilizer
def: "A substance added to a sample to prevent photodeterioration by ultraviolet \(UV\) light."
synonym: "UV stabilizer" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:UT06806
name: uptake
def: "Entry of a substance into the body\, into an organ\, into a tissue\, into a cell\, or into the body fluids by passage through a membrane or by other means."

[Term]
id: goldbook:UT06882
name: upstream
def: "Side of a membrane into which penetrants enter from the feed stream."

[Term]
id: goldbook:UT06884
name: uphill transport
def: "Process in which diffusion of a component occurs from a less concentrated stream to a more concentrated permeable stream."

[Term]
id: goldbook:UT07280
name: upper critical solution temperature
def: "Critical temperature above which a mixture is miscible."

[Term]
id: goldbook:UT07492
name: ultraviolet
def: "Region of the electromagnetic spectrum extending from about \\\(100\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) to \\\(400\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\). This region is divided into four sub-bands as follows\: Vacuum UV \(VUV\)&nbsp;\\\(100\{-\}200\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)UV-C \\\(200\{-\}280\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)UV-B\\\(280\{-\}315\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)UV-A\\\(315\{-\}400\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:UT07493
name: uniaxial sample
def: "Sample characterized by a \(unique\) sample axis Z with all directions perpendicular to Z being equivalent. In other words\, the sample properties are invariant to rotation around Z. Uniaxiality exists in many anisotropic samples and simplifies the interpretation of their spectra considerably."

[Term]
id: goldbook:UT07638
name: uniaxial pressing
def: "Application of pressure in one direction during ceramization to achieve a uniform densification and the production of a compact monolith."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V05019
name: <i>ρ</i>-value
def: "A measure of the susceptibility to the influence of substituent groups on the rate constant or equilibrium constant of a particular organic reaction involving a family of related substrates. Defined by Hammett for the effect of ring substituents in meta- and para-positions of aromatic side-chain reactions by the empirical '\\\(\\rho \\sigma \\\)-equation' of the general form\: \\\[\\log _\{10\}\(\\frac\{k_\{\\rm\{X\}\}\}\{k_\{\\rm\{H\}\}\}\) = \\rho \\ \\sigma _\{\\rm\{X\}\}\\\] in which \\\(\\sigma _\{\\rm\{X\}\}\\\) is a constant characteristic of the substituent X and of its position in the reactant molecule. More generally \(and not only for aromatic series\)\, \\\(\\rho \\\)-values \(modified with appropriate subscripts and superscripts\) are used to designate the susceptibility of reaction series for families of various organic compounds to any substituent effects\, as given by the modified set of \\\(\\sigma \\\)-constants in an empirical \\\(\\rho \\sigma \\\)-correlation. Reactions with a positive \\\(\\rho \\\)-value are accelerated \(or the equilibrium constants of analogous equilibria are increased\) by substituents with positive \\\(\\sigma \\\)-constants. Since the sign of \\\(\\sigma \\\) was defined so that substituents with a positive \\\(\\sigma \\\) increase the acidity of benzoic acid\, such substituents are generally described as attracting electrons away from the aromatic ring. It follows that reactions with a positive \\\(\\rho \\\)-value are considered to involve a transition state \(or reaction product\) so that the difference in energy between this state and the reactants is decreased by a reduction in electron density at the reactive site of the substrate."
synonym: "rho-value" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06585
name: vaccine
def: "An agent containing antigens produced from killed\, attenuated or live pathogenic microorganisms\, synthetic peptides or by recombinant organisms\, used for stimulating the immune system of the recipient to produce specific antibodies providing active immunity and/or passive immunity in the progeny."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06586
name: vacuum phototube
def: "A photo-emissive detector inside an evacuated envelope with a transparent window\, the photocathode\, and the anode. The photocathode can be opaque or semitransparent."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06587
name: vacuum system
def: "Those components used to lower the pressure within a mass spectrometer are all parts of the vacuum system. This includes not only the various pumping components but also valves\, gauges and associated electronic or other control devices\, the chamber in which ions are formed and detected\, and the vacuum envelope."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06588
name: valence
def: "The maximum number of univalent atoms \(originally hydrogen or chlorine atoms\) that may combine with an atom of the element under consideration\, or with a fragment\, or for which an atom of this element can be substituted."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06589
name: valence band
def: "Highest energy continuum of energy levels in a solid that is fully occupied by electrons at \\\(0\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06590
name: valence isomer
def: "A constitutional isomer interrelated with another by pericyclic reactions. For example\, Dewar benzene\, prismane and benzvalene are valence isomers of benzene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06591
name: valence tautomerization
def: "The term describes simple reversible and generally rapid isomerizations or degenerate rearrangements involving the formation and rupture of single and/or double bonds\, without migration of atoms or groups; e.g."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06592
name: valence transition
def: "A transition observed in certain rare-earth and actinide materials in which the electronic occupation of the 4f or 5f orbital changes with external conditions for example\, temperature and pressure. Example\: The transition at approximately \\\(0.65\\ \\rm\{GPa\}\\\) where black\, semiconducting $\\ce\{Sm^\{2+\}S^\{2−\}\}$ changes to golden metallic $\\ce\{\(Sm^\{3+\} + e^\{−\}\)S^\{2-\}\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06593
name: value
def: "Magnitude of a particular quantity generally expressed as a unit of measurement multiplied by a number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06594
name: value of a division
def: "The reciprocal of the sensitivity; like the latter\, it usually varies with load. It is determined by empirical calibration."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06597
name: van der Waals forces
def: "The attractive or repulsive forces between molecular entities \(or between groups within the same molecular entity\) other than those due to bond formation or to the electrostatic interaction of ions or of ionic groups with one another or with neutral molecules. The term includes\: dipole–dipole\, dipole-induced dipole and London \(instantaneous induced dipole-induced dipole\) forces. The term is sometimes used loosely for the totality of nonspecific attractive or repulsive intermolecular forces."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06598
name: vaporization temperature
def: "The temperature of the atomization surface at which analyte loss becomes statistically significant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06599
name: vapour phase interference
def: "Interference caused by a change in the fraction of analyte dissociated\, ionized or excited in the gaseous phase. \(Note\: here 'dissociation' means the formation of free neutral atoms from free molecules in the gaseous phase. The term atomization is here not appropriate because the latter also covers the formation of free atomic ions.\) These interferences may be called dissociation\, ionization and excitation interference\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06600
name: variable
def: "The quantity or characteristic measured or computed. The corresponding numerical value may be taken for statistical handling; it may\, for example\, be a measured value or result."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06601
name: variable pathlength cell
def: "A cell whose pathlength can be varied either continuously or in steps by means of spacers."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06602
name: variance
def: "The sum of the squares of the standard deviations\, expressing the contributions to the overall precision from various sources of uncertainty."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06603
name: variational transition state theory
def: "This expression is applied to modifications of conventional transition state theory in which the position of the dividing surface in the potential-energy surface is varied. The rate is calculated with the surface at various positions\, and the lowest rate calculated is taken to be closest to the truth."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06606
name: vector
def: "A DNA molecule \(plasmid\, virus\, bacteriophage\, artificial or cut DNA molecule\) capable of being replicated and bearing cloning sites for the introduction of foreign DNA\, used to introduce this DNA into host cells.Any organism that transmits a disease between two hosts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06607
name: velocity
def: "Vector quantity equal to the derivative of the position vector with respect to time \(symbols \\\(u\\\)\, \\\(v\\\)\, \\\(w\\\) for components of \\\(c\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06608
name: velocity
def: "The flux density \\\(N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is related to the velocity by the equation \\\[N_\{\\rm\{B\}\}=c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ v_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\] The vector \\\(v_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the macroscopic average velocity at which the species B moves \(which is to be distinguished from the random molecular velocity\); \\\(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the concentration of species B \(\\\(\\pu\{mol m-3\}\\\)\). Note that the velocity is defined with respect to a frame of reference."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06609
name: verdazyl radicals
def: "Relatively stable delocalized radicals\, derived from hydrazyl\, $\\ce\{H2NN^\{.\}H\}$\, of the types shown\, in particular the six-membered ring\:"

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06610
name: vertical ionization
def: "A process whereby an electron is removed from a molecule in its ground or an excited state so rapidly that a positive ion is produced without change in the positions or momenta of the atoms. The resultant ion is often in an excited state."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06612
name: Verwey transition
def: "An electron-ordering transition occurring in a mixed-valent system that results in an ordering of formal valence states in the low-temperature phase. Example\: The prototype system\, first identified by Verwey\, is the ferrospinel magnetite\, $\\ce\{Fe^\{3+\}\[Fe^\{3+\}Fe^\{2+\}\]O4\}$ in which an ordering of $\\ce\{Fe^\{3+\}\}$ and $\\ce\{Fe^\{2+\}\}$ ions within octahedral sites is thought to occur below \\\(T_\{\\rm\{v\}\}\\approx 120\\ \\rm\{K\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06614
name: vibrational redistribution
def: "Intramolecular redistribution of energy among the vibrational modes usually giving a statistical distribution of their populations\, characterized by the 'vibrational temperature'. For large molecules\, this process does not require collisions."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06615
name: vibrational relaxation
def: "The loss of vibrational excitation energy by a molecular entity through energy transfer to the environment caused by collisions. The molecular entity relaxes into vibrational equilibrium with its environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06616
name: vibrational term
def: "Vibrational energy divided by the product of the Planck constant and the speed of light."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06617
name: vibronic coupling
def: "Interaction between electronic and vibrational motions in a molecular entity."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06618
name: vibronic transition
def: "A transition which involves a change in both the electronic and vibrational quantum numbers of a molecular entity\, as opposed to purely electronic or purely vibrational transitions. The transition occurs between two states\, just as in a purely electronic transition\, but involves a change in both electronic and vibrational energy."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06619
name: vidicon
def: "A vacuum tube containing a photosensitive area\, or target\, and an electron gun to read the signal from the target. The silicon target consists of a two-dimensional array of Si-photodiodes having a common cathode and isolated anodes. irradiation of the target causes the production of electron-hole pairs which\, by recombination\, leads to a depletion of the surface charge."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06620
name: vinyl carbenes
def: "Carbenes having a vinylic group on a carbenic carbon atom $\\ce\{R2C=CRC\{\:\}R  R2C^\{+\}–CR=C^\{−\}R\}$. E.g. $\\ce\{H2C=CHC\{\:\}H\}$\, prop-2-en-1-ylidene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06621
name: vinylic cations
def: "Carbocations having the structure $\\ce\{R2C=C^\{+\}–R\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06622
name: vinylic groups
def: "The vinyl group \($\\ce\{CH2=CH\\bond\{–\}\}$\) and derivatives formed by substitution. Informally\, a group\, such as $\\ce\{–OH\}$\, attached to the free valence of a \(substituted\) vinyl group is sometimes referred to as 'vinylic'."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06623
name: vinylidenes
def: "Carbenes in which the carbenic carbon atom has a double bond to another carbon atom $\\ce\{R2C=C\{\:\}\}$\, e.g. $\\ce\{H2C=C\{\:\}\}$ ethenylidene."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06624
name: viologens
def: "1\,1-Di\(hydrocarbyl\)-4\,4'-bipyridinium salts."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06625
name: virial coefficients
def: "Coefficients \(\\\(B\\rm\{\,\}C\\rm\{\,\}\\\,...\\\)\) in the virial equation of state for a real gas \\\(p\\ V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}=R\\ T\\ \(1+\\frac\{B\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\}+\\frac\{C\}\{V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}^\{2\}\}+\\\,...\)\\\) where \\\(p\\\) is the pressure\, \\\(V_\{\\rm\{m\}\}\\\) the molar volume\, \\\(R\\\) the gas constant and \\\(T\\\) the thermodynamic temperature."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06626
name: virtual transition
def: "A non-observable liquid-crystal transition that occurs below the crystallization temperature and is determined by extrapolation in binary phase diagrams."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06628
name: viscosity function
def: "A coefficient connecting the intrinsic viscosity\, the radius of gyration and the molar mass of a chain macromolecule\, according to the equation\: \\\[\\rm\{\[\}\\eta \\rm\{\]\} = \\frac\{\\mathit\{\\Phi \}\\ 6^\{3/2\}\\ \( < s^\{2\} > \)^\{3/2\}\}\{M\}\\\] where \\\(\\rm\{\[\}\\eta \\rm\{\]\}\\\) is the intrinsic viscosity\, \\\(s\\\) is the radius of gyration and \\\(M\\\) is the molar mass. The viscosity function is often referred to as the Flory constant."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06631
name: visibility
def: "Defined as the greatest distance at which a black object of suitable dimensions can be seen and recognized against the horizon sky\, or\, in the case of night observations\, could be seen and recognized if the general illumination were raised to the normal daylight level. The criterion of recognizing the object\, not just seeing the object without recognition\, is used. Transmissometers\, telephotometers and sun photometers are devices used to measure the degree of transmission of light. Nephelometers \(integrating\) are used to measure visibility by way of the light scattering from aerosols in the air mass."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06632
name: volatilization
def: "The conversion of a solid or liquid to a gas or vapour by application of heat\, by reducing pressure\, by chemical reaction or by a combination of these processes.Example\: \\\(\\rm\{W\(s\)\}+\\frac\{3\}\{2\}\\rm\{O\}_\{2\}\\\:\\rm\{\(g\)\}\\\:\\rightarrow \\\:\\rm\{WO\}_\{3\}\\\:\\rm\{\(g\)\}\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06633
name: volatilizer
def: "Material added to a sample to increase its volatilization or that of some component of it. Volatilizers increase the fraction volatilized either by forming more volatile compounds or by increasing the total surface area of all analyte particles \(e.g. by explosive disintegration or by dispersal of the analyte in a highly volatile matrix\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06634
name: volt
def: "SI derived unit of electric potential\, \\\(\\pu\{V\} = \\pu\{J C-1\} = \\pu\{m2 kg s-3 A-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06635
name: voltage
def: "The use of this term is discouraged\, and the term applied potential should be used instead\, for non-periodic signals. However\, it is retained here for sinusoidal and other periodic signals because no suitable substitute for it has been proposed."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06639
name: voltammetric constant
def: "In linear-sweep voltammetry and related techniques\, the empirical quantity defined by the equation \\\[\\frac\{i_\{p\}\}\{A\\ \\sqrt\{\nu\}\\ c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\quad \\left\(=\\frac\{j_\{p\}\}\{\\sqrt\{\nu\}\\ c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\}\\right\)\\\] where \\\(i_\{p\}\\\) is the peak current\, A is the area of the electrode-solution interface\, \\\(\nu\\\) is the rate of change of applied potential\, and \\\(c_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the bulk concentration of the substance B whose reduction or oxidation is responsible for the peak in question."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06641
name: volume content
def: "The volume of an \(isolated\) specified component \(C\) divided by the mass of the system \(S\)\, e.g. patient–blood volume content. Volume content should not be confused with specific volume \(or massic volume\)\, which is the volume of the system divided by the mass of the system."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06642
name: volume flow rate
def: "Volume of a component crossing a surface divided by the time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06643
name: volume fraction
def: "Volume of a constituent of a mixture divided by the sum of volumes of all constituents prior to mixing."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06644
name: volume of activation
def: "A quantity derived from the pressure dependence of the rate constant of a reaction \(mainly used for reactions in solution\)\, defined by the equation\: \\\[\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger \}V=- R\\ T\\ \(\\frac\{\\partial \(\\ln k\)\}\{\\partial p\}\)_\{T\}\\\] providing that the rate constants of all reactions \(except first-order reactions\) are expressed in pressure-independent concentration units\, such as \\\(\\pu\{mol dm-3\}\\\) at a fixed temperature and pressure. The volume of activation is interpreted\, according to transition state theory\, as the difference between the partial molar volumes of the transition state \(\\\(V\\\)\) and the sums of the partial volumes of the reactants at the same temperature and pressure\, i.e. \\\[\\Delta ^\{\\ddagger\}V = ^\{\\ddagger\}V- \\sum \\left\(r\\ V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\right\)\\\] where \\\(r\\\) is the order in the reactant R and \\\(V_\{\\rm\{R\}\}\\\) its partial molar volume."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06645
name: volume of the stationary phase
def: "The volume of the stationary liquid phase or of the active solid or of the gel in the column. The volume of any solid support is not included."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06648
name: volume strain
def: "The change of volume divided by the original volume."
synonym: "bulk strain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06649
name: volumetric flowrate
def: "The flowrate of the mobile phase \(\\\(\\pu\{cm3 min-1\}\\\)\)\, normally specified at the column temperature and outlet pressure\, although the measurement may be made at ambient temperature and must be corrected accordingly \(and possibly also for water vapour present in the flowmeter\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06650
name: volume viscosity
def: "A quantity $\\upzeta\\!$ which enters into equations at any point where the flow involves a change in volume\, i.e. is dilatational. If the deformation is purely dilatational\, the average of three normal stress components is\: \\\[\\sigma =\\frac\{1\}\{3\}\\ \(\\sigma _\{xx\}+\\sigma _\{yy\}+\\sigma _\{zz\}\)=- p+\\zeta \\ \(\\frac\{\\partial v_\{x\}\}\{\\partial x\}+\\frac\{\\partial v_\{y\}\}\{\\partial y\}+\\frac\{\\partial v_\{z\}\}\{\\partial z\}\)\\\] where \\\(p\\\) is the hydrostatic pressure at the point considered in the absence of motion."
synonym: "dilatational viscosity" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:V06651
name: volumic
def: "Attribute to a physical quantity obtained by division by volume. charge density may thus be called volumic charge which is the electric charge in a section of space divided by the volume of that section."

[Term]
id: goldbook:VT07153
name: vulcanization
def: "Chemical crosslinking of high-molar-mass linear or branched polymers to give a polymer network."

[Term]
id: goldbook:VT07496
name: visible
def: "Region of the electromagnetic spectrum extending from about \\\(400\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\) to \\\(760\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\). This is the wavelength region to which the human eye is sensitive. There are no precise limits for the spectral range of visible radiation since they depend upon the amount of radiant power reaching the retina and on the responsivity of the observer."

[Term]
id: goldbook:VT07497
name: VUV
def: "Acronym for vacuum ultraviolet."

[Term]
id: goldbook:VT07640
name: viscous sintering
def: "Sintering process by which it is possible to densify gels to glasses and ceramics at elevated temperatures."
synonym: "viscous flow sintering" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06653
name: Walden inversion
def: "Retention of configuration is the preservation of integrity of the spatial arrangement of bonds to a chiral centre during a chemical reaction or transformation. It is also the configurational correlation when a chemical species Xabcd is converted into the chemical species Xabce having the same relative configuration. The configurational change when a chemical species Xabcd \(where X is typically carbon\)\, having a tetrahedral arrangement of bonds to X\, is converted into the chemical species Xabce having the opposite relative configuration \(or when it undergoes an identity reaction in which Xabcd of opposite configuration is produced\) is called a Walden inversion or inversion of configuration. The occurrence of a Walden inversion during a chemical transformation is sometimes indicated in the chemical equation by the symbol shown below in place of a simple arrow pointing from reactants to products."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06654
name: wall-coated open-tubular column
def: "A column in which the liquid stationary phase is coated on the essentially unmodified smooth inner wall of the tube."
synonym: "WCOT column" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06655
name: wash out
def: "The removal from the atmosphere of gases and sometimes particles by their solution in or attachment to raindrops as they fall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06656
name: watt
def: "SI derived unit of power\, \\\(\\pu\{W\} = \\pu\{J s-1\} = \\pu\{m2 kg s-3\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06657
name: wavefunction
def: "The solution of the Schrödinger equation\, eigenfunction of the hamiltonian operator. Complex conjugate functions are denoted by an asterisk read as 'star'."
synonym: "state function" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06658
name: wave height
def: "The limiting current of an individual wave\, frequently expressed in arbitrary units for convenience."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06659
name: wavelength
def: "Distance in the direction of propagation of a periodic wave between two successive points where at a given time the phase is the same."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06660
name: wavelength converter
def: "A wavelength converter converts radiation at one wavelength to radiation at another detectable wavelength or at a wavelength of improved responsivity of the detector. The classical wavelength converter consists of a screen of luminescent material that absorbs radiation and radiates at a longer wavelength. Such materials are often used to convert ultraviolet to visible radiation for detection by conventional phototubes. In X-ray spectroscopy a converter that emits optical radiation is called a scintillator. In most cases wavelength conversion is from short to long wavelength\, but in the case of conversion of long to short wavelength the process is sometimes called upconversion. Wavelengths of coherent sources can be converted using nonlinear optical techniques. A typical example is frequency doubling."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06661
name: wavelength dispersion
def: "Spatial separation of characteristic X-rays according to their wavelengths."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06662
name: wavelength-dispersive X-ray fluorescence analysis
def: "A kind of X-ray fluorescence analysis involving the measurement of the wavelength spectrum of the emitted radiation e.g. by using a diffraction grating or crystal."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06663
name: wavelength error
def: "The error in absorbance which may occur if there is a difference between the \(mean\) wavelength of the radiation entering the sample cell and the indicated wavelength on the spectrometer scale."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06664
name: wavenumber
def: "The reciprocal of the wavelength\, \\\(\\lambda \\\)\, or the number of waves per unit length along the direction of propagation. The SI unit is \\\(\\rm\{m\}^\{-1\}\\\)\, but a commonly used unit is \\\(\\pu\{cm-1\}\\\). Symbols \\\(\\overset\{˜\}\{\nu \}\\\) in a vacuum\, \\\(\\sigma \\\) in a medium."
synonym: "repetency" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06665
name: weak collision
def: "A collision between two molecules in which the amount of energy transferred from one to the other is not large compared to \\\(k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\ T\\\) \(\\\(k_\{\\rm\{B\}\}\\\) is the Boltzmann constant and \\\(T\\\) the absolute temperature\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06666
name: weber
def: "SI derived unit of magnetic flux\, \\\(\\pu\{Wb\} = \\pu\{V s\} = \\pu\{m2 kg s-2 A-1\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06667
name: wedge projection
def: "A stereochemical projection\, roughly in the mean plane of the molecule\, in which bonds are represented by open wedges\, tapering off from the nearer atom to the farther atom. It is mainly used to illustrate the conformation of larger cycloalkanes e.g. cyclotetradecane\:   equivalent to"

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06668
name: weight
def: "Force of gravity acting on a body\, \\\(G=m\\ g\\\)\, where \\\(m\\\) is its mass and \\\(g\\\) the acceleration of free fall."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06670
name: weighted mean
def: "If in a series of observations a statistical weight \(\\\(w_\{i\}\\\)\) is assigned to each value\, a weighted mean \\\(\\overline\{x\}_\{w\}\\\) can be calculated by the formula\: \\\[\\overline\{x\}_\{w\}=\\frac\{\\sum w_\{i\}\\ x_\{i\}\}\{\\sum w_\{i\}\}\\\]"

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06671
name: Weller correlation
def: "Empirical correlation for the energy of full charge-transfer exciplex relative to the ground state in n-hexane as a function of the electrochemical one electron standard reduction potential of the cation radical produced upon electron donation and standard reduction potential of the acceptor measured in a polar solvent for the donor \(D\) and the acceptor \(A\) involved \\\[\\Delta H\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\}\\rm\{A\}^\{-\}\,\\rm\{hexane\}\) = e\\left \[ E^\{0\}\(\\rm\{D\}^\{+\\bullet \}/\\rm\{D\}\) - E^\{0\}\(\\rm\{A\}/\\rm\{A\}^\{-\\bullet \}\) \\right \]+e\\Delta E\\\] with \\\(E^\{0\}\(D^\{+\\bullet \}/D\)\\\) and \\\(E^\{0\}\(A/D^\{-\\bullet \}\)\\\) the standard electrode potentials of the donor and acceptor\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06673
name: wet bulb temperature
def: "In psychrometry\, the temperature of the sensor or the bulb of a thermometer in which a constantly renewed film of water is evaporating. The temperature of the water used to renew the film must be at the temperature of the gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06674
name: wetting tension
def: "The work done on a system when the process of immersional wetting involving unit area of phase $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$ is carried out reversibly\: \\\[w_\{\\rm\{W\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}=\\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}- \\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\] where \\\(\\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\) and \\\(\\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}\\\) are the surface tensions between two bulk phases $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\}$\, respectively."
synonym: "work of immersional wetting per unit area" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06676
name: Wiegner effect
def: "Synonymous with suspension effect \(in an ion-selective electrode\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06678
name: Wilzbach labelling
def: "Labelling of a substance by exposing it to tritium gas."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06679
name: wind rose
def: "A diagram designed to show the distribution of wind direction experienced at a given location over a considerable period of time. Usually shown in polar coordinates \(distance from the origin being proportional to the probability of the wind direction being at the given angle usually measured from the north\). Similar diagrams are sometimes used to summarize the average concentrations of a given pollutant seen over a considerable period of time as a function of direction from a given site \(sometimes called a pollution rose\)."
synonym: "pollution rose" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06681
name: Wood horn
def: "A mechanical device that acts by absorption as a perfect photon trap."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06682
name: Wood lamp
def: "Term used to describe a low-pressure mercury arc possessing a fluorescing layer\, which emits in the UV-A region \(from \\\(315\\\) to \\\(400\\ \\rm\{nm\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06684
name: work
def: "Scalar product of force\, \\\(\\boldsymbol\{F\}\\\)\, and position change\, \\\(\\rm\{d\}\\boldsymbol\{r\}\\\)\, \\\(w = \\int \\boldsymbol\{F\}\\cdot \\rm\{d\}\\mathbf\{r\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06685
name: work hardening
def: "Opposite of work softening\, in which shear results in a permanent increase of viscosity or consistency with time."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06686
name: working electrode
def: "An electrode that serves as a transducer responding to the excitation signal and the concentration of the substance of interest in the solution being investigated\, and that permits the flow of current sufficiently large to effect appreciable changes of bulk composition within the ordinary duration of a measurement."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06687
name: work of adhesion
def: "The work of adhesion per unit area\, \\\(w_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}\\\)\, is the work done on the system when two condensed phases $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\, forming an interface of unit area are separated reversibly to form unit areas of each of the $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\\updelta\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\\updelta\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$ interfaces. \\\[w_\{\\rm\{A\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}=\\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}+\\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}- \\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\] where \\\(\\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \}\\\)\, \\\(\\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}\\\) and \\\(\\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B2\} \\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B4\} \}\\\) are the surface tensions between two bulk phases $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$; $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\}$ and $\\ce\{\\upbeta\\!\}$\, $\\ce\{\\updelta\\!\}$ respectively. The work of adhesion as defined above\, and traditionally used\, may be called the work of separation."
synonym: "work of separation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06688
name: work of cohesion per unit area
def: "Of a single pure liquid or solid phase $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$\, \\\(w_\{\\rm\{C\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) is the work done on the system when a column $\\ce\{\\upalpha\\!\}$ of unit area is split\, reversibly\, normal to the axis of the column to form two new surfaces each of unit area in contact with the equilibrium gas phase. \\\[w_\{\\rm\{C\}\}^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}=2\\ \\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\] where \\\(\\gamma ^\{\\unicode\[Times\]\{x3B1\} \}\\\) is the surface tension between phase and its equilibrium vapour or a dilute gas phase."

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06691
name: work softening
def: "The application of a finite shear to a system after a long rest may result in a decrease of the viscosity or the consistency. If the decrease persists when the shear is discontinued\, this behaviour is called work softening \(or shear breakdown\)\, whereas if the original viscosity or consistency is recovered this behaviour is called thixotropy."
synonym: "shear breakdown" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:W06692
name: worm-like chain
def: "A hypothetical linear macromolecule consisting of an infinitely thin chain of continuous curvature; the direction of curvature at any point is random. The model describes the whole spectrum of chains with different degrees of chain stiffness from rigid rods to random coils\, and is particularly useful for representing stiff chains. In the literature this chain is sometimes referred to as a Porod–Kratky chain. Synonymous with continuously curved chain."
synonym: "Porod–Kratky chain" []
synonym: "continuously curved chain" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:WT06852
name: weathering
def: "Exposure of a polymeric material to a natural or simulated environment."

[Term]
id: goldbook:WT07250
name: wetting
def: "Process by which an interface between a solid and a gas is replaced by an interface between the same solid and a liquid."

[Term]
id: goldbook:WT07499
name: wolfram lamp
def: "Incandescent lamp that generates light by passing an electric current through a thin filament wire \(usually of wolfram\) until it is extremely hot. The lamps are often filled by a halogen gas such as iodine and bromine that allow filaments to work at higher temperatures and higher efficiencies."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06693
name: ξ-
def: "A symbol used to denote unknown configuration at a chiral centre."
synonym: "xi-" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06695
name: xanthene dyes
def: "Dyes derived by condensation of phthalic anhydride with resorcinol \(and derivatives\) or m-aminophenol \(and derivatives\)\, of which fluorescein is the prototype \(all such dyes have the xanthene nucleus\)."
synonym: "fluoresceins" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06696
name: xanthic acids
def: "Compounds having the structure $\\ce\{ROC\(=S\)SH\}$. Thus O-esters of dithiocarbonic acid. Salts and esters of xanthic acid are 'xanthates'. Use of this term is not recommended."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06697
name: xanthophylls
def: "A subclass of carotenoids consisting of the oxygenated carotenes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06699
name: xenon lamp
def: "An intense source of ultraviolet\, visible and near-infrared light produced by electrical discharge in xenon under high pressure."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06700
name: xerogel
def: "Open network formed by the removal of all swelling agents from a gel."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06703
name: X-radiation
def: "Radiation resulting from the interaction of high energy particles or photons with matter."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06709
name: X-ray escape peak
def: "In a gamma or X-ray spectrum\, the peak due to the photoelectric effect in the detector and escape\, from the sensitive part of the detector\, of the X-ray photon emitted as a result of the photoelectric effect."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06710
name: X-ray fluorescence
def: "The emission of characteristic X-radiation by an atom as a result of the interaction of electromagnetic radiation with its orbital electrons."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06711
name: X-ray fluorescence analysis
def: "A kind of analysis based on the measurement of the energies and intensities of characteristic X-radiation emitted by a test portion during irradiation with electromagnetic radiation."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06714
name: X-ray intensity
def: "Essentially all X-ray measurements are made by photon counting techniques but the results are seldom converted to radiant flux or irradiance or radiant exposure. The term photon flux would be appropriate if the measurements were corrected for detector efficiency but this is seldom done for X-ray chemical analysis. Therefore the term X-ray intensity\, \\\(I\\\)\, is commonly used and expressed as photons/unit time detected. Likewise the term relative X-ray intensity\, \\\(I_\{\\rm\{r\}\}\\\)\, is used to mean the intensity for the analyte in an unknown specimen divided by the intensity for a known concentration of the analyte element."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06715
name: X-ray level
def: "An electronic state occuring as the initial or final state of a process involving the absorption or emission of X-ray radiation. It represents a many-electron state which\, in the purely atomic case\, has total angular momentum \(\\\(J = L + S\\\)\) as a well defined quantum number."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06716
name: X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy
def: "Any technique in which the sample is bombarded with X-rays and photoelectrons produced by the sample are detected as a function of energy. ESCA \(Electron Spectroscopy for Chemical Analysis\) refers to the use of this technique to identify elements\, their concentrations\, and their chemical state within the sample."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06717
name: X-ray satellite
def: "A weak line in the same energy region as a normal X-ray line. Another name used for weak features is non-diagram line. Recommendations as to the use of these two terms have conflicted. Using the term diagram line as defined here\, non-diagram line may well be used for all lines with a different origin. The majority of these lines originate form the dipole-allowed de-excitation of multiply ionized or excited states\, and are called multiple-ionization satellites. A line where the initial state has two vacencies in the same shell\, notably the K-shell\, is called a hypersatellite. Other mechanisms leading to weak spectral features in X-ray emission are\, e.g. resonance emission\, the radiative Auger effect\, magnetic dipole and electric quadrupole transitions and\, in metals\, plasmon excitation. Atoms with open electron shells\, i.e. transition metals\, lanthanides and actinides\, show a splitting of certain X-ray lines due to the electron interaction involving this open shell. Structures originating in all these ways as well as structures in the valence band of molecules and solid chemical compounds have in the past been given satellite designations."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06718
name: X-ray spectroscopy
def: "X-ray spectroscopy consists of three steps\: \(a\) excitation to produce emission lines characteristic of the elements in the material\, \(b\) measurement of their intensity\, and \(c\) conversion of X-ray intensity to concentration by a calibration procedure which may include correction for matrix effects."

[Term]
id: goldbook:X06719
name: x unit
def: "Non-SI unit of length\: \\\(\\rm\{X\}\\approx 1.002\\times 10^\{-13\}\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\)"

[Term]
id: goldbook:XT06755
name: xenobiotic
def: "A xenobiotic \(Greek\, xenos 'foreign'; bios 'life'\) is a compound that is foreign to a living organism. Principal xenobiotics include\: drugs; carcinogens; and various compounds that have been introduced into the environment by artificial means."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06722
name: yard
def: "Non-SI unit of length\, \\\(\\rm\{yd\} = 3\\ \\rm\{ft\} = 0.9144\\ \\rm\{m\}\\\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06723
name: year
def: "Non-SI unit of time\, \\\(\\rm\{a\}=31\\ 556\\ 952\\ \\rm\{s\}\\\). The year is not commensurable with the day and not a constant. The value given corresponds to the Gregorian calendar year \(\\\(\\rm\{a\}=365.2425\\ \\rm\{d\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06724
name: yield
def: "Ratio expressing the efficiency of a mass conversion process. The yield coefficient is defined as the amount of cell mass \(\\\(\\pu\{kg\}\\\)\) or product formed \(\\\(\\pu\{kg\}\\\)\,\\\(\\pu\{mol\}\\\)\) related to the consumed substrate \(carbon or nitrogen source or oxygen in \\\(\\pu\{kg\}\\\) or \\\(\\pu\{moles\}\\\)\) or to the intracellular ATP production \(\\\(\\pu\{moles\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06727
name: yield stress
def: "The shear stress \\\(\\sigma _\{0\}\\\) or \\\(\\tau _\{0\}\\\) at which yielding starts abruptly. Its value depends on the criterion used to determine when yielding occurs."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06728
name: ylides
def: "Compounds in which an anionic site $\\ce\{Y^\{−\}\}$ \(originally on carbon\, but now including other atoms\) is attached directly to a heteroatom $\\ce\{X^\{+\}\}$ \(usually nitrogen\, phosphorus or sulfur\) carrying a formal positive charge. They are thus 1\,2-dipolar species of the type $\\ce\{R_\{m\}X^\{+\}–Y^\{−\}R^\{n\}\}$. If $\\ce\{X\}$ is a saturated atom of an element from the first row of the periodic system\, the ylide is commonly represented by a charge-separated form; if $\\ce\{X\}$ is a second\, third\, etc. row element uncharged canonical forms are available $\\ce\{R_\{m\}X=YR_\{n\}\}$. If $\\ce\{X\}$ is an unsaturated atom\, doubly bonded to another first row element $\\ce\{Z\}$\, the negative charge on $\\ce\{Y\}$ may be stabilized by $\\ce\{\\uppi\\!\\mbox\{-\}\}$conjugation\, $\\ce\{Z=X^\{+\}–Y^\{−\}Rn  Z^\{−\}–X^\{+\}=YR_\{n\}\}$. Such ylides belong to the class 1\,3 dipolar compounds. However\, 1\,3-dipolar compounds with only sextet-containing canonical forms \(e.g. vinylcarbenes\) are not ylides. E.g. $\\ce\{Ph3P^\{+\}–C^\{−\}H2  Ph3P^\{+\}=CH2\}$ \(often called a Wittig reagent\)\, $\\ce\{\(CH3\)3N^\{+\}–C^\{−\}H2\}$\, $\\ce\{RC#N^\{+\}N^\{−\}–R\}$\, $\\ce\{\(CH3\)2S=CHPh  \(CH3\)2S^\{+\}–C^\{−\}HPh\}$. Note that ylide is a complete word\, not to be confused with the suffix -ylide\, used for some radical anions. Subclasses of ylides\: Ylides $\\ce\{R_\{m\}X^\{+\}–C^\{−\}R2\}$ having the negative charge on carbon are classified by citing the name of the element $\\ce\{X\}$ before the word ylide. E.g. nitrogen ylide\, phosphorus ylide\, oxygen ylide\, sulfur ylide. A further specification may be achieved by citing the class name of $\\ce\{R_\{m\}X\}$ before the word ylide. Thus nitrogen ylides include amine ylides\, $\\ce\{R3N^\{+\}–C^\{−\}R2\}$\, azomethine ylides $\\ce\{R2C=N^\{+\}R–C^\{−\}R2\}$\, nitrile ylides\, $\\ce\{RC#N^\{+\}–C^\{−\}R2\}$. Some authors\, who wish to express the positive charge on $\\ce\{X\}$\, prefer e.g. ammonium ylides over amine ylides; such usage varies according to the heteroatom $\\ce\{X\}$ and to national custom. The ylides $\\ce\{R_\{m\}X^\{+\}–Y^\{−\}  R_\{m\}X=Y\}$ \($\\ce\{Y\} = \\ce\{O\}\, \\ce\{S\}\, \\ce\{Se\}\, \\ce\{Te\}\, \\ce\{NR\}$\) are usually named by citing the name of $\\ce\{R_\{m\}X\}$ followed by the additive nomenclature term for $\\ce\{Y\}$ \(oxide\, sulfide\, selenide\, telluride\, imide\, respectively\). E.g. amine imides; use of the less systematic synonyms amine imines and aminimines is discouraged. Some classes of ylides are known by trivial names e.g. nitrones\, Wittig reagents \(synonymous with phosphonium ylides\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06729
name: ynamines
def: "N\,N-Disubstituted alk-1-yn-1-amines\, $\\ce\{RC#CNR2\}$. By usage\, restricted to this type of acetylenic amine."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06730
name: ynols
def: "Alk-1-yn-1-ols\, $\\ce\{RC≡COH\}$; tautomeric with ketenes $\\ce\{RCH=C=O\}$."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06731
name: yocto
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-24\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{y\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06732
name: yotta
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{24\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{Y\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Y06734
name: Yukawa–Tsuno equation
def: "A multiparameter extension of the Hammett equation to quantify the role of enhanced resonance effect on the reactivity of meta- and para-substituted benzene derivatives\, e.g. \\\[\\log _\{10\}k = \\log _\{10\}k_\{0\}+\\rho \\ \[\\sigma +r\\ \(\\sigma^\{+\}-\\sigma\)\]\\\] The parameter \\\(r\\\) gives the enhanced resonance effect on the scale \(\\\(\\sigma ^\{+\}\\\,-\\sigma\\\)\) or \(\\\(\\sigma\\\,-\\sigma \\\)\)\, respectively."

[Term]
id: goldbook:YT07500
name: Yang photocyclization
def: "Intramolecular coupling of diradicals of different sizes \(1\,4-\, 1\,5-\, 1\,6-\, 1\,7-\, 1\,8-diradicals\, as well as very remote diradicals\) formed through intramolecular hydrogen abstraction by an excited ketone\, yielding cyclic alcohols of different sizes."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06737
name: <em>Z</em>-value
def: "An index of the ionizing power of a solvent based on the frequency of the longest wavelength electronic absorption maximum of 1-ethyl-4-methoxycarbonylpyridinium iodide in the solvent. The Z-value is defined by\: \\\[Z = \\frac\{\\pu\{2.859E4\}\}\{\\lambda\}\\\] where \\\(Z\\\) is in \\\(\\pu\{kcal mol-1\}\\\) and \\\(\\lambda \\\) is in \\\(\\pu\{nm\}\\\)."
synonym: "Kosower $Z$-value" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06739
name: Zeeman effect
def: "The splitting or shift of spectral lines due to the presence of an external magnetic field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06740
name: zepto
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{-21\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{z\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06741
name: zero field splitting
def: "The separation of multiplet sublevels in the absence of an external magnetic field."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06742
name: zero point
def: "Value of the pH of a solution\, which in combination with a stated outer reference electrode\, gives zero emf from the operational cell."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06743
name: zero point of scale
def: "The rest point of the properly adjusted balance with no load on the pans and the rider \(or chain\) in the zero position."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06744
name: zero–zero absorption or emission
def: "A purely electronic transition occurring between the lowest vibrational levels of two electronic states."
synonym: "0–0 absorption" []
synonym: "0–0 emission" []
synonym: "zero-zero absorption" []
synonym: "zero-zero emission" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06746
name: zetta
def: "SI prefix for \\\(10^\{21\}\\\) \(symbol\: \\\(\\mathrm\{Z\}\\\)\)."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06747
name: zig-zag projection
def: "A stereochemical projection for an acyclic molecule \(or portion of a molecule\) where the main chain is represented by a zig-zag line in the plane and the substituents are shown above or below the plane."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06748
name: Zimm plot
def: "A diagrammatic representation of data on scattering from large particles\, corresponding to the equation\: \\\[\\frac\{Kc\}\{\\Delta R \(\\theta\)\} = \\frac\{1\}\{\\bar\{M\}_\{\\rm\{w\}\} P\(\\theta\)\} + 2\\ A_\{2\}c + \\ldots\\\] and used for the simultaneous evaluation of the mass average molar mass\, \\\(\\bar\{M\}_\{\\rm\{w\}\}\\\)\, the second virial coefficient of the chemical potential\, \\\(A_\{2\}\\\)\, and \(usually\) the z-average radius of gyration\, \\\(< s^\{2\} > _\{\\rm\{z\}\}^\{\\frac\{1\}\{2\}\}\\\); \\\(c\\\) is the mass concentration of the solute\, \\\(\\Delta R\(\\theta\)\\\) the excess Rayleigh ratio\, and \\\(P \(\\theta \)\\\) the particle scattering function that comprises \(usually\) the z-average radius of gyration. \\\(K\\\) depends on the solute\, the temperature and the type of radiation employed. Several modifications of the Zimm plot are in frequent use; the most common one uses the excess scattering instead of the excess Rayleigh ratio."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06749
name: zone
def: "A region in the chromatographic bed where one or more components of the sample are located. The term band may also be used for it."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06750
name: zone melting method of preconcentration
def: "A technique based on the different solubility of microcomponents in the liquid and solid matrix. It has a limited field of application for the analysis of fusible \(and stable\) substances."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06751
name: Zucker–Hammett hypothesis
def: "This hypothesis states that\, if in an acid catalysed reaction\, \\\(\\log _\{10\}k_\{1\}\\\) \(first-order rate constant of the reaction\) is linear in \\\(H_\{0\}\\\) \(Hammett acidity function\)\, water is not involved in the transition state of the rate-controlling step. However\, if \\\(\\log _\{10\}k_\{1\}\\\) is linear in \\\(\\log _\{10\}\\left\[\\rm\{H\}^\{+\}\\right\]\\\) then water is involved. This has been shown to be incorrect by Hammett himself."

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06752
name: zwitterions
def: "Neutral compounds having formal unit electrical charges of opposite sign. Some chemists restrict the term to compounds with the charges on non-adjacent atoms. Sometimes referred to as inner salts\, dipolar ions \(a misnomer\). E.g. $\\ce\{H3N^\{+\}CH2C\(=O\)O^\{−\}\}$ ammonioacetate \(glycine\)\, $\\ce\{\(CH3\)3N^\{+\}–O^\{−\}\}$ trimethylamine oxide."
synonym: "ampholytes" []
synonym: "inner salts" []
synonym: "zwitterionic compounds" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:Z06753
name: zygote
def: "The cell obtained as a result of complete or partial fusion of meiotically \(cf. meiosis\) produced cells."

[Term]
id: goldbook:ZT07132
name: zero differential overlap approximation
def: "An approach to the systematic neglect of the small-in-value electron repulsion integrals which is used in a number of approximate self-consistent field molecular orbital schemes. It means that all the products of atomic orbitals \\\(\\upchi_\{\\upmu\} \\upchi_\{\\upnu\}\\\) are set to zero and the overlap integral \\\(\\rm\{S\}_\{\\upmu\\upnu\} = \\updelta_\{\\upmu\\upnu\}\\\) \(where \\\(\\updelta_\{\\upmu\\upnu\}\\\) is the Kronecker delta\). The ZDO approximation greatly simplifies the computation of wavefunctions by eliminating many of two-electron integrals. At the ZDO approximation all three- and four-centered integrals vanish."
synonym: "ZDO approximation" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:ZT07228
name: zwitterionic polymer
def: "Ampholytic polymer containing ionic groups of opposite sign\, commonly on the same pendant groups."
synonym: "polymeric inner salt" []

[Term]
id: goldbook:ZT07501
name: $\\pu\{\\rm\{Z\}_\{50\}\}$
def: "Number of cycles required to reduce by 50% the initial absorbance of the coloured form of a photochromic compound at a specific wavelength."
